akes him to the beach when the poor man can't lift a chair. She's the one who pushes him on the stage where he doesn't know where he is. But you notice how she leaves her out of the equation. Hmm. Odd.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, she does indeed.\par \par \uc2 Look, Laura, as Biden damages the American brand abroad, Dylan Mulvaney has demolished Bud Light. A former \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2  executive Anson Frericks puts it this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANSON FRERICKS, FOREMR \uc2 ANHEUSER-BUSCH\uc2  EXECUTIVE: This was a mistake. We wouldn't do this again because we've lost billions of dollars in market cap. Our brands are down 30 percent, and all of a sudden we're putting a lot of our suppliers at risk. They're laying off hundreds of people from jobs at some of their suppliers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Meanwhile, traveling abroad, don't worry about all those laid-off workers, Dylan Mulvaney feels unsafe, Laura. So the brand ambassadress has found a sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DYLAN MULVANEY: Surprise, I'm in Peru. And I'm at Machu Picchu. I feel very safe here. It's a little sad that I had to leave my country to feel safe. I still haven't been kissed yet, but I'm holding out hope.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, there are a lot of llamas in Peru, Laura, so maybe there's hope.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can I just say something.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But why if you're so -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I just -- the fake voice. It's all an act. Don't you feel like we're all being punked here? I mean the voice, the kitschy kind of approach, it's all so obvious to me. It's like we are all being played here.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And if you're so worried about your safety, why are you dropping a pin on your location in Peru? You may as well give them your address and phone number at this point.\par \par \uc2 But if you want a glaring example, Laura, of the fall of a once great- American brand, look no further than the \uc2 Disney\uc2  parks. Look. They're mostly empty. This was the slowest July 4th weekend in nearly a decade. A friend sent this picture to me. These are the parks. Wait times are cut in half.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile their movies, "Lightyear," "Strange World," "Elemental," even "Indiana Jones," they've all flamed out at the box office. But this is what happens when you abuse your audience, insert politics in family entertainment and they don't want to hear it anymore. They simply walk away. This is how "Sound of Freedom" beat the great "Indiana Jones."\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love it. Raymond, I still can't get over the fact that a few years ago some \uc2 Disney\uc2  minder was literally running after my sons and me at the park at quarter of 8:00 p.m. because we didn't have our masks on, but we wanted to hit the last rides before the park closed. They were running, literally going to tackle us. We outran them, though, and we still made the rides.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: News of the day, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 's extension as CEO has gone for another two years. But you know what? Like a lot of franchises, including "Indiana Jones," some shouldn't be revived too many times. This is one sequel that - -\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, how many times have you and your family been to \uc2 Disney\uc2 , how many times?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I've been going since I was five years old.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So 41, 42?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Almost every year of our lives, sometimes twice.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So if you're saying this, \uc2 Disney\uc2  should pay attention. If Raymond Arroyo is saying this, because you're a big fan of \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's too expensive, $300 a person per day, come on. It's ridiculous. And $30 in parking. Come on.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, a fast pass to our next break -- Raymond, thank you. If you thought radical teachers and administrators were after your kids before, what's just been uncovered inside a Seattle middle school is going to be chilling to you. That investigation, it's amazing, and it's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, if you thought gender indoctrination happening behind your back was bad enough, wait until you hear this next story. Documents obtained by Parents Defending Education reveal that certain Seattle public schools offer so-called gender reaffirming care on site at the school to students as young as 11 years old.\par \par \uc2 One of those schools, Meany Middle School, reveals right on the website that this, quote, health center is conveniently located across from the school's main office. And as you can see here, one of the medical services provided at no cost includes, again, so-called gender affirming care.\par \par \uc2 So how is this all paid for? Well, partially by Seattle taxpayers who voted for this investment back in 2018. But they're also supported by something called the Country Doctor Community Health Centers. Well, on their web site, the Country Doctor explains that it was founded by the Black Panthers in conjunction with the Seattle Liberation Front. It describes itself and its mission as helping the underserved access healthcare. How sick.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Nicole Neily, founder and president of Parents Defending Education, the organization responsible for bringing this shocking story to light. Nicole, thank you for being with us tonight. Now, we reached out to the middle school, the Country Doctor, and the Seattle government. The Seattle public school district did respond on the school's behalf, saying in part, "Access to these services enables to early intervention, prevention, and treatment of health-related barriers to learning with the goal of promoting school attendance and improved academic performance." How is gender, quote, affirming care improving academic performance?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE NEILY, PARENTS DEFENDING EDUCATION: Well, this is something that America's families need to know is being pushed at all levels of education. It's something certainly happening in Seattle but should worry families around the country. We have found programs like this in California, and certainly it's taking place in other schools as well. Because this is a priority of organizations like teachers unions, \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , that believe that community schools, putting health centers into schools, providing things from dental service, mental healthcare, to these reproductive services, gender affirming care, it takes a burden off families.\par \par \uc2 And again, underserved families, poor families, minority families that it's easier for mom and dad to not take time off of work, so we'll just do it all in the school. But again, this is done behind closed doors, and families have no idea what is happening on to them. And for this to be done to our children with our tax dollars is truly insult to injury.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, our producer, Nicole, went on that Meany Middle School health center webpage and dug into the registration forms for students to receive these services. And she was shocked to find out that not only can students receive and access this, quote, affirming care that they can register for, they can also get birth control, pregnancy, and abortion services at any age.\par \par \uc2 And then our producer went to submit the form -- sorry, he noticed that only one signature was required to register for this healthcare. And in Washington state, minors, we know, can get abortion and abortion-related services at any age without any consent of any authorized adult. Nicole, I don't think Americans watching this program tonight, watching THE ANGLE, have any idea this is happening.\par \par \uc2 NEILY: No, the medical age of consent in Washington state is 13, in California it's 12. And as we watch some of these abortion bills make their way through the states in the wake of the Dobbs decision, this is something abortion activists are pushing at the state level, where they're trying do this under the guise of empowering children. We don't want children to have STDs, to get pregnant. But then also at the same time it is stripping away parental notification, parental care.\par \par \uc2 And so a parent can come home and find their daughter bleeding out on the bathroom floor, not have any idea why or how it came to this, and then not be able to access the medical records to figure out what the heck is going on. It's interfering with our right to raise our children, to make medical decisions for them, and to keep them safe. It is appaling that this is being done in public schools.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Nicole, thank you for bringing this to light. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Now, we just got our hands on a preview of the GOP presidential primary. The Last Bite reveals it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, I'm told we have exclusive footage of \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and Asa Hutchinson fighting over the table scraps from the GOP contenders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, they're the cutest things ever. That is such a bad joke. All right, last night it was the sea lions snatching a fisherman's catch. Tonight elephants, that was fighting over a bagel, I believe. What animal will be fighting for it tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. Remember, it's always America now and forever. And Greg Gutfeld takes it all from here. Is there anything cuter than a baby elephant? I say now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230713ej7c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Republicans Grill FBI Director On Host Of Issues; Republicans Accused Of Politicizing \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  Hearing; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Reaffirms U.S. Support For Ukraine; Scrutiny Over Deadly Drugs Smuggled Across Border; GOP Amendment Slashes Abortion Travel Reimbursement; Tuberville Delaying Military Promotions; Vermont State Begins Cleanup Efforts After Worst Flood in 100 Years; Police in Southern California Use Aerial Drone To Help with 911 Emergency Calls; Senators Chris Coons (D-DE) \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  (R-ND) Interviewed On Bipartisan Efforts To Raise Tariffs On High Carbon Emission Products From China.\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, David Spunt, Mark Meredith, Griff Jenkins, Brit  Hume, Jonathan Turley, Chad Pergram, Nate Foy, Bret Baier, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 7,857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: How do you know a cat is embarrassed?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm not going to say it.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: This always happens when we have extra time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm going to shove this burger in your mouth.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right, well, that could be it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Gillian Turner. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Judge. Good to see you, guys.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says he is absolutely not protecting President Biden and his family. \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  faced intense questioning today up on Capitol Hill over the embattled FISA surveillance program, Republican investigations into the president's son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , and alleged political bias.\par \par \uc2 We got team coverage for you, Jonathan Turley and Brit Hume are going to join us to share their thoughts in just a moment. We begin first with David Spunt, he's at the Justice Department tonight. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Good evening. There were three narratives today at play during this hearing, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Wray came to talk about the good things the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are doing. Democrats talked about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans talked about what they perceive as weaponization of government.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): I'm curious, are you protecting the Bidens?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : Absolutely not, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has no interest in protecting anyone politically.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: Hold on, you won't answer the question about whether or not that's a shakedown.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Though he pledged to stay above the fray. \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  could not escape the politics that defined Wednesday's House Judiciary hearing.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARRY MOORE (R-AL): The number one concern and I come from a fairly rural district is weaponization of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ coming after conservative American citizens who just simply want to have a voice in the process.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: The idea that I'm biased against conservatives seems somewhat insane to me, given my own personal background.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Wray is a Republican. Democrats accused the Republicans of having an ulterior motive.\par \par \uc2 REP. JEROLD NADLER (D-NY): It is an elaborate show designed with only two purposes in mind, to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the consequences of his actions. And to return him to the White House in the next election.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Wray used his opening statement to talk 2022 statistics.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  arrested more than 20,000 violent criminals and child predators. That's an average of almost 60 bad guys taken off the streets per day.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): And he scoffed when Republicans criticized the Bureau for suppressing the COVID lab leak theory, pointing out the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the only intelligence agency that embraced that theory early on in the pandemic. He also took questions about the attack on the U.S. Capitol on January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: This notion that somehow the violence at the Capitol on January 6 was part of some operation orchestrated by \uc2 FBI\uc2  sources and agents is ludicrous and is a disservice to our brave, hardworking, dedicated men and women.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): The director also took heat for a memo put out by the Richmond, Virginia field office that proposed monitoring Catholics.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: As soon as I found out about it, I was aghast and ordered it withdrawn and removed from \uc2 FBI\uc2  systems.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): You were aghast and why don't you let us talk to the people who put it together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Wray said that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  will brief members of Congress after an internal review is complete later this summer. Republicans say that information is long overdue, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, David Spunt at DOJ for us this hour. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, let's get into today's hearing now with Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume and \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2  professor, Jonathan Turley. Gentlemen, thanks for being with me this evening.\par \par \uc2 Brit, I'll come to you first. Former President Trump was not at the hearing today. But he must have had an awfully itchy nose because folks could not stop talking about him. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HANK JOHNSON\uc2  (D-GA): Welcome to the legislative arm of the Trump reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): You're being attacked and vilified by some of the members of this committee and others outside this committee. Because the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has had the audacity to investigate serious allegations of criminal conduct by a former president.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED LIEU (D-CA): It is not the fault of \uc2 FBI\uc2  that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  surrounded himself with criminals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That was obviously a montage there of Democrats, but Republicans talked about him a whole lot too, allegations of spying on his 2016 campaign. What do you think, Brit?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Trump is the all- purpose defense and even the weapon of attack for Democrats against the Republicans in Congress. And some of those Republicans in Congress today very much worth sticking up for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and complaining about how he was -- how he was treated. So, I guess that furthers the democratic cause in some way.\par \par \uc2 Every time I watch \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  testified and I find myself somewhat sympathetic to him. He's got a tough job trying to turn the \uc2 FBI\uc2  around. He then turns around himself, and refuses to answer questions such as he was asked today. If there were -- was there one or more \uc2 FBI\uc2  informants in the crowd on January the 6th.\par \par \uc2 There is no reason that I can imagine why he can't answer that question. And yet he refused to do it. So, all that does is make people wonder. It furthers conspiracy theories that the whole thing was some kind of a put up job. I just can't understand it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Especially since, I mean, it's widely reported that plainclothes \uc2 FBI\uc2  officers are at events, big scale events all the time in the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jonathan, it would be surprising if they weren't there on January 6th. Jonathan, let's talk about social media for a second, the legal implications here. Obviously, accusations today from conservatives that the Biden administration was colluding with the platforms to censor free speech, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: We don't ask social media companies to censor information or suppress information when it comes to national security.\par \par \uc2 We will call social media companies attention to that. But at the end of the day, we're very clear that it's up to the social media companies decide whether to do something about it or not.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): A suggestion of the most powerful law enforcement operation is not a suggestion. It is, in fact, effectively an order.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think it is true. That was the finding of this judge. She just handed down 155 pages of findings involving the \uc2 FBI\uc2  censorship program and that of other agencies.\par \par \uc2 Look, none of us were expecting a Perry Mason moment where Wray would suddenly set up and say, I did it, I did it. But this was really maddening. You know, he had more details when \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  asked him about \uc2 FBI\uc2  family day, he went on length about that.\par \par \uc2 But when you deal with something that was just a subject of 105 -- 155 page report, he draws a blank, he repeatedly said that he didn't have knowledge of things that we've all been talking about. He constantly said that things were under investigation, even matters from January 6, and he can't comment on an ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 Literally, he offered nothing today. And that's really going to now push this committee, whether they are going to wait much longer for answers. He should have given them some answers today. There were matters there that he clearly as Brit said, could have answered.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I guess, Brit, It would seem then that the question before lawmakers and before the courts right now, really is how much communication should any White House going forward have with social media companies and should they have some more leeway to talk to the social media platforms during times of emergency, a pandemic, extreme weather, war time. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, that's the question. It will be before the appellate courts when they review this 155 page decision that Jonathan just described.\par \par \uc2 And there really is a serious question as to whether government agencies and those that work for them should be able to interact, at least to some extent with social media companies.\par \par \uc2 Now obviously, what happened during COVID and other times where they were basically being told to take things down and so on.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wray's testimony today, notwithstanding that it was always up to the companies as to whether to do it. When you get a call, as was suggested from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that says, we'd like you to take this down. Your instinct is probably going to be to do that.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that's been ruled to be a constitutional breach. And, you know, whether that will be devoid of that order, and all of its fullness will be upheld on appeal remains to be seen. My guess is a lot of it will.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes, I also I want to add that I thought that part of his testimony was the most dubious, he insisted that they only focused on foreign disinformation. That's just not true.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have ample evidence. We have e-mails from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , dealing with matters that were not foreign disinformation. This was -- they were complained to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that they were just doing word searches and asking for thousands of people to be banned.\par \par \uc2 So, either he just didn't know what his own agency was doing, or he was less than forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm guessing you're referring to the e-mails that were about, like COVID vaccines and that kind of thing?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: That's right. I mean, there were -- there were efforts to ban jokes. I mean, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , but specifically the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had a huge operation going and the most that Wray could say is, well, we really -- we're focused on foreign disinformation, that was not the focus that comes out of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Brit, you get the last 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Wray probably sees his job as to straighten things out of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , yes. But at the same time to have the back of the multitudes of honest and honorable \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents going about their business in a proper way.\par \par \uc2 The problem for him is that when he goes before Congress or other bodies, it seems like what he thinks his job to do is to say as little as possible and tell us a little as possible, which may end up harming the \uc2 FBI\uc2  more than if he were forthcoming. And I think that's where we are.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, gentlemen, thanks so much for taking time with us tonight. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: President Biden and the \uc2 G7\uc2  nations are vowing now to support Ukraine for the long haul. They say the country is likely to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  but at some point down the road in the future.\par \par \uc2 The president tonight is in Finland, but this follows two days of summiting and Lithuania. Correspondent Mark Meredith joins us from the White House to wrap it all up tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, Lithuania.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Speaking at a Lithuanian University, President Biden pledged to deepen us ties with Baltic States while also condemning Russia's war with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our commitment to Ukraine will not weaken. We will stand for liberty and freedom today, tomorrow and for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Hours earlier, the president sat down for a bilateral meeting with Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today, the U.S. and fellow \uc2 G7\uc2  nations pledging continued support for Ukraine's military economy and government. The commitment coming one day after Zelenskyy criticized \uc2 NATO\uc2  for not granting his country immediate membership.\par \par \uc2 Today, Mr. Biden promised Ukraine will join the alliance eventually.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I hope we finally have put to bed the notion about whether or not Ukraine is welcome in \uc2 NATO\uc2  is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Zelenskyy is thanking the American people for their continued support.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: You spend this money for our lives. And I I think that we save the lives for Europe and for all the world.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Tonight, Russia is warning more assistants will only prolong the war. A Kremlin spokesperson telling reporters we consider this extremely ill-judged and potentially very dangerous, adding, by providing security guarantees to Ukraine, they're infringing on Russia's security.\par \par \uc2 As President Biden arrives in Finland, his staff is defending his travel itinerary, White House say the president purposely skipped a dinner with world leaders to plan for today's events and to handle other business including a call with Vermont's governor to address historic flooding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): And tonight, we're learning what's next for Sweden's application into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Today, Turkey's president said his country is still going to work to remove those roadblocks, allow Sweden to join the Alliance.\par \par \uc2 But President Erdogan also warned that it's going to take some time and that the earliest he thinks his country's going to get to it will be October, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Mark Meredith on the North Lawn for us tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Most Republicans now are criticizing President Biden's administration for failing to stop drug traffickers from smuggling fentanyl across the southern border in massive quantities. Tonight we're going to look at just how severe the situation has become. Correspondent Griff Jenkins shows us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PAPADOPOULOS, \uc2 DRUG ENFORCEMENT ADMINISTRATION\uc2 : These cartels pose the greatest criminal drug threat that the United States has ever faced.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Administration officials from the DEA, DHS, CBP and the \uc2 Office of National Drug Control Policy\uc2  sounding the alarm on fentanyl's growing threat.\par \par \uc2 KEMP CHESTER, BIDEN DRUG CONTROL POLICY ADVISER: Someone in America dies from a drug overdose or poisoning about every five minutes of every hour of every day.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Warning lawmakers of a highly organized criminal pipelines.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN CAGEN, ASSISTANT DIRECTOR, HOMELAND SECURITY INVESTIGATIONS: Mexican cartels have taken over fentanyl production and operate on an industrial scale, where they obtained precursor chemicals from China and synthesize these chemicals in Mexico to produce the deadly poison.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): CBP reports seizing 1.1 billion potential doses of fentanyl in fiscal year 2022, up more than 200 percent compared to fiscal years 2019 and 2020 combined. It's unknown how much gets through, bringing untold heartache.\par \par \uc2 Alexandra Capelouto was a college student who bought what she thought was oxycodone not knowing it was laced with fentanyl, taking her life. Now, her grieving father wants answers.\par \par \uc2 MATT CAPELOUTO, FATHER OF ALEXANDRA CAPELOUTO: If we can't even stop the drug dealers right here in our own communities, how do we expect to deal with China and Mexico for their role in this?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Speaker McCarthy tells Fox News he is making it a priority. But he's also pointing fingers.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): 300 Americans will be poisoned today and die. That's the equivalent of an airliner crashing every day in America. And this administration has turned a blind eye.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): The White House is pushing back saying they're committed to working with Congress in releasing a plan just yesterday to address the growing threat of a deadly combination of fentanyl mixed with Xylazine, that Gillian is called Tranq commonly on the streets, it's getting worse as well.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The facts are pretty terrible to think. Griff, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, inflation right now is the lowest it's been in 2-1/2 years since, at least, back in early 2021. Prices were up three percent in June compared to a year earlier. That is down from the four percent figure from last May to this year.\par \par \uc2