2 Correspondent Nate Foy is in Barre, Vermont tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Residents in Vermont are assisting the state's cleanup effort. During a once in a century flood, Vermont's worst since 1927.\par \par \uc2 DAPHINE ZENCEY, RESIDENT, VERMONT: We just want to do our best to get our community back up, and earning as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Governor \uc2 Phil Scott\uc2  toured damaged homes today along with Senators Bernie Sanders and Peter Welch, and FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2 , who promises more help from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , FEMA ADMINISTRATOR: We will continue to send additional resources as we work with the governor and his team to understand what the needs are as we go into the recovery phase.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Since Sunday, crews are responsible for over 200 rescues and 100 evacuations. Three miles north of Vermont's capital water levels at the Wrightsville Dam will continue to be monitored after city officials say it came within one foot of overflowing Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 PHIL SCOTT\uc2 , (R) VERMONT: One of my biggest concerns is what about Thursday and Friday? We are monitoring the situation and trying to make sure that we allow for some capacity as we can to lower -- create more reservoir capacity at Wrightsville in particular.\par \par \uc2 FOY: And with more rain expected later in the week, business owners already dealing with extensive damage are bracing for more.\par \par \uc2 KIP ROBERTS, ONION RIVER OUTDOORS: Certainly, it's a pretty large inventory, bitter pill to swallow at the moment. Right now, we're just kind of focused on putting one foot ahead of the next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Thankfully, as of 8:00 a.m. this morning, officials say no one had been killed as a result of this flooding. Rescue operations continue as so many start the long process of rebuilding their homes and businesses that have been damaged or destroyed. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Nate Foy in Barre, Vermont, for us tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A look at this, a man seen on video attacking a pub owner next to his four- year-old son right here in Washington has accepted a plea deal from prosecutors. WRC TV reports Gregory Gibson was charged with assault with dangerous weapon and also possession of a firearm during a crime of violence. Monday, he pleaded guilty to lesser charges. The pub owner says he urged prosecutors not to offer a plea deal, but they blamed limited resources. Gibson has now been released for custody until his sentencing.\par \par \uc2 And law enforcement officials in southern California are using a brand new tool to fight crime, unmanned aerial vehicles, also known as drones. Not everyone is comfortable with the move, though. Correspondent Bill Melugin in Santa Monica tells us why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Santa Monica police new deploying a new eye in the sky to respond to 911 calls -- drones. They can get to scenes faster than a patrol car, as quickly as 30 seconds. The police have been using the drone to respond to 15 to 20 calls per day, and they say the technology is a game changer.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER PETER LASHLEY, SANTA MONICA, CALIFORNIA, POLICE: They're getting real time information and allowing them to go in and deal with the situation, and it's a huge advantage for them, relieving their stress, giving them that situational awareness that you couldn't possibly have without this type of technology in play.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Their operators can see what is happening in real time and relay that information to responding officers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're going to take a look at Fifth and Broadway for a subject in the roadway.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Here are real world incident the drone assisted in. In this case