ntries. The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . says there are real growing concerns now the country is sliding into a full-scale civil war.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight, vandals set fire and destroyed seminal artwork by one of Italy's most famous living artists, this right outside Naples city hall. The Michelangelo Pistoletto artwork titled "Venus of the Rags" have been in display since late June. The artist says there can be a lot of reasons for the attack and that, quote, "The world is going up in flames anyway." That's one way to look at it.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. This evening we see how Republicans and Democrats in Congress, at least some of them, are actually working together to get things done. Here's my colleague Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for our "Common Ground" segment. Joining us tonight, Delaware Democratic Senator Chris Coons and North Dakota Republican Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2 . Senators, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are all working on something called the Prove It Act, which essentially does what?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: It proves it. What it does is it demonstrates what I think we already suspect if not know, and that is that American businesses are put on a global disadvantage when other countries produce the same and manufacture the same products we do that are carbon intensive. What we want to do is prove that American industry is the cleanest industry in the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So some stats on that. Total CO2 emission in 2020, million metric tons, United States 4,258, China 10,081. If you look at this chart of annual emissions, China goes through the roof in recent years, Senator Coons. And obviously this bill is a little bit pointed that way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS, (D-DE): So my hope is this will lay the foundation for us to better protect American manufacturing. We manufacture steel and aluminum, glass and cement here in the United States in a low emissions way. The Chinese, the Russians, the Indians, other countries ship their products into our economy that are much higher emissions products. And if we ultimately end up putting tariffs on those high emissions products to protect our low emissions products, I think that would be a good outcome. But Kevin and I are partnering on an initial bill, the Prove It Act, that does the study to lay the scientific foundation for whether or not we might do that in the future.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But don't you kind of know the answers? You got the top steel producing countries in 2022, China overwhelmingly. We are actually fourth in this list. Why not just say, OK, we kind of know the answer, why don't we just increase the tariffs?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, there's a couple of reasons for that. One is other countries will want to use the lack of evidence against us, and already do to some degree. We also have trading partners, particularly in Europe, that have carbon border adjustment mechanisms that have already done this study. So we need to have demonstrated the same evidence, to Chris's point, so that if it comes down to that, we can, again, prove that we're at par, if not cleaner than they are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you all hopeful about U.S.-China relations, or are you skeptic? Are you worried? What's the sense, Senator Coons?\par \par \uc2 COONS: There's lots of tension in our relationship. We have a robust economic relationship. We are big trading partners. But in terms of national security, protecting America's invention and innovation, and frankly some of the grievance that's come from decades of our good jobs being shipped to China, there's a lot of tensions in the relationship. I'm encouraged by the recent visits by Secretary Yellen, Secretary Blinken to China. It has taken it from an all-time low point to at least reopening conversation. But frankly, we have very different systems. The Chinese Communist Party is really in control of that nation and its systems, and they are increasingly aggressive globally and in their region.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You all work on a number of different things. There is a lot of common ground. More and more of your colleagues are coming up here to talk about what they are working on. Ukraine seems to be a bipartisan unity factor up there. Is that still true, even on the Republican side?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, it's still true. And I think it's largely true, in fact. And I would say it's one of those things that ebbs and flows a little bit, Bret. We have probably a little more concern because we have people that are concerned about our fiscal situation in the United States appropriately, concerned about the open southern border. But all of those, as important as they are, still shouldn't keep us from doing what we know to be the right thing and what we have bipartisan support for, and that is supporting Ukraine, and by doing so, making sure that the United States is also safe and that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is strengthened.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the cluster munitions move, there is a story that, "Unprepared for the long war, \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  under the gun to make more ammo." As we are giving these cluster munitions, "The Army is spending about $1.5 billion to try to ramp up production of 155-millimeter rounds." The president just said the other day we are running low.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That's right. I supported the president's decision to dip into our stocks of cluster munitions to make sure the Ukrainians don't run out of 155 rounds during this counteroffensive. He has a plan. We are on a path towards strengthening production here in the United States as well as in Europe. But I'll remind you, the Ukrainians are running through these artillery shells at an amazing rate. They are firing 6,000 to 8,000 a day, that's a million a year. We have to, in the long run, have a stronger industrial base.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have talked a number of times about working across the aisle, and you have highlighted that. For people at who say this place is broken and it's all partisan, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, I appreciate this opportunity, Bret, to showcase these things, because several networks don't. They showcase the confrontation. It's more fun to follow.\par \par \uc2 That said, Chris and I work on a number of things together. Low income housing reforms, for example. I would also just say that Chris and I pray together on Wednesday mornings at the bipartisan prayer breakfast, Senate prayer breakfast. There is a lot more unity than people get to see, and I appreciate the opportunity to highlight a little more of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are praying that all works out. Senators, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 COONS: Bret, thanks for the segment. Thanks for a chance to be on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, you can see all the "Common Ground" segment on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and "The All-Star Panel" podcast under the big umbrella, Bret Baier Podcasts, FOXNewsPodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see an extended discussion of this discussion on FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, with that, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the panel on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's director congressional testimony plus the latest out of the NATO summit. And then, later, firefighter rescues a dog and finds out the two have something very special in common. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: When