answers on allegations that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  targeted Catholic churches, school boards, or \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , to say the least. So, if you look at that hearing and all the different topics that were covered, I don't think that was anything that \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  could have said that was going to convince Republicans or, more importantly, conservative voters that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  doesn't need to be torn down and rebuilt from the studs.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What do you say, Juan? Is there another way?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the problem is, Gillian, there are 435 members of Congress. A lot of them are trying to get attention from the media. They want people to know that they are there, that they are doing their jobs, that they're being aggressive with people who are in power. They've built up what I think of is this myth of weaponization of government. Wait a second, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 ? \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  is a Trump appointee, a lifelong Republican, he is a member of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 . The idea he is covering up for Biden or attacking a former Republican president, Former President Trump, he doesn't see it as real.\par \par \uc2 And to me, and I think everybody on the congressional package said this today, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has real work to do. Just last week a major drug bust and the like. And they have got to protect us. So the idea that he is there really as part of some theater for these congressmen I find a little offensive for a guy, he is doing a hard job.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is a hard job. I think today he called the idea that he might be politically motivated insane.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, another exchange that got a lot of attention today was very heated was between Wray and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2 , (R-FL): I am sitting here waiting for the call with my father. Sounds like a shakedown, doesn't it, Director?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: I'm not going to get into commenting on that.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: You seem deeply uncurious about it, don't you? Almost suspiciously uncurious. Are you protecting the Bidens?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Absolutely not. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has does not -- has no interest.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: You won't answer the question. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Mollie, does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director have an interest in protecting the president's family?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, I think an exchange like this explains why a lot of Americans care less about whether \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  has an "R" by his name and much more about how he has handled his administration of the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 . He has been the head of that since 2017. That's the entirety of the investigation into the Biden family business.\par \par \uc2 So, all of these questions people have about the obstruction into that investigation -- people read an exchange like that, they watch an exchange like that, and they see that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was texting Chinese officials doing this kind of shakedown, and \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  seems to be completely uninterested in doing anything about it. The record of what was actually done to investigate it is appalling.\par \par \uc2 And that's just one example. We have investigations into school parents who are concerned about their school boards instead of -- instead of actually fighting crime. We have the Russia collusion hoax which \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  did nothing to hold people accountable for that. And that was so damaging to the country.\par \par \uc2 And so I think a lot of people in Washington, if they were to be honest, would admit that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Department of Justice have a very big problem. But they also know that if they were to tackle that, it would, admittedly, help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's one of his big cases. And they are not willing to do it. And so they are allowing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Department of Justice to be weaponized and to be politicized rather than to tackle this huge problem.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I want to turn our attention -- we could keep going with this all night, but I want to make sure we talk about \uc2 NATO\uc2 , too. Big events happening on the world stage today. Phil, today President Zelenskyy said essentially that Ukraine is taking on this tremendous burden, fighting this war against Putin essentially so that the rest of Europe and the United States doesn't have to. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: This money for not just funding. You spend this money for our lives, and I think that we save the lives for Europe and all the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Then this. This was released by the group of nations today. They say "Ukraine's future is in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We will be in a position to an extend an invitation to Ukraine to join the alliance when allies agree and conditions are met." Zelenskyy, obviously, very disappointed, angry, even, it seemed. Does he have a right to be?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: I don't think that his anger is surprising. Certainly Zelenskyy would have liked to be a member nation of \uc2 NATO\uc2  not just yesterday but probably last year.\par \par \uc2 But a really funny thing happened on his way to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  forum. You saw Ukrainian president chastened for his grumbling about the slow walking of Ukrainian membership into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And the response from the west was essentially pump the brakes, with John Kirby, a National Security Council spokesperson saying yes, we believe that Ukraine's future is in \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but not right now. And you had the United Kingdom defense minister when he was presented with a list of supplies and munitions that Ukraine wanted publicly say the United Kingdom is not \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So these are not the sort of things that we have heard from western allies to date, and they are not the sort of things that you would say to someone who has been built up as a sort of Churchillian figure. I think there is a little bit of frustration.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, the president's critics, though, say he's essentially giving the Ukrainians enough weaponry to keep them from losing the war, but that's not enough.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think there is even criticism of the fact that he has given them some weaponry and whether or not it's maybe extreme because there is some potential damage or risk to civilians. But overall, what we look at coming out of this summit has been a good week for Ukraine. They have got more advanced weaponry. I think the door has been opened to \uc2 NATO\uc2  for them. And I think when you look at it, Sweden is coming into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . I think Russia in general had a very bad week.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Mollie, you get to wrap us up.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: The \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance has currently seemed to be something where the United States basically funds all of these countries' defense. And what American taxpayers would like to see is much for support than the European countries themselves.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Panel, thanks so much for joining me. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Nice to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, this is something I'm going to remember probably for the rest of my career.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: After getting a call to save a dog stuck under his owner's house, an Arizona firefighter had an unforgettable mission. Max Robbins geared up and went under. He reached in, grabbed the dog's leg to pull him out. The part that makes this rescue special is that once he got him he found out that they actually had the same name. Max the firefighter saved Max the dog.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, part of our "Whatever Happened To" series, Bret Baier is going to take a look at ongoing investigation into that infamous Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching us. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington. JESSE WATTERS PRIME TIME starts right now. Hi, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230713ej7c0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with GOP 2024 Presidential Candidate Nikki Haley; Rep Jake Auchincloss (D-MA) is Interviewed about China and Afghanistan; Concerns About Flight Disruptions; Poll on Pride and Patriotism; A Look at the Art in Embassies Program.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Alexandria Hoff, Richard Fowler, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 10,477 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A series of sweeping rulings by a sharply divided Supreme Court, including one striking down President Biden's student loan forgiveness plan ignites the left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need to find a new way and we're moving as fast as we can.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The White House insisting the fight is not over after high court scraps one of the president's signature domestic policy proposals.\par \par \uc2 Plus, other 63 rulings effectively end race-based college admissions, and guarantee protection for free speech when it comes to business owners.\par \par \uc2 LORIE SMITH, WEB DESIGNER: I am grateful for the court's decision. The government cannot force anyone to say something they don't believe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Also, unanimously paving the way for more religious accommodations in the workplace. The court's liberal justices joining with the president, blasting the ruling saying the court is taking the country backward.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Is this a rogue court?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is not a normal court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll get reaction from Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley who joins me exclusively on the monumental week for the high court. We'll also drill down on Haley's tough talk on the president's handling of U.S.-China relations.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He's going to keep ignoring the Chinese threat. We're falling behind.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And bring in Massachusetts Congressman Jake Auchincloss who is challenging Haley's foreign policy creds.\par \par \uc2 Then, the president makes his economic pitch to voters, saying his policies are good for their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Bidenomics is just another way of saying restore the American dream.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel whether Americans are buying it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the art of diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is nothing like being able to set the stage for a conversation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We take you inside a surprising and beautiful tool exerting America's influence overseas.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with striking images out of France, where protesters have clashed for days with police. There are new reports overnight that rioters reportedly targeted a mayor's home with a burning car he says injuring his wife and one of his children. This is the fifth night of unrest sparked by the police killing of a teenager.\par \par \uc2 Police made more than 700 arrests nationwide. This is the country's worst social upheaval in years, and it poses new challenges to French President Emanuel Macron. We will continue to bring you updates on the developing story as they come in.\par \par \uc2 Now to the major story here in the U.S. this morning. In just the last year, the Supreme Court's conservative majority has delivered game-changing rulings on the nation's biggest cultural issues. Last summer, it was the end of Roe v. Wade. And this past week, the court struck down affirmative action in college admissions and President Biden's student debt relief plan.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives have cheered the rulings, progressives have expressed outrage and some have said it's time for the president to expand the court. Advocates on all sides are looking ahead to 2024. In just a minute, we'll speak live with Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley who says the court got it right on affirmative action, student loans and more.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Alexandria Hoff live at the White House with the latest on the president's reaction to the Supreme Court's decisions this week, at one point calling the court not normal -- Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 That's right. The president used some strong phrasing to condemn the rulings and the court itself, calling the student loan decision unthinkable and wrong, but the White House says that it was prepared.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the court misinterpreted the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): The president's last word on the last decision handed down by the Supreme Court this week rounding out a trio of 6-3 blows to the Biden administration. On Friday, the court derailed the president's plan to use the 2003 Heroes Act to wipe out $430 billion in student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts citing a past statement by former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in his majority opinion.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): People think that the president of the United States has the power for debt forgiveness. He does not.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The three Democrat-appointed justices came to the plan's defense in their dissent. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  called the court, quote, MAGA captured.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They ruled against it, 100 percent against it. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: At a campaign rally in South Carolina, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  celebrated the court's decision as President Biden vowed to press forward with a new tactic to fulfill his 2020 campaign promise.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will ground this new approach with a different law than my original plan, the so-called Higher Education Act.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: And higher education itself also reeling Thursday when the court effectively ended the use of race-based affirmative action in college admissions. Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  wrote in her dissents, though, deeming race irrelevant in law does not make it so in life.\par \par \uc2 With Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  firing back: Justice Jackson's race-infused world view falls flat at each step.\par \par \uc2 The ideological divide was also clear Friday when the court ruled that a Colorado graphic designer's refusal to create certain LGBTQ related expressive content was protected by the First Amendment. The dissenting opinion called it a setback for gay rights.\par \par \uc2 For the Biden reelection campaign, the hope is that he can turn the legal losses into voter motivation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): And the president expressed this week that he's also looking to turn around the term Bidenomics from something that was used somewhat negatively by opponents into a positive to sell his administration, excuse me, and their economic policies -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, we'll talk about that more coming up.\par \par \uc2 Alexandria Hoff at the White House, Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican presidential candidate, former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . ambassador and former governor, Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to start with France. These images disturbing overnight, this word of an attack on mayor's home and, you know, hundreds now arrested.\par \par \uc2 When you were ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . -- you had differences with \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  -- but knowing that the U.S. has walked through similar turmoil in recent years, any words of advice or wisdom to the president there or to the people of France?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, he's going to have to make sure that he listens to his people and he's got to bring calm. It's a tough situation. I mean, we've seen that happen in the United States and at the end of the day, what wins is law and order, and making sure that people understand what you're doing and how you're doing it. And so, we'll see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's turn to our brand new FOX News polling, put this up on the screen. Former President Trump has actually gained in this latest polling. You sit at 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, the president has called you disloyal. He's questioned your honor. He is steamrolling at this point the rest of the field.\par \par \uc2 What is your path forward to this nomination?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think that we can look at past presidential elections and understand national polls just don't matter right now.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we've got a debate in August. We got a debate in September. We got a debate in November. I am qualified to be on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 Having been 3 percent in five-way governor's race, I know that this is all about hard work. And I will tell you, we have done 30 events plus in New Hampshire. We've done 25 in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 And the people of New Hampshire, Iowa and South Carolina and Nevada will tell you, it is not about what the press or what the national polls say. It's not about how many people show up at your rallies. It's how many times you show up and how hard of the questions you answer and how many hands you shake.\par \par \uc2 And that's we're doing. We're not doing shortcuts. We're doing it the old- fashioned way, the hard way.\par \par \uc2 And I am confident that by fall, you're going to see those numbers are completely different than what they are right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's talk to you some of the things you will face on the campaign trail, and the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decisions this week, Democrats actually say they show the court is dangerous. Former President Trump is out there saying this shows my gift to the American people, the three justices I put into the court.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some reaction on a couple of these. The first is that web designer who didn't want to do custom design for things that conflict with her religious beliefs, including same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  said this: No person or business should be given a license to discriminate. That's exactly what today's wrongheaded Supreme Court decision will unleash.\par \par \uc2 The court also struck down the president's student loan forgiveness plan, also ended race-based admissions into college.\par \par \uc2 AFT (ph) president \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  reacting to both of those said: Between the affirmative action case and the decision on student debt, the court has decided that if you or your family has the ability and means, you can succeed, but if you're a struggler or a striver, you are on your own.\par \par \uc2 So, the president, the left saying these decisions are not good for real Americans, people in protected classes, Black and Brown students, and potential applicants.\par \par \uc2 Is there a danger here that Republicans run a risk? You won Dobbs last year, you win these legal battles, but they wind up hurting you politically?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: This was fantastic win for individual liberty and freedom. I think whether you look at the fact that -- you know, first of all, the Democrats are upset because things didn't go their way, but people won. This is about empowering people and their rights.\par \par \uc2 You know, whether it's making sure you're not picking winners and losers on whose loans you forgive, or whether it's the fact that you're deciding who gets into college based on race that you can't control. Whether it's the web designer -- look, gay people have the right to marry the same way a web designer has the right to say, according to their religion, they don't want to do it. So, the people won in these cases.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think this hurt us at all. I think that the liberals will try and use this against us. But I think at the end of the day, what the Supreme Court showed is regardless of where you're born or raised, what color you are, what gender you are, you are going to be able to have individual freedom.\par \par \uc2 And that's a win for the United States of America. It's a win for people, and I think we should all celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. One of the other contenders in your race, former Vice President Pence was here last week on "FOX News Sunday". He reiterated his call that everybody in the field on the GOP side should commit to a nationwide ban on abortion at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 You said not realistic to tell people you could do it. You don't have the votes in the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 If you got a red wave in '24 and you're a President Haley, what would you agree to in a national level? If you had the votes, where would you draw the line? Or do you think a national ban is appropriate?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think, first of all, Shannon, we do have to be honest with the American people and I don't think they've been honest with them on that.\par \par \uc2 You know, first of all, I will say I'm absolutely pro-life, unapologetically, not because the Republican Party tells me to be, but because my husband was adopted and I had trouble having both of my children.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, I don't judge anyone for being pro-choice, anymore than I am pro-life. I think that we have to humanize this situation and stop demonizing it. But in order to get a federal law, you have to have 60 Senate votes, a majority of the House, and a presidential signature.\par \par \uc2 We might have 45, 45 pro-life senators. So, we've got a long way to go. We need to look at this and say, how do we make sure we save as many babies as we can and support as many moms as we can?\par \par \uc2 And so, in order to do that, we're going to have to say, how do we get 60 votes? And I think that we should find consensus -- consensus on saying let's not have late-term abortion. Consensus on the fact that if doctors and nurses don't believe in abortion, they shouldn't have to perform it.\par \par \uc2 Consensus on the fact that let's encourage more adoptions that are good quality adoptions. Consensus on the fact that we should have contracept -- that contraception should be available. And consensus that no state law should put a woman in jail or give her the death penalty for having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Let's start there, and whatever 60 Senate votes come to, whether that's 15 weeks, I absolutely would sign it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's talk foreign policy, a big part of your resume. You gave a speech on China today with a lot of warnings in there, talking about how past and current administrations have, in your estimation, bungled this relationship and lost important ground.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest who's coming up, like your husband, has worn the uniform and Congressman Jake Auchincloss says that you're not the right person to speak to the issue of China.\par \par \uc2 He says this: you shouldn't suggest that China's military in any respect has gotten stronger than the U.S., that you served administration that left the U.S. in a weaker position, that cozied up to President Xi, and that you would bring back failed foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 So, knowing he's coming up, what do you say to that critique?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think God bless him because if you look at the nav -- the military situation, they now have the largest naval fleet in the world. They have 340 ships. We have 293. They're going to have 400 in two years. We won't even have 350 in two decades.\par \par \uc2 They have started developing hypersonic missiles. We are just now getting started. They are modernizing their military. Our military is taking gender pronoun classes.\par \par \uc2 You know, you look at what they're doing on cyber, artificial intelligence. Space, they're ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 So, now, we have some work to do and we do need to strengthen our military. And we have to make sure we're right.\par \par \uc2 China has been preparing for war with us for decades and the way we have to deal with China is not look at it tomorrow, we -- because if we keep waiting to deal with them tomorrow, they will deal with us today.\par \par \uc2 We have to deal with them diplomatically. We have to deal with them economically. We have to deal with them militarily.\par \par \uc2 But from a domestic standpoint, first of all, let's make sure that they stop buying up U.S. farmland. They bought 400,000 acres near our military installations.\par \par \uc2 Let's the infiltration out of our universities, where they're paying millions of dollars to spread propaganda.\par \par \uc2 Let's make sure they stop bringing the fentanyl across the border. If that that means we end normal trade relations with them to do that, we do that, until they stop killing Americans.\par \par \uc2 And let's stop the lobbying that has happened from Chinese companies that are just a front for the Chinese communist party. Those like \uc2 Huawei\uc2  and Tencent and \uc2 Alibaba\uc2 , they not -- they need to stop lobbying America. And I think that we have some work to do.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to economically, they're stealing $600 billion of intellectual property from us. That's how they just came out with a fifth generation fighter jet, and we handed it to `em on a silver platter.\par \par \uc2 We have the Commerce Department that has a list of sensitive technologies that should not be sold to China, yet it's not a black list and the Biden administration approved 70 percent of those licenses last year.\par \par \uc2 And militarily, we better get to work. We have to build a strong military. We have to stop all of these favored Democrats -- these favored contracts to military companies that shouldn't be getting them. We've got to cut out the bureaucracy and the red tape. And we've got to get to work to strengthen our military. So no, we've got a lot to do with China. And we've got to get it done today.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, quickly, before we go, I want to get some clarification on where you are on birthright citizenship, this, you know, notion that the 14th Amendment is guaranteed. People who were born here are U.S. citizens. A spokesperson of yours said that you oppose birthright citizenship for those who enter the country illegally. But of course, this is for people who are born here. What's your position?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: For the 5 million people who've entered our country illegally, I am against birthright citizenship. For those that are in this country legally, of course, I think we go according to the Constitution, and that's fine. But it's the illegal immigration that we have to make sure that just because they get here, if they have a child, you're just building on the problems that have if you're making those children citizens and then allowing them to stay because of that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so people who come here illegally and then have a child here, you say that child should not have the guaranteed citizenship?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: That's right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. All right, Governor, great to see you. We'll see you out there on the campaign trail. Thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a reminder, Fox News Channel is your place for the first 24 Republican primary debate, Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum will moderate that debate live in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, August 23. Coverage begins that night 9 p.m. eastern on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're bringing our Sunday group to read between the lines on all those blockbuster Supreme Court rulings. What they could mean for you in your real life and how both sides are hoping to leverage them to sway voters at the ballot box next year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: We cannot let this decision be the last word, or the court can render a decision. It cannot change what America stands for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden expressing his disapproval of the Supreme Court's decision to strike down the use of race in college admissions. Time now for our Sunday group. Bloomberg TV Washington Correspondent Annmarie Hordern, Fox News Contributor Richard Fowler, former RNC Communications Director Doug Heye, and Concerned Women for America CEO and President Penny Nance.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. It has been a blockbuster week when it comes to the Supreme Court. This is it, the end of June. So let's talk about this college admissions case. The Chief Justice writing for the majority said this, the students must be treated based on his or her experiences as an individual not on the basis of race, many universities have for too long done just the opposite. Doug, you have connections to both of the cases.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the schools in these cases UNC and Harvard.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yeah, I'm member of the Board of Visitors at North Carolina and, you know, North Carolina is obviously a politically important state coming up in 2024. And what we see so often there are headlines and then the devils in the details. This is a much more narrow decision than the blockbuster announcement as people are running outside of the Supreme Court. But a lot of how this is going to be interpreted in the months in the years coming ahead is how do the schools handle this? So we -- we saw some college presidents and chancellors out there on TV today. We didn't see UNC's chancellors. I want to hear after spending $58 million on this case and ultimately losing. What's the plan forward? Is there a clear and consistent path forward? And we don't say one thing on Sunday and something different on Monday. UNC has to answer this. Harvard does, but also every, especially public university in the country has to address this and have a real clear and consistent plan moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, so the court says you can have these personal essays, they can talk all about race, ethnicity, whatever your personal experiences, and those can be used by the colleges. There just can't be any sort of box checking or using race as a factor. A lot of back and forth here that's got attention between Justices Jackson and Thomas, the two African American justices on the court who saw this very differently. Justice Jackson said this in her dissent, "With let-them-eat-cake obliviousness, today, the majority pulls the ripcord and announces colorblindness for all by legal fiat. But deeming race irrelevant in law does not make it so in life." Annmarie?\par \par \uc2 ANNMARIE HORDERN, BLOOMBERG TV WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, I mean, what you're seeing here is just two wildly different views on affirmative action from these two black justices. I think to Doug's point where you're going to see going forward is just these universities trying to figure out a way where they could still make sure they are having diverse college campuses. I think the Holy Cross President put it well, yes, we cannot ask for this information. But if it's given to us, and we may want to use it.\par \par \uc2 I think a bigger question that was not just universities, what happens with companies and businesses that also want to attract a diverse talent pool and also want to potentially have diverse workers is they going to also at some point, have to we're going to see more of these types of cases.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to say put, give you a little bit of what Justice Thomas said in response to Justice Jackson, he said, "As she sees things, we are all inexorably trapped in a fundamentally racist society." He goes on to say, you know, she basically labels all blacks as victims, and he says her desire to do so is unfathomable to me. So Richard, some by Supreme Court standards, some heated back and forth?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It was definitely a heated conversation about the justices. But if you look at the term overall, and I think the mistake we're making this week is we're looking at these couple of cases and not how they ruled over the entirety of this term.\par \par \uc2 Last week, and the week before that, what you saw the Supreme Court ruled \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  himself sort of saying, we do have some inherent bias, and we do have some racism in our system. We've seen in the congressional maps. And now to say that we are trying to move into a color neutral colorblind system, that indeed seems to me to be some schizophrenia on the court. But where I want to pick up where Doug left off is, now, what will universities do? What will colleges do? This conversation around affirmative action makes it seem as though Harvard has 50% of their classes, African Americans with the data sort of points at the fact that Harvard's African American population going public is only 6.6%, right? Or even take a place like the University of Georgia, where 30% of the -- the state of Georgia population is 30%, whereas in their black population, University of Georgia is 8.2%.\par \par \uc2 So what will universities do now? Will they get rid of legacy admissions? Will this be the beginning of the end of standardized testing? I think that's very likely, we've seen during the pandemic, that most universities put a pause on standardized testing, because they believe that there's some inherent bias in that, and I think that will continue moving forward. And so that's going to be the question, how will universities create diverse classes? And how will we deal with some of the other type of inherent biases that exist the admissions process like legacy, like athletics, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And the President said he's going to ask the Education Department to look into those things as well. He also was looking for a way to potentially get around the Student Debt Forgiveness Program. You know, critics are saying he gave people false hope, try to buy votes with this. That's what the cynics say. Here's what he said to our Jacqui Heinrich after the ruling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Mr. President, why did you give millions of borrowers false hope? You've dated -- doubted your own authority here in the past?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't give any false hope. I didn't give borrowers false hope. But the Republicans snatched away the hope that they were given and it's real, real hope.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, Penny, so he says, it's the Republicans fault. And he's going to take that on the campaign trail and say, I'm trying to help middle American, lower income America. And the Republicans are trying to stop me.\par \par \uc2 PENNY NANCE, CONCERNED WOMEN FOR AMERICA PRESIDENT & CEO: Well, I think that's a complete political miscalculation. Because in real America, they - - people know you have to pay for what you buy and decisions were made by students all over this country, whether or not to go to an expensive school or pick an in-state school. Many immigrants, their children go to a local school for two years and then -- like NOVA and then transfer to UVA. They work very hard for the money and they make calculations.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, you don't necessarily need to go into Ivy League school and go into debt for $200,000 to have a good education. We can tell by looking around this table, there's people who went to too inexpensive Christian schools and -- and state schools.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, there are other ways around. So, but -- but with the president now looking at other options and saying I'm going to find different ways to save money, Congresswoman \uc2 Virginia Foxx\uc2 , a GOP Congresswoman says, taxpayers just got sucker punched, again, by this administration, because he confirmed his plans to ignore the law, extend the repayment pause, ignore the Supreme Court and still try to do blanket loan forgiveness. She says, what the President is pushing is illegal inflationary and irresponsible. Annmarie?\par \par \uc2 HORDERN: There's going to be a lot of GOP pushback, because the President isn't stopping here, even after the Supreme Court. So they're going to try to use this Higher Education Act 1960s, that this came out and basically find any other workaround. Now, progressive said he should have been using this from the beginning. But there's still going to be the same legal challenges. And I think the issue the President faces politically, he's not going to be able to get this done likely, before November of next year. This is going to take months.\par \par \uc2 And then, with the debt ceiling negotiation, we're going to have those loan repayments due as well the end of the summer and into the fall. And the President is also announcing an on-ramp payment system for these individuals. So you can see him trying to strike at any of the tools in the toolbox to still maintain that this is an issue because he really also needs that youth vote to come out. Many are saying potentially they failed. They went down the wrong avenue first.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so there's also this big debate about the free speech case. This involves the web designer who said I serve anyone. I have all kinds of clients, but I won't do custom creations for something that offends my personal religious beliefs. Justice Gorsuch wrote and said, in this particular case, Colorado does not just seek to ensure the sale of goods or services on equal terms. It seeks to use its law to compel an individual to create speech she does not believe. Doug, he says, this isn't about serving people when they walk through the door. It's not about the messenger. It's about the message itself.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Well, can I say first since you quoted our Congresswoman \uc2 Virginia Foxx\uc2  is my member of Congress, I think she's watching --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There you go.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: -- right now in Watauga County, North Carolina. Look, this is also another complicated case where we have big headlines and the devil in the details is a bit more narrow. What we saw in \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 's dissent was, well, this actually isn't about compelling individuals to do things if they don't want to. And -- and this is where I think the court, you know, is so not just over the past week, but since the Dobbs decision has been seen as being divisive, and there's been a big push for that. The reality is 50% of the Supreme Court cases in this term have been unanimous.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: 89% have had at least one liberal member in the majority. We've seen a lot of six, three decisions that weren't just six conservatives. And so as we have these decisions that are going to make big headlines and big news and have real impacts, it's important to remember as the legitimacy of the court is being challenged even from the present United States that the court is still operating by and large, as it should.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to play something from the web designer, Lorie Smith, in this case, talking about it after the win for her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LORIE SMITH, PLAINTIFF IN SUPREME COURT CASE: I have clients from all walks of life, including clients who identify as LGBT. I want the LGBT website designer, the Jewish calligrapher, the Democrat speech writer. I want everyone to be able to work and design consistently with what they believe, because free speech is truly for everyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Richard, she says it's a win for everybody who believes in free speech?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I hear that and this is where I sort of disagree with Doug. I do think that the court has been unanimous on many decisions. But when it comes to the issues that divide most Americans, when it comes to the issues that stands up for the vulnerable and the least of these, it seems that the court has sort of seemed to rule one way versus another.\par \par \uc2 In this particular case, what you see here is in the limited ruling of this court, where they sort of use artistry as their term to be like she has free speech because of her artistry, they open up the floodgates for everybody to claim artistry, like what happens next is the person who rents chairs at a wedding say, well, I create chairs, and this is my artistry, does the person who, you know, has cement, does cement say, I don't make tombstones. This is the big -- this could be the beginning of a slippery slope, which is why there's so many folks in the LGBTQIA community very concerned about this decision, as well as the affirmative action decision of the African American community, as well as student loan decision, which I think the reason why the African American community particularly alarmed by that is because we know the number one hold up some loan debt in this country as African American women. They're also one of the most educated subgroups in this country, and they're suffering the most under the weight of student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Penny, what do you make about that, that this is something that could be more broadly interpreted to allow any business owner to say they won't serve certain people?\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Well, the free-market rules and if you -- as a business owner, your goal is to have more business, not less business. And certainly there are times when business owners have to abide by their own consciences. She said it and we'll say it again. She never said she would not serve someone of a particular sexual identity. She said that she would serve everyone but she could not create something that went against her beliefs like a same sex marriage website. That is a very small group of people, very small issue that we're looking at within the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 We would never expect someone who's a member of \uc2 PETA\uc2  to make a website for -- for the circus or the zoo. I mean we actually have to understand that the First Amendment gives us the right to speak or the right not to speak. And she is well within her constitutional rights. The court got it right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we will spend all summer unpacking these and getting ready for an explosive term in the fall.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, thank you very much. Don't go far. We're going to take a break.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the White House is embracing the term Bidenomics this week hoping to make the case that people are better off than they were a few years ago. We've got numbers, though, on how Americans actually feel about their own finances and how the White House is handling inflation. One of the president's biggest defenders on Capitol Hill join us to talk about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With the president's approval sagging, a growing number of Democratic lawmakers are stepping into the 2024 debate to defend Biden against the Republicans hoping to beat him. One relative newcomer here in Washington has begun to make a name for himself, calling out GOP candidates, including my previous guest, one-time Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Massachusetts Democratic Congressman Jake Auchincloss.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS (D-MA): It's such a pleasure to be on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so you heard what she said in response to your sort of critique of her China policy.\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: I did.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And she said, you have to acknowledge where they've built up their military and other hypersonic missiles and things where the U.S. is not keeping up. Valid point by her?\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: Her commentary is all hat and no cattle. First of all, as a Marine veteran, I think it's outrageous that she's saying that the Chinese military is stronger than the United States military. She should know better as someone who was governor of South Carolina, home of Paris Island, the crucible of U.S. Marines. I doubt she would walk on to Paris Island and tell Marine recruits that they're joining the second-best military in the world. So -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But when it comes to things like weaponry or those kinds of advances or a bigger Navy, those kinds of things, equipment, I mean is that a valid critique?\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: We, of course, need to invest in military strength. And the U.S. Marine Crop has pivoted to the Indo-Pacific for force redesign 2030, Army, Navy following suit. But we have to think holistically. And to use her own term, we have to think critically, creatively and courageously. And she's failing her own test here.\par \par \uc2 First of all, critically. She is just using partisan talking points to hit President Biden's foreign policy without actually engaging on the substance. So, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office, he didn't throw out the Abraham Accords because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had negotiated them. He didn't throw out the 301 tariffs on China because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had imposed them. No, he engaged with the substance of the policy and persistence on things that were working.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley is coming in there without actually offering what she might keep or improve upon. She's just kind of throwing out barbs to do it.\par \par \uc2 Number two, creatively. She's talking about cutting R&D funding for the United States, which is a sure-fire way for us to not out compete the scientific and economic behemoth that is China.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, and this is the most one to me, is -- is courage. She was the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . ambassador when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  went to Helsinki in 2018, gave a press conference standing next to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that both former GOP presidential nominees called, quote, disgraceful. She said nothing. And without courage, no other virtues can be made possible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's talk about China, because there's been a lot made of the fact that they don't seem to have a great deal of fear and respect, at least not publicly, for this administration. The incursion with our jets, our war ships, the spy balloon that flew over that there's been no apology for.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken was just over there. And we're told that during his meeting with officials and with President Xi over there that he was lectured about how the U.S.. is the blame - the one to blame for the breakdown in our relationship. We have to do more to fix it. They refuse to reopen military to military communication, which everybody, regardless of party, seems to agree that needs to happen.\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chinese came out of the message - out of the meeting and sent the message then that we had moved toward their position on Taiwan. "The Federalist" grades the trip this way. They say it accomplished nothing but humiliation. How can the U.S. maintain the confidence of our allies and its role as the leader of the free world when our officials are consistently being humiliated and scolded by CCP officials so publicly?\par \par \uc2 Did they get more out of that meeting than we did?\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: No. I disagree with the CCP that we're more to blame than they are. That's wrong. Xi Jinping is a dictator and he views individuals as pawns of the state. We view individuals as having inherent, worth and dignity, and we're going to win that ideological contest in the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 But Secretary Blinken landed in China from a position of strength. We have strengthened our Indo-Pacific alliances as Australia, South Korea, Taiwan, Japan, our freedom of navigation maneuvers in the South China Sea and the and East China Sea. We have demonstrated that we are going to be a Pacific hegemon for decades to come. We have put their - their military friend of no limits, Russia, on a path to strategic defeat in Ukraine despite the GOP trying to cut funds for the effort.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well - well, but they are still, the vast majority of them over there fighting and making the case, Republicans in the Senate and the House, the vast majority of them, that more money needs to continue to flow in aid.\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: Well -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There - there is, just within, you know, the parties, there was always going to be a fracture over certain things.\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: We saw a party line vote in House Armed Services two weeks ago. Now, two weeks ago the Ukrainians are poised for a counter offensive, trying to break through in the southeast to cut the land bridge to Crimea. The Wagner group is poised to march on Moscow. So, an opportune moment for us to be on the offensive. House GOP vote - party line vote an Armed Services Committee markup of a defense budget to cut half a billion dollars of funding for Ukraine. Think about the message that sends to our allies fighting on the front lines of the free world. Nikki Haley says nothing about it. I know she claims that she's, oh, we should support Ukraine. The real test is whether you're willing to stand up to your own party and say, you are wrong. We're going to be with Ukraine all the way to Crimea.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk about Afghanistan because you served there, thank you for that, in uniform. You've seen the problems up close. You know how complex and what a disaster it was over there for many decades.\par \par \uc2 So, we've got this after-action report there. You had said in a piece in late August, before the bombing at Kabul Airport that cost 13 lies, that the president was making the right moves, the very difficult decision to pull out. But you wanted to know more about what happened with us turning over Bagram Air Base. I saw only a passing mention of it in this after- action report from the State Department this week.\par \par \uc2 So, what questions do you still have? Was there enough there that can help us not to make these same mistakes?\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: The military is one of the best institutions in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  about conducting rigorous after-action reviews. We used to call them no ranks debriefs. Everybody took off their rank and it was just a fair and - and tough assessment of what went wrong. And I - I agree that we're going to need to investigate decisions around Bagram Air Base.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, bigger mistake here would have been to keep troops in Afghanistan for another decade, another trillion dollars, more lives lost, for a war that could not be won because we had no nation-building partner in Kabul.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But do you agree that the way the withdrawal happened, the timing of it, there have been critics all over the political spectrum who have said that did not need to happen the way that it did and it cost us 13 service members.\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: There is no right way to end a failed war. President Biden had the moral courage to tell the American people that our resources and strategic focus was best used in the Indo-Pacific to take on China. And in any of - in any accelerated timeline. And I agree that - that there was not enough intelligence assessment about how fast the Afghan government was going to fall, because I don't think even we realized just how broken their political institutions were. But the - the core decision was the right one strategically, which was, to get out of a war that could not be won and to focus on our true geostrategic threat.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's talk domestic because you're a supporter of President Biden and his re-election campaign. He's out there talking Biden economics, Bidenomics. We've got two polls, though, that show Americans do not feel like things are going well for them. The first, when we ask about their personal financial situation, 88 percent of them feel like they're either actively falling behind or just holding steady. In that same poll, 60 percent say they disapprove of how the president has handled the economy, 90 percent say they are still extremely worried about the issue of inflation.\par \par \uc2 CBO also puts out a long-term estimate that says the president is setting us up for an explosion of debt relative to the GDP. It will outflank it at some point. Is this his best argument on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 AUCHINCLOSS: Many Americans are saying that in their personal, financial situation, and there's other polls besides yours that have above 70 percent saying that they are - are in relatively good shape, but that they're assessing the overall economy as being actually much worse than the - than the actual GDP numbers suggest.\par \par \uc2 But the campaign hasn't even started yet. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's got a year to talk about the incredible investments that we made in infrastructure and domestic manufacturing and science and research in this economy that's going to improve standards of living. Last term we passed the bipartisan law that invested in roads and bridges, in electricity, in affordable internet. A couple months ago I was in one of the redder cities in my district with a Republican mayor touring those bridges that were going to get fixed with Democrats and Republicans from across the city. As more and more of those events happen, I think we're going to see that Americans feel the impact, whatever we call it here in the beltway, of generational investment in this economy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will continue to watch those numbers. And, by the way, you've got an op-ed up at foxnews.com, by the way, that talks about how Republicans can work with Democrats on a new tax bill and give some advice there. We'll see if lawmakers heed it. Folks should check it out. AUCHINCLOSS: Appreciate you having me on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman, thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next this 4th of July has been a nightmare for holiday travelers. We're going to discuss the infuriating mess and who's to blame when our Sunday panel returns, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was delayed, and then they delayed it, and then they delayed it and they eventually, at like 4:00 a.m., they said it was cancelled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is two hours on the phone and they're finally giving me at least a refund for my original flight, but I had to book another like $900 flight for my partner and I to go back home in two days. Two days from now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, just a sampling of the frustrations people across the country are facing this week at airports. That irritation increasing tensions in the middle of a potentially record-breaking holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 We are back with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Anybody flying this weekend in the next couple of days?\par \par \uc2 ANNMARIE HORDERN, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, "BLOOMBERG TV": An hour and five minutes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good luck to you, Annmarie, good luck.\par \par \uc2 Penny, where do we go with this? There has been - there have been some measures on The Hill to try to get more consumer protection. Fast Company reporting about this says, among the suggested rights for travelers is the proposal that weather cannot be used as an excuse of delays and cancelations that are actually the fault of the airline. But House and Senate haven't voted on this. Meaning, none of this is going to help you this week.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Yes. Well, and Secretary Buttigieg blames some of this on weather, and it probably is, some of it weather. But there's a couple of other issues happening. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has not updated equipment and it -- we need to concentrate on that. Apparently there's more appropriation coming that way to work on that.\par \par \uc2 But also there are shortages of personnel. And I spoke with a pilot yesterday, and he was saying, for the airlines, the issue is them being able to pass a random drug test.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Because pot is -- comes up in the test. They cannot work for the airline. So, the proliferation of drug use in our country is not good for your employment. So, the takeaway is, don't do drugs, kids.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Just say no. I'm very Nancy Reagan, 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Richard, how worried are you about your flight tomorrow? What do -- who do you blame?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR AND CONTRIBUTING WRITER, "FORBES": Listen, I think this is an interesting situation where I think part of it is we need to modernize our air - our air travel - our air traffic control system. We need to hire more air traffic controllers. I also think we need to figure out a way to hire more folks to work at airlines. If - if the problem is that we need to - we're drug testing for cannabis, then we should have a conversation when the -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you want your pilot stoned while they're piloting you to wherever you're going?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Listen, I think it's one - I think it's one thing - one thing about being high on the job. It's another thing about having cannabis in your system over a month-long period. I think those are two different questions, two different debates.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 Doug.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR AND DOUGLAS MEDIA FOUNDER: Well, I'll err on the side of not being Cheech and Chong on - on the flights, but I took -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wasn't there a movie about that?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, I think so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Didn't they fly a plane and there was smoke coming out and all that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Soul Plane. Yes, that was a good movie.\par \par \uc2 So, I took the train yesterday back to Washington. We arrived two minutes earlier. But as - as the -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: As the people that you showed demonstrated, it's not about one flight being cancelled, it's the rolling delays to find out six hours later that you flight is cancelled.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: It was multiple flights being canceled.\par \par \uc2 And so part of this, it's all systemic, but it also requires - and this is the hard part -- airlines being honest with their customers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, they don't. They will, like the one guy said there -\par \par \uc2 HEYE: You know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They push it back, push it back. But - and you know after the third or fourth time you're probably going to lose your crew. It's probably going to be canceled. Just tell them.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: And just tell them. And then you can make other arrangements. And a lot of people I've seen are either getting stuck - I have a friend who's stuck in Cabo right now. He's fine with things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, OK.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: And his wife.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Cabo, OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Stuck somewhere.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: I - I have another friend who had to rent a car in -- in Detroit to come home this weekend. And that's, obviously, not ideal. And it's because they've been told four different times, you're just delayed, you're just delayed, and they gave up. And it turned out, well, they were cancelled after all. They were just smart about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. I wonder what the train situation is from Cabo say back to the East Coast of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: A little tough.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, that would be tough.\par \par \uc2 OK, I also want to get to some polls because, listen, people out there are going to have a lot to complain about. And apparently some of them feel that way about the country.\par \par \uc2 Gallup has this headline, extreme pride in being American remains near record low.\par \par \uc2 Annmarie, we're told some of this has to do with age. Fifty percent of U.S. adults age 55 and older say they're extremely proud to be American, 40 percent of those age 35 to 54, and only 18 percent of those 18 to 34 say they have pride (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HORDERN: Yes, I guess our elected officials have to do a lot more work with the youth. But I think also it's not just age, it's also a political party and a lot more of older individuals actually are Republicans. So it's actually split as well amongst age and political parties. But it's very record lows in terms of how I think it's extremely or very proud, these individuals are, to be American - to be American.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And we have a lot of Fox News polling out this week that talks about this too and how people feel about the country. Do they feel good? Do they feel it's moving in the right direction? And those numbers, Penny, are all very negative, too.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Well, there's a lot going on, of course. People feel threatened. The economy stinks. And people know it.\par \par \uc2 I just came back from Kentucky and talked to people where the average income for a family of four is only $55,000 a year. So, when they go to buy food, sometimes they have to put things back because they can't afford it.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there's a lot going on. But, overall, and this poll showed that only a few people, I think it was only 4 percent, didn't feel proud of their country 7 percent only felt a little proud.\par \par \uc2 As a mom of a son that's getting ready to be an Army officer, an active duty Army officer, I would just say for the people -- those people, recognize that people sacrificed for your freedom and we need to be appreciative.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Our thanks to a couple of our guests, Congressman Auchincloss himself has served as a Marine in this country, Governor Haley, her husband is now on a deployment while she is running for president, both out serving our country, so we know it is the families as well. And our thanks to them.\par \par \uc2 Richard, final word.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Look, I think this is a very interesting poll. I think the reason why young folks are feeling the way that they feel about the country has everything to do with the fact of who's writing the laws. The average age of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is 67, right? So that's part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's actually younger than I thought.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Yes. But that's part of the problem, right? So, if we have younger folks in the policy-making table -- at the decision-making table, they'll feel more involved in the government and more involved in the country.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: But isn't 67 the new 57?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No, no, it's not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ah, no.\par \par \uc2 Keep us updated. All right, panel -\par \par \uc2 HORDERN: Almost a geriocracy (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 OK, you may not think of the State Department and our work in the same sentence, but there is an unconventional common ground to inspire freedom around the world. We'll show you how they're trying to do that with art, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Diplomacy is critical to American global power. And it takes many forms. Since 1963, one of those forms has been art. This year the State Department is marking 60 years of its Art in Embassies Program. It was founded under then President \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  the same year he gave that famous speech in Berlin.\par \par \uc2 Well, the U.S. embassy there recently unveiled a Kennedy portrait to mark the anniversary of that speech and of the arts program. And it's focusing this year on the theme of democracy.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin sat down with leading voices who helped made Art in Embassies a diplomatic force for American's influence overseas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN BEYER, DIRECTOR, ART IN EMBASSIES: Art can make you feel what a diplomat can only try to make you understand.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Sixty years ago, President \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  stood in front of the Berlin Wall with a message to the communist leaders in the Soviet Union about freedom and America's willingness to stand by its allies.\par \par \uc2 JOHN F. KENNEDY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I take pride in the words, ich bin ein Berliner.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The same year as the United States confronted threats to democracy around the world, President Kennedy launched the Art in Embassies Program. This year, the State Department is celebrating the anniversary of what it says has become a crucial weapon used by American ambassador in more than 200 countries as they face a new era of great power competition and resurgent threats, just like President Kennedy did.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: He seemed to understand that there's no better ambassador for freedom than an artist because an artist survives on freedom.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Megan Beyer runs the Art in Embassies Program at the State Department.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: We have a very small office with a very small budget. Eighty percent of the pieces you see at U.S. residences are simply loans.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In May she launched A More Perfect Union with a traveling exhibit from the democracy collection of modern American artists.\par \par \uc2 The first stop, Athens, the birthplace of democracy.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: We all feel that we need to unite the free world once again. There are so many threats to democracies in every hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The year started with a celebration at the Kennedy Center.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH) AND HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: It tells the story of our commitment to democracy, our commitment to peace, whenever you're committed to art.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Ambassador Randi Levin explained how she uses art curated through the State Department to broach delicate national security issues with her host government, Portugal, a founding member of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Each U.S. ambassador can choose American paintings from the State Department collection to help tell the American story.\par \par \uc2 RANDI CHARNO LEVIN, PORTUGAL AMBASSADOR: It's very, very important, especially at this time in the world, particularly in Europe, that we are filling that space with our values.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): What happens if you don't fill this vacuum?\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: So, if we don't fill this vacuum, others do. And that becomes an opening for maligned actors and competitors and disinformation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm an artist that paints with paper.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Today, art can show how even democracies can overcome threats. American artist Mark Bradford painted Pickett's Charge about the final battle of Gettysburg. It hangs in the \uc2 Hirshhorn Museum\uc2  in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: Mark Bradford tends to take pieces of American culture and bring it to contemporary times.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Bradford's "We The People" now hangs at the U.S. embassy in London.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: It talks about what's written in the Constitution. To have the Constitution in the embassy in London is a nice way of showing what we left and what we left for.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: Art is evidence and expression of our humanity.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: At the embassy in Portugal, which recently hosted the first lady as part of its Art in Embassies celebration, hangs American artist Hank Willis Thomas's painting "Ain't I a Woman," a reference to Sojourner Truth, a key leader in the abolition and suffragist movements in the United States.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): How is this a very key tool in your toolbox as an ambassador?\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: Democracy is a work in progress. And so we are all constantly striving through debate and discussion to create a greater union. We have Maya Lynn (ph) who did the Vietnam Memorial. We have Alisa Nisenbaum (ph), who's a Mexican American artist who painted her father and what it was like to bring Mexican culture to America. All of these pieces coming together creates rich tapestry of American culture that shows people, you know, the best of America.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: What message is this program sending to the world?\par \par \uc2 BEYER: There is a great yearning in humanity for freedom. And this art expresses that. When it gets right down to it, with all of our flaws, it is the best system and our art represents that, a free society, a democratic society.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): In Washington, Jennifer Griffin, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a quick note this morning, number one "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best- selling author, the man behind the hit streaming show "The Terminal List," Jack Carr joins me on this week's "Livin the Bream" podcast, brand new this morning. He talks about his newest book, why hard work and why America gave him everything he needed to succeed.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can hear all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com, or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And if you missed the best moments of FOX NEWS SUNDAY, you don't have to. Just set your DVR to record the show every week on your local Fox station or Sundays 2:00 p.m. on the Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful Independence Day and a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, pub