ion is, I will never run for president united states. Can you say that?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Yes, I'm not -- I have no interest\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Under any circumstances would you get in this primary?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: No. No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: First, remember, as governor of the most populated state in the country, it's easy to get lots of favorable press coverage. But when you're running for national office, people actually want to really look at your record. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can genuinely point to policies that worked. But Newsom, according to the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 , which is nonpartisan 66 percent believe there are bad times for California ahead in the new economy year. 62 percent think the state is already in a recession. 61 percent say they're experiencing financial hardship, and 45 percent are worried about not being able to pay their mortgage or their rent.\par \par \uc2 Gosh, no wonder why four out of 10 Californians are now seriously considering leaving the state. But wait, if Newsom became President, where do we all move to? Poland, Hungary? Anywhere we go though, country would empty out. Now extrapolating from Newsom's personal popularity in a state run by Democrats and he's pretty popular there, extrapolating from that to viability for national office over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is unwise. First, remember, the real reason Biden was the nominee in 2020. He was the only Democrat still alive, who the party could use to mask how radical they'd become.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the party picked Biden over Bernie, Buttigieg, Kamala, etc. None were acceptable to the masses. But Biden was and even with his dreadful economic record, to many Biden remains endearing, even reassuring. Wearing his banker like suits from the 1950's is aviator shades and his perfect pocket squares. He jogs memories of the Democrat Party of your and when he reminisces about the good 