ying to affect that and change children, their innocence and mold children in a new likeness. Don't you agree? I mean, this is a powerful coalition. That's why the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is worried about it.\par \par \uc2 ROYER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It doesn't mean we agree on every other political issue, but on the children, we can agree.\par \par \uc2 ROYER: That's right. And what Montgomery County Public Schools doesn't realize is that, that traditional mutual moral consensus is far more alive than they think it is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, there's one particular individual who, we have to share a soundbite with you, if we can. Wajahat Ali, who is one of the more pernicious figures criticizing the likes of you, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WAJAHAT ALI, DAILY BEAST COLUMNIST: And Muslims who are watching, you need to know that the same forces who are using us against LGBTQ have turned on us and will turn on other people of color and immigrants. To those Muslims, were so offended with some of these books that might just mention two dads, just think about what signal you're sending. What we're telling people is, we'll tolerate you, but will just sit there and barely tolerate you. Right. What does that say to the kids?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ismail, quickly?\par \par \uc2 ROYER: Well, we disagree with Wajahat, with all due respect to him. He's just simply as completely out of touch with the mainstream of Muslim America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we appreciate both of you. And I hope this alliance continues and thank you for coming into the studio.\par \par \uc2 ELHADJ SAL: Thank you for having us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's really nice to meet you both.\par \par \uc2 ROYER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, would Americans pay a bit more if it means supporting American jobs over outsourcing especially to places like China. Well, The Angle hit the streets to find ou