ment of you talking to people on the street. I guess they're growing awareness about it. But there's something broader here. Sometime ago, maybe 25 years ago, people in both parties made this decision that we were living in this new world now, post-Cold War, and it didn't matter where things were made anymore. We were all just consumers of this global economy and citizens of the world. Place matters. Country matters. And now we're starting to see why. The pandemic reminded us of it. But we need to be able to make things in America. If we can't make things, we won't be a powerful and prosperous country much longer.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The libertarians, the free traders, \uc2 Cato Institute\uc2 , Chamber of Commerce, they're all against changing this rule, which again, allows these companies, these multibillion-dollar companies to get away with paying absolutely no duty into the United States, whereas our retailers are paying hundreds of millions of dollars in duties on the other side when they do their own importing. So this makes absolutely no sense and it's killing U.S. jobs.\par \par \uc2 So this never should have been changed in 2016. It happened under Obama. But now the House Select Committee on the China question looks like they want to do something. How confident are you, Senator, given all your advocacy on the China issue that the Senate and your counterparts in the Senate, especially in leadership, will actually do the right thing here?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I don't know because there's a lot of powerful lobbying interest groups that will come out against it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: And you talked about -- and look, even when we did the Uighur forced labor act, we had \uc2 Nike\uc2  and all kinds of companies -- they weren't doing it publicly. They weren't running any commercials during the Super Bowl about their opposition to our bill, but they were working to kill it.\par \par \uc2 But you talk about the libertarians. Look, I think in the laboratory that is how the economy should work. The problem is we don't live in a laboratory. We live in the real world where people need jobs, stable, good paying jobs. You need jobs so you can have family, you can have community, the kinds of thing that countries need in order to survive and thrive. And the reality of it is we need to start thinking about the market as a way to serve the country as opposed to the country existing to serve the market. And I'm for the free market. I hate socialism and I despised communism.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Chinese believe in the free market.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The real world.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: But we have to have a market that works for us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, great to see you. Thanks so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 A political controversy on the Supreme Court, plus why making fun of Joe's declining faculties, that is not enough to sweep Republicans back to the White House. My battle plan for the GOP is right ahead. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right now, the Supreme Court is controlled, is dominated by six very conservative justices. They have begun to make very, very radical, extreme, and activist rulings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Supreme Court has lost the confidence of a great portion of the American public.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: An institution that has been scandal-free for most of its life and has now been corrupted by the dark money, dark money influences.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What? All right, there are some that are saying that this constant drumbeat of criticism and of protests at people's houses, that has gotten to the newest Republican appointees on the court. While the conservative court has leveled a series of rulings that, to some, look like an attempt to maybe insulate them 