gether. And the themes of the book showed themselves.\par \par \uc2 And so, in the book, they're divided into three sections. But beyond you know band of brothers and a higher purpose and shoulders to lean on are three other themes, my dad and all the words of wisdom he had for me, football, and hunting. And even Stacy, the lone female in the book, it's like this culture that that we all ended up sharing in one way or the other. Not everyone in the book hunts, not everyone in the book is connected to football, and only a few of them knew my dad, but this wisdom that comes from those activities exist in all their lives. And it was really cool just to see how much about myself other people see.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: How did the people that you featured in the book, for the ones who are still here, react to the idea that you were going to do this?\par \par \uc2 JONES: All 10 of these folks are still alive and I interviewed each of them, but they all talk about someone who isn't.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I just said, hey, tell me about the bonds from -- your bond, unbroken bonds of battle. For Stacy, it was her husband. For Keith and I, it was our best friend, Chris, that we tried to save. For others, for the first three chapters in the book are three Marines that were all connected in service. And without knowing what the other person talked about, they both brought up two people, Adam Benjamin and E.J. Pate who aren't with us anymore, and the impact they had on their lives. One of them being Amos Benjamin, Adam's brother that's in the book.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, the way we're all connected, the feedback from all of them which today th1ey're all getting their books and getting to read the other chapters. But it's one text after the other of, you know, just thank you for letting me share my story.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I just noticed that you acknowledged -- in your acknowledgement, you thanked Pete Hegseth. But I'm -- I can't seem to find my name in here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Listen, I think Pete Hegseth --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not all heroes are in the military. You know that? I fight a war every day. I fight a war every day. I want to ask you a question in seriousness, because every now and then you will text me after a show and you'll be concerned about something.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What in the area of stress -- OK, so, you were in one of the most dangerous parts of the world. You all nearly died, and you're doing this new job. Do you find any kind of correlation in stress or is -- could TV create an irrational stress for you that is actually worse or is it -- is this like so easy after what you've been through?\par \par \uc2 JONES: This isn't stressful. This is stressful because I look at all of you as, you know, titans in this world. I mean, you all have careers --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. I'm on it. But seriously -- yes, but literally, I look at all of you and I have a little bit of imposter syndrome walking in this building. This part of it I'm good with. I know these folks. But I also know I represent these folks when I come on T.V. They don't all agree with my political views, but I should conduct myself in a way that makes them proud and hold a conversation with you all, do my homework, and be here. Talking to 303 million Americans at a time, man, I know those folks. I live with them.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I just wanted to ask you about Bass Drive.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Bass Drive is where I grew up. It's a cul-de-sac. It's literally a hollow, down in the hollow on the other side of the hill. That's where my dad, his two brothers, and my grandparents all live. My papa bought five acres of land from a Native American Cherokee. Her last name was Bass. And the way the story goes, they gave the land back to her family in some lawsuit. So, it went from the state back to the Bass family of the Cherokee Indians.\par \par \uc2 And so, like, her family fought for decades to get it. She takes it, sells it to my papa who buys a Trans Am moves to California, and smoked American thing (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PERINO: America.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, that's America.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. My favorite part of the book are the picture section because you can see you with long hair and it's worth the read for them. Joey Jones, thank you so much and congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I'm proud of you, Joey.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, so go out and get your copy of unbroken bonds of battle. It's out today. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: All right. So, check out this Massachusetts boy who invented a musical bike rack by attaching a xylophone to his wheel.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That would be the most annoying kid in the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Totally. It's not the best melody. Don't know if he's cycling to the top of the charts anytime soon, but he certainly gets points for creativity and cuteness. And that watermelon t-shirt --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's pretty cute.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Adorable.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He's going to have a rough childhood. No, I'm kidding. I'm kidding. I'm joking. Fantastic, right? All right, tonight, Jimmy Failla, Kennedy, Johnny Joey Jones --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I never heard of him. Kat Timpf tonight. And --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Robots are great! Robots are great! Robots are great!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, \uc2 University of Tokyo\uc2 , it's in Japan. Anyway, they created something that is kind of greedy. Like, who wants to have extra hands? Now, I can understand if you're missing an arm, but now they've created arms that are triggered by your body movement. So, you can have six detachable robot arms. And you know what, Jesse? You can replace your arms with claws as you claw your way up the corporate ladder. Oh, 8:00. Let's get to --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: 10:00.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: 10:00.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, so, they had a grandparents weekend at the racetrack. You guys want to see grandparents race each other? Check this out.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, my God. Really?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Whoa. Where is this?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And everyone keeps falling and almost dying. This was Emerald Downs. No serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Where is that?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Let's go to the slow motion.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's insane.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Where is this.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you can see -- you can see where -- he pops his Achilles.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Where's Emerald Downs?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," the angry New York City pizza launcher.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, you got him.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very upset.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That was coup.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: (INAUDIBLE) He's the best.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The booking war.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You got him and not us?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, a quick one here. Season three, the finale of Everything Will Be Okay is out now. And it has great lessons in it. So, here's the thing. If you're a graduate, my summer assignment for you. Try to listen to the entire season. It's really good. And then comment and I will respond. That's a great lesson.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It certainly is.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And now, I'm never going to have to do that podcast again.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: By the way --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, yes, honey.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Emerald Downs is in Washington.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, now we know. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020230628ej6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 President Biden and World Leaders Closely Monitoring Situation in Russia; Former President Trump Holds Firm Lead in GOP Primary Polls; Wagner Military Group Stands Down On Its March to Moscow; Interview with Senator Ben Cardin (D-MD)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Karl Rove, Juan Williams, Jonathan Turley, Greg  Palkot \par \uc2 9,835 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Mercenary fighters rebel fighters but then retreats. Now, the U.S. tries to determine what this moment reveals about Putin's hold on power.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Rebel forces rolled their armed vehicles out of a southern Russian city that they had seized just days ago. It's the end of a short but stunning challenge by defiant mercenary leader, who had ordered his troops to march on Moscow and now appears to be exiled.\par \par \uc2 Global leaders are left to game out what's next for a superpower already struggling to wage its war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We have a live report on what this means for the Kremlin and we will sit down exclusively with a key member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, Democrat Ben Cardin, to ask what the U.S. knows and how we should respond.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we'll ask about critics who say President Biden is bungling the U.S. relationship with China.\par \par \uc2 And we'll debut a brand new survey from the Reagan Institute on how Americans really feel about the U.S. position on the global stage.\par \par \uc2 Then, one year after the Dobbs decision, states make bold moves on abortion and some candidates draw a line.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The Supreme Court of United States sent Roe versus Wade to the ash heap of history where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  calls for a 2024 litmus test on one of the nation's most of divisive issues, and asks for his rivals to join him.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I did this week call on every other candidate for the Republican nomination to support a minimum standard of a 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  joins me exclusively to discuss his pitch to voters one poll showed may not be a winning argument.\par \par \uc2 Then, yet, another Republican joins the crowded race to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in his bid for the White House. Our Sunday panel is here on the ever- expanding Republican field.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 World leaders are only just beginning to analyze the fallout after a group of Russian mercenaries challenged about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Those mercenaries who had been fighting alongside Russia and Ukraine accuse Moscow of a deadly strike on their troops. The group then began advancing on the Russian capital as the world watched. But, ultimately, they retreated.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been briefed by advisors, and all this is unfolding in a country where there's very little press freedom but we will bring you what we know this morning.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Senator Ben Cardin of the Foreign Relations Committee joins me to talk about how the U.S. should respond.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Greg Palkot who's live in Kyiv with more on what happened and what it means for Ukraine's plans to beat back Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's the morning, the day after. And it looks like we're back from the brink in Russia, but I also looks like there's a lot of damage and a lot more questions.\par \par \uc2 It was the biggest challenge to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in more than two decades of rule, an attempted armed uprising. The man responsible, the Wagner group mercenary chief \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 . He had complained about the war, Russian military leadership and conduct, and then pulled his fighters out of Ukraine and into the Russian city of Rostov-on-Don where little trouble he took charge. Then he sent his forces towards Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin braced for an attack, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  branding the action of treason as Washington washed. As now reported, U.S. intelligence had earlier suspected something at least was afoot.\par \par \uc2 It reportedly took Belarus President Lukashenko, on authority of Putin, speaking with Prigozhin to cool things down. The Wagner chief pulling his forces back to their camps in Ukraine in exchange for guarantee of security. We still don't have confirmation of the Wagner movements.\par \par \uc2 Prigozhin was supposedly to be sent to Belarus. His demands for Russian defense reform unaddressed. Some of his mercenary army to be absorbed by the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 During all of this here in Ukraine, officials and a lot of others watching as well. President Zelenskyy said it was a sign of chaos and instability Russia, taking advantage of the confusion across the border, Kyiv launched new inroads into Russian front lines all in the past 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, the big loser according to most today is really \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Hired partners running rampant. A mercenary boss criticizing your war plans. Not a good look for a man supposedly in control.\par \par \uc2 Maybe that is why state TV is running reportedly a pretaped interview with Putin today in which he says, he is confident in the special military operation. That's what he calls the war here. Confidence, no doubt shaken by this weekend's events -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Greg Palkot reporting live from Kyiv, Greg, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Democratic Senator Ben Cardin of the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): Shannon, it's good to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, how concerned are you at this moment? What does it say about Putin's hold on power? There's instability in a country that has thousands of nuclear warheads.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, Putin brought this on himself, doing business with the Wagner Group. We know what they are. They are thugs dealing with thugs.\par \par \uc2 So, this was not unexpected problem would develop. He's lost, that's Putin, lost his credibility with his own people. His account of what he went to Ukraine is now being challenged I think with public opinion in Russia itself.\par \par \uc2 We recognize that we have to be very careful when regards to the nuclear capacity of Russia. There's been no signs to date that they've made any movement in that direction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, "The Wall Street Journal" just has a piece -- the editorial board this morning saying this: This moment was seen right Ukraine to accelerate its summer offensive and retake more of its territory from the invaders. If only the U.S. had provided more advanced weapons sooner, Ukraine would be better position to do so. Have we failed on that front?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: The United States has provided incredible amount of military equipment to the Ukrainians. I think $75 billion to date. We've organized the international community, provided a great deal of assistance.\par \par \uc2 Can we do more? Should we do more? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 This is a critical time for Ukraine. This counteroffensive is going to be defining as to where we're going to be in the next year or two. So, it's a critically important we maintain our support and not be fool by what's happening in Russia today as to the needs of the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I know you've been supportive, as you said, of continuing that aid as this drags on, now 16 to 18 months into this. The Reagan Institute has a brand-new summer survey out and they ask people while a majority -- a slim majority does support continuing U.S. aid, those who have concerns or who are opposed about the aid going over there say this -- they're worried about unmet domestic needs here in the U.S. They're worried about risking provoking Russia even further, corruption in Ukraine, depleting U.S. weapons stockpiles.\par \par \uc2 What's your case to the American taxpayers who have those concerns?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Ukraine is the frontline for protection of our democracy. We know that Mr. Putin would not stop with Ukraine. He will continue his efforts in Europe and we're not safe. So, this is our frontline and protection of our democracy. This is an investment in America.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we have to deal with our domestic needs, but we have to also protect our national security. And we need to be part of helping Ukraine defend itself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Russia has been saying in essence that the West being involved in what's going on in Ukraine is pushing them closer to an alliance with China. There's been a lot of talk about -- obviously, we've telegraphed that China should not be sending legal aid into Russia. But is there now an opportunity with what the Wagner group leader Prigozhin has come out and he's criticized Russia's military. He has criticized Russian leaders.\par \par \uc2 Is there chance now for the U.S. to telegraph to China, this alliance with Russia, if it gets any tighter, is going to be a losing one? Should we use this moment in a conversation with China?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, I think Secretary Blinken deal did telegraphed out to Mr. Xi and -- in his meeting in China. So, yes, absolutely. We want China to recognize that Russia is not a good partner and what Russia has done invading the sovereignty of an independent state is something that China does not want to be on that side.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we have made it clear that they need to stop their support for Russia, and certainly not increase the amount of help they're giving that country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You reference Secretary Blinken's visit there. He said one of the key things he wanted to accomplish was a military to military communication. That's very important to avoid things escalating out of control like when one of their jets seem to buzz one of our reconnaissance jets, our warships in the Taiwan Strait, those kinds of things. But he said China would not agree to that, even those communications there.\par \par \uc2 Did we get anything to that -- from that meeting and what do you say to those who say President Biden and his administration are bungling this relationship with China?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, first, we understand that China's main concern (INAUDIBLE) the economies, they want to continue to do business with American consumers. We recognize that. We leverage that for our own strategic needs.\par \par \uc2 We also recognize that it's extremely important for both China and the United States to have military to military conversations to deconflict. That is absolutely essential.\par \par \uc2 So, I think some steps were made in that direction during Secretary Blinken's visit. Not as far as I think many of us would have liked to have seen. But it was a first step after a very, very frosty relationship.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are lot of folks who publicly were saying they didn't think the secretary should even go and sort of reward this behavior. We've not had an apology for the spy balloon, and any number of other issues -- the phony police stations that seem to be tracking dissidents here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Should he have gone? And again, did he get any deliverable from that meeting?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I don't think he was rewarding China. I think he was there for our interest, the U.S. interest. We have strategic interest in what's happening in China. So, absolutely, we need to have a communication.\par \par \uc2 We do it under our terms, under our values. We're never going to condone the human rights violations or the way they're conducting international commerce. But we need to have those conversations in order to be able to advance U.S. interest.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You mentioned human rights issues. You sign on to a letter this week when India's prime minister was coming into town. You said to the president -- this letter, that numerous lawmakers, dozens of you sign onto.\par \par \uc2 You said: A series of independent credible reports reflect troubling signs in India, toward the shrinking of political space, the rise of religious intolerance, the targeting of civil society organizations and journalists and growing restrictions on press freedoms and Internet access.\par \par \uc2 There were actually a handful of House Democrats that didn't show up for the joint address to Congress by the prime minister. They call it an embarrassing spectacle, and said it undermines our ability as the U.S. to speak out on things like human rights violations.\par \par \uc2 Should this administration have rolled out such a red carpet for India's prime minster?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Our relationship with India is critically important to the United States. We already talked about China, but India is part of the Quad, which are the countries of Japan, Australia and the United States, dealing with Indo-Pacific region because of China's influence. It counters China's influence.\par \par \uc2 So, our relationship with India has been critically important in regards to our concerns about China's influence in that region. Are we concerned about your human rights record? Should we raise that during the bilaterals? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But this is a matter of -- we conduct our foreign policy within our values. And India is not doing what it needs to do to protect the rights of its citizens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, I want to ask you about abortion as well. This weekend marks the one year of, marks since Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 I talked earlier with Vice President -- former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  about this. We're going to play that interview in full in just a minute. But I want to play what he says is a Democrats' position on abortion. Here's what he says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats actually support taxpayer funding of abortion all the way up to the moment of birth. That supported by maybe 10 percent of the American people, leaving aside how deeply offensive it is to millions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you signed on to a Senate bill called the Women's Health Protection Act of 2023. Critics say it goes far beyond Roe and it actually codifies what the former vice president was saying there. So, what is the Democrats' position? Where do you draw the line?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, we support Roe v. Wade. We thought that was established law. It was established law for almost 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision was a radical decision that reversed the rights of women to make their own health care decisions. So, we support Roe v. Wade. We support the right of women to be able to make their own decisions about the reproductive rights. And it shouldn't be subservient to what state legislatures are doing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about the states where it's actually legal up until the due date? Is that something Democrats support?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: We support the right of women to make their own decisions. This is a personal decision made by women with advice of their doctors and their family. And we don't think we should try to tell women when they can make those decisions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there a cut off for you before that point?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: No. To me, it's a reproductive -- it's a healthcare decision and up to women to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a year out from the Dobbs decision, the Supreme Court seems less settled than ever before with all the states acting in many different ways.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, thank you. We appreciate you stopping in today.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, our politics panel on a surprising new analysis about why a very critical voting bloc is drifting away from longtime support of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Cheers appear to be for the Wagner forces departing a Russian city backing off that planned march to Moscow. Tensions appear to have cooled for now. Now U.S. Leaders are trying to assess the impact on Russian President Putin and his war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 For more analysis and the week's biggest political stories, let's bring in our Sunday group. Former State Department Spokesperson Morgan Ortagus, Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams, former Bush White House Adviser Karl Rove, Reuters White House Correspondent Jeff Mason.\par \par \uc2 Good to see all of you. All right, Morgan, because of your time in the State Department, I want to start with. How does the GOP field now handle this? How do they respond? Is there going to be an advantage to those like Nikki Haley that have more foreign policy experience? How do they address it?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATES DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Well, we'll see. And I'm interviewing her Wednesday in New Hampshire. So, I will ask her that very question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: When we looked at the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  polling that you talked about -- talked earlier, that came out. One of the things that was surprising to me is how much of the Republican Party, still 70%, support military -- want Ukraine to -- want Ukraine to win, and then an overwhelming majority actually still support military aid to Ukraine, even in the Republican Party. So that's a little bit different from the narrative that we see.\par \par \uc2 And, listen, when you -- when you saw what happened yesterday, Putin has spent a year and a half trying to take over another country, and he could barely keep control of his own country yesterday. So you have the Ukrainians on a counter offensive. They certainly have the momentum. And, you know, what was fascinating to me and everything going on since Friday night in Russia is Putin's best friend, Xi Jinping was totally silent. They are supposed to have a no limits partnership. His bestie was nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They might be having some conversations right about now. Also, in that Reagan Institute summer survey polling, when asked about this, is the U.S. better served by withdrawing from international affairs and focusing more attention on the problems at home? Overall, the number was 65%. I mean, Karl, you go through the GOP, they had the highest number followed by independents and Democrats. But a majority of Americans had this attitude that I found surprising in the polling, that they want more domestic focus.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I'm not surprised that's the way we've always been. There is believing that we are protected by two great oceans and friendly neighbors to the north and south. There's sort of a reluctance by America to be involved in the world, but we hold sometimes conflicting and complex views.\par \par \uc2 And as Morgan pointed out, the poll shows very strong support for not only the maintenance of our support for Ukraine, but also 74% believe it's important that we defend human rights abroad, 85% said we ought to maintain a strong military, and 76% said we need to promote international trade. So these sort of natural isolationist tendencies of America, protected by the pacific and Atlantic are in conflict with other views that we hold deeply.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so just one of the things that the candidates are going to have to be out there talking about. Some polling out this week showing that governor DeSantis might be making some tiny inroads to the massive lead that former President Trump has. But this is what Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , who ruled out his own GOP run, had to say this week about his take on the DeSantis campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 , (R) FORMER MARYLAND GOVERNOR: I think the campaign is one of the -- one of the worst I've seen so far, and he's dropped like a rock.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you make of that, Jeff? That assessment as they're out there -- it's getting serious now.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, it is getting serious. I think that that assessment is probably one that's shared by some other Republicans who expected that Governor DeSantis would come in and really knock the socks off of the Republican Party and certainly the people out there in the Republican realm who did not want to support former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 That said, it still early. President Trump does have a huge, huge lead, but that poll shows that there is room for movement, and the fact that Governor DeSantis has moved a little bit is no doubt, giving some hope to his supporters. But I don't think the clip that you just played is -- is an unusual one in terms of the thoughts among some other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he got more company this week because former Congressman \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , who long worked in the CIA, also joined the group this week. And Axios, Juan, notes that this is the most diverse GOP field ever, that half the folks running are people of color.?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it's sort of stunning when you look at it, especially with \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2  jumping in this week, Shannon. I think there are lots of people of color who see an opportunity to gain exposure by helping the Republican Party deal with identity politics, white identity politics. That comes from the fact it's an 85% non-Hispanic white party.\par \par \uc2 And what comes to my mind is after 2012, the party had a post mortem, which it said, we need to get more diverse, we need to do better with women. But what we see now is a GOP at war with itself, because what happened in '16 was the Trump came in and it doubled down on the base on the white populist, grievance, anger.\par \par \uc2 And now you have people like Nikki Haley, like Tim Scott, like \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , who are saying the future of the party is really moving in a more diverse direction. And I think they're making a big effort there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Karl, I see you itching over there to join in this conversation.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You know, I think that they're doing it for more than just cosmetic reasons, as Juan suggested in his opening remarks. They -- they are conservatives who just 