t jump out. But don't -- don't claim him any credit for the resolution of what's happened in the last two days.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Are you kidding -- oh, Joe -- let me get this straight, DeSantis, it's a -- it's a territorial dispute. You know, former President Trump --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You're reaching.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- It's my friend. Putin is my friend. I would have handled it.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You're reaching.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs to be acknowledged when he has success even by --\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I will acknowledge, he did a smart thing by keeping his mouth shut and heading to Camp David.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Jeff, almost all of our recent polling shows him upside down on a number of issues, and certainly even when it comes to foreign policy, people don't see him as a strong leader on that front?\par \par \uc2 MASON: Well, and to pull kind of all these conversations together I think it's true what Karl said before, people do vote based on domestic priorities. But they also want the country to be run well, and they want the fact -- they want to know that they are secure. They want to know that these other foreign policy issues are being handled. But if you lose track of the important domestic things, then you're in trouble. And I think opera posed some of the discussions we're having about Hispanics and African American voters.\par \par \uc2 The Biden people -- the Biden campaign is certainly not ignoring the fact that President Trump picked away some people from that -- from those constituencies in the last election. And that's something that the Biden campaign will almost certainly be focusing on, because whether it's about principle or whether it's about convenience, it's political reality. And that's something that will be important to both parties.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: A significant number of the people that he stole away from the Democrats in 2020 were black and brown.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROVE: The candidate was a white replacement theory, I guess there, Juan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, you're going to have to pay extra pay per view to see the rest of that conversation over there. Panel, thank you all very much. We'll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my exclusive with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  pressing his Republican primary opponents to draw their lines on abortion, one year after the end of Roe v. Wade. But can the party agree on a position voters will actually back?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the year since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the impact of that landmark decision has been playing out, still not over. Polls show that a majority of voters still disagree with ending Roe and Democrats says that's a big part of the reason they not only held the Senate but also picked up seats.\par \par \uc2 But former vice president, and Republican presidential candidate, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , says he thinks he's found a way for his party to win on the issue.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with him this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And welcome back to FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Shannon. Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start here. One Republican strategist outlines her efforts at the GOP nomination this way. He says, quote, the Trumpets are angry with him. The never Trumpists are mad at him for his being part of the administration and support of impeached, convicted insurrection promoter. It's a hard path.\par \par \uc2 So, you're at single digits of the polls right now. What kind of constituency or coalition can you put together with that in mind?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, first off, it -- it's good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Over the last 20 years I've been a vice president in the United States, I've been a government of a successful state and I've been a leading House conservative for 12 years in the Congress. And we just felt compelled to step forward and offer our candidacy. And we're going to - we're going to take our case, our background, our experience, our vision to bring America back to those common sense conservative values to every voter.\par \par \uc2 You know, you and I have known each other since my days in the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 . You - you know back when I was fighting the big spenders in my own party. And you know the stance that I took for the right to life when I was a member of Congress. I authored the first legislation to defund Planned Parenthood that had ever been authored and passed in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 And then years as a governor and then - and also the story of our family and our values. We'll be ready day one if we have the privilege to serve as president of the United States, to steer our country back to those common sense conservative principles that have always made us strong and prosperous and free.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you're not afraid of getting into some of these difficult issues, like abortion. You have said you want the entire GOP field to commit to at least a 15 week national ban.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here is a warning from a poll that is out that says Americans overwhelmingly oppose a federal law banning abortion nationwide by 80 to 14 percent. Those surveyed opposed the idea of a national ban, including 65 percent of Republicans and 83 percent of independents.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you sell this to an American public that, according to these numbers, doesn't want a national ban?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, first off, you - you - and I'm pro-life. I don't apologize for it. And this weekend we're celebrating an historic victory. When one year ago the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  sent Roe versus Wade to the ash heap of history and - and gave the American people a new beginning for life. I couldn't be more proud of the some 20 states that advanced protections for the unborn and support for women facing crisis pregnancy. And I think it's imperative as - not only this primary campaign begins, but as men and women step forward for office in the Republican Party all across the country, that we speak with clarity about a commitment to the sanctity of life, that we make it clear that we'll - we'll stand on principle, but we'll also stand with compassion, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Look, we -- 62 million unborn lives lost. And - and just about as many women who have endured this -- the two generations under abortion. I think we need to bring a message of grace. We need to bring a message of kindness. We need to come alongside women in crisis pregnancies. That's how we're going to win hearts and minds.\par \par \uc2 It's so much more important than politics to me. But I also think - I also think it's a winning issue. I did, this week, call on every other candidate for the Republican nomination to support a minimum standard of a 15-week ban on abortion at the national level. I - I -- that would align American law with most of the countries in Europe that literally ban abortion after 12 to 15 weeks. Our - our laws at the national level today are more aligned with North Korea, China and Iran than with other western countries in Europe. And so I -- I fully support that. And, candidly, as other surveys have shown, a decisive majority of Americans would support legislation that - that - that - that bans abortion after - after unborn children are capable of pain at that 15 week mark. And so I believe that we just can't rest or relent until we restore the sanctity of life to the center of American loss.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You talk about grace and about meeting people where they are. But the Defense Department, the Pentagon, came out with a policy that provides travel reimbursement and cost for women who are seeking abortions that have to travel away from where they're stationed. Senator Tommy Tuberville has put a hold on hundreds now of military promotions because of that.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The secretary of defense says it's creating a, quote, clear risk to our national security. Do you agree with his position?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I strongly support the stand that Senator Tuberville is taking. We simply - we simply cannot have the federal government subsidizing abortion in this country in - in -- directly or indirectly. And that includes at the Pentagon. And I commend him for the stand that he's taken.\par \par \uc2 But, look, a hold the view that the way forward is to expose the extremism of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats and then to stand clearly and with compassion and on principle for the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Another tough topic, Social Security, Medicare. You've - you've, you know, waded into these waters that are very dangerous and shark infested for most politicos out there.\par \par \uc2 You've floated this idea about letting younger Americans put some of their withholding into personal savings accounts.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "The L.A. Times" has a piece that says this, make no mistake, diverting any significant portion of Social Security taxes into private accounts would make the program unworkable, funnel untold wealth into the hands of Wall Street promoters and leave millions of families destitute. Why take this position when you know that's going to be the critique of it?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, first off, look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policy on our national debt is insolvency. And, sadly, my former running mate's policy is identical to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's. Both of them say they're not even going to talk about common sense and compassionate reforms to entitlements to spare future generations of a mountain range of debt. And entitlements represent 70 percent of federal spending today. And that number is only growing.\par \par \uc2 I think we owe better to those three beautiful granddaughters of mine that were born in the last two years. I mean we have a -- we have a national debt today, Shannon, the size of our nation's economy. But it is scheduled, over the next 25 years, to grow to $150 trillion. If we - if we don't act in the days ahead, and we put it off for 25 years, those granddaughters of mine and my kids are going to be faced with all bad choices. That's why I really do believe the time has come to have leadership at the national level that will -- that will be straight with the American people about the debt crisis that we're facing, make it clear to Americans that are in retirement that there will be no change in Social Security or Medicare. And for Americans that are going to retire in the next 25 years, there will be no change for them as well.\par \par \uc2 But for Americans under the age of 40, I think they'll be - they would be open to the kind of reforms that would transition new deal programs into a better deal. I have people coming up to me saying, thank you, thanks for being honest about this. And it's the kind of leadership that I'll provide if I'm president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to, if we can, do a couple of super quick lightning rounds because I want to make sure I get some answers from you on this.\par \par \uc2 You've seen the stories. Should the Supreme Court have a code of ethics, one that's enforceable?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I -- look, I - I have immense respect for Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . And I -- I take them at their word that they acted in a proper and an upright way in this regard. And I -- I just can't help but believe that what we're seeing here is politics. "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 ," left-wing organizations, I think - I think will never forgive our administration for appointing three pro-life justices to the Supreme Court of United States.\par \par \uc2 And - and so I think now, having lost in the ballot box, they're back to trying to bully the Supreme Court when, as you know, other justices who have been appointed by Democratic presidents have had some of the same issues come up. They've never been raised. So, I think we're just looking at politics. And - and I have great confidence in the justices that are serving us today.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've said it's o premature to talk about possibly president -- pardoning President Trump. There's been no trial. That assumes that he would be convicted.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If he was, would you or would you not entertain of pardon for him should you be president?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, if I - if I had the great privilege of being president of the United States, I would take the pardon authority very seriously. You know, I've been a governor. I've actually granted pardons to people. And there's a very careful process for evaluating that. And - and - and - and - and I would - I would apply that to every single case in that matter.\par \par \uc2 But I - I appreciate you raising the point. I - I don't understand why some of the other people running for president in the Republican primary assume that the president will be found guilty. I think it's - it's - I think it's terribly premature and it - and it shows really a disrespect for our judicial system to talk about that now. I - I think -- look, the - the - the charges in the indictment are serious. And I can't defend what is alleged. But the president's entitled to his day in court. And I think we need let the process go forward, let the former president make his case. And for my part, I'm going to stay focused on the challenges facing the American people and I'm going to work our heart out. Not just to win the Republican nomination, but to win a great victory for our party and for the American people 2024.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will see you out there on the campaign trail. Thanks for stopping in.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a reminder, Fox News Channel is your home for the first 2024 Republican primary debate. Our own Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum will moderate the debate which is live in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, August 23rd. Coverage begins at 9:00 p.m. Eastern that night right here on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer make a deal with federal prosecutors over his failure to pay all his taxes, but whistleblowers have come forward to say there's much more to the story about how the Justice Department handled that case. Our legal experts break down the claims, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: Mr. Weiss had, in fact, more authority than a special council would have had. He has complete - he has - he had and has complete authority, as I said, to bring a case anywhere he wants in his discretion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  pushing back on whistleblower claims that the U.S. attorney in Delaware has faced any constraints in the Hunter Biden investigation.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring in some of our sharpest legal minds to tackle this week's explosive legal news and a look ahead to the Supreme Court as well. Constitutional Accountability Center President Elizabeth Weintraub, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor, Jonathan Turley, and former principal deputy assistant attorney general, Tom Dupree.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 I want to start there with what the attorney general said. His has repeatedly said that David Weiss has not had any restrictions on where he wants to bring charges or how to do that. But you've got this email for the whistleblower presented to the House Ways and Means Committee, and he testified to this. And this is part of that email that he says about a meeting in October of `22. He says, Weiss stated that he is not the deciding person on whether charges are filed. The whistleblower went on to note, I believe this to be a huge problem inconsistent with DOJ public position and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  testimony.\par \par \uc2 All right, Tom, how do we figure out where the truth lies?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, I think a good first step would be to bring in the U.S. attorney to testify before Congress and put those questions to him directly because we are getting at the least mixed messages.\par \par \uc2 And, look, the outcome of this case, this plea agreement with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is very surprising. I suspect when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyers got the call from the Justice Department offering him a plea to two misdemeanors with no time served, they thought Christmas came early.\par \par \uc2 To me the big question here is whether the U.S. attorney meant what he said, when he said that this investigation is continuing because there are various serious allegations out here, there's the whistleblower evidence, other evidence that we've learned about from Congress or through media reports. And the question is, is the U.S. attorney done with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  or is there more to come?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Tom, a quick follow-up to that.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general says, I would support Mr. Weiss explaining or testifying on these matters when he deems appropriate. Do you think we will see him in a public forum doing that?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I hope we will and I think we will, Shannon. I think, right now, there is a huge outcry among the American people just wanting to know more about this. What were the reasons for the decision? Did this U.S. attorney, in fact, have decisional authority about what charges to pursue and to reach this plea agreement. There's huge public pressure on him. The attorney general has now given his blessing to this guy appearing before Congress and testifying. I think that's what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, some other things that the whistleblower brought up. He brought up text messages of things that he said implicated suggestions of other potential issues that needed to be investigated, but he said that he was being stymied by the Justice Department during this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Clark, who's an attorney for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , says this, any verifiable words or actions of my client, in the midst of a horrible addiction, are solely his own and have no connection to anyone in his family. Any suggestion the investigation was not thorough or cut corners or cut my client any slack is preposterous and deeply irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 So, Elizabeth, pushing back hard, reminding folks where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was in the midst of this, but not necessarily denying that these texts and things attributed to him could be legitimate.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER PRESIDENT: Well, I think almost every American family has been, unfortunately, touched by addiction and the devastation that it can bring. So, I think that that's appropriate context to bring in here.\par \par \uc2 But I think overall the most important principle, both in this case, in the Trump case, is that the rule of law should apply. And no one is above the law. Whether you're rich or famous, a former president, a son of a current president, no one is above the law. And the legal process will continue. And, in the Trump case, it will be juries of everyday Americans holding him accountable. And in these other cases with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and others who have engaged in wrongdoing and accountability will follow, it should follow according to the rule of law and the principle that no one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jonathan, what do you - what do you make of the charges in the deal that's going to formally come together in July with this court hearing because there are those I hear who say, these were overcharged. He's a first-time offender. This wouldn't have happened. When others are out there say, my client had the exact same issue and actually did jail time. So, what do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GW UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think it's hard to look at the length of time that they took, and the charges that they produced, a not see different treatment. That's what the whistleblowers are alleging.\par \par \uc2 They took five years to get a plea to two misdemeanors that they could have confirmed in the first month. You have whistleblower saying that they were prevented from pursuing certain efforts.\par \par \uc2 The statement that you just read from the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  team was astonishing. They did not deny that the message was sent. It said they - they invoke that sort of 7 percent solution. He was an addict. Well, this isn't like a purse snatching in Times Square. He was at the center of an influence peddling scheme that stretched around the world, involved dozens of LLC corporations, and accounts, and generated millions of dollars. That's - it - you can't just wave a flag here and say, well, that's - that's he addict. That's Hunter being Hunter. And so there's going to be a lot of inquiry.\par \par \uc2 One of the most troubling aspects of this is the whistleblower says that in a meeting he was present at, Weiss said that he wanted to be appointed special counsel after he was denied the opportunity to bring additional charges. If that's the case, there's a world of hurt coming down for the attorney general because it directly contradicts what he said. And here's a word to the wise, you don't have to voluntarily show up. At this point Congress has ample ability to start issuing subpoenas. And I think courts will support them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Something else that this whistleblower brought out and presented as an exhibit in evidence and testimony before the House Ways and Means Committee was talking about the timeline of the laptop. He says this of a meeting in October of 2020. So, this is in the heat of the presidential election. He says -- he notes back to November of 2019, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had determined that the device was registered to Sportsman. That's the name used for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . The \uc2 Apple\uc2  ID account/iCloud account.\par \par \uc2 So he says, Tom, that they knew back in November of 2019. He also says in that meeting where they were recalling all of this and memorializing it in October of 2020, that the attorney involved here, the AUSA, Lesley Wolf (ph), said we have no reason to believe there's anything fabricated nefariously on the computer or the hard drive, that there are emails and other items that corroborate the items on the laptop and hard drive. This is before the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: It is, Shannon. And what's so staggering about all this is that we are now learning about information that the Justice Department, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the \uc2 IRS\uc2  had it in its possession a long, long time ago. And from my perspective, that just deepens the mystery here. In other words, they have this information, they knew what was going on, they knew what was on the laptop, they knew who owned it and yet here we are, four or five years later, with this big investigation. To Jonathan's point, five-year indication ending not with a bang but with a whimper to two misdemeanor accounts, no time served.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are conflicts, as we all realize. So maybe Weiss will come forward or other explanation.\par \par \uc2 But, in the meantime, I have all the experts here. This is the closing week of the Supreme Court. Elizabeth, you and I spent a lot of time over there.\par \par \uc2 We're waiting on so many opinions, the really big stuff. It should wrap up this week.\par \par \uc2 What you looking for what and what do you think the court will do?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Yes, absolutely. So, you know, we are reaching the end of the term and there are still some really big cases that have yet to be decided. Whether it's race-conscious admissions in higher education, the affirmative action cases, 303 Creative, which is a case about anti-discrimination laws and the rights of LGBTQ couples who are getting married. We also have the independent state legislature theory case which has massive ramifications for properly functioning democracy and we have a 2024 election coming up, so that case is an incredibly important case.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it has been a year since the Dobbs decision and the court overturning Roe versus Wade. And so a lot of eyes have been on the Supreme Court this year that I think the heat of the spotlight has been on. And some of these ethics questions I think, while the eyes of the country have been on the court, you know, that has not been a good look. Whether or not, you know, there's any there there, the law requires both the appearance and the reality of impartiality in the administration of justice. So, I think having the court reconsider, or maybe Congress consider a meaningful ethics code with meaningful enforcement is something that could be good for the court and good for the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, quickly, Professor, 10 seconds on what you're -- which one you're watching for.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, there's a big concern about the recent standing decision, how that's going to affect the student loan case, which has a major standing issue. But for me the case to watch is 303 Creative. It could completely change how we deal with speech in this country from bakers to photographs - - to photographers to web designers. So whether they can, for religious reason and speech reasons in this case, say, no, I don't want to take this job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, panel, we've got to leave it there. Thank you for your expertise.\par \par \uc2 Up next my conversation with former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Benjamin Watson, unafraid to weigh in on some of the days most divisive issues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A year since the Supreme Court overturned Roe, the abortion discussion feels more polarized than ever. That's not stopping former pro- football star Benjamin Watson from joining the debate. He played tight end for 16 years in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , Patriots, \uc2 Browns\uc2 , Saints and Ravens. He was also a Walter Payton Man of the Year finalists several times and retarded in 2020. In the years since, he's been an analyst for the SEC Network and continues to write books. His latest, "The New Fight for Life," is out this week. We sat down to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Benjamin Watson, great to have you with us on FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN WATSON, : Good to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about the book. I think it's interesting. One of the very first chapters you say, the elephant in the room. And that's the question of why a retired \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, a man -\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is diving into this conversation about abortion. But you're not shy when it comes to getting into any kind of difficult conversation.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yes, there is a narrative out there that men shouldn't talk about this issue. And I always push back on that. It's imperative that men be involved in this conversation. And so the challenge I think not only for me but other men is, you have a voice in this issue. Not only do you have a voice, it's necessary that we stand up, not only for pre-born children, but for mothers who are making these decisions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A lot of folks have said, in a post-Dobbs, the decision that took down Roe, that the pro-life community, they say, is just about making sure these babies are born and they don't really care about the situations these women have or the lives that these children are going to have if they are brought into this world. You say it's got to be a lot more holistic approach.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Definitely. How to we, in a post-Roe world, as a pro-life movement, shift toward these connection points of justice that prove that, yes, we care about pre-born children, but we care about if mom can go and have a sustainable wage at work. We care if a community has been systemically oppressed over time and how that impacts income levels. Those sorts of things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, in that vein, a lot of pro-choice advocates out there will say, when women lose access in some of these, you know, mostly red states, that it disproportionately affects black and brown women who are having economic struggles. If you take away this option for them, it is just another, they would say, injustice on their lives.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Well, I would say, yes, it does impact black and brown communities. I talk about this in a book. Historically, black communities have been the ones who have suffer the most from programs, policies and the people who have discriminated over time. And so we have to take a step back and look at things from a holistic context. And when we do that and we see such disparities in wealth and health outcomes. Even the maternal mortality rate is something that - that we talk about many times. To me that is the more holistic, that's the more powerful, that's the more permanent way, an holistic way, to care, not just for children but from mothers, for fathers and for whole communities.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you talk about in the book that all of this, in these tough conversations, this is tied up also in the conversation about social justice.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: There's a statistic that gets thrown around a lot. Black women are three to four times more likely to have an abortion in there white counterparts. And so, for me, when I hear that, being a black man in this country, having black children, having a black wife, black parents, I say, why? What are the different factors that are making this community, my community, more vulnerable to the stain of abortion and how do we address those factors? That's what "The New Fight for Life is About," addressing those things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've talked to a lot of pro-choice folks to understand how you can have some kind of understanding of each other. Is that missing?\par \par \uc2 WATSON: I look in people's eyes and I see their humanity. Even if I disagree with them on this issue or a bunch of other issues. Even if I vehemently disagree. I think when we do that and we strip away some of the politics from it, we will more adequately see the best ways to serve people and advocate for human flourishing across the board, not just trying to win a certain argument or to win our side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you can hear more my conversation with Benjamin Watson on my podcast "Livin' the Bream." It's out brand new this morning.\par \par \uc2 Plus, all of today's program is on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewpodcast.com or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And don't miss the best moments of FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Always set your DVR to record the show each week on your local station or Sunday's 2:00 p.m. Eastern on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you back here for next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission o