edom of Information Act a possibility as well here, for certainly for journalists. I'll tell you why they don't. Because they don't want to admit that this issue is about the "big guy". Ditto for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . Now absent from their little write ups was a line saying we reached out to the White House for comment, that wasn't in there.\par \par \uc2 But chose puppeteers extend well beyond the media and all their enablers to more powerful organs like the DOJ. Now among the more just lunatic charges, because this is real lunacy when you think about it, of the DOJ interference that was laid out by the whistleblower was where he described how justice had no interest in investigating any leads about the "big guy" whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 Now we wanted to question \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's associate Rob Walker, about an email that said 10 held by H for the "big guy". But the AUSA, Lesley Wolf interjected and said she didn't want to ask questions about Dad, saying there's no specific criminality to that line of questioning. Garland was asked about the allegations today, of course, he dismissed them and then predictably closed with a well-worn bromide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: Some have chosen to attack the integrity of the Justice Department and its components and its employees by claiming that we do not treat like cases alike. This constitutes an attack on an institution that is essential to American democracy and essential to the safety of the American people. Nothing could be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, the irony and garlands lame little warning is obvious. The DOJ media loop meant to shield the President is among the most democracy destroying arrangements imaginable. And late this afternoon after all the incoming, the White House did respond through the White House Counsel's office saying, "the President was not in business with his son. As we have also said many times before the Justice Department makes decisions and its criminal investigations independently."\par \par \uc2 We didn't say he was in business with him. That's -- you see how they're parsing words there. Now, what the Counsel's Office refused to answer was whether Joe had ever spoken with his son about his overseas business dealings. Now, you can't count on direct answers from the White House. We know that. but you can count on the DOJ to provide perpetual protection.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Byron York, Chief Political Correspondent for The Washington Examiner, Fox News contributor, and Chris Bedford, Executive Editor for the Common Sense Society. Byron, I thought that line from the White House Counsel's Office you know as a former white collar criminal defense attorney that line saying he was not in business with his son. Go ahead, Byron, what is that reassure of?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: That's why they have a word called lawyerly. That's very lawyerly. Remember that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has over the years denied repeatedly that he knew anything about his son's businesses, about the foreign shady connections that took place when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the Vice President. And after he was the Vice President, and continued perhaps until he became the actual President.\par \par \uc2 The WhatsApp message you're talking about July 30,2017, that's the biggest piece of new evidence that we've seen in quite a long time. And there's it Hunter Biden simply says he's threatening this Chinese business associate saying, "if I don't get what I want from you right now, dad is right here. And he knows a lot of people, and it's going to be really bad for you." I - - It's the strongest piece of evidence we have yet that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was aware and was there. And if he wasn't, if \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was lying, if there's some other circumstance, then we need to know that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Chris, Hunter's Attorney Chris Clark released a statement today, and they're never on camera, of course, saying, "any verifiable words or actions of my client, in the midst of a horrible addiction, are solely his own and have no connection to anyone in his family." Chris, he is a victim. They you knew they were going to play this game. It's the drugs that did it. This wasn't real. I wasn't at my dad's house where apparently photos, you know, actually show him that he was there. What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, COMMON SENSE SOCIETY EXEC EDITOR: And it certainly was connected to his bank account, because just a few weeks after that message was sent, over $5 million was transferred from the Chinese company. He was talking to our accounts associated with it, two accounts associated with Hunter. So it absolutely worked. He was in business for this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you see the Department of Justice now redacting information in the non-classified document that they were forced to share with Congress that showed examples of actual reference examples of actual voice recordings of not just \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  talking to the President of \uc2 Burisma\uc2  but also, allegedly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself on two different occasions, talking to the President -- -- of the President of \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , about these actual accounts they had.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , they finally tackled the story, but they tackle it in a way that's designed to take it down. You've got multiple \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents coming out and saying the DOJ was stopping this investigation, they wouldn't let it go forward, they're blocking the U.S. Attorney. Everything they're saying is a lie. And here are multiple examples.\par \par \uc2 And both Post and \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  took those some specific examples and said, well, we don't actually know where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is sitting at that moment. But that's not the accusation, even though that's a bombshell. The whole thing was the DOJ is shielding the president from this, and so is the media.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the White House Press Secretary today, went back to the podium to try to play a little defense herself. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You stated that the President stands by his comment from the 2020 campaign that he never wants to discuss his son's overseas business dealings with his son. And you stood at that podium and you reaffirmed that. Do you stand by your resume?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What I will say is, nothing has changed, nothing has changed. And I will leave it there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Byron, they need to realize this, you know, this enough at this stage? I mean, a lot has changed. Even if, you know, we have the reporters like Andrea Mitchell who said, well, we can't authenticate these WhatsApp messages. Like how about pursue the story? OK, pursue this story.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, yes, well, you know, White House's can exist in bubbles. And certainly as of Tuesday, the White House seemed to think that the whole Hunter Biden thing was over and closed. He pleaded guilty to two misdemeanors, they dropped the -- the gun thing, and everything's fine. And, you know, 48 hours outcome with Republicans with this evidence that they have gathered, and it is by no means all of the evidence that they have got.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the idea that President Biden could actually believe that he's over this thing, that he's gotten past this thing, I find that actually believable, the White House has not yet really figured out that another House -- another party controls the House of Representatives, and it's not going to go away.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, this was an amazing week, and also another week, when the American media, the corporate media has utterly embarrassed itself and the way that they covered this or didn't cover it or didn't pursue it. It is, I mean, it's why this is, you know, all of it's going down. They got -- their credibility is shot. Byron, Chris, have a great weekend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Former \uc2 NCAA\uc2  swimmer Riley Gaines, remember she's been an outspoken opponent of men competing in women's sports. She was on Capitol Hill this week giving emotional testimony.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RILEY GAINES, FORMER NCAA SWIMMER: In addition to being forced to give up our awards and our titles and our opportunities, The \uc2 NCAA\uc2  forced me and my female swimmers to swim, to share a locker room with Thomas, a 6'4", 22 year old male equipped with and exposing male genitalia.\par \par \uc2 Let me be clear about this. We were not forewarned that we would be sharing a locker room. No one asked for our consent, and we did not give our consent, we were forced to take off our swimsuit in front of a man who was doing the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. And another swimmer NC State's Kylie Alons says she actually changed in a storage closet at the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  finals instead of being subjected to that in the locker room. Joining me now exclusively is Kylie Alons, 31 time all American and former NC State swimming star and Penny Nance, CEO and President of Concerned Women for America, which does great work on this.\par \par \uc2 Kylie, I now -- I was a college athlete, I never had to change in a storage room. And I never in my wildest dreams would have thought that something like this would be foisted upon someone like you. Top of your sport, incredible competitor. Tell us what was going on there and what made you push into that storage room?\par \par \uc2 KYLEE ALONS, FORMER NC STATE SWIM STAR: Absolutely. Thank you for having me. Like you said, the 2022 \uc2 NCAA\uc2  it's something that I never expected to ever experience. And it was, it was horrible to go through that at the best meat of my life. The most prestigious meat and collegiate so many women were being forced to change completely out of their clothing and into these tight tech suits that take up to 40 minutes sometimes to get on.\par \par \uc2 And the way that I had -- handled it was to change in the storage closet. And people have joked with me about how this gender confusion stuff forced me into a closet. But honestly, I was just so thankful and so thankful that I had at least that little bit of privacy, even if it was in a closet.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Was there any attempt to be, you know, to cover up?\par \par \uc2 ALONS: I actually was lucky enough to not be in the same quarters at the exact same time. But even when I was having that experience of changing in the locker room, when Thomas wasn't in there, I was just constantly on edge. I was completely covering myself with my parka, with my towel, just in case he happened to walk in on me while I was trying to get into my practice and racing suits.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Penny, here's what your old pal Senator Dick Durbin had to say. Again, another big champion of women, about the rhetoric coming from us conservatives. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Our children are listening. And they are in danger. In fact, today transgender youth are among the most at risk of homelessness, depression, and death by suicide. So when these young people who are already struggling here, politicians amplify hateful rhetoric that denies their very existence. What message does it send?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So Penny, by discussing this we're committing rhetorical violence, apparently, that's what they're are given us. You can't disagree, in other words.\par \par \uc2 PENNY NANCE, CONCERNED WOMEN FOR AMERICA CEO: Over two thirds of Americans are in deep agreement with us. Leading in those polls are African Americans and women and independents, by the way. And so any presidential candidate should be aware of this is a key issue. This is a direct violation of Title 9; this is sexual harassment. They did not consent to be naked in front of a man and they didn't consent for him to be naked in front of them. And so I think there's lawsuits that are going to abound. Concerned Women for America has been on this for five years. And we're so proud --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, you saw where it was going. Yes. You saw --\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Young women that are leading on this, you know, it's very, it's very courageous for them to take a firm stance.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kylie, did you -- you swam directly in competition against Lia Thomas. Yes?\par \par \uc2 ALONS: Yes, I was -- we both were on the 100 free and I was beat by him.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How much did he beat you by?\par \par \uc2 ALONS: I think he was seventh or eighth and I was 13th or 14th. I can't remember the exact placing -- by a few places.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Kylie and Penny, thank you very much for joining us. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Thank you. Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The largest public policy gathering of conservative and Christian activists in the nation is going on right now just down the street. So we sent a producer and we wanted to go down there to ask folks what's on their mind. Why make the trek here? That video. Plus, the head of the Faith and Freedom Coalition \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2  in moments. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thousands of evangelicals are gathering in Washington this weekend for a huge conference that's attended by almost every Republican Presidential candidate. It's called the Faith and Freedom Coalition's Road to the Majority Policy conference. And we decided, why not send one of our producers there to find out why it's so important for a candidate to embrace this movement? Is it important at all? Does the voting bloc even matter anymore? And why is this persistently felt like such a threat to the Left. Here's what they said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Left has already demonstrated what happens when you let them have control of anything, they don't have direction, they don't have a foundation. And when you don't have a foundation, you can't build anything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They see that we have a real voice of truth. And they feel threatened by so many of the things that the Left is, you know, moving forward, their agenda is so immoral and so anti-biblical, that we're a threat to them. When we and kindness say we're not going to stand for some of this crazy ideology that you have.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The best that they can do is try to separate us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why do you think it's so important for the GOP to embrace the evangelical movement?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We cannot let families to go down the drain. They want to destroy everything that have to do with the common.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have to save the family. And the family value is the art of the American nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a country that was founded on Christian principles. And every form of cultural craziness that we're seeing right now is an attack not just on America, but to the attack on evangelical traditions, the Bible, the nuclear family, you name it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How important do you think it is that the Conservatives or the Republican Party continue to embrace the evangelical base?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I think they're intertwined. You know, the conservative base comes from the religious base, which is infused into the Constitution, The founding of our nation came from that. Most of those, the leaders and the founders of the nation, were all Christians.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you still have hope for America, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my goodness. I have so much, so much hope for America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The way we take back America is by taking back that God given right and that faith in God that our country is founded, where everybody, no matter who you are, has the opportunity to succeed, be a blessing to their family, be blessed in their community and move this country forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are finally at a point where we can no longer put our head in the sand and pretend like the things that are happening right outside of our doors aren't occurring anymore. We didn't start the culture war, but I think we can and we will win it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You know who they are the former Republicans dining out on being former Republicans who want you to think that the emerging Republican Party isn't just destroying conservatism, but it's actually destroying religion itself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC HOST: -- Have not only taken over and twisted and distorted the Republican Party, they've done it to conservatism and they've done it to evangelicalism.\par \par \uc2 PETER WEHNER: That's been the most personally painful thing for me to see, not just the damage to the country, but to the damage to the faith, which I think is almost incalculable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining us now is Raul Reed founder and chairman of the Faith and Freedom Coalition. Ralph, I've heard a lot of attacks on the populace on the other kind of anti-establishment part of the GOP. I like to call them the Reagan Republicans, but now destroying religion itself.\par \par \uc2 RALPH REED, FOUNDER, FAITH & FREEDOM COALITION: Yes, you know, the only thing that these Christians are guilty of committing is citizenship. That's the only crime they've committed. I mean, the reality is, Laura, that throughout American history, citizens have engaged in the civic process. They've lobbied on behalf of legislation that reflected their values, they've gotten involved in political parties, they've supported the candidate of their choice.\par \par \uc2 And every great social reform movement in the history of this country, from anti-slavery to suffragism, to the settlement house movement, to the temperance movement, to the civil rights movement, to today's pro-life movement flowed out of the churches, based on a moral fervor based on their reading of the scripture, and what they believe was morally right and wrong.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And what they're getting at, Ralph, is that, you know, certain candidates are more flawed characters than others.\par \par \uc2 REED: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And the lurch toward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and, you know, his personal flaws and foibles as if we don't all have them.\par \par \uc2 REED: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That -- that any evangelical, I think that's what that Pete Wehner is getting at that clip, that any evangelical whatever consider supporting him --\par \par \uc2 REED: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- Has completely lost their --\par \par \uc2 REED: You mean -- You mean kind of like \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  working with Lyndon Johnson to pass the Civil Rights Act? I mean, look, what they're really saying is that they want Christians to stay in their stained glass ghetto, and they don't want to get their hands dirty with the reality of politics.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of governing.\par \par \uc2 REED: Yes, exactly. Because the reality is that no one's perfectly clean. We're all sinners saved by grace. And the reality is if you go back and you look at the 2016 primaries, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not the first choice of a majority of evangelicals. But once he won the nomination, and you were confronted with either him or \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , filling the vacancy left by the untimely death of \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , it was not a hard call. It may have been difficult in some ways, because --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why don't the Republican address the pro-life, annual March for Life and in person?\par \par \uc2 REED: What other presidential candidate in American history ever released a list of 21 jurists and said if you elect me President, I will replace Scalia with one of these 21? And he kept the promise.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. Right and actually followed through.\par \par \uc2 REED: And had we followed the advice of the critics, Laura, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  would have filled that vacancy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, they prefer -- they would prefer right now if the choice was -- but if -- if Wehner and those guys could choose whether it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I bet they'd say the same about DeSantis actually, but Trump versus -- versus Biden, then pick Biden, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 REED: The good news is, is that 40 to 50 million evangelical and prolife faithful Roman Catholics rejected the advice. They overcame their concerns about Trump. They voted for him; they elected him. He appointed not one, not two, but three Supreme Court justices. And today Roe is on the ash heap of history.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think they've run with some success on the abortion issue. And I think, you know, in those polls, and Michigan and so forth, abortion issue did help them.\par \par \uc2 REED: It did. But here's why. Because the Republicans put their head in the sand and thought they didn't have to address it. They thought all they had to do was talk about inflation, and gas prices and not talk about the life issue. You can't do that. When you have a decision like Dobbs that repeals a 50 year precedent and all of a sudden in your polling, it's the one two or three issue for voters, you got to address it, you got to lean into it. And you've got to make an argument about why your opponent is an extremist.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm pro-life, I am very pro-life. But I think this election is about abortion. It's got to be economy, inflation, the border. To me, those are the top three issues with the pro-life candidate, and then you're going to win. But if the focus is abortion, that's a dream for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 REED: I think with only one exception in my career, and that was 2004, the election is all about the economy. But, Laura, Republican candidates and the Republican Party can no longer be afraid of their own shadow on this issue.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Absolutely. And the Democrats, if they're so far Left, they've left America. Ralph, great to see, you have a wonderful conference this weekend.\par \par \uc2 REED: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: India's Prime Minister gets a front row seat to Biden's blunders and Ray gets another three wishes. Of course, it's Friday follies, and of course, it's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY STROHMIER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This is a FOX News alert. I'm Ashley Strohmier live in New York.\par \par \uc2 Moscow is on high alert as it issues an arrest warrant for the leader of the Wagner mercenary group. Russian officials say the group's leader is calling for an armed rebellion against its military leadership. In a series of angry videos, the group's leader blames Russian military personnel for killing thousands of its soldiers in Ukraine. He also says his forces have entered the Russian city of Rostov without resistance. The group has been helping Russia with its war efforts in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And a new declassified report from the office of National Intelligence is rejecting the theory that COVID-19 was leaked from a lab in China. That report was released today. It says that American agencies remain divided on how the pandemic started. Some intelligence officials believe we may never know the actual cause.\par \par \uc2 I'm Ashley Strohmier. Back to THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. For all of your headlines, log on to FOXnews.com.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, favorite part of the week is Friday follies, let's face it. Let's start with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , your fav.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, the president had a rough week, Laura, especially at that state dinner, which may commemorate his state of confusion. When Prime Minister Modi of India showed up, Biden took his hand and wouldn't let go. Watch Dr. Jill here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I love how the schoolteacher emerges here. She tries to get in the middle of them, and then she moves Modi around, and then takes his hand and guides them both down the carpet. How nice of her.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, she's just very caring. She is a doctor after all, so she's always looking out for people's well-being.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 And then when it was time to play the anthems Laura, Biden thought they were playing our national anthem until he slowly realized they weren't.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was classic.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The slow withdraw. The slow -- nobody will see it if I move my hand slowly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The good thing was his like, really. I thought something happened to the video there. It's like, why is everything -- then I saw the flags moving fast. That was very odd.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, just the hand. And during the India state dinner, in between introducing Hunter to the assembled, there was this moment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hunter?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Please join me in a toast, but I don't know if we have any glasses.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Enjoy your meal.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Where are we? Well, we're at a state dinner and it's the White House. That's where we are.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, the great thing is that he's trotting around, Hunter is at every state dinner, going over to Ireland with him. Jim Biden, all the gang's there, all the corrupt-ocrats (ph) were there. All of them.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It was a rough way to start a campaign, and this is why the Democrats are worried.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Cornell West, keep going. Stay in that race.\par \par \uc2 All right, Ray, it's time for your three wishes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, my favorite --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Your wish is my command here, as always.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- part of the week.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, I wish audiences would stop hurling objects at the people they paid to see. This week Harry Styles was hit in the face by a bouquet of flowers in England, and in New York, Bebe Rexha, her best f-ing night of my life concert clearly wasn't. A guy threw a phone at the singer, and mid-act ended the concert. He was taken into custody and charged with assault. Rexha was discharged from the hospital with lacerations. If people want to boo or they want to applaud, fine. You're not allowed to take part in the act, and you're not allowed to assault the people on the stage. This is way out of control.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You've never seen a \uc2 Phillies\uc2  game, obviously, because they whip stuff all the time at the games.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Or a jets game.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Raymond, do you remember when they threw that shoe at poor George H.W. Bush, remember, they threw the shoe at him that one time during the --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He did that quick move, the Bush shuffle.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, I think, was it W or H.W.? I don't know, but the shoes were flying at one point.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It was W.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Ray, the second wish.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, my second wish is that Senators \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  and Jon Tester would stop touching each other and just get a room. This is Tester's latest ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER, (D-NJ): So I'm asking you to step up and join the fight, protect -- whoa.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Give some money, please. We need your help, really, for this campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: These two, Laura, are as touchy feely as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at Eva Longoria's house. Give us a break. What is this, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  combine? They're jamming and pushing each other into walls. And my question is, is that a public wall? Did we pay for that?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, well, it certainly seems like they're trying to really emphasize their masculinity in this we're bros, and we're brawling bros, we love each other. It's like one of those deals.\par \par \uc2 I notice that we kind of skated by that Longoria video though. We didn't get the slow-mo on the Joe move up the waist.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: We did that last week. We didn't want to punish the audience twice.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. All right, what's the last one.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, my final wish is that those who graduate would take not only their diplomas but some grace and civility along with them. Not like this lady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Let go. You didn't let me get my moment, so I want to say my name Kadiadiamo (ph) and I'm graduating today. I didn't like how you snatched the mic out of my hand, so today it's going to be all about me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, drops the mic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, Laura, she explained her mic drop moment this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have to announce our names and say our name before we get on the stage. The people that went before me, they all got to say their name. She was putting the mic down superfast for some black people. I just felt like I had to reclaim my moment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, look, if somebody was taking the mic from her at her graduation, I understand her getting upset. She should have said something to someone. But you don't lower yourself to the actions of others when you're offended, and certainly not at a moment of graduation. Take the moment with class and dignity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Summa cum loudmouth.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, that was something. I had to think about that for about 10 seconds. All right, Raymond, have a great weekend, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Good one.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: While the wretched \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  is squawking as usual about the nonexistent book bans, what's happening to our kids in school? It's all good, right? The kids are all right? Well, we have all the details, the real truth. Plus, a teacher and parent on what's contributing to the ongoing decline. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Kids and families that are harmed by this Republican frenzy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're almost invading school districts where they're in the minority in order to destroy the education that black and brown and tan and LGBT kids really need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think this is an unbelievably damaging, generational --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Generational.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- harm because of the cynical nature of one political party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, that's right, Nicolle. But that harm is actually coming from the left. Now, it's something we on the Angle have been saying for years, that Democrats are not putting our kids first, no matter how many times Pelosi says, do it for the children. And most of what, of course, they are doing will and has irreparably harmed these children.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the lockdowns. Just a few days ago, the CDC revealed that youth homicides and suicides hit a 20-year high in 2021. Another report from the CDC from earlier this year said overdose deaths spiked amid the pandemic. And the new test scores from the just released National Assessment of Educational Progress shows us that math and reading scores for 13-year-olds have hit the lowest levels in decades. So the last time scores were this bad, by the way, was in 1990 for math and 2004 for reading.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Tony Kinnett, who is a former science teacher and an education policy advisor. Also joining me is Abe Hamilton III. He's a father of six and general counsel of the \uc2 American Family Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tony, now, I know that you saw this firsthand with your students. Tell me what you've seen, let's say from, several x number of years ago to today, the change. What is it?\par \par \uc2 TONY KINNETT, FORMER SCIENCE TEACHER: Well, a change is the exact word for it. When I started in education, I was encouraged to make sure that my students were learning how to read and write and the sciences, to make sure they were actually growing in their education. And then I moved to a school close for Indianapolis, and by the time the pandemic hit I wasn't allowed to collect homework. I wasn't allowed to ensure my students were learning materials. I wasn't allowed to grade things. And I was just expected to pass those students on to the next grade level.\par \par \uc2 And it was horrifying, because my students weren't any more prepared to participate in tenth or junior science rather than when they were in my class. And that's unfortunately become the standard in the American public education system.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Abraham, you decided you couldn't take it anymore and you're the father of six children. What did you do?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM HAMILTON III, \uc2 AMERICAN FAMILY ASSOCIATION\uc2 : Yes, my wife and I prayerfully made the decision to resort to what the founders of our country did to educate their children, which was to do that from our home. Looking into a lot of the research and the things that are coming out with the NAEP scores as you mentioned, a lot of this has been the product of what has been designed for quite some time, to utilize the education system by people, they call them progressives, but really I call them regressives because they're not advancing anything.\par \par \uc2 They've utilized the education system to contribute to really impacting the nation's output by causing the children to suffer from a lack of true education. What you see, the trillions of dollars that have been poured into the educational system, yet these outcomes are consistently manifesting in this direction. And as Tony pointed out rightly, their focus is on everything but reading, writing, and arithmetic. It seems to indicate that it's more than just coincidental. It's by designed by some of those regressives who have a stranglehold over our educational system in our country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What else is contributing to this? Because this decline in attention span, decline in performance, clarity of thought, and all of that, that's been going on for some time. What other factors contribute to it? Like, perhaps, drug legalization efforts nationwide, has that had an effect?\par \par \uc2 KINNETT: There has. According to a recent study by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," there is a considerably prefrontal cortex thinning, a cortical thinning, that occurs when adolescents decide to kind of flirt with drug use. Whenever you're an adolescent and you start messing around with things that have now been deemed perfectly safe for societal consumption, your brain, which is developing at the time, does not see that as something that you can tamper with. It messes with your endocrine system, your nervous system.\par \par \uc2 And those lasting effects are not just shown in the classroom where it can mess with attention span, how you respond to thing, reaction time, emotional distribution and regulation, but it can also, of course, cause lasting effects throughout the entire life. You can't flip a switch and reset your brain and nervous systems.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, especially the developing brains.\par \par \uc2 And Abraham, \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , of course, who is the head of the teachers' union, she had her own theory about why things are where they are. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: The scores went down because of COVID. And I thought all of us were wrong when we said there was no learning loss. Of course, there was learning loss. Of course, there was a mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 What COVID did was it created huge loneliness and dislocation.\par \par \uc2 Both Fauci and Walensky are being demonized in a way that's wrong. They were trying to do the best they could.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Abe, is that good enough? I mean, European countries, certain European countries really didn't ever shut down, but we had so many states, mostly Democrat-run states, of course, that kept it going and going and going. And now the victims are the children.\par \par \uc2 HAMILTON: Yes, and to say that this is because of COVID is just completely disingenuous. I'm from the hood in New Orleans, Louisiana. There have been metal detectors in the public schools in my neighborhoods long before COVID. The inability to have textbooks and utilize them in classes happened long before COVID. It's just a convenient excuse.\par \par \uc2 What we are literally reeling from is, unfortunately, a designed plan to utilize education to create, as Chester Pierce said in 1972, the international child of the future, where reading, writing, and arithmetic takes a backseat to using the classroom really to create social activists in a particularly leftist organization.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tony, it's being a good global citizen. You hear that in school. I have school-age kids, but I hear that a lot.\par \par \uc2 KINNETT: It's not a community involvement like you and I were raised with, where you make sacrifices to ensure your community has what it needs to get better. This is a new kind of community that is based what kind of sexuality you have, what kind of color of your skin you have, and the many oppressions that you suffer through. And instead of focusing on reading, writing, math, and history, we have started focusing all our time with our students on how they can be activists toward racial equity, toward celebrating pride month all year round, making sure that we are trying to one up each other on this fantastic pyramid of social justice. And no wonder the kids aren't learning. They're too busy learning how to cry over a gender study's degree. It's pathetic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, everybody's a victim today. Tony and Abe, great to see both of you. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 KINNETT: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 HAMILTON: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Up next, a very special birthday wish to a truly amazing person, someone I've known for decades. I'll tell you who it is, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Seventy-five years ago on June 23rd, 1948, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was born. He lived in a very poor area of Pinpoint, Georgia, abandoned by his father. He went to live as a young boy with his grandparents in a very poor area. He went onto Yale Law School, of course, assistant attorney general in Missouri, ultimately D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals, worked in the Reagan administration, then to the Supreme Court. He was nominated in 1991 by George H.W. Bush. I had the great privilege of clerking for Justice Thomas on his second year in the court.\par \par \uc2 And recently for a big documentary on him called "Created Equal," he looked back on his confirmation hearing, as contentious as it was, and specifically remembered one particular senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: Come on, we know what this is ball. This isn't about what they say it's about. So people should just tell the truth. This is the wrong black guy. He has to be destroyed. Just say it.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's a fervent and aggressive school of thought that wishes to see natural law further inform the Constitution. That may be lost on all the people. You know and I know what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I have to be perfectly honest with you. You sit there and you have no idea what they are talking about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What a bozo. He's an originalist. Ever heard of it? Happy birthday, Justice Thomas. Thank you for all you've done for our Constitution, reinvigorating its meaning, the court, all those who clerked for you, all those whose lives you've impacted. You've meant an enormous amount to me and my family. This Christmastime this past year, he always brings his clerks in and former clerks, and there are a lot of us now. Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Thanks for watching this special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. Have a lovely weekend. Remember, it is America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld takes it all from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and 