could get worse.\par \par \uc2 Wow. Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 FOX on top of Mother Nature getting a tad over the top.\par \par \uc2 To my buddy Rick Reichmuth on how much over the top.\par \par \uc2 What are we looking at here, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: So not only is it abnormal to -- Neil, to be at the C named storm already. It's where these storms are forming that is really abnormal.\par \par \uc2 And these storms have been forming out here in the open Atlantic. These were waves that came off the coast of Africa, and that generally happens once we're into mid-August and September. We get into that type of tropical development. So, getting these -- in fact, we have never had two that have formed to the east of the Lesser Antilles ever.\par \par \uc2 And now we have got that right now, all forming in a time when we thought we might be looking at a lower-than-average season because of El Nino. I will talk more about that in a second. But, currently, we have got Bret. We have got Cindy. There is no real threat to land anywhere from this.\par \par \uc2 We have had some strong wind gusts. If you throw up weather map 16 behind me, that will be the good map so that I can show you what's going on here. But we had a wind gust of 69 miles an hour in St. Lucia, so that was our strongest wind gust.\par \par \uc2 Again, weather map 16, Char (ph), if you can put that up here behind me, that will give me the good map, so that I can show you what's going on.\par \par \uc2 Bret down across parts of the Atlantic, it's going to continue. There we go. Thank you. Continue to pull off towards the west. It'll dissipate by the time it has any more interaction with land. That's good news. Cindy is going to stay out over the open water. So we don't have to worry about this.\par \par \uc2 But getting two storms that have formed in the Atlantic Basin to the east of the Lesser Antilles has never happened before at this time. And as you were just saying, Neil, generally, the typical first A name is June 20. That is right about where we are now. To get to the C name, that's August 3 when we would normally see that.\par \par \uc2 And about 98 percent still of our hurricane season is remaining still. September 10, 11 is about the peak of when we have the most of our hurricane activity. But take a look at these ocean temperatures, Neil. These ocean temperatures here across parts of the Eastern Atlantic are around seven or eight degrees above where they would typically be.\par \par \uc2 Water does not heat up as fast as air. So to get water that warm this early is certainly worrisome. And anywhere you see yellow, which is almost everywhere, those are water temperatures that are above average. You will also notice out here in the Pacific, this is El Nino.\par \par \uc2 And when you have an El Nino, generally, you see less activity in Atlantic because it makes hostile winds across the area. And we are now into El Nino and likely going to get into a pretty strong El Nino.\par \par \uc2 And so later on in the season, maybe that begins to have a bigger impact. And just because we have seen these three storms doesn't mean necessarily that that will be what we see all season long, but getting these three storms so early certainly is very strange, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, Bret, right here, this was that first one forming in the East Atlantic and that here in towards the Caribbean. It'll continue to dissipate, but we are off to a strange season, no matter how you slice this, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, my friend, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the heat, incredible, record-breaking heat, particularly in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Casey Stegall in Dallas with more on that side of the story -- Casey?\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon, 101 here in downtown Dallas the heat index at the moment.\par \par \uc2 And next week is the real concern, as we look to forecasted temperatures for all of next week to be well within the triple digits, and those heat index values or the real field temps reaching up to 120 degrees in some places.\par \par \uc2 So, breaking in the last hour, \uc2 ERCOT\uc2 , the agency in charge of the state power grid, has issued now a weather watch alert from this Sunday through next Friday. However, the leaders say they do expect enough in reserves to handle the anticipated demand.\par \par \uc2 For now, it is holding, no controlled black or brownouts to report, thankfully, despite record demand for June. Now, clearly, we know this can be extremely dangerous weather. A Dallas mail carrier of nearly 40 years died earlier this week after his family says he collapsed in a front yard while on his route; 66-year-old Eugene Gates died at a local hospital.\par \par \uc2 The family still waiting for the official cause of death from the medical examiner's office, but they do believe it was heat-related -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that. Let's hope things cool down quite literally.\par \par \uc2 Casey Stegall on that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Bryan Norcross right now, just take a look at all this weather phenomena, particularly these hurricanes that are sort of like backing up like planes at La Guardia International Airport, even though it's nowhere near La Guardia.\par \par \uc2 But, Bryan, what is going on with just not only the number of them, but the fact that this could accelerate?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN NORCROSS, FOX WEATHER HURRICANE SPECIALIST: Well, there are multiple things going on, Neil.\par \par \uc2 First of all, indeed, as Rick was showing, the Tropical Atlantic is spectacularly warm this time of year. But it's not just the Tropical Atlantic. The global oceans are warmer than we have ever seen -- well, than we have seen the last 40 years since we have had satellite and been able to measure them really accurately.\par \par \uc2 And they're suddenly extremely warm, and nobody really knows why that is. And so that's concerning. We'd like to know, like, to understand these things better. We know there's a lot more heat in the Earth's system now, and much of that heat is in the ocean. Is somehow -- have we reached some kind of tipping point?\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of discussion about this, but scientists around the world are very concerned. So when you heat up a lot of parts of the Earth, a whole variety of things happen. And one is that the Tropical Atlantic heats up. Another is that the systems don't move as fast.\par \par \uc2 So we have longer heat waves over Texas. In the Northeast U.S., it's been persistently cool all year. And we see things last longer because systems don't move. So you throw a lot of energy, extra energy in different parts of the world's system, and you get kind of unpredictable and different kind of responses.\par \par \uc2 And one of them has been this production of early season storms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan, thank you very much for that, Bryan Norcross, the FOX meteorologist, hurricane expert, on all of this.\par \par \uc2 We are going to keep track of this. And, of course, tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time live on my weekend show, we will be monitoring all these unstable weather developments across the country, so I put it in perspective for you.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, getting the latest right now, the day after the fact, what we're learning about that \uc2 OceanGate\uc2  sub vehicle, and now growing concerns that they knew ahead of time that this could be a problem, and the lawyers are getting involved -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, now the lawyers are getting involved a day after the confirmation that all five aboard that submersible are now dead, now the questions back and forth, what happened and sort of trying to get a timeline on how it happened.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin with more on that side of the story in St John's, Canada -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, one of the things we know is, all those days of searching were in vain. The \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  has confirmed to FOX News that acoustic technology they have out in the sea picked up the sounds of an implosion right about the time the Titan submersible lost contact with the surface.\par \par \uc2 Still, this searching went on for days. We chased down sounds of banging in different areas, or reports that sounds of banging had been detected. And, ultimately, what it took was that Canadian ship, the Horizon Arctic, to arrive on the scene with its Odysseus 6 ROV, which is able to make it to the seafloor in the area where the Titanic went down and locate the wreckage of the Titan about 1,600 feet off the bow of the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 Now, there -- no decision has been made as far as whether the wreckage of that submersible will be recovered, but there are some people who want it. There is still equipment out at the scene in terms of ships that are at the site where the Titanic and now the Titan both sank. We do know also that the Polar Prince, the ship from which the Titan launched, is on its way back here to the port of St. John -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Mike.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, the back-and-forth on how this could have happened. Apparently, there were warning signs. And I had a chance to speak to one Fred Hagen, who had been on this very vehicle, knows of some of those warnings. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are such possibilities laid out when you sign various waivers to get on these and be part of these. Maybe you could tell us about that part of it, because I have no doubt you signed the same waivers.\par \par \uc2 FRED HAGEN, FORMER OCEANGATE PASSENGER: I did, indeed.\par \par \uc2 And it was clearly articulated that there was a significant risk of death. Most people read those things, and they discount it. I mean, it will happen to someone else, not for me. And I'm no exception. But I did realize that there was a significant risk of death or harm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so there are those waivers, and the significance of that, and people who signed them anyway, whether they pay attention to that, some stand out, especially one in this case where the word death is repeated three times, and that's just on the first page.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes Colwin right now, attorney extraordinaire, she can help me make sense of this.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes, a lot of people don't really pay attention to waivers. Maybe several have in the past. I know that was the case with Fred Hagen, when he told me it did jar him a little, but he went ahead and took the trip in the past.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of the waiver thing and the impact that it has?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: It's always great to be on with you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of people think that, when they sign a waiver, they sign their rights, and that's just not true. If there's evidence of gross negligence - - and, certainly, there's a treasure trove of admissions by Mr. Rush talking about how he broke the rules, had to bend the rules.\par \par \uc2 All of those statements he made, he made very public statements about not the integrity of the vessel, obviously, but just some of the issues about the vessel itself, that he felt, as an innovator, he couldn't possibly create a vessel capable of going down 12,000 feet into the ocean to look at that Titanic without bending some of those rules.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is no admission that says specifically that the bending of the rules resulted in unsafe conditions for the individuals stepping into that vessel, but that's a treasure trove that any plaintiffs firm that is going to be representing the families -- and you know that there's going to be at least an inquiry as to whether or not these families can recover anything for the catastrophic loss of their family members.\par \par \uc2 So you -- they're going to use those admissions and certainly pummel the \uc2 OceanGate\uc2  with them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is leading the investigation into this, much as we would for a plane crash or what have you, but part of the stuff that's been getting out there is all these prior concerns -- we just don't know how many -- were raised about the safety of these particular devices and these particular submersibles.\par \par \uc2 James Cameron himself was not a fan of these companies' submersibles. He, in fact, was -- in an interview today, Mercedes, he said, it is quite possible that those in the Titan, the one that imploded, probably got an alarm or an early warning sign that there were problems.\par \par \uc2 So, while the explosion itself might have been milliseconds, there was a potential heads-up there. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Gosh, I really hope not.\par \par \uc2 Well, if there are going to be any wrongful death claims -- and, obviously, presumably that might be something the family members are considering -- there will be any fraction of that emotional upset, psychic injury. If you have an expert that steps forward and says they knew for a period of time before that implosion took place, the panic that went through them -- of course, could you imagine what that panic would be like, knowing that your life will be over within seconds, hours, whatever it was?\par \par \uc2 That's compensable under these wrongful death claims. So that's one. Two, there's going to be economic loss. Lots of these individuals, especially the young Suleman Dawood, who is young, 19 years of age, loss of companionship, loss -- the fact that his life was certainly -- he was only 19, many, many years ahead of him -- to be a successful contributing member of society, all of those things are bundled into what could be a really significant recovery for the family.\par \par \uc2 And we will have to see how it plays out. There's so many moving parts here, Neil. There's so much investigation. It'll be critical for the pieces that were left of the Titan vessel to be retrieved. An expert will look at them. An expert will certainly look at the construction itself.\par \par \uc2 How is it that this vessel, presumably just within an hour of immersing itself, was actually imploded? Well, that certainly speaks volumes about the integrity of the vessel itself. So we will have to see. A lot of moving parts to see what's going to happen with the families.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that will be a while, to your point, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that, Mercedes Colwin, following this very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I will have as my special guest tomorrow Guillermo Sohnlein. He is the co-founder of this \uc2 OceanGate\uc2 , and what they knew at the time. Now, he left a few years ago, but he might be able to speak to some of the signals that were out there and trying to sign up individuals who would pay up $250,000 for the ride, but the \uc2 OceanGate\uc2  co-founder my special guest tomorrow 10:00 a.m. Eastern time, as we pick apart what happened and what happens right now.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, one year ago, one year ago this very day, looking back at Gianno Caldwell losing his brother. What has he learned in that time and what has him very concerned about what's developing now? He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lab-grown meat has arrived. This is like chicken lab-grown meat. And guess what it tastes like? Chicken.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chicken.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GIANNO CALDWELL, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: There's so many victims of violent crime in the city of Chicago, so many people who don't even know how to pay for a funeral because they're so costly.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel safe when you're walking through the streets of Chicago?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chicago is probably one of the worst cities with crime. I pray for my life every day I get on that train. It's horrible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is not safe. Look at all these shootings. This is way too much.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: If you don't think that it's time to call in the National Guard or get extremely tough on violence in Chicago, then you're not paying attention at all.\par \par \uc2 How many other people are just one number in the deck of homicides that occur on a daily, weekly, or monthly basis in Chicago? I cannot allow my brother Christian's name to mean nothing. As his older brother, I can't do that. His legacy will mean something. And, yes, we got to push back on those soft-on-crime policies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It was a year ago, a year ago technically tomorrow, that my friend Gianno Caldwell lost his beautiful brother Christian in a random shooting, just like the hundreds, the thousands, probably, over the years that you have heard out of the Chicago area and so many other cities across this country, aimless, senseless, endless.\par \par \uc2 Gianno is with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Man, I don't know where the time went, Gianno. I know you have devoted this entire time to finding out what happened and trying to make sure, desperately trying to make sure in trips all over the country, including a memorable one to Washington...\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... to see that it doesn't happen again, but yet it does again and again. How are you doing, my friend.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It's a tough day. Tomorrow is going to be even tougher. It's been tough for my family.\par \par \uc2 My grandmother called me. I could barely understand 