w can you tell me there's a guy in federal custody right now who you believe there's a high likelihood is responsible for the murder of my brother and there be nothing done since then?\par \par \uc2 How can you not call me back? How could that be the case? Last year, I called Congressman Burgess Owens, who sat on the Judiciary Committee last year, and I said, hey, I want to get a bipartisan letter together requesting that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  get involved. I want Republicans and Democrats alike on it, because crime and violent crime is a bipartisan issue.\par \par \uc2 It should be a bipartisan issue, because Democrats and Republicans and independents alike are dying. Congressman Burgess Owens said: "Absolutely, I will help you."\par \par \uc2 And I have been working behind the scenes with Congress on this very issue to be a voice for victims all across the country. Now that we have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director coming up before Congress really soon, I am going to ask those members of Congress, Republicans and Democrats alike, ask, what is going on with the investigation into the murder of Gianno Caldwell's brother?\par \par \uc2 I have reached out to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  personally multiple times, have not heard anything back for them. So I want to request for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office in Chicago, whoever's there, call me. You have my number in your system. If you're in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters in Washington, D.C., call me. You have my number in your system.\par \par \uc2 It should not be that victims of violent crime across this country are suffering because prosecutors and these soft-on-crime prosecutors, these progressive prosecutors across the country are not doing their jobs, or these police departments where you have officers -- and I respect police departments. I'm a big support order of them.\par \par \uc2 But for those who don't want to do their jobs, they need to be called out. And that's why I'm here today, Neil. I am pissed the hell off with injustice