O, who says, what's going on here is big and is moving fast, very fast.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS SIEBEL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN AND CEO, C3.AI: We basically have infinite computing power available for very, very low cost today.\par \par \uc2 And these and some other technologies enable us to deploy what we call predictive analytics or enterprise A.I. So, this began with the Internet of Things, and then unsupervised learning, supervised learning, deep learning. And generative A.I. is just kind of the latest step, OK, in these developments.\par \par \uc2 It's a big step. But, today, we are able to apply predictive analytics to literally predict the future with when things are going to break, optimize supply chains, optimize demand chains, handle contested logistics, deal with hypersonics in the \uc2 Missile Defense Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so we're able to solve problems that were previously unsolvable.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were on this, I think it was your fourth book, "Digital Transformation." I was checking my notes here today, because that came out in, what, 2019?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: 2019.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you were talking about cloud computing, but, among the other things, this A.I.\par \par \uc2 And you were talking about the -- how fast things could move and how this could change things. Some fear that change. They think this is "2001: A Space Odyssey" and HAL is not opening the door.\par \par \uc2 How -- what do you tell people who are afraid of this, the crowd that says, pause it because we don't know where this is going, or this could destroy man? What do you...\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: The fears are very legitimate, though.\par \par \uc2 We have been working on A.I. now for 15 years and talking about it very vocally. And the rest of the world kind of figured out that this is legit. But -- and we will use A.I. to improve health care, to lower environmental impact of manufacturing, to help manufacturers deliver products on time, in full to more satisfied customers.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there are very significant deleterious implications of these technologies. I mean, pretty soon, it will be absolutely impossible to distinguish between real news and fake news. These technologies will be used, are being used today to propagate public health hazards. See social media for details.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You can be so easily fooled, right?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... one of these beta test cases for these various search engines.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was amazed how it was clumsy at first for A.I. to get the story right, but, within minutes, it was advancing, progressing, going way beyond, and not only getting it right, but dramatically changing the game in the process.\par \par \uc2 That builds the fear part, I guess.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: This is powerful stuff.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: There will be enormous good that comes out of it, but it will be used in deleterious fashions by bad actors and by...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who are the bad actors? We're afraid of China these days.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Well, there's a good reason to be afraid of China.\par \par \uc2 And I think these people are very competent. They're very focused. And they're dealing with -- they want to be leaders in A.I. and ship design and hypersonics and...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And military applications.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: And they will. And be afraid.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if we pause when they're doing that, what happens?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: This is not a good idea.\par \par \uc2 We're -- this idea of pausing science is just kind of crazy talk. I mean, we're -- nobody is going to stop. This is not ephemeral. This is a secular change. And this A.I. phenomenon is very big. It's very real. It changes everything. It changes everything in positive ways and it changes everything in some scary ways.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So that sounds like a long-term bet on your part that this is not going to be a fad.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think that business leaders and government leaders are facing today the extent, to what extent is A.I. going to impact their work force?\par \par \uc2 And, as a leader, the choice you have to make is, are you going to replace your work force or are you going to train your work force? And even at C3...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I thought A.I. was removing chunks of the work force.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: No, I think, with A.I., I think, for every job it eliminates, it'll create 10 to 100, just like investment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're in better shape if you have a computer science expertise.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: That's right.\par \par \uc2 So, as a business leader, I have chosen to motivate our people to go on to Coursera and take courses in deep learning, supervised learning, cloud computing. If our employees want to elect and want to go out and get their master's degrees in computer science or data science, and we pay for that, when they get their degree...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just not philosophy, right? Don't do philosophy.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: No, I'm all for philosophy. I'm all for history. I believe in liberal arts. My first degree is in history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You won't pay for those people pursuing that, right? I mean...\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Well, I think that, as we -- as it relates to preparing the work force...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: ... for the new economy, I mean, they really need to understand these technologies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I did want to get your political viewpoints, because I was noticing, in your giving, about 90 percent of it is to independent candidates, and some to Republicans. But I couldn't figure out where you are on this.\par \par \uc2 So, looking at the presidential race, does it make a difference to you who's the next president?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And we -- you know, I have participated in supporting candidates on both parties over the years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: And I am...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But why the disproportionate interest in independents?\par \par \uc2 I mean, is this a sign that you like the No Labels approach?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think that I like limited government, free enterprise, and individual liberty. So, I'm a -- I would say I'm an old-school liberal, and in the true sense of the word.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, does it matter to you whether it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or a Republican?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think that we will kind of see how that works out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you still go the independent route?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: Yes, but I would say that independent, leaning hard toward libertarian. And so...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. So, the why reason I pursue it...\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: So, I mean...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... your name comes up with this No Labels group. So...\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: No, I'm not associated with it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know that, but that you might...\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... make that kind of a candidate for them. Do you ever think of that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: There is no chance, Neil. And, no, I'm not associated with No Labels.\par \par \uc2 I know some of the people involved. I think there are fine people involved, but I have not -- I have no association.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you like the concept of a third party?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think it is probably a futile effort. I think this two-party system is pretty tough to beat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, it's a Republican or a Democrat?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And leave it at that?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: I think so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you're not -- you're not running for anything?\par \par \uc2 SIEBEL: There's no chance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Watch him closely, because you could learn a lot about where his money could be going and a particular candidate he is looking at right now.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, taking a look at the anniversary of the -- well, the defeat of Roe v. Wade, or at least so we thought about a year ago. But it's caused more problems, particularly for Republicans, than they ever imagined.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace on why that is -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The battle for life is far from over. We have not come to the end of our cause. We have simply come to the end of the beginning.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If Congress acts, I will sign a federal law to restrict abortion as well as president.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank God almighty for the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that depends.\par \par \uc2 Politically, if you look at the fallout, particularly for Republicans, who maybe because of that decision, it sort of dinged them at the polls in the midterm elections. Of course, there are a lot of other reasons for that.\par \par \uc2 But leave it to Nancy Mace, the South Carolina congresswoman, to say, we as a party -- she is, of course, a Republican -- have to find some sort of a middle ground on this or we're going to lose big.\par \par \uc2 She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you have been critical of some of the efforts to go beyond the court's decision and some states' moves, even in Florida, to say no abortions after six weeks, et cetera. Where do you think this is taking you and the party?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, we saw last year in the '22 midterm elections, we didn't win as many seats as we should have, this being a defining issue for our party.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, Republicans won the Super Bowl with the Dobbs decision. They got Roe overturned. But what happened as a consequence of that is that voters really rejected the idea of Roe being overturned, even pro-life voters. And I saw it in my own district. I actually made the issue of rape and incest as a cornerstone of my campaign in the general election because I wanted women to know that I cared about them.\par \par \uc2 And you're right. We have to find middle ground in '24 if we want to win over suburban women and others. We have to show that we care about women at the same time that we care about life. And I'm trying to show a path forward and how you do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the argument is that there might not be a path forward right now. You can't win for love or money on the issue. I know the DNC says that you're trying to have it both ways, that you have been a hypocrite on this.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I haven't.\par \par \uc2 My voting record speaks for itself. In fact, they dinged me for a fetal heartbeat bill in South Carolina. But, at the time, we were the only state in the nation to have any exceptions for rape or incest on that bill, because I told my story about being raped as a teenager.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that I want to emphasize with voters, whether they're Republican, Democrat, or independent, is that we should, as a society, all of us, find some common ground, and that being we need to show that we support women that are facing difficult decisions and are in difficult positions.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that we can do and -- is, I think that we can all agree that we want to reduce the number of abortions in this country. That is something I think most Americans, regardless of your party or political affiliation, we all can agree to do that. It's just how we get there and do it together as a nation is the greatest question.\par \par \uc2 And I'm trying to show a path forward on those issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you agree with a standard definition, maybe one that's accepted more over the world, at 12 weeks?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well. I would say 12, 15 or 20 weeks. Most people don't want to see a ban before the second trimester. Most people don't want to see abortions in the third trimester, so, some -- at some point during the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, do you have -- do you have your own personal preference?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, I have always said sort of 15 to 20 weeks, and as long as we have exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 But I think that's the wrong conversation to have today. I want to talk about how we protect women that are in difficult decisions, if they have an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, I understand that, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But does a six-week move like what Governor DeSantis just did in Florida, does that go too far to you?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It goes too far.\par \par \uc2 And what that Florida bill also did is, it mandated reporting of rape to the state. And I -- those are things that, if you're a victim of rape or a girl is a victim of incest, those are very hard pills to swallow. And it's not supported by the vast majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of Americans support some sort of gestational limit in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: On this whole push for Republicans should support whoever the nominee is for president -- there are a dozen candidates now, maybe more down the pipe -- how do you feel about that? Some are balking.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Some are what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Balking, not keen on that, because they can't take that pledge.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well -- yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, that has always been the Republican Party pledge. On any ticket I have ever run on as a state lawmaker or for Congress, that is a pledge you make as a Republican to support the nominee no matter what. And it often is a requirement for the debate stage as well. We will see how that shapes up in August. It'll be interesting.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I think \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  had said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't honor that. Why should he?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, it'll be a very interesting election, especially if you -- as you say, Neil, that there are going to be more candidates in there.\par \par \uc2 It's still very early on. We're not even through Labor Day yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And the contests, we're several months away. So it'll be a very interesting race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congresswoman, very good seeing you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, the one thing that is not several months away, it's like today, lab chicken, made in the lab that tastes just like chicken without any harm to the chicken -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it's not Impossible Meat. It's lab-grown meat. And there is a difference, especially when it comes to chicken.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady on what has been unearthed -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: So, Neil, it's called cell- cultivated chicken, right? So it's not plant-based. This is real meat made from real animal cells.\par \par \uc2 And two California start-ups called \uc2 Upside Foods\uc2  and Good Meat, they have just received approvals from the \uc2 USDA\uc2  and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to sell their product. So how it works is, animal cells are fed a mix of nutrients like sugars, vitamins, minerals. The cells multiply, and then they develop into fat and muscle tissue in the lab.\par \par \uc2 The process takes roughly one to three weeks. You won't be seeing this in the grocery store anytime soon, though. They're beginning small, partnering with local restaurants, because right now it's really expensive to make and sell this. But the hope is scaling up will make the process more economical.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks, though, they're pushing back. Critics of cell-cultivated meat share, it may take too much energy to make. And we actually spoke to a chef who worries the line between food and pharmaceutical is blurred.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GRUEL, RESTAURANTEUR: I personally believe that there needs to be a lot more research, more years to understand the effect that it has on our bodies. You're effectively synthetically building something in a petri dish with a lot of cells, and you're rapidly producing growth.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, this is more of a pharmaceutical.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: So not everyone's excited. But, Neil, this is a trial and education phase. I kind of thought controversial foods like veal and foie gras, you could eventually make those in a way that doesn't hurt animals.\par \par \uc2 I would try it, though. What about you, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's supposed to taste like the real thing, because, technically, I guess, it's the real thing, right?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Mm-hmm. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. If they can do that with pastrami Reubens, and away we go.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anyway, Kelly, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, Abby Hornacek is joining us right now on this very issue. FOX Nation host, Rikki Schlott as well, The New York Post columnist.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 There are all sorts of implications here. I understand that \uc2 PETA\uc2  is kind of liking this here because no animals would lose their lives as a result of this, again, if it extends beyond chickens. And that, by the way, I raised with the \uc2 Upside Foods\uc2  CEO, who has told me that there's more like that coming down the pike.\par \par \uc2 So, Abby, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 ABBY HORNACEK, "PARK'D" HOST: Neil, I wouldn't eat it. But I am perfectly thrilled if someone else wants to eat this, because if there's a way to reduce waste, why not?\par \par \uc2 About 450 million chickens die every year just on the farm or in transport to the Slaughterhouse. So they're so cute. I'm all for it. And the reality is, Kelly kind of mentioned this in her report, but this isn't going to really be on grocery store shelves soon.\par \par \uc2 Upside\uc2  is producing about 50,000 thousand pounds of this cultivated meat every year. Of course, that number is going to grow, but compare that to 50 billion pounds of chicken that the U.S. produces as a whole. So I am all for it. Of course, I think more research needs to be done, but if someone's willing to take one for the team, kudos to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rikki, what about you? I mean, when I was talking to the company CEO behind this, that just a matter of time before this extends to cows and to pigs or what have you. Then what?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI SCHLOTT, THE NEW YORK POST: Well, I haven't had meat in 10 years myself, so I will sign up to be the resident guinea pig, because I do it...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In 10 years?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: In 10 years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Since I was in high school. So I'm dedicated. I will be the guinea pig and take one for the team because I'm just in it for the animals and the ethics. And I think that this is actually a sustainable and potentially more environmentally, ecologically and ethically good option for a lot of people potentially down the line.\par \par \uc2 But I understand that it's a little creepy to have meat that's grown in basically a tank. So I will take one for the team, for sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, there you go.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you though, Abby. We're told that unlike people who have an Impossible Burger or something like that, where there are health benefits to the human being eating it, there isn't here because it is actually chicken. If this extends to cows, it is actually beef. If it extends to pigs, it is actually bacon.\par \par \uc2 So the notion that you get off lightly because this isn't real meat, it is.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do you think that's going to go down?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Well, I think it's great because people who don't eat meat for the ethical reasons, like you were just mentioning, it gives people an alternate way to get that protein.\par \par \uc2 I personally don't really like the Impossible Burgers. I just -- I feel gross after eating them. And if you can get that fuel and those nutrients that people who do eat meat can without killing the animals, it's great.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, net, net, it's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 Rikki, this is going to extend to a lot of other things. So it's on. I don't know how soon it's on, but it's on. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the way that \uc2 Beyond Meat\uc2  has grown and become so ubiquitous, you can get it at \uc2 KFC\uc2  and \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  and \uc2 Burger King\uc2  now. It's getting cheaper and cheaper, the more available and the more innovation that there is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: And so I think that this is potentially really the future. And I understand that it might be a little creepy and a little sci-fi, but I do - - I'm bullish. I'm into it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. All right.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I think they need to remarket it, though, because no one wants to eat cell-cultivated meat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they got to come up with something.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: If they think of something different to call it...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, like lab meat, whatever you want to call it. We got to do something.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes, no thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The chickens are delighted by this, though. They just -- they heard the news and they're just saying, well, our job here is done.\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: They should be.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, they're pretty pleased with this.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you very much. You're both vegetarians, right?\par \par \uc2 SCHLOTT: I am.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I am not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're not? So what are you, Abby?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I eat meat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I am a meat eater, I guess, a little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, yes. Well, I'm all vegetarian all the time myself.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why are you laughing here?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Really?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I'm not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But this is a good sign, as you both pointed out. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230623ej6n0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,145 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The battle for life is far from over. We have not come to the end of our cause. We have simply come to the end of the beginning.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If Congress acts, I will sign a federal law to restrict abortion as well as president.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank God almighty for the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, that depends.\par \par \uc2 Politically, if you look at the fallout, particularly for Republicans, who maybe because of that decision, it sort of dinged them at the polls in the midterm elections. Of course, there are a lot of other reasons for that.\par \par \uc2 But leave it to Nancy Mace, the South Carolina congresswoman, to say, we as a party -- she is, of course, a Republican -- have to find some sort of a middle ground on this or we`re going to lose big.\par \par \uc2 She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you have been critical of some of the efforts to go beyond the court`s decision and some states` moves, even in Florida, to say no abortions after six weeks, et cetera. Where do you think this is taking you and the party?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, we saw last year in the `22 midterm elections, we didn`t win as many seats as we should have, this being a defining issue for our party.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, Republicans won the Super Bowl with the Dobbs decision. They got Roe overturned. But what happened as a consequence of that is that voters really rejected the idea of Roe being overturned, even pro-life voters. And I saw it in my own district. I actually made the issue of rape and incest as a cornerstone of my campaign in the general election because I wanted women to know that I cared about them.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right. We have to find middle ground in `24 if we want to win over suburban women and others. We have to show that we care about women at the same time that we care about life. And I`m trying to show a path forward and how you do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the argument is that there might not be a path forward right now. You can`t win for love or money on the issue. I know the DNC says that you`re trying to have it both ways, that you have been a hypocrite on this.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I haven`t.\par \par \uc2 My voting record speaks for itself. In fact, they dinged me for a fetal heartbeat bill in South Carolina. But, at the time, we were the only state in the nation to have any exceptions for rape or incest on that bill, because I told my story about being raped as a teenager.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that I want to emphasize with voters, whether they`re Republican, Democrat, or independent, is that we should, as a society, all of us, find some common ground, and that being we need to show that we support women that are facing difficult decisions and are in difficult positions.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that we can do and -- is, I think that we can all agree that we want to reduce the number of abortions in this country. That is something I think most Americans, regardless of your party or political affiliation, we all can agree to do that. It`s just how we get there and do it together as a nation is the greatest question.\par \par \uc2 And I`m trying to show a path forward on those issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you agree with a standard definition, maybe one that`s accepted more over the world, at 12 weeks?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well. I would say 12, 15 or 20 weeks. Most people don`t want to see a ban before the second trimester. Most people don`t want to see abortions in the third trimester, so, some -- at some point during the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, do you have -- do you have your own personal preference?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, I have always said sort of 15 to 20 weeks, and as long as we have exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 But I think that`s the wrong conversation to have today. I want to talk about how we protect women that are in difficult decisions, if they have an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, I understand that, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But does a six-week move like what Governor DeSantis just did in Florida, does that go too far to you?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It goes too far.\par \par \uc2 And what that Florida bill also did is, it mandated reporting of rape to the state. And I -- those are things that, if you`re a victim of rape or a girl is a victim of incest, those are very hard pills to swallow. And it`s not supported by the vast majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of Americans support some sort of gestational limit in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: On this whole push for Republicans should support whoever the nominee is for president -- there are a dozen candidates now, maybe more down the pipe -- how do you feel about that? Some are balking.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Some are what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Balking, not keen on that, because they can`t take that pledge.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well -- yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, that has always been the Republican Party pledge. On any ticket I have ever run on as a state lawmaker or for Congress, that is a pledge you make as a Republican to support the nominee no matter what. And it often is a requirement for the debate stage as well. We will see how that shapes up in August. It`ll be interesting.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I think \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  had said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t honor that. Why should he?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes, it`ll be a very interesting election, especially if you -- as you say, Neil, that there are going to be more candidates in there.\par \par \uc2 It`s still very early on. We`re not even through Labor Day yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And the contests, we`re several months away. So it`ll be a very interesting race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congresswoman, very good seeing you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230623ej6n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Estimated 40-Hour Countdown For Crews To Find Sub; Sonar Searches Underway In Hunt For Titanic Sub; Coast Guard Races To Find Sub In "Complex" Mission; Hunter Biden To Plead Guilty To Tax Charges; Barr On Hunter Plea Deal: Not Looking That Good; Comer, Raskin To See New Alleged Biden Scheme Docs; Trump, Christie Trade Barbs As GOP Race Heats Up; Martha And Bret To Moderate First GOP Debate Aug 23; China Talking To Cuba About Military Facility\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Ashley Webster, Trey Gowdy, Bret Baier, Edward  Lawrence \par \uc2 8,909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Climate envoy, \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , facing an ethics complaint from a government watchdog group. It is arguing, Kerry spread misinformation about climate change when he said greenhouse gas emissions kill 15 million people every year. The complaint says, Kerry grossly overstated the death toll by 10 million people. Data from the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . predicts climate change will cause 3.4 million deaths by the year 2100. And that does it for us. Thanks so much for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We'll see you again tomorrow. "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you very much, guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. So, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  and Bill Barr will join us just moments away on this stunning plea deal for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , after five years of investigation into an investigation that Bill Barr said would be very complex, and that's why it was taking a lot of time.\par \par \uc2 Also joining us is presidential hopeful \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who will sit down with our voter roundtable today, and we'll guarantee a lively conversation with the governor from New Jersey coming up. That is short -- coming up shortly, I should say, as well.\par \par \uc2 But first, we want to get to this absolutely gripping story, as we now learn that there are 40 hours of breathable air left for the five people, five adventurous souls who are aboard the Titan. And it was a dream trip to the bottom of the ocean to see the wreck of the Titanic up close. And now, according to the very latest that we just heard, this is where we are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you were to find the submersible at this moment, and would that give you enough time to save these five people on board?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FREDERICK, FIRST COAST GUARD DISTRICT CAPTAIN: Yes, I don't know the answer to that question. What I will tell you is we will do everything in our power to, to affect a rescue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Heroic efforts and civilian efforts are coming into the arena here to try to help out. This is what it looks like in there. These are previous images from previous expeditions, not the current one, but you can see very close quarters there, it is -- it gets very dark.\par \par \uc2 It's essentially a tube inside about the size of a minivan just enough space for five people that has a small toilet not much else on board here. There appears to be a waiver that passengers sign before they board and, on that waiver, they do need to acknowledge that they understand the danger of what they're doing and that it could possibly result in their death.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is -- these are very, very stressful times for these individuals on board, this expedition to the, to see the Titanic, the wreckage of the Titanic. Fox Business Correspondent Ashley Webster has the very latest on this search. Hi, Ashley.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hi, Martha. Yes, that waiver, by the way, the word death appears three times just on the first page, so they understand the risks. But you know what, the search goes on. We did get the latest update from the Coast Guard about the search and the word complex was used three or four times along with statements that it will take time to get the necessary equipment to the search area and time is not what they have.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER (voice-over): The Coast Guard says the five people aboard, as you pointed out, Martha, have about 40 hours of oxygen left but we should say it's very difficult to get an accurate read on that because there are so many variables. The \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  says they have already searched an area of 7600 square miles, which is bigger than the state of Connecticut but they also admitted that they don't have all the necessary equipment yet. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 FREDERICK: While the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  has assumed the role of search and rescue mission coordinator, we do not have all of the necessary expertise and equipment. Those search efforts have not yielded any results.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: And they have to wait for that equipment. The five people on board include 48-year-old, British-Pakistani businessman, \uc2 Shahzada Dawood\uc2 , and his 19-year-old son, Suleman; 58-year-old, British businessman and explorer, Hamish Harding; Paul-Henri Nargeolet -- he's a former French Navy Diver who has explored the Titanic before they call him, Mr. Titanic; and then there's Stockton Rush, the Founder and Chief Executive of Ocean Gate, that is the company that charges $250,000 per ticket to see the Titanic wreckage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: So, Martha, crews including the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 , \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 , Canadian assets and more are working around the clock but finding a sub in such a vast area is proven to be a very complicated operation, and the clock is ticking. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, indeed. Thank you very much. Ashley Webster, let's bring in retired Navy Master Chief, Jim Phalin, who worked 28 years in deep sea diving and he -- including teaching underwater escape training, he's now Executive Director of the Navy Divers Association. Sir, good to have you with us today. Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 JIM PHALIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, NAVY DIVERS ASSOCIATION: Yes. Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, as we talk about this, I want to put up the pictures of these individuals. Obviously, they are brave individuals, people who are very interested in exploration, and being part of this Stockton Rush, who you see the second one in there is the Chief Executive and the Founder of Ocean Gate.\par \par \uc2 And I believe he's also shown in the, in the video that we showed a moment ago, from a prior exhibition at Ocean Gate, and he's trained as a pilot, you have a father and son, you have a Frenchman who is a former commander in the French Navy, for 25 years. Tell me, you know, what is the best sort of assessment that you have? If they are still alive, and if they're there with 40 hours to go? These are experienced people, what would they be doing to try to survive?\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: They should be really relaxing. It's hard to do, but even sleeping right now to preserve that precious oxygen and, and reduce the carbon dioxide. I mean, that's, that's what I see is their biggest danger, right, right now, especially if they don't have any battery power or anything like that is the increase of carbon dioxide in, in that space, where that will kill you a lot sooner than running out of oxygen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: So, that's my big worry right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and that was their worry as well, you know, looking at some of the things that they said before they undertook this. Mr., Mr. Nargeolet, talked about how, you know, if something was to go wrong in this situation, it would happen extremely quickly. And you might even not know what happened it. Do you agree with that assessment?\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: I do. Yes. Yes, this is, you know, minds will be up in space, because that's what, that's what the same hazards are in space. I also would be taking that CO2 canister and start spreading the chemical around there. So, make sure it absorbs it before they succumb to it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, there are reports that in, you know, prior years, there have been some issues with the battery that they had to fix. They also faced a big lawsuit over the depths that this vehicle could safely travel to. You know, explain to us in, in your experience, what are the reasons why this is obviously so treacherous and a dangerous undertaking?\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: Well, first of all, first of all, it's the depth. I mean, as soon as they go out of the, you know, 2000-foot range, then there's really nothing conventional that could rescue. Even the Navy, the Navy is under sea rescue command; they have a submarine rescue diving and decompression system that is designed to rescue our guys from our submarines or any of our allies' submarines, because they have to call a \uc2 NATO\uc2  escape block that this system can, can hook to, to get it.\par \par \uc2 That's at 2000 feet. When they're talking, you know, more than 13,000 feet, this is far beyond what any conventional rescue mission would be able to handle. You'd have to have a likes -- submersible to go down there and then try to pull them up. I mean, that's the, that's the only way they're going to survive is they get to that surface, and oh, it's not open that hatch, and breathe fresh air. And the logistics about going there.\par \par \uc2 And all the weather, the sea state, the temperatures, it's, it's freezing down there. I mean, it's about you know, 35-40 degrees down there. So, they don't have the heaters running, they're going to, they're going to freeze.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's just terrifying.\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: There's a lot of, a lot of a lot of risk.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A lot of risk, indeed. It's a terrifying situation, but it's, it's heartening that there's such a tremendous effort underway, and now civilian forces coming in as well, because they need all eyes in the hopes that perhaps it bounced to the surface. But, you know, the questions of battery and also the fact that they have no communications with it whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 If it had come to the surface, that would mean that it was functioning, at least well enough to, to trigger that response in this vehicle, that it knows that it has to move up. Do you think the communications would have survived if it were in that situation?\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: I do, I think, you know, they supposedly had some good, good comms on the previous dives. And at the start of the trip, you know, it's a possibility of losing comms when you, you -- the submersible gets blocked by anything, but they should be getting it back. That's, that's a big concern of mine is that they're, you know, they've lost comms perm, permanently.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jim Phalin, along with the rest of us watching this very closely with your deep experience with the Navy divers, and we thank you very much for being here. Obviously, if we get any information, we're going to break in with it, as soon as we get any developments in this case. Jim, thank you very much. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: You bet, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We all are hoping and praying for these five individuals on this expedition that, that we're going to get some good news and get it soon. Thank you, Jim.\par \par \uc2 PHALIN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former Attorney General Bill Barr and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , on the huge news today in the Hunter Biden case that his guilty plea has come forward, and whether this means that the rest of this case in Delaware under David Weiss is also over? We're going to tell you what we're now learning in that case from Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And presidential candidate, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , joins us live here in studio with a roundtable of voters to engage in some lively conversation with the New Jersey Governor as well. So, we'll be right back with that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, here is the reaction earlier today from a reporter when news broke that after five plus years of investigation, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , who you see here in some of the photos from the infamous laptop, pled guilty to two tax charges of not paying his taxes in two different years.\par \par \uc2 John Levine has been covering this story and the Biden family finances and scandals for the New York Post for years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON LEVINE, POLITICAL REPORTER, SUNDAY NEW YORK POST: That's a long time for to basically like the equivalent of parking tickets. There were many crimes that could have spun out from the larger influencing peddling story that we're not going to see now.\par \par \uc2 I wrote a story in July of -- in 2020, sometime, which talked about all the history of the Biden family arrests. You know, between, you know, Ashley and Caroline and Frank Biden.\par \par \uc2 You've got petty theft and driving vehicles under the influence of controlled substances and credit card fraud, and not a single one ever served jail time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Delaware U.S. Attorney David Weiss, who is prosecuting this case says that the investigation is ongoing. But \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer says something very different. He says this, "With the announcement of two agreements between my client, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , and the U.S. Attorney's Office for the District of Delaware, it is my understanding that a five-year investigation into Hunter is resolved."\par \par \uc2 Here's Jonathan Turley, Fox News contributor and responding. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And you have the son of the president, who's at the center of one of the greatest influence peddling scandals in history, and that's saying a lot in Washington, D.C. And he's going to walk away with a couple of misdemeanors and a gun charge that they can likely expunge. That's not going to sit well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "SUNDAY NIGHT IN AMERICA" host, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , standing by -- former House Oversight chair and federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 First, we bring in former Attorney General Bill Barr.\par \par \uc2 General Barr, good to have you with us today. Thank you for being here. So --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: Good to -- good to be here, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What's your reaction? What did you think when you heard this news of this plea deal this morning?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I think the indictment of President Trump, which was a grave, significant act really demands that the department assure everybody that the investigations into the Bidens was thorough, and that the final decisions reached were reasonable. And I agree with Jonathan Turley, it's not looking that good.\par \par \uc2 The key question, as you say, is, is this just the end of the gun and the tax case? Or is this the end of the whole shebang, all the other things that were swirling around the press reported on. Alleged bribery, alleged money laundering, and the influence peddling? And even allegations of -- by whistleblowers of internal hanky-panky and the investigation.\par \par \uc2 This demands a thorough accounting. And so, I don't think the department can do what its normal act would be here or normal posture would be, which is to say nothing.\par \par \uc2 I think the public interest demands that they assure people of the integrity of this investigation and the fairness of the final decision. And they have a good opportunity to do that with Chairman Comer's committee.\par \par \uc2 If everything is hunky-dory, then, I think they have to lean forward and look at that as an opportunity to clear the air and show that this was a thorough and fair investigation.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well --\par \par \uc2 BARR: And remember --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BARR: The House -- the House Republicans -- the House Republicans in 2017, and '18, did an excellent job of getting to the bottom of Russia gate. They were right about it before everybody else. They were right about it and Mueller wasn't.\par \par \uc2 So, they conduct good investigations. And as I say, I think it should be looked at as an opportunity to clear the air.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, on that note, Jacqui Heinrich reports that Representative Comer told her today at 4:00 p.m. that he and Representative Raskin will view two additional \uc2 FBI\uc2  documents, stemming from bribery allegations against the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 No further information on what they entail, but they're referenced in the 1023 form. So, what's going on here with this drip, drip? You know, so why today, suddenly, oh, we have two more things that you can look at if you come into the skiff.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes. I can't explain it. As I say, you know, the department is typically reluctant to provide access to materials provided by confidential sources, raw material, and so forth. And there's good reason for that as a general rule.\par \par \uc2 But there are also cases where the public -- the public interest demands being more forward leaning. Half the country believes there is a double standard of justice, or more than half of the country.\par \par \uc2 I believe there is a double standard, and I've been an insider. And I said even before these pleas were announced, it was critical that the department provide assurance to the American people that these cases are going to be fairly and thoroughly followed through on. And now, that burden is especially heavy.\par \par \uc2 These charges, frankly, could have been brought within the first few months after I became attorney general. I don't see -- you know, I don't see why we've waited five years if this is -- well, there is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If this is all they've got. You know, so, what --\par \par \uc2 I mean, David Weiss is the prosecutor on this case in Delaware. The Department of Justice oversees it. What responsibility -- what are you asking them to provide in terms of additional information?\par \par \uc2 For starters, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  attorney says, we're all done. We're done with the five-year investigation, and Weiss says it's ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Given what's going on in the country right now, with all of this, as you point out the Trump indictment, don't -- what more information? Tell them right now what you believe they need to disclose?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I think -- I think, they should cooperate with Chairman Comer's investigation, and to, you know, all the business dealings. And part of that investigation, I think, will be to look at the thoroughness of the department's investigation.\par \par \uc2 I'd be interested to know. You know, Dave Weiss, you know, his office initiated this. It's not like they were pushed into doing it. They initiated the investigation, look to me, like they had things well in hand.\par \par \uc2 Now, more and more information about money laundering came in, that makes things more complicated. But, you know, was he provided the support that was needed? What were his views on this? There were reports of some disagreement as to whether there should be a felony or misdemeanor on the tax? Was he rolled on that?\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are some of the things that have to be looked into.\par \par \uc2 He requires the cooperation of other components in the department to get bank records and other things like that. And it's a cumbersome thing to conduct an investigation like this.\par \par \uc2 I want to, you know, I'd be interested in whether he was provided sufficient wherewithal to do it.\par \par \uc2 But other than that, I think there is some questions that have to be answered about the whistleblowing, about where their efforts to undermine the investigation and shunt aside important information.\par \par \uc2 I -- I've already talked about how the Pittsburgh office did develop important information and provided it to Delaware. And --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going on in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they -- you know, it seems like that that's where things just go to die. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, no.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BARR: I'm not -- I'm not -- I'm not saying that. I'm not saying that. That might be a good explanation for it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I know you didn't say it. I said it.\par \par \uc2 BARR: But that -- but, yes, but that's what -- that's why, I think people should welcome the opportunity to allow Chairman Comer's committee to look into things.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Attorney General Barr, thank you very much. Good to have you here. It just -- there are so many questions about what's going on.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, sir. Always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 BARR: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in "SUNDAY NIGHT IN AMERICA" host, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former House Oversight chair and federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Trey, great to have you with us. What do you think happened here? What's your assessment?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know, Martha, there is some really, really complicated cases in the criminal realm. These would not be among them. Literally, you need a calendar to prosecute the tax case. That's all you need, a calendar, and a piece of paper.\par \par \uc2 Why that takes five years? Even for lousy prosecutors, you ought to be able to do it a little quicker in that. The gun case, you just need the piece of paper. Are you a convicted felon? Have you been court martialed? Have you been convicted of domestic violence? Are you a habitual user of unlawful drugs? These are simple cases. And yet it took five years.\par \par \uc2 Now, what I can't tell you is whether or not this outcome is dissimilar to what other similarly situated people would have gotten.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are not -- I mean, obviously, they're not felonies two are misdemeanors, and one is going to go through diversion.\par \par \uc2 What I don't know is whether or not there are plenty of people serving long prison sentences, who did exactly what \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has plead to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me ask you this. If you don't pay your taxes for two years on $1.6 million, you're not going to go to prison? I mean, I think everybody would love to know that, because maybe they'd rather not pay their taxes for a few years.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I don't know. I don't know what this district's history is with treating similarly situated people.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure if \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer were on with you, he would say he did ultimately pay his taxes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: My question is, did he pay him because you said? Look, we're looking at you and this would be really helpful if you would go ahead and pay your taxes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I mean, that obviously mitigates the appeal in front of the jury. But why did he pay his taxes? Was it because he got a tip from someone?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He just paid them in March of 2022.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: That, hey, this might be a good idea to (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That was long after this whole thing began.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He paid them in March of 2022. So many questions about potential bribery.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Three years, four years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All of this. And, you know, as we heard, this is such a complicated case. And yet, it boils down to some very simple dealmaking, and, you know, sort of program that he's going to have to go through and promise not to do drugs for 24 months. There is a lot right here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I can tell you what's not complicated.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I can tell you it's not complicated. Either the investigation is ongoing, or it's not. And if it's not ongoing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, that's what I was asking.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Then, there is no reason not to give Congress --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Well, then you should give Congress everything they want, because it's no longer an ongoing investigation. That's a simple question for the department.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, what do you think that he means, Trey, when he says -- David Weiss says it's ongoing, and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer says it's done? So, don't we deserve an explanation?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Well, yes. I mean, between a prosecutor and a criminal defense attorney, I'm usually going to take the word of the prosecutor. But in this case, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: But to Bill Barr's point, they owe Congress an explanation because you can't not cooperate with a congressional investigation, citing a pending investigation, if it's no longer pending.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No. I mean, this is, you know, people already don't feel right about the justice system.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: They shouldn't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And this is not going to be helpful.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: They should not feel right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, it's really sad.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Agree with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey, thank you very much. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes, ma'am. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And take a look at these. Wild video capturing a mob of teens, looting \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's