ead the whole country?\par \par \uc2 All he cares about, Martha, is himself. And if you watch Bret's interview last night, and I'm sure we'll get more of it tonight, that's what he does. He puts himself first and everyone else second. So being insulted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a compliment to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. let's ask you a couple of questions about what you would do because -- when you look at the polls, there would need to be a big shift because right now, the former president's ahead by 30, 40 points, something like that. What is that big shift? What do you envision is going to happen that's going to make these numbers very different and give you an opportunity?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: It's called a campaign. You know, no one has campaigned against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  yet. And in fact, it's one of the reasons I got in the race because no one was campaigning against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The other candidates in this race seem to think if they just kind of talk like Trump and they hope Trump maybe goes away for some reason, and then they'll inherit some of those voters and some others. I don't think that's the way it works.\par \par \uc2 The person who's in front is the person you have to campaign against and you have to beat. And I spent seven years prosecuting cases, Martha. What you're going to see over the next six months, I'm going to prosecute the case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's second term. He failed. He didn't build the wall. And I want all the viewers out there to know that he's going to tell you something different.\par \par \uc2 There's a quarter of the wall done. He said Mexico was going to pay for it. They haven't paid one peso yet. He said he was going to balance the budget in four years. He left with the biggest deficit of any American president. He said he was going to repeal and replace Obamacare. And when he had an entire Republican congress, he didn't get it done.\par \par \uc2 And so, you got to take with a