to record high $14.6 billion for a city with 800,000 residents. And she's -- in that budget, she's even cut money to the Sheriff's Department. This is really ridiculous. She is losing control of this city.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: While maybe should go the way of \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 . Richie, thanks so much for joining us tonight. And a submersible -- a submersible carrying five people vanished on it. way to see the wreckage of the Titanic. The latest reporting, plus what dangers do the passengers face more than two miles under the surface. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: A frantic search and rescue mission is underway for a tourist submersible on its way to see the wreckage of the Titanic. The vessel disappeared shortly after launch and no one has heard from anyone on board. Fox News Senior Correspondent, Eric Shawn is here now with all the details. Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Judge. Good evening. On April 15 1912, the Titanic sank. It took more than 1500 lives with it. And tonight right now there is a desperate race against time to prevent the loss of five more lives tied to that iconic disaster more than a century later. The Deep Sea submersible \uc2 Titan\uc2  remains missing at this hour. It is used as a tourist and research vessel to visit the Titanic site that's about 900 miles east of Cape Cod.\par \par \uc2 The sub disappeared in the open ocean about an hour and 45 minutes after they left that ship taking the dive Sunday morning from the mothership on the surface. Officials say there is a pilot and what they call four mission specialists on board. The \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 , New York National Guard, Canadian Forces tonight using aircraft ships and sonar in the search.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER, \uc2 UNITED STATES COAST GUARD\uc2 : It is a remote area and it is a challenge to conduct a search in that remote area. We are deploying all available assets to make sure that we can locate the craft and rescue the people on board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Oh the sub is about the size of a minivan and it's made of carbon fiber and titanium to withstand the pressures at the depths of the ocean floor where the Titanic lies, that's about two and a half miles down. Officials say this had about 96 hours of air onboard. That means right now roughly 60 hours or two and a half days of oxygen are left. The sub is operated by the company Ocean Gate Expeditions, you know it charges customers about $250,000 for the privilege to see the Titanic wreckage for themselves.\par \par \uc2 We're told onboard is a British businessman who's an explorer as well as a French Titanic expert. And within the hour, the company says that they have yet to establish any communication with the crew. They say the sub though is designed to surface by itself if it does run into trouble. But sadly, Judge, so far as of this moment, there is still no sign of it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Eric, thanks so much. Joining me now is Michael Zinszer, a Navy Diver for 20 years and former director of the dive program at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2 . Michael, you call this a hostile underwater environment. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ZINSZER, NAVY DIVER FOR 20 YEARS: Well, that's anytime you work in an extreme depths, especially when you're talking over 13,000 feet, it's going to be a hostile environment. You first you got surface conditions that you have to deal with. And right now I don't know what the conditions are, where the search is going on. But if the vessel is on a surface, it would be very difficult to see.\par \par \uc2 You know, that's if you look at the image that you have on screen right now, you're only looking at the very top of a submarine that was on the surface. And they're looking for radar to see it, it would be very difficult to track.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, Michael, I guess what you're saying is no one knows where it is at this point?\par \par \uc2 ZINSZER: Well, I don't -- I don't know what they're actually doing in Military and the searches going on. But you would know if they found it. That's one thing --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That we know. One former British Navy officer warned of a major concern, take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RADM CHRIS PARRY (RET), BRITISH ROYAL NAVY: There could have been an accident, it could have become entangled in the wreckage of Titanic. It could indeed have had a catastrophic failure. The answer is we don't know just yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, entangled in the wreckage. Interesting.\par \par \uc2 ZINSZER: That's, that's true. But it would have to get pretty deep. And it would have to sit right on it to be entangled. Unless there's some type of rating that's still floating, the sub would actually have to go hit on the bottom to probably get entangled with it. That's a possibility. That is a possibility. But if they're that close to it, and they had power and they had lights, there's a good chance that they would be able to send some type of float some type of signal to the server saying, hey, we're -- we're stuck.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure all the equipment attached to that vessel. But submarines definitely had -- typically have some type of emergency response to get to the surface.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, if the Navy -- if the Navy had any possibility of getting in touch with them, even with a robot, they could do that. But unless they don't know or unless it's entangled, there's that can you -- you can't read the tea leaves here?\par \par \uc2 ZINSZER: I wouldn't even attempt to read the tea leaves. Yes, there's so many variables that you're dealing with when you're working at that depth. Especially when you're doing searches. You know, the sonar images, the sound images, if you look at the thermoclines down about 200 meters, it -- the sound that penetrates the thermocline is minimal compared to the sonar image sounds that they are sending down. It's like a ping-pong ball hitting a table at it bounces, reflects off the thermocline. So being able to -- down to about 1,000 meters where the Titanic sits is like the worst area to penetrate. In fact, during the Cold War submarines, used to hide in that thermocline because it's so hard to locate, identify, and track any vessel in that area.\par \par \uc2 So here surface searches with sonar, with other technology, if it's being done on the surface, that makes it very difficult. They need to get below the thermocline to try to track and identify. And if the submarine has a ping device, sending off pings, again, the sound would bounce off the thermocline and make it very difficult to locate.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I understand. All right, Michael, thanks so much for your expertise.\par \par \uc2 And two Democrats just can't get it out, another is historic. And Meghan and Harry get the royal heave-ho. Raymond Arroyo has all the details. "Seen and Unseen" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we expose the stories behind the headlines. For that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, Biden met his match on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, that's one way to put it, Judge. For the first time, Biden seemed like the coherent one. This is Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  who may have commented on the president's state of being.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D-PA): And now I'm standing next to the president again, next to a collapsed bridge. This is a president that is committed to infracture (ph), and then on top of that, the jewel kind of a law of the infriction (ph) bill that is going to make sure that there's going to be bridges all across, like this all across America getting rebuilt.\par \