2 , (R-KY): This is what we are talking about, this piece of plastic. And I am holding it here, but I won't be holding it on June 1st because I'll be a felon.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So this is, this is the big threat.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: That's the big threat to America is a piece of plastic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So that rule was supposed to go into effect today, and as you heard Congressman Massie say, you could be considered a felon if you didn't register the brace. But late last night, hours before the grace period for the rule was ending, a federal judge in Texas temporarily blocked in response to a lawsuit from gun right activists.\par \par \uc2 Joining me is Georgia Congressman \uc2 Andrew Clyde\uc2 . He introduced a resolution tried to block what he says is an unconstitutional move by the ATF. Congressman, that's minor win, but what's next? Isn't this a backdoor push to impose a federal gun registry on millions of legal gun owners?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ANDREW CLYDE\uc2 , (R-GA): Thank you, Rachel, great to be with you tonight. I will tell you, this is extreme government overreach by the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, and Firearms. That's what this is, plain and simple. They are redefining a rule, redefining a definition to make stabilizer brace pistols into short-barreled rifles. And the amnesty period ended last night, but fortunately we did have a couple of wins in the court. One was by Gun Owners of America, and one was about a week ahead of time, ahead of that.\par \par \uc2 But we are still fighting this because those lawsuits only cover just a few people. This entire issue is affecting millions and millions of American citizens, including those with disabilities, service-disabled veterans, all of them unless they have removed their brace or register their brace. And some states don't allow registration of short-barreled rifles, so in those estates they don't get to keep them. But they will become felons overnight as of last night, so today you had better not have a pistol, an unregistered pistol with a stabilizing brace on it, or you will be a felon, again, like you said, 10 years in jail, and actually a $250,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Do you think that maybe this latest law is getting around to all the gun owners, this may be something that might galvanize gun owners to start directing more of their energy towards gun rights, because the \uc2 NRA\uc2  is kind of weakened these days.\par \par \uc2 CLYDE: I'll tell you that this is an issue that every gun owner should be concerned about, and we in Congress should be concerned about it, too. I introduced H.J.-Res 44 with 190 cosponsors on it. It is a bill that needs to come to the floor. I was told by leader Scalise on Tuesday afternoon that it will come to the floor next week. I am little concerned because I saw the rules notice and it's not there. So I'm hoping that our Republican leadership is taking this seriously enough, because this does affect millions upon millions of Americans, law-abiding Americans who overnight become felons because of the ATF overreach here. So this is a serious, very, very serious issue that we in Congress have to take seriously.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: But the federal judge has blocked this at this time. But again, this is an issue that will affect disabled Americans who are losing their right to defend themselves. Congressman, thank you for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Well, two pro-life advocates were viciously assaulted outside of a Planned Parenthood, and one of them joins me exclusively, next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: The party of tolerance just viciously attacked two peaceful pro-life advocates, even sending one of them to the hospital, 73-year-old Mark Crosby and 80-year-old Dick Schaefer were praying outside of a Planned Parenthood in Baltimore on Friday when they say out of nowhere a man aggressively approach them, arguing over abortion. Police say surveillance footage shows the suspect tackling Schaefer into a large flowerpot. And when Crosby tried to help, the suspect turned on him, shoving Crosby to the ground and then punching and kicking him in the head with what police describe as extreme force.\par \par \uc2 Look at these injuries. He is 73 years old. Brutally beaten by some thug who didn't agree with his beliefs. So much for coexist. That's the party of acceptance and tolerance.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now exclusively is Mark Crosby along with his attorney Jordan Sekulow. He is the executive director of the American Center for Law and Justice. Mark, this story really tugs at my heart because I have elderly parents who have also prayed outside of abortion clinics, and I just can't imagine what happened. Explain your injuries. It looks awful. Mark? Can you explain your injuries?\par \par \uc2 MARK CROSBY, ASSAULTED PRO-LIFE ACTIVIST: Yes. I have -- he kicked me in the face. He hit me. I went to the ground. He kicked me. I was unconscious. It turns out that I am still blind in this one eye. This eyeball is bleeding. The orbital shelf area is all cracked and fractured, and I need surgery there. But I'm getting better. I'm getting better. I have two fractured fingers and aches and pains. But God has given me everything I need, and I am surrounded by wonderful people.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I think you are lucky to have walked away with your life the way he was kicking you in your head. It's just terrible. Really quick, one last question with you. There have been over 60 attacks on pro-life centers and activists since Roe versus Wade was overturned. Were you at all expecting that perhaps this could lead to violence, you being outside peacefully praying?\par \par \uc2 CROSBY: Yes. I look at what is going on, and it's getting dangerous. And especially in Baltimore, our court system, when a pro-lifer gets in front of a judge, he is guilty, no matter what. That's how it is in Baltimore city. These Planned Parenthoods are put in poor neighborhoods. And if you look at Margaret Sanger and the history of Planned Parenthood, they are putting these neighborhoods to eliminate the negro population and also the disabled and mentally retarded. So everybody should look in the history of American eugenics and Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes, no, there is clearly a eugenics roots in that organization.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, we asked Planned Parenthood about that attack, and they told us it was, quote, an unfortunate incident between a pedestrian and antichoice protestors. We condemn all violence. When abortion care is under attack across the country, we cannot tolerate violent acts from anywhere, especially when it gets in the way of the patients receiving proper healthcare." They call abortion healthcare. What a euphemism. Jordan, what's your response to Planned Parenthood's statement? And also, what is your strategy?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN SEKULOW, PRO-LIFE ACTIVIST'S ATTORNEY: That first response, they actually had to walk that back and issued a second statement saying all violence is bad. And the reason why is because you really read that response, it was bad what happened because less women got access to abortion. That wasn't because of Mark that day. He was peacefully praying on the sidewalk. That was because not of a pedestrian who attacked him but a pro-abortion extremist who attacked him on the street. Notice, Planned Parenthood calls it a pedestrian. It was not a pedestrian. We know from the police report he was engaged in a back-and-forth conversation about abortion rights with Mark and his colleague Dick, and then attacked.\par \par \uc2 CROSBY: I was out on the street. I was out on the street. I give out rosaries. I don't talk to anybody.\par \par \uc2 SEKULOW: The second part of the strategy --\par \par \uc2 CROSBY: In my opinion, in my opinion this was arranged. This was planned.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes, gentlemen. I am so sorry I have to cut you off, but the Senate vote is underway. Mark, we hope that you heal quickly, and Jordan, thank you for representing him. We are going to come right back with the latest from Capitol Hill on that debt deal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Right now, the Senate is voting on the debt deal. Let's go live to FOX News senior Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram. Chad, where do we stand right now?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Rachel, good evening. The vote is still open right now. This is final passage of the debt ceiling bill. And by my count here, there are more than 60 yeas for this. This would require 60 yeas, and they're going to be well above that threshold. The Senate voting tonight to avoid a collision with the debt ceiling. The House and Senate will soon be in sync on a deal to suspend the debt ceiling through January of 2025. It's a deal cut between President Biden and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 However, the Senate was not a major player in the bill until tonight. There was concern that ratings agencies could downgrade the credit worthiness of the United States if Congress did not act. There were also worries about a possible market shock and interest-rate spikes. The deal also draws down federal spending for the next several years. Now, passage of the bill requires this bipartisan coalition of members to vote yes, and by my count they are well above 60 counts. The vote is still open. The vote is not final until they gavel it down, but the House and Senate will soon be aligned. This will then go to President Biden for his signature. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Thank you, Chad.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Sean Duffy, my husband, and cohost of "The Bottom Line" on FOX Business. Sean, sure, there were some military -- I'm sorry. I'll go to you right now. You were in Congress. How would you have voted on this bill?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST, "THE BOTTOM LINE": Republicans who voted for the bill will say it was wonderful in a sense we have expanded work requirements, we're cutting \uc2 IRS\uc2  spending. We clawed back COVID money. We're going to cap growth of spending at one percent, all really good things, especially we you have a Democrat president.\par \par \uc2 But we are $32 trillion in debt, and I think we're going to double that debt in the next 10 years. We're going to have a debt crisis, whether it's three years, eight years, 10 years, it's going to come. It's going to be on our doorstep. And that will be cataclysmic for all of the American people. And so I don't know that I would have voted for this. I don't think I would have.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: What does this mean for Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: So Kevin McCarthy, he was dealt a tough hand. He was trying to negotiate with a guy, President Biden, who said I'm not going to negotiate. Give me a clean debt limit increase, that's all I'm going to go for. Kevin McCarthy was relentless in pushing this deal. He got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to give him cuts. By the way, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  negotiated because Kevin McCarthy with Republicans, a slim majority, passed that first bill that would raise the debt limit with cuts which gave them a lot of leverage. So I think Kevin McCarthy's stock is actually going up after tonight's vote passing in the Senate and the debt limit will be raised. I think it shows that Kevin McCarthy is a force to be reckoned with. He has control of his conference, and they're standing behind him.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I had \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  on, Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  on earlier. He said he was voting against it. I asked him, what do you do? The Democrats want to vote for more spending. You don't have the Senate majority, you don't have the White House. So what do people who say, what are they proposing should have been done, and what can be done moving forward now that this has passed?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: I think if you would've held up maybe a little bit longer you could have gotten a little bit more. But I think it's important to recognize that this is one deal, this is the debt limit deal. But the House is drafting appropriations bills which is the next spending package for fiscal year 2024. You have another bite at the apple. So it's just not one and done. The debt limit is here, it's passed, and there is no more spending controls the Republican Congress can implement. They have another kick at this cat that is going to come over the summer and into the fall. And hopefully Republicans will be hard drivers as well with a Democrat Senate and a Democrat White House, and again, I think they can get more cuts, more spending reform.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Quick, you say that this is a win for Kevin McCarthy, this makes him look stronger. Can \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  say this is a win for him as well? His name is on the bill.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: It is. But when you come in and say, I want a clean debt limit, I'm not going to negotiate, and gave McCarthy the Heisman bid for, what, 72 days, and then in the end you capitulate and you actually negotiate and you give him some of the things that he asked for, I think you look like the loser in that deal. Kevin McCarthy actually looks like the winner and looks stronger, plays a stronger hand. And when you go into the next negotiation, which is going to be over how do we fund the government, Kevin McCarthy, I think, will have much more strength and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will take him much more seriously, which means Republicans can get some more cuts at the next go around.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And yet there's still talk about motions to vacate Kevin McCarthy. Are those really real? Are they a real threat to him?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: I think those are real, but I think the conference is still going to stand behind him, which is, I think, important. So McCarthy is not going anywhere, though he's going to have to deal with his conference. And I think what a lot of congressmen realize is, this was not a perfect deal. I may not have voted for it, but I understand Kevin McCarthy was dealing with a president who was going to be a big spender and wants to spend more, not less.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And when we look at the spending, the American people are the losers.\par \par \uc2 All right, we'll go back to Capitol Hill when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Now back to Capitol Hill. Chad Pergram, you have an update.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Yes, the vote is now closed on the debt ceiling bill. It's official, they have passed the bill. The vote there was 63 to 36. Most of the no's on this bill came from the GOP side of the aisle. More than 30 votes that were in the negative from Republicans, five Democratic no's, Fetterman, Merkley, Markey, Sanders, and Warren. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Thank you, Chad.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. I am Rachel Campos-Duffy in for Laura Ingraham. Make sure to check out the podcast I have with my husband, Sean Duffy, "From the Kitchen Table." This week, we'll talk to \uc2 FBI\uc2  whistleblower Steve Friend and expose how \uc2 Disney\uc2  is destroying yet another girlhood experience. Plus, we take some fan questions.\par \par \uc2 Gutfeld is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230602ej6100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Will Stand Up To China, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Wants To Be Friends; GOP Candidates Must