ent support open borders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The Biden-Harris Administration announced those sweeping measures that I mentioned moments ago from the state and DHS to expand legal pathways, launch regional process centers, urge asylum officers and judges and more\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She reads really well. Trump is opposed to never-ending wars and wants to reform our national security establishment. Both Biden and the GOP establishment support it. And both have a record of spending trillions and trillions on failed wars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we will support Ukraine's defense for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Supporting Ukraine as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's how long we're going to be with you, Mr. President, for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He was walking off the wrong side of the stage. All right, Trump has proven record of putting real conservatives on the bench. Now Biden would put hard Left lunatics there and the GOP establishment, let's face it, it has a record of appointing folks like David Souter, and others like John Roberts. So that's four major issues, issues that should be critical in any election, where Trump right now has a clear policy advantage over both Biden and the GOP establishment.\par \par \uc2 And that all leads to the following on domestic policy, Trump has a proven record of building an economy that generated higher real wages for American workers. Now, neither the Biden Administration nor the old GOP guard can say the same. And on social policy, Trump led us to a huge victory to overturn Roe vs. Wade. We know what the GOP establishment failed to do. Yes, overturn Roe vs. Wade, and for only 40 years.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Trump did all of this while facing impeachment, and the fraudulent Mueller investigation. So, simply attacking Trump as a person or his style, it isn't going to work. If you want to win the GOP nomination, you're going to have to convince the voters that you're going to do the right thing on China, on the borders, obviously, the economy, on the military, and judges. I don't think that many people really believe that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , Tim Scott, Nikki Haley, would really be able to stand up to the GOP establishment on any of those issues.\par \par \uc2 So if you vote for them, you're effectively voting for \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and almost no one in the GOP anymore agrees with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . So that leaves \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who certainly has shown a willingness to think for himself and to take positions and to push policies that don't match up with that GOP establishment he's taken on the CCP by barring their land purchases and estate. H stood up to the corporate woke stirs and he's built an impressive, diverse base of support.\par \par \uc2 So, tomorrow night, when he announces, it's time for him to make his positions crystal clear on the key issues where Trump currently has a significant advantage. Now check out this head-to-head poll. If he does the right thing on those issues, well, we could have a real horse race, could? If he thinks he can run as Trump light, I believe he's going to be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 Next summer, we'll skip ahead a year. The GOP base wants its nominee to make four big points at the convention. Number one, we will stand up to China. Number two, we will enforce our border. Number three, we will reform the national security state. And number four, you bet, we're going to appoint socially conservative judges. They also want a candidate who's going to campaign everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Remember, our motto here on The Ingraham Angle, no state and no city left behind. And that means appeal to working people of all backgrounds build a multicultural majority. Now for years, Trump has been their only option. Remember what Trump did to grow Hispanic support and some African American support among black males. DeSantis changed the math with Latinos in Miami Dade County, huge turnaround there. And now we're going to see if he can replicate that elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 So, we hear that DeSantis is going to announce his candidacy on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , I guess interviewing him. Well, last November, Musk endorsed DeSantis. But now he says he's neutral. And he just wants someone who's normal as president. I don't know what that means. People run for president. And I guess, are they normal? I don't know. Although Musk is impressive, as we have said in many, many ways. He's obviously a genius in many, many ways. His neutrality is probably good news for DeSantis given the fact that \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's dependence on manufacturing and sales in China could be an issue that Trump brings up.\par \par \uc2 But for anyone running on the GOP side, this has to be a campaign for America, not a campaign for the donors. Again, Trump's already proven his America first bonafide ease DeSantis still has work to do. He has a lot of people to convince. That's the way I see it. Tonight. late May 2023. And that's the angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Mollie Hemingway editor-in-chief of The Federalist, Fox News Contributor, and \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , former senior adviser to President Trump and founder of America First Legal.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, it's going to be a big night for DeSantis you're going to have one of the more controversial but also popular in many circles. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  there with him, although he says he's neutral in this race. What does he need to do tomorrow to kick this off in a grand fashion?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE FEDERALIST: So, we've been watching \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  run without actually running and starting tomorrow, we'll actually get to see what it's like when it's an official campaign. I think you made some really good points. The problem for anyone taking on Trump is that Trump is wildly popular in the Republican Party, particularly for what he did.\par \par \uc2 So, probably be wise for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to say that he -- may not sure if he needs to say this, but convey that he supports the Trump agenda that he loves everything that that brought the country and that he is a more winning, he could do a better job of winning, that might be the message that he should convey tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the Trump team is already slamming DeSantis's planned announcement. And advisor telling Fox News announcing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is perfect for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . This way, he doesn't have to interact with people and the media can't ask him any questions. Stephen, is that a fair criticism of DeSantis? I mean, one thing I'd say about him is he doesn't seem to be shying away from answering questions of the media. I'm not sure that is the right way to criticize DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Well, I think at a national campaign, Laura, obviously interacting with reporters, and particularly in the smaller states, with local reporters at the town level is obviously a critical part of running for president. But I think in particular, Laura, go back to your monologue, where this race is really going to center around is going to be immigration policy, trade policy and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 The issues that you identified as being transformational under the leadership of President Trump, and in particular, I think the foreign policy element is going to be at the center of the conversation moving forward with President Trump being crystal clear about his desire for an immediate ceasefire and political settlement in Ukraine. And that issue I believe more than any other in the weeks to come is going to be the center of the dialogue and so I would hope, I would hope that reporters in many settings\par \par \uc2 I believe more than any other in the weeks to come is going to be at the center of the dialogue. And so I would hope, I would hope that reporters in many settings have an opportunity to grill the candidates on that issue.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Mollie, the polls right now, I don't think they mean a heck of a lot. I mean, everyone's -- we throw them up and everyone goes crazy. Oh, DeSantis is winning here, Trump is winning there. But this is really up to them. I mean, this politics is the art of persuasion. And voters want to vote for someone they believe, can win, number one, and then number two, actually get stuff done. Abraham Accords, USMCA, border enforced, economic growth, optimism among Investors. I mean, we had all of that in 2019 before the stupid Pandemic, like we had all that\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Everything seemed to change from the Trump Administration, in terms of what Republican voters now realize is possible to demand or expect from any Republican president. And I think that that is reflected in the fact that the polls, whatever, however much credence you put in them, the vast majority of Republican voters are with Trump or DeSantis, the two America First candidates who do care about a realist foreign policy, who do care about conservative judges, who care about an economic policy that benefits average Americans more than the worst corporations on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 And so these two men are very different from what was being offered to Americans during that 2000 to 2015 era. And it shows by how much support they have in the polls, and how much they're shaping the conversation that's there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Stephen, the idea that a Nikki Haley, or a Tim Scott, I mean, they're both lovely people. But they're obviously more aligned with kind of the \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wing of the party. Is there any pathway to victory for someone more connected with the old Bush era view of the world then with the populist America first view of the world,\par \par \uc2 MILLER: All of American politics could be divided into the era before Trump and the Trump era, the Bush-ism that preceded Trump, the Ryan-ism, the McConnell-ism, that era of American politics is completely dead, buried and gone. Many adherents are still hanging around, I will acknowledge, but what President Trump did to shift the conversation to be about the working class, the working people, their concerns, their dreams, their hopes, who don't want uncontrolled immigration, who don't want stupid foreign wars, who don't want our jobs being sent to China, that multi-ethnic coalition that President Trump created, is going to dominate American discourse and American politics for a long, long time to come.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Mollie, Axios is reporting that some powerful GOP donors who won't support Trump but are beginning to be concerned about DeSantis are pushing Youngkin to jump into the field. But his team, Youngkin's team is saying no, we're not interested. But do you believe that? I mean, I mean, if enough donors are squirrely about DeSantis, and it seems like DeSantis does have quite a bit of support. Does that mean the Youngkin ultimately gets him perhaps,\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I do think that people should watch Youngkin, it seems like something he's been interested in before, that you might wait and see if there's an opportunity to enter later. But it's also funny how people are talking about DeSantis not doing enough. He hasn't even announced yet. And he's polling fairly well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Nobody wonders like what is Nikki Haley, or, Tim Scott, like -- their campaigns supposedly doing so much better. But more than anything, I think the Republican donor class needs to realize that the Republican Party has been changed that the Republican voters have a huge say in who gets 