is because Biden has a horrific economic record. Wages, they haven't kept track with inflation. growth that was disappointing last year, and it's on track to disappoint this year and the year after.\par \par \uc2 And a new \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's survey reveals that the share of adults who reported being worse off financially than in the year earlier climbed to 35 percent, the highest on record going back to 2014. And what's just as disturbing some 54 percent of adults said their budgets had been affected a lot by higher prices, and black adults, adults and parents living with children and those with disabilities were more likely to report such financial strain. Not good. Now, meanwhile, the squad they're threatening riots if Biden agrees to spending reductions,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But if the President agrees to spending caps or if he agrees to work requirements on certain social safety programs.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): It's going to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think there would be a huge backlash from our entire House Democratic Caucus suddenly the progressives, but also in the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Jayapal, a backlash in the streets. That's not what CNN's new polling is showing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID CHALIAN, CNN POLITICAL DIRECTOR: Our brand-new poll conducted by SSRS shows that a majority of Americans six and 10 Americans want to raise the debt ceiling only if spending cuts are included as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's a segment CNN didn't want to do. Well, I'm personally loving this because it's this burgeoning split between Biden's Wall Street donors and the hard Left. Now talk about an unholy alliance and it serves both of them right. Both of them refuse to work with the populist movement, to have a sound financial system, and at the same time, help working class Americans, they wouldn't do it. So now that's where they are.\par \par \uc2 So, now that the Left sees the tide turning against them, they're coming up with Insane Clown Posse theories. They think that the president has the authority under the 14th Amendment to issue his own Biden bonds, and then use the money raised by those bonds to pay the interest on the old bonds that are coming due. But even if this was constitutional, which our next guest, I'm sure will say it's not, who the heck would buy the Biden bonds answer no one, because there is no guarantee that Congress would ever appropriate the money to pay them off.\par \par \uc2 Because they weren't congressionally authorized, as is required under our constitution. Do you see how nuts this is? Joining me now is Jonathan Turley, George Washington constitutional law professor and Fox News Contributor, Professor Turley, good to see you tonight. Now explain to us the best you can, why the Left thinks that they can issue bonds out of the White House without congressional approval?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GW CONSTITUTIONAL LAW PROFESSOR: Well, the theory in my view is completely frivolous. The 14th Amendment does not deal with raising the ceiling for debt, it was designed to make sure that the debt that was incurred in the Civil War would be honored. In fact, the first draft referred specifically to civil war debt. It's been read, obviously, to go beyond that.\par \par \uc2 But there's both practical and constitutional problems with this argument. You know, the question about raising the ceiling on debt has nothing to do with what the 14th Amendment is about. The Congress is honoring its debt, is willing to, in fact, raise the ceiling for the debt.\par \par \uc2 But the White House has to negotiate on the budget that goes to the very function of the legislative branch, it is the power of the purse, that's what the framers gave to Congress as a critical part of the separation of powers. And it's really other worldly to see Democratic leaders begging the president to effectively make them constitutional non-entities by saying just circumvent us, just take away the power of the purse and become a government onto yourself where you can order the payment of college tuition without a congressional vote. And then you can just actually produce the money yourself to pay for those types of things. It's really out of line.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, well, I'm going to get to what the court might do in a moment. But what's this trillion-dollar platinum coin theory that's also been bandied about? I liked that that just a big old coin with Biden's face on it. That'd be great.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right, this is right out of the Mr. Burns School of Economics. The idea is that Congress gave the executive branch the ability to print coins of any denomination that Bill never envisioned this particular use. There's no question that this violates the intent of Congress. And so it's really shocking to see people suggesting this \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  herself, called this a gimmick.\par \par \uc2 But people like Laurence Tribe and others are saying, well, you could probably get away with it because no one would have standing to challenge it. By the way it was Laurence Tribe that was cited by the president for the 14th Amendment concept. You have to keep in mind it was Tribe who was also cited by the president for the unconstitutional moratorium on evictions, even after the Supreme Court and it is clearly stated that it was unconstitutional. He was cited and for the Biden asking the CDC to reissue --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He has had a great track record.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Constitutional.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, he had a great track record. I don't know if he was involved in any of the COVID mandates, but some of those also ran into legal trouble. Now, back to the court. So let's say they actually are desperate, he is between a rock and a hard place, this is challenged in court. Would the court consider this a political question, Jonathan, and not want to get involved? But it is a constitutional determination that has to be made in this case, does it not?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It does, I don't see how the court could treat this as a political question. The interpretation of the 14th Amendment doesn't allow a president to unilaterally increase the debt of the government. If they just turn a blind eye to that it will effectively gut the authority of the legislative branch in our tripartite system of government.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I could also see John Roberts not wanting people think the court's responsible for the country going into technical default. I could see them worrying about that as well. Do you see that as a possibility?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Not really, because the Biden administration has the ability to pay the interest on the debt. It doesn't have to default. And so the key to this entire matter is to make sure we pay that interest, that's what is the focus. And they can do that. But more importantly all they have to do is reach a compromise with Congress. The House has already approved raising that ceiling.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Professor Turley, thank you for my ESL credit that I am getting. I am getting extra credit for my bar renewal. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight we're introducing a new segment, thanks to the Biden administration, "Open Borders, Deadly Consequences." Two shocking stories, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: New series tonight we are starting on the Angle. It's called "Open Borders, Deadly Consequences." This happened last week in Maryland not too far from where we are broadcasting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jose Hernandez-Espinal, who is originally from El Salvador, is accused of raping a woman this week, six days after raping a teenage girl. Back on May 10th, Hernandez-Espinal appr