tional system that we're dealing with. We have violence that we're dealing with every single day. There are so many issues that we're faced with. But yet, our leaders continue to make us try to think -- continue to try to make us think that white supremacy is the number one thing that we ought to be faced with and that we ought to be concerned with, when it's definitely not the number one issue of the black community.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke at the White House to a group of African-American small business owners, and this is what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , 49TH U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: We do talk about equity. We actually believe it is a good principle. In spite what some so-called leaders might try to suggest, we are proud of the fact that equity is one of our guiding principles, proud of the fact that we understand equality is important, but not everybody starts out on the same base.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, once again sort of dangling the racial spoil system instead of just pushing for a strong economy, which you obviously saw during Trump.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, HOOVER INSTITUTION: Well, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , but especially \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , they have a dilemma, because never has the black upper classes, middle classes had more influence, more visibility, more power, more success, whether it's Barack Obama or the women interview or \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  (ph), they're very successful, and they should be proud of the hard work that obtained that success.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, the inner city black community is in a crisis. And when you look at these spikes in violence, the data shows that young black males are double their numbers in the demographic as perpetrators of hate crimes, and rare interracial crimes. They are over represented three or four times. And yet, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  cannot o