nd others. But all eyes right now are also on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He's expected to announce his 2024 run any day, and he's starting the big week off with a major address at an evangelical gathering in Orlando.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I actually just got back from being in Israel.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, I pledged as governor that we would be the most pro-Israel state in these United States, and we've delivered on that promise.\par \par \uc2 The state of Florida stands unequivocally in defense of the family and in defense of our children.\par \par \uc2 I was fortunate enough to sign the heartbeat bill, which is the strongest pro-life bill Florida has seen in its modern history. We got it done.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: That is humane. That is not harsh. It is the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 We flatly reject the inclusion of gender ideology in our K through 12 schools.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Disney\uc2  doesn't like that, well, here I stand. I am not backing down one inch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: FOX News analyst Raymond Arroyo was at the speech tonight and joins me now. Raymond, why was this moment important for DeSantis before he announces he's running?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: First of all, it allowed \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to step on Tim Scott's announcement today and make a play for the biggest and arguably one of the most important blocs in the Republican Party. Evangelicals make up 25 percent of the entire electorate, 81 percent of those people voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last time, Laura. Now, here's how they reacted to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's outreach to them today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The main issue, I think one of the dividing issues of this generation is going to be parental rights, whether or not parents have the role, the God-given role to direct the upbringing of your own children. He's clearly got his finger on the pulse of that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was thrilled to see him come out and begin his remarks to a Christian audience about how significant Israel is geopolitically, politically, and to his own faith.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very encouraging to hear and listen to somebody that is addressing the issues that we as conservatives, that many Latinos, many Mexican Americans that live here in this country like me that have worked hard and have to build a family and to live the American dream.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A leader for the future for the country. And so we are super excited about supporting the things that he does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ray, what else did you hear from the evangelical leaders there? Because I know there's been a split among evangelicals in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, look, I spoke to a pastor of a very large church. He said the two burning issues for evangelicals are life and Israel. You heard \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  make a play for both of those. Interestingly enough, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight during the DeSantis address was giving an interview to John Solomon where he promised a peace corps for adoption. You know what all these means, Laura? The curtain has gone up on 2024. Let the games begin.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love the adoption angle, so I like that. But this is exciting.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Smart idea.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is going to start to get very exciting. Raymond, great job. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 So with DeSantis About to enter race and Trump still, of course, the frontrunner by quite a bit, who is going to be stronger against, presumptively, Biden in the battleground states? Joining me now at the big board, Chris Bedford, Common Sense Society executive editor. Chris, great to see you tonight. Let's start with Georgia, 16 electoral votes, Trump lost it, remember, by less than one percent in 2020. So what are the polls telling us now about how Trump would do there against Biden?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, SENIOR EDITOR, "THE FEDERALIST": \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in almost all the polls that we've seen that have been done so far, granted, it's early, is behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running pretty badly against generic Republicans, maybe against a Tim Scott or against a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  type figure, but he is consistently beating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And part of the reason for that is probably because Trump as well as Biden are so well defined in American mind. Both of them have some of the same issues. They're well known. They're not a new person on the stage. They both have age concerns. But Trump is extremely well defined, so we are seeing him behind. But we are seeing folks like, maybe, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  actually beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in all these different states.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let's look at the polls, DeSantis head-to-head against Biden. Now, this is Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: This is Georgia, and this is a good sign for general Republicans to look at because, like you said, Georgia was a state that was just lost, Georgia has got some Democratic senators now. To see Republicans this far out leading, except for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , leading \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  shows a lot of weaknesses that he's got.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chris, Chris, look at where that A.P. poll, I think it was today that came out, I think it was A.P., showed that the economy and immigration are the two biggest issues, two biggest concerns for key voters. So that's got to be Republican territory there, and especially a state like Georgia that's getting hampered.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It is. I walked into the grocery store, for the first time since COVID I heard that awful cliche in these difficult times, in these hard times. No one was talking about a pandemic. He was talking to shoppers about how high inflation is, over how high the prices have risen. And you can't hide that stuff for speeches from Washington.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chris, let's go to Arizona, another state that Trump lost by less than one percent, 11 electoral votes. What are the polls saying about how he would do against Biden?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: A lot of the same thing. And what we've seen, really, from everything that's shown, all the numbers we have seen have shown that in 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  probably hit the ceiling he's going to get, a little bit over 46 percent of the country. Didn't get the popular vote but won at the Electoral College. A part of that was due to some of the Democratic base not activated. Those folks are activated right now. So while Trump has really solidified his core, he's not seeming to gain new voters. He's a very well-established person.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: DeSantis versus Biden in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Another good sign for DeSantis or generally for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is one poll. This is 500 Arizona voters just taken as plus 4.4 margin of error.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: You're right, it's just one poll. But it is a broader trend. In matchups between X Republican and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , X Republican usually wins, except when it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is something for Republicans. If you add this state to Georgia, that beats a loss in Michigan. That might beat a loss out in Pennsylvania. It's 27 electoral votes between the two of them. So if Republicans are able to win those like some of those generic polls are showing right now, then they'll actually maybe be able to get the White House back.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Looking at one of the early primary states, New Hampshire, where I spent four long winters, Trump has a big lead over DeSantis. He sits at about 24 points on the RCP average. That's stunning.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Yes, and this is a poll that more than anything we have talked about so far probably is going to matter the most to who is actually running in Georgia, in Arizona, is who gets out of the Republican primary. In Iowa, New Hampshire, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is absolutely dominating. He's bringing crowds. He has also had six months to really have the campaign stage to himself. And never before has he run against someone who is part of the new right, who kind of shares his ideas, who is not part of the old Tim Scott Republican guard. So this is going to be an interesting when DeSantis actually comes, but it's a really hard thing for him to overcome is that rock hard base that is really going to be voting Trump.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So Trump, let's say he wins Iowa, wins New Hampshire, then you go to South Carolina. Is Tim Scott a factor in South Carolina?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Yes, you have got two home sons in South Carolina. You've got Tim Scott, you've got Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Two establishment Republicans, where is that working?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: That's an interesting thing. It shows the Republican Party how little they've actually learned from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are the only people running on the populist slate out of all of them. But still, you get that respect for the hometown candidate. People don't like to run against folks in their home statement. They don't even usually put the money there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How did Jeb Bush do in Florida?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: He was pretty well done by then.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hometown advantage. Chris, great to see you. Awesome job.\par \par \uc2 A professor gone wild. The cause? Pro-life students having a table display. The shocking video, and the student at the center of their wrath, is next. So stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just when you thought college campuses couldn't get any nuttier, something happened when pro-life students at Hunter College encountered Shellyne Rodriguez. They say she's a professor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHELLYNE RODRIGUEZ: You're not educating -- this is -- propaganda. What are you going to do, like anti trans next? Is that what you're going to do next?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK RUBI, HUNTER COLLEGE STUDENT: We are talking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: This is -- this is violent. You're triggering my students.\par \par \uc2 RUBI: I'm sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: No, you're not, because you can't even have a -- baby. So you don't even know what that is. You don't even know what this is. So get this -- out of here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Unbelievable. Joining me exclusively is the student you just saw, Patrick Rubi. He is a rising junior at Hunter College. Patrick, what set her off in your display? Now, this is a professor at the college. What is she professor of, first of all? Do you know?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK RUBI, HUNTER COLLEGE STUDENT: I'm not sure exactly what she's a professor of. She's in the arts department.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, shocking. OK. So what was she so offended by?\par \par \uc2 RUBI: I think she was mostly offended by our general presence on campus. Our tabling was mainly about chemical abortions and the harms of chemical abortions for mothers and for the children involved. But I don't know if she looked at our supplies before she came and started to berate us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We reached out to the dear professor. We did not hear back. But we did get a statement from Hunter College, saying they take this matter very seriously. The provost has opened an investigation into the professor's action. An investigation? You have the videotape. What do you need an investigation? She called you guys violent. Then she violently cleared your table, like a two-year-old. Her grammar wasn't so hot, and her language was profane. I can't imagine if that was some type of, I don't know, trans-table and some conservative professor, if they still exists, came in and did the same thing. Can you imagine, the college? They would be locking Hunter College down in protest tonight.\par \par \uc2 RUBI: Yes, absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: so Patrick, an hour after that happened, a student actually stole a model that you had from your table. It was a fetal model.\par \par \uc2 RUBI: Correct.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And then it came back after -- it came back in white type of shape? I'm not trying to make something out of nothing here, but this is pretty gross. Actually, we'll play the soundbite. Here goes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bye girl.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Kill yourself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is the daily life of what it's like to just be pro-life on a college campus today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So tried to mutilate your little exhibits.\par \par \uc2 RUBI: Absolutely. So she had -- that student had come along when we were tabling, and it's my understanding that she did not say anything before she proceeded to take two of our fetal models and put them in her mouth and start to chew them in front of us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What?\par \par \uc2 RUBI: And then -- yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 RUBI: So she chewed them in her mouth, and then left. But then she came back and returned our fetal models, except she had colored red all them and come up with a sign calling us fascists. And --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She said die, she also said die, which kind of fits for the pro- abortion movement. Patrick, you're very brave, and we appreciate you very much. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the Last Bite.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So you are a daily runner?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER, (D-NJ): I have a streak going now. Next week I'll break through the 500 days in a row of running.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow. So you run cold, rain, sleet. You're like the postal service.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: I run on days that used to be excuses not to run for me, but now I get this sense of triumph. Like, how long can I keep this streak going?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Democrats have no streak of economic wins for America, but at least \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  is in good shape.\par \par \uc2 Gutfeld next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230523ej5m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. Ted Cruz (R-TX); Interview with Sen. \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2  (D-RI); Looming DeSantis 2024 Decision Casts spotlight on his Record; Trump leads GOP Field as DeSantis, Sununu, Christie and Others Weigh Running; Durham Repot Prompts Range of Partisan Reactions; Montana Faces Legal Challenge after Banning TikTok App\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Peter Doocy, Juan Williams \par \uc2 9,806 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 President Biden comes out swinging against Republicans in a rare press conference overseas, as debt limit negotiators face a deadline back home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America has never defaulted and it never will.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The president tries to reassure global leaders as the parties duke it out in D.C. over a deal to keep paying our bills. Americans remain concerned about the impact of a potential default on markets, small businesses, loan rates and U.S. global influence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): The president of the United States is playing Russian roulette with the American economy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have a deep dive on what it will take to get a deal through Congress to stave off an economic nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ted Cruz on whether Speaker McCarthy can hold the line. And Senator \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2  on Democrats' fears the president may be selling out some of his biggest agenda items and setting a dangerous precedent.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we'll ask both senators about news the U.S. will train Ukrainian pilots to fly F-16s in the fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Then, we are kicking off a major week in the Republican 2024 primary. Senator Tim Scott files paperwork to run. Florida Governor DeSantis could join the race within days.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: I honestly believe that we have an opportunity to right the ship.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there are others hinting they could be in, too.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: I have debated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  many times, just not with the cameras on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our Sunday panel takes you beyond the headlines in what promises to be a big week for the 2024 GOP field.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Breaking this morning, the president slamming Republicans over stalled talks on the debt limit. The comments coming in a rare press conference. The president says Republicans have to accept that there is no bipartisan deal if they stick only to what he deems their partisan terms.\par \par \uc2 But Speaker McCarthy says the president is letting left wing Democrats call all the shots. So, now, the president is on his way home and is expected to talk directly with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy while on the flight.\par \par \uc2 But with no breakthroughs this weekend, the talks are down to the wire. The president was asked whether he'd consider going around the Congress by invoking the 14th Amendment to avoid the U.S. defaulting on its bills. I'll tell you what he said.\par \par \uc2 Congress has just about 10 days left to get some kind of deal together.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll sit down with Texas Senator Ted Cruz for his reaction to the president's hard line comments this morning.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to our own Peter Doocy live in Hiroshima, Japan.\par \par \uc2 All right. Peter, let's start here. Does the president actually think he's got authority to raise the debt limit on his own, go around Congress, to use the 14th Amendment?\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Shannon, we heard for the first time this evening here in Hiroshima, he thinks he does.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Would you be blameless in a default situation?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: On the merits, based on what I've offered, I would be blameless.\par \par \uc2 On the politics of it, no one will be blameless. And, by the way, that's one of the -- that's one of the things that some are contemplating.\par \par \uc2 I actually had -- well, I got to be careful here. I think there are some MAGA Republicans in the House who know the damage that it would do to the economy and because I am president, a president's responsible for everything, Biden would take the blame and that's one way to make sure Biden is not re-elected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, that sound bite speaks to President Biden's description of Republican ideas during these debt talks as extreme and unacceptable, which means it's unclear who he thinks should get the credit or the -- who should get credit for a deal or a default.\par \par \uc2 We also do have his explanation about why he thinks that he could evoke the 14th Amendment and go around Congress right here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm looking at the 14th amendment as to whether or not we have the authority. I think we have the authority. The question is, could it be done and evoke in time that it could not -- would not be appealed and as a consequence pass the date in question and still default on the debt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 G7\uc2  here in Japan put their heads together to try to figure how to put Ukraine on a path towards F-16 capabilities and they tried to figure out how to work with China without letting China take advantage of the world's economies.\par \par \uc2 He says that he expects relations with Xi to thaw soon and that comes after what he described as an incident earlier this year with a, quote, "silly balloon that carried two freight cars of spy equipment".\par \par \uc2 He calls it silly now. But remember, he did order a fighter jet to shoot it down this winter -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. We've got more questions after that press conference, but we're glad you got yours in.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy, reporting from Hiroshima -- safe travels home, Peter. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now, Texas Senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Good morning, Shannon. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, you heard a lot of Peter's reporting and what the president had to say overnight, their early -- late in Japan, early here.\par \par \uc2 One of these ideas that he says, MAGA Republicans will let the economy suffer serious damage because -- then he'll just take the blame and not get re-elected.\par \par \uc2 That's a pretty serious allegation to make against Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: You know, it really is unfortunate to see how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is approaching this job. It's all politics all the time, and he consistently goes to the hard left.\par \par \uc2 He is off in Hiroshima right now, in Japan. He should be in Washington, D.C. He should be sitting down and working out a deal, working out a compromise.\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives did its job. Weeks ago, it passed legislation raising the debt ceiling and it passed responsible legislation that makes real steps to rein in the out of control spending.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has handed his entire agenda over to the radical left, to the Bernie Sanders and the AOCs of the world. And his view is that he is willing to tank the economy because he's unwilling to give even a penny in the trillions of new spending that have caused inflation that's hurting Americans all across the country.\par \par \uc2 It's a blatantly unreasonable position, but yet he nonetheless smiles and just blames the other side.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, is it fair to -- you know, he raised eyebrows by saying Republicans would let the economy default, damage, because they don't want to get him re -- him to get reelected, but fair for you to say that he's willing to tank the economy?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes. It is because there's one person on Planet Earth with the power to ensure that we don't have a default and you just played him a moment ago, his name is \uc2 Joseph Robinette Biden Jr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A responsible president would have stood up in that press conference and said, understand and hear me now, the United States of America will never, ever, ever default on our debts. We will pay the interest of our debts. The president can say that.\par \par \uc2 Why can he say that? He can say that because every month, revenue from federal taxes exceeds the interest on the debt. He could take default off the table.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't want to take default off the table, why? Because he wants to scare-monger, he wants to scare people into saying, look at this bad thing that I, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , am threatening is going to happen, the only result is to leave $32 trillion in debt.\par \par \uc2 Do you know what's amazing, Shannon? Biden's position is way to the left of most Democrats. Recent polling shows 58 percent of Democrats think that we should have reasonable deficit reduction as part of raising the debt ceiling and yet, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's position is, absolutely -- not.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and yeah.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And it's worth --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, I was going to say that's the accusation on both sides, is that both sides are being controlled by the extremes of their party. You would say the left and many others, Republicans will say it's the progressive, it's the Squad, it's the people who say that they won't even entertain work requirements for government assistance, those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 But the White House continues to say -- by the way, we invited somebody from the White House to come on, they chose not to come on and talk about the debt ceiling today but they did send us several memos, and one of them uses a lot of language referring to you and your party as extreme. You've taken the economy hostage. You're fighting tooth and nail to protect tax breaks for the wealthiest of Americans and biggest corporations.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: You know, you're right. They use lots of angry rhetoric and they call everyone else extreme. This is where actually truth and substance matters.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the bill the House passed, it's a very reasonable bill. The bill the House passed saves $4.8 trillion over 10 years. Now, that's a reasonable down payment. We owe $32 trillion. So, that's not trying to solve it all, but it's taking a reasonable step to solve it.\par \par \uc2 Now, how does it do it? The big thing it does is it reduces spending from current levels to what we were spending in December of 2022. In other words, five months ago.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden White House says, these are draconian cuts, the world will end.\par \par \uc2 I'll ask you, Shannon, in December 2022, Christmas time, did you think suddenly the federal government had withered away? Just reducing it by -- to what it was five months ago and then allowing it to grow 1 percent a year results in those savings.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats are all in. They want to spend, for example, on 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. They want those \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents to harass the American people. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says it's extreme not to give them this army of \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants to spend $30 billion that was appropriated for COVID funds. Now, Biden has declared the COVID emergency over, but he wants a $30 billion slush fund that hasn't been spent.\par \par \uc2 I think what the House has done is very reasonable and I'll tell you what Biden and the Democrats hate the most are work requirements for welfare.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I believe if you're an able-bodied man, you ought to be working. And I think work requirements are one of the most compassionate things you can do, because they get people back into the workforce, back supply (ph) - - providing for their family.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is threatening to veto the whole thing and default on the debt because he is so opposed to getting people back to work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I mean, he --\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: That's an extreme party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He voted for that at that point, he did.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yeah, yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there are the voices in his party who now say it is cruel to take away that safety net from people who need something in their life to protect them when the economy is struggles, which it clearly is.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that we get to the 14th Amendment, because you are a constitutional scholar.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I've seen you argue cases at the Supreme Court. You know this in and out.\par \par \uc2 Today, you heard what Peter Doocy just played, the president. The sound bite where he said, I think we have the authority to use it. Essentially, the president is looking at something that would allow him to go around Congress.\par \par \uc2 He's not the only one. Not surprisingly, somebody you disagree with quite a bit from time to time, your former Harvard law professor Laurence Tribe, talking to "Harvard Gazette" said this: Once Congress has authorized the executive branch to spend money directed where and how it is to be spent and appropriated the money, there's no question given Section 4 of the 14th Amendment which says public debt has to be paid, that the federal government must somehow pay in full those to whom its promises have been made pursuant to the law.\par \par \uc2 Your Democratic colleague Bernie Sanders among many others signed on to a letter this week, including with our next guest. It said -- he said it's unambiguous the president has that power. You heard the president say it this morning, too.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Well, listen, I think Biden's position on the 14th Amendment is legally frivolous. By the way, someone else who agreed with that was Barack Obama. The left tried to convince Obama to do this, and Obama said, no, you can't do this under the Constitution, under the terms of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 That's a provision that was put in place to say that we're going to pay our civil war debts. It refers to the civil war that we had just fought and paying those war debts off.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Tribe's position doesn't hold water either. As I said, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can ensure that we don't default on the debt. He has ample authority to do that and to do that right now by saying we're going to pay the interest on the debt.\par \par \uc2 What they want is not to pay the interest on the debt, what they want to do is they want to pay the $6 trillion in other government spending, and there's nothing in the 14th Amendment that gives them the power to do that.\par \par \uc2 Once -- once they run out of money they have to obey what Congress has appropriated and what's happened -- the position of the Democrats for two months was they wouldn't talk at all. And now, their position is that we can't cut any government spending reasonably. We have to let debt keep skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, it's irresponsible, what we are doing to the next generations. To give you an order of magnitude -- in 2017 total government spending was about $4 trillion. Tax revenues were about $3.3 trillion. So, we had about $700 billion deficit.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to today. Total government spending has gone from $4 trillion, all the way up to nearly $7 trillion. We've nearly doubled government spending since 2017.\par \par \uc2 What have tax revenues done? They've gone from $3.3 trillion to right about $5 trillion. So, we're taking in a lot more taxes but they can't keep up with the Democrats' wild spending binge.\par \par \uc2 And this proposal is very reasonable. It's what a large majority of Democrats back home think we ought to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Which is have some reasonable restraints limiting spending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. But fair to say, Republicans have added to what we have now in the debt. I mean, they voted for a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Sure, yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You voted multiple times to raise the debt ceiling under President Trump. So, why is this different?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Well, it's different because the magnitude of where the debt is, is massive. And what has happened in the last two years, you are right, during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's presidency, during COVID, Republicans and Democrats in a bipartisan way passed emergency COVID relief that was very expensive. We're facing a pandemic. We had politicians who had shut much of the country down, and in that emergency, we acted.\par \par \uc2 What happened subsequently is that you had \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  do a complete takeover of the federal government and they went on a spending binge. And all of that -- that was predominantly hard partisan spending, trillions and trillions of dollars of hard Democrat objectives, things like 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 I've yet to find someone in Texas who wants 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. They understand those agents are being brought in to harass and target the political enemies of the administration. That's not an economic objective.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: That's a political objective.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're being told that they are being brought in to bring more revenue, which we know we hit a record level in fiscal `22 for government revenue that -- that was brought in. So, talking about where that money that goes is another part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 I want to quickly --\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: But the more revenue -- sure, the more revenue is going to be from the people they target, which sadly under this administration, has proven to be their political enemies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. And it does tend to be lower income people. It's not always the billionaires out there, who are getting those audits.\par \par \uc2 Quickly before you go, I want to ask you about --\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- you've written to \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2 , to ask them to investigate a deal that Bud Light had with influencer Dylan Mulvaney, and whether that amounted to wrongfully targeting alcohol advertising at young people.\par \par \uc2 There's been some pushback as you would imagine. And there's been this warning from a lot of folks, including the "Intelligencer Magazine", says this, as Republicans prepare for 2024 presidential campaign, the fact that one cannot win a GOP primary without titillating culture war addicts is undermining the party's prospects for winning the next general election.\par \par \uc2 "Reuters" has been reporting that GOP megadonor Peter Teal doesn't want to get financially involved this time around because he's worried about focusing on things like transgenderism, on abortion.\par \par \uc2 What do you make about these warnings that you're going to alienate big donors and potentially voters by focusing on these issues?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Listen, I think number one, the Democrat Party on issues of culture has gone really extreme. This is a party that embraces medical transition, sex changes for children, 8, 9, 10-year-old children, medical surgeries, sterilizing kids, removing perfectly healthy body parts.\par \par \uc2 I got to say, I think that is extreme. I think that's child abuse. No 8- year-old has the maturity to make a decision to give up the ability to have children for the rest of his or her life. And any adult that is doing that to an 8-year-old is abusing that child.\par \par \uc2 And I think -- the Democrats, I don't know why on these issues, they've gotten so extreme.\par \par \uc2 If you look at Bud Light, I can't think of a time when a company's gone more out of its way to alienate and irritate its customers. It's almost like they never met an actual Bud Light drinker.\par \par \uc2 And the letter I sent -- \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  and I together, sent a letter, concerned a different aspect of this, which is -- which is the beer companies operate under rules that prohibit their marketing to minors. Remember, Joe Camel and all of that investigation, that they're not allowed to market to minors.\par \par \uc2 And Dylan Mulvaney, the social influencer, is an influencer who targets overwhelmingly young girls, teenage girls, pre-pubescent girls. If you look at Dylan Mulvaney's TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , you have things like Dylan Mulvaney saying, I am a 6-year-old girl. Dylan Mulvaney going and shopping for Barbies. Days of girl hood.\par \par \uc2 It's very directly targeted at individuals who are younger than 21 and for whom it is illegal to purchase beer.\par \par \uc2 And so, what Senator Blackburn and I requested is they -- that they investigate -- the \uc2 Beer Institute\uc2  investigate, did \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2  deliberately target children? Do they have marketing documents that laid out, hey, if we can get kids starting to drink beer when they're 10, 12, 14 years old, they'll be customers the rest of their lives --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: -- because if they did, they are violating the rules that applied to the marketing of alcohol.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, trust me, if you get your hands on those documents, all of America will want to know. So let us know about any response you get.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cruz, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we're going to bring in a leading Democratic senator for his response to Senator Cruz, and we will ask exactly what Democrats will allow President Biden to trade, if anything, in order to get their votes for some kind of debt -- debt limit deal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just this morning, the president took to the mics on the world stage -- well, for a rare press conference, frankly, and he focused on Ukraine and that the U.S. just announced what appears to be a pretty major policy reversal. The U.S. will conduct joint training of Ukrainian pilots on aircrafts, including F-16s, with a possibility now of those jets getting into Ukraine -- something the president has said in February Ukraine didn't need.\par \par \uc2 The change comes as Zelenskyy himself met with leaders at the \uc2 G7\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, Rhode Island Senator \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACK REED (D-RI): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- who leads the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REED: She's breaking up.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Shannon. It's pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 I'm at Southern Virginia University for the graduation. I want to congratulate graduates all across the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and we are very grateful they work together with you and your team so that we could have you with us this morning. Our thanks to the university.\par \par \uc2 I want to start here by playing something the president had to say this morning about relations with China and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, number one, you're right, we should have an open hotline. At the Bali conference, that's what President Xi and I agreed we were going to do and meet on.\par \par \uc2 And then, the silly balloon that was carrying two freight cars worth of spying equipment was flying over the United States. And it got shot down. And everything changed in terms of talking to one another.\par \par \uc2 I think you are going to see that begin to thaw very shortly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, a lot to unpack there. But the president essentially said that things are going to be better, communication-wise with China. At the same time, he is coming off \uc2 G7\uc2  where the conversation and the message coming from that was supposed to be strength and unity against China.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what he said about where we stand, this relationship with China?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, we have demonstrated to China that the world community is standing up for the tradition of the international order that was established after World War II. But in the context of a nuclear power like China, with conflicting goals, we have to have a line of communication. It's something that we maintain throughout the Cold War with the Soviet Union, even at the height of the Cold War, because you do want to be able to communicate in times of crisis.\par \par \uc2 So, that's common sense and it actually serves the military purpose, too, to communicate our intentions and to make sure that they don't misinterpret what we're doing and we don't misinterpret what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, it sounds like that is going to reconnect there very soon.\par \par \uc2 So, at the \uc2 G7\uc2 , this announcement that we are now going to engage in training of Ukrainian pilots on numbers of aircraft, including F-16s, and that those jets may eventually flow into the country from numerous sources potentially.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board says this, at last F-16s for Ukraine. That's been the White House pattern throughout the Ukraine conflict: resist more advanced weapons, then finally provide them much later after more carnage.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people think about the Abrams tanks and other issues that eventually were provided to Ukraine after some delay.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this criticism in many different quarters that when it comes to how we equipped Ukraine, sometimes it's been too little too late?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, first of all, you have to recognize that the administration and President Biden particularly put together a coalition of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and most of the world to stand against an unauthorized, unprovoked, brutal attack by the Russians.\par \par \uc2 I don't think anyone expected that kind of action, diplomatically and economically, imposing sanctions.\par \par \uc2 And then we began to give the Ukrainians what they needed. In the first phase of the war, they needed antitank weapons, the Javelins, we provided those. They needed air defense systems, we gave them Stinger missiles.\par \par \uc2 Now, as they are organizing to conduct a counteroffensive, we provided them not only the equipment, the tanks, but we've also provided them the training so they can operate as a coordinated combined arms team.\par \par \uc2 And now, the other issue here which has been constant has been air defense, and we've given them air defense like the Patriot missile systems.\par \par \uc2 So, we're now at the point where their aircraft have been essentially worn out in many respects, and we're going to replace them eventually with F- 16s, probably from our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies but the pilots can be trained here.\par \par \uc2 So, we have been trying to keep up with not only their demands but their capacity and also the threats they are facing in the field.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and that raises questions, too. I mean, the American people polling shows wants to help Ukrainians. They are inspired by their courage. And I think, frankly, Putin and many others are surprised they've lasted this long and been such a formidable foe.\par \par \uc2 But in feeding so much of our military equipment to Ukraine, there are real concerns. I mean, we've got years overdue orders to Taiwan where they may be a real issue with China at some point, and yet, the weaponry we need to provide Taiwan and that they paid for has not gotten there, as we deplete supplies in Ukraine and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 So, what are your concerns on that front?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, I think the operations in Ukraine demonstrated that our industrial base is very strained. It was complicated, of course, by the pandemic, by a shortage of parts, particularly microchips and we took action to pass the CHIPS bill.\par \par \uc2 But our whole military industrial base, our munitions factories, et cetera, were, frankly, over many decades, left sort of -- I don't want to say dormant, but not as resilient as they have to be.\par \par \uc2 So, we're starting through the defense bill and also through the administration's proposals to encourage defense spending and provide the resources we need to cope with not only the threat in the Ukraine but also a potential threat with China.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Congress did back in 2017 is we created a fund for the submarine industrial base. So, we've been providing resources to rebuild our submarine industrial base since 2017. That was absorbed by the administration, the Department of Defense.\par \par \uc2 But this is indeed a wakeup call to deal with an issue that has not been dealt with for a decade.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 Okay, I want to make sure we can ask you about the debt ceiling in this respect, too, because you signed on to this letter with Senator Bernie Sanders and others who told the White House, you should use the 14th Amendment, go around Congress. You heard what Senator Cruz said. It's just not legally viable.\par \par \uc2 And he's not the only one. I mean, Secretary Yellen, part of this administration, said, you know, it's questionable at best.\par \par \uc2 Why are you supporting something that would go around your authority as a member of Congress?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, my authority as a member of Congress is to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution of the United States. This is the Constitution of the United States. This is not just some sort of memo from the White House or from Speaker McCarthy's office.\par \par \uc2 This is a manufactured crisis. For three times, President Trump came to the Congress and asked us to pass a unconditional debt ceiling increase. Even after he had increased the debts dramatically by his tax cuts, we did that, because we understood the risk of the economy, the risk of the American people, the risk of our national security standing.\par \par \uc2 If we default on our debt, the people in Beijing will be very happy, because they will stand up and point out how unreliable we are, how reckless, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 This is completely avoidable. All the speaker has to do is what Democrats did -- Senator Schumer, Speaker Pelosi -- pass a clean debt ceiling and then sit down and negotiate a budget and put everything on the table there.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, this is reckless and that's exactly what they're trying to do. And again, the best point to reference is the fact that under President Trump, we understood the dangers of default, and we acted responsibly and reasonably.\par \par \uc2 In fact, in 2019, Trump said he could not imagine anyone ever using the debt ceiling as leverage from political negotiations. So --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, we also remember that back in 2006, then-Senator Biden said he wouldn't vote for it because it was reckless and irresponsible, the spending that had already been incurred and it would only encourage it.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that get to this before we go with you. Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , your colleague, is back on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 REED: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A lot of celebrations about that.\par \par \uc2 She admits that her doctor wants her under a lighter workload, and seemed she has some confusion. And there are questions now from Democrats as well about whether she should be there.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something from Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , who is running to replace Senator Feinstein when she does leave office. Here's what Miss Porter had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): This is, unfortunately, not the first time that we've had this situation where we had real concerns about how senators are recovering and whether they're able to come back and really do the job. We just had Senator Fetterman who was in the hospital for a couple months. What are you going to do when someone becomes infirmed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Senator, what do you say to those critics who say it's unfair to have someone in office simply because they would vote your way if they may be struggling with physical or mental issues?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, I think Senator Feinstein has performed remarkably during her career. I think at this point she has medical issues. She's acknowledged those issues. She still is able to summon the energy and the concentration to come to vote. She is continuing to work. I think she deserves the opportunity to - to make a decision about her career.\par \par \uc2 I had the opportunity to serve with Storm Thurmond, who was 100 years old when he retired. And there were some people back then who were saying he should go. But not with the same kind of intensity today.\par \par \uc2 Again, I think this is something that Senator Feinstein should consider and make a decision.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, we appreciate your time. Have a wonderful graduation ceremony.\par \par \uc2 REED: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REED: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we could see two big named Republicans officially join the 2024 race this week. What does it mean for the dynamics of the race? We will bring in our Sunday group in just minutes on how these candidates plan to take on former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): So, we just completed what I would say is the boldest and most far-reaching agenda that we've seen in the modern history of the Republican Party. If you look at what we've been able to accomplish, there is something in there for anybody in terms of the issues that we tackled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in the notable 2024 state of New Hampshire talking big about his record at home.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., bureau chief at "USA Today" Susan Page, Fox News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Kevin Roberts, president of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , and host of "MEDIABUZZ" here on Fox News, Howie Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 Great to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 OK, so this is the line from "Wall Street Journal's" editorial board on his legislative record of Governor DeSantis. They say, there's no denying Mr. DeSantis gets things done.\par \par \uc2 And, Susan, that is going to be - that's what he's been arguing in Iowa and New Hampshire, these early places he keeps going back to, like, look at all the things I got done. Now, part of this is he's got GOP super majorities in Florida, but still he worked with them and got his agenda through.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Not just GOP super majorities in the legislature, he won re-election by almost 20 points in a big state, a state that historically has had also, you know, Democratic voters. So, that is a really impressive re-election. He's gotten big things done. He's gotten big things done that are going to give him trouble if he gets to a general election. And I think that is a challenge he faces. He needs to take on Trump in the primaries and win his - win there. If he succeeds in doing that, he'll have to deal in a - in a more nice edge way with some of the things he's done in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and as somebody gets in, we know a lot of times before they're in everybody focuses on why they think they'd be a great candidate, if that's your person, whatever, then, Howie, we know, once the media gets ahold of you and you really have to start interacting with them it can be different. And we saw pieces this week, they were not so kind to the governor and also his wife.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, HOST, "MEDIABUZZ": Yes, the knives are out and a lot of journalists are looking at DeSantis because maybe they're taking a break from beating up on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But we now know what the governor's pitch is to donors. And that is, there's only three people who can become president. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he says, can't win general elections, so it's me or Biden. Hat tip to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter who was on that phone call.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's got high favorables in those early states, Iowa, New Hampshire, South Carolina. But I said this for months, he made an historic mistake by not punching back when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was roughing him up and defining him.\par \par \uc2 So, in addition to that, you know, the Trump people text me, and other journalists, of course, every -- two minutes after every poll comes out saying Trump's up by 30, Trump's up by 40, but those are national numbers and are therefore dismissed by the DeSantis camp as being meaningless this early. So, finally, I just think that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has tremendous potential as a candidate, but he's got himself a sizeable hole to dig out of.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 And if you score well it those early states, it can change the perception of momentum in those kinds of things as well.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now somebody else who's getting in this week, Senator Tim Scott. And, listen, President Trump's world did not waste time in coming after him, but taking swipes at everybody else in this process, saying this, Tim Scott's entrance in aggressive media purchase doesn't only knee cap DeSantis, but Scott sees the same thing as Youngkin, Sununu, Burgum, Christie and others, the path to second place is wide open. They smell \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' blood in the water and no longer see him as an obstacle.\par \par \uc2 So, Kevin, I mean the idea is, we're not even going to talk about the nomination because President Trump, his team says, has that sewn up, but now they go out, you know, after everybody else who is hoping for a number two the way they put it.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERTS, HERITAGE FOUNDATION PRESIDENT: Well, news flash, the race is wide open. It hadn't even begun yet. I was talking to Bob Vander Plaats of The Family Leader, in Iowa, obviously a very influential conservative movement guy, about Senator Scott, about Governor DeSantis. And two days ago he told me, look, Iowa is wide open. Seventy-five, 80 percent of the caucus goers are looking for someone other than President Trump, even though they're very grateful for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, whether it's Senator Scott or Governor DeSantis, I'm expecting that this is going to be a wide open race. At this point, Shannon, in 2007, if you looked at polls, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  would have been the nominee. This race hasn't even begun. And from this conservative leader, I can tell you that we're very excited about the bench we have.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, speaking of the bench, there's somebody who has said so far they're not going to be part of running, and then have this ad out there. Let's give you a little snippet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): We can usher in a new era of American values. President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  changed lives. And now it's our turn. A time --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, now it's our turn. That's Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin and, Juan, has said he's not running, but there he is with the Reagan imagery and saying, now is our time. What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think, you know, what we're witnessing here is a new phase of the Republican campaign. I think everybody now has a sense that Trump, you know, has some substantial legal trouble, was just found liable in that sexual assault case. Questions there suggest that maybe, you know, being number two would be an interesting place to be, to be the option to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that right now Ronald DeSantis -- \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- is Governor DeSantis is in that spot. And what it means is, he's getting attacked by Trump, and he's getting attacked by everybody else because others are competing for that number two slot to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the question becomes, who can find an effective way to attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DeSantis could do it on things like immigration, Covid, but so far he has not been attacking former -- the former president. Instead, the former president has been attacking him. This week on the idea of, you know, you were mentioning his legislative accomplishments with the former president says, this is a guy that was backing a national sales tax. Do you want that? You know, people talk about his stance on abortion, his stance on guns, and it creates the kind of problem that Susan was talking about earlier going towards a general election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, to a general situation.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: The more Republicans who get in the race, the more they are dividing the not Trump vote -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And making it easier for former President Trump to win the nomination.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you say he's not attacking Trump, but maybe not by name. I mean he does say things like, the GOP has to get away from the culture of losing, and that seems to be a very clear swipe. And he also - I think it was in New Hampshire on Friday that he was talking about legislatively getting these policy things done and he says it can't just be executive orders because you can see that get undone by the next guy. And he said, that's what Biden's doing.\par \par \uc2 So, he's clearly going to try to make this about what he's accomplished legislatively, as you said.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Oh, no question about that. And, you know, what he's saying is, I offer you the Trump policies you'd like so much without all the baggage that Trump carried. And that - that is - that is potentially a very powerful message, although I have to say that DeSantis has had some problems out of the gate, sob problems in being an engaging retail politician the way Tim Scott is engaging on the stump.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess I most agree with you when you say this race is at the beginning, not near the end.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and it's funny because some people have said these - these cuts on DeSantis, that he's not good with people, are setting the expectation so low that when he does show up people are pleasantly surprised. The - in New Hampshire a few weeks ago when he was there and he stayed and he took selfies and he shook hands with everybody, I mean Kevin act - people acted like, oh, this is not how he was portrayed to me.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: No, it's going to work in his favor. I mean having been with the governor many times, including at a recent event here in D.C., he's very good one on one. And - and - and the best evidence I can offer of that is, when he stepped into Jethro's Barbecue it Des Moines a week ago, it was a new race because people realized the DC media have been telling us that this guy is boring. He's quite the opposite. This is the guy we're looking for. He's a - he's a - they are saying this, that he's the governor who is implementing the Trump agenda without the baggage.\par \par \uc2 Once again, my concern is just policy and ideas. We don't have a horse in the race at \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2 , but we're very grateful that whether it's Governor DeSantis or someone else that they're going to exceed those expectations. You saw that bear out for the governor in New Hampshire as well.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But, you know, I mean, look, he's become, I think, for the Republican Party, the great red hope, right? He's not Trump, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean a 19-point win doesn't hurt in Florida.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, but I'm -- wait a minute, but this week who punched him out? Mickey Mouse on the \uc2 Disney\uc2  deal, right? All of a sudden he thought, oh, I can take out \uc2 Disney\uc2 , a culture wars issue over gays and discussion of people who are transsexual. And you know what, he just lost a billion- dollar investment in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there is -- there is more to that because apparently -- there are such things. \uc2 Disney\uc2  reporters, who cover \uc2 Disney\uc2  -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which, that's a great assignment.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: No, it isn't.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But that - but right now it might be a little bit trickier.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, yes, you could get tickets. But what they said is that this was a \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  project that he had wanted. Iger comes along, maybe doesn't want to get involved with this. They had already delayed it they said at least until 2026.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Howie, we do see "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " run with it as a slap at DeSantis, which it may have been a decision independent of that.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: A lot of people are running with it as a slap at DeSantis. And, look, this is not what you want to have happen when the week that you're on the cover of "Time" magazine and you're about to jump into the race within days.\par \par \uc2 And, look, the things that he's accomplished at home, you know, more power to him, but a six week abortion ban may not play as well nationally as it is in Florida. In fact, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has criticism of -- criticized him on that saying it's too harsh. The abortion politics are very tricky in this race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That will be very tricky on the - on the primary debate stage too.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, Mickey Mouse, tougher than he looks. And I'm not sure this was a wise move by the governor, whatever all the machinations we have of him (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, there are critics who say that it's not a conservative position to go after business prestige.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It's a very conservative position. It's - it's eliminating special treatment that \uc2 Disney\uc2  has gotten.\par \par \uc2 And I'll just remind all of you that Mickey has lost one-third of market capitalization since Mickey decided to pick this fight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'll speak on behalf of grassroots conservatives. They love this because they realize there's a difference between corporatism and the free market. And that's where Governor DeSantis is in a very strong position.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And what did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he's anti-corp for going after corporations -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He didn't think it was a good idea.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Involved in a fight that doesn't speak to the average American's concerned about kitchen table economic issues. He was light weight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, yes, but - but Kevin may differ on you on the cultural issue there.\par \par \uc2 OK, we'll leave it there for now, panel. A quick break.\par \par \uc2 Up next, though, we're going to talk about the policy wars on TikTok. Montana has now banned the app from the entire state. And, yes, there's already a lawsuit. We'll talk about it. How in the world does the state enforce this?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There was - the whole thing is predicated on, it's like a rabbit hole conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 PETER STRZOK, FORMER FBI AGENT: This is a predictable, sad ending to an investigation that never should never have taken place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What you have with \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is - look, it's a big fat nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Durham is a - is a kind of pathetic character.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, look at one flavor of reaction this week after a Justice Department reports that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should not have launched that full Trump- Russia investigation. So, we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Howie, this was the headline from "The Federalist." They said the corporate media have instantly gone to work sweeping away the shocking conclusions of Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's report. The media won't relay facts from the report honestly because, of course, the media were complicit in the absolute con from the start.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: We are in parallel universes because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and many conservative media people see this Durham report as a vindication of the former president and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  probe should never have been started.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, most of the mainstream media have been knocking it down. They were so invested in the Trump-Russia collusion saga. And it's hard -- and what they're saying it's a nothing burger. It's a waste of time. It is -- not one person went to jail. Hardly the crime of the century that Trump predicted it would be.\par \par \uc2 So, you have these completely different -- and it is certainly true that - that because journalists spend so many years covering this, I would say over covering this, I would say over hyping this, they can't quite let it go.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They won Pulitzers.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Won Pulitzers. Both for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." And it just seems to me that this is -- it's like d,j. vu. This has been going on for years. It's never going to end.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and hearing one of those bites that was, this investigation should have never happened, it was about Durham, not the Crossfire Hurricane, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You could use that byte either way.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Look - you could. And I would just say, regardless of what someone thinks about President Trump politically, that if you take a step back and look at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from a - a policy and ideas point of view, it's clear that it's become politically weaponized. Ask Mark Hauck in Pennsylvania. Ask the pro-life activist who's being hounded this weekend, probably as we sit here, Shannon, by two \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. Ask anyone who's a reasonable conservative, and you know that this is an agency -- and I mean this very intentionally as a policy objective, it needs to be started over from scratch and rebuilt. This is not a law enforcement agency, it's a political weapon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, wait a second, if I - imagine if Hillary had -\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I knew you might disagree with that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Imagine if \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was sitting here and she said, oh, wasn't it the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who came out ten days before the election to restart an investigation publicly and damage, possibly killed her 2016 campaign? I mean the reality is, as Howie was saying, there was no conviction. This had been extremely heralded by the right-wing and by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right-wing media include, saying this investigation is going to reveal that there was deep corruption in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and potentially you could have people like Hillary Clinton going to jail. Nothing. Zero.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, what do you make of -- what do you make of - what do you make, though, of Durham saying, there were all kinds of abandoning of policies and of strictures that would have kept some of these things from happening and making notes about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and the Clinton investigation that investigations were not handled in the same way that they were for Trump world.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Let me say the -- first of all, the inspector general came out with this conclusion that some of the strictures may have been sloppily handled. But even \uc2 John Durham\uc2  said, no, they should have been an investigation, it's just he has questions about exactly how it was conducted.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: There were -- there were serious problems over this \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation and with bias against Trump and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But at the same time, most of that had come out earlier, years ago, and that's why a lot of journalists felt this to say, well, it's just a rehash.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, but it calls for - it calls for no more prosecutions, no big indictments. There were no successful prosecutions as a result of this special council inquiry.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: No.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I don't have a problem with the investigation - the special counsel inquiry itself because, obviously, this is an issue that divides and concerns a lot of America. I think it's good to have a serious look at it.\par \par \uc2 But then you have an obligation to read the report on its own. And what the report finds is, confirmation bias on the part of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And that's a bad thing. You don't want confirmation bias. It is not the kind of explosive conclusion that President Trump is telling us it is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Something that a lot divides America, many time along age lines, is TikTok. And Montana has said no more. The governor there said that he made the decisive action, the most of any state, protecting private data and sensitive personal information from being harvested by the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, he says that's what's happening, they're taking this data in, and you won't punish the users if somebody can get their hands on it. It's going to be about providing the app so it can be used by people who live in Montana.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: There are two huge problems with TikTok and I hope that anyone watching or listening to this, especially if they're a parent or grandparent, causes anyone they love in their life to delete it from their phones. The first is that it's owned, in essence, by the Chinese Communist Party, which is the gravest enemy the United States has faced in our history. The second is, as a father of four, three of them were girls -\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It is decidedly destructive on the self-worth, the value, the self-value that young ladies feel. And - and just whether we're liberals or conservatives, it's just common sense that we shouldn't be using this app. Hats off to the governor of Montana for providing the path.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think enforcement might be a bear. There's already a lawsuit by five plaintiffs who say that the state has no authority to enact laws advancing what it believes should be foreign policy or national security interests and it can't go about communications that it doesn't like some of the speech. It's protected by the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Susan.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. So, Montana is the leading edge on policy against China and TikTok and - and what happens -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think they're closer than a lot of the country.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes, and - and I agree there are serious problems with TikTok. Shouldn't -- doesn't it sound a little like something Washington should be handling, not Montana?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and they've had a lot of disagreement about this. I mean about how it's going to get done here.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I think Governor \uc2 Greg Gianforte\uc2 's is going to have a hard time winning this in court because there's a free speech aspect.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Now, it's true, and as a father of young girls, it is addictive. But it is also true that basically this is aimed at \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  who provide the software from their app stores. And I think -- you know, I - I share the concerns about, you know, the Chinese-owned company, but I don't know that you can make the case that therefore nobody should be able to play with it on their phone.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The case is literally in federal court now.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think - I think as we get into conversations about artificial intelligence, something I know you're concerned about, I think this is going to look quaint.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it might be.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean this is going to look like, wow, what - what were you worried about?\par \par \uc2 I mean the fact is American companies worry me just as much with daughters about things like, you know -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Jealousy, envy, negative information, division, hate, conspiracy theories. We haven't - the - the - Washington, to your point, has not started to regulate our own social media companies. And AI, we have no idea what to do about that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very quickly want to get your comments, since we had the president actually have a press conference this morning, took questions.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes. I think the media pressure about how he just doesn't seem to want to talk to reports, how his staff seems to kind of shield him from that, contributed to the decision to have a press conference in Japan today. And I hope there will be more. Even if he stumbles, at least he is getting into the news cycle and getting his message out even if he trips over his words occasionally.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Peter Doocy, they said, press conference over. Peter Doocy said, not over, stood up, got some of his questions. Like, the president is always willing to engage with him.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, all right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a quick note about this week's podcast guest. I'll give you a hint. He's a "New York Times" bestselling author whose spy novels feel like they're ripped straight from the headlines.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just a quick note, my podcast, "Livin' the Bream," drops this morning fresh. This week we've got Joel Rosenberg, a best-selling author about his brand-new book "The Libyan Diversion," and why he prays. The plot of this book, which, by the way, involves our southern border and some terrorists, never comes true.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com or wherever you like to get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230521ej5l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Special Edition of "Hannity": Drama in the Democratic Party\b0\par\par\uc2 Jason Chaffetz, Gregg Jarrett, Sara Carter, Chad Pergram, Miranda  Devine, Marc Siegel, Janette Nesheiwat, Leo Terrell \par \uc2 8,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Welcome to the special edition of "Hannity." I'm Jason Chaffetz, in for Sean.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, trust in our justice system has been shattered. This week, the Durham report revealed serious wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 According to Durham's findings, federal officials engaged in political targeting. They shielded Democrats from prosecution. They deceived a FISA court, and they worked with the Clinton campaign to perpetuate the Russia hoax.\par \par \uc2 "The Daily Mail" is reporting that in 2016, just as the bureau ramped up Crossfire Hurricane, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  shut down four separate criminal investigations into Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2 . In response, \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2  laughably claimed there has never been any evidence of wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Not only did the FBI and Department of Justice abuse their power and lie to the public, they seem to be proud of it. There were no apologies after the Durham report, no resignations, no admission of wrongdoing whatsoever. As I pointed out in an op-ed, I wrote for FOXnews.com, the Justice Department cannot and will not police itself.\par \par \uc2 In the latest example, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is still refusing to turn over an unclassified record alleging a criminal bribery scheme against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , despite a legally binding subpoena from Congress. Today, Chairman Comer once again demanded access to this subpoenaed material, but Director Wray is not budging.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this isn't just about Biden and Trump and Hillary. A new report details the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's shocking abuses against American citizens with a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " tweeting out, quote: A newly unsealed court document shows the \uc2 FBI\uc2  wrongly searched a vast digital database for information about U.S. citizens and others more than 278,000 times in 2020 and early 2021.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, the author of "Trial of the Century," FOX News legal analyst Gregg Jarrett, former Florida Attorney General Pam Bondi, and FOX News contributor Sara Carter.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all three for joining us tonight on this beautiful Friday.\par \par \uc2 Gregg, I want to go to you because you know part of the problem here is that there is such distrust by a major if not majority part of America, in not only the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , but the Department of Justice, and that's not healthy for a country.\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, I agree 100 percent and I read your column with fascination. I read it twice. Your thesis is 100 percent correct. I mean, who in the right mind trusts the government anymore, trust is earned.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the DOJ, prosecutors they've squandered that trust, Jason, and America's note, they're smart, they pay attention, they know what's going on. They watched it happen, and you know, they've witnessed the Russia hoax, for example, crooked characters at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  targeting Trump with bogus evidence, the same \uc2 FBI\uc2  censored the laptop story to help Biden get elected, 51 ex-intel liars falsely claimed the laptop was Russian disinformation, it wasn't.\par \par \uc2 And the Justice Department is clearly in my judgment running a protection racket for the Bidens in their influence peddling scandals, two dozen whistleblowers report political bias, favoritism, abuse of power and retribution against anybody who dares to tell the truth the government has been corrupted Jason by corrupt people and it moves in only one direction, and that's in favor of Democrats and against Republicans especially a guy by the name of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, it truly is amazing.\par \par \uc2 Now, Pam Bondi, you've been an attorney general of a large state, you've had to deal with whistleblowers along the way. But the story that they told this week and we're going to hear from two of them shortly again in their own words, but I got to tell you, it is not paint a pretty picture of what's happening at the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (R), FORMER FLORIDA ATTORNEY GENERAL: No, Jason, it doesn't and also echoing what Gregg said. Your op-ed was amazing. Everyone needs to read that on FOX.com and the line which I think should scare all Americans that you said to quote you -- not only did they abuse their power and lie to the public, but they also seemed proud of it. And that should frighten all Americans.\par \par \uc2 This is the ultimate weaponization and now we're learning that not only did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have four, not one, not two, not three, four investigations against the Clintons for alleged dealings with \uc2 the Clinton Foundation\uc2  once again, and President Trump's been warning us about this the entire time. And now, we know about that, the alleged dealings with Russian government and Uranium One, and look at the world in which we're living now, how frightening is that.\par \par \uc2 But we know that McCabe allegedly said shut it down. The Justice Department, hostile, Comey cease and desist, on these investigations. That's what we're being told. And if all that is accurate, it should frighten all Americans.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, you have the inspector general issued two reports more than a thousand pages of material, including criminal and disciplinary referrals to the Department of Justice. But no, they don't -- they don't pursue those and that's what's scary about it is they're so cavalier, they're given these awesome investigative police powers to do it nobody else in the country can do and then they abuse it.\par \par \uc2 But then, Sara, all of a sudden, we do get these whistleblowers who are telling us not only are there problems directly with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but this report that's out today is really pretty scary. I read a great article about this in "The Wall Street Journal," but tell us about that because they to abuse their police power, more than 278,000 times, and even "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting that, should scare all Americans no matter your political persuasion.\par \par \uc2 SARA CARTER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you're absolutely right. I spoke with the source before coming on here, somebody that has retired from the intelligence community, and they reminded me of something very important. This isn't just the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . This is across our entire intelligence agencies.\par \par \uc2 We've seen it with the NSA. In 2017, John Solomon and I broke the very first story on the illegal spying that was taking place under the Obama administration. In fact, the Obama administration had to admit to the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court that it had expanded its powers and was collecting upstream data on Americans which is kind of the backbone of the internet.\par \par \uc2 That's emails, all text messages, phone calls and just sweeping them in wholeheartedly. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing this as well, Jason, and what they're doing, they're doing it through the FISA 702, and through the 702 database which has extraordinary extensions. I mean, they're not just collecting on foreigners, and I want everybody to remember this they're unmasking Americans and collecting huge amounts of data.\par \par \uc2 And what is more powerful than money? It's information. And we're supposed to be entrusting our lives, our constitutional rights, our Fourth Amendment, you know, to the \uc2 United States government\uc2  that they're going to uphold these bureaucracies that and now they're not doing it so the only thing that our government can do now to really show that they mean business because obviously the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Department of Justice don't care it's for Congress to ensure that they defund some of these agencies and hurt them and hurt them so that they understand what's going on here because believe me, this collection of data is not going to really stop a terrorist attack.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: If we have a target and we know what the target is, we can collect that data that's still available with a warrant, but they need a warrant to do it because it's unconstitutional if they don't have it.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFTEZ: Yeah, look, when I was in Congress as chairman of the Oversight Committee, we held hearings on this very topic. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , despite the law, was collecting facial recognition for more than half of Americans, unbeknownst to those Americans. And it's these violations of the Fourth Amendment, and in this particular case, the report that was reviewed today wasn't just about the January 6th people, but it was also about people related to George Floyd. So, again, on both sides of the aisle, FISA is up for reform.\par \par \uc2 Gregg, in very short order, I want to kind of go down the line how do we fix the Department of Justice and specifically the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, you made several excellent suggestions. First of all, strengthen the inspector generals, especially the one at the Department of Justice that doesn't even allow the IG to go after you know crooked lawyers who do bad things, defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that'll get their attention, and you know, there needs to be an independent way for Congress to actually enforce their subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's just a -- you know, meaningless scrap of paper if it's issued by a Republican committee the department of justice more than happy to enforce a subpoena if it's a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has weaponized the DOJ to target conservatives. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  does his dirty work. It's so broken, so rotten.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well --\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: And it's so deeply embedded that the bureau needs to be dismantled and rebuilt with honest people at the helm.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, Pam, real quickly, my former colleague \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  used to say, hey, listen your subpoena is only good as your ability to enforce it, and guess what, you can't enforce a subpoena on the Department of Justice is playing out right now with Chairman Comer because you have to go to the DOJ to enforce it.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Right, right. And the inspector general to clarify for folks, they can only investigate. They can write a report but it's up to justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Who's going to police the police? Who's going to police the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 And you know, when I was in office, it's a tremendous amount of responsibility, but these people are taking it as a tremendous amount of power and to use it for corrupt purposes and so the IG needs more power, more responsibility to try to fix this.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Because right now, it's being used as a political bludgeoning tool.\par \par \uc2 Greg, Pam, Sara, thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We want to talk now to some of those whistleblowers because yesterday three \uc2 FBI\uc2  whistleblowers testified before Congress, and their allegations were damning. Here is former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent Steve Friend. Have a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE FRIEND, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: In August 2022, I made protected whistleblower disclosures to my immediate supervisor, assistant special agents in charge, and special agent in charge about my concerns regarding January 6 investigations assigned to my office. I believed our departures from case management rules established in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's domestic investigations and operations guide could have undermined potentially righteous prosecutions and may have been part of an effort to inflate the \uc2 FBI\uc2  statistics on domestic extremism.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  weaponized the security clearance processes to facilitate my removal from active duty within one month of my disclosures. In addition to an indefinite, unpaid suspension, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  initiated a campaign of humiliation and intimidation to punish and pressure meter resign, in violation of HIPAA, individuals at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  leaked my private medical information to a reporter at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 I pray that all members consider the information I and my fellow whistleblowers present. You may think I'm a political partisan. You may think I am a grifter. You may think I'm a conspiracy theorist.\par \par \uc2 It does not matter. Simply put, this committee should avoid the temptation to impugn the character and the motivations of the messengers seated before you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, joining us now with more of the former -- the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  whistleblower you just heard from, senior fellow at the Center for Renewing America, Steve Friend.\par \par \uc2 Steve, thank you for your service, and thank you for having the guts to step forward and testify before Congress. Having been one that asked the questions, I got to tell you, it's never fun for somebody to come testify and I'm sure they're probably coming after you trying to impugn your character despite your plea.\par \par \uc2 But tell us again from your firsthand experience how political the Department of Justice has become as opposed to that a body that's supposed to you know have a blindfold on it and treat everybody the same.\par \par \uc2 FRIEND: I think it's very apparent that the way they're selecting certain prosecutions and we've seen examples of that with Mark Hawk in Pennsylvania and Philadelphia, and that was an individual standing outside of an abortion clinic and selectively enforcing the FACE Act. I think with the latest revelation about the radical traditional Catholic memo that was generated from the Richmond field office, where they were essentially choosing certain viewpoints that are traditional marriage, pro-life, pro- border sovereignty. Those are all viewpoints that are deemed to be problematic and they're we're essentially wrapping them in the label of potential anti-government extremism that is just code for MAGA Republican.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Now, the -- to hear it from the administration, hear the president himself, he'll tell you that white supremacy is the biggest terrorist threat to the United States of America. I mean, being there in the field as an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent, is that true from your -- your vantage point?\par \par \uc2 FRIEND: It certainly wasn't the case for my vantage point, but I think that the callus has maybe grown a little bit thick on the skin from people just being called and labeled racist if they tend to vote conservative, and they're trying to come up with this new and novel term that essentially categorize them as something that's problematic.\par \par \uc2 And that's why you see two of the top four priorities with the national security branch for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are anti-government extremism and racial ethnic extremism, which is parenthetically white nationalism.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the president's concerned, if you go back to his speech at Independence Hall on September 1st of last year, those are the two qualities that he tried to paint the broad rush of anybody who not just supports former President Trump but might even vote just generally Republican in the election.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, my concern is that terrorism, foreign terrorism, bioweapons, other types of things that may truly have feels like we've taken our eye off the ball, and that's the concern.\par \par \uc2 Real quickly, Steve, what's the number one thing we can do to help reform the Department of Justice and specifically the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FRIEND: It's not about defunding federal law enforcement. It's about empowering local law enforcement. We should be looking to deputize local detectives from sheriff's offices and police offices, and those are the folks that know what's going on in main street and they're not going to be so responsive to what the demands are from K Street, when they -- when they are trying to address these crimes and bring federal charges forward.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Now, well said. Steve Friend, I wish the best to you, and again, thank you for your service and thank you for joining us tonight on "Hannity." We do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. All right, joining us now with more reaction, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent Nicole Parker.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 You know, last year, there was sort of a chilling effect, wasn't there, when a -- I remember the Attorney General Garland put out a memo kind of putting everybody in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on unnoticed. What did you take when you saw that memo that he put out?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PARKER, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Jason, we all remember this. I -- was in August of 2022, and we received several emails in one day, which was, you know, kind of different and we all remember that there was one particular email that discussed reaching out to Congress, and you know, the restrictions and the, you know, directions on \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees reaching out directly to Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of us were very confused by it. We were wondering why are we receiving this, but you didn't even want to ask, you didn't even want to go to the legal department to ask what it meant because then that might imply that maybe you had a question and then it put a target on your back. But it was very obvious that a lot of employees were very disturbed by that and it kind of gave a chilling effect. Do not speak up and although they say that's not what that was for, that's how many people felt.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, right, yeah. I got to tell you, it's somebody who's in Congress and involved in oversight, I got to tell you, it was outrageous that they did that because I think it was there my personal opinion, the one -- number one thing was supposed to do is intimidate people from who are thinking about coming forward saying don't you dare you're going to hate it. It's going to ruin your career.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, now, that -- look, I used to think that it was just a couple of people at the top of the food chain that were the problem, but it seems more pervasive than that. What's your perspective? How pervasive is this politicalization of the Department of Justice and specifically the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: You know, I've always believed that it does start at the top in Washington, D.C. It starts in the top and it starts with executive management or those on the seventh floor and it kind of trickles down. And I think that -- the thing with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is that it used to be more of a criminal agency and from, you know, the difference post-9/11, it turned in kind of more of an intel agency and there's a big drive for intelligence, and that seems to be the side of the house that tends to have a lot of the issues and concerns.\par \par \uc2 I think it would be best if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just got back to fighting crime. There are so many amazing agents that are fighting crime. You know, we celebrate national police week this week. My very dear friend special agent Laura Schwarzenberger (ph) was killed in the line of duty protecting children from a child predator.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: That is what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should be doing. While she's doing that, in the meantime, there's people up in D.C. that are politicizing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and it's just completely disrespectful to her and tarnishes her legacy. It's not okay.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah. I mean, yeah, Peter Strzok is in charge of counterintelligence for the United States. He sends ten thousand, ten thousand text messages to somebody he's evidently having an affair with, he's obviously not focused on, you know, counterterrorism maybe like the Chinese and the Russians what