EWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Jason, I don't think it's a function of age so much, even though \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  is 89, you know, and Senator Fetterman is in his 50s, but has had a severe stroke. He was hospitalized for depression for many weeks, you know, and as you say, people seem to be writing his lines.\par \par \uc2 I think it's more of an assessment of fitness which I've been saying for a long, long time. But, you know, as you know, having been in Congress, they're atrocious when it comes to this, Congress has never or the Senate has never removed anyone. Carl Month (ph) in 1969, had a stroke and didn't serve for three years and they left them in there, and Carter Glass (ph) in the 1940s, a Democratic senator from Virginia was there for two years didn't show up.\par \par \uc2 They don't have a mechanism to do this, but they need one desperately. And I think an assessment of fitness would be more mental acuity and cognitive testing than physical because you know as Dr. Nesheiwat just said, I mean, Dr. Feinstein had encephalitis which means -- which interferes with thinking. And prior to that, she had cognitive changes that were clear to everyone and now she shows up and says, I never left.\par \par \uc2 You actually wonder if she's serious about that. I mean, it's sad but somebody should be assessing that and this -- because we have -- we have serious business for the United States here going on in the Congress as you know better than anyone that has to be voted on very carefully here, the debt ceiling, the war in Ukraine, the border, judges, we can't have people there that are vacant.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, I was actually working on a bill before I left Congress that would have anybody who was running for president have to go through a series of cognitive and physical tests. If you're going to have your finger on the nuclear bomb, we should know from an independent person like a Dr. Moynihan to go through and do that assessment and provide for the American people. You're going to have to open up your kimono and show us everything.\par \par \uc2 And I also think you should have to show your tax returns and I also think you should have to show your medical records, not from somebody else, but by somebody inside the United States military who will be objective in doing it, but another discussion for another night.\par \par \uc2 Dr, Nesheiwat, Dr. Siegel, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 All right. More Republicans joined the race for 2024. We'll tell you who is jumping in and what the latest polls are showing.\par \par \uc2 Plus, don't forget to get your free tickets to see "Hannity" next week. Sean, we'll be back with a live studio audience show next Tuesday, with special guest Judge Jeanine and Senator Ted Cruz. Don't want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Welcome back to the special edition of "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 GOP primary field continues to grow. South Carolina Senator Tim Scott has filed with the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  to run for president. This as Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  long awaited campaign announcement is expected to come next week.\par \par \uc2 But despite the crowded primary race, a new poll still shows Trump with a commanding lead. And DeSantis, 43 points behind. Another poll out today asked voters about potential 2024 matchups and showed Trump with a 7-point lead over Biden.\par \par \uc2 And when voters were asked about a DeSantis-Biden matchup, the two tied at 42 percent.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, pollsters \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , along with FOX News contributor Leo Terrell.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Mark, I want to start with you. I mean at some point and it's early -- granted it's early -- at some point, though, can't you get just get so far out ahead that you can kind of just walk it home?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , THE HARRIS POLL CHAIRMAN: Well, primaries are bedeviling things. So we've seen a lot of instances where the front runners no matter how far out now can fall to the challenger candidates who aren't really well known, who haven't really engaged.\par \par \uc2 No question, Trump has strengthened as he took on both CNN, you know, here, and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , I think those who have strengthened him. But this primary is not over. It's just beginning.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Lee, how do you see the race right now happening today? I saw the rise in Trump really coming when he went and visited East Palestine and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't. He showed leadership. It reminded everybody that the president was a leader and he showed compassion and it plays well when he does that.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: Yeah, I think there's a few things that came together all at the same time. One, I think that visit was very, very well -- well done. I think it was a time where people needed to hear from leadership.\par \par \uc2 And then you remembered what he was all about. You know, he went out and he visited. He had McDonald's I think if I remember correctly when he was out the way and people were starting to look at him differently again.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that happened is the indictment and you would think the indictment would have worked against him but what it did is it rallied a lot of people to say they treat President Trump unfairly. And so they gave him another chance.\par \par \uc2 So all of these things sort of boosted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the same time as I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  made some really big strategic mistakes instead of running on the Florida blueprint. And all that he had done in Florida he started really taking on \uc2 Disney\uc2  and a lot of these woke wars and I don't think that served him well.\par \par \uc2 So I think Trump's lead is secure at the moment, but let's not forget, on June 15th in 2015, there was an article in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " that said here's the five reasons why Jeb Bush is going to be the next president of the United States, and he didn't even get anywhere close to it. So anything and everything can happen. We're still a long way away and we know that Trump is unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, that's a pretty safe bet, that he's eating McDonald's and that he's unpredictable. I think that's really going on on the field so far, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Leo, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is showing why people do like him and the lead is massive. But, you know, at the end, there's the primary and getting the nomination, but then there's also the general election. So what do you think those issues and how are they -- how are they voters of the Republican Party going to look at the two?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I'll tell you right now, first of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Jeb Bush, not even close. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jason resonates with the American people most of these candidates are inside the Beltway, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a guy who can rally a group, 40,000, 50,000 people on the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accomplished more, Jason, in four years than some President Biden, Obama of -- in eight years. I mean, this guy resonates with the American people. They believe he's real.\par \par \uc2 And that's -- he's the first Republican I ever voted for. Why? Because he puts country first and I would submit to you, I'm looking at these -- these candidates who are running against him, and I would love to ask the pollsters, where can they beat Trump in their own state?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Tim Scott running, he's going to beat Trump in South Carolina. Save this tape, I like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's chances in South Carolina. I like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  chances in New Hampshire. Where can Trump be beaten in some of these states where there's homegrown candidates? I don't see it.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Mark, good question. What's the answer to that?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, I think we're going to have to see what happens in Iowa, in New Hampshire and South Carolina. Look, these states are going to shape whether or not these other candidates can get off the ground.\par \par \uc2 Look, Tim Scott is really not known much outside of South Carolina. He has a lot of potential. I think he will play well. DeSantis obviously has had trouble, but he's got to reset his campaign.\par \par \uc2 And Trump is riding high but he hasn't really been challenged by another Republican in the ring. So as I said before, clear lead, but you know, Trump has a lot of weaknesses. Can he really win? He can win a Republican primary, but he's been losing and the Republican Party has been losing since they have been with Trump and they want to be winners again.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're going to have a real primary here, a real match, a real slug fests so to speak, and we'll see who wins. I don't think it's as predictable as it seems.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah. Lee, it's one thing to get the nomination but to win the general election, I mean, the case that DeSantis is making is, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes with drama and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to have a hard time with independence and people in the Democratic Party and the numbers for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are so weak within the Democratic Party, there's a huge opening to go grab those people but they're going to have to show them that there's a reason to believe in them.\par \par \uc2 So how do they play that when they got to go to the right in order to win the nomination?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think it's going to be really tough and I don't know that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has an answer that's going to be satisfactory to the people that are going to make the decision. I mean, right now, the biggest problem the Republican Party has is really with women.\par \par \uc2 Seven out of 10 independents say that one of the primary reasons they're going to go and vote is for women's rights and abortion. Nine out of 10 Democrats say that's what's going to drive them to the vote, even though the economy is the number one issue that impacts their daily lives.\par \par \uc2 And so, if Republicans don't come up with an answer that puts women in the center of the conversation, they're going to have a big issue because as we know, this election is going to come down to just a few -- it's -- we're neck and neck. We're a country divided, 48-48, it's going to come down just a few points here.\par \par \uc2 And so, Republicans are going to have a tough, tough fight to slog it out.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, it is going to be a fascinating race. I appreciate all three of you joining us here tonight.\par \par \uc2 More of this special edition of Hannity coming up, right after the break. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Welcome back to this special edition of "Hannity." Thanks to Sean for letting me sit in his seat, but before we go, quick programming note, next Tuesday and Wednesday, live "Hannity" audience shows.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ted Cruz and Judge Jeanine Pirro will join Sean live on set on Tuesday. Tickets are the right price, they're free. If you want to be part of the audience, go to hannity.com to get your tickets.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us, again. Thanks to Sean. Have a great wonderful weekend.\par \par \uc2 "The Ingraham Angle" is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230520ej5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 GOP Must Adopt More Aggressive Early Vote Strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Gregg Jarrett, Emily Compagno \par \uc2 8,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Will, let me get straight, so it's either wood or bugs.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS HOST: What do you think, Sean? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I pick -- I'd rather eat you, I'd starve to death before I ate either one.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Will Cain, thank you. Great show.\par \par \uc2 And welcome to "Hannity".\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are barreling toward the all-important presidential election. Now, pay attention pay attention. We are only 539 days away from November 5th, 2024. This race is beyond critical, a great constitutional republic is slipping away. The Durham report confirmed our biggest fears that our U.S. justice system is completely broken.\par \par \uc2 We know the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is politicized. The DOJ has been weaponized. Both organizations, they work hand in glove with the Democratic Party to achieve one common goal, and that is keeping conservatives out of power.\par \par \uc2 People ask me all the time, Hannity, what are conservatives -- what are Republicans supposed to do? Long story short, they have one option and one option only, they have to win elections.\par \par \uc2 Now it is nearly impossible to make any sweeping changes when both the Senate and the White House are controlled by Democrats. So how did the Democratic Party get so much power? They don't exactly nominate stellar candidates.\par \par \uc2 Let's be honest here. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is kind of essentially of -- well, a shadow of his former self, I'll be charitable. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is one of the least likable people on the planet earth. She shows next to zero leadership qualities.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, and it is sad, the newest member of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is frankly just mentally not fit to serve. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Is it a staggering -- it's a responsibility that the head of a bank could literally -- could literally crash our economy. It's astonishing. That's like if you have -- I mean, like -- and they also realize is that now, they have -- it's in -- the guaranteed -- a guaranteed way to be saved by -- again by no matter -- by how.\par \par \uc2 Should we just go on and start bailing and sailing whoever bank regardless of how however -- there's -- their conduct is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , he is a member of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  for a few reasons. One because Democrats during elections, they focus on the process of elections above all else. They're not out on the campaign trail. They're not kissing babies, shaking hands, taking selfies, making press avails, giving speeches, doing town halls. No.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , they did none of that. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  didn't even show up for one single debate, not one. Instead, these Democrats, they focused on hundreds of millions of dollars in negative ads. I like to know where they get all their money. They focused on early voting campaigns, mail-in voting efforts and many states unbeknownst to many of you have legal ballot harvesting. They've focused on that.\par \par \uc2 The practice of legal ballot harvesting enables a third party to collect and turn in sealed paper ballots on behalf of another voter. Twenty-four states in Washington, D.C. now permit this practice, 11 other states don't have any laws on the books regarding legal ballot harvesting and naturally, Democrats, they're taking full advantage.\par \par \uc2 They don't trust that voters will turn out on election day rain or shine. So they forced the issue and they bank as many votes as possible and they do it way ahead of time. For example, let's look at the commonwealth of Pennsylvania. Over a half a million Democrats had already voted before election day giving Fetterman a commanding early lead and R -- and by the way, the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , they should learn, they should adopt, they should organize, they should even outperform these efforts of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 They should have aggressive early voting campaigns, aggressive male voting campaigns or Republicans frankly are screwed. They're just going to lose. And we -- they're at a decided disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 You know, you cannot start out election day in a raise behind by hundreds of thousands of votes and then expect to make those votes up on election day. What if rains that day? What if it snows that day? What if it's cold that day?\par \par \uc2 Now, many states, Republicans have been reluctant. They have been resisting to vote early and vote by mail. Now, for the people in the great state of Florida, it works out great for them. Why?\par \par \uc2 Because Republicans in Florida have embraced both early voting and voting by mail and by the way, now, Republicans around the country need to adopt the same measures if they want to dramatically increase their chances to win. Now to another reason that Democrats have been able to consolidate power and that has to do with the controversial politics. I'm not talking about morality -- the politics of abortion.\par \par \uc2 I happen to be pro-life. Many of our viewers watching tonight are also pro- life, but many are not. And when a Republican candidate in a swing state like Pennsylvania makes banning abortion a central focus of their campaign without any exceptions, for example, for rape, incest or the life of the mother -- well, that Republican politically speaking is probably going to lose badly and drag the entire party down with them.\par \par \uc2 For example, look at the last election, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , he was a nice guy. I interviewed him. He had an energetic following, but most voters believe that his anti-abortion stance was too extreme. He lost by over 15 points, no exceptions for rape, incest or the mother's life. His Democratic opponent Josh Shapiro received a record number of votes for a non- incumbent. And not only did Republicans lose the governor's race, they ended up also losing what was a winnable Senate race with Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile in Michigan, the topic of abortion was on the ballot. Republicans got wiped out and the same recently with the Wisconsin Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Now it may sound simple but it is true, the GOP has got to understand the electorate in every single state and nationwide if they ever want to retake power, probably the first trimester, I don't know, maybe 15 weeks where Mississippi's law is (AUDIO GAP) country politically is on the issue of abortion, although each state will make that decision on their own.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, I am stating the political reality if Republicans want to win elections. So the question tonight with our country slipping away, will Republicans learn from their election mistakes or are they doomed to repeat them?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with reaction, former White House chief of staff Reince Priebus. He is also the former head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , along with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ronna, we've talked about this a lot, tell me if you disagree with anything I just said.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , \uc2 RNC\uc2  CHAIRWOMAN: Well, Sean, I agree with everything you said. And listen, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  has been doing this. Florida didn't just get that way overnight, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  invested in North Carolina, Iowa, Ohio, I think Pennsylvania is a state where culturally, Republicans at the top of the ticket said don't vote by mail, Mastriano said it. We have to encourage voters to bank their vote. We need to do that.\par \par \uc2 And it's two parts. We have to show them that we're securing their vote. We had 80,000 poll watchers and poll workers recruited by the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2022. So they need to know that we're watching the process, and then you've got to bank your vote early so that you can get out and whole watch on election day.\par \par \uc2 And this is going to be an initiative we're going to launch. We've been doing it. We're doing ballot harvesting. We turned out four million more voters.\par \par \uc2 I think the abortion issue is a huge issue that you just raised and we can talk about that more, but we have to invest early. Democrats -- let me just give you two --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But, Ronna, let me interrupt you if I may.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The Democrats are killing the Republicans with early in voting -- early voting and ballot harvesting, right, they are killing them. That's why on Election Day --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Not true. California, we just own with Duarte and Zinke --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: -- in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But why do Republicans -- why are they starting out these races behind by a hundred thousand, two hundred fifty thousand, five hundred thousand votes before election day even starts. Why are they so far behind?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: But let me say this there's two numbers that you also have to point out, Democrats spent $600 million more in soft money in 2022, and the Senate candidates, the six top Senate races, Democrats spent 288 more.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Because otherwise, Republicans won't compete.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: The \uc2 RNC\uc2  can't raise, take a million dollar check. That's all outside groups, and here's what I'm going to say -- Republicans wait until election year and then they invest and you have to invest now in the process. You cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 Text WIN to 280810. You cannot wait. We need to be getting people on the permanent absentee voter file in Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. It takes money. You can't wait until the candidates are picked, you have to invest in the process and we need to change the culture in our party.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, by saying that, though, are you acknowledging that it's not happening now?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: No, I'm not acknowledging that. We are investing in all these states, minority outreach.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Investing but you're behind the eight ball, that's my point.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: No, we are not behind the eight ball. I'm saying we need more money, we need resources, of course, we do, and I will say the Democrats are out raising us a hundred percent because they invest in process.\par \par \uc2 And I talk to voters and donors all the time. They say I want to see who the candidate is. I'm going to wait to see who the nominee is. You have to invest in both, the process and the nominee to get it done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, and you can't pick a candidate that doesn't make exceptions for rape, incest or the mother's life --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- in a state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Reince, I'll let you be the arbiter here. I see the Republicans being outplayed, outsmarted by Democrats. They hide in their basement, they run negative ads, they don't do any traditional campaigning. They don't run on better ideas. They don't have better ideas, and they're involved in ballot harvesting, and their efforts are far superior to the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Am I right or is Ronna right?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I think you're both right, how about that? I think that Ronna and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , they've never lost to the DNC head-to-head on fundraising. What we've got, Sean, and we try to clarify this for everybody, we have got a soft money hole on the GOP side that has to do with super rich major donors that are invested in something very different than the Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  said, to find every wig and bring them to the polls. What does that mean? It means, in today's language, it means you need to spend a ton of money on data, a ton of money on targeting, a ton of money with thousands of people on the doors across the country and then it means the delivery of ballots, which is early vote, absentee ballot voting, election day voting.\par \par \uc2 And what we've got getting back to the point that I think you and Ronna are actually both making is that we had people who invest hundreds of millions of dollars who are what I call candidate crazy. Our party's candidate crazy. We love candidates, we want to go to dinner with them, we go to the rallies, we go to their events.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats and their super rich soft money donors, they're process crazy. They're invested and excited about things like voter registration. Why is it that when you go to the DMV you see a group called the League of Conservations Voters clipboard, soft money, and they're there.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're making point.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: And they're there.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: This is the point. If Republicans are not matching the efforts of early voting, mail-in voting and ballot harvesting, then they are destined to start out election day in a big huge hole and the odds of them losing are that much greater.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: And so, they're -- okay, and so there needs to be two things that happen. Segments like tonight where we educate our voters to end this stupid mentality that you need to wait for election day which is what Ronna is doing.\par \par \uc2 And the second thing that we have to do is convince our uber donors that instead of giving $50 million to a candidate in a primary, take that $50 million, invest in boring stuff like mechanics, like voter education, voter registration, teams on the ground that are going to collect these ballots. These are --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are you acknowledging Republicans are behind? I'm just facing a reality. Democrats are not starting out -- they're not starting election day down 250,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: But you're comparing apples to oranges. Sean, I can't take a million dollar check at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . I can't take a $10 million check. They are doing it through soft money groups. I can't even engage with soft money groups. It's illegal.\par \par \uc2 So they're doing it all through Arabella which took one --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: There has to be somebody that takes the ball and runs with this shuttle here or else the Democrats, they'll be spiking when they cross the plane in the end zone.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: You're asking -- you're comparing the \uc2 RNC\uc2  to these outside groups, and that's not the right comparison. We absolutely need more outside groups. I encourage people to get involved and do that. The \uc2 RNC\uc2  is limited on what we can raise and do and we are still -- we still turn out four million voters. We still beat them in ballot harvesting.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Reince, you have 20 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: And we still won in every battleground state.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Sean -- here's where everyone is correct. Number one, on the hard money side, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  always beats the DNC, but what we do need is about two to three hundred million dollars in a bucket with people running an outside group that focuses on all this boring stuff that we know wins close elections. If we're within a field goal, you have to be able to go toe-to- toe on the offensive and defensive line to win the race.\par \par \uc2 That bucket needs to get filled in a third-party effort across the country. It's not just a few people here and there and a bunch of happy talk. It's hundreds of millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have 539 days. This has got to be fixed by the end of this year, my view, so Republicans are ready to employ these new tactics. Vote early, vote by mail and the match and surpass hopefully the ballot harvesting efforts of the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Reince, Ronna, thank you. We need to continue this discussion. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tonight, let's hope Republicans can find a way to win elections and then have the courage to really take on the swamp because your government is broken pure and simple. Democrats have their thumbs on the scale of our system of justice and our elections. Now, the Durham report confirmed what we have known for quite some time, there was collusion. As a matter of fact, the Clinton campaign, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the foreign nationals from Russia, the U.K. all colluded to influence the 2016 election and keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out of office.\par \par \uc2 But it's much ado about nothing according to America's most trusted bureaucrats. Listen to them. You would think there was nothing reported yesterday in the Durham report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What you have with \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is like it's a big fat nothing.\par \par \uc2 PETER STRZOK, FORMER FBI AGENT: Look, this is a predictable sad ending to an investigation that never should have taken place. They spent the next three to four years with a cognitive bias trying to build a case that somehow 