es with images of Folds of Honors Program, a great program, by the way, which provides scholarships to families of fallen soldiers.\par \par \uc2 In a statement provided to our producers, \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2  touted its century-long commitment to supporting the military and its relationship with the Folds of Honor.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, "Outnumbered" co-host Emily Compagno, former Michigan gubernatorial candidate Tudor Dixon.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here's -- I'm not sure why beer companies are even involving themselves in any social issue or any politics, Emily, makes no sense to me.\par \par \uc2 All right. But we have Memorial Day weekend coming up. We have the Fourth of July weekend coming up. We have summertime coming up.\par \par \uc2 Is it really unusual for beer commercials to be at the beach and people in bathing suits, having a good time, drinking a cold beer and a hot day? Somehow is that sexist? Do they need to apologize for this or is this just you know the woke mob putting pressure on yet another company?\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, CO-HOST OF "OUTNUMBERED": Right, it's the woke mob putting pressure on yet another company with a few decision makers that are capitulating to that. Now what the problem I have with that revitalized and revised Miller Lite commercial is that it looks at things as an and/or. She's essentially saying that the women brewers have absolutely nothing to do with girls in bikinis or that kind of marketing.\par \par \uc2 Sean, you know that my family has a small family-run winery. My father is a veteran. He owns and operates it. I dig in the dirt, I get in there and I do agricultural work.\par \par \uc2 You also know that I was an \uc2 NFL\uc2  cheerleader and as a Raiderette, we were sponsored by Coors Light. So in our two-piece uniforms, we were the spokespeople, we were on those ads for Coors Light. You can be both.\par \par \uc2 Women can love bikini ads and they can also brew their own beer, but yet again, this is sort of creating this binary false choice to consumers and saying, oh, to be a woman, you must not love bikinis, you must not love high heels and they are clearly shooting themselves in the foot by not know knowing their beer drinking, women loving, bikini wearing audience.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't think I could say it any better. Tudor, you want to try and out do that?\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R), FORMER MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I just love how Miller Lite comes out with this and she takes a picture of the girls in bikinis and throws it down and cusses and then Bud Light's like, you know what, they've got a point. Let's put a guy in a bikini because everybody's going to love that. We'll advertise with a man.\par \par \uc2 I'm like this is ridiculous. So in a poll of your audience, let's see would your audience really prefer to have you in a bikini, Sean, or would they rather have Emily and me in a bikini?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Really, Tudor, you really put me in the position? I think I can speak to my entire audience, no, not me.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's a matter of what is reality. Americans on Memorial Day weekend, they're going to slamming the beaches, they are going to have beer. They're going to be in bathing suits. They're going to be having fun.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that Americans want politics in their beer, in their sports, just stay out of politics, sell your product, and make it better than the next guy and you will do a lot better. Why subject yourself to this? Emily?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Nor did -- yeah, nor did they want a manufactured concept of what it means to be a woman or femininity shoved down their throats. This reminds me of the whitewash that M&M went through when apparently having high heels or wearing high heels was no longer acceptable. Putting sneakers on her.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, it removes at that control and the agency of putting that women -- I love wearing high heels, I love being sexy. I love finding myself feminine, but apparently, according to these commercials and this woke mob, they have stripped that definition and that identity and choice away from me.\par \par \uc2 This Memorial Day, I will celebrating their sacrificing their lives for this country but when I drink beer, I will be drinking by Old Armor, I'll be drinking it by Fav Brinko (ph) and Fighter Corps (ph) and all of those veteran owned and operated companies. I don't need to drink Miller Lite or Bud Light anymore.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And, Tudor, I will not be wearing high heels or a bikini or any of the above.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it just to me -- it gets to the point it's beyond silly and now they open themselves up to boycott campaign which I don't support, that hurts all the workers and the factory. That hurts all the drivers that have their routes.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll give you the last word.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: Absolutely. Well, I like that they saw the "Top Gun: Maverick" did well and they decided to go patriotic. Let's see if we can push more companies in that direction.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, I think smart.\par \par \uc2 All right. Everybody, have a great Memorial Day at the beach, and if you're not comfortable in a bathing suit, then I guess you can wear your sweat pants and a sweatshirt, good luck, not going to help your tan.\par \par \uc2 More "Hannity" straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. And a quick programming note. We have live audience shows tomorrow and Thursday this week. Few in-studio guests: Clay Travis, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Jimmy Failla, Tomi Lahren, Kellyanne Conway, Laura Ingraham will make an appearance.\par \par \uc2 If you want to be part of the show, tickets are free. Go to Hannity.com for all the information.\par \par \uc2 All right. That's all the time we have left this evening. Thank you for being with us. Thank you for making the show possible. Please set your DVR, never miss an episode.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Laura is here.\par \par \uc2 Now, don't tell anybody what day you're coming. You're either coming tomorrow or Thursday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230517ej5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. Byron Donalds (R-FL); Interview with Rep. \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  (D-CA); DeSantis Visits Iowa; DeSantis Holds Multiple Events with Voters in Iowa; Trump Faces Questions in Town Hall; Speculation over Manchin 2024 Run; Biden Family Financial Records; Man Faces Charges in NYC Subway Death; Bear Cubs at the Smithsonian Zoo.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Matt Finn, Alexis McAdams, Karl Rove, Gillian  Turner, Richard Fowler \par \uc2 9,857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The Trump era border restrictions end and that means that President Biden now owns what happens next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Record numbers of migrants flood the southern border now that three years of Title 42 restrictions are over.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas making this claim:\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: I want to be clear: our borders are not open.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Biden administration announces new enforcement plans and a major change to U.S. asylum policy, but both already face legal challenges. We'll have a live report from the Texas border.\par \par \uc2 Then, Democrats and Republicans finally come to the table to talk debt ceiling and tried to prevent economic catastrophe. But as a high-profile meeting with the president is postponed, are they making any progress behind closed doors?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We explicitly asked Speaker McCarthy, would he take default off the table? He refused.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: But I think what we really need is a partner that's willing to work together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This morning, we have two members of the House Oversight Committee, Republican Byron Donalds of Florida and Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California, to discuss the border, the debt ceiling standoff and the latest on the House Republicans' investigation into foreign payments made to members of the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 Then, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  draws crowds in multiple cities in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: It may be that Florida is the Iowa of the southeast.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel whether he can loosen the former president's grip on the polls in this critical state.\par \par \uc2 Plus, this Mother's Day, we head to the National Zoo, where one very proud mom is showing off her little ones.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with developments in a pivotal week along the U.S.- Mexico border. This is the situation you have been seeing on TV last few days, thousands of migrants showing up, hoping to be allowed to stay in this country. Many making the journey of the specific moment because the U.S. just ended a health restriction that had seriously blocked their entry.\par \par \uc2 Some local mayors say the amount numbers were not as high as it feared possible, though they are breaking records. And border cities are facing huge challenges. And the migrants are facing a major shift in border enforcement and in policy.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration announcing sweeping new rules that bar most immigrants from entering for five years if they tried to cross illegally. But the rules curbing asylum are already facing a major legal challenge from the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Florida Republican Byron Donald joins us to discuss his state's lawsuits over the planned release of some migrants without court dates or any plans to even track them.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Matt Finn, who's live in El Paso, with a look at what's really happening to migrants who show up at the border hoping to cross into the U.S. -- Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Inside of this downtown shelter, there are 140 migrant women and children outside as the sun comes up here. These migrants are slowly waking up after sleeping on the street.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): In downtown El Paso, outside of the overwhelmed Sacred Heart shelter, migrants are spread across two city blocks. Sleeping on pavement and sheltering in a dirty alley, before they find the means to move on to their next location, in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Not far from that shelter, is this point of entry at the border wall, where the migrants arrive. They are bused, processed and potentially released.\par \par \uc2 In Brownsville, Fox News cameras captured this video of migrant groups who were released, as they have been in this border town for well over a year now.\par \par \uc2 The White House denies there are mass releases and is now promising to fight a federal judge's ruling that the government cannot release any migrant without issuing them a court date.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, let me just say on the ruling that you just -- you just laid out to me. So, look, the way we see that, it's sabotage. It's pure and simple, that's how that leads to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDETAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And this morning, Shannon, the latest numbers from the city of El Paso, it reports it has now released 4,297 migrants into the streets here, just this past week alone. That is the highest number for any week this calendar year -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Matt Finn reporting from El Paso, Texas -- Matt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Florida Congressman Byron Donalds.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): It's good to be back with you and happy Mother's Day to you, Shannon, and all the mothers out there, including mine.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, to all of those precious women doing the toughest job the world will ever ask of any of us.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so let's start at the border there because there's not anything happening here in Washington as far as moving forward on immigration legislation, other than the House GOP has passed something, probably will die in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Mike McCaul has said: This is our blueprint. If we win back the White House, we have the House and Senate, this is the bill that we will start from.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans have held all of those key posts before and have not fixed the border.\par \par \uc2 How much does the GOP bear blame with what we're seeing now at the border?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, first of all, Shannon, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things. Under the pres -- under the Trump administration, we never saw anything like this. We actually had the border under control, it was secure.\par \par \uc2 We're still trying to figure out some side pieces of immigration policy overall because Democrats would never work with us on commonsense immigration policies, where the Democrats have always wanted is this massive surge to the border with no checks at all, no balances at all.\par \par \uc2 So, the House Republican position is a border security package that would secure the border and it was my position as well, as well as Chairman McCaul's, that's where we'll be moving when we went back to the White House in 2024. And I do stress, when.\par \par \uc2 Now, that being said, what's happening now is because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's recklessness. His desire when he became president just to rip up all the Trump era policies that actually had our border secure. And now, they're scrambling to find a solution to the crisis that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  created.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Democrats will say, yes, it is true, this administration got rid of those policies, but it's because they called them cruel and inhumane. They worked but they weren't kind to the people who are coming here.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say to them? And is there any area where Republicans will compromise when it comes to negotiating over some legislation?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, first of all, the Democrats do this all the time. They don't like a policy, they call it cruel.\par \par \uc2 But what truly is cruel, Shannon, are the young women who are being raped in the journey to our southern border. They're being raped by the coyotes. They are being raped by the drug cartels. They are being raped by other men who were in their package of people that are moving to the border.\par \par \uc2 Number two, also inhumane, the fact we've given operational control to the drug cartels under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's administration. The drug cartels are going to make about $13 billion this year, trafficking people to our southern border and then they use \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reckless policy to traffic massive amounts of fentanyl into our country, killing 75,000 Americans per year. That's inhumane.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to negotiations with the Democrats on this policy, I say to them very clearly, let's secure the border first. Let's get it done. Let's finish building the wall in doing all the other mechanisms that are crucial for our countries security, and when we have that done, then we can talk about all the immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 But the left wants to bring out young kids who came in through no fault of their own, quote/unquote, and use that as a poker chip, as a leverage point to say, we don't need to secure the border. That's insane policy for our country. We can do better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to play something that one of your colleagues said in a hearing this week. He says Republicans actually savor the situation at the border.\par \par \uc2 Here's what Congressman \uc2 Hank Johnson\uc2  said about why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HANK JOHNSON\uc2  (D-GA): Republicans like to see them lined up at the border, because they know that that's a stream of labor ready to be exploited. If you could get back to slavery, you would gladly do so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Your response to your colleague?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I don't have no response to Hank on this. Hank also thought Guam was going to tip over. So, that doesn't make any sense at all.\par \par \uc2 What Republicans want to see is a secure and orderly process at our southern border. You can't have a situation where a group of 30 or 40 migrants just walk up to a border agent, and it's border agent because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Alejandro Mayorkas policy, had to stop their job of actually patrolling the border to start filing paperwork. That's what's happening. And it makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 Again, the Democrats come up with all this hysteria. They bloviate. They -- frankly, they gaslight the American people, instead of actually doing the job of securing the border. And once again, we see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats, they have no plan. And when crisis comes, they are flailing about trying to find a solution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, the other big problem that needs a solution is the debt ceiling. We're told negotiations continue through the weekend. The president's likely we think, to meet with congressional leaders next week if they can make moves on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we got a guest essay in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " by a former Hill staffer, worked on the Senate and House Budget Committees, who says this: Republicans are the ones putting our standard of living at risk with their risky posturing. They're playing Russian roulette with America's primacy in the global financial system, a privileged position undergirding our standard of living and international influence.\par \par \uc2 You understand this better than most. You have professional background in banking. The dominance of the dollar at this point.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about the point he's making that certainly China and Russia are rooting for the dollar to fail, for us to continue to have financial instability and uncertainty here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Listen, I'm very concerned about China and what Russia are doing, but the solution is not just massive borrowing and massive spending. That's no solution. That's putting your head in the sand and is actually going to lead us to a much greater crisis down the road.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I know we just talked about the border but new problem, same issues, and the same issue is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't lead, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't have a plan. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't have a strategy. And what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  brings America to every single time is a crisis point.\par \par \uc2 A hundred days ago, House Republicans started crafting together a solution to bring our spending down to pre-COVID spending levels, and also preparing to raise the nation's debt ceiling by $1.5 trillion. We passed that plan a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Do you know what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats were doing? Nothing. They did nothing, except run to the cameras and say we should just pass a clean debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Any family in any business in our country understands, you cannot just continue to spend massive amounts of money, and borrow massive amounts of money and there will be no consequences. Every American family has already seen the first consequence from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And that is a massive inflation that has been the most insidious tax the American people have seen whether you're rich or poor, whether you're a black or white, whether you're a Republican or a Democrat, it has come for everybody.\par \par \uc2 So, to this Democrat staffer who doesn't have the courage to share their name, I would say, sit down and be fiscally responsible. Don't go and hide behind a reporter throwing out your talking points because you're not being honest and you're not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, on the issue of consequences, on this issue of default, which were told us coming days potentially in days if there's been a deal, former President Trump who you've endorsed had this to say on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I say to the Republicans out there, congressmen, senators, if they don't give you massive cuts, you're going to have to do a default.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The current president says this about default. It would raise millions of jobs, trigger a session, hit retirement accounts and increase borrowing costs. It's not an option.\par \par \uc2 So, is it an option or not? Which president do you agree with?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, look, I would you tell you right now, President Trump is a master negotiator. I don't know what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has ever done to negotiate anything of substance.\par \par \uc2 Let's be very clear, Shannon, you're going to have to curtail spending. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants to spend $7 trillion, that's his budget. We only take in about $4.2 trillion to $4.5 trillion in taxes.\par \par \uc2 So, he wants to massively spend money we don't have. He wants to massively borrow and think that there are no consequences. And like I just said, inflation is a consequence for every American under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's reckless policy.\par \par \uc2 So, the House position is very clear. Let's go to pre-COVID spending levels.\par \par \uc2 Everybody understands why we had to ratchet up spending to respond to the pandemic. We all get that, but the pandemic is now over. It's time to bring spending levels back to pre-COVID, and then we can talk about raising the debt ceiling. And, frankly, not even talk about it, Republicans have passed that plan.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  brings nothing to the table, if all he does is sit there with his hands in his pockets, and not understanding what he has done in a way to actually negotiate, then he's the one leading our nation into default. And it's very sad thing to see.\par \par \uc2 Last thing on this, there's a proposal that's been floating around Congress about payment prioritization. What Treasury can do is pay interest and principal on our debt first. That would actually stave off all this concern of this default. Then we could pay Social Security, Medicare, pay for our military and then go down the line.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats want to continue to run so many of the agencies that they basically put a bunch of their crazy policies into, and then they have to find a way to bring spending with Republicans so we can raise the nation's debt ceiling going forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'll ask Congressman Khanna about that coming.\par \par \uc2 But before we let you go, because you're on House Oversight and part of the role of big documents that show various payments to different Biden family members from foreign accounts -- from foreign actors, you said at that press conference this week, you've said to the press there, this is Pulitzer material. Well, not all of them are buying it.\par \par \uc2 This from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", an opinion piece, says: What does the GOP have on Biden? A whole lot of nothin'. The committee produced no evidence to suggest that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did anything at all to help his son's overseas business partners, and there's no evidence that one penny of any funds \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  might have been paid by foreign companies ever reached his father.\par \par \uc2 The committee (ph) set pretty high expectations on this. Are you worried about overpromising and under-delivering?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, I'm not. Listen, we were able to document nine members of the Biden family who are getting money through Jim Biden and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  from foreign companies, from China and from Romania. We've documented that.\par \par \uc2 Number two, since when are grandchildren getting wire payments and getting cashiers checks? That definitely sounds like a web of corruption to me.\par \par \uc2 Number three, how is \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and Jim Biden facilitating all these business deals? All by themselves?\par \par \uc2 I remember very clearly, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was on Air Force Two a lot when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was vice president. Maybe he was just hanging out getting, eating a club sandwich on Air Force Two? That's not true.\par \par \uc2 And number four, and let's be very clear on this one as well, there's another bank account that's just labeled "Biden". It doesn't go to Jim or Hunter or any of Hunter's wives or girlfriends or any grandchildren. It's just named Biden.\par \par \uc2 And we already have Tony Bobulinski who said that there was always 10 percent for the big guy. I wonder who the big guy is. Since the one guy who's the center of all this controversy, and frankly, corruption, in my view, is the current president, who was the former vice president, who was a United States senator for 45 years.\par \par \uc2 I would tell this opinion writer to actually do some investigation. Don't shut -- don't shut it down because anybody with basic common sense can see that the Biden family, which has no business, mind you, no business whatsoever, was making money from foreign interest because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been at the center of our politics for more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the White House and the president deny any wrongdoing. There are serious allegations and we will follow the committee's investigation.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Donalds, thank you very much for being with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we'll reaction from the other side of the aisle. Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , Democrat, joins us next from a pretty important 2024 state. We're going to ask him why he's there, too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With the end of Title 42, there are renewed calls from some lawmakers to talk bipartisan immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  says his party is willing to compromise on immigration policies but Republicans aren't.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  -- who is notably live in New Hampshire, a key 2024 primary state, this morning.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Thank you, Shannon. Happy Mother's Day to all the moms watching today.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and blessings them.\par \par \uc2 Okay. Let's start with the border. This week marked the highest recorded number of people showing up illegally at the border. By the end of the week, it was 82,000 people recorded so far.\par \par \uc2 "The National Review" points the finger at your party, saying this: Democrats made this mess themselves. They dismantled the policies that the Trump administration had used to get a handle on the problem.\par \par \uc2 At the border this week, Senator Ted Cruz said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): I have to say, I am angry because this is deliberate. This is a decision that was made by President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and congressional Democrats, to open up the border, to what is nothing less than an invasion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, President Biden did get rid of Trump era policies that he called inhumane. You heard what Congressman Donalds said that that's an easy way to label things that were actually working in his estimation.\par \par \uc2 But nothing got done in this administration the first couple of years of Democrats controlling the White House and both houses of Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, who owns this chaos now at the border?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Shannon, nothing has gotten done since President Reagan signed bipartisan immigration reform in 1986. And instead of throwing fingers at each other, why don't we come together in some common principles?\par \par \uc2 We need a safe and secure border. And we need an orderly and humane process. That means Congress needs to act. We need to provide the money to have judges there and to have immigration officers, so that we can actually be quickly processing the claims. The president is saying that these claims should be processed in hours, people who aren't legitimate should be sent back.\par \par \uc2 We need to have more money for Border Patrol, which by the way, the House has passed under Democratic leadership two years ago. The Republicans didn't vote for that.\par \par \uc2 But why not come up with a bipartisan solution? That's what I want.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, is there anything that you could work with Republicans? Because a lot of those things -- you know, ramping up immigration personnel, border agents, those kind of things are included.\par \par \uc2 They say you guys had to secure the border before you can do anything else. They have things included in their bill as, you know, E-Verify as well, that all employers would have a database to make sure they are hiring people who are here legally.\par \par \uc2 Are you opposed to that?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Their bill is extreme, even -- their Senate -- the Senate won't pass their bill. I mean, their bill takes away funding from any of the NGOs.\par \par \uc2 In El Paso, you talk to \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  who represents that area. Why wouldn't we want NGOs giving water to people coming across or helping deal with the situation on the border?\par \par \uc2 Their bill is for border wall. That is not something Democrats can or will support.\par \par \uc2 But we will support increased funding for Border Patrol. We will support increased funding for immigration judges to quickly process things. We will support better technology and security of the border. So, why not come up with a compromise there?\par \par \uc2 And the president, by the way, is doing everything he can to reverse the catch and release. You have border agents now, and judges, quickly calling people who are coming at the border within hours, and many of the folks who don't have legitimate -- legitimate claims aren't being allowed in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, just a couple of recent FOX polls on this, on how the president is doing on the issue of immigration -- 36 percent approval, 61 percent disapproval. And how he's treating illegal immigrants who show up, 9 percent say too tough, 60 percent, the overwhelming majority in this poll, saying not tough enough.\par \par \uc2 So, whatever the administration is doing, voters out there do not think it's effective. They lay this at the president's feet.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: They understand that there is the border crisis right now in terms of people not being treated in an orderly, safe and human process.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is often with Congress. I mean, we have not provided the administration with the resources for the immigration judges or processing. We have not provided the resources for Border Patrol. We have not provided the resources for securing the border.\par \par \uc2 And I don't believe this either Democratic or Republican issue. It's a both party issue. I mean, President Reagan is the last president who's gotten this done. And since then, we've had no bipartisan agreement.\par \par \uc2 So, the question I have for Representative Donaldson (ph) or others is, do you want to just score political points or do you want to actually work, compromise and get something done? I want to get something done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- listened to the two of you, it sounds like there are some nuggets there that you do agree on. And so, we wish you luck on doing that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, there were a number of GOP senators who went to the border. They are back here in D.C. talking about the issue of the fentanyl crisis. Here's what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't give a damn about Americans. And why would I say that? Seventy thousand Americans died of fentanyl overdose. Why? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't care. He's a completely open border.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETE RICKETTS (R-NE): President Biden could use this health crisis of fentanyl poisons to implement Title 42. He could do that today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I believe it's GOP Senator Bill Haggerty who's a guy that proposed legislatively to give the power to the president to do this, to trigger Title 42 with fentanyl as the health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Shannon, first of all, the exact problem with politics is what Senator Scott said. I mean, come on, President Biden doesn't give a damn about Americans? Here's someone who has dedicated his entire life to public service.\par \par \uc2 I would never say that about President Trump. I would not say that he doesn't care about Americans.\par \par \uc2 So, why don't we actually deal with the issues, instead of these ad hominem attacks on our president?\par \par \uc2 Of course, I will support stronger policy to get fentanyl out of America. I'm concerned about China that's been shipping it. It's wrong.\par \par \uc2 I've heard the testimony in Congress of mothers who've lost their children. And let's solve that. I mean, instead of launching ad hominem attacks at the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, let's do a little lightning round and get to a couple of other things.\par \par \uc2 The debt ceiling. There is a call by the White House for clean debt ceiling. There are enough Senators, Republicans who say that's not going to happen. They won't vote for a clean debt ceiling. It's filibuster proof there.\par \par \uc2 "The Federalist", though, is summing up the idea that you can't use the debt ceiling as a negotiating tact this way. It says: If the debt ceiling is mechanically lifted without any debate over spending, then it doesn't really exist. If you want to spend without any limit, just say so.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: We say that we should pay our bills, but we are open and believe their need to be cuts in the deficit. And here's how Democrats would do it.\par \par \uc2 We would increase taxes on the wealthy. We would make sure that billionaires had to pay at least a 25 percent minimum tax. We would make sure that there were strategic cuts in defense spending. We would end the cap on Social Security and have tax -- taxes and have taxes over $250,000 on Social Security.\par \par \uc2 We have a proposal. We just wanted to first pay our bills before implementing our proposal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, let me touch on this as well because you also said in Oversight Committee, the bank transactions that were outlined by Republicans this week raising questions about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , what work he was doing, and why people -- as you heard Congressman Donalds say -- like grandchildren were getting paid.\par \par \uc2 More questions than answers for a lot of folks right now, but here's something else that Congressman Donalds said this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Whenever there was like this many companies, just laying all over the place, and you see wire transfers, cashier checks over here, going the random members of the family to no apparent purpose, at the size and velocity at which all of this is being conducted, the only logical conclusion of a financial professional is, you are concealing money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you have any curiosity, any questions about these transactions?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Steve Doocy from FOX himself asked. He said, there's no evidence of anything illegal and there's no evidence of anything that ties it to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I wish the House Oversight Committee would spend more time worrying about the ordinary income of Americans going up, than they are worried about the income of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And I guess the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, no questions?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, I do have -- here's is my point. There is a U.S. attorney that President Trump appointed that is charged to look into all of this. And I have confidence in him. When he comes out with something, I'm not going to question it.\par \par \uc2 But why are we wasting our time politicizing this in Congress? Let him do his job. He's a Trump appointee. He has full authority.\par \par \uc2 He can look into all of this. I'm not going to question the facts.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Let him do it and then, let's agree to his process.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, we wait on that.\par \par \uc2 Before you go, quickly, I want to ask you, you're in New Hampshire. There is a debate about whether President Biden is going to spend much time there because there's this fight over the primary calendar, and whether it's going to count and who -- what order.\par \par \uc2 You're worried that he may overlook New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: He should be here. He would win the state decisively in the primary. But look, people are going to be here for a year in 2023 -- Trump, DeSantis, Pence. It would be a mistake for us not to make our case.\par \par \uc2 We've got to be in New Hampshire talking about what this president has done in North Country, in towns like Berlin, where he's helping revitalize those small rural towns, factory towns. The president should come. He should come and give a speech about his agenda.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Congressman Khanna, always great to see you. Thanks for dropping in today.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President Trump's biggest potential 2024 challenger just barnstormed Iowa. We've got a live report about what voters there are thinking.\par \par \uc2 And we'll bring our Sunday group to debate the GOP rivals' dueling visions for the party.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Iowa's diehard Republican voters heard firsthand this weekend from one of the former president's potential 2024 challenges. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  traveling to several cities in Iowa to test his message as he weighs a presidential run. There was supposed to be a bit of a showdown there with former President Trump, but he had to cancel his rally because of tornado warnings there.\par \par \uc2 Well, Alexis McAdams is there live for us in Iowa this morning with a look at what voters are thinking.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 That's right, we did have a chance to talk with several voters here across Iowa. They tell us that they do not like where the country is heading, and they'll back whomever will beat President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We did have a chance to catch up with Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I asked him about what he thinks is happening at our southern border.\par \par \uc2 Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): It's a disaster and it's a man-made disaster with bad policies. That border should just be shut down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, Governor DeSantis expected, Shannon, to announce a presidential campaign in the coming weeks. Fox News now confirming DeSantis will move his political operation into a new base in downtown Tallahassee on Monday. That triggers disclosure requirements with federal officials here.\par \par \uc2 And this comes as he was out in Iowa at a series of events talking with supporters in some pretty packed events. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had also planned to be here in Iowa but his team said bad weather forced them to cancel that large rally.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis then seizing on the opportunity, stopping by Des Moines, right about that time that Trumps rally would have taken place.\par \par \uc2 We asked voters what they think.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN ANDERSON, IOWA VOTER: Trump's a great candidate too. I think DeSantis is even better. But that's what the primary's for. You've got to duke it out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And endorsements, Shannon, are rolling in on both sides here. We checked in with DeSantis' team, who tells us that they have dozens of supporters, including three dozen Republican legislators from Iowa backing him to be the next president. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he has the backing of more than 100 local elected officials in the Hawkeye state.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we'll see if they're both officially be running soon.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group. Fox News correspondent and former national security council staffer, Gillian Turner, "Forbes" contributing writer Richard Fowler, former Bush White House adviser Karl Rove, and syndicated columnist Cal Thomas, also the author of the brand-new book, "A Watchman in the Night."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. Great to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 OK, so you heard about the competing endorsements there in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Karl, DeSantis isn't even officially running yet, but Trump outpacing him there. Does it matter?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, we're at the beginning of this process. Remember, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was way ahead in Iowa of a - of a fellow named Barack Obama and - and was upset on caucus night.\par \par \uc2 I, frankly, thought it was a good start for DeSantis. He wisely did - in Iowa they like to see you up close and personal. They don't want to be part of a 10,000 person crowd of a 5,000 -- they want to see you up close and personal. And he had several events like that. And I thought it was good.\par \par \uc2 I was very impressed with his legislative endorsements. The Republican Senate president and the Republican House leader both endorsed him, along with 30 of their colleagues. That's a strong - a strong start and he's being hosted by the Republican congressman in western Iowa, \uc2 Randy Feenstra\uc2 , and that's - that's a Republican stronghold when it comes to the caucus.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Meanwhile this week, at the town hall, former President Trump very much protecting as if he's already the nominee. I mean that was deafly his statue there, although Marc Thiessen called it a three-alarm dumpster fire for the GOP. "Axios" reports it this way. A source familiar with the Biden campaign's thinking told "Axios" Sophia Cai that Trump's town hall provide weeks' worth of damning content in one hour.\par \par \uc2 Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL STAFFER AND FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Marc's not the only one to call it a dumpster fire. You know, I -- I think it received widespread criticism on a whole - for a whole host of different reasons. To me they billed the event as a GOP primary town hall, yet for some reason they decided not to press the former president on the issues that Republican voters, including his own supporters, actually care about. They spent an inordinate amount of time asking him about January 6th, asking him about his allegations that the 2020 election was stolen, asking him about the accusations from E. Jean Carroll. None of those issues are things that his supporters and the Republican Party want to talk about at large. It seemed a missed opportunity to actually - if they were going to cater to Republican voters, seems like that was the moment to do so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, one of the issues that did come up was abortion, which we got a signal from the Trump campaign weeks ago that he wouldn't be for a federal ban. Here's what - here's how a "Wall Street Journal" reports it. They say, when an audience member asked what he would say to women worried about last year's \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision overturned the right to abortion, Mr. Trump told her the decision was a great victory and brag about his role in nominating conservative justices to the high court. At the same time, he refused to say whether he would sign a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 And, Cal, just to be clear, it obviously sent this back to the states. But this issue of whether or not he would sign off on a federal ban, he really demurred on that.\par \par \uc2 CAL THOMAS, SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: Well, you know, the Democrats used to say they wanted abortion safe, legal and rare. I think pro-lifers need to start focusing on the rare. I think those of us who are pro-life have been on the wrong end of this, are you trying to defend ourselves against charges of rape, incest, life in the mother.\par \par \uc2 The other side of the argument is more powerful. Would you be OK with abortion up until the moment of delivery? How about the eighth month? How about the seventh month? With viability? I think there's a much stronger argument. Polls show that's where a majority of the American people are. You're always going to have abortion, you're always going to have crime and these other things. But we can reduce it with the right policy. And I think arguing it from the other perspective, the beginning of life at nine months, eight months, seven months, when the fetus can be viable outside the mother, is a much better way to go, politically and morally.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, in the midst of all of this, when, you know, voters and polls tell us on both sides, people are not super excited about a 2020 rematch, there's a lot of, you know, whispering about a third-party potential contender. Axios says this headline, Manchin flirts with Iowa voters. Says he took time from his busy Senate scheduled to tell a gathering of Iowa business and community leaders Wednesday in D.C. that he's fiscally responsible and socially compassionate. Another hint that he's considering a potential third-party presidential bid.\par \par \uc2 Another interesting piece, Richard, out of "Axios," they talk to a group of swing voters in Georgia who said, if they're confronted with this rematch in 2020 but somebody who was independent and had the financial backing to actually win this thing, they'd vote for that person, or at least consider it.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FORBES CONTRIBUTING WRITER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, I think there's always this flirtation with a third party. And I - and we have Karl Rove here, so he could talk about this.\par \par \uc2 Here is the problem, for many third party candidates they lack the infrastructure to get on the ballot in many of these states which creates a problem for them. I think when you think about, if there's a potential rematch between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the recent ABC poll that came out that shows some - I mean the media that's been fodder in the beltway. But the truth of the matter is, I think for many American voters they're looking at it and saying, look, the election is 18 months away. There's a lot of time here.\par \par \uc2 And we're also looking at our wallets. We see that inflation is on its way down. It's stubbornly high. Now, let's not take away from that. But it seems to be on its way down and we're now getting ready to see many of the policies that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has - \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  - \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  passed, including the bipartisan infrastructure bill, start to make its way into place. And we're going to see what that looks like. And I think that's what voters are going to decide on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Karl, would a Manchin candidacy, which candidate would be most hurt by that, in your estimation?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: We have no idea. Look, first of all, there will be a - in all likelihood a no party's label on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And they're doing the infrastructure (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Because they - they're spending $50 million. I've seen their plan and their plan is meticulous, well-staffed by lawyers and being executed. And I - you know, in 2016, I guess it was, we had a third-party candidate who got on 24 state ballots and was on -- received write-in ballots in another 11. I think there are going to be 51 jurisdictions this year. The District of Columbia, and 50 states, with a no party, no labels party candidate (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 Who that candidate is going to be, I don't know. If it's \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , we don't know how that's going to play out. Is \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , if he were the candidate, going to appeal more to people who might otherwise vote for Republicans or centrist Democrats? Or is it going to be, you know, sort of a pox on both your house? We don't - this is going to be one of the most unpredictable presidential elections that we've seen in our lifetime, if not the most. But it is -- there will be a no labels candidate, I suspect, because they're going to have a label and he ain't got a good place to go home and run for the re-election to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . West Virginia, which went for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by 40 some odd votes, is not going to be particularly excited about voting for a Democrat from the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He's got a little trouble there based on votes on things for the - things called the Inflation Reduction Act and other things like that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is calling at the current president, who is running for re-election, for not doing press. They say they promise transparency but he's stonewalling the media as he runs for a second term. He should be eager to show he can handle all aspects of the job.\par \par \uc2 Cal.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: My favorite line in that editorial is, the media is not your enemy. Now, when "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has to tell a Democrat that the media is not your enemy, what does that mean? You know, they used to fault \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  for not holding more press conferences, but the American people want to see their president and feel that he is capable, especially right now when there is so much stuff out there in the water, saying that he is displaying all of the signs of mental decline. And this is serious stuff. And we've got to find out if the guy is equipped for a second term. He'd be 82 if he won. He'd be 86 when he left.\par \par \uc2 Look, Biden and I are the same age. He's a - he's a month older than I am. But I'm clean and I'm articulate, as he once said about Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Are you running? Is that what you're doing here announcing -\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I'm running from office is what I'm doing, yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Cal is announcing he's running for president.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take a quick break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the growing fight between House Republicans and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  over a whistleblower document as investigations into the Biden family divide Washington and Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE (R-SC): For years the left has said no one is above the law. Well, put your money where your mouth is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: South Carolina Republican Nancy Mace calling on Democrats and the press to look into documents Republicans provided this week regarding the Biden family with the same energy they expended investigating former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to put up these two opposing headlines here. "National Review" says it this way, Bidens used web of shell companies to conceal foreign cash, bank records obtained by House GOP reveal. But "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," same response to -- different responses to the same event says, House Republican report finds no evidence of wrongdoing by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Karl, where does the truth lie? Somewhere in between? Is this a Rorschach test on how you feel about the Bidens?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, they're all sort of semi-accurate but -- but let's be honest about it, this stuff stinks! The vice president of the United States, in 2016 - or 2014 is a guy named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And his son gets hard by an - by a natural gas company in Ukraine, which is under examination because it's - it's corrupt oligarch boss and owner is - is playing footsie with Moscow and doing bad things. There's only one reason they hired him, he was the son of the vice president of the United States. They had recently hired the longtime bag man and confidant of the - of the secretary of state, \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , who recommended his business partner, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , be on the board. And we know that a couple years later he made a million three from this and a corrupt Romanian oligarch.\par \par \uc2 When the president -- vice president goes to Ukraine in 2015, NGOs involved in the anti-corruption efforts say it really stinks to have the guy from the United States, who's been detailed by President Obama to come and lecture us on fighting corruption to have his son be on the payroll of a corrupt company.\par \par \uc2 Now, put a Republican in there. What if it had been, you know, Jenna Bush had been hired while her father was president, or \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  had been hired while her father was vice president? Would we say there's something stinky here?\par \par \uc2 And, now, maybe they didn't violate a law, but the vice president of the United States, we've got to ask him a couple questions. Did he know in advance that - when his son went on there and did he have a problem with it and did people inside the administration say, you know what, it really doesn't help our effort to encourage anti-corruption efforts when your son is on the payroll of a corrupt company.\par \par \uc2 This stinks! And I'm glad they put that stuff out. And, of course, everyone has like 11 LLCs for their family names. And, you know, you - you - you get money from foreign -- from Romania and Ukraine and Kazakhstan and you give it to your grandkid and your sister and your brother-in-law and your wife's blah blah and blah blah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Richard -\par \par \uc2 KARL: I mean this is just like crazy (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're itching over here, but what about these questions? What was \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  actually doing and why were grandkid getting checks?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure. Listen, I - I think that this committee has set high expectations. And I'm not going to defend the private criticize, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but there's a couple of things here. Number one, we've seen elected officials' families engage in business practices before. I point to the former president and his children who worked at the White House and then did business out of the White House, but I digress.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about what this committee has put out here. They put out information that's been reported before by both "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." They set high expectations behind a podium in front of cameras.\par \par \uc2 What I want to see this committee do is now get behind a dais and if they have this evidence and they believe it's hot and its good like we heard the congressman say earlier, then have a committee hearing, subpoena some people. And then when you're done subpoenaing some people, send recommendations to the Justice Department on who they should prosecute. If not, then stop standing behind a podium and stop standing in front of cameras. It's all we've seen since they've taken the gavel.\par \par \uc2 And let's - let's remind -- let's remind - let's remind the American people that this investigation started in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  when they were under Republican control in 2018. If you don't have the evidence by now, where is it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and that's a question, too, about what the U.S. attorney is doing with respect to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . That investigation started in 2018 too. We're waiting for answers there.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I'm glad Richard has sort of laid the predicate. So when -- if the U.S. attorney in Delaware indites Hunter Biden -\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: He's going to say that's a good thing to have happen?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure. Sure, absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to - I want to make sure we hit on another legal case, though, and this is Daniel Penny, who has been charge in the death of Jordan Neely on the subway. He's facing up to 15 years in prison. He's out on bail right now. But Donte Mills, who is an attorney for Jordan Neely's family, a young man who was obviously struggling , they want tougher charges. Here's what that attorney said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONTE MILLS, NEELY FAMILY ATTORNEY: There was no attack. Mr. Neely did not attack anyone. He did not touch anyone. He did not hit anyone. But he was choked to death. And that can't stand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, Cal, there's a lot we don't know in this case, but we know that these charges are moving forward.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Yes, well, that never stops anybody from speculating. You have the rhino, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , reverend in name only, out there, just like he did with Tawana Brawley, making all kinds of unfounded statements. This was a man, clearly, as you indicated, who had mental troubles. He has a history of harassing, especially women, on the subways. And it's interesting though that the crowdfunding amount of money now has exceeded $1 million for his defense. It's going to be interesting to see what comes out in the trial.\par \par \uc2 A United States Marine veteran, against a guy who had serious behavioral and mental problems, with a history of harassing women, and 40 other charges against him. And \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , the Manhattan district attorney, who likes diminishing felony charges to misdemeanors. I think the Marine has got everything on his side, even with a New York jury.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it will have to go through a grand jury and then to a jury.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" is worried about this. They say, even if Mr. Penny is acquitted by a jury, the charges against him will surely deter other potential Samaritans from intervening to subdue a seemingly dangerous person or even to stop a robbery or assault. If you do and something goes wrong in New York, you will be the one prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, this is a big talker this week.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is. You could also argue that someone ending up dead is not merely something going wrong. There's a lot of ways in which people can intervene to protect their fellow citizens. This is coming from someone - I grew up riding the subway in Manhattan. I lived there until I was, I think, 22. I've been all kinds of harassed, intimidated. This is not Sunday morning conversation, but somebody exposed themselves to me on the subway. I have lived this, right? It can be terrifying. Something has to be done to try and help people live their lives. At the same time, stepping in for self-defense, and defend other passengers, cannot encompass folks ending up dead.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, prosecutors will have to show that Penny caused Neely's death and did so recklessly, meaning he knew the chokehold could kill Neely and unreasonably chose to apply it anyway.\par \par \uc2 Very quick, final word.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Well, listen, I think, let's be very clear, I agree with Gillian. But let's remember, being a panhandler, being homeless and having a mental illness is not a death sentence. And if you're a veteran, you should know that this person wasn't trying to cause danger and that you could still be a good Samaritan and not kill somebody.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. He may have thought. We'll see what the jury thinks.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, panel. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next on this Mother's Day, I'll take you behind the scenes to meet an actual mama bear raising two adorable cubs as part of a critical study of the Smithsonian National Zoo.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And now a little Mother's Day sweetness for you.\par \par \uc2 Zoo lovers went gaga last fall when beloved Andean bear Brienne gave birth to two adorable cubs. They'll be six months old tomorrow. So, the Smithsonian Zoo invited us in behind the scenes for an up close visit with the cubs and the mom, who loved showing them off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH PITSKO, ASSISTANT CURATOR OF ANDEAN BEARS, GREAT CATS: Ian was the one that was boulder first, and Sean was a little shy. And now I think that reversed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Meet Ian and Sean, the Smithsonian National Zoo's adorably playful Andean bear cubs.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: They're out in the morning from about 9 to 11. And they're super charismatic to come and watch. And the cubs going up the tree, it's just a really nice little spot in the zoo.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They were born of the fall and stayed quietly snuggled up with their doting mother, Brienne, for months. But now they're allowed to roam and climb trees and even fall, but under Brienne's love supervision.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: Andean bears are very special. One of the reasons is they are listed as vulnerable on the IUCN Red List. And there's thought to be maybe less than 18,000 of these guys in the wild.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The family is an invaluable presence at the zoo because bears are difficult to study in their natural habitats.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: We collect behavioral data on everything they do, every day. And we can share that information with others zoos, we can share it with researchers in the wild and to help promote the conservation of these bears.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And with all of that, they have very distinct personalities too!\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: They do. Sean is always more social than Ian. He's at the top of the tree all the time. So, they're really, really sweet bears. And we are lucky to have them here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): We were with Leigh Pitsko for a small but hilarious cub first.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: This is their first box. So, we'll see how they like it. And it has peanuts inside and hay and Brienne loves both of those things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The cubs love the goodies.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: They love it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Brienne called dibs on the cardboard box.\par \par \uc2 The cubs father, Quito, is just as sweet as they are, and just as ready for a snack.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: Here he comes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Oh, Quito.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: Look, he's all wet. So, he was just in the pool.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You were in the pool.\par \par \uc2 So, we got a chance to meet Quito. He is a big, beautiful, impressive bear, but you said he's also one of the sweetest.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: Yes. Quito is 340 pounds, and he has the sweetest temperament of almost all of our animals here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You like the peanuts too!\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: He's everyone's favorite. And he's also very genetically valuable. He came to us from a zoo in Germany. And I was fortunate enough to go and pick him up and bring him on the airplane home.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Pitsko says she knows the baby bears are an easy sell to crowds who visit the zoo. And she hopes that as they grow, young visitors' interests in animals will grow as well.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: When I was a kid I loved going to zoos and it inspired me to do what I'm doing now. And I hope it does the same thing for kids that come. They can learn that there's simple things that they can do to protect these animals.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, most of us go to work - we have some coworkers, may we feel like some of them are bears when they're having a bad day, but you literally are here with animals all day. What is it like? This is your office.\par \par \uc2 PITSKO: Yes, it's amazing. Especially in this little corner of the zoo, it's so peaceful and quiet. It's just a lovely place to be at work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And just a note, by the way, my podcast, "Livin the Bream" has dropped new this morning. This Mother's Day week I chat with author Bethany Mandel. We talked about the joys and fulfilment to be found in one of the most underappreciated jobs out there, motherhood.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcast.com or wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Happy Mother's Day to my mama, Marie, and to Gillian, giving us part of her Mother's Day, joining us on the panel too.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week out there. Celebrate your moms. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230514ej5e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 DeSantis Too Focused on Woke Issues?; Trump Deposition Video Released in E. Jean Carroll Trial; Migrant Surge Expected; Interview With \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  President Austan Goolsbee; Hot Jobs Report; \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 's Coronation Set For Tomorrow; Adele Celebrates Birthday\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Palkot, Charlie Gasparino, Bryan Llenas, Griff Jenkins, Peter  Doocy, Ashley Dvorkin, Dion Baia, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,090 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Indeed, rumor has it that there's something much bigger than the monarchy going on this important day.\par \par \uc2 I'm done with my British accent.\par \par \uc2 It has nothing to do with the slightly less famous Brit celebrating a coronation tomorrow, but a certain other, much more consequential Brit, supposedly, and a global treasure, at that, celebrating a birthday today. That's right, today.\par \par \uc2 Did you think I forgot my saintly siren of song? How could I forget someone like -- you see, I do that all the time, incorporating songs. And if you don't know them, well, in the next hour, you're going to hear a ton of them. But it's happy birthday time for Adele, yes, Adele, the singer Adele, 35 years young today.\par \par \uc2 When she was born 35 years ago this day, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was still president. Maggie Thatcher was still prime minister. That's when "Beetlejuice" was on the big screen and roll-up jeans and high-tops were all the rage.\par \par \uc2 Little did the world know then that a pop sensation was about to take it by storm and capture the hearts of millions, including yours truly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We broke it first on FOX Business, the dust-up with one Adele, only my favorite singer on the planet.\par \par \uc2 The bad news is, in the middle of October, we could run out of cash in this country. The good news is, about the same time, Adele will release a new song.\par \par \uc2 Look what it did to the markets today. They're calling it the Adele rally. OK, maybe they're not, but I am.\par \par \uc2 I did pick up the phone one time and she answered, hello? Hello? Hello?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, thank you, Adele.\par \par \uc2 Well, hello to you too, Adele.\par \par \uc2 He is a big story. Now, let me be clear, not like Adele suddenly quitting a promising singing career in her prime big story. She is in her own luscious league.\par \par \uc2 So are you a Taylor Swift fan? Well, who isn't, right? By the way, she's no Adele, but I digress. Willie Nelson, the Rolling Stones, the Beach Boys, but no Adele. Where's Adele?\par \par \uc2 The Adele thing, where do you stand on this? Can we utilize Adele in some sort of a peacemaker role here?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you worried that Adele is angry at you?\par \par \uc2 We didn't even get to talk about Adele.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. TULSI GABBARD (D-HI): Adele.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe next time, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Call Neil, Adele.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Call Neil. She's not. She's not, but I'm sure I'm just missing the call.\par \par \uc2 Hey, I'm in the control room. Has Adele called?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's not. OK, fine.\par \par \uc2 And this is Kevin Fitzgerald, my producer. I told him to use my name. Apparently, he did four times. Didn't work. She said: "Thanks for reaching. As it turns out, this isn't possible. Best, Benny."\par \par \uc2 That's fine, Benny. Just pass it along to Adele. I'm sure it'll be a different reaction.\par \par \uc2 Happy birthday, Adele. Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 This makes some people uncomfortable, maybe because I'm old enough to be her father or that I am happily married man almost 40 years. My wife has no idea on, though, so I'm just going full throttle.\par \par \uc2 But, in all seriousness, one of the things I just want to do, we live in an age where we're shouting and screaming at each other, people are cursing at each other. So why don't we celebrate a British invasion, of course, with the coronation of King Charles tomorrow, and, of course, celebrating a singing legend, an angel, if you will, named Adele today?\par \par \uc2 So you're going to have to bear with me here as we step back from all the nastiness in the world and just have fun with a singer who makes us have fun. And look what she did today. At the corner of Wall and Broad, Adele single-handedly ignited a stock market rally.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know there are those of you out there saying, Neil, this had nothing to do with Adele. This had to do with a strong jobs report and the fact that banking pressures eased. Blah, blah, blah, blah. This was all Adele turning 35 today.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to be covering this on all angles here, because I figured a lot of you at home were figuring we should cover this from all angles, even though not a one of you had advised me to cover this on all angles.\par \par \uc2 But my old buddy Dion Baia is back with me. And he is on top of these phone calls we're waiting for from Adele and her people to confirm not only that she's coming on the show, Dion, but that she just wants to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 But you know better than I.\par \par \uc2 DION BAIA, FOX NEWS AUDIO TECHNICIAN: Yes. We have a line open right now. We're waiting for the call. The line works.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So, any time it comes in, and we're ready to add phones need be. In case I get a wrong number or a pizza order or something, we will be good to go to transfer her right to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, what we're waiting on, just to be clear, is her to get in here to acknowledge the fact that we obviously pointed out the significance of this day on FOX Business, which, in the past, she didn't know anything about it.\par \par \uc2 But I advised it. And now, of course, she gets FBN. So that's good news, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes, absolutely. And if you don't get FOX Business, as we always say...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: ... you should demand it.\par \par \uc2 And she's demanded it. You have demanded it, and we have got it. And now we're all good to go. And I'm just waiting right here just in case, any second, this phone could ring, and it could make your day. Could make your weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It could -- well, it could...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Make your year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Our lives, both our...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Make your marketing...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, our lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are a lot of you, by the way, who are letting me know about the rally that had nothing to do with Adele. Whatever.\par \par \uc2 And there are a lot of other people saying, this obsession with Adele is a little over the top, Cavuto. And, by the way, nothing you say indicates that she really is the world's greatest singer.\par \par \uc2 She really is the world's greatest singer. So I don't know what your people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 But Charlie Lankston here, The Daily Mail femail editor. We have got Ashley Dvorkin, FOX News entertainment correspondent extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, I end it with you, begin with you, on how one woman could move the markets the way she did today. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY DVORKIN, FOX NEWS ENTERTAINMENT PRODUCER: I mean, it's Adele. It is a huge celebration.\par \par \uc2 And I am so happy to celebrate this day with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: All those highlights you were playing from the show before, I feel like I was part of a few of those, but really big day...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: ... a birthday. Yes, a really big day, her birthday. And I feel like she's taking -- she probably has great plans, taking a break from Weekends With Adele, the Vegas residency, having a weekend for Adele.\par \par \uc2 So I'm happy to kick it off with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie, I know everyone is focused on King Charles or whatever tomorrow, and that's great. But she is not performing, along with quite number of other British pop stars and musicians, for the king tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Lionel Richie is great. Love Lionel Richie. But what's going on with that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE LANKSTON, FEMAIL EDITOR, DAILY MAIL: Well, we do understand that favorite human ever, Adele, was asked to be part of the coronation celebrations, but that she turned down the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 The same can be said of Elton John, \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2  and a number of British artists, including my favorite, the Spice Girls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why? Why did they all turn it down? Yes, they all turned it down. Why?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Well, I think right now, even though Charles has really done a great job in the first few months of his monarchy, far better than a lot of people thought he was going to, the royal family is a difficult brand to be aligned with right now.\par \par \uc2 There is so much scrutiny, speculation, controversy, that kind of overshadows the monarchy at every turn, because of Prince Andrew, because of Meghan and Harry. And I think all of these celebrities know that, no matter what they do, no matter how they perform, there is going to be some kind of negative connotation.\par \par \uc2 And people like Adele, \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2 , who's just finished a big court case, they just don't want to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, when you look at this, and the entertainers here today, of course, to see big celebrities in concert, it's very expensive. Adele's been in Vegas, of course, booking out all these shows that could go into the weeks, months, dare I say years.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering if America has caught her fancy now and she doesn't want to go back.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Well, yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 She extended her residency, and she's going back in June, June through November, so there's going to be a lot more dates for people to attend. And she announced that she's going to be doing a concert film from that residency. So, if you can't get there, you can watch it. You can still be part of all the excitement.\par \par \uc2 So I definitely think so, especially with the success of the Las Vegas show.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm curious too, Charlie. Obviously, you -- Britain has turned out some of the greatest musicians of all time. And it seems that every generation knows one.\par \par \uc2 And I joke here about whether Adele is the latest example of that or should we go -- can go on. But they're focused on the king tomorrow. And, that, I get. It's his coronation. But if you think about it, he's starting his career in his 70s. Adele was an international rock star at 19.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the fact that she's not appearing is because he's simply jealous?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Oh, I don't think that at all. I think the truth of the matter is, Charles...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you do. You took a while to answer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you took a while to answer. I think you do think that, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Look, do I think that Charles has the same singing talents as Adele? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 But I think the reality here is that Charles has been waiting for this moment for his entire life, and nothing and nobody, even Adele, is going to stop him from enjoying every aspect of this moment. So, he's not sitting there worrying about who's going to perform at his concert.\par \par \uc2 What he is worrying about is the fact that tomorrow needs to go off without a hitch, without drama, without Harry causing a scene. That's what he's focusing on. And then, when he gets home, he can put on some Adele and maybe have a little bit of a happy cry over how well it went.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, Adele is now 35. She could run for president in this country. There's that minor detail that she wasn't born here. I'm working on changing that, but that's a separate matter.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering. We think of her as a kid starting out. We think of a lot of the musicians today as kids starting out. And I'm wondering, to get back to what we were saying a little bit earlier about so many big names not performing for the king tomorrow, whether we're overreading that, or that it's a whole different crowd now with this king versus his mother.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Yes, I think it is. It's a different audience. It's a different generation of artists.\par \par \uc2 And I think, especially for them, as we were saying before, maybe just not taking part avoids the whole situation in case they were concerned about image or what -- if it does make a statement one way or the other, and also being so busy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, everybody can say scheduling conflicts for the most part, world tours, residency, a slight break. So a lot of them are also very busy with their own schedules, and maybe that's just an easier way to go about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I think you're right about that. Maybe we're overreading all of this.\par \par \uc2 But I'd like maybe to get a final take from you, Charlie, on what we can look forward to. I know the birthday is almost running out here in the United States for Adele, but, of course, all eyes turn to the coronation tomorrow. You think it all goes smoothly?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: I really do.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's going to be such a special and historic occasion. Neil, you and I have talked in the past about that amazing pomp and ceremony, which is what...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: ... the U.K. does so well, what the monarchy does so well.\par \par \uc2 And you can expect to see all of that tomorrow and through the weekend. There's going to be history. There's going to be tradition. Charles is going to be dressed in his finery. I think it really is going to be an event for the ages. And it's one that most people will only get the chance to enjoy in their lifetime on this one occasion.\par \par \uc2 So everyone really should enjoy that, embrace that. Even if Adele won't be there, it will still be something to remember forever.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, it's going to be grand. I mean, it is almost as grand as Adele's Vegas show, but we will see. That's tough competition.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm getting pelted with a lot of e-mails here, where a lot of people are saying, Neil, you can't be serious. You are comparing this to the royal coronation and that this could be even more significant?\par \par \uc2 Yes, I am. Yes I am. If you have any doubts about that, you can go ahead and catch her in concert or listen to Dion waiting at the phone for her to call in, which we're probably going to hear very soon, Dion.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Well, you have added a second phone because she is -- has a residency in Vegas. And we'd forgotten, or at least I had forgotten that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So we have added a second line just in case. So we are all ready for her to call in and chat with you about her birthday and everything going on across the pond.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm a little disquieted by the fact that the phone has not rung at all. I mean...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Well, you know, it's a very private line.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: And I have gotten some wrong numbers for, like, different kind of takeout places, but, at the moment, I'm trying to just feed through that...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: ... and tell people, you can't get a pizza here. You can't get any Chinese food or whatever.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So, aside from that, we have someone now screening the calls, so, as soon as these darn things ring, I'm going to be ready.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So she would answer like, hello, right?\par \par \uc2 (PHONE RINGING)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, OK, that could be. That could -- keep us posted. Keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Hello? Hey. No. No, you can't get a Bakewell tart here. No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: All right. Sorry. Wrong number.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did not work out exactly...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Sorry about that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, please, please, please, please.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Sorry. At least we know they work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Please.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, maybe, from rolling in the deep to stocks just rolling in the green, a lot of you were shocked to see, all right, why were we up so much? Could have been that jobs report. Could have been the fact that people are saying we might have a steady stream here with the Fed holding at bay.\par \par \uc2 Or why don't we just call it obvious? It's Adele's birthday. Markets are celebrating worldwide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that big jobs report we were telling about, 253,000 jobs added in the latest month.\par \par \uc2 And this continues the spate of activity that's surprising some folks and led to, obviously, a big rally at the corner of Wall and Broad. Would not have been possible, as you know, without Adele. But that's a separate matter.\par \par \uc2 To Peter Doocy on how the administration is responding to all of this -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, there's been a lot of bad headlines this White House has had to deal with over the last few weeks. So with this jobs report, they are trying to shine a light on what they see as a good one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Inflation is now down 40 percent since last summer. It's come down the last nine months in a row. We obviously have more work to do, but we're trending in the right direction, and I think we're making real progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden and Vice President Harris spent part of Cinco de Mayo buying tacos and churros just after he boosted the new White House line about the debt limit, that China and Russia will use a default to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, this is becoming an issue in other countries. What's the United States going to do? Are they really fooling around with not paying their debts?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And then there's this.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, President Biden tried to skirt shouted questions by promising to take more time with reporters later on today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And so I think we have got a lot of work to do. I'm -- and I'm doing a major press conference this afternoon. So I love you all, but I'd like to ask you to leave, so we can get down to business.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, here we are. It is this afternoon, no major press conference, no minor press conference either. So, a quiet week for the president is going to end with no Q&A -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you for that, Peter.\par \par \uc2 To Austan Goolsbee right now.\par \par \uc2 I haven't had a chance to congratulate him on this. He is now the president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 . He's a voting member, as such, of the Federal Open Market Committee, the folks that determine interest rates, and the folks who have just raised interest rates for the 10th time in this cycle to around the 5, 5.25 percent neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 Austan, very good to have you. And belated congratulations.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF CHICAGO: Yes, old friend, thank you for having me back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, it's good.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Adele sends along her congratulations, but I digress.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'd be curious. I mean, were you among those members who said, yes, raise it another quarter-point?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I was. It was a unanimous decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: So, I was in that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You get a good jobs report like this -- and there's no other way you can look at this jobs report. I know they talk about the revisions of the prior months. Fact of the matter is, it was a stunningly good jobs report.\par \par \uc2 So, now the question is, is it so good, it puts pressure on you and your colleagues to keep that interest rate spigot moving?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I mean, a little. That's partly -- as you know, before the banking stresses began, the economy was looking quite hot.\par \par \uc2 And inflation is above where we want it to be. It hasn't come down as fast as we wanted. And the job market has been by far the strongest part of the economy. So you got to take that into account. At the same time, we know that credit conditions like the ones we're seeing now in the past have been correlated with recessions, credit crunches, kind of have done the tightening work of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 So, in our phrase, as you know, data dependence, we got to be data- dependent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: We got to watch a lot of indicators, not just the unemployment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because I have already seen -- I don't know how much stock you put in things, because you guys decide it, not the markets.\par \par \uc2 There seems to be a higher expectation now that we will see a hike in June. Too premature to say that?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I think way too premature to say that.\par \par \uc2 As you know, Neil, even before I was in this job, I was not a fan of the Fed paying that close attention or primary attention to what the stock market says.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I think the real economy is what we all got to be watching, and it's way too premature to know what to do with monetary policy, especially because we're up, we're down on this credit, the issue of the -- of how our credit condition is going to be and what's going to be the fate of our regional banks in the immediate term.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Those are going to matter a lot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's interesting, because that does seem to change the equation a bit here, what's going on with these regional banks and \uc2 First Republic\uc2  and all these others, the mergers and everything on PacWest.\par \par \uc2 A good day, then it's a bad day, then it's a questionable day. With a backdrop like that, does that give you pause on raising rates, that you could make that situation worse?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, well, it has to give you some pause.\par \par \uc2 I gave a speech before the -- before this last FOMC meeting where I said, even if you don't think that we should use the interest rate as a way to fight off financial crisis, which I don't think you should -- I think you should use your prudential and regulatory tools to do it -- things like that, even if they don't spiral into a crisis, they are correlated with banks raising their lending standards...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: ... as they're trying to raise more capital. There's just going to be less to go around for business expansion.\par \par \uc2 And that's likely to slow the economy. And, for sure, you should think, take that into account. And so that's what we -- we all got to keep an eye on that part of credit conditions, because, as I view it, that's kind of doing the work of monetary policy. If you feel like the economy still very hot -- the unemployment rate is 3.4 percent -- the -- whatever tightening that you're going to need to do, it's got to be taking into account of what -- the banking system's impact.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you a little bit about the debt ceiling debate back and forth.\par \par \uc2 We're told -- Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  has said that June 1 is sort of like the cutoff period. At that point, we don't have any more money under the couch cushions and we could be on the brink. Do you agree with that, that June 1 is the time by which this has got to be settled?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't know the exact dates.\par \par \uc2 I know what's -- this is the -- the revenue that comes in is very lumpy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: April 15, a bunch of revenue comes in. June 15, supposed to be revenue come in. So there is a balancing act about what the exact date is.\par \par \uc2 Rather than get into that, I would just say, look, is the banking stress and crisis not enough? Like, do we really have to do this? Chair Powell said it best when he said, look, there's no -- he's not going to get involved in negotiation. They have to raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: You can't -- we can't default on Treasuries. That's the safest asset in the world. I mean, that would be a mess.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I can remember, Austan, there were times -- I think we were talking then -- about in 2011, when you were with Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, of course, we had our debt downgraded. We avoided a default, but it was all the craziness prior that prompted that slash in our AAA credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Some are saying the same thing could be poised to happen now. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I don't know if it was the crisis or if it was the fact that I left the government, but two hours after I left the government was when we got downgraded.\par \par \uc2 As you look now, this -- to not be flip about it, this is a nightmare. This is -- do we really want to pay more in higher interest costs and have financial crises or big recessions or cast doubt on the full faith and credit of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ? I don't know how they're going to sort it out. That's to be -- Congress and the president, whatever, they just need to sort it out.\par \par \uc2 Like, we -- this is not something that we can play games with. What is going to happen?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we have played games with it again and again.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I guess what I'm asking here is, what's a little different now is Kevin McCarthy and Republicans trying to push an immediate quid pro quo. We don't touch the debt ceiling or raise it until we have got some spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the 2011 experience, if memory serves me right, we got some budgetary commitments, promises to address these issues, but not tied immediately to the debt ceiling hike. So that's a big difference. I'm just wondering how you see it settled.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't -- you know far more about the politics, Neil, than I do.\par \par \uc2 And the Fed is not a political position. So, however they sort it out, whether -- whatever the negotiations, whatever the details, they just need to do it before we're going to default on Treasuries. Because, what, are we going to pay the -- the military has got to be paid.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: The veterans benefits, Medicare, Social Security.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it really does not make sense to do this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're right. You hope cooler heads on both sides prevail.\par \par \uc2 But, Austan, while I have you, I just want to get your thoughts. I know, with all the banking stuff going on, you can't address individual banks, I get that. But the irony of this situation is that bigger banks seem to be getting bigger.\par \par \uc2 Could you have envisioned at the time of the meltdown and coming out about institutions considered too big to fail, that now we have a \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  that now accounts for about one out of five deposits in this country? And I just wonder whether that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, well, look, there is a cruel irony to that.\par \par \uc2 There is another side of it, though, which is you will remember, when we talked, it was 2007-2008-2009, as we went into the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Our fear was that the biggest banks are the ones that everyone's afraid of and the bailouts and the rescues, so that they don't collapse.\par \par \uc2 It is a little bit of testament, I think, to the re-regulation of the financial sector that we had these biggest financial institutions build up their capital. They got some of the riskiest activities out of the biggest banks, so that now it's not just an automatic financial crisis when financial institutions start having trouble.\par \par \uc2 There is money going from...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, boy, if you have a stock market freefall -- if you have a stock market -- I'm sorry to bump on that, Austan.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: No, it's fine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But if you have a stock market freefall, that's the capital for a lot of these guys too.\par \par \uc2 So when that starts swooning, it's sort of like everyone heading to the exits at the same time, right?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, look, these issues of too big to fail, but also of just bank concentration in general, they have been with us for a long time. There's been a trend for 30, 40 years trending against the smaller and the regional banks. But issues like deposit insurance and how they're going to be treated, that's got to -- Congress has got to sort that out.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, for sure, cannot make decisions like that on its own. It's the American people and the policymakers that got to decide that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Finally -- I said that that was the last question. I lied, but not intentionally, Austan.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're coming to this position the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , very influential position, as someone who is more sensitive to the knee-jerk reaction, jobs go up, rates go, that you are going to have a different point of view about how you go about your job.\par \par \uc2 Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I take that as a compliment. That's what I aspire to. There are some people who view that as an insult, but I'm going to take it as a compliment.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the Federal Open Market Committee is, in this century, the world's greatest deliberative body. And it is a committee. And there are people with a lot of different views.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And I come to it from a different perspective.\par \par \uc2 But I have been really impressed. The seriousness with which everybody takes their job is exemplary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you should be proud of that.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations again, Austan Goolsbee, the president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Want to take you right now to the border. Griff Jenkins is there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, we have had thousands of migrants continuing to stream across the border here in Brownsville.\par \par \uc2 And Secretary Mayorkas just visited where I am standing. We got an exclusive interview with the Border Patrol chief, Raul Ortiz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, this is what happens when you try to take Ukrainians' flag away. A Russian diplomat learning the hard way, getting punched in the face. This new video of yesterday's scuffle at a summit in Turkey just coming in.\par \par \uc2 Back in 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just days away from Title 42 essentially expiring, with little hope that it can be resurrected or extended.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Griff Jenkins at Brownsville, Texas -- Griff.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear a loud bus passing behind me. They're transporting migrants out. And we have seen more than 2,000 migrants every single day I have been here, Neil, exactly at ground zero in Brownsville, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you up to our sky drone and show you Camp Monument as we have been covering it here. You can see the buses coming and the migrants lining up upon the levee. And we had secretary of DHS, Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, along with the Border Patrol chief here, just visiting to see these operations ahead of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing. When we talk about a calm and preparation before the storm, the storm is already coming here. We have had more than 8,000 migrants every single day border-wide across the U.S. And that is really the situation we were in last year when we were breaking records.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you, Neil, some exclusive footage we shot with our FOX Flight Team early this morning before the sun was up. You can see the thermal imaging. On the bottom there is the U.S., the groups of migrants highlighted with the heat signatures on the other side, Matamoros.\par \par \uc2 They're coming in large groups of two or 300 all day, every day, 24/7. And this is the with Title 42 in place. Now, as I mentioned, we had Secretary Mayorkas here with Chief Ortiz, and he doubled down, Mayorkas, saying that, once Title 42 lifts, under this Title 8, which is the mechanism, they're going to expel people who do not qualify for relief under asylum, saying that the consequences were going to be -- quote -- "more severe" than anything we have seen before.\par \par \uc2 So we will find out with time whether or not the words match the actions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we got an exclusive interview, Neil, with Chief Ortiz, and I asked him a little bit about what we can expect to see next Thursday when Title 42 lifts. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAUL ORTIZ, U.S. BORDER PATROL CHIEF: I fully expect us to see, come May 11, 9,000 or 10,000 apprehensions a day. That's not something that we're not prepared for.\par \par \uc2 If you get upwards of that, it really starts to stress the resources that we have available to us. But I do -- I am confident that the men and women out here are doing everything they can to prepare for those increases in traffic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And the men and women in green, the Border Patrol agents, are exhausted and tired, Neil, after two years of this border crisis, but they are ready and motivated to deal with what comes.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chief Ortiz also talked to me about what's happening in Mexico. We know there are tens of thousands of migrants in the pipeline making their way up from Mexico's Southern border to our Southern border. Only time will tell how bad this is going to get -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan (sic), thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 We will be updating you on what's happening at the border and the fear that's now materializing affecting it every day tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. on our weekend show.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, want to update you on a judge releasing video from former President Trump's deposition in that rape case brought on by writer E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas has more in New York -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, this is the first time we're seeing video of former President Trump's deposition from back in October. For more than five hours, Trump was questioned at Mar-a-Lago by attorneys representing E. Jean Carroll, the magazine columnist.\par \par \uc2 Now, she accuses the former president of raping her in a dressing room of a New York City department store in the mid-'90 s. And Carroll is suing Trump for defamation and battery in a civil case. The nine-member jury watched 47 minutes of this video deposition this week.\par \par \uc2 And in this deposition, Trump dismisses Carroll as a -- quote -- "whack job" and flatly denies the allegations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It didn't happen. And, by the way, if it did happen, it would have been reported within minutes. That's a very busy store, by the way, and checkout counters and everything else, and I would be in there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's the most ridiculous -- it's the most ridiculous, disgusting story. It was just made up. But I know nothing about her. I think she's sick, mentally sick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, despite continuing to say Carroll is -- quote -- "not my type" and that he would -- quote -- "never be attracted to her," during the deposition, Trump is shown a photo and mistakes Carroll for his ex-wife, Marla Maples.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You say Marla's in this photo?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's Marla, yes. That's -- that's my wife.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Which woman are you pointing to?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Here.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, is that...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: The person you just pointed was E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, I see. Who is that? Who is this?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Your wife.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And the person -- the woman on the right is your then-wife, Ivana?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't know. This was the picture. I assume that's John Johnson. Is that...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That's Carroll.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: ... Carroll? Because it's very blurry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, Trump will not take the stand in his defense.\par \par \uc2 Carroll's lawyers, Neil, have called 11 witnesses, including two other women who accused the former president of forcibly touching them in unrelated incidents.\par \par \uc2 Now, closing arguments are set to begin Monday -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan Llenas, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, a billionaire, usually Republican backer, named \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 , who is very impressed with one Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , but says he's spending way too much time talking about things that are woke and not nearly enough time talking about Americans who are broke.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): What happens in the future? Look, people will -- we will get on that relatively soon. I mean, there's just -- you either got to put up or shut up on that as well, so we will see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, everyone keeping an eye on Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It's just a matter of when, not if he is going to run for the White House, we're told.\par \par \uc2 But Republican megadonor \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  says you got to change what you're doing if you want to get a chance. I'm paraphrasing here. But Thiel is worried that the governor has spent way too much time talking about things that are woke and not the economic issues that are the bread-and-butter ones that can win or lose elections.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has a far better take on all of that than I do. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: My far better take is only because I speak to a lot of DeSantis donors, people that meet with him, who meet with him, and here's what they're telling me.\par \par \uc2 He's a lot more explicit about running in these private conversations. He's telling people he's planning to run, that he's putting together a team. Obviously, he leaves himself a little wiggle room when he says planning, because you can always change plans, right? So that's one aspect.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect is his message. And I think this is not a guy that has been doing this for a long time, right? He's been running on a state level. What they're recommending, and they're recommending this both in private and just broadly in public, is that he diversify his message.\par \par \uc2 It's been a lot of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which isn't so bad, but you kind of played that card already. He ventured into the abortion debate, which a lot of Republican consultants say you didn't have to go there, and limit abortion even further from where it is in -- at Florida right now. Just you're not going to get that many votes from it.\par \par \uc2 The Trump people are not going to come to you on abortion. Trump himself is like Jell-O when it comes to abortion when he personally speaks about it. So, if you put all that together, they're saying, diversify your message, get some discipline, go out there and give an economic message as well, in addition to this woke stuff, and then put together a real campaign.\par \par \uc2 And it looks like he wants to put together a campaign. He's telling people that. But he does have to diversify his message. At least, that's what they're telling him. Now, whether he listens to them or not, I can't tell you. He hasn't yet, really. He's still battling with \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the \uc2 Disney\uc2  thing is not -- I mean, when I talk to Republican and donors -- and these are the sort of mainstream, sort of big- time donors that bundle money, also small business people. They can't stand \uc2 Disney\uc2 . They think \uc2 Disney\uc2 's to the left of you know what, so they want him to take it on.\par \par \uc2 But they want more than that. They want this to be a real campaign.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Back to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I got it.\par \par \uc2 From a real journalist. Thank you very much, Charlie Gasparino, best of the business...\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Any time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... when it comes to business.\par \par \uc2 All right, now, a lot of you have been saying, Neil, any updates from Adele? Well, we do have some startling updates for you on Adele. All right, they're not startling, but they're updates -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we have some news concerning Adele.\par \par \uc2 For those of you who might be tuning in late, this is her 35th birthday today. As I have been indicating, she is now old enough to run for president states. There is a little wrinkle there. She's from Britain, so she can't run for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, we have been reaching out to her to wish her a happy birthday, happy 35th, whether she ever runs for the highest office, at least in America, or not. But the plea here has been to hear from her, to get her on this show.\par \par \uc2 And our own Dion has some startling developments on that front -- Dion.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: We have gotten a response that says: "Hi, Kevin," who is a producer from "Coast to Coast," and it says: "It's not possible. Best, Benny."\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that's really in play right here, right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wait. Wait. Wait a minute. Say it again. This is Benny, a representative of Adele...\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Of Adele. And she is...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... who has said -- say it again to me.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: She's reached out to your "Coast to Coast" producer Kevin, and said: "It's not possible. Best, Benny."\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So I don't know if I take much credence in that, as in Creedence Clearwater Revival.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That doesn't sound like an outright no to me.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: No, it could very well -- that's why we have added a third phone, the red phone.\par \par \uc2 So, if Commissioner Gordon calls, we're here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: We know what's going on. But, in the meantime, I'm ready. I have been practicing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: So..\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I would not read into that cynically.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: No, not at all. No, it could be...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That sounds like, yes, it's just a matter of time to get on your set and talk to you about it, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I'm ready to mic her up if she comes in, if she's actually in New York or if she places a call. We'll get...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely. We could do it remote. She's out in Vegas, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes, we can do it via remote.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Via this phone here. We have the beige phone. So, any time, we're ready to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: We're here.\par \par \uc2 (PHONE RINGING)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed. No.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Hello?\par \par \uc2 No. No, Commissioner Gordon is not here. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, very good.\par \par \uc2 Well, Dion, thank you for that startling development.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: We're here. We're here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know, when someone says it's not possible, I can tell you from experience, oh, it's possible.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Breaking news happens happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Greg Palkot right now, meantime, at Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 He's on the other famous Brit that will be really coronated tomorrow, I guess, the big coronation.\par \par \uc2 He's no Adele, certainly, Greg, but I'm sure he's a little chagrined that Adele is not going to be performing at his coronation. But what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that's another story, Neil. I can try and call from here too, if you want.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about the coronation for just a moment, please. Just wrapped up at Buckingham...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're mocking me. You're mocking me, Greg. And I admire that, but go ahead. Yes. Sure.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: I -- not at all. She's a fine singer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Just wrapped up at the Buckingham Palace right behind us, \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , Prince William, Princess Kate hosting a reception for everybody who is showing up here, international leaders, officials and royals.\par \par \uc2 The 74-year-old king acting like he's been doing this for a while, which, in fact, he has been, assisting his mother, the late Queen Elizabeth. Earlier in the day, the king was at Westminster Abbey, where the coronation will be held tomorrow. The ceremony is set to be modernized and streamlined, but, still, it runs about two hours and it is full of ritual, regalia and pageantry from about -- oh, about 1,000 years of royalty.\par \par \uc2 Representing the U.S., first lady Dr. Jill Biden. The president had to beg off in an earlier call to the king. One of her stops today was at Number 10 Downing Street, where she was greeted by the wife of U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also today, we had a chance to get up close to both the king and his fans. Take a look what we saw, what we heard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: King Charles doing a bit of a walkabout with the people just nearby Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 The folks are happy and looking forward to the big day, coronation day, tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: They're cheering him on.\par \par \uc2 Do you love it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I love it. And it's fun to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A moment in history I wanted to be a part of.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're here for the coronation, a once-in-a-lifetime event.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Now, Neil, there is a lot of reporting too that \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 We know for a fact that Meghan will not be coming. And it is hoped that perhaps a little bit of the controversy they have been stirring up will be left behind as well.\par \par \uc2 As for the concert, you have got Lionel Richie playing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: You got Katy Perry, Andrea Bocelli, and a very big favorite of mine, Steve Winwood. He did that great song "Give Me Some Love," and he did it when \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  was 17 years old. So we might hear that song.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, you're right. Adele and a few others, Elton John, Sheeran, et cetera, are no-shows -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, no spice from the Spice Girls. They're not there, but whatever. We will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 You're the best, Greg. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot in Her Majesty's kingdom, His Majesty's kingdom. It's the king now, right?\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Hilary Fordwich, the global business consultant, royal commentator. We got Nile Gardiner back with us, former adviser to one Maggie Thatcher.\par \par \uc2 Hilary, much has been made back and forth about, I guess, who's not there, not only \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 , but all these British pop singers who, for one reason or another, just couldn't make the day and the event.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything to read into that?\par \par \uc2 HILARY FORDWICH, ROYAL WATCHER: A pleasure to be back with you, Neil, and great to be with Nile again.\par \par \uc2 Neil, again, a few things here. No, actually, I think it's more important to focus on the positive, although I will say, with regard to somebody like Elton John, who's not there, don't forget, of course, he was very close to the late Princess Diana.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: So that's a legitimate reason. I think there's some things that are obviously very understandable.\par \par \uc2 I do think, with regard to \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  though, he will be in row 10. And the previous photographs you showed with there the new king at Buckingham Palace tonight, there was Major Johnny Thompson, very interesting, I spotted in the background in his regalia.\par \par \uc2 You will see, actually, he's actually a bodyguard, and he was placed behind \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  and \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  when they appeared for the platinum jubilee, always is there for security. Be interesting to see if he's there to keep a close eye on \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and see if he behaves tomorrow in Westminster Abbey.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Nile, how do you think this is all going to go down?\par \par \uc2 NILE GARDINER, FORMER ADVISER TO BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: Well, thanks very much for having on the show, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I think the coronation is going to be a huge and massive success. The streets of London are absolutely full. You have literally, I think, hundreds of thousands, if not millions of people from around the world who are in London at the moment to attend or to really celebrate, I think, the coronation.\par \par \uc2 I have to say it's a very good thing, I think, that \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  is not actually at the coronation. I think she would have been booed on the streets of London. So there's a very good reason why Meghan is not here. Harry is going to make a very quick exit. Apparently, within two hours of the main ceremony, he's heading back to California, which many have seen as very disrespectful here in London.\par \par \uc2 Also missing, of course, along with \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Biden is absent from the coronation. I do think this is a very rude signal that Biden is sending to the British people, a lot of unhappiness certainly on the ground in the U.K. with regard to Biden not bothering to attend.\par \par \uc2 And Biden is seen by many Brits as very anti-British.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hilary, I'm wondering about how this is received among the British people.\par \par \uc2 You hear that King Charles is not thought of in the same way his mother was, that he's got to fight that back. But his poll ratings still seem pretty high.\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do they like him thus far?\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: Yes, a few points there to go with what Nile just said about the British feelings.\par \par \uc2 It's rather ironic, actually, because Biden is an English name. His ancestors emigrated from West Sussex in England.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: And yet he dwells on this Irish heritage. And it's because, when he was younger, he had a stutter, and his family, his mother made him memorize these Irish poems that were very anti-English.\par \par \uc2 So that's where I think that sentiment comes as a boy. That's number won. But also with regard to then the polls, yes, actually, if we all remember, right after his mother passed, Queen Elizabeth, I believe, ubiquitously, you heard and saw and the polls reflected that his speech right after she passed, every single tone of it was absolutely as it should have been.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: And it was universally well-received.\par \par \uc2 So his poll numbers, yes, are good. The issue is, though, that the younger generation -- none of us sitting here right now are over 60. But those people that are over 60 are -- really think the world of him. And he's got over an 80 percent rating.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 FORDWICH: It's the younger under-35s that don't. That's the issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. All right, guys, well, one of us is over 60, but I digress.It's always great seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Enjoy tomorrow, of course, FOX's coverage with Martha and everyone else. Of course, we will be picking up at the baton at 10:00 a.m., the implications of all of that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, thank you for having fun with us about Adele.\par \par \uc2 And, Adele, all you have to do is call. Just call me.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230505ej550002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pentagon To Deploy Troops Ahead Of The Title 42 Deadline; Biden To Meet With Congressional Leaders Next Tuesday; Carroll Says Movement Inspired Her To Come Forward; Bipartisan Push For Stricter Social Media Settings To Protect Minors Online; Sen. \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  (D-AZ) And Sen. \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  (R-OK) Being Interviewed About The End Of Title 42; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Sending 1,500 U.S. Troops To Southern Border; Sen. \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  (I-AZ) And Sen. \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  (R-OK) Discuss Possible Bipartisan Efforts To Deal With Immigration Crisis At U.S. Southern Border; Recipients Of \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Memorial Scholarship Profiled; Biden Administration To Send 1,500 Troops To U.S. Southern Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Mike Emanuel, Bryan Llenas, Kelly  O'Grady, Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 7,957 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Check out Chewie. He's on a kayak, and he's so relaxed. He keeps falling asleep. He's like, what do you think is so funny? But anyway, don't fall into the water, Chewie.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Excellent work. You know, Jessica, I think we're out of time. So, I think you're going to have to wait until some other time to do your "ONE MORE THING."\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Tomorrow, maybe.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: A-block.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know. What do you think, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Yes, tomorrow. We can -- we can do it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OK, let's do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden is ordering 1,500 U.S. military personnel to the southern border. The move comes ahead of next week's exploration of pandemic-related asylum rules, Title 42.\par \par \uc2 A huge migrant surge is now expected. White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live in the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, sending 1,500 troops on a 90-day deployment undermines recent claims by officials here, the President Biden's immigration plan has been working. And the Pentagon is telling us tonight that using active-duty troops instead of the National Guard will not affect readiness.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: I think we'll see these troops arrive as early as May 10th.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's despite recent claims the border is secure.\par \par \uc2 You said yesterday that when it comes to illegal migration, you've seen it come down by more than 90 percent. Where did that number come from if the CBP is telling us the number is a 136,000 people more this fiscal year so far.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It was -- I was speaking - - I hear you. I'm about to answer. I'm about -- I'm about to answer you, if you -- if you -- if the dramatics could come down just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I (INAUDIBLE) it.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If the dramatics could come down a little bit.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What's dramatic about asking a question about --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: OK. I'm going to answer. So, I was speaking to the parolee program.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Pentagon says troops will be armed for self-defense, not law enforcement. Laying out their mission like this.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Fill critical capability gaps, such as ground based detection and monitoring, data entry, and warehouse support.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As the Pentagon preps for a migrant surge, lawmakers are trying to avoid a debt default as soon as June 1st.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): There's no reason for us to have the drama that we have right now.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: House Democrats are trying to go around Republicans and raise the debt limit with a procedural measure. House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  writes colleagues, "House Democrats are working to make sure we have all options at our disposal to avoid a default."\par \par \uc2 Republicans argue, it's the president's move.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): We got a choice between accepting the House bill or entering into a discussion, which the Speaker has been trying to have.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But that discussion may not include talk about spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: He is not going to negotiate on the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports the White House is exploring alternatives, "including one option that previous administrations had deemed unthinkable. That option is effectively a constitutional challenge to the debt limit." That's even though the Constitution is at the heart of their argument.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It is Congress's constitutional obligation to act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): There are no plans, we are told for President Biden to reschedule a trip abroad right before the debt ceiling deadline.\par \par \uc2 And we don't expect there to be any movement towards a resolution for another week when the President hosts the big four here at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Officials in the administration talking about the debt limit with great urgency. But their schedule does not reflect any of that urgency with less than a month to go. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live on the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will talk about the border and what's next with two senators during our "COMMON GROUND" segment, a little bit later in this show.\par \par \uc2 Banking sector worries sent stocks down today. The Dow lost 367. The S&P 500 was off 48. The NASDAQ fell 132.\par \par \uc2 It was one year ago that someone leaked a draft version of a landmark \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision on abortion. That triggered protests and threats.\par \par \uc2 So far, no one has been named as the culprit. Although, one justice says he has a pretty good idea who it is.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  focused not on the leak but on ethics reform with the justices.\par \par \uc2 Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Much of the debate in today's Senate hearing focused on whether Congress has the authority to impose ethics rules on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): The Supreme Court should step up and fix this themselves. For years, they've refused. And because the court will not act, Congress must.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Louisiana Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  says this push is all about politics.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): The danger isn't that rogue justices are operating without ethics. It's that Democrats aren't winning every fight and they find that reality intolerable.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  was invited to testify but declined last week. In his response, Roberts notes, congressional testimony by the chief justice of the United States is exceedingly rare due to separation of powers concerns.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are pushing for the court to be held to a higher standard of ethics, following reports that some of the justices and their families, most notably \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , have received luxury gifts, property purchases, and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thomas says -- Thomas says!\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Republicans fired back that Thomas was being singled out, because the political left hates him.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): That's the standard going and traveling and being paid for by others. Then, guess what? Just about every Supreme Court justice has done so, and done so in much greater numbers.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The Wall Street Journal editorial board warrant, "This is another front in the political campaign to delegitimize the Supreme Court, with a goal of tarnishing its rulings and subjecting it to more political control."\par \par \uc2 A former attorney general under President Bush, who also served as a federal judge blamed attacks on the court for doing damage.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MUKASEY, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: If the public has a mistaken impression that the integrity of the Court has been damaged, the fault for that lies for those who continue to level unfair criticism at the Court and its justices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All nine justices agreed to adhere to their existing code of conduct in a joint statement that was released last week. Some Republicans suggest today's effort was designed to intimidate the Supreme Court. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The woman who says former President Trump raped her in a department store dressing room almost 30 years ago is revealing why she finally came forward with her accusation.\par \par \uc2 23 is suing the former president for defamation. Today, more elicit testimony. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has denied all of the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas has the latest from outside the Manhattan courthouse this evening. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Today, the author 3 testified to corroborate allegations made by her friend, E. Jean Carroll, who accuses former President Trump of raping her in the mid-1990s.\par \par \uc2 Birnbach testified that Carroll, well, called her one night in 1996 and said Trump had allegedly raped her just five to seven minutes before the call.\par \par \uc2 Birnbach said Carroll was breathless and hyperventilating as she described how Trump recognized her outside the department store, \uc2 Bergdorf Goodman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He allegedly asked for Carroll's help shopping, and then, she followed Trump into a dressing room of the lingerie department.\par \par \uc2 Birnbach testified, I was surprised, she did that. I thought it was kind of nutty. Birnbach then said, "E. Jean said to me many times he pulled down my tights, he pulled down my tights."\par \par \uc2 And an attempt to bolster Carroll's case, 81-year-old Jessica Leeds also testified today. Leeds alleges Trump suddenly tried to kiss and grope her on a plane in the 1970s. Trump denies the allegations by both women.\par \par \uc2 Carroll, a magazine columnist took the stand for three days as Trump attorney Joe Tacopina tried to poke holes in her story, questioning her inability to remember the exact date of the alleged rape. Accusing Carroll and her friends have scheming to create a false story about Trump with whom they disagreed with politically, and asking why Carroll did not call 911 after the attack and why she did not scream.\par \par \uc2 The 79-year-old said she was a member of the silent generation and was too ashamed to come forward until she was inspired by the MeToo movement. The judge rejected Tacopina's request yesterday for a mistrial based on the judge's "unfair and prejudicial" rulings.\par \par \uc2 Today, we found out former President Trump will definitely not take the stand. In his defense, this is a civil, not a criminal case. And Carroll accuses Trump of defamation in battery and she is seeking for Trump to pay punitive damages.\par \par \uc2 An anonymous jury of nine will decide. Closing arguments, Bret, are expected on Monday. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Bryan Llenas, live in New York City. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A White House probe is underway about how companies are using artificial intelligence to monitor and manage their employees.\par \par \uc2 It's just the latest concern being expressed over a technology, many now fear but few understand, at least fully. Some of the fear comes from some of the most prominent engineers and scientists who have been working to create the technology.\par \par \uc2 Here is Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As the artificial intelligence field explodes, so does the chorus of alarm bells. Industry experts warning A.I. could facilitate a host of challenges to our society.\par \par \uc2 For example, cyberattacks could become simpler to execute. Countries or corporations could more easily surveil individuals. Bots like ChatGPT could mislead users with biased information, or even compromised data users share with it.\par \par \uc2 Samsung\uc2  is now banning the chatbot after employees accidentally uploaded sensitive information. Another key concern is how A.I. will impact a struggling economy. Many companies see an opportunity to increase efficiency by replacing 1000s of jobs with the tech.\par \par \uc2 IBM\uc2  CEO told Fox Business many roles could soon be obsolete.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ARVIND KRISHNA, CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS MACHINES\uc2 : A.I. is going to replace many clerical white-collar jobs. And that's the kind, which I expect A.I. will replace over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: These growing concerns are driving a push to regulate the space. The White House issued a memo announcing an investigation into how companies use A.I. to evaluate workers.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, Speaker McCarthy is launching an effort to educate Congress on A.I. But not everyone is convinced lawmakers are equipped to tackle a technology that's still evolving.\par \par \uc2 ALEC ROSS, AUTHOR, THE RAGING 2020S: My kids' public high school is full of people who understand technology a lot better than the United States' Congress. And so, I'm very skeptical. Remember who will do the regulating.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: A new Fox News poll echoes that sentiment. 59 percent of all voters have little or no confidence in the government's ability to properly regulate A.I.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Some point to Washington's lack of success in regulating spaces like cryptocurrency and social media, being more reactive to a crisis than proactive. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly, thank you. Up next, the push to keep dangerous social media content away from young people. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 But first, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX11 in Los Angeles says film and television writers begin their strike. It comes after negotiations between their union and major studios failed to reach an agreement. Writers are seeking increases in pay and residuals, particularly over streaming content.\par \par \uc2 FOX2 in St. Louis says a section of Interstate 55 in Illinois reopens following that deadly series of crashes that killed at least six people.\par \par \uc2 Officials blame a windstorm that kicked up clouds of blinding dust from farm fields. More than 70 vehicles were involved in that accident.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Salt Lake City from FOX13, our affiliate. One of the big stores there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Utah is named the best state in new rankings. U.S. News and World Report's says Utah beat out Washington, which had held the title for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 More than 70 metrics are considered including crime, lack of crime, the economy, education, and natural environment.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden's team is ignoring a congressional subpoena for documents relating to the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan for the third time.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is refusing to provide the information. It includes a dissent cable from the U.S. Embassy in Kabul, which would have contained any concerns officials had about the withdrawal plans.\par \par \uc2 The State Department instead offered to brief the House Foreign Affairs Committee on the document's contents. That was accepted, but the Chairman Michael McCaul, said that action did not constitute complying with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan effort is underway tonight to try to protect children on social media. Two senators have reintroduced legislation that would require sites to make it tougher for young people to access dangerous content.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has details tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): David Molak was just 16 years old when he took his own life, a victim of relentless cyberbullying, his mom says, ran rampant on social media.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN MOLAK, DAVID MOLAK'S MOTHER: They were targeting his appearance, threatened him, and told him he should never go back to school, put him in a body bag, put him six feet under.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: His story is one of the many reasons why Republican Senator Marshall Blackburn and Democrat Senator Richard Blumenthal are putting politics aside and reintroducing their Kids Online Safety Act, or KOSA, to give parents more control over what their kids see.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): This is how they're meeting drug dealers. It's how they're learning about how to commit suicide and music to commit suicide to.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The bill requires social media platforms to default to stricter settings for users 17 and under. It would also let parents disable features that are addictive and opt out of recommendations made by powerful algorithms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): This idea is not red or blue. It's not Republican or Democrat. There's no red or blue algorithm driving toxic content.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But the pair face pushback. Last year, about 100 advocacy groups sent a letter to Senate leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  opposing the bill, claiming it could limit a child's access to sex education or resources for LGBTQ Plus Youth.\par \par \uc2 But Blumenthal says he and Blackburn have updated the bill, limiting potential harmful content to substance abuse, suicide, and eating disorders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And despite all that, Bret, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  tonight says they are still not happy with this bill.\par \par \uc2 And the meantime, Fox did directly ask leader Schumer today if he would support this particular bill. He would only go as far as to say that he would like to see some kind of child protections in an online bill, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 300 people have been arrested in a record-breaking international drug enforcement operation targeting fentanyl and opioid traffickers on the dark web.\par \par \uc2 Here's Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Drug traffickers are increasingly turning to the dark web to sell illegal drugs in exchange for cryptocurrency. They may advertise their drugs on dark net marketplaces as brand name pharmaceuticals.\par \par \uc2 But in reality, the pills are often counterfeit and laced with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Justice Department says it made 288 arrest, seized 117 illegal firearms, almost 19,000 pounds of drugs and more than $53 million in cash and digital currencies.\par \par \uc2 Loneliness is nearly as deadly as smoking up to 15 cigarettes per day. That's according to the U.S. Surgeon General Vivek Murthy. His report states about half of U.S. adults say they have experienced loneliness. The Surgeon General is calling on workplaces, schools and other community organizations to help boost the country's connectedness.\par \par \uc2 Surgeon General will join us tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT to discuss this and other topics.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we seek common ground on the crisis at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight, Ukraine's president says he has not talked with anyone at the White House about the recent leak of classified material.\par \par \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  tells the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  the disclosures were damaging to both the U.S. and his country, and he had no warning that they were coming.\par \par \uc2 A man is arrested for allegedly throwing shotgun cartridges onto the grounds of Buckingham Palace just days ahead of King Charles' coronation. Police say the suspect was detained this morning after he approached the gates of Buckingham Palace and threw those cartridges onto the palace grounds.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Portugal. One of the stories there tonight, Portuguese police sees 4.2 tons of cocaine coming from Latin America. They say the drugs were hidden in a shipment of bananas that originated from Colombia. No arrests have been announced so far.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is time for this week's Common Ground segment. Joining us tonight, Arizona Independent Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  and Oklahoma Republican Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 . Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. KYRSTEN SINEMA (D-AZ): Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are working together on trying to figure out the border situation. Obviously you're very familiar with it in your home state, but it's really every state that's affected. Title 42 coming to an end. Is there an answer here in the coming days?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: Well there should be an answer. You know, the administration has known for two years that the end of Title 42 is coming. It was never intended to be a long-term solution.\par \par \uc2 And James and I together as the chair and top ranking member of the Border Subcommittee and the Senate have been calling on the administration to prepare for the end of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Bret, I'm sad to tell you that they haven't done it. They have not prepared for the end of Title 42, which comes in just a week and a half.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what happens? Like for all the folks, we've been covering this obviously for a long, long time with folks down there on the border and showing it. But what happens when this thing expires?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): So, there's about too many people a year that are coming illegally across the border. Most of them are across the border and then saying I fear in my country, which is what the cartels have told them to say, and then they are allowed to be able to come in the country and request an asylum hearing. That asylum hearing, depending on where they request it, will be about 18 years before it's actually settled in the future. And so, they're free to go anywhere they want to, working where they want to in the country. It's the pull factor into the country that you know that.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, Title 42 has only turned around about 30 percent of those folks. So, Title 42, when it ends, is kind of the last barrier that's out there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's what the Biden administration is saying. Here's what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: It is my testimony that the border is secure and we are working every day, day and night to increase its security.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: When it comes to illegal immigration, you've seen it come down by more than 90 percent. And that's because of the actions that this president has taken.\par \par \uc2 But we know that more action needs to be taken. So, it has to be legislative action. We're going to continue to call Congress to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: So, obviously, the border is not secure. Anyone with eyes can see that. And anyone who lives in a border state like I do, born and raised in Arizona, actually it takes offense at comments like that because they're just factually not true.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that border communities in my state are suffering already, and that's before the end of Title 42. Just last week I held a hearing in our border subcommittee and brought a Republican mayor and a Democratic mayor, both representing small border towns from Arizona, and we just asked them what is it like every day in your town? And Bret, there are high-speed chases going on through the city of Sierra Vista right now with young teens driving cars and sometimes being killed or causing the death of others, transporting drugs and other migrants through these communities. It is not secure. It is not safe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So when you hear the administration is sending 1,500 troops down there to the border, what's your response to that?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: Well, 1,500 troops should be helpful. Now, I will say we got that announcement this morning. My team has asked for a briefing. We have got no information about what it's going to look like. But 1,500 bodies is better than not 1,500 bodies. But no one can claim that that is going to be enough. The reality is that we're seeing over 1,000 single male adults come through the Tucson sector every single day. We don't have enough buses to transport those individuals to other parts of the country. We don't have enough holding facilities to keep them incarcerated until a disposition has been made. We don't have the space, the money, or the person power to address this crisis. So 1,500 people will help, but we both know it's nowhere near enough.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The past 72 hours, Chief Raul Ortiz, chief of U.S. Border Patrol said two agents were assaulted, 22,000 apprehensions, 806 pounds of methamphetamine, 283 pounds of marijuana, 62 pounds of cocaine, three firearms, fire maritime events, to sex offenders, to tractor-trailer events, one convicted murder, one gang member. This is in the past 72 hours.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2 , (R-OK): And, again, that's just a sampling of what they actually interdicted. That's not even close to the thousands of people that are the getaways they get in between. While Border Patrol is engaging in one area doing arrests, the cartels actually put the real criminal individuals in a different area.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So it's like a water balloon. It's like you hit down one spot, and they go to someplace else.\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: It is. And it's a big issue for us. And we are trying to be able to push back and say be serious about this and actually engage. And when they throw out numbers like they did, 90 percent of the folks we've had a decline. What they mean is certain countries, the actual number of people crossing the border goes up. But they pull these statistics out and try to convince people, hey, look, it's better. Don't believe your eyes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, so what needs to happen. You are working together, but what has to happen now? The comprehensive thing has never really transpired up on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It just hasn't happened.\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: It's so big that it's hard and unwieldy to get done. But the reality is there are a lot of bipartisan plans that we have been working on with some of our colleagues in the Senate and the House that could actually make a difference. It would be most helpful if the administration would start by actually enforcing the laws that are on the books. That would provide confidence, particularly to Republican members of the House and Senate that we could get something done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can you do something around the edges that would make a difference?\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: The edges won't make a difference on this. We really do, the crux of the issue is the asylum law itself.\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: That's right.\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: If we don't control this -- if you go into Canada right now past the United States, illegally cross the border into Canada, they will immediately ask you, did you request asylum in the United States. If you say no, they will turn you around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're are leading a bipartisan CODEL to the Arizona border at the end of the month?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So on the politics side, the administration sometimes is unhappy with you. You are an Independent. You don't -- you are not caucusing with anybody. As you look towards an election, how do you see that?\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: You know, Bret, I think this is no surprise to Arizonans. I'm happy to show this to the rest of the country. That's not what I'm thinking about. What I'm thinking about is what it looks like on the ground for my folks in Arizona every day. When I was on the phone earlier today with the folks in Gila Bend, that mayor had to put migrants in his car and drive them to Phoenix in the last couple years because they were just left in his small town that has one stoplight. So I'm not thinking about an election. What I'm thinking about is the mayor of Gila Bend and how to help him and his community.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Would you say the message to people at home there are Democrats, independents, Republicans working together.\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: I know the media is easy to show two people yelling at each other. It makes for better TV to have one person nodding and one person shaking their head. I get that. But there are people that are serious that are sitting down trying to be able to work through that are bringing real solutions to the administration, saying here are some ideas that would actually work, that are trying to be able to work together on all aspects of the immigration proposal, to say here are logical, reasonable solutions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what "Common Ground" has been about. We appreciate you taking the time today.\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 LANKFORD: Good to see you. Thanks for doing it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You can see all the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground". There is also "The All-Star Panel" podcast, falls under the umbrella the Bret Baier podcasts, FOXNewsPodcasts.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can see the whole extended discussion of this and others on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the winners of the Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Scholarship. Plus, we will talk live with Charles son Daniel.\par \par \uc2 But as we go to break, singer Gordon Lightfoot has died. He wrote and performed such hits as "Carefree Highway" and "The Wreck of the Edmund Fitzgerald." A family representative says the Canadian artist died at a Toronto hospital Monday. Gordon Lightfoot was 84.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  appeared on our program regularly over the years, and we miss his input and guidance every day. But his legacy lives on as he helps young minds grow in their own journeys in higher education. Tonight, we're honored to announce the recipients of this year's Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Memorial Scholarship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Education was always important to Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why did you choose psychiatry?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER\uc2 , SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: I was looking for something halfway between the reality of medicine and the elegance, if you like, of philosophy. So psychiatry was the obvious thing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles graduated at the top of his class at Harvard. But he decided to change paths and become a journalist, which ultimately brought him to Washington and eventually to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Just remember when you go to the Candidate Casino you go there not to make friends but to make money.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We treasured every moment, especially his commentary which cut through the noise and still remains relevant today.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: The Chinese, when they get upset, can do things, like take over an island here or there or tear up an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  unleashed, it's a pity he will be gone because it will be interesting and fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles died in 2018. But his devotion to education lives on through a scholarship program for children of FOX News employees working in partnership with the National Merit Scholarship Program. This year's recipients include Kate Parker whose father Josh is a video editor. Just like Charles, Kate plans to study multiple fields.\par \par \uc2 KATE PARKER, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP RECIPIENT: Being active in several disciplines is something that's really special and important to me, and I do think that I connect to him on that level. Being offered the opportunity to study something so different as dance and environmental studies, that's just really special to me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben Fuchs wants to pursue a career in engineering. His mother Debra (ph) is the director of marketing, promotions, and partnerships. Ben is also eager to continue playing violin now with \uc2 Penn State\uc2 's orchestra.\par \par \uc2 BEN FUCHS, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP RECIPIENT: It's such an honor winning this scholarship in Dr. Krauthammer's memory. He valued thoughtful research and analysis, which is very important to me as a future engineer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congratulations to this year's winners. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  said his father inspired him always to know everything, learn everything. Charles is passing that gift on to the next generation of leaders through this memorial scholarship. What better way to honor the memory of Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 , joining me is Daniel Krauthammer, Charles's son, himself a writer. It is so great to see you. Every year it is so fun to be able to do this, and I'm sure it touches a chord with you and your family.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: Yes. It's a wonderful thing that you and Suzanne Scott and the FOX team has set up and that you continue every year, this wonderful honoring of my father's legacy, his love of learning, which was just core to who he was in every way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Has your mom, Robyn, she had some health issues this year?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: Yes, she was diagnosed with breast cancer last year around this time. And it's a tough year in a lot of ways, but we got a great team of doctors together, and they got my mom through it. And like my dad, she is an incredibly strong person. And just last week, she finished the last of her treatments and, fingers crossed, she has now got it beat and will be cancer free the rest of her life. So we're breathing a lot easier now and it's a wonderful bonus to be able to celebrate that with you here as we honor my dad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Please give her our best. I know she is watching. We wish you well.\par \par \uc2 You know what is amazing is that every time we talk about major issues, there is some soundbite, something that your dad just hits, and it really still resonates. You know, go back all the way to when he is writing in foreign affairs in 1990. This is the unipolar moment. "The most striking feature of the post Cold War world is it's unipolarity. No doubt multipolarity will come in time, and perhaps another generation or so there will be great powers co-equal with the United States and the world were in structure resemble the pre World War I era. But we are not there yet, nor will we be there yet nor decades. Now is the unipolar moment." That's 1990. It's striking.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: It really is. And I think it goes to what you always say about my dad, that he saw through the noise. It relates to this level of learning, his deep knowledge of history, geopolitics, economics, that back then in 1990, and this is even before the Gulf War when it was fashionable to say the Cold War would yield a multipolar world, he was the first to see clearly that it would be defined by American power and unipolarity, and that that would be the question he wrestled with for what he then said would be 30 or 40 years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And now you have this rise of China and the relationship between China and Russia which he also talked about. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER\uc2 , SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: The fact that the Chinese and the Russians have been trying to change everything since the end of the cold war and to reassert themselves and not to insert themselves in a world system in which the U.S. is dominant is a plain and obvious fact. The Chinese are trying to drive us out of the Pacific Rim. And the Russians, of course, are challenging \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion, our alliance with -- semi alliance with Ukraine, and protection of the Baltic states. But that's the challenge for the United States in this era.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we so miss his voice. By of the way, you look a little bit like him, just in case you didn't know that.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's amazing, though. He hits a lot of chords.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: No, he does. He really saw through to what the underlying tectonics of the world system work. And he saw what the future challenge was beyond the day-to-day incursions, what the rival powers, rising powers like China and Russia, were really trying to do, which was to up end the world system that America created the last 30 years that my generation has grown up thinking is normal, as he often pointed out, but that the peace, prosperity, and stability that we all grew up with, my dad always pointed out, is very unnatural. And it's only something that lasts because of American power, and that's why he argued so strongly and so cogently for extending that unipolar moment as long as America could.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If someone wants to read your dad, look at some of this stuff, you have a website?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: Yes. His website, CharlesKrauthammer.com. It has articles focused on Russia, Ukraine going back to 2014 that read as if they were written yesterday. And they are also in his books and columns and essays from years' past.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, listen, I do this every year, but there is a seat right here on the panel if you're ready.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: I appreciate that so much. And I would love to when I can. And with my mom's health in the clear, life does feel -- and she actually said just the other day that tough things happen, but sometimes life wonders really opens up. So we are both feeling that and very thankful, and thank you so much for doing this and having me on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's so great to see you. And, again, give Robyn our best.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: I will.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on developments of the U.S. troops heading to the southern border and the crisis there.\par \par \uc2 But first, today's throwback to the raid that killed \uc2 Usama bin Laden\uc2 , terror leader. U.S. Navy Seals raided a Pakistani compound where bin Laden was living. The raid began 1:00 a.m. local time, May 2nd, 2011. The terrorist death came 10 years after the 9/11 attacks. His body was given an Islamic burial in the North Arabian Sea.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: When it comes to illegal migration, you have seen it calm down by more than 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Where did that number come from, because CBP is telling us the number --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I hear you. I'm about to answer.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: -- is 136,000 people more this fiscal year so far.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If you -- if the dramatics could come down just a little bit. If the dramatics could come down just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What's dramatic about asking a question about --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: OK, so I'm about to answer. So I was speaking to the parolee program. As you know, the president put in place a parolee program to deal with -- to deal with certain countries on ways that we can limit illegal migration. And we have seen, the data has shown us it has gone down by more than 90 percent. That was what I was speaking to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: White House press secretary two consecutive days answering a question on a re-follow up, 1,500 U.S. troops heading to the southern border. That story broken by our own Jennifer Griffin today. And what happens next as Title 42 is lifted?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast and former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist."\par \par \uc2 Mollie, it is interesting to hear Senator Sinema, Senator Lankford, they are singing from the same sheet of music, and it's not what the administration is saying.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": What the administration is doing is an admission what they have done for the last several years is a huge failure. This border was not secure at the time that Biden took office, but we were getting there. And the Biden administration willfully took action to remove the security at the border and to create the problem that we have now. And you can tell by \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  trying to argue differently that you might expect a Democrat that it's a huge political issue for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting that she corrected me to independent a couple of times.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the anticipated end of Title 42, and there are a lot of questions about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: These 1,500 military personnel who will be sourced from the active duty component will fill critical capability gaps such as ground based detection and monitoring, data entry, and warehouse support until CPB can address these needs through contracted support. Military personnel will not directly participate in law enforcement activities.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: If Congress would act and, again, do their jobs and meet us halfway, and do this in a bipartisan way, we would not have to do this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about that answer? It's getting kind of stale, isn't it, Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I don't think so. I think it's the truth. I think that the parties are unable, paralyzed, unable to come up with a solution to what is an American problem. The problem at the border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But isn't the president of the United States who leads on these issues?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Sure. The thing is you have to have someone to talk with who is willing to be led. And if you have people who decided, and if they are Democrats here as well as Republicans who say they prefer the political issue back and forth, finger pointing and blame game, to real solutions. And we see that with the selfies and grandstanding. I find it frustrating. But I think --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But I do find some senators, like those two on tonight, that they are trying to find solutions.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The thing is, when you see them, I worry that they are playing politics still. But I would like them to actually say here's an idea about where we can find the common ground and come together. I just think at the moment what you see here is Biden needs to act. Why? Because I think there are electoral votes in California, Arizona, the border states, that are potentially his, but he has got to show that he is doing something. Sending the additional troops, I think, is an effort by the president to meet the power of your question, which is you're the president, do something.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: Look, it didn't require Congress for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to take meaningful action to secure the border. In fact, it took willful negligence on the part of this administration to undo all of that progress. And by the way, we were reliably told for a year-and-a- half that there was no border drivers at all. So, amazing to see now that all of a sudden we have got a problem, I think you said something interesting there, Juan, to show that he is doing something at the border. I think that's more about what the politics of this are. Now they've got a political problem. And that's how they have dealt with this entire border issue rather than actually trying to just secure the border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, he hasn't really visited there that much. He hasn't really made it an issue that he is talking about on the pulpit, the president can have a megaphone.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Everything -- I mean, this is someone who for years has worked to destabilize the situation there. I agree with you, actually, Juan, that it's a bipartisan problem that Congress needs to act. And they actually didn't do as much as they should have during the previous administration. But at heart, this is about on day one, removing some of those protections that were in place, now freaking out because we have a crisis on our hands.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think there was the feeling when the Trump administration ended that some of these acts were in violation of American values in terms of how you treat people and how we receive people and separate families. I can go on. I don't want to repeat that argument. But I just want to say that a lot of this, in fact, is about the fact we as Americans can see people can come and seek asylum legitimately.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: No, that -- we have always been a country like that. What is happening at the border is completely inhumane. We have drugs crossing the border. We have let the cartels completely take care of who is coming in and who is coming out. These are not good organizations.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Human trafficking, fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: There are human rights abuses all over the place, sexual abuse of women and children.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We have no argument.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And we say that oh, to control our border is a violation of who we are. To not control our border is to allow so many human rights abuses.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So we have to find a way. And the question is, can we make a deal? I think Republicans are always insistent on the security first. And I think what you are seeing is Democrats saying, we have to find a way that we can properly handle this flow. Josh, believe me this flow was happening during Obama. It was happening during Bush. It's not unique to this administration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, it's true. And I said the big comprehensive immigration bill has never worked. I mean, you have been a part of that.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It just gets clogged up up there. But something has to happen.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Something has to happen. But again, you can take meaningful steps to prevent this situation all in the name of humanitarianism. Do we create a full human trafficking and legalized slavery in this country where we are sending unaccompanied minors to employers to work in sweat shops? That was done because we weren't being compassionate enough. You have got wonder a lot about the policies that these folks have put in place.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, there is so much to talk about. I really wanted to get to A.I. because it scares the heck out of me. But we will get there another day. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the surgeon general joins us talking about that op-ed on loneliness and other issues. Busy show today. We got a lot in there.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Because Jesse is just back, I wanted to give him 10 extra seconds. Put it in the bank.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230503ej5200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Gov. DeSantis Takes Foreign Trip and Courts Donors Ahead of Possible 2024 Bid; Fox News Polls on 2024 Race; The State of the High Court One Year after the Dobbs Leak; Interview with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 ; Interview with Sen. \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  (D-MD)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jason Chaffetz, Marie Harf, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,307 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Now that President Biden is in, we know a lot more about how Democrats feel about him as their front runner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The race for the White House is on. The president framing his case for a second term and dismissing concerns about his age.\par \par \uc2 And another Republican officially jumps into the GOP primary, challenging former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm a candidate for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As Trump himself barnstorms New Hampshire still firmly leading the field.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're 51 percent with all these people running. We're up 33 points.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  about her party's plan to win back the White House, and whether we're truly in for a 2020 rematch.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I do look forward to the debate with Joe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 And then, House Republicans claimed victory, passing a debt limit bill. Speaker McCarthy again demanding that president come to the table. Many Senate Democrats now agree, saying it's time for the president to get real about a deal to avoid default. We will sit down with Maryland Democrat \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  for the latest on negotiations.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , U.S. SUPREME COURT ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: You begin to look over your shoulder.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And one year since the leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion on abortion, the trail running cold. We'll bring in a group of legal insiders on the leak's lasting impact inside the secretive world of the nation's highest court, as it faces new ethics questions.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on a world tour to boost his foreign policy creds, as his spat with \uc2 Disney\uc2  escalates and his poll numbers fall.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel is here on his chances as we wait to find out whether he's running.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Now that both President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are officially running in 2024, get ready to hear the word rematch a lot.\par \par \uc2 The polls indicate voters in both parties are not so enthusiastic about two front runners. Many telling pollsters they would be open to alternatives.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  joins us in studio on the GOP field and the biggest issues the party faces heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson live in Washington with a look at the president's strategy as he asked for a second term.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, after months of anticipation, President Biden made it official, announcing he will be running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let's finish this job. I know we can.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden's campaign launch opens with January 6, abortion protests and MAGA, including an image of former President Trump and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Biden mentions the word "freedom" six times in a three-minute video. He did not mention his age.\par \par \uc2 But the age question came up in a press briefing.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Is the president plan to serve all eight years?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: That's something for him to decide.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The press secretary cleared up the confusion later, tweeting the president would serve all eight years.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 Republican candidate Nikki Haley did not mince words.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The idea that he would make it until 86 years old is not -- is not something that I think is likely.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And potential Republican presidential candidate, Senator Tim Scott, appeared to go there, too.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, that should be a scary proposition. The only thing worse than a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  presidency is a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  presidency.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ladies and gentlemen, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Here in D.C., at the White House correspondents' dinner last night, President Biden kept lightly.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, you all keep reporting my approval rating is at 42 percent. Kevin McCarthy called me and asked me, Joe, what the hell is your secret?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): And this week, our FOX News poll shows only 44 percent have a favorable opinion of President Biden, 56 percent say they have an unfavorable opinion of him -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas, we're going to talk a lot more about those polls. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , \uc2 RNC\uc2  CHAIRWOMAN: Great to be with you. Thanks -- thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's great to have you.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about this report that's getting a lot of attention. You guys just had a meeting in Oklahoma City. I'm told there was going to be a debrief of this.\par \par \uc2 But this is the headline from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ": GOP study on poor 2022 election showing doesn't mention Trump. According to many in the party, that would produce an incomplete analysis at best fit.\par \par \uc2 Quote: What's the point of doing an analysis of the Republican Party if you're not looking at Trump? You're just wasting your time. That's from an \uc2 RNC\uc2  member anonymously.\par \par \uc2 The former president is clearly the biggest voice in the room for the party. He had great sway in getting candidates through the primaries that didn't do well in the general, missed a chance to retake the Senate.\par \par \uc2 How can you break down 2022 without talking about that president?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, let me say, first of all, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " does not have a final draft because there is not a final draft. Many of the committees have not completed that. So, that's inaccurate reporting.\par \par \uc2 What I will say is, the biggest takeaway we are taking is independents did not break our way, which has to happen if we're going to win in 2024, which usually that's what causes that red wave. And abortion was a big issue in key states like Michigan and Pennsylvanian.\par \par \uc2 And so, the guidance we're going to give to our candidates is you have to address this head-on. The Democrats spent $360 million on this and many of our candidates across the board refused to talk about it, thinking, oh, we can just talk about the economy and ignore this big issue, and they can't.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And there's still people, though, advising Republicans that way saying this issue is bad for you. It costs you. Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 But you have said you've got to take it head-on. You want people to articulate not only the Republican position but the Democrat position -- and yet so many of them when they are forced (ph) candidates or potential candidates to talk about this, they seem to still struggle with the answer.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, they're uncomfortable, but they can't be. And it's not just talking about it once. The Democrats are putting $360 million against you on TV, in lies. So, those lies become the truth if you don't fight back. So you need to say it loud.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I'm proud to be pro-life. The consensus -- we have to find consensus among Democrats and Republicans. Let's see where the Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 What abortion is a bad idea to Democrats? Ninth month, eighth month, seventh month? They can't even articulate an abortion that's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 Gender selection, if it's a girl, you get to abort it. Tax-funded abortions for people where it's against their religious conscience. That's where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was years ago.\par \par \uc2 So I think put them on the defensive and articulate where you stand and that's going to be that critical message that we have to get out before these -- before 2024.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You guys, too, have started talking about issues early voting, mail in voting, ballot harvesting. That is something that former President Trump was one for years who railed very hard against that. He softened a bit.\par \par \uc2 But "The Wall Street Journal" has this question about whether it's too late. The damage has been done. They say his criticism of mail in voting before and since his 2020 loss helped chip away at GOP faith in early voting, polls show, party strategists say that's actually contributed to election defeat.\par \par \uc2 So, in 2020, nationwide, roughly 60 percent of Democrats voted early, and about 30 percent of Republicans. That had been more equal in 2016.\par \par \uc2 So how do you now convince people to embrace these methods that say you need to win when there's been such a negative connotation around them?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: What I would say is we did it pretty successfully in 2022 in states like Florida and North Carolina and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 So even with ballot harvesting, we beat Democrats in three statewide races, in Nevada. We did it in California, the one that Duarte case (ph), and obviously in Montana for Zinke. So, we're doing it. The process is key.\par \par \uc2 I would say the Democrats still invest more in that. Republican investors wait until the candidate is picked at until the primaries are done, and Democrats are always investing in the process.\par \par \uc2 That's why the \uc2 RNC\uc2  is so critical. You have to invest early if you're going to educate voters and talk to them about the importance of banking votes early, and we need to do that. Bank your vote and go be a poll watcher and sign up to work on election day.\par \par \uc2 But that's something we're continually working on. But we did better than that than I think the media is saying. A state we do need to improve on that is Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, and that's such a critical statement. So many of these races nationally and the Senate races in others, the gubernatorials.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about debates --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- because those are coming.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has said that nobody consulted him or his campaign about the debates you have announced thus far. I'm assuming you're in conversations with all of these campaigns. Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, we're talking to all the campaigns and potential campaigns, right, because we don't -- not everybody has announced yet. I've talked to President Trump. \uc2 Dave Bossie\uc2 , who's running the debate committee, has talked and met with all the candidates.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we're talking with them all the time but, no, we're not asking them, where should we have it? Who should be the moderators? That would be inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 What I do know is we're going to have a great field and the next president of the United States is going to be on our debate stage.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, he has said some things about whether or not he'll be on that debate stage. Let me play a little bit of what he said a couple of days ago.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: When you're way up, you don't do debates. If you're even or down, you do debates. But when you're way up, what's the purpose of doing the debate?\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He also wrote on Truth Social, he said, if you're leading by what he's called seemingly insurmountable numbers, and we get to the polls, why subject yourself to being libeled and abused.\par \par \uc2 So, what happens if he doesn't show up?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: You know, that's his choice and every candidate is going to make that calculation. They're going to decide, when I do announce? Do I go to Ohio? Do I go to New Hampshire? Do I debate? And that's going to be his decision.\par \par \uc2 What I think is the American people want to see these candidates. They want to see what they're articulating and especially what is your plan to take us out of the misery of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? What are you going to do to help our kids in school who are dealing with fentanyl and TikTok? What are you going to do to help get us energy independent?\par \par \uc2 People are really hurting right now in this country. So, I look at the debate not just about Republican against Republican. I look it as our party making a case as to why we should win the White House and defeat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 And that's one of the factors as to why I would say everybody should get on that debate stage.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Does he miss an opportunity if he doesn't come? Or is he, as he says, sparing himself from potentially taking shots and taking hits that, you know, are tough for any campaign?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, he's going to make that determination. His campaign will make that determination. I will just say this -- people are struggling and they want to see our plan as a party as to how we're going to win.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think it's important to get on the debate stage, but also remember, hey, who are we against? It's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Who's doing this to our country? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the other takeaways we took from the midterms in 2022 is that we just don't just need independence, we need every Republican. And we need to bring every Republican together. We have states where Republicans wouldn't vote for Republican nominees and that was a big factor of why we lost some key Senate races.\par \par \uc2 So we need to come together and remember who we're against in November and that's going to be this Biden White House that is destroying our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you feel like sometimes, though, you have to remind the former president about that message? Because he's been very influential in races, I think back to Georgia, you know, a couple of cycles back where people felt like they weren't going to show up because he told them that something unfair was going on.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I think there's a lot of people that need to be reminded that we need to remember that it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I think the voters feel that.\par \par \uc2 They feel that angst and they say that to me all the time. We need party unity. We need to bring everybody together because what they're doing to our country it's frightening.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to have the America we know and love if this continues, if they stack the Supreme Court, if they get rid of the filibuster, if they banned energy independence, if they're so beholden to China.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look it, China is running this White House in a lot of ways. China is the reason fentanyl is coming across our border. China is taking our kids' data on TikTok. We sold our strategic petroleum reserves to China.\par \par \uc2 This is an administration that is more China first than America first, and the American people want a president who cares about them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's talk about the strategy that this president currently has to go back it took 2024. They say, basically, they want to do what they did in 2020, it worked for him, and what they think worked in the midterms in 2022 -- lay low, keep the spotlight on what they like to call MAGA extremists in the party.\par \par \uc2 One op-ed piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " put it this way: Mr. Biden's biggest advantage might not come from anything he has actually done. Instead, it might come from the chaos among Republicans. This is welcome news for the president, who's fond of telling voters, and we both heard him say it last night, don't compare me to the Almighty, compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want to point to a split in the party, about everything from whether President Trump should be your nominee to where to draw the line on abortion, how to have that conversation.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about the president's message there that he's going to point to you guys and say, America, they are too divisive, they're too extremist, you can't trust them?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, we are seeing this happen, right? And I'm not surprised he's going to hide. He's hid from the press. He's hid from the American people.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't take tough questions. He doesn't have to answer for anything, any of the scandals that he has in his presidency. So he is going to hide again and most of the media will allow him to do that.\par \par \uc2 But I do think he's right about one thing -- we cannot win if we're not united. If Republicans don't vote for other Republicans, we're not going to win. And we don't just need all of the Republicans, we need independents, too.\par \par \uc2 You know, Shannon, Republicans are migrating. They are migrating to red states. Why? Because we got great governors across the country, low taxes, our policies work.\par \par \uc2 But it means the White House electorally isn't available to us unless we go through a purple or blue state. And those states are getting bluer, because red voters are moving to the red states.\par \par \uc2 So, this is very, very critical that we understand the path to the White House runs not just through independents, but every single Republican getting on board.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you have an idea about the pledge? Will you have everybody sign the pledge?\par \par \uc2 They did this back in 2016, included President Trump before he was president. He kind of balked at it at the end. To have them say if you want to be on the debate stage, you're going to have to support the eventual nominee.\par \par \uc2 I asked Governor Hutchinson about it last week. He's officially in now. He didn't like the sound of the pledge.\par \par \uc2 What's the status?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: So, let me ask a Republican voter, go to our voters and say, do you think it's so critical if you get on the National Republican Committee debate stage that you're going to support the eventual nominee, and everyone will say yes. They do not want to see somebody take up our time and talk about all things unless they're pledged to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They don't have to like each other. They don't have to hang out after this. I don't care about that. I want you to be focused on beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that pledge is critical to the voters who don't want to see Republicans come out of this and focus more in each other than beating -- winning the White House.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to quickly ask you about cultural issues, because we've got some new polling on this. And the issue of -- trans issues polling higher is people are more worried, voters are, about attacks on trans families than they are about trans people competing in sports. When asked about issues involving schools, the top concern you can see here was book banning.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's word major donor to the GOP, Peter Thiel, is unhappy and is going to hold his money out because he doesn't like these debates about abortion and trans and bathrooms and all of that.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that these cultural issues are hurting that GOP?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, one, the Democrats are using this word book banning. You're seeing it a lot. You saw on Biden's launch.\par \par \uc2 That's a lie. There isn't book banning. What Republicans are doing are protecting our children and parental rights and saying five year olds shouldn't be reading sexually explicit material.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  went in front of cameras read some of these books of new stations had to go away because the material was so explicit.\par \par \uc2 But now, we know their game plan, right? We can have a conversation about issues, and really what it is about is parents should have a say in what's happening with our kids. And I think most Americans support that.\par \par \uc2 But it's good to know the Democrats playbook and we're going to push on that, especially coming from the Democrat party that is banning freedom of speech, that is canceling people, that is destroying your life if you don't think with their orthodoxy. This is Democrat Party who is saying if you think outside of the box and everything we are dictating to you, you will make you lose your job, we will destroy you.\par \par \uc2 So they are banning people. They're banning freedom of speech and freedom of thought. Republicans are protecting children.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We will see you on the campaign trail. Much to come ahead the debates and beyond.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, there's renewed pressure on Democrats to potentially hold primary debates even with President Biden in as an incumbent and a clear primary frontrunner.\par \par \uc2 I'll bring a Democratic senator next to ask him whether the party should bring in all three declared candidates to their debate stage.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now that the president has officially said he is running again, he faces serious scrutiny from Democrats. That's in part because he already has two primary challenges and that means pressure to explain his record and pressure on the party to maybe hold primary debates.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): It's great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so the president has launched for reelection but he does some against challenging numbers. These are from FOX News, our brand-new polls. He's got a 56 percent unfavorable rating among registered voters. When we poll on everything from national security to immigration to the economy, he is upside down on all of those numbers.\par \par \uc2 Folks are giving him a bad report card and that includes Democrats who say they don't want to see him run.\par \par \uc2 So, are you worried about the lack of excitement, the dissatisfaction with his record going into 2024?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I'm not worried because he has a great record to run. Polls will go up. Polls will go down. But what won't change are the facts -- 12 million new jobs since he was elected president, which is a record, historical record, including the greatest growth in manufacturing jobs in 40 years, record low unemployment, 3.5 percent, I should say the lowest in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 So he's got a great economic record to run on. Inflation is coming down. That's obviously been an issue.\par \par \uc2 But I think he's got a great record to run on, and I think the polls will bounce around as they always do. But as the president said last night, you just quoted him. Don't compare me to the Almighty, compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's one of his lines. I think we'll hear again and again.\par \par \uc2 You talk about the economy but it's not translating to the average American. Our polling also shows that when people were asked how personally you're doing, because it's all about our families and how they're doing under roof, 70 percent of them said things are actually getting worse for them. I mean, that's happening on the president's watch.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, I think one of things you're seeing in polling these days is a great polarization between Democrats and Republicans even on questions like how are you feeling on the economy. If you look at --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But it was the majority of Democrats within the poll also said things were getting worse for them.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Right. So, the issue of inflation has been very real. The good news is it is coming down. The other good news is the most recent economic report shows that people's real wages are going up. So, wages have now been going off, especially for lower and middle income families at a faster pace than inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, those are real gains and I think they will to continue. So, look, obviously, what's happening in the economy will also change, but I think the structural pieces of it are sound, and that's what's going to be important going into the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you make about the debate over his age? Because both NBC and \uc2 CBS\uc2  have poll out that show, including Democrats, they are very worried about the issue of age.\par \par \uc2 "Axios" writes this: Even some of Biden's close advisors concede his age has diminished his energy, significantly limiting his schedule.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Goldberg writes over at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " -- she thinks he's a great president but adds this: It's hard to ignore the toll of Biden's years no matter how hard elected Democrats try.\par \par \uc2 She says essentially he should be taking a victory lap, not watching a reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 So what about this issue of age?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, I saw the president last night. He was in fighting form. I saw him at the event with the South Korean president, again, great press conference.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I think people should judge presidents on their performance and their record, not their age. And the president has a great record. I talked about some of the economic factors.\par \par \uc2 He's also one of the most protective presidents in recent history when it comes to a legislative record, major bipartisan victories, right? The -- we had the infrastructure bill. We had the CHIPS and Science bill to make sure we're competitive vis-a-vis China and others, and the veterans' health care bill. And there were also major wins when it came to bringing down the cost of prescription drugs and deploying more clean energy.\par \par \uc2 So, Shannon, what I would say is just look at his record. That's what we should be judging people on, not age.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do make of Senator Joe Manchin, one of your Democratic colleagues, who is very critical one of those major legislative successes for the president, for the White House? The Inflation Reduction Act, he says it's not being implemented in the way that it was written, and he's talking about if there was a vote of repeal, he would vote for it.\par \par \uc2 And he's been voting with Republicans quite a bit on a number of key issues recently. I mean, he's got a lot of criticism for this president.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing what any senator should do, which is sticking up for his state and using his leverage however he can, and that's what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing when it comes to --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Excuse me, I'm sorry. Joe --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They're two Joes.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Exactly. That's what Joe Manchin is doing when it comes to these issues. He's fighting for West Virginia and using his leverage wherever he can.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I think if you look at the results of the Inflation Reduction Act, people are already seeing their prescription drug costs coming down, people on Medicare. That's real, as is the deployment of clean energy.\par \par \uc2 In my state of Maryland, we're going to have 10,000 new jobs because of offshore wind energy driven by some of the economic incentives in the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you make though of the new estimates that are coming out on that bill, as it's playing out in real time, saying it's actually going to be way more expensive that we thought was going to be on issues like green energy?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, first of all, we have some buffer room, right? The bill reduced the deficit over a 10-year period by over $250 billion. The fact that there's a greater appetite for clean energy is actually a good thing when it comes both to jobs and dealing with the climate crisis. So that bill has a built-in buffer, unlike many of the bills our Republican colleagues passed, like the, you know, Bush -- the Trump tax cuts. This was actually something that reduced the deficit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. We'll watch as those numbers continue to come in.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask about the issue up access because you mentioned the president being out there, being vigorous. He hasn't done a serious sit-down with one of the major newspapers. Our polling shows that 76 percent of people say he needs to be holding regular press conferences. He's not doing that.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " noted this week: He has held the fewest news conferences since President Reagan. The "Axios" put this way: The White House is basically hiding Biden as he auditions for another term.\par \par \uc2 Does that lack of access feed this narrative that he is not up to the job and his staff is basically keeping him behind closed doors and that's how they plan to run the campaign, much like they did during 2020, because we had to because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, Shannon, the president has been doing what he should be doing, which is going out around the country and meeting with people and talking about the results from his first two years. He has come to Maryland more than any other president in my memory. He's been there multiple times talking about the infrastructure, modernization bill, creating new tunnels making trips faster for commuters.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what he's doing is going around the country and talking directly to people. I know the Washington press corps always wants to hear more from the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : We heard from him just the other day when the South Korean president was here and they asked him a lot of questions, including about the age issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about debates, too, because there are Democrats challenging the president within the primary. RFK Jr. comes in on our poll, 19 percent. I mean, that's a hefty number.\par \par \uc2 He's not the only one who says they should have some primary debates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR. (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think he has an obligation to democracy to debate anyway. I mean, I would hope that he does.\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD, HOST: Put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  upon that stage with \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  who's challenging him and \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  and whoever steps up to the plate and let's have a (EXPLETIVE DELETED) discussion, you know?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A bleep on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Why not?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, that's up to the president's campaign. I mean, this is early. He just announced his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 These are decisions every campaign makes both with respect to the primary and the general. I think the president is going to keep his eye on the ball, which is winning the general election. That's what's important for the country. What they decide to do in the primary, only time will tell.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, in the meantime, he's got to get through this debt ceiling issue which you all are very locked in. There's been criticism. He says he doesn't want to negotiate over anything. Republicans have to come to the table.\par \par \uc2 Well, the speaker got something passed that includes raising the debt ceiling. Who's game is it now? Because there are Democrats, plenty of folks within your party who say, okay, the president has made his point but they have to start negotiating.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : So, Shannon, what he said is that he's not going to negotiate with people who are threatening to literally blow up our economy, right? Put more people out of work, drive up costs in order to get their way. And that's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But the (INAUDIBLE) speaker does raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : It does but then it says in order to raise the ceiling, we're going to cut veterans. We're going to cut law enforcement. We're going to cut border security. We're going to cut all sorts of things. We're going to put China back in the driver's seat when it comes to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 So, what the president is saying, he's not going to negotiate with someone who's actually threatening the economy, taking the entire economy hostage. He will sit down with Speaker McCarthy to talk about these issues in the framework of the budget and the appropriations process.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has put a plan on the table, quite a detailed budget, much more detailed than what Speaker McCarthy and the House just passed, that has $3 trillion in deficit reduction over the next ten years, primarily by asking very, very wealthy people, billionaires and big corporations, to pay more.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy has a plan that cuts about I think $4.5 trillion over ten years, through dramatic cuts but threatening the destruction of the U.S. economy. So --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When do they get to table? They're clearly far apart. But they've got to get somewhere.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : What you've got to do with not threaten the detonation of the economy. So, for example, Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  years ago when we came to this kind of impasse and said, okay, if Congress doesn't want to do its job and actually pay to make sure the United States pays the bills, which as we will know, our bills that are already due and owing. Already due and owing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. Which, by the way, President Biden has voted against that as a senator.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Right. But so -- but if you want to -- if you want to do that, give the president the authority to do it, subject to a veto, subject to an override. So that's the way to do it.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans don't want to do what's necessary to prevent the meltdown of the economy, left President Biden do that and then let's negotiate on important issues, of priorities, how you best achieve deficit reduction.\par \par \uc2 Republicans always talk about reducing the deficit but they never want to raise one penny in income taxes on very wealthy people and corporations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. They differently have a difference of opinion on how to get there, but hopefully the two sides do have some chats about it.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for coming in. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Thanks, Shannon. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, brand new FOX News polls with some telling numbers for both the presidential primaries and for the gun-control debates. We'll bring our Sunday panel to analyze America's mindset heading into 2024 and a little bit of humor from last night's correspondents' dinner.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- I had a lot of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  jokes ready, but Micky Mouse beat the hell out of me and got there first.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden joking about Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who may soon join the race to try to unseat him.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 "Jewish Insider" editor-in-chief and "Axios" contributor \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , former State Department spokesperson and Fox News contributor Marie Harf, former Utah Congressman Jason Chaffetz, and author, legal commentator and Project 21 chairman Horace Cooper.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, PROJECT 21 CHAIRMAN, AUTHOR AND LEGAL COMMENTATOR: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , JEWISH INSIDER EDITOR-IN-CHIEF AND "AXIOS" CONTRIBUTOR: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You made it through the thunderstorms this morning to make it here.\par \par \uc2 OK, so let's talk about Governor DeSantis. This is the "Politico" headline. He's got a $110 million ticket to the White House. He sits atop accounts poised to support his candidacy with that total. And that's all without him opening an official campaign committee account.\par \par \uc2 Jason, you met with other major donors over in Israel this week. Will the money be enough to jumpstart him where the polls say he's lagging (ph)?\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ (R), FORMER UTAH CONGRESSMAN AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, money does solve a lot of problems. And when you've got $110 million, more than double anybody else, then it's going to go a long way. Super PACs are already out there spending money. You see ads and that fight's already on. This Florida state legislature has got to pass a piece of legislation so he could declare. But I think everybody understands that, yes, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is - is running for President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so here's our latest polling. This is among Republican primary voters about who they prefer it this point. President Trump with that commanding lead at 53 percent. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at 21 percent. And we trickle down from there. Nikki Haley bumping up a little bit and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 But, Horace, where do you think this field stands. One headline this week, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  inevitable. Is he?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: You know, we are very, very early. I will just tell you, in my experience, since 1993 I have not seen, after a presidential election, the loser ending up in surveys prevailing over the victor. And yet you didn't see that in '93, you didn't see it in '97, you didn't see it in 2001. You go all the way, even to 2017, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  never led \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We are seeing now more than a dozen times in the last year \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ahead. That is a very good position to be in.\par \par \uc2 So, there's no doubt that there is still a residual level of support among Republicans and apparently even among a large number of Americans who may now see the policies of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  may regret them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, our polling shows, as I talked about with our first two guests, that President Biden is not polling well. People are mad at him on all kinds of topics. And this poll here, on Democratic primary voters, the first one that we've had since RFK Jr. entered the race, and he comes in at nearly 20 percent. That's against an incumbent president, Marie.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think there's a lot of interesting things in these polls. I would also note that the people that have lower approval ratings than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That includes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . So, people talk about Biden's approval rating. His two main competitors are actually less popular than he is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, when you look at favorability, they're almost equal.\par \par \uc2 HARF: But they're -- but they're lower.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I think the Biden team feels good about that campaign relaunch this week. They started with this campaign video about freedom, talking about the things Republicans are doing in states, from banning books to, you know, anti-LGBTQ laws. And they say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's going to go out. He's going to talk about the economy. He's going to talk about the manufacturing jobs he's helped create - his policies have created. And the leading candidate on the Republican side is facing multiple criminal investigations.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  was so interesting earlier when she talked about independent voters. That's who elected \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And those are the people that, I think, are very tired of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , don't like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' extreme culture war policies. And the Biden team is going after with their messaging this week about freedom and about getting the job done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and on the issue of economy, because we know a lot of voters are swayed by that, Josh, our polling showed that when we talk about people who aren't doing well, as I talked about early in the show, 70 percent of people say things are getting worse for their families. That number is even higher among independents. I think it's around 73 percent. Will they vote about that or will they vote about what Marie is saying?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: They're going to vote on pocketbook issues. And that is President Biden's biggest venerability. I watched his re-election announcement, the video, didn't say a thing about policy. Didn't really talk about the economy. And it's not a coincidence because a majority - a clear majority of voters are worried about their economic circumstances. And if we have a recession, if the volatility in the stock market, in 401ks that people are experiencing hits right around the time of the election, anyone can - you know, Trump, or any other Republican, is capable of winning this election. And I think that's what's keeping Democrats up at night, the fact that poll after poll shows a very competitive race between even Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is not the type of blowout that I think some Democrats had been anticipating. So, the economy is the x factor and it's going to be the biggest issue for the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We also did a lot of polling on other hot-button issues, including guns. I want to put this up. Proposals to reduce gun violence. They have enormous support across the board for things like background checks, which, by the way, are mostly done with nearly every gun sale in this country, existing gun laws, enforce those, a lot of people for that, and other things that you can see here. But is there actually, Jason, do you think, any appetite on Capitol Hill to take some of these, where there is wide agreement, and put them into a piece of legislation?\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, certainly guns does not have wide agreement out there. The Biden administration just recently announced that they're going to go after shooting sports and - and that is not going to play well particularly in the - in the heartland.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, with a divided government, you're not going to have much of legislation anything pass. As a conservative, I happen to think that that's a good thing. I'm OK with that. But I've got to tell you, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if he doesn't get out there and talk about policy, and he hasn't, because he doesn't have - if you talk about inflation, for instance, what's the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  plan to tackle inflation. Nobody knows what the answer to that is because he doesn't have a plan.\par \par \uc2 So, you combine that with the inability to communicate and a vice president who's - who's equally as bad at communicating, I think that type of - of situation is - is just right for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Horace, how much time do you think Republicans are going to talk about the vice president because you heard what Nikki Haley said this week and she got a lot of backlash for saying President Biden might not make it to 86, but it does cast added attention on the vice presidential person that's on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: OK, So the Democrats are boosting Kamala, saying, if you watch this campaign video, she was all over it some 16 or 17 times. They're boosting saying, you vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and you'll get \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Presumably that means perhaps he's not going to finish his term.\par \par \uc2 I would say the real answer here is, promise or threat, because she is probably the least popular vice president that we have seen in a hundred years, and she hasn't improved. She's had two and a half years to get better, and she's not.\par \par \uc2 One quick thing about that 2022 election. "The National Journal" and "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " both came together and they said, Election Day 2022 was 297 electoral votes for the GOP if it had been a presidential year and that Republican votes outnumbered. If my \uc2 Dallas Cowboys\uc2  were favored by 20 for the Super Bowl and they only won 3, I'd still celebrate. The left has decided that they're going to try a rerun of what they did in 2022, not a real campaign, not a real platform, but just wedge. I don't believe it's going to work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I quickly want to ask about, because, as I mentioned, the president is under water on nearly every major issue. That includes the border and immigration. Title 42 is going away on May 11th.\par \par \uc2 "The National Review," since you mentioned them - oh, you said "National Journal."\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is "National Review," a different publican, but here - here's what they said about the plan that the Biden administration has put together. It says the plan is lawlessness atop lawlessness all the way down. They say it's really accommodating the wishes of foreigners rather than the immigration limits that have been placed by elected officials here in America, Marie.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, we heard the secretary of state and homeland security this week talk about their plan. They're going to surge resources to the borders. They're going to have these processing centers in other countries to try and help stem this - this really crush of people we're seeing. But Democrats will also say to you, you know, we've been making policy, we've been passing bills, we've been rebuilding your communities, and Republicans in Congress have done nothing about this issue that they seem to care about. It's all rhetoric. That's what you'll hear.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A quick thought or comment.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, immigration is one of the weakest issues for the Democrats. And the White house can talk about what they're doing, but once Title 42 goes away, and if you see a surge of illegal migrants crossing the border, it's going to be a big weight on the political fortunes of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I think all sides admit that is coming in May.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it is one of Washington's biggest mysteries, who leaked that Supreme Court draft ruling on abortion a year ago? Now one of the justices says he's got a good idea who it was. We'll discuss with a panel of insiders who know the court very well, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just one year ago, and unknown person kicked off a firestorm by leaking a draft of a Supreme Court ruling on abortion. The ruling, published by "Politico," suggested a legal earthquake was coming as the justices were poised to overturn Roe v Wade. Just this week Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  told "The Wall Street Journal" he has theories about who leaked up draft and why. In a moment, my panel of legal insiders, including multiple former Supreme Court clerks, joins me live.\par \par \uc2 But first, a look at what happened once that draft went public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Roe v. Wade has got to go.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We won't go back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Pro-choice and pro-life groups flooded the streets in front of the Supreme Court as soon as the high stake's draft ruling leaked. And demonstrations didn't stop there.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We said pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As abortion rights groups protested outside the Republican appointed justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 The justices themselves were stunned at the breach of trust the leak exposed.\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: It was beyond anyone's understanding, or at least anyone's imagination, that somewhat would do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  quickly ordered the court's marshal to conduct an internal investigation. But after eight months, a public statement from the court indicated the marshal was not able to find the leaker or figure out how they did it. The nine justices left with the fallout.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Look where we are. Where now that trust or that belief is gone forever. It changes the institution fundamentally. You begin to look over your shoulder.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And in the months after the end of Roe, Gallup polling showed Americans trust in the court dropped. When asked that summer about the polls, one of the justices who dissented from that landmark opinion sought to reassure.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR, SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: We have to have continuing faith in the court system, in our system of government. Continuing the battle each day to regain the public's confidence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have assembled a powerhouse legal panel to talk about the court one year after that leak, and now facing new ethics questions. Judicial Crisis Network president and former clerk for Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , Carrie Severino, Constitutional Accountability President Elizabeth Wydra, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor and former clerk for Justice Thurgood Marshall, Steve Saltzburg, and legal partner at Cooper & Kirk and former clerk for Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , Megan Wold.\par \par \uc2 Think you all for giving us your time today. We appreciate this.\par \par \uc2 One year out, we still have more questions than answers, most of us. I want to play -- read something from "The Wall Street Journal," Justice Alito speaking out this week about this said, I personally have a pretty good idea who is responsible. It was part of an effort to prevent the Dobbs draft for becoming the decisions of the courts. And that's how it was used for those six weeks by people on the outside as part of the campaign to try to intimidate the courts.\par \par \uc2 Megan, you clerked for the justice. What do you make of his comments and where we are a year later?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN WOLD, CLERKED FOR JUSTICE \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2  AND COOPER & KIRK PARTNER: Well, apparently, we will never know who leaked this draft opinion in the Dobbs case. Obviously, the standard for - for stating a name, as Justice Alito said, is very high. But it's very clear what the intention of this person was. It was to influence the outcome of Dobbs, to change that decision. And the effect was predictable. We saw immense public pressure on the justices, including protests outside of their homes, threats on their lives and one man appearing at Justice Kavanaugh's doorstep with the intent to kill. This ultimately diminishes our confidence for all Americans. It really affects all of us because this person had a vigilante impulse. What they couldn't accomplish within the law, they decided to try to accomplish outside of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He also talks about the frustration of finger-pointing at potentially the conservative justices or somebody that would support their position saying, essentially they became targets of assassination, Carrie.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, CLERKED FOR JUSTICE \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  AND JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK PRESIDENT: Absolutely. I think this is why it's clear this wasn't - - wasn't someone from the right who would do this. It's -- everybody from day one has understood that the goal here to try to change the opinion was something that would come from the left. And the threats were, obviously, targeted at the conservative justices, up to and including a threat of assassination, doxing of the justices, doxing of where they attend church, where their children go to school. This is really a serious problem for the justices. Safety and, obviously, for the internal workings of the court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a lot of frustration because we never got those answers from the Supreme Court's public release when they talked about the investigation in January of this year, some individuals admitted to investigators that they told their spouse or partner about the draft Dobbs opinion and the vote count in violation of the court's confidentiality rules.\par \par \uc2 Professor, as somebody whose clerked there, did that surprise you at all?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN SALTZBURG, CLERKED FOR JUSTICE THURGOOD MARSHALL AND GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: It did surprise me. The justices, at least when I was there, made it very clear, they trusted you and you weren't to talk to anybody about what was going on until an opinion was amounts. And I think we adhered to that and - and we understood how important it was.\par \par \uc2 Do you think we are ever going to know what's what is your thought about the long-term impact on people being able to trust the court goes on there?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER PRESIDENT: Yes, absolutely. You know, I think that the leak has had, you know, a really enormous impact on the court itself, on the inner workings of the court. I think that, you know, we've seen, as Justice Thomas said, you know, in your intro, distrust inside the court.\par \par \uc2 But I think if we look outside the court, I think the leak itself was not really what's caused these changes in poll numbers and having faith in the Supreme Court. I think it's really more about what was in the draft opinion that was leaked because I think probably if you think about Americans who are working hard, trying to put food on the table, take care of their kids, you know, they probably aren't following the leak story super closely. But pretty much everyone knows that Roe versus Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And - and one of the criticisms came with specific reference to Justice Thomas' concurrence with Dobbs. I know the people who object, not only to the broader ruling, but that as well, he said this, in future cases we should reconsider all of this court's substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell. Here's what President Biden said that day about that portion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Justice Thomas' said as much today. He explicitly called to reconsider the right of marriage quality, the right of couples to make their choices on contraception. This is an extreme and dangerous path the court is now taking us on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Carrie, we've heard those words used a lot, extreme. This court cannot be trusted, its illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Yes, I mean, so the legitimacy attacks, I think, are really just amount to name calling. We know that these justices were all legitimately appointed, but the president conferred by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the unfortune things about that concurrence is it's allowed people to demagogue about issues that there are what the opinion is actually about. Justice Thomas frequently writes concurring opinions that talk about other theories that maybe he's the only one in the justice -- on the court to hold, and certainly aren't what's going on before the case. The court and the opinion made very clear is - is clearly limited to abortion and I think it's - it's also very clear that while Justice Thomas is willing to consider rethinking substantive due process, doesn't mean some of those rights might not come in through other areas, because Justice Thomas believes in the privileges immunity clause, which other justices don't follow, right? But that there certainly aren't a large number of votes for that on the court right now. I think most of this is just demagoguing and trying to change the subject from the limited nature of that opinion itself. It's clearly significant, but it doesn't have the impact that some people are trying to henny penny about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he's been in the headlights, as many others have been, recently. Here are some of them on ethics questions. "\uc2 Pro Publica\uc2  " saying, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ' secret life of luxury. "Vanity Fair," whoops, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  also made big money from a Dubious real estate deal. "Politico," Jane Roberts, who is married to Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , made $10.3 million in commissions from elite law firms, whistleblower documents show.\par \par \uc2 Professor, what do you make of these calls for the court now to step up, draft, adopt a code of conduct?\par \par \uc2 SALTZBURG: I think it's long overdue for the court to have a code of conduct. And these revelations are historic. You look back, aside from Abe Fortus (ph), whose misconduct involved $20,000 in a payment, you look at the history, the Supreme Court's been great about avoiding financial improprieties and the like. And this is called into question I guess the -- for good reason, the ethics of this court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "The Federalist" says, though. They said, the only thing that matters is creating the perception that conservative justices are corrupt. It's all part of a concerted effort to delegitimize a Supreme Court that still occasionally upholds a semblance of constitutional limits on the statement, the one thing still standing in the way of progressive progress.\par \par \uc2 Megan, what do you make of that, of these headlines, of these questions.\par \par \uc2 WOLD: The headlines are really political mudslinging. The justices here didn't do anything wrong. Some of them accurately reported transactions and some of them didn't report things that no one would have to report. The purpose is exactly what - what that article said, it's to influence the outcome of the court, and to do so illegitimately. There are Democrats in Congress who want to impose restrictions on the justices in order to exert more control over their lives, over the lives of their spouses and ultimately to influence outcomes because if justices need to recuse themselves, there's no one to take their place, and so it really affected the makeup of the court and how cases are decided.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and, so, Elizabeth, this is coming against the backdrop of a bipartisan call now in the Senate. There's a bill coming together to force the justices to write and adopt this new code of conduct. But there's an interesting opinion piece in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " that said it's possible that that is one branch infringing on another and that really are separate for good reason, and that there are corruption laws, there's impeachment for the justices. They're still subjected to those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: So, we have checks and balances in the Constitution and the Supreme Court is, obviously, subject to checks and balances, just like the other branches of government. And it's required under the law that we have both the appearance and the reality of impartiality when it comes to our judicial branch. And the Supreme Court, as the highest court in the land, should be even more - that should be even more true. And so I think the idea that they aren't bound to an ethics code in the same way that every lower federal court judge is perhaps suggest that the appearance of impartiality, and we'll put aside the reality for a moment, is not really getting through to the public. And so I think it is really something that is appropriate for Congress, doing its oversight authority, engaging in its oversight authority, to consider whether reforms need to be made or not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see if the appetite is there, especially in a bipartisan format.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all very much for your time today. We appreciate having you in.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at what millions around the world call the original love story.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just a quick note, my podcast "Livin' the Bream" drops this morning. Brand new this week features a deep dive into the story millions consider the very first love story, Adam and Eve. Check out my conversation with podcasts, subcultural scholars, and authors Gabe and Rebecca Lyons (ph). It is the final in the series of podcasts on my new book "Love Stories of the Bible Speak."\par \par \uc2 And you can hear all of today's program on the FOX NEWS SUNDAY podcast. Download and subscribe at foxnewspodcasts.com or wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230430ej4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  Reflects On Threats After Dobbs Abortion Leak; FDIC Preparing To Rake Over \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 ; GOP Moves Security Package To House Floor; Son Of President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  To Face Arkansas Judge On Monday; Teachers Say District Rule Violates Religious Beliefs; Gender Identity Policies Typically Adopted Quietly; Family Finds Comfort In Garden Honoring Son; Parents Sue Snapchat For Enabling Drug Dealer's Sale Of Fentanyl Dose That Killed Son; Some In Congress Seek To Pass Law Ensuring Artificial Intelligence Will Not Have Ability To Launch Nuclear Weapons; Story Of Korean War Veteran's Dogfight With Soviet Planes Declassified; President Biden Announces Reelection Campaign; New Poll Shows 70 Percent Of American Unsatisfied With Direction Of Country\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Shannon Bream, Rich Edson, Chad Pergram, David  Spunt, William La Jeunesse, Christina Coleman, Lydia Hu, Aishah Hasnie,  Bret Baier \par \uc2 7,653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: They got the ticket, they went to the concert.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: A year?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Worst one more thing ever. That sucked, you should be embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We will see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend, everyone. I know we will. Bye.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF BREAKING NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Welcome to Los Angeles. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The federal government prepares to take action as another major bank risks failure.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans announced their plans to secure the border when pandemic related to the asylum rules expire.\par \par \uc2 And Bret Baier has the story of a war hero whose exploits were kept secret for decades.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, a \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justice says he has a pretty good idea who leaked the draft of last summer's controversial abortion ruling and the reason behind it.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  made the comment to the Wall Street Journal. Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent and anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream has the details. Shannon, good evening.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace. Well, Tuesday, you know will mark the one year anniversary of that leaked draft opinion that signaled the Supreme Court was prepared to overturn the Roe v. Wade precedent described by supporters as establishing a constitutional right nationwide to abortion.\par \par \uc2 The court's current majority disagreed. The author of that opinion, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , still clearly frustrated by the impact it had on the safety of the justices and the reputation of the court telling the Wall Street Journal, I personally have a pretty good idea who is responsible, but that's different from the level of proof that is needed to name somebody.\par \par \uc2 He added this, it was part of an effort to prevent the Dobbs draft from becoming the decision of the court. And that's how it was used for those six weeks by people on the outside as part of a campaign to try to intimidate the court.\par \par \uc2 That draft opinion which mirrored the final ruling nearly two months later, triggered protests outside the court and some justices homes, which by the way still continue.\par \par \uc2 Alito makes clear he and many of his colleagues felt directly and specifically under threat saying this, those of us who were thought to be in the majority thought to have approved my draft opinion were really targets of assassination. It was rational for people to believe that they might be able to stop the decision in Dobbs by killing one of us.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts ordered an internal investigation led by the court's Marshall and released a report in January concluding, the team has been to date unable to identify a person responsible by a preponderance of the evidence.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Roberts turned down an invitation from the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  to appear in person on the leak anniversary to talk about another topic of concern. Ethics questions over the justices and the refusal to adopt a formal code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we've got a special panel of former clerks, professors and experts to break this all down on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" this week, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Shannon, see you on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you then.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Also breaking tonight, a potential economic blow for President Biden as he tries to get his reelection campaign off to a good start. Federal officials are attempting to rescue another major bank in trouble. This comes as we learn more about the failure of a Northern California institution blamed on the government.\par \par \uc2 The Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson is live at the White House tonight. Rich, good evening.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials are acknowledging they failed to appreciate the scope of the problems at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . It needed to rescue last month as now the federal government is reportedly preparing to step in on another bank.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Federal regulators are preparing to seize \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 . The bank's condition has deteriorated and there's no more time to find other banks to throw \uc2 First Republic\uc2  a financial lifeline.\par \par \uc2 So, the \uc2 Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation\uc2  is getting ready to step in, this is according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DOLL, CHIEF INVESTMENT OFFICER, CROSSMARK GLOBAL INVESTMENTS: It's a tough, tough piece of medicine to take because the bankers fell asleep, the switch, the regulators did.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Bank regulators at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  are also taking an assigning blame for the failure of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Michael Barr\uc2 , the Fed's vice chair for supervision, wrote \uc2 SVB\uc2 , "Failed because of a textbook case of mismanagement by the bank. Its senior leadership failed to manage basic interest rate and liquidity risk. Its board of directors failed to oversee senior leadership and hold them accountable. And \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  supervisors failed to take forceful enough action."\par \par \uc2 The persistent risk to regional banks is another economic headwind President Biden faces as he runs for reelection.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK (R-FL): In every single poll that I know our team has run or when I talk to folks back home, across the country, they all say that it's the economy. That is the thing that is really at the top of their list that keeps them up at night.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And several polls show the president's declining popularity. Gallup says only 37 percent approve of the president's performance. 59 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 Other recent polls show Americans are generally uninterested in a 2020 rematch. Though Democrats say the president will have an advantage in next year's election.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Elections are always a choice. President Biden is for protecting unions and increasing working wages. The Republican plans are for cutting spending, for tax cuts for the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Though Khanna says the president should agree to debate his Democratic challengers. \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  and Robert Kennedy Jr. And that he's too guarded.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think his staff overprotects him. I think put him out there in a press conference. Who cares if someone makes a gaff? Every person makes a gaff in conversations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): As with most incumbent president, Biden appears set very heavily favored for his party's nomination. Though also helped by the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , which has voted to try to push down in the order of the primary calendar states like Iowa and New Hampshire in favor of more Biden-friendly states like South Carolina in Michigan, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Rich Edson live in the White House. Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Next month's schedule then to pandemic related asylum rules along the southern border has both Republicans and Democrats considering big moves. Yesterday we told you President Biden is opening new processing centers and calling up reserves.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Republican plan to deal with the expected influx of applicants. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): House Republicans talk about border security all the time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The border is wide open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Massive waves of people to come.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unaccompanied children.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But doing something about the border proved allusive for the House majority, perhaps until now.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): We're going to bring a border security package and pass it through this House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That's what Republicans pledged on the campaign trail last fall.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I will promise you this. If we get the majority, we will secure this border.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: But consensus of vated Republicans for their first four months running the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): We've only been in power for just over 100 days. It's not about a time frame.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The time frame is to pass a border security package just as Title 42 expires next month amid an expected immigration crush.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARRY MOORE (R-AL): There's an opportunity for us to bring a package that is certainly going to help secure the border and also protect people.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The GOP plan resumes border wall construction. It hikes Border Patrol pay and hires more agents.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it requires those seeking asylum to only cross into the U.S. at a point of entry.\par \par \uc2 But like the debt ceiling, the GOP must muscle the border measures to passage with a narrow majority. Democrats from border districts are skeptical of the GOP plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. VICENTE GONZALES (D-TX): If we're going to build walls just to build walls and not really have policy that prevents people from coming, it'll make no difference.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Another border district Democrat claims the GOP effort is a distraction.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAUL GRIJALVA (D-AZ): They try to recover from the wrong decisions that they've made around the issue of choice. And women's reproductive rights. It's a playbook that at least I've seen six elections in a row.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A Fox News poll found that only five percent of voters said the border was their most pressing issue. It trailed the economy and guns. The GOP also lacks the votes to impeach DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is expected to be in an Arkansas courtroom Monday in a child support dispute. The president's son is already the subject of several ongoing investigations.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt sets things up for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): While his dad eyes another term in the Oval Office, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has a courtroom in rural Arkansas on his mind and the public may get a peek at the first son's finances.\par \par \uc2 Biden's appearance is related to a fight over child support payments to a daughter he shares with Lunden Roberts. The 4-year-old lives with her mother in Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 Biden has agreed to pay, but Roberts says he's not holding up his end of the bargain. Her attorney writing, if Mr. Biden can afford a Washington, D.C., Hollywood, Chicago biglaw, and the best domestic relations attorney on the Texas side of the Texarkana border, he surely must have income for child support purposes.\par \par \uc2 Biden's team tried to reschedule the hearing, writing, the continuance is not sought for purposes of delay or to cause prejudice to any party, but rather so that justice may be obtained by all parties.\par \par \uc2 A judge denied a motion by \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys to delay the upcoming hearing Monday morning. His legal troubles are multiple, especially the ongoing five year probe into his tax affairs, overseas business dealings, and lying on a federal form to purchase a gun.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys met with career attorneys at the Justice Department in Washington for an update on the federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera