tary exercises in which China simulated sealing off the island earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Katsuura, Japan from Surfline. One of the big stories there tonight. Japan will lift most of its coronavirus border controls tomorrow. That includes a requirement that entrants show proof of three vaccinations or a pre-departure negative test.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as the country's Golden Week holiday season begins and a large influx of foreign tourists is expected.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER (on camera): Well, this evening we are hearing from the parents of one of the students killed at the University of Idaho last November.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Christina Coleman has the emotional interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Tulips planted in honor of Ethan Chapin, line a garden in Mount Vernon, Washington, close to where he grew up.\par \par \uc2 His parents say walking through the flowers helped them process their son's tragic death.\par \par \uc2 STACY CHAPIN, MOTHER OF ETHAN CHAPIN: One day I was out walking by myself, I was crying so hard.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Flowers sold from the garden also support a scholarship fund named in honor of Ethan. The 20-year-old was a triplet. He was close to his siblings and described as a loving brother, son, and friend.\par \par \uc2 Ethan was murdered along with his girlfriend and two of her roommates in a house near the University of Idaho campus last November.\par \par \uc2 CHAPIN: You never know when it's going to just life is going to throw you a curveball. I mean, I think we got the worst curveball.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Ethan's parents say the last time they saw their son was during Parents Weekend, seven days before he was killed.\par \par \uc2 JIM CHAPIN, FATHER OF ETHAN CHAPIN: Stacy and I were planning on taking a lot of trips and spent a lot of time on the road and just in a week.\par \par \uc2 S. CHAPIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 J. CHAPIN: It all changed.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: The defense attorneys for accused killer, Bryan Kohberger, are now set to interview Bethany Funke, one of the surviving roommates who was in the house at the time of the murders.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GRACE, FOX NATION HOST: 21-year-old Bethany Funke, she may have slipped through it, she may have seen something, may have heard something. We don't know. Why don't we know? Because her police report and interview is sealed.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Meantime, Stacy and Jim Chapin are facing the future without Ethan.\par \par \uc2 S. CHAPIN: The next milestone is going to be Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 That's going to be a tough day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: A tough Mother's Day indeed.\par \par \uc2 Now, Kohberger is charged with four counts of first-degree murder. His preliminary hearing is scheduled for June 26th. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Very heart-rending. Hilarious. Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the social media sites, \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  is being targeted tonight by families of dozens of children who overdosed on fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu is in New York tonight.\par \par \uc2 Lydia, good evening.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace. Families of 63 young people who died of fentanyl poisoning are suing Snapchats' parent company.\par \par \uc2 They say the apps disappearing feature and its algorithmic friendship recommendations helped drug dealers connect with kids and teens like Sammy Chapman, a 16-year-old from Santa Monica, California, who died of fentanyl poisoning in 2021. His family says he bought a fake prescription pill on the social media platform that killed him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAPMAN, FATHER: There was a colorful drug menu on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  that a dealer who found him there produced. And he ordered something off of that menu like a pizza. Snap has turned into the dark web for kids. The Snaps disappear, and so kids think they can operate with impunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now his family is one of dozens suing Snap. The families accuse the company of becoming the, quote, go to means of distributing drugs to children, teens, and young adults. The lawsuit points to \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 's key feature that automatically deletes messages. They also say it hinders investigations by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BERGMAN, SOCIAL MEDIA VICTIMS LAW CENTER: The architecture, how it's designed, facilitates the sale of fentanyl contaminated drugs. We have over 80 people we represent whose children died of fentanyl poisoning. In all but one of those cases, the child obtained contaminated drugs through \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: In a statement, a \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  spokesperson said the company remains committed to fighting the drug crisis, but the suit is, quote, "riddled with false claims about how the \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  app works, our ongoing efforts to aggressively combat drug activity on our platform and shut down drug dealers."\par \par \uc2 Now, the legal action is the latest effort by families around the country to hold social media platforms accountable for \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 . The latest claims comes as shares of its parent company fell this week, losing more than 13 percent by close today after the company reported Thursday revenue fell for the first quarter. Trace, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lydia Hu in New York tonight. Lydia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, this evening we look at the worst-case scenario for artificial intelligence. Some in Congress want to take steps to make sure that A.I. cannot launch nuclear weapons against its human creators. Here is congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. TED LIEU, (D-CA) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: A.I. is amazing. It's going to help society in many different ways. It can also kill us.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Democrat Representative \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2  wants to prevent artificial intelligence from launching a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 LIEU: We want to make sure that it's a human being making the decision.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So he has teamed up with a group of bipartisan lawmakers on a bill that would turn a current Pentagon policy into law. The Defense Department mandates that humans must be in the loop for any nuclear launches by an automated system. The new law would prevent any federal dollars being used to get around that. Several lawmakers on the Hill like the idea, but some want to know if the bill would hold our military back.\par \par \uc2 JAY OBERNOLTE, (R-CA) HOUSE SCIENCE COMMITTEE: I'm checking with the department of defense and our military leaders to make sure that some of the language in the bill wouldn't unintentionally harm our military readiness.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Others wonder --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will China follow along with this?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Does it matter if China and Russia and other adversarial countries don't do the same thing?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KEN BUCK\uc2 , (R-CO) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: It matters. Hopefully they will recognize the dangers to the world and their own people.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: A.I. is even concerning tech billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  who met privately with Senate Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on the Hill this week, tweeting after, "A.I. has great power to do good and evil. Better the former." Senator Michael Bennet just introduced legislation to create an A.I. task force, while Representative Lieu would like to create a bipartisan commission to investigate what other areas of A.I. need congressional oversight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Senator \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2  is already working on companion legislation to that nuke bill in the Senate. But things here on Capitol Hill, they work very, very slowly, and Representative Buck told me the earliest you could expect this bill to hit the House floor is sometime later this year or early 2024. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Aishah, thank you. Up next, we introduce you to an American hero that you probably have never heard of.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You found his story, obviously, tremendously compelling?\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA, (R-CA): This is even before the latest redo of "Top Gun."\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ROYCE WILLIAMS, \uc2 U.S. NAVY\uc2  (RET): I'm in the middle of this, but I've got friends that are carrying the ball.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Tonight we honor a military hero. The retired Navy captain received a Korean military award earlier this week for his service during the Korean War. My colleague, Bret Baier, shares his story.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 Captain Royce Williams served as by fighter pilot during the Korean War. He engaged in one of the toughest airborne battles at that time or any time, a 35-minute dogfight with Soviet MiGs. But it was top secret. No one could talk about it. It was unheard of, until recently. Tonight, we take a look at his now declassified story and the effort to get him the recognition he deserves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ROYCE WILLIAMS, \uc2 U.S. NAVY\uc2  (RET): I was eager to do my part. And I didn't in any way think it was going to involve shooting down enemy airplanes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In 1952, Captain Williams was assigned to the USS Oriskany stationed in the Sea of Japan. He flew an F9, F5 Panther and helped survey and patrol the region during the Korean War.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Our primary mission was air support.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But one combat air patrol flight eventually became one of the most intense airborne battles in naval history.\par \par \uc2 It really was one of the most amazing aviation triumphs that we ever heard of. Tell us what happened.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It was a blizzard, heavy winds.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Captain Williams took off in dangerous weather conditions alongside three other panther pilots. They climbed 12,000 feet to clear sky.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: At that time the flight leader had a warning light for a fuel system.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The flight leader and his wingman were ordered to return to the Oriskany, which was now warning pilots of inbound unidentified aircraft.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I now had to lead a wingman that I had never flown with before, but we were assigned to intercept.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The seven MiGs were flying at around 35,000 feet. Four broke away and headed toward Captain Williams, opening fire.\par \par \uc2 It ended up being, is this right, a 35-minute dogfight? Most of these things last for seconds.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Seconds, two or three minutes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But 35 minutes?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, 35 minutes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Williams returned fire on the Soviet planes, sending the first plane spiraling into the sea. His wingman broke away following the downed plane. Williams continued firing, taking down at least four more MiGs.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I just had another MiG smoking, losing speed and altitude, and I ran out of ammunition. And I got all kinds of battle scars, and lost pretty much the capability to fight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And 263 bullet holes and a 37-millimeter shell gash crippled your Panther.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: True.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Captain Williams landed safely back on the Oriskany but was unable to celebrate that successful battle.\par \par \uc2 For many years it was top secret classification?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. I had been informed, told not to talk about it. I didn't talk to anybody, including my wife.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Including your wife.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Including my wife.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The account was declassified in 2017. Williams could finally share his story, more than 65 years after the events unfolded.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I told it to my wife, and she said, oh, Royce.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: California Republican Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  is now working to get Captain Williams the recognition he says he deserves.\par \par \uc2 Korea is often called the forgotten war. This seems like, because of the classification at the time, the forgotten dogfight of amazing proportions.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA, (R-CA): It's an amazing story of -- would have had an absolute impact on the war had we known that Royce, now Captain Williams, was in a dogfight with Soviet pilots and Soviet aircraft. That would have been essentially World War III.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Captain Williams met with the Korean President Yoon in Washington this week. Yoon awarded him the Taegeuk Order of Military Merit, their highest award.\par \par \uc2 You have now been recognized by the Koreans.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The equivalent to the Medal of Honor. This little doodad here, I think it's a little overblown, but I have been thrilled.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Issa is still working on getting Williams the top U.S. military award.\par \par \uc2 You are on a long campaign for the Congressional Medal of Honor.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Ultimately it will be the president's decision. That's been toward thwarted about a Department of Defense. They keep talking about a bad precedent and procedures. But the reality is we believe that if the facts get to the president's desk, he will award the medal of honor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Williams received the Navy cross earlier this year. He hopes to continue sharing his experience and encouraging young people to join the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 If you had something to say to the next generation, considering all you have been through, all that you've seen, all that you've done, what would you say?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We're not quite the same nation, nor do I see the same outlet of young people who I feel should be thinking about a career in the military, because it's needed and because they are patriotic. And if I have any chance to speak to groups, I try and encourage them to think about it. We need them. They're hard to come by. My purpose, I guess, what time I have, is to try and promote that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Congressman Issa is not done advocating for Captain Williams. He says he should, quote, "at least be given the proper recognition he has not sought but richly deserves."\par \par \uc2 Trace, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Great story. Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's sluggish reelection campaign, and later, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: "Meh" is the new political strategy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is 142 years old.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I can't even say -- guess how old I am.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are two things I liked since he has been in office, but I can't remember either one of them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Biden?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I see no accomplishment that he has done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The student loan debt that really helps me and my daughter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He did mandate vaccines. That's really important.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: His greatest failure? Closing down the pipeline on day one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pulling out of Afghanistan could have gone smoother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've got inflation, unemployment, prices, housing. I mean, homelessness.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody is just more expensive because of what they have done. We had the energy, why don't we use it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Inflation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in our panel, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," \uc2 Harold Ford Jr\uc2 ., former Tennessee Congressman and co-host of THE FIVE, Meredith McGraw, "Politico" national political correspondent. Welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 Kimberley, to you first. It kind of comes out of the soundbites we just heard from some of the voters. Here is how voters feel about how things are going in the U.S. Let's put this up on the screen, satisfied 30 percent, dissatisfied 70 percent. Regardless of what is happening in the country, that is a very big negative number, Kim.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Oh, yes. And so it's quite remarkable that they are relaunching a campaign. This is a president who has very low approval ratings, they're only getting worse. And I think one of the things you are seeing happening here is the party had unexpectedly better midterm result than they thought. They should know that the reason for that was because Republicans didn't run as good as candidates as they might have. But this White House has preferred to run on the notion that was because their policies have been great and that the public likes them. And that puts the president in a really deep hole even as he begins this reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And one of the big factors, \uc2 Harold Ford Jr\uc2 ., in this is really the president's age, 80 years old. By the time he finished a second term, we are talking about 86. "Axios" writes the following, quoting here, "Many White House officials say they are amazed at Biden's stamina, often adding the caveat "for his age." Some White House officials say it's difficult to schedule public or private events with the president in the morning in the evening, or on weekends. The vast majority of Biden's public events happen on weekdays between 10:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. Some White House aides privately have compared Biden to an aging king. He has a tightknit palace guards of long time aides whose first instinct protect him and not take chances." 10:00 to 4:00 is not really the job description here, Harold.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: First off, happy Friday, and thanks for having me on. I think we are in the stage of this campaign where one is a positioning stage and one is the engagement stage or phases. And right now they are trying to position themselves knowing if they will face some incoming around questions about his age and some of the things that some of those who were interviewed in the beginning segment talked about, the border and other things. The president will talk about his infrastructure spending. He will talk about the investment in the Chips Act. He will hope that inflation is curbed and maybe, even maybe, we are cutting rates by this time next year, because that's when the engagement phase begins. And that's when you begin to define your opponent.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden is going to have to define his opponent as being out of touch with where mainstream Americans are who want everything from choice to some of the spending priorities that we have talked about. So we'll have to wait and see. But I've got tell you, it will be a challenge as every presidential reelection should be, and we'll sees a President Biden and his eventual nominee perform.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Interesting, Harold talks about his opponent, and it's interesting because I want to put this up. This is the presidential nominee preference on the Democratic side. You see the president there with 62 percent. Robert F. Kennedy Jr. at 19 percent, and \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  at nine percent. It's interesting to me, when you look at this, Meredith, that RFK Jr. has those kinds of numbers. Is that totally name recognition here? What do you think is happening?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH MCGRAW, "POLITICO": I think it's part name recognition. And I do think it is partly because a lot of Democrats are looking for a fresh face according to the polling that we have seen about this election. There is a lot of frustration, I think, from voters that we're looking at a lineup that could once again be a square-off between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is leading with Republicans in polls right now.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, you talk about Biden and Trump, the rematch, Kim, and "The Intelligencer," "New York" magazine, writes the following here, "There is reason to fear" -- the headline, by the way, "Trump could definitely beat Biden." "There is reason to fear that Biden's economic record will get worse before it gets better. He has presided over the highest inflation in half-a century and voters broadly disprove of his economic management. Judging by the spread between the three-month and 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  rates, markets believe there is a nearly 58 percent chance of the U.S. entering a recession by March 2024. Thursday's lower than expected GDP numbers lend credence to that forecast." You throw a worsening economy, Kim, in with that 70 percent negative rate, and you might have trouble.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Well, absolutely. And especially because I know that President Trump has very high negatives himself, except for this is a very closely divided country. And at a certain point some people may feel if things are just so bad anything is better than what they have at the moment. The other thing, Trace, I really believe Democrats are banking this is going to be a Biden-Trump rematch. I think that that played into the president's decision to run again. But that could be a very big mistake because, if Trump is not the nominee, and we are talking here about questions of age, if there's a much younger, more dynamic Republican nominee, and then couple that with those economic troubles you are talking about, the age difference itself alone could be a deadly for the president in terms of his re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I hit you on a different topic here, Harold. This was breaking news, we led the show with it. This is Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  saying to "The Wall Street Journal" the following, quoting here about the Dobbs opinion, "I personally have a pretty good idea who is responsible." This is for the leak. "But that's different from the level of proof that is needed to name somebody. It was part of an effort to prevent the Dobbs draft from becoming the decision of the court, and that's how it was used for those six weeks by people on the outside as part of the campaign to try to intimidate the court."\par \par \uc2 Are we at the point, \uc2 Harold Ford Jr\uc2 ., where this has become pivotal again, to find out who leaked this document?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think we would all love to know. I'm a little disappointed to see Justice Alito insert himself like this in this conversation. I do think, like many Americans, I would love to now how that was -- and more importantly I would love to see it not happen again, a leaking of anything that the Supreme Court or the justices are thinking about.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's run on now to Winners and Losers. Meredith, start with you, winners and losers?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: I'm going to go with winner this week, Kevin McCarthy, with his ability to pass this House vote on the debt bill. He was able to wrangle Republicans there in his caucus. The question now is whether or not that will bring President Biden to the negotiating table. They haven't spoken since February.\par \par \uc2 And then my losers is the free press, where a lot of us are going to the White House Correspondents Dinner this weekend, but "Wall Street Journal's" \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  is detained in Russia, and we shouldn't take it for granted.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Kimberley, quick, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes. My winner is Nikki Haley for giving what was a thoughtful abortion speech about consensus. My loser, the federal regulators who admitted today that they were indeed asleep at the wheel when it came to the regulation of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Harold, we're going to have to save your -- go ahead, go ahead yes, throw it out.\par \par \uc2 FORD: All the guys that got drafted last night in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  first round, best to them, their dreams came true. And the loser is our intelligence review process. We have to figure that out better so we don't have these guys running around --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chargers got a good wide receiver. Harold, Meredith, Kim, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, it's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That's why I'm running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: "Meh" is the new political strategy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meh?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is 142 years old.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can't even say -- I guess how old I am.\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: What we were simply looking for was clear scientific guidance. And when we couldn't get it, we did it ourselves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People just don't take responsibility and accountability for the consequences of their recommendations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The yeas are 217, the nays are 215, the bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You seemed so adamant last night.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm always adamant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We lifted the debt limit. We sent it to the Senate. We have done our job.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It is entirely normal for a president to be briefed on reporters who will be asking questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Never, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a scandal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think every once in a while in politics we need to have a little bit of fun.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Giving companies their own governments, that is not what a free market is all about last I checked.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: If \uc2 Disney\uc2  would like to move their hundreds of thousands of jobs to South Carolina, I will be happy to meet them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That chapter is over now and I'm excited about the new adventure here in New York.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Something touched me deep inside the day the music died.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And bye-bye -- Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, Benjamin Hall will have his exclusive interview with Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 . They will discuss the war in Ukraine, China, and other global hotspots.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests will be \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  and Maryland Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. Please join me for FOX NEWS AT NIGHT FOX News at night, midnight on the east, 9:00 here on the west, 9:00 pacific.\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth hosting for "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" this evening. Hey, Pete.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230429ej4s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Banking Fears; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2  (R-WA); Secret Chinese Police Stations; Biden's Primary Challenges; Republicans Push Back Against New Mortgage Rules; Economy Slowing; Border Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 Grady Trimble, Gillian Turner, Kelly O'Grady, Jacqui Heinrich,  Gary Kaltbaum, Nate Foy, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Two weeks until Title 42 goes away, and we're already seeing a tidal wave of migrants flooding across the U.S. border on foot, by train, even high-speed chases, as thousands of migrants set up camps on the Mexican side of the border, officials expecting about 10,000 migrants to cross into the United States daily when Title 42 ends on May 11.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to two border sheriffs sounding the alarm in a moment, but want to say welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First, though, let's go to Nate Foy in Brownsville, Texas, where the border search has already started -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration today sharing its plans for the end of Title 42. But, in advance of that, we are already seeing massive numbers in Brownsville. Just behind me, several hundred migrants are beginning their processing. And this comes after, yesterday, 2,800 migrants were processed in this field behind me.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video. This has been happening every single day here in Brownsville. But to mitigate the effects of the end of Title 42, the administration is increasing legal immigration pathways, especially for migrants with families already in the United States.\par \par \uc2 In fact, DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas says families in general will not be detained at the Southern border. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: We have no plan to detain families. As I mentioned, we will be employing alternatives to detention, including some innovations in that regard, and we will on a case-by-case basis use enhanced alternatives to detention as warranted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Charles, the administration also plans to open migrant processing facilities in countries like Guatemala and Colombia, so big groups don't gather at the border like we're seeing just across from El Paso right now.\par \par \uc2 Shelters there are already overrun, with 35,000 more migrants waiting across the river in Ciudad Juarez for the end of Title 42. Now, take a look at this other migrant camp in Matamoros, Mexico, right by where we are. Another thing the Biden administration wants to do is process migrants under Title 8.\par \par \uc2 And, if they're deported, they will be barred from reentering the country for five years, which is not the case under Title 42. And back out here live, one more thing to report regarding this today, Charles. President Biden is authorizing the secretaries of defense and homeland security to call individuals or units in the ready reserve to active-duty to fight the international drug trafficking that's happening on our Southern border every single day.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Nate, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Now to two sheriffs on the front lines in all of this, Brad Coe, sheriff of Kinney County, Texas, which shares a 16-mile border with Mexico. Also with us, Mark Dannels, sheriff of Cochise County, Arizona. That stretch is across 83 miles of border.\par \par \uc2 Sheriffs, first and foremost, I want to say thank you for joining us and thank you for your service.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff Dannels, let me just start with you.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this going? Upon expiration of Title 42, what are you expecting?\par \par \uc2 MARK DANNELS, COCHISE COUNTY, ARIZONA, SHERIFF: Well, first of all, thanks for having me, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And, Sheriff Coe, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 I will say this. We're already in crisis mode. This is what's being set aside, the new normal on our border. I saw a report the other day, a couple of days ago, that over 280,000 people post-Title 42 going away. Second busiest area will be the Tucson Sector with over 66,000 in the next month.\par \par \uc2 That's scary stuff, Charles. But what I have not seen, absent the media, is an operational plan from DHS.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sheriff Coe, I want to pick up on that.\par \par \uc2 So, Alejandro Mayorkas says that they expect an increase. They acknowledge that, but then, when he says things like they have innovation, innovative ways of detention, and there's other preparations, does that give you any confidence? It just sounds like fancy mumbo jumbo.\par \par \uc2 If I'm on the other side of the border, it doesn't seem like a deterrent.\par \par \uc2 BRAD COE, KINNEY COUNTY, TEXAS, SHERIFF: Well, thanks for having us on.\par \par \uc2 And I agree with Sheriff Dannels. But what the secretary is saying is, basically, alternative modes of detention. That means we're not going to do anything. I mean, alternative mode, we're going to just send you on your way, let you hole up in your house or visit with family.\par \par \uc2 There's no solution. There's nothing concrete that says these people will qualify to be put over here. These people will qualify to put over there. It's just we're just going to play it by ear and we're just going to cut loose whatever we need to cut loose.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So let me stick with you for a moment, Sheriff Coe.\par \par \uc2 I read what one border mayor said -- and this is a quote -- that he's expecting a massive and unrelenting deluge. If they're prepared to call out National Guard and other folks to help, should that be done now? I mean, should that be done ahead of this?\par \par \uc2 Because everyone knows you have got thousands of folks who are camping out at this very moment ready for that green light.\par \par \uc2 COE: It should have been done 30 days ago, because it's going to take time to get the mechanism in place, get the personnel in the right locations, get with the local police departments and sheriff's offices, and find out where the help is needed, not wait until we're going to mobilize everybody after the 11th.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sheriff Dannels, we have had this conversation too often, to be quite frank with you, and the really sad irony of it all is that, every time we have it, it's gotten worse, not better.\par \par \uc2 And I know it's been tough on you. I know it's been tough on your team. Are you speaking with the administration? Is anyone saying, hey, we will give you some sort of assistance, as they acknowledge that there will be an increase?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Well, they're not speaking to sheriffs, I can promise you.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff Coe and I were just in Texas last week for meetings. No operational plan was shared with us, because I don't think there is one. Here's the problem, Charles. There's no prioritization to the Southern border to secure it. As long as we're not going to secure the border, make it a priority, all we're having is political theater on our borders on the back of every community throughout the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 That's the tragedy right there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It is a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 And we see the fentanyl busts, and we know how many got-aways are there. And we know that among them are folks from countries that we never even associated in the past with the Southern border. It is a -- it is again in this country free for anyone with any kind of nefarious means, Sheriff Dannels.\par \par \uc2 And, at some point, you would think that would resonate. I guess it's got to become a political football for this administration to touch it, and, even then, it's doubtful, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: It's very doubtful.\par \par \uc2 I mean, past history is indicative of the future. So, after the last two years of seeing what we have seen, the tragedies, the death, the public safety challenges, an erosion with the quality of life in communities, I don't anticipate it getting better until there's an election.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sheriff Coe, I read where three million have been expelled since March of 2020, when Title 42 went into place.\par \par \uc2 Is that a number that sort of suggests what we have going to be expecting over the next year or so?\par \par \uc2 COE: Well, I think that number is going to be exceeded over the next six to eight months...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 COE: ... the way things are talking, because we have got a meeting with DHS Monday to talk about -- see how they're going to handle it versus what we're going to see here in Kinney County.\par \par \uc2 And I -- three million, I think that's just going to be a drop in the bucket over the next year.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, your work has cut out for you. We applaud it, and you're in our prayers. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Sheriff.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks. New numbers show the economy is slowing, and that's got a lot of folks worrying. Plus, a new FOX poll showing not all Democrats are riding with Biden. Was RFK Jr. right, there is a hunger for a Biden challenger on the left?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR. (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's like running against somebody in your family, but, ultimately, I think I'm in a much better position to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Read them and leap. The economy growing just over 1 percent in the first quarter, and that is a lot cooler than everyone expected, still not recession.\par \par \uc2 However, voters not feeling too great about it, though. In fact, a new FOX poll showing nearly 80 percent think the economy is just fair or poor.\par \par \uc2 I want to get the read now from Douglas Holtz-Eakin, former director of the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, forgetting about politics for a moment, when you get 80 percent, Doug, I mean, that's sort of bipartisan. You people just aren't feeling great about where we are, and I think they're feeling worse about where we're heading.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, I think there's reasons for that. In this report, you can see a strong household sector, personal consumption spending up over 3 percent at annual rate, but the business sector is flat.\par \par \uc2 And CapEx business investment has been the place in the economy that leads us into downturns. In every postwar recession except the pandemic, it's business spending that goes down first and households fall a quarter or two later. So that's been weak in the past three reports. It's flat, essentially, today.\par \par \uc2 I'm concerned about that. And the other bad news in this report is on the inflation front. If you look at the Fed's preferred measure of inflation, the sort of market-based core PCE that it's called, well, that's going up year over year at 4.6 percent. It was 4.9 when they started raising over a year ago.\par \par \uc2 So that's not a lot of progress, despite everything they have done. So there are reasons for the public to be concerned about the outlook.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The savings rate went up too, which I think is intriguing, because we keep reading and hearing about the strength of the consumer.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think economists are in a tough pickle here, because we have never seen this before. There's never been this many -- trillions of dollars were poured into this economy.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't go away overnight.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The Fed can try as much as they can, and some of the things they're doing, I think, are punishing people who've already spent their stimmy checks.\par \par \uc2 And so you look at consumer confidence, you look at this, it feels like, yes, people may still have cash...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... but they know, the American public knows that once that runs out, they may be headed to it. They may be running into a brick wall.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, certainly we have never seen anything like May of 2020, when the U.S. saving rate went to 33 percent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's just positively un-American. But Americans do not save a third of every dollar. So that that's been sitting in the pipeline. They are going through it, no question. If you look at the confidence numbers, they don't actually look that bad for the current conditions. But you ask about the outlook, they're very concerned about the outlook.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's expectations, though.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: That's the issue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, expectations yesterday versus current conditions are at the biggest gap since 2000.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I just think the American public is really brilliant, Doug. I think they know things before the folks in the ivory towers on Wall Street and certainly before the folks in D.C.\par \par \uc2 So, what -- play it out for us, because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is meeting soon. Everyone thinks they're going to hike rates again.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It just feels like a difficult -- it like we're living on borrowed time.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, I'm not -- I don't have a great news story here for you, because the Fed is going to raise again. They have to.\par \par \uc2 They have committed to getting inflation back to the 2 percent goal. They haven't made a lot of progress. Yes, there are long and variable lags, but they still know that they have to have more restrictive policies. So they will do that.\par \par \uc2 What happens to CapEx? Well, companies are not going to make acquisitions. They're not going to expand factories.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: They're going to cut back on their equipment and everything else. And what do you get in the second half of this year? You get a flat to down economy.\par \par \uc2 And so those worried about recession probabilities, I think they're dead on. The second half of this year is where the probability of recession is very high.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: After -- so we got one more hike. But here's the thing that might be a little bit more frightening than everyone agreeing that the Fed is going to hike, the massive amount of folks saying that the Fed is going to have to cut rates before this year is over, maybe in September.\par \par \uc2 Doug, that means they have destroyed the economy, right?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, if you're hiking, hiking, hiking...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... then, all of a sudden you have got to make a U-turn and start cutting, cutting, cutting, you have broken something badly.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, this is among the big disconnects that we see right now. It's really remarkable.\par \par \uc2 There's the current conditions versus expectations. There's the Fed's view of the future versus the markets. The Fed's view of the future is, we raise again in May and maybe thereafter, if the data say so. The markets are saying, no, no way. This thing is going to break, break badly, and they're going to be cutting by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 So someone's wrong there, and, usually, it's the market. The Fed has an enormous amount of power, and -- but if they're not lined up, it makes it even worse when the hit comes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Doug, I want to show its gears here. The House Republicans, they passed that debt ceiling bill.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says it could save nearly $5 trillion -- that's with a T -- over 10 years. So I want to share with you the White House's response today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Avoiding default is Congress' responsibility, and they should act in -- on it without preconditions, as they have done in Democratic and Republican administration. As the president said yesterday, he's happy to meet with Speaker McCarthy, but not on whether or not the debt limit gets extended.\par \par \uc2 That is not negotiable, and we have been very clear about this for the past several months.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Doug, five minutes ago, Democrat Senator Joe Manchin released this statement that read, in part: "Senator McCarthy did his job. He passed a bill that would prevent default and finally began to rein in federal spending. While I do not agree with everything proposed, it remains the only bill moving through Congress that would prevent default, and that cannot be ignored. It has now been 85 days since the president sat down with the speaker. Only the president can prevent this from becoming a full- blown domestic crisis."\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the market was down big, in part because of this. It's going to get a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Everyone's talking about 2011, where our status, our AAA status was knocked down a notch. How afraid should we be?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, I mean, this is pretty simple. We have to raise the debt limit. Everyone knows it. There is no moral high ground here. We have raised the debt limit with policy riders like the Republicans just passed. We have raised it in a clean fashion. So anything goes in this.\par \par \uc2 It's important for it to get done. The House needs to pass it. The Senate needs to pass it, and the president needs to sign it. There is no way you can make this Congress' problem. That doesn't make any sense.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: It is the art of politics to get to yes. They need to get to yes and get to yes quickly, because the damage of running up against the debt limit is very real. We have seen that before.\par \par \uc2 And so I don't understand the unwillingness of the White House to engage, engage quickly, and get this done. That's how bills get through Congress. The White House says, what's the House need, what's the Senate need, what's the bipartisan way to raise this debt limit?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Here we go. Needs to happen.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Just -- unless there's some kind of crazy gambit that the Republicans would take the political blame for it and pay a price and during the election, but that was one -- that would be a hell of a price to pay for Americans.\par \par \uc2 Doug, we ran out of time.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes, do you think cratering the global financial system is good to make a political point? Come on.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Listen, welcome to politics 2023.\par \par \uc2 Thanks a lot, Doug. You know about it more than I do.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, much more on the political fallout with this economy slowing down.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to Doug Schoen and \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 . I bet you they can answer that question.\par \par \uc2 But, first, come Monday, will a new Biden rule encourage risk at the expense of homebuyers doing everything right? -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, bad credit, don't sweat it. Your mortgage fees could be going down, but at whose expense?\par \par \uc2 We will have the full story and what Republican lawmakers are doing to stop this new rule from going into place next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Point, click, buy. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  shares are on fire right now, stock up around 9 percent after the company profits and revenues topped Wall Street estimates.\par \par \uc2 It was across the board, folks, online advertising, the cloud.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, changes on the way for would be homebuyers.\par \par \uc2 As of Monday, borrowers with higher -- higher credit scores will face higher rates to offset those with lower credit scores paying more for their mortgages, that plan getting pushback on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Grady Trimble is there. He's going to break it all down for us -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are rolling out a bill to try to roll back this new mortgage rule that the Biden administration supports. Under the rule proposed by the \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2 , mortgage fees are going up for borrowers with higher credit scores and larger down payments. Fees will go down for borrowers with lower credit scores and smaller down payments.\par \par \uc2 GOP lawmakers make the case the rule penalizes homebuyers who have done everything right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): It's nuts. The federal government, first of all, has no business doing any of this stuff. But it's just, again, this Biden administration's desire, this equity agenda, is completely upside down.\par \par \uc2 REP. PATRICK MCHENRY (R-NC): This is manipulating the marketplace for a political outcome. It does not make these institutions safe. And it's not fair to the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Republicans also worry the rule would reward risky borrowing and lending, potentially leading to a repeat of the 2008 housing crash.\par \par \uc2 But the head of that \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2  is pushing back. She says in a statement out this week in response to the critics: "To be clear, the series of steps taken by the \uc2 FHFA\uc2  to update \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 's pricing framework will bolster safety and soundness."\par \par \uc2 But the country's largest associations for realtors and home builders also have major concerns about the rule, which, as you mentioned, Charles, goes into effect Monday, May 1.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Grady, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So what does my next guest make of this, FOX News contributor Gary Kaltbaum?\par \par \uc2 And, Gary, I don't know. I'm getting that feeling again, these easy rates, no money down. But the problem with this one this time is that you're actually punishing people. You're going -- we're going to punish people who did everything right.\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, call me not surprised. We have been hearing from this administration, if you are of wealth, you are not paying your fair share. And now that they don't have power, they got to find other ways, and they do it with rules, regulations, fees, fines, mandates and moves in every type of business.\par \par \uc2 And here we go again. And here's the shame of this. People with good credit scores does not mean they're wealthy. So they're actually going after some people that may not be of wealth, but do have good credit scores. And the other party equation, let's just hearken back to '07, '08.\par \par \uc2 You incentivize people who may not be able to afford homes into homes. And you know what happens if the home prices go south? They drop the keys on the doorstep. And that's when you get into somewhat of a vicious cycle. So I would just wish they stay out of our way, stay out of business. We'd be so much better off without them.\par \par \uc2 But, no, they have an agenda, and that is, if you are of wealth, they are coming after you. And they have told us so.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But the point you made, though, is like, there are so many people who are not wealthy and they want to own a home. So what they do is, they pay their credit card bills on time. They don't apply for a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 They sort of -- and it's pretty easy these days, right? You can \uc2 Google\uc2  or watch a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  video or speak to someone. It's not as hard as in the past to learn how to get your credit right. It's just -- really, it's just a matter of discipline.\par \par \uc2 And that means maybe sacrifice. And this is why it bothers me, is that people who are making those sacrifices are now being told, too bad.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Look, there are advertisements 24/7 on TV, all over the Web how to get your credit score better. And I can promise you there are plenty of people out there that don't have big bucks that have good credit scores because they have been doing everything right. And now they have to gift other people with money they may or may not have.\par \par \uc2 So, all across the board here, it makes absolutely no sense. It's government just getting in the way again and somehow, some way, this will get screwed up. We're already in a housing market right now that's getting a little bit more fragile, with interest rates higher and just prices too high and affordability a factor, and now this.\par \par \uc2 What are they going to do next? By the way, we have been hearing stories about giving away zero percent mortgages again in certain areas also.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: It's all bad news. People should be able to earn loans and not be given loans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the people getting these breaks, they're going to find out soon that it really is not probably the gift that it's being advertised as.\par \par \uc2 Gary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I have got some breaking news need to share with the audience. This out of Wisconsin, folks, new video just in. Multiple people are reportedly injured after a train derailed, falling into the Mississippi River. Now, we continue to monitor the situation, and we will pass along updates as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you're looking live at the White House, where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  may be looking at some serious challengers from his own party -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles.\par \par \uc2 New FOX polling shows the president does still get most of his party's support for the primary. But you have got one challenger with a significant chunk, and now that is lending weight to some calls for the president maybe to hold primary debates. Will he do it?\par \par \uc2 We will talk about it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It's almost like crickets from everyone else in your party, even the progressive wing, that supposedly you would appeal to, certainly more than the president. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MARIANNE WILLIAMSON\uc2  (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, there's this decision made. It's, we will not primary Biden. I believe in elections. I believe in anyone who feels moved to run for president putting their ideas out there, submitting their resume, as it were, and their agenda to the American people, and then let the primary voters decide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you feel any pangs of pain taking on this president.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 I -- it's like running against somebody in your family. But, ultimately, I think I'm in a much better position to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To the 2024 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 And Neil has spoken to both Democratic primary challenges jumping in, this as a new FOX poll shows a growing portion of Democrat voters may want President Biden out.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Jacqui Heinrich is at the White House with those details -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, Charles.\par \par \uc2 FOX polls show that President Biden still is far and away the leader among Democratic primary voters, 62 percent of them saying they'd support him. But a significant chunk, nearly a fifth, at 19 percent, are throwing their support behind Robert Kennedy Jr. And that is lending weight to calls from some progressives for Biden to hold Democratic primary debates, to participate in primary debates.\par \par \uc2 But pundits say that's just not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There will be no debates.\par \par \uc2 And so he's effectively frozen the field. He runs the party machinery. And, as I said, since the left is -- seems is to be all in, the possibility of a challenge from the left, which is where these challenges typically come from, is remote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We should note that dominant progressives like Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  have both been sharing their support for Biden's second term.\par \par \uc2 So perhaps this push diminishes unless primary support for Biden splinters even further. But FOX polls all show -- also show that voters want to see this president answer questions on his feet more often, 67 percent saying that he should hold regular press conferences.\par \par \uc2 At yesterday's press conference, there was quite a stir because a photographer snapped a picture of his pocket card, which appeared to show that he got a heads-up on what one reporter would be asking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: "The L.A. Times" said that their reporter did not submit any questions in advance of yesterday's press conference.\par \par \uc2 So, to people who saw that pocket card, can you explain how that ended up there and why the president needed something like that?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, look, we do not have specific questions in advance. That's not something that we do. Our job is to get a sense of what you all want to ask him. That is our job here, to get a sense of what's the news of the day, the topics you all are interested for -- to ask of him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, of course, the president's ability to answer questions on the fly has been under scrutiny his whole time in office, but extra scrutiny now after announcing his reelection bid at the age of 80, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So what do my next guest make of Biden's challengers taking nearly 30 percent support so far?\par \par \uc2 Democratic strategist and pollster Doug Schoen is with us, along with Republican pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Doug, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Pretty impressive start so far, particularly for RFK Jr.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes, and I have read what he's had to say, Charles, and he is appealing to those Democrats who are, yes, environmentalists, but beyond that those that are anti-funding the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And he's got a compelling message about spending the money that would be spent in Ukraine here at home to help the poor. I think and I have written that he could get as much as 30 percent of the vote. And this poll confirms for me that that's certainly possible.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Lee?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: So look, the polling is significant.\par \par \uc2 At this -- he's at 19 percent and barely has announced. When you look at the Republicans, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , others aren't even breaking 10 percent. So this says to me that there's a real lane. Does it mean that there's a threat to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being the candidate again? I don't think so, but this does do damage to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you think about when this has happened before, it happened with \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . \uc2 Teddy Kennedy\uc2  tried to challenge him. It didn't go so well for him in his second term. And George H.W. Bush was challenged by Pat Buchanan, and ultimately that did a lot of damage to him as well.\par \par \uc2 These kinds of things, when you have a challenger putting the chink in the armor of the person in the front, it can do a lot of damage, not only to that candidate, but to the voters and their enthusiasm, which can have an impact on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 So I think, really, are we going to see a different candidate than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Likely not. But will he be damaged by this? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, then, Doug, with that in mind, then, the notion of sort of democracy, of this mission, before, voting always felt like we were on a mission to find the right person. It was a process. And each party had their primaries, and then they would sorted out and found the right person, and they went head to head.\par \par \uc2 And now it feels more like, hey, it's organized. You can do it from a basement. We're going to harvest votes. It just doesn't have the same feel to it. I don't know. I just -- maybe I'm just being nostalgic. Maybe I'm been silly, but it doesn't feel democratic, for lack of a better word.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I would say both parties, the Republicans who have a first-pass- the-post system gets all the delegates, the Democrats who, as you point out, appear to be pretty well buttoned down, both parties would benefit by opening up their processes.\par \par \uc2 That being said, many of the items you cited, Charles, are really, I think, a result of the pandemic. And the reason why Biden's not being challenged, and, as Lee suggested, it's very tough for incumbent presidents to be defeated in primaries.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Can they be weakened? Yes, absolutely. Defeated? Probably not.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, so we got another FOX News poll showing voters feeling really down on this economy. Will this affect Biden building support as he kicks off his campaign, Lee?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: There's no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people's view of the economy is getting worse and worse. And it's also, when you look at this poll, significant that there's a significant number of women who are impacted by the economy. They're saying that they feel worse than the general numbers that you're looking at, and also people who are under a certain income level.\par \par \uc2 So those are the people that he's really supposed to be focused on and yet they feel very, very disappointed by his economic policies. The flip side of this is the abortion issue. The abortion issue is going to be a big one, because you can see already the first thing that after announcement they did is they sent \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out to do a speech on abortion.\par \par \uc2 About two-thirds of people who are pro-choice say that's the primary reason that they're going to vote. And that's over the economy. So that becomes a little bit trickier to watch, because absolutely number one issue is the economy, until you start looking at it in a slightly different way.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Doug, one of the things that -- and I guess maybe inside the White House, they really seem to be P.R.-centric, if you will. But when you see these polls one after another showing just how people feel about the economy and every time the president speaks he says it's amazing, does that disconnect at any point come back to haunt him?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Well, I think it could, but it also explains why, in his announcement video, he spent a great deal of time attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and ultra-MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 In both 2018, 2020, and 2022, that attack on the Republicans and on the former president was in part or substantially successful. I believe, given the bad economic news, we're going to see more negative politics from the Democrats, and I'm sure the Republicans will answer in kind.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, two of the very best, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meanwhile, folks, the secret is out on China's operating police stations in the United States. Now what's the \uc2 FBI\uc2  going to do about it?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: It's frankly outrageous that the Chinese government would think that it could set up shop here on our soil and conduct uncoordinated, unsanctioned, illegal law enforcement operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  on the hot seat today over a wide range of issues, including secret Chinese police stations in the United States.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Gillian Turner is on Capitol Hill with the latest.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  is in the hot seat today here on Capitol Hill. He's making the case to Congress about why his bureau needs or should get $11.5 billion of taxpayer money. He says they need it to fight China. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: My priority has been since early during my time as director the Chinese government, the Chinese Communist Party, which I think represents the greatest threat to our country.\par \par \uc2 And we have grown the number of investigations into the Chinese government and their actors by about 1300 percent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Wray says the \uc2 FBI\uc2  right now has more than 2,000 open investigations into the China threat.\par \par \uc2 Republicans on the committee, meanwhile, spent a lot of time criticizing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid at former President Trump's Mar-a-Lago estate, accusing the bureau of -- quote -- "caring about politics more than law enforcement."\par \par \uc2 Wray insisted his agents are focused only on a law enforcement mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I have turned over the entire leadership team since I joined the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . I have put in place all kinds of new policies, procedures, trainings, systems enhancements, all to reinforce that sort of top-line message that I was describing to you a minute ago, which is that we're going to follow the facts wherever they lead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Earlier today, the Justice Department's inspector general was grilled about alleged abuses at the very powerful and secretive Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court.\par \par \uc2 Michael Horowitz there admitted that the government conducted 3.4 million searches of Americans' data without warrants, and a million of them, he says, were mistakes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HOROWITZ, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT INSPECTOR GENERAL: Obviously, very concerning that there's that volume of searches, the error rate that was reported on.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): And that error rate was what?\par \par \uc2 HOROWITZ: I believe it was around 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: Yes, I'm a lawyer, not a mathematician. But you're talking about seven figures of error.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: As you mentioned, off the top. Charles, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director also touted the bureau's recent takedown of those illegal Chinese police stations in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 He says that fits a pattern now of Chinese behavior that is only going to get worse -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gillian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party sending a letter to Wray expressing disappointment over the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's failing to answer questions about those police stations today.\par \par \uc2 Well, Wray answered some of those questions. My next guest is a member of that committee. I want to bring in Washington Republican Congressman \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, are you satisfied with the answers, with the responses that you received today?\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN NEWHOUSE (R-WA): Charles, thanks for putting some real focus on this very important issue.\par \par \uc2 I got to say, not yet. I liked some of the things he started to say, but there's a lot of questions that remain unanswered, not just for us here in Congress, but I think the American people want to know what's going on with the CCP here in our own backyard.\par \par \uc2 We should -- in our country, the United States of America should be a haven from persecution. It should not be a hunting ground for the Communist Chinese Party. We have got to figure out what's going on, combat it, and put a stop to it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I remember back in the '70s and '80s in New York, in particular, Chinatown, the pawns and the underworld that had connections back to China.\par \par \uc2 NEWHOUSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, even then, people were amazed at something like that could exist.\par \par \uc2 Now the notion that the communist government themselves can actually mete out their own form of justice in our country and intimidate people on our soil is beyond mind-boggling.\par \par \uc2 NEWHOUSE: It's beyond concerning.\par \par \uc2 This should be alarming for every single American. This should not be happening in our country. And the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , unfortunately, couldn't answer any of the questions we posed to them before a classified briefing, didn't address them during the briefing, so we had to send another letter so we can get clarity on what they're doing, how they're combating this, and how they're going to stop it.\par \par \uc2 This is not the 1970s or the 1980s. This just proves that China continues to be a growing threat, and we have got to stop it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I heard they want more money or need more money. This is always sort of the knee-jerk response.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not sure what the budgetary issues are, but it feels like, no matter what, we should be getting a better enforcement than we're getting so far.\par \par \uc2 NEWHOUSE: I couldn't agree with you more.\par \par \uc2 Why would a nonprofit think tank be able to find out this information of these police stations that are located not just in our country, but in countries around the world, before our own \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  found that information out?\par \par \uc2 I -- still, like I said, we have a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So we got the balloon. We have got this.\par \par \uc2 NEWHOUSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Congressman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And please continue to hold their feet to the fire. And, as you do that, we will continue to report it.\par \par \uc2 NEWHOUSE: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 Back to that train development, folks, in Wisconsin. Reports say four people taken from the scene with non-life-threatening injuries. Officials also saying any hazardous materials on board were contained, and they do not pose a danger to the public, at least not at this time.\par \par \uc2 We will have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So even with an initial $30 billion bailout from big banks, \uc2 First Republic\uc2  is still fighting for survival, down another 56 percent this week and sparking worries of more bailouts, but from whom?\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady is here with the very latest -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, it's great to see you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 First Republic\uc2  finally seeing green today, right? They were up roughly 9 percent. But despite that bounce, the stock is still drowning in red, like you said, down more than 50 percent this week alone.\par \par \uc2 So, zooming out, the share price losing roughly 95 percent of its value since the beginning of the year. To put that into context, the company was worth over 440 billion in November. Now it's worth just $1.15 billion today.\par \par \uc2 Now, sources tell me \uc2 First Republic\uc2  is seeking help in earnest. Remember, it already received $30 billion to shore up deposits from some of the banks you see there, like \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 . But the appetite to help yet again is weak, and so is the government's desire to step in, reportedly.\par \par \uc2 It did take over \uc2 SVB\uc2  and Signature, repaying all those deposits to customers, even those that were uninsured. But the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  did not commit to backstopping uninsured deposits across the board. And the White House today did say it's monitoring the situation and has tools at its disposal, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 But, listen, some in the banking world we have spoken to, they're questioning whether the current financial state of \uc2 First Republic\uc2  makes it worth saving. Leadership shared during earnings it lost over $100 billion since the collapse of \uc2 SVB\uc2  in deposits.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to highlight part of what you're seeing in the share price is pure panic. Management had highlighted during earnings that the deposits had stabilized for multiple weeks prior to Monday, which they were hoping would calm fears. But focus, of course, has remained on that headline $100 billion number.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 First Republic\uc2  does appear to be in a virtual no man's land. I mean, bigger banks, they don't want to seem to help, the government reportedly reluctant to act, and the bank itself would unlikely be able to survive any sort of run if it happens again.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, I will close with this. We have that Fed meeting coming up next week. You have to start thinking, are they going to hike rates?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, to that point, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  went on this one-of-a-kind, never- before-seen rate hikes that began last year, 75 basis points, 75 basis point, 70 -- interest rates skyrocketed. A lot of banks were caught flat- footed and so they're going to probably hike rates yet again. That means banks like \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , Kelly, are certainly going to -- going to have some more problems.\par \par \uc2 And so is it too early to handicap just how many more dominoes will fall?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: It's a great question.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think there was a lot more trepidation last month. I almost feel like we're starting to get desensitized to this a little bit. Like, oh, well, maybe \uc2 First Republic\uc2  will fall. You're not seeing as much of a spike in the market as you were back in March with \uc2 SVB\uc2  and Signature. So I think it's possible, if \uc2 First Republic\uc2  were to go down, that you would see a run on regionals. But\par \par \uc2 I think people do feel fairly secure in like a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  or \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , those bigger banks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Well, you know what? And I think, ironically, when \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  kept talking and talking and talking that week after \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , she implied that, hey, if move your money out of these banks to certain banks, like the ones you just named, you're going to be OK.\par \par \uc2 So whatever bank run we have, a lot of that's going to be on the administration. A lot, of course, is on the fact that these smaller banks cannot compete with money market funds and things like that.\par \par \uc2 But let's go back to \uc2 First Republic\uc2  for a moment, because I think one of the problems here is, if you bail out their depositors, this is a bank that went after rich folks. They are in the Hamptons. They re in Beverly Hills. They're in Silicon Valley. They gave these great mortgage rates to billionaires.\par \par \uc2 Do we want to bail out more billionaires? I mean, is there -- is it -- to me, that would be crazy, because if I have an account somewhere at the Bank of Idaho and I have 400 grand in there, only 250 is going to be covered by the \uc2 FDIC\uc2 . I think it would be a dangerous mistake politically and I think morally if the administration does that.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Well, and financially, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, where does the money come from? You mentioned the billionaires. I think \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  had a 1 percent rate on his mortgage. I'm like, oh, gosh, I'd love to get something like that for my first home.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I do think that's a fair point. The one thing that I will push back on, \uc2 First Republic\uc2  has a lot of small business customers too. And so that's where I think there would be the concern that there would be some toppling.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that they allowed for those low mortgage rates was, OK, you have to put all of your deposits in First Republic. And it has me questioning, why isn't someone regulating that? That feels like an accident or chaos waiting to happen.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes. That's a big question.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is being questioned about that. At least they should. They let everyone down.\par \par \uc2 Great, great stuff with this reporting, Kelly. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, folks at home, thank you for watching. Now, remember, catch me tomorrow and every weekday 2:00 p.m. Eastern on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 Again, huge day for the market. If \uc2 Amazon\uc2  holds up, we could have another huge day for the market. We may be off to the races.\par \par \uc2 Until then, here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230427ej4r0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Former U.S. Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 ; Banking Worries; Interview With Sen. Roger Marshall (R-KS); Interview With Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA); Teachers Union Leader on Capitol Hill; Interview With Former Chrysler CEO Robert Nardelli; Debt Ceiling Fight; \uc2 Disney\uc2  Sues \uc2 Ron DeSantis\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Simonetti, Aishah Hasnie, Ashley Webster, Shannon Bream,  Chad Pergram, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,206 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: From the Mouse House to the courthouse? The battle between \uc2 Disney\uc2  and Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  just got a lot uglier.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  filing a lawsuit against the governor and other state officials, accusing them of weaponizing the power of government to punish a private business. DeSantis' office is already firing back, saying it is \uc2 Disney\uc2  that is out of bounds. What is this all about? And would Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley be picking this fight? Hear what she just told me in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World"\par \par \uc2 First to FOX Business' Ashley Webster in Florida, where this battle just took a whole new turn -- Ashley.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Maybe not that unexpected. Mickey Mouse, yes, taking the gloves off and going to court in this escalating fight over \uc2 Disney\uc2 's special tax district, one that Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has been trying to dismantle.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was filed just minutes after the newly appointed oversight board struck down those last-minute agreements that were made between \uc2 Disney\uc2  and the original board. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN GARCIA, CENTRAL FLORIDA TOURISM OVERSIGHT CHAIR: We didn't learn about these agreements that our lawyers tell us are unlawful until we had been serving on this board for about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  picked the fight with this board. We were not looking out for a fight. We had agreed to meet with the leadership of \uc2 Disney\uc2  to figure out how we could work together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Well, that didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 And in the complaint filed in federal court, \uc2 Disney\uc2  names DeSantis, his five-member board that replaced the Reedy Creek Improvement District and other state officials, saying -- quote -- "A targeted campaign of government retaliation orchestrated at every step by Governor DeSantis as punishment for \uc2 Disney\uc2 's protected speech now threatens \uc2 Disney\uc2 's business operations, jeopardizes its economic future in the region, and violates its constitutional rights."\par \par \uc2 Now, this dispute began last year when \uc2 Disney\uc2 , under former CEO \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2 , criticized and called for the repeal of Florida's so-called don't say gay law to ban the teaching of sex and gender-based education to most elementary school students.\par \par \uc2 Florida's governor then vowed to strip away \uc2 Disney\uc2 's special privileges and talked about taxes on the hotels, tolls on the roads, perhaps developing some of the property that the district owns with more amusement parks, even a state prison next to Walt Disney World.\par \par \uc2 Well, for \uc2 Disney\uc2 , it was too much. But responding to the lawsuit, a statement from DeSantis' communication director said -- quote -- "We are unaware of any legal right that a company has to operate its own government or maintain special privileges not held by other businesses in the state. This lawsuit is yet another unfortunate example of their hope to undermine the will of the Florida voters and operate outside the bounds of law."\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , the current CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , has also hit out at DeSantis, calling his actions towards the company anti-business and anti-Florida. And now all of this mess, Sandra, is headed to court.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, we will be watching this.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the potential political and legal fallout from all of this.\par \par \uc2 With us now is RealClearPolitics' Tom Bevan and our own Shannon Bream, anchor of "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, what happens next now that DeSantis has responded?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, HOST, "FOX NEWS SUNDAY": OK, so we're going to wait and see how this plays out in the courts.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you this. There will be all kinds of motions to try to dismiss this thing, get rid of it, make sure it never actually gets to a courtroom. But there are a number of very serious charges in here, including contract violations, due process violations, First Amendment violations, \uc2 Disney\uc2  basically saying, we were told to sit down and shut up. We have a right to express ourselves, just like anybody else does.\par \par \uc2 And, interestingly enough, they say they were left with no choice but to file this. And they say it's all about \uc2 Disney\uc2  being punished, that the state government was weaponized against them because they expressed a view that was unpopular with state officials.\par \par \uc2 So they're coming out this -- with a number of counts, but expect the governors legal team to meet this in federal court and say it doesn't even pass the first blush. Don't put it to an actual trial.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Tom, I had a chance to ask Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley about all this as it was breaking earlier today on "America Reports." This was an exclusive interview. Here's her reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This is obviously dramatically escalating the feud between \uc2 Disney\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . They are alleging that the Republican governor has waged a -- quote -- "relentless campaign to weaponize government power" over the company.\par \par \uc2 What is your reaction to that, as we still have yet to see him jump officially into the race?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As governor, I took a double- digit unemployment state and I turned it into an economic powerhouse. Businesses were my partners, because if you take care of your businesses, you take care of your economy, your economy takes care of the people, and everyone wins.\par \par \uc2 And so that's the way we dealt with it. We are -- South Carolina was a very anti-woke state. It still is. And if \uc2 Disney\uc2  would like to move their hundreds of thousands of jobs to South Carolina and bring the billions of dollars with them, I will let them know I will be happy to meet them in South Carolina and introduce them to the governor and the legislature that would welcome it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Kind of an interesting pitch from the presidential -- presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 What do you anticipate is the political fallout from all of this?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, CO-FOUNDER AND EXECUTIVE EDITOR, REALCLEARPOLITICS.COM: Yes, I saw that interview this morning, Sandra, and it did. It shocked me.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, this is an issue that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- really helped propel him to his overwhelming victory, one of the only Republican overwhelming victories in 2022, his reelection bid in the state of Florida. It was very popular among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And, suddenly, he finds himself now not an official candidate, but on the other side of both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Nikki Haley on this issue, who are suggesting that he is, in fact, being anti-business and that he should let this go with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . And it's a fight that he's not willing to let go.\par \par \uc2 So I -- it is going to be part of -- assuming he gets in the race, it is going to be part of the Republican nomination process. And it's going to be interesting to see, because, look, DeSantis' view is, when he frames it as -- as corporate fairness, that no one should have special privileges, no corporation, especially \uc2 Disney\uc2 , no matter how long they have already had them.\par \par \uc2 They should be subject to the same rules and laws, even playing field as all the other corporations in the state of Florida. That sounds like a winning position, a commonsense, not necessarily anti-business position.\par \par \uc2 But if you frame it the other way, the way his critics are framing, the way Trump and Haley are framing it, suddenly, it does look like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a vendetta that he's carrying out against this company. He's weaponizing this government against corporations. That's not a very winning position, even among Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 So it's going to be interesting to see whose view, whose argument on this issue prevails.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will see how that plays out.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Shannon, I also had the chance to ask Haley about her stance on abortion after that big speech yesterday. She isn't taking a firm position on how it should be handled at the federal level, not really specific details, but she says that won't be an issue for her in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Is it a risk not to be more specific with your plan?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: No, I think it's about addition. It's about not alienating something that is incredibly personal to women and to men.\par \par \uc2 It's about handling it with respect. So what I will tell you is, you're going to have some states that are going to decide more on the pro-life side. I am happy to see that. I'm strongly pro-life. You're going to have some states that go more on the side of abortion. I wish that wasn't the case, but that is what it is.\par \par \uc2 Let's focus on what's closest to the people. And when it comes to the federal level, let's at least start with what we all agree on, and let's get national consensus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I should note, Shannon, this is the same day that Asa Hutchinson came out and was really specific on where he stands on that federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 But how do you think this will play out in a GOP primary, her stance here?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it's really tricky because I pushed Governor -- Governor Hutchinson this on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we got to the point of, OK, are you a 15-week ban? Where are you at the federal level? Because so many GOP candidates are saying, this is about the states. We let them decide.\par \par \uc2 Well, you saw one of the major pro-life groups last week, Susan B. Anthony List, came out and said, listen, these GOP folks who want to be the nominee, who say leave it to the states, they think that is not a pro-life position, because we do have a number of states that allow for abortion up until a due date, and including the District of Columbia here in D.C. as well.\par \par \uc2 So a number of pro-life groups are probably not going to be super happy with where some of these federal candidates are trying to land. They want more specificity, and they want a federal ban because they know the states are all over the place.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Tom, I have got a few seconds left. Real quick reaction from you.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: It's going to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was just in Iowa speaking to evangelicals there and was in favor of a federal ban. So there's a split among the Republican candidates. And, again, it'll be part of the Republican primary process.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, we saw what happened in the midterm elections. The GOP obviously took notice then, and we will see what they do about it now.\par \par \uc2 Shannon and Tom, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And now to this FOX News Alert, to the debt limit fight.\par \par \uc2 It is ongoing and a big vote that could be moments away.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with the very latest on that.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The House could vote within the hour on the GOP debt ceiling bill. Some members balked. That forced House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to change the bill. He needed to convince some GOP members to vote yes.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace called the bill weak last night. Today, she's a yea.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: You seemed so adamant last night. I mean, that's a pretty dramatic...\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): I'm always adamant. I'm always adamant.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: I know, but that's a pretty dramatic change of position there.\par \par \uc2 MACE: But we had a very productive meeting. It was very good, because, up until then, a lot of us have not been included in those conversations. And so to get that commitment from him that he will work with us on balancing the budget and what that might look like, either a balanced budget amendment or some other mechanism going forward, then I feel very good about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy altered the bill to win the support of farm state members. They opposed the bill because it eliminated tax breaks for ethanol.\par \par \uc2 That move won over members from Iowa, Wisconsin, and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK ALFORD (R-MO): Our district is very heavy with corn and ethanol and then biodiesel and soybeans. We have a lot of farmers and investments in those that we need to protect. We brought that to the attention of Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 And with great deference and understanding, he came back with a proposal that we were willing to accept and move forward with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Victoria Spartz of Indiana is now a yes, the same with George Santos. However, Tim Burchett will not budge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): Still growing over $1.5 trillion. It's adds another -- it'll be at 30 -- almost $34 trillion.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: A lot of that is defense. A lot of that is entitlements.\par \par \uc2 Are you willing to cut some of that?\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: And defense -- and defense -- yes, yes, heck, yes. Defense, every day, when you have -- the Pentagon loses over a billion dollars a year unaccounted for, and then they just let it go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's about the math.\par \par \uc2 The GOP can only lose four votes, but there's one absence. However, some Democrats are missing. Passage of the bill puts the ball in the court of President Biden. The final bill needs 60 votes to clear a Senate filibuster -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, thanks for watching it, Chad.\par \par \uc2 So, with both sides digging in, as we keep digging ourselves into more and more debt, what is the impact if lawmakers can't figure this out?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from former \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and Chrysler CEO \uc2 Bob Nardelli\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bob, it is great to see you. Thank you very much for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 We just heard from a bunch of lawmakers there on Capitol Hill. They just can't seem to get this done, not yet, at least. We will see what happens a few moments from now in Washington. But before I ask you what you think will happen, spell it out for the American people. What does it mean if this doesn't get done?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT NARDELLI, FORMER CEO, CHRYSLER: Well, Sandra, good to be with you on this very, very critical subject.\par \par \uc2 This is not something that we should take lightly. Depending on the breadth and depth of it, it could spiral down to -- this is reminiscent of some of the things we went through in '07, '8, and '9, potentially a cataclysmic event for the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, Social Security and Medicare will be impacted. By law, they have to be funded. But, again, they won't be -- the government won't be able to borrow. We will see a lot of discretionary spending put on hold.\par \par \uc2 We could see, just like \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , where a 41 percent run on the bank, we could see panic out there depending on how deep this thing goes and how long it goes, Sandra. So this is a very, very dicey situation. Look, I thought the bill was pretty solid, about the 1 percent, go back to '22.\par \par \uc2 I think, getting back, repealing the \uc2 IRS\uc2  -- I'm not upset about the comprehensive on ethanol. That's not a big deal in my mind, if it got some additional votes to put this thing through. But, again, this is a very sensitive time for us.\par \par \uc2 And, again, there's some speculation that we really don't have until August. It could be June. We could see a run on T-bills. Interest rates will go up, but people won't be able to borrow anyway. So this has a tremendous impact on our country, relative to our political position, and what it means for business, exactly to your point, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 While the Treasury has set its own deadline, there are big Wall Street banks like \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  who are predicting we could reach this moment even sooner in this country, which makes this -- what's happening in Washington right now, at this moment, so critical.\par \par \uc2 So now what do you think will happen? You just heard from some of those lawmakers like Nancy Mace. We had her on this program yesterday. She's been one of the sort of the holdouts. She seems to be coming around. Can McCarthy get the votes?\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: Yes, I'm encouraged. I'm encouraged with the change of heart, change in mind on some of the Midwest holdouts on this thing.\par \par \uc2 I think it's critically important, important that the GOP stay together and pass this bill. Now, it has to -- there has to be some compromise within the party, but we really need to set aside these political positions that we're taking. We got to be look at the bigger picture of what this means for our country, for our citizens, for our business, and, again, on a global scale.\par \par \uc2 What happens? I mean, is the government going to have to start selling gold, Sandra, to try and come up with being able to pay these debts that are out there? This is -- this is a very, very dicey game. It's not for the light of heart.\par \par \uc2 This is serious business that this administration and Congress needs to take it on and resolve it in a positive fashion and achieve some of the goals that have been set out by this GOP. I think we have got to get this spending under control.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: It has been tremendously negatively impacted on the businesses I deal with every day, Sandra, whether it's Fortune 500s, family offices, private equity, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And so the certainty of uncertainty of this just adds to the complication and the hesitation for business to move forward.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Which is why so many, no matter what side of the aisle you sit on, want to see the president meet with McCarthy. He hasn't done so.\par \par \uc2 Today at that news conference with the South Korean leader, he said he would. So we will see what happens and what comes from that. But, meanwhile, Bob, all of this is happening with the backdrop of President Biden running for reelection and the latest NBC News polling showing that nearly 60 percent of voters disapprove of his handling of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The president didn't seem to note that in his news conference he held earlier. He was touting his many successes he sees under his presidency. What's your reaction?\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, with all due respect, we hear a lot of rhetoric coming out of the administration. And, Sandra, we have talked about this before. The first day of his presidency, he took aim at the energy industry. Basically, we surrendered energy independence.\par \par \uc2 Now we see the continuation of that, where the \uc2 EPA\uc2  wants to put more pressure on power plants, some of the cleanest gas combined cycle plants out there today, more pressure on them. We lost our ability to use LNG as a political tool where we could have supplied LNG to Germany and some of the other countries that now are being pressured by Russia...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: ... and some of our, I want to say enemies, but certainly not -- not friends of the country right now.\par \par \uc2 So, if you look at inflation, we have talked about that. It has had devastating impact on...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: ... a lot of our businesses.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There's no doubt.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: The supply chain, we did nothing -- we did nothing to de-clog the ports, to bring materials and products...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: ... so that we could have momentum going.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, if I was the president, I sure wouldn't want this trail of tears to run against an opponent in 2024, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is going to be a big issue. It was the top issue in the midterm elections. Over and over again, voters said it's about the economy. So we will see how the candidates handle this.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Bob. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, well, you have heard Democrat Senator Joe Manchin take on President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for refusing to reach across the aisle on our debt disaster. Is another centrist Democrat joining him in that criticism?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Virginia Senator Mark Warner coming up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): This is the United States of America, not the divided states. And you have got to get in a room and work out the differences, for the sake of this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: This is the United States of America, not the divided states. And you have got to get in a room and work out the differences, for the sake of this country.\par \par \uc2 This is a responsibility. This is his job. This is a responsibility of the leader of the free world and definitely of the United States president. He has to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Joe, no, he didn't.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia Democrat Joe Manchin calling out the president for failing to negotiate with Republicans over the debt ceiling, as the House GOP gets ready for a vote.\par \par \uc2 What does our next guest make of Manchin's criticism?\par \par \uc2 To Virginia Democrat and Senator -- \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2  member Mark Warner, who also chairs Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 We have got a question coming up that pertains to that, but first on these stalled debt talks, OK? The president said earlier when he held this news conference at the White House -- he was with his South Korean counterpart. He was asked where these stand. It was sort of an impromptu questioning that a reporter shouted at the end, Senator.\par \par \uc2 What about meeting with McCarthy? He said: "I will meet with McCarthy if he takes default off the table."\par \par \uc2 Well, doesn't he do that in his plan, take default off the table, Senator?\par \par \uc2 Welcome.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Well, Sandra, let's look at -- and we will see in I guess another hour or so whether the House has the votes.\par \par \uc2 Of course, I remember this was the same House that promised that they were going to have no midnight changes to bills, and people were going to read the bill. Obviously, none of that is happening.\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the things I think if this -- if this proposal gets through the House, don't think it has much life at all in the Senate, even on a bipartisan basis, because when the American people hear that it's going to lay off all the Border Patrol, that it's going to cut law enforcement by 30,000 officers, that it's going to cut food safety inspections by a third...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Cut the deficits...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WARNER: ... that it's going to cut back opioid relief by a billion dollars, this is what's in the document.\par \par \uc2 But, again, we don't know if he's got the votes. But I don't -- what we should not do is default. What we should do is what we did with President Trump and go ahead and fight over things like what our appropriations ought to be. And, frankly, the appropriations process in the Senate is starting on a bipartisan basis.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. So, Senator...\par \par \uc2 WARNER: The House ought to come back with theirs, and that ought to have a fight.\par \par \uc2 But what we should do is what -- the same thing we did and take President Trump's word to say, you don't mess with default. And, frankly, the biggest -- the dumbest thing the Democrats did during the time that we raised the debt ceiling three times under President Trump is, we should have gotten rid of this political hand grenade, because I would hope your viewers would know the debt ceiling is about whether we're going to pay the bills on money we have already spent.\par \par \uc2 It is a total political hand grenade. And the times it has been used -- the last time it was used this way, it put America nearly in default.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But at what point, Senator -- can I get in here?\par \par \uc2 At what point do you say -- at what point do you say, enough is enough? You have got the average American voter, the average American citizen sitting at home, they see this debt per citizen on the rise. We were just showing that debt clock.\par \par \uc2 They know they are on the hook for this, Senator, and they have had enough. They're struggling through this.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And the...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You have got two-thirds of the country living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 They're taking on more and more credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And I know you look back at past experiences with this, and you say, we have done this before. We have had a clean bill where all we do is address the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 But what about this time? Is there nowhere where you can agree to cut spending to get this done with Republicans?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Respectfully -- respectfully, years ago, one of my first bipartisan gangs was around the Simpson-Bowles plan, where we brought a bipartisan group together to actually look at revenues, look at spending, look at some of our entitlement programs.\par \par \uc2 Candidly, neither party has much -- much validity on debt issues. I do think, if the House passes this plan -- and you never know until it's over -- when the American public finds out what's in it, I think there will be huge pushbacks.\par \par \uc2 But we ought to have a discussion over government spending. That's where we have appropriations bills, and House should do theirs, we should -- the Senate should do theirs, there be a conference.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Now you got people really worried.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And that is the normal order.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Neither party has validity on debt issues? Neither party has validity?\par \par \uc2 So, who's in charge here? And what are we going to do about the message that this says -- sends to our adversaries around the world that we cannot tackle this?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: What it says is -- what it says is, there is -- what it says is, there is no other industrial country in the world that has a political hand grenade that says, after you have spent the money, we are going to then decide whether we pay the bills.\par \par \uc2 And that is not the way the \uc2 United States government\uc2  operates. And only those, only those...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, just to be clear, McCarthy's plan is to pay the bills.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Ma'am, ma'am, only those -- only those who don't care about America's standing in the world would say we should even be dealing with a potential default.\par \par \uc2 You want to give -- you want to turn incredible opportunities over to our potential adversary in China, have the United States default on the debt.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think that that's part of the Republican plan, right, is to avoid default, but to...\par \par \uc2 WARNER: You would hope. I mean, let's...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... cut some spending in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Let's -- again, I'm anxious. We will see if this passes or not.\par \par \uc2 But I am then anxious for this. So some of the very folks who said, we need to read the bill, read the bill, to come in and explain to your viewers what's in this bill.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. Well, we...\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I would love them to get on. I would please ask you to just -- and you got a fair right to ask me questions.\par \par \uc2 I would just ask you to do the same...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: No.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: ... and explain if these cuts of laying off the Border Patrol, laying off 30,000 law enforcement officers, cutting food safety by a third, cutting back on literally millions of potential veterans' health care visits...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: ... whether that is really what they thought was in the bill they may just be voting on, I guess, shortly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, noting Senator Manchin's criticism of this, some may wonder if there's other more centrist Democrats out there that might be taking issue with the direction this is going at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Senator, before we let you go, as chair of the Intelligence Committee, can you tell us about what you would like done on the classified leaks right now? What's the update? What is happening? And where is all that going?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 I got an update a little bit earlier today. We're -- we -- Senator Rubio and I have asked a lot of questions of the administration. We have got two sets of problems. We way overclassify documents. And there's -- it becomes way too easy to simply say, almost in a CYA, let's just call this a secret document or a top secret document.\par \par \uc2 I think there needs to be some assessment. The public's got a right to know. We ought to have a smoother process for declassification. So, overclassification is one end. And, then when we do overclassify, we potentially have way too many people, more than four million people, with security clearances.\par \par \uc2 And amongst those people who have the top clearances, top secret -- top secret and above, there may need to be some winnowing on a really need-to- know.\par \par \uc2 Why was -- this I.T. guy at a National Guard base have such clearance that he could not only have access to these documents, but see the whole contents? I think that's -- and I have got a lot of questions on that. There are technology ways where you could actually see the header of the document, but not the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, and there's also big questions about it circulating as long as it did before any Pentagon official was able to pick up on it.\par \par \uc2 I have got to...\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Great -- great point. Great point.\par \par \uc2 But here's the question. One of the things that we have done...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I only have got five seconds left. We're up against a hard break.\par \par \uc2 Real quick.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Would you ask me -- would you ask me to come back and actually -- because this is an issue that I think there are solutions.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I tell you what.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And we ought to be able to have a reasonable discussion on it, all right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, that's a deal. We will call you back.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And we will have you back on real soon.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Coming up: FOX on top of a teachers union boss on the hot seat right now over school closures.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie will give us the very latest.\par \par \uc2 But, first, Republican senators leading the charge against a new rule and new fees coming for homebuyers, asking why we're punishing those that are doing things right.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: In your \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Meta\uc2  platforms surging 9 percent after-hours, as \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent company beat earnings and sales estimates for the first three months of the year, a boost in daily active users also helping the stock.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senate Republicans slamming a Biden administration rule forcing people with good credit to subsidize high-risk mortgages.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is leading 17 other GOP senators in demanding to know how this policy decision was made.\par \par \uc2 Kansas Republican Senator Roger Marshall joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We do want to mention we did reach out to the \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2 , Sandra Thompson. We have not yet heard back.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I still find this hard to believe. And, by the way, it wasn't really out there and made readily available to the American people this was happening. I remember the moment that we all, as a team, noticed it. Our colleague Marc Thiessen put it up. It was in The Washington Examiner, a really critical piece on this.\par \par \uc2 We went to the federal housing authority Web sites \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2 , \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 . There it was, but it was not being widely reported. It was almost like they were trying to sneak this by us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER MARSHALL (R-KS): Right, Sandra, so another policy done in the dark at night by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 But this policy is starting to sound a lot like the subprime policies that got us in trouble with the financial crisis. So what is going on is the agencies that oversee \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  have been told to have decreased interest rates and decreased down payments for folks with low- income, low credit scores.\par \par \uc2 And then they're going to pass on that expense to people with good credit scores. So nothing about this makes sense. I think it's setting us up for failure. It's not fair. And, to your point, they have not followed the legal process.\par \par \uc2 So, when the administration makes a rule like this, they're supposed to submit it for comments that never happened. And that's one of the reasons we wrote this letter.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You mentioned it being reminiscent of the financial crisis and what caused the mortgage crisis, the mortgage meltdown, and the financial collapse that resulted back in 2008.\par \par \uc2 This sounds a lot like that. Helping people get into homes that can't afford them?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Exactly. It's like giving somebody a credit card with a higher credit limit than they need. But that s exactly what s happening.\par \par \uc2 And, look, we Republicans have a heart. We had the Community Reinvestment Act that I know, that all of our banks use that to help folks out with that first time home loan, trying to help people in the lower income. So we do have a heart, but we don't want to lead to another financial crisis on top of the inflation that we have.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it's just new homes are unaffordable for folks right now, created by Bidenflation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I spoke to a former Obama official, one that advised the former president on the housing crisis back in '08, and he said, this is not the way to go about this. To your point, we want to help lower-income, lower- middle-class minorities get into homes.\par \par \uc2 He said to me, this is not the way to do that, and warns, just like you are, that this could result the way 2008 and 2009 went. So what do you do about it? How do you stop this from happening? Because it sends a message that, hey, you know what, you don't have to pay your bills. Your credit score, if it's lower, it's actually going to benefit you to get into a home.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, typically, the way we get a way and get pushed back is to write a letter. And, hopefully, they will respond. They will back up. They will put it out for comments. There will be public pressure, including from organizations like yours as well. But, eventually, we can do a Congressional Review Act where it only takes 51 of us, 51 percent of us to repeal this rule.\par \par \uc2 But, right now they're not following the law at all. So this could be even unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I would think that there would be some sort of bipartisan support to stop this from happening, considering it does incentivize bad bill payment history and bad bill payment behavior, something completely opposite than what we would historically encourage in this country.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you so much for joining us and keep us posted on your efforts.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: You bet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 , meanwhile, shares continue crashing. Should we be worrying a new regional bank scare is spreading? Down 30 percent today.\par \par \uc2 Then, a teachers union boss on the hot seat over COVID closures. What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Shares of First Republic falling further, after plunging nearly 50 percent in yesterday's trading session. Now folks are wondering what other regional banks may be on the brink again.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Lauren Simonetti has more on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, hello.\par \par \uc2 There are certainly worries, but there's not much panic today. \uc2 First Republic\uc2  not viewed as a systemic risk, but it does have problems. And the way they're handled, well, it says a lot about who the government thinks is important. First off, \uc2 First Republic\uc2  caters to the rich. Its customers' median net worth tops $3 million.\par \par \uc2 And some, well, they're worth a lot more than that, like billionaire \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  owner \uc2 Stephen Ross\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2 's \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 . He was offered a starting rate of 1 percent for a $6 million mortgage on his home in \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why tie up your cash when you can borrow almost for free? The problem is, borrowing is not free anymore. \uc2 First Republic\uc2  was a name mentioned during the demise of Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank. Its stock has been almost completely wiped out. Look at that. So has its deposit base, with $100 billion in customer withdrawals in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 The big question now is, what happens next? Are any of these 11 big lenders who infused $30 billion in First Republic last month ready for a round two? Or does this become another \uc2 FDIC\uc2  takeover? And it really doesn't look like the government wants to be bailing out depositors once again, Sandra, especially when those depositors are worth an average $3 million.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Indeed. All right, Lauren Simonetti on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lauren. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: You too.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 With China concerns growing, what to make of President Xi and Ukraine's \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  talking via a phone call? Where former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  sees it all heading.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALEY: This is a time where our allies don't really trust us. And it's a time where our enemies don't fear us. And South Korea is an ally. We need to work with them. We need to work with Japan in making sure that we're doing more anti-Chinese countermeasures to make sure that we don't allow them to become more dominant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A short time ago, 2024 GOP presidential candidate and former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley sounding the alarm on China's dominance.\par \par \uc2 And it comes as we are learning China's President Xi just spoke with Ukraine's President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  for the first time since Russia's invasion. We know China has been helping Russia. So, where is all of this heading?\par \par \uc2 Former Defense Secretary under President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Dr. \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Did that surprise you, when word came out this morning that President Xi of China had a phone call with \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: It was a surprise on one hand that he actually did it, because we know, when he traveled to Russia several weeks ago, we thought something might happen then, but then he backed off.\par \par \uc2 But, look, we know, in the last few days, the Chinese ambassador to France made these extraordinary remarks, saying that Ukraine and other states of Eastern Europe have no sovereignty, if you will, because they were former Soviet states.\par \par \uc2 I think, because of the blowback of that, that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  felt it necessary to make this phone call to kind of re-burnish, if you will, his international bona fides.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting analysis of that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you have got Nikki Haley. She's obviously already thrown her hat in the ring, a GOP presidential candidate. She is sounding the alarm on China and its growing dominance on the global stage. Where are you in this moment, and how big is this threat getting to us as a country now?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, I have been watching China for 25, 30 years, and they have been on this ascent. They now have the world's largest navy, the world's largest army. They're building very capable space and cyber forces.\par \par \uc2 They plan to match the size of the U.S. strategic nuclear forces by 2035. And, look, their goal is to undermine the international order and replace us by 2049 as the world's superpower. It's that simple. So we should be concerned. We should be alarmed.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Mr. Secretary, we obviously have a huge presidential election coming up. 2024 is quickly approaching, faster than I think many of us want to see it happen, but it is going to consume us, and this is going to likely be one of the biggest conversations on the debate stages.\par \par \uc2 What is at stake with who will reside in the White House for another four years? Possibly President Biden, or it could be a GOP candidate or another Democrat. We just don't know.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Yes, we have some major problems.\par \par \uc2 Look, I have been saying the biggest threat facing our country is political extremism from both sides of the political aisle, number one. Number two, we face a China that is, again, determined to supplant us in the world order, and it's our greatest strategic threat we face.\par \par \uc2 And then we have other domestic issues. I mean, the debt right now is $32 trillion. It's unsustainable. By the year 2030, we will be paying as much of GDP to the debt as it will to DOD for the defense budget. So, look, big issues. We need leaders who can lead on these issues and really take the country forward in a bipartisan manner.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, how do you tell the American people and viewers at home what that means to our -- our ability to protect ourselves, that growing national debt that we continue to take on?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I mean, today, it's $400 billion to $500 billion a year we pay alone on interest. Think what we could do with that money if we if we didn't have to pay down interest.\par \par \uc2 And I think, for every American taxpayer or citizen, it's like $100,000 a year that they're going to have to pay one day. So we have to get our nation's spending under control, or else it's just going to continue to eat away at our ability to deal with these major problems as the years go by.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Real quickly, as we wrap up here, how would you characterize President Biden's handling on foreign policy? He gives himself top grades, but how would you characterize it?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, there's parts of it that I see where he's done well, Ukraine, case in point, in terms of bringing the allies together and standing up to Russia.\par \par \uc2 But he's been very slow in terms of providing Ukraine the means to really defend itself and take the fight back to Russia. So, with Ukraine, it's like he's done enough so that Ukraine wouldn't lose, but not enough so that Ukraine can win.\par \par \uc2 And, on defense budget, he put forward a budget that is less than inflation this year, and it's going to really hurt DOD in the long run.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All really important stuff.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Thanks, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good to have you here in New York.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, that big hearing we mentioned on Capitol Hill today with the teachers union president \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  getting grilled over COVID school closures.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has the latest on that and what we heard in that hearing room next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETSY DEVOS, FORMER U.S. EDUCATION SECRETARY: That is a plain-out lie. She never asked for a meeting. She never asked to confer.\par \par \uc2 The unions and their allies never asked to confer. We worked hard to try to provide resources to have a forum that they were welcome to attend, as well as everyone, on getting schools reopened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A remarkable moment there, when former Trump Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  earlier on "America Reports" took issue with testimony by teachers union boss \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  that happened today at that hearing on school closures.\par \par \uc2 It is still ongoing. We're monitoring it.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie's on the Hill with the very latest on that.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I just popped out for a quick moment. That hearing is ongoing. And for the past 2.5 hours now, \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  is practically begging, pleading with lawmakers to believe that her union did not try to politicize or influence the CDC at all, they only did what they were asked to do, and that their number one priority was reopening schools.\par \par \uc2 A lot of House Republicans are not buying that argument. They brought her in today to find out why in the world the \uc2 American Teachers Federation\uc2  was involved with the CDC's school reopening guidance in early 2021 in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Now, Weingarten says the CDC approached them first. They handed over a number of ideas. Some were accepted, but there was really only one clear edit, she says, that was adopted. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Do you remember how many edits that you suggested?\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: We suggested -- we suggested concepts, sir.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : Do you know how many edits were included?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: One. One.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : It's unusual for a political union to have such a role in the scientific guidance process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, Democrats, on the other hand, are blaming the Trump administration for not providing clear guidance for these schools.\par \par \uc2 And they say Republicans are scapegoating these teachers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RAUL RUIZ\uc2  (D-CA): We are here to examine partisan allegations by House Republicans seeking to vilify our nation's dedicated educators.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): I have been to some weird hearings in this Congress, Mr. Chairman, but this one might be the weirdest.\par \par \uc2 How did you get my friends mad?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Weingarten, as I mentioned, failing to convince these House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 One just in there mentioned that he thinks that she should be fired -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Aishah, we will let you run back in that room. Thanks for the update.\par \par \uc2 Let's get back to Chad Pergram. He's on Capitol Hill as well, as the House is getting ready to vote on the GOP's debt limit bill.\par \par \uc2 Chad, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: We think they're going to start this vote here probably in the next 20 to 30 minutes. It's going to be a tight vote.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned earlier that Nancy Mace, she's going to be a yes. She called the bill yesterday weak. She's now on board. And here's what we have learned about the concessions that she got from the speaker. She will prep a balanced budget amendment, and she will also get a floor vote on access to women's reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 So that reflects the horse-trading that Kevin McCarthy has executed to get the votes for this bill, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Chad, thanks for the update. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That's all for today. Thanks so much for watching. I will see you on "America Reports" weekdays 1:00 Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 And "THE FIVE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230427ej4q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Florida to Rework Election Laws For DeSantis?; Teachers Union President on Capitol Hill Tomorrow; Cash Crunch; Michigan Approves Chinese- Backed Plant; Interview With Rep. Mark Green (R-TN); Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); President Biden Announces Reelection Bid\b0\par\par\uc2 Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Charlie Gasparino, Nate Foy,  Alexis McAdams, Kelly O'Grady, Chad Pergram, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 7,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It is official. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running.\par \par \uc2 But his announcement comes as tensions are running high over the nation's debt that keeps running up. And now he's running into pressure from another Joe and another Democrat, Senator Joe Manchin blasting the president for refusing to reach across the aisle to talk this debt disaster out.\par \par \uc2 We have all the ins and outs with Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, where Biden is digging in.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's get to Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill, where lawmakers are looking to hammer out a deal, and Republican South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace, who's looking for both parties to settle this mess right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "YOUR WORLD."\par \par \uc2 We begin with Jacqui with more drama surrounding the president's big announcement.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, we saw you at the White House press briefing a short time ago, but didn't see that you got any questions in.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it's been a hot second, since February 27, by my count. Quite a long time. We will keep trying.\par \par \uc2 But even more broadly, the press corps as a whole didn't get a lot of answers today about what went into the president's personal decision-making with this reelection campaign. And we also don't know where to direct those questions.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary was not able to give us a specific person to talk to, because a lot of those staffing decisions are still not finalized. However, amid a lot of looming questions about the president's age, this question to the press secretary seemed to trip her up a bit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Does the president plan to serve all eight years?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm not -- I'm just not going to get ahead of the president. That's something for him to decide. I'm just not going to get ahead of it. And there's a 2024 campaign. Anything related to that, I would refer you to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, Karine clarified her answer just after the briefing wrapped.\par \par \uc2 She tweeted: "As you know, we take following the law seriously, so I wanted to be sure I didn't go into 2024 more than is appropriate under the law, but I can confirm that, if reelected, POTUS would serve all eight years."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there's some mounting pressure for Biden to meet with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy, who has said a clean bill to raise the debt ceiling without spending cuts in the budget just can't pass. There aren't votes for it. Even centrist Democrats, though, are urging the president to sit down with the House leader.\par \par \uc2 And this was the official White House response when asked if the president risks rattling financial markets if he keeps rebuffing this invitation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It's the speaker, McCarthy, and the MAGA wing of the Republican Party that is doing this. We're not doing -- they're the ones who are saying they want to hold the American economy hostage. They're doing this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But the president is playing this game of brinksmanship, too, putting out a message today through the Office of Management and Budget that, even if McCarthy's debt plan reaches his desk, clearing not just the Republican House, but, for that to happen, the Democrat-controlled Senate too, the president would veto it.\par \par \uc2 He wrote -- or OMB wrote, rather: "The president's budget invests in America, lowers costs for families, grows the economy, and reduces the deficit by nearly $3 trillion by asking the wealthy and large corporations to pay their fair share. Therefore, if the president were presented with the Limit, Save, Grow Act of 2023, he would veto it."\par \par \uc2 Now, we have not been able to ask the president any questions about this or about his campaign announcement. Reporters haven't gotten close enough today, despite all the big news about him on his big day, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting stuff. Jacqui Heinrich, we appreciate your effort there in that Briefing Room. We will see what happens tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 Biden launching his bid with calls to come together, but now some in his own party are urging him to do just that on this debt deal.\par \par \uc2 To Chad Pergram now. He's on Capitol Hill with more.\par \par \uc2 Chad, how can they come together if they won't even meet together?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the president promised unity, but he's not negotiating on the debt ceiling. That enraged Joe Manchin. Manchin wants talks.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Manchin wants to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act. Remember, Manchin never got his pipeline in exchange for his vote on the IRA.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): This is the United States of America, not the divided states. And you have got to get in a room and work out the differences for the sake of this country. This is a responsibility. This is his job. This is the responsibility of the leader of the free world and definitely of the United States president. He has to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: \uc2 The House Rules Committee\uc2  meets right now to prep Kevin McCarthy's debt ceiling bill.\par \par \uc2 A senior Republican source says they are less optimistic today about passage of the bill compared to Monday. McCarthy isn't sure if he needs to change the bill to flip votes, but Republicans suggest the president's absence in the process is conspicuous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Should not even come within a mile of flirting with a Democratic debt default on top of all the rest. It's time for President Biden to stop the partisan stubbornness, join Speaker McCarthy at the grownups table and get talking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's about the math.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy can only lose four votes on his side. McCarthy scores a win if he passes the bill. A defeat is catastrophic. It sends a negative signal to the markets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID B. COHEN, UNIVERSITY OF AKRON: If McCarthy is unable to get a bill through, it's kind of disastrous for him, because it shows that he doesn't really have control over his caucus.\par \par \uc2 It confirms the suspicions that McCarthy has essentially an ungovernable caucus that he's trying to corral.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Ethanol is a flash point. Midwesterners oppose changes to ethanol programs. One source said there was -- quote -- "hand-to-hand combat" behind the scenes. They compared it to the January speaker's race -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Chad Pergram on that live on the Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Chad, thank you and good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest has reservations about the GOP bill, but says both sides need to come together to address this crisis now.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace joins us.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you very much for your time.\par \par \uc2 What reservations do you have at this point?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, at this point, the plan only carves out $3.5 trillion overall in savings over 10 years. And we would be looking at in the next 10 years, in 10 years, the same amount of debt that we have today, and it only pays off the interest, rather than the principal, on the debt.\par \par \uc2 There's no plan to balance the budget. When you talk about green energy, a state like South Carolina, we're dependent on solar, and we even have some manufacturing for wind components, wind energy components. And so I just want to make sure that that's not going to negatively impact energy prices in the state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But, also, I don't understand why we're not having a conversation about the budget. And some in leadership don't want to have that conversation right now. I want to know that there's a plan to balance the budget over the next 10 or 15 years, whatever it takes. I'd love to do it in five, but that's probably mission impossible.\par \par \uc2 But both sides are at fault here. When you look at President Trump, he added almost $8 trillion to the debt when he was president. President Biden has added almost $5 trillion. We're talking about $12 trillion to $13 trillion in debt just over the last six years. Both sides need to come together.\par \par \uc2 The president needs to negotiate with Republicans and fix the mess that they have both created. I don't understand. This isn't complicated to me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, still, the White House says it's Republicans who are holding this country hostage.\par \par \uc2 You heard that from the president himself, and you heard that from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  just a short time ago on the president's team at the White House. To that, you say what?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, that's quite a bit of fearmongering.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it was Republicans, when we had the majority, that we helped balance the budget in the past. We have had Democrat -- we have worked with Democrat presidents in the past to balance the budget. And so I don't buy that excuse. It's fearmongering, and it's wrong.\par \par \uc2 But both sides, Republicans and Democrats, created this problem, and they ought to find -- do a plan that actually fixes it. And that means curbing spending, cutting spending, shaving off the debt to be lower than it is today 10 years from now.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And, yes, actually having a plan to balance the budget would be a great idea.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, still, some on the left, including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , writing an op-ed in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  accusing Republicans of playing into the hands of Putin and Xi with this debt limit fight.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, she's writing this -- quote -- "Congressional brinkmanship on the debt ceiling sends the opposite message to our allies and our adversaries, that America is divided, distracted, and cannot be counted on. Competition between democracies and autocracies has grown more intense. And by undermining America's credibility and the preeminence of the dollar, the fight over the debt ceiling plays right into the hands of \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  of China and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  of Russia."\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, do you fear that we are playing into the hands of President Putin and Xi through all this?\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 And the irony here, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , is the fact that the last time the federal government balanced a budget was under your husband, President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . So I wouldn't want to hear any of that nonsense. I'm not pro- Putin. I'm not pro-\uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . Neither are most of the Republicans who want to see responsible spending, responsible cuts, and show a plan to balance things out over the next decade or so and curb the deficit that we have been holding on.\par \par \uc2 This plan doesn't even address the $1.5 trillion deficit spending just in the first six months of this year.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Since the midterm elections, many have made the case that Republicans really got the issue of abortion wrong and just how much that was weighing on the minds of voters as they head -- headed out to the polls, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge issue. We know that. Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley, she came out and addressed this head on earlier today. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have to face this reality. The pro-life laws that have passed in strongly Republican states will not be approved at the federal level. That's just a fact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Is that an acknowledgment you too are willing to make? And where should Republicans be on this issue right now?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, we need to show compassion. We need to show that we can be pro-life and pro-woman. The two are not mutually exclusive.\par \par \uc2 I'm a pro-life lawmaker. But, also, I want to make sure that women have access to birth control, so we can reduce the number of unwanted pregnancies. We want to make sure that we have protections for women and girls who are raped or victims of incest. We want to make sure, if we're talking about gestational limits, that we're not making it very difficult for women who've been raped to get exceptions, like the six-week ban that DeSantis signed into law in Florida.\par \par \uc2 That is not a bill -- it's basically a nonstarter for women who've been raped. It's very difficult for them to have to get those exceptions because of the regulations in the state of Florida based on that bill. But we want to show compassion.\par \par \uc2 And, somewhere, obviously not the third trimester, but somewhere between 15 and 20 weeks is where the vast majority of Americans are. They want exceptions for rape and incest. They want to show compassion to women. They want to make sure that rural areas, there are nurses and midwives and OB- GYN doctors that can help them with their prenatal care.\par \par \uc2 All of that makes a whole lot of sense to people on both sides of the aisle that we can get behind, and that's the message that we have to carry going into 2024.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Congresswoman, appreciate your time on all of that. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, by the way -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Nikki Haley will be joining me tomorrow in her first interview since today's major policy speech exclusively, 1:00 p.m. Eastern time, on "America Reports." So, tune in for that.\par \par \uc2 And, meanwhile, a quick look at the corner of Wall and Broad. As you can see it there on your screen, the Dow falling 344 points, falling to its lows of the session all the way into the final minutes of trading, investors jittery over mixed economic news and as major tech companies start giving us their first quarterly reports, with the Nasdaq the hardest hit of the major averages, down 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 All right, we're going to be hitting that just a bit later in the show.\par \par \uc2 And with the 2024 race taking shape, which side has more donors taking notice? Charlie Gasparino on what he is hearing coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: With Title 42 set to end at the border in just a few weeks, House Homeland Security Committee Chairman Mark Green just releasing a new border bill to hire more agents and restart wall construction. He will join us live in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first to FOX News' Nate Foy. He is in Brownsville, Texas, for us with the very latest on this crisis.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, that surge is expected to grow when Title 42 is lifted, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Sandra, even before the end of Title 42, we're seeing big numbers in the RGV Sector; 2,600 migrants have crossed illegally in the past 24 hours, including 2,000 just here in Brownsville.\par \par \uc2 Take a live look at our FOX News drone. As you can see, one group of migrants, about 100 of them, are being processed right on that levee right there. This has been happening all day. And I will ask our drone operator, Sven (ph), to pan down at the bottom of the levee, where another group of migrants are a bit farther along in their processing.\par \par \uc2 They will all be loaded up onto buses and then brought to a processing facility. But this has been happening here in Brownsville every single day for about two weeks. The RGV Sector chief says they're averaging about 1,000. Take a look at this video. We were here this morning as more migrants came.\par \par \uc2 Again, we are even seeing migrants in the overnight hours. We saw pictures with hundreds of migrants here in this same field last night. But take another look at this next video, Sandra. Just before arriving here in Brownsville, migrants are crossing the Rio Grande using a raft and a rope to guide themselves.\par \par \uc2 This part of the river is too deep, so they need to use that. But, before that swim, take a look at the conditions, Sandra, that these migrants are staying in. FOX News was in Matamoros, Mexico, yesterday at a migrant camp. A couple thousand migrants are waiting there right now for Title 42 to end. They don't have running water. They're living in tents. There's no work.\par \par \uc2 There's constant frustration with the Biden administration's CBP One app. Many can't wait and are coming over now, but some migrants don't want to be seen. Take a look at this next video from Hidalgo County. Texas DPS arrested a driver and apprehended five illegal migrants in a human smuggling chase.\par \par \uc2 You can see, the vehicle flipped. Migrants pour out of the car and run away. That driver is facing charges. And back out here live, on the same day that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced his reelection campaign, Texas Governor Greg Abbott says Texans and Americans cannot take four more years of President Biden's border policies.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Nate Foy live there in Brownsville, Texas, for us.\par \par \uc2 Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is leading the charge on a new border security bill.\par \par \uc2 House Homeland Security Committee Chairman and Tennessee Republican Mark Green joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, sir, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK GREEN (R-TN): Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So you want to hire more to work there at the border. Will this help, even though we have this predicted surge that is going to happen as a result of Title 42 being lifted?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 The cartel strategy, which, interestingly enough, Secretary Mayorkas said he wasn't aware of, but \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the attorney general, was, is essentially to use CBP and to manipulate our CBP forces by neutralizing them at crossing points.\par \par \uc2 So the more folks we can get on the line actually out in the rural areas, the better we are at stopping some of those -- the got-aways, the folks that are trying to avoid detection. Those are the real concerns for our country, the ones that don't want to be apprehended.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congressman, these are remarkable live images that are coming into us that our viewers are seeing on our screen being brought to us by that drone footage out of Brownsville, Texas, that Nate was just reporting on, just massive numbers of people that continue to make that -- there's been no deterrence to make that dangerous journey.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Absolutely. There is absolutely no deterrence. They basically have opened the border completely. They allowed the return agreements to the various countries to expire intentionally, so they can't return them.\par \par \uc2 The CBP One app that they have adjusted from what was intended to be a cargo processing commerce app to basically mass parole for anyone who uses it has just caused massive waves of people to come. And it's -- yes, there's incentive -- there's no disincentive. There's no detention, which really is the deterrence to folks coming.\par \par \uc2 The ICE beds are at record low normals -- levels. I mean, it's absolutely crazy.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That is really something as we continue to take in those pictures.\par \par \uc2 So I will get to the border wall construction in just a moment. But also part of this bill is modernizing border security technology. So tell us about that and how this would help with what we're seeing on our screen right now.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 You can do detection sensors that basically cover parts of the area of our border where their agents can't be located. There's, specifically, drones. There are other, like, dirigibles, the balloons that have cameras and forward-looking infrared radar, those kinds of things that detect people and then allow the Customs and Border Patrol folks to respond to specific areas.\par \par \uc2 The guys don't even have radios. Some of the Border Patrol agents don't even have radios. So our bill actually provides the funding so that every single agent has a two-way radio.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: As far as restarting the border wall construction, can you tell us geographically what that means, what that looks like? The funding is already set aside for this, right?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 There's $2.4 billion left over from President Trump. The -- President Biden, as soon as he got in office, shut down all construction, which cost several billion dollars to settle those contracts to the American taxpayer. But there's $2.4 billion left. We think about 900 miles of wall can be built. And we're going to tell them, spend that money.\par \par \uc2 And we're also going to mandate 200 miles of wall a year.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, as we anticipate the lifting of Title 42, can you just give us, in your own thoughts, what that's going to look like for this country