y that were coming across in that specific area.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, you still have hundreds and hundreds of miles of border that are still left vulnerable where people can come across illegally. But, of course, as a state, we're trying to do as much as we can trying to deter some of these people are coming across. But when we look at the whole situation and the totality of the circumstances that are happening right now, this is a responsibility of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 But yet we have been put in a predicament now where we have to act to do something about it because the federal government fails to secure the border, fails to protect the American people. So we're having to step in.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Abbott did that. March of 2021, we launched Operation Lone Star, and now we're using every single state resource that we have to help Border Patrol and also to help stop the flow of people coming across and actually go after the criminal activity that's taking place along the border.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Lieutenant, as we take in these live images, you can't help but wonder, where is Secretary Mayorkas? Where is the president? This is happening right now.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman Mark Green just predicted that we will see more migrants come over that Southern border than we did in the first two years of this presidency, six million in the first two years. He's saying seven to eight million possibly in the next two.\par \par \uc2 You're dealing with this firsthand on the ground, \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 . Where's Mayorkas?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: That's a good question.\par \par \uc2 Of course, you clearly cannot dispute what we're seeing right now on those images and what we have been seeing for the past two years, especially the exceptional work that our men and women are doing of DPS, our state troopers, our investigators, pilots, boat operators, everybody involved, that we have been trying to do as much as we can with every resource that we have for the past two years.\par \par \uc2 And yet we don't see no type of response or acknowledgment from the federal government. And yet this clearly continues to happen. And it's going to get much worse, unfortunately, when Title 42 is lifting.\par \par \uc2 Yet there's still no plan or policy in place. So, that's why, with this current homeland security bill, it is beneficial to Border Patrol. It will provide more agents, more infrastructure, more manpower, but that's currently what we have been doing for the past two years at the state level.\par \par \uc2 What we need is policy. Currently, right now, there is a judiciary bill that does address policy. Now, having those two in place will provide a more meaningful border security bill, and with those two will provide a fundamental change in border security and immigration.\par \par \uc2 So that's what we need. We need the policy in place. We need our Border Patrol agents back on the front lines, so they can focus on going after those criminals that are coming across the border, as well as drugs.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, to quote Mark Green directly, he told a crowd that his committee will expose Mayorkas' dereliction of duty and its intentional destruction of our country through the open Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant, we appreciate your time, sir. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Appreciate it, Sandra. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: no cash, no problem. Retailers say they have come up with a new way to help shoppers with all this inflation, but that may not be a good thing. We will explain.\par \par \uc2 But, first, hundreds in Michigan fighting back as a Chinese-owned factory gets funding to move in. The national security concerns that have folks there fuming over this -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hundreds in Michigan are charged over a Chinese-owned E.V. battery factory. We will get the latest.\par \par \uc2 And retailers offering a solution to Americans facing a cash crunch, but it might do more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Now to the growing threat from China, as hundreds of people living in West Central Michigan are fighting back after the state approved $175 million to help build a Chinese-backed E.V. battery factory.\par \par \uc2 And it's the location of that factory that has people worried.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams has the details on that for us.\par \par \uc2 What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is all part of President Biden's push for green energy that we have been talking about. But the issue is that these two massive E.V. projects are backed by companies with ties to the CCP. So, this is where the Gotion plant plans to break ground in West Central Michigan there.\par \par \uc2 Gotion, Incorporated, is a U.S.-based company that makes batteries for electric cars, but its parent company is based in China. So the company is looking to break ground in Green Charter Township, and it says it will make parts for electric vehicle batteries.\par \par \uc2 The Michigan Senate, Sandra, just barely approved $175 million in state funds for this project. Michigan Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  told you earlier that the U.S. works with these Chinese companies because they have the E.V. technology, but says the relationship is pretty complicated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): Let us be clear. If you are a Chinese business, the communist government is part of your business.\par \par \uc2 So it's something that worries me every day, and it's a balance that we have to reach.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Others are worried about national security risks here, as hundreds of residents are protesting this plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFF THORNE, MICHIGAN RESIDENT: I was amazed at how far people came from around our state to come to this, in support of this, and the people that came that spoke against it.\par \par \uc2 And we continue to meet. We continue to fight. And we're going forth with whatever option or plan that we have.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Questions are also swirling about a \uc2 Ford\uc2  plant in Marshall, Michigan, about 200 miles away, the \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  teaming up with a Chinese tech company, planning to spend more than $3 billion to build another E.V. battery plant, so a big push here right now.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen states, Sandra, have laws on the books that prevent investors with ties to foreign adversaries from buying U.S. farmland. Others, including Michigan, could soon be on that list, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That is a story a lot of people will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Alexis, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: FOX News Alert. We have got this just into us now.\par \par \uc2 The House Republican debt ceiling bill -- this is according to the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 , and this just in -- would cut deficits by $4.8 trillion over 10 years. This is obviously reviewing the proposed plan by the U.S. House Republicans to cut spending and raise the nation's $31.4 trillion debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 They say it would cut federal deficits by almost $5 trillion over a 10-year period. That is according to the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 . Just a few moments ago, that was released.\par \par \uc2 All right, meanwhile, while President Biden makes it official, Charlie Gasparino is reporting a new twist in the battle for the GOP nomination. Why Charlie says it's really getting nasty.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, this should have investors 