onsumer confidence -- this is consumer -- inflation obviously is up. But consumer confidence is down. And if you go back to Inauguration Day for President Biden, consumer confidence is down more than 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 And if people don't feel confident, they don't feel good, they're not out there spending money, Francesca.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's part of the reason you saw that President Biden today, in his first official event as a candidate, not a campaign event -- it was a White House event, but it was his first official event after announcing his reelection bid -- talked about the economy, talked about the jobs that he says that Democratic legislation, the Inflation Reduction Act, is helping to create, talking about the middle class, so going right to the heart of the issue on day number one.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And as far as those who feel that he's doing a good job on the economy, only 38 percent approval rate, according to that NBC News poll; 58 percent disapprove. So this is still a big area of concern for Democrats and for this president's reelection bid and for all of us who are living through it, right? We want the economy to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much, Francesca, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Coming up, as President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  officially jumps in, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may make a move to keep Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  out.\par \par \uc2 And a big teachers union president expected to get grilled tomorrow on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie, what is that all about?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  is headed for the Hill to testify tomorrow, but she's got some pretty tough legal help coming with her. We will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: Are you ready to cancel student debt?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Do we need the Supreme Court to do the right thing?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: When it's about our students, they challenge it. The corporations challenge it. The student loan lenders challenge it. That is not right. That is not fair. And that is what we are fighting as well.\par \par \uc2 So, we say, cancel student debt.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That was teachers union boss \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  turning up the heat on student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, she's going to find herself on the hot seat over another issue.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie is reporting live from Capitol Hill for us -- Aishah\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey, Sandra. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tomorrow afternoon, \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  will be under oath facing a panel that is looking into why schools were kept closed during the pandemic, when they feel schools should have been opened.\par \par \uc2 And she might have her brand-new attorney by her side tomorrow during this hearing. He is Michael Bromwich. The president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  has hired this new attorney who has defended in the past people like Christine Blasey Ford, Andrew McCabe, and Kim Foxx.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bromwich is coming out swinging, Sandra. He's already sent a letter to the committee's chairman, accusing this panel of using false and misleading claims, he says, as the basis for their investigation. So the COVID Subcommittee is investigating the teacher unions role in -- quote -- "editing the CDC's COVID-19 school reopening guidance and keeping schools closed longer than necessary."\par \par \uc2 You will remember, it was discovered that the unions suggested language wound up in the CDC school reopening guidance in 2021.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIANNETTE MILLER-MEEKS (R-IA): Very early on, it was shown that schools were not super-spreader events, that there was very low risk to children, that they didn't seem to transmit to adults, and also that teachers weren't getting ill from children.\par \par \uc2 No one looked at the harms that were created to children, the learning loss that was already significant in closing in the spring, the mental health problems.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, so Weingarten and her attorney say that their role was very limited when it came to that CDC guidance. We will see how she explains it all tomorrow afternoon -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will be watching for that, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And I will be speaking to the chair of that COVID Pandemic Committee, Ohio Republican Congressman Brad Wenstrup, right here on "Your World" tomorrow. It will be his first interview after the hearing, so do not miss that.\par \par \uc2 While President Biden, meanwhile, makes it official, Charlie Gasparino is reporting a new twist in the battle for the GOP nomination. He will have that for us right here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The 2024 race taking shape.\par \par \uc2 Florida may be reworking laws to allow \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to run for president while maintaining his position as governor. Our next guest is hearing former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is looking to keep that from happening.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Charlie Gasparino has been digging into all of it.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing, Charlie? Hi.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I mean, this is the rumor coming out of the Florida legislature where all this would happen in Tallahassee, that Trump would go down there, is planning some trip to Florida to Tallahassee, and is going to try to convince lawmakers not to allow this to happen.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I believe there's some precedent for allowing officeholders to do this in Florida, if they want to run for president or run for other offices, keep their current job and run for that higher office. There is precedent for it. So it's been allowed in the past.\par \par \uc2 But, again, you need legislation. And that's what I hear. I mean, at least that's the speculation. To be honest with you, I have not gotten this confirmed, but it seems like a Trumpian thing to do. And just so you know, Sandra, my sources on this are very high-level GOP operatives with sources inside this Florida legislature.\par \par \uc2 So this is clearly a high-level speculation. I think the bigger sort of issue here is whether this happens or not is what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really does going forward. Here's what I know from people that raise a lot of money for Republican candidates and want him to run. The donor class clearly wants him to run.\par \par \uc2 He's going to have more than enough money to grind it out against Trump in a primary, no doubt about that. The question is, will he hire somebody, a seasoned professional, a graybeard, someone to go in there and do this campaign and not rely just on his self and his sort of Florida kitchen cabinet?\par \par \uc2 And that's the real worry here, because they look at certain missteps that he's had. I mean, they would consider the abortion issue a misstep, because it doesn't really gain him much voters. They would consider some other things he's done, the Ukraine flip-flop, a misstep.\par \par \uc2 Again, he needs someone to hone his message and to listen to. And it's unclear if that's going to happen. But, listen, they're telling me they think he's ready to run. He's undaunted right now by the polls. If you look at state-by-state matchups, they say it gets tighter, right? It's possible.\par \par \uc2 Remember Obama versus Hillary? The president -- the man who became president was behind in national polls, but he started picking off states until he built up momentum. There is a way here. It's just that I think what the consultant class, what the donor class is worried about, he needs someone with chops in there directing the ship. And he's going to have more than enough money to grind it out.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, there's a way and there's time, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right. And Trump will rely...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Time is working in favor of them right now.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Trump will rely on those small donations. He's got the big donors locked up.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. And per your reporting, DeSantis has enough for the long game against Trump, if that becomes the case.\par \par \uc2 As the 2024 race takes shape, meanwhile, Charlie, the race for donors, as you're mentioning, kicking off. Now that President Biden is in, he can start fund-raising. So which candidate has the advantage right now? And I will tell you, you have 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Go.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Me?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Trump versus...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's impossible for you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Trump versus Biden, I think Biden, because Biden will have the establishment behind him.\par \par \uc2 I really think the establishment donor class for the Republicans is not crazy about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Listen, I'm not taking sides here. I'm just saying that Biden will have the establishment behind him full force if he can make it through the campaign, obviously. I mean, there are questions.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, there's a long, long way to go still.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Although it will go fast.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, great to have you. And great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You too.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Don't miss "YOUR WORLD" tomorrow. We will be speaking with Senate Intelligence Committee Chair and Virginia Democrat Mark Warner.\par \par \uc2 And you can catch me alongside John Roberts 1:00 p.m. Eastern on "America Reports." We will have 2024 presidential candidate on the Republican side Nikki Haley, all starting 1:00 p.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining us here on "YOUR WORLD." I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 And, for now, here is "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230425ej4p0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Announces 2024 Bid As Poll Numbers Slip; Biden Takes Aim At "MAGA" GOP In Campaign Launch; Hunter Biden To Appear In Court For Paternity Case; \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's Lawyers To Meet With Department Of Justice Prosecutors; Kyiv Mayor On Possibility Of "Stalemate" With Russia; Kyiv Mayor On Ukraine's Planned Spring Offensive; Biden Launches 2024 Bid As Age Concerns Grow; Nikki Haley Criticizes Dem Stance On Abortion; Doc Leaks: China Making Weapons To Hack Satellites; Fake \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Workers Rob NYC Store, Attack Owners; San Francisco Target Locks Merchandise As Theft Surges\b0\par\par\uc2 William La Jeunesse, John Roberts, Sandra Smith, Martha MacCallum,  Peter Doocy, Brian Kilmeade, Bill Hemmer, David Spunt, Andy McCarthy, Alex  Hogan, Kimberly Strassel, Gillian Turner, Lauren Simonetti \par \uc2 7,518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOOEY ZEPHYR (D-MT): On behalf of my constituents, I speak on behalf of my community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: So, the bottom line, John is, you know, these bills are as 500 in the state houses around the country. So, this fight in Montana is just the tip of the iceberg. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And the debate will continue, the fight as well. William La Jeunesse, thank you. Sandra, that's going to wrap it up for us.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We'll see you tomorrow. THE STORY with Martha's starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And here's the story that's developing, as we head into this Tuesday afternoon, where we'll be joined at the top here by Bill Hemmer and Brian Kilmeade as the 2024 race takes shape, in a big way, today.\par \par \uc2 The President is in despite a lot of speculation that maybe he would make another decision. He made it this morning and he vowed to fight "MAGA extremists" -- a phrase you can expect to hear a lot of in the coming months. The video shows these two likely opponents and spends zero time on threats from abroad, on foreign policy on China or Russia in this particular ad.\par \par \uc2 That is brand new polls reveal 60 percent of Americans have low confidence in the President's ability to handle an international crisis, which is likely on the watch of this next president. 80 percent of Americans are dissatisfied with the way that things are going in the country right now.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot to dig into as we start to get a look at the shape of what's to come over the next several months of this election. We'll talk to Bill and Brian in a moment. First, we got a Peter Doocy, White House Correspondents standing by. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And Martha, kind of a surprise. President Biden's first remarks since the big announcement did not mention the big announcement. Instead, he stuck to his recent stump speech with lines like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In fact, our economic plan is working. We now have to finish the job when there's more to do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voiceover): The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 's first crack at a response is an artificial intelligence-generated video programmed to forecast a worst-case scenario for a second Biden term.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Border agents were overrun by a surge of 80,000 illegals yesterday evening.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Officials close the city of San Francisco this morning citing the escalating crime and Fentanyl crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who's in charge here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like the train is coming off the tracks.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The theme of this three-minute-long Biden video is that Republicans are too extreme, there is less time spent on any kind of new Biden proposal than there is listing the top issues that he sees with the GOP.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The MAGA extremists are lining up to take on those bedrock freedoms, cutting Social Security, that you paid for your entire life, cutting taxes from the very wealthy, dictating what health care decisions women can make, banning books and telling people who they can love, all while making it more difficult for you to be able to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And we're monitoring this White House briefing that's ongoing in the briefing room. They are not saying anything about anything relating to this announcement. They are deferring everything out of fear of violating the Hatch Act to the campaign that is just hours old. And they're so careful not to say anything. The Press Secretary wouldn't even say how she found out that the President made this announcement. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yep, expect to hear a lot of that in the coming months. We can't talk about that. We can't talk about that. It's going to give them another very handy and perhaps accurate excuse to pass on a lot of those questions. Peter, thank you in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, great to have Bill Hemmer, Co-Host of "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM." He is also hosts of "WHO IS ASA HUTCHINSON?" which is available on Fox Nation; and Brian Kilmeade, Co-Host of "FOX AND FRIENDS" and Host of "ONE NATION." Great to have you guys with us today.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: For the record, I did Who Is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You did. I remember. And who is a lot of other people.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Everyone is still talking about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have no idea until you told us.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill, let me start with you. You know, this ad that the President put together, I think other people probably put it together, but he's signed off on it. It starts with scenes from January 6th, it's very dark talks a lot about MAGA extremists. What do you think about his approach to kicking off this campaign?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What is interesting about that, if you think about four years ago, it was all about Charlottesville.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And remember that reason that's why he was running and he made that a point for at least a year into his presidency. Well, what I took away from the video is that he's not running on anything that he's accomplished while he has been President. And so, that takes you into a question about the headwinds that could be there for him and a re-election.\par \par \uc2 Over the past six months, there have been eight different surveys that have taken among Democrats. All right, here are the findings. 38 percent want him to run, among Democrats. 57 percent, want someone else to run. Then when you get into the state levels, you think about the election of 2020 and what was real, what was tight, right? You think about Pennsylvania. I'm thinking about Georgia and Wisconsin and Arizona, we went digging a bit earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Between those three states, 42,000 votes determine the outcome in all three of those states. And today, his approval rating in Georgia is 42, and Wisconsin is 41, and Arizona, it's 38. So, based on the context of the economy and international issues, and the headwinds that he's running into, this is going to be a tough re-election for him to win, as it stands now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Brian, it raises the question, you know, there were a lot of people who thought he might not run, there's a piece that just came out a little while ago from Frank Bruni, and he goes into all the trappings of being the president. And how, you know, when you're used to people standing up and snapping to attention when you walk in the room, and all kinds of forces assembling every time you want to go somewhere? People don't generally want to give it up. And it appears that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't want to give it up either, even in the face of these numbers, Brian.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: You're the one who reads his columns. I didn't know that was it - - Frank Bruni. I did not know it will be an open book test. I thought I had to memorize everything before I come in. So, Bill, you cheated. A couple of things. I'm just wondering where the ambitious Democrat is? Why are you waiting so cold for your turn if you think you're the right person? And clearly, the President has got vulnerabilities that even though own party is seeing.\par \par \uc2 The Governor of Kentucky, you see the Governor of Colorado and very ambitious Governor of Illinois -- since when is Bernie Sanders waiting in line? You can't say he's too old if you're going to elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, we're some boldness to see vulnerability in the past? I don't understand who told everyone to get in line in this party?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Robert Kennedy, Jr. is in.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very interesting because --\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: He can't get a debate, though.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Three Kennedys have been the only Democrats to run against incumbent presidents. So, it runs in their in their bloodline.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That's pretty good. Only Martha would know that.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, yes. Kennedy and \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , all ran against incumbent presidents as the only ones in our lifetime. Rich Lowry says this, "To paraphrase Woody Allen, 90 percent, of getting elected for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is just showing up. His campaign could be barely evident, and still succeed." So, essentially, a basic strategy once again, fell, you know, yes, and worked -- it worked last time,\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Which was with us a bit earlier today, also. It's a very interesting point that he makes, I mean, he's turned the trick one time from his basement, and maybe from some drive-in theater parking lots in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We were there. I remember that.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Maybe he can do it again. But it's a different time now, right? You can't hide behind COVID in 2024.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. And I would say, I would say this, it would be sad. It will be sad in America, where you win by not doing anything. That's the anti-American attitude to win the ultimate American prize, and that's president of this country. You're going to have to show up. No pandemic to lean on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Going to be interesting. I think we're going to get some twists and turns here as we head into --\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: You know, we used to do this on radio.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I know --\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I know I like to try and see if I get you back. I just can't give you a contractual demands.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'll be there tomorrow. Well, it's Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Well, Bill, can you come by tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, maybe we'll get to see Bill, we will have a surprise appearance. Thanks, you guys. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 So, Andy McCarthy today says that the Founding Fathers warned that there's no greater threat to our country than corruption and influence of our leaders by a foreign government. They were very, very concerned about that. Andy's warning for the Bidens and their activities, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rising legal troubles for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  just as his dad officially kicks off his 2024 campaign. A judge has ordered him to show up in court on Monday in Arkansas as part of a paternity case. The mother of his 4-year-old is pushing for some financial support that she says she has not received. This is turning into a big issue for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , so watch that story on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Also, his lawyers are set to meet with the U.S. attorney who is running and overseeing the federal investigation into his taxes and more. Former federal prosecutor Andy McCarthy is standing by but first to Justice Correspondent David Spunt live at the DOJ. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. We'll start with a paternity case. It's been going on for several years. The most recent, as you mentioned, deals with payments child support payments. The lawsuits between \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and former girlfriend, London Roberts, who says her 4-year-old daughter is also his 4-year-old daughter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voiceover): Now, the paternity DNA test showed with near scientific certainty that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is the father. This specific fight, as you mentioned over those child support payments. London Roberts and the daughter live in Arkansas. Judge Holly Meyer ruled yesterday that all parties in the case must appear next Monday, May 1st, in Arkansas, in person for a status hearing.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there's no indication Hunter will fly under the radar as a potential indictment loom dealing with the Department of Justice. And I use the word potential as there's no certainty he'll even face charges. Now, he's been tagging alongside his dad, the president. Hunter was recently in Ireland shaking hands with throngs of excited people just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 A few days before that, he was strolling along the South Lawn of the White House for the annual Easter Egg Roll, another sign he has no problem appearing in public and his dad now running for a second term clearly wants his son out in public. Now, federal prosecutors continue to weigh misdemeanor and felony charges related to Biden, the younger Biden, on a variety of issues that include Hunter's overseas business dealings, taxes and lying on a federal form relating to a gun charge, relating to that meeting, you mentioned Martha.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): In the intro to me, we're told that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's attorneys, several weeks ago (AUDIO GAP) with was requested by Biden's attorneys to get a status update on an investigation that began almost half a decade ago. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Remarkable that they haven't been able to come forward with anything yet, in that case, after all of that time, given the specificity of what these charges are, which is a gun charge and some tax issues that they've been looking into for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David Spunt at the justice department.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Andy McCarthy, former federal prosecutor, a contributing editor with the National Review and a Fox News contributor, as well.\par \par \uc2 Andy, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You wrote a very interesting piece today in The New York Post. And the headline of that piece is "Biden's Corruption Is Enough To Disqualify Him From Office Now."\par \par \uc2 He does not feel that he should be disqualified. In fact, he announced today that he is indeed running again.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you write a little advice to Republicans overseeing the Biden investigations: "Don't worry if he's a criminal. It's bad enough to be corrupt. Political power is a privilege, not a right.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been trusted to wield the awesome powers of the presidency. There is no presumption of entitlement to a privilege." Explain that message to us, Andy.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, Martha, you know, there is been a lot of talk about potential crimes that arise out of, what I would call the broader investigation of the Biden's.\par \par \uc2 You were just referring to this subset of these kind of finite comparatively minor charges that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  could face. The more important thing for the country, obviously, is these piles of money that were coming from foreign powers, some of them hostile to the United States, into the Biden family coffers.\par \par \uc2 And I think Republicans are making a mistake, if they set the bar at suggesting that he should be -- that anybody should be indicted over this.\par \par \uc2 Because a lot of things that are more serious in terms of national security may not necessarily be indictable conduct.\par \par \uc2 And what I mean by distinguishing rights from privileges, is, if you were to indict someone for money laundering, or doing work for a foreign power without registering with the government, or even tax evasion, you would have a presumption of innocence in court, because your right to liberty would be at stake.\par \par \uc2 But when we give you the privilege of wielding the power of the United States, that's not a right, and you don't get a presumption of innocence.\par \par \uc2 What the framers were concerned about when they put the impeachment clauses into the Constitution, for example, was the possibility that the awesome powers of the presidency could fall under the influence of hostile foreign powers.\par \par \uc2 That's one of the big reasons we have impeachment in the Constitution. Congress has a -- has a, what I would say is a responsibility to get to the bottom of what the corruption evidence is.\par \par \uc2 And I wouldn't worry about whether the corruption evidence turns out to be criminal or not, because that's a secondary issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's a great point. It's the larger issue that drove 50 members of our intelligence, or our larger intelligence community to say that they thought that what they found on Hunter's laptop was Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's really important for people to remember that the core of what they were concerned about on there were e-mails that pointed to business dealings, and 10 for the big guy, and all of that with Ukraine and with Chinese officials.\par \par \uc2 So, you're saying we need to get to the heart of the matter of what kind of influence was bought. What was traded for the money that they got? Correct?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. What I would say, Martha is, if we were giving somebody a security clearance --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: That person would have to expose every transaction and foreign connection they had, and they might not get the clearance if it looked like they were compromised, even if they weren't guilty of a crime.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, a lot of questions about \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 's role here, too. And the New York Post reporting shows a very sort of cozy relationship with Hunter and \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 And then he appears to have been instrumental in pulling together the folks to write that letter. So, there's a lot in the story.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Andy, thank you very much. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Coming up a story exclusive with Kyiv mayor, \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2 , as Ukraine prepares for probably the most significant offensive of the war, a spring offensive against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And also, the leaked U.S. military documents that suggest that the war is likely headed toward a stalemate does Klitschko believe that?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ukrainian forces getting ready to go on offense this spring against Russia in a enormously high stakes push to see some territorial gains here on Putin's forces.\par \par \uc2 But the recent leak of U.S. Intelligence document shows that American officials predicted the operation will have only modest success. Obviously, that is not good news for the Ukraine leadership who wants continued support.\par \par \uc2 One top secret file from February reads like this: "Enduring Ukrainian deficiencies in training and munition supplies probably will strain progress and exacerbate casualties during the offensive."\par \par \uc2 That according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and the document is now 2 months old.\par \par \uc2 In moments, we will speak with \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2 , the mayor of Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But first, to correspondent Alex Hogan, live in Kyiv. Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Russia is discussing plans to increase the pay of soldiers. This comes about a week after the Russian government passed legislation that would allow draft letters to be sent by e-mail.\par \par \uc2 All of this really showcasing how much and how quickly Russian forces are trying to fortify their forces.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as new Russian attacks taking place across Ukraine today.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Take a look at this footage. This was the site of a Russian attack blowing up a museum, trapping civilians under the rubble here.\par \par \uc2 This is the North Eastern region of Kupiansk. The attack, killing two people and wounding at least 10 others.\par \par \uc2 Russia is still advancing in the eastern town of Bakhmut. It is been a violent battle that has dragged on for about eight months. And some civilians still live there, caught in the fighting.\par \par \uc2 And new now, a former Wagner group commander, who's described killing children in Ukraine has been brought into Russia's Saratov Oblast Investigation Committee for questioning on this.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the war, the deportation of children to Russia has been a major concern, and Ukrainian parliament Commissioner for Human Rights Dmytro Lubinets, claims that 19,000 children have been taken.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO LUBINETS, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT COMMISSIONER FOR HUMAN RIGHTS: Now we returned 361 children.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: That's only 300 of 19,000.\par \par \uc2 LUBINETS: Yes, it's not a lot. But you can imagine that returning only one children, it's like a special operation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): The department also says that it's managed to negotiate the release of 2,200 prisoners of war.\par \par \uc2 Now, back here in Kyiv, Ukrainian government officials continue to point toward this spring offensive. And in the last day, Ukrainian forces have managed to slightly advanced in the eastern town of Kreminna. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, I spoke with \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2 , mayor of Kyiv, Ukraine. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Mayor, when you look at the maps over the course of the last many months, there's not a lot of movement, a lot -- not a lot of gain.\par \par \uc2 Although, there is been obviously very strong recognition for the early efforts in Kyiv to hold off the Russians.\par \par \uc2 Is it fair as some military observers look at these maps to say that it is more by-and-large at a stalemate at this point?\par \par \uc2 VITALI KLITSCHKO MAYOR OF KYIV, UKRAINE: Yes. I want to begin from very important point. We successfully defend our country, as a lot of experts, year ago, give to us couple of days, a couple of weeks, and against one of the strongest army in the world, and actually, we successfully defend our country.\par \par \uc2 As, right now, yes, of course, this preparation is not enough. There was equipment, not enough for -- was -- the weapons. And that way right now, starting to everyone right now, not just in Ukraine, in the world, have expectation from proactive movement from Ukrainian army.\par \par \uc2 More than that, I can tell you because I'm not military. I'm not the part of military forces. By the way, thank you so much for anti-missile system. Right now, the lives of our citizens better protected because safety right now for every citizens in Ukraine is main priority.\par \par \uc2 And anti-rocket system, very important for Ukraine, and especially for capital of Ukraine, Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, there is been a lot of reporting on these intelligence leaks, Mayor. And I'm just curious to get your reaction to what was in them.\par \par \uc2 In them, it talked about Ukraine's predicament looking dire. It said that missiles for the Soviet-era, air defenses were projected to run out in Kyiv in May. And also, that your military has taken losses of more than 120,000 dead and wounded. Are those reports accurate?\par \par \uc2 KLITSCHKO: No idea. No idea. It's a lot of discussion about that is true or not. It's fake news or not. And that -- why to -- I'm not ready to give you information because nobody know -- maybe somebody know, but I doesn't have exactly information about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, just so in general, I mean, obviously, you're a fighter by training, one of the best-known fighters internationally in the ring, and now for your country.\par \par \uc2 So, tell me what your senses of whether or not Kyiv can win in this spring offensive. And what is winning -- what would winning look like? You know, what's a victory in this spring offensive tangibly?\par \par \uc2 KLITSCHKO: It's very important spirit and the will to win. We, Ukrainians is very good motivated, it's our country. It's our cities. It's our family. We defending our future for our children.\par \par \uc2 And Russian soldier fighting for the money. It's nobody ready to give the lives for the money. But it's a question about your family about your children is a motivation. The spirit is very important.\par \par \uc2 And is -- I explained because it's different between Russian soldier and Ukrainian soldier. And we, everybody expect very positive for Ukraine movement from our army. Right now, we have more equipment, trained better and our forces much more stronger than, for example, six months ago or one year ago. And that's why we -- everybody expected success.\par \par \uc2 And we have to be successful. We have to be strong as the best answer, our success, success of Ukraine is the best answer for our partners, for United States, and also success of Ukraine is the best answer for Russians.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Understood. But I'm asking what your definition of success is. Is it retaking Crimea? Is it pushing them out of the east completely, back inside the Russian border? How do you define success, mayor?\par \par \uc2 KLITSCHKO: Success of Ukraine is we have to be in the border 1991. It's Ukrainian property, it's Ukrainian territory, it's Ukrainian citizen there. And we never accept the Russians which told Ukraine never was independent. It's our Russian property. We don't want back to USSR. We don't want to be the part of Russian empire. We see our future as democratic, modern European country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much for your time, sir.\par \par \uc2 KLITSCHKO: Thank you very much for everyone who support Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, right now, a letter is circulating in the halls of Congress that is calling on President Biden to take a cognitive test as part of his decision to run again for reelection. So Wall Street Journal columnist Kim Strassel joins us on how she sees 2024 and the nomination for both of these parties playing out as Biden announces today that he would be the oldest sitting president running again in US history, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, CBS ANCHOR: Some people ask whether you are fit for the job. And when you hear that I wonder what you think.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Watch me. Honest to God, that's all I think. Watch me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, opening statements in a civil trial against former President Trump. Writer E. Jean Carroll accuses him of raping her back in the 1990s in a department store dressing room here in New York City. He says she made it up to sell a book. The jury of six men and three women will remain anonymous throughout the course of this trial.\par \par \uc2 A Trump-Biden rematch looking more and more likely, at least at this stage of the game for 2024, which of course renews the debate around age. Here's President Biden in February.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's legitimate for people to raise issues about my age. It's totally legitimate to do that. The only thing I can say is watch me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We heard that many times, right? The New York Times Editorial Board says that he has, "Given voters very few chances to watch him and his refusal to engage with the public regularly raises questions about his age and health."\par \par \uc2 Republican Congressman \uc2 Ronny Jackson\uc2 , who served as White House physician under Presidents Obama and Trump, is again circulating a letter calling on the President to take a cognitive test and to make those results public or resign and drop out, he says. With that, we bring in Wall Street Journal opinion columnist and Fox News contributor Kimberly Strassel. Kim, great to have you with us today. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY STRASSEL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Hi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, obviously, there's a lot of talk about this issue. I'm not so sure how much the number itself matters, whether you're 80, or 76, or 90. But Jackson here is getting at the core of it, which is how fit are you to serve, kind of regardless of that number.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I think we can agree, Martha, that we aren't going to see President Biden taking a cognitive test, because to even do so would be to admit that there is a problem. But I'm really happy to see that we are having this debate. Remember, just two years ago or three years ago in 2020, this was the taboo topic that were not allowed to discuss was Biden's age. It was supposedly ageist, it was disrespectful to the candidate. And now, for a number of reasons, it's simply an unavoidable topic, especially as he's decided that he's going to run again.\par \par \uc2 And it's an all important topic. You know, I'm reminded that 1984 debate, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , he was only 73 at the time, by the way, and he was asked by the moderators, said, this is one of the most grueling jobs in the world. Are you really up for it, sir? And of course, that was Reagan's moment when he turned it back and said he wasn't going to let his opponent's youth and inexperience become an issue in the case. And it was a laugh line.\par \par \uc2 But it is very good to see that we are having this discussion this time around.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think it's going to -- the fact that he has now announced his candidacy is going to shut down a lot of conversation in the White House press briefing room. We got a little taste of that today in the first time out from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 . She was asked about the age issue. Here's what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , White House Press Secretary: -- comes to age, it's the same thing that we heard in 2020, right? We heard that over and over in 2020. And if you look at what the President has done this past two years, he's been able to deliver and get things done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Then there's a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  exchange from Jacqui Heinrich where she asked, does the President's plan to serve all eight years, was one of the questions in there. She said, I'm not going to get ahead of the President. They're talking a lot about the Hatch Act, Kimberly.\par \par \uc2 Is this relatively early announcement going to give them a lot of COVID going forward?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Well, what I'm really worried about, Martha, is I think what you're going to see here is not so much a campaign as a very staged event over the next months. The President does have all the conveniences of the White House, Air Force One, the ability to have an entire team set up these manufactured settings where he's rolled out.\par \par \uc2 But the bigger question is going to be, does he go out and do any more impromptu press conferences? Does he walk the line with prospective voters and answer questions? Does he agree to go and engage in debates? You know, those are going to be the things that give the public the ability to see if he's got what it takes. So when he says, watch me, that's great, but we need to be able to actually evaluate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to get your thoughts on another topic real quick here. This is Nikki Haley speaking just a short time ago. She has announced her run for the Republican nomination. She says Republicans need to not run away from discussion about abortion in this country. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, GOP PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I would remind those on the left who demonize anyone who is pro-life that it was not too long ago when President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  said he wanted abortion to be "safe, legal, and rare." Few Democrats say rare anymore, just the opposite. Many want legal abortion anytime, for any reason, at any stage of pregnancy in every state and town in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump has signaled that he thinks this issue is a loser for Republicans. What do you think about the fact that she's sticking her neck out here?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Look, I thought it was very brave of her. By the way, she carved out a position that you may see other candidates follow on the Republican side. She said, I'm very pro life, but this needs to be something that, at least at the federal level, we need to be working harder to find consensus between both sides.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kimberly Strassel, always good to see you. Thank you very much, Kim.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So two Republican congressmen who serve on the House Select Committee on China join me exclusively as Beijing's ambitions to hijack enemy satellites come to light. What a story this is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: China apparently working on ways to hijack enemy satellites that could be just a few years away from breaching a key US cyber defense program. Some of the latest revelations from the leak of the US intelligence documents, we have two members of the House Select Committee on China standing by to talk about how a war would begin in space and whether or not China would prove dominant in that battle. First, to correspondent Gillian Turner live in Washington. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, newly leaked intelligence reveals China's military is going on offense in cyberspace, now targeting US satellites, as well as a key Biden cybersecurity program. The administration is still in the process of building.\par \par \uc2 The CIA document first obtained by the financial times says China's People's Liberation Army is planning to hijack US satellites that our military relies on during wartime. Biden officials are not confirming the leaked details, though the chief of space operations hinted at this exact threat in recent testimony. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHANCE SALTZMAN, CHIEF OF SPACE OPERATIONS: Equally alarming are the threats that endanger the satellites the nation relies on for prosperity and security. We've seen the Russians attack satellite communications, we've seen cyberattacks trying to dismantle, we've seen GPS jamming. They recognize that space is a force multiplier, and they're willing to attack it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now alarmingly, the House Committee on Science, Space, and Technology acknowledges that China already has the capability to disrupt satellite communications. A separately leaked document classified top secret, reveals US intelligence believes Chinese hackers will get access to President Biden's highly prized cybersecurity program. It is known as Zero Trust within the next five years. Zero Trust is meant, according to cyber experts, to protect the entire \uc2 US government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WRIGHT, CYBERSECURITY EXPERT: We're talking about the entire US infrastructure, the entire US. Government, DOD, Intel, civilian agencies spread across 50 states, you know, seven territories, and around the world. Think of all of our consulates, all of our embassies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's extraordinary because right now, as this program is still being built out, Biden's own intelligence agencies are now warning that China will be able to breach it in the near future. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Scary stuff. Gillian, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Republican Congressman \uc2 Dusty Johnson\uc2  of South Dakota and Republican Congressman John Moolenaar of Michigan, both serve on the House Select Committee on China. Gentlemen, thank you very much. It's good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Moolenaar, let me start with you. General Saltzman, who we just heard from in Gillian's piece, he also testified in that hearing. He said, we're seeing satellites that can actually grab another satellite, grapple with it, and pull it out of its operational orbit. This is space warfare, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN MOOLENAAR (R-MI): Well, it is. It's very concerning. We need to be ahead of the game on this. We need to support our space force. We need to make sure we're investing in cybersecurity and stay ahead of the game. China is clearly wanting to advance their military objectives in space, and we need to recognize that threat and respond accordingly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Congressman Johnson, we all remember when the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began the Space Force. And there was a lot of humor around it and, you know, question about whether or not we needed one. I think it's pretty clear that we do need one. Has this administration done what is necessary to make us competitive with China so they don't pull our satellites out of the orbit and get rid of them?\par \par \uc2 REP. DUSTY JOHNSON (R-SD): Well, you're right. President Trump was farsighted in this way, and the administration has not done enough to check this very serious threat. China has made it clear they want to be the masters of space by 2045, and they want to be at a position of parity with this country in just a few years. They've got 347 satellites with likely military or counter espionage capabilities already up there.\par \par \uc2 This is the kind of thing -- listen, we've got to get our head on straight, because this is the next battlefield, and we'd better be ready. We'd better be ready when that day comes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's John Hannah, former National Security Adviser to former Vice President Cheney. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN HANNAH, FORMER CHENEY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: If China is able to knock out our ability to see what the enemy is doing, our ability to exert command and control, and communications between our own forces, it's virtually game over for us on the battlefield here on Earth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, congressman, I think a lot of people watch this at home, Congressman Moolenaar, and they say, well, what do we do? How could we behind on this? What's your answer to them?\par \par \uc2 MOOLENAAR: Well, one of the reasons Speaker McCarthy wanted to set up the Select Committee on China was to look at these specific threats from China. They want to replace the dollar with their own currency. They want to spy on our children with TikTok, all these different areas that they're threatening around the globe. And so, this is one area that we need to be vigilant and protect our homeland.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Here's a video from a protest on another topic real quick here, Congressman Moolenaar. In your area in Michigan, protest against building a huge Chinese EV plant on rural territory in Michigan. That plant going to get built by China?\par \par \uc2 MOOLENAAR: Well, it's -- the ownership of the company, the parent company is from China. It has ties to the Chinese Communist Party. The people in the community are speaking out, having their voices heard. The leadership in Michigan, the Democratic leadership, has put a lot of funding towards it.\par \par \uc2 But our concern is, we don't want any tie with the Chinese Communist Party and investments in Michigan. We don't want to see farmland purchased by China, and we want to protect our homeland in this way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Do you think you can win that battle quickly?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Absolutely. We can win the battle and we need to. We have had experts testify before our committee that China is seeking coercive power economically over this country. We are developing a plan on how to strategically decouple from China, and we're going to get it done.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Well, it seems like we need to get in front of the eight ball on this. Thank you very much, Congressman \uc2 Dusty Johnson\uc2  and John Moolenaar. Good to see you both. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOOLENAAR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the great lengths that some retailers are going to counter rampant theft, where is this where literally everything is locked up?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This brutal crime was caught on camera here in New York City. Watch this. These two guys were dressed up as \uc2 FedEx\uc2  workers to get into a 74-year-old man's store. They then pistol whipped him, knocked him down. His 72-year-old wife was also there. And then they robbed the place. The victim's son says that the thieves apparently knew a lot about them because they threatened to hurt their granddaughter when she got home from school. It happened around 7:30 in the morning on Friday, and those two suspects dressed as \uc2 FedEx\uc2  workers in this video, still out there on the loose.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this viral video from a Target in San Francisco. Aisle after aisle of cosmetics and toiletries, and all the things you buy at Target behind Plexiglass. According to the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , the Golden Gate City is the second worst metropolitan area for shoplifting behind only Los Angeles. So great news for California. Fox Business Lauren Simonetti here with the details. Hi, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: How do you shop in a store like that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You got to be like, excuse me, can you please come with me to aisle seven?\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Just shop online.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And then aisle eight -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: That's insane.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: And it's disturbing. I couldn't shop there. Nobody wants to shop there when it's like that. But this is what it is in California. Prop 47 classifies theft of items less than $950 as misdemeanors. You're leaving businesses big and small vulnerable since they won't lock up the criminals, they're locking up the shampoo. And that's why shoppers in that Folsom Street Target are paying the price of inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 And the company is paying the price for layers and layers of security. \uc2 Target\uc2  tells us they've hired additional security guards. They're adding third party guard services, using new technologies to protect merchandise from being stolen. But guess what? Not all businesses have such deep pockets.\par \par \uc2 And those that survived the pandemic can't survive the crime. I spoke to the owner of the Grant and Green Saloon. It's a bar in North Beach. He told me he just shut his doors for good because when he was finally able to get his customers back after COVID, they were too scared to drive in and park on the street because their cars would be stolen.\par \par \uc2 This is San Francisco is short around 400 police officers. And now you have some Democratic politicians calling for the city to fully staff the police force. Residents paying the price, it's their safety, and honestly, it's their options. Because \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 , they're giving up, they're closing up. You have less choice if you're living in San Francisco and you don't feel safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's bizarre. You just think about the way we used to live. You could walk in and buy things. You didn't have to worry about someone grabbing everything and running out the door while all they can do is stand back and watch. It's just insane. Lauren, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY for this Tuesday, April 25th. Look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. Thanks for joining us, everybody. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now. A bit of a rocky day for the Down 340 points, I'll see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230426ej4p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with 2024 GOP Presidential Candidate Asa Hutchinson; Interview with Rep. \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  (D-MI); House Republicans Endorse Trump over DeSantis; Biden May Announce Bid This Week; Republicans Renew Calls to Impeach DHS Secretary over Border Security; White House Denies Interference after \uc2 IRS\uc2  Whistleblower Comes Forward; Pat Cohen Shares Her Story of Success\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Karl Rove, Juan Williams \par \uc2 9,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 This could be the week 2024 becomes official for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My plan is to run again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Within days, we expect President Biden to officially announce his reelection campaign despite lukewarm approval ratings. The goal: box out potential challengers from within his own party.\par \par \uc2 And former President Trump takes shots at Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and runs up the endorsement scoreboard in their shared home state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: DeSantis has done a great job as governor, but President Trump has shown to be a great president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Plus, former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson prepares to make his White House primary bid official.\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), FORMER ARKANSAS GOVERNOR: Well, you get in there and you make your case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He is our exclusive guest this morning as he prepares to take on Trump and a growing field.\par \par \uc2 Then, the Supreme Court dragged into the heated abortion debate once again, less than a year after overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESSS SECRETARY: We are prepared also for a long legal battle.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower says President Biden's son may be preferential treatment while under federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My client wants to come forward. He's not coming here with a political agenda.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The White House denies any interference. We'll ask Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  about the whistleblower, the Supreme Court, and what she thinks of President Biden's chances in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Then another quiet regulation change that could add to mortgage costs for some buyers. Our Sunday panel debates whether it's fair to raise fees on those with good credit.\par \par \uc2 Plus -- Hurricane Katrina took her home, but not her home. Singer Pat Cohen on finding strength while singing the blues.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with breaking news. Early this morning, U.S. Special Forces rushed to evacuate American embassy staff from Sudan's capital. The Pentagon says the operation was fast and clean. Service members spent less than an hour on the ground.\par \par \uc2 The evacuation comes amid a week of bloody battles between Sudan's army and a paramilitary group. This is video of an airstrike in Khartoum, Sudan, amid heavy fighting between the warring factions, violence continuing this weekend despite a declared truce. We will continue to bring you updates.\par \par \uc2 But back here in Washington, the "will he or won't he" debate may finally be over for Democrats. President Biden expected to announce this week that he is going to run again. It clarifies the central question now for Democrats, do they want him for a second term, and it clarifies the stakes for Republicans, as they gear up to choose their nominee over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2  joins me. He has said he will announce he's running in the Republican Party going straight at former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson live at the White House for the new details on what Biden's announcement could look like -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, the president plans to make it official on Tuesday with the release of a pre-recorded video message announcing he is running for reelection. The planning ramped up in earnest after his return from Ireland, with the DNC launching a bevy of calls to top Democratic donors and a summit planned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: We're not going to talk about what 2024 is going to look like. I'm not going to talk about that from here.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): The White House is mum on the president's plans, but his announcement has been months in the making.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our intention is to run again.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: However, a new poll from "The Associated Press" says 73 percent of Americans do not want him to run again, including a majority of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, by and large, say they're likely to vote for him if he's the nominee.\par \par \uc2 The president faces two declared Democrat challengers, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . who announced last week and is polling at 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (D), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's like running against somebody in your family. I think I'm in a better position to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Writer \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2 , who made it to the debate stage in 2020, is also challenging Biden.\par \par \uc2 The president's announcement comes the same week his son Hunter's lawyers are scheduled to meet with the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 And on the Republican side, former President Trump continues to lead in the polls. A new "Wall Street Journal" Republican poll has him leading potential rival \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  51 percent to 38 percent. It's a reversal from four months ago when DeSantis was ahead.\par \par \uc2 Trump has received endorsements from nearly a dozen Republican Florida congressmen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): If Governor DeSantis runs, he's not likely to announce his candidacy until after the Florida legislative session ends, which is about two weeks away -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, former Republican governor of Arkansas, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), FORMER ARKANSAS GOVERNOR: Thank you, Shannon. It is always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, Governor, you have said there's no point in running unless you're going straight at former President Trump. You right now, though, in the polling are at 1 percent or less in all the recent polls. So, you know going after him is like walking into a buzzsaw.\par \par \uc2 Why are you doing it?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, because it's important. What America does not want is another repeat of 2020 where we have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running against each other. That's reflected in the polls certainly on the -- on the Democrats side.\par \par \uc2 And so, we don't want to repeat that. It takes alternatives. I've got a great record of consistent conservatism as Arkansas governor, experience at the federal level. And while I come from a small state, it's a successful conservative state that has a booming economy, lower taxes.\par \par \uc2 And so, I'm running because I think that we need alternatives and we need new leadership and new direction in America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. We'll dig in to some of those policy issues in just a second. But, first, let me ask you this -- President Trump has taken a lot of heat for hemming and hawing on whether he would pledge to actually support whomever the GOP nominee ends up being.\par \par \uc2 Will you support the nominee if it's him?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, you know, we've negotiated with the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  on the terms of the debate. I expect to be on the debate stage. I don't prefer party loyalty oaths, but it is important to have the competition. I want to participate in the debate.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll see exactly what that pledge is, but I expect to be on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, we will see you there.\par \par \uc2 All right. You could be facing also on the debate stage, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . You've been critical about, as have others, him getting involved as a conservative in direct corporate affairs and business as he has with \uc2 Disney\uc2  there in Florida. But here's how he explained his position and what went on there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: Well, in reality, \uc2 Disney\uc2  was enjoying unprecedented privileges and subsidies. They control their own government in Central Florida. They were exempt from laws that virtually everybody else had to follow.\par \par \uc2 That's not free enterprise, but it's certainly even worse when a company takes all those privileges that have been bestowed over many, many decades and uses that to wage war on state policy regarding families and children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, agree or disagree when a company has some of those special statuses within a state and then uses them to fight those state political battles that it's fair to engage with them on that front?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I don't believe whether you're on the left or the right of the political spectrum that we ought -- government should not be telling business what they can and cannot do in terms of speech. And however you describe it, it appears to me that the governor did not like what \uc2 Disney\uc2  was doing in terms of what they were saying and exercising speech so they're being punished.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, those special privileges go to different businesses in Florida. I let the legislature handle that.\par \par \uc2 But I think it is important that we make sure that we don't become heavy- handed in government to punish those that are creating jobs for Americans and creating income and growing the private sector. That's not what Republicanism is about. That's not what a conservative is about.\par \par \uc2 And so, those are fine distinctions, but it is important, though, that we talk about freedom and the free enterprise system, and that's what I've supported through my career and not government telling business what they can and cannot do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you agree then like President Trump that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is getting it wrong on \uc2 Disney\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I think he's getting it wrong on \uc2 Disney\uc2 . I think \uc2 Disney\uc2  got it wrong on themselves to begin with. I don't agree how \uc2 Disney\uc2  has handled things. But you don't use the heavy-handed government to punish a business.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I think that's wrong, and I think that's indicating of motivation to go after business because you disagree with their policies and what they're saying. The left does that. I don't want the right, the conservatives, to do that either.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's talk about abortion. You signed what became one of the strictest abortion laws in the country, basically very few exceptions except for the risk of the life of the mother. You said in recent days that you think as we get into '24, this is going to be less of a hot button issue, but you know in polling and election results, it has really motivated the left.\par \par \uc2 Are you being overly optimistic about how you think it's going to play out in '24? And what now happens to women in Arkansas who do wind up with an unplanned pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I think it will obviously be an issue in 2024, but after the Dobbs decision, I expect that it will level out and I don't think that it will be quite as hot of a hot button issue in the coming election. People are going to vote their conviction. They want to know what candidates to believe in.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's important that we restrict abortions to save the lives of the unborn. I do think there has to be reasonable exceptions such as the life of the mother, and in cases of rape and incest. I think there's broad agreement in America on that.\par \par \uc2 And we also have to make sure that we provide for the maternal care. We have to do better in our child birth care and adoption services. And so, that's what we've done in Arkansas is investing in those, as well as we placed restrictions on abortion and limited that.\par \par \uc2 It's up to the states to determine that right now. I think there'll be continued debate about whether we need a national standard, and that will depend really upon whether you have a divided government, whether you have Democrats take control or Republicans take control.\par \par \uc2 And then you're going to have two choices. Are you going to lift all of the restrictions on abortion or are you going to place reasonable restrictions that will save the lives of the unborn? And that's the Republican and that's my position.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, again, let's talk about the idea of a federal ban because you have mentioned that mostly this should be about the state level at this point.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2 , a pro-life group, says that's essentially not really a pro-life position. They say that saying the issue should be decided at the states is an endorsement of abortion up until the moment of birth, even brutal late term abortions in states like California, Illinois, New York and New Jersey. They're calling for a 15 minimum -- 15-week minimum.\par \par \uc2 If you did have both houses of Congress, and were President Hutchinson, would you sign it?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: The answer is I've always signed pro-life bills. And if a pro- life bill comes to me that sets reasonable restrictions but also has the appropriate exceptions, yes, I would assign it. I would prefer that this is an issue that's resolved by the states because that's what the pro-life community fought for for 40 years, in reversing Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 But what's evident to me is that the Democrats are going to push for a national standard that overrules the states that allows a broad abortion access up until the time of birth. The Democrats are going to push for that. And so, obviously, there's going to be more pressure on the Republican side and on the life side, to put in those reasonable restrictions.\par \par \uc2 And so, I do -- would support that if those restrictions are in place and that if we can, through have a -- have a national standard to help save the lives of the unborn.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Let's talk about another hot button issue. This is about transitioning, especially for patients and young people under the age of 18. Some conservatives argued that your position on this has not been truly conservative.\par \par \uc2 You've vetoed a bill in Arkansas that would have prohibited hormone treatment or puberty blockers for people under 18, saying that the government should not get in the middle of this conversation between patients and doctors and young patients and parents.\par \par \uc2 "The Federalist" wrote this. They say: Clearly, this is a fight that government should be involved in, in the same way that government involves itself in under age drinking or sex. There's always room for Republicans to be court jesters in the left's political correctness kingdom. He, meaning you, should not expect to be welcomed back by real conservatives with open arms.\par \par \uc2 So, you say it's a private conversation between medical staff and patients, but that's the same argument that pro-choice folks are going to make on the issue of abortion. Were you come down differently?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, that's what's conservative about this is you actually debate and think about the role of government when it should step in. I would have signed a bill very quickly if it had been just prohibiting transition surgeries for minors, absolutely. But you have to debate it a little more when you're interfering with the health care decisions that a parent makes.\par \par \uc2 And so, we don't want the government telling parents when they can have vaccines for their children and not have vaccines, or have to have vaccines for children. We fight for the role of parents.\par \par \uc2 And so, these are good debates that conservatives have. You know, there's bills that I would sign. There's bills that go too far. When there was not a grandfather clause in that, I'd vetoed it. But there's been other good bills in other states that have a better balance that accomplish the objectives.\par \par \uc2 And so, a conservative position, you always are debating and think about, is this the right role of government to interfere with parental decisions? I came down on a limited fashion and said parents have an important role to play here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to quickly ask about the debt ceiling, something you'd have to manage as president. You voted for the debt ceiling back during your time in Congress. This week, and there are many Republicans who voted for it before but now, this time around are saying they're "no" vote.\par \par \uc2 This week, the White House press secretary said, essentially the speaker is taking the American people ransom, holding them hostage.\par \par \uc2 So, if these same folks have voted for this before, why shouldn't they this time around?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, Speaker McCarthy is really doing a good job and outlining a framework to get federal spending under control. It is reasonable, it is a frame work. President Biden should sign off on that very quickly.\par \par \uc2 But you know, in the end, we're going to have to make sure that the full faith and credit of the United States is maintained. But this is a very reasonable thing to say, we'll extend the debt, which we need to do as a country, but please give us a framework for bringing out of control federal spending into a reasonable level.\par \par \uc2 And so, I fully support the arguments that Speaker McCarthy has laid out. And we need to reduce that federal spending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Governor, we will see you out there on the campaign trail. Thanks so much for being with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, this week, you heard it -- House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is working on now rounding up the Republican votes needed to pass this debt ceiling package he's proposed. It includes a rooster of conservative priorities to cut spending.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll ask Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  about calls for the president to quit stalling and get to the negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Within a few days, President Biden is expected to officially announce a reelection campaign. He's already facing two Democratic challengers and polls show there's a relatively negative view of the job he's done so far.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, Democratic congresswoman from Michigan, \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): Good morning, Shannon. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, you know that -- listen, we talk about approval ratings. By an average of 11 points, the president is underwater there.\par \par \uc2 We've got polling out of key early states like New Hampshire where half of the Democratic primary voters there say don't run. "The A.P.", 73 percent of people say they don't want him to run again.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about a potential lack of enthusiasm on the '24 election for this president? And should he give someone else a chance?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I'm not worried at all. You will remember who you are talking to. I'm someone that does not believe in polls. They're a snapshot in time.\par \par \uc2 I told all of you in 2016 that I thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could win, and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to win, and even on election night, I was debating whether he was going to win. And headline that night (ph), I was right.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, when I'm out there, I'm talking to people. And I'm in communities and they don't -- you don't know who he's going to be running against.\par \par \uc2 In the Midwest, people think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  understands their challenges. They think that he is someone that cares. And when we are in the midst of a campaign, I think you will see strong enthusiasm and passion for the outcome of the election next year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, one of the things he's got to deal with here domestically, we were just talking about, is the debt ceiling. And he had said absolutely no negotiations over that. And then there was a conversation about, well, we need to see the House GOP budget. He's got the now from the speaker laying out a plan.\par \par \uc2 Senator Joe Manchin says it's time to get moving.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): You should be able to respectfully disagree and sit down and talk about your differences. My difference is this -- I believe we have to get our finances in control and under control, our debt. You know, if you can't manage your debt, you're in serious problems, and ours is going out of control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You have also said this has got to start progressing. What's your message for the White House this morning?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So, I have two messages here. I want to be very clear that raising the debt ceiling is a separate discussion from spending. It is making sure that we pay our bills. We did it three times in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And both President Trump and President Reagan said you should not play games with raising the debt ceiling, honoring our bills. It hurts us internationally with where we are now.\par \par \uc2 So, let me be clear -- we need to pay our bills, we need to honor `em, and if we were to in any way, shape, or form default on our spending, our working men and women across the country would have immediate consequences of increased credit card bills, auto payments, mortgage payments, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Do I think that people should sit down? Anybody who knows me, so let's get the debt ceiling done. Let's not play games. Let's not -- we shouldn't be doing it and it's already hurting our in -- what's going to happen in our economy, threatening it, et cetera?\par \par \uc2 Do I think that we should all be talking about how do we cut our spending? I'm always somebody that says sit down at the table, talk to each other, listen to each other. I think it's very important.\par \par \uc2 I'm concerned about the budget that they put forward this week, and I'm not sure he's got the votes for it or not because I don't think there are some Republicans want a vote to cut education, reduce veterans spending by 22 percent.\par \par \uc2 That's going to hurt veterans' health care. It's going to hurt Meals on Wheels. It's going to hurt cancer research. It's going to hurt law enforcement, first responders.\par \par \uc2 And I do think that we've got to look at who should be paying their fair share. I want to bring manufacturing back to this country. That budget is going to continue to support offshoring.\par \par \uc2 But I want to sit at the table. I want to have discussions, because Joe Manchin is right, President Biden is right, everybody is right. We got to work towards reducing our spending, but doing it in a right way that it doesn't hurt working men and women in this country, and help the billionaires and corporations who aren't paying taxes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And just as we noted on the show before, back in 2006, President Biden said he was a "no" vote as a senator then on the debt ceiling, on raising it, because he said it was just rewarding reckless spending and it wasn't an unexpected situation.\par \par \uc2 So the tables have turned. We'll see how they do on that. But one of the things that the speaker's proposed is getting people back to work who are able-bodied, do not have dependents at home.\par \par \uc2 President Biden himself said this in '96 when voting for work requirement. He said: The culture of welfare must be replaced with a culture of work. The culture of dependence must be replaced with a culture of self- sufficiency and personal responsibility.\par \par \uc2 We've got 10 million job openings in this country. Recent survey shows about three quarters of Americans, they back work requirements.\par \par \uc2 So, why not as part of this proposal get people back to work again, if they're able-bodied and there are no dependents at home?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So I do want to see people get back to work, if they can. We do have job openings, but I want to talk about the challenges that people are facing in the workforce. You just talked about it.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, women are the ones that left the workforce during COVID more than anybody.\par \par \uc2 Childcare isn't available. It's too expensive, 54 percent of people over 50 in this country are caring for someone that is older -- is elderly. We have to have the support system in place so people can go back into the workplace.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But just to be clear, on these -- on these particular proposals, it wouldn't apply to people that have dependents at home. It wouldn't apply to pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Look, we got to -- there may be people -- look, I think everything should be on the table. That's why we go to the table to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 But I also want to say there are people that want to work that aren't able to work either. So, let's sit down, let's talk about how you do it.\par \par \uc2 But I really want people to understand -- I have talked to so many people who want to go back into the workplace, but for many different reasons cannot do it. And I think we've got to address that as well. Caregiving being I think the biggest issue. The president this week laid out addressing that issue.\par \par \uc2 But caregiving makes all other work possible and we have a crisis in this country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's also talk about tax dollars when it comes to foreign policy because this week, the special investigator overlooking Afghanistan and what's happening with money there, he testified before the House Oversight Committee and essentially said he's being stonewalled by this administration in trying to tack -- track the money that we are sending into Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Here is some of what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN SOPKO\uc2 , SPECIAL IG FOR AFGHANISTAN RECONSTRUCTION: Unfortunately, as I sit here today, I cannot assure this committee or the American taxpayer we are not currently funding the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Nor can I assure you that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are not diverting the money we are sending from the intended recipients, which are the poor Afghan people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that comes on the backs of this headline from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ". Afghanistan has become a terrorism staging ground again.\par \par \uc2 How do you reassure the people that our money is not flowing to terrorism?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Well, I sincerely hope that our money is not flowing to terrorism. I think Pentagon has got to do a better job showing us that that is not happening, but I am worried about -- I spent so much of my career trying to help the women in Afghanistan and what is happening to them now makes me so devastated that we need to ensure that those dollars are going where they're intended to do in a way that we can get them there, and I think we need to ask questions and get answers.\par \par \uc2 But I -- we have a lot of people in Afghanistan that helped us as Americans when we were there and we need to be helping them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and especially getting those out that were counting on that promise.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask about a whistle-blower that came forward this week to Democrats and Republicans in Congress, a long time supervisory agent in the \uc2 IRS\uc2  saying essentially they have information that there may be political problems going on with a potential case there. It's believed to be about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Would you want to hear from that whistleblower?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So I have two different things that I want to say. One, I want to make it really clear that President Biden has made it clear since he went into office that there should be an independence of the Justice Department in any investigation. I think he has tried to make that clear.\par \par \uc2 I support whistleblowers. As a matter of fact, I think whistleblowers should be able to come forward, give us information, protect their identity, and I've led information to put protections around them.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what the facts of this case are. I have to no details. And I'm not going to speculate on things that I don't know.\par \par \uc2 But whistleblowers play an important part in our government if they believe that there is wrongdoing and they do need to be protected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. And quickly, I want to ask you about the Supreme Court leaving mifepristone, the abortion pill, on the market while the legal fight about it plays out.\par \par \uc2 I want to read you something from a pro-life OB/GYN who wrote about this battle and the fact that she says the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has ignored safety issues with the pill.\par \par \uc2 She says this: it fast tracked the abortion pill using its accelerated drug approval authority, ludicrously designating pregnancy as a, quote, serious or life-threatening illness. She says as many as 7 percent of women who use abortion pills early in pregnancy need follow up surgery, up to 15 percent will experience hemorrhage and 2 percent will have an infection.\par \par \uc2 So, why shouldn't that be part of the conversation, legal and otherwise? Shouldn't the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's number one priority be patient safety?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: The number one priority of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  needs to be about patient safety. I don't think the Supreme Court, and I think they made that clear this week, you had conservative justices joining with others that sent it back to the courts and have said, let the \uc2 FDA\uc2  do their job.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think a lot of people don't understand that they wanted to ban a drug that also is used in Cushing syndrome. I was a woman that suffered from endometriosis and was trying to get pregnant. It helps fibroid urine endometriosis.\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2  is the scientific agency that is supposed to ensure that patients are safe and studied these drugs. I'm certainly not in a position to know, I'm not a medical expert, nor do I think the Supreme Court justices are, and we have agencies designed and set up to do the scientific process, and that's where I think the responsibility belongs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will watch that legal battle play out.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Dingell, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A new poll shows former President Trump stretching his lead over 2024 hopefuls. But up next, our Sunday panel joins us on the polling that shows one of those potential rivals with Mr. Trump has something that he doesn't -- an advantage over President Biden in the general election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): In D.C., big, conservative victories are few and far between. In Florida, we deliver big victories every single day. We reject the culture of losing that has infected the Republican Party in recent years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Republican of course, selling his approach as the way to win. And seemed to take a little swipe there at recent GOP election losses.\par \par \uc2 Time to discuss with our Sunday group. "Politico" congressional reporter Olivia Beavers, Fox News senior political analyst Juan Williams, former Bush White House adviser Karl Rove, and former Senate candidate from Washington state, now chairman of rescuing the American dream, Tiffany Smiley.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Good to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 OK, so let's start there.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was in town this week. He was gripping and grinning and yet people who met with him then went and endorsed President Trump, who is racking up the wins on congressional endorsements.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something from Congressman Steube on why he chose President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GREG STEUBE (R-FL): There's been multiple opportunities where I have gone up to him, and talked to him, given him my cell phone number, asked him to reach out to me.\par \par \uc2 Not -- not even a person from his staff called me back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Olivia, we heard that again and again this week.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, "POLITICO": Yes. And I - you know, a couple of weeks ago I was asking -- my colleague and I got both the senators from Florida and all of the House Republicans from Florida, and this was a problem that we kept hearing again and again was, he is not accessible.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think that after hearing this, \uc2 Greg Steube\uc2  bringing it up repeatedly in a very public fashion, maybe the -- DeSantis will be much better about answering his phone call compared to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who they said would ring you right back as president and after he left the White House.\par \par \uc2 But DeSantis, I think, is also going to be -- starting to see -- be more aggressive. So, right now you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  filling the void. You had (INAUDIBLE) walk out and sort of do that campaign gimmick of saying that he went to see \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  talk, and then he goes, I'm endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with a press release that was already ready. And I think you're going to see him pushing out mailers, you're going to see him pushing out ads. They're going to be signing up volunteers. And I think that they're going to start being more of a stronger presence in the media than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and just a few days ago he was up in New Hampshire, one of the key early states, and they actually had this assessment of him in "Politico." It said, DeSantis bucks his robot reputation. He worked the 500-person crowd at the Amos Tuck dinner in downtown Manchester for over an hour, a flurry of handshakes and photos that the state party chair said was unplanned and that defies the notion that he lacks retail skills.\par \par \uc2 And, Karl, in those early states, that's all-important.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It matters a lot. And - and, look, he's got - he's got rock star status right now. He came to Texas. The largest campaign fundraisers for the Republican Party in Houston and in Dallas in history, raising over a million dollars each. Biggest fundraiser in the history of the New Hampshire Republican Party this past week. And better to make mistakes and start correcting those mistakes earlier rather than later. And this idea of him going out there in New Hampshire and glad-handing people and selfies and residents sitting at the head table, wandering through the audience and greeting each table, smart move on his part. Bet to do it now. Obviously didn't have a good, personal, warm and fuzzy relationship with Congressman Steube. But after a while, I think people are going to look at that and say, OK, fine, I get that, but - but what has he guy done and what's he capable of doing?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, here's the headline from "The Wall Street Journal" in recent polling. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tops \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in test of GOP presidential field. Which is true, he continues to pull away. But tucked into that article says this, Mr. DeSantis leads Mr. Biden 48-45 in a hypothetical contest while Mr. Trump lags behind the Democratic president by three points.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY (R), "RESCUING THE AMERICAN DREAM" CHAIR AND FORMER WASHINGTON STATE SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Tiffany, when you look at some of the numbers in the polling there, it shows that people say they want a specific candidate. They don't care as much about who can actually win the general. Will they change their minds during the primary?\par \par \uc2 SMILEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've been through a primary and general election and fought your way through.\par \par \uc2 SMILEY: Yes, so, you know, look, this is also really, really early. And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hasn't even announced yet. But what I'm excited about is, we have an opportunity to have robust debate and discussion about issues that are affecting the American people. That's good for our party. The Democrats won't have that because their far left extreme policies, they're already solidified. And that's - which is why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval is in the 30s.\par \par \uc2 The American people are clear, they - they know the country is on the wrong track. I have - I have seen that theme played out in a survey I just did of 2,000 registered voters across the country with my new project, "Rescuing the American Dream." The American people want to put politics aside and start to have discussions about solutions. I think the best candidate will emerge who is able to discuss the solutions that will help the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it's going to be a robust debate, I have a feeling.\par \par \uc2 SMILEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, you know, you saw former Vice President Pence saying, like, if you're serious, you've got to be in by June. So, we'll see as we await more decisions and announcements.\par \par \uc2 We will probably get one from President Biden this week. And here's the headline from "Washington Examiner," most Democrats don't want Biden to run, even as he readies an announcement, It says, the majority of them remain addiment that he shouldn't run. And when it comes - and that's Democrats. Then when it comes to all voters, only 26 percent say Biden should run.\par \par \uc2 Juan, we keep having this conversation. I mean if he's the president, he's got that power of incumbency and he's running. He's clearly - well, anything can happen these days. He could get the nomination. We would expect him to.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But if there's this lack of enthusiasm and an outright adversarial feeling about him actually running, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think it's adversarial. You know, I just think there's low disapproval. We live in an age of extreme polarized politics. So, imagine, just wipe off 50 percent, wipe off Republicans. They're not going to approve of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But a majority of Democrats saying, no thank you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, they're saying, I think, you know, look, first of all, he's going to be the nominee. What you heard from Lucas Tomlinson earlier in the show, he's going to announce on Tuesday in a video.\par \par \uc2 But I think the fact that Trump is running, and that Trump is doing so well, is the glue that's holding lots of factions of the Democratic Party behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think that you have to understand that for people who disapprove of both President Trump and President Biden, it's according to a "Wall Street Journal" poll this week, 54 to 15 percent, they prefer \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They'd vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  if that's the - that's the -- the alternative. So the age --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Karl's shaking his head.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Let me just finish here. I think age is a big factor for the people you have been talking about -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Who say, gee, this guy will be pretty old if he's elected president.\par \par \uc2 But I just think back, because I'm a pretty old guy. I think back to \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and I think of people saying, oh, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was the wise old man. It's morning in America again. And what happened? He won in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 KARL: And he was in his 60s, not his 80s.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He was on his way (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KARL: (INAUDIBLE). Come on, look, let's be honest, there's the deep concern about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's ability to handle another four years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Twenty-seven percent of Democrats in the AP poll said they preferred him to run, 73 percent, not. Monmouth, prefer to see Biden run for a second term, 25 percent of Democrats prefer to see Biden step aside, 44 have no preference. So, those are the polite people who don't want to tell a pollster, I don't want him to run. Thirty - this is - the country can do better than an 82-year-old and a 78-year-old and needs to do better when we face the kind of charges we - we face.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARL: I agree with the editors of that great newspaper, "The Wall Street Journal," in their - in their weekend editorial. He would do the country a service by stepping aside and - and he would do his party a service because then somebody could rise up who could serve eight years more, rather than four, as he might be able to do.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, let me just -- let me just - let me quote the great \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Compare me not to the almighty, but to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And here - here - and here --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's the - there's the (INAUDIBLE) -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: The choice is what the country needs, not the -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I said - well, who's here (ph)?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I think he enjoys flying home on Air Force One and riding around on the helicopter -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'm just telling - but Karl -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: But that -- that was an embarrassing trip to Ireland, to watch -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Karl. Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: It was painful. It was painful to - it was pain --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Karl, are you kidding? This guy's - he was with his family, wearing a rosary, giving tribute to the priest. Come on.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, did you watch the -- did you see how confused he was meeting with the families.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, look, I'll tell you -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Go to the videotape. It is painful.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I - I love it. Look -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: His son has to be at his elbow to tell him what to do.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Karl - Karl -- I love you, Karl.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Oh, dad, we've got to (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Karl, I love you, but -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Let me tell you who had an embarrassing week is your alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , DeSantis. A midnight six-week ban on abortion. Saying that people can walk around with guns without a permit. This is - and nobody likes it. People in Florida don't like it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This guy is slipping badly and he's supposed to be the alternative.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I don't know - let - let me just say, the American -- the Floridians liked it to the tune of a 19 point win for DeSantis in November.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: that's way back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm not sure the - ancient history, November.\par \par \uc2 KARL: And let's - let's be - let's be clear about one quick thing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quickly.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Polls at this point, Tiffany's absolutely right, they don't mean anything.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Snapshot.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Jeb Bush was leading at this point in 2015. And think about this, 30 days before Iowa, in 2004, Howard Dean was leading. In 2008, Clinton and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . Newt was leading in 2012, 30 days before the Iowa. We're going to see the polls go up and go down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARL: And we're going to have people like Juan write off \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and write off Asa Hutchinson, write off (INAUDIBLE), up and down many times.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'm not writing -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy. OK. All right. All right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: All I'm saying is, he's stumbling and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trolling (ph) him, has the man eating pudding with his fingers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. OK. Oh boy. OK, that will be happening during the commercial.\par \par \uc2 KARL: That was a stupid ad.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There will be arm wrestling on this side of the table. That's Pay Per View, though.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, stick around. Lots more to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to debate the White House plan that could force home buyers with good celt to pay more for their mortgages to subsidize the riskier loans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Your job is to secure borders, air, land, and sea. And in my judgment, and it's hard for me to say this, I've known you for quite some time, but you have failed in your job.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): Mr. Secretary, Republicans are criticizing you for not achieving something that no secretary has ever achieved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Republican Congressman Mike McCaul and Democrat \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  at a hearing with Homeland Secretary Mayorkas. Republicans blasting the way he's handling the border, but Democrats say they're using a double standard.\par \par \uc2 So, we're back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 And we have this stat, in March, 192,000 foreigners encountered at the southwest border. That, Juan, make it the 25th month in a row that the counters have been above 150,000. Is the border under control?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Obviously not, but I would say that it hasn't been under control for now decades. We haven't had immigration reform really going back to President Reagan. It's that long. And, again, the polarized politics make it very hard for either side in Congress to act. We've seen this under President Bush. We've seen it under President Obama. President Trump couldn't get it done.\par \par \uc2 What we saw this week in those hearings, it looked to me like an endless repeat of the hearings we've seen before. Republicans make provocative, angry statements, you should be fired, but they don't ask questions that could lead to some solutions, some resolution about, well, how do we deal with the border, with the fact that people are fleeing oppression and violence and wanting to come in here under our asylum rules. This is the rules we have. Those are our laws.\par \par \uc2 And they -- it seems to me that there is no new idea about how we deal with the fact that we have 10 million plus illegal immigrants already in the country. So, it seems to me it's a lot of fear, a lot of fireworks, but I don't see any solutions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Karl, former President Trump will argue that he had changed those numbers, that he was trying to institute policies that actually made a difference.\par \par \uc2 KARL: Give him credit. The number of encounters, which is basically people coming across the border illegally, was dramatically lower under his watch, on his watch, in part because he had policies like remain in Mexico. He worked it out with the Mexican government. Our laws say you have to apply for asylum in the country of origin or in some other nearby country. And these people are not applying for asylum until they cross the border into the United States. If President Biden simply said, we're going to enforce the law and we will allow nobody to apply for asylum inside the United States, the number of people who would be making that dangerous trip are - would drop dramatically.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there are lots of good things we can do. Mayorkas refuses to do it. I live in a state where every single day we are negatively impacted by the failure of this administration to enforce the laws of the United States and provide the necessary resources to get it done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Congress is not getting it done on that front, but they may have something else to talk about, which is this whistleblower who has come forward. You saw me talking to Congresswoman Dingell about she respects the protections for whistleblowers and wants to hear more about this.\par \par \uc2 Let me play something from the whistleblower's attorney talking to our Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE, ATTORNEY FOR IRS WHISTLEBLOWER: A career law enforcement officer, who knows the right way to do an investigation, when he hears a senior politically appointed official at the Department of Justice, under sworn testimony, say something and in his mind it's directly contradictory to what he knows is going on with the investigation -\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS: And what he can prove with documents?\par \par \uc2 LYTLE: What he can prove with documents, he wants to come forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Olivia, this is about, we believe, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  investigation. How much of a headache is this for the administration as the president's gets ready to roll out for re-election?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I mean I think it - it's going to be really tough for them. The lawyer was talking about his accolades, saying he's not out there for attention. He doesn't have a politic agenda. And he has documents. And we all know, as reporters, we love documents that can back up an argument. And he's trying to push, and I think that this is the right tact, he's trying to argue that he wants to testify in between a bipartisan panel so that he can make his argument in a way where it's not being pulled by one side unfairly.\par \par \uc2 Now, I'm sure we're going to both see narratives if that does happen and - and those conversations are beginning, but it will, I think, depending on what he shares, it might add new scrutiny to the Justice Department and - and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  investigation that they're having into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and whether he's receiving special treatment or not. The White House says that's not true. There's no interference. But we're going to see as this unfolds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and the White House has deferred to the DOJ, to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , to answer these questions and say they're staying out of it, as you note.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy (ph) had an interesting piece, talking about this and said, Tiffany, if it was any