Cohen, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the early 2000s my next guest was working full-time in New Orleans but pressing toward a career singing full-time. When she finally made it in the clubs of the French Quarter, tragedy struck the city and forced her to pick up the pieces. But she found support and community with an organization that helps blues singers in need. And today she gigs around the country, sharing her strength and her story with the fans who know her as mother blues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAT COHEN, MUSICIAN: I always wanted to sing, but more than sing, I always wanted to entertain.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Pat Cohen on the moment she made a leap from part-time singer to full-time entertainer.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): Rock me, baby. Rock me all night long.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): I said to myself, I said self --\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): As we have to do sometimes.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yes. Self, you wanted to come down. The reason you chose New Orleans because you wanted to come and you wanted to sing. So, either do it or get off the pot, you know what I'm saying?\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): (INAUDIBLE) me daddy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That drive helped her become the dominant but welcoming stage presence known today as Mother Blues.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): I started working on Bourbon Street then. And I built myself up to where there were a lot of people that were calling me the queen of bourbon street because I was pulling so many people into the club. I'd fill a house up every single time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And then Hurricane Katrina came and destroyed her city and her home.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): So, for you, Katrina was devastating in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: In a lot of ways. It was - it was - it was more than losing your stuff.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But Cohen says the aftermath made her resilient.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Katrina taught me how to be really strong. Every single day I just cried. When I stopped crying, I stopped crying.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And started fresh.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: You don't accomplish anything by feeling sorry for yourself. You have to keep it moving.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): But I'm a North Carolina woman.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Cohen did move to North Carolina, where a chance meeting lead her to the Music Maker Foundation. It's a non-profit that supports musicians who perform traditional American blues. Music Maker got Cohen gigging again.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): And when things would happen, they were always there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And things did happen. Cohen lost another home. This time to a fire.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): So, once again, you went through losing everything you had.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I wanted to cry, but I didn't want to cry. I refused. I just decided I was cried out. Again, again, Music Maker was there.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): You see the city was too high tech.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've been very determined to just keep moving forward. How does that infuse your singing?\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): So, I try to do things that are fun. So, a lot of my songs are kind of happy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Happy blues.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Happy blues.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): Have nobody treating me bad.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): I wrote this song called "I Ain't Gonna Have Nobody Treat Me Bad."\par \par \uc2 COHEN (singing): Shut your mouth, get up out of my house.\par \par \uc2 COHEN (on camera): And I'm sure that somebody can identify with that. You know, we go through a whole lot. You have to be strong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pat by the blues, y'all, give it up.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And our thanks to J.B.'s Restaurant (ph) in Virginia for letting us stop by and see Pat sing.\par \par \uc2 Just a quick note, pastor and bestselling author \uc2 Max Lucado\uc2  joins me on the "Livin' the Bream" podcast this week to talk about the power of friends willing to literally walk through the fire with you. We see that in the story of Shadrach, Meshack and Abednego, just one of the chapters of my new book, "Love Stories of the Bible Speak," in stores now.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week and we'll see you back here for next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230423ej4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Big City Democrat Mayors Sound Alarm On Crime; Mayors Shift Dem's Message On Crime Ahead Of 2024; Questions Surround Federal Probe Of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 ; Accusations Of Political Bias In Hunter Probe; Trump Pulls Ahead Of DeSantis After Trailing; Lawmakers Raise Concerns About AI And Kids; How Artificial Intelligence Is Affecting Kids; Prosecutors Drop Charges Against Baldwin\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Geraldo Rivera, Rich Edson, Miranda Devine,  Jonathan Turley, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 7,614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To see if they can catch these people in this case.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: That is an amazing story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's wild. A lot of gold missing and they're going to see if they can track it down.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Wow, that is something. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To get that in there. And that it, does it for us, John, I can't believe it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: You know, I'm just doing a calculation here how much it would -- there would be 10,000 ounces of gold. How do you carry that in a suitcase?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, gold is at a record high, by the way. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We'll see you next week. Have a great weekend. THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, guys. Thank you very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News Headquarters in New York. Here's a story that you are not seeing elsewhere today. This happened today in Washington, these four very influential mayors of big cities: Chicago, Houston, New York, L.A. got together, and they talked about their take on crime in their cities.\par \par \uc2 And what they were saying was quite interesting. I think you'd call this a wake-up call, an about face, however you want to interpret it. But \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , the Chicago Mayor, who has only days left in office, is now complaining that prosecutors are going easy on criminals, and that they're making it tough for the police. Listen closely to her and to these other mayors, because this is enlightening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D-IL), CHICAGO: If we say, yes, the police department is spending all this time and resources to arrest, put a case on, and then the judges and the prosecutors say, you know what, we're going to let you out on electronic monitoring to wreak havoc again. If somebody seek, musters the courage to come forward and identify the person who would just shut up their neighborhood, and then seize pokey walking