 case to do with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And he is just been obstructed by Biden appointees in California, and Washington, D.C. That is what his whistleblower complaint is about. And this is what he wants to testify to Congress about.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there is just some hoops, legal hoops that have to be jumped over before that can be done.\par \par \uc2 But it's a really big threat to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's continual denial that his son has done anything wrong, that there has been any corruption involving him, and that his claims that he knows nothing about his son, Hunter and his brother, Jim's overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 He was the product that we're selling.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEVINE: He met with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business partners. He helped \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and Jim Biden meet these business partners. He took onto Biden on Air Force Two to China, to Mexico, elsewhere, to meet business partners.\par \par \uc2 So, it just big as belief that he can continue with this charade. But he does. And the White House continues to stonewall.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And this IRA's -- IRS agent, excuse me, he may be the linchpin that starts to kind of unravel this as to where the blockages, because you've got David Weiss, who was a Trump appointed attorney, overseeing this case, you got five years of investigating, and now you got people who are at these agencies saying what is going on? We have a case here, we want to see it move forward, and this \uc2 IRS\uc2  agent may be the beginning of some of this cracking open.\par \par \uc2 And you've done an amazing job of cracking open the story all along. Miranda, thank you so much. It's great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 DEVINE: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are watching the Supreme Court very closely at this hour, where the majority Conservative court is set to decide the future of the so-called abortion pill in America.\par \par \uc2 This is a very significant case. Jonathan Turley is standing by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know you remember this story. It was tragic all around. And this is a shocking new booking photo of former Minnesota Police Officer Kim Potter. And it does reveal the toll of 16 months in prison on Kim Potter on the left side and the right side.\par \par \uc2 The 26-year police veteran was convicted by a jury of first degree and second-degree manslaughter after she made a tragic error and pulled her handgun instead of her Taser and killed Daunte Wright during a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 He was 20 years old. He was the father to a young boy. And here is the judge as you -- we go back to February of 2022. Getting a bit emotional during the sentencing at that hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REGINA CHU, FOURTH JUDICIAL DISTRICT JUDGE, HENNEPIN COUNTY, MINNESOTA: Officer Potter made a mistake that ended tragically. She never intended to hurt anyone. Her conduct cries out for her sentence significantly below the guidelines.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So now, Potter is scheduled to be released from prison on Monday, after which she will serve eight months of supervised release, and we will continue to follow that story next week when Kim Potter is released.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Daunte Wright's family continues to grieve his loss, as Miss Potter said, she did as well. She apologized profusely to the family in the middle of this hearing.\par \par \uc2 But we'll keep on top of that story as it develops.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, any minute now, we could get a decision from the Supreme Court about what to do about a drug that is used in most abortions in this country.\par \par \uc2 I just think a lot of people don't realize that more than 60 percent of all abortion that happens in this country happens through the use of these prescription drugs, often which are sent to people's homes -- mail to the home.\par \par \uc2 So, this comes after a historic ruling less than a year of the historic ruling in Roe v. Wade, again, to overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And after recent warnings that the abortion battle is a tricky political issue, obviously, it's always been. But looking ahead to 2024, and based on what happened in the midterms, it's got a lot of focus on it. So, this is a very big decision.\par \par \uc2 So, we bring in constitutional attorney Jonathan Turley, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor, and a Fox News contributor. Jonathan, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As I said, we expect that we may get this decision at any moment. The current standing expires at midnight, but it's thought that the justices will do something this afternoon, to sort of clear the air here.\par \par \uc2 Give everybody sort of the framework here, and what you'll be watching?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, you've done an excellent job, you know, at issue here, just happens to be the means of abortion used by the vast majority of women in the country, it's a medical, it's abortion through this medicine by this prescription drug, not in by going to a clinic, not by going to doctors to carry out the abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, the suspension of this drug would have an immediate and national impact on women who are seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 The problem that the trial judge found was that when these rules were promulgated, the administration effectively, sort of shortcutted the system, that they didn't do some of the things required to be done through a local APA, these are the administrative procedures that agencies have to follow.\par \par \uc2 Now, those are the same challenges that Democrats brought against the Trump administration in areas like immigration.\par \par \uc2 It's a process challenge. So, the court has a rather tough problem in front of it. It has a matter of great importance to people around the country. It has a claim that these rules are invalid because of how they were promulgated.\par \par \uc2 But it also is very cognizant of how sensitive this issue is for the entire nation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this is a quick comment from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , talking about this. Obviously, the White House was played a role in getting this to the Supreme Court to decide, because there was a Texas judge that had a decision on this, and then contradicted by a Washington judge. Here is what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: To Mifepristone, a -- as I've mentioned before, it is a drug that's been around for more than 20 years. It's been used in six -- more than 60 countries. It is a -- it is as -- it is -- has been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are prepared for whatever announcement comes out of the Supreme Court, and if need be, we are prepared also for a long legal battle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tough issue for the GOP, Jonathan. You look at these things in large context, obviously, as well. I think a lot of people thought that the Roe v. Wade decision was going to be tricky for the GOP. And now this could make it even more so as you head towards 2024.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes. Yes. The court has various options. It could return the availability of this drug pending further review, that's fairly common in these cases.\par \par \uc2 It could uphold the Fifth Circuit, which had a solid mask type of result. It allowed this drug to still be made available, but got rid of a few of the recent changes.\par \par \uc2 Or it could go for the whole megillah. It could basically do a judgment before -- preserved before judgment and scheduled this for a ruling on the merits and a rather sort of abbreviated calendar.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And Justice Alito has sort of allowed the pause here. And as you point out, this is really a process decision. It's not, you know, sort of a moral decision that's been made on the use of this drug. It's whether or not it is legal and holds up constitutionally, in terms of these decisions by the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thank you very much. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see what happens. We'll bring you right back if we get an answer here.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House keeping quiet today about the possibility of President Biden, launching a reelection bid. The speculation is it will happen on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Don't have anything to say about 2024, either way, it's not going to come from here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, as all Republicans take sides in the brewing battle between \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Why Florida Congressman Michael Waltz, who represents DeSantis's old congressional district in Florida, says he's backing the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Florida operate in administration that does not get consumed in petty controversy or drama or palace intrigue. We basically execute the mission day, after day, after day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Certain movement in the Republican race for the White House still very early in the going here, but the former president has a 13 point lead over Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . In this particular Wall Street Journal poll, you can see that the numbers were almost exactly reversed in December. I mean, that's a lot of movement between December and, what is it now, April, end of April, OK? So you can see that it, you know, it's basically a flip flop in these numbers.\par \par \uc2 But DeSantis is not in the race yet. A lot of people wondering when he will get in. There's discussion that it will happen right after his legislative session towards the end of May. President Biden is not in the race either, but he's reportedly planning to kick off his campaign next week. And it will be in the form of a video announcement, which is the way it was done last time around in the middle of COVID.\par \par \uc2 So as a new poll shows that most Democrats do not want him to run again, 52%. However, it does say that if he's the nominee, they will support him. That one's from AP.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Florida Republican Congressman Michael Waltz represents Governor DeSantis's old district in the House, but just endorsed former President Trump. Good to see you today, Congressman Waltz. Nice to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Why did you decide to do that at this stage of the game?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Well, Martha, I'll be honest with you, it's not easy. Governor DeSantis has been a fantastic governor for the state of Florida. He's led the state through multiple crises, not just COVID, where he was right on so many issues but hurricanes, Ian, Hurricane Nicole. And, you know, thousand people a day are voting with their feet to come here.\par \par \uc2 But that said, Martha, look, I'm looking as a veteran, as someone on multiple national security committees and focus on keeping the country safe. When I look at what President Trump accomplished on the international stage, whether it was taking out Baghdadi, the head of ISIS, Soleimani, the Iranian general, and then leading at the same time to the Abraham Accords, which for any other president -- would have been multiple Peace Prizes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: And we could keep going down the list, the new \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  deal. And really, I look at, for example, at Mexico. Mexico -- AMLO right now, the president of Mexico, is lecturing President Biden on the international stage, won't barely even take his phone calls, yet President Trump had him with his National Guard on his southern border to stop migrants.\par \par \uc2 I think at the end of the day, we need someone bold, we need someone experienced, and we need dramatic change to get this country back on track. At the end of the day, Martha, he asked me for my endorsement. He's been very good to me in my district, and I said yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's put up some numbers because one of the things that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said today in a speech that he gave is that he said that his team rejects a culture of losing. I'm talking about winning by one and a half million votes in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Here are some hypothetical matchups. I almost hate putting these up because these are national polls. It's very early in the race, but this is the one that came out today from The Wall Street Journal. 48 to 45 has Biden beating President Trump. The other one shows that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  would beat President Biden when people were asked, who would you vote for today. Is that meaningful to you at this point?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, Martha, like you said, it is early. These are national polls. I've got a Harris poll sitting in front of me here that shows President Trump beating Biden. In fact, beating him by more than Governor DeSantis was. And what's interesting is it shows Biden's favorability below Trump's, 41 to 43, and his unfavorability above Trump's. So it's a long way to go, but at the end of the day, that bold, experienced leadership is what I'm getting behind.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And just real quick on the way out here, why do it now? There's a lot to play out here. I know you said he asked you for your endorsement and you gave it to him, but did you think about all the things that are going to happen between now and then, and might you change your mind.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Now, Martha, again, I could spend the rest of the hour laying out the accomplishments under the Trump presidency, despite everything thrown at him. I think, like I said, there's a lot of people that are torn. This isn't an easy decision, but I can get behind that leadership, and America needs to get back to where were.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Congressman Michael Waltz, thank you very much. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The role that artificial intelligence plays in social media and the push from young Americans to get their friends and families to put down the screen and listen up about the potential harm that is inflicted on millions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMMA LEMBKE, LOG OFF MOVEMENT CEO & CO-FOUNDER: The mental health crisis for young people that we are witnessing will only continue to rise. So we cannot wait another year, we cannot wait another month, another week or another day to begin to protect the next generation from the harms that we have witnessed and heard about today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So another new warning today for parents about artificial intelligence. Even if you don't think that your kids are using it, AI could be affecting them in all kinds of ways, and some of those can be very dangerous. I'm going to talk with Emma Lembke, co-founder of the Log Off Movement, and Matt Miles, who is a teacher and writer about this issue. He's also a parent. First to correspondent Gillian Turner who has covered this extensively for us live in Washington today. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS Correspondent: Hi, Martha. So AI, as you know, is really only in its infancy, but it is already infecting nearly every interaction that kids have online. When it comes to social media, that is translating into real world dangers that most parents aren't even aware of. This is according to AI experts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WRIGHT, CYBERSECURITY EXPERT: We saw the increase in social media use, you saw the increase in the rise of suicide among teens and younger kids. Homicide used to be the leading cause of death for boys age 13 to 17, it's suicide now. So we're seeing the negative impacts of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's GPT-3 perhaps, the best known model, it powers literally hundreds of chat bots and apps has already been caught urging a research account user to commit suicide. Now lawmakers on Capitol Hill are ringing alarm bells as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS (D-MA) We've already seen that algorithms on TikTok, on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , on Snap are feeding kids an increasingly toxic array of feeds that's creating a compare and despair culture. And AI again can put that on steroids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Colorado Senator Michael Bennet recently wrote to tech leaders about an investigation which found AI taught a child "how to cover up a bruise ahead of a visit from Child Protective Service, a 13-year-old how to lie to her parents about an upcoming trip with an 31-year-old man and how to make losing her virginity a special experience by setting the mood." Even the White House now has clued into the risk that AI poses to young people, in particular through social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: In safeguards, we see the impact on the mental health and self-images, and feelings and hopelessness, especially among young people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: AI specialist that we spoke to for this piece say that these systems already know how to internalize psychology or neurolinguistics. They understand human personalities even today. And with that comes the ability to manipulate us. Children are obviously especially vulnerable, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Scary. Thank you very much, Gillian. With that, we bring in Emma Lembke, CEO and co founder of the Log Off Movement, and Matt Miles, a teacher, parent and author of a book about kids and technology. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Emma, we watched you testify before Congress, and it feels like every generation or every decade has to deal with another layer of the difficulties of social media. So now when you hear about what ChatGPT is doing to teens, that it's encouraging them. Here's how you can lie to your parents about running away with a man who is twice your age, three times your age, what goes through your mind and how do we stop this? How do we log off from this?\par \par \uc2 LEMBKE: I think that's a wonderful question. And, you know, the first thing that comes to my mind is that I'm actually not that surprised. I think what we've seen, specifically my generation, Gen Z, over the past few years as we've grown up with social media is the negative effect that it can have on our mental health, on our physical health, our well-being, safety and privacy. It can really alter life in very drastic, severe and damaging ways.\par \par \uc2 So I think what we need to do moving forward is we need to think very critically about how we allow tech companies to innovate and to develop these technologies, and make it so they develop in a way that is also conducive to the youth experience, protecting them rather than just protecting profit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, we have to insist on responsibility, Matt, from these companies. It's pretty hard to get. This is the TikTok CEO when he was asked about this broader issue of suicide. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What about those with mental health issues? Are they (inaudible) content?\par \par \uc2 SHOU CHEW, TIKTOK CEO: If a user searches words that expresses mental health issues, we actually redirect them to a safety page. Like, for example, if you -- I don't know if I should say this in public. If you search I want to die, we will redirect you to a safety page, for example.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you believe that, Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT MILES, TEACHER: No. I mean, the thing you have to understand is Big Tech is the modern day Big Tobacco. I mean, there's so much money going into keeping kids engaged with their content. For as long as possible. The safeguards they have put up are laughable, like the age verification. They just ask, are you over 18 or the parent notification.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there hasn't been any great safeguards. If they really wanted to do more for mental health, they would put severe restrictions on age limits and at what age kids could go or even what time of the day they could be on. Why would kids need to be on TikTok from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM, you know, when they're supposed to be at school. So they haven't really done anything except for put tons of money into keeping kids glued to their product.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And I know, you know, in China they have really strict limits on when they allow these apps to be available. But it seems to me we can't really wait for the tech companies to be the good guys here and we need to, as parents, really step in and set the rules in our own families.\par \par \uc2 Just quickly, from each of you, Emma, what do you think might work? What advice do you have for parents today?\par \par \uc2 LEMBKE: I think that a lot of parents just need to engage in, like you said, open, productive dialogue. I think oftentimes young people, it's hard to articulate why we use social media, why we go on it to express ourselves, explore the world. Having open dialogue as a family unit discussing why you enter these apps, how you enter them, and how you can do it in the safest manner while putting in guidelines as a family unit is going to be very conducive to building better online habits. And ensuring that when your kid is online, they are operating in the healthiest manner as we wait for lawmakers to begin to hold Big Tech accountable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Matt, quick thought on that. I mean, you can set time limits as a parent, you can take phones away, you can say during the school year day you're not going to use it. What do you advise as a parent and a teacher? What should parents do?\par \par \uc2 MILES: Well, I mean, that's a million dollar question. As a parent, one of the things as an educator, we see the biggest pushback is when educators try to implement the no phone rules. It's oftentimes groups of parents that are the biggest pushback. They want to stay connected to their kids throughout the day. So they're the ones who are worried about what would happen in the case of an emergency or this or that.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of times they're the ones who are struggling to disconnect from their kids. I mean we just have to -- we have to all disconnect, I think, is a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think it's a great point because, you know, I think that parents need to kind of rethink that a little bit and think about the fact that their kids are sitting in class scrolling in front of a teacher. I mean, we would never gotten away with anything even close to this. If we used to write notes to each other and pass them, we would get in trouble. We get a detention after class because were obviously distracted in class.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know how anybody expects to learn anything when you're allowed to scroll on your phone during your class. I think everybody needs to wake up on that and think about that part of why you're in school in the first place. Matt, thank you very much. Emma. Keep up the good work, both of you. Thanks for being here. We'll continue the conversation another time. Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 MILES: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So another important mental health conversation in a new "Untold Story" podcast. I mean, we talked a lot of things. We talked about fun things, we talked about her new book, which is so interesting. It's called "You Can't Joke About That: Why Everything is Funny, Nothing is Sacred and We're All in this Together." How laughter can actually help people connect. It can help with stress and anxiety, and all those things. And I think you're going to see a really personal side of Kat Timpf in our conversation that you may not know.\par \par \uc2 And I really encourage you to listen to our conversation. I know we had a fantastic time talking to each other and we're probably going to continue that conversation, but the book is now available, "You Can't Joke About That" by Kat Timpf. So tune in for that.\par \par \uc2 And Alec Baldwin's wife posting this photo after prosecutors dropped manslaughter charges against him in the fatal movie shooting of Halyna Hutchins. Defense attorney Mercedes Colwin explains why the actor may not be off the hook in every way quite yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: Well, the trigger wasn't pulled. I didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, ABC HOST: So you never pulled the trigger?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: No, no, no. I would never point a gun at anyone and pull a trigger at them, never. That was the training that I had. You don't point a gun at somebody and pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So this was pretty stunning. Yesterday around this time we learned that some of the charges have been dropped against Alec Baldwin. But we're also hearing you may not totally be off the hook according to prosecutors. They're dropping the involuntary manslaughter charge for the movie set shooting that killed the cinematographer, Halyna Hutchins, on the right side of your screen there.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin was holding the gun, but you saw that sound bite with George Stephanopoulos. He said he didn't pull the trigger. But prosecutors say they have new information and could refile charges against him. So let's bring an attorney Mercedes Colwin to tell us what's going on here. Mercedes, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So what happened here? There's two new prosecutors, the other prosecutors are gone, and now we got this news. So what happens now? What's going on?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, ATTORNEY: What's incredibly shocking, just as you start this segment, how could it be when you start with the prosecutor's office at the very inception of this, when they were adamant that they were going to go after there was a death of an individual, that Alec Baldwin was not above the law. But every single step of the way, Alec Baldwin kept saying I never pulled the trigger.\par \par \uc2 How about if you're, the prosecutor of the case, examine the gun. Now there's allegations that the gun had been modified and the trigger wasn't necessarily have to be pulled for the gun to discharge. It sounds like sloppiness by the prosecutors, but they're cleaning it up, Martha. If the crux of this entire case, which it is, is that Alec Baldwin held a gun and pulled the trigger, it resulted in the death of Ms. Hutchins. That's per se involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 So look at that gun, examine it forensically. It's being reported that the defense certainly did that and that's why they pushed and pushed and said the gun's been modified. It can discharge automatically. Our client, Alec Baldwin, could not be culpable of criminal negligence as a result.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, the thing that has always bothered me about this case, Mercedes, is why was there live ammunition anywhere near there, and the custody of the gun and how the live ammunition was on the set in the first place because they said they had very strict rules. We know people quit because they said the safety standards weren't being adhered to.\par \par \uc2 There's a whole lot more to this investigation about how this could have happened than we've ever learned anything about.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: So true, Martha. Think about it, I mean, you heard the gunnerer who still has charges against her, that's Hannah Gutierrez Reed. She still has two charges of involuntary manslaughter. You could see her that she was overwhelmed. She's talked about how she was overwhelmed, that the budgets were pretty strict, that she wasn't able to keep up.\par \par \uc2 Of course, someone has to be culpable for what took place. Ms. Hutchins died. And when you look at what the results were for this type of negligence, there has to be some criminal liability attack.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So how could she still have charges for involuntary manslaughter? Now, in a scenario we don't know if this is true, where she was responsible for putting that live ammunition in there, if that were to be the case, then she would have a role in it, certainly. He was holding the gun when it fired and hit her. Si how could Gutierrez be guilty or have charges and not Baldwin at this stage?\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Great point. There's an intervening factor, and that is the hall, which is the individual. David Hall, who was the assistant director, he's the one that handed the gun to Alec Baldwin and said, it's cold, meaning that it's fine. You can use it. There isn't anything that could cause any sort of danger. But you're absolutely right. If it went from just Hannah Gutierrez Reed to Alec Baldwin without this intervening step, yes, for sure, that involuntary manslaughter could be resurrected. At the end of the day, their prosecutors are still going to look into it. They're still going to examine that gun to make sure that not what it seems to be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As you say, there's a woman who lost her life and her family doesn't have her anymore. So we'll see where this goes. Mercedes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for this Friday. I will see you, though, on "THE FIVE" tonight. Look forward to seeing you then. "YOUR WORLD" starts in a few seconds. The Dow is up 32 points. I'll see you in the next hour.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230422ej4l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Space Race; All Charges to Be Dropped Against Alec Baldwin?; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  (R-PA); Green Push; Border Surge; Supreme Court to Rule on Mifepristone; Interview With Rep. Carlos Gimenez (R-FL); Biden Likely to Announce Reelection Bid Next Week\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Matt Finn, Nate Foy, Andy McCarthy, Aishah Hasnie,  Chad Pergram, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 7,962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Ready, set, go, Joe?\par \par \uc2 Well, the president is set to announce his reelection bid early next week, we are told. Now, the White House, for its part, is keeping fairly mum on exact plans. The president announcing just as \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2  is showing a solid 14 percent worth of support in a potential 2024 matchup against the president.\par \par \uc2 We're on top of all of that and the race for endorsements on the Republican side, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  racking them up against Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , even in DeSantis' home state. Who will Florida Republican Congressman Carlos Gimenez be throwing his support to? He's going to tell us in just moments.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 First to Peter Doocy at the White House, how they're planning for that big announcement without saying it's a big announcement -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, while President Biden has long said he intends to run for reelection, the imminent announcement of a campaign for a second term may be a surprise to some folks who paid attention to his campaign the first time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, I view myself as a bridge, not as anything else. There's an entire generation of leaders you saw stand behind me. They are the future of this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: About three out of every four adults polled by AP don't want President Biden to run again. And among Democrats, he's also underwater. Just 47 percent want him to run again. And that's, again, his own party.\par \par \uc2 Something the president is doing effectively, though, consolidating the field. He doesn't have any elected Democrats challenging him in the primary at the moment. In fact, one of the Democrats rumored to be considering a challenge over the last year, Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , is now all in on Biden/Harris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): The Biden/Harris ticket is -- are so important for the future of the nation. Remember that, just in two years, President Biden has gotten so much done.\par \par \uc2 And that's hard to do in a 50/50 world old, where you need to get votes maybe on the other side of the aisle, and he's been able to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Answers about this at today's White House press briefing were nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary said the Hatch Act prevented her from answering anything political and she was not able to tell us who could answer questions about Tuesday's announcement -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy at the White House.\par \par \uc2 To Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill right now with a Republican battle for endorsements. Early as it is, a candidate is racking them up, and another candidate is not -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  once led former President Trump, but now Trump is thumping a prospective field of 12 GOP contenders. A Wall Street Journal poll of 600 likely GOP primary voters shows the former president at 48 percent, DeSantis 24 percent. Republicans kept Trump at arm's length in 2016, but not now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): I will be asking everybody to support President Trump, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hasn't declared he's running for president yet.\par \par \uc2 I think he's a great governor of Florida. So, if I were Florida, I would give DeSantis a third term and everybody get behind President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Former President Trump secured the endorsements of 59 House and Senate members, 10 from Florida. Only one member from Florida supported DeSantis, freshman Laurel Lee.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis spoke in suburban Washington today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): If the Democrats are able to sweep all the offices, president, Senate and House, what will they do with that power? They will try to pack the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . They will try to abolish the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 They will try to make Washington, D.C., a state, so they get two left-wing senators virtually for life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will support the GOP nominee, but he feuds with Trump.\par \par \uc2 After Trump's indictment, reporters tried to get a response from McConnell. The leader had been absent for weeks after a concussion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Let me put it this way. I may have hit my head, but I didn't hit it that hard.\par \par \uc2 Good try.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Mr. Trump met with Florida GOP members who endorsed him at Mar-a- Lago last night. Trump appears obsessed with racking up endorsements on the home turf of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of which and whether another home turf congressman will join that support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with us now, Congressman Carlos Gimenez, who is kind enough to take time to let us know where he's going and where he's heading and who he's supporting.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, who is it?\par \par \uc2 REP. CARLOS GIMENEZ (R-FL): Well, I'm announcing that I will be supporting President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 I think he gives us the best chance of winning. He has -- he had a solid track record as president. And, yesterday, at the -- at the dinner, he laid out a solid plan for the future and where we need to go, America needs to go to restore our prominence in the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, did you let both candidates know that that was your preference?\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Yes. Yes, I did, before I -- before I announced today.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the president knows, but also Governor DeSantis knows exactly who I was going to support.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And what did the governor have to say to you?\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Well, I think the governor understands.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the governors done a great job in Florida. There's nothing -- I'm not -- it's not against Governor DeSantis. It's for President Trump. And so I'm supporting President Trump, not that I don't like Governor DeSantis. Again, I think he's doing a great job as the governor of the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 But I think President Trump is better suited and better prepared to be president of the United States and better prepared to lead us in the direction that this country needs to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The fact that so many have gone this way, and the former president is racking up an unusually high number of endorsements, particularly in Florida, of course, that's a message to Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 What do you think that message is?\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Well, I think the message is that the president is very strong, as a big portion of the base is with the president, and a lot of lawmakers from the state of Florida, especially congress men and women, are throwing their weight behind -- behind the president.\par \par \uc2 We see the same things. I think, yesterday, at the dinner, what we saw, what I heard was what I wanted to hear and what I wanted to see. And so, after the dinner, it was evident to me, as I went home with my wife and we spoke about it, that we need to throw our support behind President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Look, we need to -- we can't have another four years of President Biden. That would be disastrous. So, whoever the nominee from the Republican Party is, I will throw my full weight behind them. But, at this point, my favorite to get the nomination is President Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are many in your party, as you know, Congressman, who are dead set against giving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  another try.\par \par \uc2 They mention the indictment, for one thing, and maybe potential other indictments to come, which could make him a damaged nominee. Now, obviously, you had a chance to consider that, assess that, and you seem to have dismissed all that.\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Yes, I have, because most of the stuff that's coming down, I think is politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the first was completely politically motivated. And so, yes, I discount that. And I think a lot of Americans are seeing it the same way, that the president has been treated unfairly, not only during his time as president, but even at this time after being president.\par \par \uc2 There are things that he's being indicted for. Like, you know, President Biden had supposedly classified material that he had when his time as senator, for God's sakes, for years and years. Where are the indictments?\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the American people are sick and tired of what -- what's -- what is indictable for one person is not indictable for another. How you treat one member of one party is not the way you treat a member of another party. And I think we need to stop that. And I think that President Trump will stop that.\par \par \uc2 So I think he's being treated unfairly. And, yes, I accounted for that. And I think the American people are seeing through this. And then there are other things that are coming now about the Biden -- Biden family and their probable -- money being given to them by foreign adversaries and all that that's somehow being pushed under the rug and all that.\par \par \uc2 So, again, it's inherently unfair. And I think that the president deserves another -- another shot at being president and taking us down the road where he had us the first four years, which was a stronger military, stronger economically, much more respected around the world than we are today.\par \par \uc2 We're the laughingstock of the world right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, not all the potential Republican candidates running for president or thinking about it have signed on to the notion of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading your party.\par \par \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  had said that that would be a tough one to swallow. He has said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only prominent Republican who would lose to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Others have said that they couldn't sign onto a pledge that calls for him being the nominee and accepting him as the nominee.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Well, look, it's -- we have differences of opinion, obviously. But, at the end of the day, like I said, whoever's the nominee, I will throw my full support behind that nominee.\par \par \uc2 And I would hope that other Republicans that are saying right now that they won't support President Trump will throw their support behind President Trump, because what's the alternative? The alternative is going to be President Biden.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, by the way, President Trump has not promised to do the same.\par \par \uc2 If another candidate emerged as the nominee, he has not pre-committed to supporting that nominee. Does that make a difference to you?\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: Well, I don't think that he hasn't said that he won't.\par \par \uc2 So, look, whoever is the nominee, I will be supporting.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: And every -- all Republicans need to get behind the nominee that is going against President Biden.\par \par \uc2 We cannot, America cannot take another four years of President Biden, the - - his disastrous policies at the border, economic policy. We're a laughingstock around the world. Heck, China won't even answer his phone call, for God's sakes, all right? And so -- and I believe that he's a compromised president.\par \par \uc2 So, no, America can't survive that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GIMENEZ: We all need to rally around whoever is the nominee.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, my support is 100 percent behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, we will watch very closely.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for sharing that with us. Good news for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 In fact, endorsements at this stage of the game are closely scrutinized. But are they the impact that you think they would have? For example, if you go back to 2016 and the year before, when it looked like Jeb Bush was the prohibitive favorite, we know where that went.\par \par \uc2 He had 85 primary endorsements, against only 31 for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Meanwhile, in the case of Hillary Clinton, during the 2007-2008 emergence of the election, the governors of Maryland and New York and so many other prominent players were rallying around her.\par \par \uc2 We only raise this to say that those who look like prohibitive favorites by just the sheer number of support they're getting from prominent party luminaries, that too can be subject to change.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack with us right now on that, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that, Bob, and the significance, certainly, of the endorsements \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting in his home state? It's actually both gentlemen's home state, if you think about it, Florida. But, for Governor DeSantis, this has to be a bit of a jolt. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, THE HILL: Oh, I think it's definitely a jolt, Neil. And you made some news there.\par \par \uc2 I was just e-mailing our newsroom to say that the congressman is backing Trump over DeSantis. That is significant, not because voters say, OK, this congressman or this senator backs this candidate. I'm going to follow them.\par \par \uc2 That rarely happens. But voters like a winner. And Trump's campaign, he had a tough 2022, but, in 2023, he's come back, and he's run a better campaign, and he's snaring these endorsements. And he's got momentum. And that's why I think he's leading in the polls.\par \par \uc2 And again, voters like to vote for the winner. And, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is winning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Polls can change, as you have often reminded me. I remember talking even at the time when Jeb Bush looked like a sure thing as the nominee in 2016. That didn't turn out that way, of course.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder if a lot of this activity we see on behalf of the former president isn't built on these very strong poll numbers he's getting, even through this indictment, a first for former president. It's rallying the base, so to speak. Others are saying that can't last. We're already seeing some signs that it might be difficult for it to last. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Well, listen, I have heard that this can't last a lot with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dating back to 2015.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: And, without a doubt, Jeb Bush had the establishment behind you. And, sometimes, you really don't want the establishment.\par \par \uc2 And some of these national polls, Neil, I mean, you got to look at the early states. That's going to shake things up. DeSantis' numbers in some of these early states are a little bit better than the national numbers. But at the same time, he's going to have -- some Republicans think he's going to have to announce soon, and he's going to have to start trading punches more effectively with Trump, because Trump has gone after him on policy, on Social Security, on Medicare, on ethanol in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Those punches have landed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I'm just wondering for DeSantis, who's getting sort of picked apart and is having these troubles -- and he hasn't formally announced -- would that -- I know he has to wait until the end of the legislative session, but would that speed up the timetable here?\par \par \uc2 Because this is doing some damage.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: It is.\par \par \uc2 And I think it might speed up the timetable a little bit, because they just can't keep going like this. And the danger here is, remember, in Iowa, Hillary Clinton finished third. There's a danger of maybe a third. Nikki Haley or Asa Hutchinson, maybe they get some momentum. And so he's got to turn this thing around.\par \par \uc2 It's not easy. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a great campaigner, but they're going to have to come up with a new game plan.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I want to get your thoughts real quickly on the challenge now for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's the prohibitive favorite, being, of course, the sitting president of the United States within his own party.\par \par \uc2 But he does face sort of a bold-faced challenge on the part of Robert Kennedy Jr. And that guy's already polling at 14 percent. Now, the president's way out ahead in that, and most Democrats, even though they're not keen on him being their nominee, seven out of 10 of them would support him in that case if it came to an ultimate contest.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of RFK's appearance in the race and how that could change things?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Well, we're hearing that the president's going to announce his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 I think these Democrats who don't -- they don't scare the White House, don't scare the DNC. But, at the same time, in that poll, one out of three Democrats are supporting somebody else, or they're not sure. That's a troubling sign for President Biden. So I think that that's why that has been somewhat of the impetus of them saying, OK, we got to get into this race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Always learn a lot, Bob. Thank you, my friend. Have a safe weekend.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Bob Cusack on all of that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, a reminder that \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . will be my special guest to discuss his challenge as the president of the United States. Not since 1980 have we seen a Kennedy challenge an incumbent president. Of course, it was \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  then who took on \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 ? We all know how that went. He didn't quite get the brass ring. And that was a tough year for Democrats, losing in a landslide election when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  won in a romp. Could that happen again?\par \par \uc2 Again, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 . tomorrow 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 We will have a lot more coming up, including the big wait for that Supreme Court decision on an abortion pill used by more than 60 percent of women in this country -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We are still waiting.\par \par \uc2 Could be any minute, any hour, certainly by late tonight. We are told the Supreme Court is going to come down with a decision on this abortion pill, whether it's going to allow it to continue to be used or make a snap decision whether to forbid it. There's a whole wide range of possible outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy, the former assistant U.S. attorney, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 What are we looking at here, Andy?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Neil, it's hard to say because we were here, it seems to me, like on Wednesday, where we were hotly anticipating a decision, and then we ended up not getting one. The court gave itself more time.\par \par \uc2 So there's no guarantee it won't do that. It could issue a ruling tonight. We will just -- there's no -- we don't really have a way of -- they're not bound by any particular deadline, so there's no way of predicting whether they will impose one on themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Would the most draconian or blanket decision be just forbidding its use?\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: It would. I think that's highly, highly unlikely, though, because the Fifth Circuit decision, which reviewed the district court's decision -- it was the district court that said that the -- mifepristone should be taken off the market because the original approval of it did not comply with federal law.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit said that, even though the judge might have been right about that, there was such a profound statute of limitations issue with that, that it was going to, for now, assume that should not be the case.\par \par \uc2 And what they decided was that what should have is, we should turn the clock back to 2016. So, mifepristone, the abortion pill...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: ... would still be available, but the safeguards that were originally put in place, but were stripped away in 2016, would be reimposed.\par \par \uc2 And if I had a bet, I would think the Supreme Court would lean in that direction, rather than anything as draconian as you're referring to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The other issue is, does this -- a decision like that put it at odds with the decision that prompted all of this, the Dobbs decision, which effectively -- you're the lawyer, I'm not -- but left it up to states to decide and rule going forward.\par \par \uc2 Yet, with these pills, they're available nationally, so you could have a national way to continue abortions, the very thing that the court wanted to stop with the Dobbs decision. But help me through that.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: Yes, well, I'd say a couple of things they wanted to accomplish, Neil.\par \par \uc2 One, and the paramount one, I think, was to return the issue to the democratic process, meaning to have it decided by the political branches, whether at the state level or the federal level, rather than the unaccountable -- politically speaking, unaccountable courts.\par \par \uc2 Whether something gets decided at the federal level, as opposed to the state level, is complicated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: I'm of a mind that, for 50 years, people who opposed Roe v. Wade -- and I'm one of them -- argued to the Supreme Court that this was a state issue.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean that there isn't a federal component or couldn't be a federal component. And let's remember that, when Roe was decided and people first started to make the argument that this was a state issue, we didn't have the kind of technology that we have now...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: True.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: ... which not only was available, but can now be shifted into state commerce. So it's a very different world now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So another possibility I wanted to raise real quickly with you is whether the court rules let the states decide whether they want these abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: I don't think they will be able to stave that off, Neil, because eventually they're going to have to grapple with that issue.\par \par \uc2 The issue I think that's immediately in front of them right now is whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  complied with federal law in the actions that it took, both approving mifepristone in the first instance and then expanding its availability by stripping away the safeguards that were originally put in place.\par \par \uc2 So I consider this -- and maybe I'm being too narrow here, but I'm looking at this case, as did the \uc2 FDA\uc2  comply with federal law?\par \par \uc2 And that doesn't mean that they necessarily have to grapple at this point with what you point out is a very complex and probably necessary question, which is, how do we resolve this crash between something that is traditionally the subject of state regulation, but because of the way we now live and do commerce, is something that maybe needs federal regulation, because it really is -- these pills are in interstate commerce?\par \par \uc2 They clearly are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Man, whatever decision comes out, it's going to be hotly debated.\par \par \uc2 A. MCCARTHY: It sure is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that could be moments for now. We will see.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy, thank you, my friend. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 All right, meanwhile, at the border, you know we are less than three weeks away from Title 42 going away. Title 42 is alive and well now, but they're having a lot of problems now.\par \par \uc2 And doesn't Nate Foy know it at the border -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, you got that exactly right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats actually in South Texas today touring border facilities just one day after a group of 1,000 migrants tried rushing the bridge behind me. We will show you how officials dealt with that and how they're preparing to deal with it again, with Title 42 set to end -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, forget about now entering the space race. Has China taken a lead in it, not only with its own space station that itself we're told is a marvel, but technology that can knock our satellite technology right away?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the countdown to Title 42 going away and the threat at the border, especially on the Mexican side of the border, where thousands are sort of converging, waiting for that moment.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Nate Foy in Brownsville, Texas, with how all that is going down.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Hey Neil.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats are touring border facilities here in Brownsville and also in McAllen today. But it comes just one day after 1,000 migrants rushed the Gateway International Bridge that you see behind me. Some migrants that were processed today tell us that that happened because of ongoing frustrations with the CBP One app.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video of the group as they tried crossing, Texas DPS responding, as well as local police, CBP as well. The bridge was shut down for hours because of this. And you can hear migrants in the video say they're trying to apply for asylum on the CBP One app, but they say it's not working.\par \par \uc2 They keep getting kicked out. Migrants also say cartels are coming into their camps and kidnapping women and children. That leads to the desperation that causes this. But to prepare for this, Neil, take a look at this next video from Eagle Pass. You see border protection officers prepping for bridge rushes like we saw here in Brownsville, as well as El Paso last month.\par \par \uc2 And the border, as you mentioned before the break, expected to get busier and busier leading up to and when Title 42 expires coming up on May 11. But as for that congressional delegation, take a look at these pictures. Democrats on the House Homeland Security Committee are touring those border facilities here today. They told FOX there is no media availability.\par \par \uc2 This comes one month after these same Democrats bailed on a field hearing that was supposed to be bipartisan. Both sides accuse the other of playing politics. One more final video for you, Neil. Take a look at this chase from Texas DPS in Kinney County. Troopers arrested the driver and the passenger here for smuggling migrants and having a stolen gun.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, nobody was hurt in this chase. And back out here live, Neil, the chief of Border Patrol, Raul Ortiz, releasing new numbers today. He says agents have apprehended over 41,000 migrants in the past week alone and confiscated over 33 pounds of fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Nate, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Brandon Judd will be my special guest tomorrow on "Cavuto Live" 10:00 a.m. Eastern time to talk about what's building at the border here, he, of course, the Border Patrol Union president. Look forward to that and getting a sense of the enormity of this problem as we tick away the days, now less than 20 before Title 42 just disappears, and then everything else could be reappearing.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the latest on Capitol Hill with the push to go green spending a lot of green, and I mean a whole lot of green.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has the latest -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Democrats are talking about more and more spending as we get closer and closer to this big vote next week on House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's debt limit package. We will tell you what the White House said about him today, as a growing number of Democrats are saying, look, President Biden, just sit down and talk to the guy.\par \par \uc2 It's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a lot of green to go green.\par \par \uc2 And it's proving to be very popular among one party, not so among the other party. I want you to take a wild guess how that breaks down.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about the Green New Deal. Yes, you thought it was over. No, it's back again. We will get to that in just a second. But I want to talk about this spending showdown that is happening where Democrats and House Republicans are so far apart on spending, how they feel about it.\par \par \uc2 So a growing number of House Democrats are calling on President Biden to sit down and talk with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy ahead of this big vote on his debt limit package next week. But here's White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  earlier today. Listen to what she said about McCarthy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: While President Biden is fighting to clean up toxic pollution and lower costs through clean energy tax credit, Speaker McCarthy unveiled a ransom note holding our economy hostage.\par \par \uc2 It's a 320-page plan to kill jobs, fill our cities with smog, and give asthma to our children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Asthma to our children.\par \par \uc2 OK, so McCarthy would obviously disagree with that. He says his plan would actually raise the debt ceiling by $1.5 trillion with a promise of $4.5 trillion in savings over the next decade. How? Well, he's trying to claw back on all of the president's spending over the past couple of years, those student loan bailout, money for new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, and, of course, all those green subsidies tucked away in the Democrats' Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The president said he wanted a plan. We put forth a plan to limit, save and grow this economy.\par \par \uc2 We will repeal this extreme Green New Deal. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  actually says that this is going to cost, the plan that the Democrats passed, three times as much money as proposed beforehand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, Neil, what do the Democrats do, progressives, at least?\par \par \uc2 They reintroduce their $50 trillion to $90 trillion Green New Deal package, and they say it is a matter of life or death to pass it now -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wild. There's plenty of money there for everything, so I don't know what the worry is.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah, thank you very much. I don't know how you put up with it, Aishah. I just don't know how you do it.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  joins us right now, the Pennsylvania Republican, on trying to find middle ground here, because there are those who are trying to do that, by the way. He's with the Problem Solvers Caucus, in fact, the chairman.\par \par \uc2 Chairman, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I don't know where the middle ground is here, sir. I do know that Democrats immediately didn't like Kevin McCarthy's plans, so it doesn't look like that that's going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 What are you and your Problem Solvers Caucus, what are you coming up with?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Yes, thanks for having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We actually introduced our own framework. It's a two-party solution. We have 64 members, as you know, Neil, 32 Republicans, 32 Democrats. We believe in two-party solutions. And we advanced a framework, much like we did with infrastructure, where we operate in phases.\par \par \uc2 With infrastructure, step one was to define it. With the debt ceiling, step one was the framework, which involves guardrails around discretionary spending, a temporary suspension of the debt ceiling, but coupled with a commission, an outside commission, much like Simpson-Bowles, that will study the long-term sustainability of our debt and deficit and programs and force us to put that bill on the floor for a vote, an up-or-down vote, when they conclude.\par \par \uc2 That's where the -- that's the centrist model right now. You're going to see a lot of volleying back and forth between the left and the right. But I do believe where this ultimately lands, Neil, by necessity, because we have a Democrat Senate, a Republican House, a 60-vote filibuster threshold.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: So it's going to be a two-party solution. We already know what's going to land there. We might as well start thinking that way now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have a lot of great ideas. I do like this idea of a commission to offer a proposal to reduce the deficit.\par \par \uc2 What I didn't understand, Congressman, is whether each proposal to reduce the deficit is put up for an up-or-down vote and how that goes forward.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, the commission, so it would be an outside entity...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: ... no currently elected members of Congress. So you take politics is completely out of it.\par \par \uc2 And they're just using facts and figures and numbers and making sure that we have a healthy economy. There's two things that we're pushing here, Neil, because you're only getting one side or the other, depending on who you're talking to.\par \par \uc2 We cannot default on our debt. That is out of the question. We can't allow our markets to spiral. We have to make good on our bills. That's true. There's also something else that's true, that having a 100 percent debt-to- GDP ratio is unacceptable and unsustainable. That's happened two times in our nation's history, World War II and now.\par \par \uc2 And that threatens the long-term sustainability of our economy. China is already racing ahead of us in so many different metrics. And this is -- this is something that we all have got to come together on. It shouldn't take a cataclysmic event like 9/11 or a debt crisis to bring America together.\par \par \uc2 We got to get ahead of it before it happens.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the other issue that's come up to me, I was shocked to hear that we and Denmark are the only countries on Earth that have a system like a debt ceiling or where you broach it and, all of a sudden, you have got to scramble and do something.\par \par \uc2 Why do we have that? Why do we need to do it this way? Is there a way to avoid it? Now, some people say, well, at least it keeps a check on spending. Of course, if that were the case, we wouldn't have so much spending.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So it doesn't seem to be doing that. So why can't we just get away from this whole debt ceiling Kabuki theater stuff?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Amen, Neil.\par \par \uc2 My personal proposal was to go right out the 1917 law that established the debt ceiling as a numerical dollar amount and change it to a debt-to-GDP ratio. That's a metric that everyone can rally around. It's something they can liken to their own home personal budgets. And it's something that people can measure as a metric of economic health of America.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, that would put both revenues and expenses on the table. And everybody's got to give a little, just like our personal relationships, just like we operate our businesses.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Come to the center and find consensus. I think that's where the solution is here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, you make way too much sense. I don't know what you're doing in Washington, but we will see what happens. But I kid to make a compliment to let you know that that is promising that there are people trying to find middle ground here to get this to work.\par \par \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 , the Pennsylvania Republican, heads up the Problem Solvers Caucus, equal number Democrats and Republicans trying to solve problems, not create them. There is a wacky concept.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the latest on Alec Baldwin and having all charges now dropped, or about to -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, for Alec Baldwin, it could be all charges dropped.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Matt Finn in L.A. -- Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, lots of twists in the prosecution of this case.\par \par \uc2 The two newest special prosecutors have now dropped the involuntary manslaughter charges against Alec Baldwin, but say he still might not be entirely off the hook, writing in part -- quote -- "Over the last few days and in preparation for the May 3, 2023 preliminary hearing, new facts were revealed that demand further investigation and forensic analysis in the case. This decision does not absolve Mr. Baldwin of criminal culpability and charges may be refiled."\par \par \uc2 The L.A. Times reports the new information in this case is that the prop gun Baldwin had in his hand when it fired and killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the "Rust" movie set might have been modified, potentially increasing the odds the gun misfired, which is what Baldwin insists happened.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin's attorneys writing: "We are pleased with the decision to dismiss the case against Alec Baldwin, and we encourage a proper investigation into the facts and circumstances of this tragic incident."\par \par \uc2 Now, the involuntary manslaughter charges against the film's armorer, Hannah Gutierrez Reed, have not been dropped, and that case is moving forward -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: (AUDIO GAP) that, Matt.\par \par \uc2 To Mark Eiglarsh right now, the criminal defense attorney.\par \par \uc2 Mark, what do you make of this? I mean, prosecutors were gung-ho and they looked like they had a compelling case that could have landed Alec Baldwin in jail for years. What happened?\par \par \uc2 MARK EIGLARSH, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: Well, justice happened.\par \par \uc2 Forget about how you feel about Alec Baldwin. I know a lot of people don't like him. This is a justice -- this is justice for everybody. Any time someone is overcharged or the evidence doesn't fit, it's a wonderful day when prosecutors, when they feel the pressure, still back down and drop charges.\par \par \uc2 And, in this case, they did not have proof beyond a reasonable doubt that he committed a criminal offense. That's it. Now, could he still be sued civilly? Sure, go right ahead. We're talking about liberty. And they did the right thing, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, what happens?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if everyone is exonerated -- it's possible not everyone will be. Then what?\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: Well, listen, not everybody.\par \par \uc2 Somebody already took a plea. They accepted responsibility.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: And Alec's role is very different than the armorer, who had a specific role in ensuring that the gun was safe. Alec is an actor. And, yes, he is the executive producer, so there's a civil responsibility there.\par \par \uc2 But it's not his job to analyze every prop. He had people to do that, including a safety specialist who oversaw this. Those people somehow failed at their job. It's not a criminal offense as it relates to him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just wonder. You're the lawyer, Mark, but it looked like prosecutors were overzealous and they really had it out for him. And then there was a pile-on.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And then it seemed to unravel bit by bit. How did that happen?\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: Well, first of all, the initial prosecutors, who aren't the ones who dropped this, charged him with a five-year minimum mandatory, a statute that had been overruled, that should not have ever been done.\par \par \uc2 They should have spent more time reading the statute books and less time putting on makeup for their appearances. They were all over the news saying things about him. Go check the statute. So they had mud on their face when they had to drop that.\par \par \uc2 OK, now you're not facing a five-year minimum mandatory. It's just an 18- month sentence. And then, when the evidence was really looked at by new prosecutors, they said, you know what, I don't think there's enough here. Again, there's a difference between mere negligence and criminal negligence, which is a willful and wrong disregard of human life or property. That does not exist in this case.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And dragging this guy through the mud for nearly two years. Just amazing.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Mark Eiglarsh. We will see how it all goes down. We should know fairly soon.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, knowing that China is a big space player in -- well, in space is nothing new. The degree to which they are taking over space, well, that is new.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SHATNER, ACTOR: I wonder the lesson they learned was, oh, shoot, we shouldn't have done that. Would that have been a lesson?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know. I mean, you learn a lot, right? They're going to have to get a lot of things right. This time, there's all sorts of pieces to it.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I went down...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you knew that was a -- you knew -- that was a risk when you went up into space. Anything could have happened. It didn't, thank God, but that's a risk.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Hydrogen -- I don't know what kind of gas they use, but on the rocket I went up in, they used hydrogen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: And as I was entering the rocket, all those documentaries about the Hindenburg burning, you know what was burning, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Hydrogen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I felt for you just climbing the stairs all the way on that gantry to get into the rocket itself. Why didn't they have an elevator for you?\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: At 5,000 feet, I'm climbing 11 flights.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know.\par \par \uc2 This thing that went, that blew up, is a -- it's sad, and yet that's science.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I mean, science is full of experiment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, William Shatner watching that with interest. Of course, we're talking about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's rocket that blew up four minutes takeoff yesterday. But they did learn a lot from that.\par \par \uc2 We are learning a lot about what China is doing when it comes to intraterrestrial travels because some of these leaks have told us that they're moving along at a pretty brisk pace, some say the pace far faster than ours.\par \par \uc2 Gordon Chang with us right now on the significance of that.\par \par \uc2 Gordon, it's good to have you because some of the revelations, besides just the capacity they have, China, for a supersonic drone that can travel at three times the speed of sound, an international space station. In their case, they're the only nation, so it isn't inter. It's just their national space station.\par \par \uc2 The fact that they now have the capability of knocking out enemy satellite guidance and have that technology today, what are they up to?\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, AUTHOR, "THE COMING COLLAPSE OF CHINA": Well, they believe, and they're right, that in the first moments of a war that will be fought in space, where we will try to take down their satellites, they will try to take down ours.\par \par \uc2 And they probably will move first, Neil, which means they will be more successful than we will be. And another thing, we just don't understand the maliciousness of the Chinese system, so they could really take us by surprise.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: One of the things they have is this technology that can disrupt other technology, in other words, the ability of our technology to communicate with other forms of technology and monitor troop movements and the like, sort of like what we do in Ukraine right now.\par \par \uc2 If they could jar that, stop that, prevent us from getting information on that, that's a huge advantage.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Yes, it certainly is.\par \par \uc2 And if we go back to the Obama administration, there was a political decision made not to try to defend our satellites, because we reasoned that we had more assets in space and that if we started a race to protect satellites, we would be the losers. We wanted to try to entice China and Russia into not defending their satellites.\par \par \uc2 Well, that just misunderstood the Chinese and the Russians. And so now we're behind, and we are vulnerable in space.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, meanwhile, they're going it alone to the moon. They have made great progress, we're told. They might beat us there in the second wave of astronauts to get there. We could always say, of course, we did this more than 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 But that being said, they're active on the far side of the moon, so we're not aware of exactly all they're doing. But their plans on the moon are much more sweeping than what we're doing. Should we worry?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Chinese space officials started talking about how the moon and Mars should be considered part of the People's Republic of China and that they would exclude others from going there if they had the capability to do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, we say that that's ludicrous, but we didn't listen to \uc2 Usama bin Laden\uc2  either.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do we now have any way to counter what China is doing now, in other words, any more economic sanctions?\par \par \uc2 Or is it that we're afraid of implementing them because they're going to hurt us?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Yes, well, we have a lot of ways to go after China, which right now is in distress. It has an economy that they said grew 4.5 percent in Q1, but probably was a lot less, because we know that it contracted in January and February.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, economic sanctions. There's all sorts of things we can do in space. But we have decided as a nation not to do those things and to watch the Chinese overtake us. And that is a strategic failure that will be historic. The American people ought to demand the Biden administration start racing to the space as the Chinese have.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed. And they are racing along.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Gordon.\par \par \uc2 A reminder, we will be exploring this tomorrow on my Saturday show, along with \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr\uc2 ., taking on the president for the Democratic nomination. All that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230421ej4l0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hearing Today In Manhattan D.A. Suit Against Jordan; Speaker McCarthy Unveils House GOP Debt Limit Plan; Rep. Wesley Hunt (R-TX), Is Being Interviewed About Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's Lawsuit; Bragg Sues To Block GOP Subpoena In Trump Case; Supreme Court Extends Access To Abortion Drug; Supreme Court Extends Access To Abortion Drug; Lawmakers To Simulate Chinese Attack On Taiwan; Man Arrested In Shooting Of Two Texas Cheerleaders; \uc2 EPA\uc2  Admin Grilled Over Biden's Electric Car Push; D-Day Veteran Joe Cattini Dies At 100\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Sandra Smith, Gillian Turner, Peter Doocy, Katie  Pavlich, Robert Wolf, C.B. Cotton, Andrew McCarthy, Mike Huckabee, Shannon  Bream, Jonathan Turley, Mike Emanuel, Laura Ingle, Grady Trimble \par \uc2 8,691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I must say, Sandra, they might have doubled the height but they didn't narrow the bars so the toddler doesn't have to climb. Just has to squeeze --\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: They're still pretty narrow though.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They are, yes, I certainly could get between.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Unbelievable. OK, I can't believe it. Two hours flew by John, as we head on into the latter half of the week. Great to be with you. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We'll see you tomorrow, Thursday, Friday eve -- "THE STORY" with Gillian Turner in for Martha, starts right now.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you, John and Sandra. Good afternoon from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Martha MacCallum. Today on THE STORY. House Judiciary Republican Congressman Wesley Hunt joins us as Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's lawsuit against Congressman Jim Jordan, heads to court. Plus, Chairman of the House Select Committee on China, Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  joins us on the plan to wargame out a response to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 First, though, President Biden speaking in Accokeek, Maryland now where he's laying into House Republicans and what he calls their MAGA economic vision. This comes as Speaker McCarthy unveils his debt limit plan and the White House accuses him of dangerous economic hostage taking. We've got Katie Pavlich and \uc2 Robert Wolf\uc2  standing by, but first good White House Correspondent Peter Doocy on the North Lawn. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): And so, President Biden and Speaker McCarthy are not talking to each other, they are kind of talking at each other, both within the last hour. We've been hearing for weeks here at the White House, that President Biden's idea about the debt ceiling is simple: just raise it. Republicans did it during Trump, just do it again. Whereas Speaker McCarthy has a three-point plan that he announced minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I'm proud to announce that we are introducing the Limit, Save, Grow Act of 2023. First, we limit government spending. Our proposals also repealed Biden's army of 87,000 IRS agents. Now, that will save taxpayers $70 billion dollars. Finally, we will grow the economy. So, we are less dependent on China.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voiceover): White House officials are not commenting yet on a report that tax revenues on Tax Day this week were less than expected which could bring the debt limit sooner, and that's as polls prove the public is not buying much of President Biden's pitch.\par \par \uc2 69 percent of people polled by CNBC say they have a negative view of the economy. President Biden talks about the economy all the time. Why, why aren't people buying it?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He talks about the economy all the time, because he wants the American people to know that he's doing everything that he can to make sure that he's, that his policies that he's put forward that has shown that he's building an economy that doesn't leave anybody behind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And just as the show was coming in at the top of the hour, President Biden in Maryland blamed recent economic struggles and the bad feelings on Putin and the pandemic, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Peter Doocy on the North Lawn of the White House this afternoon, thank you. Let's bring in now Katie Pavlich, Editor of Townhall.com; and \uc2 Robert Wolf\uc2 , former Economic Adviser to President Obama, both are Fox News Contributors. Katie, so McCarthy's plan now which President Biden says he doesn't have lowers the deficit by one and a half trillion dollars through a series of cost cutting measures. He's targeting things like green energy, like the \uc2 IRS\uc2  spending, he's targeting clawing back some COVID money that the government never spent. What do you make of his proposal?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, the proposal to take back billions of dollars that was allocated for the COVID pandemic, given that President Biden has signed legislation ending that federally makes a lot of sense. McCarthy has been asking President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for months to meet on this issue, to come to the negotiating table. He had a first meeting with President Biden at the White House in January after he won the speakership. President Biden said at the National Prayer Breakfast that he was interested in having a second meeting, that has not happened.\par \par \uc2 Now, it'll be interesting to see what the White House does in response to this because their negotiation at this point has been, we don't want any strings attached at all. So, McCarthy has offered this plan to lift the debt ceiling, which is what the White House wants, with some strings attached when it comes to spending. Now, President Biden has also said that he's a bipartisan guy, he wants to work with Republicans. This seems like a pretty good deal for the White House, given that a lot of Republicans don't even want to vote for more spending, given the country is reaching $32 trillion in debt and climbing while inflation continues to stay at very high in separate levels.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Robert, the pushback from Democrats against McCarthy's plan is that it targets -- they say it targets the most vulnerable Americans: the elderly, the very young with snap cuts, things like food assistance cuts and the like. What do you make of that criticism?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I mean, Gillian, thanks for having me on. I think the debt ceiling debate is silly. It's been passed 78 times in the -- since 1960, over 60 percent with a Republican president, it was passed 18 times, with under President Reagan, three times under President Trump. We have to pay our bills. It's a full faith and credit of our nation. And so, this is about money that has already been spent. With respect to what Katie said, and, and Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 I'm all for having a tough budget discussion. We're all concerned about the deficit. We're all concerned about a fragile economy, where people are talking about inflation in a recession. But those are two separate things. The idea that we're going to tie what Speaker McCarthy wants to the debt ceiling is absurd. He didn't do it under President Trump, and they certainly didn't do under the past presidents. It's just political arguments going on, because that's what we do as a country.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But Katie, is that true? I thought that the fastest way to have more money is to save more money. The McCarthy plan?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, well, look, the bottom line is that for either party in Washington, if they want to actually address this $32 trillion problem, someone's going to have to propose spending or legislation that takes on reforming Social Security, and Medicare. Those are the things that are driving the debt. In the meantime, Republicans in Washington are the only ones willing to look at any kind of spending cuts. And when it comes to the billions of dollars with the COVID funding, that's actually not even a spending cut, that's simply just taking money that's already been allocated, getting it back, and spending it on something else, something in this proposal too is really just capping a number of these programs at spending levels of last year, rather than increasing spending.\par \par \uc2 In D.C., that's considered a spending cut rather than simply just staying within the budget from the previous year. So, when it comes to the principle here, it's good that Kevin McCarthy and the Republicans are negotiating to try and do something and to say, yes, it had -- the debt ceiling has been raised a number of times since 1960 and that's why we're in $32 trillion of debt. When you count interest, that's more than $100 trillion. In the United States, the full faith and credit is not going to be taken seriously. On the world stage, if Americans continue to, their government continues to spend like this with no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Robert, to that point, the President himself in some instances has tied the debt ceiling argument to government spending. He talks all the time, including, again, today about how the wealthy need to pay their fair share, the obvious insinuation being that they're riding on the backs of the middle and lower classes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes, listen, I'm all for having a debate on deficit and government spending. I think it's interesting that under President Trump, 25 percent of all spending in the history was done under President Trump $8 trillion in a four-year period. As, as we know, revenues were not -- didn't go anywhere near expected because we actually reduced taxes at probably the worst time possible. So, listen, I'm all for having a real debate. I've been more fiscally responsible than where most conservatives have been. So, let's have it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Well, guys, thanks so much for taking time with us this afternoon. We got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Talk to you both soon.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Courtroom showdown is now underway in New York City. Manhattan's District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is suing to block a subpoena from the House Judiciary Committee. Congressman Wesley Hunt serves on that committee. He's here along with Andy McCarthy, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right now, a courtroom showdown is underway. Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is suing to block a subpoena from House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan. Bragg says, Jordan is trying to intimidate him and interfere with the indictment of former President Trump. House Judiciary Member Congressman Wesley Hunt and former Federal Prosecutor Andy McCarthy are standing by, they'll join us next. First, though, we go to correspondent C.B. Cotton, she's live at the courthouse in New York City. Hi, C.B.\par \par \uc2 C.B. COTTON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, good afternoon. We're just learning that the judge will issue a ruling in writing. This comes after today's hearing where she gave both sides 30 minutes to make their case. Now, Mark Pomerantz is slated to testify before a congressional committee tomorrow about his time as lead Trump investigator in the Manhattan D.A.'s office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voiceover): House Judiciary Committee Chair Republican Jim Jordan wants Pomerantz to talk about the office's investigation into Trump. Pomerantz resigned from Bragg's office in 2022, frustrated that the Manhattan DA didn't bring charges against Trump at the time. He went on to write about this work in a book: Jordan and other Republicans have criticized the Manhattan D.A. since the grand jury indicted the former president arguing Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is focused on getting Trump instead of being tough on crime. Bragg has called this criticism a political stunt.\par \par \uc2 And his effort to block Jordan subpoenas, Bragg filed a lawsuit requesting a restraining order saying, "The subpoena to Mark Pomerantz is an unfounded and unconstitutional attempt to disrupt an ongoing state criminal prosecution." During Monday's House Judiciary Field hearing here in New York City, Jordan urged the court to block Bragg's attempt to hamper the Congressional investigation that he argues is within the committee's power. Now, as for Pomerantz, he agrees with Bragg and says the subpoena is meant to impede and interfere with Trump's pending prosecution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): Back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thanks, C.B. Let's go ahead and bring in Texas Republican Wesley Hunt. He serves on the House Judiciary Committee. Sir, in his suit filed last week, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  says that the committee you serve on is mounting a political intimidation crusade here. He's also, his attorneys in their latest filing say this. The subpoena is part of a plan to intimidate, harass, retaliate, and hold accountable D.A. Bragg for enforcing New York's criminal law against a then-New Yorker Mr. Trump. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 REP. WESLEY HUNT (R-TX): \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is a woke, Soros funded D.A. that's more interested in downgrading felonies to misdemeanors than he is at taking care of the people of New York.\par \par \uc2 In that field hearing, we heard the stories of these Americans that have been ripped apart because of the crime that we -- that we've seen running rampant in New York City because of his failed policies.\par \par \uc2 And we're seeing the same failed policies in all these major cities across the United States, and it's because of liberal days, just like him that do not want to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: OK. Respectfully, what does that have to do with the constitutionality of the subpoena from the Judiciary Committee?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, I think, what we tried to do was we're trying to convey what the point is here. And I want to talk about what the number one demographic for gun -- for rising gun ownership in this country is, and is actually black women.\par \par \uc2 We hear the testimony of a woman named, Ms. Brame, who's -- whose son was killed in New York City, and she still just wants justice.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're trying to do is point out that he's not worried about doing his job. He is more worried about going after President Trump instead of doing the job, and that's keeping the people of New York City safe.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, but Congressman, he is on the receiving end of a subpoena from the committee on which you serve.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So --\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, do you believe that it's a constitutional subpoena? Let's start there.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, first of all, he did use federal money to investigate President Trump. So, then -- you are now involved in the federal government, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, it's actually our job to keep the American people safe. So, we, as congress men and women on the Judiciary Committee have a job to do to the American people. And that's to point out when there's malfeasance like this.\par \par \uc2 When you have D.A.s that are unwilling to uphold the law, and then use federal money for investigations that have nothing to do with downgrading felonies to misdemeanors, that's our job and that's what we had the field hearing there.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, Mark Pomerantz, who is also on the receiving end of this, he's filed this on Monday. Take a look. He says, "I believe the Committee seeks my testimony not for any legitimate legislative purpose, but rather to impede and interfere with the D.A.'s Office's ongoing work, assist Mr. Trump in his defense, probe my political views, and harass me because I was the author," of a book that Mr. Trump and some of his supporters did not like.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Did the authoring of that book play into Chairman Jordan's decision to issue the subpoena in the first place?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, it's clear that Pomerantz influenced Bragg, and it's clear that Pomerantz has a vendetta against President Trump, and that is the evidence behind this whole charade.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is -- this is kind of our point. This is the weakest case that we've actually seen brought against the president -- of -- by a D.A. in New York, a former president, excuse me, or Trump by a D.A., and it's just wrong.\par \par \uc2 It's just because he has a vendetta against President Trump. We were pointing that out. That's literally the reason why we're in New York City.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Thank you, sir, for taking time with us today. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Thank you so much. God bless you. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Let's go ahead and bring in Andy McCarthy now.\par \par \uc2 He is former federal prosecutor as well as National Review, contributing editor and Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Andy, I believe you got the chance to listen to the Congressman there.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I do.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What did you think?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, it's a shame, I think, where we are. We're -- this is an exhibition on both sides of some theater. There are good points to be made. But at the same time, you know, it wasn't long ago that if someone was in Congress, on Fox, being interviewed and make it -- saying that the committee gives federal money or Congress gives federal money to the states, and therefore, you know, we have oversight authority over the states.\par \par \uc2 You would have assumed that was a democratic argument. I mean --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The question you asked is, is this constitutional? You know, it actually Congress has no general oversight authority over the states. But there are aggressive arguments that if they take federal money there, the Congress is entitled to examine where the federal money goes.\par \par \uc2 And there's an even more aggressive argument that under the 14th Amendment, the states have to give their citizens due process and equal protection of law, and that empowers Congress to legislate and to enforce that provision, the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 But those, Gillian, don't have always tended to be, you know, progressive arguments. Republicans used to be pretty strong on federalism.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, it's an interesting point at which -- moment at which we find ourselves in the midst of this legal fight.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: One of the arguments that the judiciary committee has been making - - actually, Andy, I'm going to ask you to stand by for a moment we have to bring in some breaking news.\par \par \uc2 This just announced, the Supreme Court is extending access to a drug used and most abortions that happen in this country more than 50 percent of them until Friday.\par \par \uc2 This gives the justices more time now to consider arguments over whether the medication, it's called Mifepristone, should stay on the market or not.\par \par \uc2 This comes after conflicting rulings from lower courts that have been ping ponging back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Andy, I'm going to ask you to do a pretty hard shift here, if you don't mind. What's your reaction to this?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The Supreme Court has decided to extend the access to this abortion drug get again through Friday, it sounds like they're giving themselves another extension to make a bigger picture, longer term decision. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think it underscore -- Gillian, I think it underscores that this is really complicated. It's not a -- you know, if you hear that political debate over this, it's, you know, over whether the drug is safe or not, which is obviously an important concern.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that justices have to go through is whether the Administrative Procedures Act was complied with, in the way that they took away some of the restrictions over the use of the abortion pill. And it's a complicated procedural history that goes back to the turn of the century.\par \par \uc2 So, if they've given themselves another 24 or 48 hours to work that out, I wouldn't blame them for that. It's pretty complicated stuff.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Does it in any way indicate which way they might end up ruling on this?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: It does indicate to me at least that they're looking very hard at the way the Administrative Procedures Act was flouted, at least in the second round of the later round of regulation.\par \par \uc2 So, I suspect that what may happen here, and this is just, you know, we're spit balling here, because we haven't gotten anything to read.\par \par \uc2 But I suspect the pill will be -- will continue to be available. But you may run the clock back a few years and put back some of the protections that were removed during the Obama-era.\par \par \uc2 If I had a -- if I had a guess that, that was what they were wrestling with, it would be that.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What about -- it's also the reality that during COVID, so over the last three years, more and more women have been obtaining this drug electronically after virtual visits with their physicians, and then, using it at home.\par \par \uc2 Does that weigh into the judges' sort of decision making on this? It is one of the biggest issues playing out sort of culturally, I don't know if the judges take that into account.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, yes. Yes, it sure -- it sure --\par \par \uc2 Gillian, I feel like we're having Constitution Day here. It's, you know, amazing. The threads that run through all this. But I think, you know, the problem is you can't -- you -- in today's world, we can send these things across state lines. But traditionally, these kinds of health issues are state issues.\par \par \uc2 So, you have the crash of you know, the federal regulation and the fact that a lot of these states are putting in more restrictive laws.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right. OK. Well, Andy, thanks for sailing that with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Sure. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We'll have you back soon. You bet.\par \par \uc2 More on this breaking news out of the Supreme Court in just a moment with Jonathan Turley.\par \par \uc2 Also, ahead this hour, Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , chairman of the House Select Committee on China, will talk to us as his group prepares now to conduct a war game to plan for a possible Chinese attack on Taiwan. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Back to our breaking news now. The Supreme Court extending access to an abortion drug through Friday, as the justices continue to consider arguments in the ongoing legal battle over the medication, it's called Mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , former Republican Arkansas Governor and presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like the Supreme Court is kicking the can down the road, not -- in a pejorative sense, but they're giving themselves another two or three days to figure out the big picture here. How they want to move this forward?\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Wouldn't be unusual for them to want to wait and see, you know, can they come up with a better solution and maybe the one they've kicked around so far.\par \par \uc2 I think the bigger issue that Americans really are going to have to adjust to is whether or not we want to focus on creating a safer environment for babies are a more dangerous one.\par \par \uc2 And that really comes to be the crux. It wouldn't that many years ago, the Democrats platform on abortion was that it should be rare, safe, and legal.\par \par \uc2 I haven't heard a Democrat use those three words in 20 years. They now talk about it being unrestricted right up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 So, this -- the discussion, we need to have it, it needs to be a serious win, not a yelling back and forth, but an honest assessment of what we think about human life, and when it begins, and whether or not we treasure and value it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, politics aside, Governor, it is the case that today, more than half of the abortions that are take place in this country every year happen through this drug, a lot of them are sort of done at home, they so- called at home abortions, there are clearly plenty of physicians out there who feel the drug is safe enough to prescribe it to women.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: A lot of physicians too. But then, that there are a lot of physicians who think it's perfectly safe to perform a surgical procedure, in which one of the patients is absolutely going to die, namely a surgical abortion.\par \par \uc2 The bigger issue, we don't know over a long term, what the effects are, and there are complications that can come from this drug.\par \par \uc2 The bigger issue still remains at what point do we decide as a culture and as a society, that every life -- every human life has purpose and value and worth?\par \par \uc2 Or do we believe that some lives are expendable and disposable, just like we would say that certain things in our homes are disposable and expendable.\par \par \uc2 Are their lives that are just not worth living, not worth preserving? That really gets to the crux of it. And the method of which we disposer expend the baby is really secondary to the bigger issue of whether we think the child has no value.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I mean, the reality of this case that's before the court today, Governor, I don't think they are taking that broader issue --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: No.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: -- into consideration, right?\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: No. No, they're not.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I think this is really a limited case where they're looking at the efficacy and the safety of this particular drug on the women who take it.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: And I think, to be fair to the court, their job is not to legislate. Their job is not to determine what should be their job is to determine what is, what the law actually says not what the law should say. And frankly, I'm glad the court is not trying to legislate. That's been one of the problems that the Supreme Court has gotten itself into over the last 75 years. It decided that it knew what should be done, the legislature didn't have the courage to do it.\par \par \uc2 So the court took it up and just decided, here's how it's going to be. So I don't fault the court for taking this in a very limited way. I fault the bigger political discussion for not getting down to the meat of the matter, which is, at what point do we deem that some individual human beings are simply not worth the fight.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Do you still have faith, governor, in the workings, in the goings on, in the process of the court in the light of everything we've seen happen over the last couple of years? The justices themselves have been the focus of a whole lot of criticism, protests, some of it violent. How do you feel about all that?\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: I would be probably only able to say that I think sometimes they get it right, sometimes they get it wrong. And why should I be surprised? They're human beings. I'm not perfect. I don't expect them to be. We can't afford a perfect quarter of nine people who do it right every time.\par \par \uc2 What I would love for them to do, though, is to stick to the duty they have, which is simply to interpret the laws that have been passed, not tried to create laws that the legislature never had the courage to create. And when the court gets into legislating, when it gets to decide what should be, I think that's when the court has overstepped its bounds, and that's what we can't really have or afford as a court.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Governor, we got to leave it there but thank you for pivoting with us. Really appreciate it and for sharing your thoughts with us, as always.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: Thank you, Gillian. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: With me now is Fox News Sunday host Shannon Bream. Shannon, the court could have made a ruling today appears they decided not to this as a sort of remedial or interim measure. They're buying themselves some more time with this. Are you surprised?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS SUNDAY HOST: No, because I think it shows that they're actually taking this very seriously. They know how much is at stake here. They've heard from all of the stakeholders in this conversation about whether this pill should be available. And remember, it's just for the time that the case underlying that's actually on the merits would be ongoing. That could be months, that could be years.\par \par \uc2 So you can understand how people who want access of this to remain on the market are asking this court don't step in here and put a hold on this. Let the case play out, however long it takes, and leave this on the market for women who rely on the access to it, we know that more than half of abortions that are done in this country are done through this medicated abortion process. So I think the court gets the heaviness of this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was just a year ago that they were getting ready to overturn Roe v. Wade. And I think they want to as much as possible, avoid ever looking like they're getting into the politics of a situation. And abortion, as you know, is just one of the most heated.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We're just learning now that Senate Democrats have announced on the Judiciary Committee, they're going to hold a hearing of their own on the 26th concerning the fallout over this. The concern for Democrats or a lot of the concern revolves around the idea that if you restrict access to this pill, a whole lot of women who don't have access to abortion in-person now won't have access to this. It's a limiting -- it's a severely limiting effect.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And that's been the argument of not only the maker of the drug because, of course, we would expect that they would go to the court and say, please leave this on the market, but the DOJ. So the Biden administration, in its own arguments last week when it went to the court to say, hey, please keep this on the markets. They talked about the regulatory chaos and the limits that would come, as you said, for a lot of people if they didn't have access to this.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, there was an interim decision from the Fifth Circuit. We have that Texas judge who said, the pill goes away, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn't approve it correctly. It went to the Fifth Circuit who said no, we're going to allow the pill back onto the market but we're going to reinstate some of the safety regulations that were stripped away about in-person doctor visit required to get the pill that you can't send it through the mail as a dispensing --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And you can even -- more than that, as I understand it, correct me if I'm wrong. You can have like a tele medicine appointment with your doctor so they don't see you physically, and then they prescribe it to you, receive it by mail. There's no human contact.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That's sort of the underlying mechanism here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. So the Fifth Circuit, put those restrictions back into place. You've got to see a doctor in-person, this can't be shipped through the mail, that kind of thing. So this court could do any number of things. They could go back to that Texas ruling, which I suspect they probably will and say listen, we can't take this thing off the market for a year or two while the legalities play out. But we won't know until Friday at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Interestingly, Shannon, I was reading earlier that this same drug is available in more than 60 countries around the world. Is that something that factors into the judge's calculations? Meaning the idea that other countries around the world have deemed it safe for at home use or they have to set those kinds of facts and context decide when they make this decision?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. For the most part, the Supreme Court disfavors looking to other countries for their decisions about these kinds of things. And this is strictly at this point, a \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulatory issue. The argument against this that the one that the Texas judge agreed with was this wasn't done properly. This drug has risks like any drug that's on the market, and it was not handled and approved properly. And then the safety regulations were stripped away starting in 2016. So there were a lot of arguments on all sides about whether this is a good or a bad thing, or whether it should be on the market or not, so we got to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is the court I'm glad they're giving it a couple of more days. It sends to me the message that they're taking this briefing very seriously. And they know as they always do the important whatever decision they make.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And we'll be leaning heavily on your stream court expertise, I suspect, over the next couple of days. Shannon, if you could just stand by for a moment. I want to go ahead and bring in constitutional law attorney Jonathan Turley. He's also a GW law professor and Fox News contributor. Jonathan, what do you make of this interim measure?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that this makes a great deal of sense. This is an issue of great importance to the entire nation. The use of this type of medication for abortion represents over half of the abortions in the United States. So you're talking about a decision that is going to have very direct, immediate and national impact. And the court is proceeding with circumspection, which is what it needs to do.\par \par \uc2 The arguments that the pill itself is a health risk, is a hard argument to make. It has been around a long time. The medical profession overwhelmingly supports its safety, but there's a lingering issue here of how changes were made in making this pill available, for example, without seeing a physician.\par \par \uc2 The lack of procedural regularity, particularly in the second round of changes, is going to give some of these justices pause. You know, for them, it's not a question necessarily of the merits, in terms of the risk of this medication as to whether it was in fact promulgated as a rule in compliance with federal law. So in some ways, the Fifth Circuit decision may be attractive to some of the justices. The Fifth Circuit allowed this drug to be continually made available, while adjusting some of these changes and while it's looking more closely at them, like the need to consult with a physician.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, one of the claims made in the suit is that the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , these pro-life groups say the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has been ignoring essentially, risks and dangers stemming from use of the medication since it was approved back in 2000. Is that going to be adjudicated in this case or not really?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, on this type of expedited review, the court is not necessarily going to do a deep dive into the merits of things like risk. I think they're going to be primarily looking at how this rule was promulgated by the federal government, and whether they met all of the requirements of notice, and to have comments including those opposing these changes. There is some merit there as to how this was handled under the APA.\par \par \uc2 So the Supreme Court has really not just a momentous but also a maddening decision to make here. They may agree on the merits of the safety, but they may have some reservations as to the means.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. And it is certainly a high stakes cases the White House has been pointing out every day now for weeks. Jonathan, got to leave it there. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, just hours from now, the House Select Committee on China will meet to conduct a whole series of war games to prepare for the possibility that China may invade Taiwan. The chair of that group, Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , only on "The Story" coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: How should the US respond now? Should China decide to launch a full scale invasion of Taiwan? That question is what members of the House trying to select committee will try to figure out in war game exercises slated to start just hours from now. The committee hopes to give lawmakers a real sense of how a conflict would unfold, where US military weaknesses may lie and showcase the potential global consequences at stake.\par \par \uc2 In just a moment, Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , Chairman of the House China Select Committee, joins us. But first we go to Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi, Gillian. Yes, some key lawmakers conducting those WarGames tonight to help them think through what could happen if China invades Taiwan. US officials are clearly concerned about China potentially launching an attack on Taiwan to force unification. A Pentagon expert in charge of that region of the world told lawmakers about the value of WarGames\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN AQUILINO, US INDO-PACIFIC COMMAND COMMANDER: About learning and understanding vulnerabilities, strengths and helps you go forward and figure out how to adjust and what you might need. So when you go into this event, I hope looking at it in the same way. With regard to the munitions piece --\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice-over): Another senior Pentagon official responsible for the Indo-Pacific Region talked in a congressional hearing yesterday about a key area of focus.\par \par \uc2 JEDIDIAH ROYAL, ASSISTANT DEFENSE SECRETARY INDO-PACIFIC SECURITY: I will say that invasion is neither imminent nor inevitable. And the work that we're doing in the department every single day is to focus on deterrence. And we do that by increasing our lethality, our posture and our readiness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But Aquilino told lawmakers, the Pentagon and industry need to move more quickly to limit the odds of war over Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AQUILINO: It doesn't matter what the timeline is. The secretary given me this mission today, so I'm responsible to prevent this conflict today. And if deterrence were to fail to be able to fight and win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That's why some are calling for moving heaven and earth to enhance deterrence and denial. So Chinese leaders conclude, Gillian, they cannot do it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: No. Mike Emanuel, thanks so much for joining us appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Let's bring in Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  of Wisconsin. He's the Chairman of the Select Committee on China, also a member of the Intelligence and Armed Services Committee as well as a US Marine veteran. Sir, I think, you know, the biggest question on American minds when it comes to this is, what is the US military prepared to do should this scenario unfold, should China launch some kind of an invasion on Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): Well, the goal of course, is to deter China from launching the invasion. One of the obvious lessons from the failure of deterrence in Ukraine is that when it breaks down, it's very costly in terms of lives, and blood and treasure. And so, we are conducting this war game in order to prevent a war.\par \par \uc2 I think members of the committee, Republicans and Democrats, are committed to peace and stability across the Taiwan Strait. But peace can only be achieved through strength. And particularly since \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  appears to be preparing his country for war, I'm sure he preferred have conquest without war. We need to be ready and we are not moving fast enough.\par \par \uc2 So tonight, we hope to learn what we can do in a bipartisan fashion to enhance deterrence, so the Pentagon never has to face that choice.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, President Biden's top military advisors, General Milley and General Austin, both recently said, in their own ways, that they think it's time to tone the rhetoric down when it comes to the possibility of an imminent invasion. Are you concerned that these WarGames are at odds with that guidance?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm not. In fact, I think we do ourselves no favors by minimizing the scope and scale of the challenge we face. And every time Secretary Austin or General Milley goes out there and says, well, you know, it's unlikely that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is going to do this in the next few years. I actually think that undermines our ability to force industry to do things like start building the long range precision fires we need, that we need to surge west of the International Dateline.\par \par \uc2 And so, the most important thing we need to do is talk with clarity about this problem so that we can galvanize actual action. If we're so afraid of provoking the Chinese, the thing we fail to realize is that weakness and hesitation is itself provocative.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And the stakes are very high as well all know. Sir, take a look at this out in The Hill today, an opinion piece. While many of game tower conflict might materialize on the battlefield, surveying the disastrous impacts of the homeland in the masses within it is not widely known. This includes the food we buy, the cars we drive, the prescriptions we take, and the energy we use.\par \par \uc2 All of this clearly, congressman, a threat to us national interest. Are these war games going to touch on these risk factors as well?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We're very much hoping to incorporate the economic and energy component into the war game tonight, which is something we rarely do. From a defense perspective, we tend to focus solely on the hard power aspect when we know that the Chinese Communist Party is likely to weaponize pharmaceutical supply chains, for example. They've actually threatened to do that in the past.\par \par \uc2 And I think what that will reveal is the fact that we're just unexpectedly dependent upon China economically. We need to take some steps to reclaim our economic independence. So that in the event of a conflict, they're not cutting off the export of life saving drugs. That's part of what we're trying to do on the Select Committee on China.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Where do you see Congress' biggest potential role here, the biggest contribution that you and your colleagues might make?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: In the short term, I think we can figure out a way to draw down the backlog of weapons that Taiwan has purchased. But we haven't delivered. There's $90 billion dollars worth of weapons that haven't been delivered. It's not going to happen overnight. But the more we talk about it, the more we put pressure on the department, the more we can fix that.\par \par \uc2 The second thing I think we can do by changing the way we buy weapons and allocate money for Pentagon to buy critical munitions is start to refill our stockpiles of key things that are just severely depleted right now. Hard power will help us deter war.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Last question for you, sir. You are part of the delegation that met with Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  when he was in Washington yesterday, paving the way for a 2024 presidential runner up closer to endorsing him?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well I was -- I really enjoyed the governor's remarks. I think he's done a great job in Florida. And my goal is to talk with the 2024 prospective candidates about how we beat the Chinese Communist Party and what I believe to be an existential competition. So I'm glad he came here. I thought it was a very productive discussion. I'm going to focus on the committee's work and I'll leave the 2024 presidential politics, people that are smarter on that stuff.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Well, thanks for taking time with us. We hope you'll come back after the war games to let us know what lessons you learned from them. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, a Texas man is now in custody accused of shooting at two cheerleaders critically injuring one of them after one mistakenly got into his car, that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Two cheerleader shot after one trying to get into the wrong car in a parking lot. She says trying to apologize when the gunman opened fire. Now the suspect is in custody. Senior correspondent Laura Ingle reporting live from New York. Hi, Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi there. You know, this is an incredible story of survival and another case of being in the wrong place at the wrong time when these two cheerleaders who were switching vehicles in a parking lot was struck by gunfire. 18-year-old Payton Washington and 21-year-old Heather Roth were carpooling with two other cheerleaders Monday night. They were returning home from practice when Roth mistakenly tried to get into a car she thought was hers.\par \par \uc2 Now this all happened about 25 miles east of Austin. And after seeing that there was a man inside this other car, Roth says she panicked, got back into her friend's vehicle and tried to apologize when the man approached her. Her cheer coach just told me that's when he opened fire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LYNNE SHEARER, WOODLAND ELITE CHEER CO-OWNER: And the guy got out and he kind of raised his hands, and they saw that he had a gun. And he started shooting at the car and they tried to speed off as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 INGLE (voice-over): While Roth was only grazed with a bullet, Washington was shot in the back of the leg. Her coach says, the miracle is that Payton who was born with only one lung wasn't hit there, but other organs were damaged and her spleen was removed. Police were able to track down the suspect and arrested 25-year-old Pedro Tello Rodriguez Jr. who has been charged with deadly conduct and is being held on a $500,000 bond. Police say additional charges may still be filed.\par \par \uc2 And Roth, Washington and other teammates were practicing for a cheerleading competition this weekend. The girls coach says Roth still plans to compete while Washington will have to watch from her hospital bed. We'll continue to follow this one. Back to you, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Wow. Laura Ingle reporting live from the New York Newsroom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGLE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is facing opposition from Capitol Hill Republicans over a new push to force more electric cars onto roads across America. It's just a proposal right now and if approved, it wouldn't even kick in for a few more years, but it is generating some pretty explosive feedback. Fox Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports live from Capitol Hill. Hey, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. And today's hearing was the first time \uc2 EPA\uc2  Chief \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  faced lawmakers questions about that proposal the strictest ever tailpipe emission standards and Regan is defending them. Of course, the goal of that proposal is to try to get more Americans behind the wheels of electric vehicles, but it's not just conservatives who are saying now that the Biden administration is overstepping its authority.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): I think this is absolutely ridiculous that we're going to force the public in the market to go in a direction they might not be ready to go to in the time and say want to do it fast enough. You know, we're paying people $7,500 then to entice them to buy a vehicle. And then on top of that, they said well, we'll just go ahead and outlaw rollout to combustible engine. That's crazy.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice-over): Separately, GOP lawmakers are pushing back against the Department of Energy. Its proposed new gas stove rules that could block the sale of about half of the gas stove models on the market right now over safety and climate concerns. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  chief on the other hand, seemingly contradicting the Biden administration's Department of Energy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK ALFORD (R-MO): Are gas stoves save for the American people? Are they safe for the environment?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2 , ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 EPA\uc2 : I'm sure my mom is watching this hearing and she loves her gas stove and she seems safe to me.\par \par \uc2 ALFORD: So your mom would vote for keeping gas stoves in America?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: She sure would.\par \par \uc2 ALFORD: All right. I'll take that as a good answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Now, today's hearing was in front of the House Agriculture Committee, so a lot of the questions centered around clean water rules that conservatives say would detrimentally impact farmers and ranchers. But there were still lots of questions about electric vehicles and whether the administration is forcing Americans to purchase them even if they're not ready to. A lot of questions about gas stoves as well, Gillian, and lawmakers are now wondering, on the Republican side, what will the Biden administration target next. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Grady Trimble on Capitol Hill for us this hour. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of the last living veterans to survive D-Day has now passed away. Joe Cattini was not supposed to be in Normandy that day in 1944. He was a British military driver. At the last minute, an officer grabbed him to drive an ammunition truck as part of the invasion. He told the Times of London, some of what he saw. He describes bodies in the sea and on the beach. He talks about troops getting sick and crying out for their mothers.\par \par \uc2 His son said his dad made it through the war "relatively unscathed." Joe Cattini was one of about 25 surviving veterans of D-Day. He passed away yesterday in England at the ripe old age of 100. God bless him and his entire family.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" of today Wednesday, April 19, 2023. But as always, "The Story" goes on. We'll see you right back here tomorrow at 3:00 PM Eastern. Now Your World with Neil Cavuto starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230420ej4j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2  (R-NE); Leak Suspect in Court; Travelers Facing Sky-High Airfares; Border Battle; Police Officer Attacked in New York City; Interview With Rep. Lance Gooden (R-TX); Trump and DeSantis Battle For Endorsements; Interview With Rep. Michael McCaul (R- TX)\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexis McAdams, Connell McShane, Bill Melugin, Ted Williams, David  Lee Miller, Rich Edson, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, don't look now, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are at it again.\par \par \uc2 One of these guys is raising a lot more dough and the other is securing a lot more endorsements. Both are crucial, but who really has the overall edge?\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to former Trump official Ken Cuccinelli, who's launching a political action committee, not for his former boss, who's already running, but the Florida governor he is pushing to hurry up and get running.\par \par \uc2 Then, fair and balanced, meet the guy who's not so DeSantis-smitten, Texas Congressman Lance Gooden. He met with the Florida government -- governor in Washington and right afterwards announced that he was going with team Trump.\par \par \uc2 Team "Your World" starts right now. Glad to have you, everyone. Welcome. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it right now with Rich Edson in Washington on this grand old fight picking up in the Grand Old Party -- Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They're already picking sides, and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has made no official declaration he's even running for president.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is on a national book tour promoting the idea that what he's doing in Florida is a blueprint for the rest of the country, his message again through a few stops in South Carolina today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): While there's a lot of pessimism in the air for what's going on in Washington and our country, my belief is that decline is a choice. I think Florida shows that success is attainable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: DeSantis swung through Washington yesterday and met with more than a dozen Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 A source with knowledge of the event says the room was filled with conservatives. Excited to hear from DeSantis. Another Republican in the room says they were intrigued by the governor's D.C. visit and hear what he wanted to say.\par \par \uc2 They told us DeSantis made time for brief one-on-one conversations. Florida Representative \uc2 Laurel Lee\uc2  announced her endorsement yesterday, joining Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  and Chip Roy in backing DeSantis. Then, Florida Representatives John Rutherford and \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2  and Texas' Lance Gooden backed former President Trump, joining dozens of other House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Polling averages show DeSantis largely trailing Trump, though ahead of other declared or expected Republican candidates. Trump is largely focused on DeSantis, repeatedly criticizing the governor on social media over his administration's fight with \uc2 Disney\uc2 , writing this week -- quote -- "DeSanctus is being absolutely destroyed by \uc2 Disney\uc2 . This is also unnecessary, a political stunt."\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is expected to announce his presidential decision after Florida's legislature wraps its session next month -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Rich, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Ken Cuccinelli, of course, a former official in the Trump administration, but now using a political action committee he has just kicked off to help \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Earlier, he was with me on FOX Business to explain his strategy and why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEN CUCCINELLI, FORMER ACTING DEPUTY SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the most successful Republican chief executive we have seen in decades in America, and he's done a spectacular job in Florida, as demonstrated by his winning record without ever backing down on his conservative principles.\par \par \uc2 He won over almost by 20 percent in the largest swing state in America. He had the biggest coattails we have seen in a long time, right down to school boards, and nobody else who's looking at running for president can say that or anything like it.\par \par \uc2 So we hope he gets in this race and believe he is the best opportunity for the future of America starting in 2025.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ken, has your old boss gotten back to you on this and called you a traitor?\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: No, it hasn't gotten personal with me, even though he's gotten personal with Governor DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: And I explained in short terms that I thought DeSantis was the best choice. It was a polite, civil exchange, but via text. But that's it. And, otherwise, because I believe \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did he text you back? Did he text you back on that, Ken?\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: No, not after that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: Not after that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: He initiated. But -- and I have found that across the country.\par \par \uc2 I have been traveling to states all across America, and ordinary voters, conservatives, Republicans, independents are -- they can't wait for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to get in this race. They are so impressed with what he's done in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And when we tell them the rest of the DeSantis story, his personal story, working his way through college, winning and having those coattails that they don't necessarily know in the rest of the country, they get very excited to support a DeSantis for president campaign that we hope will arise some time in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, because the poll numbers don't bear that out. Of course, it's very early, as you and I have talked about these kind of things at different times.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even, oddly, after the indictment, the first ever for a former president, is as popular as he's ever been in the party.\par \par \uc2 That might be short-lived, to your point. And also to your point, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is raising a lot of money, I believe north of $30 million, supporting a presidential run, if he ever decides to pull the trigger. Do you have any idea when he will pull the trigger?\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: I don't have any more idea than you do, Neil. He's obviously working hard in the Florida session and getting an awful lot done.\par \par \uc2 You all were cataloging the battle with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . And, frankly, Republican voters want to see a fighter. And here we have the best conservative, principled fighter in America who's also the most electable potential candidate.\par \par \uc2 And continuing the fight with \uc2 Disney\uc2  and winning the fight is important across the country, as we, unfortunately...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Why is it important, Ken? Why is it important? I know what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: Well, let me get to that. That's a great question.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But think about it. He's fighting \uc2 Disney\uc2 , and I understand over this whole don't say gay thing. I get that. But \uc2 Disney\uc2  is a big state employer. A lot of people have fond views of \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And this is sort of messing up he's -- is he pro for a big employer in his state? Is he not? Is it muddying the waters? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: Well, look, so he didn't start taking on \uc2 Disney\uc2  until \uc2 Disney\uc2  started interfering with the efforts to protect children in schools in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to kind of look back to the beginning, but when \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  engaged, he did not back down. And you all were reporting on some of the maneuvers \uc2 Disney\uc2  is trying. And Governor DeSantis keeps on fighting. And we're so used to seeing Republicans roll over when they get challenged like this. It is a breath of fresh air for Republicans and conservative grassroots folks across the country to see someone actually keep up the fight like this all the way through to winning.\par \par \uc2 And it's unfortunate that others who are already in or looking at getting in the presidential race are on the other side. They're on \uc2 Disney\uc2 's side.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: And this cultural fight, and that is what it is, is important to a lot of voters because they understand it's really about the values that make America a great country.\par \par \uc2 And only one candidate, only one governor has ever protected America against the scourge that \uc2 Disney\uc2  has become in the last couple of decades. The fond memories people have that you referred to of \uc2 Disney\uc2  are from a long time ago. And \uc2 Disney\uc2  has become more of a force to undermine good, healthy, positive, patriotic American culture...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: ... than it is a supporter of it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know, Ken. Their parks are still packed, right? People are wall to wall there. So it's not everyone.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: That's all right. I mean, he got a 20-point reelect in Florida...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, fair enough.\par \par \uc2 CUCCINELLI: ... with everybody seeing this fight going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, fair and balanced now, endorsements and those who are picking up those endorsements, very different story.\par \par \uc2 Ken Cuccinelli, of course, a former Trump administration official, now supporting \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . But the fact of the matter is, far more prominent Republicans at this early going are supporting the former president than toying around with the idea of even considering \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and that despite the Florida governor heading to Washington this past week.\par \par \uc2 He had a chance to meet with Congressman Lance Gooden of Texas, the Republican,after that powwow, though, opted to come out and say he's on team Trump.\par \par \uc2 And he joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, good to have.\par \par \uc2 REP. LANCE GOODEN (R-TX): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Your endorsement came after you had a chance to meet with Governor DeSantis. I think you had said you hold no -- no issues with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . You just like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  better for the White House.\par \par \uc2 Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 GOODEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but I wasn't going to just endorse him out of loyalty. I wanted to do my due diligence for this next time around. I wanted to visit with the serious 