ark Green, said that DHS' budget proposal request for 2024 is -- quote -- "an insult to Americans."\par \par \uc2 He said that's because DHS wants to spend $3.9 billion for a -- quote -- "climate resilience program," but only spend tens of millions of dollars on fighting fentanyl -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 To Representative Michael McCaul right now. The Texas Republican was part of that grilling process, in fact, a pretty heated grilling, at that.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, what came of this today?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Well, I think what you're seeing is the anger from the American people through their representatives.\par \par \uc2 They're really sick and tired of this border being completely open. And he claims he has operational control, but his chief Border Patrol agent says that he does not. I grilled him on the fact that, from day one, they rescinded the Migrant Protection Protocols, remain-in-Mexico, which the Trump administration put in place that was actually working.\par \par \uc2 When they rescinded this, Border Patrol told me it had a direct cause and effect to opening up the border wide open. And Mayorkas said they did that because of the unjustifiable human cost. And I went down a litany of, well, can you justify five million encounters? Can you justify 100,000 people dying in this country from fentanyl's kind of pouring across this border, not to mention the 100 suspected terrorists, to the migrants who died in 18-wheelers in my state?\par \par \uc2 And it just goes -- it goes on and on and on. And I think the arrogance that he can't even recognize how bad the situation is really defies reality on the ground. And I have been doing this for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I know. And you're pretty -- a sharp questioner.\par \par \uc2 I think the issue comes up, though, from you and your colleagues what to do with Mayorkas. Now, many of them have said he's got to go. But I have a feeling that his boss, the president of the United States, doesn't want to let him go, and that, in fact, Mayorkas is actually doing the president's bidding, even if he might not entirely agree with the lack of attention that he's showing at the border.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think you're absolutely right. He knows better.\par \par \uc2 I have known him when I was chairman of Homeland Security. He was a U.S. attorney like myself from a border state. He gets this. He's smart enough to know this. He's just -- he's misrepresenting this to the American people, and he's just following orders.\par \par \uc2 He won't defy any of this stuff. If we could renegotiate the remain-in- Mexico, which my bill coming out of Foreign Affairs Committee will mandate next week, we could get ahold on this problem. But they -- and they know it would work, but they don't really seem to care.\par \par \uc2 And it's the arrogance that's really -- that's where you're seeing the anger coming from the members. And we're getting it from our constituents. Me particularly, my home state of Texas has gotten the brunt of this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I would imagine, in your state, it's going to get a lot worse. We're three weeks away from Title 42 going away. It's bad enough as it is with Title 42 in place.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of these cases that have now been adjudicated on the Mexican side of the border will now come to our side of the border. What are you hearing about how many come over and how these numbers could surge?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, that's right, because, before this administration, they'd have to remain in Mexico pending their asylum claim. Only 15 percent qualify.\par \par \uc2 But now they come into the United States. We don't have detention, so now we have catch and release, and they're let go. So, I asked the secretary. Five million encounters at the border since they came into office. What in the world are we going to do with five million people that have no legal status?\par \par \uc2 It's the biggest human trafficking event in my lifetime. And the younger girls and women are going to get swept up into sex trafficking. And then the younger males, their home, their family will probably be gangs like MS- 13.\par \par \uc2 This is going to create a generational problem for us here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know you were in Taiwan last week, the week before. You probably heard that, right now, between our military drills and China now having a permanent presence around Taiwan, not to the degree they were when they had practically an armada circling the island, but there's very little wiggle room here for something to go -- or risk going really bad.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that it is going to go that way, that there could be a trigger event? And what do we do in the event that there is?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, that's always a concern.\par \par \uc2 And I was there, Neil, when the armada circled the island...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: ... 10 battleships, one aircraft carrier, 90 fighter jets in an act of bravado, intimidation.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. What they're doing when they do these game -- war game exercises is, they're testing the capability and vulnerability of the Taiwanese. But all it takes is for one thing to go wrong to escalate this to a higher level of tension. And, as you know, that's how bad wars get started.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think deterrence is key here, and we got to get the weapons that I signed off on three years ago in there, so that we don't have a war and a conflict with communist China.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, you have been a busy fellow of late.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for taking the time to chat with us.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman Michael McCaul on all of that.\par \par \uc2 An update again on this abortion pill situation. It keeps getting pushed back. But we're told that Friday is the drop-dead time that all these -- this hold on new restrictions imposed by a federal judge in Texas will be decided one way or the other. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The Supreme Court today has extended yet again access to that abortion pill until at least Friday. And, by then, they could rule one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 The significance of this with Katie Cherkasky, the constitutional law attorney. Former federal prosecutor John Yoo is with us as well, former deputy assistant attorney general.\par \par \uc2 John, end it with you, begin with you. What are we to make of this latest delay? I guess it's a drop-dead point on Friday. How do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: It shows that this case is no easy win for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 This delay means that there are people on the court who need more time to think about these issues. The courts are in a tough spot, because you have got a court in Texas has essentially banned the sale of mifepristone. You have got another federal court in Washington state that wants the government to keep selling, making mifepristone available.\par \par \uc2 And both of that almost guarantees it's going to get to the Supreme Court eventually. The justices have to decide now whether they want to intervene right away, or what they usually do is to let it percolate up the lower courts until the issues and legal claims can get better identified and resolved before it gets to them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that could also, in that scenario, Katie, mean that the drug is available until the Supreme Court itself ultimately decides this.\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 So, as it stands right now, any of the restrictions on the drug and the availability are not in place, and it's going to continue at least until Friday at this point. But, ultimately, if the court decides that they will let the case play out in the usual course of litigation through the circuit courts and then eventually look at it when it's officially before the court on the merits, that could mean that the drug remains available during that entire period of time.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court can really fashion any sort of outcome here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is an unusual kind of bind, John, for the Supreme Court to be in, right?\par \par \uc2 Their ruling on Dobbs essentially left it to the states to decide abortion issues. They are ultimately deciding on a drug that accounts for more than half the abortions that are handled in this country. So, the very issue that was left up to states is now at a point of being left up to nine Supreme Court justices for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 YOO: That's a good point, Neil.\par \par \uc2 In Dobbs, the court clearly signaled it. Wants to get out of the abortion business, wants to send it back to the states, let elected politicians make their decisions. These ancillary issues are going to keep returning, though. The court's never going to be fully free of the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 The other thing to keep in mind, Neil, something we have talked about in this show before, is, this court also is extremely skeptical of administrative agencies. They have been very, I would say, disrespectful of things like the Biden administration's vaccine mandate, its eviction moratorium, and so on.\par \par \uc2 You could read this case as part of that, that this court is also not entirely respectful and trusting -- trusting of our public health authorities and the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . And that might be a reason it will get involved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Katie, it is kind of a paradox for the court right now, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, it is a paradox, but I think we have to remember that the grounds on which Roe was overturned with Dobbs is a completely different analysis than what they're being asked to look at now.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, it all boils down to abortion access, but this is a question about whether a federal agency, the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , appropriately followed its own laws and regulations in approving these drugs and then in rolling back some of the restrictions that they had previously put on there.\par \par \uc2 So I think the court is in a very difficult position, but they don't have the same legal questions before it that they did with regard to Roe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, maybe some temporary closure on Friday. We will see you.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you both, Katie and John, on that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there are more delays to look forward to in summer travel, perhaps record delays, even with packed planes.\par \par \uc2 Connell McShane at La Guardia Airport on that -- Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's shaping up, Neil, to be a summer travel nightmare in all the worst ways.\par \par \uc2 While you're paying more while you're traveling, the problem is, you're not necessarily always getting more for your money. We're live at La Guardia in New York with all the details coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the numbers appear good for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in the latest quarter, earnings coming in kind of as expected, revenues kind of as expected. Might be an issue of deliveries. Don't know.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Tesla\uc2  that has been busy cutting the prices of its automobiles six times this year, the sixth just over the last 24 hours, the shares are down 4 percent in after-hours trading, of course, a closely watched technology stock, as well as an auto stock. So we will keep an eye on that and the implications for the economy.\par \par \uc2 Also, one of the things we're following as well is, we're talking about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and, of course, what he does when he talks about going into space and everything else. I'm going to have a special guest on this whole phenomenon, William Shatner, who has actually been to space, not just on "Star Trek," but in reality.\par \par \uc2 He's going to be hosting a new FOX reality show, "Stars on Mars," and he's going to stop here to tell us all about it. Really enjoy talking to him over these many, many years, 90-plus-years young and, man, oh, man, sharper than anyone can get at. So that's coming up.\par \par \uc2 Talk about really, really sharp, our own Connell McShane, who's also 90- plus years old, but doesn't look it.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I tell you, after you go through what he has to go to, or what he's seeing could be a problem in the airports...\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... a lot of people have a habit of aging quickly doing that.\par \par \uc2 Connell, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: That was impressive, the way you put all of that together, Neil, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 The -- people are paying more, and they're waiting around more, which doesn't put them in a very good mood these days at the airports. Here's a few examples on the airfares that we dug up. First of all, you want to go L.A. to Boston, right? The increase in price this year compared to last year is about 16 percent, which overall isn't that bad.\par \par \uc2 A lot of them are up even more than that. I mean, another example that we have is New York to Dallas, where the fare now on a round-trip ticket is up over $500 at $509. And \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  executives were today saying in a conference call a lot of this is simply just high demand that's expected to stay high.\par \par \uc2 Other factors, the pilot shortage, delays at aircraft deliveries. Plus, some analysts are now saying that airlines are starting to pass along costs associated with going green. But, whatever the reason, it just adds to the level of frustration when you talk to passengers. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You want more money, but you don't want to do better service. It's crazy. If you get like this, I start driving again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm not necessarily sure that the people that make the decisions are consulting with the people that actually implement the decisions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Prices are going up. Inflation, it won't stop.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I used to go to school, so I'd get flight tickets every year. It used to be $80. And now it's like twice the price.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Yes, twice the price. You heard it. They pay more. They don't always get more.\par \par \uc2 Events like the ground stop yesterday at \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  leading to delays for passengers already shelling out the big bucks. And if that was a one-off, that would be one thing. But, Neil, events like that have been popping up more and more at airports around the country here in recent months -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Connell, very much.\par \par \uc2 Connell McShane at La Guardia International Airport.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, more leaks from the leaker, and you won't believe what's leaking -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, more leaks on the leaker. What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams has more right now from Boston -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yet, it was a quick day in court for 21-year-old Jack Teixeira. He stood in front of a judge and found out he will be in federal custody for at least two more weeks.\par \par \uc2 Let's get to these brand-new court sketches we got in just a short time ago from that court hearing this morning. You can see him standing there in an orange jumpsuit and some shackles. The 21-year-old was supposed to have a detention hearing today, but his legal team, Neil, asked for more time to prepare, and the judge granted the extension.\par \par \uc2 Teixeira also waived his right to a preliminary hearing. The appearance coming nearly one week after Teixeira was arrested on at least two federal charges. Investigators tell us the Massachusetts Air National Guardsman posted hundreds of top secret documents online.\par \par \uc2 And, today, all senators were briefed on that leak. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The core challenge we have on our hands right now is whether Congress is going to, on a bipartisan basis, assert not just our right, but our obligation to conduct oversight over these agencies, which we cannot do without full access.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And Teixeira, Neil has not yet entered a plea. He was only in court today for three minutes, and then he was taken away in those handcuffs. His dad was in that courtroom.\par \par \uc2 If he is convicted, he will face about 15 years behind bars -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alexis, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, we're learning right now that the Senate, it was has briefed on not only the leaker, but some of the latest leaks.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 , Nebraska Republican, with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I know there's not much you can share with us, Senator. I appreciate that. But we are learning that some of the leaks go beyond those that we were originally discussing. What do you know or what can you share?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEB FISCHER (R-NE): Well, I want to be clear that when you have an intelligence leak like this, it's a major blow to our national security. It can reveal sources and methods. It can cause our allies to be in jeopardy, and it really is a major blow to our foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 I was disappointed when the Biden administration came out at the earliest breaking news on this that it was no big deal, because this is a big deal. We need to get to the bottom of it. We need to know exactly what documents were out there, which ones were doctored for Russian disinformation, and what's going to be the process going forward to make sure that there is more control on this by the Department of Defense and the Department of State going forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, I wonder sometimes whether we just have too many documents, too many classified documents, too many that wander and get lost or holed up at a former president's home in Florida, another one a present chief executive in Delaware, a former vice president, itinerary travel plans of the president of the United States while he's in Ireland, all of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 What is going on here?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, I think the main thing we need to look at is who has access to these documents.\par \par \uc2 I know, when many people retire from government service, whether it's with the Department of Defense, secretary of defense, generals, for example, they are still getting briefed after they leave government service. So I think we need to look at things like that on who has the need to know on these things.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: I happen to support looking at declassifying some material, as long as we don't put individuals in jeopardy, reveal sources, things like that, how we gained that information, for example.\par \par \uc2 But when we look at our peer competitors, Russia and China, and the advancements that they are making, for example, with their nuclear enterprise, their triads, I am a strong supporter of getting information like that to the American people, because I believe, if the American people have a fuller understanding of the threats that this country faces, they will be more supportive, more open in bolstering our national defense to meet those threats.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, Senator, more than two million have some sort of security clearance that gets them access to stuff like this. That sounds like about maybe 1.5 million too many.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: That sounds like an awfully lot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: And that's one thing I want to look at.\par \par \uc2 You know, my staff has to go through months and months to get a security clearance...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: ... to be able to attend some of those classified briefings with me.\par \par \uc2 And these are people that have worked on the Hill for years. But, in order to get the clearance that I have to go to some of these hearings, they have to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you got to crack down. You got to crack down, to your point.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sorry to jump on you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 Very good seeing you, Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 , on that.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020230419ej4j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 FBI\uc2 : Teixeira Searched "Leak" In Classified System; Intel Leak Suspect Makes First Court Appearance; Biden Wants To Know Why Leaker Had Access To Docs; The Pentagon's "Protocols" Failed; Suspected Leaker Charged Under Espionage Act; Suspected Leaker Back In Court On Wednesday; Psaki Downplays Leak Saying Not "Earth-Shattering"; \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 : Intel Agencies Knew Of Up To Four Additional Chinese Spy Balloons; Biden Downplayed Chinese Spy Balloons; Foreign Leaders Flock To Xi; Trans Activist Disrupts Turning Point USA Event; Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Criticizes Florida Bill Restricting Abortions; Former Biden Administration Official Sam Brinton Pleads Guilty To Stealing Luggage at McCarran Airport; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Argues Possible Downside To Artificial Intelligence Includes Civilizational Destruction\b0\par\par\uc2 Kayleigh McEnany, Sean Hannity, Jonathan Hunt, Sean Duffy, Newt  Gingrich, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 7,532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: That's all the time we have left, this Friday evening. We appreciate you joining us. Thank you for making this show possible.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. And Laura Ingraham, THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend. We'll see you, Monday.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , in for Laura Ingraham. And this is a Special Edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, from New York City, tonight.\par \par \uc2 If you're a 21-year-old gamer, illegally leaking classified government documents? Here's a tip. Do not search classified Intelligence reporting, for the word, "Leak," on your government-issued computer. Seems like commonsense!\par \par \uc2 But that's exactly what the leak suspect, and Massachusetts Air National Guardsman, Jack Teixeira, allegedly did, after news broke that the government knew about the leaks, and were desperately trying to find out, who was behind it. Remember, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " beat them to it!\par \par \uc2 We learned all that and more, after 21-year-old Teixeira, made his first appearance, in court, today.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Correspondent, Jonathan Hunt, is live, in our West Coast newsroom, with all the details.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 It was a brief court appearance, for the accused leaker, today, in federal court, in Boston, beginning a likely lengthy legal process, and potentially a very long prison sentence, for 21-year-old Jack Teixeira, if he is ultimately found guilty, under the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: There are very serious penalties associated with that. People who sign agreements, to be able to receive classified documents, acknowledge the importance, to the national security, of not disclosing those documents. And we intend to send that message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Court documents, released today, showed that the gaming chat platform, \uc2 Discord\uc2 , helped lead the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , to Teixeira, who despite his age, and low-level ranking, in the Air National Guard, had the highest level security clearance.\par \par \uc2 And according to the court documents, Teixeira had been posting the classified material, online, since December, raising more questions, about the oversight, or lack of oversight, from the military and Intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 Wrapping up his tour of Ireland, President Biden said he too, wants answers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I've instructed the Department to make sure that they get to the root of why he had access in the first place, number one.\par \par \uc2 And number two, to focus extensively on the extent to which it all occurred. And that's going on right now. I have nothing to report beyond what's already been reported.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, Teixeira's next court appearance will be a detention hearing, on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, as many of you know, I've worked in an administration before. I've had a security clearance. And what I find completely inexcusable, about this entire thing, is how the government allowed this to happen, in the first place, despite their so-called protocols.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIGADIER GENERAL PATRICK RYDER, DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE PRESS SECRETARY: We do have stringent guidelines in place for safeguarding classified and sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 Again, let me just emphasize my point that this was a -- you know, we have rules in place.\par \par \uc2 We do have strict protocols in place.\par \par \uc2 Look, again, we have procedures. We have protocols in place.\par \par \uc2 We have safeguards in place. We have processes. We have procedures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Hmm!\par \par \uc2 Well, joining me now is Sean Duffy, Co-host of "The Bottom Line" on Fox Business; and Horace Cooper, Project 21 Chairman.\par \par \uc2 Sean, you've dealt with classified information, before. You were a congressman.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon Press Secretary, he says, "Procedure," four times, "Process," three times, "Rules," eight times, "Guidelines," three times. Great if you have all these rules and guidelines! But it's like Alvin Bragg, bragging, "I have all these criminal laws, but I'm not enforcing them." Something is amiss!\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, "THE BOTTOM LINE" CO-HOST: Yes. You have protocols. But you don't use your protocols, to actually keep Intelligence safe, I mean.\par \par \uc2 So, any American who tries to sign on to their bank account, knows how hard it is, if you use a different server, than your home server. There's like multiple authentications that you have to go through, to verify that it's you. Or, if you try to sign into \uc2 Google\uc2 , or Hotmail, these companies have protocols to verify that it's you, who's getting on that server or that Hotmail account.\par \par \uc2 The problem here is, yes, Teixeira should not have had the security clearance that he did, as a National Guardsman, 21-years-old, from Massachusetts. But he did. And too many people have these classifications. And he's a young man.\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of old men, who are in the Pentagon, and the NSA that are supposed to have procedures, and guardrails that make sure that if someone, like Teixeira, tries to access very sensitive information, there are red flags, there's lights and sirens that go off that trigger that adults go, "Hey, well, why is this young man trying to access this information? Is he -- does he need it? Should he have it?" And they go to his house. They go to his computer.\par \par \uc2 None of that stuff happens. We've had Snowden. We've had Assange, Bradley Manning. It's not the first time this has happened, Kayleigh. It keeps happening. And the government doesn't change course.\par \par \uc2 And Kayleigh, you know this well. $800 billion a year? And this is the kind of security we get, for our Intelligence?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It's unbelievable!\par \par \uc2 You don't hear this, from any of our allies, any of our enemies. They don't have these kind of leaks. But America, we do. We got to change course.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: We have to.\par \par \uc2 Horace, "Politico" reporting, this was astounding to me, "It's not uncommon for relatively junior servicemembers to have access to top-secret information." They go on to say that more than 1 million -- 1 million -- \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  employees have access.\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, PROJECT 21 CHAIRMAN: Look, there are serious consequences that this country faces. We live in an extremely dangerous world. And we are probably the most attractive jewel on the planet. And it's essential that we have Intelligence agencies, operating, to make sure that we, Americans, are kept safe, and this crown jewel is protected.\par \par \uc2 When I worked in the Bush administration, in public diplomacy, I too, had a security clearance. And I made absolutely sure that the information that I had access to, the conversations that I had, remained exclusively, either here, or simply reported back, to the appropriate parties. Never would I have thought about sharing whether it was on a game site, or at a cocktail party, nothing of the sort.\par \par \uc2 And yet, since we can't have people operate with self-restraint, self- control, what we need is an administration that recognizes how serious this is.\par \par \uc2 And I started off by pointing out that this is a real threat. Our Administration is far more interested, in figuring out, whether traditional Catholics, or conservative Catholics--\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Right.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: --are in somehow, a threat to the country, rather than staying focused, like a laser, on the actual issues that we need.\par \par \uc2 The Gang of Eight, in the Capitol, need to come forward, immediately, and demand that there be a representation, made by this administration, explaining what has occurred.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: We are jeopardizing, in many ways, our relationships, based on what has happened, here, in such a casual way, by this administration that's lost its focus.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Horace, you describe it, so well.\par \par \uc2 I remember the -- you didn't deal with classified information, a lot, in your capacity, as press secretary, in my capacity, I should say.\par \par \uc2 However, I remember receiving the Presidential Daily Brief. I got to observe it. There was a pertinent news story. And you're right. I mean, I had to go into this room. Someone had to come in. They had to essentially present the information. There were so many safeguards and procedures, which is why it's so hard, to comprehend how this happened.\par \par \uc2 Sean, I want to get this in.\par \par \uc2 The former press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , had some thoughts, on the Discord leaks. She downplayed it, much like her former boss. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The contents of what we have seen? None of that was earth-shattering.\par \par \uc2 The challenge, though that's of interest to me, is what are the conversations, behind-the-scenes, in foreign capitals? I mean, you have this leak.\par \par \uc2 You had the Mar-a-Lago documents, classified documents, laying all over the place. What are people, the Five Eyes partners, where we share our Intel, pretty freely with, what do they think?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Sean? First of all, I seem to remember classified documents--\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: --by the wheel of a Corvette. That was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Putting that aside, nothing earth-shattering? These were private communications, from the \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , the Security Forces, in Israel, from the National Security Council, in South Korea. We learned about new Chinese spy balloons. We learned about U.S. Special Forces, abroad, in Ukraine. Nothing earth-shattering? How could she say that?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  mentioned -- first of all, she's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 But Horace said, and he mentioned the Gang of Eight. I'm sure this is information that, even the Gang of Eight, in Congress, doesn't have access to.\par \par \uc2 And it begs the question, how is the Chinese military advanced so quickly? They steal the billions of dollars that we invest, in planes, and ships, and missiles. They steal it wholeheartedly, because we haven't secured our Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we have to pivot. And you can't have a President, a former press secretary, or an administration, that doesn't understand the serious nature, of this.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think, the Congress is going to come hard on this administration, both Republicans and Democrats. This is not about a set of documents you might have in your basement, at Mar-a-Lago, or by your car, in your garage. This is the most classified information this government has. And it was leaked.\par \par \uc2 Probably downplayed them (ph). But Teixeira should never have it. You should have a small group, maybe 100 people have access to it, but not a million, as Horace Cooper just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, Sean and Horace, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 What's inside those leaked documents? It's been slowly trickling out. And tonight, we are learning disturbing new details, and yes, they are earth- shattering, about what the Administration has been hiding, from us, as it relates to China.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting Intel agencies knew about up to, get this, four additional Chinese spy balloons, including one that flew over a U.S. carrier strike group, in a previously unreported incident.\par \par \uc2 And then, there's one named Bulger-21, which reportedly carried sophisticated surveillance equipment, and circumnavigated the globe, from December 2021, until May 2022.\par \par \uc2 But don't worry! Remember what Biden told us, about that first spy balloon?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: And the idea that a balloon could traverse, break American airspace is -- anyway, this is not a major breach.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Joining me now is Steven Mosher, President of the Population Research Institute, and Author of "Bully of Asia: Why China's Dream is the New Threat to World Order."\par \par \uc2 One of the interesting things, we learned, about these new spy balloons, Josh Rogin, and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," Steven, also had some rather alarming reporting. In short, it was basically that China has longer-range hypersonic missiles.\par \par \uc2 But here's the President, again, downplaying the spy balloon, which now we know, had surveillance technology, and there might have been four more.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MOSHER, AUTHOR OF "BULLY OF ASIA," POPULATION RESEARCH INSTITUTE: Yes, Kayleigh. It just gets worse and worse. It turns out there wasn't just one Chinese spy balloon, but a whole series of Chinese spy balloons, floating around the world, over sensitive places.\par \par \uc2 And look, let's not call them "Spy balloons," because that makes it sound like we're at a kid's birthday party.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: The Chinese called them "Espionage spy airships." They're airships. They're very sophisticated airships. They carry enough solar power panels, to generate 10,000 watts of electricity. They carry sophisticated radar. They carry sophisticated cameras.\par \par \uc2 They can gather information, as they were, undoubtedly doing, over Malmstrom Air Force Base, up in Montana, as it surveilled the Air Force Base, and made figure-eights, around the Air Force Base, to make sure that it had located all the planes, and missiles, and so forth, that it was interested in finding.\par \par \uc2 And we still don't know all of the capabilities, of this airship, because instead of doing the sensible thing, and bringing it down, overland, intact? We waited until it was over water--\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Right. Why?\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: --shot it down, blew it up, submerged it, destroyed it. And now, we're trying to put the pieces back together. It doesn't work that way.\par \par \uc2 Once again, we learned we cannot trust our government to be honest with us. Part of leadership is setting a good example, when it comes to things, like handling classified documents, taking responsibility for your mistakes, when something like this happens. And we have to ask ourselves, whether President Biden, ever, ever does this, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Steven? And President Biden, he's over discovering his genealogy, on the taxpayer dime, in Ireland.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, China's meeting with Spain, Singapore, Malaysia, France, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Brazil. Russia, we know they've met with. We know they brokered relations, between Saudi Arabia and Iran. Are they trying to create a new World Order?\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: Absolutely. They've been trying to create a new World Order, for the last 30 years. Once the Soviet Union fell, Deng Xiaoping, the then leader of China, in 1991, gathered his senior leadership, together, and said "The Old Cold War is over. America won. The New Cold War is now beginning. And we will win this one." That was 30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  sees his opportunity. He is seizing this moment, of American weakness, which, quite frankly, is on full display, every time President Biden, sadly, opens his mouth, to try and convince America's allies that America, the century of America, is over, and the century of Communist China is about to begin.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: China's now brokering peace deals, in the Middle East, if you can believe it!\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Right. Pleased to do that.\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: He's encouraging European countries, France's Macron, to make a separate peace with Beijing. He's building a network, of Allied states, and bases, in places, like Sri Lanka, and Cambodia, and Djibouti that may one day rival America's own. This is a major threat. We've got to focus on it now. Not next year. Not next decade.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well-said. Steven, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOSHER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, first Al Roker. Now, this! White House staff undoubtedly, in dismay. Did another reporter just trick Biden, into admitting, he's running again, in 2024? It seems so! Newt Gingrich reacts to yet another Biden blunder. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, if nothing else proved that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not ready, for another four years, in office, just take a look at how he represented us, on the world stage, this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KID: What's the top, what's the top step to success?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: What's the top what?\par \par \uc2 KID: Step for success?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: What's the top step for success?\par \par \uc2 KID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: Well, making sure that we don't all have COVID -- what -- why -- what are we talking about?\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 , AMERICAN ATTORNEY, SON OF U.S. PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: Dad, if you can -- what's the -- what's the key to success?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: What's the key to success? You know what I found out the key to success is? And I'm not sure I'm the best guy to explain it.\par \par \uc2 This was given to me by one of these guys, right here, was a hell of a rugby player. And he beat the hell out of the Black and Tans.\par \par \uc2 I met more with \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  than any world leader has over the last 10 years.\par \par \uc2 I've traveled 17,000 miles with him.\par \par \uc2 Being here does feel -- I know it's going to se- -- it feels like coming home. It really does.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not going home, I'm staying here.\par \par \uc2 Let's go la- -- lick (ph) the world. Let's get it done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Joining me now is Newt Gingrich, former Speaker of the House, and Fox News Contributor.\par \par \uc2 Newt, America knows that our President is not entirely with it. The CNN poll reflects that recently. 32 percent say he doesn't have the stamina or sharpness to be president. A third, say he shouldn't be reelected.\par \par \uc2 But how do Republicans turn an unpopular president into electoral defeat? Because it doesn't necessarily translate, as we saw, in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that's exactly the right question.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that rather than focusing on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who clearly has very significant cognitive problems, and who has to rely, on his son, who himself, Hunter, has all sorts of clouds, over his head. But if you watch them, in Ireland, it was embarrassing, how much President Biden, had to rely on his son, just to seem semi-competent.\par \par \uc2 Rather than focus on that, I think Republicans need to focus, as Speaker McCarthy has been doing, on finding positive solutions, making clear that we are with the American people, being reasonable, at a time, when extremists, dominate the Democratic Party, and offering the American people hope that there is a better future.\par \par \uc2 I just have recently been in a series of meetings, with people, who are sophisticated, and who are genuinely frightened. They look at what's happening, in Saudi Arabia, allying with China, in Brazil, cutting deals with China, in what the Iranians are doing, what the Russians are doing. And they're really genuinely scared.\par \par \uc2 These are very sophisticated people, who look at how utterly incompetent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is. He's not Commander-in-Chief. He sort of Cognitive-Problems- in-Chief. And I think that we have to recognize that the key is not to focus on Biden. We don't want to be the anti-Biden party. We want to be the pro-America party.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's right. Give voters a choice, and optimistic vision, for the future.\par \par \uc2 Axios had reported that President Biden was going to delay his announcement, because he wanted to seem presidential. He was relying on chaos, in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 But then, he said this, today, which suggests perhaps a quicker timeline. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Can you tell us whether the last few days have changed your calculus on when you'll make an announcement on your plans for 2024?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: No, no, no, no. I've already made that calculus. We'll announce it relatively soon. But the trip here just reinforced my sense of optimism about what can be done.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: So you've made a decision, you just have to announce it?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: I told you: My plan is to run again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: What do you think of the timeline? He said "Relatively soon."\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, look, first of all, nobody should underestimate how likely it is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 Gerry Ford, who had been appointed President, defeated \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , at the bottom of his popularity, defeated \uc2 Teddy Kennedy\uc2 . Incumbent presidents, inside their own party, have enormous power.\par \par \uc2 This guy is spending, several trillion dollars, of federal money, his ability, to build a network, to get things done. Even though he may not totally understand what's going on, he's got a lot of friends, who want to make sure he survives, so they survive. So, I think, Biden, if he wants to, will be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 I also think that we kind of overestimate all this. Remember, when you and I are chatting, in April? In May of 2015, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had not yet come down the escalator. He did it in June.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So true.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Now, he hadn't even gotten in the race yet. So, who knows what's going to happen, in the next few months?\par \par \uc2 I do think that Biden, if he runs, will be the nominee. And I do suspect that Trump, if he continues running, will be the nominee. So, we'll see a rematch, and we'll find out whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be like Grover Cleveland, and be the only other president, to be reelected, having lost in between.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Fascinating, Newt. We rely on your wisdom. Thank you so much, for joining us, on this Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well it's been nearly two years, since the Biden administration's disastrous pullout, from Afghanistan. 13 servicemembers, absolute heroes, were killed. And still, to this day, not a single person has been held accountable. And if it were up to the Biden administration, no one ever would be, because they are proud.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: You guys are proud of the way that this mission was conducted?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: Doesn't mean--\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Proud of that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Proud of the fact that we got more than 124,000 people safely out of Afghanistan? You bet!\par \par \uc2 Proud of the fact that American troops were able to seize control of a defunct airport, and get it operational, in 48 hours? You bet!\par \par \uc2 Proud of the fact that we now have about 100,000 Afghans, our former allies and partners, living in this country, and working towards citizenship? You bet!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Wow! He also said it wasn't chaotic. We all saw the images!\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone, who definitely doesn't agree to that and wants to do something about it. House Oversight Committee Chairman, James Comer.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, you are holding a hearing on this, with Inspectors Generals, starting on Wednesday. And what you said was you want the American people, to have answers. They deserve them. They certainly do.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 John Sopko\uc2 , the special I.G. for Afghanistan Reconstruction, he notified you that the Biden administration was interfering, in his investigation, which he has a right to conduct.\par \par \uc2 How is the Biden administration interfering?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Well, that's what we're going to find out. I mean, the Biden administration is not cooperating, with the Special Inspector General. These Inspectors Generals are put into place, in a bipartisan manner, to oversee spending, and various different types of oversight, from within this particular cabinet or agency.\par \par \uc2 When we started spending a lot of money, in Afghanistan, when we had the war, in Afghanistan? There were Special Inspector General, for the whole Afghan Reconstruction, because this was a significant amount of taxpayer dollars, being spent here.\par \par \uc2 So, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , became president, and certainly, during the time of the debacle, of a withdrawal? He quit communicating, with the Inspector General. And he continues, to this day, not to cooperate with him.\par \par \uc2 Now, an important part of oversight is to hold people accountable, for wrongdoing. We had 13 servicemembers, who died in this debacle. I don't think their families are proud of the way that the Biden administration handled this, the way that their spokesman just said.\par \par \uc2 We want to get answers for the American people. And we want to know who made decisions, who's to blame, for the decisions that went wrong, and how can we hold these people accountable, for the disastrous withdrawal, and the waste of billions of dollars of taxpayer money?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I want to play this for you, because this is the most pressing question, I have, about Afghanistan. Biden said no one told him he needed to leave a certain number of troops. But General McKenzie said otherwise, to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Take listen to the contradiction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, ABC NEWS CHIEF ANCHOR: Your top military advisers warned against withdrawing in this timeline. They wanted you to keep about 2,500 troops.\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: No, they didn't. It was split.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANOPOULOS: They didn't tell you that they wanted troops to stay?\par \par \uc2 J. BIDEN: No, not at -- not in terms of whether we were going to get out, in a timeframe, all troops. They didn't argue against that.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH F. MCKENZIE JR., MILITARY PERSONNEL: I recommended that we maintain 2,500 troops in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I also have a view that the withdrawal of those forces would lead inevitably to the collapse of the Afghan military forces and eventually the Afghan government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, is Biden lying, there?\par \par \uc2 COMER: Well, that's what we're going to find out.\par \par \uc2 And that's an important question, because there are a lot of people that suspect that maybe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  isn't the one that makes a lot of decisions, in this White House. And we want to know if the military is acting unilaterally, or if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was dishonest with the American people?\par \par \uc2 Now, I know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a pattern of dishonesty, with the American people. I can tell you that with our other primary investigation, of the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 But with respect to the Afghanistan withdrawal, and the loss of 13 servicemembers, we're going to find out who, in fact, is telling the truth and who in fact, is lying, because the American people deserve answers. The taxpayers deserve answers. And the families of those 13 servicemembers who were lost, in the withdrawal, they deserve answers.\par \par \uc2 And that's what the House Oversight Committee is going to attempt to get, next Wednesday, is answers, to exactly who made what decisions, who knew what, when, and why this turned out the way it did.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Those 13 servicemembers, they certainly deserve answers.\par \par \uc2 We will be watching you, Wednesday. Thank you very much, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 COMER\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, up next, we show you the tolerance, of the radical-left. And we reveal what happens when an angry Trans activist meets a conservative group, on campus.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the big move, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  just made, and what it's going to mean, as we move forward. Dr. Ben Carson is here to react, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Laura told you about the physical assault on swimmer Riley Gaines by radical leftists when she spoke at a Turning Point USA event last week. Now a trans activist crashed another event and again turned violent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, no, you just support the genocide of trans kid and you want everyone in support of that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, no, you do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is such an assumption.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is such an assumption, right, with all of this shit. Right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What is anti-trans on the table?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're TP USA you dumb --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Wow, those "I heart USA" pins, they must have really been triggering.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Michael Shellenberger, cofounder of "Public," a \uc2 Substack\uc2  publication. It's interest, we hear this often, a genocide against trans individuals. A genocide is what's happening in China to the Uighurs where their birthrate has been reduced by 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHELLENBERGER, "PUBLIC" \uc2 SUBSTACK CO\uc2 -FOUNDER: Yes, that's right. And not only that, but I point out that the number of documented trans folks that have been killed over the last three years has consistently gone down. It's under 50 people and even those people, we don't know if they were killed because they were trans. And we also don't know if the rate of homicide is any higher than it is for the general population.\par \par \uc2