might be violating someone's civil rights if you forget their pronoun, and they have a list of pronouns, we'll pop them up, "Zis, Hirs, Eirs, Vers." I don't know what any of these mean, I can barely remember names, but now I have to remember this or I'm committing perhaps a civil rights violation, Michael?\par \par \uc2 SHELLENBERGER: Yes. It's absurd. Look, I think we know now from Europe where there's significant reform effort underway to protect children. We know that about one third of all the kids that transition, so-called transition, are suffering autistic spectrum disorder or some other neurological or developmental disorder. We see a lot of gay and lesbian kids who if they allowed to go through puberty would end up becoming gay and lesbian adults with full sexual function.\par \par \uc2 Instead, these are kids, I think it's important to say it, they're being sterilized, and they're being deprived of a life of sexual measurement. This by a movement that had, just until recently, dedicated itself to the liberation of gay and lesbian people. And we as a country have accepted gay and lesbian marriage, but I think what we've seen here is a very different kind of political movement that's really hijacked the sort of the freedom and rights that have been gained by gays and lesbians, really abused it, and similarly I think preying on really vulnerable kids in particular.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So true.\par \par \uc2 SHELLENBERGER: I think most of us think if you're a grown adult you can choose what you want to do, but we're talking about children at this point.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Children. It's a tragedy. Michael, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn to Florida now where Republicans there are actively trying to protect women, including the lives of the unborn. Here's how the administration reacted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They attack the freedom of a woman to make decisions about her own body. Just yesterday in Florida, extremists there signed a six-week ban before most women even know they're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 In the midst of all these attacks, on fundamental freedoms, these so-called leaders dare to tell us they are fighting for our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Joining me now is Dr. Ben Carson, former Trump HUD secretary and founder of the American Cornerstone Institute. Dr. Carson, contrary to what Stacey Abrams tells us, at six weeks you can hear a heartbeat. I saw my baby on an ultrasound just last year. I heard the heartbeat, it wasn't manufactured. You're a world renouncer, separated the first conjoined twins. Tell us about life and what it's like at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 DR. BEN CARSON, FORMER HUD SECRETARY: Well, first of all, you have the gametes, a male gamete and a female gamete with 23 chromosomes. Those have no potential to become a human being. But when they join together after fertilization, you've got a complete set, 46 chromosomes, a completely different individual, not part of the father, not part of the mother.\par \par \uc2 And what the shame is, we've managed to convince women that they cannot be complete and they cannot be free unless they have the right to kill this baby that's inside of them, which is a blessing. It's not a disease. These medications, they call -- they call them medication, the antiabortion pills, or the abortion pills, but it's not a disease. It's not a sickness that's being treated. It's a blessing from God.\par \par \uc2 And what we have to start thinking about in this country is the importance of life. As we move further and further away from respecting life, look what's happening to our relationships with each other. We're not respecting life anywhere, from the womb to the tomb. And I think that's one of the reasons that we see so much violence. We see people going out and committing horrendous acts because they no longer respect people. And we have to change that somehow, someway, soon, because our society is going down the tubes pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's so beautifully said. As scripture tells us, "Before I formed you in the womb I knew you. You were fearfully and wonderfully made." The converse of the six weeks, let's go over to what the left says, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Hobbs, Katie Hobbs, they've all told us that they want no bans on abortion whatsoever. Why is there never a discussion of pain? And if you could let us know from a doctor's standpoint, the baby does feel pain, and certainly at the point where you're going to 38, 39, 40 weeks.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Absolutely. And even at 15, 16 weeks we know they're able to feel pain. And when you see an abortion that's being performed, on the screen you see the tube being introduced into the uterus, sometimes the baby actually seems to try to move away from it before it attaches, and then all of a sudden pulls off an arm, pulls off a leg, you see all this blood and gore going down the tube. It is absolutely barbaric.\par \par \uc2 And then by the time you get late into the second trimester and into the third trimester you can't do it that way, the tissues are too tough, and you actually put a forceps in and grab and just twist and pull and out comes a shoulder, out comes another part of the anatomy. It's incalculable how mean and cruel this is. And we talk about some of the ancient civilizations and say that they were barbarians. What are we when we do things like this?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Dr. Carson, it's hard to hear, but I'm glad you described it that way. And if you want to really understand what goes on with abortion in the late stages, our viewers should go read the Carhart opinion by the Supreme Court where they describe in even more graphic detail than you just did what happens to a baby, and nevertheless upheld that practice. It's astonishing.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Dr. Carson, but 32,000 babies saved since Dobbs. So thank you so much for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Thank you, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has an exclusive interview with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  airing Monday. There's a warning from Elon that you need to hear right now. We'll have it for you.\par \par \uc2 Plus, jailtime or no jailtime for Biden's gender fluid bagman. Find out after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: What a surprise, another criminal escaping jailtime, except this time it's fired Biden official and known women's luggage thief Sam Brinton. FOX News White House correspondent Kevin Corke has all the details. Kevin?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Evening, Kayleigh. Imagine, if you will, someone raiding someone else's luggage or closet, you know, rummaging through their dresses, their shoes, their jewelry, unmentionables, and then with stubbled chin and mustache, still reeking of Aqua Velva, putting those clothes like they were their very own. That is essentially what ex-Biden nuclear energy official Sam Britain admits he did, and now he's going to pay, but not in the way that you might think.\par \par \uc2 You see, as part of a plea deal Sam Brinton was charged with a misdemeanor instead of a felony for stealing women's luggage at Las Vegas airport, McCarran. A judge ordered him to pay more than $3,600 bucks in restitution for the stolen goodies, which included jewelry and clothing and makeup, a fine, no jail, no orange is the new black, just a fine.\par \par \uc2 But don't go out there and scuff your Manolo Blahniks just yet, because Brinton still faces felony charges in similar case, which is a way of saying justice may not always be blind, but sometimes the scales do tip in the right direction. Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Kevin, great reporting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A.I. can help deal with some very difficult challenges like disease and climate change. But we also have to address the potential risks to our society, to our economy, to our national security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, do you think A.I. is dangerous?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It remains to be seen. It could be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: While the president dances around the dangers of A.I., \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , he is not holding back in an exclusive interview with our own \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TESLA CEO: A.I. is more dangerous than, say, mismanaged aircraft design or production maintenance or bad car production in the sense that it is, it has the potential -- however small one may regard that probability, but it is not trivial, it has the potential of civilizational destruction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Joining me now is Mike Davis, founder and president of the Internet Accountability Project, also the founder and president of the Article III Project. Mike, destruction of civilization, that a pretty dire warning.\par \par \uc2 MIKE DAVIS, PRESIDENT, ARTICLE III PROJECT: Yes, and I actually agree with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about this. Congress and states must act proactively instead of reactively, as we're dealing with this powerful new tool called artificial intelligence, we can't repeat the mistakes of the past with big tech where we gave them 25 years of Section 230 immunity and anti-trust amnesty. They used their power, their gatekeeping power, to control information and commerce. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , we need to break up these big tech monopolists. And we certainly can't let these big tech monopolists take control of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No, we can't. And Mike, even if we find a way to regulate A.I. here, who is to say that Russia and China will do the same? There's this headline that we see, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  lays out how Russia and China plan to dominate the A.I. arm's race. It's a troubling prospect.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: It really is, and that's why it's so important that we get out in front of this. We can't have it where it's going to be too difficult 10 years down the road to put this genie back in the bottle.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, it will be. And there's such scary potential with A.I. just in our day-to-day lives. I want to play a soundbite. This is a mother who was convinced by a voice that sounded identical to her daughter, except it was A.I., she was convinced that her daughter was kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER DESTEFANO, TARGET OF A.I. KIDNAPPED SCAM: It was my daughter's voice crying and sobbing, saying "Mom." And I'm like OK, what happened? She's like, Mom, these bad men have me. Help me, help me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: DeStefano says a man demanded ransom in exchange for Briana. It was all a scheme using artificial intelligence to replicate her daughter's voice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Frightening stuff there.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes, it really is. And it just shows that there is a bipartisan need right now to set aside politics for these policymakers in Congress and the states to get together and come up with the rules of the road going forward so we don't have a nightmare scenario where we're controlled, we're the slaves to these robots.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: One last question for you. I quoted you earlier in the week on "OUTNUMBERED" on a different topic. There was a transgender vandal who vandalized a church, scared a parishioner out of her mind, resisted arrest. No jailtime from the DOJ. Thirty-four pro-lifers, however, have been targeted, including Mr. Houck, who was exonerated in a court of law. Your thoughts about the disparity there?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: This is outrageous. The Biden Justice Department Civil Rights Division has been completely politicized and weaponized. When they are trying it put a father in jail for 11 years for protecting his 11-year-old son, praying outside of an abortion clinic, he got into a scuffle, yet he's given this transgender terrorist no jailtime. It's insane.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It's insane. And if you doubted that there was an anti-Christian bias happening at the Biden DOJ, doubt it no longer. We have the facts. Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Final thoughts when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's it for us tonight. I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  in for Laura Ingraham. Check out my forthcoming book, I have my copy here, "Serenity in the Storm, Living Through Chaos by Leaning on Christ." It comes out May 2nd, but it's available for pre-order now wherever books are sold. Get your copy today.\par \par \uc2 I'll be a guest on FOX NEWS SUNDAY with Shannon Bream this weekend. Don't miss that on Sunday morning. And don't forget to tune in weekdays at 12:00 p.m. eastern for when I cohost "OUTNUMBERED."\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching this special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. Get your copy of "Serenity in the Storm" today. The great Greg Gutfeld, he takes it all from here. Tune in.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230415ej4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Suspect Jack Teixeira On The Classified Documents Leak Makes First Court Appearance; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Appears To Minimize Impact Of Certain Issues; Republican Presidential Candidates Speak At Convention; \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  Opting Out Of 2024 Contest; Supreme Court Order On Enforcement May come Next Week; Communicating With Deceased Loved Ones; Artificial Intelligence Being Used To Help Those Who Have Lost Loved Ones Grieve; Experts Examined Possible Threats Of Uncontrolled Artificial Intelligence; House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan To Hold Hearing In New York City On Crime; Former Secretary Of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  Announces He Will Not Run For President In 2024; Florida Passes Law Restricting Abortion To Six Weeks Into Pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Alexis McAdams, Jacqui Heinrich, Garrett Tenney,  Shannon Bream, Mark Meredith, Rich Edson \par \uc2 8,029 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Pictures of beans on everything.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: They got to be on the motor's camp.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But I submitted you, so I hope you win Jeanine.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR\uc2 ., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: You put the Judge in the contest?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She doesn't want to go Mars.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Judge, you're going to the camper. We'll see you guys on Monday.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  has made a decision. He is not running for president in 2024. He will be here live on set to explain why.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  makes a decision on the fate of the so called abortion pill, but the legal fight is far from over. Shannon Bream is here with the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says artificial intelligence has the potential to destroy civilization as we know it. Our five part series on A.I. wraps up today and we'll analyze all sides with a trio of A.I. experts.\par \par \uc2 But breaking tonight, the suspect accused of leaking our nation's top secrets appearing in court for the first time today. 21-year-old National Guardsmen is facing two federal charges for removing and sharing classified information.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're learning more about the investigation, which led to his takedown as criticism mounts over the methods of interesting highly sensitive secrets to thousands of people including a 21-year-old Air National Guardsmen from Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials insists they are tightening the entire process.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight, Jacqui Heinrich with reaction from President Biden as he continues his Ireland trip. But we begin with Alexis McAdams outside the courthouse with that appearance happened today in Boston. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. It was a very active day here in Boston at that federal courthouse behind me as investigators try to figure out how a 21-year-old with a high school diploma had access to some of the country's biggest secrets. This all comes as new documents we just got to look at today show that Jack Teixeira might have known that the feds run to him so much so that he was trying to search it in his database.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Tonight, 21-year-old Jack Teixeira remains in federal custody. He appeared in court today wearing a jumpsuit in handcuffs and did not enter a plea.\par \par \uc2 The Massachusetts Air National Guardsmen hit with two federal charges, suspected of posting top secret U.S. intelligence on the internet.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is not just about taking home documents. That is of course itself illegal. But this is about the transmission. Both the unlawful retention and the transmission of the documents.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: In this newly released affidavit, investigators say Teixeira had become concerned that he may be discovered making the transcriptions of text in the workplace. So, he began taking the documents to his residence and photographing them.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say the leak reasonably could be expected to cause exceptionally grave damage to the national security.\par \par \uc2 Our cameras captured Teixeira's dad leaving court today after he listened closely to the charges against his son.\par \par \uc2 On the way out, Teixeira telling his dad he loved him before he was taken away in cuffs.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  arrested the Massachusetts Air National Guardsmen at his mom's house, just outside of Boston.\par \par \uc2 This video shows Teixeira slowly walking down his driveway as agents had their weapons drawn. The leak potentially damaging U.S. relations with allies and compromising our intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Local shocked that their neighbor is at the center of the largest criminal leak in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I went to high school with him. I knew of him, good kid. Not really a whole lot of -- I wouldn't ever have kind of taken him of something like that. So, it's kind of crazy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And Bret, sources don't believe that it might be just Teixeira involved in this leak. So they're looking into that at this time, but that 21-year-old remains in federal custody. He'll be back in this courthouse here in Boston on Wednesday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis McAdams live in Boston. Alexis, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is calling on federal agencies to step up their security measures in the wake of the massive classified documents leak. The action comes one day after the president downplayed the leak's significance.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich just traveling with the president in County Mayo, Ireland tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Ahead of his final event on his four day tour of Ireland.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Everything between Ireland and America runs deep.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden made his first and only comment on the arrest of 21-year- old Jack Teixeira now facing the same violations under the Espionage Act as notorious leakers, Edward Snowden, \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2  and Julian Assange.\par \par \uc2 Biden writing, while we're still determining the validity of those documents, I've directed our military and intelligence community to take steps to further secure and limit distribution of sensitive information, and our national security team is closely coordinating with our partners and allies.\par \par \uc2 Biden initially downplayed the significance of the breach.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you concerned about the leak?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well? I'm not concerned.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This is a pattern from the president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover of Afghanistan now inevitable?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it is not.\par \par \uc2 There's nobody suggesting there's unchecked inflation on the way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me ask you about the Chinese balloon, wasn't there a major breach of security breach for the United States?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There's not a major breach.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President's attorney general today had a much different assessment on whether the incident merits concern, speaking on the seriousness of the charges against Texiera.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: There are very serious penalties associated with that. People who sign agreements to be able to receive classified documents acknowledge the importance to the national security of not disclosing those documents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): This is one of the last times we're going to see President Biden before he heads to Rehoboth for the weekend and he's not set to be back at the White House until Monday night, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich traveling with the president in Ireland. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The annual \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention kicked off today in Indianapolis featuring addresses from all declared 2024 Republican presidential candidates. The meeting comes following two recent mass shootings. Many Republican presidential hopefuls are in attendance there. Correspondent Garrett Tenney is at the gathering in Indianapolis tonight. Good evening Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Now, Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 This year's \uc2 NRA\uc2  gathering gave us a bit of a preview of what the Republican primary could look like. Here's a list of those who spoke today coming here to try to win over gun voters which are a key voting bloc of the party's base.\par \par \uc2 The issue of gun control was front and center today in the wake of mass shootings in Louisville in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  argued that Democrats aren't doing enough to stop them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Democrats in Washington continue to hold common sense school safety measures hostage to their radical gun control agenda, which in virtually all cases would do nothing to prevent attacks by demented and disturbed individuals.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who has not announced to run got a lukewarm welcome here in his home state, with some loud boos mixed in as he took and left the stage. But he got a lot of applause when he argued that creating more laws on gun control isn't the answer.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We don't need gun control. We need crime control. We don't need lectures about the liberties of law abiding citizens, we need solutions to protect our kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): In a pre-recorded video, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who's seen as a leading competitor to President Trump touted his defense of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): As Governor, I've resisted calls to take up gun control even when such a stand is superficially unpopular, because I understand that it is precisely at those moments when a right is unpopular, that it needs true champions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: One challenge for the candidates is that while pro-gun policies will play well in the primary, 69 percent of registered voters in the latest Fox News poll said gun laws are a major concern for them.\par \par \uc2 So, in a general election, this issue plays out a lot differently, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Indianapolis. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . Mr. Secretary, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Bret, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have made a decision.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I have made a decision Bret, I was on your show a number of months back and you asked what I was going to do in 2024. And Susan and I have now been thinking about this working at it and have prayerfully come to the conclusion that we're not going to join the race in 2020.\par \par \uc2 That, while we care deeply about America and the issues that I've been talking about this last year and a half and frankly, four decades, matter an awful lot. This isn't our moment. This isn't the time for us to seek elected office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you made that decision, were you thinking about how what lane you would take and how you would go up against your former boss, former President Trump? If you look at the latest polls, he's up, you know, exponentially on the nearest competitor, did that factor in?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: No, not at all. This is a deeply personal decision for Susan and me and our son Nick and his wife, Rachel, it wasn't about what this would look like.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, this race, I hope it isn't about people, I hope it's about these ideas. These are things since I was a young soldier that I've been working on, right? The fact that we're losing our military edge, the fact that we spent -- we're spent $2 trillion more than we take, spent $2 trillion in additional deficits. These are things that I care about, that we were thinking about, can we impact them? Is this the right time for us to go seek the presidency to go try and work on these and we just simply came to the conclusion this wasn't -- this wasn't the moment for us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Will you do you think indorse, get behind somebody?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I promise you, when the primary is over, I'm going to get after -- I'm going to get after the fact that what President Biden is doing to this country is disastrous for every working family across this country.\par \par \uc2 As for the primary, we'll see how it plays out. It's not about any one person. I want to find that person who can not only talk about the things that matter to every family in America, but who can actually build an organization, create a team and deliver that for the American people. That's what matters to me.\par \par \uc2 When I -- when I figure out who the right person is, I will -- like I've always done in my life, I'll get behind them and do everything I can to help them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it might not be former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: They might not be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's talked -- just a couple more questions on the -- on the field. He talked about loyalty in previous interviews, one with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , talking about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Here's just a clip of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Ron came to see me, tears in his eyes. He said, I need you to do me a big favor. He said, I need your endorsement. I got him the nomination.\par \par \uc2 By the way, couldn't have never gotten the nomination. He wouldn't be working in either a pizza parlor place or a law office right now. OK. And he wouldn't be very happy.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's about loyalty.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It is to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, that wasn't a factor in your decision. But as you look at this race, do you think anyone can beat former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I think the American people will sort this out. Yes, I absolutely think there's a long ways to go. I think Americans are thirsting for people making arguments, not just tweets. I think they're looking for someone who can clearly articulate their vision for what parents ought to be doing to help their kids be successful in schools and how we take back crime from our streets and cities.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that will end up being President Trump that the American people choose or if that's who our party will choose to be its nominee.\par \par \uc2 But I'm hopeful that it will be those debates about real arguments, things that really matter, Bret. And if that happens, America will be in a pretty damn good place.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, let's talk a couple of issues. One is that's getting a lot of attention now is abortion. You tweeted out back in June of 2022, now with Roe overturned, we will see which politicians supported the pro-life cause to win elections, and which actually believed it.\par \par \uc2 Now we have this battle, legal battle, we'll talk about in a bit about the abortion bill, we've seen how abortion affects at least the midterms, and maybe even that Wisconsin State Supreme Court race. How do you think that issue plays for Republicans? And what should Republicans in your mind be doing?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I think the Republicans should defend the right of the unborn, just in the same way we protect the Second Amendment and religious freedom.\par \par \uc2 These are things -- you know, I wrote my book it was called Never Given an Inch. This is one of the issues that it's just you can't give an inch. These are human beings, Bret and I'm convinced most Americans actually get that.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats want to talk about abortion on demand, all the way up till the time of the baby leaving the woman, right? That's indecent. It's immoral. I think most Americans get that.\par \par \uc2 And so, this isn't a place that the Republicans should try to play politics, we should get this issue right. And I'm convinced when we do, the American people will reward us for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing on foreign policy, and that is this leak case. We've seen all of the focus on the leaker not as much focus on the substance of what was in those documents and concerned about what's happening on the ground in Ukraine. What do you say to your party on that issue about Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: President Biden has screwed this up. He has screwed it up by not doing enough. He didn't provide the resources Ukraine needed to actually win. He's only given them enough to sort of keep a stalemate. That's not in America's best interest.\par \par \uc2 It's in America's deep interest that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  not control Kyiv or worse yet, Helsinki. Putin's intention to stop in Kyiv wasn't on his plate.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, President Biden hasn't done enough. If we'd had gotten that right, if we'd have kept the deterrence that we had for four years while I was Secretary of State, the Ukrainian people wouldn't be suffering the way they are today.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's fault. It is his war. He is the one who caused this. But the President Biden hasn't handled this well in a way that actually reflects America's best interests.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Secretary, thanks for the time. Thanks for coming on. The one positive is that we get to continue to see you.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: You will.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I'm glad you're excited about that. I'm sure there's others who aren't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Bret, it's wonderful to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks off today on growth concerns and rate hike expectations. The Dow dropped 143, the S&P 500 fell nine, the NASDAQ lost 43.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up 1-1/5 percentage points. The S&P 500 was up three-quarters, the NASDAQ was up a quarter.\par \par \uc2 Up next, in the legal battle over the access to the so called abortion pills, Shannon Bream is here with the latest.\par \par \uc2 And later, artificial intelligence is helping people cope with the loss of loved ones. It's also raising some serious moral and ethical questions. Our series concludes and a panel of experts is here to analyze.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has signed into law an abortion ban, after six weeks of pregnancy. The measure will take effect only if the state's current 15-week ban is upheld, and an ongoing legal challenge that is before the state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and abortion rights groups are labelling the measure as extreme. The law contains some exceptions including to save the woman's life. Abortions for pregnancies involving rape or incest are allowed until 15 weeks of pregnancy under Florida law.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has ruled that access to the abortion pill Mifepristone can continue without limitations for now.\par \par \uc2 Today's decision follows an emergency filing from the justice department. But this legal battle is far from over.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY," Shannon Bream shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (voice over): After a week of legal whiplash, the Supreme Court has stepped in. Temporarily making access to Mifepristone, one of the key drugs used in medicated abortions available nationwide without limitations, at least, until 11:59 p.m. Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 The order from Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , follows an emergency filing by the justice department, which argues that failing to allow access to the drug would unleash chaos.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has vowed to fight attempts to restrict the FDA- approved drug.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Attacking the very credibility of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  on this one matter for the sake of politics and a political agenda. The wide sweeping ramifications this can have.\par \par \uc2 Alito has given those challenging the legality of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug and opposing its continued use until Tuesday at 12 p.m. Eastern to file their objections.\par \par \uc2 And while they're likely to push for the drug to be stripped from the market altogether, at the very least, they're likely to argue in support of the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals mixed decision, allowing access to the drug, but only along with regulations that were discarded in 2016.\par \par \uc2 They had limited the time into a pregnancy for using the drug, required in person doctor visits, and prohibited the drug from being dispensed by mail.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HAWLEY, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: We respond to that until the Supreme Court that we very much agree that at least these safe guards and guard rails should be put back in place to protect women at a very, very minimum.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And given the expedited timeline, Justice Alito has laid out it's very possible -- a decision on whether the drug will continue to be available nationwide, could come from the High Court by midweek next week.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, this is only about access while the case plays out on the merits in the lower courts. And Bret, of course, that could take months or even years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Long time, potentially. Big weekend news wise. Who you have on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY"?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: On this issue of abortion, we've got Republican congresswoman Nancy Mace. She has said the GOP is getting this wrong. We'll talk to her about that.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the aisle, \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  is going to talk to us about A.I. whether it's coming to get all of us and how it should or shouldn't be regulated.\par \par \uc2 And General Jack Keane on the leak. Why does so many people have access in all of the hotspots around the world?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sounds good. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, as aforementioned, artificial intelligence is helping people cope with the death of loved ones. But \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  warns A.I. could destroy civilization. We'll discuss with a panel of experts.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 WSVN in Miami, as Fort Lauderdale International Airport reopens two days after unprecedented deluge left planes and travelers stranded, turn streets into rivers. A lot of rain in a short time.\par \par \uc2 The Fort Lauderdale area saw a record rainfall amounts in a matter of hours ranging from 15 to 26 inches of rain.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Philadelphia from FOX29, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight. The Professional Football League, USFL returns this Saturday, as the season kicks off.\par \par \uc2 The Memphis Showboats will take on the Philadelphia stars at 4:30 p.m. Eastern time. You can catch that action on the big FOX.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The official in-charge of a secret of Pentagon effort to investigate unexplained aerial incursions has co-authored an academic paper with a Harvard professor.\par \par \uc2 The theorizes recent unidentified objects could actually be alien probes from a mothership sent to study Earth. That's according to POLITICO tonight.\par \par \uc2 Authors acknowledge they do not know for sure that there are any functioning extra-terrestrial crafts near Earth. POLITICO notes that one astronomy professor called the notions highly questionable and odd.\par \par \uc2 Fox has reached out to the defense department. So far, no response. We hope to have something, Monday.\par \par \uc2 This evening, we continue our series on the rise of artificial intelligence by examining how A.I. is helping people cope with the death of their loved ones. And the solution is raising some serious ethical questions.\par \par \uc2 Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Instead of saying goodbye forever, artificial intelligence lets people live on virtually.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JUNG, FOUNDER AND CHIEF FINANCIAL OFFICER, DEEPBRAIN A.I.: To create (INAUDIBLE) real person to A.I. avatar, we usually shoot three, three hours.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Michael Jung is CFO of California based Deepbrain AI. For roughly $10,000, the company records a person's voice, face, mannerisms, and asks them questions to help a computer later generate personal responses even after they're deceased. The technology can provoke strong emotional reactions, but creators say that's the point.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, a South Korean documentary crew captured the heartbreaking moment had when a mother reunited with her deceased seven-year-old daughter through AI. While she couldn't physically touch her daughter, the moment is chilling. Psychiatrists telling FOX technology like this can also create problems.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE BONANNO, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY: The potential for it to come up with sort of wildly inappropriate or wildly inaccurate behaviors, comments, images, even, is right now, I think, quite high. And that could be extremely disturbing.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Experts believe while the tech is improving rapidly it could also backfire. One philosophy professor writing in 2021 that AI grief means it will be more difficult for people to arrive at some form of closure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The Portland Institute for Loss and Transition recently did a study on how people mourn with chatbots. It found that people who used the AI saw a boost in self-confidence after using the tech in the grieving process. But Bret, researchers admit this is so new, it's a complex issue, and they believe further study is needed. But this is certainly where the future is headed. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, great job with this series all week long. And I know it's not the end of this.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: No, it's not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's discuss the future of artificial intelligence with our panel of experts tonight, cybersecurity expert, CEO of Conceal Gordon Lawson, Max Tegmark is AI research professor at \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 , and Carl Szabo is vice president and general counsel at Net Choice and a professor of Internet law at George Mason University's Scalia Law School. Gentlemen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Gordon, let me start with you. When you hear \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  say this, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TESLA CEO: A.I. is more dangerous than, say, mismanaged aircraft design or production maintenance or bad car production in the sense that it is -- it has the potential -- how does one make that probability, but it is not trivial, it has the potential of civilizational destruction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think when you hear that?\par \par \uc2 GORDON LAWSON, CONCEAL CEO: Well, I'm not sure I agree with the hyperbole of civilizational destruction. But what I'm most concerned about, Bret, and we saw it in the previous segment, the ability to manipulate people for nefarious purposes. and I don't think that being able to recreate a lost loved one is necessarily nefarious, but certainly what we are seeing on the cyber side is unprecedented levels of cyberattacks, targeted \uc2 FBI\uc2  phishing attacks that use tools like ChatGPT or other AI tools to make them much more sophisticated and harder to detect. And that's why cybercrime is going to cost the world $10 trillion this year. So it's something we absolutely have to look out for and get more resilient in defending against.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Max, I guess when I talk to people and they say \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says that on the far end, but there are other experts in the field who say we need a pause on all of this because it's going too fast. People get freaked out. They think about "Terminator," and they think are we headed down that road? What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 MAX TEGMARK, MIT AI RESEARCH PROFESSOR: Yes. There are two kinds of AI. There is the narrow AI we just heard about here, which can cause problems with cybersecurity and all sorts of other stuff. And then there is also AGI, which I think of as artificial godlike intelligence, which by definition can do everything at least as smart as us humans. That's what Elon is referring to here. And this is what a number of our biggest tech companies are officially trying to build. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  recently had a paper out saying that they see sparks of AGI already in GPT-4. And that's what we want to slow down, the kind of godlike AGI which we have absolutely no guarantees that we can control at this point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Carl, I think as we are talking about a pause and we're thinking about how Congress can regulate on this front, and it's a big, big question, then you also think about what is China doing with AI, and are they having any issues abroad, or are they just unleashing this thing that's going to be a competition to us?\par \par \uc2 CARL SZABO, \uc2 GEORGE MASON UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Yes. Thanks for having me on. So, if you look at what you have been talking about all week long, we see both positives and negatives from AI. And with respect to China, as you mentioned, they are developing fast and furiously. For years, for decades, the U.S. has been the world leader, the world innovator on technology, software development, and everything. We are an exporter of that technology where China was basically stealing it for decades.\par \par \uc2 Now, they are developing their own AI, and the AI that they are developing actually makes it illegal to challenge the leadership. It makes it illegal to say anything negative about communism. But what I worry about is, if we take a pause, if we decide to stop, then only the bad actors will keep going forward, and we will be dependent on the technology that they create, not the technology that we should create, not the technology that will do the most good for the most people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Gordon, that's great question. Your thoughts on what Carl just said?\par \par \uc2 LAWSON: Exactly. I think our potential adversaries are definitely investing in the technology. And just take a look at the example with this recent Pentagon leak. The data is out on Discord, and it's not just Discord. Social media is obviously very prevalent with hundreds of millions of users. The Russians took that sensitive data, manipulated it. Certainly, they were using AI to be able to scan social media platforms to find this and to be able to have national security implications.\par \par \uc2 So we have to watch out for what you are adversaries are doing. We have to get ahold of it, and we have to invest within our technology industry in the United States to make sure we stay up with the potential threat and be able to ensure we're competitive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, Max, I know from covering this place, Washington, that Capitol Hill has no clue on a lot of this issue. The AI caucus was one member a few years ago. And they have hard time sometimes getting their head around social media questions, and no disrespect, but it's true, on some of these fronts, on these hearings. But you look at AI, and you think the potential is there to change the world, healthcare. We have done this all week on a number of big fronts, Max. But there just are concerns about how far it goes.\par \par \uc2 TEGMARK: Yes. You have had a lot of reporting on FOX about the problem of big tech having too much control over what you find on the Internet. And I think it's crucial that the politicians in Washington don't just listen to the lobbyists from these tech companies, but also reach out to the scientists and researchers building this stuff. \uc2 Yoshua Bengio\uc2 , the first signatory on the letter, right, he is one of the pioneers who invented deep learning that gave us all this. Get people like that educating our political leaders so they can get the upside of AI and not allow stuff that they're just going to lose control over. Because you don't win any wars by developing stuff you just lose control over yourself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. Last word, Carl, if these folks sign this letter and say, hey, we need to slow down, shouldn't we listen to them?\par \par \uc2 SZABO: The last thing we want is innovation at the speed of Congress. We have seen what this type of ban everything until we allow it regime works like in Europe where they have no technology sector to speak of. The last thing the U.S. can afford to do at this point is to give up our technological advantage much in the way that we have given up our energy advantage around the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gentlemen, this conversation is going to continue, something tells me. Hopefully, I will be here and it won't be AI anchor asking the questions, but we really appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 LAWSON: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the chairman of the House Judiciary Committee Jim Jordan on a field trip to District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's doorstep next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The murder suspect accused of killing Cash App founder Bob Lee will remain in custody following today's court appearance. Thirty-eight-year-old Nima Momeni faces a murder charge in Lee's stabbing death. Sources tell FOX Momeni was giving Lee a ride back to his hotel before the attack. His next court appearance will be April 25th.\par \par \uc2 House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan is moving forward with his decision to hold a hearing in New York City next week focusing on crime. Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's office is calling it a political stunt. Tensions between the two have been rising since Bragg's indictment of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Senior national correspondent Rich Edson has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: A Republican-led hearing about crime in a Democrat-run city.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: We want to hear from the victims, the families, the people in Manhattan, the people in New York who have been impacted by this surge in crime that has happened because you have D.A.s who are more focused on coddling criminals.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Monday's House Judiciary Committee hearing in Manhattan will include victims and victims' advocates. Jose Alba is scheduled to appear, the bodega clerk who stabbed and killed a man who had assaulted him. Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  charged Alba with second degree murder and then later dropped the charges after significant public outcry.\par \par \uc2 Bragg is also the prosecutor who indicted former President Trump. Committee Republicans maintain this session has nothing to do with the former president.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JEFF VAN DREW\uc2 , (R-NJ) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: This is not the time we're going to talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Major crime in New York grew 22 percent last year over the year before. Robberies and burglaries were up, shootings and murders were down. Mayor Eric Adams says nearly every major crime category is down now compared to last year. Adams dismissed the GOP hearing.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: It's just unfortunate during a time like this they will use taxpayers' dollars to host this charade.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Bragg's office says New York is safer than major cities in Ohio and, quote, "If Chairman Jordan truly cared about public safety, he could take a short drive to Columbus, Dayton, Cincinnati, Cleveland, Akron, or Toledo in his home state."\par \par \uc2 Democrats say they plan to focus on Republican inaction on gun control and Trump's calls to defund the FBI and Justice Department as federal law enforcement continues investigating him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The confrontation between Bragg and Jordan will continue beyond this hearing. Jordan is investigating Bragg's Trump indictment. Bragg is suing Jordan to halt the inquiry. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday lightning round, the 2024 presidential landscape, which changed tonight. Reaction to my interview with \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . Plus, abortion politics and winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. France's constitutional council approves an unpopular plan to raise the retirement age from 62 to 64 in a victory for French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  after three months of mass protests over the legislation. Macron can enact that bill within 15 days.\par \par \uc2 And a European spacecraft rockets away from South America on a decade-long quest to explore Jupiter and three of its icy moons that are believed to hold buried oceans. It will take the robotic explorer dubbed Juice eight years to reach Jupiter.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Susan and I have now been thinking about this, working at it, and prayerfully come to the conclusion that we are not going to join the race in 2024, that while we care deeply about America and the issues that I have been talking about this last year- and-a-half and, frankly, for decades matter an awful lot, this isn't our moment. This isn't the time for us to seek elected office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  not in the 2024 presidential race, said the reasons were personal, not about the polls or his positioning, and going forward wants to talk about substance. The folks who are in, it's an interesting mix. And we will see how many are in in the end.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel and start there, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Charles Hurt, opinion editor for "The Washington Times," There are, Charlie, so many lanes that are not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And right now, the former president is really sucking up all the oxygen. If you look at the polls, they are growing, not shrinking. So maybe that's a part of it.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, OPINION EDITOR, "THE WASHINGTON TIMES": Yes, it kind of reminds you of another election, maybe 2016. Although, it does appear to be that maybe there are going to be fewer people in the race this time than there were in 2016. But I thought it was interesting what \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  said. He said this is about people -- about issues and not about people. And it is sort of an interesting moment to stop and reflect the degree to which the Republican Party has been completely reshaped over the past seven years. And all of that is owing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I would argue that the Republican Party, whoever they wind up with, on the issue -- on the issues, is a wildly winning group of issues. And I think they are better off for it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": I think a lot of people conflate personality and political issues. The Republican voters are overwhelmingly between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , two interesting personalities, yes, but two people who are very different from this old guard Republican Party, the type that \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  might better represent. And they are excited by these issues, this focus on economic well-being of the middle class and a different foreign policy than the one that marked the Bush era of interventionist wars without good strategy in site, things like bailouts for corporations. This is very different, but the voters seem to like these issues, and they like the personalities that go with them, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think what you are seeing is a lot of turbulence at what I think of as sort of second tier. I think Mollie, Charlie hit it right on the head. Trump is way ahead, and then you have DeSantis, if he gets in, right behind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And most assume he is going to get in. He is telling contributors maybe.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you. But the question is, what happens if the Trump bubble bursts? Are you positioned to take advantage of that moment? I think that's why this week we saw Tim Scott get in. Then you have Nikki Haley there. You have \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  there. You have all those three cases, by the way, they are competing for evangelical vote very strongly, thinking, again, if a Trump bubble bursts, could it be that evangelicals lose their flavor, their taste for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  given his past behavior?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of evangelicals and issue that is getting a lot of attention now, Charlie, is abortion. There are, as you look at a map, states that have passed six week abortion bans, a number of them, including Florida just recently. "Axios" saying "Florida was considered a refuge for people seeking abortions in the south, particularly since nearby Alabama, Georgia, Louisiana, and Mississippi have bans in place that have forced abortion clinics to close. If enacted the six-week ban would not take until the Florida Supreme Court rules on the legality of the state's current 15- week ban which is now being challenged."\par \par \uc2 It's an issue. The abortion pill is an issue in the Supreme Court. How do you think Republicans work with this issue considering where the midterms were?\par \par \uc2 HURT: I think they have done a very poor job so far in defining where they stand on it. And obviously they can complain about the media and all that kind of stuff, the distortions. But at the end of the day, the Republican view on abortion is far more in line with people than the Democratic view, where you have this hot defense of abortion up to the last moment of a pregnancy. It's kind of amazing that Republican haven't found their voice and figured out a way to turn the focus away from them waffling on the issue, and just putting a laser pointed focus on how out-of-step most Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And they haven't gotten that across the boards on the party. They just haven't figured out the messaging, at least not yet, candidate to candidate, Mollie.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And their big promise they are not even trying. And the real issue is that the media lie about this issue. They lie in a manner that makes it seem like what the vast majority of Americans believe, which is that should be some protection for life at some point in the pregnancy, that that is some radical viewpoint. That's what most Americans believe. That is something very much in line with what Republicans believe. And yet, they have allowed themselves to be -- they have allowed the media to reshape and lie about this. Playing defense will not work. They need to affirmatively make the case if they want to win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some breaking news just got in from President Biden. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have already made that calculus. We will announce it relatively soon. But the trip here just reinforced my sense of optimism about what can be done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So have you made a decision, you just have to announce it?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I told you my plan is to run again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So he said it in a different way, Juan, that his plan, he has made the decision to run again and that announcement will be soon. It's a little bit maybe farther than he went on the Easter egg roll.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That was Monday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, so the evolution in Ireland has taken us to a different place.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Look, I think the man intends to run. You know what, you don't have to wait for what he says because politicians will kind of play word games. But you look at the idea that this week they announced the convention will be held in Chicago. I think that's a Biden decision. You look at the decision that was made about how the states, the primaries, will operate, the fact that South Carolina is now going to be the first as opposed to Iowa. That's a Biden decision. So you see the political machine rolling, and it rolls for who? Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It also potentially helps \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , maybe. You could say that.\par \par \uc2 Winners and Losers. Charlie, first to you, winner then loser.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Winners are the unborn children of Florida. Governor DeSantis signed a heartbeat bill there, as you pointed out, with exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Loser of the week is Bud Light. They, I guess, decided to move away from frat bros and managed to lose several billion dollars in market cap.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Winner, Jon Rahm, that beautiful Masters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nice job.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It was great to watch. And then the loser would be the intelligence community which has had yet another embarrassing leak which revealed that so much of what they've been claiming about how the war in Ukraine and other things are going are not actually true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I've got to go with the \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2 . Wow, what a week, right? So they have won every game and they have a chance to break the record for starting the season with the most wins.\par \par \uc2 The loser would be the Tennessee House Republican majority. You see these two black Democrats who had been expelled back in the legislature in less than a week, and celebrated nationally.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thanks. Make it a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see you.\par \par \uc2 You know that sound. It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is so much cheaper for you to operate an electric vehicle than it is to fill up your tank.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a war on the car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you accept to lose your sovereignty, you put yourself in a situation not to decide for yourself.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: You got this crazy world that's blowing up, and Macron, who is a friend of mine, is over with China kissing his ass.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't want to have any decoupling from China because we really think that this is illusion, but we want to de-risk from China.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Macron would be eating schnitzel instead of croissants if it wasn't for us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think anyone who bets against the United States does so at own peril.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have active shooter in our building.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our LMPD officers went into that building knowing that they were risking their lives.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're obstructing our constitutional duty to do oversight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: You've got highly sensitive classified information now in the public domain, and that is simply unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not concerned about the weight. I'm concerned that it happened.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did the leak concern you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am very concentrated, and all Ukrainians very concentrated on the preparation of our counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let's go lick the world. Let's get it done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's go lick the world. All right, have a great weekend. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230415ej4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pentagon Leak Suspect Hit With 2 Federal Charges; Intel Committee Member On National Security Impact Of Leaks; U.S. Intel Policies Unders Scrutiny After Doc Leaks; Protests Erupt In France Over Pension Age Overhaul; \uc2 NRA\uc2  Convention Draws Top GOP 2024 Hopefuls; DeSantis Speaks In Virginia As 2024 Buzz Picks Up; Biden: Nothing Of "Great Consequence" In Leak; Flashback: Biden Downplays Issues Facing Nation; New Details Revealed About Pentagon Leak Suspect; Students Tempted By AI To Cheat On Schoolwork; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Issues New Warning About Threat Of AI; SCOTUS Keeps \uc2 FDA\uc2  Rules On Abortion Drug For Now\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum Alexis McAdams, Greg Palkot, Garrett Tenney,  Jacqui Heinrich, Geraldo Rivera, Doug Schoen, Lucas Tomlinson, Joe Concha,  Kat Timpf, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voiceover): Ports, for I think, almost 40 minutes. And this was the first time the country got to see these images of what was happening there in Del Rio, by far the craziest moment of my border coverage so far.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I know I speak for our colleagues for the network. And for our viewers, thank you so much for everything you're doing to cover the southern border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): No problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And beyond.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Big story. And it's my pleasure, and I look forward to joining you live at the border once again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Excellent. Thanks so much, Bill. Thanks for joining us, everybody. Have a great weekend. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And I'm Bill Melugin, THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News Headquarters in New York. And breaking right now, on THE STORY new details this hour of 21- year-old, Jack Teixeira, and the charges that he is now facing that could put this young man in prison for 10 years for taking and disseminating -- that's the crucial part here -- top secret U.S. intelligence.\par \par \uc2 But it is also looking more and more like a tale of sloppiness in our systems, and yet more negative impacts on our society of COVID's locked down isolation as this young man allegedly escaped into a world online forming an alternate community that somehow evaded our top investigators for months. So, how did that happen?\par \par \uc2 Congressman Brad Wenstrup from the House Intelligence Committee stands by with his thoughts on all of this. First to Correspondent Alexis McAdams, she's in Boston where all of the action is happening this afternoon at the courthouse in Boston, Massachusetts. Hi, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, it's been an active day here outside of that federal courthouse in Boston. This is where that suspect was in front of a judge just a short time ago. This all comes though is the question remains, again, this afternoon, Martha, of how would a 21-year-old with a high school diploma possibly have access to some of the country's top secrets and some new documents? We just got to look at Martha show that Jack Teixeira looks like he knew that some federal authorities were looking into him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voiceover): Take a look at those new documents we just got in our newsroom a short time ago. Those are the newly released affidavit there saying that the I.T. specialist with the Massachusetts Air National Guard had been using his clearance to search the intelligence database trying to figure out if officials knew about that leak.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say, he had become concerned that he may be discovered making the transcriptions of text in the workplace. So, he began taking those documents to his home and photographing them. Here's a look at the 21-year- old in court today in these new sketches. You can see him wearing a jumpsuit they're standing in front of the judge, as he learned about the two federal charges he faces.\par \par \uc2 Federal agents did raid his house yesterday, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  arrested the Massachusetts Air National Guardsman at his mom's house. The video there you're looking at shows and slowly walking down that driveway as agents had their weapons drawn. He was an I.T. specialists in the Massachusetts Air National Guard, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the top-secret documents had detailed maps of Ukraine and assessments of Russia's army. The online leak potentially damaging U.S. relations and compromising our intelligence.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): So, I think -- I'm not giving this guy any credit. He needs to go to jail for a long time. And we need to plug the leaks that allow someone who didn't have a valid need to know all of this information to have unfettered access.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, on his way out of court today, Martha, as he was in handcuffs, he told his dad he loved him, he'll be held in federal custody until he's back here next week, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, sounds like his family's pretty shocked about everything that's going on. Alexis, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Republican Congressman Brad Wenstrup, a member of the House Intelligence Committee, he served in the Army Reserves for 24 years, including a tour in Iraq, where he was awarded a Bronze Star and Combat Action Badge for his service. Congressman, always good to have you with us. Thank you very much for being here. Your thoughts on this as we get a look at some of these documents and the charges here as all of this becomes more clear, and I think more frightening in a lot of ways today?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD WENSTRUP (R-OH): Yes, you know, it really is more and more frightening as you look at it. And, you know, here is a 21-year-old Massachusetts Air Guardsmen. His job is cyber transport systems journeyman. He's supposed to install, configure, and repair systems. He's a lowly enlisted rank, and I'm not sure what clearance he actually had, but he certainly had access to way too much considering his rank, and time, and grade.\par \par \uc2 And so, they also have said they were supposed to be at Fort Bragg and never saying he was never there. I'm curious as to whether he was activated or was he going through two weeks of training as a Guardsman. His training should just be around simulation type of materials. There is more questions than there are answers and they keep coming out every day. The question is, what was really the motive?\par \par \uc2 Now, you touched on something before I came on the effects of the lockdowns in the pandemic and this group formed -- was he a 21-year-old just trying to impress them with the information they could that he could put out and why were these documents as reported altered? What was the motive for that? You know, we're concerned on the Intelligence Committee too that the media was aware of this, but for our intelligence committee was --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that, that -- you know, we talked about the sloppiness, I think, it goes to so many different levels. And I think it has really permeated so much of our functioning post-pandemic here. It seems like, you know, we're still really off our game and a lot of ways, and I think some of that is maybe reflected in this story, he had top secret clearance that was given to him in 2021. Then, he also gained SCI, which is Sensitive Compartmented Access as well, in 2021. So, he had very sophisticated access here. Here's \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  talking about this earlier. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Department of Defense is leading an important effort now to evaluate and review the national security implications, and most important, to conduct a review of the methods of access accountability and control procedures that the department has so that something like this can never happen again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I mean, he's a fairly soft-spoken person in most situations. But does that give you a sense of confidence? If you're at home watching this, that the government is all over this in the way they need to be?\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: No, it doesn't. And it's just all concerning too, I just don't understand why this young man had that type of a clearance. 25 years in the Army Reserve and a deployment and I was always at the secret level, not top secret, not know -- no foreign. Now, on intelligence committee, I have all access, as you might imagine, but, but the military through that time, I always had secret. Why did he have such a high level of clearance?\par \par \uc2 This whole thing is very upsetting in so many ways, because American lives and even the lives of our allies are on the line. And this was a major, major breach of documents, charts of troop strength weapons that are being delivered. This began in early March. And where are we now? The middle of April, just finding out about it. That's very concerning is well, as you as you mentioned, just seems sloppy all the way across.\par \par \uc2 I just, I just don't understand it. And as an intelligence committee member, I know we're going to be conducting hearings and try and clean some of this mess up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's the next question. It's my understanding that the, that our intelligence agencies don't have a unit that is dedicated to screening these sorts of darker areas of the Internet all the time to see if something is out there. Doesn't it seem like that's something that we ought to have in 2023?\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: Yes, you know, it just seems that we could have pretty strong search engines for the things like that, that we should be interested in knowing. And especially, how about anything that pops up that has no foreign on it? Or TS-SCI on it? You know, why can we not be looking for these types of things? There are so many ways of doing it. Actually, this young man probably knows how to do that. And he certainly beat everything by doing this. The question is: the motives. Is this a misinformation campaign? Was he acting alone? Was he just trying to show off? We got a lot of questions that need to be answered and our systems definitely need to be cleaned up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is for sure. Our Jacqui Heinrich reporting that the charges that he faces are the same that Edward Snowden faced in the past. So more on this as we get it in here this afternoon. Congressman Wenstrup, thank you. Always good to have you here, Sir.\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Geraldo Rivera, Joe Concha, and Kat Timpf, all on the big wall for THE STORY all coming up this hour as some of the biggest names in the running for the GOP presidential primary descend on Indiana this afternoon. They are live at an \uc2 NRA\uc2  gathering and we will take you there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some new fiery protests in Paris at this hour after a victory for President Macron in his battle to shake up the pension system. He raised France's retirement age from 62 to 64 years old without going through Parliament triggering months of sometimes violent demonstrations and there was reaction in the streets tonight, as well as this news hit just a short time ago. Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot following this story for us from London. Hi, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Yes, we might be looking at a final boost to a very beleaguered French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , but his critics promise to fight on. Yes, it's all about Macron's efforts to raise that retirement age in France from 62 to 64. Officials say, with an aging population that just can't afford the pension system as it is, and that has prompted months of protest. Well, nationwide days of strikes and marches just this year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voiceover): Today, France's Constitutional Court, is basically the top court in the land, ruled -- got a lot of barricades -- largely in favor that bill that was rammed through parliament. The court did have a few issues left the door open to a possible referendum but agreed with the main point that people in France have to work longer. The plan does remain hugely unpopular. However, the streets were full of angry protesters today clashing with police setting fires with the promise of more unrest to come.\par \par \uc2 Although, it must be said Martha, that the numbers protesting and striking have been slipping in recent days. And in fact, as this day goes on, Macron, who on this day toured Notre Dame, checking out reconstruction efforts after that fire that badly damaged the cathedral is intent on trying to rebuild support. He says, he's open to discussing the bill with union leaders but seems set on holding on to this bruised achievement in his second and last term in office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Martha, must be said, the popularity of Macron and his party slipping badly, he's going to hang on but it's not quite clear what else he could accomplish in the coming years. There's a lot of bad feelings out there in the street.\par \par \uc2 Back to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, they're running out of money for these programs, and he's trying to stretch it as much as he can across the people who are trying to be beneficiaries of it.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot, thank you very much. We'll keep an eye on that, with your coverage there from London.\par \par \uc2 So, a live look now at the \uc2 NRA\uc2 's annual meeting, which is underway at this hour in Indianapolis. Lots of Republican presidential hopefuls in this crowd.\par \par \uc2 Potential candidates, already declared candidate speaking. This is Kristi Noem, the governor of South Dakota, who's gotten a lot of attention over the past few years and is certainly somebody whose name could enter this ring.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is recording from there, live. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Martha. In my best (INAUDIBLE) voice here.\par \par \uc2 This event is a rite of passage for GOP candidates who are trying to win over this key voting bloc of the party. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  just wrapped up his remarks a little bit ago, and he is going to have a little bit of a challenge, it seems, as he took in left the stage.\par \par \uc2 There were some boo's mixed in for him. But he got a lot of applause, as he called for a federal mandated death penalty for mass shooters and also criticize Democrats for their handling of the crime -- rising crime crisis across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: President Biden, the Democrats have returned to the same tired arguments about gun control and gun confiscation. But we don't need gun control. We need crime control. We don't need lectures about the liberties of law-abiding citizens, we need solutions to protect our kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): President Trump will be the final speaker today. And there will be video messages from former Ambassador Nikki Haley, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 This conference comes on the heels of mass shootings in Louisville and Nashville that have renewed the debate over gun control, which is definitely going to be an issue that plays into the general election.\par \par \uc2 But almost without exception, folks we spoke to hear, say even after these latest shootings, and they don't think that new laws on gun control will make a difference.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think we can change it with laws, I think we need to be more careful. We got to address the mental health issues in this country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If they're mentally ill, they should not be able to get a gun. And I think that's the case. Anytime anybody kills people, they're mentally ill.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, we need to do something with the people. We need to punish them. Hold them accountable for what they commit and not quit blaming the guns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Yes. Voters as a whole, has a different story, though.\par \par \uc2 In the latest Fox News poll, 69 percent of registered voters said they are concerned about gun laws. So, this is going to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 But to keep an eye on especially going into the general election next year. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Garrett, thank you very much. We'll keep an eye on that live event ongoing this hour in Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 And also, President Biden's critics saying that he has a tendency to downplay some of the significant threats that we face.\par \par \uc2 He has barely addressed the Intel link so far. He's been in Ireland for four days on this trip, but he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: But there's nothing contemporaneous that I'm aware of that is of great consequence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo Rivera and Doug Schoen debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a bill that passed in the Florida Legislature to ban abortion after six weeks, with exceptions to 15 weeks for medical emergencies, rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis tweeting a photo of the private bill signing, bit different than the large public ceremony that was held a year ago when he's signed a ban on abortion after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 And this is the closest he came to mentioning this new six week ban in a speech that he gave today at \uc2 Liberty University\uc2  in Virginia. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We have elevated the importance of family and promoted a culture of life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Liberty University\uc2  did mention the Heartbeat Abortion Ban. I'm going to introduce the Florida governor who is widely expected to run for president.\par \par \uc2 And after ignoring reporters' questions about the leak of classified intelligence, about the war on Ukraine, about China's hypersonic missiles, President Biden just said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you concerned about the leak?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I'm not concerned about the leak. I just -- I'm concerned that it happened. But there is nothing contemporaneous that I'm aware of that is of great consequence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In a moment, Geraldo Rivera and Doug Schoen. First, to White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich, live in County Mayo, Ireland.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Martha. Yes. Well, the president's attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  seems to think that this is a pretty serious situation, talking about the charges that Jack Teixeira is facing. One of them for mishandling classified material, the other falling under the Espionage Act, the same violation that Edward Snowden faced.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: There are very serious penalties associated with that. People who sign agreements to be able to receive classified documents acknowledge the importance to the national security of not disclosing those documents.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president weighed-in for the first time since the suspect's arrest, praising law enforcement for rapidly responding to the incident, and saying in a statement, "While we're still determining the validity of those documents, I've directed our military and intelligence community to take further steps to secure and limit distribution of sensitive information, and our national security team is closely coordinating with our partners and allies."\par \par \uc2 But Biden's comment yesterday that he's not concerned about the leak because there is, "nothing contemporaneous of great consequence there," prompted concern from both sides of the aisle about Biden's perceived apathy.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): This puts our intelligence officers at risks. This threatens our military. This puts the Ukrainians at risk.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH): This is not a game. And I think that level of concern, understanding that these documents actually translate to people's lives.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): So, it's unclear if the president intends to make any mention of the arrest or of the intelligence breach here in Ballina. He chose not to when speaking at a banquet dinner last night, and this is a comparatively much more festive atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 But it is one of the last times we're going to see the president before he heads back to United States. And he's spending the weekend in Rehoboth not due to be back at the White House until Monday night, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Four full days on the ground for the president in Ireland. Jacqui, thank you very much. Let's bring in our panel, Geraldo Rivera, Fox News correspondent-at-large and co-host of "THE FIVE".\par \par \uc2 And Doug Schoen, Democratic strategist and pollster, former adviser to President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 I'd actually like to replay --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Right.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: You too, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- start by replaying President Biden's reaction when he was asked about these leaks just a short time ago in Ireland watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you concerned about the leak?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I'm not concerned about the leak. I just -- I'm concerned that it happened. But there is nothing contemporaneous that I'm aware of that is of great consequence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo, what do you think about that answer?\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Well, it sounds a little laid back, Martha. But I will say that having read just about anything I can find on the arrest of this kid and the group that he was hacking with, there is a conclusion from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  documents do not appear to contain much if any information from human sources.\par \par \uc2 In other words, there, no one is outed, no spies are revealed. That's always the biggest concern when you have one of these leaks, that your confidential spy, the person that you have deep, ingrained in the -- in the enemy camp will be revealed, exposed.\par \par \uc2 That does not appear to be happening. There is no -- as far as we can tell, no information from human sources.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's less about, you know, wicked evil traders, although the kids in terrible, terrible trouble. Don't get me wrong. And more about dopey hackers who have really gotten themselves weigh in over their heads.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we've also had very different takes on it in terms of what was revealed about how we surveil others, how we surveil Russia, China, Israel, some pretty sticky stuff, and a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, it goes to sort of a long list, though, of times when this president has sort of sought to downplay that was his, you know, has been his choice his go to in a number of different occasions.\par \par \uc2 Here is a montage that kind of reflects this. Watch this, Doug.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover of Afghanistan now inevitable?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it is not.\par \par \uc2 There is nobody suggesting there's nonsense inflation on the way.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF, SENIOR CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWSHOUR: When you said just possessing classified documents is -- you said, totally irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: In the best of my knowledge, the kinds of things they picked up are things that from 1974, and straight papers. Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me ask you about the Chinese balloon. It wasn't a major breach -- security breach for the United States.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is not a major breach.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Doug, what do you make of that? Obviously, not correct on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , not correct on inflation, and not correct on the fact that it wasn't a major breach, we now know that they did pick up intelligence from a number of our military bases. They have the ability to do that from that balloon.\par \par \uc2 You know, would you advise him to have a different tone with some of these things?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Absolutely, Martha. To my way of thinking, what I would have told him to say is, look, we take this very seriously, we're very worried that our intelligence gathering, however, it be done, has been compromised.\par \par \uc2 We're going to do a thorough review of our intelligence, and that anyone complicit or indirectly involved will be prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law. And I think --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But no one believes that, Doug, because that's what never happens. OK?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we have these exhaustive investigations into the former president, you can say those are legitimate. But if that is -- but if it is, we don't see. I mean, how about, you know, whoever gave him this clearance, we are going, you know, could possibly be fired.\par \par \uc2 The person who's his superior could possibly be fired. This is absolutely unacceptable. We're going to, you know, investigate to figure out who is in charge here of this young man and make sure that they are held accountable.\par \par \uc2 We don't ever hear language like that.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Absolutely. Absolutely, I couldn't agree more. And the tone is wrong. The policy is not tough enough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo, quick thought before we go.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Hundreds of generals, and admirals, and navy captains, and colonels, and aides, and then staff to aides get these documents.\par \par \uc2 We make too many things top secret. We distributed them way, way too much. And, you know, it's an -- it's a -- it's an accident waiting to happen.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Or a spy thriller waiting shooting to happen, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's for sure.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there is a sloppiness that has crept into so many of our institutions that we should be able to have ultimate confidence in. And I think people are sick of it. I think they really are.\par \par \uc2 Doug, thank you very much. Geraldo, good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo, good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we have a new statement just in from the parent company of Bud Light, now responding to the backlash over their controversial partnership with influencer and transgender activist Dylan Mulvaney. Breaking moment ago a new statement from the head of, \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2 . They've lost a ton of business in the past week or two over to the backlash to this Bud Light teaming up with this influencer, Dylan Mulvaney.\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Brendan Whitworth\uc2  saying, "We never intended to be part of a discussion that divides people. We are in the business of bringing people together over a beer. I spend much of my time traveling across America, he says, listening and learning from our customers. He goes on to say, they're distributors and others, moving forward, I will continue to work tirelessly to bring great beers to consumers across our nation. And to that, I just say dilly-dilly, dilly-dilly. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're learning a bit more about how this online chat group apparently helped expose US intelligence secrets to the world. That members telling the New York Times 21-year-old National Guard Airman Jack Teixeira was their unofficial leader, and they bonded during the isolation of the COVID pandemic. Yet another negative that came out of those lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 They say Teixeira was trying to inform and impress them with the classified documents that were supposed to stay private in their chat room. But a 17- year-old member decided to make them public, maybe not even realizing what he'd done.\par \par \uc2 Former Pentagon intelligence analyst Kara Frederick and retired \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director Jack Bennett both know a lot about this. They are standing by. First to correspondent Lucas Tomlinson live in Washington. Hi, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. First, some more from the Justice Department. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said the US military could also be bringing charges in the future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , US ATTORNEY GENERAL: This case is being pursued in federal civilian courts, and the next appearance, as I said, will be a detention hearing on Wednesday. That's how the Justice Department proceeds. I don't want to say anything about what else might be possible.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Jack Teixeira led an online group called Thug Shaker Central on the social messaging app \uc2 Discord\uc2 , where he first began posting classified information, some labeled top secret in December to impress its young members, according to the affidavit released today. In January, Teixeira began posting photographs of the documents.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports he first started posting the first of over 350 documents even sooner going back to October. The online group was originally formed during the COVID pandemic. The group consisted of young men, many in their teens. It was also a place to play video games and chat. Some said they looked up to the 21-year-old guardsman as a "father figure." Many current and former members of the US military have long been concerned with how many people have access to the military's most secure server, known as the Joint Worldwide Intelligence Communication System, better known by its acronym JWICS, which houses the nation's most sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Many are asking how one of the lowest ranking members of the Massachusetts Air National Guard was getting access to the briefings for the highest ranking member of the US military, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lucas, thank you very much. Let's bring in Kara Frederick, former Pentagon intelligence analyst, now Director of Tech Policy at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , and Jack Bennett, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director, now Managing Director in the cyber risk practice at \uc2 Kroll\uc2 . Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, Jack, one of the questions I'm sure you've heard people talking about since this happened is, how did the media find out that this was going on before the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JACK BENNETT, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: Well, make no mistake, Martha, the media is exceptionally good at what they do. So this was a lot of points were out in the public, and the media does what they do best, and they can track a story down, as well as some of the most seasoned investigators.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But why -- I heard somewhere else, in another source this morning, that there's not really a dedicated group at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or at the Pentagon for that matter, Kara, that really sort of spends all of their time on these networks making sure that things are not getting out into the public through these venues. Is that true that we don't have a dedicated agent group to this?\par \par \uc2 KARA FREDERICK, FORMER PENTAGON INTELLIGENCE ANALYST: There's been a lot of red teaming, there's been a lot of exercising, there's a lot of counterintelligence professionals that work at the Pentagon who are dedicated to these sorts of missions. But to Jack's point, you have your open source researchers like \uc2 Bellingcat\uc2  that are so good, as you said, at what they do. They have regional expertise in many ways. They have language expertise. They have graphing capabilities.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is sort of the frontier of counterintelligence. The technical acumen that a lot of these groups have that can outpace the more sclerotic employees of the Pentagon, frankly. That's the name of the game, the private sector all the way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, that feels scary, Jack. That feels not right. I mean, shouldn't the \uc2 FBI\uc2  hire people from \uc2 Bellingcat\uc2  and make sure that when these things happen, we don't have to wait for months of having them out there before we find out?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I think, Martha, it's a great point that you bring up. There are dedicated teams that work with Department of Defense counterparts. Air Force OSI is very good at what they do, as well as Army CID. And to Kara's point, there are great resources back and forth talking about what is anomaly that some agency may be picking up on. And everybody will start diving into that. If you think about the millions of people that are involved in the DOD community, in the Intel community, it's a pretty daunting task to overwatch all of that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He enlisted, Kara, in 2018. In 2021, he was given top secret clearance. He was also given, after that SCI clearance systems compartmentalized access to all those documents. And the question is why?\par \par \uc2 FREDERICK: Yes. In order to perform some of these functions, it's important that they're able to access these systems. I think it's also worth noting, you know, Snowden was a systems administrator, as a contractor for the NSA. This guy is an IT specialist, as far as we know. And Americans, I don't think they necessarily understand what was previously talked about JWICS, that top secret system, there are massive distribution lists here that have those finished intelligence products like \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  has been talking about on your network all day.\par \par \uc2 So it is very difficult to determine chains of custody for a lot of these finished intelligence products which had those assessments that he leaked. So, you know, unfortunately a function of the military, a lot of young people are given great responsibility. They should be able to handle it, and this guy couldn't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We talk about the effects of COVID, the lockdowns, isolation. How does the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , do you think, handle that now, Jack, in terms of seeking out what's going on out there? Because it feels, again, like these sort of tech savvy groups are at an advantage with this information and that there's a sloppiness that has gone on since these lockdowns in some of our institutions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Martha, as you well know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just can't monitor discord or other social media. There's got to be a predicated reason for an investigation to be moved forward on. So it is -- unless somebody is letting the \uc2 FBI\uc2  know or it's being picked up in other intelligence channels from our partners around the world, it is something that it is -- there's a lot of it out there. And the Bureau just can't willy-nilly be monitoring social media.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I understand that. And, you know, that they would need a warrant basically to go after some of these places which seem to be proliferating and getting away with a heck of a lot. So Jack Bennett, thank you very much. Kara Frederick, it's great to have you both experts in your field. And we really appreciate you coming by today, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FREDERICK: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So students dish to Fox Digital about whether they're tempted to turn to AI to get their homework done, as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  issues this warning to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, \uc2 TWITTER\uc2  CEO: AI is more dangerous than, say, mismanaged aircraft design or production maintenance or bad car production in the sense that it has the potential. How the small one may regard that probability, but it is nontrivial. It has the potential of civilizational destruction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe Concha and Kat Timpf on civilizational destruction next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No more writing notes on your hand. Artificial intelligence is giving students a very high tech way to cheat, or at least to get some groundwork put in on essays or papers. That's how they're using it in many ways. Fox News Digital asked kids at the University of Texas if they knew. Do any other students who are doing this, getting some help from AI? Well, here's what they said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I definitely have heard of like a couple of people using it for certain things. I do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Unfortunately, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes. Yes. Not other people, actually me. Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for the Hill and Fox News contributor and Kat Timpf, "Gutfeld!" co-host and author of the new book "You Can't Joke About that," which we're going to be talking about coming up as well. Great to have you guys here.\par \par \uc2 You know, should it be OK? Should people be able to use chat GPT to write papers and to answer questions on homework?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Sounds like using a calculator during a math exam, right? It'd be like me going to the gym and then having a machine lift the weights for me, right? Recentstudy.com poll, just to latch onto that piece by Foxnews.com, 89% of students admit using ChatGPS to complete homework --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: GPT.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: GPT, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It doesn't direct you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Oh boy, GPT complete homework assignments. That's nearly nine in ten. The good news is there are inexpensive apps out there like GPTZero that can detect ChatGPT.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So crazy.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And teachers are using --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And now you've got to have AI to figure out if people are cheating using AI. Here's Joe Rogan who did this interesting thing. He put together an entire show that is completely driven by AI. It's not Joe Rogan, sorry. So weird the world we're living in, OK? This looks like Joe Rogan but it's all made up and fake. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROE ROGAN, PODCAST HOST: Ladies and gentlemen, welcome to the very first episode of the Joe Rogan AI Experience. I'm your host Joe Rogan, or at least that's what this AI model thinks I sound like. I am not the real Joe Rogan. Let me repeat it once more. I am not Joe Rogan, and the guests are not of the people they portray. This is purely fiction, just for fun. Don't go around making little clips for your TikToks and make the world believe I said things I never said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Certainly sounds like them, right? Kat, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX HOST: Yes. I think -- I mean, obviously, in the long run, this isn't going to make people will have to work less, it's going to make them have to work harder. Because if ChatGPT can do something, there'll really be no reason for you to be hired to do it. I think that's why it would be more important to cultivate your own writing skills and learn to do things that ChatGPT can't do.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you see it as a tool, that's one thing, but if you're using it to just cheat and do the work for you're ultimately only cheating yourself.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: No US test scores.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sounds good -- yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Right, 30 year low. Where do you think they're going to go after this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: The students aren't doing the work themselves. And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  reported today that he's going to launch a rival to ChatGPT. So this industry, it's 27 billion now, AI. It's going to be ten times bigger just by 2027.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: You just have to make yourself irreplaceable. You have to figure out what that is and work towards that specific thing, because this stuff is not going away. We can't just say, oh, this looks like this is bad. Nobody -- it doesn't matter, there's money to be made there. This is technology that's advancing. It's not going to stop.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I can't figure out if it feels like the weird future that we've heard about is actually here and we don't understand it. And it's already unfolding. And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , you heard him essentially saying that there's potential for this to be civilization ending, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes. Movie night tonight will be Terminator 1 or 2.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's not like they didn't tell us it was coming.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: The technology is moving at such an alarming rate past anything that we could keep up with. It doesn't seem like too many people in Washington are concerned about this. They should be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They don't understand it.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: No.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They have no idea. And you know what, neither do I, for that matter. I mean, it's like you can get bits and pieces of it and try to understand it and people tell you it's scary.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: (Inaudible) wrote her book, by the way.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I did. That's (inaudible) right? I wrote every single word of my book.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you actually here right now?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes. And if you read my book, I feel like nobody else could have written the book that I wrote except for me. A robot could not have written my book. So kids, get on it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. My son sent me this poem the other day that was like, write a poem about this. And it was in a text message, and it was crazy.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it was written in Shakespearean prose style. I was like, wait, where did this come from? Who wrote this?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: A robot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He said I just put it into ChatGPT-4. There are people who are pushing back against this. Jennifer Garner is one of them. She's saying that she's not even allowing her kids to be on social media, which isn't, you know, just sort of the baby layer of all of this, but here she is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GARNER, ACTRESS: I just said to my kids, tell me, show me the articles that prove that social media is good for teenagers, and then we'll have the conversation.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC HOST: Oh my gosh, facts.\par \par \uc2 GARNER: Find scientific evidence that matches what I have that says that it's not good for teenagers, then we'll chat.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC HOST: And how do they feel at this point?\par \par \uc2 GARNER: My eldest is grateful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Guys, I'm going to have to leave you with that thought. We've got some breaking news that we've got to get to. Thank you so much, Kat, for being here and, Joe, for being here. And,, Kat, look forward to your book launch and talking to you about that on the podcast next week.\par \par \uc2 So breaking moments ago, the Supreme Court keeping the current \uc2 FDA\uc2  rules in place for now, on the medication used in most abortions in this country. 61% of all abortions that happen in this country, 61 do not happen at a clinic. They happen with this medication at home. Shannon Bream joins us live with this breaking news. Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Hey, Martha. So we just got these orders from Justice Alito. He is the justice that oversees this particular circuit out of Texas, and what he's done for now is put that lower court decision from the district court judge, the one a couple of weeks ago. Well, it may be less than that. It feels like a lot has happened between now and then.\par \par \uc2 But essentially saying that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  drug could not be used as approved, that the approval process was flawed in some way, and said you can't have it at all. He has put that on hold until at least Wednesday. What he's asked these parties in the meantime to do is to file their responses with the court by Tuesday at noon Eastern. Meaning the court expects to act very quickly.\par \par \uc2 So, what that means for right now is this Mifepristone, which is one of the components of the drug catalog that comes together for these medicated abortions, which you note are the majority of abortions in this country. The drug will be widely and fully available with no restrictions for now. So the access continues, and we'll see how quickly they make a decision.\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't be surprised at all if we get one by late Tuesday or Wednesday. For now, though, things are on hold. That lower court decision, meaning access is available everywhere in the US.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And so that is how it's been dealt with at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: For now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I will -- so I think a lot people were expecting that perhaps this would go to sort of a full Supreme Court process. Is that off the table?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, what will happen in all of this is emergency petition material. So it's outside the normal rules in the way that you would handle this. It's not going to be a fully argued case at this point. What happens is you had the filing from the DOJ today saying, this can't stand. You got to open up access.\par \par \uc2 So what Justice Alito has said, other side, you get to argue. You get to file your response by Tuesday at noon, and then the court will deliberate. It's not going to be an oral argument that we'll all be able to hear. This is a behind the scenes situation where they will vote. And I do think we could find out as early as late Tuesday or Wednesday. So it's a very expedited process.\par \par \uc2 And just to remind folks, this is only about access while the case plays out on the merits in the lower courts. So what the Supreme Court is deciding is just these lower court cases that could take months or even years, will there be access to this particular drug while those arguments play out on the merits. And that's what I expect them to decide by midweek next week.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And this is really an \uc2 FDA\uc2  process about the safety of this drug, which is very controversial. It's been used, I think, for 20 years, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. It has been. But the interesting thing is that Texas judge said, no, the approval was incorrect. It's not safe. It can't be used. He did stay his decision so that it could be appealed. So when it went to the next level at the Fifth Circuit, it was a mixed bag there. Both sides felt like they won something, they lost something, because what they decided at that level was the drug could still be accessible.\par \par \uc2 But some of the regulatory precautions and guidelines, and safety measures that were stripped away in 2016, meaning it could be used later in pregnancy, that you could get it without seeing a doctor, that it could be dispensed through the mail, all of those things were thrown out. So what that appellate court said was, access still available, but those safeguards that were stripped away in 2016 have to come back.\par \par \uc2 So what's happened today with the Supreme Court, it's saying everything is back on the table. The drug is back out there, fully available, none of those 2016 restrictions, at least until they decide next Tuesday or Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's interesting how this plays into the post Roe period, because essentially that put it back to the states to decide. And I think there was, for people who wanted to allow abortions in their state, sort of the assurance that this would still be available regardless of the changes in some of the other laws. But this seems to put that into question, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. And you had those dueling decisions just a couple of a week ago, essentially, where a judge in Washington said unfettered access to the drug. The decision in Texas last Friday was, no, you're not going to have access at all. So for now, access for everybody until the Supreme Court weighs in and we'll see if they put some limitations on this drug while the case plays out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So -- and do we think that that can happen? What's the timeline, do you think, for the next action here?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Honestly, I think by midweek we'll know. I think that you could know late Tuesday or even sometime Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So many things going on in this topic. Thank you very much, Shannon. Good to have you here today. We'll see you on Sunday. All right.\par \par \uc2 So did you hear, ugh, about this? There is such an enormous rat problem in New York City that they had to hire the first ever rat czar. We are not going to creep you out with a lot of creepy rat videos because we just gave you this one little one. That's enough, guys. OK, that's good. People here live it every day. Mayor Eric Adams has called these little rodents, big rodents in some cases, public enemy number one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, NEW YORK MAYOR: Rats impact how you feel about the city that you're in, and that's why we take it as serious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he's right about that. The new rat czar Kathleen Corradi warning the rodents that there is a new sheriff in town. Here's the rat czar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN CORRADI, NEW YORK CITY RAT CZAR: You see, I have a long history with rats. My introduction to public service came when I was ten years old, getting signatures on petitions for anti-rat measures in my neighborhood. For three years, I spearheaded the Department of Education's rodent reduction efforts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Listen, I think there's bipartisan support that she is very successful. Let's hope, right? She's going to get $155,000 a year, about $100,000 less than the mayor. She's got a very big important -- I mean, everyone's going to know if she fails because you'll still see the rats everywhere, right? If she succeeds, she could be worth a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 The city is also spending $3 million to go after these troublesome critters. And one of the problem is that they have all these COVID era restaurants that are in the street, and the rats love it out there because there's a lot of food in the street everywhere. So she's going to try to clean up the streets and see if that takes away their food source. Anyway, we're going to stay on this, I promise.\par \par \uc2 Also, one very interesting podcast is coming up. This is completely different. I spoke this week, had a really interesting conversation with the former UK Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . And although her time was very short at Downing Street, she says she's not going to stop talking about building conservative momentum in the world, which she thinks is essential for the future of the planet.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us, everybody, today. Have a great weekend. See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230415ej4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Arrest Made In Probe Of Classified Documents Leak; DOJ Confirms Suspect Jack Teixeira In Custody; 21-Year-Old Guardsman Arrested In DoD Leaks Probe; Department Of Defense Confirms Suspect Jack Teixeira In Custody; Former Vice President Pence Reacts To United States Intelligence Leaks; Trump: Biden Gave Rebuilt Military To Ukraine; New Video Of Pentagon Leak Suspect Arrest; San Francisco Officials Give Update On Murder Of Tech Exec; 21-Year-Old Guardsman Arrested In DOD Leaks Probe; DOJ Asks SCOTUS TO Intervene In Abortion Pill Case\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jennifer Griffin, Bret Baier, Marc Thiessen,  Sean Duffy, David Spunt, Claudia Cowan \par \uc2 11,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And this all happened really quick. WaPo, with that report this morning, with the guy who was in a Discord channel, giving some information on him, then we get reports that they possibly I.D. the guy. Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has him and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  just had a press conference and we're expecting his first appearance whenever that's going to be.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, the department saying they'll share more information at the appropriate time. We hope to learn more soon. Bill, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Great to be with you as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good to have you here. Thanks so much for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And I'm Bill Melugin, THE STORY with Martha McCallum starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. And we continue our live coverage of this breaking story this afternoon. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Right now, on THE STORY, incredible new video of this arrest of the suspected leaker of this highly sensitive U.S. intelligence was taken into custody moments ago. These are the video that we have of his arrest. It is a stunning development today.\par \par \uc2 This 21-year-old who was taken into custody. The leaker is a member of the Massachusetts Air National Guard. He's 21 years old, as I said, active duty. And we now know that his job was working on military intelligence systems, which Gillian Turner was just saying, is essentially I.T. He's essentially working on technology. He's not involved at a high level with this kind of information, but apparently, he found a way to get it, which is the world that we live in today.\par \par \uc2 21 years old, Jack Teixeira, excuse me, Teixeira. He reportedly changed his phone number in recent days and the Attorney General calling this a deliberate criminal act moments ago in a very brief statement. We waited for a while for him to come out. He made a very brief statements that will tell you more when we can.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is with us with his reaction to that developing story. Bret Baier joins us as well from Washington; Marc Thiessen and Sean Duffy are here on set with us in New York. But first to Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin, with the very latest as she has been uncovering new information on this all throughout the day, every hour. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. We just came out of the Pentagon briefing and here's Brigadier General Pat Ryder on the arrest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PAT RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It is important to understand that we do have stringent guidelines in place for safeguarding classified and sensitive information. This was a deliberate criminal act, a violation of those guidelines.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voiceover): Cyber sluice and journalist for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  seem to have honed in on the leaker faster than the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . An arrest came shortly after. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has identified the leaker as 21-year-old Jack Teixeira of the 102nd intelligence wing of the Massachusetts Air National Guard who was promoted to Airman First Class last July. We heard the same from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  just moments ago. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  managed to track down and interview a member of the online chat group that Teixeira lead for the past few years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any claims that he is a Russian operative or pro-Russian is categorically false. He is not interested in helping any foreign agencies with their attack on the U.S. or other countries. He was, he was a young charismatic man who loved nature, God, who loved shooting guns and racing cars.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: This teenager in California told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  he was one of 20 or so members who met with the alleged leaker during the pandemic on a server called Thug Shaker Central on Discord. He acted like a father figure to the teens. They called him O.G.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every single day, we would watch movies, play games do activities together. We'd stream our houses and mess around and dance and have fun. When members would have issues like mental health crisis, O.G. and other members would help them out.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The team described the small group as his online family and said unlike Edward Snowden and \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2 , he was not a whistleblower, just a guy showing off for some impressionable kids locked down during COVID. A few days ago, after news of the leak broke, the man known as O.G. spoke to the group.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I mean, we had members crying, we had people just, just distraught and sad and it was almost like seeing the family member pass away. He seemed a bit confused as to what to do in his scenario, which was unfitting of him; he always seemed to have the answer to everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Confronted at his home by reporters this morning, Airman Teixeira's parents said he now needed a lawyer and that they had expected the Feds would have arrived anytime. And we saw that arrival just moments ago live on television, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's incredible. You know, I mean, so many things go through my mind as you're reporting the story, Jennifer. I guess the most important is that it feels like we're living in an environment where people who are good on these Web sites and on \uc2 Bellingcat\uc2  and all these places appear to be kind of running circles around our own intelligence in Pentagon is that, is that fair to say?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, I think the problem is after 9/11, we opened intelligence up to so -- we didn't want to silo intelligence so you allowed people with security clearance to have access. And the fact that this frankly, doesn't happen every day is kind of miraculous. But the fact that there aren't computer systems and digital systems in place to track if somebody is doing this and maneuvering around these systems, it's just very surprising, very disappointing and disappointing that this was online for six to eight months before it came to light.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's incredible. I mean, you know, and you would like to see a very forceful reaction from the highest levels of the government, because this is serious. Obviously, it shows that there needs to be some kind of change. The other comment that you made in your reporting, Jennifer, also struck a chord with me because you think about the impact, the wide-ranging impact of COVID on the country, right.\par \par \uc2 And you've got these guys all sort of leaning on each other, like a family in their isolation. And this one who they treat as a father figure, essentially, who's 21 years old, what can be sort of gleaned from this kind of cultish environment, which you said almost reminds you of the school shooter environment?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: It really seems like a very similar profile, Martha, and I think that the intelligence community, the counter intelligence community is going to be looking at that. We know that Russians have attempted to, to recruit gamers in some of these Web sites using these techniques. There's no evidence that this particular airman was linked to a foreign government. But that is a tactic that's been used in the past because these people are very vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Jen Griffin, thank you very much. I know we'll be hearing from you throughout the afternoon. Thank you. So, with that, we bring in "SPECIAL REPORT" Anchor, Bret Baier, who covered the Pentagon for years as our former National Security Correspondent. Brett, good to have you with us. Your thoughts as you watch, very brief comments from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  here, and a very sort of staid, presentation from the Pentagon on all of this as well so far?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Well, it's really interesting to see, first of all, how extensive this leak was, and who this man is: 21 years old. And it appears according to Gillian Turner's reporting, essentially an I.T. worker, communication system -- not someone steeped in the intel world, and how that person gets access to these documents.\par \par \uc2 And some of them, you know, you could see being a part of an intel circle. But these -- some of the stuff that was out there was finished product by the CIA, and by the DIA, and by Pentagon planners. I mean, this was in deep, dark places where only a few people are supposed to see it, but many, many people have seen it. That's one thought about it.\par \par \uc2 The second thought is, this is different than we've seen in previous cases. By all accounts right now, the information was meant for this small Thumb Shaker Central group online, and was not meant for wide distribution. It wasn't that this person was leaking it to the media. It wasn't that he was a whistleblower of some kind, saying, look at all the things that are going on. He was sharing it with that group that you called his family.\par \par \uc2 But these people have talked out and spoken about how O.G. dealt with them. The last thing I'll say is, where was the investigator, the investigators in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and elsewhere? That seems like the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and then the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , scooped all of those investigators in getting to the heart of who this was and finding them. We don't have the backstory yet, but it does seem like they were ahead of the curve.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, there's -- I would say, it has to be embarrassing on levels here for them, that this \uc2 Bellingcat\uc2  organization and other people who are very astute at how to get around and find things in these places on the Internet. And you think about General Austin the other day saying, well, you know, it's somewhere in the web, but we're not exactly sure where. It -- I don't think it engenders a great deal of confidence on the part of Americans in the highest echelons of our defense and national security.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and that's the biggest issue on 30,000 feet, you know, this case will go forward. We'll find out about more about this guy and, and what he was thinking and perhaps his motives, as the trial goes forward, and the Attorney General saying that that process in the arraignment will happen this afternoon. But bigger than that is what allies around the world think of this, about not only the information that got out, and in some cases was embarrassing, but also the ability of the U.S. to keep a secret. And that's a big issue when you're dealing with allies that have choices of who to ally with.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Bret, thank you. We know you'll have coverage on this tonight on "SPECIAL REPORT." Look forward to seeing you there if not before. Thank you, Bret. So, joining me here on set former Wisconsin Republican Congressman Sean Duffy, Co-Host of "THE BOTTOM LINE" on Fox Business; and Marc Thiessen, former Chief Speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  Senior Fellow, and a Fox News Contributor. Gentlemen, thank you. This is, as I said it, you know, it's disturbing on a lot of levels. Is this -- given what we know right now, Marc, is this a treasonous crime?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. It is, it's treason. And look, Jennifer Griffin made the point that, you know, after 9/11, we loosened access to intelligence. I had a top-secret clearance, security clearance after 9/11 in the Pentagon and the White House. And I can tell you, the goal was not to give 21-year-olds access to finish CIA product that reveal sources and methods.\par \par \uc2 The goal was to break down the walls between the intelligence community and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , well, because they weren't talking to each other. No one was talking about spreading intelligence this far and wide. And the idea that a 21-year-old kid can explore expose sources and methods of how we spy or how we have been penetrated the Chinese People's Liberation Army, because apparently, we've exposed the fact that they, that we know they have a new hypersonic missile that can match the United States.\par \par \uc2 That they exposed our penetration of the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  that could affect the war in Ukraine. I mean, the incompetence is stunning. And it wasn't unlike Edward Snowden and \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2 , it wasn't ideological -- it was some kid trying to show off to his friends in a gaming chat room, and has literally affected global national security by doing so. It's -- it how this happens is just beyond me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, that's one of the big questions. You know, there's, there's the micro question here and the macro question here. The macro question is, as Marc says, there were pieces of intelligence here that were talking about the tensions that exist between the Wagner Group and the head of the Russian military assessments about how successful based on all of our calculations Ukraine could potentially be in this spring offensive.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are very foundational understandings of what's going on in the world that are not shared outside of this environment, right? And so, what really strikes me, you know, is, is this young man. I think technology is advancing faster than a lot of the old folks at the Pentagon can, can keep up. That's what I think just watching this, right. And I wonder how many other young people have the ability to circumvent this.\par \par \uc2 I don't know, Sean, if he -- if he was able to get into a system and read what was there. They said that he was transcribing this stuff initially. Then, he started taking pictures of it, and then sharing the pictures with them, because transcribing it all got a little bit cumbersome.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FOX BUSINESS HOST: But, but roll back for a second, we spent $800 billion --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: -- to the Pentagon. You'd expect they have missiles and ships and planes. But also, they have advanced technology to make sure they keep our intelligence safe. $800 billion a year. And here you have this kind of leak, and you made this point, I think the American people are shocked that a 21-year-old guardsman can get access to this information. If a guardsman can get access, can't the Russians, can't the Chinese? Who else can get access?\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I think we hold the guardsmen accountable. He accessed the information. I don't think it was for ideological reasons. However, it has the same impact in undermining our, our credibility with our allies. But what General is going to lose their job? Which general is at fault for the for national security to make sure you can't breach into the Pentagon and get this highly classified information?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Or when it's out there for six to eight months, you don't notice?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Where's the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Where's the DOJ? Exactly. The problem is administration is known as held accountable. Someone has to lose their job. I mean, again, this guy should go to prison. But we have no problems in the Pentagon. I don't think the, the administration wants to look inside to go, who is the problem? Who else we have to bring in to make sure our secrets are safe?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, the President's in Ireland. He spoke to Parliament today. What would you expect, you're a former speechwriter at the White House, if you were advising this president and all of this is coming down? What would you say to him about how to how to address it today?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I would, I -- well, first of all, I would, I would look at what the, what has been exposed and say fix that. Like, the fact that we're going to we expose the fact that we're -- that they don't think that the Ukrainian defensive is going to work. Well, that's because they're not providing them the weapons for it to work. Expose the fact that the that Egypt was trying to sell artillery to the Russians.\par \par \uc2 You know, well, maybe we should put some consequences on that. So, first thing is let's fix the problems that this has exposed, that we all didn't know was happening, but he did -- this administration did there. I mean, this is just lifted up a rock and we're seeing some of the just incompetence that's happening in this administration. In terms of this leak, take it seriously.\par \par \uc2 He said today, you know, it's not that big a deal. What is he talking about? We we've exposed sources and methods of how we penetrated the \uc2 Russian Ministry of Defense\uc2  and the Chinese People's Liberation Army, it's a very big, to quote \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it's a big effing deal, you know, say that it's a big deal, say that I'm going to get to the bottom of this. I'm going to hold people accountable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, that the balloon wasn't a major breach -- there's a lot of, there's a lot of, in the messaging that we're hearing from the White House, Sean, there's a lot of, sort of, downplaying. Like, OK, everybody -- no, it's not a big deal. This isn't a big deal either. You know, they're shooting down our drone. Not a big deal. Balloon flying over our airspace, not a big deal. This is also not a big deal. I mean, at some point --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: \uc2 Taliban\uc2  taking over Kabul, not a big deal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, It's -- I feel almost like the whole apparatus is kind of COVID -- like, chilled out, you know?\par \par \uc2 Like, oh, we're not -- we're only back at work three days a week.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: W we're not really keeping a close eye on what's going on. Pretty soon, everybody will be back to, you know, full throttle. When?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And here's -- and here's the problem with that. So, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  undermines how serious this really is, which we all agree, it is incredibly serious, all of a sudden, that Congress is going to step up, Republicans are going to engage, and they're going to go to war with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Where I think that's horrible for the country.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I think, this is a bipartisan moment where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says, this is serious. Republicans' go, yes, Mr. President, and serious, let's work together, Republican and Democrat to make sure this never happens again. This is not political, this isn't partisan.\par \par \uc2 But it will become partisan, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  minimizes the serious nature of it. And, and again, I think, to the point of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, listen, we got real problems. I'm not a tech guy, but I'm going to find out what went wrong, and this will never happen again, and Republicans, helped me out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Precisely. Precisely.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that's you want to hear from the top.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is treason. We're going to pursue this to the Justice Department. But if it is, what it appears to be, it's treason.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: there will be accountability.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If this young man does not face the appropriate penalty, what is to dissuade anyone else who can find their way around a computer? And let me tell you, I guarantee, there are many of this young man --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Those who hire this guy.\par \par \uc2 They got to hold -- be held accountable too. Who hired this guy? Who gave this guy access to this intelligence?\par \par \uc2 Who -- up the chain of command, there has to be accountability, or else, it's never going to get fixed?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that was one of the questions in the Pentagon briefing, you know, what happens up the chain of command? Because the captain of the ship is the leader in these environments, right?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if people under you are putting American secrets at risk, there has to be accountability. And I think that is -- it's almost like a kind of a disease that has crept across all of our apparatuses, like, where it's like, nothing really matters, and no one's really held accountable. And I think people are so fed up with it, Sean.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: But -- they are.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And I think that every American recognizes that mistakes happen. No one is perfect. No administration -- I mean, there's 1000s of people who work for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . There will be mistakes.\par \par \uc2 They can go, they can accept there was a mistake here, I take full responsibility, we'll fix it, and they really forgive. The American people are very forgiving.\par \par \uc2 But when you play the other hand, and go, wow, it's not a big deal, everybody, and play it down. And again, the seriousness -- the serious nature of the leaks are real.\par \par \uc2 Again, that's when I think the American people get angry. They get mad.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: And just look at last week when they had the report on Afghanistan. And they said, well, I didn't see -- Kirby says, I didn't see chaos. I don't know what you're talking about. Like there's no -- nobody got fired over that. Nobody got fired over that. No one gets fired over anything in this administration.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And nobody (INAUDIBLE), John Kirby, either -- in his credibility.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: And no -- I mean, Kirby should be fired for saying that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he completely destroyed his credibility, and in the process, the credibility of this administration.