\uc2 Now this arrest comes after the mayor of San Francisco pushed back on reports in the media blaming this attack on the city's spiraling crime crisis. Earlier this week, she said that when the facts come out, "people are going to be surprised." Krista Lee says this arrest is the first step toward justice following the death of her former husband.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTA LEE, BOB LEE'S EX-WIFE: I don't want to let my emotion to the best of me on this one. But I can say that that our family is very happy that the arrest was made. And I'm looking forward to assisting the police in any way shape I can to further bring justice.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COWAN: That police press conference starting any moment now, Martha. We hope to get more details about the arrest. Let's go to it live.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Claudia. Thank you so much. Bill Scott is the San Francisco Police Chief. Let's go and listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL SCOTT, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE CHIEF: --AM, San Francisco police officers from Southern Station responded to the 300 block of Main Street for reports of a stabbing. Officers arrived on scene and located the victim later identified as 43-year-old \uc2 Robert Lee\uc2  suffering from an apparent stab wound. Officers rendered aid and summon medics to the scene.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lee was transported to Zuckerberg San Francisco General Hospital with life threatening injuries. Despite efforts by first responders and medical personnel, Mr. Lee died from his injuries. The San Francisco Police Department homicide detail responded and took over the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Our homicide investigators developed information that identified the suspect as a 38-year-old Nima Momeni of Emeryville, California. We can confirm that Mr. Lee and Mr. Momeni knew each other. However, we will not be able to provide further details at this time regarding the evidence that confirms that information.\par \par \uc2 This morning, members of the San Francisco Police Department's Investigations Bureau and Tactical Unit serve several search warrants and an arrest warrant in San Francisco and Emeryville, California. Mr. Momeni was taken into custody without incident in Emeryville, California. He was transported to the San Francisco County Jail and booked on one charge of murder, California Penal Code Section 187.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to remind everybody that this is still an open and ongoing investigation, and we will not be able to provide further details at this point. Before I turn this over to our district attorney, I want to assure everyone that our investigators have been working tirelessly to make this arrest and bring this case to justice for the family and his loved ones, just as we would for every homicide that occurs in this city.\par \par \uc2 I like to thank our homicide investigators, led by Detective Brett Dittmer and Detective Thomas McWilliams, also Lieutenant Kelvin Sanders and our entire homicide team who have worked not only on this case, but all of our open cases.\par \par \uc2 You know, I said from the start, that I have every confidence in the men and women, and the members that work homicide, they have an 85% clearance rate year to date, and that is expected to go to 92% because we just saw the 1994 case yesterday as well. We never give up finding justice for the victims in this city. And I want to make sure that everybody knows how hard the men and women, and members of this department work to keep the city safe.\par \par \uc2 With that said, just a reminder, we won't be able to provide many details in this case today but this is an ongoing investigation. We still have work to do. And our job is to provide the best evidence possible to this district attorney and her team so we can bring this case and any other homicide of crime in the city to justice. So with that, I'd like to turn it over to our District Attorney Brooke Jenkins will take questions after.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE JENKINS, SAN FRANCISCO DISTRICT ATTORNEY: So as you're aware, I'm Brooke Jenkins, the district attorney here in San Francisco. And I want to thank you, first off, for being here.\par \par \uc2 I also want to start off by first acknowledging the pain and anguish that Mr. Lee's family and friends are currently experiencing. There is nothing worse than a parent having to bury their child, or a young child having to bury their parent. My prayers go out to them during this very difficult time. I too want to commend the San Francisco Police Department for their work in identifying who committed this heinous crime. They worked around the clock to solve this case.\par \par \uc2 While in some cases we do immediately have a suspect, that was not the situation here. As a result of their hard work, Mr. Lee's killer has been identified arrested and now will be brought to justice. As a former prosecutor in our homicide unit, I have personally worked on cases with the detectives that solve this case. They are dedicated to justice and have further demonstrated --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We're going to keep a close eye on that. That's Brooke Jenkins. She is fairly new, I think inside -- around a year or so she joined the team there as the DA in San Francisco. Let's bring in criminal defense attorney Mark Eiglarsh with his thoughts on this case and this development today. Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK EIGLARSH, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: How you doing? Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to be with you as well. Good to have you with us as well.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, what do you -- the video is so hard to watch in this case. This man is stabbed and staggers to the door to try to get some help. He's 43 years old, Bob Lee, a tech executive. It turns out this suspect is, according to reports, somebody that he was acquainted with and that they were at a party very late at night. What do you know about this so far, Mark?\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: First of all, my heart goes out to the Lee family. Obviously, this is a tremendous loss. He was well-loved and liked in the community. But one thing that was said during the press conference that I don't know if anybody caught, it was stab wound. It was wound. The reports leading up to this suggested wounds, and you have this image of Mr. Momeni, the accused, stabbing Lee numerous times.\par \par \uc2 That's not what he said. He said wound, it means one. So now the question is, where was that one wound? Was it forward or was it to his back, you know? And what story did he present to law enforcement? Because let me tell you, it's not a who done it. We know he was involved. The question is why? And if it was at his hand, there's only two possible defenses I could think of insanity, which never works and quite frankly doesn't fit because everyone who knew Momeni is saying that he was a nice, pleasant, impressive fellow and probably did not suffer from mental illness, or what I likely will hear is possible self-defense that he reasonably feared death or great bodily harm from Lee, which is going to be tough to pull off unless Lee somehow possessed a weapon and/or there's witnesses who can corroborate that Momeni reasonably feared death or great bodily harm.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You make a great point. And I heard that as well. They said to the chest, a stab wound to the chest. And it may, you know, it may turn out that there were more wounds. And that this Chief of Police is only referring to this one stab wound to the chest. But you're right, it makes the whole thing seem different.\par \par \uc2 This is not a random act that we initially assumed based on so many random acts these days of, you know, a crazed person who ran up and stabbed him. They were in the car together, it appears, and then Bob Lee tried to get out of the car, you know. So if you're working with Mr. Momeni, you know, how are you handling this as a defense attorney right now?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the first thing I do is say, you know, what happened, tell me, it's confidential. I'm not going to tell anybody. And you get a sense. Clearly, I would expect him to say I was angered. And the question is over what? And then why did you fear Mr. Lee, assuming that's where it goes. I'd have to listen to him first.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to say everyone in San Francisco, fortunately, can take a sigh and a breath right now that this wasn't some, like cycle of violence continuing that was painted by many. Again, it's one act between two people. And we'll see what his motive was, hopefully through them subpoenaing his cellphone records to see if there was any communications that support a motive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Mark, thank you. Always great to get your take on it as we cover that breaking news in San Francisco. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So we are awaiting a possible update around a potential court appearance for Jack Teixeira, 21 years old. This video is pretty stunning. You can see him slowly backing up with his hands behind his head, red shorts and a t- shirt, as the officers wait to take him into custody in that armored vehicle. It's really just a stunning scene that we saw there today. And that is in connection with the massive leaks of US intelligence, a lot of questions around what happened here.\par \par \uc2 Reporting on his identity began early this morning, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  now saying a short time ago that they had been caught aggressively pursuing investigative leads since last week on this. Let's bring in, for the big picture here, Victor Davis Hanson, \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2  senior fellow Victor, great to have you back on the show. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 This is a stunning story on a lot of levels. What strikes you about it?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, HOOVER INSTITUTION: Well, I mean, I think there's a lot of things about the tech industry we don't understand. I don't know if this was a business relationship or whatever. But it's -- we've had the -- we don't understand why banks fail in Silicon Valley, we don't understand the Bankman-Fried thing. And it's such an esoteric industry with such enormous amounts of wealth and influence, and talent, that we don't understand what goes behind the circumstance -- might be nothing but it could be something else.\par \par \uc2 But I think the whole industry is sort of foreign to everybody. They don't understand the esoteric of it. And so why would a very successful entrepreneur be killed by a very successful entrepreneur in the middle morning. It's sort of analogous to the \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , not similar but analogous with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . So we still didn't understand that. And there are things that are going on in that corridor between San Francisco and Silicon Valley that I think baffle a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, you know, and you make the point about technology and how esoteric and difficult to understand it is. And now we've got this 21- year-old who has been digging into US intelligence documents and spilling them around his group chat. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS HANSON: Yes. I don't understand that either. I don't know how somebody who seemingly doesn't have sophisticated espionage skills can tap in to the Pentagon and then disseminate some of the most important secrets despite what the President says. They're obviously critical to the national security of the United States and our allies, and get away with it for weeks without being uncovered.\par \par \uc2 And then I think, in that case, it's part of a larger problem that there are no consequences for leaking. We found out about the Roe versus Wade leak, there were no consequences. There was no consequences to \uc2 James Comey\uc2  leaking that confidential conversation with the President to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . So I think there's a larger end. We have the so called whistleblower leaks. I think there's a larger culture that these people feel there's no consequences to uncovering or disseminating the most important secrets of the way that the \uc2 United States government\uc2  works.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS HANSON: We'll continue until there's deterrence restored, I guess.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And until people do pay consequences, and then the next 21- year-old might think twice about spilling these kinds of secrets all over the place. In terms of the White House and leadership on this issue and the significance of it, the Pentagon, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  appear to have not been able to find this guy, but other tech sleuths were able to find him, and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 's got a hold of another teenager who knew all about this, according to that that report.\par \par \uc2 So, what's going on when the solution to this and the investigative work is being run circles around by outside technology groups on the internet?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS HANSON: I don't know. I think that's a really good question. I think it plays into a narrative of that we saw in Afghanistan, we saw with the balloon surveillance caper where the Pentagon gave misleading information about it for a week. They gave misinformation about what actually happened in Afghanistan, that the post facto report was not accurate. We were seeing things that were leaked in this that we have troops in Ukraine that we were assured was not happening.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think there's a general malaise at the Pentagon, I think under \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , I hate to say it, and \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 . Something's gone terribly wrong. There's a misapplication of resources and energy, I think, to other agendas other than battlefield efficacy, and national security, at least that's the impression that a lot of Americans have.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What questions do you have about this young man and his motivation? Because what we're hearing so far, is that he was just showing off to this other group that he was affiliated with online. But, you know, given what we know about international players here, would you want to dig deeper?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS HANSON: Well, I think the media is trying to portray him as some alt right gun -- and not adherent or extremist or something. But I think the type of people who have a small little coterie in a chat room, and they are obsessed with video games, they don't really fit any of these efforts to put them in a political mentality or ideology. So I don't know what he's doing but there's a lot of people that that are following our military, apparently, and they apparently have skills that we under appreciate. And we have CIA, we have \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we have pentagon, we have Director of National Intelligence. But these bureaucrats are not able to stop these free these freelancers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Victor David Hanson, thank you very much. Great to have your thoughts on this today.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS HANSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you, Victor. So we have a new picture to share with you. This young National Guardsmen who was arrested today in Massachusetts. This is 21-year-old Jack Teixeira. He is a member of the -- he's an active member of the Air Force National Guard, and he is the person that you see in the other video, backing up in his red shorts and gray t-shirt as he's being taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 We understand he's being processed now. There may be an arraignment, likely today or tomorrow, according to David Spunt at the Justice Department as we watch this. But you see him in uniform, this young man, you know, you listen to his friends talk about how they admired him, and he was sharing information with them about all of these, you know, sort of things to help them understand the world. It's a very, very strange story and it raises a lot of questions about who is in charge and who's keeping our information and our national security at the absolute highest levels of confidentiality.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now by phone is Pennsylvania Congressman Brian Fitzpatrick, a member of the Intelligence Committee and a former undercover \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent, a great person to have joining us today. Congressman, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, gosh, I mean when you learn about how this is unraveled here today, what -- goes through your mind?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRIAN FITZPATRICK (R-PA): Well, thanks for having me, Martha. First and foremost, it's been an ongoing debate for several months now regarding overall classification policy, in light of the recent documents found in the personal residences of elected officials who had security clearance. But as far as the protocol in this specific situation, obviously, the first order of business is containment, stop the bleeding, mitigate the damage. But then there needs to be a review, both of review of the general policies of classification specific to the Washington Air Guard and National Guards, and any entity that generally has a window into this level of information.\par \par \uc2 And in second, and perhaps even more importantly, reviewing this specific decision to grant this specific individual access to this specific information. We should all know who was approving that level of access to this 21-year-old.\par \par \uc2 And, Martha, we don't know now, we will know in short order, whether this is a case of immaturity of a young man or if this is a counterintelligence issue. That'll be an important question for us to answer as well. And lastly, I would say, Martha, typically, depending on the level of access you have, there needs to be regular counterintelligence measures in place regular polygraph check, regular background check, to make sure that people with this level of access, are using it responsibly and not leaking it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Can I ask you a quick question about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Because I raised this earlier and, you know, belling cat and these other investigative sleuths who operate in, you know, the sort of unknown to the rest of us places on the internet, were able to find this guy before it appears the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or the Pentagon. So does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have the capability to go into these spaces and operate effectively it raises I think, a legitimate question about that.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes. This has always been a question mark. And oftentimes the private sector is lightyears ahead of law enforcement, particularly when it comes to cybersecurity and technology, that's nothing new. That's one of the reasons why the bureau, the agency, so many federal and state law enforcement agencies seek, actively seek public-private partnerships, so they can take advantage of the innovation of the private sector that oftentimes are lightyears ahead. So there's no question about it.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, again, we're going to learn a lot more, probably in the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It seems like the world and our security have really moved into a tech layer that we need to be so much more savvy about understanding for national security purposes. Congressman Brian Fitzpatrick, thank you, sir. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: You bet. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We're going to get one more story in here, which is important today, the battle over a medication used in most abortions in the United States, maybe going all the way to the Supreme Court. The Justice Department asking for emergency action on this after an Appeals Court handed down a split decision on this drug. It is still available, but it has more restrictions on its use. Justice Correspondent David Spunt joining us once again live from the DOJ on this one. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. That's right. Mira Carlin, the attorney general, hours ago said that DOJ will go to the Supreme Court. This comes after this ruling from the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in New Orleans. And it's a bit of a mixed bag that will satisfy both sides of the abortion issue, at least temporarily.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mifepristone has been on the market for 23 years. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved it in 2000. It's also used to help women with miscarriages. Last week, a federal judge in Texas put a nationwide hold on distribution of the drug arguing that the approval process done by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago was flawed. DOJ appealed. Here we are this afternoon. Now, let's dig in on what those watching need to know.\par \par \uc2 Now, while Mifepristone is available, according to this three judge panel, it will now be harder to get. The big takeaway, the appeals court judges said if a woman wants it, she needs to go get it in-person with the approval of a doctor. It cannot be mailed. Yesterday it could be mailed. The vice president and attorney general weighed in yesterday at the White House before the court ruled at a roundtable on this very issue. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: This, indeed, is a health care crisis in America. And we have to acknowledge and understand it to be just that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  as a group that's praising this decision. But clearly, it's confusing, because last week, you had a federal judge in Seattle saying leave Mifepristone the way it is. So you have two federal judges with two weighing things clearly going to go to the Supreme Court, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fascinating. David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And thank you at home for joining us today on this Thursday, April 13th. THE STORY goes on and we look forward to telling it to you tomorrow when we join you then. Thanks for being with us, everybody. "YOUR WORLD" is about to get rolling. The Dow having a very good day, up 300 and change almost 400 points right now. We'll see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230414ej4d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pentagon, DOJ Hunt For Leaker Of Sensitive Intel; Egypt Secretly Planned Sending Russia Rockets; Intel Leaks Sending Shockwaves Through Pentagon; \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  Sues Jordan Over Probe Into Trump Case; Sen. \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  (R-LA), Is Being Interviewed About Mr. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's Motivation On Suing Rep. Jim Jordan; Rep. Anna Paulina Luna (R-FL), Is Being Interviewed About Compelling Mark Pomerantz To Testify In Front Of Congress; Bragg Sues Representative Jordan Over Prove Into Trump Case; Abortion Pill Ruling Ignites New National Battle; RPT: Biden Readies Army Of Influencers For 2024; Intel Leaks Send Shockwaves Through Pentagon; Blinken, Austin Will Hold News Conference\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jennifer Griffin, Mike Pompeo, David Spunt,  Katie Pavlich, Mark Penn, Lydia Hu, Rich Edson \par \uc2 7,708 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): That seems to be a boost for markets. I don't know, Bill, you probably watch these two, these markets have been resilient.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: They have oh --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What has been some, you know, bad --\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: With negative headlines, crazy stuff going on, but we'd like to see in green, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is so great to have you here, here, Bill. We'll have you back here tomorrow again. Thanks so much, everybody for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And I'm Bill Melugin. THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. So, breaking moments ago, Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  who indicted the former president last week, has now taken an extraordinary step. He is suing Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan for what Bragg is calling an "unconstitutional attack" on his case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We're going to have more in a moment on this breaking news with Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 . So, stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 But first, we are about to hear live from Secretary of State Blinken and Defense Secretary Austin, no doubt, they will have to answer to what they know about the extraordinary and damaging documents leak that ended up on a gaming Web site. I mean, you really can't make this stuff up with top secret U.S. intelligence on it. 53 documents have surfaced so far, but chillingly, the White House basically says they're not sure if that's all of them, there could be more coming.\par \par \uc2 One defense official telling Politico the embarrassing and worse leaks, made them feel sick to their stomach when they learned what was in here. So, former CIA Director and Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is here in moments with his reaction. First to Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin with brand new information on this from her reporting at the Pentagon. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEW CHANNEL CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Well, many of the documents came from February 23 and March 1st, but there are other highly classified documents that do not come from the Pentagon daily briefing slides suggesting the leak may have occurred outside the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voiceover): The first images appeared on \uc2 Discord\uc2 , a messaging platform popular with gamers. The images which included folded papers were hastily photographed with items such as gorilla glue in the background and a magazine that shows rifle scopes on the cover. The DOD briefings from a month ago include details of the war in Ukraine. Those briefings are typically delivered electronically on secure iPads, and are not usually printed out. If they are printed, then investigators can track where they were printed from -- they have to be printed on secure printers and are often numbered.\par \par \uc2 Those sets of DOD daily briefings, however, are distributed to between 1000 and 5000 people with appropriate clearances each day. Separately, there is highly classified intelligence that was not part of any of the DOD briefing books that has appeared online. Those documents appear to be produced by either, by other U.S. intelligence agencies like the CIA and NSA. This intelligence is not easily accessed by those in the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 For instance, one of the more damaging documents concerns Egypt, which has received $50 billion in U.S. military aid since 1978. New reporting suggests President Sisi of Egypt plan to secretly ship 40,000 rockets to Russia for use in Ukraine at the same time that he was holding friendly phone calls with President Zelenskyy. The documents show real-time intelligence about the war in Ukraine that may affect the upcoming Spring offensive, for instance, that the Pentagon believes the Ukrainians will run out of air defense missiles by mid-May.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has just requested a classified briefing for all senators on the leaked documents. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jennifer, thank you very much. And with that, we bring in former CIA Director and former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , Fox News Contributor. Secretary, good to have you with us today. I just want to play because we think these documents, or many of them are from February 23 and March 1st, as Jennifer just reported. This is President Biden in Ukraine where you just were with President Zelenskyy. But this is him on February 20th, just a few days before these documents, this document was written, and here's what he was saying then and there. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You're succeeding against all and every expectation, except your own. We have every confidence that you're going to continue to prevail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he's saying that but the reporting in these documents from the Pentagon, three days later, are saying that Ukraine's troops, they're worried about the numbers, the ammo, and the equipment and that it will fall well short in the spring offensive. So, what do Americans to think here, sir?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Martha, thanks for having me on. Look, I think it's been pretty clear for some time now that while the Ukrainians have had enormous successes and have far exceeded are the expectations when this kicked off just over a year ago that the challenges still remain, and President Biden's happy talk not-withstanding the real challenges for Ukraine, not the least of which is the one that appears to have been contained in some of these documents was an absence of sufficient munitions. That's been my core critique of the Biden administration all along. We should have been providing them with more faster, better -- we could have avoided this war continuing as long as it has.\par \par \uc2 And so, it doesn't surprise me to see that the Pentagon is doing a hard cold analysis of, hey, here's what the Ukrainians have in stock, here's their burn rate. Here's how many missiles they're planning to fire each day. Here's what their air defense air defense systems look like. These are the things we need to make sure that Europe and the United States provide to them if they're going to be able to conduct the campaign that they were contemplating doing this spring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you heard a Jennifer Griffin's reporting there, some suggestion that not all of these documents came from the Pentagon, and the nature of these photographs of unfolded documents sitting next to magazines taking a picture and put on this, on this exchange, \uc2 Discord\uc2 , what do you make of this? How does this add up to you, you're the former head of Central Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: So, Martha, I lived something not terribly dissimilar to this. A leak that had taken place about a year before the Trump administration came in, was discovered in March, I'd been the CIA director for about 40 days at this point in time. And it took an enormous amount of resources to figure out what had happened there, how to determine the scope and scale of what had actually been compromised. I heard Kirby say or someone said, they're not sure this is all of the documents.\par \par \uc2 It probably isn't all of the documents. I'm sure there's another shoe to drop. This, this is embarrassing. This is gut-wrenching, to see recent, highly classified information get online. I hope it all turns out that in the end, these weren't real, they were fake. But it sure appears from what the administration is saying that these documents are indeed authentic, and that there's real national security risks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I'm glad you brought that up, because we talked about this in the, with the backdrop of talking about AI, talking about how to create things that aren't real and make them look like they are real. You kind of wonder what you can believe in what you can't believe anymore. Is there -- are you suggesting that maybe these are not real documents?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: So, Martha, I've seen reporting this as some of these appear to have been altered. I've seen multiple governments; foreign governments now say that the information that's in there isn't accurate. So, I know what everyone else knows, I haven't seen any of these documents in their original form. We should all be cautious. But watch the administration's behavior, you can see that they are taking this investigation very seriously, suggesting that they believe at least some of what's been presented so far, is real and accurate and a real compromise of American national security.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a good data point right there. All right, we're going to leave it there, sir. Thank you very much. I just want to remind everyone that we give Egypt 1.3 billion in military assistance every year. And it turns out that they're talking about giving 40,000 rockets to Russia, while we're supplying the other side in Ukraine. So, we'll talk more about this, sir. Thank you so much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Martha, thank you, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you. \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Secretary of State, former Head of the CIA. So, as we mentioned at the top of the hour, this story is just breaking late this afternoon. The Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's favorite person, as you might remember from last week, is now suing Republican Congressman Jim Jordan to block a subpoena that was issued over the criminal prosecution of the former president, accusing him of a campaign to intimidate him, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is, of Jim Jordan. So, we're going to get some reaction from Capitol Hill. We're going to speak with Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  when he responds to this breaking news only on THE STORY, coming up next. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're watching this because this is breaking as we speak, Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , who now the whole nation knows who the D.A. is from Manhattan. He is now suing the House Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan. He's accusing Jordan of an "unconstitutional attack" on the investigation that Bragg is doing into the former President Trump. So, let's get right to Chief Justice Correspondent, Justice Correspondent David Spunt, I should say, live at the DOJ. Hi, David. What are you learning?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Well, you know, it's not every day we see the House Judiciary Chairman, go after a local District Attorney and a local District Attorney go after the House Judiciary Chairman. These are two levels of government not something we see. But here we are right now, alas. This has been a fight that's been brewing now for several weeks since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was indicted, actually before he was indicted when we knew those grand jury was getting ready to finish up its work in New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voiceover): A lot of arguments in public between Jim Jordan and the House Judiciary Committee Chairman and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . Now, this was filed to essentially stop the judiciary committee from issuing a subpoena to Mark Pomerantz. Mark Pomerantz, a former employee of the District Attorney's Office who has come out spoken against the office in many ways. Clearly, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , not too fond of that, that's why he has filed this temporary restraining order asking a judge to essentially stop that testimony from going forward in that subpoena.\par \par \uc2 I want to read part of -- and it's a 50-page document Martha, this lawsuit. Part of it says, "members of Congress are not free to invade New York's sovereign authority for their or Mr. Trump's political aims. Congress has no authority to quote conduct oversight into District Attorney Bragg's exercise of his duties under New York law in a single case involving a single defendant."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): So, from the beginning \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has said this is me, this is my investigation Jim Jordan has said that he wants to see if there's been any type of political interference there. We'll see what happens. But right now, this lawsuit just filed. We're waiting on some reaction from Chairman Jordan's office.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Fairly lengthy too.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: 50-plus pages from \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , suing Jim Jordan. Thank you very much, David.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with that, we bring in Republican Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  of Louisiana. He also sits on the Judiciary Committee. Senator, it's always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Well, what's your reaction to this news this afternoon? What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Pre-committing the issue of whether Mr. Bragg should be called before Congress. I have -- I've watched Mr. Bragg. It's clear. He is not exactly Oliver Wendell, Scalia. My guess is he is scared to come before Congress.\par \par \uc2 I've watched Mr. Braggs. press conference after he announced his indictment. He was not impressive. I have read his indictment. Legal scholars much more qualified than I have, they have criticized it as being an inferior document.\par \par \uc2 I read it, it looked to me like at some legal product, someone knocked over a urine sample. My guess is Mr. Bragg will not try this lawsuit himself. A good criminal defense attorney based on what I've seen so far, will take his head off. So, he doesn't want to be naked and alone. Not literally, but figuratively, in front of a congressional committee having to answer tough questions. That's my assessment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, just on the legal grounds of whether or not Bragg -- whether or not Jordan and his committee can compel the Manhattan D.A. or someone who used to work for him Mark Pomerantz, to come down and be subpoenaed about this case.\par \par \uc2 You know, Bragg is saying that's interference. He's saying that is, you know, not -- that they don't have the jurisdiction to do that. It's interference of the case that he's has here in New York, just basic legal grounds. Do you agree with what he's saying or not?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I don't know. I need to read the pleadings. Congress has pretty wide jurisdiction. I am sympathetic to the separation of powers argument.\par \par \uc2 But I guess the point I'm trying to make, in my opinion, some may disagree. Mr. Bragg's primary motivation is, he is worried that he can't handle himself in front of tough questioning from -- questioning from other lawyers in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean, I've watched his performance. Mr. Soros did not pick him as the -- his preferred D.A. candidate because of his legal acumen. In my opinion, he was picked because of his activism.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, when you talk about that part of his story of \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's story, I think one of the things that strikes a lot of New Yorkers, when you watch this, as they -- you know, just to came up with a 50-page suit against Jim Jordan, while you watch crime rise, double digits here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 You know, in terms of what his mandate is, as a D.A. in this city. And you know, we see similar situations in cities across the country. Should this be the priority, Senator?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: In my opinion, no. Mr. Bragg is a politician, first. He is not an impartial member of the criminal justice system. He has demonstrated that. He has ignored the rule of law in terms of prosecuting people who break the law in New York and except with respect to the allegations about Mr. Trump, I think Mr. Bragg believes that cops are a bigger problem than criminals.\par \par \uc2 I think Manhattan is -- in New York, is one of the greatest cities in the world. And Mr. Bragg is helping to destroy it, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator Kennedy, thank you very much. Always good to have you here, sir.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for rolling with the breaking news there this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And moments ago, Congressman Jim Jordan tweeted his response to this lawsuit that he just received from \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . He said, "First, they indict a president for no crime. Then, they sue to block congressional oversight when we ask questions about the federal funds they say they used to do it."\par \par \uc2 So, we will actually be talking to Jim Jordan tomorrow on this program. We'll get his reaction to all of this then. But with that, we bring in Florida Republican congresswoman Anna Paulina Luna, who is a member of the Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Luna, good to have you with us today. You know, I think one of the big question -- well, let me get your headline reaction to this back and forth here with \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , filing a 50-page lawsuit, saying that Jim Jordan's request to have Mark Pomerantz and probably others testify about this process to indict the former president, he says is, is interference. And that they're doing this to keep Congress from interfering with the business-at-hand here in the Manhattan D.A.'s office. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANNA PAULINA LUNA (R-FL): That's an absolute farce. I find it very interesting, as Fox had pointed out earlier that they were trying to say that, as Jim Jordan is investigating this, as well, as I assume House Oversight will be looking to this as well, that somehow, we were interfering with the New York investigation, when in actuality it seems very much so that Mr. Bragg, as well as his political activist in New York are interfering with a national federal election.\par \par \uc2 And so, the fact that they are doing this to try to hide information, the one thing I learned, Martha, is whenever you do something, and someone immediately has a knee jerk reaction to hide things, it's because they are guilty. And I very much so look forward to helping Representative Jordan as well as the other members of House Judiciary in covering this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, where do you think that this will go next? And, you know, how will the Committee be successful, do you think, in subpoenaing Mark Pomerantz, who, just to remind folks at home, I think we have a picture of him. He was working on the Trump case in the D.A.'s office.\par \par \uc2 When that case was dropped, initially by \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , Pomerantz was unhappy about that. He felt that there was evidence that they could be pursuing in that case, he didn't want the case dropped, he wrote a book about it. And after he left, Bragg's office ended up indicting the former president. So, can he be compelled to testify in front of Congress?\par \par \uc2 LUNA: Most certainly. And if there's one thing I know about Representative Jim Jordan, it is that he is very good at his job, which is exactly why he is the chairman of House Judiciary and why he also sits on House Oversight.\par \par \uc2 But I want to remind people, earlier today, I believe I came across a headline that had some text message correspondence between \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his attorney that said, don't worry, basically, in a nutshell, there are going to be more indictments coming against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, do I believe personally that this is absolutely a weaponization of the DOJ and was there potentially coordination and communication with \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's office, the state of New York, and the DOJ in order to target President Trump most definitely.\par \par \uc2 And again, as my job as a member of House Oversight, it is our job and obligation to the American people to ensure that not only is the justice system held accountable, but these elected officials are not abusing that authority. And so we need to get to the bottom of this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Before I let you go, I mean, there have been times in the past, including with Congressman Jordan, that members of the opposite party have subpoenaed members of the other party to testify, and they've been refused.\par \par \uc2 They don't show up, including Republicans. So, you know, is there an obligation when subpoenaed by Congress to appear or not? It seems selective at times?\par \par \uc2 LUNA: I think that there is an obligation. And I will say, Martha, that I think in the past that Congress has been a little bit weak on that enforcement. But I think now what we're seeing, especially over the last six years of this just craziness coming out of the radical left, that is really hijacked the Democrat Party, that ultimately, we have to take a strong stand against this.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that under Speaker McCarthy's leadership with the United Republican Party, we're going to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Anna Paulina Luna of Florida. Congresswoman from Florida, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LUNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As I said, tomorrow, we will speak with Congressman Jim Jordan, about this case. He subpoenaed \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , people -- and people who worked on the Trump case to come explain before Congress. Now you've got a 50-page suit against Jim Jordan coming from the Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . So, a lot -- a lot of moving parts here folks to keep track of, and we will do that here. We'll talk to Jim Jordan tomorrow. We look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Also, Katie Pavlich and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  on the complicated politics of 2024 and the issues that will be front and center.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And on the abortion pill ruling, what are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My thoughts are it's completely out of bounds, what the judge did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking moments from now, we will take you live to this state department briefing, which of course, everybody is going to be watching very closely. Because \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , the secretary of state, Secretary of Defense Austin, will both be there and they will, no doubt, get questions about these embarrassing and potentially dangerous leaks that show our allies, Egypt and the UAE, talking about making deals to sell rockets to Russia, talking about deals to sidestep American and U.K. intelligence.\par \par \uc2 There is stuff in here that truly undermines and raises questions about where the United States is and exactly who our friends are at this point out there.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention concerns about whether or not Ukraine can succeed in this spring offensive. So, a lot of stuff in here, we're going to go straight to that when they come to the podium. And when they start taking questions about this. We'll certainly make sure that you hear what their answers are to that, because that will be pretty important.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a new legal battle could make abortion a huge issue in the 2024 election. You could argue it was a pretty big issue in the midterms, as well.\par \par \uc2 So, now, the justice department is appealing a judge's order to suspend \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of a drug that is used to end most pregnancies in this country.\par \par \uc2 Most pregnancy ending procedures are prescription, OK? Like 61 percent of the abortions in this country are done this way. So, this fight could go all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, with that, we bring in our panel. Katie Pavlich, editor of townhall.com and a Fox News contributor. And \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton adviser and pollster, and chairman of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2 , and the Harris Poll.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you with us today. Here is the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  headline, one of the headlines from an editorial piece today.\par \par \uc2 "Republicans' longtime opposition to abortion is coming back to haunt them."\par \par \uc2 Katie Pavlich, your thoughts on how much this is going to matter in the coming election?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think it all depends on how Republicans handle it.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you look at the 2022 midterms, the candidates that took on this issue full on and didn't bury their head in the sand and act like it was just going to go away and, you know, not allowed the Democrats to define them.\par \par \uc2 J.D. Vance, for example, came out and talked about this issue and beat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia won the governorship, talking about his strong pro-life views.\par \par \uc2 And so, if Republicans for a long time, and especially in the aftermath of jobs, have allowed Democrats to define them as extremists, when a Harvard Harris poll in the aftermath of jobs taken a month later, after the opinion was leaked and released, shows that 72% of the country, including a majority of women, believe in restrictions at 15 weeks. You can't find a single Democrat on the campaign trail in 2024 or in 2022 that will issue a single restriction on abortion. So Republicans have allowed Democrats to define them rather than leaning into the majority opinion about abortion in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Do you agree with that, Mark? Do you think that Democrats were largely allowed to define the entire narrative on this through, you know, TikTok influencers and other places that really made a difference last time, and may this time as well?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think Democrats are winning on this issue. I think even though the public is somewhat split, they oppose third trimester, they favor first trimester abortions, they crossover somewhere in the middle around 15 or 16 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the Republicans better hope that the Justice Department wins this one in the higher courts, because 64% do support use of these drugs in the first 10 weeks. And we're really talking about early term abortions here with these drugs. And that includes 51% of Republicans. So to the extent that this drug then exposes extreme conservatism on the part of Republicans related to choice, it will be a losing issue for them. I think that's pretty clear.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Here's Nancy Mace asked about how she felt about this judge's ruling. And here's what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): This is an \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved drugs. I support the usage of \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved drugs, even if we might disagree. It's not up to us to decide as legislators or even, you know, as the court system that whether or not this is the right drugs use or not, number one. So I agree with ignoring it at this point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Katie, what do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, ignoring a federal court ruling is quite the slippery slope when it comes to possibly causing a constitutional crisis, not