r anything like that. As you know we -- here at the White House we met very often, use influencers to try to meet Americans where they are. We've seen those recordings I just don't have any thing to share on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: So she didn't confirm a plan but she also did not deny the report. This comes as President Biden has not officially announced his own bid for reelection, but this social media strategy would connect him squarely with young voters. And that's why some tech experts call this move genius.\par \par \uc2 Biden's following on both official presidential social media accounts as well as unofficial public accounts trail that of former President Trump by as much as 50 million followers. But it also raises a host of concerns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE DENTON, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Really, it's going to be difficult in the '24 cycle to discern an influencers own perspective, or if they've been given a briefing or an incentive to align with a particular candidate. Or in this case, you know, the city president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: And this tech researcher also says Biden's reliance on influencers is also a conflict of interest. He questions how the President can both leverage the reach from a TikTok influencer, as his administration has voiced support for bipartisan efforts to rein in the technology in the midst of conversations around national security. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's a twisty kind of spot to be. And, Lydia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that we bring in Iowa Republican Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 . Her state is considering banning kids under 18 from having social media accounts without their parents' permission. Governor, good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think a lot of viewers, and a lot of people, a lot of adults don't really understand the influence of TikTok. So these folks on TikTok in huge followings. They tell people what they should eat, what they should buy, what they should wear. And more powerfully, I think in the midterms, who they should vote for, and what issues should be important to them, regardless of any fact check going on, on what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 So, how seriously do you take this if the Biden team decides to really implement use these people in 2024?\par \par \uc2 KIM REYNOLDS\uc2 , IOWA REPUBLICAN GOVERNOR: Yes, Martha. Well, thanks for having me on. I think the hypocrisy that continues out of the Biden administration and with Democrats across this country is just ridiculous. At the same time, he's asking Congress to disallow TikTok because of the ties to the CCP and the national security that it presents to the United States of America. He's falling right into the hands of the CCP by actually using the app to influence him to both boost his reelection efforts. It's a conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, and several other Republican governors across the country have followed suit, we've banned the use of TikTok on state devices because we felt with the ties to the CCP, it was a national security threat. So if in fact it is then he needs to hold firm and the Congress and the President needs to ban TikTok in general. So they need to, you know, put their money where their mouth is and instead of always, you know, do as I say and not as I do, it continues to just drive the hypocrisy that's coming from this administration. It's win at all cost. It's win at all cost.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, during the midterms, there were influencers who basically said, Republicans want to ban abortion nationwide, right, which is not the case. The Supreme Court decision meant that states would have the discretion and the vote over what they want the rules to be in their own state. But that's not what people were told. And you saw students wrapped around the block to vote in Michigan and Wisconsin, it was clearly a factor.\par \par \uc2 So here, I'm going to show people some more of these TikTok influencers. And this is all about how happy they are to be at the White House. But they also talk a lot about issues. And in many cases, they don't necessarily know what they're talking about. But watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNY DRAMA: This is actually the entrance to the West Wing. This is so fun, and it's really prestigious. A POTUS\par \par \uc2 NIA SIOUX, INFLUENCER: Meeting with Madam Vice President, you are a leader.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NIA SIOUX: That makes me so proud.\par \par \uc2 VIVIAN TU, INFLUENCER: We then had it to the official watch party and saw the President cut off. Strengthening the economy was the public's top concern ahead of the speech. All in all, I had a great time love seeing and meeting new friends, and we as a country have a lot more work to do to finish the job. Bye.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So what is your party's way of approaching this? How are you going to sort of up the game in terms of reaching people with the message that you'd like them to have?\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Well, I think we need to be realistic about what's happening. I mean, big tech and social media is having a horrible impact on this country, whether it's politics, Martha, or COVID, or it's the impact that it's having on our young kids. And so, we need to take that into account when we're passing laws, like they've done in Utah and Idaho. And I think Arkansas last night.\par \par \uc2 I mean with COVID basically they suppressed information if you didn't buy into the vaccine mandates, the lock downs or the \uc2 FCC\uc2  on facemask, they were kicked off of the site. And what it's doing to our kids, whether it's the depression or the anxiety or the suicide rates that we're seeing a skyrocketed because of the influence. We need to take this into account we need to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 We're passing -- we're working on a bill through the legislature right now that addresses some of that. But we need to be proactive, and we need to go after them, and we need to hold them accountable for what they're doing and how they're using influencers, and especially TikTok.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, it's clear that Democrats have a big advantage on that platform. And you point out a lot of the contradictions in what they're saying as part of that. Governor, thank you. Very good to see you, Governor Reynolds. Thanks for being here today.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Take care.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we are moments away from Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  and Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 . They have a lot on their minds, these gentlemen, because they're dealing with a 53 documents that leaked, that are embarrassing and raise a lot of questions about who our allies really are, what our national security really is in this country. They also have designated Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  as wrongly detained. So, we think there's going to be some pretty interesting Q&A here. So we're going to keep a close eye on that.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  has graciously agreed to keep an eye on this with us and give us his reaction to what we learn in this press briefing moments away. Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Leaking documents ending up all over the social media networks, and we're going to hear a little bit about this in a moment Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  are about to hold a joint news conference with their Filipino counterparts. But we do expect that some of the questions will be about whether or not they have been able to lock this down, whether they've identified who this leaker is. It's highly sensitive, in some cases embarrassing, information about things that our allies are up to. And also reaction to the Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  wrongful detention status in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson joining us live now from Washington as this gets underway. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. That official designation that \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  is wrongfully detained in Russia means the United States views this as a hostage situation, that the Russian government fabricated these charges. It elevates Evan's case to his special state department office, the envoy for hostage affairs.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is traveling to Belfast. The White House says, on the way Biden spoke with Evan's family. Before leaving on this trip, the President stressed his administration is focused on freeing Evan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: We're making it real clear that it is totally illegal what's happening. We declared itself changes the dynamic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And this new State Department designation gives the hostage negotiators office broader power to coordinate the \uc2 US government\uc2  response and even consider a prisoner exchange. The US has recently negotiated a prisoner swaps with Russia to free Marine veteran Trevor Reed and \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner. White House officials refused to say whether they'll offer another prisoner swap to free of Evan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATION TODAY: We, internally, we're certainly having discussions about what we could do to get him released. Again, I don't want to go into details about these internal deliberations. But having things out in the public sphere this early might actually make it harder to get to get Evan and Paul home. And that's what we're focused on.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: So Paul is Paul Whelan. The US is demanding his release and other American that Russia has charged with espionage and that the US has designated as wrongfully detained. His brother says Paul has now been a hostage of the Kremlin for more than 1,500 days. White House officials say they're still trying to get American diplomats to the prison to meet with Evan. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, we hope that situation loosens up. We certainly should be able to get people in there to check on him. Rich, thank you very much. Rich Edson at the State Department.\par \par \uc2 Glad that back with us now, Republican senator from Louisiana, \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 . Senator, as we wait for this news conference to get underway with Secretary of State Blinken and Secretary of Defense Austin, I wanted to get your thoughts on the leak of these documents and what you think is the most egregious that has been revealed here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): America has 18 intelligence agencies, Martha. We have the greatest intelligence capability of any country in all of human history. I don't believe for a moment given that sophistication that our 18 intelligence agencies can't find, and perhaps don't already know who leaked this information. Just like I also believed that these 18 intelligence agencies can't find and don't already know who leaked the Supreme Court opinion.\par \par \uc2 I think there are powers in the federal government who don't think that the American people are entitled to this information. I think they are wrong. I think both the leaks I just referenced are treason. I think the American people are entitled to see people held accountable for treason. But I don't expect much to come from this unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. Yes, we always hear that no one's above the law, but apparently, maybe some people are. So, senator, before I let you go just very quickly if I can. What was your reaction to the Egyptians talking about potentially giving 40,000 rockets to Russia when we send over a billion dollars in aid every year to our ally Egypt?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well as I've said before we need to stop giving much money to countries that hate us. If they want to hate us, they should be able to hate us for free. If you -- look, we know by now that Mr. Putin has the morals of a hyena. If you want to do business with him, to the detriment of the American people, then you're just as bad as he is, in my judgment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll see what Egypt has to say. We'll see if we learn who this leaker is. There's going to be some questions coming up momentarily. Senator, thank you very much for staying with us today. It's always good to see you. Senator Kennedy from Louisiana. We'll be right back\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, folks, still waiting for the briefing but it will happen eventually. That's THE STORY for today. Thanks for being with us. THE STORY goes on as you know. And we thank you for joining us here everyday. Tomorrow do join us because Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan is going to be here at 3:00. We'll talk to him. Also my podcasts with Father Mike Schmitz is getting tons of attention. Check that out on the Untold Story podcast. I will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230412ej4b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. Jim Jordan (R-OH) on "The Bottom Line"\b0\par\par\uc2 Dagen McDowell, Sean Duffy \par \uc2 2,612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: And let's get right to our guest.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Let's get to it. Let's go. I'm just so excited.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Straight to this FOX Business Alert.\par \par \uc2 Manhattan district attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , the man who is charging former President Trump with 34 felonies, now suing House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: The question is, why? Well, because the Ohio congressman wants answers from a former prosecutor who once led Bragg's investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And who wouldn't want to know?\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Jordan issuing a subpoena for Mark Pomerantz just last Thursday, and, today, Bragg responding, telling FOX News -- quote -- "Chairman Jordan's subpoena is an unconstitutional attempt to undermine an ongoing New York felony criminal prosecution and investigation. This is an unprecedented, illegitimate interference by Congress that lacks any legal merit and defies basic principles of federalism."\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: For more on this, let's bring in the man himself, Jim Jordan, congressman from Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): It's good to be with you guys.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, listen, you just heard us read and you have heard what \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  said about you.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: They used federal funds to indict a former president for no crime.\par \par \uc2 And then, when we want to look into it, when we want to investigate, they take us to court. And they take us to court over the guy we want to talk to, Sean, Dagen -- the guy we want to talk to quit working there a year ago. He wrote a book on this subject, solely on this subject, done all kinds of interviews.\par \par \uc2 But, no, no, no, no, we're not allowed to talk to him. The real interference is coming from Mr. Bragg to our investigation. We have a duty under the Constitution to do oversight and to investigate things like this where they are interfering in a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Have you read the lawsuit that \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported on today? How did you find out about it, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: No. I got -- we got notice from our staff that we -- that this had happened. We're just now reviewing it. We will see what our next steps are.\par \par \uc2 But what I do know is, Mark Pomerantz left that office. He went there solely to go after President Trump, left the office, wrote a book about this subject, done all kinds of interviews. We'd like to talk to him frankly. We want to -- frankly, what -- we want to know what kind of coordination may or may not have happened between the DA's office there in Manhattan and the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 We'd like to know those kind of things. They used federal funds. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has admitted to that. So we want to take a chance -- take some time and look through all this and see what our next step is. We know we're going to have to be in court next week, and we will be ready for that, but we haven't had a chance to fully review it. We're doing that right now.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: So, Pomerantz is named as a defendant in this lawsuit, in fact. And so, by naming him as the defendant, they're trying to block him from testifying after the subpoena last week.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: But I find it interesting. What Bragg is saying is, you're trying to interfere with his criminal prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think it's a fair point to say, well, you're trying to interfere with a presidential election where the lead candidate in the Republican primary is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and you're trying to take him down out of the Manhattan DA's office.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes, he's leading in every poll.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: He's likely our nominee, likely to be the next president. You're a partisan going after him. You're going after him, when the Department of Justice said there was no case here, when the Federal Elections Commission said there was no case here, when your predecessor, Cy Vance, said there was no case here, and when you yourself, when you first get elected, even though you campaigned on it, said there was no case here.\par \par \uc2 And the reason you said that was because \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  was your star witness, a guy who lied six times in front of Congress, went to prison for lying. And then, when Mark Pomerantz resigns, throws a fit, gets the left all fired up, suddenly, you change your mind and bring a case against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And then when we ask to talk to the guy who caused the flip-flop, you say, no, no, no, and you take us to court. I think that just shows how political this is. And it's not just Republicans who are saying that. Even \uc2 Andy McCabe\uc2 , everyone on the left says -- last week, when he rolled out his indictment and they had the arraignment, everyone said, that's it? That's all you got?\par \par \uc2 Andy -- when \uc2 Andy McCabe\uc2  says that, that tells you how political this operation is and why it's our duty in Congress to investigate.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And how bad the case is.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Congressman, do you plan to subpoena \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  himself? Does this lawsuit, based on what you know so far, does it prevent you from doing that? Is that next steps?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: We have -- yes, we have said all along that everything is on the table. We want to go. We want to first look at what they have done to us here. We want to see how this plays out and be ready for going to court here.\par \par \uc2 But nothing -- everything's on the table, because it is -- as I said now a couple of times, it's important for us to get to the bottom of this, get the facts, and then, frankly, look at, is -- are there legislative solutions and remedies that we should take, that we should put into place?\par \par \uc2 One example is, if you're going to have local DAs take federal officials to court, particularly president -- former presidents, to court, and when they're candidates, maybe we should have a law that says that case has to be removed to federal court. You can't try it in a local state court, a local DA bring this kind of action.\par \par \uc2 So we're looking at some legislative remedies as well, but everything's on the table.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Let's move on, because the congressional Gang of Eight now has access to classified documents taken from the homes of President Biden, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and former V.P. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Meanwhile, a report from America First Legal has found ties between the Biden administration and the raid at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 So, again, they're saying, hey, the White House who said, we had nothing to do, we were shocked when we found out that there was a raid at Mar-a-Lago, now America first legal is saying, well, no, no, in our -- in our request for documents, you actually were facilitating the documents through the -- through the records-keeping organization to the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: What do you know about this?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Just what I have read. I find it a big concern if that took place.\par \par \uc2 And if it did take place, it doesn't really surprise me because, remember, when it came to President Biden and his classified documents issue, early on, it wasn't Republicans who said this. It was The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  who reported that there was a -- quote -- "shared understanding" between the Biden White House and DOJ and NARA when they went to get his documents that they were going to keep that quiet for when they went to look for them.\par \par \uc2 So, this shared understanding that the Biden White House had with the DOJ, again, if that took place, as reported by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  -- so maybe this did happen. I don't know. I have just heard the news, like -- like you guys did, earlier today.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I want to move on to what your committee is doing with the issuing a subpoena to \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  over a memo that the bureau sought to cultivate sources in Catholic Churches that use the Latin mass in order to combat domestic extremism.\par \par \uc2 Sean and I reported on as soon as it happened.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: This memo came out of the Richmond field office and drew criticism even from -- well, of course, from Wray himself. He did condemn the memo when he was asked about it last month.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , \uc2 FBI\uc2  DIRECTOR: When I first learned of the piece, I was aghast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As you should be.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: And we took steps immediately to withdraw it and remove it from \uc2 FBI\uc2  systems. It does not reflect \uc2 FBI\uc2  standards. We do not conduct investigations based on religious affiliation or practices, full stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: But, Congressman, they were trying to -- had every...\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: This was signed off on by \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, including an attorney in the Richmond field office.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: They were planning to cultivate clergy to rat out members of their parishes and churches.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: As sources.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: How far did this go? How many field offices got the memo? How many...\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: How widespread is this?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, we know it was sent to more than just Richmond. So Richmond put together the memorandum.\par \par \uc2 And you're exactly right. They were trying to develop sources within the Catholic Church, develop informants within Catholic parishes to spy, I guess, on fellow parishioners. Now, that's frightening, in and of itself.\par \par \uc2 But, as you point out, two senior analysts signed off on this and the chief division counsel. So the top lawyer in the Richmond field office signed off on this. I wonder where this person went to law school, because this is such an attack on the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 And I don't know that -- I mean, it's good that they pulled this and they stopped it, as \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  said. But would they have done that if we didn't find out about it, if we didn't have brave whistle-blowers come forward and tell us what was going on?\par \par \uc2 So this is as wrong as it gets. And when you read through the somewhat unredacted copy that we got -- there's still redactions in it, but when you read through it, you can sort of read between the lines. Look at the footnotes where they talk about pro-life Catholics. They talk about the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 They were really targeting people just like Sean and his family, pro-life Catholics, pro-life traditional Catholics. That's who they're defining as radical traditional Catholics. They're just pro-life Americans, pro-life Catholics who are going to church every Sunday. And to think that some element of the clergy or some other layperson that's in their church leadership is actually reporting back to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on their activities is truly frightening.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I often go to Latin mass. And I -- listen, I served in Congress as well, Congressman Jordan.\par \par \uc2 And of all the places you would want to look for radical extremism, those who go to the Catholic mass are the least likely people to be radical extremists, the nicest, holiest, most generous people you will come across.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: But they don't agree with the regime.\par \par \uc2 The regime says, if you don't agree with us...\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: ... on abortion and politics, we're going to come for you.\par \par \uc2 But -- and listen, when I first read this, Jim, I -- listen, this was a memo. But now, and as we dig a little deeper, we find out that they had an undercover agent inside one of the churches that helped them produce the memo.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, they have already infiltrated the church and wanted to go even further.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I wonder if this is just the beginning. Are we just getting the crumbs of actually what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing to spy on Americans?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes, two things.\par \par \uc2 One, Sean, when you read through this, the family you think about is you and Rachel's family, like, good Catholic families who are -- God bless you. That's who you think. Like, that's who they're going after. That's who they're saying is radical.\par \par \uc2 So that's scary, in and of itself. But then when you view it in the broader picture, we know we learned four weeks ago that the FTC sent several letters to a private company saying, who are the journalists you're talking to? They named four personally. Two of those four came and testified in front of our committee.\par \par \uc2 While they're testifying, Democrat members of Congress asked those two journalists, who are your sources? While they're getting those kind of questions from Democrats, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is knocking on one of those journalists' door.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and then you put all that together with what's happened to parents, to Catholics, to pro-lifers from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and you're thinking like, wow, this is why we formed the committee under Speaker McCarthy to look into how agencies have been turned on the American people, and we think targeting the American people, instead of serving -- serving them like they're supposed to.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: When will you be able to talk to, interview, again grill \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, we want the documents. We the unredacted version of this memo. We want the documents that were going back and forth when they developed this memo.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, we want to talk to the people who wrote it and approved it. So we'd like to talk to those individuals. And then, at some point, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  will be in front of our committee sometime between now and the and the summer break of Congress. We expect to have \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  in for his annual trek to the Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 And we will have a lot of questions for him. Some of them certainly will be about this issue.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Congressman, there's a lot of people who think that our government operates the way it did back in 1980s, 1990s. It doesn't. The government is at war with half of the country.\par \par \uc2 God bless you and your fellow Republicans for having the Weaponization of Government Committee and exposing what the government is doing to half the country. Without you guys, I don't think we will ever know. I think it's great work. And thank you for doing it. And thanks for joining us on "The Bottom Line." We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, you bet. Thanks for what you guys do. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020230411ej4b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Employee Kills Four And Wounds Nine Others; Expelled Lawmakers Seek Reappointment; China Comment Following Military Drills Around Taiwan; White House Coy On Biden Outreach To Xi; President In No Hurry To Announce Another Election Run; U.S. Lawmakers Seek To Reassure Pacific Allies; Rep. Michael McCaul (R-TX) Is Being Interviewed About China Conducting Taiwan Drills After Prominent Visits; Two Judges Issue Contradictory Rulings On Legality Of Abortion Medication Mifepristone; Texas Governor Plans To Pardon Man Convicted Of Murder For Shooting Black Lives Matter Protester; Biden Administration May Propose New Rules To Limit Tailpipe Emissions From Vehicles; Company Uses Artificial Intelligence To Test If Essays Submitted By Students Actually Written By Artificial Intelligence; China Increasing Tensions With Taiwan And U.S.; President Biden Still Yet To Announce Presidential Reelection Bid\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Garrett Tenney, Charles Watson, Greg Palkot, David  Spunt, David Lee Miller, Casey Stegall, Grady Trimble, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 7,766 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: He probably had stick them on his hands from the Super Bowl. So much illegal stick in them that it's still on there months after the game.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," Johnny asked people about Easter. Let's see a little tease.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hurry up. Hurry up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY BELISARIO, FOX NEWS PRODUCER: What happened on Easter?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There was a lot of people throwing eggs at each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): We waited for that soundbite. That's good.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 China says it's ready for a fight over Taiwan, and the country is moving military equipment. What the Biden administration is saying and doing in response.\par \par \uc2 The legal battle over the so-called abortion pill intensifies. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 And we begin our series on the rise of artificial intelligence with a look at how it impacts education.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", at least four people killed this morning during a shooting at a bank in Louisville, Kentucky. The 23-year-old shooter live streamed that attack on social media until he was taken down by authorities.\par \par \uc2 The man was an employee at the bank. Three police officers are among nine wounded. One who's been on the job less than two weeks is in critical but stable condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Louisville with the latest. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Now, Bret, good evening tonight.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are searching the suspect's home and his digital footprint, seeking an explanation for why he carried out this horrific attack, killing four of his co-workers and injuring nine others this morning when he walked into \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  in downtown Louisville with a rifle of some kind and started shooting. Forcing other employees to run for their lives as he livestreamed the rampage on social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was with -- looking around the -- open the door to see where he was at, and I could see him still shooting. I didn't see his face. And then, I took off running out the front door.\par \par \uc2 And I saw a rifle, and just -- and that we heard it click. And the lady next to me turned around and said, what the hell, and then, he just started shooting. Whoever's next to me got shot and get bloods on me from it.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): Officers arrived within three minutes of the first 911 calls and immediately confronted the gunman. 23-year-old, Connor Sturgeon, who started shooting at the officers before they took him down.\par \par \uc2 Three officers were injured in that gun battle, including 26-year-old officer Nickolas Wilt, who was shot in the head and remains in critical condition after undergoing brain surgery this afternoon. He has been on the force less than two weeks.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, city leaders said the quick response of those officers was nothing short of heroic, and there is no doubt they saved lives because of it.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GREENBERG, MAYOR OF LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY: Our LMPD officers went into that building, knowing that they were risking their lives to save -- their lives to save other lives. And that's exactly what they did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): This attack hits close to home for both the mayor and Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , who were both close friends with several of the victims killed in today's attack. And both men got emotional during press conferences today talking about that.\par \par \uc2 And while the White House has already jumped on this, calling for more gun control reform, local leaders here say, this is not the time to have those conversations. And right now, the focus needs to be on those victims and their families. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney, live in Louisville. Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There were new demonstrations today in Nashville, Tennessee, over the expulsion of two Democratic state legislators there. And there are efforts tonight underway to have them reinstated.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson is in Nashville tonight with the latest on that story. Good evening, Charles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Protesters are back out in Nashville today. I want to show you a picture of some of them.\par \par \uc2 Some of these folks coming from as far as Nebraska to demand the Tennessee legislature take action on gun reform.\par \par \uc2 WATSON (voice over): Today's protest comes as the state House is back in session for the first time since successful Republican-led efforts expelled two Democratic lawmakers for holding a demonstration on the House floor, which GOP members say broke the quorum.\par \par \uc2 Many of those folks have called the move unfair and say they want to show support for the two -- the two Democrats, Justin Jones and Justin Pearson.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to Jones, we just learned moments ago that Nashville's Metro Council voted unanimously to reinstate Jones back to his seat. He is now headed back to the Tennessee State House as an interim representative, just four days after losing that seat.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to a constitutional scholar, who tells us the Metro Council is well within its right to bring Jones back. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARRIE RUSSELL, PROFESSOR, I POLITICAL SCIENCE DEPARTMENT, VADERBILT UNIVERSITY: The Constitution requires representation and it allows for the local government to choose who that person is that will be filling that vacancy until the next special election can be held.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Now, the letter sent to Republican House Speaker Cameron Sexton. Lawyers representing both Jones and former Representative Justin Pearson of Memphis, urged the Speaker to promptly swear in both members of the general -- as general -- members of the General Assembly, should they be reinstated and grant them, "the same benefits, rights, duties, and liberties as any other member."\par \par \uc2 Sexton's office tells Fox News in an e-mail, if Jones or Pearson are reinstated, "Those two individuals will be seated as representatives as the constitution requires."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON (on camera): And we just saw Jones arrived to the Tennessee State House just a few moments ago. He will be a part of the session tonight. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles Watson live in Tennessee. Charles, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also, "BREAKING TONIGHT", China's military says it is ready to fight after completing large scale combat exercises around Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 The drills followed a week in which Taiwan's president met with the Speaker of the U.S. House in California and a congressional delegation visited Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration says China's actions are an overreaction. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot begins our coverage on this tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Yes, China has never renounced the use of military might to get control of Taiwan. The last few days, that was very apparent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Taiwan, facing another strong show of Chinese force, the target of deadly realistic combat exercises.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of Chinese war planes and Navy ships crossing the strait between China and the self-governed island it claims as its own, into Taiwan self- defense air zone, seeing if Beijing can seal it off.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, fighter jets from a Chinese aircraft carrier launched on mock missions. Also, in the air, nuclear missile capable Chinese bombers.\par \par \uc2 Chinese military even posted a video showing an obliterated Taiwan. Saying, in part in a statement, "The theatre's troops are ready to fight at all times and can fight at any time to resolutely smash any form of Taiwan independence and foreign interference attempts.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's defense is on high alert, officials telling Fox, war fears are real.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH WU\uc2 , TAIWANESE FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): Any accident, my spark, an uncontrollable war in between Taiwan and China.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: China's exercises were triggered by Taiwan President \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen's meeting in California last week with Republican House Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 When Tsai returned, fireworks started. They also started after another congressional delegation left Taiwan, Capitol Hill sounding hawkish.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I'd be very much open to using U.S. forces to defend Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: And Beijing held off until French President Macron ended his China visit. In an exit interview, Macron sounded dovish, saying Europe should not get involved with U.S. in China over Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Crisis watchers think any real Chinese invasion of Taiwan is a way's off, but the war threat is present.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HO, SINGAPORE-CHINA EXPERT: I think it's the risk for Beijing to pick what it really wants to embark on. Which conflict across the Taiwan Straits.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: As for life on Taiwan recently, remarkably, according to reports, there's no sign of panic or disruption among the locals. Maybe they're used to it and the officials can only hope it could possibly stay that way in the future. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Palkot live in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There is still no indication when President Biden might actually speak with his Chinese counterpart. But there is new information about what the president is planning regarding a possible reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is at the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: What we hope is next, is that, again, tensions deescalate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The White House, critical of China, following multiple days of military drills aimed to rattle Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden has yet to confront his Chinese counterpart directly.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): We know the president can pick up the phone anytime and call President Xi. We've been told that this call is coming for months.\par \par \uc2 Why hasn't he just picked up the phone and call President Xi to say knock it off?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president looks forward to having another conversation with President Xi. And we'll do that at the appropriate time. And it's important that those lines of communication stay open.\par \par \uc2 The tensions are certainly high right now. We like to see this relationship get on to a better footing.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): But the White House, first, said Biden and President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  would talk two months ago, after the U.S. shot a Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the president will head overseas. Nowhere near China or Taiwan, but to Ireland and Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 On another international issue, the White House says it doesn't know if it is contained the leak of classified U.S. documents dealing with Ukraine's planned military offensive. While confirming, some of the material appears doctored.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We're still working through the validity of all the documents that we know are out there.\par \par \uc2 The department of defense has stood up, an interagency effort to try to look at the national security implications.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The only public appearance for the president today on the White House South Lawn for the Easter Egg Roll, a tradition dating back to 1878.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President!\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: When pressed on his 2024 plans --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I plan on running out, but we're not prepared to announce it yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): OK. So, the timeline, Bret, has moved back a little bit. We were told President Biden would be talking with his family about 2024 plans over Christmas. It's possible he's made a decision, but all indications are he's not ready to announce it just yet. Could even be months away, according to some that are close to the president. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will track that. David, we also understand the president signed a measure tonight, officially terminating the national COVID-19 emergency?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: That's exactly true. And it came unceremoniously after more than a million deaths tragically in the United States, and after three years since COVID-19 hit this country more than three years.\par \par \uc2 We just got this notice from the White House. I'm just going to hold it up, it says, on Monday, April 10th, 2023, the president signed into law House Joint Resolution 7, which terminates the national emergency related to the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Bret, this was a move by Senate Republicans and House Republicans to force him to end this. And while the president did not actually want to do this, according to White House officials, ultimately, he did not veto it, he gave in and signed it.\par \par \uc2 So, just like that the COVID emergency is officially over. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One line on a piece of paper. David Spunt, live on the North Lawn. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will talk with House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman Michael McCaul, who led that congressional delegation to Taiwan in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the House Judiciary Committee will hold a field hearing in New York City to talk about what it calls the pro-crime anti-victim policies of Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The announcement comes less than a week after the arraignment of former President Trump on 34 felony counts of falsifying business records. The former president has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Bragg's office has responded by saying, "Don't be fooled, the House GOP is coming to the safest big city in America for a political stunt."\par \par \uc2 Well, House Judiciary Committee chairman, Jim Jordan, says he is issuing a subpoena to \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  over an alleged effort to acquire informants within Catholic churches.\par \par \uc2 Jordan says the bureau tried to develop sources among clergy to report on people, "practicing their faith". The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it is cooperating.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Wray, said he was aghast when he learned of the allegation. He insisted the \uc2 FBI\uc2  does not conduct investigations based on religious affiliation or practices.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today, ahead of