70 Chinese fighter aircraft as we departed the island, they are actually surrounding the island at that time.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it's one of the larger provocations they've done, but also the sort of saber rattling kind of bully system, if you will, against Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what is the feeling in Taiwan about that? I mean, we heard the foreign minister, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  has this quote from the Taiwanese Foreign Minister, I wouldn't count on the partners in this area to defend Taiwan, he said. I need to make it very clear that defending Taiwan is our own responsibility. And we have the will to defend ourselves. And what we need in this process is for the United States or other major countries, to provide Taiwan with means for Taiwan to be able to defend itself. And the United States has been doing that.\par \par \uc2 Essentially saying, we got that as long as you provide us the stuff.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, first of all, we haven't provided the stuff.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I mean, that's the problem. I signed off on these weapons three years ago, Bret. 22 weapons systems that have yet to go into Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Deterrence is really key here, not for war, but for peace. That's why we need the weapons there to deter an invasion by Chairman Xi. And if we don't have that, they can't adequately defend.\par \par \uc2 We also need the training exercises, combat, and we met with the Japanese and South Koreans at INDOPACOM and Hawaii get briefings on the plan, the strategy.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure our allies are there, our partners are there, or at least provide us with the forward basing that we can use in the event of an invasion, which hopefully will not happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, so, what's the holdup on the equipment? I mean, is Ukraine hindering the ability to get stuff to Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: No, it's not that. We're putting an old equipment in Ukraine, Russian stuff in Ukraine, this is new equipment. So, this is a production issue. It's a Pentagon problem. State Department problem.\par \par \uc2 We need to reprioritize, put those weapons in, and use allies that have the weapons to put those into Taiwan as well.\par \par \uc2 For without deterrence, you will invite aggression. And this administration has always projected weakness not strength.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've heard you say that it's possible that China could take over Taiwan without a shot being fired if the former pro-China president is returned to power in January. So, that's a real concern politically in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Yes, I think that's an important point is that a former President Ma of Taiwan was in China meeting with leaders of the PRC to discuss who would be their preferred candidate in the presidential election next January, less than a year from now.\par \par \uc2 I think what they're going to try and do is win this without a shot fired by influencing the election and having the PRC candidate become the next president of Taiwan. Not unlike what they did in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, after Hong Kong fell, and then Ukraine got invaded by Putin and Russia, Taiwan is very nervous and they should be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The other person who was in China was French President Macron, who emphasize that Europe must resist pressure to become America's followers.\par \par \uc2 And while in China, said that the West is in decline and China is ascendant and the relationship, he questioned the U.S. relationship, in not so many words in this political article, but the quotes are pretty clear. Are you surprised by that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Yes, it was. I think he's a little bit desperate politically back home and that's why he's doing this. He's trying to throw the United States under the bus. He is not leading.\par \par \uc2 If anything, he emboldened Chairman Xi and Putin by taking this trip, particularly after, you know, President Tsai met with the speaker McCarthy, and with my delegation, right at the same time, I think it was a moment of weakness for Macron.\par \par \uc2 And if he's not leading, and if we don't have the president of the United States leading, it's dangerous, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, what does that say? So, China's doing deals with Saudi and Iran. China is obviously getting close to Russia. You have the French president saying, you know, maybe we should focus on China. I mean, is this a signal from Europe also that the U.S. is projecting weakness? Is that what you're hearing?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: No, I think ever since Afghanistan fell we have projected weakness, not strength, and that's why you saw Putin invade Ukraine and Chairman Xi threatening, you know, Pacific.\par \par \uc2 What I have found in my studies, anytime we don't lead the United States, our enemies and adversaries will fill the void. Anytime we don't lead, China always fills that void, whether it be Saudis, and the Middle East to Eastern Europe to the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Chairman, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Thanks so much, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Up next, the governor of Texas says he plans to pardon a \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Sergeant accused of murdering a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protester. We'll bring you to that story.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Biden administration continues to try to force you to buy an expensive electric vehicle, we'll tell you about that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's considerable confusion tonight over the fate of the so- called abortion pill. Competing rulings by two federal judges over the availability of mifepristone are reigniting the political battle over the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is appealing one of those decisions tonight and the group suing to block government approval has been told to file a legal response by tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Lee Miller has the latest from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The legal battle over the abortion inducing drug mifepristone is escalating. The Justice Department is seeking an emergency stay to keep the drug available. Its legal brief reads in part, "When mifepristone is used as \uc2 FDA\uc2  directs, serious adverse events are exceedingly rare."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, federal judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee on the bench in Texas, issued a temporary order slated to take effect at the end of this week, reversing the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's 23-year-old approval of the drug, citing safety concerns. Complicating matters, almost simultaneously on Friday in Washington state, U.S. District Judge Thomas Rice, an Obama appointee, granted an order requested from 17 Democratic controlled states and Washington, D.C., to prevent the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from limiting access to the drug in those states.\par \par \uc2 Seeking guidance if both orders remain in force, Justice Department lawyers today filed a brief in Washington state asking which court has the final word. While some Democrats have called for just ignoring the Texas ruling, the White House says they must win in court.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It would also set a dangerous precedent for this administration to disregard, right, a binding decision. So that's what I'm getting to. But again, we are ready to fight. This is going to be a long fight.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Many Republicans have avoided public discussion of the legal battle. South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who has proposed a bill banning most abortions after 15 weeks, is streaking out.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): We can win this issue at the ballot box if we show up with reasonable positions. If we have our head in the sand, we're going to lose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MILLER (on camera): Legal experts say the legal limbo of mifepristone could fast track the matter to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Lee, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The mother of the Virginia first grader who shot his teacher in January is being charged with felony child neglect and endangerment. It says the 25- year-old Deja Taylor is accused of leaving the weapon where that child could get to it. The boy is not being charged criminally. The teacher is suing administrators for $40 million, saying they ignored warnings about that child.\par \par \uc2 Police in Florida have arrested a third person in connection with the murders of three teenagers. The last of the three suspects was arrested Saturday. All three are minors. Authorities have blamed the killings on what they call wannabe gangs.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Texas says he plans to pardon a \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  sergeant convicted of murder for shooting a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protester. The soldier says the man raised a gun at him. The governor insists it was a case of self-defense, but there are many questions tonight. Senior correspondent Casey Stegall is in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We, the jury, find the defendant, Daniel Perry, guilty of --\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Despite being convicted of murder late last week by an Austin jury, Texas Governor Greg Abbott and other top GOP leaders are calling for Daniel Perry's full pardon.\par \par \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2 , (R) TEXAS LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: He never should have been charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Perry, a \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  sergeant was driving for Uber back in July, 2020, and says his vehicle was suddenly surrounded by \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protesters, one of whom he claims pointed an AK-47 his way. Witnesses say that's not true and his weapon was pointed down the whole time.\par \par \uc2 Yet, Perry says he fired in self-defense, killing 28-year-old Garrett Foster, a \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  veteran. Foster's family now feels a sense of justice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are happy with the verdict.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Governor Greg Abbott, however, is not. His administration believes this is clearly a case of self-defense and nobody should be criminally charged.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: We have strong stand your ground laws, the Castle doctrine in Texas, which expands to if you are in your automobile or anywhere.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: They also accuse Democratic D.A.s of playing politics.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , (R) TEXAS ATTORNEY GENERAL: The legislature needs to address malicious prosecutions and prosecutors who are not doing their jobs, who are not prosecuting real crimes. This guy ignores real crime.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Travis County D.A. Jose Garza, a Democrat, calls Governor Abbott's pardon request troubling. In a statement Garza goes on to say quote, "In our legal system, a jury gets to decide whether a defendant is guilty or innocent, not the governor." Twelve jurors deliberated more than 15 hours to reach their unanimous verdict Friday following a nearly two week long trial.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: And tonight, the \uc2 Texas Board of Pardons and Paroles\uc2  actually confirms to FOX that the governor has, in fact, asked for expedited investigation, and they say they will commence one immediately. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall in Dallas. Casey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration also is said to be ready to propose new rules to limit tailpipe emissions from vehicles. It's a continuation of the effort to force consumers to purchase expensive electric automobiles, or at least incentivize that. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble is at the White House with this story tonight. Good evening, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The new tailpipe emission standards could ensure that electric vehicles make up more than two thirds of all new vehicle sales by 2032. They will be the strictest rules to date according to "The Wall Street Journal." "The Journal" reports the new standards will first apply to model year 2027 vehicles and gradually increase through model year 2032 vehicles. The White House hasn't confirmed these rules because they are not final yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm certainly not going to get ahead of an ongoing policy process. I want to be careful here. But look, when it comes to climate crisis it requires ambitious actions. And the president has been delivering on those ambitious actions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The Biden administration has a long way to go, though, to hit its E.V. goals. Right now, fewer than six percent of all new cars sold are electric, and most of the batteries come from China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE SOMMERS, \uc2 AMERICAN PETROLEUM INSTITUTE\uc2 : We shouldn't just be looking at this from a perspective of emissions, but we should also be looking at it from a national security perspective. We should not be sacrificing American energy independence to a regime that is certainly hostile American interests.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The \uc2 EPA\uc2  is expected to announce those new tailpipe emission standards in Detroit on Wednesday. Some of the automakers in that city have warned that if they have to put more money into gas powered vehicles to meet these new standards, that's less money, Bret, they can invest in electric vehicles. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble at the White House. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we begin our series on the rise of artificial intelligence by looking at its effect on education.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York as thousands of professors, part time lecturers and graduate student workers at New Jersey's flagship university, go on strike. It is the first such job action in the 257 year history of \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 . Classes were still being held as picket lines were set up at the school's campuses.\par \par \uc2 WFXT in Boston with the aftermath of a fire at a Cambridge church just minutes after Easter Sunday services. Clouds of black smoke could be seen billowing out of the steeple at the Faith Lutheran Church as balls of bright orange flames occasionally erupted during the fire fight as firefighters tried to put out that blaze.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Tampa from FOX 13. One of the big stories there tonight, the Rays are on a major role to start the baseball system. Tampa the first team to start with nine straight wins in 20 years. \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2  have won every game by at least four runs. No team has done that since the 1939 \uc2 New York Yankees\uc2 . There you go, a little baseball history.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we begin a new series on the rise of artificial intelligence. This evening, how A.I. is affecting education. Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thanks to Artificial Intelligence, complicated assignments like essays and reports can be produced by chat bots like ChatGPT within seconds. Now schools nationwide are trying to figure out how best to detect who is cheating with A.I.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER CUI, GPTZERO: At a basic level we are using A.I. to catch A.I.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Alexander Cui helped create GPTZero. The site, launched in January, allows educators to upload a student's work to determine if the content is original or likely computer generated.\par \par \uc2 CUI: Essentially, we have our own A.I. that we check against. And we feed it the document and we ask it how likely it is that you would written the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: For schools the abuse of A.I. poses a real threat. But some educators see opportunity.\par \par \uc2 DIEGO MARIN, OLD ORCHARD JUNIOR HIGH SCHOOL: I want you guys to use ChatGPT to help you along the way.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Diego Marin, an eighth grade math teacher in Skokie, Illinois, encourages students to use A.I. on some assignments.\par \par \uc2 MARIN: I think this has helped kids ask better questions and not just saying I don't get it, but what specifically don't you get.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Some colleges are also embracing, A.I., hoping to equip students for what is likely to be a growing and lucrative career field.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are endless healthcare applications of A.I.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Georgetown University Medical Center and \uc2 MedStar Health\uc2  joined forces to develop lessons on how to use A.I. for those studying health equity. Nawar Shara is with MedStar's Health Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 NAWAR SHARA, MEDSTAR HEALTH RESEARCH INSTITUTE: We are trying to equip these kids with the tools that would make them competitive and have the ability to work in the near future.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: While Shara compares the future of the A.I. to the industrial revolution, others warn its true impact on everyday life, including education, remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The \uc2 U.S. Department of Education\uc2  says it, too, wants to know more about how A.I. impacts America's classroom. The Department is expected to release the first federal report on A.I. in America's schools later on this spring. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Mark, thank you. Tomorrow, the push to possibly regulate artificial intelligence right here in Washington. That's part two. We'll bring it to you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel. China says it is ready for a fight. President Biden says he is all but in the presidential race, and the former president issues a warning. We'll get reaction to all of that.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Thousands of Israelis led by at least seven cabinet ministers marched to evacuated West Bank settlement. It's a defiant signal that Israel's government is determined to accelerate settlement building on those lands despite international opposition.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  prohibits families and women from restaurants with gardens or green spaces in Afghanistan's northwestern Herat province. The move comes following complaints from religious scholars and members of the public about mixing of genders in such places. It was the latest in a slew of restrictions imposed by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  since it took back power after the U.S. military withdrawal in 2021.\par \par \uc2 And Pope Francis renews call to pray for peace on Easter Monday. The Pope celebrating during a traditional prayer in St. Peters Square. Sunday, the Pope invoked prayers for the Ukrainian and Russian people. He had skipped the Good Friday nighttime procession after being hospitalized for bronchitis.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We are monitoring the exercises closely, as I think you might imagine. They appear to be a reaction to something that didn't need to be reacted to.\par \par \uc2 REP. AMI BERA (D-CA): I think we have to keep all options open. The goal here right now, though, is really deterrence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): Strategic ambiguity is not working. China after Afghanistan believes the store is open, that you can go in and take whatever you want on Biden's watch.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH WU\uc2 , TAIWANESE FOREIGN MINISTER: I think we need to think of something to stop the war from happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's tense. Taiwan, China, military drills, some 70 sorties by Chinese military over the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel and start there, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina. Brit, how do you interpret all the actions back and forth over the past few days and what we are seeing from China?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I think it's kind of a peculiar combination of strength in the sense of military strength, which they have in abundance, and insecurity, that they would be so upset about the kind of visits back and forth between Taiwanese leaders that have been going on for decades. There's no abandon the administration intends to abandon its one China policy, which has always been a little ambiguous because we want Taiwan to be left alone, yet we are not declaring ourselves in support of its independence. So we really haven't changed very much. And it's a little puzzling that a nation so strong would be so sensitive to something that's going on so long.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Mara, there are questions. People out there that say China maybe is not as strong as we perceive or make them to be. They have their own internal problems. And yet when these delegations go to Taiwan, when the Taiwanese president comes here, their reaction is that.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, and it could be a reaction out of insecurity. I think the big question is, and Representative McCaul raised this earlier in the show, can the United States and its allies arm Taiwan enough that it becomes a porcupine, in other words it becomes really unpleasant and untenable for China to try to swallow it? And I think that that depends partially on how many weapons we can get to Taiwan and how fast.\par \par \uc2 It also depends on the lessons that China takes away from the war in Ukraine. Is the message, boy, we better move fast and take Taiwan, or, we'd better think twice because Russia thought it would be a piece of cake and it wasn't?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is John Kirby at the White House on all of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: There's no reason for tensions across the Taiwan Strait to devolve into any kind of conflict. Nothing has changed about our one China policy, nothing has changed about the fact that we don't support Taiwan's independence. And nothing has changed about the fact that we don't want to see the status quo change unilaterally and certainly not by force.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R-TX) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I think ever since Afghanistan fell, we have projected weakness and not strength. Any time we don't lead, China always fills that void.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Yes, it's not just Afghanistan. The Republican Party can't make up its mind whether or to support Ukraine. Fifty Republicans voted to remove all troops from Syria, and yet they want Biden to retaliate more heavily against Iran. This is what John Kirby said that I don't get. He is waiting on the right time to have an appropriate conversation between Biden and \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . You had a spy balloon, you had China meeting with a wanted international war criminal, and now you have them threatening Taiwan. If that's not the right time to have a phone call, I don't know when it would be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of right time, right time to get in a presidential race officially. This is the president at the Easter egg roll this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I plan on at least three or more four Easter egg rolls.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At least three or four more?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Maybe five.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maybe five?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Maybe six, what the hell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you saying that you would be taking part in our upcoming election in 2024?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I will either be rolling an egg or being the guy who pushed them out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Help a brother out. Make some news for me.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, no, no. I'm planning on running, Al, but we are not prepared to announce it yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Or just tell Al Roker there, Brit.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's being equated with an announcement of his candidacy, which I don't think that's what it is. It is consistent with what he has been saying all along, which is that he intends to run, he plans to run, he wants to run. But it's not an announcement.\par \par \uc2 And look, there is something you have to keep in mind here. And look, I'm not saying he's not going to be run, but I will say this. If you announce at this stage of a presidency with a year-and-a-half in his term that you are not going to run again, you are an automatic, instantaneously, automatically and instantaneously a lame duck. So I'm not sure if he was not going to run he wouldn't be saying the same things that he is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play this soundbite. This is from President Biden with "60 Minutes" September 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The pandemic is over. We still have a problem with COVID. We are still doing a lot of work on it. It's -- but the pandemic is over. If you notice, no one is wearing masks. Everybody seems to be in pretty good shape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is April 2023, and just this afternoon, with no fanfare, came this statement from the White House, mara. Monday, April 10th, 2023, the president has signed into law this resolution which terminates the national emergency related to the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yep. That was a technical end to the pandemic, even though \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said it was over long ago. And most every American has felt the same way. I think COVID is in the rearview mirror. I think that's good for the president.\par \par \uc2 I just wanted to say something about what Brit said. The minute he doesn't announce or announces he is not running, he's a lame duck. The other thing that happens is the minute he does announce he becomes a candidate instead of a president, and that changes things for him, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there's this sense in the White House briefing room, a kind of an exasperation. Mara, you are there a lot. And it kind of bubbled up today about not having a formal press conference with the president. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why can't the president take questions from the press?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Right now we just don't have anything on the books for a press conference at this moment. The president always loves to take your questions, shouted questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We haven't had that opportunity in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'll say this. It is also unprecedented that a president takes as many shouted questions as this president has, and he has.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, no.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I don't have anything to share with you at the time. And I will just leave it there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mara, you're in there. There is some frustration in there.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. This is just the perennial frustration. We want our chance to have a formal press conference with every president as much as possible. And this White House has decided it's just not a good idea to put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in front of the press in that kind of a venue, in that kind of a format.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I think they are right. Did you listen to the interview with Al Roker? That's a friendly interview and it's not a tough question. And I want to be sensitive and not overly critical, but he didn't shine brightly in a friendly interview about whether or not you are going to run again. So do you really think he's going to be able to handle classified information beside his Corvette kind of questions?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, Brit, we ask every week the White House for the president to come on SPECIAL REPORT, a fair interview right here. He hasn't taken us up on it yet.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Nor anybody else's lately.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please don't drop her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I won't. I got her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Police officers in Indiana worked together to rescue six people including a baby from a burning house. Police were on scene within one minute of a 911 call from a family trapped on the second floor of this house ablaze. Officers evacuated the first-floor occupants. Another officer located a nearby ladder, climbed his way up to the upstairs window. The family then carefully handed off their child to him, and soon as after all were guided down to safety. All three officers seen here involved in that rescue have been nominated for the Medal of Valor. Congratulations. Great story.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our monthly look at what the polls are telling us about the 2024 presidential race with Republican and Democratic pollsters Kellyanne Conway and Celinda Lake.\par \pa