 trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, CHAIRMAN AND CEO, \uc2 UBS AMERICAS\uc2 : You work in L.A. So, young people are moving there. That's just a fact.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, they're not, they're not filling our office space. Office vacancy space in the business district, 23 percent -- it was 13 percent pre pandemic. All right, I'm going to have you both back because it's a good conversation. Thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I will. Good to see you, Robert.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And Bill Melugin, it's awesome to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We're going to keep watching that White House press briefing and also monitoring the news conference out of Louisville. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And I'm Bill Melugin, THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Phil and Sandra, thank you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in from Martha MacCallum Any moment now officials will provide an update on the fatal shooting at a bank in Louisville, Kentucky. You're looking live there. That's the podium. We anticipate this will begin any second, any minute. At least four people are dead, nine injured, including two police officers.\par \par \uc2 The deputy police chief has revealed the shooter who was killed had a connection to the bank possibly a current, maybe a former employee. President Biden and Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  had been briefed as we wait for this news conference to get underway any second now. But first, let's get a breaking news update live to Nate Foy. Nate, what are we learning at this hour?\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Trace, police say at about 8:30 this morning, the shooter opened fire at an old National Bank which is in downtown Louisville. You mentioned four people are dead, at least nine others are injured, which is one more than police last told us in the previous update. Two a police officers are injured, one of them is in critical condition and is undergoing emergency surgery this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The other officer is described as having less serious injuries and is expected to survive. Kentucky Governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , the Louisville Mayor and the Louisville Police Department are all praising the quick response from police officers who arrived at the bank within three minutes of the shooting. Again, we're expecting another press conference any second now. But the last time that we heard from officials, Governor Beshear shared his personal connection to this attack. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): This is awful. I have a very close friend that didn't make it today. And I have another close friend who did neither, and one who's at the hospital that I hope is going to make it through.\par \par \uc2 PAUL HUMPHREY, LOUISVILLE METRO P.D. DEPUTY CHIEF: This is a tragic event. But it was, it was the heroic response of officers that made sure that no more people were more seriously injured than what happened.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voiceover): Trace, today's deadly shooting comes two weeks to the day after the Covenant School shooting in Nashville, and it's reigniting the debate over our country's gun laws. President Biden put out a statement calling on Republicans in Congress to pass gun control legislation. Kentucky Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who's also the Senate Minority Leader said he and his wife are "devastated by the news."\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  also said that he and his wife are praying for everyone involved. Now, there are some questions that we still have as we await this press conference from local authorities. How many people are hurt? Again, the latest number is nine, that number is coming from the hospital, not from police. We heard eight from them last time, four people have been killed. What is the condition of the police officer who's undergoing emergency surgery?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): And do we know anything else about the gunman or his motive in this horrific attack? Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, those are all great questions. Nate Foy, we'll get back to you as the news breaks. \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agents clearly are a huge part of the law enforcement response to this shooting, to all shootings. Maureen O'Connell is a former FBI Special Agent. She joins us live now. Maureen, it's great to have you on. It's interesting, because Nate brings up some very good questions there that we anticipate are going to be the first questions asked during this news conference. And the first one is the connection.\par \par \uc2 We think he's a former employee, maybe a current employee. That's pivotal in these types of investigations, because it means he's got inside information. He knows, he knows where the bank is located. He knows how many people would be in certain meetings at certain times. He knows the security setup. So, this is all valuable information to have. If you're planning something nefarious like this.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O'CONNELL, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Absolutely, Trace, an insider like this would have, as you said, inside information, but it also shows the, the importance and the severity of how devastating someone from inside an organization can be. When it comes to a retaliatory act like this. It's it can just be catastrophic, as we've seen today, and many other times unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We should note, Maureen. We just got the two-minute warning about 20 seconds ago. So, we're about a minute and a half away we believe from this news conference. It's interesting because these workplace shootings I've covered a lot of them over the years. They always tend to be over a rift. There's some type of problems, something that these people are disgruntled about, they just don't go in and shoot their, their fellow employees or former fellow employees for no reason.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Exactly and some of them are injustice collectors. They, they spend their entire life thinking that everyone did them wrong and it builds up and builds up and builds up until we get to a juncture like this and it just becomes a terrible situation for everyone.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, you know, what we don't find in these types of situations? You know, as soon as there's a school shooting, one of the big conversations is hardening of schools, do we harden the schools, we put more police officer there. When there's a business, when it's a bank or some other business where the shootings happen, that conversation is never really brought up. It's not like they, they, they willingly are saying, OK, we're going to figure out a way to harden this certain location.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Especially a bank, because it's open to the public, and people could just walk in, and that's the nature of that business. And any, any, any, you know, business like that, you could just walk in, and they want you to feel welcome. And they want to create an environment that makes you feel welcome. Well, that, that's not conducive to a hardened security at Target, as you said, yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But the question is, when they bring up the connection of the shooter, Maureen, we all think, OK, well, he was maybe a former or current employee, and what does that solve going forward? Is it, is it one of those things that brings us down the road to mental health? Are we talking about, you know, scanning current employees? What does that help the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or other people who are trying to profile these people ahead of time?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Well, we're looking for pre-incident indicators. So, we're looking for all kinds of things. Like I said, is he an injustice collector? Is this a person that has illustrated any type of rash behavior in the past, aggressive behavior toward women or other employees? What does his social media content look like? What does he do on his time, in his time off, and some of these things can be reported, and some of them just aren't illegal? And it makes it so challenging for law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, we're still waiting for the news conference to begin a lot of questions underway. I want to put these numbers on the board. This is mass shooting so far in 2023. And the way we define the mass shootings, here is four or more people killed or injured. And so far, this year, there have been 146 of them. There's a lot of talk, Maureen, when you have these mass shootings of any type, or any kind of shooting.\par \par \uc2 The immediate reaction is to go after these assaults, these assault rifles, these assault weapons, right? And you know, and people don't realize a lot all over the country that you know, assault weapons account for 4 percent of shootings in this country, handguns account for 94 percent, 96 percent of the shooting, never really any call to go after handguns. It's always these. It's because the, the high-profile nature of these guns and the shootings.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Well, long guns, in general, they're the ones that we see in so often in these movies, where, you know, a number of people are being taken down at once. I think there's this perception that a long gun can take down more people more quickly than a handgun, and to a certain extent if it's an automatic weapon that is, in fact true. So, that's why I think that they're targeted more often than small arms.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right, but these are not automatic weapons. These are semi- automatic weapons that have been used in these in these recent shootings, in fact, in most shootings. And I'm wondering if, if the conversation is there more clarity needed in these conversations about gun control and these immediate pushbacks even in the school shooting a couple of weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Clarity would be great, but I don't think, I don't think everyone is open to that. People just see the, the frame of a long gun, and they think it's, it's awful. I mean, even, even a shotgun they think is terrible. So, the whole, the whole gun issue is taking on a whole another meaning with the number of killings that we're seeing these days. I mean, it's just getting to the point like every week, we're sitting in the same position talking about the same thing.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, just a quick update. The shooting happened in Louisville, if you know the area of Louisville. This happened right across the street from slugger baseball field, Louisville Slugger, of course, the minor league baseball field, \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  is downtown, the Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , that is his bank. He said, he ran his one of his campaigns out of the bank. So, he knows everybody who is in there and two of his friends were actually killed in the shooting. Five killed including the shooter, nine wounded, three of those, our understanding is, have been released.\par \par \uc2 The conditions of the police officers remain unclear and the conditions of the others who were wounded also remains unclear. As you walk into this bank, Maureen, what are they looking at? The investigators walk in? You can see them kind of filing in for the news conference here. What are they looking at as they walk into \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2 ? Is it, is it a crime scene that takes a while to process?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Well, one of the first things they're going to be looking at is this -- the, the office space of this person that used to work there and potentially still work there. And they're going to lock that thing down and go over it with a fine-tooth comb and try to glean all the information they possibly can as quickly as possible. And from there, it's that's their, that's their stepping off point. Obviously, interviewing all the people that work there very carefully letting them --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Maureen, one -- let's, let's listen to the news conference.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUELYN GWINN-VILLAROEL, LOUISVILLE POLICE INTERIM CHIEF: At 8:38 there's report of shots fired at \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2 . Officers were on scene within three minutes. The suspect shot at officers. We then return fire and stopped that threat. The suspect is deceased. This is the only time that I will mention the suspect name in this case. The suspect has been identified as Connor Sturgeon, white male, 23 years of age, who was employed at \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2 . His weapon of choice was a rifle. We have five total deceased, which includes him. But I want to acknowledge with heartfelt condolences, prayers to the family of those who lost their life today.\par \par \uc2 Tommy Elliot, 63 years of age. Jim Taht, 64 years of age. Josh Barrett, 40 years of age. And Juliana Farmer, 57.\par \par \uc2 We have a total of nine victims who were treated and are being treated at University of Louisville Hospital. Three, which are in critical condition, including our officer; three who are listed in non-critical condition; and three who have been treated and released.\par \par \uc2 The two officer who's struck and exchange of gunfire, one of the officers, he was grazed to the left side, and the other officer suffered minor injuries to the elbow. I just want to let everyone know that the officer who was in critical condition today, Officer Nicholas Wilt, 26 years of age, just graduated from the police academy on March 31st. I just swore him in and his family was there to witness his journey to become a police officer. He was struck in the head, engaged in this incident. Nick has come out of brain surgery and is in critical but stable condition as we speak.\par \par \uc2 I've been down there with the family and given my love and support. Been with all the families today to show our love and support for them. And if anything else that they need from LMPD, we will provide that. The next few days are important and very critical for Nicholas's recovery. This will be a long complex investigation involving local state and our federal partners. I'd be remiss if I did not acknowledge our federal partners who have just been just outstanding, our local partners: ATF, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I thank you. Our local partners, University of Louisville Police, EMS (Emergency Management Services), thank you, thank you for everything.\par \par \uc2 And for my LMPD officers who took it upon themselves to not wait to assess everything but just went in to stop the threat so that more lives would not be lost, thank you. Thank you for showing up. Even when sometimes you are just felt like you're not appreciated. But I asked my officers when I addressed them today, if we don't do it, who will? And so, they're very committed, committed and their resiliency show today and their professionalism show today. I want to thank the governor, I want to thank the mayor, who has been with us every step of the way through this trying and critical time.\par \par \uc2 But I want to encourage the public. If you have any additional information, please call five seven for LMPD and assist us this should not continue to happen. Evil should not try to prevail and take over our city and we let it happen. So, I'm encouraging everybody that we united and that we continue to do the good work and LMPD will continue to keep this community and this city safe.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GREENBERG (D), LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY MAYOR: Thank you very much chief. Today is a day that's heartbreaking for our city, for all of us. And especially for the people who are in that office at \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  and their loved ones. We just heard the chief give the names of the people who lost their lives in this mass shooting this morning.\par \par \uc2 One of them was Tommy Elliott, a very good friend of mine, of the governors. To Tommy's family, to the families of Jim Tutt, Josh Barrick, Juliana Farmer, Rachel and I join you, and families across our entire city and beyond, in grieving this shocking and painful loss of your lives.\par \par \uc2 The chief, the governor, myself, we visited in the hospital this morning with several of their families to offer them our support, and our love. During this unthinkable day, we will continue to offer our support and love and anything else we can provide to help them.\par \par \uc2 We were also there along with several of my colleagues from \uc2 Metro\uc2  council to thank and support the officers of LMPD. For their heroic work today and every day.\par \par \uc2 And say thank you to the new graduating class, who was there to support their colleague, Officer Wilt as he continues to fight for his life.\par \par \uc2 Let's be clear about what this was. This was an evil act of targeted violence. And to add to that tragedy, a few blocks away, shortly after this happened, another man lost his life. And a woman was shot in a completely different act of targeted violence.\par \par \uc2 The two incidents appear to be entirely unrelated. But they both took lives. They both leave people scarred, grieving, and angry.\par \par \uc2 I share all of those feelings myself right now. This is really difficult for all of us, across the entire city to process and accept. I asked that we respect the needs of the families and loved ones of the people whose lives were lost today. And the people whose lives will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 I'm a survivor of a workplace shooting. To the people who survived. Whether you were physically hurt or not, I know that you're hurting too. We are here for you as well.\par \par \uc2 To the survivors in the families, our entire city is here to wrap our arms around you. We've set up a Family Assistance Center through the Red Cross to provide support and services to those families today, and in the coming days, weeks, and months ahead that we know will be challenging.\par \par \uc2 To provide you with whatever you need to deal with the trauma of this moment. The same applies to our police officers and first responders.\par \par \uc2 Our city is here for everyone impacted. We are a strong, fat city, and we will be here for support. As the chief mentioned, our LMPD officers went into that building, knowing that they were risking their lives to save -- their lives to save other lives. And that's exactly what they did. They exchanged gunfire with the shooter.\par \par \uc2 To Officer Wilt and his family, we will continue to pray and support you. To the other two LMPD officers who were injured, thank you, and all of your colleagues for your service today, and every day.\par \par \uc2 I also want to thank all of our other first responders: Fire, EMS, Jefferson County, Sheriff, state and federal agencies that are helping us at this time.\par \par \uc2 And to Dr. Smith, to you, and all of the other doctors, and nurses, and team at UofL Health for your work today. And every lot and every day. You too, are saving lives in our city, and we thank you.\par \par \uc2 For the rest of us in our city, let's hold each other close and provide comfort where we can. That's what we've done in the past, and that's what we must continue to do now.\par \par \uc2 We've already received offers of assistance from people across the city and country. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the day, we'll be providing specific information on how to provide donations to those impacted today if you would like. We will also have more information by the end of the day about a community wide vigil for the targeted acts of violence that happened today, and any day in our city.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to move forward with strength, prayer, with love, support, and determination. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 I'm now going to turn it over to Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Today is a tragic day in Louisville, and for the entire Commonwealth of Kentucky. Acts of violence like this hurt. They tear at the fabric of who we are at our society, at our state, at our country, and the city.\par \par \uc2 Today, I'm hurt. And I'm hurting. And I know so many people out there are as well.\par \par \uc2 We lost four children of God today. One of whom was one of my closest friends. Tommy Elliot helped me build my law career, helped me become governor, gave me advice on being a good dad. It's one of the people I talk to most in the world and very rarely are we talking about my job.\par \par \uc2 He was an incredible friend. We also lost Juliana Farmer, Jim Tutt, Josh Barrick, each amazing people whose families grieve them, whose community will mourn and will miss them. These are irreplaceable, amazing individuals that a terrible act of violence toward from all of us.\par \par \uc2 We also pray for our LMPD officer and two others that right now, while perhaps stable are still fighting for their lives with a need for our care, and yes, for our prayers, because I believe -- I believe, and I believe that prayers lift all of us up and those families would appreciate them.\par \par \uc2 And while I hurt today, and while I will grieve when I'm able, I'm also grateful. I'm grateful for an incredibly quick response from LMPD, the Jefferson County Sheriff's Office, Louisville Fire and Rescue, Louisville Metro EMS, getting to this scene in three minutes. I'm convinced saved other people's lives, and I know, saved two other of my friends.\par \par \uc2 One, I received incorrect information this morning, I thought was gone, and I'd started to mourn. And now I know he's OK. And when I got to see in the E.R. that while it's going to take him a while to heal, it was so good to see my friend, see him there with his wife and know he is still with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank KSP, ATF FBI, every single law enforcement agency that is going to bring their entire weight and their entire resources to bear so that we know exactly what happened in this situation.\par \par \uc2 And you know, there are more first responders than the ones we've listed, because the team at UofL Hospital, our first responders too. The first battle was stopping that gunman. The second is doing everything we can to keep those that have been wounded alive.\par \par \uc2 And I want to say to this good doctor behind me, and we have worked together through pandemics, and now this, that I'm really proud of what his team is doing.\par \par \uc2 I was in a room where one of those families that are rightfully worried, got an update. And it was one that was specific but also provided with compassion. These doctors and nurses are fighting for the lives of our friends, of our family, and we are deeply grateful to them.\par \par \uc2 Like what we need most right now and a word is love. I mean, just love, love for these families. They're going to need it. I can tell you. I've talked to them. I'm very close with one of them. They are going to need that love. And we need love for each other.\par \par \uc2 It seems like we argued so much, in this country, so much anger. But I still believe that love and compassion and humanity can lead us to a better place.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is hard. It's really hard the day after Easter. Now, Easter is about a rebirth, a better world, one where we're all supposed to work together to get there. One that's supposed to teach us love from a savior that came.\par \par \uc2 Now, well fell in love with him not because of his power, but for his compassion, and I believe what we can do, whether it's any faith, or just your values, is ensure that today, more than anything, is about compassion.\par \par \uc2 And I believe what we can do, whether it's any faith, or just your values is ensure that today, more than anything, is about these families. It's about those families that are afraid they're going to lose their loved one. About those that have been through the worst, and while may have survived, are going to bear the scars inside and outside today. It's got to be about them, and the heroic actions of everybody who responded.\par \par \uc2 And yes, I know, in the days to come, we'll talk about issues. But I think the thing that people get wrong after the most difficult of times and days and as governor, I've had to see a lot of them, is that today, I ought to be focused on my friend, and on everybody else's friends and loved ones that are no longer with us.\par \par \uc2 My faith teaches me that while the body is mortal, the soul is eternal. And so, I know that I will see Tommy again. And I know that all of these families and friends in this community, we will be reunited in a better place without violence.\par \par \uc2 Without the senselessness and hurt that we feel today. We will get through this. We'll get through this together. It's the only way.\par \par \uc2 And we'll march forward. And my hope is that in honor of those that have fallen today, or that may still fall, we can be better. And we can do better. We can care about love each other just a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 Today, to honor those that have fallen, and as so many families grieve, I'm ordering our flags statewide, fly at half-staff until Friday, Friday evening, to make sure that we are paying tribute in every single part of this Commonwealth to those that we have lost.\par \par \uc2 We are one Commonwealth. United with a city, united with these families. Well, we've had to do a lot in these last three years is wrap our arms around each other. And we need to do it here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And next, we're going to hear from Dr. Smith, who is leading an incredible team to try to make sure as many of those that were harmed in this incident get through it.\par \par \uc2 And let me just again say that he and his hospital have my full support and confidence. I've seen it firsthand. What they're doing is nothing short of amazing.\par \par \uc2 And listen, I know they're going to do their best. And while we don't know what that outcome can be, we ought to be appreciative of them for doing their very best.\par \par \uc2 DR. JASON SMITH, CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER, UNIVERSITY OF LOUISVILLE: Well, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 As of reported this morning, University of Louisville Hospital received nine patients. Three of which are currently in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 The three, they're in critical condition did require operative care in -- from our trauma team and our emergency department services.\par \par \uc2 We were able to release three and three are currently still in our hospital with non-life-threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 I want to say thank you to Louisville as a city. I want to say thank you to our police officers and our EMS services. I have no doubt in my mind that they save lives today.\par \par \uc2 I think they as many in the health care industry have been through a difficult time recently. But they have stood up and stand up for Louisville and the people of Louisville constantly, and they have our thanks from UofL Health.\par \par \uc2 I also want to thank my nurses and the emergency department, my physicians and the emergency department. Through all of this they remained as the professionals that they are. They were able to care for a large number of people coming in, severely injured.\par \par \uc2 In a very short period of time, we were able to get them to the services they need. And as of now, no one that arrived at the hospital has expired.\par \par \uc2 Now, I do not know what the days ahead will be. I do know that they are still injured. But I also know that we are all Louisvillians and it doesn't matter where we come from, it does not matter who we look like, we are all part of Louisville. LMPD, EMS, UofL Health, we are here for everyone.\par \par \uc2 And so, I would like to thank everyone who's helped us today. I would like to thank all the well wishes I've received both personally and from our health care system from around the country, support for people who have been through this around the country, and know the pain that my team is currently facing, know the pain that the LMPD and EMS teams are currently facing, and will be with us. Because, as again, we are all Louisvillians.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank everyone again for what they do for us every day. We are a wonderful and integral part of this city, and I am proud to be part of U of L Health, and I am proud to be part of U of L Hospital. So with that, thank you, and I'll turn it over for questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you very much, Dr. Smith. We're prepared to take a few questions from the media.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dr. Smith, obviously, with brain surgery, do we know about (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I believe everyone who had surgery today are still sedated. No one is currently awake. It's still very early in this process from any of that information. Really, the next 24 hours for all involved in this who required an operation are going to be the critical time period for us to understand really how well they're going to recover from this acute part of their overall care.\par \par \uc2 They've got long roads ahead of them, but if we can get through it 24 hours, then we will take another 24 hours. And we will keep doing that until they get out of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You mentioned non-life threatening for those other victims. For the average person, does that mean gunshot wounds? Can that be considered non-life threatening in this case?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. You can have gunshot wounds that are non-life threatening. Correct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that is the case for these three.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We had -- we treated a total of five gunshot wounds today from this scene.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For the police chief, what is that moment like to hear an officer was shot in the head and then ultimately to hear that for now he is stable, but he may make it through this. What are your thoughts there?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: That is one of the worst moments within any police chief's tenure, being in this position, to hear that any of your officers have been injured in any kind of way, but critically injured. It's also, too, it rubs me and it just rocks my world to have anybody being injured and losing their life.\par \par \uc2 And so, I am just truly have so much respect for the families because they were still present and were very appreciative of what was done and how LMPD responded today. They were actually embracing me as I was embracing them.\par \par \uc2 But let me say this. The evil that took place today, we shouldn't hear the tears that were coming out from these families. And that right there sticks because they are weeping because someone decided that they wanted to take life today. And so with that, I'm very grateful and hopeful. And I'm thankful to the doctor for stabilizing so many that could have possibly lost their life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible) what exactly type of gun was used and where the shooter buy (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: I don't know where the shooter got the weapon from, but I just can say that it was a rifle used, as I mentioned earlier.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you talk more about what happened inside the bank building prior to the shooting this morning?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Repeat that again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you talk more about what happened inside the bank building prior to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Again, this is an ongoing investigation. And again as like the governor has stated, we want to focus in on the families. We want to focus in right now what happened. As this investigation continues to unfold, then we'll be able to reveal a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A lot of information (inaudible) being on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Is that something when you took it down and was he communicating on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  before this incident?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: I will say this, that the suspect was live streaming and unfortunately that's tragic, to know that incident was out there and captured. And we're hopeful that we can have that incident removed, that footage removed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Has the home of this shooter in this case been searched? Have you guys discovered anything manifest or things (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Again, it's still ongoing within this investigation, still unfolding. And so I don't want to get into any additional particulars at this time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (Inaudible) being identified and you realized who he was, had he had any encounters with the police department in his past?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: From my understanding right now, preliminarily wise, that may change. But my understanding we have not had any prior engagement with this individual.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And is he a citizen of Louisville?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: He worked at National Bank, and so I am assuming right now that he was a citizen of Louisville.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And has there been a search warrant conducted on his home as of today?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: I don't want to get into particulars on that investigation portion of it, but again it's ongoing so I respect your question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible) all the victims police officers, are you able to say (inaudible) employees of the bank?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And any officers.\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Any officers, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chief, was the suspect shot by police?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Yes, sir. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Were there guards inside the bank or was there any security? How was this (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: I'm not familiar with any other additional security with that -- inside the location. But again, let's focus back on this initial incident right here that we were responded in a time quick fashion and we stop the threat so that no additional loss of life could be taken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We will continue to provide updates as we have more information. This has been a very active day where we are. As the governor and chief said, we are using every resource available from local, state and federal agencies to provide all of these answers to the entire community, to the loved ones who lost victims today.\par \par \uc2 And so, we are going to get back to work. We will continue to keep you all up to date as we have more information. Thank you all very much. We will let you know when the next update is going to be shortly. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, there you have everybody from the mayor of Louisville to the governor of Kentucky and the police chief. They are giving us a news conference about what happened. As we know, a shooter walked into \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  in downtown Louisville this morning, opened fire and killed four people and injured wounded nine others. Three of those had been released from the hospital. We are told five of those injuries were gunshot wounds, including a police officer, Nicholas Wilt, 26-year-old, who just became a police officer in the past few weeks. He was struck in the head. He is in critical but stable condition after undergoing brain surgery to remove that bullet. His status, again, is stable right now, but still critical, and the others are in various stages of serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 The shooter is identified as 23-year-old Connor Sturgeon. He is or was an employee of the bank and he apparently walked in. And now we're being told that he was live streaming this, reportedly, on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . And now the police officers are trying to see if they can get that taken down. That's the first time we have heard that news, that it was being live streamed.\par \par \uc2 They would not talk about motive. We don't know motive. We know he worked at the bank. Let's bring back in Maureen O'Connell. Because in these types of situations, Maureen, these usually turn out to be somebody targeted. He walks into the bank. It's not as if security would stop him if the gun is concealed. They would just say, hey, Connor.\par \par \uc2 He walks in. He knows there's a meeting going on. He walks into the meeting. It appears that he was looking for somebody.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: It does. And the security may not even have been on scene or they may have been, you know, not even really working until the meeting was over because the bank wasn't open for business. They were apparently having a meeting beforehand. So I would agree with you that it probably was a targeted attack. The fact that he's live streaming it on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is just utterly shocking.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: What does it tell you about mindset when he's Instagraming this, livestreaming? Is it preparation? Is it something that they are going to go to his house and they're going to be now kind of scouring his social media footprint to find out what his motive may have been?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Right. The motive is going to be important because it will help people in the After Action Report to understand what's happening and how we can potentially combat this in the future. But yes, authorities will be looking at his whole digital exhaust, everything that he's ever put out on the Internet, and they are going to search -- they're going to get -- they probably already have a search warrant and they're probably already searching his house. And they're going to find all kinds of information there, and as I said earlier at his desk where he worked at the bank.\par \par \uc2 Between those three things, they're going to come up with a lot of information. None of it is going to help any of these victims and none of it is going to help these law enforcement officers and the trauma that they're suffering today, that entire department, the whole law enforcement community, the first responder community, has just been hammered lately. And it's just a lot.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Does it tell you anything when one of the police officers, the first ones to go in the building is a, for all intents and purposes, a rookie? He's 26 years old. He just recently joined the Louisville Police Department. Is that a standard operating procedure? Is that unusual? Or is it just happens that it's the first people who arrive are the first ones to go in?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Trace, when on a call like that comes out, the person closest -- everyone gets there. And that call comes out, you're listening to it, you know shots are fired. You know that it's a dangerous situation and you're the first one there, you're the first one going in. That's exactly how it works.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. It's amazing to me as the investigation starts moving into the later stages of this now, of course, they're grieving for their family, their friends, their loved ones there. As it moves forward, what's inside this person's apartment or his home that they are eager to go after and uncover? Because he did not have a criminal record, so it kind of brings up the whole thought of, how do you prevent this for somebody who doesn't have a criminal record, doesn't have any history of mental illness. What's the remedy?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: There's no way that this happened without a parade of red flags happening beforehand. And who is going to be privy to those red flags, family members. Family members have to start reporting and talking about this thing, and submitting tips and leads to their local police, federal police departments, anything you can think of. People need to get, you know, become more active in trying to stop these people or at least getting them the help they need, because this did not happen in a vacuum.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. It's just a tragic situation in Louisville. Again, four people were shot and killed. The shooter was shot by police. We didn't understand in the early going if this might have been a self-inflicted gunshot wound or if he was shot by police. The first thing the police chief came up and said is that they went in and they stopped this threat, meaning that they neutralized the shooter. Again, he is identified as 23-year-old Connor Sturgeon.\par \par \uc2 We should point out, Maureen O'Connell, that we still don't know anything about the mode of the investigation in the very early stages. Thank you for your insight. Great stuff as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Thank you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, breaking updates from the White House this hour as President Biden confirms he intends to run again, and the US suffers one of its most significant leaks of classified documents in history. Karl Rove, former Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  and Juan Williams standing by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, President Biden once again pressed on if he is running in 2024. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Well, I plan on running now but we're not prepared to announce it yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: This in the face of some more tough poll numbers, his overall approval down 5 points from one year ago to 38%. His ratings on the big issues, the economy and foreign policy are also down from last year. Let's bring in Karl Rove, former deputy chief of staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and a Fox News contributor, \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , former Arkansas governor, and Juan Williams, Fox News political analyst. Gentlemen, thank you for coming on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Karl, I wanted to put this up because this is what \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  said and we're talking about going to 2024. And I thought it was interesting because he made a similar point that you made in your recent Wall Street Journal op-ed. Here is the former governor and I'll get your response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only Republican he can beat. I don't think he could beat any other Republican who could get nominated, but the economy will still be the thing that will determine this.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He talks about the economy, but kind of similar to what you were saying, Karl, is that maybe the Democrats want President Trump, former President Trump, to win the primary because they believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will beat him in the general election.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think that's accurate. I think one other thing that the Democrats have going on in the way of a problem is this Monmouth of Democrats. This is a Monmouth poll. \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2 , last month, 25% of Democrats want him to run again. 44% say, step aside, let somebody else, and 30% say no preference. And I bet you a lot of those 30% are just being polite.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats have a weak candidate, but he may be, as \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  says, they may be hoping that they get put up against the weakest Republican and hope that they can come out ahead on that battle.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Is that a fair assessment, Juan? Do you think that maybe the Democrats are hoping for a Biden-Trump rematch?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. I clearly think the polls would indicate that President Biden would do well in that rematch. But, you know, I mean, the reality is I think the polls are right. I think there's concern about his age. The other part of the polls, though, is that President Biden has like 80% approval among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So while people are concerned about him and think maybe they would like to see some other candidates, they pretty much approve. And secondly, there's no other major Democrat who's saying, yes, I'm right here in the race. He's the only one that's running right now.\par \par \uc2 You think about \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , the governor of California, he just said he's endorsed Biden and that he will be an enthusiastic supporter and points to his legislative accomplishments. So It's like break glass in case of emergency but nobody else is in the race.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. But a lot of Republicans, Governor Huckabee, they point out, and a lot of Democrats as well, that President Biden appears to be a weakened candidate. And they're also frustrated because it appears, as Juan was saying, that they do want this Trump-Biden rematch because they think that's the one way that the President can beat the former president, that the President can remain the president.\par \par \uc2 Politico wrote this piece, as Trump dominates the airwaves, it feels like effing 2016, going on to say this is deja vu all over again, said Terry Sullivan, who ran \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 's 2016 campaign for president. Trump dominates media coverage, making it impossible for his competitors to get any coverage or forward traction.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it really is problematic for the Nikki Haley's of the world, the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who's not in, but potentially could be. And, governor, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FORMER ARKANSAS GOVERNOR: Well, it's so early. I think sometimes people get all worked up. Here we are, we're barely into 2023, and everybody's focused upon who's going to win a year and a half from now. I don't think we know.\par \par \uc2 We don't know yet how this is going to shape up. I mean, if we go back to 2008, it was going to be President Giuliani. If you would go to 2016, it was going to be President Jeb Bush. 2020, Trump was a locked for reelection until COVID hit. What's going to happen? Nobody knows, and I think it's a little early.\par \par \uc2 But if I were \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I'd be really worried that an overwhelming majority, even in his own party, deep down, don't want him to run again. I think that's pretty troubling for an incumbent president because his policies and his personality are such that people just say, this guy doesn't need to put America through another four years of what he's already put us through.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And Karl, why is he waiting? I mean, I know he keeps saying, yes, I'm going to run, but why the wait?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, he doesn't need to jump in. He's the president. He gets to go be president instead of being the presidential candidate. And particularly now as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  goes through the -- New York, I think is a convoluted theory on which it's based, but nonetheless it puts in front of the American people once again, that the issue is how -- hush money. So the President Biden has nothing to gain by jumping in and being a candidate today and a lot to lose.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Juan, wrap us up, 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I mean, I just look back at the history here, and if you look back at the Bush reelection campaign, I think Karl can tell you, I think that was May '03. and if you look at Obama, look at a second term reelection, he was April of 11th. So this is in the same ballpark and from what I'm hearing, President Biden is looking now at midsummer.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Juan, Karl, governor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, moments ago, the Justice Department asking the court to block a judge's decision to suspend the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of an abortion medication, calling it extraordinary and unprecedented. The pillage -- the abortions in this -- also calling for the decision, saying it puts all medications update -- from the -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Trace, I just got out of a briefing with press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 . She spoke about this at length. The Department of Justice going to the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals asking the appeals court to overturn a ruling by a Texas judge based in Amarillo, Texas, Judge Kacsmaryk, who essentially is telling the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to stop distributing Mifepristone. You see it right there.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone has been on the market, Trace, since the fall of 2000, for almost 23 years. The White House says it's completely safe as it's been on the market for almost a quarter century. There's no reason to take it off the market. Detractors and critics of this, specifically this alliance for Hippocratic Medicine based in Texas, is saying it's dangerous, it's harmful, and it should not be on the market. White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  about 30 minutes ago, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What I can say is that we stand by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's approval of Mifepristone, and we are prepared for a long legal fight. That's what I can say from here. That's what we are committed to do -- doing for the providers who are making sure that this is available to women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And that legal fight likely means the Supreme Court, although nothing's been filed yet, Trace. Friday night, it was fascinating. We had this judge in Texas put a stay on Mifepristone. And then we had a federal judge in Seattle, Washington, in Washington State, essentially telling the \uc2 FDA\uc2  not to stop distributing Mifepristone. So two dueling decisions by two federal judges, which likely sets up a ruling by the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 , Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. David Spunt live for us in the White House. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the hunt is on for the source of an apparent leak of classified documents containing US intelligence on a wide range of highly sensitive issues, including a potentially grim new development around the war in Ukraine. The chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin is following this live from the Pentagon. Hey, Jen.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Trace. Well, the revelations have sent shockwaves through the Pentagon and the national security establishment here in DC. Sources tell me could be worse than the Edward Snowden leak. Fox News has agreed, along with other news organizations, not to publish the leaked highly classified documents which were discovered last week. Details about the scope and scale of the leak remain sparse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you believe the leak is contained? Are there more documents out there that have not been released publicly? Is this an ongoing threat?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY\uc2 , NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMS: We don't know. We truly don't. The main concern is that they're out there, period. They don't need to, they shouldn't be, absolutely shouldn't be in the public domain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Defense Secretary Austin was first briefed on the breach last Thursday morning when the photos were first discovered online. We've since learned that some of the photographs of the classified documents first appeared online more than a month ago, and that they were first shared by gamers on a platform known as Discord. They then migrated over to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and Telegram.\par \par \uc2 Some of the documents appear to have been altered, but others many of the other documents have not been altered, and their revelations are already straining relations with close allies like Israel, South Korea, and the UAE. What's most stunning is the granularity of the intelligence that the \uc2 US government\uc2  has when it comes to the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2 . The intelligence is based on wide scale signals, intelligence, but also some human sources.\par \par \uc2 There are sensitive issues that involve US requests and pressure on the South Korean government to provide hundreds of thousands of 155 millimeter artillery shells for Ukraine. It's not clear if the documents that have already appeared online are from one daily briefing book. Some of the pages appear to be folded as though they were placed in someone's pocket. It's notable trace that most of the top officials here at the Pentagon receive this information by a secure iPad, not a printout, suggesting the source of the leak may not be inside the Pentagon. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, great information. Jennifer Griffin, live forest at the Pentagon. Jen, thank you. Let's bring in Dan Hoffman, a former CIA station chief who served in Moscow. He is also a Fox News contributor. Dan, it's great to have you all.\par \par \uc2 I want to play one more sound bite from \uc2 John Kirby\uc2  because it's pivotal. It's kind of asking if we are losing this battle to our adversaries. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Given this leak and previous leaks, American people think that the administration is losing the battle against whoever wants to steal our secrets, whether it's foreign adversaries or hackers or nothing.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think the American people need to know and deserve to know that we're taking this very, very seriously. The President has been briefed on this. He will stay briefed on this. He will stay briefed on this The Department of Defense is looking into this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Dan, it's a fair question because people are kind of baffled. I mean, this thing was bubbling up on social media on a chat site that was used by gamers, and it didn't get noticed by anybody.\par \par \uc2 We don't have Dan? OK. We don't have Dan Hoffman. So here's the concept. The concept is this stuff, it is national security, right, intelligence. And it bubbles up on a website. We're working on Dan's microphone. It bubbles up on a website, right, social media. It's on a chat service that was primarily used by gamers, right? It was on there for a while until somebody and some other social media sites started picking it up.\par \par \uc2 And that's when the national intelligence world got grasp of it. and that's when they started kind of panicking a little bit that this stuff was out there. And the key concerns about this -- I'm sorry, control. Is Dan ready? OK, it's not.\par \par \uc2 Well, let's go back to Jennifer Griffin. Jen, I mean, I think this is a valid question for you as well, because if you can hear me, Jen, what's the concept here is that we learned in these documents that Ukraine has weakness in their air fighting power, right? Their air defenses are weaker so say this intelligence. How serious is that for that to get out to the world?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We don't have Jennifer either, OK? So we're trying to get both those microphones up there. So the reporting came in on a couple of key things. One of them, one of the big takeaways is that a few months ago, several months ago, that Russia nearly shot down a British spy plane. That's one of the key aspects of this intelligence.\par \par \uc2 And if you think about that, if Russia did shoot down or was close to shooting down and was successful in shooting down a British spy plane, we could now be involved in a bigger war. Well, that did not happen, but the intelligence revealed that -- kind of going back to Jennifer Griffin now.\par \par \uc2 This report, Jennifer, I was talking about that Ukraine could run out of air defenses by maybe as early as May. I mean, that is important information that can be used to nefarious means by people like Russia.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, what's interesting, Trace, remember, some of these documents have been altered. We do know that from Pentagon sources. So it's not clear what to make of this. But we do know that there were concerns about the Ukrainian air defenses and now we see it in black and white because of this leak. It's a very big issue here at the Pentagon and certainly creates a great deal of distrust with the Ukrainian officials as well, because these are secrets that they don't want out there. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Jennifer Griffin, thank you for stepping back in with us.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we got some breaking news to report. Louisville Metro Police Department now tweeting this photo. This is Officer Nicholas Wilt. He is in critical condition at this hour after a gunshot wound to the head. He ran towards the gunfire to save lives in the bank shooting that killed four and injured nine others. We put this up here because he is the one we told you, is in critical condition. He has been in surgery. He is now stable but still critical. He took a gunshot wound to the head. He just joined the force a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY of Monday, April 10, 2023. As always, THE STORY goes on. I'll see you back here on Fox News at night. "YOUR WORLD," now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230411ej4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC); Interview with Sen. Ben Cardin (D-MD)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Aishah Hasnie, Alexandria Hoff, Juan Williams \par \uc2 9,546 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 From the Middle East to the Taiwan Strait, there are rising tensions around the globe this morning. And almost two years since the end of the war in Afghanistan, the Biden administration releases its report on America's withdrawal and points the finger of blame elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR: The president's decision to end the war in Afghanistan was the right one. For all this talk of chaos, I just didn't see it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): White House officials shift the blame, the cause of the chaotic exit from America's longest war to the president's predecessor.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It was a general sense of degradation and neglect there that the president inherited.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But critics on Capitol Hill insist the buck stops with the current commander in chief.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): This is the commander in chief. He is responsible for what his administration does.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then, China makes veiled threats to the U.S. as Taiwan's president visits with the House speaker in California.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: The friendship between the people of Taiwan and America is a matter of profound importance.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And in Taiwan, the head of the House Foreign Affairs Committee indicates Congress could consider deploying U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): If communist China invaded Taiwan, it would certainly be on the table.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll get reaction from senators on both sides of the aisle, Republican \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Democratic Ben Cardin. Graham and Cardin exclusively on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2 , MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Thirty false statements made to cover up other crimes.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: The only crime that I have committed is to fearlessly defend our nation from those who seek to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel about former President Trump's historic arraignment.\par \par \uc2 And Kathie Lee Gifford and her close friend Rabbi Jason Sobel sit down and tell us how the key stories of their different faith backgrounds show they aren't so different after all.\par \par \uc2 KATHY LEE GIFFORD, TV HOST: It's one story of redemption.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration admitting there were some missteps during the withdrawal from Afghanistan that left 13 U.S. service members dead, but assigning much of the blame elsewhere. A report by the National Security Council summarizing the administration's assessment blames the chaotic pullout in August 2021 on decisions made during former President Trump's administration.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who demanded the documents are strongly condemning the report. And as tensions with China boil over, a top lawmaker on Capitol Hill now says the U.S. would consider sending troops to the self-governed island democracy of Taiwan if China dared invade. In a moment South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  will join us with his take on both of those stories.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with team coverage. Alexandria Hoff at the White House on the reaction to the Afghanistan withdrawal review.\par \par \uc2 But first to Aishah Hasnie live in Taipei, Taiwan, where a bipartisan congressional delegation spent several days -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There is a very tense situation unfolding right now about 50 miles away in the Taiwan strait. Reuters is reporting there's about 20 naval vessels, about half Chinese, half Taiwanese that are reportedly caught locked up in a, quote, standoff just near the median line.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you know, this is the second day of military drills by the CCP, a whopping 70 aircraft, Chinese aircraft, were detected just today. Half of those crossed the median line. The military also simulating precision strikes against Taiwan this weekend.\par \par \uc2 All of this in reaction to historic events this week between the U.S. and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice-over): U.S. lawmakers standing firm with Taiwan's President \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen, during a tour through the Pacific to rally ally support for the tiny island under the threat of communist China.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Not since World War II have we seen such a struggle for the global balance of power.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: House Foreign Affairs Chairman Michael McCaul telling FOX the goal is deterring an invasion, but if it happens, sending U.S. troops is not out of the question.\par \par \uc2 So you're saying that the option to authorize war powers is on the table?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: If Communist China invaded Taiwan, it would certainly be on the table and something that would be discussed by Congress.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: That warning to China fresh off the heels of a highly anticipated meeting between Tsai and House Speaker McCarthy on U.S. soil.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Our bond is stronger now than any time a point in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: China's peoples liberation army launching three days of military drills in response, conducting exercises with fighter jets and war ships.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan is China's red line, the leading maker of advanced chips around the world. China hosting French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  this week who has so far failed to convince President Xi to change his stance on the war in Ukraine, as Russia formally charges "Wall Street Journal" reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  with espionage.\par \par \uc2 Senate leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  putting politics aside to jointly condemn Gershkovich's arrest and demand his release.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And back to that reported standoff happening right now in the Taiwan Strait, a spokesperson for the American Institute in Taiwan tells FOX News they are monitoring Beijing's actions closely and that there is no reason for Beijing to overreact -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Aishah Hasnie, reporting from Taipei, safe travels, Aishah. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now let's turn to Alexandria Hoff live at the White House on the administration's review of the Afghanistan withdrawal on a mixed reaction - - Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, that's right, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And this summary is, indeed, the White House reflecting on its own decision. This was not an independent review. Now, the State Department and Department of Defense, they provided separate reviews to Congress on Thursday. Those will not be released publicly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): In 12 pages, the Biden administration released its first assessment of the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan, placing a majority of the blame on former President Trump's prior exit plan. Quote, President Biden's choices for how to execute a withdrawal from Afghanistan were severely constrained by conditions created by his predecessor. The report also admitted that intelligence surrounding the situation was inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president repeatedly requested assessments of the trajectory of the conflict from his military and his intelligence professionals. No agency predicted a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover in nine days.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The end of America's 20-year presence in the country launched those left behind into desperation. A bombing attack at Kabul's air base killed 13 service members, all as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took swift control.\par \par \uc2 But the White House's John Kirby disagrees with calling it chaotic.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I just don't buy the whole argument of chaos. It was tough in the first few hours. You would expect it to be. There was nobody at the airport.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Now, the new House Republican majority, they see it very differently. They are investigating on their own and called the withdrawal a, quote, unmitigated national security and humanitarian disaster.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden departed the White House around the same time this report was released to spend the Easter weekend at Camp David -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alexandria Hoff from the White House, thanks, Alex.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. We'll get to Afghanistan, but I want to start with China. And you heard Aishah's reporting there about --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- what "Reuters" has been calling a standoff in the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 You served in the Air Force. We noted weeks ago, an Air Force general, a four-star general, said that he thought we would be in actual combat with China by 2025.\par \par \uc2 Chairman McCaul has said the same thing on the show.\par \par \uc2 Where do you think we are this morning? Where does this go?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, number one, we're celebrating Easter. So, if you're looking for some good news, it's Easter Sunday.\par \par \uc2 To Taiwan -- here's what I think: I think they are setting the stage possibly for a blockade of Taiwan. That the communist Chinese party is going to test us dramatically this year and next year before the election.\par \par \uc2 So, in 1961, the Russians tried to isolate West Berlin. So, I'm fearful the Chinese may be sending conditions to blockade Taiwan into coming months or weeks, and we need to respond forcefully. If they do that, we need to blockade oil shipments coming from the Midwest to China and let them know if you blockade Taiwan, we're going to cut off your oil.\par \par \uc2 We need to train more aggressively Taiwanese forces so they can fight like the Ukrainians. We need to put nuclear-tipped missiles back in our submarines. Biden won't do it. The military has asked for the nuclear- tipped cruise missiles to be put back in submarines like the Russians did. We need more forces in the region.\par \par \uc2 I think if you do those things, you could deter a blockade.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It sounds, though, like a ratcheting up for the situation and if Taiwan is not fully equipped --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- trained, armed, ready for this --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- we don't want to be leading to World War III.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, nobody wants World War III. But what kind of world do you want to live in? Do you want to live in a world where an island called Taiwan could be taken by China, 90 percent of the high end chips are made in Taiwan? They would have a monopoly on the digital economy.\par \par \uc2 Do you want to just let Putin take whatever he wants in Europe?\par \par \uc2 I don't want to live in that kind of world. There's no reason for this.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I believe in a One China Policy but I would be willing to fight for Taiwan because Taiwan is a democracy. We've stood with them for decades.\par \par \uc2 So, Taiwan's not the problem. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's not the problem. It's Putin and it's Xi.\par \par \uc2 So, what would do I to deter a blockade? I'd like the Chinese to know if you blockade Taiwan, you're going to have a hard time getting oil from the Mideast.\par \par \uc2 I would increase training and get the F-16s they need in Taiwan. There's a backlog. I would solve that backlog. I would move war forces to South Korea and Japan.\par \par \uc2 I would put nuclear tipped cruise missiles in all of our submarines all over the world, like the Russians did when they got out of the nuclear treaty and started using cruise missiles, nuclear tipped. That's what Reagan did in the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 So, I would up our game. If you don't up your game now, you are going to have a war.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Very quickly, because I want to get to a lot of other topics. Chairman McCaul discussed with Aishah Hasnie there about the possibility of authorizing force and the possibility of sending U.S. troops to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you -- would you support that scenario?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Strategic ambiguity is not working. China, after Afghanistan, believes that the store is open, that you can go in and take whatever you want on Biden's watch.\par \par \uc2 So, the question for Congress, should we have a defense argument with the island of Taiwan? We don't. Should we have one?\par \par \uc2 But, yes, I'd be very much open to using U.S. forces to defend Taiwan because it's in our national security interest to do so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. China also an actor with regard to fentanyl, Mexico, the border. You've been very vocal on that.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mexico's president has sent a letter to China: please help us out with the fentanyl. They've said basically there's no such thing as illegal trafficking of fentanyl between Mexico and China.\par \par \uc2 You have talked about getting tough there about using U.S. military at the border along the border with the cartels. Mexico's president, this is how "AP" reports his response to you: \uc2 Lopez Obrador\uc2  said Mexico would not accept such threats, calling them, quote, an insult to Mexico and a lack of respect for independence and sovereignty. Lopez threatened to start a campaign in the U.S. asking Mexicans and Hispanics who live there to not vote for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So what are you proposing, keeping in mind they're a sovereign country?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, here's what I'm proposing, that no sovereign country has the right to kill 70,000 Americans without some kind of repercussion.\par \par \uc2 Seventy thousand Americans died last year from fentanyl poisoning. This year alone, 13,000 pounds of fentanyl has been seized at the U.S./Mexican border. Okay? That's enough to kill billions of people, 2.5 billion people.\par \par \uc2 My point is, I want to work with Mexico, but the president of Mexico denies that the drug cartels are in charge of part of his country. He's delusional about what's going on in his backyard, writing a letter to the Chinese president means nothing.\par \par \uc2 So, what I want to do -- I want to designate drug cartels in Mexico as foreign terrorist organizations who are sending in fentanyl to poison Americans by the tens of thousands, and put military force on the table to eradicate those drug cartels if Mexico does not want to work with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to work with the president of Mexico, but he's in denial about the problem. And I'm not going to sit on the sideline and watch Mexico be used as a staging area to ship fentanyl into the United States that killed 70,000 Americans last year.\par \par \uc2 What Mexico is doing is insufficient. And China needs to be put on notice, too. FTO designation of drug cartels in Mexico would allow us to prosecute the chemical manufacturers in China that supply the product to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. But to be clear, U.S. military assets this side of the border, correct? Not in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, let's move on to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes, I'm not about talking about -- yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, okay. That's clear.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about Afghanistan. You served in uniform there. You know the situation there.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We got the report this week. You saw Alex's reporting. She said that there's a portion that says President Biden's choices for how to execute withdrawal from Afghanistan were severely constrained by conditions created by his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 They said that President Trump had drawn down too many troops, that the special immigrant visa program to get people out of there had been starved with resources, that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  had been emboldened by us engaging in peace talks without including our allies, the Afghan government. They talk about the Doha agreement, the release of 5,000 \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters from prison.\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to say any of those things handcuffed the Biden administration's ability to make a smooth withdrawal?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, some of the things President Trump did I disagreed with. But he didn't (ph) withdraw.\par \par \uc2 And here's the point, this report is a political whitewash by the Biden administration to shift blame. The report says that our mission had been accomplished. That's why Biden got out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 He's claiming that we eradicated all terrorist threats in Afghanistan. That's why he withdrew to end the longest war.\par \par \uc2 General Kurilla said last week, who's the head of CentCom, that the rise of ISIS-K and Al Qaeda in Afghanistan is so severe, we could be attacked in America in the next six months without warning.\par \par \uc2 So when the Biden administration tells you there are no terrorists in Afghanistan, they are lying. There's a lethal cocktail forming, rise of terrorism in Afghanistan, a broken border here in the United States. It's only a matter of time until there's another 9/11 if we don't change policies.\par \par \uc2 My view of what happened in Afghanistan by Biden was he paved the way for another 9/11.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. We're going to talk a lot more about Afghanistan later on in the show. But before you go, I want to talk to you about the issue of abortion. We've got two federal judges. There are dueling rulings on the pill that's being used in abortions approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 It's an issue that's been a struggle for Republicans post-Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade. You're very passionate about this.\par \par \uc2 But critics, including conservatives say, it's costing the Republican Party. Kim Strassel writing in "The Wall Street Journal", an opinion piece, says: Conservatives need to decide if they want GOP majorities that will enact common sense protections or Democratic takeovers that will open the abortion floodgates. That's the choice. How much clearer can voters make it?\par \par \uc2 Isn't that what happened this week in Wisconsin, where the pro-choice candidate won by ten points getting a seat on the Wisconsin Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, I'm very pro-life. I think being pro-life is a winning message. I oppose late term abortions. I have a bill that set a national minimum standard of 15 weeks. Fifty of the 53 European nations ban abortion at 15 weeks. I have an exception for rape, incest and life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, you had an 1840 statute that banned abortion pretty much across the board. And the Republican Party did not put an alternative on the table.\par \par \uc2 If you're pro-life, you need to explain what that means. Here's what it means to me. I want to protect as many babies as possible. I want exceptions for pregnancies as a result of rape, incest, if the life of the mother's in jeopardy, then the family can decide.\par \par \uc2 I do believe in common sense restrictions on abortion. That's where America's at.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic solution when it comes to abortion is taxpayer-funded abortion up to the moment of birth. That's barbaric. That's like China. That's like North Korea.\par \par \uc2 We can win this issue at the ballot box if we show up with reasonable positions. If we have our head in the sand, we're going to lose.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator Graham, we will talk about that with the panel as well. Thank you for your time. Happy Easter to you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we'll get reaction from Maryland Senator Ben Cardin and ask him about the rising tensions this morning in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The chairman of the House Select Committee on China, Republican \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , warned this weekend that the U.S. must take seriously the threat posed to Taiwan by China, and is encouraging Congress to speed up military aid to the island.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Maryland Democratic Senator Ben Cardin. He sits on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, among others.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cardin, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): Shannon, it's good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, let's start with China. There's a conversation about not making the mistakes of Ukraine, of pre-equipping people before an invasion, that we need to do better with getting military equipment to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 But here's what House Foreign Affairs Chairman Mike McCaul told our Aishah Hosni just days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think we are prepared were this to happen in the next five years?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): You know, I sign off on all foreign military weapon sales. I can tell you we have a problem with our defense industrial bases. These weapons I have signed off on to Taiwan, 22 weapons sales, I signed off three years ago, and they've yet to get to the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, three years ago, and now, it looks like another round of provocation from China this morning. What more can lawmakers do, if anything, to speed up these deliveries?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, I think we need to do that. We got to give Taiwan the ability to defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 So I hope that we will find a way to get to Taiwan exactly what they need. Look, we believe in a One China Policy, but we also believe that mainland China cannot try to take Taiwan over by force.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of tools in our tool kit that we can use to make that a reality. But bottom line is, that we have to make sure Taiwan has what it needs to defend itself. I agree, we have to -- we have to train. We have to make equipment available.\par \par \uc2 The experiences from Ukraine show us that if we give them capacity, they have the will that China will I think re-judge, or reconsider their strategies on trying to take over the island by force.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, I'm sorry if there are technical difficulties there. Hopefully, you can hear me okay.\par \par \uc2 My next question is a follow-up because there are concerns about our ability to supply equipment to Taiwan based on the fact that we've had to send so much to Ukraine. A report to Congress last year that came to you says this: The diversion of existing stocks of weapons and munitions to Ukraine has exacerbated a sizable backlog in the weapons already approved for sale to Taiwan undermining the island's readiness.\par \par \uc2 So, they are also warnings that other key military inventories here in the U.S. could take years to backfill. How do we manage these competing obligations knowing that China probably senses that deficit there and there could be other conflicts to arise around the world?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I think the first thing we need to do is to pass a budget for the next fiscal year and do a bipartisan budget that's strong on our national defense, that replenishes our supply chain so that we have the military equipment we need to take care of our own internal security issues as well as help the Ukrainians and the Taiwanese deal with their security commitments. That means let's put aside the politics of this debt ceiling, let's increase it to where it needs to be.\par \par \uc2 Let's get together and negotiate a budget that's strong on our national defense because we do have commitments around the world for our national security. And the best thing we can do is get a budget approved in an orderly process and not threaten the full faith and credit of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And to that point, since you sit on the Finance Committee and you mentioned the debt ceiling, are you concerned that there are zero negotiations going on?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Senator Joe Manchin, one of your Democratic colleagues, was here last week. He's called out the president and says, he's got to step up to the plate and get everybody to the table. This no negotiating tact is going to take us to the cliff again.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, I do think we need to negotiate. There's no question. But negotiate the budget for the next fiscal year that starts October 1. We shouldn't negotiate whether we are going to pay our bills for what we've already spent. That's what the debt ceiling is about. We've increased it in the past without any of this debate and drama.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the debt ceiling extended and then sit down before October 1, negotiate a budget which will not be the president's budget. It won't be the Republicans' budget. It won't be the Senate budget.\par \par \uc2 It will be a budget that's negotiated between the House and Senate, the Congress and the administration. That's what we need to do. That's how Congress works best.\par \par \uc2 But don't do it on the debt ceiling. The debt ceiling is the full faith and credit of the United States. That should be beyond any question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. I'm not sure that it is for House Republicans especially for the deal that was made to get Kevin McCarthy into the speakership. It's going to be a complex situation.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure I touch on the Afghanistan issue with you as well, as we did with Senator Graham.\par \par \uc2 Here is what NSC spokesman John Kirby said when this was rolled out. It sounded like a lot of this report was laying blame at the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 And here's his characterization of how it played out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR: And so, for all this talk of chaos, I just didn't see it, not from my perch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, he didn't see chaos, but this is what Americans saw -- people hanging on to planes as they were taking off. People swarming the airport. People handing over children and babies to our marines there. A bombing that killed 13 service members, left many more with severe injuries.\par \par \uc2 There are thousands of people still in that country we had promised to get out. And I know of personally the case of at least one American still trying to get out.\par \par \uc2 How do you characterize this withdrawal?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Shannon, I also worked to get Americans out during that period of time. It was a real challenge.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake about it: the Biden administration had very bad options. The decision to withdraw from Afghanistan was made by the Trump administration. The reduction of troops to 2,500 where President Biden started his term in office.\par \par \uc2 The president had little options left. Admittedly, the intelligence information about the strength of the Afghan government to stand off against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was not accurate. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over much quicker than anyone had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden had basically one of two choices. Was he going to get out of Afghanistan or put more troops into Afghanistan with no end in sight? And there are not good options. And I think he, based upon the intelligence he had, made what he thought was the best judgment.\par \par \uc2 Did it present real challenges for Americans and others getting out of Afghanistan? You bet it did.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, how about this issue of, you know, abandoning Bagram Air Base, our allies who were so furious that they actually -- I mean, the U.K. called that emergency session and blasted us in parliament. Allies who said they weren't coordinated with or informed of what was going to happen. I think most Americans agree that it was time to go, but the way in which it happened leaves a lot to be desired, especially for these families who lost people and still deserve answers about what happened.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, when you had a limited number of troops there, 2,500 is what we were down there and we were withdrawing from there. And you had circumstances that were not anticipated by the intelligence reports that were given. It doesn't give you a lot of good options.\par \par \uc2 I hope we can learn from this experience. I hope we can figure out if these circumstances in the future present itself, how we can protect U.S. interests better than we did in Afghanistan withdrawal. But, quite frankly, I think the major mistake was made in negotiating directly with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , not involving the Afghanistan government, unilaterally withdrawing our troops to a level that did not give us the ability to be able to protect U.S. interests moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask you about the Middle East tensions there this week. It has been a lot of back-and-forth rocket attacks, a couple suspected of terror attacks.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Guardian\uc2 " writes that, for now, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Israel have pulled back from the brink, they say. But as two mortal foes continue to stalk each other across the battlefields of the Middle East fighting shadow wars in Lebanon, Syria, and Iraq, and as far away as Yemen, the risk of seemingly measured provocation spiraling out of control is perhaps greater than ever.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about the current tensions in that region?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I am very worried. During the holiday season, it's always a high tension moment in the Middle East, in Israel.\par \par \uc2 We recognize that Israel still has mortal enemies that are there. The circumstances with Iran's activities in the surrounding countries, the attacks coming from both Syria and Lebanon into Israel, these are all major concerns.\par \par \uc2 And Israel is defending itself. United States stands with Israel and its ability to defend its sovereignty. We know that there are controversial issues within Israel. We urge them to resolve those issues.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake about it: the United States is firmly with Israel and its right to defend itself, and we will do everything we can to help in that regard.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator Cardin, thank you for being a trooper through some technical issues this morning. We appreciate your time and wish you a blessed Passover. Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, thank you. And happy Easter to you. It's always good to be on your show, Shannon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you. Come back soon.\par \par \uc2 All right. Could the timeline for former President Trump's criminal case in New York run right up against the 2024 GOP primary calendar?\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll ask our Sunday panel about the political impact at play for his campaign.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Now these radical left lunatics want to interfere with our elections by using law enforcement. We can't let that happen.\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt nevertheless that we will make America great again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President Trump going on the offense after pleading not guilty to 34 felony charges in the New York investigation into an alleged hush money scheme, calling the investigation politically motivated. Let's see what our Sunday group thinks: former State Department spokesperson Morgan Ortagus, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist Marc Thiessen, and Axios contributor \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody.\par \par \uc2 All right. We're going to get to the political stuff, but, Morgan, because you were part of the last administration, I want to give you a chance to respond to the Afghanistan withdrawal report, which laid a lot of blame at the feet of the administration you served under.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Yes. I -- I think that this is sort of CYA move before the House Foreign Affairs Committee under Chairman McCaul comes out with a more detailed report. We've already seen explosive testimony from the snipers and other present.\par \par \uc2 Listen, here are the facts that we know. We know that we have 13 Americans dead, billions in American equipment that was left behind. We see a resurgence of ISIS and al Qaeda, enough that we could face attacks from the homeland, according to senior Pentagon official testimony. And so everything that the administration said wasn't going to happen is unfortunately unfolding, including we still don't have a full count of how many Americans are left behind. I'm missing the number. We think it's around 1,000, but we have never been given a clear answer, including, who knows, probably thousands of Afghanistan allies that were abandoned and that were left there.\par \par \uc2 The buck stops here with this president. It was his disastrous withdrawal and his team's failure to plan appropriately, is why we saw one of the worst foreign policy crises since the Vietnam War, since that era. And it has had ramifications to today. It's one -- one of the reasons why you saw Putin invade and why you see \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  acting with impunity around the world, because they are not scared and they are not deterred by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, as you say, more reports and hearings to come on that. Let's get back to the politics, though. And New York Magazine, "The Intelligencer," on this whole Trump indictment, says, it has actually put the GOP, they say, in an impossible trap. "Trump was already opening up a commanding lead before the indictment came down. Now in the eyes of the conservative base, to attack Trump is to aid and abet the president's persecution at the hands of Soros and his minions. To question his electability, meanwhile, is tantamount to calling on Republicans to let the terrorists win."\par \par \uc2 Marc?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, THE WASHINGTON POST: There's -- that's a strong way of putting it. But, look, this -- the indictment helps \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and it helps Democrats because it makes it more likely that Trump will be the nominee. I mean, there was a terrifying poll, CNN poll came out, only 37 percent of Americans think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did anything illegal, 76 percent think that politics was involved, and 60 percent approve of the indictment. And the numbers with independents are even more stark.\par \par \uc2 So what that means is the majority of Americans, a majority of independents think that the left is weaponizing the justice system to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that he committed no crime, and they are fine with it. What does that say about -- first of all, what does that say about our country? That we are turning into a banana republic where -- where, you know, we -- people are OK with us weaponizing the justice system.\par \par \uc2 But also, what does it say about Trump's chances of winning? If you're an independent voter, a swing voter, this election is going to be decided by about 200,000 people in five states. And Trump has made himself so toxic with those swing voters, that, yes, this can help him get the nomination, but those voters are not -- they -- they want to see him indicted and possibly put in jail to stop him from running. That's -- that's troubling for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and the person who polls a distant second to him right now, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and we heard a little bit more about his strategy this week -- strategy. NBC News reporting this. That his team is "already plotting at a strategy to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the long haul. The plan focuses less on making a quick splash in places like Iowa or New Hampshire and more on outlasting the former president in a battle for Republican convention delegates."\par \par \uc2 Josh, any chance this comes down to a convention decision?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , AXIOS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, whenever a campaign is talking about skipping the early states and focusing on the delegate math, usually means they are running a losing campaign. I mean, this is very early. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hasn't even announced. But, look, Iowa should be a very favorable state for the Florida governor with a lot of conservatives, evangelicals. You have a state that Trump lost in -- to Ted Cruz in the 2016 caucuses. And yet DeSantis's allies are saying, yes, maybe Iowa is not going to work for us, maybe forget New Hampshire, let's look at the delegate math for these Super Tuesday states. That's what \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2  did for the Democrats in 2020. That's what \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  did in 2008 for the Republican primary. Look how it worked for them.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I -- I think it's a big red flag for the DeSantis operation that they're already downplaying expectations even before he has announced his campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And yet we still wait to find out if the president is going to run again, officially make a decision. You cited CNN polling. I want to -- Marc, I want bring out some of this for Juan, because the headline there is a third of Americans say that Biden deserves to be re-elected. A -- a majority of his party say, though, they would like to see someone else as the Democratic nominee for president next year.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Juan, we keep talking about this, but, you know, if a third want him, that means, you know, two-thirds don't. But we await his decision.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think the polls are accurate. I think there's a lot of concern about his age, the man's 80. But it's also the case that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running. I don't think there's much doubt about that, Shannon. And every other serious candidate on the Democratic side thinks that they would damage themselves to come forward and challenge the incumbent, the president, at this time, especially because you have the likelihood, as we heard around the panel this morning, that, you know, former President Trump will be the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has defeated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So you have people ranging from \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , the California governor, to Pritzker in Illinois, to Polis in Colorado, even Harris, the vice president, people who are kind of saying, you know, here's the emergency glass, break if necessary, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'm here. But nobody is directly confronting President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think they're going to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see. OK. In the meantime, bad news, it seems like, for conservatives this week in these -- these elections in Wisconsin and Chicago. The editorial board at The Wall Street Journal says it's a five- alarm warning to Republicans about 2024. Morgan?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Well, I don't know how much chance we have in Chicago, but Wisconsin, certainly. I mean, what are we learning from that state supreme court race? First of all, all politics is local, even in supreme court justice cases. I also think that Republicans and conservatives have spent a lot of time sort of decrying Soros and making him the -- the boogeyman. And after the supreme court decision, not just Roe v. Wade, but also getting so many conservatives, a majority of conservatives on the Supreme Court, we kind of had a party and went home and went to sleep.\par \par \uc2 And what did Democrats do? They clearly have gone to work. There was about $45 million in funding per week for this state supreme court seat in Wisconsin. So conservatives have to get to work. They have to get in the fight. Complaining about Soros is not going to be enough.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and on the issue of abortion, the Federalist had an interesting piece that they say that the GOP has to control messaging on abortion but not run away from it. They say: "Republican politicians who boldly went on offense by passing pro-life legislation and aligned themselves with grassroots activists for the unborn successfully warded off legal and political challenges in fall 2022, and as a result they were rewarded with double-digit victories."\par \par \uc2 Marc, they talk about Ohio, Florida, Texas.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So is it go harder on the messaging or leave it alone and -- and worry about Wisconsin happening?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, it's go smarter. I mean, as a -- every governor who signed an abortion restriction in 20 -- won in 2022. So and they are the people who are directly responsible for these policies. I have skepticism that like a low turnout off-year, off-month election in Wisconsin is a bellwether for the Republican Party. If you look at the polling, to Harvard CAPS poll, 72 percent of Americans support restricting abortion to 15 weeks or less, including 60 percent majority -- supermajority of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So most Americans are not for the Democratic orthodoxy of abortion-on- demand up until the moment of birth. When Republicans -- as long as there's a safe harbor that you don't want to ban all abortion but you want to have reasonable restrictions, it's a winning issue for Republicans. It's only when the Democrats portray them as being against every -- against any exceptions that it becomes a...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, when they go judge-shopping in Texas for a law -- a drug that has been available for 20 years with almost no problem, and the judge says, oh yes, that's -- this is -- it looks like they want to ban abortion totally. And that's a loser politically, not only...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... with suburban women...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... but, Juan...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... but I think Americans overall say abortion should be legal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Juan, would you say though there was -- there was judge-shopping in the -- the case that came out of Washington State?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, I wouldn't.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... to say there was only one judge in that -- in that area, one federal judge. They went to that area with that specific intent to find a judge, a Trump appointee...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I will tell you that...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... with a long history of being opposed to abortion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I will tell you, I've seen a lot of cases filed in the Ninth Circuit for that very reason, I mean...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, you could say it's a liberal court...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... so, yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... but I'm telling everybody, one judge alone.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I gotcha.\par \par \uc2 OK, we've got to leave it there. But now that you're warmed up, panel, stay put.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden met with tech leaders this week to discuss the recent artificial intelligence boom. Are predictions of an AI takeover overblown or right on target? We'll discuss.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Dealing with some very difficult challenges like disease and climate change, but we also have to address the potential risk to our society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: There's an expert from the Machine Intelligence Research Institute who says that if there is not an indefinite pause on AI development, this is a quote, "literally everyone on earth will die." Would you agree that does not sound good?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Your delivery, Peter, is quite -- it's quite something.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I love her face there. White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean- Pierre\uc2  reacting to a question from our own FOX News's Peter Doocy about a report that future AI development could be the end of humanity. We're back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 We are laughing about this, but there are people who take this very seriously. I want to read something from the New Atlas: "The case for how and why AI may kill us all." "Let's be clear from the outset, if we agree that artificial superintelligence has a chance of wiping out all life on earth, there doesn't seem much we can do about it anyway. Who is going to make the laws? The \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 ? The United Nations?"\par \par \uc2 I mean, Josh, who gets a -- a handle on this thing?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's -- it's pretty scary stuff. And, you know, what's really alarming is, you know, we're relying on 70- and 80-year-old senators and congressmen to possibly regulate this -- this technology, many of whom don't even know how to use their -- their smart phone, and we are relying them to deal with this really, really remarkable new technology.\par \par \uc2 I don't trust -- as much concern I have about the new technology and how it's handled going forward, I don't know if I trust our lawmakers to handle the regulation of such an important sector.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , on the "Rachman Review" podcast, said there's nothing to worry about. Here's part of what he said. "It's fine. There's no threat." He talks about your "ability to research articles or have things summarized for you will help you write better." He says, "We're backlogged in terms of educators and doctors," and so, Marc, this is actually going to be a positive.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, so this is Easter Sunday, the day of the resurrection. So I am not going along with the -- humanity's doomed narrative here today.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Look, AI, we can't be Luddites about this. Every technology that has emerged in human history has over time made the world better. If the printing press, the -- you know, any -- the industrial revolution, you know, put -- displaced some people at first but it lifted billions of people out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 So like AI, for example, scientists just used AI to come up with an antibiotic for antibiotic-resistant bacteria. They had AI analyze 100 million different molecules and come up with the one that could defeat this -- these antibiotic-resistant bacteria. Humanity couldn't have done that.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, that same technology could come up with a bioweapon. So, you know, we're -- we're entering a new period where we have to -- where mankind has to harness this like we have in every other technology throughout history, harness this for the cause of good. Make sure that bad people can't use it to -- to cause harm. And we'll be better off in the long run.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: I agree with Marc, but China has absolutely no interest in that, right?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Especially -- as it relates to any technology, they have an anti- West agenda. They have an agenda to collect on us in every means possible. I -- I totally agree with you, Marc, on what -- on America's perspective. But we have to understand that bad rogue actors around this world like the Chinese Communist Party, want to take this technology and light it on fire as it relates to collecting it and using -- and weaponizing it against us.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But that's why we can't pause. We have to be ahead of them on it.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Absolutely, yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We have to be the leader in the world on it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But we don't have -- we don't have -- we don't know who would regulate it, to Josh's point, the United Nations? Who is going to regulate this, the Chinese? TikTok is already a huge problem. You look at the idea that they can manufacture a FOX News Sunday panel and make people think we are saying things that we are not saying. It's really troubling.\par \par \uc2 It's -- it's disruptive to our politics. We know this from the past election cycles. I won't mention one in particular because everybody will explode.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But we know about disinformation, misinformation, spin, trolls, and the like.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: You mean \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and the 2022 -- yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, you talk to NPR about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Wait, I want to make sure that we get to another big headline- making story this week. This \uc2 Pro Publica\uc2  report on Justice Thomas saying that he was having secret vacations with a high-dollar donor. He has issued a statement that says in part that they -- he has been longtime friends \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2 . That he as advised early on that this personal hospitality did not turn into business before the court, did not have to be reportable.\par \par \uc2 Ben Olinsky over at \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  says this. "Congress must investigate the breach of ethical conduct and call on Thomas to testify at a public hearing. It's long past time for Congress to pass legislation that requires Supreme Court justices to be subject to the same binding ethics and recusal rules as other federal judges."\par \par \uc2 And, Morgan, for right now, they are not. They don't -- they have a -- I would say they have a different set of rules, but they don't have a set of rules.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Yes, I think that there's two different discussions here. One is the ethics discussion and what Supreme Court justices should have to sign off on. I think most commonsense Americans watching your show this morning are going to want the most -- the highest ethical reporting standards for their Supreme Court justices, the same thing that members of Congress have to abide by. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 The second issue here is that there is a -- a vehement response to go after Justice Thomas from the left ever since his confirmation hearing. And the -- the way that they go after him and the antagonistic nature is different even from the other Supreme Court justices. One has to ask themselves why they target him the way that they do?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, The Wall Street...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But I think the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... integrity of the court is at stake here. And it -- it has been for a while with Justice Thomas, who is a friend. So let's put that out there. But I'm going to tell you something, I think that when you understand that he is palling around with people like \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2 , these aren't people who Sheldon Whitehouse, the senator said this week, who have an interest in a case in the court, these are the people who make a business of the court. People like \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , this is who he's hanging out with and...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: How does \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  make the business of the court?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'm just telling you...\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: How does he do it?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... they are interested...\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's not true.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It is true. They are interested...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He had...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... in controlling...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We've got to go, but I...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... certain speech.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Crow has not...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... just think it's troublesome.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But \uc2 Crow\uc2  has not had cases or business before...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, not specifically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... that's clear.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  is a -- is a wonderful person, kind person. And he has been caught up in and attacked in this, which is just shameful. Justice Thomas didn't do anything wrong.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: There's no evidence, say, of corruption. But, boy, this looks bad.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We've got to go. And so you're going to pay extra to see during the commercial how this...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... continues. Thank you, panel. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, TV host, bestselling author Kathie Lee Gifford talks to me about her new passion projects.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Major holiday week for many faiths. This year Easter overlapping with Passover celebrations and the observance of Ramadan. Amid these holy days, former talk show host and bestselling author Kathie Lee Gifford and her good friend Rabbi Jason Sobel are partnering together to bring ancient stories from the Bible to life. They've co-authored a new book titled "The God of the Way: A Journey into the Stories, People, and Faith that Changed the World Forever."\par \par \uc2 Their new book is paired with Gifford's film of the same name, a sweeping musical celebration of the Bible. I chatted with Gifford and Rabbi Sobel about their work earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's this beautiful musical experience, Kathie Lee, I don't even know how to describe it, other than spectacular, that you've put together. And you really bring these stories to life. What was your hope in that?\par \par \uc2 KATHIE LEE GIFFORD, CO-AUTHOR, "THE GOD OF THE WAY": Well, they're epic stories. You know -- oh, you know, Shannon, because of the books that you write. Those stories are -- first of all, they were real people. And their -- their stories are epic. And they're almost ripped from the headlines today. So we wrote -- I wrote four oratorios with four different sets of composers. And the movie "The Way" is -- is separated into four different oratorios with the most amazing performers in the world today playing different characters.\par \par \uc2 And it's just -- it's breath -- I wanted it to be epic, I wanted it to be breathtaking. And I -- but I wanted it to be so modern in a way that we could make it relevant, all these stories are relevant today. We can learn from the mistakes of Sarah and Abraham. We can learn from -- you know, every one of these stories, as I say, is precious and included in the scriptures for a reason.\par \par \uc2 And I just wanted to find a brand new way to tell these ancient stories. And so I called my dear friend Rabbi Sobel, Rabbi Jason. And he and I had -- you know, very surprising success with a book called "The Rock, The road, and the Rabbi," that we couldn't believe how many people loved that book. It's just -- I didn't know that people were so spiritually hungry, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 People are really heart and soul looking for truth. And they're broken. And what the world has offered them has not satisfied. And so we just wanted to go deeper. And every time I want to go deeper into these stories, I call people that know so much more than I know. I am not a biblical scholar. But Rabbi Sobel is, and the other people that I write with are. So I hang out with people like that and I -- I look smart.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, listen, I agree with you, Kathie Lee. I -- I turn to the experts and I always learn so much new. And the format that you put this in makes it accessible to people in a lot of different ways that you can reach them. And I think music is always one of those really special connecting ways.\par \par \uc2 GIFFORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Rabbi, as we are now in the midst of Passover and Easter, what is your hope that people in a very divided, contentious, and, frankly, frightening world will take from faith this weekend?\par \par \uc2 RABBI JASON SOBEL, CO-AUTHOR, "THE GOD OF THE WAY": Yes, absolutely. I mean, I think it's so important. I mean, Egypt, the Passover celebrates the coming out of Egypt. Jesus died at Passover. Egypt is not just a place, it's a state of mind. It literally means a place of confinement, a place of restriction, a place of being boxed in. And God doesn't want us to remain stuck in the box. He doesn't want us to remain stuck in our prejudices and our fear and our anxiety.\par \par \uc2 And so much of the hate and discrimination that we see today is rooted in fears and lies. And this season tells us God is about love. Love overcomes hate.\par \par \uc2 GIFFORD: Amen.\par \par \uc2 SOBEL: Fear overcomes -- fear is overcome by hope. And the lies are overcome by truth. And there is truth and love in this story that is meant to bring us and unite us together as people made in God's image.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Rabbi Jason Sobel and Kathie Lee Gifford, thank you and God bless you both.\par \par \uc2 GIFFORD: God bless you.\par \par \uc2 SOBEL: God bless.\par \par \uc2 GIFFORD: Shalom shalom, my sweet friend.\par \par \uc2 SOBEL: Shalom shalom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just a quick note. It has been called the raciest part of the Bible, so why is Song of Solomon in there? Well, Pastor Ben Stuart explains on my "Livin' the Bream" podcast, drops at 10am Eastern. We break down one of the stories that happens to be in my new book, "Love Stories of the Bible Speak."\par \par \uc2 And you can hear all the interviews from our program today with the "FOX News Sunday" podcast. You can download and subscribe to all these podcasts by heading to foxnewspodcasts.com or wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230409ej4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Swimmer Riley Gaines Assaulted For Speaking Truth; GOP Supports Gaines Fight For Women's Sports; WH To LGBTQ Activists "We Have Their Back"; Biden Admin Proposes Change to Title IX; Liberal Mob Attacks Conservative Speakers; VP Harris Stands Behind Expelled TN House Members; Former OnlyFans And Reality Star Angela White Interviewed On Changing Her Life; Reverend \uc2 Franklin Graham\uc2  Discusses Christian Beliefs In Meaning Of Good Friday And Easter; New York City Plays Classical Music In Subways To Remove Homeless; Composer Changes Lyrics To Song In "The Little Mermaid"; CNN Interviews Court Sketch Artist At Trump Arraignment Hearing\b0\par\par\uc2 Tammy Bruce, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,371 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS HOST: Sean will be back Monday with a live audience show. Tickets are absolutely free. Just go to Hannity.com. Thank you everyone for tuning in. Have a great weekend. Happy Easter and have a happy Passover. Thank you everyone.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Raymond Arroyo, in for Laura Ingraham. This is a special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from New York City. We have a big show for you tonight, along with a special guest. Model and influencer, Blac Chyna, is here on her shocking new transformation.\par \par \uc2 But, first, there is a pattern that I see in the news on this Good Friday, and it is related to faith and its absence. As many of you know, this is the day when Christians celebrate the sacrifice of Jesus Christ on the cross for the sins of all humanity. But, remember, it all began with a mob.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The unreasonable mob rushed in, arrested and killed Jesus for the things he said, which upset and rankled them. Truth was snuffed out by error, at least for a few days. That Wall Street Journal poll recently revealed that the importance of religion among Americans has dropped from 62 percent 25 years ago to 39 percent today, but I'm not sure that's really true. You see, religion seems alive and well to me. Just look at the fervor of these religiously minded people. Look at him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Communication with the truth and of the experience of humanity through these times of catastrophic climate change is needed now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's religious activism, liturgies repeated again and again, rooted not in God, but in this case, the Green Gospel. They must commit these acts of artistic vandalism to further the cause of environmental justice, never mind that pouring soup or gluing yourself to a Van Gogh does nothing to help the environment, especially the artistic environment. Facts don't matter. This is a religious devotion, one inculcated in predominantly young people, since they were children. They're merely following the dictates of the only religion they've ever known. And the mob is now invading our state Houses with the same religious conviction. Look at these trans religionists in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Catchy (ph). We've been told for years that citizens must never invade a lawmaking body to protest anything. When you invade these temples of democracy, democracy itself is imperiled. Oh, sorry. The rules just changed again.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. MAX ROSE (D), NEW YORK: Year after year after year where we see these mass shootings, we see acts of protest, acts of real incredible courage by young people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Different rules for different people. We'll be getting into the protests led by Representatives at the Tennessee statehouse later in the show. But, what we're witnessing is the enforcement of secular dogma. And if you don't believe in the shifting cause of the day, whether it's trans rights or climate change, or abortion on demand, the mob shows up to shout you down, cancel you, and if necessary, physically attack you, or they'll just deface and blow up your place of business. It's not a debate. It is a moral conviction, a belief that heresy must be stamped out. To oppose their evolving orthodoxy is an unforgivable sin.\par \par \uc2 So, when a federal judge, known for his originalist readings of the Constitution, for instance, shows up at Stanford Law School, nobody debates him. They shout him down and call them names. The college Sanhedrin do this for two reasons. One, because they know there are no consequences. And these undergrad Lord of the Flies reenactments are actually celebrated. And two, in joining the mob, they attain their highest held value, likes and approval on social media. Sure it's wrong, but look at the views on my TikTok Ma.\par \par \uc2 Just last night, a 12th time all-American female champion swimmer, Riley Gaines, tried to argue that biological men should not compete in sports against biological women, because, well, they're biological men. The mob at \uc2 San Francisco State University\uc2  assaulted, grabbed her torches, and chased her down.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: There was a video of another point in this melee when the protesters demanded money, $10 a person, to let Gaines leave the venue and her safe room. 30 pieces of silver might have done the trick. We're going to dive into all of this in a second. But, free speech is an essential pillar of this republic. Everybody should have their say. And disagreement, debate is at the heart of this democracy. But, for it to work, we must all listen, which means you can't yell every time you're disturbed by something, or call people have seen names or hit them. And when a society fails to protect women and girls, it's probably not going to protect anyone else either. This new religion of revolution without a cross or a savior carries only chaos and violence, but at least you'll know they're coming. The mob is always the first tell.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Kim Jones, her daughter competed against trans swimmer Lia Thomas. She is also the Co-Founder of Independent Counsel on Women's Sports. Also joining us is Michele Tafoya, former NFL Sideline Reporter. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Kim, Riley Gaines game was basically held hostage by these trans activists. For three hours, she was in that room. She also claimed she was hit by a man in a dress. My question is, why is this acceptable behavior at a university when they have codes against just this sort of thing?\par \par \uc2 KIM JONES, CO-FOUNDER, ICONS, & DAUGHTER COMPETED WITH LIA THOMAS: Well, it's not. It's absolutely not. But, right now, we have men and boys who have been emboldened by policies that have allowed and facilitated the abuse and discrimination against women. It's looked at as justifiable. As soon as we tell women that they're not worthy of fair competition and decent respect in sports, they aren't allowed to undress in a place outside the male gaze. We are telling men that they can have their way and they don't deserve to be treated fairly. So, this - I lay this entirely at the hands of politicians who are not sticking up for women and girls, not developing policy and enforcing rules, and definitely at the hands of the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , who is actively discriminating against women and making this kind of behavior possible.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So, you see this as an extension of what your daughter and others have seen in the locker room, and in the competition.\par \par \uc2 JONES: 100 percent, and I'm calling on any governor and any attorney general to come join us and help us call for the - to make things right--\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: --to get this back.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Michele, these protesters claim this is about trans rights. But, isn't everyone missing the larger point here that biological men have larger hearts, lung capacity, muscle mass? How is this fair competition for a woman or girl in a sporting event?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE TAFOYA, FORMER NFL SIDELINE REPORTER: To say that trans women are real women is just false. Follow the science, right? You mentioned a couple of things there.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAFOYA: I hate to get a little bit graphic here. But also, men don't get a period, biological men. Women often compete during that time of the month. That's a massive difference. There are so many biological reasons that women and men should not be competing in the same arenas. Now, look, I think what gets lost here is, people think this is anti-trans. They think this is taking away opportunities from trans women to compete. No, no, no. It's maintaining the fairness for biological women to compete. As Jill Biden said recently, that they fought so hard for with Title IX. There needs to be another option for trans athletes. Simple as that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. No. Should there be a trans category?\par \par \uc2 JONES: No.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No? You don't think they should compete against each other--\par \par \uc2 JONES: No.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: --separately?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think we've got categories that encompass everyone on Earth. We have - sports are designated by categories. That's how we allow for inclusion in sports, and there is a protected categories and then there is a category for men and anyone who doesn't fit in the protected categories.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is a good segue. This week, the Biden administration is proposing a change in Title IX. Title IX was created to protect women, particularly in competition. Under the new rule, any school that receives federal funds, any school, may not ban athletes from competing, even outside of their chosen - or their biological gender. Is - I want to play this for you. This is the White House spokesman supporting the protesters, and get your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , PRESS SECRETARY, WHITE HOUSE: LGBTQIA+ plus kids are resilient. They are fierce. They fight back. They're not going anywhere. And we have their back. This administration has their back. We are so proud of the kids across this country who have organized protests and school walkouts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Kim, very quickly, your reaction to this and of this proposed change in Title IX.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Condoning this kind of increased behavior that's leading to physical assaults and violence against women is inexcusable. There is no place for it in civilized society. And I cannot imagine a more regressive notion than using the only legal protection for sex-based rights that women have in the United States and turning it into a tool to say they must compete against men and boys. It's ultimately misogynistic.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Kim and Michele, thank you both for being here. I wish we had more time. Happy Easter to both of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TAFOYA: To you as well.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My next guest has personal experience with the mob we'd been speaking of. Both tried to speak at universities when angry protesters attacked.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Isabel Brown, Turning Point USA Contributor, and Ian Haworth, Turning Point USA Speaker, Washington Examiner Columnist, and Host of "Off Limits".\par \par \uc2 Isabel, I want to start with you. Something happened to you when you tried to give a pro-life speech at \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2 . I want to share this with the audience. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Isabel, did you even get a chance to speak, and how did \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2  respond to this attack?\par \par \uc2 ISABEL BROWN, CONTRIBUTOR, TURNING POINT USA: I unfortunately never got the opportunity to break down the lies that pro-choicers believe because of the powerful abortion lobby in America accompanying the incredible Kristan Hawkins on her Students for Life of America speaking tour this spring. Before we even had our names introduced at \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2  last week, this violent mob decided to chant, "Nazis, go home", "fascists, go home" so loudly we were unable to complete the dialogue. We came to campus for inviting students from the pro-life and pro-abortion side of things to ask real questions, and embrace dialogue in a way that college campuses are no longer facilitating.\par \par \uc2 Things escalated so quickly that many students felt that they were physically in danger. One student, in particular, was physically assaulted by this mob, and instead of removing the violent Antifa community members that weren't even students at \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2 , Kristan Hawkins and myself were essentially detained in a locked room for several hours for our own safety on a public college campus we were invited to speak at.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's outrageous. Ian, here is what happened to you just yesterday at SUNY, Albany. Sadly, it looks the same. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Ian, this goes back to my monologue at the top of the show. You were there to talk about free speech on college campuses, of all things. Why are trans activists swarming around you and forming a conga line?\par \par \uc2 IAN HAWORTH, COLUMNIST, WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, the conga line is something I think we should just set aside. I mean, that's cultural appropriation on its face. I think it's quite hilarious. But, the fact that they are focusing on trans rights, I think it's just the issue of the day. They've decided I'm a transfer (ph) because I'm conservative. They actually bothered to read any of my stuff. They discover I'm not in quite the opposite. But, they don't care. Really, it's all about shutting out any speech that disagrees with their fundamental views, which is that the left should have full power, the radical left, and college campuses is a place where they've really enjoyed full power for decades.\par \par \uc2 And so, when groups like TPUSA dare to show up and share obvious truths, they get very, very angry. It's all about forcing ideas, like, my - ideas by myself, by Isabel, off campus so that people won't be, in their eyes, indoctrinated with the truth.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. It just seems so off topic here. I mean, you were there to talk about one thing, and then they run off in another direction. They're protesting something else. Isabel, VCU, the school you tried to speak at, said this about what happened. "VCU is committed to promoting a safe environment for our students, faculty, staff, and visitors so the right to gather and speak freely is protected." Did you feel protected, and why are they so tolerant to violence and harassment of women in this case?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I certainly did not feel protected, at least not by the campus police department who decided not to show up to the event despite forewarning that there may be violent protesters with people who they have previously arrested in the past, and a history of conflict with the Students for Life Chapter on campus and these radical protesters. At other events, it took 30 or so minutes. That's why we decided to livestream on my \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and someone getting punched and physically assaulted for the police department to even warrant walking in the room.\par \par \uc2 It's nice a few days later or a week later for these campuses to come out and say that they support free speech. But, in actuality, these public universities that are beholden to the United States Constitution and our First Amendment rights to speak and gather freely, they're not upholding this reality. In fact, they are pushing a one-sided lockstep leftist authoritarian narrative on the next generation, which is why it's never been more important for us to continue showing up, to continue speaking the truth, and inviting dialogue that academia is supposed to be all about.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, I thought the university was about entertaining different thoughts, new thoughts, new ideas, when it's an echo chamber in a silo. What good is that? Ian, they even destroyed a Bible during your speech or before your speech. What is that about? And does that perhaps run to the heart of what I was talking about early - earlier that this is a counter religion, if you will, it's a religious belief without real form?\par \par \uc2 HAWORTH: Absolutely. I mean, I wrote a column for The New York Post about the fact that transgenderism has become the less new religion. You saw Joe Biden talking about anti - so-called anti-transgender laws being sinful. You've got people bearing down Dylan Mulvaney. This is a full on religion. And so, I think tearing up the Bible is an obvious part of that. The only thing they destroyed more was the free pizza that we were handing out. So, I think it was just really classic socialism on its face.\par \par \uc2 But, again, when it comes down to the colleges, a lot of this is actually motivated by fear. They are scared of these people. They are scared of being held to account by their own students. And so, they're really just bowing down to them too. And I think colleges need to stand up and actually push back against this--\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well,--\par \par \uc2 HAWORTH: --with action rather than words.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: There is also a hostility to organize an established religion, which begs the - for true dialogue to take place, we need representatives of those religions accounted for on campus as well. Isabel, Ian, thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 On this Good Friday, a true act of redemption, model and influence her Blac Chyna is here to talk about why she is leaving only fans and embracing an unexpected new path. Plus, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visits Tennessee not to attend the funeral of one of the children killed by a trans gunman, but to defend two lawmakers who protested on the Capitol floor, the details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is a Fox News alert. Tonight, we're learning a new batch of classified documents exposing critical national security secrets has been leaked online. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting that approximately 100 documents detailing sensitive information on everything from Ukraine to the Middle East to China, surfaced on social media sites. The Pentagon telling Fox News that they are actively reviewing the matter, and the DoJ is now investigating. We'll bring you more details on that as they come in.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned at the top of the show, storming state capitals is becoming routine for progressives, from Kentucky to Florida to Colorado, now to Tennessee. Two Democrat lawmakers in the Volunteer State were just expelled, were leading protests in the House chamber against guns. The left is furious that people are being held accountable for breaking House rules. So, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tonight rushed there in a last minute trip to defend the expelled Congressmen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , 49TH U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: It was about who they were representing. It's about whose voices they were channeling. A democracy says you don't silence the people. You do not stifle the people. You don't turn off their microphones when they are speaking about the importance of life and liberty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I wonder if that applies to pro-lifers. Well, we'll check later. We'll send the White House.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New Jersey Congressman, \uc2 Jeff Van Drew\uc2 , and Xaviaer DuRousseau, PragerU personality. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made a beeline for Tennessee to chastise the House, but she failed to attend even one of the funerals of the six slain individuals, including three children, from last week. Your thoughts on all of this.\par \par \uc2 REP. JEFF VAN DREW (R-NJ): My thoughts, number one, it's the first time I've seen \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  take a beeline to go anywhere to do anything. The only thing she should be doing is, really if she wants to, is to teach people how to be the worst Vice President in the history of the United States of America. That was shameful. I mean, there were so worried about January 6, but here we have not just people, not just constituents, not just average folks, but we have legislators who are part of a gang philosophy and carrying on inappropriately and breaking down the halls of the statehouse. That's wrong. She should never be supporting that. And it's so typical of the ultra-liberals, so typical. It is OK for us to do things, but never for you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: And that's what she was doing. I'm just disappointed in her, and that's pretty hard for me.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. Well, the double - I don't like the double standard.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: Double standard.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I have one rule for everybody. And Xaviaer, these Congressmen, they weren't just expelled because people didn't like them. They were expelled because they violated the House rules of decorum. Now, these two Congressmen are saying that this is racist. OK? And the media is going along with this narrative. Now, the reason for this is the third white Congresswoman who joined them in the protest was spared. Watch the media reaction, I want your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN ANCHOR: It's racism. It's a double standard in our society. Obviously, again, I'll have to say, I would doubt they would have done this to a white member.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When you see the racism like that just out in the open, it does take your breath away,\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHAPRTON, MSNBC HOST, "POLITICSNATION": This kind of blatant racism is what is going to backfire on them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Xaviaer, is this racism or members being held accountable for violating statehouse rules?\par \par \uc2 XAVIAER DUROUSSEAU, PRAGERU PERSONALITY: It's typical leftist behavior to just totally deflect and put everything on racism. This is called holding them accountable, because we've already seen, the left has no problem burning down buildings if they're not getting what they want. And if we don't start holding these lawmakers accountable, then our democratic republic is just going to turn into a battle of who can lead the bigger protests. People need to stop treating our Capitol buildings as if they're Section 8 eight housing. We need to start having accountability, and we need to start looking at the left and questioning what are their priorities.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to not have time to attend any funerals or to even meet the families of these victims, but she does have time to push the agenda of the left, to take away our constitutional rights to guns, and to make sure that she is keeping these far left lawmakers into position of power. They do not care about you and I. They care about their agenda.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, we're seeing cops in the statehouse in Tennessee being jostled and pushed. Last time I checked, this was called an insurrection, Congressman. We had images like this. Is this another case of activists feeling that their personal crusade trumps any rules of decorum up for even decent etiquette?\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: Absolutely. It's an insurrection if you do it, but if we do it, it's really what's required needs to be done. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't - never visits Veterans, has never worried about what happened to the families of the Afghans that were lost in battle, the Americans that were lost in Afghanistan, never worried about our border, went there once, didn't do anything at all, isn't protecting our country. Yet, this, she runs over to. Is she a Vice President or she a political hack? I think she behaved a lot more like a political hack here. It's disappointing to see that whatever party you are.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, what breaks my heart is you have people who are caught in the middle of this. And when you have a community suffering, the way Nashville is,--\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: --I have friends there, the first impulse should be, be with those who are grieving, these children who never asked to be shocked during the school day, tend to that, then move to the politics. But, here, they move right to the politics and demonize people. It's the worst thing you can do in a community.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: They won't even talk about it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: They won't even talk about it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Xaviaer, I want to give you the last word on this. As you watch this play out, your thoughts on what we're seeing here, and what the takeaway should be.\par \par \uc2 DUROUSSEAU: The takeaway is that the less priorities are all backwards, and once again, they are capitalizing on trauma, and they are capitalizing on death to push an agenda because they do not compare or they do not care about us. They only care about what benefits them.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's heartbreaking. Thank you both for being here. Congressman, Xaviaer, Happy Easter. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Next, a beautiful Good Friday story, Blac Chyna shocking her fans with a new transformation and an embrace of faith. She is here next. Plus, I took to the streets to see if anybody knew what Good Friday was. Bad idea. The devastating details, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: On this Good Friday, I want to tell you a story of transformation and redemption. If you look back at the last few years, model Blac Chyna was starring in tabloids on OnlyFans, and stuck in some major public feuds. But now she's deactivated her OnlyFans accounts, she got baptized, and she's even started going by her birthname, Angela Renee White. This week she announced her latest achievement, getting her doctorate from a Bible college.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Angela White. Angela, thank you so much for being here. Happy Easter or Holy Week, anyway. What was the deciding factor for you to make this change? You were making great money. You achieved fame. Why did you give that life up?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA WHITE, REALITY STAR, FORMERLY BLAC CHYNA: I had to. Honestly, the life was living, it was fast paced, money coming in, money going out. You know, I just honestly kept running into all of these problems and these issues and this and that, and lawsuits. And I was like, you know what, I need a change. I have to change my life. I can't be living like this. I have two beautiful babies. I have businesses. And I had to really just get back to myself. I kind of lost myself, honestly.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Was there one moment that you said, I've got to change this now? Was it one deciding moment?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: It was -- the deciding moment was on my birthday. So I got baptized on my birthday. And then from there, I just started putting in the work. People have to also realize once you get baptized, you have to be willing to change everything about you so you can start moving into the light.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, and Angela, you talked about removing one of your tattoos on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . I want to talk to you about this. Listen.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WHITE: You all now that I had this tattoo, right. It has got to come off. You know what I mean? I'm not about to have no mark of the beast, anything like that. I just don't want anything negative or demonic on my body anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Angela, when did you realize how dangerous this was spiritually? And this wasn't the only addition that you removed from your body, by the way.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: The more I started reading the Bible, and it's so bizarre to me, because at the time I didn't see nothing wrong with it. And that's like the issue. We don't see things that we're doing wrong because we're actually sitting in that moment. And I just started like praying to God and taking the proper steps, going to church and just really just, really just focusing on myself and my spirit.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I love that it's not only spiritual but a transformation but a physical one. You've had fillers in your face dissolved, removing tattoos. What is next for Angela White?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: What's next for me? I'm going to continue my journey with the Bible, for sure, and just work on myself, just honestly work on myself. And work on my businesses and different ways of making income because the way I was making income wasn't right. So now I have to like really dive deep into my studies and into my work ethic.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, the new Angela fits you perfectly. Thank you. Have a blessed Easter. Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: You, too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: With Good Friday upon us, I hit the streets of New York City to ask people what it means and why it is good. Things started a bit rocky.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I call it Good Friday because it's a really great Friday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's Friday, and we're on holiday and we're in New York. It's the day after Thursday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because it's Friday.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: We're asking people about Good Friday. Why is it and why is it good?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because you're off two days after that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What is Easter and why do you celebrate it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's obviously a holiday.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know, but why? What are you celebrating?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Weed. It's legal now.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know, but I don't think that came out of the tomb.\par \par \uc2 What is Good Friday and why is it good?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's the day that Jesus was crucified. And it's good because he paid for our sins.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every human being should know that and accept Jesus as their savior and lord.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: A couple got it right.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Franklin Graham\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Billy Graham Evangelistic Association\uc2 . Reverend Graham, the fact that so many people didn't know what Good Friday is, how do you introduce them to really the center of Christian belief again? And how important is faith in the continuance of this democracy?\par \par \uc2 REV. \uc2 FRANKLIN GRAHAM\uc2 , THE BILLY GRAHAM EVANGELISTIC ASSOCIATION: Well, we've been watching your program tonight, and you just look at the chaos in our country and around the world. And this is what a world looks like without God. And we've turned our back on God as a nation, as a people. And the only hope for this nation, the only hope for everyone who is watching is God.\par \par \uc2 Politicians aren't going to change this country. Only God can do that. And he does that through individual hearts. Changing my heart, yours, and everybody who is watching. Jesus Christ came to this earth the take our sins. He didn't come to condemn us. He came to save us. And he gave his life as a ransom. And he shed his blood, poured out his blood on a cross, on Good Friday, for the sins of all mankind.\par \par \uc2 And if we're willing to turn from our sins and believe on him, God will forgive us. On the third day, Jesus came up out of the grave. He laid his life down, and because he was God, he could take his life back up. He laid his life down for you and me, and on the third day he took his life back. And he's alive, and he's going to come back. But today, God is willing to forgive us, Raymond. He will forgive you and me and everyone who confesses their sins and repents. Repent means to turn from those sins, and then to believe on the name of his son Jesus Christ.\par \par \uc2 This is the Christian faith. This is what we believe, this is what we stand for. And there's only one way to God. It's not many roads to God. Not many religions lead to God. There's only one path to God, and it goes through the cross, because Jesus paid the debt of sin.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Reverend, Reverend, Graham, I want to show something to the audience. We have a shot from the 1960s of skyscrapers here in New York City. I saw it this week, and they were all lit with crosses, all of them. And I thought to myself, what an expression of faith that was once part of the general culture. How do you get that sense of faith, that witness, if you will, back in the public conversation where it's not something demonized or protested, but something in the center of the culture? And something that is seen as holding it together?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, Raymond, Jesus said that the world hated him, and the world will hate those that follow him. He was talking to his disciples. They will hate you because of me. And the world has a lot of tolerance for all these other faith systems. But when it comes to people of faith in Jesus Christ, they have very little tolerance.\par \par \uc2 And so how do we get it back? I'm not sure, Raymond, we'll ever get it back. This country is changing, and it's not changing for the better. It's changing for the worse, because we've turned our back on God. And this nation was built by men and women who had a strong faith in God. And we have been slowly kicking him out of our schools, out of our education system, out of our government, out of our workplace, off of television. We've taken him out of everything.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, well, there are consequences for that action. And I hope we don't have to suffer through them. Reverend Graham, happy Easter to you. And I want to remind people on your special on FOX this Sunday. You'll ben offering a special Easter message from Rome at 12:00 p.m. eastern. Thank you for being here. Happy Easter.\par \par \uc2 And don't you dare kiss the girl. "Little Mermaid" lyrics are being modified for the reboot. And L.A.'s latest so-called solution to the homeless problem is a little low-fi. Sean Duffy reacts in Friday Follies next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies. For that, we're joined by special guest Sean Duffy, cohost of "The Bottom Line" airing 6:00 p.m. weekdays on FOX Business. Sean, thank you for being here. In Dem-led cities across the country, there's not just a crime problem, as you well know, but a homelessness problem. Los Angeles is trying to do scatter loiterers from their subways by blasting classical music through metro speakers. I asked New Yorkers what they thought of that strategy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Do you think that would work in New York?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To drive them out, no. But I think it would be definitely a turnup.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think you'd make more homeless people and loitering.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He doesn't even like classical music, so I don't know where he gets that idea.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Years ago in Rite Aids in southern California, they blasted Barry Manilow to drive the homeless and loiterers out.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That would drive me out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, that might work. Barry Manilow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can only hear it so many times.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I doubt classical music will do anything to send them away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A nice lobby intro music or something, elevator music.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would like dance to it, honestly.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Go, go, go. I like it. I love this. We're doing it. This is good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Sean, you never know what you're going to find in New York City. Now, you live here. I bet you've never danced with strangers on the street, though. Would classical music scatter loiterers? Do you think that works at all?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FBN'S "THE BOTTOM LINE" CO-HOST: So first off, you say you never know what you're going to see or find in New York City. Raymond Arroyo dancing in the streets. That was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So wasn't it at Guantanamo Bay -- didn't they, at Guantanamo Bay, didn't they play hard rock music to break people down? But here in L.A. they're going to play classical music to drive people out? I have a better solution. If you have vagrants, Raymond, arrest them. Put them in cuffs. Get them out of there. Enough with the classic music. Enough with the light on crime business. Take charge of your city, take charge of your subways, arrest them, and get them out. On Good Friday even.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and look, if you really want to repel people, play Cardi B or Maroon 5. That will clear the streets.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And stop banging up poor Barry Manilow. I just saw him in Vegas. He still sings pretty well. At least you get melodies from Barry.\par \par \uc2 Sean, I have got to swim along here. You've probably seen this movie before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, lyrics like that won't exist in a live action remake, Sean. Composer Alan Menken revealing he is changing the song so that it's clear to viewers that Prince Eric would never force himself on Ariel. Sean, the Little Mermaid's voice was literally taken from her in the original movie. Is she supposed to carry a written consent form to give to the prince? I don't understand this at all.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So first off, you look at her body language. She's actually leaning in. She wants Prince Eric to kiss her. Kids are smart. They get there's body language between the two of them. But to your point, are you going to have to take a sobriety test, then sign a consent form, maybe do a bad breath test as well for the two people coming together? Listen, if \uc2 Disney\uc2  really was meaningful about undoing the harm that they have done with this movie and giving the wrong message to boys about forcing themselves on girls, give the profits of the little mermaid to a class of people who really need help. Help women out. Maybe give a scholarship to trans men who think they're women and help them get in to school. Or maybe even a woman's studies program, Raymond. That might be nice as well. But be serious. Give the money away if you are sending the wrong message to men.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You know, I don't like -- if you have to change the lyrics and you're embarrassed by something that was, don't reproduce it. Leave it alone, and don't make any more money off it. Write an original story. Write about the little Merman. But don't do this. Don't distort these great songs. People love them. My kids certainly did.\par \par \uc2 CNN must have run out of people to interview about the Trump indictment, Sean, so they interviewed the sketch artist who drew Trump's courtroom photo. You have got to see this to believe it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What was it like in the courtroom that day?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How are you preparing for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to sketch him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your sketches become the historic record of that case.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Suddenly he pled not guilty. I thought, oh, I better draw that. I thought I had to have him with his mouth open talking to the judge.\par \par \uc2 He did that turn with the glare at the prosecutor, the side eye expression. I thought I have got to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Sean, I can't wait for the exclusive with the court stenographer and the men's room attendant. Imagine what they'll tell us. What do you think of this amazing exclusive from CNN?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I wonder why ratings for CNN are so low. But we were just talking about the Little Mermaid. I think what she did when she drew the sketch was she had a picture of Ursula alongside \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as she drew this sketch. He looks evil, mean, angry. It was a nasty sketch. But for CNN to take to it the next level, to go we have nothing to cover on our airwaves, but we're going to interview the sketch artist who drew this formal picture of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think it goes to the point how much this network actually hates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , how far they will go to demonize the former president of the United States. It's unbelievable, Raymond.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It is. That sketch, by the way, is the cover of "The New Yorker" this week. And they have got photos of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  coming and going from the courthouse. He comes in with the cops, you know, on either side of him. They didn't use any of those. They used this sketch. And I agree with you, he has got this pompadour, this scowl on his face. They could have very well used the regular photos. So you think it's with intent, that this is - -\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: He looks like Ursula from the little mermaid. These stories are connected, Raymond on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This could be a whole new reboot. The little merman and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be Ursula in the new version, Ursulo, maybe.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: All right, my friend, we will leave it there. Happy Easter to you. Happy Holy Week as well, Sean.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Thank you, and Rebecca and the whole Arroyo family.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Thank you very much. Look, I end the day with a Duffy. I'll begin tomorrow morning on FOX & FRIENDS with the other Duffy. So I get my own Easter treat early.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a sneak peek at my latest project for the whole family. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: With history slipping away from us, most children know little to nothing of the great American lives. So I wrote a new book, "The Unexpected Light of Thomas Alva Edison," which shows how a deaf boy with ADHD dismissed by his school masters as unteachable became the greatest inventor of all time. His mother had something to do with it. Now we've created a FOX Nation companion special for the whole family. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: After filing patents for about 400 new inventions, \uc2 Edison\uc2  relocated his lab from Menlo Park to the sprawling new facility in West Orange, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Here in the \uc2 Edison\uc2  labs there's a neat tribute to his home laboratory when he was a boy. This is his private chemistry lab. \uc2 Edison\uc2  continued to tinker and play with acids and electricity, ultimately perfecting right here at the \uc2 Edison\uc2  lab the \uc2 Edison\uc2  alkaline battery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Be sure to check out the new FOX Nation special, "The Unexpected Light of Thomas Alva Edison," and get the book, share it with your family. It will inspire, I promise.\par \par \uc2 I'm Raymond Arroyo wishing you a blessed Easter and Passover. And thanks to Laura and the whole team for letting me sit in.\par \par \uc2 "GUTFELD!" is next. Happy Easter.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230408ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Michael McCaul Does Not Rule Out Troops To Taiwan; Controversy Over Ousting Of Democratic Lawmakers; Juvenile Suspects Identified In Murder Of Three Teens; Sheriff Billy Woods Cautions Against New Gun Restrictions; At Least One Dead After Car Rams Into Pedestrians; Moulton: Evacuations Began Too Late; Biden Dismisses Vietnam Comparisons In July 2021; American Journalist Formally Accused Of Spying; Swiss-Born Billionaire Donations To U.S. Politics; Animal Tranquilizer Mixed With Fentanyl Causes String Of Deaths In Milwaukee; Rise And Fall Of Biofuels As Possible Replacement For Fossil Fuels Examined; U.S. Continues Efforts To Move Semiconductor Chip Manufacturing From China To U.S. And Its Allies; Bipartisan Congressional Delegation Visits Taiwan; Two African American Lawmakers Expelled From Tennessee State House For Participating In Gun Control Demonstration\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Aishah Hasnie, Charles Watson, Jonathan Serrie, Trey  Yingst, Lucas Tomlinson, Greg Palkot, Bret Baier, Garrett Tenney, William  La Jeunesse \par \uc2 7,790 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Me too, this is crazy.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Lawrence?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANALYST: I'm going home back to Texas and make sure you watch my tomorrow before I go back.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Cross country. That's it for us everyone, we'll see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Mike Emanuel in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, one of the most powerful lawmakers in Congress says the possibility of sending U.S. troops to Taiwan would be on the table if China invades. House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman Michael McCaul is leading a bipartisan delegation to the island this week. His comments will undoubtedly trigger a fierce reply from Beijing. McCaul spoke exclusively with Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie today. She joins us from Taipei where it is 6:00 a.m. Saturday morning. Hello, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hello there. Good evening to you, Mike. That's right. So, these lawmakers will soon meet with President Tsai here in Taipei in just a few hours. This is their last stop of their three day tour through the island and it's really been focused on deterring a Chinese invasion.\par \par \uc2 But if that day does come, the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee -- Relations Committee, excuse me, tells Fox sending U.S. troops to this island is not out of the question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Conflict is always the last resort.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): House Foreign Affairs Chairman Michael McCaul doesn't want war. But if China invades Taiwan, he says the U.S. military could step in.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy and yourself have said that multiple times now that we need to arm Taiwan now before there's any sort of invasion. What about U.S. troops?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think, you know, then you're talking about an authorized use of military force.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: If the American people support this, the Congress will follow.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, you're saying that the option to authorize war powers is on the table?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: If Communist China invaded Taiwan, it would certainly be on the table.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: That lines up with President Biden's repeated comments.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would U.S. forces defend the island?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, if in fact there was an unprecedented attack.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The White House has on several occasions now walked back those remarks insisting the administration has made no such promises to Taiwan, and hasn't pivoted from the One China policy.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan is China's red line, the leading maker of advanced chips around the world and one of the few democracies in the Pacific. All important as the CCP tries to grow its influence.\par \par \uc2 President Xi hosting French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  this week, giving him a generous state visit some say is a calculated move to woo an E.U. member state away from the U.S. But it's this tiny island Chairman McCaul warns Xi should back away from.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I believe he will try to influence this election, this presidential election in Taiwan this January. If he does not prevail in that, then I think Plan B is going to be either a blockade or an outright invasion of the island.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): Mike, day after day China continues to take actions today, China announced that it is sanctioning the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library, among others for hosting a meeting between Taiwan's President Tsai and of course, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 So, as Tsai prepares to meet with this bipartisan delegation in just a few hours here, we await to see what China does next, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Fascinating. Aishah Hasnie starting us off live from Taipei. Aishah, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 There is fierce criticism tonight for the Republican dominated Tennessee State House, it has expelled two or three Democrats accused of breaking rules for encouraging an anti-gun rally on the chamber floor following last week's school shootings in Nashville. Vice President Harris is scheduled to meet with state legislators soon.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson is in the Tennessee Capitol tonight. Good evening, Charles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will meet with lawmakers moments from now on to discuss a way forward on gun reform. The V.P. surprise trip comes hours after she described successful Republican efforts -- successful Republican efforts to expel two Democratic lawmakers is called undemocratic and dangerous. This after Tennessee Representatives Justin Jones, Justin Pearson and Gloria Johnson held a protest in support of gun reform on the House floor last week in the wake of the Nashville school shootings where six people including three young children were killed.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say the three broke decorum and things got way out of hand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the floor since I'm speaker, we have rule, we have decorum, we have process, we have procedure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the fact that they continue to keep their foot on the accelerator when they knew the world was watching really just shows what this Republican Party has sunk to.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: On Thursday, protesters loudly voiced their support for the trio of Democrats and booed Republicans after they successfully ousted Jones and Pearson who are both black and built along party lines.\par \par \uc2 The effort to expel Johnson who was a white woman narrowly failed by one vote. Johnson argued that while she participated in the protests, she didn't use a bullhorn like Jones and Pearson. Despite making the distinction, Johnson called the outcomes racist but GOP members see race was not a factor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, it's unfortunate. She's trying to put political racism in this which there was nothing on this. They were all given due process.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES, FORMER STATE REPRESENTATIVE OF TENNESSEE: They expelled the two youngest black lawmakers, which is no coincidence. We see clearly the nation is seeing clearly what is going on in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Now, Jones and Pearson could be back in their seats within days, at least a dozen members of two respective voting bodies in their districts reportedly say they plan to reinstall the two young Democrats to their vacated seats in the interim.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WATSON (on camera): And under Tennessee law, the two Democrats are eligible to run for reelection once special elections are held to officially fill those vacancies, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll follow it. Charles Watson in Nashville. Charles, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 A major break in the high-profile case involving the deaths of three Florida teenagers, authorities have begun making arrests as the search for an additional suspect continues. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie i