ng then-President Trump for the situation in Afghanistan that led to the chaotic U.S. military withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats are joining the Republican chorus in disputing the findings. Lucas Tomlinson is live at the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): U.S. officials say 19,000 Afghans are still waiting for visas to enter the United States, after languishing in refugee camps after helping the U.S. military for years.\par \par \uc2 Some Democratic lawmakers including a Marine combat veteran are joining Republicans in saying part of the blame for the chaotic withdrawal belongs with the current commander in chief, despite White House efforts to deflect the blame to the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): I don't think it's enough to just blame the previous administration. Literally, they did not start this evacuation soon enough.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The White House tacitly admitted the same in this 12-page report. "We now prioritize earlier evacuations when faced with a degrading security situation."\par \par \uc2 President Biden's approval rating sunk after the hasty withdrawal from Afghanistan, coupled with rising inflation. Critics say it was a mistake for the U.S. military to abandon Bagram Air Base in the dead of night, in early July 2021.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over a month later, they freed a known ISIS prisoner, who carried out the deadly suicide bombing, killing 13 U.S. servicemembers and 170 Afghan civilians.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. drone strike days later killed 10 Afghans, including seven children, mistaking them for terrorists.\par \par \uc2 President Biden had promised the evacuation would not look like the fall of Saigon.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was going to be no circumstance you're going to see people being lifted off the roof of a embassy in the -- of the United States from Afghanistan. It is not at all comfortable.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: A former marine corps sniper told Congress it was even worse.\par \par \uc2 SGT. TYLER VARGAS-ANDREWS: \uc2 UNITED STATES MARINE CORPS\uc2 : The withdrawal was a catastrophe in my opinion, and there was an inexcusable lack of accountability and negligence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Mississippi Senator Roger Wicker, the top Republican on the Armed Services Committee, called the withdrawal a "historic blunder", and added, in a functional administration, there would be many individuals held accountable for this failure. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Lucas Tomlinson, live on the North Lawn. Lucas, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will meet a billionaire who comes from Switzerland and is spending a whole lot of his money on American politics.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): The American journalist being held in Russia on spying charges was formally charged today and will get an appeal hearing, April 18th.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal report, \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  was arrested by the Russian government's \uc2 Federal Security Service\uc2  on charges of espionage on behalf of the U.S. The publication has vehemently denied the charges.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Production begins on the new 10-pound banknotes, featuring the portrait of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  says the new notes should enter circulation by the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): There are new questions tonight about the involvement of a Swiss-born billionaire in U.S. politics. Some of this concern his tax documents, and what they reportedly reveal.\par \par \uc2 Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot, reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): His name is Hansjorg Wyss. He was born in Bern, Switzerland, went to Harvard, stayed in the U.S. worked in a range of businesses. Now he's worth $5 billion.\par \par \uc2 He's also set up funds donating millions to nonprofit groups, which support environmental and social issues as well as political causes and candidates, mostly Democratic leaning.\par \par \uc2 According to U.S. election law, that's off limits to foreign nationals like this.\par \par \uc2 Experts say these nonprofits don't have to shed light on where the money is going, why it's called dark money.\par \par \uc2 SAURAV GHOSH, \uc2 CAMPAIGN LEGAL CENTER\uc2 : Do you have these groups spending these millions of dollars to try and elect certain candidates and oppose others. And they are completely opaque about where their money is coming from.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: The Wyss Group, telling Fox in part, "The Berger Action Fund and the Wyss Foundation have established strict policies prohibiting their funds being used for get out the vote activities, voter registration, or supporting or opposing candidates or political parties."\par \par \uc2 And the group receiving the cash tells us, "Every contribution is used in compliance with all guidelines, regulations and laws."\par \par \uc2 Still, there is a push to brand groups like the ones to which this contributes as political to get greater clarity on how the money moves. Election officials have mostly just been promising scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): With the growing use of dark money from all over the world, from mega donors on both sides of the aisle, experts tell us more scrutiny, more exposure is in order. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Greg Palkot live in London. Greg, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we examine what officials in Milwaukee call a bad batch of drugs has killed at least 18 people in just a few days. The opioids laced with fentanyl and animal tranquilizer have affected not just younger people but victims all the way up to 83 years old. Correspondent Garrett Tenney has that story tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They need to do more to stop these drugs getting into our community.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Officials in Milwaukee are begging the White House to do more to stop the flow of illegal drugs coming across the southern border after at least 18 overdose deaths in Milwaukee County in just four days.\par \par \uc2 AARON LIPSKI, MILWAUKEE FIRE CHIEF: The average age of 50 something, that's not the normal demographic of folks that we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Authorities believe the string of deaths are linked to a bad batch of street drugs that have been mixed with not just fentanyl but with an animal tranquilizer xylazine, which amplifies the effects of fentanyl. Xylazine not only makes drugs more potent and deadly, it's also known as the zombie drug because it can cause human tissue near the injection site to rot and require amputation. Because it's not an opioid, the often life- saving overdose drug Narcan doesn't work with xylazine.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA PARISH, MILWAUKEE FIRE DEPARTMENT: This is not just an opioid addiction problem. This is a problem that's present in every drug that we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: The DEA is sounding the alarm on xylazine, issuing a public safety alert on the sharp increase in the amount of fentanyl coming across the border that's been mixed with the drug, and warning, "Xylazine is making the deadliest drug threat our country has ever faced, fentanyl, even deadlier." Far from the border in Milwaukee, addiction recovery counselors are saying the same thing.\par \par \uc2 RYAN GORMAN, COMMUNITY MEDICAL SERVICES: When people are out seeking out substances, there could be anything in it, and it's really just like Russian roulette right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): In 2019 Milwaukee saw just one overdose death linked to xylazine. Last year it was 51. And Milwaukee is by no means alone. Other cities across the country are seeing similar trends. The DEA says nearly all the fentanyl powder it seized last year contained xylazine. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Garrett Tenney, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco police are searching for the person who fatally stabbed a tech executive early Tuesday. We have surveillance images of Bob Lee as he struggles following the attack. A warning, some of the images may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 This exclusive video was obtained by "The Daily Mail." It shows Lee as he staggers down Main Street searching for help but finding none. The 43-year- old tech innovator had moved away from San Francisco because of fears about crime. He was there this week for a visit.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "Whatever Happened To?" segment, the pursuit of biofuels. They were once thought to be the antidote to fossil fuel dependence, but not anymore. National correspondent William La Jeunesse shows us why this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first thing that comes to my mind is yield and cashflow.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Remember the biofuel revolution?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We'll be able to manufacture fuel for your automobiles from switch grass.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Kudzu, safflower, Camelina, switch grass, jatropha, and algae.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is about ready to harvest.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Would power our future.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We're making new investments in the development of gasoline and diesel and jet fuel that's actually made from a plant-like substance, algae.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Subsidized by government loans, grants, guarantees, and tax credits, business and academia gorged at the public trough.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's a simple concept, turn grass into gas.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Even the Navy said alternative fuels would power half its fleet by 2020.\par \par \uc2 RAY MABUS, FORMER NAVY SECRETARY: Every single ship is operating on alternative fuels.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Defense, Ag, Energy, the EPA, and more began handing out money.\par \par \uc2 SHON HIATT, UNIVERSITY OF SOUTHERN CALIFORNIA: Since 2005, we have spent about $330 billion on biofuel subsidies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Produce 10,000 barrels of algae biofuel a day.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Even \uc2 Exxon\uc2  recently gave up turning algae into fuel after investing $350 million.\par \par \uc2 HIATT: It's just not economically viable.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So what happened? Fracking. In the 18 years since the Energy Policy Act, biofuels still can't come feet with the price of oil.\par \par \uc2 STEVE ELLIS, TAXPAYERS FOR COMMON SENSE: At some point you have to say it is a failure and it's not working, and we are going to step away from it, and that just doesn't really occur.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: What the U.S. does produce is corn ethanol and mostly soybean-based biodiesel, which gets blended into every gallon of fuel sold.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): The Navy now tells us they have stopped using any biofuels, yet federal agencies still hand out about $200 million a year in subsidies on fuels many say don't make economic or environmental sense. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: William La Jeunesse, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the west is trying to chip away at China's dominance in the semiconductor industry. And in a few minutes, this week's "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The Chips Act is encouraging semiconductor companies to increase manufacturing here in the U.S. But to take on China, the Biden administration is looking elsewhere for assistance. Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier looks at some of those strategies and the importance of increasing semiconductor production around the world.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has taken his pitch to ramp up chips manufacturing to America's allies abroad. The U.S. wants to take on China alongside top producing countries in Asia and Europe while also increasing chips production across North America with Mexico and Canada. Tonight, we look at how strength in numbers could help the U.S. and its allies dominate the semiconductor industry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are stronger and better when we work together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This year, President Biden has traveled north and south in an effort to get U.S. neighbors Canada and Mexico on board with increasing semiconductor manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 , CANADIAN PRIME MINISTER: There's no doubt why it matters that we turn to each other now to build up a North American market on everything from semiconductors to solar panel batteries.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But getting Mexico involved has proved challenging. The U.S. and Canada want President \uc2 Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2  to dedicate more funding for clean energy that could potentially help run semiconductor fabrication plants.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: These investments are going to create jobs and opportunities for Mexican workers and businesses.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Obrador said he and U.S. climate envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  discussed a possible $5 billion investment in solar energy near Arizona. Mexican officials have said solar energy will help run Arizona's semiconductor hub across the border. Some manufacturers say renewable energy needs to increase overall to meet industry demands.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are operating internationally. What's the thing that keeps you up at night?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CAULFIELD\uc2 , \uc2 GLOBALFOUNDRIES\uc2  PRESIDENT AND CEO: There's a lot of things that keep us up at night. It's always the things that are outside of our control. I think energy and the availability of electrical power is an issue for our industry. Our industry uses a lot of energy, and we need to lead the effort to make sure we are using it from greener sources over time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One country is causing concerns for nations around the world.\par \par \uc2 China, how big a deal is it for you as a national security threat to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): The biggest.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last year the U.S. adopted sweeping measures to hinder China's ability to make its own chips. But for those restrictions to be effective, other countries needed to implement similar rules. U.S. officials wanted a top European producer in the Netherlands and a leading Asian producer in Japan to join in.\par \par \uc2 JOHN NEUFFER\uc2 , \uc2 SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY ASSOCIATION\uc2  CEO: If we try to put controls on ourselves, our non-Chinese competitors can fill in the holes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Both countries reportedly agreed to join the U.S. effort but little is known about specific details.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We talked about a lot of things, but a lot of it is private.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: China is the fifth leading producer in the world. South Korea, Japan, and the U.S. all make more. Beijing's aggression toward top manufacturer Taiwan is creating concerns around the globe.\par \par \uc2 Do you think China is closer to moving on Taiwan now?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, I think it's closer than we have thought in previous years. They make the best microelectronics in the world, the best semiconductor chips. If we lose access to that, the estimate that is in a couple years, we would a lose up to 10 percent of our GDP.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 TODD YOUNG\uc2 , (R-IN): Chips are arguably more valuable in today's economy than hydrocarbons. The Chinese are trying to weaponize our economic interdependence with their country. We can't allow them to do that with a mission critical component like a semiconductor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some companies design their chips and have them manufactured in Taiwan, making China's intentions toward that island nation all the more concerning.\par \par \uc2 You have seen China be increasingly aggressive toward Taiwan. There is a semiconductor element to that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX): Absolutely. The reason they make them there is because it's about 30 percent cheaper to make Taiwan as opposed to the United States. So it's a great business model for them. But it's a huge national security and economic vulnerability for the United States.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some manufacturers are also cutting ties to China. \uc2 GlobalFoundries\uc2  decided against opening a fabrication plant there in 2018.\par \par \uc2 CAULFIELD: Part of that pivot of the company was to abandon our plans to expand manufacturing in China.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think the rest of the industry is where you are in thought process when it comes to China and the world picture?\par \par \uc2 CAULFIELD: I think it's a reality. As business leaders, we don't get to set the rules of the playing field. But it's our job to deal with those issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most lawmakers and experts believe staying a step ahead of China when it comes to semiconductors is crucial.\par \par \uc2 Is there enough to turn this ship around as far as the U.S. production of this critical piece of technology?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 , (D-CA): Bret, it's a start. The bulk of the production for the chips that go into our consumer products, our cars, our dishwashers, our dryers, our refrigerators are still not produced in the United States. And I don't want a situation where the advanced chips are made in America but everything consumers buy is made in China. So we need to do a lot more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Demand for semiconductors will only increase as new technology develops. We'll continue to follow U.S. efforts to ramp up production here and, once again, try to become a leader in the chip industry. It's a big deal across the world.\par \par \uc2 Mike, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Bret, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, Reuters reports a federal judge has stayed federal regulators approval of the abortion pill mifepristone. The judge partially granting a request by pro-life groups and dealing another setback to pro- choice groups. The ruling allows the Biden administration one week to appeal the decision.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round, the possibility of U.S. troops in Taiwan, President Biden's problems with crime and the economy, and later, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SETH MOULTON\uc2 , (D-MA) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: Clearly, they did not start this evacuation soon enough. Both administrations should take responsibility here, including the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: You can't now come back and try to remake this in front of the American people. Our allies saw it. They felt humiliated by the United States. And our adversaries have been taking advantage of us.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. DANIEL DAVIS, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  (RET): There was a lot of good things about the way the U.S. military handled this, but, man, there's lots of things that were bad that need to be called out. And to just basically whitewash all that I think is a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A lot of tension between Washington and Beijing as a bipartisan congressional delegation led by Chairman Michael McCaul is on the ground in Taiwan. Our colleague Aishah Hasnie is there. And one of the questions Aishah asked Chairman McCaul about was getting arms to Taiwan. Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think we are prepared were this to happen in the next five years?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R-TX) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I sign off on all foreign military weapons sales. I can tell you we have a problem with our defense industrial base. These weapons that I've signed off on to Taiwan, 22 weapon sales, I signed off three years ago. And they have yet to go into country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: With that, let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and FOX News chief legal correspondent and anchor of FOX NEWS SUNDAY Shannon Bream. She is also the author of the new book "The Love Stories of the Bible Speak, Biblical Lessons on Romance, Friendship, and Faith." We look forward to reading that. Panel, welcome.\par \par \uc2 So, Shannon, let's start with you. We have got a bipartisan delegation in Taiwan a lot of tension between us and Beijing, and then Chairman McCaul saying we have a problem getting weapons where we need them in a hurry.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, and that's been one of the critical things that people talk about Ukraine and say we should have had things in earlier there. We knew it was coming. But now we are spread even more thinly. As he said years ago some of these things were approved and, yet, they haven't gotten to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 There have been studies here raising the red flag, raising the alarm that our inventories are running low on hardware, on artillery, and that there are key missiles, by one estimation, that if we got into some kind of incursion involving China in that region, we'd be out of them in a week. But I think that shocks people, but it seems to be the reality.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, where are you as we look at tension between the U.S. and Beijing? We had high profile meeting in California. We have a bipartisan delegation on the ground in Taiwan. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I understand why President Tsai was here, and I understand why Speaker McCarthy would meet with her out at the Reagan Library. I understand why Chairman McCaul is also meeting with her, and I understand why \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  visited, actually went to Taiwan and visited with her. All of these people have a strong degree of passion in saying that we, as the United States, have a promise, a treaty with Taiwan that says we will defend them and we will protect them against China having a kind of incursion against a sovereign state, the kind of which we have seen Russia do against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But I just, you know what, Mike, as we come to the end of this week, I just think everybody needs to slow down. We're talking about World War III. We're talking about taking on China, not Russia. We already, as Chairman McCaul was indicating, give a great deal of money to Taiwan. We already, in fact, offer weapons to Taiwan, including sanctions against China. It's not going to be like putting sanctions against Russia. It's very different. China has a great investment in the U.S. This would be, I don't know if the word "cataclysmic" is appropriate, but it would be tumultuous. It would disrupt America. And McCaul is even saying there is a possibility we would send American troops. I don't know about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think when you listen to the people who know the most about the region, particularly I would advise people to listen to Elbridge Colby who has written an entire book about deterrence in the region to the former ambassador to Japan, \uc2 Bill Hagerty\uc2 , now the senator, obviously, and to \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , who is the head of the bipartisan China focused committee here. They will all say that what we need to be doing now is the things that we didn't do in advance of Russia's invasion of Ukraine. We need to be making the porcupine have as many spines as possible.\par \par \uc2 And I think that when that comes to Taiwan, unfortunately, what we've really learned over the past several months is that we are behind the curve when it comes to munitions, when it comes to being able to supply them the kind of weapons that they need to deter China. And look, whether you think of China as being the strong entity that a lot of people think or whether you buy into the idea that some other people say that it's going to be a nation that is collapsing in certain ways, both driven by the consequences of one child policy and others as well, it certainly is very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And I think that as Juan sort of sounding the note of let's pump the breaks, we don't want to get into World War III. I think the strongest thing that would lead to something like that is weakness in this moment, and I think that we need to make sure that Taiwan is as strong of an entity as possible at this point so that China reconsiders what it might go and do in the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk domestic politics and try to get around the horn pretty quickly. Juan, another big story today, the president is in Nashville after two African American lawmakers were kicked out of the state legislature there. We had Brit on earlier. I want to get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think it's outrageous what's been done here by the Republican legislature. I think it's self-defeating for Republicans because I think it makes them look bad. But the point is that those two legislators represent real people. And now those people in big cities, Memphis and Nashville don't have representation until special elections are held. That's just not rational. Yes, they broke the rules. But you've got to have some margin of consideration for the actual voters, for the idea that they are people who want a voice in their state legislature.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, a couple poll numbers I want to point to. CNN poll, how would you rate the economic conditions in the country today? Very, somewhat good, 29 percent, very, somewhat poor 71 percent. Same CNN poll, do you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  deserves to be reelected or not, 32 percent yes, 67 percent no. Both have to be alarming to the White House, Ben.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: They absolutely do. And I think that one of the things the White House has really missed in this moment is how much they need to be leaning in and changing the argument about the economy, because that drives things more than anything else. And I, frankly, think that they have been meandering ever since this midterm that went well for them in terms of good fortune, but they certainly have not righted the ship from the perspective of --\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Nearly out of time, Shannon, but I want to tap into your expertise about this judge's ruling on this abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, a federal judge out of Texas has said that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of one of the two drugs that are used in medicated abortions, which is more than half of the abortions performed out there, that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  did not approve the drug properly. So he is saying it's got to leave the market. Now, he is putting a seven day stay on this so this can be appealed to the next level, so we'll see.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: I have a sneaking suspicion we will hear about that on FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: On Easter Sunday. We look forward to that. Thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday. That means it's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2 , (D) MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was arraigned on 34 felony counts of falsifying business records.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , who campaigned on the fact that he would get President Trump. I'm going to get him. I'm going to get him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This thing has a feeling of like a legal Slurpee. It's instantly satisfying but has no nutritional value.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is the indictment of your predecessor politically divisive?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The friendship between the people of Taiwan and America is a matter of profound importance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are stronger when we are together.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: The president's decision to end the war in Afghanistan was the right one.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Who is going to get fired over this?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The purpose of it is not accountability. The purpose of it is --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Military leaders --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The purpose of it is to study lessons learned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One of the challenges under like earlier opioids is we have got to find a way to interdict sooner in the process.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to give people hope. The joy, the absolute joy in long-term recovery.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: I'm going to tell Joe I think Iowa should come too. Winner and losers, that's sportsmanship.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is my Augusta National tie.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you do your whole show in a golf whisper?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There will be a golf clap.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All in one week in American politics.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we will begin a new series on the rise of artificial intelligence. We will look at whether A.I. is a force for good or bad in education.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Ben Cardin.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. To all celebrating this weekend, a very happy Easter, and to our Jewish friends a happy Passover.\par \par \uc2 I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hello, Jesse, a few seconds early.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230408ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Debate Around Legal Merits Of Trump Charges; Unsealed Trump Indictment Raising Legal Questions; Impact Of Trump Legal Issues On 2024 Race; Speaker McCarthy Defies China, Meets Taiwan Leader; Blinken: No Doubt Wall Street Journal Reporter Wrongfully Detained; United States, Allies Push Russia To Release WSJ Reporter; Biden Faces New Test From Putin With Reporter Arrest; Former Biden Aide Questioned Over Role In Doc Scandal; Tornadoes Tear Through Parts Of The Midwest; Cash App Founder Stabbed To Death In San Francisco; Speaker McCarthy Defies China, Meets Taiwan Leader; Liberals Wins Control Of Wisconsin's Supreme Court; The "Podcasting Priest" On Shift In U.S. Values\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jeanine Pirro, Kellyanne Conway, Greg Palkot,  Joe Concha, Claudia Cowan, Bret Baier \par \uc2 8,787 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: To get historic pieces of legislation done. When you think about the infrastructure legislation, something that, you know, we heard many times during the last administration, infrastructure week, infrastructure week is what this President was able to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes, that's what the President was able to do. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still claims the new cycle.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: He does.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: He does. Thanks for joining us. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We will see you again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm Gillian Turner. "THE STORY" with Martha MacCallum starts now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Thank you very much, John and Gillian. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum breaking right now on THE STORY, brand new legal reaction pouring in around the indictment of former President Trump, criticism for Manhattan's D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  coming from some unlikely places.\par \par \uc2 This popular piece that is widely read today over at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  calls the indictment a dangerous leap from the, on the highest of wires debatable at best, unnervingly flimsy at worst. And here's former Deputy \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 , you remember -- him a harsh Trump critic and a long, with a long and storied history with the former president. Watch what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCABE\uc2 , FORMER FBI DEPUTY DIRECTOR: That indictment landed like, like a dud, right? Commentators across the spectrum are saying: boy, there's really not much in here, raises all kinds of questions about the legal theory behind this case. He's going to -- they're going to have a tough time facing motions to dismiss an unimpressive document.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So interesting, right, Kellyanne Conway and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , on the politics of all this, but first legal analysis from Judge Jeanine Pirro, Co-Host of "THE FIVE." Judge Jeanine, great to have you here today. You know, so, you know, a day into this, you look at it, you go, well, what's he doing? Does he, is there a method to this madness or is it just madness?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You know, a day into it, you wake up and you say, you know, maybe I missed something. And then, you look at the indictment again, and you say, no, he missed something. He would not, that means D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  would not identify this second crime, why? Why not illuminate what it is? Why not prosecute it on its own? Why hide it from the rest of us? What is he afraid of?\par \par \uc2 And when he has asked the question directly comes back with, well, you know, we've got more testimony, and it couldn't be the New York State election law. He doesn't reference a federal crime. So, what we have here is a, is an indictment that for the most, I think, the first 20 counts are beyond the statute of limitations on its face. Facially, this is insufficient. Factually, it isn't sufficient. We need a bill of particulars to tell us what it is.\par \par \uc2 So, what I'm thinking in my mind is like everyone else, and this is what I did for a living, this is my wheelhouse for 32 years. What I'm thinking is there's got to be a reason for him not to identify it. Is it because maybe he's worried about the sufficiency of the indictment? And if he doesn't really identify it, maybe then Georgia could continue to prosecute that crime, and not haven't been barred on the basis of double jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 I don't know. But what I do know is that the stuff that everyone admits to on the left, on the right, in the middle, and the agnostics is that this is a piece of garbage -- it means nothing. And to drag a president, a former president of the United States, into the bowels of the criminal courts in New York City, is a horrendous act unless you have something, at least identifiable -- you cannot do that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I haven't even gotten into the issue of intent. How were you going to show what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's intent was, former President Trump? This is a bookkeeping offense. We don't bring presidents down on that kind of thing. But the amazing thing is when you've got one party indicting another party, and this is Mark, and Kellyanne's (INAUDIBLE), it stinks to high heaven even in a criminal courtroom.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, here's what he said yesterday, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , when he was asked, you know, your predecessors turned this, this down. So, why are you basically putting your whole professional career out here on a limb on this thing? Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your predecessor took a hard look at this case; he decided not to charge it. Federal prosecutors took a hard look at this case and decided not to charge it. You believe you have new evidence that led you to decide to charge this? Or, why now?\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2  (D), MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: As I just mentioned, we have had available to the office, additional evidence, that was not in the office this possession prior to my time here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does he look confident to you in that?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think so either.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The fact that he doesn't identify directly, you know, you have to think back what is new. The only thing that happened recently is they brought back David Pecker from AMI after Bob Costello, the attorney, who said \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who is at the heart of this case. I don't even need to go back there.\par \par \uc2 That you could try to prosecute a former president on the grounds and the, and the credibility of a perjurer who has perjured himself to Congress in courtrooms is, is, is beyond what any of us in the criminal justice system have seen. This is an embarrassment. He wants to be the first. I can't say if that's the reason, but there's no other reason for this.\par \par \uc2 This is not a case to prosecute, but I'll tell you what it does do. What it does do is it identifies that this is a man who, as he said last night, is a man who has been hunted for six years, seven years. And this flimsy indictment, which is nothing more than a piece of paper, is the cream on top of the cake, that fizzles in everyone's mind.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, the question of federal election law, right, I mean, it's also raised by this case, because there's an impact on running for president.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With this indictment around you as well. So, is that a violation of federal election law?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: This could be a violation of federal election law. Let's assume it is. He doesn't have the jurisdiction to try it. I was a district attorney. If I called the U.S. Attorney in the Southern District of New York and said: hey, it's Jeanine, don't worry about it, I got this federal crime covered. They'd come in and indict me for something.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I mean, you -- that's not done. Everything as it relates to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is outside the rules.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This Justice Merchan, what's your read on him? Because he was in the read through and in the reporting, respectful of the former president in that room yesterday? I mean, he has an opportunity here. He could say, you know what, it's too late. There's not enough here for what you're doing, the magnitude of this is too large and you don't have this stuff, will he?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, not now, but there will be a time to make motions. And I thought that he was very calm yesterday. And the fact that he did not issue a gag order, which would have been found unconstitutional. I think he may be worried too, based upon his reaction, and his experience previously, where Bragg thought he had the right judge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely, and he's had two successful prosecutions of \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . So, does he really want this on his record if he doesn't have the confidence in what Bragg has brought to him? He has that opportunity. So, justice, thank you so much. Great to see you, Judge Jeanine Pirro, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's bring in Kellyanne Conway, former Senior Counselor to President Trump, now President of K.A. Consulting, and a Fox News Contributor; and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton Advisor and Pollster and Chairman of the \uc2 Harris Poll\uc2 . Great to have both of you with us. So, Kellyanne, to you first -- you know, let me put up a couple of polls here, because, they're not your polls, but let's take a look at them.\par \par \uc2 This one actually comes from CNN SSRS: Independence, on whether on the role of politics in this indictment, 76 percent of independents say, yes, politics is clearly playing a role here. But here's the scary part, in the, in the next one, Independents approve 62 percent of the decision to indict. So, they're saying, yes, it's political, but I also think that it was the right thing to do. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, they know the threshold for indictment is fairly low. But Martha, I think that first poll is particularly advisable for each of us, because independents will decide who the next President of the United States is. They will carry the day in November 2024. And they look at this as political more than legal. All the polls say that very clearly. And in that 76 percent, CNN poll of independents saying that politics played a "major role," 52 percent said strongly so.\par \par \uc2 And we as pollsters are always looking for that intensity, I can tell you, it's very difficult to find almost any kind of three-fourths of the country to agree on anything, let alone 52 percent strongly so. I think there's something else going on here. That if it's a weak case, legally, if you see a former president in the United States, and may I say, we're not hearing much from the living former presidents in the United States either, not hearing much from the Clintons, the Obamas, the Bushes. You know, hurray, they finally got Trump and this would never happen to us.\par \par \uc2 Everybody's eerily quiet because they know this sets a dangerous precedent. But I think the reason that Trump is higher in the polls right now, even people I know who said before I'm \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I'm moving on from him, I want to look for someone else. They're saying, I want someone that tough. I want someone that resilient. I want someone that, you know, that sort of -- that firm.\par \par \uc2 But here's what's happening, a lot of voters are saying, please stop \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , the mainstream media, whoever you are, the January 6th, whoever you are, please stop robbing me of one of my choices. I may not end up voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they're saying, but you are removing the landscape of choices for me, and I should at least have that choice. Last thing, if I were a victim of a, God-forbid of robbery in New York City under \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , I might come forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I might surface myself and say you let that person go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very disturbing, very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: You let that person go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If I may, thank you. Just standby if you can, Mark and Kellyanne, we're going to go to this Taiwan President, \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen, who is speaking now with the Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy at the Reagan Library in California, big moment. Let's watch this for a second.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TSAI ING-WEN, PRESIDENT OF TAIWAN: Speaker McCarthy, for you a warmth, and for your friendship. Thank you to all our friends in Congress, for standing by Taiwan. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ING-WEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that's the Reagan Library. You see that Air Force One in the backdrop there, obviously, a big moment, as the President of Taiwan is greeted by the Speaker of the House. We know \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  went to Taiwan when she was Speaker of the House just several months back and it's always an important, not just a gesture, but an important meaningful moment to recognize that our relationship with Taiwan supports their democracy. And currently, the One China policy as well. Let me go back to \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . Mark, you want to give us your quick thought, as you look at that, especially given the escalating tensions with China. And clearly, this is going to be a big issue in the coming election.\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON ADVISER AND POLLSTER: Well, look, I think that the Biden administration is trying to show it's a little tougher on China and so, by having this meeting and having Air Force One and the President there. I think that it is just, it's just that -- it, it says to China, hey, we're going to go ahead and continue to recognize, you know, Taiwan as we have, and we're not going to be bullied by what you say. And I think that's going to be very important in this upcoming election. Because as you know, people think China now is not a friend, possibly an enemy. And they're really unsure where China falls and what its ambitions really are in the world stage.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark, sticking with you for a moment, I want to get your reaction to the first question to Kellyanne in terms of the political implications for Democrats and for other Republicans of this indictment of President Trump yesterday?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, look, I think the implications for the country are sad. Someone has got to stop the politicization of the justice system. I think the implications for Trump, it's revived his campaign. He's raising money, he's the center of attention. And I think for Democrats, it doesn't do anything one way or the other, except that I think Biden is consolidating the notion that he's actually going to run for a second term. And I think that's the 1-2-3 of this.\par \par \uc2 If you're \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , you're wondering, how do you get back in the ring in a way that you don't want to support this indictment, but you do want to replace \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? And that's the person today who's trying to figure out his political maneuvering.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, that, that's a huge question. You know, if you're anybody, if you're Nikki Haley, or others who are considering getting in, like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Kellyanne, I guess you also know, as those candidates that they're going to be, there's going to be a lot more of this, there's going to be the Jack Smith investigation, there's going to be the Georgia investigation. So, how do you play this as another Republican candidate?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: You know, Martha and Mark, it's so fascinating to me that there's no candidate out there either announced or presumed, who's figured this out the best way that they're going to be asked about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more than they're going to be asked about their own platform, their own policy positions, their own record of accomplishments. They need to find a way to answer this quickly and get out of it, and pivot toward why they would take on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think, the cardinal mistake that was made for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , whether he made it or people around him, he allowed himself in the last five months to be seen as the alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . From the beginning, he should have said on the alternative to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and he should have taken on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  one on one, and made Trump a little bit more of a footnote.\par \par \uc2 The problem now is Trump will never be a footnote again. If you look at the top 10 trending stories, usually eight or nine, say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and the other one about a different Republican candidate is then being asked about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Someone has to figure this out, and nobody has mastered it yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Mark, what, what about what do you say about that, because we saw the same thing in 2016 and in 2020 to a great extent as well, anybody who's compared to the former president gets asked more about him, they'd love to talk about their own policies and what they would do, but they don't always get that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Look, I think DeSantis has got to run on character and competence. And I think that he's gotten off and run on conservative values in an attempt to get, you know, Trump voters, and he's been very unsuccessful that, he's stalled in the polls since he did that. He's got an ability here to differentiate on character. Look, he's not going to be as you pointed out, involved in these kinds of payments, because that's not his character. That's where he has an edge, and he's got to get back to it if he wants a chance to win this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a long road. And there's going to be a lot of turns in it between now and the nomination process. Thank you both, Kellyanne Conway and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , great to see you. Thanks for being here today. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Biden administration is upping its rhetoric against Russia on this very important issue of the American reporter for The Wall Street Journal who has been wrongfully detained. They're working on that status in the state department.\par \par \uc2 Bret Baier weighs in on that. And Kevin McCarthy's visit with the Thai president. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , taking questions from a Wall Street Journal reporter today about her colleague, Evan Gershkovich, about what is being done to secure his freedom from a Russian prison cell. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: In Evan's case, we are working through the determination on wrongful detention. And there's a process to do that, and it's something that we're working through very deliberately.\par \par \uc2 But expeditiously as well. In my own mind, there is no doubt that he is being wrongfully detained by Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lawyers for Gershkovich were able to meet with him in his cell.\par \par \uc2 They say he is quoting good health, and that he's grateful for the outpouring of support from around the world.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Special Report anchor Bret Baier weighs in on this and the meeting between Speaker McCarthy and the president of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 But we go first to senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot on this story.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Hi, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the 31-year-old American journalist has now into his second week of Russian captivity. Remember, Evan Gershkovich was charged by the Russians with espionage, which he and others, of course, deny. That carries a sentence of up to 20 years in jail, starting with a two-month free trial detention.\par \par \uc2 Yes, his lawyers finally got to see him at his bleak Moscow prison. The best news probably is that he's in good health. He said to be held alone in a so-called quarantine cell. No word yet, when U.S. embassy staff can get access.\par \par \uc2 And also, as you noted, no word either when Washington will give his arrest that wrongful detention label that will give officials more clout to get him free.\par \par \uc2 The White House, again, today said, the reporter's released is a priority and they are working on it.\par \par \uc2 With international pressure building, U.S. Ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . \uc2 Linda Thomas-Greenfield\uc2  spoke to her Russian counterpart there. There are reports the issue could be raised at the Security Council, interesting as Russia heads up that body this month.\par \par \uc2 And as for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his government, they are not budging. A spokesperson saying they'll ignore current appeals for Gershkovich's released.\par \par \uc2 Putin telling a group of diplomats, including new American ambassador to Russia, Lynne Tracy that U.S.-Russia relations are in a deep crisis.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Martha, to mark one week since Evan Gershkovich's arrest, the Wall Street Journal called for and got what it called a social media store, but tweets using the #IStandWithEvan, a very good reason to go viral. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Greg, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in "SPECIAL REPORT" anchor, Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Bret, we just heard the secretary of state, talking about the fact that they're working on this wrongful detention designation for Evan Gershkovich.\par \par \uc2 That's something that has to come through our State Department. And that sort of triggers the ability to make this a stronger priority. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): That is right. And it's an official designation. It really shouldn't take that long.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state could have just said we're making it happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it's going to happen very quickly. I think he was getting there. But, you know, what's interesting here, first of all, the concern about this reporter, and we should say, it's our colleague in the -- in the broad umbrella, and it is good to hear that he's in good spirits and in good health.\par \par \uc2 But he is being detained, wrongfully detained. And there are more than 30 Americans around the world who are being wrongfully detained.\par \par \uc2 And the critics of this administration say that they really made things tougher around the world to get these people back home in the negotiation about \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner, and the trade with the convicted arms trafficker, Viktor Bout.\par \par \uc2 By doing it that way -- in that way, the critics would say that, that opened the door to different governments doing this again and holding people with the thought that the administration was going to do other negotiations.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Here is John Ratcliffe, the former DNI on that. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN RATCLIFFE, FORMER DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: When we said at the time, this is exchanging, you know, this is a short-term feel-good publicity win in exchange for a long-term bad precedent and a loss that would only encourage Russian bad behavior in this regard.\par \par \uc2 And I think we're seeing that in connection with Evan Gershkovich.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, we've seen trades before, but it does feel like it's accelerating. And \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  was asked if the president had spoken with his family.\par \par \uc2 She said, I don't have anything to read out -- any call to read out on that. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, the thing is, is that there's also the former U.S. Marine, Paul Whelan, being held in Russia. And his family is still very concerned about. And obviously, everyone is still concerned about getting him back. But they're not getting progress there as they're also trying to focus on this now with Evan.\par \par \uc2 So, it is complicated. And dealing with the Russians is difficult. But perhaps, you know, some of the criticism is that they made it more difficult.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And more complicated in the middle of the Ukraine war as well. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 I want to get your thoughts on -- we are working on turning around a piece of sound from Speaker McCarthy, but I don't think we have it yet.\par \par \uc2 With this appearance at the Reagan Library with the president of Taiwan. I said the Thai president on the way to the break, it's the president of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 You know, obviously, this is a very important moment for the president of Taiwan to show that solidarity that show China that we stand side by side. Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I think it is a big moment. And just the images of the Speaker there meeting the president of Taiwan at the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library, which is, obviously -- that historic spot that's iconic in the images. Especially, Air Force One in the main pavilion there.\par \par \uc2 But, and you're seeing shake the hand of John Heubusch, the head of the Ronald Reagan Library, and presidential library there.\par \par \uc2 It's a message. Right? And remember, all of the congressional delegations, including House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who went to Taiwan and the Chinese went ballistic about that visit.\par \par \uc2 Well, they're doing so with this visit as well. And I think it sends a message of unity with Taiwan. And there is probably going to have to be more of that because the threats from China on Taiwan are increasing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Bret, thank you very much. We'll keep a close eye on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us today. We'll see you tonight on "SPECIAL REPORT".\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a former executive assistant to Vice President Biden involved in the movement of documents from the White House to the Penn Biden Center, testifying that she was notified about retrieving boxes in May of 2022.\par \par \uc2 But the timeline for this story doesn't start until six months later. So, what happened between May? And then, who told her to move the boxes at that point and why?\par \par \uc2 Joe Concha, live from New York after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Congressman, yesterday you talked to President Biden's former executive system with links to -- with links to Hunter about how those documents got into the Penn Center and others.\par \par \uc2 You know, who is picking up stuff and who wasn't? What did you find out?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: House Republicans say that they have startling new information that undermines White House claims about how they handled classified documents. It comes as a former aide to Vice President Biden, who apparently got the job after being recommended for it by \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Kathy Chung has now spoken to the House Oversight Committee. Here's Chairman James Comer about that discussion with Kathy Chung on Fox and Friends.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY), HOUSE OVERSIGHT CMTE CHAIRMAN: What we learned was that these documents were transported from the vice presidency from his office to the private sector in private cars. They went to at least three different locations initially, one of which was in Chinatown, the other of which was in the Penn Biden center, which we're finding more and more evidence that it was almost exclusively funded by China and there was no lock on the door. All the things that the special prosecutor is apparently going to try to indict trump on mishandling classified documents, Biden did, and he did it about ten times worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe concert is here, media and politics columnist for "The Hill" and a Fox News contributor. Great to have you here, Joe. I want to skip forward here, control room two, call for number four, which is a flashback quote from "the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " with regard to Kathy Chung. And the headline was inside the White House document strategy and its pitfalls. And this is about the Biden situation.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of these classified document stories floating around, but in this one about Biden, it says, Kathy Chung has confided to associates that she's distressed that she might have inadvertently been involved in moving or storing classified material at the center, planting the seeds of the current uproar. So what do you make of that, Joe, and about what we just heard from James Comer?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's one thing for James Comer, right, or anybody on the Republican side in oversight to make an allegation against the President, right? It'll look partisan on some level, even though in many cases with Comer, he's actually getting receipts now with the Biden bank records, for instance. When it's Kathy Chung who worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , well, that is a whole different ballgame now, because that's coming from inside of his circle.\par \par \uc2 And the key point here is that she said that she may have inadvertently moved around these documents. We're talking to three different places, including a location in Chinatown at the Penn Biden center, places that don't resemble Fort Knox in any way, shape or form, more like Fort Wilderness and \uc2 Disney\uc2 , where I just came back from.\par \par \uc2 And she says that she discovered these in May of 2022, five months before the midterms. We didn't learn about these documents at all being in Biden's possession until after the votes were counted. Coincidence? You decide.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, and it all -- it comes into sharper focus now because we know that we all watched this indictment yesterday. One of the other cases which has been reported on quite a bit lately is that they're working on a potential other indictment from the Department of Justice against the former president for obstruction in how he dealt with his documents. So we're going to see a lot of this, you know, one side did, what the other side did is one worse than the other situation.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And Martha, look at it from a macro level. Look at it from a level of somebody who doesn't watch cable news or reads the newspaper every day. They hear these general stories. OK, so President Biden took documents when he was vice president, even as a senator, when he shouldn't have, and was in possession of them in multiple locations. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as president, apparently took these documents that were also classified and kept them at Mar-a-Lago. So what's the difference between Trump and Biden or even a \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who had classified documents as well?\par \par \uc2 It seems that a lot of people in government at high levels are doing this. So is this therefore a crime where Trump gets charged but Biden or anybody else who was involved doesn't? At a macro general, common sense level, it's hard to see how this wouldn't be a complete and total situation where we have one tier of justice for one side and another tier for another, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's interesting because when the former president spoke at Mar-a-Lago last night, he talked about this and he said that as a senator or a vice president, you don't have the same ability to declassify or to hold on to documents as you do as a president. Now, that's the legal argument that they're going to have to make.\par \par \uc2 But he -- and it's been said by other people as well that to take things as a senator we remember Sandy Berger who, you know, stuck things in his socks and I think in his pants all over the place in order to get them out of there, you know? So, I mean, why did he have stuff as a senator from all those years ago is a big question.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Right. There are skiffs. You're not supposed to bring it out of those places, right? The bottom line is that we have now an indictment against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in New York. It's trump Arraignment Syndrome in the media. But if this indictment fails, as so many legal analysts even on other networks are saying, then you're going to go after him for another thing and another thing, this will bring fatigue upon the American people and it will look like weaponization and politicization of our Justice Department, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's a lot of it, that's for sure. Thank you very much, Joe. Great to see you as always. Welcome back to Disney World.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Oh, yes. I'll be working a third job now. Be barking nights on weekends, to pay for thing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, still ahead, Father Mike Schmitz pays a visit with us, really excited about this. He is host of the hugely popular podcast number one, number two on \uc2 Apple\uc2 . Also does the Bible in a Year podcast. So we're going to talk to you about what's going on with this decline in Americans faith and patriotism and wanting to have kids as we also follow closely the breaking news today out of San Francisco. What a story this is. Powerful tech executive reportedly the victim of a fatal stabbing last night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You can hear it. You can see it. That is a stunning image of a very destructive tornado that moved its way through Iowa. A storm chaser spotting the funnel clouds south of Des Moines. The National Weather Service confirms a twister touchdown in that area yesterday. No word, thankfully, of injuries or how bad the damage is.\par \par \uc2 But boy, that is a stunning, stunning image of a fully formed tornado. But look at the devastation in Glenallen, Missouri, where at least four people lost their lives. Homes were flattened. It hit around 3:30 in the morning. Imagine this in the dark, in the middle of the night, at your home. Rescue crews are out there now looking for people who may be trapped in these completely destroyed homes.\par \par \uc2 And this stunning story of a tech executive, he is the man who created the Cash app, but he has a long history in technology in San Francisco. Bob Lee was stabbed to death at the age of 43. A friend says that it looks like this was a random attack and the killer is still on the loose out there. Senior correspondent Claudia Cowan reporting live from Sausalito, California. Hi, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, as you might imagine, this murder is sending shockwaves through the tech community and comes as the city is reaching out to the feds for help. Police say a man was stabbed multiple times at around 2:30 yesterday morning near some high rise condos in the south of market neighborhood and died at the hospital. The killer got away and police have not identified a suspect.\par \par \uc2 Multiple sources, including Mayor \uc2 London Breed\uc2 , say the victim was 43-year- old Bob Lee, being remembered today as a loving father, generous mentor and tech pioneer. According to his father, Lee recently moved to Miami. He was the founder of Cash app and worked for \uc2 Google\uc2 , Square, and MobileCoin, a cryptocurrency startup in San Francisco. That company's CEO was among the first to confirm \uc2 Lee\uc2 's death online. Joshua Goldbard saying, quote, one of the things that made him truly special was his capacity to dream big and to summon those big, crazy dreams into our world.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  also sharing his condolences, adding, quote, violent crime in SF is horrific, and even if attackers are caught, they are often released immediately. In a statement this morning, Mayor Breed expressed confidence that when police make an arrest, quote, our district attorney will do what's necessary to hold any individuals accountable.\par \par \uc2 Now, this homicide coming just weeks after Breed asked for help from the U.S. Attorney, saying the city's police force is understaffed and struggling to tackle the rampant drug dealing plaguing San Francisco. Police are now combing through surveillance video, talking to witnesses and asking that anyone with any information reach out to them. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What a tragedy. Thank you very much. Claudia Cowan in Sausalito.\par \par \uc2 So, as we mentioned, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy speaking alongside the president of Taiwan just a short time ago. They just met with lawmakers from both parties at the Reagan library in California, even after China warned that it would retaliate if this meeting took place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: His belief in democracy and commitment to the ideas of peace and freedom. Those values have always served as the bedrock of our friendship with the people of Taiwan. And they are more important now than ever before. I believe our bond is stronger now than at any time or point in my lifetime. And, of course, President Tsai is a great champion of that bond. Taiwan is a successful democracy, a thriving economy, and a global leader in health and science.\par \par \uc2 And whether it's our deep commercial ties, strong people to people relationships, our shared values, our cooperation with the people of Taiwan continue to expand through dialogue and exchange. The friendship between the people of Taiwan and America is a matter of profound importance to the free world. And it is critical to maintain economic freedom, peace and regional stability. We will honor our obligations and reiterate our commitment to our shared values, behind which all Americans are united.\par \par \uc2 Today was a bipartisan meeting. Republicans and Democrats united together in a place that symbolizes the freedom and the commitment and the bond that it's only become stronger with the President with us today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so the House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  was not there today. He says he met with Taiwan's president last week in New York City about security and economic issues and, quote, our shared commitment to democracy and freedom, obviously a very important meeting with the tensions between the United States and China.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats pulling off a huge win in Wisconsin last night, taking control of the state's Supreme Court for the first time in 15 years. Abortion was a big issue in this race. So what does it mean for politics, for Democrats and Republicans as we head into 2024? Special Report anchor, Bret Baier, back with us on this really big story. You know, Bret, it's always interesting to watch these elections that happen in between the cycles as a tea leaves check in with where things may be headed.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, this is a big deal. Scott Walker talked about this with Bill Hemmer yesterday, about what it would mean if this Supreme Court justice, who is more liberal, fair to say was elected last night. Here he is. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT WALKER (R), FORMER WISCONSIN GOVERNOR: Every reform we fought for, whether it was Act Ten, the big union reforms, and changes in collective bargaining, changes in terms of requiring a photo ID, congressional redistricting, which could affect not just Wisconsin, but potentially who the speaker is after the 2024 election, and particularly public safety in this state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is a state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won in the presidential election in 2016 and Biden in 2020. It's obviously one to watch, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, I mean, Wisconsin, Martha, is the swingiest of swing states. And you look at the recent elections, statewide elections, they go back and forth. And they're usually super, super tight. This was not that tight. And that goes to show, I think, the power of this issue of abortion and what it means perhaps going forward. We'll see how it plays out. But we did see some of the effects of that in the 2022 midterm election.\par \par \uc2 This is significant. This is you can't undersell the significance of this, because this makes what was a conservative state Supreme Court now a liberal leaning state supreme court, and not only on the issue of abortion, but on the issue of election challenges and redistricting challenges and a host of things, as you heard from former Governor Walker there. This is a big deal in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 You know, and it adds to on the same night the more progressive candidate in Chicago wins the Chicago mayoral race. It is a message that gave a shot in the arm to progressives and left leaning liberals.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And there was a ton of money poured into these races, a record $42 million I think. And you don't see that kind of money in a Supreme Court race. You saw it in Wisconsin. They knew how important it was there. Here's \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  saying that the message from her party needs to change when it comes to abortion in terms of defining it. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , \uc2 RNC\uc2  CHAIRWOMAN: When you're losing by 10 points, there is a messaging issue. And abortion is still an issue. And we can't allow the Democrats to define Republicans and put millions of dollars up in lies and have it go unanswered. It is not an issue that's going away for our party in a post Dobbs world, and we can't our head in the sand and think it's going to heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just a few seconds here. Bret, quick follow up on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIE: It's a big issue and it's going to continue to be in '24.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, no doubt we're going to be talking about it a lot. Bret, thank you so much. We'll see you tonight at 6:00 on Special Report.\par \par \uc2 So very excited to have Father Mike Schmitz with us today also known as the podcasting priest, he is a superstar in the podcast world, and he is here to talk about the deep fraying that we saw in that "Wall Street Journal" survey of faith in America and why there's reason for hope in this Holy Week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FATHER MIKE SCHMITZ, CATHOLIC PRIEST AND PODCAST HOST: Religion is not meant to keep people in line. It's to give them freedom. But it's a freedom that gives us a lot of responsibility as well, which is part of what America is built on. Here is freedom, but not freedom to do what I want, freedom to do what I ought.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Passover begins tonight. Easter is this Sunday, so we're in the middle of Holy Week and also of Ramadan. But a new poll shows religion is not as important to Americans as it used to be. Just 39 percent say it's very important in "The Wall Street Journal" survey, which has gotten a ton of attention this week for these numbers, down more than 20 points from 1998 when they first asked that question. The importance of patriotism and of having children also dropping by huge margins. Fox Digital asked Americans in Washington, D.C. This week, what are your top values?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Togetherness and harmony and independent spirits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Work and family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Honesty, hard work and perseverance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would say liberty and progress.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Faith in God, honesty, and integrity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Being able to say what you want to say without the fear of reprisal. I think that's a big one because that's not something you have in other parts of the world as much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Patriotism, community, looking after my kids, being kind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Being able to live your own life and make your own decisions, but, you know, just being able to come together when it matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, I am really thrilled today to have Father Mike Schmitz joining us. He is a very well-known priest in this country. He's the host of the top. religion and spirituality podcasts in the entire world called The Bible -- "The Bible in a Year" which I'm on day 93, 94, Father? So I'm a little bit of a day behind at the moment, but --\par \par \uc2 SCHMITZ: Not a problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm really happy to talk to you because your voice is in my head every single day. I gave up \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and social media for Lent, and I'm doing Bible in a year. Anyway, I'm really happy to talk to you. But, you know, I keep, as we cover the news this week, and there's been a lot of it, I keep going back to this "Wall Street Journal" polling, and I look at this and you look at the drop in faith, religion, patriotism, desire to have children, have a family. What do you think is going on here when you look at these numbers, Father?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITZ: Yes, you know, that's fascinating because I think there's a couple -- a lot of things happening. One of the things is, you probably heard this a thousand times, that people are more and more saying that they're spiritual but not religious. And that's, it's kind of a common phrase. It's so many people. And that's why the polls come in like, how important is religion to you? We'll not very. Why? But if you ask them how important is spirituality to them, that comes incredibly high.\par \par \uc2 And that reveals a couple of things to me. One is it reveals that the human spirit, right, that we still recognize that we're made for more. We still recognize that there is something more out there. And so even if someone is saying like, well, I don't really need religion, there is some impulse that no one needs to tell them this. They just -- they have it. They're holding onto it, that they realize they're made for more. That's the first thing.\par \par \uc2 So even if I don't really like the phrase I'm spiritual, I'm not religious, that's really, you know, I don't think it's enough. And that's the second part. So, first, people have this hunger for the spiritual life for more. But the second -- on the second hand, ultimately, I would say that, as you mentioned, there is a decrease in importance of religion or a patriotism or even international values or family. At some point, spirituality is not enough. Because why? Because it's all about something I invent.\par \par \uc2 And so when it comes to like the structures of life, when it comes to realizing that, wait a second, if all I believe in is spirituality, then my belief is only as big as me. But if there's more, there really is more, if there's actually a God out there, has he revealed himself to me? And that's where we would say religion, or, you know, I would say good religion. True religion comes in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what does it tell you that because I think you're doing a lot to spread the word to help people understand your faith, to help people understand rules around your faith and why they matter. So when you see that you are, you know, number one, number two on \uc2 Apple\uc2  with these podcasts, my son introduced me to, you, said, hey, you got to watch this podcast, mom. So is that heartening to you? Do you feel like, well, I guess I'm doing something right because I've got millions of people listening to me?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITZ: Well, that's the whole problem ways, right? You want to just say, OK, especially as a person of faith, I want to say, OK, God, what is it that you want me to do? And then to actually do that is the goal of life, right, is to say essentially what we'll hear Jesus say on Good Friday and Holy Thursday, not my will, but your will be done. That's the heart of what it is. So that's heartening. At the same time, you mentioned talking about rules a lot. And that's the incredible thing. I think that if I really want to have a real relationship, like with anybody, but if I want to have a real relationship with God, that the God of the universe, that's going to involve a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 That's going to mean that I have real rights, meaning I have access to him, that I actually get to talk to the Lord, that I get to actually have a relationship, but also means I have real responsibilities. And so when it comes to the rules you're talking about, even in the Bible, it's all in the context of a real relationship. And that's the heart of things. And so if I just want to have like, you know, a vague idea of the kind of God I've invented, well, then I can't really have a real relationship because there's no real rights, there's no real responsibilities. But if God's revealed himself, then I get to not only have a real responsibility and real rights, but I also get to have a real relationship.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a great message, especially this week. I think there's an opportunity for all of us during all of the religious observances of this week, to kind of look inward and look outward at what we're doing and who we're serving. So, Father Mike, thank you so much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 And for anybody who wants to hear more, I would tell you to tune into my podcast and also your podcast, which mine are nowhere near as popular as yours. But to listen to more of what you have to say and the message and also how you got to where you are, how you made the choices you made, we're going to talk about that on the podcast which is coming up. So thank you so much. Father Schmitz, good to see you today. Good to have Father Mike with us today.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY of Wednesday, April the 5th, 2020 now, already, I mean, 2023. What am I saying? Three years. So THE STORY goes on. Thanks for being here, everybody. Always great to be with you. We'll see you back here tomorrow. "YOUR WORLD" gets started in just a few seconds from now. Have a great afternoon and evening. Hope your story is a good one. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230406ej4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Public Deserves To Know What Was In Killer's Manifesto; To The Left, Christians Are The Emerging Enemy; \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  Claims He Didn't Know About USMS Training; Radical Left Victimizes Trans Killer; California Law Concerning Mental Health Of Children And Restrictions On Parental Involvement Examined; Biden