ed to the front of the line.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, with the Covenant School shooting in Nashville, we once again find ourselves searching for answers. Last night a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  profiler told us why the release of Hale's manifesto was important, but potentially just as crucial is the eventual release of her toxicology report. So we reached out to Tennessee authorities to ask if they would commit to a public release, and we still haven't heard back.\par \par \uc2 Until then, we will continue to search for answers. But joining me now to talk more about this, Dr. Mary Makary, \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2  professor and FOX News contributor. Dr. Makary, why does this matter?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MARTY MAKARY\uc2 , JOHNS HOPKINS SCHOOL OF MEDICINE: Well, look, People are understandably very upset. And there is never one cause. It's multifactorial. But parents have a right to know whether or not the tox screen shows that the person was under the influence of some substance, be it cocaine or amphetamines or some crazy interaction of seven different psyche meds. We don't have good data on this so it's hard to make any conclusions, and we shouldn't. But people have a right to know right now and researchers need to study the trends, because 23 percent of mass shooters are on some psyche meds. That's a study from \uc2 VOA\uc2 . So there may be something there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We do know that antidepressant medications have been linked to violent and aggressive behavior. And it's on the warnings. You read it on the warnings, we put them on our show a while ago, and they're stark warnings. That doesn't mean everyone on antidepressant medication is going to be violent. But in certain personalities, especially with different drugs interacting, this could be a toxic mess, could it not?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Yes, there may be something there. So the reality is when doctors prescribe this, it should be in a limited context, and they should understand. But a lot of these medications now are being given out willy- nilly. You can get a diagnosis in 15 minutes and go on a couple medications. And we don't have long-term data on drugs that just came out. So we can't really make conclusions.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you anxious today? Have a Xanax. Can't sleep? Have a Zoloft, right? It's like candy. The national police chief addressed the shooter's mental health yesterday. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She was under care, a doctor's care, for an emotional disorder. Law enforcement knew nothing about the treatment she was receiving. But her parents felt that she should not own weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Makary, under doctor's care for an emotional disorder. I have done a lot of reading and reporting over the years. I have never heard a mental disorder described as an emotional disorder, but maybe I've missed that.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: There is clearly more there that we are not being told, and when you have a public health disaster like this, a crisis, something that's really tragic, people have a right to know more information.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mental health is a massive problem in this country right now, is it not, among young people?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: It's a massive problem. And at the same time, we have the most medicated generation of young people in the history of the world. So we've got to look at causes, not just play whack-a-mole with meds.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And confusing messages to children as well about a variety of things, their identity, who they are, when they're really an adult, when they have real cognitive control over their own behaviors and judgment.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: We put them in hard core isolation, feed them bad food, and then say we've got to medicate, do bariatric surgery, and give them all these psyche meds, and wonder why do we need to do more and more.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Makary, so good to see you in person tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: You too.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When we come back, something a little different. And I'll require your help to complete this mission. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Later this week, we're going to bring you a debate, one of my favorites, on airline etiquette. But tonight we're learning that Congress is proposing a new no-fly list. It's going to cover unruly passengers, acting so drunk that you get taped to your chair or maybe you don't want to switch your seat to let a mom sit next to her kid. Could that put you on the no-fly list? We're going to bring you more details as we get them.\par \par \uc2 But first, I want to know what you think. So go to my Twitter @IngrahamAngle and answer the poll. We'll reveal the results later this week.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us tonight. Thank you for watching. Remember, it is America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020230330ej3t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Nashville Police Chief Details Hale Gun Purchases; Dozens Killed In Migrant Detention Center Blaze; Lawmakers Highlight Drug Smuggling Influx; Biden Hits The Road To Tout Economic Agenda; Republicans Demand Biden Make Spending Cuts; Gen. \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  And Gen. Mark Milley Testify On Budget Proposal; IRS Agent Visited Journalist Home \uc2 Matt Taibbi\uc2  After Testimony; House Subcommittee On Coronavirus Pandemic Holds Hearing On Negative Consequences Of Mass School Closures; U.S. States Compete For Manufacturing Of Semiconductors As Result Of Bipartisan Bill Promoting Computer Chip Production In U.S.; Biden Administration And House Republicans Trade Criticism Over Negotiations To Raise U.S. Debt Ceiling; Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas Testifies To Senate On Security Of U.S. Southern Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, CB Cotton, Griff Jenkins, Alexis McAdams, Jacqui  Heinrich, Jennifer Griffin, Kelly O'Grady, Bret Baier, Mike Emanuel \par \uc2 7,707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And he landed on his feet?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: He did.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: He's awesome.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And they want to make sure that these guys get the due for their heroic efforts. I love this guy. God bless you. Thank you for serving the military. And happy birthday!\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's how you do it.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's not made of powder, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Happy birthday!\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No powder in that one.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, we got to go. What's up, Trace?\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Hi, Greg. My kids play volleyball as well. Just so, if you're right (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Oh.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I mean, I have to give these a little bit that Thank you, Greg. Thank you, everyone.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very generous.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Good evening and welcome to Los Angeles. I'm Trace Gallagher, in for Bret Baier. President Biden's Homeland Security chief endures withering criticism over the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are wrapped over an \uc2 IRS\uc2  visit to a journalist involved in the release of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files. And the U.S. tries to ramp up domestic semiconductor production as tensions increase with China.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", we are learning new details about yesterday's horrific attack at a private Christian school in Nashville, authorities have now released images of Audrey Hale's assault and the police takedown of the 28-year-old.\par \par \uc2 She killed three children and three adults before being fatally shot by police. Hale is a former student at the Covenant School. Police say she left a manifesto, but her motive has not been announced.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent CB Cotton is live tonight in Nashville with more on this. CB, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Trace, well, we know that shooter intentionally targeted this school behind me. We know specific people were not targeted in this violent attack.\par \par \uc2 Today, also, we got an up-close look at the take down.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): Newly released body camera video shows the urgency of the police officers arriving at the Covenant School within minutes of the first call.\par \par \uc2 REX ENGELBERT, OFFICER, \uc2 METRO\uc2  NASHVILLE POLICE: Let's go!\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Entering the school.\par \par \uc2 ENGELBERT: Metro police!\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Making their way to the second floor.\par \par \uc2 ENGELBERT: Let's go. Go.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: And fatally shooting 28-year-old Audrey Hale. Stopping the violence, 14 minutes after it began.\par \par \uc2 Two officers, Rex Engelbert and Michael Collazo are credited with the takedown. We're learning more about the shooter today. Officials, say Hale was a former student at the school, who lived at home with their parents.\par \par \uc2 Police say she was transgender and was under a doctor's care for an emotional disorder at the time of the shooting. Nashville Police Chief John Drake, spoke to FOX earlier today.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): You mentioned fighting Audrey Hale's manifesto. Now, 24 hours later, what new insights have you learned?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRAKE, CHIEF, METRO NASHVILLE POLICE: So, a new insight that we have, is that we were able to talk to the parents of Audrey Hale and we found that she had purchased seven firearms from five local gun stores.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Drake says all of the guns were legally purchased. Only three used at the school.\par \par \uc2 He says officials are still combing through the lengthy manifesto.\par \par \uc2 Vigils for the six victims were held Monday night. The children: Evelyn Dieckhaus, Hallie Scruggs, and William Kinney, all 9 years old. And the adults who were likely working to protect the kids, Cynthia Peak, Katherine Koonce and Mike Hill, all in their 60s, all school workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Drake said that manifesto also contain details right down to what the shooter planned on wearing during the attack. Some lawmakers have called on that manifesto to be released, but officials haven't said when that may happen. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: CB Cotton, live for us in Nashville. CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, dozens of migrants are reported dead and many others injured in a fire at an immigration center just south of the U.S. Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say migrants set mattresses ablaze over fears of deportation. This comes as we get the latest numbers on migrant encounters at the border.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Griff Jenkins on Capitol Hill with President Biden's Homeland Security secretary, testifying before senators.\par \par \uc2 But first up, correspondent Alexis McAdams in Mission, Texas tonight. Alexis, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Trace, good evening.\par \par \uc2 These migrants had been waiting to get into the United States and had just found out that wasn't going to happen. So, investigators say someone set a fire, leading to these deaths and more chaos at our southern border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Tonight, a young mom desperate for answers. The woman says she saw dozens of people on the ground after the migrants were left trapped inside of that burning building in Ciudad Juarez, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 That Blaze killing at least 40 people. Dozens were hurt. Some left behind because they were detained. So, they died of smoke inhalation.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's president says these migrants started that fire after they found out they were going to be deported. Reports say the migrants were also fed up with the lack of basic supplies.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES MANUEL LOPEZ OBRADOR\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF MEXICO (through translator): (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The Mexican president says the migrants blocked the door with their mattresses, then let them on fire in protest.\par \par \uc2 This comes as tensions between Mexican immigration officials and migrants have reached a boiling point. Just two weeks ago, at least 1,000 migrants near that same facility rushed this bridge, trying to get into the United States. But they were stopped by U.S. and Mexican authorities, and taken back to those crowded facilities. Where they're either taken to other parts of Mexico or deported all together.\par \par \uc2 The migrants who were being held in that facility in Mexico were part of the 1000s waiting to cross into the U.S. wanting to enter through El Paso, Texas. U.S. border patrol says that sector is the busiest in the country.\par \par \uc2 FIDEL BACA, AGENT, UNITED STATES BORDER PATROL: Between October 1st and the end of February, we have over 224,000 apprehensions. That's going to be in 156 percent increase from the same timeframe of last year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And Trace, tonight, border patrol says that the migrants who were hurt in this fire will now be allowed in the United States for medical treatments. We just found that in new information out.\par \par \uc2 But this all comes as 1000s of people are still waiting to get into the U.S. at the southern border. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Alexis McAdams, live for us on the border. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas face some tough questioning by senators today on Capitol Hill about the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkin says that part of the story tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): You should be fired.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: For more than three hours, lawmakers grilled Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Secretary, you understand that you have a credibility problem with the -- with the Congress and with the American people, don't you?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: I have unflinching confidence in the integrity of my conduct.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Republican senators pulled no punches over a perceived lack of operational control at the border.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): The men and women of the Border Patrol, they've never had a political leader undermine them. They despise you, Mr. Secretary, because you're willing to let children be raped to follow political orders. This is a crisis.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: What the senator has said was revolting. I'm not going to address it.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And a recent spike in Chinese nationals crossing illegally.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): I'm asking you if they're members of the Chinese Communist Party? And you won't -- you don't know, apparently, you won't say.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: We are very focused on all things with respect to the People's Republic --\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: At times, Democrats came to the secretary's defense.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH (D-VT): I would just say to all of my colleagues, you know, we can have whoever happens to be the secretary of Homeland Security and bring him in and beat him up. Or we can do our job. And my hope is we could do our job.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: But there was bipartisan scrutiny over the influx of fentanyl coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): This problem has risen under Republican presidents and Democratic presidents. This is not a partisan issue. This is an American issue. This is an American crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And today's grilling comes as the committee chairman Dick Durbin sent a letter to the administration signed by 18 other Democrats, opposing a return to a policy of family detentions seen under the Obama and Trump administrations. And it comes ahead of the anticipated lifting of Title-42, Mayorkas said today, no decision has yet been made. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: More on that with the panel. Griff, thank you. Stocks were down on tech performances. The Dow lost 38. The S&P 500 fell six. The NASDAQ dropped 53.\par \par \uc2 While President Biden was in North Carolina today to kick off what he calls his investing in America tour. This comes as Republicans continue to criticize the president over his refusal to talk about the debt ceiling and government spending.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that story tonight. Jacqui, good evening.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Trace. The administration is fanning out over 20 states in the next three weeks to highlight the impact of Biden's past spending after the president unveiled his new budget proposal, which includes $2.6 trillion in new spending.\par \par \uc2 But were Democrats see investment, Republican seat fiscal risk, and that means an impasse in talks to raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): House Republicans are telling President Biden, he must consider spending cuts if he wants their votes to raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Kevin McCarthy offering up a range of options from trims to non-defense spending, to recouping COVID funds to means testing for welfare programs.\par \par \uc2 REP. PATRICK MCHENRY (R-NC): We're at a standstill. The only path for an agreement between this administration is through the House of Representatives and with the Speaker, and there's been no return conversation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Speaker Kevin McCarthy, noting the recent uncertainty in the U.S. banking system over continuing rate hikes, telling Biden "I am incredibly concerned that you're putting an already fragile economy in jeopardy by insisting upon your extreme position of refusing to negotiate any meaningful changes to out-of-control government spending.\par \par \uc2 The White House says, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA DALTON, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: When they put forward a budget proposal, let's take a look at that, and we'll have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In North Carolina, the first stop of a multistate blitz touting the benefits of Biden spending, the president argued budget cuts would help China.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Remain ceding the future of innovation and technology to China.\par \par \uc2 I've got news for you, and for MAGA Republicans in Congress. Not on my watch.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But new polling from the A.P. shows 68 percent of Americans disapprove of the way Biden's handling the economy, and three times as many fear it will get worse than think it will get better.\par \par \uc2 A Monmouth poll also shows only a quarter of Democrats calling for Biden to pursue a second term.\par \par \uc2 The GOP accusing Biden of trying to run out the clock and force their hand.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I think there is a way that we can get this done early, which would help the economy, instead of the brinkmanship the president is trying to play.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Senate Democrats remain unmoved.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Speaker McCarthy has failed to unite his conference behind a single proposal that can win 218 votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The president today also reacted to the school shooting in Nashville. Lamenting that he's really exercise the limits of his executive authority, and anything new in the form of gun control is going to have to come from Congress.\par \par \uc2 He also spoke for the first time about the Israeli government's judicial overhaul plan, saying that he will not be inviting Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to the White House anytime soon. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Bret Baier will look at the race among states to produce more semiconductors a little bit later on in the program.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the defense secretary says one senator's actions will impact the military's readiness.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles as the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2  begins the layoffs of 7,000 workers. The cuts are to be conducted in three rounds.\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , says the first group of impacted workers will be notified this week.\par \par \uc2 FOX 9 in the Twin Cities with a trial of vape manufacturer Juul and tobacco marketer \uc2 Altria\uc2 . The case by the Minnesota attorney general is the first against Juul to go to trial.\par \par \uc2 12 other states have sued the company since 2019. The complaint also cites multinational tobacco company Philip Morris as a defendant.\par \par \uc2 And the live look now in Orlando from FOX 35, one of the big stories there and across the country tonight.\par \par \uc2 Five planets plus the moon will be visible in an almost arc shape in the nighttime sky.\par \par \uc2 The rare planetary alignment of Jupiter, \uc2 Mercury\uc2 , Venus Uranus and Mars can be see very low in the West just after sunset if the conditions are right, and you have some maybe binoculars or a telescope.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight's look, live "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: On the murder conviction of a man whose story was featured on the podcast Serial has been reinstated. Maryland's Court of Appeals ruled against Adnan Syed because the family of his alleged victim was not given prior notice of a motion to vacate his conviction.\par \par \uc2 The court ordered a new hearing Syed will not be taken back into custody. He was sentenced in 1999 for the murder of former girlfriend Hae Min Lee. Prosecutors dropped charges last September 2022 after additional DNA testing excluded Syed as a suspect.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Russia's Defense Ministry\uc2  says it test fired anti-ship missiles in the Sea of Japan today. It says two boats successfully launched a simulated attack on a mock enemy warship about 60 miles away. The Moskit anti-ship cruise missile has conventional and nuclear warhead capacity. It is capable of flying at a speed three times the speed of sound and has a range of up to 155 miles.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon officials are defending their latest budget request in front of Congress. The $842 billion request is the largest in history, and it is largely focused on China.\par \par \uc2 But today was about another danger to national security. The Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: It's a powerful effect and will impact on our readiness.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Pentagon's top official accused Congress of playing a dangerous game by holding up more than 150 officer promotions.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Not approving the recommendation for promotions actually creates a ripple effect through the force that makes us far less ready than we need to be.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Senator Tommy Tuberville has placed the hold to protest the Pentagon's policy to reimburse travel for female service members who need abortions not offered in the state where they are deployed.\par \par \uc2 The block nominations affect a pipeline of officers including the next head of the Navy, Marine Corps, and 73 and four star officers preparing to deploy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): This is about not forcing the taxpayers of this country to fund abortion.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Almost one in five of our troops are women but almost 80,000 of our women are stationed in places that where they don't have access to non- covered reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Democrats piled on after the hearing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Senator Tuberville is blocking all general and flag officer confirmations. Taking our military, our national security, our safety hostage.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Additional frustration erupted when Republican lawmakers asked why it took so long for the Pentagon to alert Congress to the attacks by Iranian proxies on U.S. troops based in Syria last week, accusing the Pentagon of intentionally keeping Congress in the dark.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): I believe that your office specifically withheld notification.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: That is absolutely not true.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: I don't believe that.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  introduced an amendment on the same day as the attacks that would have halted the repeal of the 2002 authorization for the use of military force until the president certified Iran is not supporting violent groups in Iraq and Syria.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is unacceptable that no one informed the Senate of this attack in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Pentagon officials push back on accusations that COVID vaccine mandates contributed to the lowest army recruiting numbers in years, arguing the 8,000 service members kicked out for refusing the vaccine could reapply for their jobs now that the mandate has been repealed, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  comes calling on a journalist involved in the release of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, thousands of protesters marched as part of a nationwide day of action against the French government's raising the retirement age concerns over possible violence led to what was described as an unprecedented deployment of 13,000 officers, nearly half of them concentrated in the French capital.\par \par \uc2 A man wielding a large knife kills two women and injures several other people at an Ismaili Muslim center in Lisbon. Portuguese authorities say they are investigating the stabbings as a possible terror attack. Local Afghan community representatives and Portuguese authorities described the suspect as a refugee from Afghanistan, who was receiving help from the Israeli community.\par \par \uc2 And Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is pledging a new era of partnership with Africa. Harris told an audience in Ghana that she sees a future propelled by African innovation. The speech on her side second full day in the country is part of a weeklong trip that will include visits to Tanzania and Zambia.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  now responding to President Biden's comments we told you about in Jacqui Heinrich's story. Netanyahu says quoting here, the alliance between Israel and the United States is unbreakable and always overcomes the occasional disagreements between us. Israel is a sovereign country which makes its decisions by the will of its people and not based on pressures from abroad, including from the best of friends.\par \par \uc2 While the Manhattan Grand Jury hearing testimony about hush money paid on former President Trump's behalf will not take up that inquiry again this week, that delay is a potential vote on an indictment until next week at the earliest. Jurors met Monday to hear from one witness.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has ruled that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will have to testify before a grand jury in the federal probe and into efforts by former President Trump and his allies to challenge the 2020 election. Pence was subpoenaed last month by the Justice Department's special counsel as part of the probe into the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 Pence's team filed a motion to block that subpoena. It claimed that as Vice President, Pence's conversations with the President and top White House aides were protected by executive privilege claims.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are serious questions being raised tonight about a visit from an \uc2 IRS\uc2  agent to one of the journalists responsible for the release of the internal documents from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Kelly O'Grady is live with us here in Los Angeles with more on this story, Kelly?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, it's so great to be with you tonight, Trace.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  files journalist \uc2 Matt Taibbi\uc2  receiving an unexpected house call from the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . While in-person visits can happen, most contact is initiated via mail, but it's the timing that's causing particular concern.\par \par \uc2 So, on March 9th, the same day he testified before Congress on alleged government censorship. The journalist claims an \uc2 IRS\uc2  agent left a notice at his residence requesting he call four days later. Taibbi says he was then informed his 2018 and 2021 tax returns had been rejected over identity theft concerns.\par \par \uc2 Taibbi asserts the 2018 filing was in fact accepted over four years ago. And the 2021 return has been rejected twice despite following protocol using an \uc2 IRS\uc2  PIN number. Given the time, some lawmakers questioned the \uc2 IRS\uc2 's motive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): They could have sent a letter. They could have had someone make a phone call. They could have reached out to \uc2 Matt Taibbi\uc2 's CPA to have dealt with this. Instead, they send an agent to his home, again, on the very day that he is to testify? This reeks of intimidation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan also seeking answers. In a letter to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  Commissioner Danny Werfel and Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  he demanded the \uc2 IRS\uc2  hand over all communication regarding the visit, saying, quote, "In light of the hostile reaction to Mr. Taibbi's reporting among leftwing activists, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 's action could be interpreted as an attempt to intimidate a witness before congress. While Taibbi is refusing to comment until the \uc2 IRS\uc2  responds, his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Files colleagues shared this concern with FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHELLENBERGER, AUTHOR: Maybe somebody was trying to send a message. If it's the latter, this obviously is deeply inappropriate and maybe illegal. It's extremely troubling if, indeed, we've seen the \uc2 IRS\uc2  being weaponized against \uc2 Matt Taibbi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: We received no comment from the \uc2 IRS\uc2  or Treasury. But Trace, critics are saying this is just another example of a weaponized government targeting free-speech.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It takes most people hours to get an \uc2 IRS\uc2  agent on the phone, right, and now they're making house calls? Interest. Kelly O'Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Banking Committee is investigating what led to the collapse of Silicon Valley and Signature Banks. A top federal regulator says \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  did a terrible job of managing risk before its failure. Some lawmakers are blaming bank watchdogs for missing warning signs. The failure of the institution set off a broader loss of investor confidence in the banking sector that pummeled stocks and stoked fears of a full-blown financial process.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors have unveiled a new indictment against \uc2 Sam Bankman- Fried\uc2 . They accuse the founder of the now bankrupt \uc2 FTX\uc2  cryptocurrency exchange of conspiring to pay $40 million bribe to Chinese government officials. Prosecutors had previously accused Bankman-Fried of stealing billions of dollars in customer funds to plug losses at his \uc2 Alameda Research\uc2  hedge fund. He is also charged with orchestrating an illegal campaign donation scheme to buy influence in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The newly created House subcommittee on the coronavirus pandemic is looking at the consequences of mass school closures. There are serious concerns about setbacks in student development and physical well-being along with increases in psychological distress. Here is the chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD WENSTRUP, (R-OH) HOUSE CORONAVIRUS SUBCOMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: Our children have paid the price.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: The House panel investigating COVID-19 dug into the intended and unintended consequences as school closures during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 WENSTRUP: The closing of schools proved to be harmful to students, their academic, mental, physical, and social development.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Congressman Wenstrup sent more than a dozen letters to several agencies and unions today, including the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  and the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , writing his subcommittee is, quote, "concerned about the potential for undue influence of nongovernmental groups on CDC scientific guidance." The witnesses were more pointed.\par \par \uc2 VIRGINIA GENTLES, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: The school stayed closed primarily because the teachers' unions in our country have enormous power.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The epidemiologist called to testify argued social distancing and masking were not necessary and not evidenced-based.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACY BETH HOEG, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA, SAN FRANCISCO: We did not obtain any new high-quality evidence during the COVID-19 pandemic that masks are effective mitigation strategy.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The \uc2 WHO\uc2 , CDC, and the AMA, among others, disagree. Some Democrats cautioned against Monday morning quarterbacking.\par \par \uc2 REP. KWEISI MFUME, (D-MD) HOUSE CORONAVIRUS SUBCOMMITTEE: In case no one remembers, COVID and those days of COVID were dark, dreary, desolate.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The face of the public health response defended the initial closure of schools.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: We are going to go back and examine that is were things done too long or not? But the initial decision to lock things down unequivocally saved a lot of lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Key lawmakers say that damage is done for a generation of students and suggest policymakers must learn lessons from COVID to avoid making the same mistakes again. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A staff member for Kentucky Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  was stabbed over the weekend in Washington. Phillip Todd reportedly sustained life- threatening injuries requiring surgery. Police say 42-year-old Glynn Neal knocked him to the ground and stabbed him multiple times. A friend of the victim pushed the attacker away, enabling them to escape. The Federal Bureau of Prison Records showed Neal was released from prison on Friday, the day before the stabbing. Senator Paul is asking for privacy so everyone can focus on healing and recovery.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how states are competing to get the advantage as the U.S. tries to boost its semiconductor development. And later, the panel on the president's trip to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: For almost three years, a shortage in computer chips has impacted companies and consumers across the technology sector. In tonight's "Whatever Happened To?" segment, my colleague Bret Baier looks at what the U.S. has done to ramp up production and whether we can dominate the industry in the near future.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 A perfect storm created the semiconductor, or chip shortage. The 2020 COVID-19 pandemic exposed the first sign of vulnerability. Lockdowns coupled with an increase in demand for computers and devices for remote work created a limited supply. Tension with China also escalated, prompting the U.S. to place export controls on Beijing. And the push to go electric across the country creating an increased demand among automakers.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we look at how the U.S. is working to increase its own production and whether we as a country can to compete on the global market.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are determined to lead the world in the manufacturing of semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): And we need to bring that capability back to the United States.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 TODD YOUNG\uc2 , (R-IN): You can't turn on and off anything with an on-off switch these days without microchips.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: And the fact that we don't make them in this country is a huge vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The United States was once a leader in semiconductor manufacturing. As recently as 1990 the U.S. produced 37 percent of the chips used worldwide. That number has since fallen to 12 percent since.\par \par \uc2 Are we paying the price for some decision made a long time ago on semiconductors?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 , (D-CA): Absolutely. It wasn't just semiconductors. Both parties just watched as industry after industry went offshore. And now we're in this position of vulnerability where we don't have enough good paying manufacturing jobs in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Constructing and operating a fabrication facility, or FAB, in the U.S. became expensive, around 25 to 50 percent higher than abroad.\par \par \uc2 JOHN NEUFFER\uc2 , \uc2 SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY ASSOCIATION\uc2  CEO: There's been an overconcentration of certain aspects of our supply chain. And what we're trying to do right now is diversify.\par \par \uc2 Right now, 75 percent of manufacturing is happening in Asia. That's probably too much. We need to spread our risk around so that our supply chains are more resilient.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Global Foundries is one U.S. manufacturer has FABs located both in the U.S. and abroad.\par \par \uc2 So with new incentives here, through various ways, but specifically the Chips now, does that make it more appealing to manufacture and the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CAULFIELD\uc2 , GLOBAL FOUNDRIES PRESIDENT AND CEO: The entire point of the Chips bill is to create globally competitive economics so there is an economic hurdle to add capacity in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When the COVID-19 pandemic began in 2020, production around the world was paused, and that brought to light an increase in demand for technology and microchips.\par \par \uc2 NEUFFER: The reality is we had a whole world clamoring for electronics to work at home and to study at home, and those electronics are chockfull of our chips. So no surprise, demand for chips just went off the charts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most of those chips are produced in Asia making supply in the U.S. even more limited.\par \par \uc2 NEUFFER: Our FABs were running at near full capacity, really kind of burning the midnight oil, doing everything possible to get as many chips out the door as we could.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden signed the Chips and Science Act into law last year. Lawmakers wanted the bipartisan legislation to end reliance on Asia when it comes to semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 Are states competing as well?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Every governor out there thinks the next chip factory will be in their state. They will compete. I'm sure they'll put incentives on the table, and that's what they should do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Many companies had already begun breaking ground on facilities before the incentives were official, including Global Foundries, which plans to expand production in upstate New York.\par \par \uc2 Could you tie that expansion directly to the Chips Act?\par \par \uc2 CAULFIELD: Absolutely. We have a facility that needs to grow to full scale in upstate New York to create that capacity that can compete globally against all the players and manufacturing for semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some FABs could start production as soon as next year. Arizona has a significant showing of planned projects over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is in the middle of really building up to have a facility there to compete.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Facilities, multiple.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Multiple. And that's a big deal for the state that you're in, and also for the country.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: A big deal for the country. Its helps in our state. It's jobs, it grows the economy. It also grows the economy nationwide.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Texas is another state that will see in significant increase in manufacturing, especially from local company \uc2 Texas Instruments\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How soon could the U.S. through Texas and Arizona and other places be up to speed on this?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX): It's probably going to be 2024 until you see manufacturing come from the Phoenix-based Taiwan semiconductor factory there. So all this takes a little while, but we've got not a minute to waste.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While the U.S., domestic supply is expected to increase in the near future, it could take a lot longer to be back on top of the semiconductor industry worldwide.\par \par \uc2 How long do you think that will take for the U.S. to be on par with some of the foreign competitors?\par \par \uc2 CAULFIELD: It didn't happen overnight for our industry. It took roughly three decades to create this big imbalance of demand being serviced outside the United States, and it's going to take decades to correct that issue. And so this is a good start.\par \par \uc2 Probably the biggest issue we still need to contend with is to make sure that demand for all this capacity we want to put on in the U.S. materializes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, if you have auto companies going electric, right there is a huge supply needed.\par \par \uc2 CAULFIELD: Huge supply needed, but let's make sure it gets built and used in the United States and not elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A shortage of chips has eased somewhat, but the push to expand production in some business sectors is creating further demand for chip manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 More electric cars.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Electric cars, but more important than that, F-35s, F-16s. We don't want to have to rely on supply chain that goes across the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Next we'll take a look at U.S. efforts to work with allies to ramp up production and compete with our adversaries around the world.\par \par \uc2 Trace, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel as one law member says Homeland Security Secretary Mayorkas has a credibility problem. And later, an 11-year-old lacrosse player inspires a groundswell of support for a grieving professional athlete.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Extreme MAGA Republicans, not all, but extreme MAGA Republicans are threatening to undo all this progress. They're putting our economy in jeopardy by threatening to refuse to pay America's bills.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The president has misled the members of Congress, misled the American public that he was going to negotiate. He hasn't done that.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES, (R-TX): What I see people right now is people are fed up with the environment that is happening. They want real action, and whoever delivers that for them is going to get their vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com and host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, and Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President Obama. Welcome to you both. You just heard what Speaker Kevin McCarthy said there, Guy. I want to play this. This is from the president responding to that. He said, quoting here, "Unfortunately, the tax proposals from the House Republican Conference would exacerbate the debt problem I inherited by over $3 trillion in new tax spending skewed to the same constituencies who should be paying more. That is one reason why seeing your full set of proposals would be useful before we meet so we can understand the full combined impact on the deficit." Looks like there is going to be back and forth before there is any true negotiation here, Guy.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: It's a little laughable to see President Biden or Democrats pretending to care about the deficit, something they almost never do unless it is for political posturing, which is what this is. But the president makes a point that Republicans need to come to the table with a unified proposal before a meaningful conversation and negotiation could really get going. That has not happened yet. I think that will be difficult for the Republicans to do. But that's what they have to do in order to bring a case to the American people, let alone to the opposite side of the aisle to try to get something done.\par \par \uc2 The deadline is still a couple months away. We know in this town everyone kicks the can as far as possible before actually getting down to brass tacks. But Republicans have some work to do before the real negotiations can get underway in earnest.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Indeed. Meantime, Marie, President Biden kicked off his investing in America tour, though it's kind of tough to tie your economic acknowledgments and your accomplishments, right? Because I want to put this on the screen. This is from "The Wall Street Journal"-NORC poll. This is state of the economy. You have 80 percent of those polled who say the economy is poor, not so good. It's kind of a drag as you are trying to get out of the gate, Marie.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This is why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is getting out of Washington and going to many states throughout the country, going to communities where his policies are having a direct positive economic impact. Whether that's manufacturing jobs that are being created at these new factories that are reopening, whether it's shovel-ready infrastructure projects that are part of his bipartisan infrastructure bill, he's getting out across America and saying, look, I know that you still feel some pain. Inflation is coming down, but it needs to come down further. We have created a record number of jobs. Unemployment is low, and I am doing things that are going to help your community. It's retail politics at its best, and I think if he is going it run again, which I think he is, this is what he has to do to get that message out.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And you talk about running. Let's also bring in Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner." Byron, welcome to you. You talk about running here, and you have got to bring up in Monmouth poll. It's interesting. You have got 25 percent of Democrats who say that the president should run again. I mean, that is a bad number when you're just coming out of the gate, Byron.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Yes, 25 percent, and 75 percent who don't want the president to run again. There's a couple of reasons. To what Marie was saying, it is interesting Biden has placed a lot of faith in his ability to travel around the country and tell people look at all the stuff we have given you. And if you reelect us, we will give you even more.\par \par \uc2 And yet, people still have deep economic anxieties. Obviously, the banking problems recently have not done anything to help that. And I'm not sure how a debt limit standoff is going to play in that context. But Biden is doing all of that in the larger context in a significant number of people believing he is too old for a second term. So he is fighting really on multi-fronts against perceptions.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I want to move on, if I can, to the border. You had DHS Secretary Mayorkas on the Hill today. Here's the back and forth between Mayorkas and Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): Our southern border is not secure, is it?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We are so focused on the security of our southern border. We are doing so much with respect --\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: But you are not succeeding.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Let me share with you the challenge of migration that we are experiencing at our southern border is not exclusive to the United States.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: My job is to worry about the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I have watched a lot of these hearings with this Mayorkas guy, and I have just never really seen one where he is not willing to say now that the border is secure. He is just staying clear of that topic all together. And it's really instructive to see how far we have come in a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: The numbers are so bad the gaslighting can only get them so far, although he did try to tweak the definition of operational control of the border to again assert that this administration has achieved operational control of the southern border, which is a joke. I think most people understands it's a joke.\par \par \uc2 And if you don't believe me and you certainly don't believe the DHS secretary, you can talk to the Border Patrol chief who was asked under oath at a hearing down in Texas just a few weeks ago by Republicans, the Democrats decided to boycott it for political reasons. But the hearing went on, and that exact question about operational control was posed to the men in control of the process on the ground. Does the U.S. have that control? His answer was no.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's interesting, Marie, because you look down at the border. We see the migrants continuing to cross and the tragedy, also. We had the train where the two migrants suffocated. You had the fire down there where 29 migrants died.\par \par \uc2 And I want to put this on the screen, because this is migrant deaths attempting to cross into the U.S. And you go back to 2019, you had 300, 2020, 247. But look at the past couple of years, 546 deaths, 853, Marie, and you've got to believe if this was a Republican administration they would be screaming from the rooftops on the left.\par \par \uc2 HARF: This is why we can't forget that at root this is a humanitarian catastrophe. And Secretary Mayorkas has made clear that they need legislative action to fix this problem. They have surged resources to the border, so encounters between ports of entry have continued to fall. I think they're at their lowest point in two years. And sadly, the other humanitarian part of this is fentanyl, which largely comes through ports, through cars and trucks. So this is a problem that there is no easy answers, but we need Congress to not just grandstand in hearings but to actually come to the table with legislative ideas. And I didn't hear a lot of that today.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, I want to, if I can, Byron, get to you wrap us up on the Nashville shooting, the school shooting yesterday. "The Federalist" writes the following here about the bodycam video, "The six-minute shot from the perspective of Rex Engelbert and Michael Collazo, among the first two officers on the scene. It shows impressive bravery, determination, urgency, and skill. Their courage is also a reminder that if Uvalde cops responding to the Robb Elementary School mass shooting last year had shown any urgency or a modicum of bravery rather than sitting around a parking lot and hallway debating what to do, they would have prevented 19 children, two teachers from being killed." Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, I agree with absolutely all of that. These cops in Nashville were absolutely heroic, going in there. All they knew was somebody had a high powered rifle who would try to kill them. And they instead prevailed. And I hope it means in the policing world we will just never see the tactics that were employed in Uvalde again in another emergency.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, that would be a great thing. Byron York, Guy Benson, Marie Harf, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to make a donation to our nearest children's hospital for every time I score a goal. And we want you to try to do the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: A young girl creates a unique charity challenge to support her favorite lacrosse player who is grieving after the loss of a newborn child. Eleven-year-old Karaline Apoian took to social media with the #challengegoalsforavery. She is asking participants to match donations to their local children's hospital for every goal she scores in her league. Atlanta Swarm goaltender Craig Wende says the gesture brought tears to his eyes.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, results of a new FOX poll dealing with the economy and the 2024 presidential primaries.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. Please join me for "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" this evening, midnight eastern, 9:00 pacific. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230329ej3s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Downing Of U.S. Drone Raises Tensions With Russia; Markets Rattled By Growing Bank Crisis Fear; Report: White House Looking To Ensure Accountability For SVB; Stocks Tumble Amid Bank Contagion Fears; Former Treasury Official On Spreading Bank Crisis; Markets Rattled By Growing Bank Crisis Fears; SVB Leadership Under Scrutiny After Collapse; Federal Judge Mulls Banning Abortion Pill In US; All-Women School Students Vote To Admit Trans Men; GOP Holds Border Hearing Boycotted By Dems; Missing TX Girl Found Alive Locked In NC Shed; Social Media's Impact On Teen Girls' Mental Health; Exclusive First Look At "Sacrifice And Survival"\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Mike Tobin, Jack Keane, Edward Lawrence, David  Spunt, Jonathan Turley, Bill Melugin, Laura Ingle, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 8,285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): If they changed this, would you go back?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MORRIS, AUTHOR: I knew that was coming. Let's do that in the next segment.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: OK. Jonathan Morris, thanks so much. Good to get your thoughts on this. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MORRIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But the interesting --\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: What a time to be out of time.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We love Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We do.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks to him for joining us. Jam-packed two hours, John. Great to be with you. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We'll see you again tomorrow. THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thanks, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And right now, on THE STORY, the Pentagon, giving us an update amid new concerns about the war in Ukraine triggering a larger conflict between the United States, and Russia potentially, after the downing of one of our unmanned drones in the Black Sea. This is a very tense issue. The United States, the Russia rather warning to stop when it calls hostile military action near its borders. The Pentagon saying that the Russian jet hit the U.S. drone in international airspace where we have every right to be forcing it to crash into the Black Sea. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: This hazardous episode is a part, is part of a pattern of aggressive, risky, risky and unsafe actions by Russian pilots in international airspace.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , JOINT CHIEF OF STAFF CHAIRMAN: We do not seek armed conflict with, with Russia. And, and I believe that at this point, we should investigate this incident and move on from there, but we will continue to exercise our rights in international airspace.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so what about the tone of this news conference that we just got from the Defense Secretary and from the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs? General Jack Keane standing by with his reaction. First, to Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  live in Kyiv tonight. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  just confirmed that there was an undoubtedly tensed conversation with himself and his Russian counterpart, Defense Minister \uc2 Sergei Shoigu\uc2 . They talked about this contact, of course, between the MQ-9 U.S. reaper drone, and two Russian SU-27 fighter jets in international airspace over the Black Sea. The Defense Secretary stressed that this is a part, a part of a pattern of reckless and dangerous behavior on the part of Russian pilots. And he's emphasized the fact that the, the U.S. will not back down with its use of reconnaissance drones.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: I'll just reiterate that the United States will fly and operate wherever international law allows.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voiceover): Now, the U.S. has said that the Russian fighters dive bomb this reconnaissance drone for a period of a half hour, dumped fuel on it, ultimately struck the propeller forcing the plane to ditch. Russia has had a different take. Russia has said there was never any contact between the aircraft and, and here is the Russian military spokesman.\par \par \uc2 IGOR KONASHENKOV, RUSSIAN DEFENSE MINISTRY SPOKESMAN (through translation): I want to emphasize that the Russian fighters didn't use their weapons, didn't come into contact with the unmanned aerial vehicle, and they safely returned to their base.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And Russia Secretary of the Security Council has stated that they will scramble to gather up the wreckage of the U.S. Reaper drone, and that gives Russia an advantage because the U.S. does not have any warships in the Black Sea -- Russia, of course does. But we just heard from the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  something remarkable saying one, the U.S. can rely on allies to get ships in the water here. But also, the fact that this drone crashed in four to 5000 feet of water, the recovery will be very difficult. Also, he said anything that was of value from this drone was likely destroyed and is no longer of value. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fascinating. Mike, thank you very much. Reporting live from Kyiv. Let's bring in retired Four-Star General Jack Keane, former Vice Chief of Staff for the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 , now Chairman of the Institute for the Study of War, and Fox News Senior Strategic Analyst. General, good to have you with us. What was your takeaway from what you heard there from the Secretary of Defense and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, it's a fact that Russia has been harassing our airplanes for years, and most of them frankly, had been piloted airplanes. And I mean, it's been dangerous and irresponsible and reckless. And we're fortunate, you know, that we haven't had pilots killed as a result of it. Suddenly, this this incident because there's a war going on, you know, is considerably more dramatic as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think probably -- I mean, it's easy to talk tough at what you got to do is you got to be smart and tough in my judgment in dealing with adversaries like this, who like to throw their weight around. I think the answer was probably to fly the drone again today with a fighter cap above it. And you know, tell them Defense Minister I'm going to keep doing that for a period of time here.\par \par \uc2 And I also -- I've never agreed with us not being in the Black Sea with our Navy ships. The Turks shut it down. They said all warships are not going to go through the Bosporus Straits, because of the war that's taking place in Ukraine. But Russia has a beach in the Black Sea, Sevastopol, and they are able to keep their warships there.\par \par \uc2 I think we should have counted that not a long time ago, and say those are international waters, we have a right to be in those international waters and we fully intend to go through the Bosporus Straits when it suits us, and when we believe it's, it's the right thing to do. And the other thing I would do, listen, it's high time that we got these long-range missiles, (INAUDIBLE) that the Ukrainians have been asking for, for almost a year now.\par \par \uc2 And why are they so important? Well, because those (INAUDIBLE) can reach depo and fuel munition bases inside of Crimea, staging areas for Iranian drones and other military capabilities that the Russians have in Crimea, out of the range of our HIMARS. Let's fix this problem once and for all, and use this as a rationale to do it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's General Milley, he was asked whether or not this was an act of war, and then I want to ask you a little bit more about what she said on Crimea. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: As far as an act of war goes, I'm not going to, I'm not going to go there. Incidents happen. And, and clearly, we do not seek armed conflict with, with Russia. And, and I believe that at this point, we should investigate this incident and move on from there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'm curious what you thought about their overall posture and what it sends, what signal does it send to you about the White House's discussions with them before this briefing began about the message that they wanted to send?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, I don't know what was said. And, you know, I mean, I don't think it's an act of war. This is, this is suddenly the sophomore aggressive behavior that Russian pilots have displayed. I mean, my sources told me they made 19 passes at, at the drone. And you, you can imagine a drone, it can go up to 50,000 feet, it can go out at a distance of about 1200 miles, this is a very viable instrument that we've had and it's helped us quite a bit in Afghanistan and Iraq and it's doing so here.\par \par \uc2 And listen, so our audience really understands here, we are providing -- this amount of public record -- we are providing absolute significant intelligence on a daily basis, using these drones and things like it to the Ukrainians on where the Russian troops actually are with a degree of fidelity, and where are they going? So, that's why the Russians are harassing us. So, 19 passes, you go by in a, in a fighter jet.\par \par \uc2 It's like it's standing still. They if they wanted to eliminate it, they would have shot it down and immediately. So, I think when you put this together, I think the right answer was to show some strength by flying it would fight a cap today and also make some other points to them privately. And I'm, I'm hopeful that the Secretary of Defense did that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're hopeful -- do you think, yes, that, that says a lot right there. Thank you very much. Good to see you as always, General Kane.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, good talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. Take care, Sir.\par \par \uc2 So, also breaking right now we're keeping an eye obviously on the markets in the final hour of trading here as bank contagion fears trigger a dive. It is off its lows of the session, but still down 330 points here better than 1 percent. David McCormack served in the Treasury Department under President Bush and as CEO of one of the world's largest hedge funds, he's standing by along with Mark Tapper of \uc2 Strategic Wealth Partners\uc2  when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, we are now in the final hour of trading, and there's major turmoil at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  bank on top of those historic bank failures here in the United States that has rattled the markets, of course, and it spreads fear to regular folks across the country. As they wonder how does this affect me, how does it affect my investments in savings?\par \par \uc2 President Biden has not commented on this today. He's in Las Vegas. So, we're going to keep a close eye on any remarks that he might have about it to reassure the American people, there's a lot going on. And we'll continue to we'll keep an eye on it, as I said and let you know if he does make any comments on that. Fox Business Correspondent Edward Lawrence reporting live from the White House. Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. Yes, so the economy ran I was like a choppy ocean moving a boat up and down. When you take a step forward, you fall in another direction. So, the Producer Price Index came out today and it showed that inflation is easing on the things companies need to buy, and that's good news.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales were released today, and that shows Americans are slowing down their spending more than expected -- that's bad news. Now, you mix in two banks that failed in the last week and talk of \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  close to failing, it means concerns and a big sell off. But this is what you get from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHALANDA YOUNG\uc2 , OMB DIRECTOR: The unemployment rate has fallen to 3.6 percent. One of the lowest rates in over 50 years. We've taken action to lower prescription drug costs, health insurance premiums, and energy bills while driving the uninsured rate to historic lows.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voiceover): So, the President in Las Vegas right now talking about tax increases, he's there live to save Medicare, extend the life solvency 25 years as the market is selling off over banks stability fears. Now, the plan for \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  specifically supported by the Treasury Secretary will make the insured and uninsured depositors whole. That plan, getting different reactions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CYNTHIA LUMMIS (R-WY): They banked to a lot of venture capital risky money. And there were a lot of incentives for risk takers to bank with \uc2 SVB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Because of that, \uc2 SVB\uc2  should be allowed to fail. And those people who put their money at risk there should have to eat the consequences.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): When we roll back regulations, so that bank executives can use these banks to boost their profits, to boost their own salaries, to get big bonuses. They are doing it by taking on more risk.\par \par \uc2 Baking should be boring. And we have a chance here in Congress to make banking more boring again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): And the bailout of that, bank causing some image problems for the president, because the wealthy are the ones who will get the beholden this, as their $250,000 or more than $250,000 in the bank will be recouped by them. Back to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's a great point. Edward, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, we are pleased to bring in David McCormick. He was most recently a candidate for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in his career, he was Second lieutenant in the 82nd Airborne in Iraq. Served in the Treasury Department under President Bush, where he was directly involved in the response to the 2008 financial crisis and the international effort to right the ship of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 He was also the CEO at \uc2 Bridgewater\uc2 , one of the world's largest hedge funds.\par \par \uc2 So, his new book, which came out yesterday, is called, Superpower in Peril, which I think there are a lot of indications of in all of the stories that we're covering today, A Battle Plan to Renew America.\par \par \uc2 Dave McCormick, welcome. It's good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK FORMER TREASURY UNDERSECRETARY: Thanks, Martha. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I mean, pretty much all the breaking news stories that we're covering today, I think I could ask you about. But I do want to focus on this, because you do talk about this in your book, Superpower in Peril.\par \par \uc2 And when you listen to Elizabeth Warren and you listen to \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2  -- Senator Lummis. They have very different observations about what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren seems to think that the government should step in and control everything about banking. And \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2  is saying, why are we, the U.S. taxpayer bailing out these uninsured depositors who understood the risk that they were taking when they made those investments?\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm with Senator Lummis in opposing the bailout. But I think we have to step back and put this in context. What's happening here is unfortunate. I think the canary in the coal mine, it is the result of excess.\par \par \uc2 Excess in policy in terms have low interest rates for an extended period of time, which have continued over the last couple of years, and enormous spending over the last decade. But in the last two years, under President Biden, we've had a huge jump in in spending 40 percent increase in discretionary spending, $19 trillion of incremental spending. And our debt and deficit is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 Those policies have led to inflation, first, 40-year high in inflation and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in responding to that has raised rates, and that's created a crisis in the banking sector because banks had balance sheets, which had long duration. And they have now lost money on those portfolios. And that -- and that's putting deposits at risk and making things shaky.\par \par \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  is a disaster, in particular, because of terrible management. Terrible risk management, and terrible regulatory oversight.\par \par \uc2 So, here you have the San Francisco Federal Reserve that didn't see this. This was very obvious. It was obvious in the financial statements.\par \par \uc2 And so, we got bad behavior, bad regulation, and unfortunately, a bad oversight rather, and unfortunately, a bad response. Because what happened is that the government now has guaranteed the uninsured deposits.\par \par \uc2 And let me just describe very quickly why that's a problem. That's a problem, because if you're the landscaper that had a $10,000 outstanding bill with \uc2 SVB\uc2 , and the credits just got wiped out, you just lost $10,000. That creditor lost $10,000.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, if you're a wealthy venture capitalist that lives down the street and a $10 million mansion, your deposits were completely protected.\par \par \uc2 The second reason is the problem, is now -- it's created moral hazard. There is $8 trillion of uninsured deposits, and certainly, this has set the expectation that those are uninsured too. Or those who are insured too is - - as a result of this, and that's going to encourage riskier behavior.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: And that's part of the reason we're seeing the market volatility today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And so, you know, you've got regular folks across the country, looking at this, and they are saying, you know, I didn't take this kind of risk. And now, I'm going to have to figure out, you know, there is always an offer on effect from this kind of bailout. Why should I have to suffer?\par \par \uc2 What do you -- what should the president say to regular investors and folks out there today? He -- there is no indication he's going to say anything about addressing this when he speaks in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 And should they be worried, Dave?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, it is sort of -- yes, they should be worried in the following sense. That picture I painted of inflation, the Fed raising rates -- that's an environment that's very tough for all Americans, but particularly working people.\par \par \uc2 Because inflation affects you on a day-to-day basis if you're living week to week and on fixed income. So, he's got to address those problems.\par \par \uc2 Those problems are the root causes of the crisis. Confidence is the key in the banking business. So, people have to believe that their deposits are safe.\par \par \uc2 The big banks have huge balance sheets, they are safe. The reason you're seeing that uncertainty in the regional banks is because people are pulling out their deposits and moving them either into treasuries, T-Bills or bigger banks where they think they're safe. And that's the problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Dave McCormick, thank you very much. It's good to have you here. A lot of experience in a lot of different areas that touch upon this.\par \par \uc2 And your book, Superpower in Peril, maps out a lot of ways to try to renew America and unwind some of these mistakes and get us back on track.\par \par \uc2 So, it's good to have you here. We hope to see you again soon. Thank you very much. Congratulations on the book.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now is Mark Tepper Strategic Wealth Partners, president and CEO.\par \par \uc2 Mark, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MARK TEPPER, CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, \uc2 STRATEGIC WEALTH PARTNERS\uc2 : Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I guess we're supposed to feel good that the market is off its lows.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Actually, starting to dip again down about 400 and change right now.\par \par \uc2 How much should people be worried about this situation?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: So, I think the fear is with regards to our larger banks. I think they are overblown.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the J.P. Morgans, the \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , is they're well- capitalized, they run prudent risk management practices, unlike \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Where the issue lies is with the smaller regional banks. In my opinion, the next shoe to drop is going to be commercial real estate loans. OK.\par \par \uc2 So, when we look at commercial real estate loans, a lot of them are variable. So, they're going to have to be refinanced now at higher rates, on office space, maybe Class B, Class C office space that is much more vacant. So, appraisals are going to come in a heck of a lot lower.\par \par \uc2 So, what I would expect to happen over the course of the next several months -- this few years, possibly, is delinquency and default rates on those buildings is going to go up significantly.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the regional banks versus like a \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , those commercial real estate loans make up about 30 percent of regional banks assets, only five percent of like a \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 's assets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: So, big difference.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here is what I'm hearing and what you're saying. Tell me if I'm wrong. A, you're going to see a lot of people moving their money into some of these larger banks.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's going to put a lot of pressure on these small regional banks. I'm also hearing that most of this goes back to the solution, which wasn't really a solution from the pandemic, because you've got an enormous amount of money.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just, just flood the market with money to keep things going during the pandemic. Then, let's have everybody stay home from work.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And many of them have never gone back. That's commercial, real estate's in trouble.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: And then what happens when no more money starts -- continues coming in? And that's precisely what happened with \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The -- all of the money stopped coming in, and they had to invest in long dated treasuries. So, that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 But with regards to money coming, moving from the smaller regional banks to the bigger banks, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  has already seen $15 billion of inflows over the last few days.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  is probably seen more than that. I just don't have the data. But you're seeing a lot of that happen. So, the regional banks are under pressure. The big banks are -- they're too big to fail. We know that. I mean, we saw that the last go round in 2008.\par \par \uc2 But they are a heck of a lot better position --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, why are we bailing out \uc2 SVB\uc2 ? Why are we bailing out these banks that took these enormous risks?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: And I'm glad you're -- I'm glad you're calling --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what's the lesson in it?\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: I'm glad you're calling it a bailout, because President Biden is avoiding calling it a bailout. He's and he is also telling us that the banks, the other banks are going to pay this.\par \par \uc2 We're not dumb. We know that the interest we're going to collect on our deposits is going to go down, the interest we're going to pay on our car loans is going to go up. We may not see that we're paying for it.\par \par \uc2 But you and I are -- and everyone watching, we're all going to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  warned about \uc2 SVB\uc2  back in November. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  was also concerned about this bank. As David McCormick said, where were the regulators? Where was the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank in San Francisco\uc2 ? This is the definition of their jobs. They're supposed to keep an eye on these institutions to make sure that the little guy doesn't get banged up in these situation.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: \uc2 SVB\uc2  senior management has been lobbying to increase the -- there's a $250 billion limit where regulation goes up a notch.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: And they've stayed under that radar and their senior management has been lobbying to boost that number up. So, bad intentions,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unreal. And OK. And no consequences that we can see so far.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: No.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark Tapper. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TEPPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And our thanks to Dave McCormick for joining us today as well.\par \par \uc2 So, students at an all-girls college sending a message that trans men should be accepted on campus in a move that could rattle single gender schools, boys, girls, girls' school -- schools all across the country down to the college level, the high school level. And we're going to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking right now, a hearing in Texas is now over in a case -- in a case with nationwide and immediate implications for access to abortion pills in America, which is actually the majority of the abortions that take place in the country are done by medication.\par \par \uc2 Professor Jonathan Turley standby as we await the ruling.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're waiting for a big ruling. Any minute now, a federal judge could put sales of an abortion pill on hold nationwide.\par \par \uc2 A study shows that more than half of all the abortions that take place in America are now done mostly by mail order or prescription pills that people take two doses of in the comfort of their own home.\par \par \uc2 So, justice correspondent David Spunt joins us now reporting live on this from the Department of Justice. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. The judge asked some tough questions on both sides of this polarizing issue. This hearing today lasted a little bit more than four hours. Ended about 45 minutes ago. And as you said, we could get a ruling at any time now, though, he did not technically rule from the bench inside the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mifepristone is the first pill in a two-pill regimen to end an abortion. Despite it being on the market, almost a quarter century, a local group in Texas sued the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , arguing the drug can cause complications, and the approval was rushed in 2,000.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's possible that this judge could issue a nationwide injunction, temporarily stopping sales across the country, even in states where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's a second drug called Misoprostol that's used after Mefepristone. In other countries, it's the only medication abortion used but has serious side effects when taken alone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENISE HARLE, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: We want these dangerous drugs taken off the market and for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to do its job, which is to protect women and girls from dangerous drugs like this. It's something that should have happened a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: This is a look outside the court in Amarillo, Texas this morning, a group outside protesting against this group. Now Judge Matthew Kaczmarek was appointed in 2019 by then President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He heard the case today. Opponents of the lawsuit argue that Judge Kaczmarek should not be overruling scientists at the Food and Drug Administration who determined more than 20 years ago that this medication was safe. Listen.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Most Americans could look in their medicine cabinet where they will find medication prescribed by a doctor that they use on a daily basis and have available to them because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  engaged in a process of determining the efficacy and safety of that medication. Mefepristone is no exception to that process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: OK. Let's say, Martha, the judge does issue an injunction on Mefepristone. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  will appeal almost immediately. What's not clear will the \uc2 FDA\uc2  appeal to the Circuit Court of Appeals or go right to the Supreme Court, which puts the abortion issue right back in front of the Supreme Court not even a year after it overturned Roe v. Wade, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's interesting. Once it became clear, I think, that such a majority of these procedures were done this way, these drugs became sort of on the front line. And we'll see what this judge decides about that in this very consequential decision. David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David Spunt at the Justice Department. And students at Wellesley, one of the original seven sister colleges, very prestigious American institution, an all women's college, there aren't that many of them out there anymore, calling for historic change, allowing trans men to apply to Wellesley, women who are transitioning to be men in that category. But the college is now pushing back, saying that it won't change their admission policy. Telling The Hill, "Although there is no plan to revisit its mission as a women's college on the admissions or its admissions policy, the college will continue to engage all students, including transgender, male and non-binary students in the important work of building an inclusive academic community where everyone feels they belong.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Jonathan Turley, George Washington University Law Professor, constitutional attorney and Fox News Contributor. Good to have you with us today, Jonathan. Obviously, these are institutions that have been around for a very long time. \uc2 Wellesley College\uc2  is very proud of the fact that they support women and that they were founded for that purpose, to try to help women get a great education. And they -- and I suspect many other single sex institutions are going to find themselves facing a lot of these questions in the coming months and years.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that's true, Martha, and this has many of the elements that we see across academia. There is an obvious argument that one of the Seven Sisters should be limited to sisters, should be limited to people who are living the life of a woman in society. That's the premise of this educational model.\par \par \uc2 The university president simply made that point and was immediately attacked by her own student newspapers being transphobic. And that's what we've seen in so many other debates, that is the minute you raise this question, you are attacked as discriminatory. We saw that with the author, JK Rowling, of the "Harry Potter" series fame. She's been attacked relentlessly because she feels that some transgender policies undermine feminism and the strides that have been made on behalf of women.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. As I mentioned, Jonathan, it's easy to see this moving into and we sent our kids one to a coed high school, one to an all girls, one to all boys. Each one of these options have really great opportunities for different kinds of kids. But I have spoken with people at these institutions who say they see this coming, right? They see this challenge coming where they're going to be asked to admit students who are not -- who don't fit the mission of that institution. And I think that do you see this issue eventually, maybe heading even to the Supreme Court, how institutions will be -- have the freedom to uphold their mission in the face of all of this?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, the Seven Sisters obviously were premised on what is technically a discriminatory act. They would not allow male applicants and students at their schools, but they did that because they wanted to create this special place for women to be educated. It's a long tradition in the United States. This could ultimately be challenged depending on the trajectory of these rights as at what point will this become just unacceptable forms of discrimination.\par \par \uc2 What the president of this college was trying to say is that, we have a unique history here and a unique mission. And while she supports trans rights, this is endangering the identity of the school. But the problem is that you can't have a civil debate anymore. The immediately response was to accuse her of being transphobic as well as the board, and to sort of cut off further debate. We're seeing that across the board.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what we have to really look to is that the vast majority of us want to have this debate to discuss the extent and limitation on these types of rights. These are new emerging issues. We're not going to have that if this is nothing but a scream session as we on both sides yelling at each other.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Jonathan, thank you very much. Jonathan Turley joining us today. It's always good to have you, sir.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. So House Republicans headed to the southern border to get answers about what is happening there. And in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. AUGUST LEE PFLUGER (R-TX): Do you see the cartels acting more brazenly? And in fact, let's go a step further. Do you see them employing terroristic tactics?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MCCRAW\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : They've been employing terrorist tactics for years. This is nothing new. Kidnappings of Americans is nothing new. Kidnappings, extortions, torture, these things go back decades to the Mexican cartels. So I don't mean to be gratuitous, it's just not covered in Mexico. And the reason it's not, I mean, being a journalist is not a good position to be in Mexico writing about cartel violence, plain and simple ill-targeted. And I don't care if it's a blogger or a professional journalist or a judge or a police officer, no one's exempt from the cartels and their level of brutality.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very dangerous work. Correspondent Bill Melugin joins us now live from the border in far Texas. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon to you. There were no Democrats here today to listen to the testimony of the chief of the US Border Patrol. And that chief said there has to be consequences for illegal immigration, prosecutions and deportations. And he was directly asked, do we have control of our border? Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: Chief Ortiz, does DHS have operational control of our entire border?\par \par \uc2 RAUL ORTIZ, US BORDER PATROL CHIEF: No, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Now, the chief's answer obviously directly contradicts what we've heard in the past from DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas who has repeatedly said the border is secure and we do have operational control. Chief Ortiz was also asked about how he feels about the Biden administration ending all border wall construction upon taking office. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JOSH BRECHEEN (R-OK): So under the prior administration, we had 200 plus wall that was appropriated, and the President of the United States, President Biden, by executive order, shut that down. Do you disagree with his decision to shut down the construction?\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And again, there were no Democrats at this hearing today. They boycotted it, accused the Republicans of playing politics. Republicans not happy about that. They say they are appalled that their colleagues on the other side of the aisle did not show up today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DALE STRONG (R-AL): The thing I'm most distraught about, there's not a Democrat here. This is an American issue. It's appalling that the Democrats didn't show up and America is under attack.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: DHS released a statement to Fox News writing in part, "Today's hearing highlights the vital work the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  does every day to enforce our laws, secure our border, and combat cartels and smugglers, despite inheriting a dismantled immigration system and facing unprecedented migration that is affecting nations throughout the Western Hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 And, Martha, back out here live. We learned something new from Chief Ortiz's testimony today. He said that in the last roughly six months alone since the new fiscal year began on October 1st, there have already been 385,000 known gotaways at our border. That now puts us up to 1.4 million gotaways since the beginning of fiscal year 2021. Jaw dropping numbers. I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And where did all those people go in the country? We have no idea who they are or why they're here, or where they ended up, Bill Melugin, and also stunning that the border chief says that they -- the border control people do not have control of the border, which is very obvious in a lot of your reporting, Bill, and all of the video that you have been able to shoot for us down there. Thank you very much, Bill Melugin, reporting today from Texas.\par \par \uc2 This stunning story, awful story, a 13-year-old Texas girl was abducted by a 34-year-old man who she was talking to online. She was later discovered in this shed thousands of miles away in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHIE SIMMONS, DAVIDSON COUNTRY, NC SHERIFF: Messages (inaudible) send out from the Davison County Sheriff's Office is how dangerous these platforms, these chat platforms are. As parents, they've got to wise up and see that the dangers to these kids, it just continues to go on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He said. Parents must wise up and see the dangers that social media harbors for young children after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a terrifying story. A 13-year-old girl from Texas was found locked in a shed in North Carolina. She's 13 years old. She was more than a thousand miles away. Her 34-year-old alleged captor accused of grooming the teen, building a relationship with her over social media, and now faces half a dozen felony charges, including human trafficking and, sadly, the rape of a child under the age of 15.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Davidson County, North Carolina Sheriff Richie Simmons, but first to senior correspondent Laura Ingle with the backstory here from New York City. Hi, Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Indeed, this is a terrifying example about the warnings of online predators we all hear about. A young girl enticed by a stranger online, taken away from her family and kept against her will far away from home.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to investigators, the 13-year-old girl was found locked up inside a garden shed in Lexington, North Carolina that a 34-year-old man was living in. Investigators were able to quickly locate her after identifying a vehicle from video cameras in the same area of the girl's home along with tracking her online communications with the suspect. 34- year-old Jorge Camacho was taken into custody after police pulled him over and determined the underage girl was locked up in his shed.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was a makeshift living space at best. Police found a mattress covered in plastic, a TV, board game and a few food items. The owner of the property said she knew Camacho through her son, but could have never imagined he'd be accused of something like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TROP SIMM, PROPERTY OWNER: I was very surprised because I didn't know what was going on. My son was his friend. He used to live here but he got married, moved to Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGLE: While Camacho was charged with a list offenses including abduction, human trafficking and statutory rape, he is being held on over a million dollars bond. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says on any given day, there's about between 500,000 and 700,000 predators online looking to target children. Online safety experts say that is why it is so important to talk with your kids about who they are communicating with and what is being discussed, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Boy, you can say that again. You have to know what your children are doing on their phones and have as much transparency as you possibly can to keep them safe. Laura, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Davidson County, North Carolina Sheriff Richie Simmons. His department's emergency response team was able to rescue this girl and save her life from this shed thousand miles away. Sheriff, good to have you here. And congratulations to you and your team for reuniting this girl with her family. We hope that she can heal and recover from this horrific experience. How did you do this so quickly, sheriff?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : We were contacted by the Dallas FBI Violent Crime Task Force about 6:00. We mobilized some undercover units to sit on the house because we didn't know where the young girl was. So we worked very quickly. We were able to stabilize the area and actually after we did that, got more units in the area, he actually -- the target actually left the location and we're able to stop him, make a vehicle stop on him and get him in custody.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Incredible. How much did -- I know that Laura Ingle reported that the mom said that she was able to give the password and the code to her daughter's phone. How important was using that phone in tracking this girl down?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : We had similar case like this in 2021 where the girl was taken from a small town here in Davidson and was located in Arkansas. It happened to us so many times. It's so important and it gives us the ability to track these phones in our forensic labs that we did on her. They were able to do it here and it was really nice that we got to pay back the favor to Texas and locate this girl for them, for her family.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What would you tell parents they should know and do about their children's cellphones, and obviously to not communicate with strangers in this way? But how important is it to have the technology and the access to your kid's phones?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : It's holly, you know, I wish we had more classes to teach parents because the kids seems to be a lot smarter at this area than the parents because it's their generation. There's apps out here, one app in particular called Bark that parents can use to set up and monitor, help them monitor what their kids are viewing, what they're watching. These kids have no right to privacy, they're your kids. And I've raised three myself, and they may not like me but I did what a parent should do. I got in their lives.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Those are very wise words, sheriff. And Parents have to be parents, not friends, to their kids when it comes to their interactions online. Thank you, sheriff, and congratulations. We're so glad you were able to reunite this girl with her family.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: and we wish her well. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Thank you so much for making this, bringing this out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. That is one of our big goals here on THE STORY and we continue that here. Some young women now comparing unregulated social media use to a weapon of mass destruction for their own generation. Correspondent Gillian Turner was the latest on her series on teen mental health and the role that social media plays in it. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. So, Aly Drake, take a look at her. She's a 19-year-old diagnosed with bipolar disorder. She tells us that being a teenage girl today means that your whole life is lived on social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALY DRAKE, 19-YEAR-OLD DIAGNOSED WITH BIPOLAR DISORDER: No one's ever asked me for my number. It's always been like, hey, do you have \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 ? Without social media, you would probably never really understand what people are even talking about in conversations because a lot of time it is about pop culture and social media.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Like many girls her age, Aly posts about her struggles on TikTok in order to connect with other people, even when she admits screen time makes her feel bad about herself. American teens rack up an average of four hours per day on social media outside of school, some spending nearly two hours on TikTok and an hour-and-a-half on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  alone. Girls' social media apps of choice TikTok, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 , they're likelier than boys to use all three.\par \par \uc2 Last month, teen activist Emma Lembke told Congress that social media apps are weapons of mass destruction for young people. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 EMMA LEMBKE, TEEN ACTIVIST: Right now as the status quo creates it, I, a 12-year-old girl, could go onto \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and research a healthy recipe and within seconds be fed pro anorexic content.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Dr. Delaney Ruston, who's the creator of Hit documentary Screenager, says that girls are exposed to very different content on social media than boys, particularly what she calls looks based content.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DELANEY RUSTON, INTERNAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST: Girls are socially inclined to higher levels of self comparisons, low self esteem related to body image.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Experts tell us that girls should beware of beauty tutorials trends like that Girl and Vanilla Girl, which promote elite lifestyles also to be wary of thinspo, which promotes anorexia and bulimia. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Boy, it is a wild and dangerous world out there and really tough for parents to navigate. Gillian, thank you very much for this important series. That can be really helpful to parents. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We hope so. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. So coming up, we are going to show you a clip of never before seen footage of the attack on our Fox News team covering the war in Ukraine, struck by incoming fire on the outskirts of Kyiv, severely injuring our correspondent Ben Hall and killing our beloved Fox News photojournalist Pierre Zakrzewski and Ukrainian journalist Sasha Kuvshynova. A preview of the documentary that I have just watched called "Sacrifice and Survival," a story from the Front Line is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: We thought were going five minutes around the corner to film these abandoned villages. There was a bombed out gas station, filmed that for a little bit. I sad you want to go see a little bit further? And so, we went a little bit further.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the market is all over the place, down about 300 points right now as we head towards the close. The banking fears have sent the Dow on a real roller coaster today, so we'll keep a close eye on that. And tomorrow, a really special day here on THE STORY. We'll be joined by Fox correspondent Benjamin Hall and his wife Alicia to talk about the family side of their story.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to show you never before seen video and footage of the harrowing extraction from the war zone and the difficult recovery that followed. That all airs on Fox Nation's documentary, but we're going to have special clips of it for you tomorrow here --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALL: Someone comes up to me, walks into the room quickly with my state department badge.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD JADICK, MILITARY DOCTOR: I said, hey, Ben, I'm Dr. Jadick, Rich Jadick. I'm here to help get you out of here.\par \par \uc2 HALL: He says, Benji, you want to go? I just say, straight away, let's go.\par \par \uc2 JADICK: He goes, about right now.\par \par \uc2 HALL: He offered me a way out when I was going home. If you asked me if I was going to crawl out of there, I would have crawled out of there, whatever it took.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a very emotional story and we will have clips of it for you tomorrow with his wife, Alicia. We will see you then. Have a great afternoon, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230316ej3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SVB\uc2  Collapse: A Warning Sign Of What Is To Come; \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  Is The Largest US Bank Failure; Biden Administration Drains Strategic Petroleum Reserve; Democrat Lawmaker Uses Hearing To Call A Witness A White Supremacist Instead Of Talking About Issues; Lower Hiring Standards Put Us All At Risk; South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  Vetoes Central Bank Digital Currency Bill; Our Media Has Been Pushing Race Hatred For Years; Student Punished For Standing Up For His Beliefs\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson \par \uc2 7,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Tucker is up next and always remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 We are starting to get a better, a more precise sense of what it means when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  brags about the strongest and most equitable economic recovery in modern history. What it means is it could be time to buy gold and stockpile food. Oh, there's a deal on Russian steel case, let me go pick up a pallet. Just kidding, sort of.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, some of the biggest banks in this country -- Wells Fargo, Bank of America, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  , and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  collectively lost more than $50 billion in market value in one day. That's quite a hit.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, those banks still exist, and you can't say that for \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  . As of this morning, \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  or \uc2 SVB\uc2  has gone under completely. That makes the second biggest bank failure in the history of this country, and that's significant.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  financed nearly half of all venture-backed healthcare and technology companies in the United States. It also apparently held significant cash reserves for some of the biggest cryptocurrencies, and it's now gone.\par \par \uc2 Federal regulators have renamed it and taken it over and that means an awful lot of people lost an awful lot of money and no, most of that money was not insured, no matter what they tell you. The \uc2 FDIC\uc2  only guarantees bank deposits up to $250,000.00 and according to some reports, more than 90 percent of all deposits at \uc2 SVB\uc2  exceeded that and it's unclear whether those people will ever see their money again.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when customers showed up at \uc2 SVB\uc2  's branch in Manhattan today to get their deposits back, managers called the police.\par \par \uc2 So, what we have here is a 1929 style bank run and that's not a good sign for anyone. The question is whether the people who run \uc2 SVB\uc2  saw it coming. The CEO, a man called \uc2 Greg Becker\uc2  , apparently sold more than $2 million in bank stock over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 According to the site, "Unusual Whales," several other high-level employees of \uc2 SVB\uc2  , including Chief Marketing Officer \uc2 Michelle Draper\uc2  , Chief Operations Officer \uc2 Phil Cox\uc2  , General Counsel \uc2 Michael Zuckert\uc2  , all sold significant amounts of stock in \uc2 SVB\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Did those employees know their bank was in trouble? We don't know and once again, where were the regulators? They're supposed to prevent this. Once again, we don't know, and the business press who is supposed to be telling ordinary people what's happening with business, apparently, nobody noticed anything.\par \par \uc2 In fact, as with the \uc2 FTX\uc2  crypto scam, you may recall, self-described financial experts in the media were busy promoting \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  as a great investment that would last forever.\par \par \uc2 The formerly great, but now quite embarrassing "Forbes" Magazine, in fact, named \uc2 SVB\uc2  to its list of America's best banks, not once, but five years in a row. And of course, inevitably you saw this coming, here is professional BS artist/dumb person, \uc2 Jim Cramer\uc2  of CNBC telling his viewers to buy \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  even though it was in the process of going under. This is last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM CRAMER\uc2  , CNBC: \uc2 SVB Financial\uc2  , this company is a merchant bank with a deposit base that Wall Street had been mistakenly concerned about.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  is the old \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  , recently bought one of our favorite research firms Moffett Nathanson and it's become less dependent on private equity and venture capitalist offerings.\par \par \uc2 Wait a second, those dried up last year, they could come back.\par \par \uc2 Long-term private equity and venture capital, they're not going away, being a banker to these immense pools of capital has always been a very good business.\par \par \uc2 SVB\uc2  's nearly 40 percent rally this year is barely a drop in the bucket\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: \uc2 SVB\uc2  is great. If that guy ever endorses anything you're doing, move to the Canary Islands, change your name because disaster is coming, but just like \uc2 FTX\uc2  , all the geniuses told you to buy \uc2 SVB\uc2  , even when it was on the brink of total collapse. What explains that? Surely something does. We don't really know at this point. Hopefully we will someday.\par \par \uc2 What we can say tonight is that \uc2 SVB\uc2  , like the scammers at \uc2 FTX\uc2  , had quite the public relations department. We're not some greedy finance outfit that exists solely to generate cash for overpaid sleaze balls who run it. No, that's not us. We may be a bank, we've got the soul of an NGO. We care. We're saving the world and to prove it.\par \par \uc2 There's a whole page on \uc2 SVB\uc2  's website about their plans to "monitor and reduce our own carbon emissions." Yes, because you want a bank that cares about carbon emissions and of course, there's endless posturing because it's all free about diversity, equity and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 Marisa Phan, the Managing Director at \uc2 SVB\uc2  , wrote this on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  just three weeks before the bank collapsed, "Feeling inspired and energized after hosting an extraordinary group of pioneering and dynamic women in \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  's Female Founder and VC Tahoe Ski Retreat."\par \par \uc2 Adjectives are free. They use a bunch of them and then she attached pictures of the ski trip showing the group of these pioneering and dynamic female bankers having a great time because, of course, they're having a great time because few things are more empowering to oppressed female bankers than skiing in Tahoe.\par \par \uc2 And they kept this crap up to the very end.\par \par \uc2 As their bank began to slip beneath the waves, \uc2 SVB\uc2  posted this on its social media feeds: "As part of Women's History Month, we're celebrating founders of women led companies like Suma Wealth's, Beatriz Acevedo," a company that \uc2 SVB\uc2  described lovingly as "focused on closing the Latinx wealth gap."\par \par \uc2 It's the problem with Latinx. There's a wealth gap and there was a video, too. Of course, there was.\par \par \uc2 So, now that \uc2 SVB\uc2  has blown up and lost billions of other people's money in the process, we thought we would show you the video because, oh, we can't resist. Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEATRIZ ACEVEDO, CEO AND CO-FOUNDER, SUMA WEALTH: My name is Beatriz Acevedo and I am the CEO and co-founder of Suma Wealth.\par \par \uc2 I was born in Tijuana, Mexico. I am a very proud border girl who had the privilege to grow up with the best of both worlds.\par \par \uc2 I went to school in San Diego. I went to \uc2 UCSD\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I have a lot of scars from my days going across the border. I remember I got this memo and it just said that all my shows were cancelled, and it was a time where women, never men, were put into something they called their refrigerator.\par \par \uc2 If they felt like you had a lot of power, they would just put you there to cool you off for a while.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Unaddressed in the video is whether she's actually a capable banker. We hope so. We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 We know the guys at \uc2 SVB\uc2  were not capable bankers. They didn't seem to spend a lot of time banking or paying close attention because they had videos to make about dynamic, pioneering, glass-ceiling shattering women.\par \par \uc2 So, when interest rates were at historic lows, \uc2 SVB\uc2  bought more than 20 billion in long-term Treasury bonds and to be fair, they were encouraged by the government to do this. All banks were.\par \par \uc2 The problem was when interest rates went up as they were always going to, because they can't stay at zero forever -- did you know that? Well, when that happened, those bonds lost their value and that, of course, was a disaster for a ton of banks, but a true disaster for \uc2 SVB\uc2  and then a disaster for a lot of individuals who had their money there and companies that had borrowed money from \uc2 SVB\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So, you can expect a lot of bankruptcies in the coming weeks due to this and then the countless downstream effects from those bankruptcies, all of them bad. This is a disaster, not just for the tech industry, people who we could easily make fun of, but for everyone, for all of us.\par \par \uc2 But the interesting thing is the Biden administration doesn't seem to care at all. They're not that interested because numbers, whether you're making money or losing money or doing something efficiently, you're doing it sloppily doesn't really matter as long as you're making videos about pioneering female bankers and as long as they get to ski in Tahoe. That's the real concern.\par \par \uc2 So, just today, Biden's top economic adviser, \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  , addressed reporters in the White House in the Briefing Room about what had just happened and she assured everybody that the bank run we're watching is an isolated incident.\par \par \uc2 No, it's not a big deal, it is just this one bank. It's not connected to anything else at all and the fact that this bank failed, just like the fact that \uc2 FTX\uc2  failed, has nothing to do with the fact that the people who run it spend a ton of their time doing completely irrelevant crap that had to do with politics and changing American culture.\par \par \uc2 Were you reassured by that? We should know that \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  has been thinking a lot about this. When she worked at Princeton, which is a university in New Jersey, she wrote her research agenda on diversity, equity, and inclusion. The title is, and we are quoting, "Diversity in the Economics Profession: A New Attack on an Old Problem." Because the color of your economists is essential. Their competence? Not so much. Just so you know.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, these are the consequences of that attitude. See South Africa, where they don't have electricity tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coincidentally or not, that is the very same topic, diversity in economics, that our Director of our National Economic Council, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2  , is also fixated on. Another genius.\par \par \uc2 When Brainard was at the Fed in 2021, she wasn't too concerned about future bank collapses due to the insane and reckless Fed policy she was overseeing. No, why worry about that because there's climate change? That's what she was really worried about, climate change: "As part of our prudential and financial stability responsibilities, we are developing scenario analysis to model the possible financial risks associated with climate change and assess the resilience of individual financial institutions in the financial system to those risks."\par \par \uc2 Just a pause for one second. Anyone who writes that way is a moron, M-O-E- R-O-N, moron. So, if you find someone in a position of authority, particularly economics, who cannot write a declarative sentence, be prepared for your banks to fail.\par \par \uc2 We should be honest that ESG and equity are only part of the problem here. Stupidity is a huge part of the problem. \uc2 SVB\uc2  and \uc2 FTX\uc2  for that matter are just two of several major players in the financial industry that have gone under in recent months. Are you noticing any of this?\par \par \uc2 There was the bank \uc2 Silvergate\uc2  , more than $11 billion in assets. There was a crypto broker, \uc2 Voyager\uc2  , a billion in assets, the crypto fund 3AC, $10 billion in assets, Block-fi and a number of others.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  customers reported they didn't get their paychecks as expected. The bank says that was just a computer glitch. We don't really know, but we're paying attention.\par \par \uc2 What is very clear is that we're witnessing something serious happening in the finance sector and in other sectors of our economy. We don't know where any of this is going. We're certainly praying for the best. We don't want to see hysteria. But if you're the Treasury Secretary of the United States, you would be very concerned. You'd be paying really close attention because that's your job.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is not paying close attention. She's not worried about it because it's in this country. She's worried instead about funding the pensions of Ukrainian government workers. She flew to Kyiv the other day to tell us that, and as always, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is highly focused on her core duties as Treasury Secretary. That would be equity, climate change and abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , US TREASURY SECRETARY: The Biden-Harris administration has made racial equity a centerpiece of our economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade and access to reproductive health care, including abortion, helped lead to increased labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, and I feel the same way too, believe that climate change is an existential threat that absolutely must be addressed.\par \par \uc2 The message I bring you from President Biden is simple. America will stand with Ukraine for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay. So, she is very upset about Ukraine and we don't have enough abortions and equity, equity, abortion, and Ukraine. Okay. Got it. Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 The children hour is over. It's time for the adults because we have actual problems, we might even call them systemic problems with our economy and so when you have those, you want smart, wise people with foresight and self- control and the ability to focus on things of actual consequence, not on the fashionable crap that occupies most of our time here on cable news, but in the long-term stuff that will ensure your country remains strong and prosperous when you are gone, which for most of these people won't be long because they're very, very old.\par \par \uc2 Where are those people? Where are the adults? Here you have the second biggest bank failure in American history, the biggest since 2008, the second largest failure in our nation's history and they're yammering on about racial equity. Really? Yes, they are because nobody in Washington at this point even acknowledges economics is real unless it can be tied somehow to climate change or racism.\par \par \uc2 Here is Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  of Saint Louis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): My Republican colleagues have unironically invited a for-profit think tank and oil titan whose expertise is in maximizing profits, especially at the expense of our Black, our Brown, and our indigenous neighbors' health, safety and well-being.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I joined Ranking Member Raskin and all of my Democratic Oversight colleagues in calling on Oversight Republicans to denounce White nationalism and denounce White supremacy in all its forms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: White supremacy, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Yes, great. That's fine and we get peace and prosperity. You can handle some dumb people at the helm and it's kind of amusing. They're fun to laugh at, but if things start going south, the dumb people have to leave and they should be replaced by the smart, wise people who have some idea of how to fix things.\par \par \uc2 But those people have not yet arrived. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just introduced a $7 trillion budget. Oh, a budget? That's funding, that's money, right? That should address the core economic concerns that every person can feel. Most people don't understand it. Everybody can feel it.\par \par \uc2 So, the $7 trillion budget mentions "climate change" 148 times. He mentions "equity," never defined, 63 times, "environmental justice" 25 times and cross-dressing, now known as transgenderism, eight times. Feel better? Are the adults back? No.\par \par \uc2 But it will add $20 trillion to the national debt over ten years and that means more inflation and more interest rate hikes and more bank failures. This could be very, very, very serious. We hope it's not. We hope it ends today, an isolated story, but it very much could be. But our leaders don't care. In fact, they seem to be doing everything they can to accelerate it, and that's not reassuring.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Pomboy is an economist. She founded MacroMavens where she is the CEO. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So can you just give us your coolest, most attached assessment of what we're seeing? This seems like a big deal, is it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE POMBOY, FOUNDER, MACROMAVENS: Well, I mean, I can't believe that you're not allayed by listening to Treasury Secretary Yellen. I mean, clearly, she's got the situation in hand.\par \par \uc2 I also am just stunned, Tucker, that you don't believe that diversity, equity and inclusion are the root of a sound financial system. I mean, come on, where have you been for the last year, Tucker?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is time the kids to be quiet, and like, let's get some real economist here, who aren't just like repeating "Atlantic" articles. This is insane.\par \par \uc2 POMBOY: It is absolutely insane. And in the layers of insanity, I mean, I can't even believe the videos you showed about that the people at \uc2 SVB\uc2  , you know, focused on the women's ski vacations and whatnot. It's just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, what we're facing right now is really serious. I mean, we are on the brink of a 2008 style financial crisis and I'm not trying to be hyperbolic. In fact, you and I discussed this at length in our long-form interview several months ago, and at the time, the Fed hadn't even raised rates nearly as high as they have today.\par \par \uc2 And I was saying, look, you know, raise rates in record fashion on an economy toning record leverage at maximum speculation and expect no consequence.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this was clearly going to happen and now we're seeing the weak links in the chain break. You know, the areas where speculation was most rampant and most egregious are clearly coming down and they are doing so as they seem to do in all the three letter acronyms just like they did in 2008. You know, we're back to the three letter acronyms and there'll be a lot more of those.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, I think, you know, this is the unintended consequence of the Fed's monetary policy, layered with really bad fiscal policy on top of it, but essentially, they've been encouraging people take reckless risks for years, just because you had no alternative, you could get zero percent sitting in a T bill or having your money in the bank, or you could run out and you could speculate, and that's what they've done, and that's all coming back to bite them hard.\par \par \uc2 And we've got some major consequences coming at us, and I think it's going to devolve very rapidly, because of all the leverage that's been built up here.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's exactly right. If you treated your children like the Fed has treated Wall Street, your kids would all be in rehab. You know, what they have done is bad. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Pomboy. I appreciate your wisdom on this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 POMBOY: Thank you, Tucker. Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you want to know what's actually happening, ignore the noise. Oh, transgender, equity. Okay, fine. Look at the things that