ure" and we're happy to have him join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And for your measured and very smart response to \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  . The whole thing made me worry, though, because her lack of seriousness was just so obvious. I mean, she doesn't, I don't think understand even what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: I mean, you know, I really spent a lot of time preparing for this hearing, looking at every detail of the SPR, oil security, thinking of every counter argument possible to see if I might be wrong about something. And then what they do is -- what do they do? They go to Kinko's and print out this poster of something I said in college that they misinterpret, because I said "Freedom is superior." And they think that's racist.\par \par \uc2 So this is really childish, and really embarrassing,\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, and also really dangerous. I mean, you said it in the clip that we played. You've been around the world, you've looked at how other countries have collapsed. If you have people making energy policy who are that frivolous and political, that partisan, like what happens?\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: Well, I mean, look at Europe, right? I mean, Europe, their response to the shale revolution, the greatest energy achievement of our time was to ban fracking. So they saw it and said, let's ban it, and look at them. They have this huge dependence on Russia. We have a global energy crisis. There are poor nations who can't get electricity, can't produce electricity, because Europe is buying up the natural gas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this should be a wake-up call, and instead, these guys are using the exact same playbook.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's really distressing and I appreciate again, your coolness under pressure there. Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN: And I did, by the way, Tucker, I did offer them all -- I gave this to you, and I know you