ti-White agenda of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 In his e-mail, which is virtually illiterate, Suarez announces that DEI is now the guiding idea at \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  . The word "safety" never makes an appearance.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge change in the way that airlines operate and Suarez seems to understand that some people who work at Southwest will be nervous about it.\par \par \uc2 But Juan Suarez has a solution. Southwest pilots are not allowed to, "resent or question" the skills of colleagues who have been promoted on the basis of strict race and gender based affirmative action quotas. Henceforth, pilots must ignore incompetence. That's going to solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 But will it solve the problem? Not allowing people to notice that their copilots are incompetent? Well, as one veteran Southwest pilot put it: "A core tenant of commercial aviation is to avoid distractions and prioritize tasks that foster a safe flight. Now, the company is purposefully distracting pilots with something completely unrelated to the safety of flight. This is criminal negligence. Spending millions of dollars on this when we're having runway incursions and near misses is insane."\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is insane. And as we've said before, with both sadness and certainty, at some point, many people are going to die because of this. Unfortunately, that's true.\par \par \uc2 So the drama in the banking sector, the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  has a lot of Americans concerned about how to protect their money and of course, politicians are using that concern to their advantage, as always.\par \par \uc2 Several States are now trying to centralize currency with a so-called Central Bank Digital Currency, which is, by the way, not currency at all. It's software. But they're going to do it for your own good. Obviously, this is a tool of total social control.\par \par \uc2 If they control your money, if they can zero out your bank account with a keystroke, then you have no autonomy. They control you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in the State of South Dakota, legislators just passed a bill that would have changed the definition of money to exclude cryptocurrency, and that would put the state on a path to Centralized Digital Currency.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota's Governor, Kristi, Noem, the only Governor we're aware of who is paying attention to this vetoed that bill. She joins us tonight to explain why.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you very much for doing this first, and for coming on. Why did you do it? You're obviously under pressure not to veto it, but you did. Why did you?\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): While Tucker, it was the right thing to do. I became aware of this bill. It wasn't introduced until almost halfway through our legislative session.\par \par \uc2 We started reading through this bill that was over 110 pages long. It was sold as an update to the guidelines of the Universal Commercial Code backed by all of our financial institutions, our banks.\par \par \uc2 As we started reading through it, we saw the section of the bill that changed the definition of currency, and essentially what it did was paved the way for a government-led CBDC and it also banned any other form of cryptocurrency or Bitcoin or digital currency that existed. So for me, it very clearly was a threat to our freedom.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, we are the session that completes its business earliest in the year. We are the first ones to really look at this bill and find out the truth of what's in it, and I did veto that bill.\par \par \uc2 I'm asking my legislators to change their minds, to make the right decision and help me kill this bill once and for all. But I'm telling you, Tucker, we've got the same language coming to over 20 other States. I believe it's to pave a way for the Federal government to control our currency and thus control people.\par \par \uc2 It should be alarming to everyone and it's being sold as a UCC Guidelines Update.\par \par \uc2 There is no rush to do this. We need to be smart and make sure that we're doing what we can to protect people.\par \par \uc2 I find it ironic that we also are having this discussion at the same time we have banks and credit card companies talking about coding gun and ammunition in a separate code so they can track it.\par \par \uc2 So not only can they tie these two issues together, if the government doesn't approve of what you're purchasing, if they have the only form of digital currency out there that is endorsed and utilized in the country, they can control how you spend that money and thus, take away all your freedom.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is so nicely put, and I don't think you're overstating at all the significance of this.\par \par \uc2 Really quickly, do you think and you want to give everyone the benefit of the doubt on their motives -- but do you think that legislators in your State understand what this bill was actually designed to do?\par \par \uc2 NOEM: You don't know if they read it, Tucker, that is what is alarming to me. It was over 110 pages long and they were told by lobbyists that they had listened to for the last 20 years that it's fine. It's just a regulation update. This is what we do is adopt Federal regulations.\par \par \uc2 But if you start reading it, you see in there, there is a redefinition of currency that it says government, CBDCs are okay if they're run by the government, but any other form is then banned.\par \par \uc2 It is clearly a change for how people's assets are utilized. It clearly limits the freedom of people to use other forms of currency that they may choose to if it is a digital currency and it is clearly putting power into the hands of government.\par \par \uc2 If anything, we should have learned the last several years is the government can't be trusted. You know, Tucker, what I was surprised by was that you know, the bills that have made it to my desk this year, people think about South Dakota being conservative and being a very Republican State. The first bill I had to veto was a tax increase.\par \par \uc2 Now, this has made it to my desk. We've got other challenges as well.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I really hope that what you've done inspires other Governors. I appreciate your coming tonight. Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  of South Dakota. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NOEM: Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the race stuff has just gotten completely out of control. No one benefits except the people in charge. Now "The LA Times" acting on behalf of the people in charge is blaming an entire racial group for air pollution -- air pollution. Well, we've got more on that.\par \par \uc2 And of course, environmentalism is no longer about protecting the environment, it actively destroys the environment. Jordan Peterson joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, one of the saddest polls ever came out the other day, Rasmussen found that nearly half of African-Americans, 47 percent don't agree with the statement "It's okay to be White." That's just all awful, of course. It's awful. Why is it happening?\par \par \uc2 Well, in part because the media for the last several years have pushed Rwanda-style race hatred on the entire population, and far from getting better, it is getting worse. Why does this wind up in the end, you wonder?\par \par \uc2 Well, "The LA Times" just published a piece with this title, and we're quoting: "How white and affluent drivers are polluting the air breathed by LA's people of color."\par \par \uc2 Oh, right. We looked this up, it was written by yet another decadent, rich kid who went to Harvard Westlake, the most expensive private school in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 But here, he is claiming in "The LA Times" that just by breathing, White people are hurting others. Why not just genocide them at this point? Like what is this? It's bad is what it is.\par \par \uc2 We don't need to hate each other on the basis of color, and "The LA Times" should stop pushing this along with the rest of the media.\par \par \uc2 Environmentalism is supposed to be about protecting the environment, and most people are completely in favor of that, but the environmental movement of today has nothing to do with protecting the environment, they are destroying the environment.\par \par \uc2 Killing the California condor with their wind projects and whales and basically wrecking Africa with all the elements needed for their new battery revolution, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. It's not about the environment, it is some kind of weird cult around climate, and who better to assess a cult than Jordan Peterson, who is a famous psychologist and author.\par \par \uc2 We asked him about what is happening recently. Here is the conversation that resulted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Jordan Peterson, thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Just to begin with the premise that the modern environmental movement doesn't seem to be doing a lot for the actual environment, it's my observation, what do you think that's about?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN PETERSON, PSYCHOLOGIST AND AUTHOR: Well, I think the best example of that is probably both Sri Lanka and Germany. I mean, Sri Lanka is an absolute wasteland at the moment, as a direct consequence of idiot, hypothetically, environmentalist policies, and then in Germany, energy is much more expensive than it was, it's much more unreliable than it was. The Germans are dangerously dependent on dictators and the Russians.\par \par \uc2 And that's all a consequence of hypothetical environmentalist policies. So even by the standards laid down by the environmentalists themselves, the policies that were put in place that were pro environmental are failing.\par \par \uc2 But there is something that's even deeper lying underneath the surface. Alex Epstein has made a fair bit of this in his new book, "Fossil Future." But I started delving into this, I guess, 30 years ago, when I wrote my first book "Maps of Meaning."\par \par \uc2 So Tucker, it's become pretty clear recently as a consequence of converging evidence from a lot of different disciplines -- neuroscience, artificial intelligence, and literary criticism, odd bedfellows, by the way, and there's more scientific and humanities endeavors involved in this convergence than those that the structure through which we see the world is essentially a narrative, is a story.\par \par \uc2 And so -- and in fact, I asked the world's greatest neuroscientist about a month ago on my podcast, Karl Friston, if even our object perceptions were micro stories, and he said that that was clearly the case. And he is speaking as a scientist, by the way.\par \par \uc2 And so that begs a question, if we see the world through a story, what should that story -- what is that story and what should it be? And what are the fundamental elements that the story has to contain?\par \par \uc2 And so the environmentalists offer us a story to live by, and it's a pseudo religious story and it essentially elevates the biosphere, the Earth, Gaia, the Earth Goddess, let's say to the status of primary deity, and characterizes her as sort of a waiflike innocent victim, easily taken advantage of and fragile.\par \par \uc2 It casts the entire human endeavor on the social front as a raping and pillaging patriarchal monster only interested in power, and it casts the individual as like a devouring mouth riding on the back of that giant, essentially. And so that's a description of the world.\par \par \uc2 And there's some truth to that, right, because we can wreak environmental havoc.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETERSON: Social structures can become power mad and we can be carelessly consumerist, but it's a very incomplete story. It demonizes in a very pathological manner.\par \par \uc2 So when you hear people say things like, human beings are nothing but a cancer on the face of the planet, or there's too many people on the planet for the Earth to feed, let's say, then you're seeing reflections of that underlying pseudo religious narrative, and it's extraordinarily dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Now the reason that you can see that in some ways it is a religious structure rather than a careful attempt to grapple with the full realities of the world is that it has these odd features.\par \par \uc2 So for example, the environmentalists tend to radically oppose nuclear power and also natural gas and it is clear to the case that there is nothing that reduces carbon production more effectively than nuclear power. I don't think anybody with any sense ever debates that.\par \par \uc2 And it's also clearly the case that if we were careful with nuclear power, and we could be because we've been building nuclear plants for a long time that we could be providing extremely low cost energy to people, especially poor people throughout the world.\par \par \uc2 But we were not going to do that. In fact, we have an anti-energy policy in place, especially in any places that are ruled essentially by the left. And the consequence of that is, well, I just started -- hey, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  just released a report showing that there's been a 25 percent increase in the last year-and-a-half in the number of women and children that are starving around the world. It's a direct consequence of the increase in energy prices, which are in themselves a direct consequence of anti-industrial policies put in place by hypothetically well-meaning deluded pseudo religious environmental worshipers of the apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 It's an appalling situation. And it's likely to get worse, I would say before it gets better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I am not cheering, but probably true.\par \par \uc2 Well, in Canada, a Catholic school is punishing one of its students because he dared to believe that men and women are different. He dared to be a Catholic in a