ust asking the attorney here as a legal matter, how is this -- how was your son still behind bars?\par \par \uc2 CHANSLEY: Okay.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SHIPLEY, ATTORNEY: Well, Jake did not have a lot of postconviction options. The plea agreement that Albert Watkins talked him into signing waived all of his appeal rights.\par \par \uc2 So we had no opportunity to go to the Circuit Court of Appeals to raise any issues connected to his conviction. He had limited rights to challenge the performance of Albert Watkins as his lawyer. When I came into the case that's the first subject I began discussing with him, was my criticisms of what Watkins talked him into doing.\par \par \uc2 It is a terrible plea agreement. It was an unconscionable plea agreement. And I've done this for 35 years. I recognized it immediately what Watkins had done wrong.\par \par \uc2 Watkins had to plead guilty before the government was even willing to say it had produced all the video. The government in August of 2021 was still telling Judges, we haven't been able to get all of the evidence to defense lawyers, so please don't set trial dates.\par \par \uc2 Albert Watkins didn't care. He convinced his client to take the offer the government made and plead guilty.\par \par \uc2 So he's in a little bit of a mousetrap.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But now that we know that this was a travesty, I mean, the people responsible for it should be punished. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  should lose her job today as a Professor at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  , she knew this. She destroyed a man's life. But how do you get Jacob Chansley out of jail now?\par \par \uc2 SHIPLEY: Well, we're looking at that. I'm not sure there's an easy solution. There's not an easy road to that to get the matter back in before Judge Lamberth in the District Court here, and to have him reconsider his sentence.\par \par \uc2 In many ways, it's procedurally barred. We're going to have to come up with a creative way to get it back before Jud