roving that he was in the kennel. That's the scene of the crime just moments before the killings happened.\par \par \uc2 Murdaugh told investigators that he was not in the kennel, but after being caught red handed on tape, he later testified that he lied about not being in the kennel because his drug habit had left him paranoid.\par \par \uc2 Murdaugh wanted the jury to believe he left the kennel, went to his house, did not hear any gunshots, returned to the kennel and found the bodies of his wife and son.\par \par \uc2 He also testified that he held their bodies despite having no blood evidence on his clothing. It is notable the murder weapons were never found in this case. So it was impossible to connect Murdaugh to any of the weapons and there was little if any forensic evidence implicating Murdaugh, but the circumstantial evidence, as you heard was robust.\par \par \uc2 In fact, a lead prosecutor moments ago in that news conference that is now over, says they knew if they had a chance to put on their case that truth would prevail. And it's important to remember, Alex Murdaugh testified in his own defense. Most analysts thought he was a very effective witness. The jury clearly did not share that opinion -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amazing. Very sad story. Trace Gallagher, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So as you just heard, prosecutors did hold a press conference very recently. One prosecutors spoke about the victims in this crime. Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALAN WILSON, SOUTH CAROLINA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Winston Churchill said democracy is the worst form of government except for every other kind. I kind of want to adopt that and say we may have the worst criminal justice system in the world, but it is better than every other kind that there is, and our criminal justice system work tonight.\par \par \uc2 It gave a voice to Maggie and Paul Murdaugh, who were brutally mowed down and murdered on the night of June 7, 2021 by someone that they loved and someone that they trusted. And they couldn't be here to testify for themselves tonight.\par \par \uc2 Their testimony came through the evidence and the information that was gathered by the men and women of the agencies I have just mentioned, it came from the testimony of the agents and the investigators and the and the attorneys and the folks in our staff who were able to get it to the Court record.\par \par \uc2 And so I want to say tonight, their voice was heard tonight, and justice was brought for them. We can't bring them back, but we can bring them justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Judge Jeanine Pirro is co-host of "The Five" and has also been following this case and we're happy to have her join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Judge, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 What's your takeaway from this very, very sad and ugly saga?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Well, you know, I followed this case from the beginning. There were something like 28 days of testimony. It was a span of about six weeks, incredibly fast verdict, especially in a case that is circumstantial, but all murder cases, essentially, because the victim isn't going to come back and say, you know, he killed me, or unless there's a video or an eyewitness, they are all circumstantial.\par \par \uc2 This family was from one of the wealthiest, most successful legendary family in that county in South Carolina. This defendant was arrogant enough to think that he could take the stand and convince the jury that yes, I took pills, and I was a drug addict, and I stole from people, a quadriplegic and young women and my housekeeper and all that other stuff, but I didn't kill my wife and son. And okay, maybe they could buy that.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is that he lied until he was mandated to admit as a result of a \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  video that was released not that long ago that had his voice at the scene of the crime within minutes of the crime. And it was curious to me why, Tucker, the defense wanted to take the jury to the scene of the crime.\par \par \uc2 If anything, by the defense taking the jury there, they were able to assess how far the house was where the defendant said I was taking a nap and sleeping and I didn't hear any gunshot (wounds) to the distance at the kennels where you would have heard the gunshot (wounds).\par \par \uc2 So that was kind of bizarre, but I think his taking the stand was bizarre. Everyone agreed, he was a good witness. But he was too slick, because in the end, the jury bought none of this. The Judge did not even offer the jury dinner.\par \par \uc2 He said you've got beverages, soda chips, and, you know, cookies or whatever. The judge eventually said there was an overwhelming evidence of guilt here after the jury returned the verdict.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: May I ask you a question?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you said he was slick. He was very good as a witness. This guy is an ambulance chaser, right? Isn't he a slip and fall lawyer who is used to arguing cases?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He is an excellent attorney and that's what people remember him for.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, I meant that as an insult.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But look, he was an attorney with a huge law firm that made a lot of money. He made a lot of money. He spent a lot of it on drugs and just stealing. He just kept stealing to the next level.\par \par \uc2 But this this case came down, Tucker, to the classic motive means an opportunity. The shell casings, although they never found the guns were consistent on the property, a 1,700-acre property as the shell casings from the weapon that killed Maggie, the wife, and the kind of gun that was used was his favorite kind of gun.\par \par \uc2 So what you had here was a guy who had a wife who was speak