ey, Long Island metro area. The tickets are absolutely free. We love to have you join us.\par \par \uc2 That's all the time we have left. Please set your DVR, never missed an episode.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Let's give it up for Laura Ingraham, shall we? Our friend Laura, how are you?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230302ej3100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Addresses Conference Amid Lab Leak Dispute; Congress Holds Hearing On Drug Crisis; Havana Syndrome Intelligence Review Does Not Link Cases To Adversary; New Warnings About Prescription Pills Sold In Mexico; UCLA Study On Counterfeit Drug Overdose Risk; Jury Hears Closing Argument After Crime Scene Visit; Closing Arguments Begin In Murder Trail Of Alex Murdaugh; Congress Votes To Overturn Labor Department's Environmental, Social, And Governance Rule; Chicago Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 's Reelection Campaign Fails; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director States COVID-19 Likely Originated With Lab Leak In China; Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  Testifies Before Senate On Investigation Into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  And Fentanyl Crossing In U.S. At Southern Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Aishah Hasnie, Jennifer  Griffin, William La Jeunesse, Jonathan Serrie, Grady Trimble, Garrett  Tenney \par \uc2 7,931 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: With all the golden jewelry by the way, I'm unhappy with all the golden jewelry.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: No, the jewelries took someone with them with all the gold and jewelry. By the way, I'm on "HANNITY" tonight. I forgot to say that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Glad you were able to get that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Thanks, Jesse. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you. No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, thank you, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next with the Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So, just to be clear, Greg's been to Europe but not Egypt.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Just want to clear that up. OK. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. The prosecutor in the Alex aaa double murder trial delivers a scathing final argument after the jury visits the crime scene.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  gets tough questioning on Capitol Hill on a host of topics, we'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And a House committee takes on the Fentanyl crisis.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Biden is in Baltimore right now. He is speaking this hour to the House Democratic Caucus issues conference. One of the issues the president's team is dealing with this week, the determination by his own energy department and the scientists there that the COVID pandemic likely began with a leak in a Chinese laboratory.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  told me in the most straightforward way of any public official so far that the Bureau has reached the same conclusion. But many of the administration are still not on board.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is with the President tonight in Baltimore with the latest. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. White House sources say they're not fully embracing any single agency's findings because there isn't consensus yet about origins, leaving questions unanswered about when or even if that will happen, and what it will mean for China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): With two federal agencies declaring its most likely COVID originated in a Wuhan lab, the White House is facing questions about why there haven't yet been consequences.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president is not afraid. This is a president that's not afraid to go to a warzone. He's not afraid to go there when there's no military presence on the ground. So, there's nothing that this president fears.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: With four federal agencies still saying they think the virus stem from natural transmission and two undecided House Democrats are saying Biden first needs to get the full picture.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): I think first we need to have a directive to come to some consensus, because there are other agencies that still are questioning that.\par \par \uc2 And so, I would make sure within the next few months that we try to get to a consensus opinion. And then I would ask the agencies for recommendations about what action needs to be taken.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Republicans acknowledge a complete picture may never come.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): It's doubtful we'll ever have a smoking gun because frankly, China's a closed society. They're not going to put that stuff out there.\par \par \uc2 But all the circumstantial evidence and there was a mountain of it, all point to some accident at that lab.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The Senate unanimously passed a bill in 2021 that would require the Director of National Intelligence to declassify information linking the virus to the lab.\par \par \uc2 But House Democrats never took it up. And even with two Biden agencies now pointing to a lab leak, Democrats still aren't ready to support the bill without the OK from Biden speaking to the conference tonight in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): We know that there have been some executive agencies that have taken some different viewpoints. We believe in letting committees of jurisdiction guide the discussions ahead. But declassification conversations are best left to the executive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): President Biden is expected to speak here momentarily, touting historic investments in America as House Democrats huddled to come up with a message to win back the house in 2024. So, it's possible but not probable that any mention of China will become part of that rallying cry, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, the president's travel schedule ramping up as he gets closer to what we expect to be a reelection bid at some point. Any news on that front?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, well Bret, he's in -- he's here in Baltimore tonight. Yesterday, he was in Virginia ragging on MAGA Republicans as a different breed of cat. We have nothing else confirmed yet except there is rumors swirling that he may, after all, add the sight of that toxic train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio to his travel schedule.\par \par \uc2 We question about that, the White House press secretary answered today with nothing but a smile, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Jacqui Heinrich in Baltimore. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Congressional lawmakers took on the Fentanyl crisis today. The president's top law enforcement official admitted the current policy is not preventing Americans from dying because of that drug.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers heard that point driven tragically home by another parent who lost a child to fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie shows us tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN RACHWAL, MOTHER OF LOGAN RACHWAL: No family is immune to this danger.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Erin Rachwal sobs next to a picture of her son Logan killed when he took just one tiny pill thought to be Percocet, but laced with deadly fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 RACHWAL: For all of us, losing Logan was like a bomb going off.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Rachwal is the second mother to share her devastating story on Capitol Hill this week, as House Republicans hold a series of hearings on the gruesome realities of the fentanyl crisis.\par \par \uc2 DR. TIMOTHY WESTLAKE, EMERGENCY PHYSICIAN: People just stopped breathing, that can be the same thing as chemical execution.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The DEA says fentanyl is killing Americans at an unprecedented rate. More than 70,000 Americans died from synthetic opioids like fentanyl in 2021, the majority of the drugs smuggled across the southern border.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): Combating this poison and those who smuggle, manufacture and sell it should be a bipartisan issue.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Aside from securing the border, Republicans want to make the sale and distribution of fentanyl a capital crime.\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY NEHLS (R-TX): If you start strapping a few of these ruthless bastards to a gurney and start filling them with the same drug they were selling to kill Americans, it would bring me a great deal of satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Democrats oppose harsher mandatory minimum penalties.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We cannot incarcerate our way out of this.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: As lawmakers grapple over how to legislate.\par \par \uc2 DEREK MALTZ, FORMER AGENT, DEA: The people in this town don't listen to the experts.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Desperation for Congress to act appears to have reached a breaking point.\par \par \uc2 MALTZ: I apologize for yelling and screaming. But you know what? I've been dealing with the families for many years and there's no action going on in this town.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): So, Bret, we've seen several hearings on the fentanyl crisis, the border crisis over the past several weeks. But there is legislation coming soon, at least according to majority leader Steve Scalise, who tells Fox that Congress is working right now to bring bills to the floor. And that could possibly include a bill to permanently schedule fentanyl as a Schedule I drug, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  faced tough questioning today in a Senate hearing where Republicans quizzed him on a number of issues. Correspondent David Spunt has that story tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  again promising independence in the ongoing criminal probe of the president's son Hunter while testifying before the Senate for an annual oversight hearing.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I promised to leave the matter of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  in the hands of the U.S. Attorney for the District of Delaware, who was appointed in the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The younger Biden has been under investigation since 2018 reportedly for tax violations and other matters.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  asking for assurances, Prosecutor David Weiss could charge someone outside his Delaware base.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): I'd like clarification from you with respect to these concerns.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: He has full authority to make those kinds of referrals that you're talking about or to bring cases in other jurisdictions if he feels it's necessary.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Garland once a Supreme Court nominee was also asked about protests outside the homes of conservative Supreme Court justices last summer following the release of a draft version of the opinion that overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: We sent more than 70 \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  for this purpose. Those marshal's priority is protection of the lives of the justices and their families.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Also, following that Supreme Court leak an increase in attacks on pro-life pregnancy centers. The DOJ has charged more than 20 people with attacking abortion clinics but only two for attacking pro-life centers.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: We will prosecute every case against a pregnancy resource center that we can make.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Garland again defending his 2021 school board memo, critics say is aimed at treating parents as domestic terrorists. The one page memo does not use the word terrorist or even parent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Didn't you understand the chilling effect that it would have to parents?\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: The memorandum was aimed at violence and threats of violence against a whole host of school personnel. It was not aimed at parents making complaints to their school board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And today marked a deadline for DOJ to turn over school board memo documents to the House Judiciary Committee. A DOJ spokesperson tells Fox News they turned in those first round of those documents meeting that deadline, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of questions, a lot of different topics. Anything else strike you from that hearing?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The big one was on violent crime and fentanyl coming from both sides of the aisle. The Attorney General said simply they're doing everything they can on both fronts. They just don't have the resources right now.\par \par \uc2 In a lighthearted moment from both sides of the aisle, the Attorney General asked if \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is investigating the Taylor Swift ticket fiasco from a few months ago, and we learned that the attorney general is a Taylor Swift fan, even working on a Taylor Swift pun and from senators.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow. That could be painful. All right, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks were mixed as inflation worries persist. The Dow gained five, the S&P 500 lost 19, the NASDAQ fell 76.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we have new information about the so called Havana syndrome, hundreds of cases you may recall of brain injuries and other symptoms reported by American personnel around the world.\par \par \uc2 Well, it turns out one of the major theories about the cause has been rejected now by the intelligence community. Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin has details tonight from the Pentagon. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. A new intelligence assessment by seven agencies, including the CIA concludes that a foreign adversary is "very unlikely to be behind Havana syndrome", which has left hundreds of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  workers debilitated with traumatic brain injuries and vertigo.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Today's icy assessment does not call into action the very real experiences and symptoms, like we acknowledged that.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): A U.S. intelligence official familiar with the findings told reporters there is "No credible evidence that a foreign actor has a weapon or collection mechanism capable of causing the sensory effects." A deliberate cause is unlikely, even though the U.S. military itself has funded research into the very microwave energy pulse weapons that open source reporting suggests Russia and China also have obtained and victims believe were the cause of their ailments.\par \par \uc2 Lawyer Mark Zaid represents more than two dozen former and current federal employees, many of them from the intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZAID, ATTORNEY: It is just contradictory to evidence that we know of, particularly that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has seemingly and deliberately ignored.\par \par \uc2 It just doesn't make sense to how all of these individuals over a period of decades would somehow have similar, if not same types of symptoms and diagnoses of traumatic brain injuries that can't be faked.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: CIA director William Burns wrote, we applied the agency's very best operational analytic and technical tradecraft to what is one of the largest and most intensive investigations in the agency's history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The victims of Havana syndrome to whom Fox has spoken, say they feel they are being gaslighted by their government, adding in a statement, "weapons capable of causing these types of injuries are known and have existed for decades. This cannot and must not be the last word on this matter, because it is neither definitive nor comprehensive". That according to a statement by the victims, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's quite something after all this time. Jennifer, on another front, we're learning more about the Air Force commanders fired this week due to a loss of confidence. What's the deal with that?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That's right, Bret. Two Air Force commanders and four of their subordinates responsible for the safety of nuclear weapons. At Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota were fired after failing and inspection.\par \par \uc2 Minot is home to both the strategic bombers and missile silos that make up two parts of the nation's nuclear triad. The results of the inspection remain classified.\par \par \uc2 The commander of the fifth Mission Support Group and the commander of the fifth Logistics Readiness Squadron were relieved to command on Monday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jen, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Americans heading to Mexico for spring break are being warned about something that could kill them. We'll tell you what that is.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight. Fox 8 in Cleveland as Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  and his wife visit the site of the East Palestine train derailment again.\par \par \uc2 The governor says he's impressed by the cleanup operation so far. They also visited sites where surface water testing and sediment washing are underway.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in Atlanta as former University of Georgia football player Jalen Carter faces charges after police say he was involved in the crash that killed a teammate and one of the school's recruiters. The accident happened after the team's national championship victory celebration in January.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Charlotte from WJZY. One of the big stories there tonight, the historic Occoneechee Speedway in Hillsborough is now part of the North Carolina moonshine and motorsports trail. It was one of the first two NASCAR tracks to open. It has not hosted race in decades.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Taiwan's defense ministry\uc2  says it scrambled jets today in response to more than two dozen war planes and three ships entering its defense zone from China. The ministry says 19 of those planes fully entered the island's air defense identification zone. The CDC here in this country has a message for Americans headed to Mexico for spring break. Be very careful what medications you buy. There are major concerns about fentanyl and other substances in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent William La Jeunesse reports from Los Angeles tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You name it, I got it, homie. 40 percent off on any medication.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): More than a million Americans each year buy prescription drugs in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 This seller, typical. Greeting tourists looking for the cheaper meds that often don't require a script.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Cialis, Viagra, (INAUDIBLE), oxycodone, morphine.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But a new study, calls that practice dangerous. UCLA sent in researchers to Mexican pharmacies, posing as tourists, to buy Xanax, Adderall, and oxycodone.\par \par \uc2 44 percent of drugs were counterfeit. Oxy pills contain fentanyl and heroin. 82 percent of Adderall pills contain methamphetamine.\par \par \uc2 SHABBIR SAFDAR, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PARTNERSHIP FOR SAFE MEDICINES: Now, that the cartel has adopted Adderall with methamphetamine and learn to press those pills, our problem is going to get a lot worse before it gets a lot better.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The CDC is warning spring breakers about the counterfeit pills. A follow up by the L.A. Times found, 71 percent of the medications from three Mexican cities tested positive for fentanyl and methamphetamine.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): I am shocked that more Americans aren't up in arms over what's going on.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: At a meeting, Tuesday, hosted by a medical nonprofit, experts said it is difficult to tell cartel manufactured fake pills from the real thing.\par \par \uc2 Packaging for other drugs like blood thinners also looked real, but the pills contain zero active ingredients. The only giveaway, the barcode here, somebody got lazy, numbering it zero to nine.\par \par \uc2 SAFDAR: They have found counterfeit arthritis medicines, printed with English labels to fool Americans. Cancer medications, same thing. And, of course, they found plenty of fake pills and fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The bottom line, experts say, to stay safe, buy your meds at U.S. pharmacies not mail order or Mexico. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TikTok says it's making an effort to limit the screen time for users under the age of 18.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese own video sharing app says there will be a default 60 minute daily screen time limit in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Minors will then have to enter a passcode to keep watching. For accounts where the user is under the age of 13, a parent or guardian will have to set or enter an existing passcode to allow 30 minutes of extra viewing time once the initial 60-minute time limit is reached.\par \par \uc2 Today, the House Foreign Affairs Committee approved a bill that would make it easier for the Biden administration to ban TikTok entirely. Critics said the bill would be unconstitutionally broad.\par \par \uc2 Up next, jurors in the South Carolina double murder case take a field trip as closing arguments wrap up. We'll tell you, where.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Greece's transportation ministry resigns. This minister resigns following the collision of a passenger train and a freight train in Greece.\par \par \uc2 Rescuers are coming through flattened burned-out carriages for survivors and bodies. At least 36 people are dead scores injured there.\par \par \uc2 An advisor to Ukraine's president says the country's military might decide to pull troops back from the key stronghold of Bakhmut.\par \par \uc2 It comes as Russia pursues a bloody monthslong offensive to capture that city. The battle for the eastern city has become a symbol of Ukrainian resistance, as defenders there hold out against relentless shelling from the Russians. Russian troops have suffered heavy casualties in the campaign there.\par \par \uc2 And South Korea's president calls Japan a partner that shares the same universal values. \uc2 Yoon Suk\uc2 -yeol spoke of renewed hopes to repair ties frayed over Japan's colonial rule of the Korean Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Yoon spoke in Seoul at a ceremony marking the 104th anniversary of Korea's independence movement.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", the \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  says one of its officers prevented an explosive device from being loaded onto a flight at Lehigh Valley International Airport in Pennsylvania. It happened Monday.\par \par \uc2 The TSA says the device was detected during the routine screening of checked baggage. The suitcase triggered an alarm when it entered the baggage screening unit.\par \par \uc2 The officer, then, found an item inside the suitcase that appeared to be suspicious and was believed to possibly be a live explosive device.\par \par \uc2 Bomb technicians called in confirmed that suspicion. A suspect was arrested yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Jurors in the double murder trial of a prominent South Carolina attorney went to the scene of the crime today. Prosecutors say Alex Murdaugh killed his wife and son at his 1700-acre hunting estate.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, the jurors heard the state's summary of the case.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has details tonight from Atlanta. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's closing arguments, prosecutors tried to portray Alex Murdaugh as a successful attorney, living a lie. And they say once his financial schemes caught up with him, he murdered his wife and son to create a distraction.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CREIGHTON WATERS, CHIEF ATTORNEY, STATE GRAND JURY SECTION, SOUTH CAROLINA OFFICE OF THE ATTORNEY GENERAL: Not a single person who was close to him knew who he really was. Not a single person close to him hadn't been lied to by this man. And I would submit to you that this one is the most blatant one yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The prosecution pointed out Murdaugh refused to admit he was at the family kennel with his wife Maggie and son Paul the night of the murders until multiple family friends testified that they recognized all three of their voices together in the background of this video that Paul recorded just minutes before the apparent time of the shootings.\par \par \uc2 Before closing arguments began, police and court personnel escorted jurors to the Murdaugh family's 1,700 acre hunting estate to see the crime scene and the family home. The tour was requested by the defense. It was an apparent effort to show the jury the vastness of the property and how it might be possible for gunfire at the kennel to go unnoticed by someone at the house. Some of the law enforcement personnel protecting the jury on this visit were also trial witnesses who had responded to the crime scene the night of the murders.\par \par \uc2 And, Bret, tomorrow the jury will hear closing in defense. Murdaugh's attorneys likely to argue perhaps two shooters were involved and that the crime scene was not thoroughly investigated. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll watch it. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's green investment policy has been rejected by Congress. Late today the Senate followed the House in voting to repeal the rule. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble is on Capitol Hill tonight to tell us where things go from here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The joint resolution is passed.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Republicans in Congress potentially forcing President Biden to use his veto pen for the first time. The president says he will block their attempt to overturn the Labor Department's environmental, social, and governance, or ESG rule. It allows but doesn't require retirement fund managers to consider climate change and other social justice goals when making investment decisions.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE BRAUN\uc2 , (R-IN): If he vetoes it, then that is his political consequence. I think he will have to explain that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT, (R-MO): It's a Trojan horse for the Green New Deal. It gets them all of the stuff and it starves capital from, again, family farms and manufacturers, jobs. It makes everything more expensive for hard- working Americans.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: GOP lawmakers blast ESG as woke investing, arguing it prioritizes environmental and social initiatives over returns. They won over some lawmakers on the left, including Senators \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  and Joe Manchin, both moderate Democrats in red states.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): ESGs by itself just could kill our economy, could kill our whole economy as far as in America basically.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The White House doubling down in its defense of the rule which impacts some 150 million Americans collectively investing nearly $12 trillion.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Republicans talk about their love of free markets, small government, and letting private sector do its work. The Republican bill is opposite of that. It forces MAGA Republican ideology down the throats of private sector and handcuffing investors as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): Republicans in Congress don't have the votes to overturned that promised veto from President Biden, but the ESG rule also faces a legal challenge from 25 Republican-led states. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble live on the Hill. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Chicago is getting a new Mayor. Next month's runoff between the top two finishers in yesterday's election will not include incumbent \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 . Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Chicago tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT, (D) CHICAGO: We didn't win the election today. But I stand here with my head held high.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Four years after winning every ward in the city and becoming Chicago's first black female and first openly gay mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  is out of a job, becoming the first windy city mayor in 40 years to not win a second term. Crime and safety were the top issues in this race, and under Lightfoot's watch crime has surged to some of the highest levels in a generation. Both candidates vying to replace her in an April 4th runoff are making public safety a focus of their campaign, albeit with vastly different strategies to turn the city around.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON, (D) CHICAGO MAYORAL CANDIDATE: Well, here's Brandon.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Cook County Commissioner Brandon Johnson, who has the backing of the politically powerful teachers' union, was a vocal supporter of defunding the police, and says if he is elected he will increase taxes and invest more in social services to address the root causes of crime.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No matter where you live or what you look like, you deserve to have a better, stronger, safer Chicago.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: \uc2 Paul Vallas\uc2 , the former CEO of Chicago public schools, is endorsed by the police union. He argues the city needs more officers to get crime under control and wants to reverse recent policy changes that he says restricts cops from doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 PAUL VALLAS\uc2 , (D) CHICAGO MAYORAL CANDIDATE: Public safety is the fundamental right of every American. It is a civil right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): This race will also give us an indication of where the nation's third largest city is politically and whether voters here embrace an even more progressive candidate and agenda than Chicago has had the last four years. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Chicago. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel, the fallout from last night's interview with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  and the Bureau's determination of the COVID pandemic origin, also the latest from the House Select Committee on China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: They are continuing to double down and try to get to the bottom of this. Our relationship with China has not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: I don't have a whole lot to add other than just make the point that I have been trying to make really since my first few months as \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, which is about the threat of the Chinese state's surveillance efforts and spying efforts, which are very broad.\par \par \uc2 RITCHIE TORRES, (D-NY) HOUSE CHINA SELECT COMMITTEE: The U.S. fears that the CCP will bring a totalitarian police state to the world and will do so to an extent that not even George Orwell himself would have imagined.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI) HOUSE CHINA SELECT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: The CCP laughed at our naivete while they took advantage of our good faith. But that era of wishful thinking is over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Select Committee on China, bipartisan pushback against Chinese actions around the globe as we learn what we learn from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director last night on the lab front and other issues. Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters. Mollie, the hearing, there is a lot of bipartisan support for finding something to do, pushing back on the CCP. But whether the administration buys into what comes out of this House hearing is really yet to be scene.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, it's so interesting that this came out yesterday about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  also agreeing with the Department of Energy Chinese ran a lab that this virus escaped from. And it's been three years. That actually wasn't breaking news. Three years ago you had intelligence people saying that.\par \par \uc2 What's been done? This was a virus that through negligence or otherwise managed to kill millions of people, and we haven't really seen the type of rhetoric that you would expect, the type of action you would expect against a country that had done so much to lie against this communist government that had done so much to lie. In part I do think it's related to the administration which has tried to have a pretty friendly relationship with China, and it's a problem.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's take a listen to Peter Doocy, and he asked the question following up on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: If we now know, according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, who was most likely responsible for all those empty chairs at all those kitchen tables, why not do more to try to hold them accountable?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So I'm going to flip that on its head for a second. It was because of this president that took action. By the way, the last administration did not. They did not have a comprehensive plan. He went to the intelligence community and said we need to figure out how to get to the bottom of this. We need to figure out how this all occurred, because who knows. We have to try and prevent any future pandemic. So that is the work that this president did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, what do you make of all this?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I would say this about your interview last night, Bret. I don't think -- it was one thing for the stories that Mollie just referred to that came out two years ago, two or three years ago, saying that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's investigation had reached that conclusion, it's one thing for that to come out in news stories, and quite another for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director to say it as firmly and clearly as he did he in his interview with you last night, which suggests to me, Bret, that he sees the handwriting on the wall not that the Energy Department has said that based on the work of the laboratories that are part of its domain, that there is emerging consensus on this.\par \par \uc2 Also, I think he is alarmed about China. He said as much several times in his interview with you. So he came out, and not quite contradicted the administration, but getting out ahead of the administration in making that point. I think it's interesting that he is where he is given the fact that he wasn't all together forthcoming in everything else he said to you last night, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's to say the least. Jeff, I think there's an interesting thing about the administration here in that they have this back and forth over the lab leak theory. They keep on talking about there's other agencies, and there are that haven't reached a determination yet. But do they want to get to it a determination? Because, at that point, they have to make some decision, to Mollie's point, about what to do.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "REUTERS": Yes. I can't speak for them. I think what the White House has said had is that the president very much does the want to know, and that's why he directed the intelligence community to investigate this, to come up with the results that they are seeking. I think every story, everything in Washington sort of has a little relationship to momentum. There is now going to be momentum to the extent there wasn't before, there certainly will be now behind the theory of a lab leak. And I think that will challenge this administration and others who maybe don't want to talk about that piece of the story when you have the interview that you did and others saying that right now that's going to put a lot of pressure on it. But I do think they want to know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, they want to know. So you are in those briefings. Are they sensitive to that criticism that the president is somehow soft on China, the balloon interaction, his interaction, are they sensitive to that? Do you sense that?\par \par \uc2 MASON: I couldn't say whether -- I sense that they are sensitive, but I think they don't want to give that impression. I think they don't want to give that impression. We all know looking into the next presidential campaign that it's going to be a big issue on the campaign trail. Former President Trump made it a big issue in 2016, will make it a big issue again in 2024. And the Biden administration and President Biden knows that. And that's not the only care about it. I think this administration also sees China as a threat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To that point, China's relationship with Russia and the Ukraine war, "China's Xi heralds unbreakable friendship with Belarus, an ally of Russia. Xi said the two sides should support each other's chosen development path, support each other in safeguarding core interests, oppose interference in internal affairs by external forces, safeguard the sovereignty and political security of both countries." There are some people, Mollie, who are saying some of this stuff is coming out now and pressure on China on the COVID front because they are sending a warning signal that the closer you get to Russia or you give lethal weapons, the more we are going to press on other fronts. But it seems too good by half.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes, and this is stuff that should have been talked about for a long time. But there is this truth that in foreign policy we have long believed that you don't want to have China and Russia, then the Soviet Union, too closely allied. And what we have done through our involvement in this Ukrainian conflict is, unfortunately, drive Russia and China closer together. They're even more important as trading partners. They're also able to work with a lot of other countries in order to make sure that Russia's economy can keep going strong, which is unfortunate because it means our sanctions are not working. So it is a foreign policy concern to try to not have those two countries together. They are more formidable when they work together against the U.S.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was something, Brit, to watch that House Select Committee on China and the bipartisan agreement that we are seeing on Capitol Hill when it comes to figuring out what to do to push back on the CCP. Seems like that has that turned a corner.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I agree with that, Bret. And I think it's a good thing that there's this bipartisan support for the work of the committee which is really going to highlight this concern about China. But I think it's widely shared in the country so that these politicians all feel in both parties that they best get on the right side of that issue, and they are behaving that way right now and were in that committee hearing last night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, stand by. Up next, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  on the hot seat on Capitol Hill. And later, the creation of Yellowstone National Park.\par \par \uc2 If you want to check out that interview with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, I have got it posted now on "The Bret Baier Podcast." Just go to FOXNewsPodcasts.com, or if you download podcasts, you can hear the whole thing unedited right there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2 , (R-IA): Has the U.S. attorney sought permission of another U.S. attorney's office such as in the District of Columbia or in California to bring charges? If so, was it denied?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: So, I don't know the answer to that, and I don't want to get into the internal elements of decision-making by the U.S. attorney, but he has been advised that he is not to be denied anything that he needs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Attorney General Garland up on Capitol Hill, a host of different issues. There he is talking about the Hunter Biden investigation. "Politico" reports, "Garland promises free reign for prosecutors probing \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Garland did not share any substantive update on the investigation into the president's son. The probe is reportedly focused on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's failure for years to pay a federal income tax bill that eventually amounted to about $1 million, and a false statement \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  allegedly made about his drug use when purchasing a gun in 2018." \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has said he is confident the investigation will clear him of wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel, Brit, as you mentioned before, I tried to get some answers on this as well. The whole umbrella of investigation ongoing, which is ongoing for a long time, continues.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, he's in the position, Bret, is Garland in handling the question like the one he got from \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  to say, well, it's under investigation, he has full, free reign, and so on, and not say anything more.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, I watched some of that hearing today, and he did not have a good afternoon on Capitol Hill, or a good day on Capitol Hill. He got into an exchange with Senator Hawley about the way anti-abortion activist in Philadelphia was arrested with a huge SWAT team and all of that. And his answers were weak and very poorly spoken. Same thing happened in an exchange with Ted Cruz, which may not have been entirely fair on Cruz's part, but nonetheless he does not come across as a strong spokesman for his Justice Department or for his positions as the attorney general. And I can only imagine what people are thinking now who thought he ought to be on the Supreme Court because I suspect there is some relief in some quarters about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned Senator Hawley's exchange, and that was about Mark Houck, which I also asked the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director about, the show of force to make that arrest. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2 , (R-MO): Do you think that it was objectively reasonable and they followed your guidelines in sending 20 to 30 armed agents to terrorize these people? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The facts I have, which are those presented by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , are not consistent with your description.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The show of force for that arrest, that decision to use that force, was that by the book?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: So, there's a whole lot of things that goes into the judgment about what is the way to conduct arrests safely and securely that are made, I think, appropriately by the career agents on the ground who have the closest visibility to the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So that's basically the question about dual systems of justice. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. And first off, in the case of Mark Houck, his attorney had actually already agreed to bring in Mark Houck voluntarily if they wanted to go after him. So that's just not even understanding the basic facts on the ground. But whether it's this sort of smug and arrogant response that was given to you last night by \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2 , or the smug, arrogant testimony by \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  today, in which \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said we go after peaceful pro-lifers and not the people who are firebombing pro-life centers and Catholic churches because those fire bombings take place at night, and it's easier to go after the peaceful pro-lifers.\par \par \uc2 That is an absurd answer. It's so absurd it's almost like they want to celebrate how much the department has been politicized. There are so many ways. It's not just the pro-lifers versus Antifa. It's not enforcing the border. It's going after parents. It's about this ridiculous raid at Mar-a- Lago compared to the very nice treatment of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his problem with classified documents.\par \par \uc2 And it's also about how they have not given the resources necessary to actually investigate the Biden family business. It's not just that they didn't make that a special counsel in Delaware, which a lot of people at the Department of Justice think it should have been. That is a very poorly resourced office in Delaware. They also shut down a bunch of investigations, according to whistleblowers, and it sounds like \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  has been talking to some of whistleblowers that looked into all of the various parts of the Biden family business.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned fentanyl and the border. There was exchange about Mexico's involvement in fentanyl. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX) SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: I have been just astonished at the lack of sense of urgency to deal with this issue. It seems we have become so desensitized to it that that sense of urgency is simply gone.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC) SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Is Mexico helping us effectively with our fentanyl?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: They are helping us, but they could do much more. There is no question about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: If this is helping, I would hate to see what not helping looks like.\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: The cartels are creating these pills and that are distributing them within the United States are the most horrid individuals you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And this issue of fentanyl and tied to the border is really taking over politically not just border states, all over. If you look at issues Republicans handle better than Democrats, border security now, Republicans in our latest poll, plus 24. And you see the other elements that Republicans lead in. And then the Biden job approval, performance on national security, opioid epidemic, immigration, and you see the upside- down numbers there. The issue at the border and fentanyl seems to be grabbing a lot more people, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 MASON: We were talking earlier about some of the issues that will be big in the campaign, China being one of them. These will clearly be big ones as well. We know that President Trump, former President Trump will talk about immigration and the border. Fentanyl is an issue that both sides are talking about, but if the polling is right and suggests that the public associates a stronger response to the fentanyl crisis with Republicans, that will absolutely hurt the Dems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, where do you see it?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think, you link the fentanyl issue now with the immigration -- with the border immigration. Before it was an immigration issue, illegal immigration. People didn't like that, but it wasn't that big an issue. Now, if you connect the two, the failure to seal the border, to have a tight border becomes a double whammy for the administration and for this president because he really has not done much of anything meaningful to stem the flow of illegal immigration at the border, and the things that come in with it to include fentanyl in the eyes of many people now, politically.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Brit, do you think that that changes by a visit to the border, by a focus on it? It was thought that before the midterm that maybe they didn't want to do it because they thought it was an issue that they were vulnerable on.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, he didn't want to go down there to make a display of the border situation. He needs results on a border, and a visit will not do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, today's throwback. This day in history, President Ulysses S. Grant signed the bill creating the nation's first national park at Yellowstone, March 1st, 1872. Native Americans have lived and hunted in the region for hundreds of years before the first Anglo explorers arrived. The park settles between the states of Wyoming, Montana, and Idaho. It's also the namesake of a popular neo western starring Kevin Costner, who, by the way, also has a FOX Nation special on Yellowstone. You may want to check that out on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, a complete review of the coronavirus origin's timeline. We'll go back to the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now on time.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230302ej3100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Downplays COVID Lab Leak Report; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Sidesteps Questions About Running In 2024; President Joe Biden Shrugs Off Criticism From Ohio Mayor; Who's Zooming Who? Biden Stumbles Through Answer On Ohio Train Site Visit; Squatters Repeatedly Set Fires At Abandoned Home; No one Wants A Printer, But We Need To Print!\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, we'll watch it very closely. Charlie, great catching up with you my friend.\par \par \uc2 You have Disneyfied yourself, you're like a \uc2 Disney\uc2  character in a good way. I mean that in a good way. I do, I think.\par \par \uc2 All right, THE FIVE is next. We'll see you tomorrow and the fallout from this in the Magic Kingdom, not so magical. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 So much for a conspiracy theory, President Biden's Energy Department has concluded that COVID most likely escaped from a lab in Wuhan, China. And now, conservatives are saying, I told you so. They claim the revelation is yet another black eye for the media and the big tech.\par \par \uc2 At one point, you could find yourself banned from social media for even bringing up the possibility of a lab leak. And remember when the media and Dr. Fauci were quick to dismiss it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Just weeks ago, Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  rejected the conspiracy that Coronavirus was man made in a lab in Wuhan, China.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, NBC NEWS HOST: Both scientists and the U.S. intelligence community agree that this Coronavirus was not manmade, that is not a possibility.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Really just take a critical scientific look at the evidence that's available. Our belief is that it's much more likely this came from an animal.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , BIDEN CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Who said the overwhelming amount of evidence indicates that's a lab leak. I believe most card- carrying viral file phylogenies and molecular virologist would disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm really sorry that the lab leak has become such a distraction for so many people, because frankly, we still don't know. There is no evidence really to say.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And the White House is downplaying the report because nothing is conclusive. And also then defending Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: There is not a consensus right now, in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  about exactly how COVID started. There is just not an intelligence community consensus.\par \par \uc2 The president believes it's really important that we continue that work, and that we find out as best we can, how it started, so that we can better prevent a future pandemic.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: And we have been grateful to Dr. Fauci's wisdom, we've been grateful to Dr. Fauci's advice during the COVID response. This is someone again, who has spent his almost entire career fighting for the wellbeing, the health of American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But that's not the only thing people got wrong about the pandemic, one study concluded natural immunity provided just as much protection as being double vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And another, this one gets me, found that masks made no difference in stopping the spread of COVID. But the ladies of "The View", they don't seem to care about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, ABC HOST: So, as people will tell you, yes, take off your mask, don't worry about it, we're not going to check it.\par \par \uc2 Just know, the flu will fly right in all day because the flu flu got me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I guess maybe the answer is just wear a mask for the rest of your life so you won't get the flu.\par \par \uc2 Wouldn't it be great if you had just a tiny bit of humility from the media? That's all it would take, just announce.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We've been talking about this story for three years. And we said at the beginning that there are going to be times when we're going to be wrong. You have measured somebody by how they can deal with being wrong and how they can adjust their beliefs. And not through arrogance, or just hiding.\par \par \uc2 And you know, if you look at the people, like you know, Nicolle Wallace, or Tom Nichols, or Anne Applebaum, or all of these people, it was the arrogance, and they're dismissive mockery of the people who were -- who had a different opinion to them.\par \par \uc2 You know, I don't say we should bring back tar and feathering. But I'm not saying we should not bring it back. Because there's something -- you know, there's something that pisses you off about creeps skulking away from the mockery of this belief, you know, chortling, and so self-satisfied, and that they can pretend that we don't remember. We remember because, you know, this is not news what they're talking about. The news is how we're not able to punish these people for basically calling out everybody for a sensible explanation of COVID.\par \par \uc2 Going back to three years ago, we said this on this show, a novel virus erupts in a town with a lab that makes a novel virus. And that's somehow a conspiracy to point out a virus that is not natural, appears so different that it might be manmade, could have come from a lab? No, you can't say that.\par \par \uc2 But this is also I'll shut up, as a great example of how accusations of racism can suppress conversation, right? It's about -- it happens in every issue.\par \par \uc2 So, when you brought up that the origins of COVID had to be in China, then you were obviously Asianphobic, right? And it was racist to say Chinese or China virus, and they would link it to Asian people are getting brutalized by including homeless people in Oakland, California, somehow that was due to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as if -- as if homeless people were watching THE FIVE and getting angry.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that, you know, what the race car does is it can crush actual dialogue and theories based on the fact that, oh, it comes from somewhere else, you have to talk about it. And we have to investigate.\par \par \uc2 And you know, if you think the 9/11 lawsuits were 20 years, this is going to go on for a century, this is going to cast a long step -- a long shadow forward with coming litigation, because you're talking to billions of people.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, there's seven million died, that we know of Judge, because I think China way undercounts how many of their people have died. Think of that learning loss with educators, the businesses that were lost. And not to mention on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , you could actually lose your business. If you posted something saying I think the lab leak is real, you could be banned for two years. And if that was where how you publicize yourself, you'd be out of luck.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: You know, the devastation from COVID. And the way t