ion of everyone. Vice Admiral Brad Cooper says he has seen a rise in what he describes as Iran's malign activities over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  meets with Greek emergency rescue workers, applauding them for their work following the deadly earthquakes that struck Turkey and Syria. Blinken toured the earthquake zone and pledged more financial assistance from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo. One of the big stories there tonight, Japanese panda fans bid a teary farewell to their idols Xiang Xiang and super papa Eimei and his twin daughters. They were sent to China to swap their home at the zoo for a protected facility in the Sichuan Province. Hundreds of people took photos, some wiped tears with handkerchiefs and waved at a white truck carrying Xiang Xiang away as it slowly drove past them on its way to the airport, parting with pandas.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: With this agreement the United States and Russia, the two largest nuclear powers in the world, also send a clear signal that we intend to lead.\par \par \uc2 JENS STOLTENBERG\uc2 , NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: With today's decision on new START, the whole arms control architecture has been dismantled.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Responsibility for fomenting the Ukrainian conflict for its escalation and for the increasing number of victims lies entirely with the western elites.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Putin's craven lust for land and power will fail, and the Ukrainian people's love for their country will prevail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden over in Poland with dueling speeches as Russia says they are not going to take part in this arms control, the new START treaty they signed on to. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " writes "Biden in Poland proclaims \uc2 NATO\uc2 's unity against Russia. Biden's speech came just hours after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  delivered his own fiery speech, claiming that the west is plotting to destroy Russia. The dueling speeches highlighted a sharp contrast in worldviews, testing Biden's arguments that democracies will outrun autocracies. Biden did not offer any diplomatic offramps, repeating that it is Ukraine's decision as to when and whether to enter negotiations with Russia." This as Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  may be visiting Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is senior political correspondent for "Axios," and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Marc, former speechwriter, two speeches happening hours apart. What was your take?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": So \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said that the west has been trying to turn Ukraine against Russia since the 19th century in the Austro-Hungarian empire. So apparently this wasn't \uc2 NATO\uc2  after all. It started much sooner than that.\par \par \uc2 As for Biden, look, he keeps saying that we will support Ukraine as long as it takes, which sounds very robust. But the problem is it's taking a lot longer because of his strategy, because he doesn't have -- or lack of a strategy. It took him nine months to approve a single patriot battery for the Ukrainians. He still hasn't approved the ATACMS. He still hasn't approved F-16 fighter jets. I had General Jack Keane on my podcast we just recorded today, and he said that if we gave Zelenskyy everything that he asks for to do a combined arms operation against the Russians, they could probably drive Russia out of all of the territory it has taken including Crimea by the time of the second anniversary of the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 So if that doesn't happen, it's because the United States and Biden administration doesn't have a strategy to win. And that's why people are starting to question it, because the president keeps saying as long as it takes. That means another unending war. If he laid out a strategy and says here's how we are going to win, here's the goal, here's what we are going to provide them, then people would rally to that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So about people, Katie, favoring the U.S. continuing to supply weapons and money to Ukraine, you can see the breakdown, Democrats and Republicans and independents. As we put up this poll, money, 79 percent, weapons 79 percent for Democrats. And you see the drop off for Republicans, 51-55. And independents a little bit more, 55-56. There are cracks in the wall here when it comes to Republicans, how far to go. But largely, there is still bipartisan support up on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There is, and you saw that delegation of Republicans in Ukraine today. There has been bipartisan support, but money is one thing and sending money for weapons. But the president in his speech in Poland didn't lay out how, to the American people, how he plans to keep this from spiraling out of control. The idea that President Putin is saying he is not going to be in the START treaty which limits U.S. ability to have eyes on the number of intercontinental nuclear weapons that Russia has, that's a huge problem that diplomatically this treaty was supposed to run out in February of 2026. They are going to have to try and renegotiate that while we are supplying Ukraine with weapons to fight against Russia. I don't think that Putin would be willing to come to the table to renegotiate any kind of agreement for accountability on that system.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that I heard over the weekend and with President Biden's visit was from President Zelenskyy who said if China enters this war, it will be World War III. Well, what is President Biden doing to prevent China from entering this war? Because so far when it comes to what they have done to the United States, whether it's the spy balloon or spying on universities, et cetera, the president's posture is just we're not really enemies with China. They are just simple competitors. So, he hasn't really given the American people much when it comes to strategy about how he is going to prevent America from getting embroiled in a longer conflict.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, about China, it could have been in the speech in Poland, could have mentioned it on this trip. He hasn't so far. Chinese, this is "The Wall Street Journal," "China's \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  plans Russia visit as Putin wages war in Ukraine. Beijing says it wants to play a more active role aimed at ending the conflict, and the people familiar with Mr. Xi's trip plans said a meeting with Mr. Putin would be part of a push for multiparty peace talks and allow China to reiterate its calls that nuclear weapons not be used." That would be great if they push for negotiations, but if they provide heavy weapons, lethal weapons, what does the U.S. do, Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes, it's a challenge because you want to keep China on the sidelines, but they are meeting with Russia. They have an interest in maintaining an alliance against the United States and our allies. So it's a tricky situation.\par \par \uc2 But I think despite all the nitpicking, this is one of the highlights of the Biden presidency. It's one of his stirring moments going to Kyiv in the middle of a war, giving a soaring speech defending democracy against autocracy. It's the type of rhetoric we would hear from \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  or \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , and now you hear the Republicans, Bret, you talk about the polling, there is a divide in the Republican Party over whether we should continue funding Ukraine and the war. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  gave a speech 