WS CHANNEL HOST (voice over): The pup's incredible journey using only her nose and mental map of the area took over two days. She was exhausted from the journey and slept part through the whole night. Next morning, she was able to go home with a new family.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): That's amazing.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That is.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Great "ONE MORE THING".\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you very much, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I hope your arm feels better.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you so much. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Sorry for your loss, Jessie. OK.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We go inside a migrant shelter in Mexico where people seeking entry to the U.S. They are trying to do it legally. We bring you there.\par \par \uc2 A major development and notorious murder case in Florida, involving the death of a young \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  executive.\par \par \uc2 And Russia ramps up its offensive in Ukraine ahead of the one-year anniversary of the invasion, as the state department re-ups a warning to America's -- Americans in Russia.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", increasing concern over aerial objects begin -- that were spotted and then shot down over the U.S., and Saturday over Canada.\par \par \uc2 The total right now is four. But there are reports the number of incursions into the U.S. airspace may be far higher. China blamed for at least one of these, so far. Says, the U.S. has sent multiple balloons over its territory in the past year. The Biden administration denies this.\par \par \uc2 More of the Chinese spy craft has been recovered and is being analyzed from off the coast of South Carolina. But as for the other objects, there is still very little information about what they are, what they were doing, and who is behind them, as the search for those remnants continues tonight.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Brit Hume has analysis of the whole affair. Jacqui Heinrich is at the White House with the increasing pressure for President Biden to address the issue personally.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon with what the defense department is doing and saying tonight. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Today, The White House said the Pentagon is not tracking any unusual aircraft over U.S. airspace. That could change. Unlike the Chinese spy balloon, these objects were much smaller, flying much lower, and we're not being steered.\par \par \uc2 Some officials have characterized them as sky trash.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: And I want to reassure Americans that these objects do not present a military threat to anyone on the ground.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): After landing at \uc2 NATO\uc2  headquarters in Brussels, Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  addressed public fears for the first time, since U.S. warplanes shut down three objects over North America, since last Friday.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: They do, however, present a risk to civil aviation and potentially an intelligence collection threat.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Austin said, the Pentagon has not recovered any debris from the three recent incidents, and still can't say what they were. Adding, they could be researched aircraft flown by commercial entities. There is no evidence these objects came from China.\par \par \uc2 After the Chinese spy balloon entered U.S. airspace, the U.S. homeland defense adjusted its radar.\par \par \uc2 GEN. CHARLES Q. BROWN, JR.: CHIEF OF STAFF, AIR FORCE: The adjusting of the radar sensitivities, which means we're seeing more things than we would normally see. But we don't fully appreciate or understand exactly what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The head of NORAD was asked whether these were simply weather balloons.\par \par \uc2 GEN. GLEN VANHERCK, COMMANDER, NORTH AMERICAN AEROSPACE DEFENSE COMMAND AND UNITED STATES NORTHERN COMMAND: I'm not going to categorize them as balloons. We're calling them objects for a reason.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The U.S. warplanes sent up to observe these objects before they were shot down, were flying too fast past the object to make a positive identification and gave conflicting descriptions of the objects.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials say it's not clear the last three were even transmitting any information. Off the coast of South Carolina, a crane ship lifted a large intact portion of the balloons payload that has most of the crafts intelligence gathering apparatus.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: Over the course of the weekend, they were able to raise some of the debris, including some of the electronics and some of the structure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Pentagon officials say they feel confident they will receive a mother lode of intelligence on the Chinese balloon surveillance program, which would have been lost if they had decided to shoot the balloon down overland.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, the National Weather Service said all of its balloons are accounted for. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers from both sides of the aisle are now calling on President Biden to talk to the American people directly about the situation. White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that part of the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House cleared up one thing, saying that there are no indications of alien or extraterrestrial activity related to these objects after the Pentagon said over the weekend that they weren't ruling anything out.\par \par \uc2 But the president still has not explained what has been happening in our skies or how he plans to deal with it. Officials today only announcing a new multi-agency team to study policy proposals going forward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): We have never shot anything down over the United States. Now, we shot four objects down that we can't even fully describe in the last week.\par \par \uc2 This is a time to hear from the president --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Despite mounting pressure, not a word from President Biden after he directed three more objects shot down since Friday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): As the Biden administration just dial the sensitivity of our radars all the way up. If so, what are these objects that we are just now noticing for the very first time?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Today, the White House revealing one new piece of the president's thinking after he waited to down the Chinese spy balloon off the east coast.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): What change? Why are we shooting them down all of the sudden?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We don't think -- we don't -- we don't know for sure whether they had a surveillance aspect to them, but we can't rule it out. So, there was a little bit -- there was enough uncertainty there.\par \par \uc2 That, again, out of an abundance of caution, doing the prudent thing, the president directed that they get taken down.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But they're stopping short of calling this a new policy.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I get where you're going, is this the standard going forward? And we're going to -- we're going to dive into this. We're going to learn from these three events.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: China still claiming their spy balloon was a weather device, now accusing the U.S. of 10 intrusions over Chinese airspace since last year.\par \par \uc2 Saying, "The U.S. needs to reflect on its own behavior and change course rather than attacking others and stoking confrontation."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. denies the claims. And in response to the spy balloon, also blacklisted six Chinese entities for supporting their militaries efforts to gather intelligence with airship and balloon programs.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Industry and Security\uc2  calling it "contrary to U.S. national security and foreign policy interest."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: All this is expected to come up if Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  meets with China's top diplomat at the sidelines of the Munich Security Conference, which is coming up. That possibility is now being discussed.\par \par \uc2 It would be their first face to face meeting since the U.S. took down the Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the White House says any video or images of any of these recent takedowns would be up to the Pentagon to release if appropriate. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're trying to get that. Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, what's your take on all of this. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Well, certainly there's been a change in the administration's policy or practice here, after letting that first balloon as it turned out to drift all the way across the continental United States.\par \par \uc2 They shut that one down. And now, it seems they're shooting everything down that they can see. And, you know, the interesting question is well, you know, what really -- what are we shooting down? When we shot these things down, we don't know what they are. We didn't know what they are when we shot them down.\par 