s about the First Amendment, freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 WENDILEE LASSITER, PLAINTIFF: To be told I can't wear my shirt in there, that those documents guarantee that right, is just mindboggling.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Wendilee Lassiter and others were in Washington for March for Life. Lassiter says when she and her group walked into the National Archives an employee confronted them.\par \par \uc2 LASSITER: He said I had to take my sweatshirt off before I could enter the building. And I was just kind of like, what? This is America. Things like this shouldn't happen.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: She also claims pro-choice activists were allowed to wear their ideological clothing in the building. Lassiter and others are suing the National Archives. The agency out with a statement saying its policy typically allows such expressive clothing. "As the home of to the original Constitution and Bill of Rights which enshrine the rights free speech and religion, we sincerely apologize for this occurrence. We are actively investigating to determine what happened."\par \par \uc2 JORDAN SEKULOW, AMERICAN CENTER FOR LAW AND JUSTICE: These apologies they issue they only issue after we come out to the public and say what happened. So these are not proactive apologies.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The National Archives is not alone. A similar incident happened the same day at the Smithsonian Air and Space Museum.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY, STUDENT: I even said this is a violation of our First Amendment right. This is a government funded building. How are we paying for this with our taxes and I'm not allowed to wear this hat?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The Smithsonian issued a similar apology and is also facing a lawsuit. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a whistleblower's claims about a transgender center at the St. Louis Children's Hospital has the attorney general asking requests. We 