ess increasing in teen girls, girls feeling sad or hopeless, 36 to 57 percent in that same time period.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Bret, this is shocking. And I spoke to Dr. \uc2 Debra Houry\uc2  today who is the chief medical officer of CDC, and she handles violence and injuries. And she is very, very disturbed about this. And as you just said, two out of three girls now hopeless or saddened to the point of that 20 percent considering suicide, and one out of 10 or more raped, and sexual violence up 60 percent just over the last few years.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first obvious thing here is the pandemic. The pandemic has increased isolation. It has increased dependence on social media. It has decreased socialization. But that's only part of it, because, as Dr. Houry pointed out, Bret, this was going up before the pandemic. I think social media has a lot to do with this. We saw it in New Jersey the other day, that horrible case of the girl who committed suicide. They had showed a violent video clip, and it went viral just a day before. There's a lot of meanness involved.\par \par \uc2 Houry said to me that she thinks the key is connectivity, parents being mover involved, a lot of communication, after school activities, schools being more involved. That's all correct. Whether it will be enough, though, maybe to reverse the trend, we're in trouble with teen girls. And boys not as much, Bret. Boys are buddying up more. They are not as vicious to each other. There's a clear distinction between girls and boys here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But the schools and the parents have to communicate, and if they see something that's going south with these kids, they have got act, right?\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: That's a hugely important point. There can't be a disconnect between the schools and the parents. If a parent is coming to a school with concern, the schools has got to act. The school should be monitoring this to begin with. And, again, parents out there 