ents. So two more objects shot down, doubling the total to four. But despite more things happening, we really don't have any more concrete answers. We have little details, you were rattling off a few of them there, but in terms of what are these objects, where are they coming from, what are their capabilities, we know nothing beyond what we knew 72 hours ago, which is kind of extraordinary considering that the whole country has been talking about this all weekend long, and here we are closing in on the end of your show on Monday and still kind of nada.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The General, Susan, briefing over the weekend, saying I can't rule anything out, but John Kirby and \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  today saying it wasn't aliens. It's not extraterrestrial. Who knows? Defense Secretary Austin, though, asked specifically about the policy. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it American policy to now shoot down these unidentified objects on sight? And has there been a delegation of authority from President Biden to yourself on making decisions on when to take down these objects?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: Our policy hasn't changed. We will evaluate each and every event on its own merits.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If we just look at each event, it's clearly changed, right? They have a different status now.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": From the first one to the next three, yes. They responded to the uproar over the failure to shoot down the first one. I think it was a strategic error to rule out aliens, though, because we talk about our polarized nation. What would unite us more than alien crafts coming down that we are shooting down but missing with our sidewinder missiles.\par \par \uc2 But actually, to be serious, isn't that the most alarming thick that we've reported, that this sophisticated missile couldn't hit what some administration officials are saying might be sky trash?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the reaction on the Hill? Is there outrage? What's most of lawmakers -- what are they saying?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": I talked to a few House Republican sources today, and I think they are pretty frustrated. They are going through trying to figure out what the Biden administration knew or didn't know. One source pointed out that there was a report from last year that said these balloons had been spotted back as far as 2019. And they are wondering if there was a breakdown with the White House in terms of communicating. Are they trying to distance themselves, or did they just not know about it. And sources I talked to said either one of those answers is not a great look for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is the Senate majority leader over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: We got enormous intelligence information from surveilling the balloon as it went over the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did the Chinese get enormous intelligence as well?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: They could have been getting it anyway. But we have to know what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, at this point, and, again, we are back to a lack of information, should President Biden be speaking out on this? And that's some of the calls on Capitol Hill is to actually hear from the president to the American people.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I would like to hear from the president, but I would like to hear from the president when he has something of substance to tell us, because we have seen his spokespeople coming out. I mean, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , with all respect, went on another network this weekend and her answers were sort of disastrous, partially because they didn't have new information to impart, partially because she was just struggling to form sentences.\par \par \uc2 But I think it's worthwhile for the American people to hear directly from the commander-in-chief about what precisely is happening. The concern is, he doesn't know precisely what is happening yet. We collectively don't know what is happening yet. When they get good information, we should hear from him. Before that, it would just be an exercise in box-checking, and we would all be saying, well, what was the point of that?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are looking at images, Susan, of the recovery of the first spy craft, the balloon, and the analysis of that. When ask about that by Judy Woodruff, also by Telemundo, the president seemed to blow it off as far as its significance. However, you talk to intel officials, and they say this is significant that China is this bold.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It's pretty brazen. It's not exactly a surprise that China spies on us, and we also spy on China, but for them to float a balloon over the sensitive military bases is pretty extraordinary. And I think we do take it seriously. And we don't know a lot. There is a lot we don't know about this. We know this is blowing up U.S. China relations. The effort to move toward more cooperative relationship, that is over for the moment until we figure this thing out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn to Ukraine as we look at this map. Russia offensive is developing, and we are seeing more movement by Russian force forces into Ukraine and some serious battles in the eastern part of the country. Here is John Kirby on Putin's decisions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Clearly, he hasn't made sound decisions nor has his military with respect to their performance on the battlefield. They are still suffering some of the same problems they were a year ago. Logistics, sustainment, integration of joint fires, manpower personnel, unit cohesion, I could go on and on. The Russian military is still struggling. They have not surmounted these problems. And it's borne out by the fact that he continues to change generals the way I change socks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is there optimism up on the Hill on bipartisan way of how this the Ukrainians look, or do they fear this offensive?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I think that there are ongoing conversations. Some House Republicans tend to be really loud that they don't support Ukraine, but from my conversations most generally do want to keep supporting them even if they are pushing for reassessing or looking at where the funds have been going. So they say we want to look at where the money is going even if we still support these efforts. And the war is expected to drag out, and Putin might be trying to wait out U.S. support. And that's definitely a strategy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it could get ugly, Guy, if this offensive is as big as some people are saying.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: And this offensive has been expected for a while, right? And so the U.S. has given an awful lot of money already to the Ukrainians with this in mind, and hopefully the Ukrainians will use that well and wisely and will continue to punish the Russians for this invasion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not a lack of will on Capitol Hill, you think, Susan?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I think it's a risk. I think Americans want to support Ukraine. That won't last forever. There needs to be some sense of what would comprise victory. What could you say this is the outcome that is acceptable to us and, importantly, acceptable to the Ukrainians, because it's at the end of the day it's going to be their decision.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day, very special day, because there's the plane touching down, Super Bowl champion \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  arriving back home a short time ago. The Chiefs winning their second championship in four years by coming back from a 10-point half