buted, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): And to get concessions, it's never going to be as good as we like it but if we make movement in that direction, that can help with ultimate credit rating.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 , senator from the state of North Carolina, we'll see and the fallout what he was talking about tomorrow night at the State of the Union address. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, everyone, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and she plays hopscotch on a waffle, Dana Perino. THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  finally popping the giant Chinese spy balloon, but did it take too long for it to go boom? After letting it fly over most of the continental U.S., the military finally shooting this sucker down off the coast of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The president just a little while ago says it was always his plan to take it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We made it clear to China what we're going to do. They understand our position, we're not going to back off, we did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Has it always been your view to shoot down a Chinese spy balloon? Or was it only because it became public?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh no, it was always my position. Once it came over to the United States from Canada, I told the Defense Department I wanted to shoot it down as soon as it was appropriate.\par \par \uc2 They concluded -- they concluded we should not shoot it down over land, it was not a serious threat, and we should wait until it got across the water.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Clearly, this is his finest moment since corn pop. Parts of this commie (PH) spy craft had been scooped up and sent over to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for further analysis. And despite assuring the American people there was no threat, a senior U.S. official now says there may have been self-detonating explosives on the vessel, just like Jesse's hair. Republicans are reacting with their usual pouncing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The message they were trying to send is what they believe internally. And that is that the United States is a once great superpower that's hollowed out that's in decline. And the message they're trying to send the world is look these guys can't even do anything about a balloon flying over U.S. airspace.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): A spy balloon complete its spy mission before we shot it down, I'm afraid is an embarrassment to the United States.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH): The president taking it down over the Atlantic is sort of like the quarterback -- sort of like tackling the quarterback after the game is over.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): It makes us look weak and flat-footed on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And China is pushing back against America claiming what happened is a "clear overreaction". And you can probably guess who agrees with that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They love this. They love that Republicans turn this into this big drama.\par \par \uc2 If you're going to spy, you're not going to send a giant balloon all across the United States for a week, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Chinese know how to spy. They -- you know, they can do it better. They wanted to create a crisis, a national crisis, an international crisis. They wanted it to be elevated and it'll be visible and be talked about. And they got it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana to be fair, if you're looking for experts on giant balloons, you got to go to "The View".\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I mean, that's always my go to.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And for foreign policy. And I actually go for foreign policy --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Everything could be handled better. That's -- you could say that about anything. What do you make of this in your sober, objective criticism?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think hindsight is 20/20 right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Did you just coined that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, I mean, it's a really good phrase. If you think about it, if you think like if hindsight when you look back, things are always perfect.\par \par \uc2 A few things, so \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported that President Biden was keeping it quiet, because he didn't want to give Republicans any fodder for suggesting he's weak on China.\par \par \uc2 I do think that if they had shot it down over Alaska, they'd come down, there would have been criticism of why did you shoot it down so quickly, you should have let it blah, blah, blah. I mean, there's a lot of sort of counterpoint on that.\par \par \uc2 However, because they kept it quiet, they ensured that there would be more mystery. Now, if there's more mystery, there are a lot more questions, including this thing where over the weekend, the administration from the Pentagon floats this thing that says, three of these balloons came across during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Well, that story all fell apart pretty quickly, because it's like, what do you mean by near, everybody in the Trump administration, including the former president himself, and everybody that was on national security said, no, I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 So, now the president is just hanging out there by himself. And if you think there's -- all the criticism is one thing, this is a moment where President Biden could have said, let's all rally together, let's all do this together. I mean, this is -- tomorrow night is going to talk about the State of the Union, we can all agree that this is not acceptable. We could all do that.\par \par \uc2 But instead, because they tried to keep it quiet. It was never going to be kept quiet. Then they tried to basically blame President Trump or like try to solely Trump over this that invited all these other conversations rather than just saying, we are united against China. And stay tuned for Tuesday, and I'm going to tell you more.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, you know, Jessica, it does seem that we should all be pointing fingers at China instead of each other. But having said that, is it fair to say that Joe is the Manchurian candidate under the thumb of the CCP?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: That's actually what I was going to lead with.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I knew because you're wearing red.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I was trying to be nice, and she's wearing blue. So, let's see where that's going when she could talk.\par \par \uc2 So, this was really -- I was thinking about the last time that there was kind of foreign policy outrage over what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. Afghanistan is obviously the big, the kind of mother lode of everybody is criticizing him.\par \par \uc2 And in that particular instance, it was -- he really wanted that, right? And the military, the Pentagon, and all the generals were saying we need to do this at a slower rate. You can't just pull everyone out of there, we're going to be leaving people behind. We're going to lose lives because of it. We created a civil society there that's in tatters at this point. I mean, horrific stories of what's coming out there. This wasn't that, right?\par \par \uc2 So, Biden's first reaction, which is perfectly in line with his personality was shoot it down.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: In the leg.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Shoot it down. And the Pentagon said, that's not what's safest. And can you imagine what would happen if suddenly there were explosive devices? Or if there was another -- let's for those who think it that COVID was a bioweapon, right, that leaked out of this plant in a lab in Wuhan. And there was something like that in the balloon, we had no idea what was going on there. And that would have been so much astronomically worse than what happened.\par \par \uc2 So yes, of course, a few days of people able to see this thing and take pictures of it on their iPhones is obviously a problem. And we'll hear more about the previous balloons that apparently our technology couldn't even track properly, which speaks badly of us, right?\par \par \uc2 If they have been floating these balloons, even over Alaska, which last time I checked as ours. We should know about it every single time that that happens.\par \par \uc2 But I don't see how this is some major flaw in the Biden foreign policy apparatus or some major point of embarrassment. And I think that when people jockey for points with something like this, it kind of hollows out more valid arguments that they might make of where he has fallen short, like in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jesse, she makes a valid argument there, care to retort?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Sure. So, this thing flew across the entire country and got nothing. It didn't spy on anything. They never picked up any intel. Well, that's what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying this thing came 4,500 miles across the continental of the United States and trust me, it didn't pick up anything. That's the same thing they said about the woman that slept with Swalwell. She didn't really get much, \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 's driver was a Chinese spy, it didn't really hear much in the backseat.\par \par \uc2 They say that every time about China. I don't trust that. These Chinese guys are in Beijing with a little joystick. If they're not picking up anything, then why are they zigzagging the balloon over all of our military bases?\par \par \uc2 Well, Joe says he took countermeasures. It's like when Jesse Jr. tries to hide in the living room, he takes a blanket. Where's Jesse? I don't know. Peekaboo. That's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How many times has the \uc2 United States government\uc2  says we've taken countermeasures to make sure the Chinese don't steal our stuff? They've been saying that for three decades, but they keep stealing our stuff. So, I'm not taking Joe's word for it.\par \par \uc2 Maybe we did black out the military bases. What if they flew over \uc2 General Electric\uc2 's headquarters, Caterpillars' headquarters, \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2 's headquarters? Do they have the countermeasures that the Defense Department has?\par \par \uc2 Did \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  throw a blanket over his hard drive up? They got nothing. So, I'm not really trusting what they're telling us.\par \par \uc2 Now, imagine this happens with Trump. Imagine the Chinese sends $6 million dollars to the Trump family. The Chinese send $100 million to the Trump Center at Penn and then give Don Jr. a few diamonds.\par \par \uc2 And then, Trump lies all about it, tries to cover it up and the Chinese float a spy device all over. And he doesn't touch the thing until after it spies on us and shoots it out of the sky. They would impeach in a second.\par \par \uc2 Montana has no one living in Montana, Alaska. Even less people. You know how the radius was to pick up this thing that fell into the ocean? Seven miles. You're saying you couldn't find a seven-square-mile area in Alaska or Montana to recover this thing?\par \par \uc2 Last time I checked too, when electronics get wet. They fizzle out. When it lands on land, they probably survived better. It's almost like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wanted the balloon sack to get wet.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- the Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's almost like he wanted it to get wet. So, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  takes it, so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  now has the laptop and it has the Chinese balloon sac in Quantico. They're analyzing both.\par \par \uc2 The best part about this is that Joe's overruled by the generals, Joe said shoot it down. And the general said, let's wait. Let's wait.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Judge?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: This is -- this is the latest act by China in terms of espionage and spying on the United States. And if we look at it any differently than that, then we are being naive.\par \par \uc2 Whether it was those \uc2 Confucius Institutes\uc2  at the colleges, whether it's they're hacking into a hundred American country -- companies, with the backing of the Chinese Communist Party, whether it's TikTok, the honey pots, it's a constant effort by China to sabotage the United States.\par \par \uc2 And but for those reporters in Montana that we found out about, we would never have known about this. And I give you as evidence of that the fact that Biden had no intention of pulling Blinken back from going to China. Blinken wasn't pulled back until Friday, when a Biden apparently knew and the administration knew on Saturday, but oh no, once the American people knew, I'm going to be tough. I'm not going to have Blinken go over there. Hogwash. We just called your bluff.\par \par \uc2 Now, China says initially, it was a meteorological surveillance, and the U.S. was good with that. There's no problem here. They're just doing climate -- checking climate stuff.\par \par \uc2 And so, they -- the White House then says they didn't get much of anything. Well, how the hell do you know that? And what are they doing hovering above are intercontinental ballistic missiles sites?\par \par \uc2 And I understand for military people that they can actually see the lay of the airplanes underground in those missile sites.\par \par \uc2 And the here's another one for you. For those why the balloons? The balloons fly above aircraft, they fly 60, 80, 100,000 feet above ground. What does that tell you? That level of height doesn't permit the shooting of it down.\par \par \uc2 In other words, an F-22 cannot take down one of these, it's not a damn balloon from a birthday party, it's a spycraft. An F-22 cannot take it down, if it's 60, 80,000 or 100,000 feet up.\par \par \uc2 So, what we've got is them hovering over Montana, our intercontinental ballistic sites. And Biden then says, the military said, no, no, don't take it down. Hogwash. If we learned anything from Biden and Afghanistan, it's that the top generals, when they were put on the road said, we told Biden you got to leave people there. We told Biden, you have to leave people in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And Biden said no, they never told me, they never warned me. The bottom line is this, when \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  gets up today and she says, well, it's up to China to decide what the relationship is. That's naive. It is up to us to set the parameters and to say you have violated our sovereignty. You have breached our security. There's going to be consequences, but not with this guy.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. Great start for a Monday?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Maybe we could probably just go home.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, let's go home.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. Coming up, President Biden ready to serve up some B.S. at the State of the Union, but Americans are calling his bluff.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Popular music. President Biden expected to take a victory lap and boast about how wonderful of a job he's done during tomorrow's State of the Union. Here's just a little preview of the coming attractions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Am I taking blame for inflation? No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it was already there when I got here, man. Remember what the economy was like when I got here? Jobs were hemorrhaging. Inflation was rising. We weren't manufacturing the damn thing here. We were in real economic difficulty. That's why I don't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But the verdict is already in on the Biden economy. A record number of Americans 41 percent say they're financially worse off since the big guy came into office, except maybe Hunter and his booming art business.\par \par \uc2 And with numbers that bad, the spinmeisters and the administration are having a tough time. Watch as Mayor Pete struggles to list any Biden accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: Let's go to the President's State of the Union. Look, he's got a lot of things to tell. Why do you think that it has not penetrated the American public?\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Well, these things don't sell themselves. And it's one of the reasons I'm really looking forward to that State of the Union address.\par \par \uc2 I will say that there have been so many accomplishments under this administration. It can be difficult to list them in a distilled way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The woman asked you, you know, there's just so many reasons he can't even list. Where to begin? That never happens to me.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, never. He says inflation is already here. It was already here. That's like starting a seven-alarm fire and then saying, well, there was a duraflame already in the fireplace. So, it's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 And it just -- I mean, the only people apparently they're doing well in the Biden economy is Biden's family. I mean, it is incredible, I guess like they're not going to say who's buying these half a million dollar paintings? I don't blame them, because it probably is somebody unsavory, but yes, he's very lucky. Biden's presidency came about at the perfect time, we have a frantically distracted population. If this -- if he was -- if he became president in the 80s, in the 70s when I think he tried to run, it would have -- he would have been even more of a disaster because there were fewer cheaper escapes from your daily trials.\par \par \uc2 If you could say, oh my God, these eggs cost a lot but then you can go back to scrolling on TikTok or popping an edible and it's not so bad. We've made so much progress in society in terms of tech and leisure that a recession is actually less felt because we've numbed ourselves. We've numbed ourselves against so much misery, that maybe we'll just endure it. It's kind of sad, but it's also progress.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Things are so bad that Greg's edibles now cost less than a dozen eggs.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's amazing. Can you make an omelet with edible?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I would not try to do that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. Well, if you do, you get back to us and see how it is.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What do you expect tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think there has been a flurry of bad polling for the Biden team, even though they don't like to admit it. In fact, today, \uc2 Brian Deese\uc2 , who's on his way out as economic director there, he said that the ABC poll showed that two-thirds of Americans said that circumstances are better. That's not what that poll said. 16, One six percent of people said that their circumstances are better.\par \par \uc2 And what Mayor Pete just said was, well, we just have to communicate better. It is not a communications problem. And I think what you'll see is tomorrow night, it's like when you tell an angry woman to calm down. How does that go down?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not well.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And when you tell somebody to cheer up? It just makes them more mad. And this is a feeling --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Women, right, Dana?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, just smile more.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I know.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Turn that frown upside down lady, and then they just get more mad. So, I think that you'll hear a little bit of that tomorrow night that everything's great, no, look -- no, look, we have all the jobs. No, like, no, we actually do have all the jobs, people have two or three jobs. People are coming back from the pandemic, they just -- there is a feeling in the country that they just do not feel it.\par \par \uc2 And now what \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did at his point in the presidency, but remember, he said, I feel your pain. I get it. I hear you. Don't think you're going to hear that tomorrow night, because they are talking about how great things are. And they are going to try to draw a contrast immediately.\par \par \uc2 This is not just a State of the Union speech, I think that this will be like the anchor for the 2024 reelection effort. And I wouldn't be surprised if during this unity speech he decides to say that if you look at the Republicans, they're the ones who want to take away your Social Security. They're the ones that want to take away your Medicare. They're the ones this, that and the other thing, and it's going to come off pretty badly for some people, some people might like it.\par \par \uc2 Must be kind of hard to be him though, right? Because their stories that the economy have better jobs numbers, but his vice president gets terrible headlines, again, in the New York -- in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  today, saying -- they're saying -- basically saying that, gosh, no one thinks she's doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 There's only two stories for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . It's either she screwed up, or that everybody thinks she's about to screw up. Like there's just no other choices. And he's feeling though like he's done all these great things. And yet, most people, a majority of Democrats don't want Biden to run again. But tomorrow night will be the precursor to his reelection speech.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Why hasn't he been able to emotionally connect with the country? She mentioned, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Biden was always supposed to be the guy that was like feeling your pain, regular Joe. Why can't he say yes, this is tough and acknowledge that?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, he did. He won in 2020 because he was the guy that said that, right? He was empathy Joe, talking about what's going on as COVID is pillaging the country, people are losing their jobs, their loved ones, etcetera. And if we were called the midterms, not that long ago, his numbers were also terrible around that. And Democrats have won of the best performance of that since the 1930s for a first term president.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not at all convinced that if we had the midterms again tomorrow, that we wouldn't have the same results and that people can say, I feel like I'm not better off, I think the economy is in the -- in the dumper or whatever. And I prefer the Democrats to the Republicans. I don't know, we don't have an election tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, we would have known about declassified documents at the Penn Biden Center.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK maybe, and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  by the way is getting a visit from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for his documents. That's new to add to the pile there. It's not about the documents, the frustration that the administration feels in terms of these accomplishments. And there are real accomplishments, we're talking about the China, the CHIPS Act is something that really, if you want to piss off \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , take away his semiconductor business. And that's what the Biden ministration did.\par \par \uc2 So, you have that good bipartisan gun reform that had never happened. We had 5-year-olds slaughtered in Sandy Hook we couldn't get gun reform. He got that done.\par \par \uc2 You have the infrastructure bill, people are getting hundreds of millions into rural broadband that will change the lives of people. The investment in HBCUs, now we have insulin capped at $35.00 a month.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Will you give the State of the Union address?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm trying. Well, do you like it?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Are my ratings good?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Judge Jeanine? Are you going to be watching the edge of your seat tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I will. You know what, I'm going to time myself and see how long I can last listening to him.\par \par \uc2 First of all, 62 percent of Americans think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hasn't accomplished very much. So, you know, you can have, you know, Jessica, you can say x, y, and z. And I really think the reason that he came through in the midterms that the -- that the Democrats did, I think it was all about abortion, abortion, abortion, abortion.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think it was a testament to how great \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is. The truth is 71 percent of the country thinks we're on the wrong track. People can't afford food anymore. Only 31 percent of the country thinks the man is competent, they think he's incompetent.\par \par \uc2 And if you don't think this so-called balloon, which is a euphemism for a Chinese spy craft, if you don't think that impacts Americans in a very unusual way, we're not used to them being over our land. This is the homeland, this is America. They're now buying millions of acres near these intercontinental ballistic missile sites so that they can watch grow corn, and then have an impact on our food supply?\par \par \uc2 It's not what we think it is. I really believe that, you know, Americans recognize inflation isn't transitory, that he's surrendered to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , and that all of the bad stuff if they can at least bring it out in terms of the fentanyl crisis, the southern border, what's going on in the air and the border. You know, they don't want if someone said this, they don't want cheap talking points. They want something that reflects who they are as Americans, and how they can survive in the next few years. That's all.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. And you will be doing some live commentary tomorrow for the State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'll be there with a group.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We'll be here at the table while you're in D.C. Yes, we're not allowed to travel.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Will you miss me?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We don't know who's sitting there yet. We're thinking about getting some animals from the Columbus Zoo.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We got to go. \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  calling \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a coward. We'll tell you why, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Black Lives Matter Founder Patrisse Cullors is furious with President Biden and other top Democrats for trying to distance the party from the Defund the Police Movement. She says, "there has been deep cowardice from the officials. The people who hold the power have chosen not to wield it on behalf of Black life. They've chosen to side with violent police forces."\par \par \uc2 That comes as Congress tries to give police reform another shot. Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  is urging his fellow Democrats to compromise this time so that they can actually get some something done.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jesse, you know, when you've got Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , OK, who's urging the Dems to give some ground saying basically that, you know, if you can't get a perfect piece of legislation, let's try for a good piece.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is that a starter?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not for me. I don't want police reform. I think the police are doing a great job. I see some of these things they're trying to do, banning choke hold. Sometimes you need to get the arm around the neck to subdue the guy. And now people are afraid to even go near the neck and bad things can happen. The no-knock warrants, I don't know enough about it but I do know what it's going to do if they do it. They're just going to instead of no knocking, they're going to go knock, boom, and hit the battering ram.\par \par \uc2 The immunity thing, so then you can get sued for millions of dollars for something that happens on the job. No other union has that. Teachers unions don't have that. Why do police have that? I don't like that they're getting singled out. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  probably likes the fact that some \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  thief, a lot of money, is telling him you're a bad guy for not defunding the police. That makes \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  look good.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. You're nodding your head, Greg. Absolutely right. If you want to look less like an idiot when an idiot turns on you, that helps. And the White House must love this news. BLM took millions and millions of dollars from people and offered nothing in return, right? They're BL -- they're BLDM, Black Lives Don't Matter, because they would have done something.\par \par \uc2 But the good news is I think most of the money came from gullible White liberals, not Blacks. I think uh hard-working Blacks would never give their money to an organization this loose and this weird. I think it was never really about helping Black people, it was about -- and this is brilliant. I wish I thought of this. It was about monetizing white liberal virtue signaling. It's a smart business model. And they just took that money. I'm now beginning to admire this person for being so in your face about swindling all these guilty White liberals.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, it's like the mob, you can't go out and buy a fur coat after the --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They did.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's the thing. They did.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They lied low.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, that's exactly, Dana, what Patrisse Cullors did. She went out and bought a $6 million mansion. She gave, you know, what, several hundred- thousand-dollar jobs to I think one was her brother and then it was her -- I think her baby's father. And you know why -- but let me ask you this. Why if the inner city Blacks and Brown people 80 percent want more police, why aren't they uniting and saying bring this money back, these people are frauds?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's a -- it's an excellent question. And I -- you know, we do these stories every Monday morning. You can do a story about how terrible the weekend was in Chicago and all the people that were either injured in shootings or were killed. Many of them are African-American or Hispanic and they -- I think that they -- look, I'm not in their shoes, obviously, but I do wonder if they just feel beaten down, overwhelmed, and they're just trying to survive day to day. And it's up to the leadership to do something more.\par \par \uc2 So, you have Mayor Lightfoot who is running for reelection and is not promising any change. There's -- and then you had even in Philly, there was a recall of the prosecutor, got reelected. Same thing in California. So, there -- I don't know where that change is going to come from.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats have pinned themselves into a bit of a corner especially when it comes to Congress on police reform. Last year, Republicans went pretty far down the table to try to do a negotiation run by Senator Tim Scott. \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  of New Jersey was on the Democratic side. Apparently, those two have a very good relationship. But the left just wanted so much more. And so, they're preventing any progress from happening.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  I think is trying to be the elder statesman that says, we can be smarter about this both from a substantive standpoint and from a political standpoint, but the left won't let it happen. They are going to - - they're going to keep doing this to them. So, then all the Republicans can say the Democrats want to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And in the end, Jessica, I mean, what you've got are -- you know, you've got the same scenario with and what Jesse pointed out to was the qualified immunity.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You can't take that away from a police officer. You just can't.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think these conversations which I think is part of the problem only happen in earnest after some extreme tragedy, right, a George Floyd or Tyre Nichols or \uc2 Laquan McDonald\uc2 . And I hope that Tim Scott and \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  who do have this great relationship can continue with this and that they can work the margins to the point that the defund the police side of things and the people on the right who are not interested in any compromises are rendered irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 And the numbers can work out that way. I mean, the margins are so slim and George Santos, you know, where's -- who knows where he'll be next week. So, I think that that's possible. What \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 , the organization, did is such a betrayal of the movement. It's pretty horrifying. So, \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  is a philosophy, right? It's not a group. It's not necessarily something you should be giving $5.00 to, $10.00 to. It's an idea that Black lives should matter equally to White lives and to Blue lives.\par \par \uc2 And the corruption of that message has set the movement back so far that people who just want to say there's nothing wrong with the police can point to this and say you're con artists running the most important organization for this. And you're right, it was mostly White people, and I would say really well-intentioned White people who saw what happened to George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They're the worst.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well-intentioned White people.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm sitting right here. They saw what happened to George Floyd and said what can I do. And they saw an organization with the name of the thing that they believe. And it's really just -- it's so fundamentally messed up because there are great organizations that do good work for protecting minority lives. Any cause that you can find out there, you need to do more research. And they took advantage of the fact that people would be so upset that there would be this frenzy giving and I really hate that. And they also amplified defund the police as if it was the policy position of the party and it wasn't.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And they never said, can we have our money back, you're not doing with it what you said. And those White people are -- what would you call them?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know. But I love the idea of well-intentioned White people ruining everything.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That should be a book.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That should be a book.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, coming up, what could go wrong?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: A major airline is about to lower pilot standards even after two airplanes almost collided.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, this is reassuring. Southwest is cutting back on pilot hiring standards even after a scary near collision involving one of its planes. The struggling airline will be reducing the required flying hours for prospective pilots in half in an effort to boost staff. But is this really the right time?\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 FedEx\uc2  cargo plane was forced to abort a landing in order to avoid hitting a passenger Southwest jet on the same Runway that was about to take off.\par \par \uc2 Greg I feel like Southwest needs to not be in the headlines anymore.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I would advise them as just a freelance consultant to cut back on other things first, right? Don't just start the pilots --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Like foods?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, exactly. Maybe some ice cubes. But you know we're seeing lack standards now or standards being relaxed everywhere, customer service, fitness standards, with military, police, and now you have airlines. Maybe this is just a natural evolution towards automation. You know, we're worried that robots would take our jobs but it looks like we really don't want them. And we can't be bothered with meeting the standards so maybe this is how it's supposed to be.\par \par \uc2 Remember, you know, A.I., robots, they don't need to practice. They're ready to go from day one and they never screw up, ever screw up, never, never.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, this is the next step in the simulation?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it is. It is. And frankly, I can't wait.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All I could think of today was that the CEO of \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  must just like want to throw in the towels.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's like -- it's like every time his phone ring, he's like, what now? And it's never good. Although with these collision type things, this is the third. And if bad things happen in threes, maybe we're done with it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I like that. What do you think, Jesse? Optimism, threes we're done?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You never admit you're lowering your standards. Guys don't. Some start dating fours now. \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  doesn't say --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You're not dating anymore.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I said guys, I didn't say me. \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  doesn't say, you know what, we're going to mess with quality control. Good luck with your next vehicle. You never -- you do it in secret. You just don't announce it. And the reason they're doing this is because of the vax.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, of course, right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Everything is because of the vax.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, I missed that in my pocket somehow but --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You just can't say it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, you did.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, well, I'm me.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, a lot of people are watching too.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, first of all, I don't fly Southwest anymore. There's been too many things that are screwed up. But look, a group of senators is pushing for a passenger bill of rights bill that would give travelers at least $1,350 if they're bumped due to a oversold flight.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's a lot.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Let me tell you what the problem is. Your flight is oversold. You're sitting in the seat. They try to get people in the terminal to take some money, $200, and we'll fly you later. They don't do it. Then you're sitting on the tarmac for an hour while they're saying we'll give you $500, we'll give you $1000, we'll give you $1500. I don't want to sit there anymore.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Just put the people in the plane who got there and leave.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It would be cheaper. You won't have to pay anybody anything, you know.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's a good point.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Crisis solved.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The next CEO of Southwest right here.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Not a chance.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: All right, up next, it's like a pop -- a plot -- sorry, I was still in the old topic -- out of a movie, the crazy but true story behind this Coast Guard rescue of a suspected international boat thief.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: A stolen boat, a daring Coast Guard rescue, and a dead fish at The Goonies' house. How does this all come together? Well, it sounds like a crazy movie but it actually happened this weekend. It all started when Oregon Police saw this incredible Coast Guard rescue. Check it out here. A rookie swimmer saved a man's life on a yacht that was struggling in rough water. The boat does a full barrel roll. The lone passenger was taken to the hospital and later released. And that's when the plot twist comes in.\par \par \uc2 Police recognize the man in these photos as the same person wanted for leaving a dead fish on the steps of the home feature in the classic movie The Goonies. And that boat, well, it was stolen. The perp who is now arrested is originally from Canada. He crossed the border illegally and is wanted for crimes there too.\par \par \uc2 Might we deport him, Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. But I have a feeling he'll find his way back. He might not come to the Northern Border. He may come through the Southern Border. You know, it's not surprising. Usually, when you're involved in one criminal activity, I mean you've got a life of crime. By the time you steal a boat like that, you've done a few things, so it's not surprising. But leaving a fish on The Goonies -- on the set of The Goonies, that's like -- that's penalty eligible.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It raises a serious question, what did Robert Davi do to that man that caused him to leave a fish on the house?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Like, why would you do that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't have -- I mean, what kind of signal is he sending.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know. Isn't that kind of ironic that he throws a dead fish and then he almost dies in the ocean?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He was swimming with the fish.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, he could have been a dead fish.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, I'm kind of scared on boats anyway. Jessica, are you?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, definitely out that deep. And it's kind of a lame read of this but just kind of in awe of the power of the ocean just watching it. I feel like we don't see footage like that. We see like whales --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I have great respect for the ocean.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, how about you on this story?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, the Canadians are hitting our people in heads with hammers, they're stealing our boats, and then they're not giving us a heads up when these Chinese air balloons are floating into our territory? We need to take a second look at the Canadians.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, yes, by the way, why didn't Justin Trudeau shoot it down?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: What's wrong with Justin?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What was he -- tell him it's a truck.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: What's going on up there?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tell him it's a truck.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We're even. You know why? Apparently, the illegal immigrants in New York don't like it here, they're going to Canada.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I know. And the Canadians are -- you know, maybe they will be more welcomed. Maybe the food is better.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's so delicious.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, yes. A great deal of our french fries.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It does sounds good.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Maybe that's a good "ONE MORE THING" Feeding Frenzy. But stay tuned. You might see one next.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's on you.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana just spit out some ice cream.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh. you're awful.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is disgusting. All right, time for "ONE MORE THING." Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, it's National Frozen Yogurt Day. And this is my Feeding Frenzy, the wonderful people. And I mean wonderful. These people are my people. Pinkberry gave us some of the most delicious flavors they have. We have original which I think is their best flavor. Then you have ruby chocolate that looks like strawberry but it plays a trick on you. And then you have chocolate hazelnut.\par \par \uc2 Fun facts about frozen yogurt, it improves bad breath, so Greg, have a few more.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The original name for froyo was frogurt, and we're all glad they changed it. I haven't had this in a while because we moved so now we're taking a basically a truckload back to the new place. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," Lindsey Graham, Monica Crowley, and Ted Cruz, 7:00.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, that's delicious. Let's do it, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so there's a couple from Indiana. They're about to have the chillest -- chill-est baby ever. Kyle and Abby Weiner were surprised to see their baby throwing up a peace sign. See those fingers? It's a peace sign. 31 week ultrasound, the couple says they've decided to name their daughter Ellie, and they're psyched about her seemingly serene behavior. She's a peacenik.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Fantabulous.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's cool. Avoid sick joke.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight, a great show. You got to watch. David Angelo, Emily Compagno, Brian Brenberg, Kat Timpf. Dana said Brian Brenberg is a real jerk. I haven't met him.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, he's such a jerk.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, he's jerk. OK.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He's mean to everybody.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, let's do this. Greg's Auction House. OK, you got -- you've heard about the sand from the beach where Tom Brady retired? Last Wednesday, it hit \uc2 eBay\uc2 . 119 people have bid on the sand with the current bit of $99,000 for that thing of sand which made me think since, you know, he's the loser from Serra High School and I'm the winner from Serra High School, I'm putting this on \uc2 eBay\uc2 . We're going to -- we're going to break his record, OK. So, this is my unicorn mug. It's got a little broken unicorn.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Wait, Greg, you're going to need a lot of money. I don't think that's legal.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Like, I don't care. Look, man, I'm --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We'll split it off.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We'll split it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Deal.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, cool.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Did you see his underpants picture that he put up tonight?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Did I see it? Oh, I took it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: What underpants picture?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'll send it to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OK, I should shut up now. Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, well, this man performing at halftime trick. Here's to Dana's Crazy Tricks. Michigan basketball game, he stole the show. Look at - - check this guy out.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I just thought this was quite impressive.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How did he do that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know. Look at that. Think of how much time and effort winning that crowd, that practice. He catches them all. It's amazing. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: My hat's off to him. Where do I come up with these?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jessica, try to end this show on an upbeat note.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I will do my best. And I got a good one. So, you might be smarter than a fifth-grader, but this 9-year-old high school graduate will give you a run for your money. David Balogun is now the youngest student to graduate from Reach Cyber Charter in Pennsylvania. The school allows for gifted students like David to take classes online year round and go at their own accelerated pace. He's already a member of the high IQ Society Mensa, and will receive his official diploma in June. Here's what he had to say about his achievement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BALOGUN, YOUNGEST STUDENT TO GRADUATE FROM REACH CYBER CHARTER IN PENNSYLVANIA: I wanted to do it because I had the abilities to do it. So, why not use those abilities to the greater good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. And he's already taking college-level classes now.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Good for him.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He's probably taller than me too. All right --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He would have shot down the balloon.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I wasn't going to go there.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He would have shot down the balloon.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, 