o time, we get all of that. But you got -- he gives you enough ammunition with some of his policies. Why don't you just stick to that?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'd rather save America instead of his mess-ups and screw-ups and his horrible policies.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Do you think America is better off since he's been president looking at inflation and energy independence, looking at our border? Do you think the world respects and fears \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the way they did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Tat's very specific.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, no, I'll be honest. That that's my feeling. Obviously, there are liberals and progressives who feel otherwise. I mean, I'm a guy that -- I'm in the middle, I'm a centrist, I can go on either side depending on one issue or another.\par \par \uc2 But I'm going to tell you right now, I don't like the excessive spending. I think that ultimately, it comes back to bite us as a nation. I pay attention to that.\par \par \uc2 I pay attention to issues involving national security .That's very, very important to me. I don't like what I'm seeing with that sometimes, there's no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 I think his hearts in the right place, I think he tries to do the right thing. Whether or not he's doing that depends on what side of the aisle that you lean on, because what you keep missing and I keep telling you this, you have a lot of people who --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We take notes --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Take notes because this is very important, you're under the impression that a lot of people don't know better. What I'm saying to you is a lot of people want the country to be in the direction he's in. Now you might feel differently, I might even feel differently -- some people actually want to what has happened.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Listen to you the point, there are way too many people in this country that want socialism.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But socialism, I wrote a chapter in my last book that "Free or Die", socialism, whatever form, manifestation, name, every time it's promised, promises are never fulfilled. It always ends in more poverty and a loss of freedom.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Let me tell you what I said -- let me tell somebody what I said the defense of you one time. I said, listen, I'm a proud captain. Listen I ain't apologizing for that to anybody.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We know that. I read your contract.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There you go.\par \par \uc2 I'm not apologizing for that to anybody. But when you say socialism, I think there's another category. There are some people who don't oppose capitalism, they just believe that they don't have a fair equitable shot at taking advantage of a capitalist -\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And you agree with me that any American that puts their head down, works hard, plays by the rules provides, goods and services that people want need and desire can succeed.\par \par \uc2 SMITIH: I think they can, I think they can, but that doesn't take away from the fact --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I love your book --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- more people that deny it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: -- and I love you as a friend. You're a good man.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Stephen A. Smith.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, you won't believe why a group of Catholic students were thrown out of a Smithsonian museum. Wait until you hear this story, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. A terrible story of the Smithsonian Air and Space museum in Washington, D.C. A group of Catholic school students they were thrown out of the museum during their visit for the national March for Life, and they were thrown out for wearing hats that had a pro-life message on them. The message read: rosary, pro-life.\par \par \uc2 A mother of one of the students is claiming that they were asked to remove the hats or leave and after explaining that the hats were used to identify each other and keep the group together as they were escorted, they threw them out of the museum.\par \par \uc2 The museum did release a statement saying what happened is not in line with their policy and that they provided training to prevent this from ever happening again and keep in mind, the Smithsonian receives over one billion of your taxpayer dollars a year.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, he's leading up the case for the American Center for Law and Justice, he's their director, Jordan Sekulow, and one of the students thrown out of the museum Patrick Murphy.\par \par \uc2 Welcome both of you.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, I'm fond of that name. That's my son's name. Okay, tell us what happened.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY, PRO-LIFE STUDENT: Yes, so we got done with the March for Life and we decided to go the Air and Space Museum, we walk in and it is a breeze. We go through in 30 minutes and we end up at the Wright brothers museum or the exhibit.\par \par \uc2 And, at this time, we are dressed by two women in all black presumably security guards and they tell us in-directly and they say, all people wearing pro-life hats, take it off. And immediately, we're confused. So, one of the students with us, he preceded to say, we used these hats for identification purposes, this is for our group.\par \par \uc2 So, the two women blew us off and we walked out. After that, we decided to sit against the wall and approached by a large man as we were walking out because we had to catch our bus.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He comes up to us saying y'all are about to make my day. With a massive smile on his face.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: And he tells us he's got reports saying we said no to taking our hats off. We are in trouble for some odd reasons. And, one of the students saying it is used for identification. He was not having it. I then said --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Keep going.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yeah, I then said this is a violation of our First Amendment right, this is a government-funded building. How are we paying for this with our taxes and I am not allowed to wear this hat.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I got to give you credit.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He said this is as neutral zone.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He said that's a neutral zone, and that does not apply here, and we were blown away. We had no words.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I am running out of time, Patrick. I want to hear about the legal side, I don't mean to interrupt you and I am very proud for what you did and you stood your ground.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, legally, does the statement mean anything to you, what are you going to do? And by the way, I want to help the ACLJ win this.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN SEKULOW, AMERICAN CENTER FOR LAW & JUSTICE: Absolutely. Legal action is imminent. We've already put them on notice to save everything, because in that statement, they said they did a training afterwards.\par \par \uc2 We want to know, Sean, about the training before, and they didn't apologize to these students. They picked on the wrong students, as people can tell from Patrick. We are going to get to the bottom of it because we suspect now that we've gone public, we're going to hear from more people who attended March for Life, who have been harassed at other museums and told for some reason, you have to take your hat.\par \par \uc2 Of course, they were never to do it. That's not the Smithsonian's policy. You can wear what you want there. We are fighting back. And if you need help, ACLJ.org. We want to hear from you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. And I'm going to try and help you on this case and we'll follow until the end.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, great to see you. Patrick, great job. We're proud of you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, this audience, last call, they get the final word, they get to grade me. That's next, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Back to my bartending days. We call last call. The audience now gets to grade the show. Tell us what they like and didn't like.\par \par \uc2 I believe -- up there. Go ahead, sir. How are you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, thanks, Sean. First time, long time here.\par \par \uc2 Give you an A minus, the minus just because I didn't catch a football, but I had a -- I had a quick question. Yeah. Do you think the folks who got duped into voting for Biden primarily from biased media groups will ever admit they made mistake?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That played a part. Democrats also don't run normal campaigns. They're in the ballot harvesting business. Only one state insists that voters turn on their own ballot. That's Alabama.\par \par \uc2 We've got to change it. And Republicans have got to resist early voting and voting by mail.\par \par \uc2 Next, who's next?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, actually, I give you an a for the show today. But I think we actually have to go out and use their tactics. We have to do ballot harvesting.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Legal ballot harvesting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have to get people out, legal ballot harvesting. And one of the problems I see, especially here in New York, we have Republican- itis where people don't come out and vote because they always think they're going to lose.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All the Republicans in New York moved to Florida. There's about a thousand about people a day moving to Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell people going out to vote, and Democrats are voting for Republicans. The Republicans aren't voting --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Next, real quick. Who's next? Hi. Anybody want to be next?\par \par \uc2 Yes, ma'am? Hi.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'd like to know how we can get Biden impeached.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He'll get impeached, but the Senate won't convict him. However, we have to win in `24. We got time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all. Love you all. Thank you for being here. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for making the show possible. Set your DVR so you never miss an episode.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled, here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230203ej2200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Left Plays The Race Card In Nichols Case; Another Day, Another Blunder For The Biden Admin\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 8,622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, a pro-life activist who was raided and arrested by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  over a protest outside of an abortion clinic was just acquitted of all charges. He will join us straight ahead with his first interview. Also, we have the exclusive video from the day in question. We will show you that video.\par \par \uc2 But, first, growing calls for justice in the horrific death of Tyre Nichols. Now, of course, this gut-wrenching case will be adjudicated in a court of law, rightfully so. But many on the left know just who to blame and what to blame -- white supremacy. According to them, the five African- American cops that allegedly beat Nichols to death are racist participants in a deeply bigoted system and that is the narrative.\par \par \uc2 Now here's the "Time Magazine", quote, well, there can be no doubt that entrenched institutional racism lies at the heart of this case. "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ", quote: Black cops killed Tyre Nichols. Systemic racism is the death of us all. "Forbes", quote, five Black Memphis cops upheld institutional and cultural racism in Tyree Nichols murder. Fake news CNN, quote, the police who killed Tyre Nichols were Black, but they might still have been driven by racism. And CNN's Mondaire Jones, quote, if you think that the Memphis police officers had to be white in order to exhibit anti-blackness, you need to take an AP African-American studies course that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  just banned.\par \par \uc2 And the always obsessed, race obsessed Jemele Hill tweeted, quote: The entire system of policing is based on white supremacist violence and we see people under the boot of oppression carry its water all the time.\par \par \uc2 And here's Congresswoman Cory Bush, quote, the mere presence of Black officers does not stop policing from being a tool of white supremacy. And she then accused the government of slaughtering African Americans at the altar of white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 And on TV, the rhetoric was even worse. You decide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I mean, the idea that Black cops can't be racist towards other Black people on the street, in school, at traffic stops must come as a huge surprise to millions of Black people in this country who've had to deal with Black cops.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It shouldn't be a surprise to people that individual Black people can actually do anti-Black things.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I think a race is still on the table.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We sometimes forget, unfortunately, African-Americans can also be guilty of hatred and bias and bigotry against other African- Americans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And race still is involved, George, you and I spoke about on the phone, because I don't believe those five cops would have done that to a young white on a -- on a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fact that it was Black officers shows it doesn't matter the color of the cop, it's the victim.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How many times do we have to -- do we need to see White people also get beaten before anybody will do anything?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Everything you just heard there is false. Here's something that Whoopi, the ladies of "The View" apparently don't know, White people do get beaten by the police. In fact, far more White people are killed every year by cops than any other race. According to an online organization that tracks this data, 374 White Americans died during police altercations in 2022. If you average that out, a little more than one per day. I don't know if the ladies of "The View" know any of the names of the people -- the 374 people.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the media mob devotes entire news cycles to these deaths? Did they even count anymore the number of people shot, shot and killed in Chicago anymore on any given weekend? Of course, not.\par \par \uc2 So, why are these incidents largely ignored? According to a study from \uc2 Washington State University\uc2 , listen to this, they were reported on by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " in 2016. Quote: officers are three times less likely to shoot on unarmed Black suspects and unarmed White suspects.\par \par \uc2 And look at this study from the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  covered by "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " also in 2016, quote, when it comes to the most lethal form of force, police shootings, the study finds no racial bias.\par \par \uc2 So, based on both academic studies and actual data, there is no systemic racism in policing. It doesn't exist. Now, of course, there are good cops and I would say that's the vast overwhelming majority and there is a small majority of bad cops.\par \par \uc2 What we saw in Memphis was horrible policing from really bad cops but blaming some kind of phantom all-encompassing boogeyman is not going to solve any problems at the Memphis police department, or any other police department around the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, the left, they're not concerned seemingly at all. They're not interested in actually improving policing. They only seem to care about their own divisive narrative.\par \par \uc2 Maybe these Democrats, maybe these media personalities, maybe they're just lying for pure political gain, or maybe they harbor racist animosity and hatred towards people that happen to be white or Asian or other races. I don't know, maybe they just don't know the facts or maybe they just overcome with the emotion. Maybe they just pick and choose.\par \par \uc2 How come the 99.999 percent of people that are killed in any form of violence is rarely reported on? Truth always matters. That is, unless you're \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . His own DOJ is now starting a civil rights probe in search of anything that could back the less deeply flawed narrative.\par \par \uc2 Now this brings us to tonight's HANNITY hot seat. And joining us now, attorney Daryl Parks, along with the president of the National Fallen Officers Foundation, retired Dallas Police Sergeant Tre Pennie is with us.\par \par \uc2 Daryl, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 Tre, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 Daryl, I just read all the statistics. I just told you the truth. I just gave you the academic studies.\par \par \uc2 So you tell me all these people that are claiming five Black officers, one Black suspect killed which was horrific, we all saw it, I do believe that there rightly will be justice, but explain to me how they race to the -- no pun -- intended how they run to the race narrative immediately?\par \par \uc2 DARYL PARKS, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Sean, I probably can't explain that at all, but what I will tell you though is like you just said, the truth matters. I think the conversation should really be about dash cam and body cam, and how those types of things actually bring transparency both to law enforcement --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I called for those for years.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But wait a minute but this is not the topic. The topic here is simple, why do people take a case that involves five Black officers, one Black suspect and turn it into a race case?\par \par \uc2 Now, there's going to be a civil rights investigation. You represent Trayvon Martin's family. That did not happen in your case, did it?\par \par \uc2 PARKS: No, it didn't, it didn't. And let me say this here, probably injected a race into this case takes away from the conversation of Tyre's life right, and the bigger conversation that we all could be having about safety without -- throughout our country.\par \par \uc2 For example, Sean, in my state alone, we still have sheriff's departments that reject body cam and dash cam. And so, in our communities we ought to be asking about what are we doing in our community, that's a better conversation that would give some benefit to what happened to Tyre and do what was very responsible that the people in Memphis and had done here.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Let's get your take on this, Tre Pennie.\par \par \uc2 TRE PENNIE, PRESIDENT OF NATIONAL FALLEN OFFICER FOUNDATION: Look, Sean, the bottom line is you got a lot of people that want to play into this victim mentality. That's what -- that's what the Democrat Party is doing is really they're going to the -- to the Black community and they're trying to get them up in arms about an issue that that happens very -- and think about this.\par \par \uc2 We got over 700,000 police officers in this country that have thousands and thousands of interactions with police every, every day and you very seldom hear these type of situations that happen because they happen only 0.001 percent of the time. So we got to make sure that we are putting this whole thing in context, and we are addressing the issue that they as they are.\par \par \uc2 Now I will say this, Sean, this is really what they're trying to do. They're trying to promote this George Floyd bill. They want to go they want to get it on paper that they can put these consent decrees in place for law enforcement across the country so they can have -- take qualified immunity from police officers because they want to be able to prosecute us every time they look around.\par \par \uc2 That's why you have less people, less and less -- more -- less and less police officers --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 PENNIE: -- want to go into the profession.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're the head of the Fallen Officers Association. On average, how many police officers do we lose every year? How many did we lose last year? How many did we lose in 2020 and 2021?\par \par \uc2 PENNIE: Yeah. No, look, we're losing and kind of threw the numbers off. We had about 600 last year. But if you look at the actual line of duty deaths, we're losing about 200, 150 officers every year. So we got to make sure that we are qualifying what's happening with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, I asked -- I asked Congress last year that we needed the same level of protection that they're talking about civil -- we got civil rights violations that are put in place when police officers go out and do something to the community, and they can -- they can prosecute officers federally.\par \par \uc2 But what happens when individuals attack police officers and the state drops the -- drops the ball? Then why can't we go and file in the federal court and have them to protect law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Daryl, can you name one officer who lost his life in the line of duty in the last three years?\par \par \uc2 PARKS: All right. I saw -- well, actually, that's the one lost his life right here in Cairo, Georgia, over the weekend. So, yes. But, Sean, let me say --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Do you know the name of one officer? Can you name one officer?\par \par \uc2 PARKS: His name was -- his first name was Clarence, yes, I can, but it's not a contest about me, Sean. I think --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this then, this is the next question.\par \par \uc2 PARKS: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And that is, do you support this insanity of defund, dismantle, no bail laws? Does it make American cities and towns less -- does it make cities less town -- less safe and towns less safe or more safe?\par \par \uc2 PARKS: I don't support that. Let me see what I support, Sean, I think the issue with mental health in this country, right, has to be addressed at its core. That's what we haven't done. Until we address comprehensive mental health, almost all these mass shootings involve some high level of mental health. That should be the discussion and no one is having that type of discussion. That's what I'm for.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know how mental health came into this discussion.\par \par \uc2 I'll give you the last words, Tre.\par \par \uc2 PENNIE: Sean, we have a culture issue and I just want to be honest with everybody. Look, I'm from the Black community. I'm in the Black community, actively in the Black community across the country. And I'm trying to do what I can to change the culture.\par \par \uc2 Guys, we have a culture issue that we need to fix. We allow criminals to come in and victimize us and then what do we do on the other side? Politically, we're going to advocate to get those victims out of jail and then come back home and victimize us more. We got to do -- so we got to stop doing that because we're hurting the people that live in these communities. Some people can't pack up and go leave and go move to the suburbs like most of us can.\par \par \uc2 The reality is people got to sit here and they got to live amongst these violent offenders. We need to start taking care of the people and I'm asking everybody, if you're watching this channel to get behind me and helping us the National Fallen Officer Foundation, advocate for the interest of law enforcement, advocate for the interest of these communities, so we can keep everybody safe and protect law enforcement as a whole.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Tre Pennie, Daryl Parks, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And now we turn to the White House where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is resting peacefully after another big day on the road. Late last week, he managed travel a whole 12 miles away for a bizarre speech in northern Virginia. And today, he was at it yet again. This time only a few miles up I-95, in the real life hellscape known as Baltimore. He wasn't there too long and by the way, who can blame him, but he did manage to make a fool out of himself yet again.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at Biden over the last few days and you ask yourself, is this man mentally capable of being your president? Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: -- the Pence disclosures and classified documents?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Sir, are the searches in your home completed?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Where's Doug, Congressman? He's around here somewhere. Doug knows. Look, here's the deal --\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN: You know, they view the world from Park Avenue. I view it where the wealthy view it -- if everything works well for them, if everything works well for them, all is going to trickle down and help the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: Come on up, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN: Come on dance! I want to dance!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: No, you're not going to dance, believe me.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN: Talk about being deprived of your pride. Look at your child. Child.\par \par \uc2 If you don't think we have a climate crisis, come travel with me around the country. No, I'm not joking. Go back. Go back.\par \par \uc2 If you don't think we have a climate crisis, come travel with me around the country. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 I misspoke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Unfortunately, your vice president is not doing much better. Let's put it this way.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is in way over her head as well. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are doing the work that's about collaborating around the small businesses that will need to do the work -- we talk about our small businesses. Not only it's intergenerational but also we've got entrepreneurs in their teens and 20s, to families who have been doing this for generations, and, you know, are seniors. So, really -- that's also what's also exciting about our small businesses and who they are, because it spans the general races in addition to being intergenerational.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, intergenerational. It's been going on for generations. The intergenerationalists. It's been going on for generations, in case you missed it.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, FOX News contributor Kellyanne Conway, Outkick founder Clay Travis.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's -- part of it is funny to me. The other part of it is these policies are a disaster, and, Kellyanne, people are suffering greatly because of them, and they have no ability to communicate, and yet, nobody in the media besides a few of us seem to want to even talk about it. Why?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, they look away. They help to create the myth of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . They covered her big election rollout in 2019. She ended up with not a single electoral vote. Zero. Quite unimpressive, and she's been unimpressive ever since.\par \par \uc2 I want to make it clear. The challenges for and the criticism about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are not rooted in racism or sexism. They are centered on confidence and communications. She's got deficits in both, clearly.\par \par \uc2 Sean, there has never been a pleasant surprise out of Camp Kamala. Nobody there, none of the staffers, 21 or so who's left, and you've got her not even be able to give a speech off the teleprompter. She faced that teleprompter every day. The teleprompter, when Biden (ph) never used one. Look, the polls have caught up with her.\par \par \uc2 She was asked a question in the debates about -- she was criticized about her criminal reform record in California, as a prosecutor. \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , a matter of fact, criticized her and she asked this and her comment was, I'm hiding the polls from \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 . Let's look at all the polls where she told NBC News Lester Holt that the border was not that important, who cares if she visits it, and then she told Chuck Todd last September of NBC news, quote, the border is secure.\par \par \uc2 These are just flat out lies. She's a liar. She's not a great communicator, not competent. But guess what every single poll says, including NBC News poll. She's far more unpopular than she is popular and I think if she were remotely competent, A, she would have been asked to campaign for more candidates last fall and, B, somebody would get to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Sean, and Clay, and they'd be able to say, look, step aside. He got rid of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You got the brass ring. Your president, but you can get credit now for elevating and electing the first female president, in fact the first female president of color.\par \par \uc2 They can't -- a president of color, they can't do that, because she's so bad at everything. She's got a Hillary problem. She's ignoring Wisconsin, she doesn't have people around her who are going to tell her she's not doing a good job, all we hear from them is reset, reset.\par \par \uc2 And finally, I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does the impossible every day. She makes \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  seem like this lucid, likable, linguist. Hard to do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, look, Clay, let me ask you this. I mean I wish there was a little more time between the midterm and the general election, but this time next year, we will be in the throes of a -- of a real nomination -- nominating race in both parties, how do you assess it as of right now, does \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? Are they going to stay together? Is Joe going to be at the top of the ticket? Is Joe going to know what day it is?\par \par \uc2 I mean, all questions I can't answer.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, OUTKICK FOUNDER: Joe's not going to know what day it is. I'm not sure Joe knows what year it is. Sometimes, I question whether he knows what country he's in.\par \par \uc2 He's got dementia and you can try to argue against it, but anyone who saw the clips and sees the clips regularly of the vacant stare in his eyes, he's getting progressively worse. But I don't think Democrats care, Sean. I think he's going to be at the top of the ticket in 2024, and what I'm calling the "Weekend at Bernie's 2" presidency, they already dragged him across once. They'll try to do it again, running a basement style campaign.\par \par \uc2 And Kellyanne's right. The reason why Democrats are doing that is because Kamala is not a viable option and if kamala isn't the nominee in `24, by Democrats own standards, given the fact that she's the vice president, it would be racist and sexist.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 University of New Hampshire\uc2  poll, Sean, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  polling at 2 percent right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What if \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  says, you know what, I don't think this guy can win, would he get in?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is doing 2 percent. He showed up in the most disrespectful move which I couldn't help but respect, Sean. He visited the White House and did a press availability when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was overseas I've never heard of that happening before. He rolled up his sleeves, he kicked up, probably tried out the resolute desk, he was doing it all.\par \par \uc2 I think somebody should challenge \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in '24 because again, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  polling two percent. How bad is that in New Hampshire? Mayor Pete who was probably the worst secretary in modern American history --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He was the worst mayor.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: -- candidate, he's an atrocious worst mayor, he wasn't any good at that either, he's polling at 23 percent in New Hampshire, over times what Kamala is.\par \par \uc2 Here's the guy I think is going to run. We talked about it on the radio recently. I think Eric Adams is going to jump in. I think it's why he's gone down to El Paso. He's not a beloved guy in the Democrat Party right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's going to run full term.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: He's not going to wait his time. Somebody's challenging him.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, if anybody challenges him, it's going to be Gavin.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne, what's your bet? We got about two seconds.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: I think I owe you $500. Double or nothing, Sean?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Double or nothing, yep. Kellyanne, I'm doing my \uc2 DraftKings\uc2  here.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: You have to do is -- that's okay, well, I'm a \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  fan. So I don't have to make believe. We're winners.\par \par \uc2 So, moving right along to \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he's only talking about that because the Democratic field is so poor, the Democratic bench is so vacant.\par \par \uc2 But, folks, whether it's Eric Adams in New York City or \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  from California, just take out your iPhone. You don't have to spend money on fancy consultants and ad, show everybody what's happened in these states. Nobody wants to be like California with the homelessness and the taxes in all this.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, that's what the last campaign would be about for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right. Kellyanne, thank you. Clay Travis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Biden's classified document scandal not going away as many now calling for increased oversight and people want to see the actual documents. New questions of being raised. We'll check in with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And later, the acquittal of the man that went up against Planned Parenthood. We've got the exclusive video of the day in question, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Tonight's Biden's garbage-gate or garage-gate if you want to call it, anyways, is far from over. As Jonathan Turley points out, there is a treasure trove of documents from Biden's time as a senator that is housed at the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2 . Unfortunately, we have no idea if any of those documents are classified, neither Biden nor the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2  will let anyone review the material. They've been trying to organize it since so we are told.\par \par \uc2 Now, if only the DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had some way to search those documents, but apparently search warrants are off limits when it comes to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  who had access to all the classified documents located in the Biden home where supposedly he was paying $49,000 a month to live in? Well, $49,900.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, listen to what Senator Ted Cruz of Texas -- what he said earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): It seems he leaves classified documents wherever he goes and we also know that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  at times was -- declared his residence to be those very same places. And so, I believe the natural next step that is necessary is for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to examine the 1,850-plus boxes of documents from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Senate tenure that's at the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2 , and I also believe it is critical for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to search \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's homes -- home and office, residences to make sure there are no classified documents there given all the evidence that's piling up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here now a lawmaker he is leading the effort to secure America's secrets. That's Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, all right, so we've got \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  who had access to Joe's, you know, top secret classified documents when he was staying at his house. I think I agree with Senator Cruz, based on his background and his associations, I think it would be prudent to examine his home. You have a trove of documents, Jonathan Turley writes at length about it, at the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2 . But the Department of Justice in a letter to Jim Jordan requesting these documents says they will not be forthcoming because it would somehow hurt their investigation.\par \par \uc2 Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Well, we got a similar letter in the intelligence committee. It's a bipartisan letter because it was sent by the chairman who's a Democrat, Mark Warner, and myself. And their answer is that it would imperil the investigation.\par \par \uc2 It's a silly letter. It's a ridiculous letter. It doesn't even answer the question we asked.\par \par \uc2 This is very straightforward. We are the committee in charge with making sure that our intelligence agencies are doing a good job. Part of their job is to protect classified information from espionage and from putting our country in danger.\par \par \uc2 Do we know that there are letters and documents and material -- the media seems to know more about it than Congress does -- that are out there that were not stored properly, they're in his garage or whatever? We want to know what those materials are.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department possesses them, but the intelligence community knows what they are and they are refusing to tell us which materials they are. It's not that we're not cleared, we've probably seen some of these documents and materials over the years. They won't tell us which ones they are.\par \par \uc2 So how can we know if the -- if there's a risk here. How can we know of any damage has been done and whether the plan to mitigate that damage is sufficient if we don't know what we're talking about?\par \par \uc2 Their refusal to tell us what was exposed is not sustainable, it's not acceptable and I think you're going to begin to see us take action, I hope and I expect, on a bipartisan basis until they do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't think there's going to be any bipartisan anything with the Democrats. That's my prediction.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to -- I want to focus specifically. There are two investigations in the House. This in tandem especially with some of the what you're looking for as well, and that would be the House Oversight Committee, that would be Jim Comer, also looking into the Biden family, their foreign business dealings, whether or not there was influence peddling, but also the document issue. And then Jim Jordan looking into the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ, is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  politicized, the DOJ weaponized.\par \par \uc2 It would appear that the DOJ is now taking the position that they are going to stonewall every investigation, every step of the way. They'll probably end up taking it to court and they don't want to be transparent and they won't be transparent. That's my guess where this is headed.\par \par \uc2 Does Congress have any other means available to it? Can you expedite the legal process?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, not just the legal process. But listen, every one of these agencies, their budgets are approved by Congress. They have to be authorized by the committees that have oversight. We have oversight over the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's counterintelligence division, and so it gives us an angle on the Department of Justice, and we certainly have oversight over all the agencies who these documents by the way belong to.\par \par \uc2 These documents that they have in their possession do not belong to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or the Department of Justice. They belong to the intelligence agencies that author authored them and they should be answering to us, and if they don't, then I think that we're going to have to begin to do some of the things that we can do here --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But you know --\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: -- in terms of not authorizing, signing off funding, et cetera, until they respond to us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know and I know what they're going to say, though. They're going to say, well, this is impeding an investigation and special counsel, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Right, but it isn't because I don't -- I'm not asking for details about their investigation. I simply want to know what the materials are. That's all I want to know.\par \par \uc2 I want to know what the materials are and I want to know what the intelligence agencies have concluded. Is the damage that was done from those materials being taken and I want to know what plan we have to deal with that damage? That is the job of our committee and that's what we're focused on.\par \par \uc2 The House committees have different jurisdictions. They can go broader. They can go to different areas. Ours is pretty laser-focused.\par \par \uc2 We need to know what those documents are so we can make a determination as to whether or not there was damage done and whether the plan we have to mitigate that damage is sufficient. But we can't know that if we don't know what the material is, and the refusal to tell us is unacceptable and unsustainable, and they're going to be consequences. And I hope they'll voluntarily begin to cooperate or we're going to have to start doing some of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. Now, let's say they don't cooperate. What are the consequences?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, and I said I'm not going to get in the business of threats, but I can tell you what's at our disposal, and it goes beyond just blocking nominees. You know, every authorization, every one of these agencies in the intelligence community, they need a bill. They need us to write laws that authorize them to do this. They can't pass that of our committee if we don't authorize that.\par \par \uc2 The funding, we can start fencing funding. Meaning they won't be able to use money even if it's appropriated to them, by law, they won't be able to move monies around. So we have -- they've got nominees obviously they bring before us, they're coming to us all the time to reprogram funds, to move money from one part of the budget to another because they need it for some other purpose they didn't anticipate. These are all the options at our disposal and we're going to begin to use them if we don't get answers.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And with the Republican majority in the House, they could defund it until they cooperate. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: They can defund it, but we in the -- in the Senate can even refuse to pass bills that allow them to spend the money even if they got the funding. Because you need two things -- you need the money and then you need the authorization. You need a law that authorizes you to spend the money that way. So it's a two-pronged approach.\par \par \uc2 And we can deny both of them, not just with the House majority, but I think we have enough votes in the Senate to do that until we get answers.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , appreciate your hard work as always.\par \par \uc2 All right. When we come back, straight ahead, a new poll reveals what you the American people think the top issues are facing our country. Number one will probably surprise you. Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  will join us for reaction and we'll talk about his time with President Trump this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Also I have a bone to pick with \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and it's a real bone to pick.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, also last call tonight, you get to tell me what you liked and like about the show. It's getting a little nerve-wracking.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, we have exclusive video pro-life activists was acquitted after an altercation with a Planned Parenthood worker. We have the never before seen video we will show you for the first time, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. A new poll released by Gallup today highlights Americans' growing disdain for Washington, an incredible of 21 percent of the American people, respondents selected the government and its poor leadership as the biggest issue facing our country. By the way, placing it far ahead of the second most popular answer and that's Biden's surging inflation, and six points ahead of where it was in the last Gallup poll.\par \par \uc2 Now, this poll reflects the sentiment that is often repeated by one of the greatest presidents in my lifetime as we unveil a new segment, what would \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  say? Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: We as Americans have the capacity now as we've had in the past to do whatever needs to be done to preserve this last and greatest bastion of freedom. In this present crisis, government is not the solution to our problem, government is the problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The American people were right then, Reagan was right then, and they're right today.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who this weekend was campaigning and endorsing President Trump as the kickoff to the 2024 election cycle has now begun.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 All right. Before we get to that. So, we agree on a lot of issues. We don't always agree. Over the years, we've had our differences.\par \par \uc2 I can -- there's every once in a while, I want to read through this television set and pull you by the tie and kind of like say, Lindsey, wake up, what are you doing? Ad that happened over Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Why? Can you explain to this audience why on Earth knowing that the Republicans were coming into the House --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I've lost you. I can't hear.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, you can't hear. It's a great setup to a question.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I'm sorry, I'm not dodging the question. I can't hear.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I believe you --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You just had -- can you hear me?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I can hear you, yep.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thumbs up.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You just had \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  on. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  protected the Defense Department and made it strong. This ominous bill did suck. You're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 We put $1.7 trillion in a pile, nobody knew what was in it. I had three bad choices -- shut down the government, we're not going to do that. Continuing resolution, which kills the Defense Department. Inflation's at seven percent.\par \par \uc2 I voted for the bill because it increased defense spending by 10 percent, which means that we actually have more money to defend the nation. I'm hoping my friends in the House can do a better job, so I'm not put in this position again.\par \par \uc2 But to my House Republican colleagues, you send a bill over the Senate that cuts defense, I'll try to kill it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Can you hear me now, Senator?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Got you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You got me.\par \par \uc2 All right. I love how you almost you kind of heard but didn't even happen. I was going to go a lot harder.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I sort of knew where you were going and you're right to be -- I listen, I get it, I didn't like it either, yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I didn't, but you didn't have to go along with it. You could have worked with the House, but we'll go -- we'll save that for another day.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you. So you're with the president this weekend. He kicks off his campaign, gets your endorsement, gets the governor of South Carolina's endorsement.\par \par \uc2 And what you said this weekend was, you know, how many times have we heard, we like Trump policy, but we want somebody new. And then you said, there are no Trump policies without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's interesting you asked that question because two potential contenders in this nominating process would be Nikki Haley and former Secretary of State Pompeo. People we both like and I asked both of them the same question, what policy differences do you have with President Trump? Neither one of them really had an answer on any policy differences at all.\par \par \uc2 It seems like the Trump agenda is the -- is the agenda for the entire Republican Party, so that -- how will other candidates distinguish themselves against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if they agree with his policies?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I really don't know, but I do know this. We're lucky as a party to have \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  who was a terrific secretary of state. Nikki Haley, a strong voice in the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . for American first agenda. My buddy and love Tim Scott, right? We got \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  maybe the most decent guy to ever serving the government all talking about running for president.\par \par \uc2 Here's why I'm with Trump. I know the downside of Trump but let me tell you about the upside of Trump. There are no Trump policies without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You know why \uc2 NATO\uc2  gave $431 billion in defense spending increases, because they thought that Trump really meant it when he said he was going to pull out.\par \par \uc2 You know why the capital of we moved the embassy of the United States to Jerusalem, every president before Trump said I will do it, but then all the smart people said it will create World War III, Trump said, I said I would do it, I would do it.\par \par \uc2 He is responsible for securing the border through his personal will. When he put tariffs on Chinese products, it scared the crap out of Mexico and they said yes, we'll be glad to give you 28,000 troops. We'll be glad to keep people in Mexico because he thought Trump -- they thought Trump would put tariffs on Mexican policies. There are no Trump policies without the man \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And to all these people who are very talented, I don't think you could do what he could -- what he did and I want him to have another shot, unfinished business.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me, you know, politics gets -- is a nasty business. You know as well as anybody and you mentioned a lot of names. You mentioned Tim Scott, Nikki Haley, both from your state of South Carolina, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , Larry Hogan's thinking about it, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's thinking about it. Then you got Governor Glenn Youngkin, I think the rumors are probably true that he's thinking about it. We keep hearing \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he's been a great governor for Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I -- he's -- according to every report, he's thinking about it. The question is, you know, at the end of the day, primaries always get nasty. Look at your state of South Carolina, he used to be friends with \uc2 John McCain\uc2  who did ticked me off more than any one politician in the world, but you were friends with him.\par \par \uc2 Okay, remember when it got to South Carolina how vicious that campaign was with \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . I don't think -- so it always starts out people trying to be friendly, it never ends up that way, does it?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: No, we need to get ready for a real spirited contest. Now, why Trump? Now, you mentioned like DeSantis. If you try to tell me that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is not a good governor in Florida, I'm not going to listen to you. If you try to tell me \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is not qualified to be president, I'm not going to listen to you because I think he is.\par \par \uc2 I am for Trump not because of the flaws of anybody else. I'm for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because I know what I'm going to get. We need somebody that on day one can get this country back on track, that can secure our border and bring order out of chaos, somebody the Russians and the Chinese fear, somebody that can take the fight to the terrorist, the Abraham Accords is the result of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  impressing the Arabs and the Israelis. He is the best solution to the problems we face not because of the flaws of others but because what I know he can do.\par \par \uc2 And I'm telling your listeners right now, when you hear "I like Trump policies but I'm ready for somebody new", there are no Trump policies without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , always great to have you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. When we come back, a big verdict today. A jury acquitting a pro- life activist after an incident with a Planned Parenthood worker. We'll talk to him and his attorney, and we have for the first time got an exclusive video that shows what really happened. You'll see it for the first time, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Good news tonight as pro-like activist Mark Houck was acquitted by a jury in Pennsylvania earlier today. This was after he was raided and arrested by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , some 25 agents, in September, in front of his family, guns drawn, and he was charged with violating what's called the FACE Act.\par \par \uc2 Now for this altercation whether if you take a look with a Planned Parenthood escort. Now as you can see in your upper left hand corner, Mark accompanied by his son standing away from the entrance to the clinic when he is approached by the escort identified as Bruce Love, who repeatedly try to antagonize Houck, allegedly, verbally harassing him and his son, before Houck eventually shoves him after multiple attempts to defuse the situation.\par \par \uc2 The Planned Parenthood manual clearly states that escorts are not to antagonize or even engage with any protesters, a rule Love clearly broke. But this begs another question, why is Biden's DOJ so fixated on taking down pro-life activists but seemingly uninterested in the hundreds of attacks on pro-life centers around the country or what's happening outside of Supreme Court justices homes for example?\par \par \uc2 Mark joins us now along with his attorney Peter Breen Whitmore (ph) is with us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Mark, let's talk about this situation. So, first, the arrest part and you got arrested, they had 25 agents, 15 cars I read, guns drawn. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 MARK HOUCK, PRO-LIFE ACTIVIST: Yeah, that's true. On September 23rd, about 6:45 in the morning , that's -- when those 20 or 30 so called agents, full SWAT gear, heavily armored vest, ballistic shields, helmets, battering ram, banged on my door.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And your kids were inside, your wife was inside.\par \par \uc2 Okay. Let's run the video here and why don't you explain to people where you are in proximity to the Planned Parenthood facility, and this guy keeps coming up to you and tell us what happens from your perspective. There's the guy in the yellow and you're the guy that he's approaching.\par \par \uc2 So what happened?\par \par \uc2 HOUCK: Sure. So at this point, he's -- he's about a foot away from my son after already having one altercation with him and now he's making my son extremely nervous, friends coming over that's out there praying, redirecting him. My son moves behind me. I can tell he's visibly scared.\par \par \uc2 There proceeds to be further insults at me directed at my son about me and at this point, I step in and I tell, Mr. Love, you'll see right now, to go back to where he normally stands. So I walk him there and tell him to stop talking to my son, he doesn't have any permission to talk to my son. Next thing I know, he's turns around and he's talking to my son again and then you see the push.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. So it's not the worst thing in the world but didn't you face up to 11 years in jail for this?\par \par \uc2 HOUCK: That's correct. Yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Let's -- let me bring in and I want to keep running this because -- you know, I don't know, maybe I'm just a little old- fashioned Peter Breen, and you're the attorney that won this case. But if a guy comes up to you repeatedly on the street and is a threat to you and your family and you're not even in front of the facility, isn't he down the block from the facility?\par \par \uc2 PETER BREEN, EVP & HEAD OF LITIGATIONM, THOMS MORE SOCIETY: He was well away from the entrance and the FACE Act, the federal act that they charged here, is about access to the clinics. It's not about dealing with issues down the sidewalk and this issue should have been in the municipal court and actually was. And the municipal court threw it out because Mr. Love wouldn't show up for trial.\par \par \uc2 So right here today, what we did was win a big victory for the pro-life movement against the Biden administration. They were trying to scare pro- lifers from coming out on the sidewalks and being active. Biden -- that DOJ sent their best prosecutor the top guy for FACE prosecutions from Washington to help deal with this case in Philly, and that jury once we finally got it seated fully, it took about an hour to find Mark not guilty on all charges.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Listen, I grew up watching the broad street bullies and Dave Schultz and Hound Dog Kelly and Bobby Clark and Rick McLeish (ph) and all these guys and Bernie Parant (ph), I'm sorry, that push did not warrant 11 years in jail. However, the -- it was the Planned Parenthood guy that kept going up to him and I don't know -- did he ever threaten you, Mark, at all?\par \par \uc2 HOUCK: Yeah. We've had many encounters over the years. It's been about 20 years that I've been out there. It's the first time he's really overstepped his bounds with -- and my son really was the bait for him, and you know, he says all -- he throws all sorts of insults, vulgarity at me and my son, and, you know, I usually just let it flow off my back like a duck.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Well, glad you won this case. Good job.\par \par \uc2 And, Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 You're going to continue I assume your activism. Now, the question is will the Biden DOJ go after those groups that are attacking pro-life organizations?\par \par \uc2 When we come back, you get the last word. Back to my bartending days, last call.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Back to my bartending days, we call it last call where you the viewer, you get to grade the show, what you like, what you didn't like. If you want to participate between the hours of 3:00 and 6:00 Eastern Time, call my radio show, 1800-941-SEAN, for a chance to be featured.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, Mike from Deerfield Park and beautiful, the free state of -- are you in the free state of Florida and you've got a Yankee shirt on, which means you're one of the smart New Yorkers that got the hell out of New York and went to Florida. I give you an A plus for that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE, DEERFIELD PARK: In the `70s, yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Ain't it smart?\par \par \uc2 All right. What do you like? What do you dislike about the show? How can I improve? And what grade do I get?\par \par \uc2 MIKE: Well, let's see, you don't have nothing to improve, and I want to say quickly is that you refer to Mark Levin as a great one. I consider you one of the great ones, your honesty, your unwavering integrity and your love for this country and the people.\par \par \uc2 And last week, somebody gave you a C minus and the doctor gave you an A minus. So I'm going to give you an A and two pluses tonight, to wipe out there.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, I also like the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  like you.\par \par \uc2 Do you know -- you know what it is, it's with talk radio and the few of us on FOX News, because we have all sorts of opinions on the channel. There are only a few of us that are allowed and have the opportunity -- and you give us this opportunity to give a different point of view. And you know, we don't have this camera in front of me without you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for that. You make it possible and everybody else that watches. I'll take it with humility, and keep working hard every night to earn your viewership. We appreciate you being with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. That's all the time we have left this evening. Thank you for being with us. Set your DVR so you never, ever, ever miss an episode of HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230131ej1u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 6th Officer Suspended After Death Of Tyre Nichols; Tyre Nichols' Death Reignites Policing Debate; House Oversight Chair Pushes Classified Documents Reform; Former DNI On DOJ's Stonewalling Of Classified Docs; Report: DeSantis Advisers Prep For Possible White House Run; Verdict In Trial Of Anti-Abortion Activist; Kremlin Denies Putin Threatened Former UK PM; Watchdog Knocks \uc2 NIH\uc2  Oversight Of Wuhan Lab Funding; NYC Relocating Migrants To Cruise Terminal Shelter\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, John Roberts, Trace Gallagher, Steve Harrigan, Andy  McCarthy, Hillary Vaughn, Karl Rove, Mark Meredith, Rich Edson, David Lee  Miller \par \uc2 7,816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): For our \uc2 Chicago Blackhawks\uc2 , that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes. You know, one of the one of the great things about Bobby Hull was he was such a fan of Gordie Howe that he adopted his number. So, the old number nine gets retired.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Thank you so much, everybody, for joining us on this Monday. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We'll see you again tomorrow. THE STORY starts right now with Trace in from Martha, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: John and Sandra, thank you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum right now in THE STORY. Another Memphis police officers suspended after the death of Tyre Nichols, five others had been fired and charged with second- degree murder. No word on how the sixth officer was involved. The deadly beating is prompting new calls for police reform. Former Federal Prosecutor Andy McCarthy is standing by but first to the senior correspondent Steve Harrigan, he's live for us in Memphis. Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Trace, we are learning some new details about this sixth officer and it's coming from \uc2 Benjamin Crump\uc2 , an attorney for the Nichols family. This sixth officer is white. Keep in mind, the five other officers who have been accused of second-degree murder, all black. His name is Preston Hemphill and according to Crump he tased Tyre Nichols during the first part of that traffic stop, tased him and then said later, "I hope to stomp his blank."\par \par \uc2 Right now, according to Crump again, he has been relieved of his duties. This police officer No charges known to be filed against him yet per Crump, and Crump is saying he and his family are disappointed no charges yet against the six officers, a white officer. And we're continuing to see now day by day more fallout from this violent activity on January 7th, two EMTs now have also been relieved of duty.\par \par \uc2 These rescuers arrived at the scene of the second beating when Nichols was slumped over and for at least 15 minutes according to the tape, it does not show them checking to see if he's breathing or trying to help stop the breathing. No criminal charges yet, but we could see a series of more firings and more criminal charges as more information comes out. People really taking the hit for standing by and doing nothing while this man bled to death.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL HOLLAWAY, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: You can't just go to a scene like that where your co-workers are beating somebody to death and stand around with your hands in your pocket. That's not good enough. That is just like committing the crime yourself.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voiceover): The funeral for this 29-year-old is set for Wednesday. They had an online fundraising goal, initially, of $10,000.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): That goal -- that now almost well over $1 million has already been raised exceeding that goal by multiple times. Back to you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Steve Harrigan, live for us in Memphis. Steve, thank you. Let's bring in Andy McCarthy, a former Federal Prosecutor, National Review, Contributing Editor, and a Fox News Contributor. Eddie, it's always great to see you kind of picking up where Steve left off there. Stepfather, Rodney Wells, Tyre Nichols' stepfather said the following, quoting here: "I just felt there was more than five officers out there.\par \par \uc2 Now, five were charged with murder because they were the main participants. But there were five or six other officers out there that didn't do anything to render any aid. So, they are just as culpable as the officers who threw the blows." Do you, do you find that a fair assessment, Andy, do you see more officers being charged in this as we go forward?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Trace, I think it's entirely possible you'll see other officers charge where I part company with, with what Phil had to say, and some of this other stuff is, generally speaking, people who are on the scene, who as a moral matter, we feel like they should have acted in order to intervene, don't necessarily have a duty to intervene. And to say that they're just as culpable as the people who beat the living daylights out of this guy, I just don't think is a is a viable legal argument.\par \par \uc2 I think it's a very viable moral argument. But you know, if you're talking about second-degree murder, there's got to be, you know, common sense says, there's a big difference between somebody who actually puts his hands on somebody in a lethal force type manner, and carries out an act like that, versus somebody who is on the scene, probably should have intervened, but didn't.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, Andy, we were on the air live Friday night during the release of these tapes, and it was horrifying to watch. There wasn't a lot of talk on Friday night about race. And now, suddenly, through the weekend, you're hearing more, and more talk about race. And you say that if you're having a hard time understanding how five young black men might have killed another black man out of racial animus against young black men, welcome to the club, and it seems to be a fair assessment, Andy, because we're out trying to put the puzzle together here of how this suddenly became about race.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, you know, I think the way it happens, Trace, is the common person, this common-sense person doesn't look at an event like this and see, you know, five young black men assaulting another young black man and say, ah, white supremacism. I mean, you know, nobody thinks that way what they look at, and they say that's atrocious policing, and those cops ought to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we have here is a situation where you have a very small sliver of the society. But unfortunately, one that punches way above its weight, in academia and in the media, which wants to turn everything into a passion play or a morality play about race, when in fact, that's actually counterproductive to getting better policing, because we ought to be judging people on their merits.\par \par \uc2 You know, we shouldn't be hanging on the edge of our seats to see if the next police officer who's arrested is white or black, or I don't know if the next one is Hispanic. Does that mean he's half racist? I don't even know what to make out of it. It's such nonsense. What we ought to be concerned about here is atrocious policing, and what we do to fix that, and that's not a race thing. That's a, you know, that's a policy matter.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And on that note, Andy, you talk about atrocious policing, and better policing, what do you make of these calls for, for nationwide police reform? On the federal level, shouldn't this be a local city by city town by town?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: You know, what's amazing to me about this, Trace, is who has performed more notoriously badly over the last six or seven years than the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? And I don't mean to stick it to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  today, because they're not part of the story. My only point is what makes anyone think the federal government has all the answers about policing? You know, the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  is probably the finest police department in the world. And they've developed their own practices over time.\par \par \uc2 The same thing with municipal police departments all over the country, the way we get to the best policing is by letting the best ideas compete with each other. And then we get a better model that municipalities over time can imitate, but the idea that the federal government has all the answers and they can come in and tell everybody how it's done is just -- I mean, what, what nor experience says that that's accurate.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, yes, and that, that is a very fair assessment. Andy McCarthy always great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, a big lineup ahead on this Monday afternoon: Karl Rove, Nigel Farage and former Director of National Intelligence, John Ratcliffe, with a breaking update from the DOJ to the Senate Intelligence Committee on the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While the Chairman of the House Oversight Committee calling for a fundamental change to the way classified documents are handled as scandals continue to plague two administrations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): We have to reform the way that documents are boxed up when they leave the President and Vice President's office and follow them into the private sector. This is something that I think will be bipartisan legislative fix.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Speaking of bipartisan accusations are coming from both sides of the aisle that Congress is being stonewalled and left in limbo around the documents found at President Biden and former President Trump's properties. The DOJ now says it is working to get lawmakers some answers. In moments, former Director of National Intelligence under President Trump John Radcliffe. But first, the Fox Business Correspondent Hillary Vaughan, she's live for us on Capitol Hill. Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Trace. Well, the top Republican and Democrat on the Senate Intel Committee are fed up. They feel like they're not getting enough information from the DOJ about what was in these classified documents that were found in President Biden's garage and office at the Penn Biden Center, and also whether or not national security was breached because of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-TX): These are probably materials we already have access to. We just don't know which ones they are. And it's not about being nosy, you know, that somehow the only people who are not allowed to know what was in there are congressional oversight committees.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Are the notion of we're not going to give the Oversight Committee the ability to do its job until the special prosecutor somehow says it's OK, doesn't, doesn't