ation with the committee."\par \par \uc2 And again, it's one more time where they say, well, we can't really say anything, because we got the special counsels, and we have to give them, you know, we have to give them a chance to really dig through this stuff.\par \par \uc2 It's a timing thing. It's a waiting thing. And by saying, well, it's only on January 12th, these people were appointed, they're trying to buy more time.\par \par \uc2 JOHN RATCLIFFE, FORMER DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: Well, Trace, everything that the DOJ says would have made sense if they didn't act differently after the raid at Mar-a-Lago with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 You know, now, DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  don't want to share information with Congress. But remember, after the raid of President Trump and the execution of that search warrant, it was DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that leaked photographs of classified documents, leaked information about what was in classified documents, the result of their search that showed up in all sorts of news stories.\par \par \uc2 That information only could come from DOJ. And of course, you know, there was a special counsel appointed in that matter early on.\par \par \uc2 So, you have to ask yourself, Trace what changed?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: And I think what changed is the fact that now there are two special counsels, and the fact that they didn't think that there was ever going to be a side by side comparison with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: I thought this prosecution and what they were doing, would be -- would stand on its own. And then, subsequently, of course, we've had the developments with President Biden, and his actions as vice president, and as senator, frankly, that were -- that were more alarming and raise more questions.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for that matter, really have an issue with the perception that they're now applying the law differently, and they're taking a different approach to sharing information that they were willing to take six months ago.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I'd like to get your take on, you know, some of the newer information we're getting. We're learning that President Biden's notebooks found inside his Delaware home might also have classified material on them. Kind of a drip, drip, drip. And beyond that, there is hundreds and hundreds of boxes at the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2  that will not be released.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: So, here is why that's important, Trace. First, the notebooks themselves could contain classified information. So, I have information for my time as DNI that, that I still remember. If I were to write that down on a piece of paper that may create in and of itself, a classified documents.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why those notebooks would be important is did he record classified information? And if so, for what purpose? Which gets to the second point that's -- that I think DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , or in this case, a special counsel would be looking at, which is -- you know, it's been disclosed that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  wrote a very detailed e-mail before being appointed to the \uc2 Burisma\uc2  board.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: That had detailed analysis, geopolitical analysis, economic analysis, military analysis related to Ukraine that many speculate is based on classified information. And so, what we'll do is see whether there are fragments from Hunter Biden's e-mail or fragments from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's notebooks that match actual classified documents that are known to exist.\par \par \uc2 So, imagine that that, that search and analysis is taking place and may actually be driving some of what the certain position that DOJ is now expressing so differently than they did six months ago.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And the information could be fascinating, but you need all the pieces of the puzzle to actually put the puzzle together.\par \par \uc2 Former DNI John Ratcliffe, thank you for coming on, sir.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, meantime, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , reportedly plotting his next move as 2024 speculation ramps up.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk to Karl Rove next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): People just need to chill out a little bit on some of this stuff. I mean, seriously, we just ran an election.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I know around the country of Florida was kind of the biggest bright spot, it was not so bright in many other parts of the country. It was a -- it was a -- it was a substandard performance given the dynamics that are at play.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, former President Trump, facing possible criminal charges over a hush money payment to a porn star. Fox confirms prosecutors in New York City are now presenting evidence to a grand jury.\par \par \uc2 Stormy Daniels claims that she had sex with the former president back in 2006. He denies it. But his former lawyer admits paying Daniels $130,000 right before the 2016 election. A Trump lawyer told Fox today, "no comment."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , reportedly getting ready for a possible run for the White House. His advisors already meeting about a future campaign and looking for staffers in Iowa and New Hampshire, according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , co-founder and advisor for American Crossroads. He is also a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Karl, it's great to see you as always. I want to put this first poll on the screen. This, of course, is coming from Granite State Poll by the \uc2 University of New Hampshire\uc2 . It's the first choice, right?\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , you can see there for the 2024 New Hampshire GOP primary. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of 42, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at 30 percent. Nikki Haley, eight percent.\par \par \uc2 Is this an indication? In your estimation, Karl, that maybe former President Trump has an uphill battle?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it says that the president - - former president does have challenges.\par \par \uc2 I'd be cautious. This is a good number for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He hasn't even campaigned in the state. But let's remember at this point in 2007, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was the leading candidate to be the Republican nominee in 2016.\par \par \uc2 There were three or four other candidates between -- in 2015 between the time that the contest got going, and the time it crystallized at years' end, who had various leads in the polls. So, we've got to be a little bit careful.\par \par \uc2 But if I were the former president, I'd be a little bit concerned about it, which is why he is trying to speed up the calendar. He wants to force potential contenders into the race. But I think they're wise not to, and all you got to do is look at history, and know that that's the case.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. You mentioned good news for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I want to put up question number two in the poll. It says, "Would you like to see \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  run for president in 2024?" It's a big number up top. 64 percent, say definitely, probably.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: 26 percent, probably not. You know, the small number down in the bottom. This again, good news for the DeSantis campaign if there is a DeSantis campaign.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Absolutely. And look, they got to take advantage of the fact that they've got good, good feeling out there, had a fantastic victory last fall. 90 percent victory.\par \par \uc2 Incidentally, the person who was the superintendent of that, the 