f Abraham tanks are perhaps up to a year away. So we are supporting a lot of missiles that we've sent to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But I think actually the truth is we're a little bit fearful. We want to show a gesture that we don't approve of what Putin has done, invading Ukraine. We're a bit half-hearted, to be honest with you, when it comes to absolutely getting involved. Now, I suspect the truth is that this war in Ukraine is going to drag on for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, you could be rot in this. But I want to get your point, because former Obama Defense Secretary \uc2 Bob Gates\uc2  was slamming President Biden for not doing a lot of this stuff sooner. Listen to him and I'll get your final response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 , FORMER OBAMA DEFENSE SECRETARY: This could have been done sooner. And they're talking about it potentially being six months, a year or more before the Abrams tanks get there. I think the key thing about the Abrams tank decision was that it unlocks the Germans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Which goes to your point, Nigel, which is this thing could go on for a long time if the US isn't concerned about getting Abrams tanks there for six months or a year, it could be a protracted battle.\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Yes, it could be. And you have to remember, at the end of the day, these decisions that are being taken in London and in Washington, DC, this is taxpayers' money that is involved here, and I don't think any of us want our governments to irresponsibly spend too much money.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Nigel Farage, great to see you. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, there's a damning new report, and it finds the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  failed to properly monitor the safety of the research it was funding in Wuhan. Former CDC Director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2  is with us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While the feds accused of not paying enough attention to what was happening in Wuhan partly paid for with our tax dollars, a government watchdog says the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  didn't properly monitor potentially risky research and could have done more to reduce the risk. Former CDC Director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2  in a moment but first to the national senior correspondent, Rich Edson. He's live for us in the nation's capital. Rich?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi. Good afternoon, Trace. Will even more scrutiny and criticism of the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 , a US-based nonprofit, and the funding they ultimately sent to a Chinese government lab in Wuhan. And that nonprofit is still receiving government money. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  inspector general says \uc2 NIH\uc2  failed to properly oversee grants to \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 , a nonprofit in New York. The report says EcoHealth missed notifying the federal government of unexpected results from the research it funded in Wuhan and that the Wuhan Institute of Viralogy has refused to share reports and data with the \uc2 US government\uc2 . Now, some in Congress are calling for an immediate and permanent end to government funding of \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): It is reckless that we continue to fund this organization that refuses to release information to the public as required by law. We should not be spending it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The inspector general says it examined $8 million in grants to EcoHealth from 2014 to 2021. $1.8 million of that went to several other groups, including the Wuhan Institute of Virology for research on bat coronaviruses. EcoHealth says it has since addressed many of the concerns in this report, Republicans in Congress say they plan to hold hearings on EcoHealth's funding and call Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  to testify. All this during an intense debate over the type of research the government funds and whether it's helping scientists prepare for another pandemic, or is too risky and is creating more dangerous viruses that could escape into the population. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Rich Edson live for us in DC. Rich, thank you. Let's bring in former CDC director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2 , now senior medical advisor at AM LLC, a public health firm. Dr. Redfield, thank you for coming on, and we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, basically to kind of nutshell version of this is the \uc2 NIH\uc2  was funding EcoHealth, EcoHealth was sending money to Wuhan, and nobody was overseeing the Chinese scientist. I mean, how is this possible?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFIELD\uc2 , FORMER CDC DIRECTOR: You know, Trace, I think it's really important. I think we have to be realistic. I think, personally, I think it's unlikely that the \uc2 NIH\uc2  would ever be able to provide the appropriate level of supervision that would be necessary for this, what I consider very high risk research. This is why Dr. Siegel and I in our op-ed in The Wall Street Journal really strongly recommend that we have a moratorium on this gain of function research and have a much broader debate about whether this research should be conducted. And if so, how and where it can be done safely.\par \par \uc2 Very disappointing to see the lack of adherence to their own policies, very disappointing that the Wuhan, the Health Alliance group, when they saw they had increased replication dynamics of the virus in the lab, didn't immediately report it like they were required to for that. It would take two more years for them to report it.\par \par \uc2 So a very damning report, a very disappointing report. But I just want to underscore I think it's unrealistic for us to think that we can fund this type of high risk research all over the world and maintain the supervision that would require to make sure it's done in a safe and responsible way.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And as you might imagine, Congress, of course, wants answers. Here's some sound from the House Oversight Chair James Comer and then we'll get your response. They've never cared about it. They've tried to brush it aside. The one entity that's at the top of all of this gain of function research is \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 . And this administration just gave them more money even though the IG report and even though everything that's come out about the money they've already received proves that they were never in compliance with the grant to begin with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He made two valid points there. One, they really did try to brush this aside. And two, the money just kept on flowing.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Yes. I think, again, very disappointing, very hard to justify and understand why we continue to fund research that has been shown to be very high risk and to an agency, an organization has shown a total disregard for the requirements of reporting this research by the manner that they're supposed to.\par \par \uc2 But it comes back to the fundamentals of whether we should be doing this gain of function research. I think it's really been concocted that people try to argue that this doesn't meet the definition of true gain of function research. We've heard that from our \uc2 NIH\uc2  leadership. I was very happy to see that the National Science Advisory Board for Biosecurity recently recommended that we have a much broader definition because, in fact, this was gain of function research. This was teaching this virus how to replicate better and gain the function to have better replication and replication now in human tissue.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I got to go, Dr. Redfield. I get like ten seconds. But what do you make of this new report saying the world is dangerously unprepared for the next pandemic?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: I think it's true. We're not prepared. And I make the one comment that the United States national security is much more potentially affected by what I call biosecurity than it is from China, Russia, Korea and Iran. And we ought to invest proportional to that threat. And we haven't done it. We're not there. And I think we really need to relook at a broader role for our Defense Department. Not just the traditional defense, but we need to build a robust biosecurity defense capacity and we do not have that as we sit here today.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Dr. \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2 . Great to have you on, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Yes. Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, dozens of migrants instructed to leave hotel housing in the heart of Midtown Manhattan. They camp out on the street in a showdown over relocation to a new facility in Brooklyn opening today. Former New York Republican Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2  when THE STORY continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While Chicago Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  celebrating the Lunar New Year by dancing in a parade, not everyone thought it was the right move with crime in the city on the rise. The mayor tweeted this video over the weekend. Some commenters pointed out crime is up 61% compared to this time last year, and one third of storefronts on the famous Magnificent Mile shopping strip now empty.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to the mayor's office for a response, but we have not yet heard back. Surprise, surprise.\par \par \uc2 A live look at a hotel in Midtown Manhattan where some migrants are refusing to leave here's what happened when the bus pulled up. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Keep in mind the city is trying to transfer the migrants to a new shelter at a cruise ship terminal in Brooklyn. Former Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2  was the ranking member on the Homeland Security Committee and a federal prosecutor at the border. He will join us in moments but first to correspondent David Lee Miller. He's live outside the hotel in New York City. David Lee?\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Trace, some of the migrants not only refused to leave the hotel, they refused to vacate an encampment on the street outside of the hotel. Now, we're talking about single men here. All the migrants are single men. They were told over the weekend that they had to leave their hotel rooms in order to accommodate migrant families, including women and children.\par \par \uc2 Now, the plan was for all the single men to then be relocated to a cruise line terminal in Brooklyn that had been set up as a temporary shelter. Many of the migrants say the terminal is not acceptable and want better quality housing. They are not being allowed back into the hotel, and some holdouts were removed this afternoon. Some boarded buses taking them to that Brooklyn site, while just as many others decided to camp out on the street.\par \par \uc2 New York City's homeless outreach workers have tried and failed to convince the men that living on the street is not an option, although the migrants did agree to remove nearly a dozen tents.\par \par \uc2 One of the migrants from the Dominican Republic who said his name was Wilson, broke down in tears discussing his plight. He told me that he came here to work and that he is not being treated humanely. He said the shelter is cold, uncomfortable, and does not have enough bathrooms. He says it's too far from the subway and they locked the doors at 11:00 at night, which makes it difficult to keep a job.\par \par \uc2 Now, the mayor's office has issued a statement that reads in part, and I quote, "The facility at the Brooklyn Cruise Terminal will provide the same services as every other humanitarian relief center in the city. And the scheduled relocations to Brooklyn Cruise Terminal this weekend took place was planned. We remain in serious need of support from both our state and federal governments.\par \par \uc2 As of now, Trace, there is really a standoff on the streets. The migrants say they are going to continue to camp out. The city wants them to relocate. The big question, what will happen later this week. Temperatures now hovering around 50 degrees by the end of this week, it is expected to be in the 20s. And these men do not have the gear for sub freezing temperatures. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David Lee Miller live forest in midtown Manhattan. David Lee, thank you. Let's bring in former Republican Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2  of New York, former ranking member on the House Homeland Security Committee and former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I'm kind of baffled by the scene down there in Hell's Kitchen because it's amazing. These are single men who came in this country illegally, and now they're refusing to leave a hotel because they want better accommodations. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KATKO\uc2 , FORMER NEW YORK REPUBLICAN CONGRESSMAN: Well, you're exactly right, and you got to be got to back up but think about it. They illegally entered this country, number one. Number two, they were given release conditions by this administration, which I think is foolhardy. Instead of having a hearing and keeping them incarcerated until that hearing is done.\par \par \uc2 They then get sent to New York City. And in New York City, they don't like the way that release conditions are working for them so they're trying to dictate the narrative here. They're getting food, they're getting clothing, they're getting shelters. Now they're trying to dictate more than that.\par \par \uc2 To me this is the end result of colossally failed policy by this administration and we haven't seen the end of this. This is just the beginning, I think, the unrest amongst these illegal immigrants because I think they're feeling wrongly empowered. And because of that, they're going to display more problems down the road.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: They do feel empowered. And you mentioned the mistakes by this administration, and yet you have the mayor in New York blaming Republicans for this. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: To go to the source. The source is real comprehensive immigration reform. The Republicans have been hiding, holding on and blocking it for too many years. We must get this resolved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: This better than anybody because you've spent time down on the border. You've worked this. The whole immigration reform thing, that is not going to secure the border. It might speed up citizen rates, becoming citizens. It might make more people legal citizens but it is not going to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: You can't have an intelligent conversation about immigration reform unless and until the border is secured. That is step one. Step two is then to talk about immigration reform. And this country definitely needs workers in certain areas and that demand flexes year by year. For example, agricultural area, that is an area where we desperately need help from foreign workers. You cannot have the immigration reform and ignore the flood coming across the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 Immigration reform portends that it is legal immigration, not illegal. So you've got to stop the illegal immigration before you can have the immigration reform discussions, which we need.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, yes. And we just got some new sound from Texas Governor Greg Abbott. I want to play it, and then we'll get your response on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS GOVERNOR: Building a border wall and adding hundreds of vials of other barriers is only one way that Texas is responding to President Biden's refusal to enforce the immigration laws of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I mean, the governors are clashing with this administration, building new wall in Texas. What are your thoughts on these different stances taken by the southern border governors?\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Well, it's an act of desperation on that point. I've been down there many times. I've led congressional delegations. In fact, I started my career as a federal prosecutor back in the '90s, going after cartels in El Paso, Texas. But I can tell you the border is ten times worse now than it was back in 1994.\par \par \uc2 And so, you have to have the border security. There's not a country on earth that would put up with what we're putting up with on our southern border. I don't care how liberal that country is. They would not put up with it. Everyone knows having immigration starts with having secure borders. And you can have fair immigration. You can have discussions about it. Unless and until you secure the border, it's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 And these governors are under it. They have no choice. They're overrun. These local communities are overrun with these illegal immigrants and all the problems that come with them. So they're doing what they have to do but our government on the federal level is not. And this is -- the end result is what's happening in New York City.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And I got to go here, Congressman, but your thoughts on \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Democratic governor of Arizona, now flying migrants to different cities, and I've got 10 seconds for you.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Well, she's just that symptomatic a larger problem, and this administration is ignoring it. They better get their act together. It's going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it is. Former Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2 , thank you, sir, for coming on. Always appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, there you go. Well, that is THE STORY for whatever the date is today, I lost track, January 30th. I'm Trace Gallagher. I'll see you back here at "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020230131ej1u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Seven Killed In California; Suspected Gunman Kills Co-Workers; Attorney Discovers Classified Material At Pence Home; Senators Puzzled On How Biden Left With Documents; Congress Reacts To Multiple Document Scandals; Director Of National Intelligence To Brief Lawmakers; United States Moves Closer To Sending Abrams Tanks; Pompeo On Assassination Of Saudi Journalist; Implications Of Document Discoveries; Possible Joe Biden White House Challengers; Prolife Activist On Trial For Violating Federal Freedom Of Access To Clinic Entrances Act; Justice Department And Several States Sue \uc2 Google\uc2  Over Its Digital Advertising Policy; Congress Holds Hearings On \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 's Technology Failures When Selling Tickets To Taylor Swift's Concert Tour; Child Deaths Due To Fentanyl Increasing At Alarming Rate; Classified Document Found In Indiana Residence Of Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ; House Speaker Kevin McCarthy Says President Biden Should Negotiate Over Raising Debt Ceiling\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Claudia Cowan, Peter Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Alex Hogan,  Mark Meredith, Chad Pergram, David Spunt, Bryan Llenas \par \uc2 8,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME", an undercover journalist exposes Chicago street crime. He will be in spot shadow.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, that sounds fascinating.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Judge, you had nine, eight, seven, six, five --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Seven, six, five.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Four.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Three.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Three, two --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bret, are you there?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): I am here. Keep counting. It's good.\par \par \uc2 In honor of \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , I'm going to start the show. I'm going to stay for an hour and I'm sorry to see you leave.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Laters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT," the latest developments in the classified documents scandal, which now has a new Republican player.\par \par \uc2 But leading off this evening, the third mass shooting in California in a little more than a week. This time it happened in Half Moon Bay, south of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Seven people were killed, another critically wounded at two locations.\par \par \uc2 This is the third mass killing in that state in eight days.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Claudia Cowan is in Sausalito tonight with the latest. Good evening, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. That's right. At least 24 people have been killed in three mass shootings in California in little more than a week.\par \par \uc2 California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , visiting another stricken community today. He says the Second Amendment is becoming a suicide pact.\par \par \uc2 The latest incident of deadly workplace violence has rocked to Half Moon Bay, acquaint bay area community, famous for its annual Pumpkin Festival and art galleries.\par \par \uc2 Authority say on Monday afternoon, 66-year-old Chunli Zhao open fire at the mushroom farm where he works, killing four co-workers and injuring a fifth, as parents and children who lived on the property looked on in horror.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff says, Zhao, then, drove to another farm a few miles away where three more bodies were found.\par \par \uc2 The five men and two women killed were all of Asian or Hispanic descent. Though officials wouldn't say if anyone was specifically targeted.\par \par \uc2 Translators were brought in to provide a description of the shooter and his car. And within hours --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hands up -- hands up.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: An arrest. After a deputy spotted the alleged gunman sitting in his vehicle outside the sheriff's substation. With illegally purchased semi- automatic handgun.\par \par \uc2 EAMONN ALLEN, LIEUTENANT, SAN MATEO COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE: The subject was still seating in the vehicle. The subject exited the vehicle and was taken into custody without an incident.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: It's unclear if Zhao planned to surrender. But officials say he acted alone and is cooperating. While authorities described him as disgruntled, what motivated him to open fire, remains unknown.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All of the evidence we have points to this being the instance of workplace violence.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Your hearts are with the people of California. They've been on rough, rough couple of days.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: in Washington, there are renewed calls for gun control legislation.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: President Biden like most Americans believes that this is an urgent issue. That too many of our neighbors, colleagues, kids, are losing their lives to gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COWAN (on camera): And more than 24 hours later, the county coroner is still working to identify all of the victims and notify next of kin. Chunli Zhao makes his first court appearance in Redwood City tomorrow. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Claudia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also, breaking tonight, news the Justice Department may expand its search for classified documents on President Biden's personal property.\par \par \uc2 This comes as we learned that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says he found some classified material at his home in Indiana. White House correspondent Peter Doocy, fills us in live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Officials here won't say whether or not they think this story was initially leaked by someone trying to bruise President Biden politically, ahead of a reelection announcement, and we asked. But this document mess is casting a long shadow over a president who was struggling to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm excited about the leadership we have here in the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Leaders on both sides have a similar problem, as Biden and his Republican successor as V.P., \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , each discovered classified materials at home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As we sit here in your home office in Indiana. Did you take any classified documents with you from the White House?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I did not.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Three months later, his lawyer found some. At least two small boxes slipped through the cracks, with Pence lawyers blaming a hurried transition.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The attorneys on my staff went through all the documents at both the White House in our offices there, and at the vice president's residence, to ensure that any documents that needed to be turned over to the National Archives, including classified documents were turned over.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Attorney General Garland twice declined to comment on probes into documents.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: I'm unable to comment on your question. I'm unable to comment on this.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Republicans on the Hill are giving Pence a pass. House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer, writes, "Former Vice President Pence's transparency stands in stark contrast To Biden White House staff who continue to withhold information from Congress and the American people."\par \par \uc2 For now, White House officials reject parallels between Biden and Pence.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm not going to comment on any other -- former elected official.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Press shop now punts, most questions about classified materials to White House lawyers, as previous comments from the podium attract new scrutiny, like, this one from the day \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  named a special counsel to investigate Biden, January 12th.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The search is complete.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The next day, the president returned to his Wilmington house. The next week, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went looking for themselves.\par \par \uc2 After a special counsel was named before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched, President Biden went to his house in Wilmington. What was he doing in there?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I would refer you to the White House Counsel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There is no question, the president had these documents. He says they were misfiled. His lawyers say they were misplaced.\par \par \uc2 The question the White House is not answering tonight, why did the president do this? And we asked, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Many times. I know you did. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Intelligence Committee met this afternoon inside a SCIF. That's a room reserved to view classified information. Members of Congress know they are not allowed to remove material, classified material from that room.\par \par \uc2 And they want to know how senator, and then, Vice President Biden was able to circumvent that rule. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill with that tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Questions are swirling tonight on Capitol Hill about how then Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  walked away with classified documents in tow. The revelation even mystifying top Senate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I can't understand how any individual or senator can take possession of the classified document, let alone remove it to another location. It's just unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Senators can view classified materials that pertain to their committee assignments, but only when they're inside a SCIF. A Sensitive Compartmented Information Facility.\par \par \uc2 During his tenure as senator, Biden chaired the Foreign Relations and Judiciary committees, gaining access to those classified files.\par \par \uc2 Fox asked GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  if the SCIF rules were perhaps different back then, when Biden served as senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I never knew it was possible to take classified documents out of the SCIF.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Some, are now calling the spiraling scandal a systemic problem. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  says Democrats are working on a legislative solution.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Senator Peters is looking at bipartisan legislation in terms of classified documents, I support his efforts.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: However, Biden moved classified documents, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  doesn't think it was malicious.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I known President Biden for a long time. I don't think there is -- I'd be shocked if there is anything sinister hear.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Others want the facts.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): I'm not going to take \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's word.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And as the spotlight grows on Capitol Hill, more and more senators are going on the record to deny they ever carried away sensitive materials.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): I've never taken classified documents outside of our SCIF.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): Now, the Senate Intelligence Committee got a briefing today inside one of the SCIFs in the Senate. But a source tells Fox, Bret, that the recent document drama were not discussed, whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 That could change tomorrow when there is another briefing for the Senate Intel Committee. And this time, they will be briefed by the Director of National Intelligence Avril Haines. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow gained 104. The S&P 500 was off three. The NASDAQ lost 30.\par \par \uc2 We are now 11 months into the Ukraine war. It appears Ukrainian forces may be closer to obtaining much needed tanks as they continue to fight off Russian invaders.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine's president is making personnel changes amid allegations of high level corruption in his country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan reports, again, tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Washington could announce supply in Kyiv with U.S.-made Abrams tanks as early as this week, a U.S. official tells Fox News.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: We are always looking for ways to offer security assistance for them.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: 11 months into the war, it's a move that Germany might follow. Russia, today warned the equipment would impact Moscow's future relations with Berlin.\par \par \uc2 The need for tanks is most centered around the contested eastern territory of Bakhmut. Civilians there, often relying on humanitarian aid to survive.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER TAYLOR, MEDIA AND IMAGES, GLOBAL EMPOWERMENT MISSION: We take it in trucks and vans, so we don't have a single location that can be targeted.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Fox join the nonprofit Global Empowerment Mission in Sirakunya (PH), east of Kharkiv. GEM travels to Ukraine's most desperate villages with vehicles full of food and clothes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Really, all of this looks like a ghost town. No one here has had power since February 24th of 2022. So, we're approaching a year.\par \par \uc2 We can see a lot of stray dogs clearly, animals have been left behind. People have fled.\par \par \uc2 77-year-old Maria is one of the few who remains here. She was born in this house and says she won't leave it to spend the rest of her life anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 MARIA, UKRAINIAN (through translator): Where would we go, and who would be waiting for us there?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Meanwhile, back in the capital, major personnel turnover as Kyiv attempts to combat high level corruption allegations.\par \par \uc2 The Zelenskyy administration dismissed more than a dozen senior officials, at least one, is accused of bribery over reconstruction contracts.\par \par \uc2 DENYS SHMYHAL, PRIME MINISTER OF UKRAINE (through translator): Our government continues to conduct reforms necessary for our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): New indication tonight that Germany will move forward green lighting the movement of Leopard tanks. The country is set to debate the topic in its parliament session tomorrow morning. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan, live in Kyiv. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is taking criticism tonight for a reference in his new book to murdered Saudi columnist Jamal Khashoggi. Pompeo writes Khashoggi was more of an activist than a journalists, and the relationship with Saudi Arabia for the U.S. was too important to step away from.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  calls Pompeo's remark about Khashoggi, a vile falsehood, and a ploy to sell books. That, from CEO and publisher \uc2 Fred Ryan\uc2  today. I talked with Secretary Pompeo about it on my podcast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): And write that to sell books. I wrote that to explain how we were thinking about keeping the American people safe.\par \par \uc2 We were very clear the murder of Jamal Khashoggi was heinous. And we sanctioned 13 Saudis for what they did there. We also knew that the life of Mr. Khashoggi nor of any individual is more important than saving lots of American life that includes our soldiers, sailors, airmen, and marines all across the world was a hard decision to make. Because you'd had someone murdered and no one's going to say -- no one's going to say no, that's OK. We didn't say that was OK.\par \par \uc2 And we were never naive about the reality of the world. It is indeed a nasty, brutal place. And it was far more complicated. This is a man who praised \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  when he passed away. Right? It's just -- it's we could spend a lot of time going into the details, most of them are public. But suffice it to say, this wasn't somebody working for the Wichita Eagle cub covering soccer games.\par \par \uc2 This was -- he was -- he was a multifaceted person. But that -- none of that -- none of that is intended to diminish the fact that he was murdered. Just simply to say that this is a complicated problem set in a complicated part of the world. And America's function in that is that we weren't responsible for killing him, our responsibility is to make sure that we saved American lives, and I am confident that we did that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can find this whole discussion and more like this on my all-star panel podcast under "THE BRET BAIER PODCAST", Foxnewspodcasts.com.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take a look if and how the president's documents scandal and all the other documents scandals could affect the 2024 race? We'll be joined by pollsters Kellyanne Conway, and Celinda Lake in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden's documented scandal now a scandal that involves others could have major implications for the 2024 presidential campaign. Here to discuss that and more, our pollsters, Kellyanne Conway and Celinda Lake. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 CELINDA LAKE, POLLSTER: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This weekend, the question was asked many times on Sunday shows about this, and how does it have implications. Here's Senator Chris Coons on what it means for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): I do not think that when we get to the next election, this will be the deciding issue. The fact that this will take up time and be a distraction. Yes, that has a political impact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think Celinda? I mean, how much impact does it have in this time when all these candidates are potentially forming and the president hasn't announced yet?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, I think that first of all, the president's job performance has been improving, not declining during this period. The documents scandal, so called scandal has absolutely no correlation with his job approval.\par \par \uc2 And when you said -- and I was amused Bret when you said lots of people have been asking questions, what the voters have been asking is what's happening with jobs? What's happening with inflation, not what's happening with documents in the bottom drawer of the vice president's office?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, what do you think, Kellyanne?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, POLLSTER: Well, I think that the vice president should be listening to Celinda more because she's very honest and very smart. The fact is, this is hurting him.\par \par \uc2 You guys did a poll last week, even before the 12-hour search of his main residence in Delaware, Bret. And that poll found that a combined 53 percent of Americans said that the Biden document scandalabra, as it's becoming now is just as serious or more serious than the Trump one.\par \par \uc2 And I don't even think that equivalence is a good idea because if you've negligent mishandled a document, you're admitting culpability.\par \par \uc2 Here's why I think it hurts \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a lot. It plays into this idea that we can't get a straight or truthful answer from this White House he says on almost anything. Independence left him after the chaotic Afghanistan, pull out and they never really returned to him. They had a sideline President Biden and Vice President Harris to have a wildly successful midterm.\par \par \uc2 I think when your name is on the ballot, Biden, Harris, you have to go and campaign for yourselves. The question in 2024 is simple. These vulnerable Democratic senators in Montana, in West Virginia, in Ohio, in Arizona, in Nevada, and so on. Do they want to campaign with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? They vote with his agenda, but do they want to actually be seen with him is a big question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned the approval ratings that RCP Real Clear Politics has the president at 42.9 percent, disapprove 52.1. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  says this is \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , former labor secretary writing for \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , unless Biden announces within the next six months or so that he won't be running again, it will be difficult for potentially attractive Democratic candidates to gain enough traction to have a good chance in 2024. Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, first of all, the president I think is running and 92 percent of Democrats want him to run, 92 percent of Democrats would support him. So, but I think it's good that he's more worried about governing right now in the State of the Union than when he's announcing his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think -- we know who our nominee is. And we're -- we can't wait to sit back and watch the Republicans. If it was anything like McCarthy votes, it's going to be a real show.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of Republicans, all kinds of people are signaling. What are your thoughts? I mean, the latest polls have former President Trump with significant increase over other possibles and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . You see \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , Nikki Haley who we talked to last week about possibly getting in the race.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That's right, the Emerson poll actually has Trump going up seven points from where they had him in the previous poll. And I think part of it is he's the only announced candidate in the race. And at least people saw him out there in the midterms unlike the sitting president.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is competitive with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in these head to head polls losing by a point which is not measurable. And President Trump is actually beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the most recent Emerson poll 44 to 40.\par \par \uc2 Here's who I think has an opening. A third party independent candidate can really tip the balance and do the damage that was done to \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  or George Herbert Walker Bush in those respective elections where you have somebody come in, doesn't get many electoral votes if any, but we get a -- we get a president elected with less than 45 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you're saying if it's former President Trump against President Biden, that someone else like throwing it out there, Joe Manchin, someone else gets in the race --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Or a businessperson.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Or a businessperson.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: It's tempting for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think -- do you think that's possible, Celinda?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, I think it's very hard given the two party system to get nominated unless you have a lot of money. I mean, \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  was the best example I think in my worked in the Clinton campaign of --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was like 19 percent.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Right. And he had significant vote, but in the end, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  won.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: But with 44.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Right, but it was a governing majority. And most of the \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  voters had \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  as their second choice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that there'll be a real battle for the Republican primary?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: No, I'm not an expert on Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, but it's interesting to hear your perspective.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: It's a fun game to watch. Yes, it looks to me like there'll be a real battle. And I think it represents the speaker's race demonstrated 15 and unheard of 15 votes, people standing out, not being able to work together, not getting united afterwards. I think there'll be a real fight, but the Democrats are united. We're straight ahead. We know what we're fighting for. The Republicans are all over the place.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: The independents matter here. They're not really high on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's presidency. But I would say this, if unity means that we all think alike and talk like and vote alike, I mean, zero Democrats voted against Inflation Reduction Act, zero voted for the tax cuts and Jobs Act, zero voted for \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  who was more qualified than many of the people who were questioning her, grilling her.\par \par \uc2 If unity means that you don't think for yourself and cast an independent vote once in a while, I don't want to be unified that way. But I think lots of Republicans wind up running, maybe five or six minimum. And the reason is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they feel -- they smell blood in that water. They don't see him easily getting a second term, even if as Celinda correctly points out, voters are talking about the economy or the border, or crime or education. None of those issues are going well for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in anyone's polling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I guess the Biden people would point to the midterms and say we did OK.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And good point to \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's job performance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, let me just say very quickly put up -- put up the Senate map. And that's really going to be an interesting look. We are a long way away, a lot can happen. And candidate choice as we saw this last time really makes a difference. But when you look at that map, what do you see? And what do you see?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I see a lot of Democrats up in a lot of exposure. The map was clearly better for us this year than it is in 24. But we have unique candidates, John Tester, who I worked for in his first campaign, a unique Montana Democrat, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , a unique Ohio Democrat. We have an incredibly strong field of candidates, even though we're running a lot.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, Republicans should be able to reclaim the Senate, you're right about candidate quality, there's no question and we were outspent miserably too, that has to change. We also as a party need to get it together and start competing for ballots and not just votes.\par \par \uc2 I don't like all the vote two months ahead and count them three weeks after. But if people are going to change the way they cast their ballot, we need to be competitive in that way too.\par \par \uc2 I think you look at \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  was probably the best Democratic Senate candidate of the cycle, definitely as a challenger, and he lost by eight points.\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  was last elected in 2018, Bret and Celinda, you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning the state by eight and you have J.D. Vance winning the Senate seat by eight. I think \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 's time is up because again, he's a little too left for Ohio, you're going to see that play out again and again in these other swing states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 RNC\uc2  is going to make a big decision about who leads them later this week. Ladies, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the trial of a prolife activist accused of assaulting an abortion clinic worker, we'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Taylor Swift ticket disaster, Congress gets involved, we'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): The ticketing and live entertainment markets lack competition, and they are dominated by a single entity.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Give the consumer a break. Not every kid can afford whatever it is $500 to go see Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A prominent Catholic pro-life supporter is on trial in Philadelphia, accused of assaulting a volunteer worker at an abortion clinic. Mark Houck insists he was merely defending his 12-year-old son. Correspondent Mark Meredith has details from Philadelphia tonight. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Tonight, the pro-life activist you are talking, Mark Houck, faces potentially faces 11 years behind bars, that is if he is convicted of federal FACE Act, or the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances. Federal prosecutors say it was back in October of 2021 that Houck allegedly assaulted an abortion clinic volunteer escort outside a Philadelphia Planned Parenthood. The defense is arguing that this incident was simply a shoving match and that Houck was actually acting in self-defense, sticking up for his 12-year-old son who was with him outside the clinic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER BREEN, HOUCK ATTORNEY: This was a sidewalk squabble that has now been turned into a federal case. It should have ended in the state courts which threw this matter out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Houck argues also as a pro-life activist he is being targeted by the Justice Department because of his religious beliefs. He has also claimed that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was too aggressive when it came to arresting him last year. But we have heard from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who said that they did not believe that claim at all, insisting that the SWAT team was not involved despite claims otherwise.\par \par \uc2 This case is certainly notable because the Justice Department even this week making it clear they are going to continue to pursue anybody who is violating the FACE Act in federal court. This case also getting a lot of national attention. Jury selection wrapped up late today, opening arguments tomorrow. Bret, this case should wrap up by the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith live in Philadelphia. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, new legal trouble for \uc2 Google\uc2 . The Justice Department and several states suing the tech giant over its digital advertising policy. Correspondent David Spunt has details tonight here on set. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret,