IDENTIFIED MALE: As we sit here in your home office in Indiana, did you take any classified documents with you from the White House?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: I did not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you see reason for anyone to take classified documents with them leaving the White House?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: There would be no reason to have classified documents, particularly if they were in an unprotected area.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Revelation today that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did have classified documents in his home office in Indiana and talked about that and came forward with what he is dealing with it. In fact, Representative Comer, the chairman of the Oversight Committee said "Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  reached out today about classified documents found at his home in Indiana. He has agreed to fully cooperate with congressional oversight. Pence's transparency stands in stark contrast to Biden White House staff who continue to withhold information from Congress and the American people."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and David Drucker, senior writer for "The Dispatch." Guy, it just gets muddy.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: So \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's server, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Mar-a-Lago, President Biden with this rolling mess, and now \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , I'm reminded of the famous quote from Casey Stengel, 1962 Mets manager. They lost 120 games, and he looked around and he said, can't anyone here play this game? And I feel like Americans might be looking at our political elites in both parties and wondering, can any of you people protect national secrets and follow the rules? And it seems like kind of across the board the answer recently has been no.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marie, to Guy's point, does this just get wiped all together and people say oh, they all do it?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think there is some of that going on here. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is much more similar to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I am sure if you went to former senator's houses, former cabinet members, you would probably find around the same number of documents that Pence had and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are trying to make the case that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is very different because he lied about it. His team lied about it. They repeatedly refused to let the DOJ, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the National Archives in, and they had to get a warrant to get them back.\par \par \uc2 Will the American people understand those nuances? I'm not so sure. I think there is a lot of, look, everyone in government is corrupt and takes things home. But legally, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in a different position for those reasons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. The Trump people would point out that it was in a facility that was protected by the Secret Service in a locked area that people dealt with before. But David, I don't want to go down into the weeds of each individual thing. Jonathan Turley said, "The refusal of \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  to answer any question on classified documents lacks any legal or logical rationale. She just refused to answer whether the administration thinks other former elected officials should take care in checking for classified documents." Part of the issue here is that there is not a lot of transparency coming from what these documents were, how they got there, and we don't know a lot.\par \par \uc2 DAVID DRUCKER, "THE DISPATCH": Yes, listen, at this point I think maybe we ought to execute search warrants at Monticello and Mount Vernon and see what we can dig up there.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think politically because, look, there is the legal factor, which is possession of classified documents not where they are supposed to be, and the lawyers will hash that out. I think politically the question is how are the American people, how are voters going to look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and anybody else that we are about to find out has kept classified documents? And it's too early to predict because we don't actually know how each instance is going to play out.\par \par \uc2 What I think we can say now, given the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  situation, is that the only thing that sets former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  separate from these other two is that he fought the authorities tooth and nail, rather than saying hey, listen, come on in it my private club, which is not just a private residence but a private club where other people get to come in and out, do everything you want. Take to the archives, and then I will hash it out with you there. And if he had done that, I think that might have looked different.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: House Republicans also have to decide how they want to handle the investigation now that it's not just Trump versus Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, so now \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says he has the documents. He is, it seems, going to run for president. If he is running for president, does he get a special counsel? Do you get a special counsel? Does everybody get out of special counsels.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: We're going to run out of special counsels. That's maybe my concern here.\par \par \uc2 But I think average Americans may not care all that deeply about the intricacies and every little specific detail that we are learning. But you can look back to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , this bootleg email server she set up in her house to go around the proper protocols. She clearly compromised national security information, top secret information on that server, then destroyed evidence and lied about it. She was not charged. And so I think it's very unlikely based on that precedent any of these other people we're talking about will get charged.\par \par \uc2 However, if you're just an average member of the U.S. military or working at Langley for some reason, and you make a mistake -- not a nefarious thing where you are trying to sell secrets, but you make a mistake and get caught with this type of stuff, you are in handcuffs. And I think that is the type of double standard, setting aside the participated stuff that would tick off a lot of normal Americans saying there are two tiers, and that doesn't seem fair.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I wanted to talk about Ukraine and the tanks that are now going over there. There is a decision for U.S. tanks and Germany is going to do it and Poland is going to put it in. But just a few moments ago the new House Speaker, McCarthy talking about the negotiations about the debt ceiling had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The whole government is designed to have compromise. But here's the leader of the free world pounding on the table, being irresponsible, saying no, no, no, just raise the limit, make us spend more? No. That's not how adults act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's interesting about this, and we didn't have time to pull up the soundbite, but Senator Biden, Marie, voted against increasing the debt limit. He wanted to negotiate, and he publicly said that there were people not willing to compromise, much -- very similar to House Speaker McCarthy. We always talk about the cycle of life here in Washington, but this one is pretty interesting.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, and Kevin McCarthy voted to raise the debt ceiling and House Republicans did three times when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, so they care about austerity when Democrats are in the White House. The real question is, is there actually anything Kevin McCarthy and his caucus can agree on that they want to negotiate over. Some want to cut defense spending, spending on Ukraine. Some of them said no, that's a nonstarter. Some want to cut entitlements, Medicare, Social Security. Others have said that's a nonstarter.\par \par \uc2 So at the end of the day they want to negotiate. It's not clear that they are actually willing to give something because they're all on different pages policy-wise, and it's not clear that they actually want to negotiate. They just say they want to. But when the proof gets there, I'm not sure there is actually any there there.\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: Listen, voters in the past have found holding the debt ceiling hostage for spending cuts to be illegitimate form of combat, political combat. They may agree that spending cuts are necessary. They don't think you should hold the debt ceiling hostage to do so. That's why President Biden is saying he won't negotiate. He thinks he has the voters on his side. And until he thinks otherwise, he is not going to change his tune.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You should talk to Senator Biden. He has got other things to say.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Vice President Biden led the negotiations in 2011 and said compromise is in the offing. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's Tuesday. It's time for "Tuesday Tweets." First up, Dr. Strangelove asks, "Do you expect more politicians to declare classified documents stored in their homes?" I would bet we are probably not done with these revelations. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Kurt tweets, "Judging candidates based on policy positions and actions used to be a thing. Now we seem to sacrifice logic and reason on the altar of political allegiance. Do you think an independent minded candidate could get elected today without bending a knee to a party?" We talked about this with the "pollstresses," as the call themselves, and they thought that that might not happen just because of the way it is structured. We'll see if it's Biden versus Trump. Who knows?\par \par \uc2 Last, Austin asks, "What golf club have you mastered? How long did it take you?" Zero golf clubs. I thought I was a good putter. Seve Ballesteros said he four-putted. He missed, he missed, he missed, and he made it. So you never can master it.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we're going to get into a bunch of different stories. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Is there a golf club you've mastered?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230125ej1o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Document Disaster Goes From Bad To Worse\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Gregg Jarrett, Kellyanne Conway, Ari Fleischer,  Jeanine Pirro, Gianno Caldwell \par \uc2 7,993 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, carnage in Atlanta, windows smashed, vehicles torched, police attacked. Six are arrested for domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 The defund, dismantled, no bail riots, they are back, but you're not allowed to even call them violent, according to fake news CNN. By the way, where's \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  when you really need them looking into violence and rioting?\par \par \uc2 Remember, there were no hearings on the 574 riots in the summer of 2020, we had thousands of injured cops, dozens of dead Americans, billions in property damage. Kinzinger is too busy selling autographed copies of his one-sided January 6 report. We've got full coverage tonight, that's straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 And stick around for tonight's last call. The lowest grade we've gotten so far is from a regular call to my radio show, Velma. She gave me a C. Well, she's back and she will tell us if she thinks I'm improving tonight.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with a bombshell new development from the Russia hoax. Listen to this, tonight, big news -- by the way, I hope the congenital liar Adam Schiff is hanging on to see because someone was just arrested for colluding with Russia, but it wasn't \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or someone from his campaign or the White House or Adam Schiff himself when he was pranked by Russians talking about compromising materials. Remember that one.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it was a man named Charles McGonigal, who was a high-ranking \uc2 FBI\uc2  official who worked with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's Trump Russia collusion investigation for years. The whole hoax, he was there. You literally can't make this up.\par \par \uc2 Now, McGonigal is now accused of taking secret payments from a powerful Russian oligarch in exchange for political favors, including an investigation into a rival oligarch. And he now faces up to 20 years in prison. Now that was one of the people investigating Trump for collusion.\par \par \uc2 Now, another was former lead investigator Peter Strzok who was yet to mention the arrest of his former colleague. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he's too busy defending \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and fantasizing about Trump's impending prosecution here, there, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Now, the disgraced former agent is the poster boy for political corruption and bias, how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been politicized. He tweeted on Sunday, quote, applying the facts of what we know of both Biden and Trump's document sagas, Trump should be prosecuted, Biden should not. Okay, sure, Strzok tweeted that -- by the way, remember, he's the one that called you a smelly Walmart Trump supporting shopper.\par \par \uc2 Strzok also tweeted that after even more classified documents were found Saturday in Biden's Delaware home over the weekend. This is now the fifth discovery of stolen or misplaced classified top secret material and Joe's home or office and on Friday, after the first four discoveries, Biden, quote, graciously invited the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ into his home for a follow-up inspection. And during a 13-hour search, guess what? Agents found them. Agents found more classified documents and markings -- by the way, classification markings surrounding materials, some of which were from the president's service in the Senate and some of which were from his tenure as vice president.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has the ability to declassify. There's no set process. There's no law regarding how to do it. That's not something \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ever had.\par \par \uc2 This now raised us very key questions. First, how many classified documents are in those items. Fifty, 100, 50? Second, how and why did Biden remove classified documents from his time in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and where of those documents been for the past 15 years. Thirdly, we're the top secret documents stored there in that box labeled important docs in photos.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, this photo was reportedly unearthed from -- you got it -- the laptop from hell of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Here's another question, when will the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid Biden's other properties like they did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Or they're just going to wait for Joe's personal attorney days and aides that don't even have security clearances to keep looking to sanitize the crime scene first, like they did at his Wilmington home?\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not receive the same gentle treatment, favorable treatment. And every time a Democrat or the media says, well, Joe is cooperative and Trump was it, well, that's not true. Remember, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had unfettered access to the very room where the documents were found during the raid they had been there before, when they were there and had unfettered free access, they took nothing, asked for nothing, the only thing they asked for is that that room be padlocked which it was.\par \par \uc2 And according to my sources, even after that point, Trump hired an outside firm to search all of his properties he invited the Justice Department to join in, and they said no. That consideration is only for Democrats. Biden mishandled classified material for well over a decade in multiple locations, but federal officials seem to need a personal invitation mission to conduct a search.\par \par \uc2 Still this is a major scandal for the Biden White House. Even Democrats are now acknowledging and even so-called journalists in the media mob of starting to voice concern as well. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN, CBS NEWS HOST: How does it, Senator, accidentally take classified material home?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Margaret, I don't really know the answer to that question. So that's why there needs to be this independent investigation, an independent prosecutor, how many documents are we talking about dozens a handful or hundreds, how serious are they, why were they taken, did anyone have access to them?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): But I can tell you one thing, it's unbelievable how this could happen, it's totally irresponsible and who's at fault?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): To think that any of them ended up in boxes in storage one place to the other is just unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, unacceptable. Oh, okay, it took you a while, took five separate times of finding these documents. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  for her part, she has a very different take. Her only concern is that MSDNC is too harsh in their coverage of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She's angry at them, telling "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", quote, I'm not a big fan of MSDNC.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Pelosi has more important things to worry about. Apparently, she recently invited Catholic priests into her home to perform an exorcism, according to her own daughter. I certainly pray on her behalf. I hope you'll join me in prayer and lift her up in prayer, that these evil spirits have left her house.\par \par \uc2 Here we're now with all reaction to tonight's big developments, "The Price of Principles", Harvard law professor \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2 , along with FOX News legal analyst Greg Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 Professor, let me begin with you because there are distinct differences and this goes back to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  as well, although I would argue that deletion of 33,000 emails, the use of Bleach Bit, hammers, devices, missing SIM cards, that brings up a whole obstruction angle and the sheer volume of documents.\par \par \uc2 But the main question is either \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  had the right to declassify. Do you know of any system that exists currently -- any law that exists currently that mandates that a president declassify in any specific way because I'm not aware of one.\par \par \uc2 ALAN DERSHOWITZ\uc2 , HARVARD LAW PROFESSOR: There is none. That's clear. It's a gap in the law. The law should provide that there's some documentation the classification it can happen instantaneously and apparently it did when President Bush years ago had a conversation and declassified something on the spot.\par \par \uc2 But there is no criteria. Now it's a pure question of evidence and the burden of proof is on the government. So I do not believe the government is going to be able to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that the material that was in Trump's possession was actually classified.\par \par \uc2 On the Biden side, there's another problem because the statute requires that the classified material be knowingly removed, that is the person has to know it was classified and it was removed with an intent to retain it, and what Biden is going to claim is I didn't know, it was just among other papers proved beyond a reasonable doubt that I knew it was classified. That's why I have concluded after, you know, 50 years of litigating criminal cases, there will be no criminal prosecution of either Trump or Biden.\par \par \uc2 The only reason the special prosecutors were appointed was to satisfy the appearance of justice not the reality of justice.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I agree with your analysis. I think that's how it's going to end up being a wash, and I agree with you also, Professor, that this should be in law a specific means by which classification ought to occur by a president.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, Gregg Jarrett, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said on this program that he could have the power to declassify anyway he wanted to declassify.\par \par \uc2 Now, he got ridiculed and attacked for it, but the -- but the truth is, he's right, isn't he?\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Oh, he's totally right. It's an exclusive power uh contained in the Constitution through the commander-in- chief clause and you know, the vice president doesn't have that right nor \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  as secretary of state. I thoroughly agree with the president that -- or with Mr. Dershowitz that this will be a wash.\par \par \uc2 But I disagree that intent and knowing and willful is the only standard if you examine section F of 18 USC 793. That also provides for gross negligence or carelessness. Well, the increasing number of locations where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's classified documents have been discovered renders inadvertent, implausible, and instead seems to be the definition of recklessness, gross negligence under the statute.\par \par \uc2 So I think legally, there would be a fairly strong case. And look, President Biden's excuses in his bag of alibis, his whack-a-mole rebuttal to every newly discovered batch of documents is growing more and more preposterous. First, it was, you know, inadvertence and then it was the well-established legally recognized Corvette defense. Then he had the arrogance to say that he had no regrets over declassifying or over national security documents that were jeopardized.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, as I wrote in a column today, what's his next excuse? Corn pop did it? I mean, it's getting to the absurd.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, it's getting to that point. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Exit question for both -- exit question for both of you. Professor, is there a double standard in the way that our government don't prosecute or prosecute? That's \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2  in July of 2016, the way the media and Democrats are treating Biden, the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was treated with the raid. Is there a dual system of justice? I believe there is.\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: Well, I think there is the -- certainly, the reality and the perception that there is a dual system.\par \par \uc2 Look, I do not think \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  should have been prosecuted. I don't think Trump should be prosecuted. I don't think Biden should be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You don't think deleting 32,000 emails like that was obstruction? Bleach Bit, have you ever heard of Bleach Bit? I never had.\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: Well, her claims that they were personal emails and that she was not destroying classified material --\par \par