eing an advisor to me on my brother since he was murdered back in June. But I'll tell you, Sean, for some on the left, intolerant has become certainly the exception and certainly, the norm -- not the exception.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to tell you from what I experienced in that conversation, I met up with some folks that live in my building here in Miami, my first time ever hanging out with them, first time hanging out at this particular establishment, what we talked about was FOX, how do you like working at FOX was their question to me? Are you a conservative? In fact, I am, I have a book out call taken for granted, how conservatism can win back to America's liberalism fail. Get -- get a copy .\par \par \uc2 I talked about my brother. I talked about the work that I'm doing with Congress on Capitol Hill for victims of violent crime. Then a owner comes over to the table and she says, I've been listening to your conversation and you are not welcome here.\par \par \uc2 And I said, did I say anything to you that triggered you? She said, no, we're just not politically aligned.\par \par \uc2 How dare her? This is modern day Jim Crow to me. I can't believe that me being a Black man in America to give my views on conservatism, which I believe saved my life, my Christian values saved my life and the life of many other people that I know be told that I cannot be in an establishment. This makes no sense to me.\par \par \uc2 If you're not capitulating to their narrative, their views, you are unwelcome, and that should not happen whether it be in Florida or anywhere in the United States of America. We should be far beyond this but that's what I experienced on Saturday in this restaurant.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's outrageous. Anything you plan to do? Are you going to sue them?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, I'm thinking about what I'm doing next very carefully. People can follow me on social media @giannocaldwell for my next steps. But I got to tell you, they've been taking a hammering over on social media. People don't like that, because this can happen to anyone that happens to be conservative --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Has anyone listen to anyone else --\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Who's a black conservative --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's a great point, too, and we see this over and over again.\par \par \uc2 When is anybody that works at a restaurant and I worked at them for, you know, 10 years of my life, give a rip about what somebody's talking about at their table. It's none of your business.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It shouldn't be any of this lady's business. She -- to me, she seemed like a from her how she was moving like a white progressive and what I found out is a lot of the folks there in ownership are progressives. But you should not be listening in on your customers, that's called eavesdropping, but that's what she did and she chose to take that moment to take a stand.\par \par \uc2 And I posted a tweet immediately after which has had two over two million views and now, they're in trouble. Because what she did to me was plain and simple discrimination. Again, this is modern day Jim Crow. If you don't go with what the left wants you to go with, if you don't follow their narrative completely, then you're in trouble in their eyes.\par \par \uc2 And that should not be the case, for any American, it doesn't matter what color you are. You white, black or indifferent, you should not be discriminated against. But that's what we saw.\par \par \uc2 But I'm hoping that Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to take a very close look at what's going on here because he said no discrimination in Florida and what I saw was discrimination.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, I tell you, this has happened to me, but I'm angrier when it happens to my friends like you. As I wrote you this weekend, you handled it perfectly, I admire all that you're doing for your family had a very tough upbringing yourself.\par \par \uc2 Gianna, we think the world of you. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 His book, by the way, \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , Hannity.com, "Taken For Granted: How Conservatism Can Win Back the Americans that Liberalism Failed".\par \par \uc2 Up next, Velma, she gave me my lowest grade. She gets the last word. Last call, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Back to my days as a bartender, last call where you the viewer what do you like what do you dislike about the show.\par \par \uc2 The lowest grade we've gotten so far was from Velma. It was a regular call to my radio show, and by the way, if you want to call in, it's 800-941- SEAN, between the hours of 3:00 and 6:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Velma's back. She gave me -- you gave me a C minus, Velma. The lowest grade of anybody. Have I improved? Had you liked the show tonight? What did you like, what did you dislike, what's my grade?\par \par \uc2 VELMA, CALLER FROM LAS VEGAS: Most of all, you know I love you dearly, Sean. But most of all, being you hearing your voice but, you know, you put all these people on that I have zero respect for. Ted Cruz --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, wait a minute, wait --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Hang on you didn't like my buddy Gianno? He's awesome, a good Christian man.\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Well, you know what, I don't mind -- you know I'm a conservative myself, I can understand --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You are not a conservative. You loved Obama and you loved Biden. You're not a conservative.\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Sean, just because I don't agree with your politics, there's not -- I'm a Christian, I love the Lord, I love --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Amen to that part. But you're not a political conservative.\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Just because I don't agree with you, Mr. Hannity?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Just because I don't agree with you, and you know I hate to give you a good break because you know I know you, and you're a wonderful man, you're a wonderful uncle to my children. You wonderful --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. What's my grade? We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Okay, a C.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I go from a C minus to a C? That's it?\par \par \uc2 VELMA: Yeah, because I gave you a C minus the last time, don't forget.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, okay. Thank you, Velma.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled. Laura's next. We'll see you back here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020230124ej1n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 March for Life in D.C.; Penny Nance and Elizabeth Wydra are Interviewed about Abortion Rights; Haley Hits at 2024 Run; DNC Proposed Primary Changes; \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  Hearings on Capitol Hill; Baldwin Charged in Shooting. Interview with House Problem Solvers Caucus, Democratic Congressman of New Jersey \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  and Republican Congressman of Pennsylvania \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Marie Harf, Raymond Arroyo, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A new round of classified items found in the president's home and new concerns about financial fallouts as the U.S. hits the debt limit again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they want to play a game of chicken, we'll play a game of chicken.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): We've had these games before and it should not be done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The Treasury Department now buying time with extraordinary measures as Republicans bicker with the White House.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president has been clear on this. It should not be used as a political weapon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Swing district, moderate Republicans are calling for the president to drop the take it or leave it approach and come to the table.\par \par \uc2 We'll sit down for a bipartisan conversation with two co-chairs from the Problem Solvers Caucus. Republican \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  and Democrat \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  join me to talk about how to find consensus on the debt limit, immigration and more. It's a "FOX News Sunday" exclusive.\par \par \uc2 Then -- thousands of pro-life advocates come to the nation's capital for the first March for Life since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. We'll look at the legal state of play now that abortion laws are up to the states, and sit down for a conversation with prominent voices from both sides.\par \par \uc2 And eight months after the unprecedented leak of a draft Supreme Court ruling, there are still no answers from the high court about the leaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): The only way you're going to stop this in the future is to make sure you find out who did it and hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel if we will ever find out who did it.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, at least ten people are dead, another ten injured after a mass shooting near Los Angeles. It happened late last night at a dance club in Monterey Park, California, close to where a lunar New York celebration had been taking place.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say they believe the shooter is male and at this time it appears that person is not in custody. No word yet on a motive.\par \par \uc2 Deputies say they are reviewing security video in that area. Monterey Park is about ten miles east of Los Angeles. We'll keep you updated on any developments we get in from there.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking this morning, the Justice Department seized more classified documents from the president's private residence just this week. The news comes as President Biden prepares to speak in person with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to discuss the new Congress, a range of challenges there, where they disagree. And that, of course, includes the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Congress is facing a deadline to strike a deal or risk a financial crisis as the Treasury department steps in to avoid a government default.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will chat with two moderate lawmakers, Republican Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  of Pennsylvania, and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  of New Jersey. They are trying to press their way into the most polarized policy debates and find solutions.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson outside the White House where there are multiple developing stories this morning.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, the president is facing renewed criticism after more classified documents were found in his Wilmington, Delaware, home. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducted the search.\par \par \uc2 The president also facing a debt ceiling fight amid reports his top aide is leaving. White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  could step down in the coming weeks, the biggest administration departure since President Biden took office two years ago. The move was expected. Klain has been at Biden's side through the legislative triumphs of his first two years and through his Supreme Court confirmation, but also through major controversies, like the disastrous U.S. departure from Afghanistan. Klain may be best known for helping the president pass massive spending bills like the $1.9 trillion COVID relief plan, the $1 trillion infrastructure bill and the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the spending helped accelerate the current inflation crisis and added to the national debt. In 2000, the debt was just over $5 trillion. It's climbed to over $31 trillion today. It's money already spent and now the U.S. is over its borrowing limit which could put the nation's credit rating at risk.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT: To threaten default, to threaten economic catastrophe, nobody, Democrat or Republican, should do that no matter how much they believe in their agenda.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Republicans say they want spending cuts if they're going to raise the debt limit higher. The White House says raise the limit period. No negotiations.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It is a basic responsibility that Congress has to deal with the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: President Biden plans to huddle with top Democratic leaders at the White House Tuesday. He also plans to host Kevin McCarthy, their first meeting since McCarthy became House speaker.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy responding, President Biden, I accept your invitation to sit down and discuss a responsible debt ceiling increase to address irresponsible government spending.\par \par \uc2 A White House official spinning this differently, calling it a, quote, general meeting, not a negotiation.\par \par \uc2 All this while the president faces continued discussions over his handling of classified documents. The new house majority leader tells FOX --\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA), HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: The rules of justice should apply to everybody equally. In a case like this, it's one more example that they're not being applied equally to Democrats as they are to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Now two Democratic Senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Tim Kaine are also calling for a full investigation. They're both up for re- election in 2024 -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House Lucas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, co-chairs of the House Problem Solvers Caucus, Democratic congressman of New Jersey, \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2 , and Republican congressman of Pennsylvania, \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressmen, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2  (D-NJ): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's start here. The headlines overnight are not the ones the White House wants to see. Yet another discovery of classified material at the president's Wilmington home.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley, well known to our audience and a legal professional, a professor and constitutional scholar, tweets this: With the latest discovery, there's no real question that the Biden documents were grossly mishandled. There only remains who was responsible. However, the discovery of documents at yet another location used by the president is crushing for his defense team.\par \par \uc2 He says he's done everything right, he has no regrets. Congressman Gottheimer, should he, at this point?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, I think we've seen the Justice Department is conducting an on going investigation. That's the proper process here. And you want to get to the bottom of these things, which is why they were at his home yesterday in full cooperation.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the -- what you need to do here. The White House needs to cooperate with the Justice Department. That's what they've been doing for months. Obviously, that's in stark comparison to the other investigation of classified documents with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I think as long as the White House does what it should do, which is cooperate fully so we can get to the bottom of this, I think that's the appropriate process. And, of course, in a broader sense, we should understand in any White House, whether it's the last White House or this one, how any documents would ever get out. And the key is to make sure that never happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, also in contrast is the fact that there have been on going negotiations with this Justice Department, with the Biden White House and legal team. And there was an agreement for them to be there together Friday during the search, but what we found is leading to more questions than answers for a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy, former federal prosecutor, tweeted this out: It's not six classified documents, this latest discovery. Awkwardly worded statement by Biden team, i.e., spin is favorable to Biden as possible, were six items consisting of documents with classified markings and we don't know what they meant by item. Box, envelope or how many classified documents in each item.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a special counsel investigations already under the way -- under way. You heard Lucas Tomlinson say, maybe some Democratic senators are also interested. House oversight has already said that it's going to investigate.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Fitzpatrick, any word you have that the GOP is going to overreach on this or look like they're politicizing it?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: I don't believe so, Shannon. Two things are true. Number one, classified documents can never be taken out of the SCIF ever. I'm both a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent and I'm currently on the House Intelligence Committee. That's number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we need equal application of the law. And that's what we're going to make sure that we ensure. We're going to do a deep dive into the circumstances surrounding the prior administration and the current administration, both dealing with classified documents and making sure the law is applied equally across the board. That's what the American people are demanding.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So the Wall Street Journal had reported on conversations between the two teams, and the Biden side, and the DOJ side about whether or not \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents or others should be doing these searches now that we're on umber five.\par \par \uc2 And up into the point on Friday, they had agreed to let the Biden team handle it. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " also has this, that the White House was hoping to keep this quiet. The White House was hoping for a speedy inquiry, planning to disclose this matter only after Justice issued its all clear.\par \par \uc2 But the approach would end up prompting accusations that the Biden's team had purposely kept the public in the dark.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gottheimer, we know all of this started before the midterms. Do you get the perception that some people have that there are differences in the way the DOJ handles Republican and Democrat cases?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: I mean, there's a huge difference in the fact, if you look at the last administration, the Trump administration, what happened in Mar-a- Lago, there was a refusal to cooperate for nearly a year, right? There was obstruction. You needed subpoenas. President Trump didn't come forward and offer up his documents. He was keeping them locked up.\par \par \uc2 And this administration, I believe, has cooperated in a constructive way with the Justice Department from the beginning. They didn't take months. They didn't stall. The president wasn't trying to hold up these documents as trophies.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's a huge difference. But the bottom line is this, and Brian is exactly right. We need to have a full cooperation investigation. We need to get to the bottom of what happened, why and when, to understand everything.\par \par \uc2 And in a broader sense, to make sure that no White House, no administration is ever able to handle classified documents this way and take them out of the White House from a classified setting. And I think, you know, we should get to the bottom of both, and I think we should run a reasonable process. And that's what's happening right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you as members of Congress may have more of a hand in that in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 There's this, you know, piece again -- Andy McCarthy speaking out on this saying after the White House has said there are no logs there, this is the personal home, where in the past they said the president does work from there. There are questions about whether there are logs of visitors or people who would have been there.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy writes this: I'm very confident that if the agency, meaning the Secret Service, believed it was in the interest of the president's security that the information would be produced for the bureau would be produced at warp speed.\par \par \uc2 We've got at least one source saying to us the Secret Service is prepared to come forward with some information about visitors there but Congress is going to have to ask.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Fitzpatrick, will you do that?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: We will, Shannon. There's multiple ways to gather evidence and conduct investigations. Obviously, visitors log would be very helpful. If that doesn't exist, you go to other forms of evidence, video surveillance, physical surveillance, witness interviews. There's a whole host of ways to collect evidence.\par \par \uc2 So, I have no doubt that certainly we're going to make sure of this, that the bureau does their job investigating this case. And again, they got to apply the law equally. That's very, very important for people in America to have faith and confidence in our justice system.\par \par \uc2 It's a system I have worked in. It's very important for me that they do things the right way so that the American public has confidence in it. And that requires equal application of the law, consistent standards on investigations. That's what we're going to make sure happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. You all are trying to make sure something else happen, which is that we don't default on our credit ratings. And you're negotiating and talking actively about the debt ceiling in a deal that could possibly come together.\par \par \uc2 The White House says no negotiations at all. We know that's not practical given some of the demands that House GOP members that spending cuts and other things be included in order to get their vote.\par \par \uc2 Our Edward Lawrence over at FOX Business has been reporting, Congressman Fitzpatrick, you've been working on something that would be a debt to GDP ratio, potentially. If you hit a certain number, then automatic cuts kick in.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us any sense of where those negotiations are? What would be the ratio? How in the world do you decide whose programs automatically get cut in that scenario?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yeah. Well, first thing that has to happen, Shannon, Speaker McCarthy has made several overtures to the White House. He wants to sit down and work a reasonable solution out.\par \par \uc2 I hope the White House accepts his offer. In fact, it was White House that offered Speaker McCarthy the opportunity to come in. I hope that happens. And the White House's position cannot --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, but they say it's not going to be a negotiation over the debt ceiling. They say that is not what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: That's a -- that's a problem. That's a problem and that's not leadership. When you have a divided government, four-vote Republican margin in the House, a one-vote Democratic margin in the Senate, divided chambers, you have to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 That's what the American people elected us to do, is to work this out. So nobody should be taking the position that we're not going to negotiate. That's very irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 Pertaining to your question, Shannon, one thing that we're just going to offer up as a possible bridge-building solution is to go right at the 1917 law itself that established the debt limit. It established it as a number, a numerical dollar amount, which doesn't make any sense. We think that more practically speaking, it should be a debt to GDP ratio.\par \par \uc2 In 2008, our debt to GDP was about 40 percent. In 2018, it was about 70 percent. It's now at 125 percent. That's not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 So, when you have a child that has a spending problem, you do two things. You pay their bills, and take away their credit card. You don't do one, you do both.\par \par \uc2 That's what has to happen here. So what we're going to propose is whatever negotiated amount we can agree on on a debt to GDP ratio and have a cure period there after. If that cure does not happen, certain budgetary reforms automatically kick in on the discretionary side.\par \par \uc2 So, we're still putting the meat on those bones. Me and Josh are going to work through our proposed solution. But the first thing that's got to happen, Shannon, the president has to sit down with Speaker McCarthy. Speaker McCarthy offered to negotiate in good faith. I hope President Biden does as him (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is quoting a senior Democrat over in Congress that says he had a conversation with \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , White House chief of staff who may soon be leaving. But he essentially said, no holds barred, there will be no negotiations. You've got to make it look like we're the responsible ones and that the Republicans are essentially irresponsible, trying to kill entitlements, and those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gottheimer, can you work with that? If that's the strategy, if that's the theory, how do you move the ball at all?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, I've had conversations with the White House just this weekend. And I'm optimistic that they will sit down as this White House always has. That's why we were able to accomplish so much in a bipartisan way last Congress. It takes constructive conversation.\par \par \uc2 I think there's things reasonable on the table and things that are unreasonable. Gutting Social Security and Medicare is obviously on the unreasonable side.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which the speaker has said is not on the table.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Just to be clear, the speaker has said that those -- that those -- that that's a talking point and that Republicans are not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: By the way, that's great, because I don't think it should be on the table. Things like 30 percent sales tax should not be on the table.\par \par \uc2 But there are plenty of things that should be. And, you know, Brian and I and the Problem Solvers Caucus are obviously having discussions. I think the White House, you see Kevin McCarthy and the president will be sitting down, the speaker and the president will be sitting down. I think that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 What we can't do is put the full faith in the credit of United States of America at risk. We can't put people's 401(k)s at risk. We need to have these constructive conversations, make sure that we raise the debt limit responsibly so that people can have faith in our country and our currency. And we can also talk about our fiscal health and do that in a responsible way.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to do both. And I'm optimistic that everyone will sit down. We'll work this out, because we have no other choice. We have to work this out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Quickly, Congressman Fitzpatrick, with that in mind and the potential departure of \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , knowing what his strategy reportedly is, do you think his departure changes the way that you are able to work with this administration?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: I don't think so. Again, we're going to let our speaker take the lead on this with his negotiations and then Josh and I are going to offer our solution that hopefully can be constructive. But I don't believe it's going to affect our relationship with the White House, Shannon. We've had a constructive relationship with them.\par \par \uc2 That's our job as representatives, no matter what party we're from, is to do our part to make government work. And we've worked well with a lot of people over there and I expect that to continue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the American people wish you well in trying to find solutions at the most treacherous problems we have now.\par \par \uc2 Congressmen, thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: We'll get it done.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: You bet. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the president's downplaying concerns about how he handled classified materials from years ago as yet another batch is found at his Wilmington home. And a special counsel is ramping up to investigate. We're going to bring in our Sunday group to discuss all this, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think they're going to find, there's nothing there. I have regrets on following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do. That's exactly what we're doing. There's no there there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden brushing off concerns after weeks of questions about how he handled the discovery and disclosure of classified documents from his time in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. Now we understand maybe as far back to his Senate career.\par \par \uc2 It is time four Sunday group. White House correspondent Francesca Chambers; former State Department spokesperson, Marie Harf; former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Josh Holmes; and FOX News analyst Raymond Arroyo.\par \par \uc2 Panel, welcome.\par \par \uc2 If only there was something to discuss today.\par \par \uc2 Okay. So he says there's no there there, but apparently there's another batch of there there. Given this discovery, the fifth batch on Friday, as DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents were there. Jonathan Turley writes this about the personal's statements. He shrugged off the matter like finding a borrowed hammer in his garage. Since the standard is gross mishandling of classified evidence, the last thing you want to do is give a grossly mishandling answer.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO SENATOR MCCONNELL: I think he's got deep regrets, right? Especially coming outs so forcefully saying this whole thing is over, you're not going to find anything more, we're done here with only a handful of documents and nothing to see here. Every day, seems like everywhere he's had a cup of coffee he's got documents behind him. And now, DOJ is finding all this.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest issue as we turn forward with it is the access piece to it, right? We know what the Mar-a-Lago raid, were all in one room and the access was fairly limited. It's in his garage, right? In certain office space. We don't know who has access to this stuff. I think that's the biggest question going forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion piece said this, oh, Biden, what have you done. It's hard to exaggerate the level of exasperation with him for squandering a huge political advantage on the Mar-a-Lago story and for muddying what may have been the best chance to convict Mr. Trump on federal charges. Marie?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Look, the reason we know there were more documents found is that Biden's personal attorneys invited the department of justice in to look for them. A couple were found, I think six last night. So they are fully cooperating.\par \par \uc2 The Biden team will tell you, we are fully cooperating, we are inviting them in. That is in stark contrast to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who repeatedly lied, he and his attorneys did, to the federal government. He claimed they were his. He claimed he declassified them.\par \par \uc2 We have no idea who had access to them at Mar-a-Lago. We know there have been security concerns there.\par \par \uc2 So, would I prefer not to be talking about this on "FOX News Sunday" as a Democrat? Of course, I would. But I think attend of the day the distinction of how the Biden team has handled them, inviting DOJ and fully cooperating, letting them look into whatever they can, compared to the Trump team which is at every single turn really pushed back, even lied to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and to the Archives. I think at the end of the day that distinction will be quite clear to people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Raymond, some people point to the distinction and the reporting in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and "Wall Street Journal" that there were conversations with the Biden team an DOJ, oh, no, we're going to let you investigate it. And now, we're going to let you come in. And so, those conversations make people at least have the perception that there is a different standard.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS ANALYST: Well, there seems to be a different standard here because, look, you have private and personal lawyers going in and looking and discovering these caches. Did they have security clearance? Which we don't have full clarity on yet.\par \par \uc2 But, look, Shannon, years ago. I remember seeing Dean Martin. He'd walk out on stage and say, how did all these people get in my room? That's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  every time he finds a document. How did this document get here?\par \par \uc2 Well, it didn't walk in. You placed it there or someone near you did. We need to know why and how. When you look at the Penn Biden Center, that first discovery is particularly disturbing because the classified documents were mingled in among personal files. They were even in envelopes marked personal files. Why? Who put them there?\par \par \uc2 And when you overlay the timetable of the documents discovered and what was happening in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's life, they do track with the Hunter \uc2 Burisma\uc2  deal, the Iran nuke deal, the Chinese natural gas deal that we've discovered, or has come to light, via Hunter's lap top.\par \par \uc2 So, tons of questions that need exploration here. But I'm not quite certain, Shannon, we've discovered the last Biden classified document just yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we have been told that we think this is it. They think they're wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, it blew up your story and deadline last night when there was another discovery.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, USA TODAY WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And House Oversight Republicans are trying to get their hands on the documents that were found. We don't know exactly what is in those documents yet. They gave the White House this week to answer questions about this. They say they've heard crickets so far from the White House, despite the public responses.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, White House administration officials telling me they're still deciding how they want to respond to these request for communications.\par \par \uc2 But as far as President Biden goes, the revelation that some of those documents included documents, we don't know how many, dating back to his time in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , is at odds with the statements from the White House that he has handled the classified information in an appropriate manner and takes it very seriously, so to speak, bringing new scrutiny on that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. It puts the headline back on the document situation. Other than what is the strategy on the debt ceiling as to saying the Republicans are crazy and they're going to crash the economy.\par \par \uc2 Josh, "The Hill" has this headline, GOP risks overplaying their hand on the debt ceiling. They're pressing for big spending cuts but there's a real chance the GOP could end up blamed for cascading negative effects if the U.S. even approaches a scenario where it defaults on its financial obligation. How much danger for them there?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, I think everybody has to get on the same page from the very beginning. There is going to be a negotiation, right? You've got Democrats basically at this point saying we're going to have a clean debt ceiling. That's about it.\par \par \uc2 There's zero chance that that happens. There's going to be a discussion between Speaker McCarthy and the administration. I assume the Senate will be part of that discussion.\par \par \uc2 It's not unprecedented. I remember sitting there in 2011 when we were doing this with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with the Budget Control Act.\par \par \uc2 So, this is something that has lot of history, lot of precedent. What U.S. Republicans need to do is figure out what ignites them to get to question, because that's the other piece that has to happen. It's not a negotiable outcome. The outcome has to be there. But they've got to get as far down the road on the conservative side with spending cuts and the like to get that conference together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Marie, we know \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is for the hard ball plan on this. That's the public line the administration continues to hold. Do you think his departure will change the way the White House operates in these kinds of non-negotiation negotiations?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Look, I don't think their strategy is going to change necessarily. Republicans in Congress voted three times to raise the debt ceiling under President Trump, not demanding any of the kinds of spending cuts they're demanding now. So, Republicans find fiscal austerity generally when a Democrat is in the White House.\par \par \uc2 But the case that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  or any other senior adviser to Biden will tell you is under President Biden, record unemployment, 11 million jobs created, record numbers of small business applications being put in in the last two years. Contrast that with Republicans taking over Congress. The first thing they do, cut Medicare and Social Security, default on our obligations, get our credit rating downgraded.\par \par \uc2 I think the Biden White House feels very confident that if you take those two messages to the American people, they're going to come out on the winning end of that policy wise economically but also politically, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, yeah, there's the political and optics side of that. Very quickly before we go, I want to get your comment on the fact that the Supreme Court has been investigating for quite sometime the May leak last year. We have no answer about who it is.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and the question is why don't we have those answers? I mean, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should have been brought in, Shannon. As you know, the investigator here was the marshal of the court. They don't have the investigation power to seize records and to obligate some of those clerks who have already left.\par \par \uc2 As you were talking about earlier this morning, many of these clerks have already left. Their tenure ended, they moved on. We didn't get testimony for some of them and records for some of them.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think we'll ever know who this leaker is. Too much time has passed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Amen.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But we should know. These justices and their families, I can tell you, they are very worried and the threats have not stopped since that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Demonstrations do continue at many of their homes on a weekly basis.\par \par \uc2 OK. Francesca, you'll kick us off next time.\par \par \uc2 Panel, up next, March for Life returned to Washington this week celebrating the end of Roe v. Wade. But legal battles continue to sprawl all across the states in the debate over access to abortion. We sit down with prominent voices from the pro-choice and pro-life movements here together for a conversation on one of the nation's most contentious issues.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In 1973, the Supreme Court ruled in Roe v. Wade that the right to privacy implied in the 14th Amendment meant women had a right to abortion. That kicked off one of the most hard fought political and legal battles of the past half century.\par \par \uc2 Then last summer, after a leaked ruling indicated it would, the high court did indeed overturn Roe. But instead of settling the issue, it shifted the battle to new fronts. This morning we will sit down with two of the most prominent advocates in both the pro-life and pro-choice movements to discuss the new paradigm.\par \par \uc2 But first, a look at the surge in advocacy on both sides seven months after tend of Roe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): It was nearly a party atmosphere outside the Supreme Court Friday as pro-life demonstrators marched their 50th March for Life.\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH (R), MISSISSIPPI ATTORNEY GENERAL: This year is different. We have overturned Roe v. Wade with the Mississippi Dobbs case. We have done it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The march started in the 1970s as a protest against Roe v. Wade. This year, it had a victory lap feel as marchers celebrated the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We just won a huge case in the Supreme Court. And that's - that's incredible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Did you ever think that you would see this day where Roe was no longer the law of the land?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's incredible. We hoped. We prayed. You know, there's always hope. But I think nobody could believe it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe that the culture is moving in our direction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that's been what's really cool for me is to like see that we're not the only ones. There's so many people here. And people are coming with so much joy and so much hope.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But even as the pro-life movement celebrates seven months since Roe was overturned by the Dobbs decision, the legal rules around abortion are more unsettled than ever. Now the abortion question is up to states.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR) (June 26, 2022): This is a day that those in the pro-life movement has worked for, for over 40 years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: About a dozen states banned most or all abortions immediately. Many saying those bans saved lives. But many states did the reverse, passing laws to ensure women had greater abortion access.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): California, Oregon and Washington are building the west coast offense to protect patients' access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Voters also weighed in as midterm ballots saw a record number of abortion questions. In Kansas, voters turned out in huge numbers rejecting a law that would have banned access. The margin, 59 percent to 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 GOV. LAURA KELLY (D-KS): And an overwhelming majority of Kansans believe that a woman's right to make private medical decisions should rest with her and not with politicians.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fundraising for both sides exploded after Kansas, with statewide races seen as critical. A Wisconsin judicial race is getting headlines now because an existing but unenforced abortion ban from 1849 could reach the state's highest court.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration ramped up federal protections with an executive order preemptively pushing back on limits to obtaining federally approved abortion medication, or on women traveling to states where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 And the legal battles continue to unfold. South Carolina's State Supreme Court struck down a six week abortion ban in early January. Pro-choice advocates are pressing Congress to pass the original Roe precedent into law.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (October 2022): I believe Congress should codify Roe once and for all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: By contrast, some congressional Republicans want a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If Republicans get their way with a national ban, I'll veto it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The president warned before the midterms the end of Roe would cost Republicans politically.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think you're going to hear women roar on this issue, and it's going to be consequential.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fox News voter analysis in November found 62 percent favored a national law guaranteeing access to abortion, 38 percent opposed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): This year's rally was different than marches in the past, going instead between the U.S. Capitol and the Supreme Court, as organizers say they're shifting their focus from the legal battle at the high court to the political one over on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But marchers also acknowledge a federal ban may never happen. And, in the meantime, their movement is under pressure to help mothers and children after pregnancy. Something they say they've been doing for decades and have ramped up in the wake of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And there will be children that come into this world that these women don't feel prepared for or it doesn't fit in their life. So, what do you say to them post Roe?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, we definitely have to step up our game with the whole adoption system and then supporting women who are in these situations where they don't have the money or the resources to have these children. We can't tell the world that we want women to have children and then not give them the resources.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining me now, Elizabeth Wydra, president of the Constitutional Accountability Center, and Penny Nance, CEO and president of Concerned Women for America.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for coming here today. I know - I think it's fair to say you're united in the dignity of women and caring about them in a moment that they may not have foreseen would come into their lives. So, thank you for coming to have the conversation. Penny, I want to start here.\par \par \uc2 Many of the marchers I talked to said this is just the beginning, getting rid of Roe, and that's something that the White House finds alarming. This is what the president said in a proclamation this week about the Supreme Court overturning Roe. He said, never before has the court taken away a right so fundamental to Americans. In doing so, it put the health and lives of American - of women across the nation at risk.\par \par \uc2 So, opponents of your work, including the president, say you're actually hurting women by this work.\par \par \uc2 PENNY NANCE, CEO AND PRESIDENT, CONCERNED WOMEN FOR AMERICA: Well, let's remember the pro-life movement has always been led by women, starting with \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  and Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who in their paper, The Revolution, called it sadly child murder. You had women like Alice Paul who said, the - that abortion is the ultimate exploitation of women. And then now, the pro-life movement is led by women all over this country.\par \par \uc2 Concerned Women for America have worked to protect women and their children from the very beginning. We have marched to the Capitol. We have gone and advocated for our position. We've gone to the state capitals and women have gotten elected to advocate for this position because we believe that in the dignity and human life, there are two people, two lives at stake in this question. And we want to love them both.\par \par \uc2 There was a poll that just came down, a Marist poll, that said 90 percent of people want to protect the babies and their mothers. People recognize this is a lie. Roe wasn't settled because people knew in their hearts there was a baby at stake.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And so now we have all these state fights and we have different things that we talked about the administration has taken steps to make sure that women have more access.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  not only now allows abortion pills through the mail, but this headline too, that now retail pharmacies from corner drug stores to mayor chains like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  will be allowed to offer abortion pills in the U.S. under a regulatory change that was made by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . So, available through the mail. There's no prohibition on women traveling across state lines. So, is there a problem with access in 2023?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, PRESIDENT, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER: Yes, I mean there is a problem with access for sure. There was actually a problem with access even when Roe was on the book. But I think what we're seeing here is millions and millions of Americans, particularly women, woken up after Roe was overturned to this idea that many had taken for granted that a decision so fundamental, so personal, so life changing such as whether to terminate a pregnancy or to carry a pregnancy to term was now not going to be their own decision. That instead it could be made by politicians and not be made by the person themselves with their family, with their doctor, with their god, but instead could be determined by their state legislatures or their governors.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we've seen this wave of activism where people are saying in states, you know, not just super liberal states, we're also talking like Kentucky, Kansas, you know, states where, through ballot initiatives, people are saying, we want to ensure reproductive care access. We want to make sure that reproductive care, including abortion, is still available. And it is up to the person themselves to determine whether or not to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Both of you have mentioned the fact that this did go to the states and there were ballot initiatives. There were different places that we covered and talked about how people have voted on this. I remember, Penny, talking to you years ago, in 2016, when President Trump got into the primary. A lot of pro-life leaders were very worried about him. They weren't sure where he was going to be. You wound up calling him the most pro-life president in history.\par \par \uc2 Then after the midterms did not go as well as expected for the Republicans, he said this. It wasn't my fault that the Republicans didn't live up to expectations in the midterms. It was the abortion issue poorly handled by many Republicans that lost large numbers of voters.\par \par \uc2 So, what's your response to that accusation this issue actually hurt at the polls?\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Well, of course, you know, the pro-life movement is very grateful to President Trump. He appointed three constitutionalist justices that Concerned Women for America worked very hard to get confirmed. And I even called him after the overturn and thanked him for that. But, you know, we just basically disagree.\par \par \uc2 What we saw happen was Republicans listening to the consultant class, running away from the abortion issue, being told only to talk about inflation and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and being outspent 35 to one on this issue.\par \par \uc2 The other side spent $391 million on ads to Republicans pettily little $11 million in ads. The other side painted them as extremists when the real extreme opinion on this is the other side that says abortion should be legal, any time, any reason, any number, all paid for by the taxpayer. We know that is not the position of the American people.\par \par \uc2 This question has been returned to the American people. And we are happy to advocate for our position and go state by state. And that's the next step. That's the next phase of what Steve Scalise said at the march.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and - and there are things that are still happening here in - in -- both on Capitol Hill as well. And there's talk about a national ban or a national codification. But a couple things the House has already voted on, again in the control of the GOP now. They voted on a resolution to condemn attacks and violence on pregnancy centers and pro-life organizations. Only three Democrats out of more than 200 voted yes on that. There was also a bill that would have called for medical care for a baby that survived an attempted abortion. Only one Democrat voted for that.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, why vote no on those kinds of measures?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: You know, I haven't seen the particulars of those bills. You know, I think we should condemn violence on, you know, all activists who are, you know, people who are -- have been - there have been subjects -- threat subjects and danger to people who are trying to facilitate abortion care.\par \par \uc2 So - but I think, you know, one of the pieces of legislation that I find really interesting for this moment is actually the Respect for Marriage Act that passed with bipartisan support in the last Congress. And I think what that showed is that, with overturning of Roe, people woke up to the idea that other rights that they thought were deeply embedded in constitutional principles of equality and liberty, like the right to choose abortion for oneself, including marriage equality for LBGTQ Americans, interracial couples, they saw that those could be on the chopping block, too. And that's why this legislation that would protect the marriage equality that we have come to expect from our constitutional rights into law. So, I think we're seeing a great awakening of people recognizing that. If this Supreme Court isn't going to recognize the full spectrum of equality and liberty that was supposed to be guaranteed to us in the 14th Amendment particularly, that they are going to need to use other channels like the congressional enactments that I just talked about and like their ballot measures. You know, we, the people, are coming back to say, we need equal citizenship stature. We need to be able to come to the public square as people who can make fundamental decision about our own bodies, about our own lives, for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it sounds like, with Roe over -- the one thing you both agree on is that the issue is not over. Like, we're just going on to the next phase of it. So, we hope we can continue a conversation with you guys, and we thank you for coming to have it together today.\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Thanks for having us, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, coming up next, former President Trump may soon face opposition from a familiar face in his third White House bid.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what to expect as the Senate investigates the \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  meltdown left all those Swifties just hanging.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR: So, a lot has changed. And when I look at that, I look at the fact, if I'm this passionate and I'm this determined, why not me?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley appears to inch closer to saying she might challenge former President Trump, her old boss, for the 2024 Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, Francesca, I'm kind of itching. Like, let's get this thing going for '24.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": It's still early, Shannon. It's still early.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it? I feel like we're already there. Like, it never is over. We're always in the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, it's still January and I'm hearing from Republicans that they think that they have plenty of time to make decision around this. But the same thing from Democrats, we haven't seen President Biden get in the race either, and he is expected to run for re-election. And the fact that he directed his own 2024 primary schedule is seen as an indication that he is planning to run for re-election. And next week Democrats will be gathering together to talk about, however, what to do about the state of New Hampshire, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Given that Governor Sununu, the Republican governor of New Hampshire, is saying they're going to go first no matter what in 2024. They are going to have the first primary in the nation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: So, Democrats have to figure out what to do about that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Those headlines are a nightmare for him. Here are a few more of those headlines. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Democrats face obstacles and plan to reorder presidential primary calendar. You see there your piece in "USA Today," talking about the governor saying, no matter what we're going. "Politico" just sums it up this way, New York Dems put Biden on blast. I guess as the kids say.\par \par \uc2 Marie, what does this say, though, about the current president's power and ability to call the shots if these states are like, no, no, not so fast?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, first, I married into a New Hampshire family, so I have to be Very careful on this one.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I know, this is -- this is tricky.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think Democrats realize -- have realized for some time that the primary calendar needs to better represent the country, be more diverse, better represent the electorate. For those of us who were on the ground in Iowa during the last cycle, we know that that caucus wasn't run well for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HARF: We knew there had to be changes.\par \par \uc2 I -- I love the fact that New Hampshire voters are - are really invested in the process early. They get a lot of face time with candidates. It's a small state. They get to really ask them questions, probe their different policies. So, I want New Hampshire to still be near the front of the calendar. It's in their state law, actually, that they are first in the nation.\par \par \uc2 But states like Nevada, states like South Carolina, I think those better represent the full picture of the electorate. And Democrats are trying to figure out how to do both things at once. The New Hampshire conversation is going to be a very interesting one. And I - you know, with my in-laws, I have to just say they should always be first.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, I don't know what else you can do with the in-laws. You got to - you want to keep the peace, but -\par \par \uc2 HARF: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes, in-laws are a tough thing to navigate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But - but this - but this does say, you know, that the president is, obviously, setting things up that worked well, like South Carolina. That was a turning point for him.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That actually, you know, probably propelled him to the presidency. But it doesn't sound like these states want to go along yet.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: No, I mean, I - look, I think New Hampshire had the audacity to vote Biden fifth in 2020, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not a good finish for him there.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I mean we forget it wasn't just bad, it was real bad in terms of his performance in 2020. So, yes, I'm not surprised that they're going to try to reorder things. He turned things around in South Carolina. Clearly they would like that to lead the way.\par \par \uc2 But, again, there's a rich history in New Hampshire that is going to be resistant right to the very end, as you said, in state law. Not much they can do anyway.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, Shannon, this feels like people trying to rig the game. I get inclusivity. They're the first in the nation. New Hampshire is it. It demands retail politics. As you know, Francesca, we've been on the ground there. They demand a certain something from candidates, one on one, and they talk back to candidates.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So it's a great training ground. And, I'm sorry, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  feels he's not quite up to that task, then he shouldn't run for president. Go through the blades of New Hampshire. If you can survive that, then you get to reach South Carolina and the rest of the primaries. I'm sorry, changing the rules early because you don't like the way the game might play out, Shannon, it's a bad look for the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, how many times have we all been in Pizza Kitchen in Iowa. If you're out there, Iowa, you know what I'm talking about. And these people are like, oh, yes, I've talked to them three times each, the candidates. I mean they are - they really engage with you and they want to know you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, now, Marie, you're back because we have a very important senate hearing to talk about. You were here last time we talked about this, the T. Swift meltdown over getting tickets.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  is going to chair this subcommittee hearing this week about what happened. She says, the issues within America's ticket industry were made painfully obvious when \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 's website failed hundreds of thousands of fans hoping to purchase tickets for Taylor Swift's new tour.\par \par \uc2 I did reach out to a couple of the senators to say, like, is T. Swift showing up, and I got crickets. So, I'm guessing that's a no.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Don't we wish? Oh, what a day on Capitol Hill that would be. \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  going for the T. Swift fan base here if she's going to run for president again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, smart move.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Look, it is - it is bigger than Taylor Swift, though. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is a monopoly. It has been problematic with every huge release they've had of tickets. I tried to get Garth Brooks tickets for - for Vegas. Couldn't do that. So, I think it's been long overdue, quite frankly, for someone to have some regulatory action here and investigation because America -- this is something that Americans care about, right? This is something that they don't always pay attention to the debt ceiling or, you know, any of these topics we talk about, which are important, I wish they did, but at the end of the day if you say, what drives you crazy, a loft them will say \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And we talked about this during the commercial break about how, I don't even try to go to the initial marketplace for whatever the ticket is, you just automatically are thrown to the secondary market because it's almost impossible to get them.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And who knew, Marie, that it only took Taylor Swift to bring Republicans and Democrats together in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I love it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Look, the White House says that they don't see much more that the president can do on this particular issue, but administration officials tell me that they do expect additional action on junk fees, as they're called. Those are the service fees that, in this case, you pay on - on going to an event like this. That's something that \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  and the senators who are investigating in this are also looking into. And also, to your point, certainly something Americans are paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Who thought we could find a website worse than \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 ? \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But here - here's the rub. Remember, \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  and \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  are merged.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Live Nation controls 200 venues, 400 artists.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The venues. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So they're policing and running the game in the front of the house and the back of the house. That demands scrutiny. I think people on both sides of the aisle would agree to that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, staying in our Hollywood lane, which we really do here on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Word this week that Alec Baldwin is going to be facing involuntary manslaughter charges. He has denied all along that he pulled the trigger, that he's responsible. Prosecutors feel otherwise and they have charged him.\par \par \uc2 "The Federalist" has this headline, Alec Baldwin baffled that he, not the gun, will be charged with manslaughter. According to Baldwin, everyone and everything are to blame for the shooting except for himself.\par \par \uc2 Josh, this -- this has all kinds of currents to it. We have a woman, Halyna Hutchins, who lost her life here and yet it's a very prominent case involving a very prominent celebrity.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes. No, I mean, tragedy, obviously, with the loss of life.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think Alec Baldwin probably would not be in this amount of trouble had he just kept his mouth shut. I mean it's just the perfect example of a celebrity going out, trying to justify themselves in the court of public opinion, not knowing that ultimately down the road everything that he said in all these interviews with Stephanopoulos and everything else is used and is admissible to the ultimate criminal charges that are up against him. That's his problem. I think that's why he's in this hot water.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Shannon, I spoke to a number of action -- guy who have led action franchises. When they say cold gun on a set and the armorer brings you that gun, you expect there is nothing in that chamber. That's what he was assured. I don't think you can hold the actor culpable here.\par \par \uc2 But the one charge of manslaughter might apply to him as a producers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Because the protocols on this set were shotty. The armorer and the prop person were the same. Shouldn't have happened. No live rounds should have been there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will follow that case.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much, panel. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, saying good-bye to a beloved member of our family here at Fox.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have some difficult news to share this morning. A treasured member of the Fox News family passed away this week. Alan Komissaroff died following a heart attack. He was our senior vice president of news and politics. For decades Alan was a guiding force here.\par \par \uc2 He started as a writer when Fox News Channel launched back in 1996. Alan then worked his way up through the ranks. A diligent, perfectionist who became indispensable. Alan was part of every major news story and every election night for decades, meaning he had a hand in how Fox covered the biggest stories that affected your world.\par \par \uc2 Alan, who I will always remember with a smile on his face, was just 47 years old. He is survived by his loving wife Rachel, who was his high school sweetheart and prom date, and their two children, 17-year-old son Ben and 13-year-old daughter Olivia. Alan was so very proud of them.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague, Bret Baier, said this week, Alan made all of us better. He will be remembered in a service today in East Brunswick, New Jersey. We are wishing comfort to his family and his friend and to everyone who is feeling this loss.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020230122ej1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 March for Life in D.C.; Penny Nance and Elizabeth Wydra are Interviewed about Abortion Rights; Haley Hits at 2024 Run; DNC Proposed Primary Changes; \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  Hearings on Capitol Hill; Baldwin Charged in Shooting. Interview with House Problem Solvers Caucus, Democratic Congressman of New Jersey \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  and Republican Congressman of Pennsylvania \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Marie Harf, Raymond Arroyo, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A new round of classified items found in the president's home and new concerns about financial fallouts as the U.S. hits the debt limit again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they want to play a game of chicken, we'll play a game of chicken.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): We've had these games before and it should not be done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The Treasury Department now buying time with extraordinary measures as Republicans bicker with the White House.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president has been clear on this. It should not be used as a political weapon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Swing district, moderate Republicans are calling for the president to drop the take it or leave it approach and come to the table.\par \par \uc2 We'll sit down for a bipartisan conversation with two co-chairs from the Problem Solvers Caucus. Republican \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  and Democrat \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  join me to talk about how to find consensus on the debt limit, immigration and more. It's a "FOX News Sunday" exclusive.\par \par \uc2 Then -- thousands of pro-life advocates come to the nation's capital for the first March for Life since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. We'll look at the legal state of play now that abortion laws are up to the states, and sit down for a conversation with prominent voices from both sides.\par \par \uc2 And eight months after the unprecedented leak of a draft Supreme Court ruling, there are still no answers from the high court about the leaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): The only way you're going to stop this in the future is to make sure you find out who did it and hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel if we will ever find out who did it.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, at least ten people are dead, another ten injured after a mass shooting near Los Angeles. It happened late last night at a dance club in Monterey Park, California, close to where a lunar New York celebration had been taking place.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say they believe the shooter is male and at this time it appears that person is not in custody. No word yet on a motive.\par \par \uc2 Deputies say they are reviewing security video in that area. Monterey Park is about ten miles east of Los Angeles. We'll keep you updated on any developments we get in from there.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking this morning, the Justice Department seized more classified documents from the president's private residence just this week. The news comes as President Biden prepares to speak in person with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to discuss the new Congress, a range of challenges there, where they disagree. And that, of course, includes the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Congress is facing a deadline to strike a deal or risk a financial crisis as the Treasury department steps in to avoid a government default.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will chat with two moderate lawmakers, Republican Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  of Pennsylvania, and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  of New Jersey. They are trying to press their way into the most polarized policy debates and find solutions.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson outside the White House where there are multiple developing stories this morning.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, the president is facing renewed criticism after more classified documents were found in his Wilmington, Delaware, home. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducted the search.\par \par \uc2 The president also facing a debt ceiling fight amid reports his top aide is leaving. White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  could step down in the coming weeks, the biggest administration departure since President Biden took office two years ago. The move was expected. Klain has been at Biden's side through the legislative triumphs of his first two years and through his Supreme Court confirmation, but also through major controversies, like the disastrous U.S. departure from Afghanistan. Klain may be best known for helping the president pass massive spending bills like the $1.9 trillion COVID relief plan, the $1 trillion infrastructure bill and the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the spending helped accelerate the current inflation crisis and added to the national debt. In 2000, the debt was just over $5 trillion. It's climbed to over $31 trillion today. It's money already spent and now the U.S. is over its borrowing limit which could put the nation's credit rating at risk.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT: To threaten default, to threaten economic catastrophe, nobody, Democrat or Republican, should do that no matter how much they believe in their agenda.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Republicans say they want spending cuts if they're going to raise the debt limit higher. The White House says raise the limit period. No negotiations.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It is a basic responsibility that Congress has to deal with the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: President Biden plans to huddle with top Democratic leaders at the White House Tuesday. He also plans to host Kevin McCarthy, their first meeting since McCarthy became House speaker.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy responding, President Biden, I accept your invitation to sit down and discuss a responsible debt ceiling increase to address irresponsible government spending.\par \par \uc2 A White House official spinning this differently, calling it a, quote, general meeting, not a negotiation.\par \par \uc2 All this while the president faces continued discussions over his handling of classified documents. The new house majority leader tells FOX --\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA), HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: The rules of justice should apply to everybody equally. In a case like this, it's one more example that they're not being applied equally to Democrats as they are to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Now two Democratic Senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Tim Kaine are also calling for a full investigation. They're both up for re- election in 2024 -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House Lucas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, co-chairs of the House Problem Solvers Caucus, Democratic congressman of New Jersey, \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2 , and Republican congressman of Pennsylvania, \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressmen, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2  (D-NJ): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's start here. The headlines overnight are not the ones the White House wants to see. Yet another discovery of classified material at the president's Wilmington home.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley, well known to our audience and a legal professional, a professor and constitutional scholar, tweets this: With the latest discovery, there's no real question that the Biden documents were grossly mishandled. There only remains who was responsible. However, the discovery of documents at yet another location used by the president is crushing for his defense team.\par \par \uc2 He says he's done everything right, he has no regrets. Congressman Gottheimer, should he, at this point?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, I think we've seen the Justice Department is conducting an on going investigation. That's the proper process here. And you want to get to the bottom of these things, which is why they were at his home yesterday in full cooperation.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the -- what you need to do here. The White House needs to cooperate with the Justice Department. That's what they've been doing for months. Obviously, that's in stark comparison to the other investigation of classified documents with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I think as long as the White House does what it should do, which is cooperate fully so we can get to the bottom of this, I think that's the appropriate process. And, of course, in a broader sense, we should understand in any White House, whether it's the last White House or this one, how any documents would ever get out. And the key is to make sure that never happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, also in contrast is the fact that there have been on going negotiations with this Justice Department, with the Biden White House and legal team. And there was an agreement for them to be there together Friday during the search, but what we found is leading to more questions than answers for a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy, former federal prosecutor, tweeted this out: It's not six classified documents, this latest discovery. Awkwardly worded statement by Biden team, i.e., spin is favorable to Biden as possible, were six items consisting of documents with classified markings and we don't know what they meant by item. Box, envelope or how many classified documents in each item.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a special counsel investigations already under the way -- under way. You heard Lucas Tomlinson say, maybe some Democratic senators are also interested. House oversight has already said that it's going to investigate.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Fitzpatrick, any word you have that the GOP is going to overreach on this or look like they're politicizing it?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: I don't believe so, Shannon. Two things are true. Number one, classified documents can never be taken out of the SCIF ever. I'm both a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent and I'm currently on the House Intelligence Committee. That's number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we need equal application of the law. And that's what we're going to make sure that we ensure. We're going to do a deep dive into the circumstances surrounding the prior administration and the current administration, both dealing with classified documents and making sure the law is applied equally across the board. That's what the American people are demanding.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So the Wall Street Journal had reported on conversations between the two teams, and the Biden side, and the DOJ side about whether or not \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents or others should be doing these searches now that we're on umber five.\par \par \uc2 And up into the point on Friday, they had agreed to let the Biden team handle it. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " also has this, that the White House was hoping to keep this quiet. The White House was hoping for a speedy inquiry, planning to disclose this matter only after Justice issued its all clear.\par \par \uc2 But the approach would end up prompting accusations that the Biden's team had purposely kept the public in the dark.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gottheimer, we know all of this started before the midterms. Do you get the perception that some people have that there are differences in the way the DOJ handles Republican and Democrat cases?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: I mean, there's a huge difference in the fact, if you look at the last administration, the Trump administration, what happened in Mar-a- Lago, there was a refusal to cooperate for nearly a year, right? There was obstruction. You needed subpoenas. President Trump didn't come forward and offer up his documents. He was keeping them locked up.\par \par \uc2 And this administration, I believe, has cooperated in a constructive way with the Justice Department from the beginning. They didn't take months. They didn't stall. The president wasn't trying to hold up these documents as trophies.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's a huge difference. But the bottom line is this, and Brian is exactly right. We need to have a full cooperation investigation. We need to get to the bottom of what happened, why and when, to understand everything.\par \par \uc2 And in a broader sense, to make sure that no White House, no administration is ever able to handle classified documents this way and take them out of the White House from a classified setting. And I think, you know, we should get to the bottom of both, and I think we should run a reasonable process. And that's what's happening right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you as members of Congress may have more of a hand in that in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 There's this, you know, piece again -- Andy McCarthy speaking out on this saying after the White House has said there are no logs there, this is the personal home, where in the past they said the president does work from there. There are questions about whether there are logs of visitors or people who would have been there.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy writes this: I'm very confident that if the agency, meaning the Secret Service, believed it was in the interest of the president's security that the information would be produced for the bureau would be produced at warp speed.\par \par \uc2 We've got at least one source saying to us the Secret Service is prepared to come forward with some information about visitors there but Congress is going to have to ask.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Fitzpatrick, will you do that?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: We will, Shannon. There's multiple ways to gather evidence and conduct investigations. Obviously, visitors log would be very helpful. If that doesn't exist, you go to other forms of evidence, video surveillance, physical surveillance, witness interviews. There's a whole host of ways to collect evidence.\par \par \uc2 So, I have no doubt that certainly we're going to make sure of this, that the bureau does their job investigating this case. And again, they got to apply the law equally. That's very, very important for people in America to have faith and confidence in our justice system.\par \par \uc2 It's a system I have worked in. It's very important for me that they do things the right way so that the American public has confidence in it. And that requires equal application of the law, consistent standards on investigations. That's what we're going to make sure happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. You all are trying to make sure something else happen, which is that we don't default on our credit ratings. And you're negotiating and talking actively about the debt ceiling in a deal that could possibly come together.\par \par \uc2 The White House says no negotiations at all. We know that's not practical given some of the demands that House GOP members that spending cuts and other things be included in order to get their vote.\par \par \uc2 Our Edward Lawrence over at FOX Business has been reporting, Congressman Fitzpatrick, you've been working on something that would be a debt to GDP ratio, potentially. If you hit a certain number, then automatic cuts kick in.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us any sense of where those negotiations are? What would be the ratio? How in the world do you decide whose programs automatically get cut in that scenario?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yeah. Well, first thing that has to happen, Shannon, Speaker McCarthy has made several overtures to the White House. He wants to sit down and work a reasonable solution out.\par \par \uc2 I hope the White House accepts his offer. In fact, it was White House that offered Speaker McCarthy the opportunity to come in. I hope that happens. And the White House's position cannot --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, but they say it's not going to be a negotiation over the debt ceiling. They say that is not what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: That's a -- that's a problem. That's a problem and that's not leadership. When you have a divided government, four-vote Republican margin in the House, a one-vote Democratic margin in the Senate, divided chambers, you have to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 That's what the American people elected us to do, is to work this out. So nobody should be taking the position that we're not going to negotiate. That's very irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 Pertaining to your question, Shannon, one thing that we're just going to offer up as a possible bridge-building solution is to go right at the 1917 law itself that established the debt limit. It established it as a number, a numerical dollar amount, which doesn't make any sense. We think that more practically speaking, it should be a debt to GDP ratio.\par \par \uc2 In 2008, our debt to GDP was about 40 percent. In 2018, it was about 70 percent. It's now at 125 percent. That's not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 So, when you have a child that has a spending problem, you do two things. You pay their bills, and take away their credit card. You don't do one, you do both.\par \par \uc2 That's what has to happen here. So what we're going to propose is whatever negotiated amount we can agree on on a debt to GDP ratio and have a cure period there after. If that cure does not happen, certain budgetary reforms automatically kick in on the discretionary side.\par \par \uc2 So, we're still putting the meat on those bones. Me and Josh are going to work through our proposed solution. But the first thing that's got to happen, Shannon, the president has to sit down with Speaker McCarthy. Speaker McCarthy offered to negotiate in good faith. I hope President Biden does as him (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is quoting a senior Democrat over in Congress that says he had a conversation with \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , White House chief of staff who may soon be leaving. But he essentially said, no holds barred, there will be no negotiations. You've got to make it look like we're the responsible ones and that the Republicans are essentially irresponsible, trying to kill entitlements, and those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gottheimer, can you work with that? If that's the strategy, if that's the theory, how do you move the ball at all?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, I've had conversations with the White House just this weekend. And I'm optimistic that they will sit down as this White House always has. That's why we were able to accomplish so much in a bipartisan way last Congress. It takes constructive conversation.\par \par \uc2 I think there's things reasonable on the table and things that are unreasonable. Gutting Social Security and Medicare is obviously on the unreasonable side.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which the speaker has said is not on the table.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Just to be clear, the speaker has said that those -- that those -- that that's a talking point and that Republicans are not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: By the way, that's great, because I don't think it should be on the table. Things like 30 percent sales tax should not be on the table.\par \par \uc2 But there are plenty of things that should be. And, you know, Brian and I and the Problem Solvers Caucus are obviously having discussions. I think the White House, you see Kevin McCarthy and the president will be sitting down, the speaker and the president will be sitting down. I think that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 What we can't do is put the full faith in the credit of United States of America at risk. We can't put people's 401(k)s at risk. We need to have these constructive conversations, make sure that we raise the debt limit responsibly so that people can have faith in our country and our currency. And we can also talk about our fiscal health and do that in a responsible way.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to do both. And I'm optimistic that everyone will sit down. We'll work this out, because we have no other choice. We have to work this out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Quickly, Congressman Fitzpatrick, with that in mind and the potential departure of \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , knowing what his strategy reportedly is, do you think his departure changes the way that you are able to work with this administration?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: I don't think so. Again, we're going to let our speaker take the lead on this with his negotiations and then Josh and I are going to offer our solution that hopefully can be constructive. But I don't believe it's going to affect our relationship with the White House, Shannon. We've had a constructive relationship with them.\par \par \uc2 That's our job as representatives, no matter what party we're from, is to do our part to make government work. And we've worked well with a lot of people over there and I expect that to continue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the American people wish you well in trying to find solutions at the most treacherous problems we have now.\par \par \uc2 Congressmen, thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: We'll get it done.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: You bet. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the president's downplaying concerns about how he handled classified materials from years ago as yet another batch is found at his Wilmington home. And a special counsel is ramping up to investigate. We're going to bring in our Sunday group to discuss all this, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think they're going to find, there's nothing there. I have regrets on following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do. That's exactly what we're doing. There's no there there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden brushing off concerns after weeks of questions about how he handled the discovery and disclosure of classified documents from his time in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. Now we understand maybe as far back to his Senate career.\par \par \uc2 It is time four Sunday group. White House correspondent Francesca Chambers; former State Department spokesperson, Marie Harf; former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Josh Holmes; and FOX News analyst Raymond Arroyo.\par \par \uc2 Panel, welcome.\par \par \uc2 If only there was something to discuss today.\par \par \uc2 Okay. So he says there's no there there, but apparently there's another batch of there there. Given this discovery, the fifth batch on Friday, as DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents were there. Jonathan Turley writes this about the personal's statements. He shrugged off the matter like finding a borrowed hammer in his garage. Since the standard is gross mishandling of classified evidence, the last thing you want to do is give a grossly mishandling answer.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO SENATOR MCCONNELL: I think he's got deep regrets, right? Especially coming outs so forcefully saying this whole thing is over, you're not going to find anything more, we're done here with only a handful of documents and nothing to see here. Every day, seems like everywhere he's had a cup of coffee he's got documents behind him. And now, DOJ is finding all this.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest issue as we turn forward with it is the access piece to it, right? We know what the Mar-a-Lago raid, were all in one room and the access was fairly limited. It's in his garage, right? In certain office space. We don't know who has access to this stuff. I think that's the biggest question going forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion piece said this, oh, Biden, what have you done. It's hard to exaggerate the level of exasperation with him for squandering a huge political advantage on the Mar-a-Lago story and for muddying what may have been the best chance to convict Mr. Trump on federal charges. Marie?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Look, the reason we know there were more documents found is that Biden's personal attorneys invited the department of justice in to look for them. A couple were found, I think six last night. So they are fully cooperating.\par \par \uc2 The Biden team will tell you, we are fully cooperating, we are inviting them in. That is in stark contrast to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who repeatedly lied, he and his attorneys did, to the federal government. He claimed they were his. He claimed he declassified them.\par \par \uc2 We have no idea who had access to them at Mar-a-Lago. We know there have been security concerns there.\par \par \uc2 So, would I prefer not to be talking about this on "FOX News Sunday" as a Democrat? Of course, I would. But I think attend of the day the distinction of how the Biden team has handled them, inviting DOJ and fully cooperating, letting them look into whatever they can, compared to the Trump team which is at every single turn really pushed back, even lied to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and to the Archives. I think at the end of the day that distinction will be quite clear to people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Raymond, some people point to the distinction and the reporting in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and "Wall Street Journal" that there were conversations with the Biden team an DOJ, oh, no, we're going to let you investigate it. And now, we're going to let you come in. And so, those conversations make people at least have the perception that there is a different standard.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS ANALYST: Well, there seems to be a different standard here because, look, you have private and personal lawyers going in and looking and discovering these caches. Did they have security clearance? Which we don't have full clarity on yet.\par \par \uc2 But, look, Shannon, years ago. I remember seeing Dean Martin. He'd walk out on stage and say, how did all these people get in my room? That's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  every time he finds a document. How did this document get here?\par \par \uc2 Well, it didn't walk in. You placed it there or someone near you did. We need to know why and how. When you look at the Penn Biden Center, that first discovery is particularly disturbing because the classified documents were mingled in among personal files. They were even in envelopes marked personal files. Why? Who put them there?\par \par \uc2 And when you overlay the timetable of the documents discovered and what was happening in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's life, they do track with the Hunter \uc2 Burisma\uc2  deal, the Iran nuke deal, the Chinese natural gas deal that we've discovered, or has come to light, via Hunter's lap top.\par \par \uc2 So, tons of questions that need exploration here. But I'm not quite certain, Shannon, we've discovered the last Biden classified document just yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we have been told that we think this is it. They think they're wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, it blew up your story and deadline last night when there was another discovery.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, USA TODAY WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And House Oversight Republicans are trying to get their hands on the documents that were found. We don't know exactly what is in those documents yet. They gave the White House this week to answer questions about this. They say they've heard crickets so far from the White House, despite the public responses.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, White House administration officials telling me they're still deciding how they want to respond to these request for communications.\par \par \uc2 But as far as President Biden goes, the revelation that some of those documents included documents, we don't know how many, dating back to his time in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , is at odds with the statements from the White House that he has handled the classified information in an appropriate manner and takes it very seriously, so to speak, bringing new scrutiny on that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. It puts the headline back on the document situation. Other than what is the strategy on the debt ceiling as to saying the Republicans are crazy and they're going to crash the economy.\par \par \uc2 Josh, "The Hill" has this headline, GOP risks overplaying their hand on the debt ceiling. They're pressing for big spending cuts but there's a real chance the GOP could end up blamed for cascading negative effects if the U.S. even approaches a scenario where it defaults on its financial obligation. How much danger for them there?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, I think everybody has to get on the same page from the very beginning. There is going to be a negotiation, right? You've got Democrats basically at this point saying we're going to have a clean debt ceiling. That's about it.\par \par \uc2 There's zero chance that that happens. There's going to be a discussion between Speaker McCarthy and the administration. I assume the Senate will be part of that discussion.\par \par \uc2 It's not unprecedented. I remember sitting there in 2011 when we were doing this with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with the Budget Control Act.\par \par \uc2 So, this is something that has lot of history, lot of precedent. What U.S. Republicans need to do is figure out what ignites them to get to question, because that's the other piece that has to happen. It's not a negotiable outcome. The outcome has to be there. But they've got to get as far down the road on the conservative side with spending cuts and the like to get that conference together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Marie, we know \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is for the hard ball plan on this. That's the public line the administration continues to hold. Do you think his departure will change the way the White House operates in these kinds of non-negotiation negotiations?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Look, I don't think their strategy is going to change necessarily. Republicans in Congress voted three times to raise the debt ceiling under President Trump, not demanding any of the kinds of spending cuts they're demanding now. So, Republicans find fiscal austerity generally when a Democrat is in the White House.\par \par \uc2 But the case that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  or any other senior adviser to Biden will tell you is under President Biden, record unemployment, 11 million jobs created, record numbers of small business applications being put in in the last two years. Contrast that with Republicans taking over Congress. The first thing they do, cut Medicare and Social Security, default on our obligations, get our credit rating downgraded.\par \par \uc2 I think the Biden White House feels very confident that if you take those two messages to the American people, they're going to come out on the winning end of that policy wise economically but also politically, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, yeah, there's the political and optics side of that. Very quickly before we go, I want to get your comment on the fact that the Supreme Court has been investigating for quite sometime the May leak last year. We have no answer about who it is.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and the question is why don't we have those answers? I mean, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should have been brought in, Shannon. As you know, the investigator here was the marshal of the court. They don't have the investigation power to seize records and to obligate some of those clerks who have already left.\par \par \uc2 As you were talking about earlier this morning, many of these clerks have already left. Their tenure ended, they moved on. We didn't get testimony for some of them and records for some of them.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think we'll ever know who this leaker is. Too much time has passed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Amen.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But we should know. These justices and their families, I can tell you, they are very worried and the threats have not stopped since that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Demonstrations do continue at many of their homes on a weekly basis.\par \par \uc2 OK. Francesca, you'll kick us off next time.\par \par \uc2 Panel, up next, March for Life returned to Washington this week celebrating the end of Roe v. Wade. But legal battles continue to sprawl all across the states in the debate over access to abortion. We sit down with prominent voices from the pro-choice and pro-life movements here together for a conversation on one of the nation's most contentious issues.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In 1973, the Supreme Court ruled in Roe v. Wade that the right to privacy implied in the 14th Amendment meant women had a right to abortion. That kicked off one of the most hard fought political and legal battles of the past half century.\par \par \uc2 Then last summer, after a leaked ruling indicated it would, the high court did indeed overturn Roe. But instead of settling the issue, it shifted the battle to new fronts. This morning we will sit down with two of the most prominent advocates in both the pro-life and pro-choice movements to discuss the new paradigm.\par \par \uc2 But first, a look at the surge in advocacy on both sides seven months after tend of Roe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): It was nearly a party atmosphere outside the Supreme Court Friday as pro-life demonstrators marched their 50th March for Life.\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH (R), MISSISSIPPI ATTORNEY GENERAL: This year is different. We have overturned Roe v. Wade with the Mississippi Dobbs case. We have done it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The march started in the 1970s as a protest against Roe v. Wade. This year, it had a victory lap feel as marchers celebrated the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We just won a huge case in the Supreme Court. And that's - that's incredible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Did you ever think that you would see this day where Roe was no longer the law of the land?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's incredible. We hoped. We prayed. You know, there's always hope. But I think nobody could believe it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe that the culture is moving in our direction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that's been what's really cool for me is to like see that we're not the only ones. There's so many people here. And people are coming with so much joy and so much hope.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But even as the pro-life movement celebrates seven months since Roe was overturned by the Dobbs decision, the legal rules around abortion are more unsettled than ever. Now the abortion question is up to states.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR) (June 26, 2022): This is a day that those in the pro-life movement has worked for, for over 40 years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: About a dozen states banned most or all abortions immediately. Many saying those bans saved lives. But many states did the reverse, passing laws to ensure women had greater abortion access.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): California, Oregon and Washington are building the west coast offense to protect patients' access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Voters also weighed in as midterm ballots saw a record number of abortion questions. In Kansas, voters turned out in huge numbers rejecting a law that would have banned access. The margin, 59 percent to 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 GOV. LAURA KELLY (D-KS): And an overwhelming majority of Kansans believe that a woman's right to make private medical decisions should rest with her and not with politicians.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fundraising for both sides exploded after Kansas, with statewide races seen as critical. A Wisconsin judicial race is getting headlines now because an existing but unenforced abortion ban from 1849 could reach the state's highest court.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration ramped up federal protections with an executive order preemptively pushing back on limits to obtaining federally approved abortion medication, or on women traveling to states where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 And the legal battles continue to unfold. South Carolina's State Supreme Court struck down a six week abortion ban in early January. Pro-choice advocates are pressing Congress to pass the original Roe precedent into law.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (October 2022): I believe Congress should codify Roe once and for all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: By contrast, some congressional Republicans want a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If Republicans get their way with a national ban, I'll veto it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The president warned before the midterms the end of Roe would cost Republicans politically.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think you're going to hear women roar on this issue, and it's going to be consequential.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fox News voter analysis in November found 62 percent favored a national law guaranteeing access to abortion, 38 percent opposed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): This year's rally was different than marches in the past, going instead between the U.S. Capitol and the Supreme Court, as organizers say they're shifting their focus from the legal battle at the high court to the political one over on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But marchers also acknowledge a federal ban may never happen. And, in the meantime, their movement is under pressure to help mothers and children after pregnancy. Something they say they've been doing for decades and have ramped up in the wake of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And there will be children that come into this world that these women don't feel prepared for or it doesn't fit in their life. So, what do you say to them post Roe?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, we definitely have to step up our game with the whole adoption system and then supporting women who are in these situations where they don't have the money or the resources to have these children. We can't tell the world that we want women to have children and then not give them the resources.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining me now, Elizabeth Wydra, president of the Constitutional Accountability Center, and Penny Nance, CEO and president of Concerned Women for America.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for coming here today. I know - I think it's fair to say you're united in the dignity of women and caring about them in a moment that they may not have foreseen would come into their lives. So, thank you for coming to have the conversation.\par \par \uc2 Penny, I want to start here.\par \par \uc2 Many of the marchers I talked to said this is just the beginning, getting rid of Roe, and that's something that the White House finds alarming. This is what the president said in a proclamation this week about the Supreme Court overturning Roe. He said, never before has the court taken away a right so fundamental to Americans. In doing so, it put the health and lives of American - of women across the nation at risk.\par \par \uc2 So, opponents of your work, including the president, say you're actually hurting women by this work.\par \par \uc2 PENNY NANCE, CEO AND PRESIDENT, CONCERNED WOMEN FOR AMERICA: Well, let's remember the pro-life movement has always been led by women, starting with \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  and Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who in their paper, The Revolution, called it sadly child murder. You had women like Alice Paul who said, the - that abortion is the ultimate exploitation of women. And then now, the pro-life movement is led by women all over this country.\par \par \uc2 Concerned Women for America have worked to protect women and their children from the very beginning. We have marched to the Capitol. We have gone and advocated for our position. We've gone to the state capitals and women have gotten elected to advocate for this position because we believe that in the dignity and human life, there are two people, two lives at stake in this question. And we want to love them both.\par \par \uc2 There was a poll that just came down, a Marist poll, that said 90 percent of people want to protect the babies and their mothers. People recognize this is a lie. Roe wasn't settled because people knew in their hearts there was a baby at stake.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And so now we have all these state fights and we have different things that we talked about the administration has taken steps to make sure that women have more access.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  not only now allows abortion pills through the mail, but this headline too, that now retail pharmacies from corner drug stores to mayor chains like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  will be allowed to offer abortion pills in the U.S. under a regulatory change that was made by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . So, available through the mail. There's no prohibition on women traveling across state lines. So, is there a problem with access in 2023?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, PRESIDENT, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER: Yes, I mean there is a problem with access for sure. There was actually a problem with access even when Roe was on the book. But I think what we're seeing here is millions and millions of Americans, particularly women, woken up after Roe was overturned to this idea that many had taken for granted that a decision so fundamental, so personal, so life changing such as whether to terminate a pregnancy or to carry a pregnancy to term was now not going to be their own decision. That instead it could be made by politicians and not be made by the person themselves with their family, with their doctor, with their god, but instead could be determined by their state legislatures or their governors.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we've seen this wave of activism where people are saying in states, you know, not just super liberal states, we're also talking like Kentucky, Kansas, you know, states where, through ballot initiatives, people are saying, we want to ensure reproductive care access. We want to make sure that reproductive care, including abortion, is still available. And it is up to the person themselves to determine whether or not to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Both of you have mentioned the fact that this did go to the states and there were ballot initiatives. There were different places that we covered and talked about how people have voted on this. I remember, Penny, talking to you years ago, in 2016, when President Trump got into the primary. A lot of pro-life leaders were very worried about him. They weren't sure where he was going to be. You wound up calling him the most pro-life president in history.\par \par \uc2 Then after the midterms did not go as well as expected for the Republicans, he said this. It wasn't my fault that the Republicans didn't live up to expectations in the midterms. It was the abortion issue poorly handled by many Republicans that lost large numbers of voters.\par \par \uc2 So, what's your response to that accusation this issue actually hurt at the polls?\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Well, of course, you know, the pro-life movement is very grateful to President Trump. He appointed three constitutionalist justices that Concerned Women for America worked very hard to get confirmed. And I even called him after the overturn and thanked him for that. But, you know, we just basically disagree.\par \par \uc2 What we saw happen was Republicans listening to the consultant class, running away from the abortion issue, being told only to talk about inflation and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and being outspent 35 to one on this issue.\par \par \uc2 The other side spent $391 million on ads to Republicans pettily little $11 million in ads. The other side painted them as extremists when the real extreme opinion on this is the other side that says abortion should be legal, any time, any reason, any number, all paid for by the taxpayer. We know that is not the position of the American people.\par \par \uc2 This question has been returned to the American people. And we are happy to advocate for our position and go state by state. And that's the next step. That's the next phase of what Steve Scalise said at the march.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and - and there are things that are still happening here in - in -- both on Capitol Hill as well. And there's talk about a national ban or a national codification. But a couple things the House has already voted on, again in the control of the GOP now. They voted on a resolution to condemn attacks and violence on pregnancy centers and pro-life organizations. Only three Democrats out of more than 200 voted yes on that. There was also a bill that would have called for medical care for a baby that survived an attempted abortion. Only one Democrat voted for that.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, why vote no on those kinds of measures?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: You know, I haven't seen the particulars of those bills. You know, I think we should condemn violence on, you know, all activists who are, you know, people who are -- have been - there have been subjects -- threat subjects and danger to people who are trying to facilitate abortion care.\par \par \uc2 So - but I think, you know, one of the pieces of legislation that I find really interesting for this moment is actually the Respect for Marriage Act that passed with bipartisan support in the last Congress. And I think what that showed is that, with overturning of Roe, people woke up to the idea that other rights that they thought were deeply embedded in constitutional principles of equality and liberty, like the right to choose abortion for oneself, including marriage equality for LBGTQ Americans, interracial couples, they saw that those could be on the chopping block, too. And that's why this legislation that would protect the marriage equality that we have come to expect from our constitutional rights into law. So, I think we're seeing a great awakening of people recognizing that. If this Supreme Court isn't going to recognize the full spectrum of equality and liberty that was supposed to be guaranteed to us in the 14th Amendment particularly, that they are going to need to use other channels like the congressional enactments that I just talked about and like their ballot measures. You know, we, the people, are coming back to say, we need equal citizenship stature. We need to be able to come to the public square as people who can make fundamental decision about our own bodies, about our own lives, for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it sounds like, with Roe over -- the one thing you both agree on is that the issue is not over. Like, we're just going on to the next phase of it. So, we hope we can continue a conversation with you guys, and we thank you for coming to have it together today.\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Thanks for having us, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 NANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, coming up next, former President Trump may soon face opposition from a familiar face in his third White House bid.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what to expect as the Senate investigates the \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  meltdown left all those Swifties just hanging.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R), FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR: So, a lot has changed. And when I look at that, I look at the fact, if I'm this passionate and I'm this determined, why not me?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley appears to inch closer to saying she might challenge former President Trump, her old boss, for the 2024 Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, Francesca, I'm kind of itching. Like, let's get this thing going for `24.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": It's still early, Shannon. It's still early.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it? I feel like we're already there. Like, it never is over. We're always in the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, it's still January and I'm hearing from Republicans that they think that they have plenty of time to make decision around this. But the same thing from Democrats, we haven't seen President Biden get in the race either, and he is expected to run for re-election. And the fact that he directed his own 2024 primary schedule is seen as an indication that he is planning to run for re-election. And next week Democrats will be gathering together to talk about, however, what to do about the state of New Hampshire, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Given that Governor Sununu, the Republican governor of New Hampshire, is saying they're going to go first no matter what in 2024. They are going to have the first primary in the nation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: So, Democrats have to figure out what to do about that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Those headlines are a nightmare for him. Here are a few more of those headlines. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Democrats face obstacles and plan to reorder presidential primary calendar. You see there your piece in "USA Today," talking about the governor saying, no matter what we're going. "Politico" just sums it up this way, New York Dems put Biden on blast. I guess as the kids say.\par \par \uc2 Marie, what does this say, though, about the current president's power and ability to call the shots if these states are like, no, no, not so fast?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, first, I married into a New Hampshire family, so I have to be Very careful on this one.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I know, this is -- this is tricky.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think Democrats realize -- have realized for some time that the primary calendar needs to better represent the country, be more diverse, better represent the electorate. For those of us who were on the ground in Iowa during the last cycle, we know that that caucus wasn't run well for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HARF: We knew there had to be changes.\par \par \uc2 I -- I love the fact that New Hampshire voters are - are really invested in the process early. They get a lot of face time with candidates. It's a small state. They get to really ask them questions, probe their different policies. So, I want New Hampshire to still be near the front of the calendar. It's in their state law, actually, that they are first in the nation.\par \par \uc2 But states like Nevada, states like South Carolina, I think those better represent the full picture of the electorate. And Democrats are trying to figure out how to do both things at once. The New Hampshire conversation is going to be a very interesting one. And I - you know, with my in-laws, I have to just say they should always be first.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, I don't know what else you can do with the in-laws. You got to - you want to keep the peace, but -\par \par \uc2 HARF: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes, in-laws are a tough thing to navigate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But - but this - but this does say, you know, that the president is, obviously, setting things up that worked well, like South Carolina. That was a turning point for him.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That actually, you know, probably propelled him to the presidency. But it doesn't sound like these states want to go along yet.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: No, I mean, I - look, I think New Hampshire had the audacity to vote Biden fifth in 2020, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not a good finish for him there.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I mean we forget it wasn't just bad, it was real bad in terms of his performance in 2020. So, yes, I'm not surprised that they're going to try to reorder things. He turned things around in South Carolina. Clearly they would like that to lead the way.\par \par \uc2 But, again, there's a rich history in New Hampshire that is going to be resistant right to the very end, as you said, in state law. Not much they can do anyway.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, Shannon, this feels like people trying to rig the game. I get inclusivity. They're the first in the nation. New Hampshire is it. It demands retail politics. As you know, Francesca, we've been on the ground there. They demand a certain something from candidates, one on one, and they talk back to candidates.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So it's a great training ground. And, I'm sorry, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  feels he's not quite up to that task, then he shouldn't run for president. Go through the blades of New Hampshire. If you can survive that, then you get to reach South Carolina and the rest of the primaries. I'm sorry, changing the rules early because you don't like the way the game might play out, Shannon, it's a bad look for the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, how many times have we all been in Pizza Kitchen in Iowa. If you're out there, Iowa, you know what I'm talking about. And these people are like, oh, yes, I've talked to them three times each, the candidates. I mean they are - they really engage with you and they want to know you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, now, Marie, you're back because we have a very important senate hearing to talk about. You were here last time we talked about this, the T. Swift meltdown over getting tickets.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  is going to chair this subcommittee hearing this week about what happened. She says, the issues within America's ticket industry were made painfully obvious when \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 's website failed hundreds of thousands of fans hoping to purchase tickets for Taylor Swift's new tour.\par \par \uc2 I did reach out to a couple of the senators to say, like, is T. Swift showing up, and I got crickets. So, I'm guessing that's a no.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Don't we wish? Oh, what a day on Capitol Hill that would be. \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  going for the T. Swift fan base here if she's going to run for president again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, smart move.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Look, it is - it is bigger than Taylor Swift, though. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is a monopoly. It has been problematic with every huge release they've had of tickets. I tried to get Garth Brooks tickets for - for Vegas. Couldn't do that. So, I think it's been long overdue, quite frankly, for someone to have some regulatory action here and investigation because America -- this is something that Americans care about, right? This is something that they don't always pay attention to the debt ceiling or, you know, any of these topics we talk about, which are important, I wish they did, but at the end of the day if you say, what drives you crazy, a loft them will say \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And we talked about this during the commercial break about how, I don't even try to go to the initial marketplace for whatever the ticket is, you just automatically are thrown to the secondary market because it's almost impossible to get them.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And who knew, Marie, that it only took Taylor Swift to bring Republicans and Democrats together in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I love it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Look, the White House says that they don't see much more that the president can do on this particular issue, but administration officials tell me that they do expect additional action on junk fees, as they're called. Those are the service fees that, in this case, you pay on - on going to an event like this. That's something that \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  and the senators who are investigating in this are also looking into. And also, to your point, certainly something Americans are paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Who thought we could find a website worse than \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 ? \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  is this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But here - here's the rub. Remember, \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  and \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  are merged.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Live Nation controls 200 venues, 400 artists.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The venues. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So they're policing and running the game in the front of the house and the back of the house. That demands scrutiny. I think people on both sides of the aisle would agree to that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, staying in our Hollywood lane, which we really do here on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Word this week that Alec Baldwin is going to be facing involuntary manslaughter charges. He has denied all along that he pulled the trigger, that he's responsible. Prosecutors feel otherwise and they have charged him.\par \par \uc2 "The Federalist" has this headline, Alec Baldwin baffled that he, not the gun, will be charged with manslaughter. According to Baldwin, everyone and everything are to blame for the shooting except for himself.\par \par \uc2 Josh, this -- this has all kinds of currents to it. We have a woman, Halyna Hutchins, who lost her life here and yet it's a very prominent case involving a very prominent celebrity.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes. No, I mean, tragedy, obviously, with the loss of life.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think Alec Baldwin probably would not be in this amount of trouble had he just kept his mouth shut. I mean it's just the perfect example of a celebrity going out, trying to justify themselves in the court of public opinion, not knowing that ultimately down the road everything that he said in all these interviews with Stephanopoulos and everything else is used and is admissible to the ultimate criminal charges that are up against him. That's his problem. I think that's why he's in this hot water.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Shannon, I spoke to a number of action -- guy who have led action franchises. When they say cold gun on a set and the armorer brings you that gun, you expect there is nothing in that chamber. That's what he was assured. I don't think you can hold the actor culpable here.\par \par \uc2 But the one charge of manslaughter might apply to him as a producers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Because the protocols on this set were shotty. The armorer and the prop person were the same. Shouldn't have happened. No live rounds should have been there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will follow that case.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much, panel. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, saying good-bye to a beloved member of our family here at Fox.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have some difficult news to share this morning. A treasured member of the Fox News family passed away this week. Alan Komissaroff died following a heart attack. He was our senior vice president of news and politics. For decades Alan was a guiding force here.\par \par \uc2 He started as a writer when Fox News Channel launched back in 1996. Alan then worked his way up through the ranks. A diligent, perfectionist who became indispensable. Alan was part of every major news story and every election night for decades, meaning he had a hand in how Fox covered the biggest stories that affected your world.\par \par \uc2 Alan, who I will always remember with a smile on his face, was just 47 years old. He is survived by his loving wife Rachel, who was his high school sweetheart and prom date, and their two children, 17-year-old son Ben and 13-year-old daughter Olivia. Alan was so very proud of them.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague, Bret Baier, said this week, Alan made all of us better. He will be remembered in a service today in East Brunswick, New Jersey. We are wishing comfort to his family and his friend and to everyone who is feeling this loss.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230122ej1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 March For Life, First Rally Since Roe Reversal; Justices Questioned In Supreme Court Leak Probe; Migrants Continue Pouring Into U.S. From Mexico; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Remains Defiant On Classified Documents Probe; Defense Officials Review Plans; Protester Killed And Trooper Injured In Shooting; Protesters Charged With Domestic Terrorism; Mexican Drug Cartel Activity In U.S. Connected To Violence; Former Trump Administration Officials Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , And \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  Possibly Considering Presidential Bids In 2024; First Anti-Abortion March For Life Event After Overturning Of Roe Versus Wade Takes Place In Washington D.C.; Biden Administration Still Getting Questions About Classified Documents Found In President Biden Possession; Record High Numbers Of Immigrant Crossing U.S. Southern Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Lucas Tomlinson, Shannon Bream, Griff Jenkins, Jacqui  Heinrich, Jennifer Griffin, Jonathan Serrie, William La Jeunesse, Rich  Edson \par \uc2 7,784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Don't forget the sacrifices of our troops there. You can go to lastoutplay.com. Most of the actors are veterans and there are still tickets available for the show in Chicago this weekend.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: In Chicago, good to know.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: SPECIAL REPORT is not up next. Have a nice weekend everybody. We'll see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to SPECIAL REPORT I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the latest development in the investigation concerning the leak at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  over last summer's Roe vs. Wade decision. This occurs as prolife demonstrators from around the country rallied today in Washington. It was the first March for Life since the Supreme Court overturned the landmark abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight, Shannon Bream will tell us what we're learning about that leak investigation. But we begin at the high court with Lucas Tomlinson and the March for Life. Good evening, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Thousands marched here to the Supreme Court in celebration. Now they want to take the abortion fight to the states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Prolife supporters from across the country descended on the nation's capital Friday for the 50th thing you won't March for Life. And for the first time since Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision brought abortion back to the state level. We're many thinks the next fight begins.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We got to get to the state levels now, right? So that's the emphasis on. That's what we're doing in front of the Capitol is to you know, show at each state level this is not over, it's only the beginning.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Currently 13 states have a complete abortion ban. Five have partial bans. Six states are in limbo pending current litigation. Abortion is legal in the remaining states.\par \par \uc2 New House Majority Leader Steve Scalise says despite the Supreme Court's ruling the issue is far from settled.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): The court didn't get rid of abortion, they just allowed this debate to continue with elected leaders instead of judges.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The new Republican majority in the House wasted little time passing prolife legislation.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS SMITH (R-NJ): The House did pass the Born-Alive Bill and Wagner's Bill, and of course a resolution to demand that the terrorists against pregnancy care centers, prolife offices and churches be investigated.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Mississippi's Attorney General Lynn Fitch argued the Dobbs case before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH, MISSISSIPPI ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is the new chapter we get to write in history. So we've got to be talking and then taking action on affordable accessible childcare, workplace flexibility, child support enforcement to make the dads equally financially responsible for their children.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Making his first ever appearance at the rally. NFL Hall of Fame coach \uc2 Tony Dungy\uc2  addressed the crowd and touched on \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  safety Damar Hamlin, who almost lost his life during a recent game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DUNGY\uc2 , NFL HALL OF FAMER: That game was canceled. Why? Because a life was at stake. And people wanted to see that life saved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Today's March for Life was peaceful with just a few counter protesters showing up, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson live outside the high court. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon. We heard from the Supreme Court marshal today about this investigation.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): We did. Bret, one of the questions leftover after their internal report released yesterday was were the justices among the dozens of employees who were questioned.\par \par \uc2 So today, the marshal stepped up and answered that question. She is the one who has led this internal investigation.\par \par \uc2 She says this, during the course of the investigation, I spoke with each of the justices several on multiple occasions. The justices actively cooperated in this iterative process, asking questions and answering mine. I followed up on all credible leads, none of which implicated the justices or their spouses.\par \par \uc2 On this basis, I did not believe that it was necessary to ask the justices to sign sworn affidavits. But Bret, yesterday, they indicated everybody else they interviewed did voluntarily sign the sworn affidavits.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" you have some guests talking immigration, some big news there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do. There are already calls as you know in the house. In fact, articles have been filed of impeachment against DHS Secretary Mayorkas. Republicans don't seem united on whether or not they're going to press for an impeachment or not. But what both parties are united about is that the border is a mess.\par \par \uc2 We've got two of the co-chairs of the problem solvers caucus. We've got Republican Congressman Fitzpatrick, Democratic Congressman Gottheimer. They're going to join us to sit down and have a conversation about as you know, the idea of common ground and whether there is any to be found on the debt ceiling, immigration or anything else, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot to talk about. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have new information tonight about how many of the men and women charged with maintaining the nation's security along the southern border say they have lost confidence in the man in charge of Homeland Security as just mentioned by Shannon.\par \par \uc2 A significant number of Border Patrol Agents support Republican efforts to impeach Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas. This comes as we see new video illustrating just how rampant human smuggling has become.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the story tonight from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In the span of six hours in the Del Rio sector, we have Fox News cameras embedded with Texas DPS, trooper stopped three separate smuggling attempts, recovering more than a dozen migrants and arresting two individuals for human smuggling.\par \par \uc2 In the Rio Grande Valley, Fox cameras, trail troopers and border agents as they pursue migrants evading apprehension.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ANFINSEN, UNION VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: This is truly a disaster down here and we need help.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Border Patrol Council Union Vice President John Anfinsen says agents on the front lines have lost faith in the Biden administration's border policies and are joining House GOP calls to impeach DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 ANFINSEN: If at the very least puts a bigger spotlight on what's happening on here, hopefully get some of the resources here and maybe gets Border Patrol Agents out of the asylum business and putting us back in the field, then we're all for it.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: For the cartels, it's business as usual. This 22-year-old Mexican national named Edu (PH) says he paid $1,000 to cross the border, owes another 5,000 when he reaches his family in Dallas, and did not hold back when I asked him if he feared the cartels.\par \par \uc2 ERIKSON EDU HERNANDEZ, MEXICAN MIGRANT (through translator): Yes, I am somewhat scared but I have to take the risk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you fear the most, the cartel or the Mexican police?\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ (through translator): The bad guys, the cartels, of course. We have access codes that we have to give them on our trip or we'll get kidnapped and then bad things happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Secretary Mayorkas has more to answer for House Oversight Committee. Chairman James Comer sent him a letter this morning requesting all DHS documents indoor communications regarding the removal of migrants from sites in El Paso prior to President Biden's visit, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins live along the border tonight. Griff, thank you. Later in the hour, we'll look at how the actions of drug cartels are extending far north of the Mexican border.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, President Biden remains defiant over the scandal involving his mishandling or alleged mishandling of classified documents. He insists there is no real story here and he did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 Critics say his position calls into question the president's claim that he takes such matters very seriously.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, the president left the White House for Rehoboth for the weekend opting not to go to Wilmington where those documents were found. Reporters shouted questions after him but he just walked on by.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The press secretary backing up President Biden.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He does indeed take classified documents seriously. I'm just not going to go beyond that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: After his musings on the outcome of the investigation, his administration is broadly refusing to talk about.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think you're going to find there's nothing there.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Threatened to undermine Biden's other statements that he takes classification laws seriously.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have no regrets.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It's a question Biden himself directed at President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 's nominee for CIA director back in 1977 when Biden was a senator on the Intelligence Committee and sided with Republicans to kill Ted Sorensen's nomination. Biden dug up an affidavit showing Sorensen mishandled classified documents for a JFK biography and asked at the confirmation hearing, does he respect and understand the rule under which he will serve. I'm not sure whether or not Mr. Sorensen could be indicted or convicted under the espionage statutes or fired from his White House job if that were the issue.\par \par \uc2 Those same questions are now being asked of Biden by the House Intelligence Committee saying the mere discovery of Biden's classified documents "would put President Biden in potential violation of laws protecting national security, including the Espionage Act and the Presidential Records Act".\par \par \uc2 The committee's request for national security damage assessment is still going ignored 10 days later. Despite refusals to officially comment, the administration may tacitly be trying to pin this on Biden's aides from when he was vice president.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports Kathy Chung, Biden's longtime executive assistant confided to associates that she is distressed that she might have inadvertently been involved in moving or storing classified material at the center.\par \par \uc2 The post cites a person briefed on Chung's account suggesting someone inside the government is pointing fingers at Chung and it's sinking in.\par \par \uc2 DOUG JONES, FORMER ALABAMA SENATOR: Somebody made a mistake at some point, inadvertently transferred. That is the clear implication from everything you've seen, everything that you've read.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The president did make some other news today, he invited House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to what he called a little discussion to talk about the national debt. McCarthy tweeted that he would accept the president's invitation this evening and looks forward to a discussion about how to responsibly raise the debt ceiling, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, the president has been spending a lot of weekends in Wilmington, Delaware. The focus of a lot of this investigation. This weekend he goes to Rehoboth Beach, right?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: That's right. And we asked the press secretary today if the documents had anything to do with that, maybe he doesn't want to go to Wilmington where those documents were found. She did not want to answer that question and once again, referred us to the White House Counsel, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a common refrain. OK, Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Job cuts continue at big tech companies. \uc2 Google\uc2  says it is laying off 12,000 employees. That's about six percent of its workforce that adds the tens of thousands of other job losses recently announced by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , parent Mehta and other tech firms.\par \par \uc2 That news didn't spook the markets though, rally for tech stocks sent Wall Street investors home for the weekend with significant gains. The Dow surged 331, the S&P 500 finished ahead 74, the NASDAQ jumped 288 today.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost 2-3/4 percentage points. The S&P 500 fell two thirds, the NASDAQ was up a half point.\par \par \uc2 Up next, is the U.S. getting enough help from its allies as it continues to send billions of dollars in weapons to Ukraine? We'll take a