 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Social media personality Andrew Tate will be held in Romania for another 30 days, after a judge granted the prosecution's request for another extension.\par \par \uc2 He was arrested more than three weeks ago on charges including human trafficking, rape, and being part of an organized crime group.\par \par \uc2 More than 500 people are forced to evacuate after a fire sweeps through a poor neighborhood in Seoul. Many of the homes in the area are made from cardboard and wood. At least 60 were destroyed in South Korea. No casualties have been reported.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Earth from the International Space Station. One of the big stories out there tonight. The first Native American woman in space steps out for her first spacewalk. Nicole Mann and a Japanese astronaut are installing equipment for additional solar panels. Mann is a member of the Wailacki of the Round Valley Indian Tribes in Northern California. She will continue her mission at the space station for about another month.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we follow up on a story we told you about earlier this week. There is serious concern this evening in Georgia about possible violence against law enforcement in the wake of a deadly incident near a construction site of a controversial police training facility in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie is in Atlanta with the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Police are on alert after a shooting left a Georgia state trooper hospitalized and a 26- year-old protester dead.\par \par \uc2 It happened just outside Atlanta, Wednesday, as officers tried to remove demonstrators from an 85-acre site, where they had set up camps, blocking construction of a new public safety training center. Also known by protesters as Cop City.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGISTER, DIRECTOR, GEORGIA BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: An individual, without warning, shot a Georgia state patrol trooper. Other law enforcement personnel returned fire in self-defense and evacuated the trooper to a safe area.\par \par \uc2 The injured trooper remains hospitalized in stable condition, with a wound to the abdomen.\par \par \uc2 COL. CHRIS WRIGHT, GEORGIA PUBLIC SAFETY COMMISSIONER: I want to thank our medics who are embedded with our SWAT team for their quick action. I'm certain that their quick action helped save his life.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Police arrested seven protesters at the site, all from out of state, on charges of domestic terrorism and criminal trespass.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  suspended a protest account after it called for a night of rage and reciprocal violence to be done to the police and their allies.\par \par \uc2 But other protesters deny any connection to violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do not let their cops overtake this narrative that we are terrorists. That they shot one of our own in self-defense.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have to counter that.\par \par \uc2 Some of the groups and individuals trying to block the training center site environmental concerns, while others opposed the increased police presence it would bring to the area.\par \par \uc2 But project advocates say, it will provide better training for police and first responders that ultimately will make the community safer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And Bret, top Georgia officials, including the governor and director of the \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , say they embrace the right to protest, but cannot stand by when protesters resort to violence and jeopardize innocent lives. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week a sheriff in central California blamed the Biden administration's border policy for gang activity that may have contributed to the death of a half dozen people. Tonight, we look at just how pervasive drug cartels are becoming inside the U.S. Here's national correspondent William La Jeunesse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: The less drugs we use, the less pressure there will be in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: In 2019, the U.S. pledged to fight the Mexican cartels. Billions of dollars later violence once contained there is here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT ALMONTE, RETIRED U.S. MARSHAL: They are here, and they are dangerous.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The latest, a family executed cartel style near Fresno.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOUDREAUX, TULARE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA SHERIFF: I can tell you cartels are here. They are here for multiple reasons.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: A drug cartel is a criminal organization. Like any business, there is a chain of command with each cartel controlling its own supply chain -- manufacturing, smuggling, transport, enforcement, accounting, and distribution.\par \par \uc2 BILL BODNER, DEA SPECIAL AGENT IN CHARGE L.A.: The drug cartels, the kingpins rely on that to make them their money. So they are pushing the drugs up here. It's those street gangs that are selling the drugs, making the profits to send back to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Today, there are five large Mexican cartels. The Gulf Cartel, Zetas, and the Jalisco New Generation compete for routes into Texas, while the Tijuana and Sinaloa cartels fight over Arizona and California to move synthetic drugs like meth and fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: We are working tirelessly to get deadly fentanyl out of our communities.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Today each cartel is a diverse, multibillion dollar business. They smuggle migrants at five to 20,000 each, and drugs worth billions.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LINES, (R) YUMA COUNTY, ARIZONA, SUPERVISOR: This is human trafficking. This is the trafficking of narcotics that are killing Americans.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Once over the border, U.S. based members deliver the drugs to street gangs.\par \par \uc2 BODNER: Someone will go to that city and set up a distribution center, and then once they're there, get those drugs out to the lower, nonaffiliated cartel people who are actually controlling the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): In reality, anyone involved with the illegal drug trade in the U.S. is working for a cartel whether they know it or not. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Last night we brought you an exclusive interview with former U.S. ambassador to the United Nations, former South Carolina governor and prospective 2024 presidential candidate Nikki Haley. But Haley is getting heavy criticism from another prospective candidate, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . Senior national correspondent Rich Edson takes a look this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: Do I think I could be that leader? Yes.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: Former United Nations Ambassador Nikki Haley getting as close as any Republican to challenging former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the 2024 nomination. Haley says she is still considering an official announcement, putting her among former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's your timeline?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Sometime in the spring.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: To capture the GOP nomination, former Trump officials will have to separate themselves from each other and their former boss.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I don't think you need to be 80 years old to go be a leader in D.C.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And now some infighting among these Republicans. Pompeo is releasing a book next week. In it he accuses Haley of scheming with \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  to replace then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  on the 2020 ticket. On SPECIAL REPORT, Haley denied it.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is my friend. There's no truth to it, and Mike even says in his book it's just gossip.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: A source close to Pence responded to Haley's alleged maneuvering with three letters, "L-O-L." Pence has been on his own book tour meeting with influential pastors at megachurches, leaders in the faith movement who largely backed Trump in 2016 and 2020 and are now withholding endorsements to see how this primary field plays out.\par \par \uc2 Trump has called evangelical leaders disloyal for doing so. A Trump spokesperson refused to comment on Haley's interest in running. Taylor Budowich the head of the Trump super PAC MAGA Inc Called Haley, quote "another career politician whose ambitions matter more than her words. That's the last thing we need."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The fight for staffing is already on. A source with Pence's policy organization Advancing American Freedom tells FOX News the group has just poached the executive director of Haley's advocacy group, Stand for America. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the March for Life and the latest on the president's classified documents scandal. But, first, as we head to break, we remember one of our own. We are heartbroken that our colleague Alan Komissaroff has died from a heart attack. Alan was senior vice president of news and politics. He has been a leader here for decades at FOX. Allen started working at the network in 1996 before the official launch. He has been a crucial leader in our coverage for every major news event and every election night. Alan recently led a discussion about the midterms at a company townhall. He was a happy warrior. He always had a smile on his face. He was true professional. He always thought outside the box and made us better every day. Our FOX family is hurting tonight, but our sincere condolences and heart felt prayers go out to Alan's family. He is survived by loving wife Rachael who was his high school sweetheart, his 17-year-old son ben and 13-year-old daughter Olivia. Rachael, Ben, and Olivia, he talked about you a lot. We all really miss you, Alan. Alan Komissaroff was 47.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm not just here to overturn laws. I'm here to defend all life. If they can reverse it once, they can do that over and over again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that it's going to mean more to people, because the ball is moving, and we are just gaining row momentum.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fight has to continue, and it's going to be in a different manner, working with the states more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are here to support the unborn. It's as simple as that. We are giving the voice to the voiceless.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: March for Life in Washington, the first since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, and this as we get some new polls out from Monmouth. How much would it bother you if abortion was banned nationwide? A lot, 57 percent, a little, 13 percent, not at all, 30 percent. Asked which comes closest to your view on abortion, always legal 36 percent, legal with limitations, 30 percent, illegal with exceptions, 22 percent, always illegal 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute. Mollie, it's interesting to see changes on the issue of abortion and how much it played in the midterms this past year.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, we had for so many years no ability to even weigh in on what abortion policies should be because of that Roe v. Wade ruling. With Dobbs the American people are allowed now to have an input in what abortion law should be in their states and nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And you note in that polling, which seemed pretty favorable to abortion supporters, even in that poll, the 64 percent of people who would like at least some protections for unborn children and their mothers. And that's what is so significant about Dobbs. It now allows people to work to have some protection for children, whether you want full protections or just a little. And that's where, at the March for Life today, excellent reporting on this show on what was going on there. There is a move and focus now on what can be done at the state level.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Well, first off, great to be here on Friday, and sad news about Alan and best to his family. I believe -- I'm pro-choice but I believe in the constitutionally protected right to protest and march for whatever you believe in. And we are at a moment in our country's history where Roe was the law of the land for 50 years. The court decided with a case before them to overturn that. And there are those who now are focusing their efforts in the states who are pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 There's an effort in Washington still underway, and I think I heard from the March for Life marchers and some of those behind this effort today that a national ban on abortion is something that many are seeking. Your polling data, Bret, just showed, I believe, that showed the 10 percent of Americans, at least in the poll data you showed, polling data you showed, wanted abortion to be illegal altogether.\par \par \uc2 It's good for my party, from a partisan standpoint in elections for this to be on the ballot, but I hope for the sake ever the country that we can find some agreement on when abortions might be allowed, some exceptions that should be imparted or imposed, because this is an issue that in many ways rips at the heart of the country.\par \par \uc2 So, I support protests and marching, whatever your position may be. But what I do support more than that is trying to bring the country, trying to build some consensus around an issue and around a set of solutions and outcomes that we can all -- can all live with. And I hope that's where we end up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason, if you ask the question about late term abortions, the numbers are very small who are in support of it. But that's not the question that's often asked. How do you see this issue politically and where it stands on this day?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Well, I think what we're seeing is that the Dobbs decision hasn't settled the matter in terms of the national debate. In one sense, it's where some of us thought it should have been all along, which is at the state level. But now the real work begins in terms of people who are opposed to abortion trying to convince people at the state and local level to take their side.\par \par \uc2 And we saw a lot of Republicans struggling with that in the midterm elections. There wasn't a lot of agreement on if there should be exceptions. If there should be exceptions what those exceptions should be and so forth. And so going forward we're going to see that play out, and, frankly, the pro-life crowd has its work cut out in terms of establishing what -- what should qualify as a true pro-life position. And so that's where I see the debate going forward, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, so the sights and sounds of this week really dealt with this documents story. The White House taking still a ton of questions about it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The president going to his home in Rehoboth Beach today, does that have anything to do with classified documents being found at Wilmington?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: As you know, he often travels to Delaware on the weekends. I just don't have anything else to share.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " saying "68 days of silence, why the White House stayed mum on classified documents. Advisers to President Biden calculated that the Justice Department would view possession of the documents as little more than a good-faith mistake." Mollie, what about this? Where does this go?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": We do have a Department of Justice that has gotten quite politicized. They unwisely raided the previous president's home over a classified documents dispute, and then they spent two months basically covered up that Biden had a classified document issue before they kind of came forward with a little bit of information on it.\par \par \uc2 I think the Biden team has done a very good job of spinning this, and they have a very friendly media that can help them with that. But there are so many questions that need to be answered, from why, exactly, was an attorney looking for documents when he came across them in the office space in D.C." And do these documents deal with the Biden family business? We know they involve Ukraine and China, two locations where the Biden family business, where you give known members of the Biden family in exchange for stuff we're not quite clear about. Those are places where the Biden family business is quite active. And so, there's a lot that needs to be known here that we don't know yet. And they are hoping that people stop asking questions, but nothing about the way they have been acting makes people want to stop asking questions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, Jonathan Turley, "I have no regrets. President Biden breaks long silence with shattering admission." Turley writes, "It's never a good idea go public with expresses of no regret when you're being investigated on whether you took classification laws seriously. The statement was right out of the Alex (sic) Baldwin school of criminal defense in claiming that the gun did it. Fortunately, the president is not yet saying that the corvette did it." Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Both of those are serious issues I won't conflate, and I know that Jonathan was probably trying to play light-hearted about this. I think what is happening with President Biden is serious. I have said this all along. The timeline between November 14th and January 5th when a U.S. attorney was asked to look into this body, Attorney General on November 14th. And then a special counsel was appointed on January 5th, which means that there's something serious about this.\par \par \uc2 Democrats who say that's not the case, I think, are wrong, and Republicans who try to compare this directly to President Trump's case are not being honest. I don't always agree with Mollie, but I agree with her in this regard. Transparency, I think, is key here. And I hope that President Biden, who has rightly so, and someone who is my friend, prided himself on and staked his campaign and candidacy and now presidency on being transparent, I hope they are that way.\par \par \uc2 This is not to say that there is anything untoward or illegal that's been done. But I do think many of the questions that are being asked, particularly about the damage assessment from an intelligence standpoint, deserve more transparency and answers and forthrightness than what's being provided.\par \par \uc2 But we should all take a break here. There's a special counsel. He should get to the bottom of this. But I only mention that transparency of this White House, because that's what President Biden when he ran and now as he is serving, that's one of the things that he staked his integrity and his campaign and now his presidency on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we'll see if they hold up to it soon. Up next, the Friday lightning round. We have some breaking news on immigration numbers, plus Winners and Losers, and later, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we've just received the latest Customs and Border Protection numbers on illegal immigration at the southern border. You see the report there, 251,487 migrant encounters in December alone. That makes December the busiest month ever recorded by Customs and Border Patrol. There have been 717,660 encounters in the first three months of the fiscal year. That does not count so-called got-aways.\par \par \uc2 With that we are back with the panel. The breaking news there, Jason, these numbers are astronomical. People can't even get their head around them. If you look at this at the size of cities, they are bigger than some cities in the U.S. in population. What about this?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: There are two things going on here, Bret. One is that there's a significant labor shortage in the U.S. There are far more job openings, millions of more job openings than there are people looking for work. That news gets across the border. People come to address that issue. And so long as the people coming believe that if they get into this country even illegally there is a good chance they will be able to stay, they're going to continue to come.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration will not address that issue. It will not make it clear that they will not get into the country or that they will be turned away at the border. And until they do that, these people will continue to come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think we have to put these numbers in different perspective, because people, it kind of glazes over. The overall number is about the size of Denver, the population. Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, it's not surprising that this is happening since the Biden administration worked so hard to tear down any kind of border security or protection that had been put in place under the previous administration. What makes this such a problem in part is that we have completely ceded control of our border to these cartels. And as was mentioned earlier in the show, now that there is cartel activity in Tulare County, California, and other places in states that are in border areas, it's a very serious security issue and it's a very serious human rights issue. It's not just about the people crossing the border but also about the drugs crossing the border, and also about how the cartels are able to exploit these people. It is unbecoming of a country to not have borders that they understand and enforce, and there seems to be really no interest in D.C. to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : This is a national security issue. I would hope that now that the Republicans have the majority in the House, I hope that one of the first things they do in addition to the things they are doing now is to propose a very serious, if not aggressive and tough border security bill, force Democrats and force this White House to work with them. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has also staked himself out as someone who can negotiate and negotiate real deals. They should challenge him on that because this is, as Mollie said, a national security issue more than anything else.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's do a quick Winners and Losers. Jason, first to you, winner then loser?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, my winner who is the person who leaked the Supreme Court Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade who continues to stay one step ahead of investigators. And my loser is \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  for his incredibly narcissistic remarks at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  about saving the planet from climate change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My winner is Ivan Provorov, the \uc2 Philadelphia Flyers\uc2  defenseman, who declined to wear a gay pride jersey because of his Russian Orthodox Christian faith and has now had his jersey selling out because people are impressed by his courageous stand. My loser are the National Merit scholars in northern Virginia who schools have not told them about that they are being National Merit scholars, and Governor Youngkin is working on it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, winner, loser, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD: My winner are those of us who love the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . We've got four games this weekend, eight quarter backs under 30. A new generation is being ushered in. The Giants, go Giants. I love Eagles, but go Giants. And my loser is George Santos who has embellished a lot of things about resume, even about his mom, bless her, and where she was when she passed. But he took it a step too far when he stole money from a veteran trying to raise money to have surgery for his dog. I got three dogs in this house, Bret, and that wasn't right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That wasn't right. There you have go. Panel, thank you. Enjoy the games this weekend.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday. You know what that means, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's absurd to have a climate denier in charge when the world is trying to solve of the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're dealing with the maddening complexity of an ossified boomer built bureaucracy that they are unable to fix.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Money, money, money, money, money, money, money.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Almost $32 trillion in debt, how could you do this to a future generation?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Fiscally demented, I think.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They don't quite get it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can't scare the bejesus out of people, saying we are going to get rid of Social Security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our cities are being undermined. We don't deserve this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We must have a bipartisan solution for our broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any comment to Republicans who are calling for your impeachment, sir?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I would refer you to the Department of Justice. We're just not going to comment. We're moving on.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have no regrets. I'm following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How long before a body starts to smell?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Reparations was something promised and something owed.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I stand here humble, being the first sitting president to speak as Ebenezer Sunday service.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you going to run for president?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: We need to go in a new direction, and can I be that leader? Yes, I think I can be that leader.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I almost went pro, being 5'2", 5'3" didn't make it happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week in Washington. Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll talk with Indiana Republican Senator \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  about his recent trip to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon, FOX NEWS SUNDAY, her guests, co-chairs of the House Problem Solvers Caucus, and Democrat and Republican, see if they find some common ground.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Jesse, do you have a bet with Harold Ford? He's calling for the Giants to beat the Eagles.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020230121ej1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Probe Has Not Identified Who Leaked Abortion Opinion; Alec Baldwin To Be Charged With Involuntary Manslaughter; Biden White House Deflects Classified Materials Queries; America Hits Debt Limit Raising Economic Fears; Haley Slams Unequal Treatment Of Trump-Biden Probes; Haley Denies Any Role In Alleged Pence Ouster; Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas Tells Conference Of Nation's Mayors Financial Assistance Will Be Provided To Deal With Immigration Surge; Biden Administration Falling Short In Effort To Restock Strategic Petroleum Reserve; Teachers Worried As New Artificial Intelligence Program Doing Students' Homework; Former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley Indicates She May Run For President In 2024; White House Continue To Deal With Fallout From Classified Documents Being Found In President Biden's Possession\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Matt Finn, Shannon  Bream, Bill Melugin, Gillian Turner, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 8,167 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2023\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2023 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: The K9 took an oath to tell the truth but he's exercising his right to remain silent.\par \par \uc2 Another cop said he ate his sandwich. They're talking about putting him in jail. It's not right, it's just not right.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You can't get Pauly P.'s (PH) mugshot.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. And by the way.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I offered to defend them pro bono.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jessica, we owe you. That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Dana. My two favorite things are the couple in the dog crate, and then stop for next year (PH). I think that's really sweet. OK, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baer, former U.S. ambassador to the United Nations former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley opens up when asked about running for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: I think we need a young generation to come and step up and really start fixing things. Can I be that leader? Yes, I think I can be that leader.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on that. Actor Alec Baldwin faces and involuntary manslaughter charge on the onset shooting of a cinematographer.\par \par \uc2 And the Secret Service says it does have a record of visitors to President Biden's private residence after all, the place where he kept those classified materials.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, investigators trying to find out who leaked a draft of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's ruling overturning the Roe versus Wade abortion decision. They admit they had been unable to do so, that report says the probe will continue but there's no sense that a culprit will be identified.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, Bret. Well, we did get significant insight into the courts investigation into this leak. But we're left with more questions tonight. And word that the court says the investigation is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 The court says 82 people had access to either electronic or hard copies of the Dobb's decision on overturning Roe v. Wade. 97 employees were interviewed, some of them multiple times, all of them denied being the leaker.\par \par \uc2 The investigation also concluded that an outside hacker penetration of the court system, their internal I.T. system is highly unlikely suggesting that this was indeed an inside job.\par \par \uc2 So, the court also disclosed that it recently invited former DHS Secretary \uc2 Michael Chertoff\uc2  one time, a judge himself to review its work and that he did not suggest any additional investigative measures.\par \par \uc2 While the court says employees who were asked to did turn over call and text records for their personal phones, nothing relevant was found there.\par \par \uc2 In addition, all personnel who were asked to submit to an interview, they did so, all of them also signed a sworn affidavit under penalty of perjury that they did not disclose the draft opinion to anyone who didn't work for the court.\par \par \uc2 But many admitted they didn't stick to the procedure for handling the draft including this "some individuals admitted to investigators that they told their spouse or partner about the draft Dobbs opinion and the vote count in violation of the court's confidentiality rules."\par \par \uc2 In the end, the court's internal investigation concluded that it was unable to identify the person responsible for the leak by a preponderance of the evidence, that is a very low standard, essentially, the ability to say that something is more likely than not. And that suggest the court just doesn't have any strong leads, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I do believe. All right, Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, actor and producer Alec Baldwin is facing an involuntary manslaughter charge over the death of a cinematographer on a movie set in October of 2021.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin was pointing a prop pistol at Halyna Hutchins during a rehearsal when that gun went off.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn has details tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Today, a New Mexico district attorney announcing she will file to involuntary manslaughter charges against Alec Baldwin and "Rust" armorer Hannah Gutierrez-Reed.\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMACK-ALTWIES, SANTA FE COUNTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Mr. Baldwin had a duty at the base level to never hold a gun and point it at a person while pulling the trigger. But he also had a duty as an actor and a producer on that set to have the bullets checked or to check them himself to make sure that they weren't live.\par \par \uc2 FINN: The special prosecutor tells Fox News that safety on the 2021 New Mexico set was loose and reckless with accidental gun discharges and that Baldwin did pull the trigger on the gun he fired, a claim he denies.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin faces up to 6-1/2 years in prison if convicted on the stronger involuntary manslaughter charge that carries an enhanced sentence.\par \par \uc2 He's attorney responding "This decision distorts Halyna Hutchins' tragic death and represents a terrible miscarriage of justice. Mr. Baldwin had no reason to believe there was a live bullet in the gun or anywhere on the movie set. He relied on the professionals with whom he worked, who assured him the gun did not have live rounds.\par \par \uc2 Gutierrez-Reed who faces the same prison time was reportedly inexperienced and overwhelmed. Her attorney telling Fox Hannah is and always has been very emotional and sad about this tragic accident but she did not commit involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 Halyna Hutchins' family is thanking New Mexico authorities writing: Our independent investigation also supports that charges are warranted. It is a comfort to the family that in New Mexico no one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And the New Mexico D.A. says Baldwin agreed to turn over his phone but there were records missing. It's not clear if that was intentional. The D.A. adding Baldwin will not be arrested. It's procedure that he'll be given notice to make his first appearance, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn in Los Angeles. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and his team are still refusing to provide specifics about the discovery of classified documents at his Delaware residence and his private office in Washington think tank.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the president is in California where he's touring some of the damage from deadly winter weather the past several weeks.\par \par \uc2 And we're hearing it could be a while before we know whether he will run again in 2024. At least officially.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has tonight's update live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's announcement on a 2024 run will not come until after the State of the Union. I am told that that timing is not at all influenced by the documents probe. A Democratic strategist telling me it's better for Biden to tout his administration's achievements and let that moment breathe before making any other major news.\par \par \uc2 But there is short time to turn the public's attention to other matters, with many remaining questions surrounding the documents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The House Intelligence Committee formally requested a national security damage assessment over President Biden's apparent mishandling of classified documents.\par \par \uc2 But nine days later, there's been no response from the Director of National Intelligence and nobody within the administration is willing to say if risks are being assessed. The special counsel's office also declined to comment setting long standing practice.\par \par \uc2 DOJ officials say they've given no orders on what the administration can or cannot say. But the president indicated last week he's been advised to keep quiet.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am going to get a chance to speak on all of this, God willing, soon.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports early on Biden's attorneys and DOJ officials had a shared understanding to keep the matter quiet. Biden hoping for a quick resolution that would find no intentional mishandling, federal investigators hoping to avoid media attention complicating the probe.\par \par \uc2 And now, frustration in the West Wing over the attorney general's decision to appoint a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you satisfied with the way your team is handling the document situation?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Meanwhile, House Oversight Republicans are turning their attention to the Penn Biden Center to learn who may have had access to the documents there. And if there's any nexus with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's foreign business dealings.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): We're very concerned about the return on the investment that our adversaries around the world were expecting to get with respect to the millions of dollars that they were transferring into the various Biden account.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: University tells Fox the Penn Biden Center has never solicited or received any gifts from any Chinese or other foreign entity. 100 percent of the budget for the Penn Biden Center comes from university funds.\par \par \uc2 But new reports show the university got several donations from anonymous Chinese sources, including a gift of 14 million in 2018. And Comer wants to know if any of the sectors money that came from the school originated in China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Comer also wants Secret Service logs detailing who may have had access to the president's home in Wilmington and new tonight, the Secret Service says they are willing to turn over that limited information that they have when they're asked by Congress.\par \par \uc2 But remember, the president did not have Secret Service protection from mid-2017 until 2021 in March when he was made the Democratic nominee for president, so there is a gap in time there.\par \par \uc2 The president just addressed these documents in his remarks in California, saying that they immediately turned over those documents. They're fully cooperating with the Justice Department and looking forward to getting this resolved quickly, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There are growing tensions tonight on Capitol Hill over the debt ceiling. Republicans are calling on Democrats to engage in talks to come up with a plan.\par \par \uc2 So far, Democrats are refusing, they want a clean debt ceiling increase. Let's find out where things stand tonight. Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has our report. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. So, we are now seeing senators step in to try to avert a crisis here.\par \par \uc2 Today in Kentucky, GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  sounding confident that the U.S. will not default on its debt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): We'll end up in some kind of negotiation with the administration over what are the circumstances or conditions under which the debt ceiling will be raised.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): This comes despite the fact that House Speaker Kevin McCarthy isn't getting anywhere closer to a sit down with the president. He is trying to come up with a plan to raise the debt ceiling while also balancing the budget.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the concessions McCarthy made with a group of conservative hardliners in order to get the speaker's gavel. Republicans have worked with Democrats before to increase the debt limit under Republican presidents but Republicans say they didn't run up the discretionary spending like Democrats have in the last few years, although the national debt still ballooned by about $7 trillion under former President Trump, most of that due to the COVID response.\par \par \uc2 Now this week, Bret, Democratic West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin offered to get House and Senate committees together to examine trust funds like Medicare and Social Security without getting rid of them instead analyzing them in exchange for a debt ceiling hike. But the White House continues to refuse to negotiate with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, BIDEN SENIOR ECONOMIC ADVISER: To threaten default, to threaten economic catastrophe. Nobody, Democrat or Republican should do that, no matter how much they believe in their agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): The Congress does have some time, it's expected to have about until summer to figure this out. So, we could be in this budget battle for some time, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Renewed worries about a possible recession sent stocks down today, the Dow lost 252, to go negative for the year. The S&P 500 fell 30, the NASDAQ was off 105.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk about some of the big stories of the day and her own political future with former U.S. ambassador to the United Nations, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley, is she getting in the race?\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 2 in St. Louis with the manhunt for five inmates who escaped from prison. Authorities describe three of them as known sex offenders. The jailbreak occurred around 7:00 p.m. Tuesday when the group entered a secured cell then forced their way through a door.\par \par \uc2 Fox 12 in Portland, as officials approve a half million dollars for relief fund for the city's marijuana shops. The funds will come from the cannabis sales tax. The city's sights crime and the pandemic is the reasons for the hardships for some dispensaries.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Salt Lake City from our affiliate Fox 13. One of the big stores there tonight. Fox 13 says downtown charter school tries to raise money to renovate its athletic facilities. City academy is crashing cans to solve a problem that's been developing since the school opened two decades ago. It's asking the public to donate cans in a drop box behind the school.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 We are joined tonight by former ambassador to the United Nations and former governor of South Carolina Nikki Haley. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER GOVERNOR OF SOUTH CAROLINA: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, I want to talk about some news of the day, first, with you, and this document story really has gotten a lot of attention. The handling of classified documents. You dealt with classified documents.\par \par \uc2 How do you see this with President Biden and what we saw with former President Trump and where you come down?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, classified documents should never be taken into an unsafe place.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that, clearly, President Trump and President Biden did it. They both should be investigated to make sure that there was no harm to our national security.\par \par \uc2 But what really gets conservatives upset is the fact that you can have Republicans, the Democrats do the same thing. And Republicans are vilified. And Democrats, they just sweep it under the rug. And so, that's the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DOJ, double standard.\par \par \uc2 You know, at some point, something's got to give. You can't weaponize our agencies and have it play out this way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the world stage this week, a lot of focus in Davos, Switzerland, in the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you listen to those speeches, climate change is the biggest issue on the world stage. Obviously, you have Iran protests, you have China on the move on a number of fronts. What do you see is the biggest challenge for the world?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: That is so -- Davos is so tone deaf. Because if you look at the fact, families can't afford their groceries, mothers can't find baby formula.\par \par \uc2 You've got crime at an all-time high, you've got inflation that's up, you've got children who are so far behind in education, I don't know that we're going to catch them up.\par \par \uc2 That's great that they can go talk about the environment in Davos, but that's not what Americans are feeling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were governor of South Carolina, you had a good economy while you were governor. But balancing the books on a federal level has been very difficult for Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Governors have to balance their budgets, right? So, we do understand what it means to kind of pick and choose what you're going to spend on.\par \par \uc2 I think when you look at the debt ceiling, we're $31 trillion in debt. We are borrowing money just to make our interest payments, our debts higher than the economy.\par \par \uc2 We haven't had that since World War II. We're certainly in a place that's not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 We talked in the fall about your ambitions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When do you make a decision whether you're going to run for president? And whether former President Trump decides or not, does that affect you?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Look, I've said for a long time, if there's a place for me, and I put my hat in, I've never lost a race. I'm not going to start now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You said after the holidays that you would look at it, consider it. You would read the tea leaves in your words. We are in the New Year, how are those tea leaves looking? Are you going to run for president?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I'm not going to make an announcement here. But when you're looking at a run for president, you look at two things.\par \par \uc2 You first look at, does the current situation push for new leadership?\par \par \uc2 The second question is, am I that person that could be that new leader? Yes, we need to go in a new direction. And can I be that leader? Yes, I think I can be that leader. I was, as governor, I took on a hurting state with double digit unemployment. And we made it the beast of the southeast.\par \par \uc2 As ambassador, you know, I took on the world when they tried to disrespect us. And I think I showed what I'm capable of at the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 So, do I think I could be that leader? Yes. But we are still working through things and we'll figure it out. I've never lost a race. I said that, then, I still say that now, I'm not going to lose now. But stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It sounds like you're close. It sounds are we getting to the exploratory committee stage here?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I think stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. A reporter was asking you if you would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he ran again in 2024. And you told this A.P. reporter, "Yes, I would not run if President Trump ran, and I would talk to him about it."\par \par \uc2 And you added, "That's something that we will have a conversation about at some point."\par \par \uc2 Obviously, President Trump -- former President Trump is running. What has changed if you are where you are?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I had a great working relationship with the president. I appreciate all the foreign policy issues we worked on together. But what I'll tell you is the survival of America matters. And it's bigger than one person.\par \par \uc2 And when you're looking at the future of America, I think it's time for new generational change. I don't think you need to be 80 years old to go be a leader in D.C. I think we need a young generation to come and step up and really start fixing things.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, all of that when I said that was before we surrendered to Afghanistan. It was before we saw this high inflation and high crime, it was before we saw drugs infesting all of our states. It was before we saw our foreign policy in disarray. So, a lot has changed.\par \par \uc2 And when I look at that, I look at the fact, if I'm this passionate, and I'm as determined, why not me?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, I've talked to a number of high profile conservatives. Some of them very supportive of Trump. Trump supporters from the beginning, who say, maybe it's time to your point for a different person to hold the torch.\par \par \uc2 They love Trump policies, but didn't particularly love all the controversies around the man. Is that what you hear?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I don't so much listen to that, as hearing all the negatives against Biden. We have lost the last seven out of eight popular votes for president.\par \par \uc2 Seven out of eight Republicans have lost. Something is wrong.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think it's time that we bring in a new generation that can bring in more people to our party. That can really talk about the solutions that we need to go.\par \par \uc2 If I run, I'm running against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That's what I'm focused on. Because we can't have a second term of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You already have supporters, even though you're not declared. Vanity Fair had piece, in which it said the Kushner's family is cozying up to Nikki Haley. Saying that, basically, they support you. "\uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 's parents invited about 20 friends to meet former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley at Kushner's beach house in Jersey Shore. At the event, Jared's father. \uc2 Charles Kushner\uc2  predicted that Haley would be the first woman president. Some attendees made donations to Haley's Stand for America PAC."\par \par \uc2 So, is the president's son-in-law a supporter of yours?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Look, the president's son in law is a friend of mine. Jared and Ivanka are dear friends. That's what they are. I fully suspect they will -- expect that they will support the president. That's where they should be. That's who they are. But the Kushners are just dear people, and they have been supporters, and I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One person who doesn't seem to be a supporter is Secretary \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , who writes in his book about an Oval Office meeting.\par \par \uc2 He says, I received a call one evening from a \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , chief of staff, who apologized for allowing Ambassador Haley to go to the Oval Office. She had gone -- not gone in for a personal matter, but rather had entered the Oval Office with the president's daughter Ivanka and her husband, Jared. "As best Kelly could tell, they were presenting a possible Haley for vice president option. He was certain he had been played."\par \par \uc2 Is that true? Were you trying to get \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  off of that ticket?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: No. And you know, Pompeo even says he's not sure if it's true. I never had a conversation with Jared, Ivanka, or the president about the vice president ship.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, what I'll tell you is it's really sad when you're having to go out there and put lies and gossip to sell a book. I mean, I don't know, you know, why he said it. But that's exactly why I stayed out of D.C. as much as possible. To get away from the drama and get away from the gossip. I focused on my job and that's what I always did.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senior Trump advisor Kellyanne Conway was asked about this on Brian Kilmead's radio show today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (via telephone): That is true. That there were people trying to get rid of \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  off the ticket.\par \par \uc2 I also thought it was so naive from some of the political novices like \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  that I worked with in the White House to actually think, well, let's just get rid of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 This whole idea, let's just get a woman -- get a woman -- get a woman. You know, who did that? One \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And look what we got for goodness, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I'm glad Secretary Pompeo has said this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what do you say to Kellyanne Conway on her recollection of this?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I literally did not speak with anyone in the administration about that at all. It was gossip. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is my friend. It is gossip. It was never discussed.\par \par \uc2 If somebody else discussed it, they certainly didn't discuss it with me.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this -- it's just gossip. This is all gossip. But there is no truth to it. And Mike even says in his book, it's just gossip.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, do you expect this to be kind of a bruising primary if you do get in?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Listen, politics is a blood sport. It was a blood sport in South Carolina. It was a blood sport at the United Nations. There is -- there is nothing I can't handle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, may the best woman win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. I have this thing, candidate casino. You have $100 in chips. You know, of those $100, where do you think you are on getting in or not getting in?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, stay tuned. I think I've given you a lot today. We're leaning in. We are leaning in, in a big way. We're focused on it. Stay tuned. It will be fun.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It sounds like 75 to 80. We will see. Thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More SPECIAL REPORT after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: After months of criticism, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is now reportedly offering a reward for information about a tax on reproductive health services facilities. The bureau will pay $25,000 for helping the investigations. There were at least 10 strikes against such operations.\par \par \uc2 Embattled Homeland Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas says the Biden administration will help local governments deal with the consequences of the illegal immigration surge along the southern border. Secretary Mayorkas told a conference of the nation's mayors financial assistance is being distributed. He continues to take heavy criticism from Republicans over border policy. They plan a hearing on the issue next month.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin tried to get reaction today from the secretary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Excuse me, Mr. Secretary, any comments to Republicans who are calling for your impeachment, sir?\par \par \uc2 Good morning. They have announced an oversight hearing for the border next month. Will you cooperate, sir? Sir, any comment for Republicans calling your impeachment, sir? We'll let you go.\par \par \uc2 Answers from DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas few and far between as he arrived at the annual conscious of mayors in Washington, D.C., today. There he told mayors from all around the country the federal government will help them shoulder the cost of the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: \uc2 FEMA\uc2  is providing emergency food and shelter program funds to help cities around the country recover or defray the costs of non-citizen arrivals.\par \par \uc2 JIM ROSS, (I) ARLINGTON, TEXAS, MAYOR: \uc2 FEMA\uc2  resources can help put a band- aid on the problem right now, but it doesn't address the long-term efforts of immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: At the same time, GOP calls for Mayorkas's impeachment continue to grow, with Congressman \uc2 Kevin Hern\uc2  tweeting in part, quote, "We must impeach Border Secretary Mayorkas. He is refusing to do his job. He must be removed," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , a frequent critic of the administration's border policies, disagrees.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2 , (D-TX): I don't support the impeachment because I don't think it rises to a constitutional level of violation of high crimes and misdemeanors. Now, saying that, do I disagree with some of the things that the secretary has done in the past? Of course.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, House Speaker Kevin McCarthy has said he wants to wait until December border numbers are released before making any decision on impeachment. And speaking of those numbers, CBP sources tell FOX those December numbers are going to be the highest month ever recorded at the southern border with over 250,000 migrant encounters in a single month. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Striking. OK, Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we update you on the Biden administration's effort to restock the nation's depleted oil reserve. It appears the president's team is failing to meet its own goals, and that raises questions about whether the nation's emergency supply can be restored. Correspondent Gillian Turner takes a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  is pushing back against a new bipartisan draft bill that limits President Biden's ability to withdrawal oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. That's the supply that has historically helped shield Americans from international supply crises. This bill would result in more oil supply shortages in times of crisis and higher gasoline prices for Americans. President Biden withdrew 220 million barrels from the SPR in 2022 in an effort to lower gas prices, leaving the reserve at a 39-year low.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The \uc2 United States government\uc2  is going to purchase oil to refill the Strategic Petroleum Reserve when prices fall to $70 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Today that price is still a long way off, with one key benchmark, WTI, trading at $79 a barrel. The Energy Department is now backtracking on the Biden team plan to purchase 3 million barrels in the month of February, citing still high prices, "DOE will only select bids at a price that is a good deal for taxpayers." This week the president is criticizing a bill that would make it much harder to release oil until the SPR is replenished.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: House Republicans introduced another bill on the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, blocking action that would help lower gasoline prices and help consumers.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But Speaker McCarthy insists the legislation fixes the president's mistakes.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: We also got a bipartisan vote to stop that Strategic Petroleum Reserve for selling our oil that is supposed to be there for a time of emergency off to China and others -- 113 Democrats joined with us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): As Washington hashes out the politics of depleting the reserve, analysts tell FOX News refilling the SPR may be out of reach in the near future and warn Americans should come to terms with a depleted reserve being the new normal. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it could be a student's dream and an educator's nightmare. A new artificial intelligence program that pretty much can do the assignments on its own. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 But as we head to break, musician David Crosby has died. His wife shared a statement with variety saying Crosby passed away after a long illness. He was a founding member of Crosby, Stills, Nash, and Young. David Crosby was 81.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Defense Department has announced another round of security assistance for Ukraine. The package is valued at up to $2.5 billion. That includes hundreds of armored vehicles, critical support for air defense, and additional artillery.\par \par \uc2 Some alarming messages today out of Moscow. A former Russian president says Moscow's defeat in Ukraine could trigger a nuclear war. \uc2 Dmitry Medvedev\uc2  is considered a prominent member of President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's inner circle. Meanwhile, the head of the Russian Orthodox Church says the world would end if the west tried to destroy Russia.\par \par \uc2 Russia and China will conduct naval drills in the Indian Ocean off the coast of South Africa next month. It's considered another indication of their strengthening relationship with Africa's most developed country amid the war in Ukraine and global financial uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in the U.S. some parents and administrators are expressing concerns tonight over a new chat bot that many fear is helping students circumvent traditional assignments. It is an artificial intelligence language model that can actually do much of the student's work for him or her. That's the problem. Here is correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Launched in late November, ChatGPT, an artificial intelligence platform, has already sent shockwaves through America's education system. Darren Hick is a philosophy professor at \uc2 Furman University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DARREN HICK, \uc2 FURMAN UNIVERSITY\uc2 : The first thing I saw it used for was plagiarism, which is a bit of an awakening.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The chat bot, created by California based \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , allows users to generate original content like essays with a simple prompt by pulling information found from books, websites, and past news articles. School districts from New York to California are banning access to the site on school campuses, a move some education experts believe goes too far.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATKINS, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY: I would have preferred if they come out and said we are temporarily suspending access until we can prepare teachers with the professional development so they know how to handle the new technology.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Text generation, while groundbreaking, is not perfect. It can produce incorrect information and have limited knowledge of events after 2021. Hick says he failed a student last month after she was caught cheating through the chat bot.\par \par \uc2 HICK: The sentences were clean. This was not gibberish. This was perfectly coherent. It was just wrong.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: We decided to try it for ourselves to see if it could help if for our story. We asked for a 10-second news script on the future of artificial intelligence. Almost instantly the computer says "The future is now as artificial intelligence continues to revolutionize industries and change the way we live. From healthcare to transportation, the potential for AI is endless."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Endless indeed. Tech companies see a lot of potential and money in these programs. This week \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , an investor in the company, announced it's going to expand the chat bot into its own cloud computing products. Also to mention, there are third party groups that are building tools for educators to better detect computer generated texts, all in an effort to prevent more people from cheating going forward. But, Bret, this is technology you have to see to believe. You have got try it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So mark, did you write that last part, or?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: I think a reporter's job should be safe, so I'm not going to comment further on that. You can't replace the reporter, that's for sure. I can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, you got it. Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the 2024 news that we just heard from ambassador -- former ambassador and former governor Nikki Haley. And later, a very special day for a 100-year-old woman.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Hundreds of thousands of people protest in France over proposed pension changes that would push back the retirement age. Strikes severely disrupted transportation, schools, and other public services all across France. The new rules would require people to work longer before receiving a pension and raise the nominal retirement age from 62 to 64.\par \par \uc2 A stampede outside a soccer stadium in southern Iraq leaves at least two people dead, dozens injured. Witnesses blame the deadly incident on what they say was bad organization of that tournament.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New Zealand. One of the big stories there tonight, Prime Minister \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2  says she is leaving office. She fought back tears as she announced February 7th would be her last day. Ardern faced mounting political preciouses at home over her handling, strict handling, of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: The survival of America matters. And it's bigger than one person. And when you're looking at the future of America, I think it's time for new generational change. I don't think you need to be 80 years old to go be a leader in D.C. I think we need a young generation to come in, step up, and really start fixing things. And all of that, when I said that, was before we surrendered to Afghanistan. It was before we saw this high inflation and high crime. It was before we saw drugs infesting all of our states. It was before we saw our foreign policy in disarray. So a lot has changed. And when I look at that, I look at the fact, if I'm this passionate and I'm this determined, why not me?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why not me? Can I be leader? Yes, I can. Those are the words from former ambassador to United Nations, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast, former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye.\par \par \uc2 Doug, I'll tell you, it's as close as she has been to officially jumping in. It sure sounds like it's very soon. And it's interesting to see that the notification from the Trump folk, that Reuters has, "Trump to make first 2024 campaign appearance in South Carolina. Democrats with Biden's backing have recently taken steps to make South Carolina their first primary in 2024." Obviously, this is the former governor of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Yes, it makes South Carolina a much more interesting state as one of the first four. Clearly, she sounds like a candidate. We also know there are a lot of people who are sounding like candidates who have not pulled that trigger yet. It's still historically early. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has set the timetable a little differently for how everybody is going to make their decision, whether they make a decision based on what he does or just the timing of what he does. But she sure sounded like a candidate tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Josh, she denied the statement in \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  book trying take out \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  as V.P. with the help of \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 . She said that's not true. Kellyanne Conway said otherwise on Brian Kilmeade's show. Here's another snippet from Kellyanne about the exit for Nikki Haley from the administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: She wanted to make sure that the MAGA base knew she was leaving on good terms. But here's what I never understood. You don't leave a job like that if there is still work it do. She went. She was making $200,000 a speech. That's her right, I suppose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, again, the aforementioned \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  in that book also says something like that in his book, "Never Give an Inch." Haley flat out threw in the towel after two years as U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations, a job far less important than people think. She abandoned the governorship of the great people of South Carolina for this important role, and quit it after just months on the job." I mean, no love loss there between Pompeo and Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: No. The knives are out already. I think it's pretty clear, and I think Nikki Haley know this traditional MAGA base is going to be pretty difficult for her to tap into, not that she doesn't have some admirers in that space. But I thought what was so interesting about her interview with you is how she is sort of road-testing potential thesis statements for this candidacy. You heard her on multiple occasions talking about generational change. That's interesting, right? Obviously, you have got an older \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is already in the race, that's an inherent critique. It's also something that resonates with primary voters right now vis-a-vis \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So you can see her trying some different things here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. She said may the best woman win, too, Olivia at one point in that interview. I should point out that we reached out to Pompeo's people. They sent a statement saying, "Secretary Pompeo stands by everything he wrote in his book." Your thoughts, Olivia?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": I love that Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might all be on the campaign trail at one point together, and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . What stood out to me, though, is Nikki Haley's back and forth with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You showed the tweet from the A.P. reporter saying that she wouldn't run. Her tone has changed, saying that they need a new generation.\par \par \uc2 But she has also changed in the past before. After January 6th she said he had fallen so far that she didn't think that he would run again. She said that she thought he was out of the picture. But now that he is in the picture, she is not ruling it out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Clearly, she suggested that he has vulnerabilities. Among them, Josh mentioned, the generational one.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to what we just received, Doug, in the past few minutes, and that's President Biden talking to reporters about this document, classified document issue and the scandal around it. He said this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We found a handful of documents were failed -- were filed in the wrong place. We immediately turned them over to the Archives and the Justice Department. We're fully cooperating and looking forward to getting this resolved quickly. I think you're going to find there's nothing there. I have no regrets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No regrets, Doug.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: I think there are a lot of regrets, and you can just look at last Thursday for regrets. Thursday was supposed to be, just one week ago, a good day for the White House. They were going to talk about good economic news. Instead, you had a pretty disastrous press conference where Biden struggled to answer questions from Peter Doocy, started talking about his corvette, and then the White House press briefing later that day.\par \par \uc2 And it gets to the problem for Biden, that's his central core promise in the election was that he and his team of pros were not going to make these kinds of mistakes, and obviously they have. And we can argue what degrees they were versus what Trump or what Biden did. But there is old adage -- Josh knows this very well from his time on Capitol Hill -- if you are explaining, you are losing. And so far, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and hits team have been explaining.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, "Wall Street Journal" op-ed, "The Biden classified document payback. This is the permanent politics of retribution, and it can get worse. The investigations of two special counsels, Robert Hur for Mr. Biden, Jack Smith for Mr. Trump, will hang like two cement shoes waiting to drop across 2023 and perhaps into the 2024 presidential campaign. Potential candidates soon need to visit primary states to campaign and fundraise. But the frozen legal status of Trump and Biden will hamper that process." You think, Josh?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, clearly, sure. And I think all Democrats thought that they had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  politically dead to rights with the Mar-a-Lago raid. But he spent four months trying to convince America that this is the worst possible thing that anybody ever could have done. It turns out your own guy has got the same problem.\par \par \uc2 I think what was interesting earlier in your show when you had Jamie Comer on talking about the investigations, where this goes from here, the concerning part is where these documents actually were, right. When you are talking about the Penn Center, the Biden Center, when you are talking about his garage, who has access to that. Now we hear that Secret Service has some logs. The question of foreign funding and what kind of access potential donors would have had, those are all areas of inquiry here that can get a lot worse than Mar-a-Lago where you basically know everybody who is coming in and out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I had someone ask an interesting question, Olivia, and that is, do you think we would know about the classified material if the House was in the hands of Democrats? In other words, would this leak have come out to various news organizations if there wasn't the pending possibility of House Republicans digging into just about everything?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: That's a hard hypothetical to answer, but we like leaks. It's part of the transparency.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: And I think that Republicans are just planning on pushing full speed ahead. I think you're going to see Chairman Comer trying to transcribe interviews with the National Archives officials. And good sources tell me in the House GOP that you should be watching them to try to subpoena the Secret Service for records about who had access to some of these private residences. And so, they're going to be able to get some information. It won't be complete, as Jacqui pointed out earlier in your show. But it shows that they are going to be trying to build information as they try to hammer Biden on this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, from a communications point of view, they had to put the president out to say something, because \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has just had a tough, tough week.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be his best advocate here, but he often struggles to make easy answers on what should be easy questions. They have got to come up with definable answers. And if they can't do that, this is going to