gime in North Korea? No, actually it's not, and doctors should be speaking out against that, because it is corrosive of the public trust in Medicine and it's just lying.\par \par \uc2 But not many are in fact, virtually none. Some of them are moving in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Marino, for example, is an emergency medicine physician in Ohio employed by University Hospitals. He is often cited as an expert by outlets like NBC News.\par \par \uc2 Recently Marino unbeknownst to those of us who are not on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has written several posts on that social media app attacking a woman who suffered a documented mRNA vaccine injury. He says, she is "looking for money." What? He has no evidence of that. This is a suffering patient. He is mocking her.\par \par \uc2 Then he has also posted this image on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  mocking the idea that a small amount of fentanyl can kill somebody. Mocking people who die of fentanyl? A doctor? Why is this allowed in this country? Why would any hospital still employ a so-called doctor like this? What exactly is going on?\par \par \uc2 Alex Berenson broke this story. He is the author of course of, "Pandemia." His work is on \uc2 Substack\uc2 . He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Alex, thanks so much for coming on and for bringing this to light.\par \par \uc2 So you know, you don't want to pile on one guy. He is just some doctor in Ohio. And you know, you want to be fair and everything but the guy is a doctor, tell us why it is important for the rest of us to know about him?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERENSON, AUTHOR, "PANDEMIA": Sure. So, by the way, it is actually even worse than what you said in that he is not attacking this lady because she had a vaccine injury. Her son who is 15 had a documented case of myocarditis, and he is going at her and saying she wants money for that.\par \par \uc2 So look, Dr. Marino, look, he is entitled to think that fentanyl should be legal and that all drugs should be legal, which he does and