CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's all just peaches and cream according to Joe and his team. But what's the truth? According to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis\uc2  , the personal savings of Americans are roughly one eighth of what they were right before the pandemic. That's the lowest level of savings since the 2008 recession. And Americans are up to their ears in debt as well. They owe $925 billion to credit card companies. That's a 15 percent year-over- year rise. That's the biggest jump we've seen in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 And now the layoffs are coming. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  plans to fire more than 10,000 workers while \uc2 Meta\uc2  will axe another 11,000. \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  is cutting 1,200 and media companies like AMC are slashing 20 percent of their workforce. So is all this a sign of a healthy economy?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Peter Schiff, chief economist and global strategist for Euro Pacific Capital, host of "The Peter Schiff Show" podcast, and Robert Wolf, former \uc2 UBS Americas\uc2  CEO and FOX News contributor. Robert, you see a bright side here. What is it?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FORMER \uc2 UBS AMERICAS\uc2  CEO: I would say I see a brighter side. This is nothing like the 2008 recession. I was at that Lehman crisis that weekend, and it was leveraged throughout the system with banks and everywhere else. We are not seeing that. The labor market is stronger than what you just depicted. We're going to see north of 200,000 job gains tomorrow. Inflation has subsided a bit, although I'm still very nervous about a long tail. Consumer spending has been strong. And GDP came in very strong yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So I would say I'm nervous about inflation, but I don't see the hard landing like Peter sees, although the inverted yield curve is probably telling him he is right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Peter, without getting too wonky about inverted yield curbs, do you agree with this fairly positive assessment?\par \par \uc2 PETER SCHIFF, EURO PACIFIC CAPITAL: I agree in a way. It is not like that 2008 recession or financial crisis. It's actually going to be much worse than that. This is just the beginning. I think we've been in a stealth recession all year. But I think the recession is going to get much worse in 2023. But what is also going to get a lot worse is inflation, because one thing that is kept a lid on consumer prices in 2022 has been the strength of the dollar. But I think the dollar has lost that strength. It had the worst month in 12 years in November. It's off to a horrible start in December. I think we're going to have a currency crisis in 2023, and that is going to fuel the inflation fire just as the unemployment rate is really spiking and this recession is kicking into a higher gear.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Robert, the problem here is that we also have a lot of consumer pessimism out there. And after we've blown through the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, we're kind of out of ideas about energy prices. When energy prices continue to rise and the Russian sanctions are obviously kicking in, where does that leave us? Because costs are continuing to go up.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Laura, you're right. I'm very nervous about energy prices. Obviously, I think we should buy in the open market as needed to re-put oil into the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. But we've had the perfect storm. We've had the embargo of the early 80s with Russia. We've had the Persian Gulf type crisis of 92. And then we've had the post-recession demand of 08. So we've had the perfect storm between the Ukraine and post-COVID. So I'm not bullish at all on energy prices staying low. I actually think into the winter months it's going to be a bit tough, especially in eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 I think that being said, I recommended that we rid the federal sales gas tax gas tax and rid state sales tax. Let's put money right back into consumers were it's being hurt the most, which I actually think Putin is a major issue of why gas taxes -- gases have gone so up.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We keep wanting to prolong this war by spending an anonymous amount of money on munitions to Ukraine as well. That's not making it any better.\par \par \uc2 Peter, Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  was asked about what was causing this record high inflation last night, and this was her answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , TREASURY SECRETARY: Remember, everybody stop spending on services. There were in their homes for a year or more. They wanted to buy grills and office furniture. They were working from home. They suddenly started splurging on goods.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, so this is the consumers' recession, or inflation, I should say, Peter? I never quite have heard anything like that before.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: What \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is overlooking is just where did all these consumers sitting at home get all this money to buy all that stuff? They got the money from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that flooded the economy with PPP money and unemployment benefits that in many cases were to two to three times what people earned when they actually had jobs. And so the government made it possible for the unemployed to sit at home and keep spending money as if they still had jobs and were productive members of society.\par \par \uc2 So the inflation was created by government. And tax cuts are not the answer right now, unfortunately, because we just substitute inflation for taxation. What we actually need is big cuts in government spending. And unfortunately, we are not getting that. We're getting more government spending.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, we're going to get the omnibus bill thanks to Republicans. So gentlemen, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Is the Republican lieutenant governor of Georgia actively suppressing the vote in his own state? Plus we just learned something very interesting about the voters in these past midterms. We are going to tell you what it is in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There have been a number of famous performances over time. The Beatles at Shea Stadium in 1965, the Three Tenors in Rome in 1990. And who can forget Whitney Houston's National anthem at the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 But there is another performance, and it's getting a lot of traction, at CNN yesterday. It caught our eye. This up and comer sees the future not in politics, but perhaps in a contributor-ship. Meet Republican Lieutenant Governor of Georgia Geoff Duncan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF DUNCAN, (R) LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF GEORGIA: I showed up to vote this morning. I was one of those folks who got in line and spent about an hour waiting. And it was the most disappointing ballot I've ever stared at in my entire life since I started voting. I had two candidates that I just couldn't find anything that made sense for me to put my vote behind, and so I walked out of that ballot box showing up to vote but not voting for either one of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let's unpack this, shall we.\par \par \uc2 He claims he waited in line for an hour but didn't realize that he wasn't going to vote for either candidate until he actually physically saw the ballot? Does anyone on earth believe this story? Think about what this means. If Duncan is lying, it's a sad attempt at gaining new friends at CNN. But if he's telling the truth, he is kind of in essence suppressing votes. And he's just not that smart. So we are going to say this tonight on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, if you were in line with Geoff Duncan, if you saw that man in line when you were voting, we at the Angle want to hear from you. We welcome any corroborating evidence of this event.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of Georgia, as we await the results of the runoff this Tuesday, the midterm results paint a dire picture for the future of the Democratic Party according to the analysis from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2  ." The evidence so far raises the distinct possibility that the black share of the electorate sank to its lowest level since 2006. It certainly did in states like Georgia where authoritative data is already available.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Florida Congressman Byron Donalds. Congressman, the data here reveals that the Democrats remain almost wholly dependent now on white college educated voters, especially women. So how damaging is this now on the African American side for the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R-FL) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: It is very damaging simply because black voters have seen, frankly, with year after year of the same gaslighting, the same talking points, the same promises that go nowhere, and the actual ineffectiveness of what happens when they govern. Economies go bad, borders are wide open, people are -- they are actually subverting the law. They're suppressing free speech. World War III may be breaking out.\par \par \uc2 The implications of Democrat governments are clear for everybody to see, not to mention crimes. And, oh, yeah, by the way, they'd want your kids to get the best education possible. Black voters have had enough. And that is why you are seeing this constant shift.\par \par \uc2 Now, I will say to my colleagues on the Republican side of the aisle, that doesn't mean they love us. We have a job to do with black voters and, frankly, all voters in the United States to demonstrate what conservatism actually is and how that can help all people in America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That doesn't include agreeing to omnibus spending bills that give Democrats all the power and us no leverage next year. It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, we have some breaking news tonight that Georgia Lieutenant Governor was back on CNN again today with another masterful performance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF DUNCAN, (R) LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF GEORGIA: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to go down as probably the worst Republican candidate in the history of politics, right. There is just no way to run away from that. And I think the energy level behind his support, the best case being made right now is he's going to be a Republican vote for us. And surely that means a lot to me. I care about Republicanism. I care about conservatism. I'm passionate about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, this is a top Republican official in the state of Georgia throwing our candidate under the bus. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Laura, I'm normally professional on your show, but I'm going to discard that. Listen, I'm a Tea Party activist. That's how I got into politics. We were lectured time and time again that you've got to support the team, get behind whoever the nominee is so we could go win. So for the gall for this guy to show up and say I couldn't vote, it's absolutely ridiculous. You want to go do that, Geoff Duncan, stop being lieutenant governor, go be a normal citizen. It is atrocious when you have a Republican on the ballot, you show up and you get the job done if you're going to be an elective office. But to do this mess, no, I'm sorry. This guy has got to go. I'm sick of him.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, he is a complete nightmare. The Democrats never pulled that kind of stunt on their candidate, never. Congressman, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we take you inside the meeting between \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joe thinks he and Emmanuel have such a budding romance that he doesn't want to share it with anyone, even with his friends in the media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: President Macron and I have had a chance to spend some time together. We had a private dinner last night with our wives. And we just came to tell that we're leaving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You can almost hear their hearts breaking.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. By the way, I saw his motorcade today, and it was quite long for a climate change activist.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221202eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Democrats, Winning Majority In The Senate, Think They Have The Mandate To Rule Out Their Progressive Policies To The American People; Democrats Brag Their Win In The Senate But Many People Say That It Was Roe v. Wade And The Student Loan Forgiveness Plan That Saved Them; President Biden Met Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  In Indonesia For The G20 Summit, And President Biden Did Not Call Out China For Its Human Rights Issues Or The Drugs That It Brought To The U.S.; Student Debt Bailout Sham: Admin Accused Of Bribing Voters With Loan Handout; Airline Chaos: Police, FBI Investigating United Passenger Altercation; Lion King Interpreter Was Fired For Being White\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Dana Perino, Jessica Tarlov, Jeanine Pirro, Jesse  Watters \par \uc2 8,553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR: All right, Mark, we'll see you again real soon on this. And that'll do it for me. Thank you for watching. Catch me Tomorrow on Making Money in a \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2 . "THE FIVE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters, along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfeld. It's five o'clock in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 America, I hope you're ready for more out of control radical policies. Democrats barely squeaked out a win in Nevada to keep control of the Senate and now they think it means that they have a mandate to shove their outrageous, progressive policies down our throats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This victory belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats who got out there and fought for working people. The things we did were important and popular.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approach and agenda were exactly the things that we needed in this country, and that's what people voted for.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), SENATE MINORITY LEADER: I predicted the young people were going to vote for us. Why? We focused on climate, which they really care about.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): There is no question that this will be the most progressive Democratic caucus in decades,\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But do they not realize the Democrats are still expected to lose the House? Maybe the liberal cheerleaders need to listen to the guy they're all praising.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\pa