om, but we know that they don't include the 17,000, door 3 votes and they do increase \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  lead.\par \par \uc2 What this does not include is the you know what you mentioned there the 290,000 ballots that were dropped off record breaking number of ballots dropped off in person on Election Day of the early ballots. Now, that wasn't enthusiasm, enthusiasm for \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That was those were Kari Lake voters. And so we're really looking forward to them finally telling the whole story in Maricopa County, they still have over 325,000 or so to go. And the numbers are only going to get better from there. And so even if they had dropped those 17,000 alone, that would have destroyed the lead that the Maricopa County is artificially propagating out there. So we do feel pretty good about the top of the ticket in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So again, this is the gubernatorial race. And even though \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is now up, I think 1.34 percent with what we know, right at this moment, you're saying that ballots that were brought to the polling centers on Election Day, I guess they would mail in ballots that were actually physically dropped off by voters. Those who have reason to believe are going to skew pretty favorably to Lake, correct?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Very favorably. So and the 17,000 that were not able to be tabulated of the voters who voted in person, again, huge Kari Lake advantage over there. Those have not been released as yet either. And I think that what's happening here is some petty little game playing by some Arizona county officials, but eventually they have to release all the ballots and when that happens we are really very comfortable that our that our projections are going to hold so no worries here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Kari Lake was on the Sean in the last hour. She actually made a promise, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF ARIZONA: That