e 2015, right, like when Trump kind of announced that he was running, think about how things have changed, just from that point, right. At that point, no one thought Republicans were going to have a big surge of Hispanics, we have that. A lot of people were pro-China that's changing. So a lot of good stuff has happened. And there's a lot of trend lines that are really positive for Republicans, obviously, we're going to get into, you know, the why and the lessons learned. But people have to remember where we came from and where we are now. And progress isn't sometimes overnight or in a straight line. So we'll get into all that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And things are turning out a lot better than maybe people thought last night, and we'll wait for these races to come back. And I'm pretty optimistic about them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All, right, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But have a great show. Looking forward to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I enjoyed yours. And I am Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from Washington tonight. Let's get right to it. 'Big Takeaways', that's the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 I know, so Sean alluded to this, like tonight, they're breathing a sigh of relief right at the White House, even as Democrats almost certainly will lose the House, and they could even lose The Senate. Now, the media, of course, are obsessing over the losses of Trump back candidates and States like New Hampshire and Pennsylvania. But little attention is being paid to the astonishing fact that Democrats do not intend to do anything differently over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is even with 75 percent from Fox News voter analysis, saying that they think the country right now is on the wrong track. Despite that, The White House thinks their policies don't need changing, because Biden has created what they're calling stable, sustainable growth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What in the next two years do you intend to do differently, to change people's opinion of the direction of the country, particularly as you contemplate a run for President in 2024?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Nothing. The major piece of legislation we passed and some of the bipartisan takes time to be recognized? It takes time to get a movement?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, this is typical the Democrats, they win or they think they win, and then they over read their mandate. Well, yes, Republicans should have won more Senate seats and flip more House seats. But still, the number one issue worrying voters, including African American voters, is inflation, which he was asked about today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The GOP and KKF conducted a study of black voters, that said inflation was the number one issue. What can you promise concretely? And these next two years that will help turn the pocketbook for the better in the midst of staving off a recession?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But what I can't do is I can't guarantee that we're going to be able to get rid of inflation. But I do think we can. We brought we've already brought down the price of gasoline about $1.20 a gallon across the board. And the oil companies are really doing the nation a real disservice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, gosh, all right. So Biden's repeating that same line. Oh, you just you just haven't seen the great effects of my policies yet. And he's saying it takes time he's blaming the oil companies really? Even CNN wasn't buying it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GLORIA BORGER, CNN POLITICAL ANALYST: When he was asked, would you change anything? He said, no. now you have 75 percent of the country, saying that we're headed in the wrong direction. He said he just wouldn't do anything different because of course, our achievements take a long time to be recognized. That's insulting people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That is insulting. And even David Axelrod seemed little less than enthused.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: He's proud of what he did. Did. He's done he wants to talk about it. But this, this was an opportunity to tell a story about the country to be really big and tell a story about the country. And I think it was a missed opportunity. There were a fair number of people who didn't have a favorable opinion of his performance in office who voted for Democrats, because it was about something larger, and it was also about the alternative.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Biden's message today featured all the usual slights of hand, just as in 2020, when he campaigned then on uniting the country, remember that? Only then go on and brand the country as systemically racist and then trash Trump supporters as semi-fascist. Well, today, he raised the goal of uniting America again, only to make false claims about Republican leadership and of course to push gun control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Circumstances. Will I support the proposal put forward by Senator Johnson and the Senator from down in Florida, to cut or make fundamental changes in Social Security Medicare that's not on the table. I will not do that. I'm going to ban assault weapons, they're going to try like the devil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Biden's obviously learned nothing. And as of now, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is projecting that the Republicans will end up with about 50 seats in the Senate and will narrowly capture the House majority. So they're taking the House, even if not by the numbers obviously they'd hoped for. That will do a lot to rein in the craziest radicalism of the Biden Administration. But again, we all get it the GOP is very disappointed.\par \par \uc2 So at this point, we have to 