 fell out the chair. Biden admitted in this speech that he can't guarantee that he can solve inflation, the number one issue for voters. But because some new Congressman calls him about high speed rail in Scranton, he's like, no problem. We'll get that done. I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 Now, last night's results notwithstanding, voters reported that they still do trust Republicans more on the issues they care about most. And if the Democrats continue to preside over the type of economic and crime problems that we've seen just over the last year, even early voting will not be enough to save them the next time. And that's 'The Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Mollie Hemingway, Fox News Contributor and Editor-in- Chief of the Federalist and Scott McKay, Contributing Editor at the American Spectator and author of The Revivalist Manifesto. Mollie, now with the caveat that we're still waiting on a number of big races. What's your takeaway from last night?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, THE FEDERALIST, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: Yes, I think things will improve for Republicans in Nevada and Arizona. But we really did see, given all the enthusiasm that was in the country, that Republican leadership really failed Republican voters. Republican voters were excited. The ground was very fertile for a big Republican victory. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval is in the toilet. 75 percent of the country thinks we're going in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 Just objectively speaking, things are bad in the country, whether it's the southern border, or crime or foreign policy, inflation and that Republican leaders couldn't not turn that into a big victory for Republicans really is an indictment of how they're running things I could not agree more with your opening where you talk about the importance of understanding that elections are not run anymore like they were in the 1980s. There is now extensive period of voting where people who are smart are running get out the vote operations 