rida, these like 17 point swings toward Republicans. That's big. What makes everything so weird, is that you look at Georgia and Arizona and some of these other Pennsylvania, those races seem to be very much like they were in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Why is it that we're seeing these big swings in other States, but not in these battleground States? And I think it's because Democrats targeted their wins. They have, they were very focused on which seats they wanted to win in the House, and in the Senate, and they cranked up their machine they're voting, they're, they're, you know, get out the vote machine in the targeted places.\par \par \uc2 Republicans need to be smart and savvy on that as well. But showing how when you focus on a place like New York, you can have tremendous success, but you do have to put the money and you have to be strategic. And you also have to have a compelling message. This is another thing where Republican leadership really failed. You know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who, you know, I'm not totally critical of him. But he said that he didn't think that they needed a message that would just be sufficient that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a disaster. Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a disaster. But people still need a compelling vision. They need to have really good arguments. And so that's another thing that Republicans can do better going forward.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mollie really quickly, do you think Kevin McCarthy is in jeopardy? As House Speaker assuming the Republicans do take the House? I've already heard rumblings tonight from Freedom Caucus members, that because it's such a narrow majority for the Republicans that he's in trouble, is that wishful thinking on their part?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Probably wishful thinking. I mean, obviously, both Kevin McCarthy and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  would have well, particularly Kevin McCarthy would have wanted much bigger victories to feel more secure. I just don't know if there's someone else really trying to run there. But it's definitely a wakeup call for everyone that the strategy that was employed was nowhere near sufficient, and it cannot go on again. But all Republican leaders need to understand ground game is key, it is not the 1980s. We have very wildly different voting rules that were instituted a couple of years ago. Those are the rules. You might not like them, but you have to play that game.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bingo. Early voting, get the early votes out Mollie - Mollie and Scott, we had a little problem with Scott's audio but we'll have him back. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now Monica De La Cruz won a Texas border district for Republicans for the first time ever. And Zach Nunn knocked-off Democrat stalwart in Iowa. They're both here next to tell us how they did it. Coming back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MONICA DE LA CRUZ (R) TEXAS REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT: I also put the sense a message to the millions of Latinos who are watching me at home tonight. Regardless of how you voted, and stay as to party though. We are the party that wants Hispanics to prosper, not depend on the government. We are the party that understands Hispanics do not worship government. We worship God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was Congresswoman Elect Monica De La Cruz who made history last night by becoming the first Republican to win Texas's 15th District ever. Now there were races like that all over the country, and Iowa's 3rd district Republican, Zach Nunn he flipped the Democrat seat defeating \uc2 Cindy Axne\uc2 . It was described as a painful loss for the Democrats by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats have been viewing last night's results as this huge victory, but they're still likely to lose the House, as I said in the Angle, and control over major committees and are losing rising stars in the process.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Texas Congresswoman Elect, Monica De La Cruz and Iowa Congressman Elect, Zack Nunn. First, I want to say congratulations to both of you on your victories. You both ran fantastic campaigns. Monica, we're going to start with you. Why do you think you succeeded? Some didn't succeed Republicans who wanted to flip seats, but you did and turning your district from Democrat to now solidly Republican and this turnout?\par \par \uc2 DE LA CRUZ: Well, I think that our success started in the 2020 election. We ran for the first time and swung this district 18 up points. It was historic, because I was running up by up to excuse me, I was running against an incumbent. We never stopped running this since 2020. And I think that the name recognition help the fact that we did so well. We scared the incumbent out of the district and made it an open seat was extremely beneficial for us to get our message out to the voters.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, it came down to two things. Number one, the Biden administration's disastrous economic policy coupled with the border, open borders that he has caused in his administration that is affecting our border patrol agents. And I believe that those three things helped us push our campaign across the line, this 2020 election, 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it's very, it's very exciting. And in Iowa, well, this is fascinating, because Iowa has been a stalwart on two things. Number one, getting government off your back. Number two, they were they were great on handling COVID. And, Zach, how did you do it? How did you continue this success that your governor obviously saw him getting reelected?\par \par \uc2 ZACK NUNN (R) IOWA CONGRESSMAN-ELECT: Yes, Laura, hey, it's been fantastic here in Iowa, like Texas and Florida, and congratulations to Monica on her incredible victory there. We did experience that Iowans a perpetual purple state had clear choices, what works in government, like our Governor here in Iowa, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , she'll say, guys from