upposed to be a disqualifier for election. Instead, you have Florida voters coming out and actually supporting DeSantis. Yes, he may not be carrying the water explicitly on the big lie. But there's still so much of Trumpism that he is bringing into his own camp. Including the.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean, the breathless commentary on that. Isn't it just pragmatic conservatism, that that won in Florida that's winning in Texas, that won in Iowa. Monica, we'll start with you. Isn't just pragmatism and then populism?\par \par \uc2 DE LA CRUZ: Look, at the end of the day, the voters are saying they want common sense legislation. They want things that are going to help them be prosperous, be able to afford to put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 Look, as a single mom myself, I know that single moms out there, and all of American families, are struggling. They're worried about Thanksgiving coming up and how they can afford a turkey. We're talking about just basics at this point. And I believe that common sense and the Biden administration hitting people in the wallet, that's what drove people towards conservative candidates such as myself.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Zach, do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 NUNN: Yes, yes, Monica hit a really solid point here. We're a family of four. We took in two foster kids in December, in the middle of an election campaign. We saw the impact it had just on the childcare side. I talked to my kid's preschool teacher, and she said she bought a pizza and filled her car up with gas. It was the most expensive date night she had had with her husband since their honeymoon. These are real impacts on real people.\par \par \uc2 And you don't have to be a farmer in the field trying to fill up with diesel this fall to know how much that impact really reduces your ability to be a successful business, a successful farmer, of just a family that can get through the week. So these are things that really did resonate at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 I'll also say this. We didn't see any solutions being offered by the leadership out of D.C. on the Democrat side. I will compliment Republican leadership for actually coming up with a commitment to America on tangibles that this new Congress can work day one to start delivering.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Monica and Zach, congratulations to both of you, and we'll be watching you very closely when we get to Washington.\par \par \uc2 What everyone running for public office should avoid to do if they wish to win, and the unseen factor that tipped these midterm elections from Raymond Arroyo. "Seen and Unseen" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we expose the cultural forces that shaped these midterms. For that we turn to the author of "The Wise Men Who Found Christmas", FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Ray, you have some lessons for those seeking public office in the future.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, these midterms were instructive, Laura. There was much to learn from the candidates who didn't make it. First rule -- don't try singing at a campaign event if you can't sing. This is Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC, (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE SENATE CANDIDATE: Country roads take me home to the place I belong. West Virginia, mountain mama, take me home, country roads.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, boy. May I recommend the perpetual masked singer for him. The other rule, Laura, is unless you're auditioning for dancing with the wannabe stars, which could be career options for these next two candidates, don't dance in public like Stacey or Beto.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't know what one of those words was.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: How is that qualifying for office, Laura? How does that prove your leadership ability, I ask you?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember, Obama actually could sing. So when he sang Al Green, I mean, he has a good voice. But --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He already won the office by that point, though.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's true.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He wasn't campaigning, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is when they're deciding.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, that's more --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But the most vexing election of the night, Laura, came out of Pennsylvania from the state that brought you \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . You know something is wrong when a man who's been dead for a month is elected state representative. That's right, Democrat Tony DeLuca died last month. He was reelected last night. You can't make this up. But I guess after Fetterman, Laura, everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, that is terrifying. Ray, why do you think the red wave failed to materialize?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, what did I say? And I know I got nasty emails and tweets about it over the last few months. I said over and over, "let's go Brandon" is not a governing agenda. You need a clear, actionable series of policies that you can defend and advance. Give the voter something to aspire to. The GOP really didn't do that.\par \par \uc2 And I know they have that commitment to America, but, Laura, I will just read two quick goals. Economy that's strong, a future built on freedom. Those are not policy action items. Those are just hopes. It's not enough to -- there's not enough meat there on the bone.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, when you peal back the polling data, it was young voters that gave the Democrats their advantage. Of all of the age groups, it was Gen Z who delivered for the Democrats, this group who mostly live, and I'm not making this up, they mostly live with their parents. They voted, 63 percent of them, for Democrats. That's plus 28 points for the Democratic Party, and may have blunted the red wave that came from other groups.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think a lot of the younger people, the lockdown wasn't maybe so bad, right, for them? They got to order Uber Eats and catch up on the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and get their checks.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, again, the Democrats were also very deliberate in their pitch to young people. They offered them drugs, recreational drugs, abortion, paid off student loans. Yes, so there were, again, actionable policies that they were promising to advance, and climate change. And the GOP did not get in the mud and wrestle over any of these issues trying to win those young people over. That was a mistake.\par \par \uc2 Despite the setback of losing at least one house of Congress, Biden was back at being the old Biden apt his press conference tonight, Laura.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've been given a list of ten people I'm supposed to call on, and you're supposed to ask one question.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not, what would happen if, in fact, I think the context is that whether or not they're pulling back from Fallujah. And the -- I mean from the -- the Kherson, the city of Kherson. Biden's being apoca -- Biden is being extremist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, Laura. Clarity should be the mandate, I think, coming out of these midterms.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is what I'm saying, that MSNBC gal was like, well, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  can't string two sentences together. Has she listened to Biden, Fetterman? Come on. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , come on, he's Socrates compared to the other two. Give me a break. I like Herschel.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It is remarkable, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ray, where are you going off to your book tour next, the book tour that never ends?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, I'm in Birmingham, Alabama, on Tuesday, New Orleans on Saturday. Go to RaymondArroyo.com. "The Wise Men Who Found Christmas" tour continues.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Ray, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And new batches of votes are still being dropped in the Senate races in Arizona and Nevada. Full analysis coming up. Plus, what's going to happen in the GOP leadership races. Congressman Jim Banks and Tom Bevan here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Republicans will work with anyone who's willing to join us to deliver this new direction that Americans have demanded. But there's no time to waste. Our work begins now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin McCarthy has announced his run for Speaker of the House. And as the GOP prepares for its House majority, leadership will matter more than ever in managing a smaller than expected majority. Congressman Jim Banks wasted no time. He threw his hat into the whip race this morning. Congressman Jim Banks joins us now. Congressman, does a slimmer margin affect these leadership elections? Because I know \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  from Minnesota, he's interested in the leadership position that you're after. Some are saying that McCarthy could be vulnerable. I saw some comments from the Freedom Caucus tonight. Your reaction to this, the results from last night?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS, (R-IN) REPUBLICAN STUDY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: Well, Laura, at the end of the day, the American people gave us the majority. It might be a very slim majority, but they gave us a majority to fight back against the radical left, not to give in to the demands of the radical left and the radical Democrats, but to fight back against them. Over 80 percent of Americans believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing a bad job and America is on the wrong track. And they want us to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why I threw my hat in the ring to be the whip of -- and a part of the Republican leadership to make sure that we do what we say we're going to do. I actually believe the outcome would have been better last night if the voters trusted Republicans. But they've given Republicans majorities before, and we let them down. So, if over the ex-in two years we do what we say we're going to do and hold Democrats accountable and stop the radical Biden agenda, then I believe the voters will give an even bigger majority in 2024 and the White House with it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, according to the Washington compost, \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , who I mentioned is also running for whip, told reporters that he agreed with a lawmaker who told them that the smaller GOP majority will have to seek bipartisan consensus to pass legislation. So, he's pushing towards what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said today that we have to work together, even as Biden went on to insult Republicans in his speech. So, what's your reaction to Emmer's comment.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: It's my hope that the Republican majority will work with Republicans to pass our agenda, to get to 218 plus votes to pass the agenda that we promised the American people in the commitment to America and the other promises that we made along the way. And the whip will play a very big role in doing that with Republican votes, not with Democrat votes. Again, don't give in to the radical left. Do what voters ask us to do. Stand up to them instead. And the whip -- the majority leader, the Speaker of the House, that's what the voters are entrusting us to do. We can't let them down.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, do you any \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  is too much of an establishment guy to get the excitement going in the caucus that does exist?\par \par \uc2 BANKS: Again, Laura, I've thrown my hat in the ring because I believe that leadership matters in the Republican Conference. And we have to have a leadership team that's ready to roll up our sleeves and ready to go and push, advance this agenda, not just the policy agenda, but the vigorous oversight agenda of the Biden administration that we have not yet seen, with Jim Jordan in charge with the Judiciary Committee, a lot of other committee chairman who are ready to go to provide oversight and investigations of what happened in Afghanistan, holding big tech accountable, investigating China for the origins of COVID-19. We have a lot of oversight to do as well. It takes strong Republican leadership to get it done. I intend to be at that table.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, you're being very diplomatic. Congressman, it's good to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 While we await results in Arizona and Nevada and the Senate races there, that will prove vital for the GOP majority. It's important to note why these are so crucial beyond this cycle. Now, of the 33 Senate seats up for election in 2024, 23 are held by Democrats. Here now is Tom Bevan, co- founder and president of Real Clear Politics. Tom, we're talking about 2024, but this is going to be quite something, is it not, and another opportunity for Republicans to get this right?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, COFOUNDER, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: Yes, the map in 2024 is actually very good for Republicans. I think they're just defending very few seats. I think the top Democratic -- top target on that list for Democrats was \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who barely won his race ousting \uc2 Bill Nelson\uc2 . But given what happened in Florida last night, they may want to rethink that strategy.\par \par \uc2 And then you go down the list, Braun in Indiana, \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  in Tennessee, these are safe seats. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  in Missouri. These are seats that Republicans should easily hold on to. Ted Cruz is going to draw a lot of attention, especially during a presidential year. He was in a fairly close race last time around against \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 . So I suspect Cruz will be one. But the Democrats are defending an awful lot of territory in states that Republicans have a shot at.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  is going to be up. You've got Manchin in West Virginia. You've got \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  in Wisconsin. And you've got \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  in Michigan. So there's a lot of targets on the table for Republican in 2024.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tom, there's some interesting insights about the independent exit polling last night. What did you see there?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Well, that's probably to me the most stunning thing about those exit polls is that Democrats won independents by two points. If you look back at midterm elections, independents have traditionally sided with the party -- the out party, the party that's seeking change, not for the status quo by 10, 12, 15, 20 points if you look back at 2010, 2014, 2018. So this is a strange development from last night, and it looks like Republicans turned out, but it looks like while they turned out, they lost the independent vote in a lot of these really close House races around the country, and obviously it's playing at the Senate level as well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And to what do you attribute that?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: I'm not sure. It looks like -- it looks like independent voters, while they told pollsters, and I think it was true even from the exit polls that the economy was their number one issue, they did not hold Biden responsible for the economy and for inflation in the same way that they did with other issues.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Tom, we'll be unpacking this for days to come.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We'll see what happens with these last vote counts. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Now, when we come barks, a bright spot from last night's election results you might not have heard about. The Last Bite will tell you, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIE PICKREN: We now have 32 percent of eighth graders in America are illiterate. Among Hispanics it's at 37 percent. We do not have time for this in education. We have to get back to the basics and keep this social agenda out of the classroom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was Julie Pickren at our Houston town hall last month. She was running for the Texas State Board of Education. And tonight, we can tell you that not only did she win, but the board strengthened its GOP majority to 10 out of 15 seats. That's fantastic news. Congrats, Julie.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Thank you for watching. Remember, it's America now and forever. Keep the face smiling. "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With New York Congressman-Elect Mike Lawler (R); New Arizona Vote Totals; Interview With Congreswoman-Elect Anna Paulina Luna (R); Hurricane Nicole Expected To Bring Strong Wind And Storm Surge To Florida\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Bryan Norcross \par \uc2 8,909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: There is a little button on your remote control, it says "DVR," I think. You just hit it and then it automatically records the show.\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is up next.\par \par \uc2 And always remember one thing, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 What exactly happened in yesterday's Midterm Elections? Well, we wish we could tell you with some precision, that's our job, but we can't because in a number of key races around the country, we still don't have a vote total.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, for example, there is no declared winner in the Senate race or in the Governor's race. At this point, it seems likely that both Kari Lake and Blake Masters will win. We'll be speaking to Kari Lake in just a minute about that.\par \par \uc2 But according to news reports, the official results may not be in for more than a month -- a month. Officials in Arizona told CNBC today that they are, "Prepared to work through Thanksgiving, and possibly Christmas as well." That means results by New Year's in a race that was held in early November. That seems late.\par \par \uc2 How late is it? Well, by comparison, the results of the 1862 Midterm Elections, which were tabulated by candlelight, without machines or even electricity in the middle of a raging Civil War were clear before the end of the week. That was the entire country. Arizona is a single State, which by the way is a fraction the size of Florida, which as you may have noticed counted its votes in less than a day, sort of Brazil, an entire country.\par \par \uc2 That seems embarrassing, if not like a full blown emergency. Counting the votes isn't some added extra you get for government. If they have a surplus, like fighting climate change or bringing equity, counting the votes is a core function of government, along with law enforcement, maintaining the roads, keeping the border secure, efficient elections are the reason you pay taxes.\par \par \uc2 But Arizona doesn't seem to have them. Why is that? "Don't ask," command CNN. If you've got questions about this or any other election, no unauthorized questions. Instead, watch CNN, or if you don't have cable, simply trust your local officials.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KING, CNN CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: You see, that's our first votes and that's the wonder of democracy, whether you're a Democrat or a Republican.\par \par \uc2 And I wanted to point that out to be a little bit of the crank in the room following Brianna there. Stay off social media people. If you're trying to figure out -- if you're trying to figure out, are there really issues with voting? Trust your local officials, trust us here, trusted a news source that you know, and trust to be honest about this.\par \par \uc2 If they are doing their jobs, they're doing it right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "If they are doing their jobs, they're doing your right." Really CNN? Could we have a little more reporting? And how right are they doing it? Pretty funny. But we digress.\par \par \uc2 The state of our election systems in many places is indeed pretty embarrassing, but so honestly, were the results of last night's elections. Republicans swore they were going to sweep a red tsunami. That's what they told us and we, to be honest, cautiously believed them, but they did not sweep, not even close to sweeping.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party in the end may take control of the House and the Senate, but only by a tiny margin at best. That's great. But it was not the plan. The plan was really simple, it seemed easy we could go -- an unpopular President, a faltering economy, an open border, the looming risk of nuclear war. How about that? Put all those together, how could there not be a massive Republican win nationally? It wins everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Well, there weren't. Some exceptions, but overall, there weren't. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was not punished.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he was out there bragging about himself today. Pretty frustrating.\par \par \uc2 You wanted Republicans to win not simply because they are so great, but because Democrats are so very bad and that is not an overstatement. So, what happened?\par \par \uc2 Well, before we give you our theories as to what happened, one obvious point, the people whose job it was to win but did not win should go do something else now. We're speaking specifically of the Republican leadership of the House and the Senate and of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . It is nothing personal, some of them are no doubt nice people, but they took hundreds of millions of dollars to paint the map red and they didn't.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't mean they're evil, it doesn't mean they should be jailed. It does mean they shouldn't be promoted. No one should ever be rewarded for failure.\par \par \uc2 If there is a truly conservative principle in life, it is the principle of the meritocracy. You reward excellence, you do not reward mediocrity, and when you do things, it falls apart.\par \par \uc2 Democrats kept promoting \uc2 Tony Fauci\uc2 , despite his obvious ineptitude. What is that? That's corruption. Republicans should never do anything like that. And if they do, what's the point of voting for them? They're no different.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, why did Republicans underperform last night? You're hearing a lot of people saying, it was about abortion. Suburban ladies were mad about Roe. That's certainly plausible in some places, probably true.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, a number of resolutely pro-life Republicans thrived statewide. That would include Ron Johnson in Wisconsin, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  in North Carolina, pro-lifers all. So abortion may have been a factor, but it's not the whole answer.\par \par \uc2 Many others are saying that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the reason Republicans didn't do as well as they thought they would. That's a more complicated question. The truth is we can't really see the entire picture this early. Excuse us.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the truth is Trump has always been a mixed blessing politically. The downsides are marbled in with the upsides. But in this case, he is certainly not the single cause of anything.\par \par \uc2 Republicans last night suffered a fair amount of down ballot losses in races that had nothing to do with Trump. In Michigan, for example, so whether you like Trump or not, and many don't, and a lot do, it's a lot more complicated than just him.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the most amusingly stupid explanation of all, excuse us. Election talk, so bad candidates were the problem and that's all over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . All the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  pundits are telling you now, the candidates were subpar and that was the problem, candidate quality matters. Well, of course, strictly speaking, that is true. The quality of the candidate does matter. But really, how much does it matter? Well, let's see.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got elected President two years ago from his basement. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  became a US senator last night. Does anyone think \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was a quality candidate? Is that why he won? Because they had quality candidates on the left. Did the voters of Pennsylvania really want a brain damaged candidate who's never had a real job? Did they think he was more impressive than the guy who spent his career doing heart transplants? Probably not.\par \par \uc2 You've got to give them credit for at least knowing who they were voting for, and they voted for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He won anyway. What does that tell you? It tells you that in some cases, candidate quality is not actually the most important thing. What is? Well, the mechanics of an election, they matter. In fact, they matter sometimes more than any individual running in the election. The way people vote makes a big difference to the outcome.\par \par \uc2 And so by the way does access to channels with communication. Why does that matter? Well, because you can say whatever you want, but if no one hears you, you're not really speaking. And that's the case for Republicans, so often.\par \par \uc2 As if tonight, Republicans can communicate their message, unencumbered on a single cable television channel and a handful of relatively low traffic websites. That's it. The rest of the American media amounts to a gigantic filter designed to distort Republicans are saying, it's a campaign apparatus, and only the Democrats have it.\par \par \uc2 You can whine about that, "Oh, the media are liberal." But it's not about liberal or conservative, it is about winning elections, and Democrats can win because they have that. So, if Republicans want to win elections, too, they might spend some money to fix that to achieve parity.\par \par \uc2 So to restate as of tonight, Democrats have far more control of the election machinery and almost total control of the American media, and Republicans don't. These are not ideological problems. It's not a question of who is right on the issues, that is settled, certainly in our mind, but probably in the minds of even people who would vote Republican if it occurred to them, but it doesn't because they don't know what they stand for.\par \par \uc2 These are questions again, not of who is right or who is wrong, but of who makes it into elected office, of who wields power, and many on the right don't seem to understand this at all. They don't care about the details.\par \par \uc2 Two and a half years ago, the last administration, those Republican allies in Congress watched passively, seemingly in glassy-eyed sedation, as the Democratic Party used the pretext of COVID to rewrite election laws around the country in order to get its own candidates into office. They didn't do it by accident, they knew what they were doing.\par \par \uc2 Last night, those laws, many of which are still on the books paid off generously. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  bombed in his one public debate, you saw it. He humiliated himself, he made a mockery of the election, but it didn't matter by that point. Thanks to early voting, Fetterman's margin was already in the bank.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 70 percent of Democrats had voted early in the Pennsylvania races, only 20 percent of Republicans did. So, okay, it's over. But it doesn't need to be repeated. These are fixable problems. You can get your message out, you can force the other side, if you try hard enough, to agree on fair election rules, but you can't do any of that unless you acknowledge these problems exist. So those are probably the real problems.\par \par \uc2 But enough of the depressing recap. There were bright spots last night. There were, as dispirited as you may have been, and we think it's important to enjoy them, always. The good news is we're savoring.\par \par \uc2 So the first piece of good news is actually the flip side of the worst piece of news, which is the \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  one in Pennsylvania. Not only did \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  win, but he won without speaking a single coherent sentence for the entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now, you may have been appalled by that, but in some sense, it is an achievement. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , in winning, shattered the thickest of all glass ceilings, which for centuries has prevented the unapologetically brain damaged from winning statewide election.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is not just a US senator, now, he's a pioneer and there is nothing the Democratic Party loves, reveres, and elevates more quickly than a pioneer. Take a look at the White House Press Secretary.\par \par \uc2 So the question is given his awe inspiring achievement, how can \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  not be the frontrunner for the Democratic nomination in 2024? We can't control that, we want him and Kamala to fight it out, but in the meantime, expect a huge amount of uncomfortable entertainment value from Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , savor every moment.\par \par \uc2 So the second piece of good news for Republicans that they really should celebrate because it's great, sometimes watching your enemies get vanquished is a virtue in itself. The DCCC, Congressional Chairman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  last, probably the oiliest, slipperiest, and by far not the stupidest member of Congress on the Democratic side, lost to a man Michael Lawler in a race that nobody thought a Republican could ever win. In a moment, we will speak to Lawler and ask how we did it.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like Lawler won for a couple of reasons. The first is that \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  like so many other Democrats spent the last year-and- a-half huffing and puffing about January 6th. They're going to win on January 6th, and unlike most of them, clever as he is, Maloney seemed to actually believe it. He is the one who claimed that Officer Brian Sicknick was "killed on January 6th."\par \par \uc2 Last night after his defeat, Maloney once again invoked, can you guess it? January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): A short while ago, I spoke to my opponent, Michael Lawler and congratulated him on a good win. We hope the high watermark of some of the anger and the division that we have dealt with this entire cycle from January 6th on through, and we hope for something better for our country because that's what all Americans deserve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We mentioned he was clever. So, what seemed like a gracious concession, and we're all in need of those on both sides, was in fact, another attack on January 6th, the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 What's interesting is that Maloney's district is fairly upscale. It includes the North suburbs of New York City. It went for Biden by five points just a couple of years ago, and that support collapsed last night.\par \par \uc2 Now, if there is one group Republicans have had trouble winning recently, since the inversion of 2016, it is upper income White collar voters, but in that district, they went Republican. So, Republicans should study what happened there and not just there, all over New York. Democrats lost several seats across the Hudson Valley and Long Island in very blue areas, and that means the Democrats, this is their base, upscale suburban voters, Democrats are more vulnerable than they believe they are. And again, Republicans should study how exactly that happened.\par \par \uc2 One race that will be studied for quite a long time and enjoyed for at least six years is what happened in Ohio last night. Republicans in Ohio stunned the Democratic Party. Now, they'll tell you, "Oh it was a Republican State." But even by the standards of a so-called Republican State, Republicans dominated in Ohio last night. No Democrat came within 10 points of any Republican in races for executive positions or Supreme Court races.\par \par \uc2 Now in the now famous race for Senate in Ohio, Democrats spent tens of millions of dollars more than the winner did. That would be JD Vance. But JD Vance won, and not only did he win, he won as a decent person, and that's rare in any legislative body, and he won on the right message. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO SENATOR-ELECT: Now, the people of Ohio have given us a job and what we need to do over the next couple of years, over the next six years for the full -- the full length of this Senate term, whoever is in the majority, whatever the President looks like, we have a very simple job to do, it is to go to work every single day and fight for the people of Ohio, fight for our workers.\par \par \uc2 Fight for our families, fight for the people struggling with the opioid addiction problem, fight for the people -- the single moms struggling to raise babies just like my mom raised me.\par \par \uc2 We have got a great State.\par \par \uc2 Whether you voted for me or not, the thing that I promise to do is go to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and fight every single day for the people of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to you, we get an opportunity to do just that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, we have lost more than 100,000 Americans in the last year, mostly young people from drug ODs, that's just the last. Year many hundreds of thousands over the past five years, and yet, when was the last time you saw someone mention it in a victory speech? I will fight for them, their families. The people left behind from this epidemic from China through Mexico destroying huge parts of America. JD Vance said that, and he clearly means it.\par \par \uc2 And he won by a lot despite being outspent by a lot. So, that is really good news. Almost as good. We will not see for quite some time the likes of \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , again, because they lost, yet again. How many offices have these to run for? How much money have Democratic donors spent to get them elected? A lot. Hundreds of millions of dollars, and both are unemployed, because it doesn't matter how much you spend on the advertising campaign for the dog food, if the dogs don't like it, they won't eat it. That's an old saying from political consultants, and in this case, it is true.\par \par \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  rejected thoroughly in favor of people who are not professional politicians in a number of cases on the Republican side. So, now the last and probably biggest piece of good news, as you already know, is what happened last night in the State of Florida, put bluntly, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  absolutely killed it. And the closer you look at the numbers, the more interesting and revealing and heartening it is.\par \par \uc2 Voters of every education level supported \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  over \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , that has not happened typically in elections statewide. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  this even one among post grads by seven percent. If you think that sounds normal, go look at the past five elections. Republicans do not win statewide among people with postgraduate degrees, but DeSantis did. He won all, but five counties in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So to the astonishment of MSNBC and the entire Democratic political establishment, DeSantis even managed to flip Miami-Dade County. Now, Miami- Dade is by definition a Democratic county, but he was not the only Republican who won there, another did as well.\par \par \uc2 Miami-Dade went for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  by 30 points in 2016. Listen to the audible gasps at MSNBC as they realize Hispanic voters had betrayed their explicit instructions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: I'm told we just got Miami-Dade. This is a big one in Florida. Let's take a look at -- okay. This we got the -- yes.\par \par \uc2 OFF-CAMERA PANELIST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So let's put this in some perspective. Miami-Dade County has two-and-three-quarter million people.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, this was a Democratic county by 30 points. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  won this county by 30 points. Miami-Dade is 70 percent Hispanic.\par \par \uc2 And look at this, in the mail-in and early vote, which again tends to be more Democratic friendly; \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , the Republican, is outright leading in Miami-Dade County by seven points.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Exactly and good for them for being honest about what happened. Really no lying about it and again, it wasn't just \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It was also \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No Republican has won in Miami-Dade County in 20 years, but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  did it. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  did it. In the end, DeSantis won by nearly 20 points.\par \par \uc2 Things were so bad for Democrats in Florida that \uc2 CBS\uc2  went looking for \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  supporters and couldn't find any in public. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: By the way, I've got to point out that once we started digesting our food and digesting the day, we realized that on that day long trip, we spoke to dozens of people up and down the coast of Florida, all in precincts, by the way, that voted for President Biden in 2020, but to our surprise, we did not run into a supporter of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' Democratic opponent, that would be \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Poor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , but this -- I mean, of course, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  was the lamest possible candidate the Democrats ever could have run, probably because they knew that DeSantis was going to win.\par \par \uc2 So, this is one of those rare elections in which voters were, of course, rejecting the other side, but in this election, a lot of those voters appear, and the polling shows this, to be affirming the side they voted for.\par \par \uc2 They weren't just voting against, they were voting for. They were voting for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Pretty amazing, actually. According to DeSantis, this is a realignment. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): You know, over these past four years, we've seen major challenges for the people of our State, for the citizens of the United States, and, above all, for the cause of freedom.\par \par \uc2 We saw freedom in our very way of life and so many other jurisdictions in this country wither on the vine, Florida held the line.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We chose facts over fear. We chose education over indoctrination.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We chose law and order over rioting and disorder.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Thanks to the overwhelming support of the people of Florida, we not only won an election, we have rewritten the political map.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, as disappointed as you may have been by the results last night, what happened in Florida is legitimately a big deal. It will probably a while before we know exactly what it means, but we know for certain that it was out of the ordinary, unexpected and profound.\par \par \uc2 And as we told you a minute ago, the other thing that happened that was kind of great that we are enjoying is that the guy in charge of getting Democrats elected to Congress himself lost, his name is \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 . He represented the Hudson Valley. He was beaten by Mike Lawler who is now the congressman-elect and he joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Lawler, thanks so much for coming on. How did you do this?\par \par \uc2 MIKE LAWLER (R), NEW YORK CONGRESSMAN-ELECT: Thanks for having me, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 Well look, the Democrats control everything in Washington, Albany, and New York City for the first time in our nation's history and they have created an absolute mess: A 41-year record high on inflation, surging crime, skyrocketing energy costs, and a porous Southern border with not only a massive inflow of illegal immigration, but drugs coming into our communities killing 300 Americans a day.\par \par \uc2 And voters in New York, were fed up and they had enough. They wanted to restore balance and commonsense to every level of government. We've picked up four congressional seats in New York, and we are going to have 11 of New York's 26 members as Republicans come January and that is historic.\par \par \uc2 And I think, in my district, especially, you know, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  as chair of the DCCC sent a memo to State Democrats back in January of this year demanding that they gerrymander New York's congressional maps and knock Republicans down to three seats.\par \par \uc2 Well, they did it and then the Courts intervened. The Democrat-appointed Court of Appeals threw out the maps, called it gerrymandered and unconstitutional, and appointed a Special Master, and we got a fair map.\par \par \uc2 And when I looked at the map, I knew I could win the seat. Forty-two percent of it is Rockland County, where I'm born and raised. I've lived my entire life. My family has been here over a hundred years. We won Rockland by 10 points and that's why I defeated \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just -- we're savoring the irony, probably not enough victories to celebrate, but the ones that came through are worth celebrating and this one is especially amazing, because this is the guy in charge of getting Democrats elected to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Do you think he saw it coming?\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: No, not really, because he was galivanting across the globe to Paris and Geneva and London just a month ago, raising money for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and it wasn't until about two weeks ago that he realized he had a problem when he had to call in \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  to come campaign. He asked \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  to send an e-mail and not 30,000 that she deleted.\par \par \uc2 But he also asked Jill Biden to come in and do a fundraiser for just last week. You know, this district -- this district is home to the Clintons and \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , and the fact that they now have a Republican Congressman speaks volumes to where the electorate is.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's pretty great. It really is, and we're going to celebrate. Congressman-elect Mike Lawler of Rockland County, New York. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we told you, the counting is still underway in Maricopa County, Arizona where about 63 percent of the State's entire population lives. Apparently new results about to come in. You're seeing it on your screen right there.\par \par \uc2 The final results of two of the most closely watched races in the country, Kari Lake for Governor and Blake Masters for Senate.\par \par \uc2 What exactly is going on with these races? Well, a live report from the \uc2 RNC\uc2  attorney on the ground and also Kari Lake herself joins us straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, for all the focus on the elections, the story has been missed by many, another hurricane on its way to the United States, probably worse than a lot of people expected it to be. I hate to tell you that, but it is coming very soon.\par \par \uc2 A full report, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: This is a FOX News Alert: These are long voting lines in Arizona. That was yesterday's chaos at polling sites across the State, particularly Maricopa County, the population center of Arizona. That chaos continues today.\par \par \uc2 There is a live picture from vote counting underway in that county, which includes Tempe and Phoenix. We totally received new results at the top of the hour out of that county, but they are apparently still delayed. What is going on exactly?\par \par \uc2 Harmeet Dhillon is the Chairman of the Republican National Lawyers Association. She's in Phoenix tonight where legal challenges may soon be underway. She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet, great to see you. What is the latest in these two races?\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, CHAIRMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL LAWYERS ASSOCIATION: Well, the latest, Tucker is that the Maricopa County party is like way behind. The county is way behind in counting these votes. And one of the reasons here is that they switched a couple of years ago to what is called a Vote Center Model.\par \par \uc2 So instead of the old-fashioned model, where you would go vote in a precinct, they actually cut the number of 