nna Paulina Luna won last night, too. She took over \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 's old seat in Congress. Pretty great. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations on your victory. How did you do it?\par \par \uc2 ANNA PAULINA LUNA (R), FLORIDA CONGRESSWOMAN-ELECT: I can tell you that there was a strong emphasis from the beginning on grassroots activism. You know, Tucker, we were outspent $12 million to one. I had \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  dropping a robocall the Friday before the election, and I beat them into a pulp while I was doing the grassroots outreach. So, that is the emphasis. That's the secret sauce and also to hold true to your convictions.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  did a robocall. I can't -- now, you've baited me. I can't resist asking. What did he say?\par \par \uc2 LUNA: You know, I didn't actually listen to it, but I know that he did do the robocall. And ironically enough, my Democrat opponent was actually funded by a single billionaire that he was related to who was tied to not only Obama, but \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2  and you know what they cooked up.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amazing. How many -- you just got elected, you should not know this. I don't know what I'm just interested. Like, are there any Republicans who had more money going into the races last night, anywhere, do you know?\par \par \uc2 LUNA: I don't think so, at least not in the State of Florida. It seems as though the Democrats don't like conservatives, especially not those that can target the largest voting minority in the country.\par \par \uc2 You know, I am first and foremost American, but I am a veteran. And I also too know how to target the largest voting minority in the country. So it seems as though that they saw me a threat, but I hope to be their worst nightmare in the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, good luck in Washington. You'll find it interesting.\par \par \uc2 LUNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Great to see you. Thank you, Anna Paulina Luna.\par \par \uc2 LUNA: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, we told you, just joking, by the way at the top of the hour that hey, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has broken the glass ceiling where people with brain damage never get elected, but he did. So, maybe he'll be the Democratic Party's nominee for president.\par \par \uc2 Well, MSNBC took it seriously. We've got the tape.\par \par \uc2 Also news out of Florida tonight that is not political, a hurricane hitting there tonight. A live update, minutes away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We opened the show by trying to look at the bright side of last night and we joked, entirely joking, of course that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  may be the Democratic nominee next time. I mean, he is kind of out of it, but he is not as out as the guy who currently holds the job. So, we were just mocking the Democratic Party, but then we realized it is impossible to create a parody of anything left-wing right now in the United States because they always beat you to it. Parody is indistinguishable from reality.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC, hours before we spoke, and we didn't even know this had suggested it but in dead seriousness, here's the august, Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Fetterman as a nominee at some point for President, I know there's some variables obviously. But --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just a few.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Just a few, but I just -- you know, what he did in the super red, deep red parts of Pennsylvania and the way that he ran ahead of Biden, as you were saying, ran ahead of Trump, I mean, it just makes -- it makes you wonder about his future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, it does make you wonder, and by the way, Fetterman should demand it. If he is not the nominee of the Democratic Party in two years, it's an ableist party and a racist one. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  2024.\par \par \uc2 So, not to brag, but we thought Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was doing interesting things a year-and-a-half ago, when we sat down with him for about an hour and asked him questions that weren't strictly speaking related to politics, including what do you think makes a good leader?\par \par \uc2 And so, we dug up the tape because we thought it was relevant to what happened last night here it is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I think leadership matters and willingness to take decisive, strong action without being worried about your own shadow really makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 I'll talk about the first part of my term and then more recently with COVID. When I became Governor, I fired \uc2 Scott Israel\uc2 , the Sheriff of Broward County who had mishandled the Parkland massacre a year before. I fired Susan Booker, the Palm Beach County Supervisor of Elections, who had botched a number of elections and then also got \uc2 Brenda Snipes\uc2  from Broward County to resign.\par \par \uc2 So those were very quick, decisive actions. And the results were, well, at least with the elections, we had one of the smoothest selections in the country in 2020. People at the time, it was considered controversial, but it was the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward to COVID. You know, I had to make a lot of decisions that cut against the grain of conventional elite opinion, certainly the media, we took incoming every single day, but you make the decisions, you know why you're doing it, you stand your ground.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're in a situation where March of 2021, States wished they could have had Florida's performance. I mean, our unemployment rate is 4.8 percent. States like California are nine percent. Our COVID mortality is less than the national average, even though we have the oldest population in the country and we've had schools open, one of only three or four states in the country were 100 percent of our parents have access to in-person education.\par \par \uc2 And so we got the big things right. In the moment, there was a lot of people that were throwing stones at us, but take the action, stand your ground, and hold the line.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you went to Harvard, you went to Yale. You were a naval officer. You served in Congress for six years. You've certainly been around a lot of elite institutions. You ignored them as they criticized you.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Of course. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Why?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, first of all, when I first went to Yale, I grew up in Florida as a blue collar kid. I showed up my first day in jean shorts and a t-shirt, because that's what we wore in the West Coast of Florida. It was a major culture shock, that was not something that was received very warmly, and I never quite fit in there. It was a total culture shock to me.\par \par \uc2 And I really rebelled against the leftist orthodoxy that I saw on that campus, and then when I see those in other institutions, and so I've always had a really strong distrust of some of the elites in American society, because I think they've gotten a lot of things wrong over the years. And so that was actually natural.\par \par \uc2 I don't seek the approval of elites. I got elected by people here in Florida. Those are the folks that I was elected to serve and I don't really care what people in the beltway or in New York City think about what we're doing in Florida.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just interesting though, because people who -- and you see it a lot -- people come from some little town out in the provinces, and they show up at a place like Yale or Harvard Law School where they become a naval officer, which I think is maybe not that different. It is an elite institution. And they decide, you know, this is the world I really love. These are the values that I believe. It sounds like you came away from your experiences more contemptuous.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: And appreciate where I came from, quite frankly. I mean, I remember going to Yale, sitting in class, I had never heard anybody criticize America like everything was about how bad America was in any these classes. I never heard anybody -- I go to church, you know, I never heard anyone criticize God or Christians. And it just it was all so --\par \par \uc2 You know, Yale's motto is "For God, for country, for Yale." They hated God they hated the country and they obviously had a weird way about going their institution.\par \par \uc2 So seeing that was really eye opening and I realized very early on, this is not quite where I personally fit in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: FOX News Alert for you: New votes, 24 hours after the election coming out right now in Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , total 953,783; Kari Lake 940,716. What does this mean, exactly? Kari Lake joins us now with her view.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, thanks for coming back. What does this tell you about where this is going?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, I look back at last night and we were down 15 points. Now, we're down two-tenths of a point and it is going in our direction and it is really odd that Maricopa County election officials who are the most incompetent in the country are dragging their feet in delivering what everybody wants to know, the 650,000 votes that they are hopefully going to start telling us about starting tomorrow. That were the Republican voters, the ones who showed up on game day to vote, and that's what we're waiting for.\par \par \uc2 We think they're going to start delivering those results tomorrow, 275,000 of those votes, Tucker, were people who had a mail-in ballot but did not trust the system and wanted to walk it in on election day.\par \par \uc2 So we know that this big group of voters, 650,000 are going to swing very heavily our way and we believe we will be victorious at the end of the day and maybe in a very comfortable way we're going to be seeing this continue. As I said, we were 15 points down last night. Now, we're down two-tenths of a point, we're going to see where I start to take the lead very soon.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I don't think that we should get used to being treated like this by our government, and we pay them to run elections. They can't. Who is in charge of elections in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: A man named Stephen Richer is a big, a big part of it in Maricopa County, and then my opponent, shocking. My opponent in the Miss I don't debate, and I hide in the basement, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , Secretary of State is in charge of our elections as well.\par \par \uc2 So we have two of the most incompetent people in all of government in this country, running elections in Arizona. But I want the people of Arizona to know this. We're going to see this start going in my direction. And on day one, we're going to start working to reform our elections so that all voters can feel confident that we have honest elections.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, yes, I mean, are you struck by how the news media keep telling us this is totally normal. It's always been this way. It's like muggings on the Subway, it's always been that way. This has not always been this way, has it?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: No. I mean, when I started voting in 1988, you voted, you showed up on Election Day, you were given a ballot, they hand counted them, and you knew who the winner was before you went to bed.\par \par \uc2 I remember the first election. I mean, I've been covering elections since the 90s, as a journalist in my old job. And I remember in 2000, when that election went all night, and it was disputed between Gore and Bush, that was kind of the start of when we started seeing a lot more disputes and distrust in elections.\par \par \uc2 This is not rocket science, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 We can go back to the way we used to do it, which worked. It's a shame that my homeowners association election is far more secure and accurate than the elections we seem to have here in Arizona, and we're going to change that and this is one of the reasons people are voting for me. And one of the reasons that I will win is the voters in Arizona are tired of shoddy elections that are run by imbeciles and that's going to change.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They're kind of proving your point. Really quick, what's your plan tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, we're going to wait for the votes to come in, and we think they're going to start -- really starting to go heavily our way and we'll see what happens from there. We do feel very confident, I have a hundred percent confidence that we win this, so I am sleeping well. We know how things work in Maricopa County, they drag their feet and we will wait. God did not, you know decide that this was going to be an easy route.\par \par \uc2 When you are trying to work through corruption at high levels and incompetence at high levels, you've got to dig through and get to the other side of that, and when we get to the other side of this and when, that's where we will start to make sure we root out all corruption in our government and all incompetence.\par \par \uc2 We don't want incompetent people in our government anymore.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, we certainly have them. Kari Lake, great to see you tonight. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Back to our FOX News Alert. A major hurricane is amazingly about to hit the State of Florida again. Our FOX Weather hurricane specialist, Bryan Norcross joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Bryan, when is this coming? And what will it be like?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN NORCROSS, FOX WEATHER HURRICANE SPECIALIST: It's coming tonight, Tucker. And the big thing about this storm is not the intensity so much, it is the size of it. It's the scope of it.\par \par \uc2 Take a look here, here's the center of it now. When that comes ashore. Yes, there are going to be strong winds and so forth, but this big band here is already coming ashore with strong winds that can take out power and so forth. And then when we look at the wider view, we have these relentless winds pushing the water, the ocean water up against the coast.\par \par \uc2 We have flooding all up and down the Florida coast right now. It's undermined homes along the coast and that's going to continue to be the situation through the night.\par \par \uc2 The National Hurricane Center makes this big yellow bubble to say where are the disruptive winds? And then we have the strongest winds right here in the orange. Watch as it comes ashore here tonight.\par \par \uc2 So about one o'clock in the morning, we expect the peak winds right at the coast. It's called the Treasure Coast up to Melbourne just south of Cape Canaveral. That's where the biggest push of water is going to be, but the water is going to be piled against all of the Florida coast. And then tomorrow, it continues as the strong winds cover the entire northern half of the State during the day.\par \par \uc2 And then finally by late in the day, things start to let up and the system moves up the East Coast. Heavy rain all the way up to New England over the weekend -- Tucker\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: All the way up to New England. Bryan Norcross for us, FOX Weather hurricane specialist. Great to see you. Thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 We've got continuous coverage of what is being called Hurricane Nicole. You can get it on the FOX Weather app and stream FOX Weather 24/7.\par \par \uc2 We are out of time, obviously a lot going on in the world and we will keep you up to date on it as it unfolds. It's pretty amazing moment to be watching.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow night at 8:00 PM, but in the meantime, the Great \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  takes over from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  On What He Plans To Do Differently; Control Of Congress Hangs In Balance; \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Touts Re-Election Victory; Early Predictions Of Republican Surge Fall Short; Trump Footprint On Political Landscape; Some \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  Races Not Yet Called In Midterm Elections; Georgia Senate Election Between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Headed To Runoff; Arizona Senate Race Too Close To Call; Republicans Likely To Gain Control Of House Of Representatives While Democrats May Hold Senate; Russian Officials Announcing Withdraw Of All Troops In And Around Russian Held Ukrainian City Of Kherson; President Biden States He Will Not Change White House Policies After Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Chad Pergram, Steve Harrigan, Doug  Schoen, Aishah Hasnie, Alicia Acuna, Mike Emanuel, Greg Palkot \par \uc2 7,814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Members who worked both in their community as well, as either the army National Guard or army reserve and they are all guaranteed placement in the army reserves after graduation since 2015. The Minutemen campaign has provided 5,800 cadets with over 115 million in scholarships.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We knew you could get that one more thing in under time. Thanks, everybody. It's been great to be with you tonight. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I miss you guys.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Come on back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's lonely in Washington. All right, see you again.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the nation's capitol and much of the rest of the country still waiting to find out who will be running things here for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Many key races in both the Senate and the House remain uncalled tonight. Too close to call, too early to call following a midterm election that failed to produce sweeping gains for the Republicans and once again signaled how deeply divided this nation remains.\par \par \uc2 We have reporters in all the hotspots tonight as well as complete analysis. President Biden just made his first post-election comments a short time ago from the White House, calling it a good day for America and a good day for democracy.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you Bret. It's clear the president believes the midterm takeaway is Americans support his agenda. Even if voters end up stripping Democrats control of Congress. He's doubling down on his approach to policy despite being confronted with the fact that 75 percent of Americans believe the country is on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 He was asked what he would change over the next two years, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Nothing, because they're just finding out what we're doing. The more they know about what we're doing, the more support there is. I'm confident these policies are working and that we're on the right path and we need to stick with them.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden said polls don't factor into his decision to run for reelection and he'll make that decision early next year. But he dodged when he was asked if he'd rather face former President Trump or Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in 2024, although he hinted at an effort to keep Trump from holding office again through the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, we just have to demonstrate that he will not take power by if we -- if he does run, making sure he under legitimate efforts of our Constitution does not become the next president again.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President Biden didn't elaborate on what exactly that means or if he was referencing the investigations that Trump is currently facing. But if Republicans end up controlling the House, Kevin McCarthy has pledged investigations that would roil the Biden White House, the U.S. exit from Afghanistan, the crisis at the southern border and performance of Cabinet officials and not least of all, the president himself and his son Hunter. Biden was dismissive.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the American public wants to move on and get things done for them. But I think the American people will look at all of that for what it is. It's just almost comedy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the president never once mentioned the southern border or immigration reform, even when asked in what areas he might be able to work with the GOP and the White House has repeatedly blamed Republicans for inaction on that front.\par \par \uc2 There were no questions on those topics either and he didn't respond when I shouted. And he said he had to end the news conference because he had to make some calls with Republican leadership including Kevin McCarthy, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Even though we do not have the final results in yet, plans are already being made for new leadership and new agendas in Congress. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has that part of the story tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats fared better than analysts expected but Republicans surged in Florida and upstate New York, the GOP toppled a giant, the Democrat in charge of House election efforts. \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  of New York.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN OF THE DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSIONAL CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE: House Democrats stood our ground and we believe November 8th, 2022 will be a signature date in American political history.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Control of the House may not be known for days but Republicans are forging ahead.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We will be in the majority and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will be in the minority.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Kevin McCarthy's angling for speaker, Steve Scalise for majority leader but a narrow GOP majority could hamper McCarthy's quest. McCarthy stumbled to succeed Speaker \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  seven years ago, lacking the votes.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): I'm supporting, Kevin. I know a lot of people like to focus on all of the drama in Washington.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's about the math. The Freedom Caucus could oppose McCarthy. Some conservatives aren't convinced he's committed to aggressively chopping spending or sold on impeachment. Other Republicans oppose those tactics.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): That's not something that I support at this juncture without a heavy investigation. Impeachment has been weaponized over the years.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The speaker isn't the member who collects the most votes, McCarthy must win an outright majority of the entire 435 member House. Democrats expected House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to retire but they now believe the attack on her husband embolden Pelosi to stay.\par \par \uc2 The lack of a red wave could also boost Pelosi. The speaker is now off to the climate conference in Egypt without tipping her hand.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): That's a conversation for another day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The president's party has not fared this well in the first midterm since President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  after 9/11. The GOP has stumped about its poor performance, especially since President Biden's approval ratings are in the tank, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Chad. Let's take a look at the current breakdown in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Here you have 49, 48, these three white boxes, those are yet to be called.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin's race was called early this afternoon with Republican incumbent Senator Ron Johnson defeating Lieutenant Governor Democrat Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 Democrats picked up the closely watched seat of Pennsylvania. The Lieutenant Governor there \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  besting Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 Now, as things stand 49, 48, as we mentioned, three seats are still at play.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at the House races when it comes to those contests and how that looks. First, let's go out to Arizona and where incumbent on the Senate race Democrat Senator Mark Kelly is ahead of Republican Blake Masters. A bright spot for Republicans though in Arizona is appearing in these House races where some redistricting, forcing some shift of seats, GOP candidates have won three so far, they lead in two of the four races that are still too close to call.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's take you out to Nevada and see how this picture with the House is a little bit different. In the Senate race, Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is more than two points ahead currently of incumbent senator Democrat, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . This would be a pickup.\par \par \uc2 On the House side, as you look at these districts, just one seat has been called for Republicans. All three incumbents that are Democrats are ahead in races that are still too close to call.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we take a look at the situation with the Senate races, let's just say for the sake of argument that the leads hold, and we just call those with the lead that's in place now. That means Nevada would go to the Republicans, Arizona would go to the Democrats and there you see 50 to 49 with once again, the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 What's happening in Georgia? The Democratic incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  will be in a runoff with Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , that will be December 6th. If it panned out like that, what if scenario, it would be control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  coming down to December 6th, much like it was when it was in January after the last election with two Senate run offs that determined control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republicans had a good showing elsewhere in the state in the state of Georgia. They had nine House races actually in Georgia that went to the Republicans and that is one more than in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Let's take you out now to really where you saw the biggest red wave for House candidates. Republicans won 20 total seats in Florida, four more than in 2020. Adding to that are the commanding wins for Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the victories once again really solidify Florida as being in the GOP column. Florida politically looks a lot more like Arkansas than it does of Florida of years past.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Tampa tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  won a landslide reelection in Florida over \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  by almost 20 points in a combative speech, DeSantis said the victory was a win for the ages and with the state of Florida has become a bastion of freedom for the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We reject woke ideology. We fight the woke in the legislature. We fight the woke in the schools. We fight the woke in the corporations. We will never ever surrender to the woke mob. Florida is where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: It was a big night for Republicans in Florida with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  winning easily over Representative Val Demings by 16 points.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN MACMANUS, FLORIDA POLITICAL ANALYST: This is important because Florida has been trending red and people are wondering just by how much and of course, at stake here is the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The victories not only solidify Florida's moved from a swing state to a red state. They've shaken up traditional coalitions. DeSantis was able to win in democratic strongholds like Miami-Dade County, which has a 70 percent Hispanic population, the first Republican to do so in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2  (R), MAYOR OF MIAMI: Republicans gaining with Hispanics is not just big for Miami Dade County for Florida, it's big for the United States. It's a template for success for Republican elections for a generation national.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: DeSantis's margin of victory has increased speculation that he will pursue the Republican nomination for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The governor is back in Tallahassee preparing for another potential hurricane, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan in Tampa. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down after the midterms. Some poor profit reports and other plunge for crypto today. The Dow lost 647, the S&P 500 resolve 80, the NASDAQ plunged 263.\par \par \uc2 Up next, just how reliable was the polling ahead of the midterms? We'll take a look inside the poll numbers, we'll talk to a pair of experts.\par \par \uc2 First, here's some of our fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami as Tropical Storm Nicole forces people from their homes in the Bahamas and threatens to grow into a rare November hurricane, possibly heading to Florida. Storm shut down theme parks and airports. It also prompted evacuation orders that included former President Trump's Mar- a-Lago club Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in San Francisco as \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta\uc2  lays off 11,000 people, about 13 percent of its workforce. The announcement came in a letter to employees from CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 . The move comes just a week after widespread layoffs at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  under new owner Elon Musk.\par \par \uc2 And a \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  capsule launched from Virginia Monday delivered several tons of supplies to the International Space Station despite a jam solar panel. Only one of the cargo ships two round solar panels opened following lift off. Flight controllers tried in vain to open the stock panel but were able to draw enough power with just one.\par \par \uc2 Some stories from outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT way outside. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Jam them up. We held the line.\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, COLORADO: We stayed true to our core, and our beliefs, and our values. And it didn't work out, not this time.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: We need better leadership in Washington, DC. And that's exactly what I promise to fight for every single day.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, winners and loser there. A big night last night. Let's talk about what the polls had right what they had wrong, and what we're seeing from that election. Joining us tonight, \uc2 Jeff Roe\uc2 , founder of Axiom Strategies. And Doug Schoen, former Clinton adviser, democratic pollster.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff, let's get 30,000 feet first to look at last night. What you take from it?\par \par \uc2 JEFF ROE, FOUNDER, AXIOM STRATEGIES: Yes, I think the immovable object met the irreversible force. And, you know, we like to have election midterms to be a referendum. But the Democrats made a choice, and that that hurt some of our gains. It was a split decision.\par \par \uc2 We want a unanimous decision, but we didn't get it. And so, the voters aren't convinced yet we have another election cycle to turn that page.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Doug, what did you take from it?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes, I think it was a very good night for the Democrats, given the expectations before Election Day. Basically, we're a polarized country.\par \par \uc2 Democrats came out, notwithstanding the bad economic news that they were facing. A very big win for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  personally. A great night for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Problematic night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , given that Masters is behind in Arizona, Dr. Oz lost, Bolduc lost in New Hampshire, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is trailing by about a point in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So, that's what I take. But Jeff's view is basically right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You know, President Biden just wrapped up this news conference. And he was asked specifically about what he takes from it, what change he would make from it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What in the next two years do you intend to do differently, to change people's opinion of the direction of the country, particularly as you contemplate a run for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 I believe we took the right steps for the country and for the American people. In fact, if you look at the polls, overwhelming majority, I don't look much anymore, because I'm not quite sure how to read them anymore. I hope you are uncertain as well.\par \par \uc2 But overwhelming majority of American people support the elements of my economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, is that what you took from it, Doug?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: No, it isn't. And I don't think they do. I actually think that it was a mobilization of the Democratic base around issues like abortion. The fact that former presidents \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Clinton got involved to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 And that the Republicans, and I think Jeff was right in suggesting didn't close the sale, didn't come up with the kind of positive argument that Governor DeSantis so compellingly made in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Jeff, we have the Fox News voter analysis, and the issues that most -- was most important to people -- and the economy, jobs, was number one by far. But number two, in our list was abortion. Do you think that, that -- I've heard you say that it's a short-term play that might have implications later for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ROE: Yes. I think their -- they are creating a real problem with their base. Hispanics are more pro-life than whites are. African-Americans are more pro-life than they are Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think their obsession with Trump and their obsession with abortion will cost them in the long term -- in the long term. And let's make it clear, we won last night. And well, I think we are going to find out for sure if we won on December 6th in Georgia in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But we -- it's tough to beat an incumbent United States senator who is going to have $150 million at their disposal, and an operation based on turning out their voters. And they did a nice job of that, and that halted some of our gains.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And --\par \par \uc2 ROE: But I think their obsession with that being essentially a two-issue party, it will help them when 100 million people are turning out. It's going to help them get 50 million of their voters out. Then, a presidential election with 125 to 150 million, I think it's going to cost them.\par \par \uc2 Do you think former President Trump factored into this, Jeff?\par \par \uc2 You know, he comes out, there is this rumor that he is going to declare at the J.D. Vance rally. And then the close from Democrats is a threat to democracy, which a lot of people took as kind of a veiled Trump reference.\par \par \uc2 ROE: Yes, I think that's -- it's theoretical about the threat to democracy, but I don't think he plays into this much as people want to, or nor any more than \uc2 Obama\uc2  plays into it.\par \par \uc2 When President Trump is getting more votes than our own candidates are getting, then, he is an asset to utilize to motivate your base. And if every single person that turned out for President Trump voted for our candidates last night, we would have won overwhelmingly.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not to make the case that he hurts for sure. There is an argument to make that candidates matter. We -- the Democrats had headwinds and to fight against. And we had candidates that couldn't make the sale.\par \par \uc2 When you are running up against an incumbent United States senator or an incumbent member of Congress with that much money, you better pack a lunch, because they are going to be tough to beat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Jeff is right, Doug, that we don't know how these races are going to go yet. And it's possible that the Democrats could eke out wins in Nevada and Arizona, or could go other way in Georgia then becomes - - you know, the control as I pointed on the map earlier.\par \par \uc2 But next cycle, Doug, it does not set up as a great environment for Democrats as you look two years down the road.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes. I agree with that. And where I think Jeff is exactly right, the Democrats need an affirmative agenda beyond democracy, election denial by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies, the abortion issue, and steady as we go.\par \par \uc2 Biden has made reference today to being more bipartisan, reaching across the aisle. He's got to do that if he wants to have a real shot at re- election, especially if there is a nominee other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff, Doug, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Overall, very quickly, gentlemen. Did the polls get it right? Wrong? Were they way off? Jeff first, then Doug?\par \par \uc2 ROE: Yes. I think they are -- they are pretty close. I mean, we have a lot of anticipation and a lot of energy at the end that wasn't really reflect in the polls. I think it was wishful thinking on our part.\par \par \uc2 But the polls were pretty close to being on. And so, there is some lessons, though it's very hard and getting very expensive to poll people that don't want to answer the phone. And when you call 100 people and get 1.7 to answer, we're not going to be conducting polling for very many more cycles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I would just say, Bret, that with so under polled Republicans, for so many years, all of us in the business thought that there would be a hidden Republican vote than in most instances did not show up and did not manifest itself in the poll.\par \par \uc2 In the polls or at -- on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Overcorrected maybe.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I'm just happy to not talk about polls for a little while. Thank you very much, gentleman.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Me too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ROE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, a look at to the big Senate races still uncalled. Plus, an update on the governor's races throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we just talked about it. It was Tropical Storm Nicole, it has now strengthened into a hurricane over the past couple of minutes.\par \par \uc2 The system has pounded the Bahamas. It's a rare November hurricane. For the state of Florida, where only two hurricanes have made landfall since record keeping began in 1853. This is rather the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 The National Hurricane Center is saying as of 6:00 p.m. Eastern, the storm was 105 miles east of West Palm Beach. Maximum sustained winds were recorded 75 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 The system was moving west, 12 miles per hour, and Nicole is expected, as you see this track to make landfall in Florida later tonight. Please stay with Fox Weather for the latest information.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, a handful of crucial Senate races, as we have been talking about, still uncalled tonight. We have FOX team coverage, Alicia Acuna, has the latest on the Arizona contest from Phoenix. First up, congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Atlanta where the Georgia Senate race is headed to a runoff. Hello, December 6th. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. That's right, so the ballots here in Georgia are already being built and every single county in the state is getting ready tonight for a December 6th rematch. So today, Georgia secretary of state made the call official, saying there are just under 10,000 outstanding ballots. Half of those are provisional, and the math for them equals a runoff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The votes that are left look like they are from the Atlanta metro area, which would probably lean blue. What are you guys looking at right now?\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE: We are just looking at the total number of outstanding and where they are right now. If you added 100 percent to this candidate or 100 percent to the other candidate, neither one of them would still be over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Shortly after, incumbent Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  tweeted, "We are going to a runoff, Georgia. Let's do this one more time," adding a donation link to that tweet. Meanwhile, a spokesman for Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  tells us, "It's overtime, and we're confident." Walker will hit the ground running at a rally tomorrow in Canton, Georgia, with Senator Ted Cruz. While the camp gets a second chance, note the difference, though, between how Walker performed compared to the other big Republican on the ballot last night. Of course, we are talking about incumbent Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . He easily defeated Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  by eight points, but he also had 200,000 more votes than Walker, meaning there were split voters last night. Walker trailing Warnock in his race shows just how he struggled to convince Georgia Republicans that he was the right man for the job, and now he will get four more weeks to do that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go now to Arizona for another uncalled race there to my brilliant colleague Alicia Acuna.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Well, thank you so much, Aishah. We are inside the Maricopa County tabulation and elections center in downtown Phoenix. And in this county alone, we are waiting on the count on more than 400,000 ballots. In the Senate race this morning, Republican hopeful Blake Masters tweeted "With the remaining ballots outstanding we are confident we will win." Masters currently trails Democrat incumbent Senator Mark Kelly by about 83,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans say they see a path to victory in Arizona in those outstanding ballots because they are from Election Day voters who trend Republican, and those folks who dropped off their early ballots yesterday and over the weekend. The Kelly campaign has been silent today. Last night he encouraged his supporters to hang tight and settle in. Control of the Senate could come down to this race, and elections officials in Maricopa say it won't be until Friday that 95 to 99 percent of the results will be in.\par \par \uc2 They are also doing a deep dive into how a printer setting glitch on tabulation machines at about a quarter of their voting centers prevented ballots from being counted onsite. Instead, they were taking to a county facility to be tabulated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES, (R) MARICOPA COUNTY BOARD OF SUPERVISORS: There is no perfect election. Yesterday was not a perfect election. But again, we will learn from it and do better. I would say, again, this team, our techs, and our poll workers worked quickly to get these issues fixed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: And as the day goes on, we are seeing some results trickle in from smaller areas of the state. However, in about an hour-and-a-half we are supposed to get a specific set of results from Maricopa County. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're watching all of that very quickly. Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's check in on the state races now. Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel has the latest on the governors' contests across the country. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Some interesting races, and some governor's races too close to call. In Oregon a very tight race between Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  and Republican Christine Drazan in a crowded five candidate field. Homelessness and crime are major issues there.\par \par \uc2 In two states that also had Senate races, Arizona and Nevada, the GOP candidates for governor are in a better position than their Senate counterparts. Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is leading Republican Kari Lake in Arizona, but it is still a very tight race.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, Republican Joe Lombardo is leading the incumbent Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE LOMBARDO, (R) NEVADA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: This race is very close. We have done a significant amount of work the last 18 months in this campaign to bring us here tonight. And with all your help, and all your support, it brings us in a razor thin race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Another surprise was how quickly New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  rolled to victory over Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . Republicans were hopeful that Zeldin's tough on crime stance with crime being seen as the number one issue would give him a strong chance to win New York, but Hochul, after replacing former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , now becomes the first woman elected governor of New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: Yes, the glass ceiling, like the one that's above us here today, has finally been shattered in the state of New York, and you made it happen.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The updated tally of governors once sworn in, Republicans 24, Democrats 22. That leaves four governorships to be sorted out.\par \par \uc2 Two Democrats who had all the hype and raised millions from supporters for their races but didn't live up to expectations. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in Georgia raised more than $51 million and \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  in Texas who raised more than $63 million. Both Democrats lost, but Abrams conceded the race in Georgia, which she refused to do back in 2018. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I was thinking that in that New York race, the governor's race, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  may be to thank for Kevin McCarthy becoming speaker in the end, because that close race did, it seem, pull some of those House races to Republican way.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Every vote seems to count in those suburban districts in New York, and so Zeldin running a strong race may have carried some across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, another apparent major setback for the Russian military in Ukraine. We will take you there. And later, the panel on the midterms as neither Democrats nor Republicans have officially claimed a majority in the House or the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lawyers for American basketball star Brittney Griner says she has been sent to a penal colony in Russia to serve her sentence for drug possession. A Russian court rejected an appeal of her nine year sentence last month. Griner was convicted in August of having vape cannisters containing cannabis oil in her luggage. This afternoon, President Biden said now that the election is over, Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may be more willing to serious talk about a prisoner exchange.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Russia, Russia's military says it is pulling troops from a key location inside Ukraine. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot tells us why tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: A potential major setback for Russia in its invasion of Ukraine. Russian officials announcing they're withdrawing all their troops in and around the Russian held southern city of Kherson and bringing them across a nearby river. Kherson is the only regional capital Moscow has taken in its eight-plus months of war.\par \par \uc2 COL. SERGEI SUROVIKIN, RUSSIAN DEFENSE FORCES (through translator): I understand this is a very difficult decision. At the same time, we will save the lives of our troops.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Taking back Kherson has been a main objective of Ukrainian forces for months now. They've been slowly gaining ground. There have been signs of Russian military movement, but Ukrainian officials fear this could be a deadly trap.\par \par \uc2 NATALIA HUMENIUK, UKRAINE SOUTHERN MILITARY COMMAND (through translator): We cannot say absolutely that their statements correspond to their actions.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: It would be welcome news for the people of the region, who have been evacuated by the Russians have endured months of war.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): The situation is tense. The city is empty. Everyone is very nervous because the shelling has intensified.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: This as battles continue on several fronts. Russia targeting Ukraine's electric grid, knocking out power for millions amid talk by the U.S. and allies that Ukrainian President Zelenskyy should begin to think about talking with Russian President Putin. Don't tell that to former Ukrainian President Poroshenko as he and others rush supplies to the front line.\par \par \uc2 PETRO POROSHENKO, FORMER PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: This is the price Ukraine should pay to be free, to be democratic, to stop the crazy maniac Putin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): On TV here, President Zelenskyy confirmed that something was happening around Kherson, but added "Our emotions must be restrained." And in comments late today President Biden indicated that Washington was aware of Russian problems, but then backed off a line that suggested it might lead to a compromise between Kyiv and Moscow. Complicated stuff. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot in Kyiv. Greg thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on what happened in the midterms last night, what comes next as we look down the road.\par \par \uc2 First Beyond our Borders tonight. Workers walk off the job in Greece and Belgium during nationwide strikes there against increasing consumer prices. The actions disrupted transportation, forcing flight cancellations and shutting down public services. Brief clashes broke out in Athens and one other Greek city with just a few arrests, one injury to a police officer.\par \par \uc2 A 23-year-old man is arrested after hurling eggs and insults at King Charles III and Camilla, the queen consort. It happened as they walked in the northern England city of York. Video showed several eggs in motion and smashed on the ground. None appeared to hit the royal couple, who continued to be greeted by local dignitaries and to meet assembled well-wishers.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Doha, Doha, Qatar. One of the big stories there tonight, Qatar is unveiling a 6,000 cabin fan village in an isolated lot near its airports to be used for affordable housing during the World Cup. The site includes a metro station, a bus stop, a planned restaurant and a convenience store. The World Cup begins November 20th on FOX Sports.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: While the press and the pundits are predicting a giant red wave, it didn't happen. I felt good during the whole process. I felt we were going to do fine.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: We not only won an election. We have rewritten the political map. Thank you for honoring us with a win for the ages.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I think if they win I should get all the credit. And if they lose, I should not be blamed at all, OK? But it will probably be just the opposite. I will probably be given very little credit even though in many cases I told people to run.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, there you have some reaction to the midterms. Here is how "Politico" writes it, "Presidents who suffered much more punishing midterms went on to win second terms, so give Biden his due. But it's hard to argue Democrats overperformed on Tuesday because of Biden rather than in spite of him."\par \par \uc2 And to that point, the FOX News voter analysis as we look into those numbers, the president's job approval with the people that voted that we talked to, again, this is tens and tens of thousands of people, disapprove at 57 percent. Right track, wrong track, the direction of the country, that question, wrong direction 75 percent. And the president said today he wouldn't change anything.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, David Drucker, senior congressional correspondent for "The Washington Examiner," and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." All right, 30,000 feet first, reaction to last night and what's continuing today. Guy, first to you.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Certainly, an underperformance and a disappointment for the Republicans based on the expectations that had been set and really building for a number of weeks. As I keep thinking about what actually happened last night, paradoxically it was a surprising result without many surprising results, if you know what I'm saying. There weren't that many races where people said whoa, that is a stunner. Maybe the margins in Florida was a big one. Certain races went a certain way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maybe Fetterman winning by as much as he -- not a shocker, but as much as he did.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right, so I think the overall result is more surprising than any individual result. And you dig deeper in, as of right now, this could change in a few different places like Nevada, but as of right now, not a single incumbent senator or governor in the country lost from either party. So this was, despite the numbers that we just put on the screen about President Biden and the state of the country, this was somewhat of a status quo election even though the opposition party still might win one or both houses of Congress. A lot of strange dissonance about this one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Olivia?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": I have been talking to house Republicans all day today. You are seeing one thing that they are saying publicly, which is they flipped the House, that's what's important. But behind the scenes they're doing this postmortem of what went wrong. They were saying, you know, I was feeling the energy of these voters, talking about inflation and crime. But maybe abortion was a bigger issue, and they are trying to assess what went wrong with their evaluations, because they really did believe they were going to see a red wave.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is it possible, David, that that close with threat to democracy hit on something that was a better close than we thought it was?\par \par \uc2 DAVID DRUCKER, SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT, "WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, there is always a chance that somebody who gets themselves elected president of the United States knows what they are doing. And we all like to sit in the cheap seats and look at polling and say, that is idiotic. Why is he talking about that?\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, I traveled the country's last six weeks, Wisconsin, Ohio, Washington state, Pennsylvania, Nevada, Georgia. I talked to Democrats all over the country. And when I would ask Democrats what is the issue motivating you, they would talk about kitchen table issues. But I cannot tell you how many times Democratic activists knocking on doors and Democratic voters told me I'm concerned about the state of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 So when you would look at the polling, it would make sense that you would talk about inflation and gas prices and how you are going to address Americans' quality of life issues. But when you are looking at a midterm election where you need to turn out your base and, in fact, give your own voters, even if they are not in your base, a reason to show up, if your President Biden talking about democracy at least as a part of the menu of issues you are discussing, makes a whole lot of sense. And I think that it is what Democrats wanted to hear, and it may be why we saw a real robust turnout from Democrats, which you usually don't see from the party in power in the White House that is on its heels in a midterm election. Usually, the out party makes a lot of gains because the other side just doesn't show up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Here is President Biden, I want to play it again because it was interesting to see how he was interpreting or what he took from the election and what is he going to do. This is a reporter question, and a little different part of his answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What in the next two years do you intend to do differently to change people's opinion of the direction of the country, particularly as you contemplate a run for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Nothing, because there is just finding out what we are doing. The more they know about what we are doing, the more support there is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nothing, Guy? Nothing? Wrong direction, 75 percent. Nothing?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: He's saying well, look, we didn't get shellacked or even close to it, so we'll just keep doing what we're doing. I don't think that's an answer that will play well with a lot of people. This was not an election that was in any way an embrace of the Democrats or trying to empower them further. But also voters did not go running into the open arms of the opposition party in a midterm election in spite of all the fundamentals being as bad as they are or should be for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 That is, to Marc Thiessen's point on our election coverage last night, really an indictment of the Republican Party right now where certain types of Republicans, especially governors, did quite well, even much better than expected, but other types of Republicans were ultimately toxic to an awful lot of voters. And they better figure it out, because there is another election in two years, and I think this was a jarring one for people who were expecting to win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should point out that there is still a possibility that Republicans gain control of both chambers. And there is a path in the Senate. The projection now is, and we don't have this nailed down, but it's roughly 222 seats in the House for Republicans, which is the same majority that Democrats had, which is very slim. Is there a threat to leader McCarthy, potentially Speaker McCarthy in this new Congress in that form?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: So in the phone calls that I've had, and I've been talking with Freedom Caucus members and people on McCarthy right wing, they don't think that at the moment there is anyone who is looking to challenge him. But his challenge is in another form. The Freedom Caucus is looking for concessions on institutional changes like restoring the motion to vacate the chair. And I have heard that he has been doing sort of phone calls trying to see how strongly that red line with these Freedom Caucus members as well as other sort of concessions the Freedom Caucus is pushing for, like having some of their members on the committee that decides who the chairman is and where people sit --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So there's a big negation going on.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: There's negotiations going on behind the scenes. And so that is his biggest sort of threat is making sure he makes them happy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're looking at kind of a ripple, red ripple, but the red wave really did hit in Florida. And "Axios" writes it this way, "Republicans win big in Florida, flipping long Democratic Miami-Dade. Tuesday's election serves as further proof that solidly red Florida is past its days as a swing state. Governor DeSantis won the formerly solid blue Miami-Dade County, becoming the first Republican gubernatorial candidate to win the county since 2002."\par \par \uc2 Today, the former president posted this on Truth Social, "Now that the election in Florida is over and everything went quite well, shouldn't it be said that in 2020 I got 1.1 million more votes in Florida than Ron D got this year, 5.7 million to 4.6 million? Just asking?"\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: Sure, OK. Look. I think when you look at what happened in these midterm elections, a lot of the candidates running for the House and the Senate and for governor that were running in the style of Trump, pugilistic culture warrior, found themselves coming up short. The Republicans that did well --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The one we don't know about, Kari Lake in Arizona. She's still --\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: Correct. And by the way, there were still plenty of Republicans in the style of Trump that won election that will contribute to the new House majority and possibly the new Senate majority. But I think when you look at Governor -- let's look at Georgia where I just returned from. Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  wins with 53-and-a-half percent of the vote. He romped, right? In the same election, in the same contest, where -- the same state where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval ratings were under 40 percent, Herschel Walker is battling neck and neck a point behind in a runoff. He ran as a culture warrior more so than a kitchen table issued oriented candidate. And I think you see what works, what worked last night and what didn't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we will see you back in Georgia December 6th. We will see if it's control of the Senate. Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a pair of SPECIAL REPORT salutes. First, we take to you Arizona. They are doing some counting there, but this is more important. An 88-year-old veteran took to the desert skies in a World War II era plane. The nonprofit Dreams Flights dedicated to honoring seniors and military veterans sent Teddy Devries, airborne, supported by his children, grandchildren, and great grandchildren. That's awesome.\par \par \uc2 Next up, 95 Vietnam veterans returned from their trip to Washington to see the memorials and the moments. These are the Honor Flights. They recognized their service. Honor Flights San Diego organized the travel at no cost, providing a first class experience. Upon their return, hundreds of friends, families, strangers, gave them a memorable homecoming. Thank you for your service, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll see what we can call and what we can't.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, still unafraid, back in Washington. Jesse, over to you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Gave A Press Conference Where He Says Support For Ukraine Will Continue But If The New House Under Leader McCarthy Will Suppress It, He'll Have To Veto; President Biden Said That He Doesn't Believe China Is Supporting Russia's Invasion Of Ukraine As He Sees President Xi Is Distancing A Bit From The Issue; The Panel Weighed In On The Result Of The Midterm Election, And They Say They Can't Believe That \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Won; President Biden Gave A Hint That He'll Be Running In 2024 But It's Going To Be Decided Depending On The Result Of His Consultation To His Family; DeSantis Dominates: FL GOP Governor Wins Re- election In Landslide; Almost Comedy: Biden Sounds Off On Potential GOP House Investigations; Investigations Imminent: Republicans Eye Probes Of Biden Admin If GOP Wins House\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 10,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I think that Elon Musk's cooperation and or technical relationships with other countries is worthy of being looked at whether or not he is doing anything inappropriate. I'm not suggesting that, I'm suggesting that worse, worse, being looked at. And - and, but that's all I'll say. There's a lot of ways. All right. Kristen. Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, CORRESPONDENT, MSNBC: Thank you so much, Mr. President. I appreciate it. I want to follow up with you on working with Republicans. Leader McCarthy again suggested that he is not prepared to write what he has called a blank check to Ukraine, and yet you expressed optimism that funding for Ukraine would continue that the policies toward Ukraine would continue.\par \par \uc2 Why should the people of Ukraine and this country have confidence in that given the comments by Leader McCarthy. And just to follow up with you on your comments to Zeke, you said you don't need to do anything differently. If Republicans control the house. Don't you need to recalibrate to some extent to try to work across the aisle with a Republican-Led House?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, let me put it this way. What I meant was I don't have to change any of the policies I've already passes. That's what they said they want to go after. And so, what I have a simple proposition. I have a pen that can veto. OK. So that's what I mean. I don't have to recalibrate whether or not I'm going to continue to, you know, fund the -- we're going to continue to fund the infrastructure bill or we're going to continue to fund the environment, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 We have to, I hope (Inaudible) I think there is a growing pressure on the part of the American people expecting both parties and all elements of both parties to -- to work out their substance of differences and not just, I'm not going to do that because it would benefit that party. Just make it -- by making it personal.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- and you know, it remains to be. What the makeup of the -- of the House will be. But I'm hopeful that Kevin and I can work out, modus vivendi as how we'll proceed with one another.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So, will aid to Ukraine continue uninterrupted?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That is my expectation. And by the way, we've not given Ukraine a blank check. There's a lot of things Ukraine wants. We didn't -- we didn't do. For example, I was asked very much whether we prefer -- we'd provide American aircraft to guarantee the skies over Ukraine. I said, no, we're not going to do that. We're not going to get into a third World War taking on the Russian aircraft and directly engaged.\par \par \uc2 But would we provide them with the all the rational ability to defend themselves? Yes. We find those HIMARS. Well, the HIMARS there's two kinds of -- but and the average person's parlance rockets you can drop in those. One that goes over 600 miles and one that goes about 160 miles.\par \par \uc2 We didn't give any ones to go to 600 miles because I'm not looking for them to start bombing Russian territory. And so we want to make sure that there's a relationship that they're able to defend themselves and take on what is purely a -- the ugliest aggression that's occurred since World War II on a massive scale on the part of Putin within Ukraine. And there's so much at stake.\par \par \uc2 So, I would be surprised if Leader McCarthy even has a majority of his Republican colleagues who say they're not going to fund the legitimate defensive needs of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And just a quick one. Obviously, a lot of attention on 2024 now that the votes have been cast in the midterms. Two thirds of Americans in exit polls say that they don't think you should run for reelection. What is your message to them and how does that factor into your final decision about whether or not to run for reelection?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It doesn't.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: What's your message to them? To those two-thirds of the --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Watch me.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: OK. One more. Come on, very quickly. You saw Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with a resounding victory in Florida last night. Who do you think would be the tougher competitor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or former President Trump. And how is that factoring into your decision?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It'd be fun watching him take on each other. All right. David Sanger.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER, NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Thank you, Mr. President. I also have a question for you about China, but before I do, I just wanted to follow up on something you said earlier. When you said it remains to be seen whether the Ukraine government is prepared to compromise with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Previously, you've told us the only thing for the Russians to do is get completely out of Ukraine. Go back to the lines that existed prior to February 24. Are you suggesting with the word compromise that you think that there is room for territorial compromise now that --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I'm not to, -- that's up to the Ukrainians. Nothing about Ukrainian without Ukrainian.\par \par \uc2 SANGER: So, what kind of compromise did you have in mind?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't have any in mind. You -- I've asked the question whether or not if I recall, whether or not what would happen if in fact, after this, I think the context is that whether or not they pulling back from Falluja and the -- I mean from the Kherson, the city of Kherson, and they're coming back across the river to the eastern side of the river, the Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 And I said, what's going to happen is they're going to both lick their wounds decide whether what they're going to do over the winter and decide whether or not they're going to compromise. That's what's going to happen. Whether or not, I don't know what they're going to do, and, but I do know one thing we're not going to tell them what they have to do.\par \par \uc2 SANGER: You were asked before about your meeting with President Xi. At this point the Chinese government by the estimate of the Pentagon, it's getting ready to bring their force of nuclear weapons up to over a thousand weapons. Significant increase from what they've had for many decades.\par \par \uc2 You've seen the threats from President Putin about the use of his nuclear force.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You remember how you all went after him when I said that was real?\par \par \uc2 SANGER: And what, what in your view happened? Do you think he backed off because of that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, no. I'm just saying, I just -- I just found it interesting that Biden is being a pop -- a cop. Biden is being an extremist. And it turns out you all are writing about it now, kind of fascinating.\par \par \uc2 SANGER: So, my question is, do you think that they are putting together a real alliance of Chinese and the Russians, and do you believe that you need to begin speaking with President Xi about some form of arms control if he's going to get up to a level of weapons similar to what the United States and Russia have right now?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No and yes. No, I don't think there's a lot of respect that China has for Russia or for Putin. I don't think they're look into that as a particular alliance, matter of fact, they've been sort of keeping their distance a little bit.\par \par \uc2 I do think that it remains to be seen whether \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has decided that, or backed off of his initial judgment that he wanted Ukraine, excuse me, China, to have the most powerful military in the world as well as the largest economy. And, but he's a long way from. But I think it -- I think talk about nuclear weapons and location a number of them, and access is important to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all so very, very much.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. I think that the president has done, he has set the microphone down, even though he is now -- he might be taking another question. So let me just hang on here. He's in the east wing of the White House.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We'll do another --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: On the day after the midterm.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We'll do another hour a little later. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. So, I think that he has done, so traditionally a president does a press conference after a midterm, and that's what President Biden did today. Had a pretty conciliatory tone, I think. And he feels like they escaped some of the races that they could have lost.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake, the president is facing a very changed Washington with the House likely to be in Republican hands. We don't have a final count yet. Some of those races haven't been announced, but that is looking very likely. And the Senate still too close to call, but the Republicans have a path to get to a majority.\par \par \uc2 So, the Washington will be changed. The president though didn't seem to indicate that he would be willing to change any policies. He said he couldn't change the policies that he already passed anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, Judge Jeanine, you are the only person who, at this table who has actually run for office. You know what it's like to win and to not win.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And so, let's get your take today.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right? The last time I ran was in New York for attorney general. I ran against \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  and he won. And it was a relatively close race, but you know, you lose and you move on. But you know what, what's interesting about Lee Zeldin's loss is that he ran so strong that there are all these congressional seats that he took with him.\par \par \uc2 I believe New York gained 10 congressional seats. The Hudson Valley, the one that I was always interested in, the \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the head of the DCCC. That is emblazed in my mind, Greg, of him being embarrassed raising money with Adam Schiff.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And he was Chief Boyardee guy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Yes. And the truth is that we had a turbulent economy, an unpopular president, and yet it wasn't the red wave they said it was going to be, but you know, we shouldn't be, you know, we shouldn't be down. I mean, you know, if, take the arsenic off the table. But at the same time, we have the House and certainly a clear path to the Senate, as you say.\par \par \uc2 I think what is -- what is really disturbing though is the fact that most of the country thinks we're headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Seventy-five percent.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Seventy-five something said 80 percent. And yet so many people voted on issues that weren't the issues we thought they were voting on. We thought it was all about the economy and inflation and crime. But as Jessica, I'm sure will say it was a lot about abortion. It was a lot about the threat to democracy. And even in spite of that, I think Republicans did very well. And I think Kevin McCarthy is to be saluted for, you know, a mission that he's been on for many years.\par \par \uc2 And finally, I want to say one more thing. With voters saying 47 percent, saying that their situation is worse off than it was two years ago. They didn't make the dramatic changes that they thought they would. And my final concern is New York. The fact that Hochul won and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  lost means to me that New York City is only going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 We need to change the laws to protect New Yorkers. And that's what I'm depressed about today. I'm happy about the House. I'm happy, I'm confident about the Senate, but at the same time, I'm worried about this city.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The city. Yes. One of the things, Jessica, that was really shocking to me last night but it makes a lot of sense is, for example, in Michigan where there was a referendum on the ballot for abortion, and then in Wisconsin that kept that Senate race very close. But Ron Johnson pulls it out at the end, but it was super close.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that was part of the reason is the youth vote.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We kind of make fun of the youth. We say they're unreliable voters are not going to turn out. They turned out with gusto and maybe, and if they are voting now, they might vote like that for the rest of their lives until maybe they start paying more taxes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, and they got the results that they were after, right?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: This wasn't, I turned out, I voted and I didn't get what I wanted.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: This is I got what I wanted. So, youth vote up 360 percent in Wisconsin, up 207 percent in Michigan and add Kentucky to that list. And it was really interesting watching the referendum tick along, alongside \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . And the referendum was running way ahead --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, the referendum was --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: To make sure that abortion could remain legal there, that there wouldn't be a constitutional amendment. It was very similar to Kansas. You're talking about two deep, deep red states. Right. Where people came out and just said, we are not interested in having this right completely stripped away.\par \par \uc2 Abortion was certainly a huge plot line from last night. The number one issue for Democrats, the number two issue nationally. And it's interesting because I think a lot about 2016, I was like haunting on a number of levels. And the quiet Trump voter, right? The people who didn't respond to polls who just went in there and did their business, right? And they voted for him and came out and there was an expectation that that actually would happen again in this election that a lot of Republicans would do that. And it seems like there were a lot of quiet abortion voters.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That everybody was going around having conversations about the economy as they should. Right? Because it's not normal to sit down to your pal and say, you know, for the millionth time, I can't believe they, you know, rolled back Roe v. Wade. You say, it was so expensive, the grocery store. Right? Or, my gas is too much.\par \par \uc2 And I think Republicans and I said this when the Dobbs rolling happened that it really misunderstanding how important this issue is to people. And there was so much talk about the issue of abortion as if it's something that people use as a birth control method. That it's just wild women going out and saying, you know, I don't want to use a condom, I'll just get an abortion. No big deal.\par \par \uc2 When in fact abortion is so much a part of healthcare of family planning. There are so many people who are forced to have an abortion from wanted pregnancies, and those are the conversations that are going on all over this country.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, one of the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Are you campaigning for Planned Parenthood or what?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: One of the things that the Supreme Court said --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Greg, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, that's -- the people who are pro-life actually do take the issues more seriously than you think. Right? You want to talk about the quiet majority. Talk about the unborn. They don't have a word in this at all.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think a lot about the unborn, actually I had an unborn inside of me for a long time.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And reasonable people would actually have a conversation about science.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I know, but you're -- you're --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And they would talk about a viability\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- lying that people who are pro-life, don't take it as seriously as you are.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it. That's it. That's all what I want to say.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, no, no. I don't think that, and I know a lot of -- I know a lot of pro-life people who are no exceptions. Right. People who believe that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's not what I meant, but that's OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. Let me -- let me get --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm not talking about the exception rules. So don't worry about it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, no, no. Who just think that --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Let me jump in one thing, guys.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- the second that you conceive, (Inaudible) of that is a life.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's not what I meant. What I'm talking about that -- that's not, that I would not say is a nuanced argument. I'm saying that you are criticizing the -- that you're saying that there isn't a nuance side that we just think, my God, people are using it for birth control. That's not true.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, I do not think that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No one says that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And I was actually acknowledging that there is nuance to your argument.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She said she didn't think that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I do not think that. I think that there are a majority of Americans who want to talk about a reasonable viability level and 15 weeks was too short for them. But I'm sure if it was 20 weeks or 24 weeks, that there are a lot of Democrats and independent minded people who would say we should be discussing that. But when you have women in Louisiana who are waiting to go into sepsis, you have that 10-year-old in Ohio who's getting shipped to Indiana to get healthcare. You're not, Greg, we just had the election --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I know what this -- I know.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- that said that you are wrong about this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Nope. It's you're getting, again, this is part of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Don't say I'm getting a motion off it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, no, I'm trying. What I'm trying to do, I believe it had a role, but there are other things at play. But when you sit, when you bring up two exceptions --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Also, that you guys run terrible candidates.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Two exceptions very rare. Very rare. Very rare.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: One of my reasons --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The 10-year-old.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: One of my reasons for bringing it up, Jesse, was just because on the referendum side of things, it did bring out voters and it did have an effect. And what Jim Messina, Democrat from the Obama era said to us today on Newsroom is that you can expect to have that in 2024. My -- in many other states. My only point then is that it's this the turnout was interesting.\par \par \uc2 You had the number -- one of the number one issue in the country is inflation. Now you do have some change, but it doesn't look like there's going to be any policy changes from the White House.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Well, I'm just glad my red wave prediction is accurate. In Florida --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How much money --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I was talking about in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How much money do you owe Geraldo?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Eleven hundred.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you a hundred, which adds to 1,100 and the thousand will go to charity. Many people see me as a charity case, so we'll see where that donation goes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What? Are you saying? You're receiving money?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: To the Watters Foundation. No, I'll pay up and --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Give us your take overall.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, the House is now in McCarthy's hands. Pelosi will probably go to Rome to be ambassador, and that's fine. And there'll be investigations, which is good. I was disappointed in the Senate outcome because the poll showed it was going to be 52, 53.\par \par \uc2 If you look at what happened, let's say in Pennsylvania, I can't believe \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won. I can't believe it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I honestly can't believe they voted for this guy. So, abortion was critical. We know in Pennsylvania. I did not see that it was going to be that critical. Oz was not a great candidate. He had a brutal primary. He took the summer off and he campaign hard at the end. But people didn't trust him. They didn't trust the guy. He wasn't from Pennsylvania, and it was fraudulent the way that Fetterman hit his condition and then skipped this debate, debated the last week.\par \par \uc2 By that time, they banked so many early votes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, Republicans really have to get a hold of this early vote thing. There's no Republican early vote strategy. Can anybody tell me what that is? Republicans vote on election day. Democrats vote for five weeks.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, there's this mass mobilization campaign that's underway in these early voting states, and Republicans are sitting it out. You know, they get Souls to the Polls in Philly as they say. They're knocking on doors. They're handed in ballots. There's paper everywhere. What's the Republican plan? I don't know. So, they need to plan for that.\par \par \uc2 Georgia, I'm nervous about because it looks like Laxalt is going to hold on in Nevada. Masters is cooked in Arizona, so it all is going to come down to Georgia runoff again. So, we're, this is about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And you know why --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're counting on Herschel who I like.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know why the hate him, the abortions.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Who I like and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: They're admitting that he did pay for that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They crushed him on these abortion attacks. I mean, this is going to be another nailbiter and it makes me very nervous. So, you also have good races run by Ron Johnson. He's in there. He's going to investigate the hell out of the Biden crime family. Biden was very against his election. That's good.\par \par \uc2 But there was never a shot apparently in New Hampshire. I mean, that guy got railroaded and if you put up a guy in, he was running for governor in Pennsylvania who just got slaughtered. I mean, these things have down ballot consequences that hurts Oz or hurts the Republican House candidates.\par \par \uc2 Zeldin did great considering this is a deep blue state. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  had help from every Democrat crooked, Bill, Obama, Kamala. Everybody came into to save her and she barely held on, but Zeldin had coattails and got a bunch of House Republicans in there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And it looks like a bunch more House Republicans are going to come in through the West coast. You think this is going to be a nailbiter? They're going to have the House. It's not going to be the biggest margin of all time. It's going to be bigger than they say it is right now.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But this was a red wave. If you're looking at in Florida. Stacey is no longer with us. She's going to go back to writing erotic novels. Abbott won. Beto, bye-bye. Find another office to run. There's just not the hatred for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that there is for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and for the Clintons. There's not a hate Biden vote that's out there.\par \par \uc2 You know when you go with Trump's on the ballot, there's that hate Trump Democrat vote. People just don't feel the same passion against the guy that they also feel for other people.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Maybe he's just really nice guy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And we have a problem. Demographically, single women are voting for Democrats by 30 points.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thirty-seven.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thirty-seven points.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And Urban America, they're cleaning our clocks in the cities, cleaning our clocks. I'm not saying anything the fair is going on, but they are cleaning our clocks in the cities and we're giving it away. And the fact that these youth voters are coming in so strong on an off year is very concerning. It looks like they've been brainwashed.\par \par \uc2 This new generation is totally brainwashed because a lot of these single women vote 37 spreads for Democrats are teaching all of our younger generation in these schools.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And they're polluting their minds, and then they grow up and then they're 