House, admitted to our own \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  that the Benghazi hearings were theater and they did what they were supposed to do because they got \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's approval rating down. That sounds like faking it, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You're nodding.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, we were -- I think if watch this show, Jessica, you wouldn't say we were faking anything about Benghazi.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, it's different, you know, with the theatre. There was a whole production company in Congress with January 6. They had sound and then they would bring in someone and then they got the --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I got to get Jesse in here. The other Jesse. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: This won't be a Hunter Biden investigation. This is going to be a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  investigation, and they're going to have bank records, and they're going to have witness testimony. And if you follow the money, you're going to find out something that might be a threat to democracy if the president United States has been compromised, or if he's engaged in illicit activity involving foreign powers.\par \par \uc2 It's worth looking into whether or not the media puts it on the air or not. That's why he needed the Senate because the media takes Senate testimony more often than they take House testimony. I also want to see Fauci testify because that's going to be good. I want to see border testimony. And I'd like to see Mayorkas impeached. And then I want to see \uc2 FBI\uc2  corruption exposed and all of the conspiracy with these big tech companies and the CIA because that is something the American people should know about.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We should come up with a list of investigations just for stuff that really bugs us, right?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Traffic.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Traffic. Just like --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Like, bicycles on the sidewalk?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, food sizes that are getting smaller but you're paying more. Have you noticed that?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Can we do that --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The little Gatorade, they're this big now.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: When are they going to --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: A baby Gatorade?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- do the national Greg Gutfeld day?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, that's the other -- and what about the statue in Allentown? I know what's going on.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You do.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They're building a statue of me in Allentown apparently.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very small.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Very small one. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Lifelike.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, it's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, tonight, what a great show. We are back. We got -- it's not live. I was going to say that. Emily Compagno -- happy birthday, Emily. Jimmy Failla, Kat Timpf, Tyrus. It's going to be great. Let's do this quickly, OK. Greg's Nautical Uber news. You didn't notice but Uber is now coming to the ocean. They'll pick up a fish on the spot. Check out this boat going along. This is Uber, and then oh.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, hello!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And so, he got a ride.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Is that -- is that a shark?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Whoa, shark.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That is a shark.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But they -- so they just --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Is he stuck?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's in New Zealand which is a country. That's where they make the Lord's and the Rings.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: They're like upside down.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The Lord's and the Rings. Is that what -- that's where the hobbits come from?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The lord and the ring.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, and where Air Supply --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, what happened?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Air Supply comes out of there when they made that great song Satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We don't know. We don't know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And Alice Cooper.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I want to -- I want to show --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, that. I got it. So, there's this little kid named Nash and he went -- he's in Kentucky. He's 8 years old and he really wanted a job. Watch here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why did you apply for a job here besides you love Drake's.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So I couldn't get enough money to buy an Xbox series X.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This little kid. He gets $5.00 a week for his allowance and it wasn't going to get him to his Xbox, so he told his mom he wanted to work at Drake's. So, he applied, and they said come back when you're a little bit older, but they got in the Xbox which is pretty sweet.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What a terrible lesson. You don't have to work to get an Xbox.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, it's Biden's America.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's Biden's America.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, all right, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, I've got a little girl and a 911 prank calls. So, her parents got creative in disciplining their 2-year-old daughter for not sharing her toys. They used a scripted 911 call intended for misbehaved kids to scare this 2-year-old straight. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How about right now you apologize for misbehaving. Will you do that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who's that? (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who's that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right, good job. That was a good choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The police?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I will go ahead and call the police right now and tell them that you decided to start behaving?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so she's -- hopefully that was enough for her to scare her straight and she learned her lesson.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, Jesse, go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Great parenting. You've heard of The Shawshank Redemption. What about the claw shank redemption. This is a crab seconds away from becoming a crab cake and never gave up and clawed his way out of his restraints.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And the crab put up such a fight, the hunger guy getting ready to eat him actually let him return to his home.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, let him go, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tonight, I'm not going to let \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  go at "PRIMETIME" at 7:00. What the hell happened to the Senate, Lindsey?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, we will find out on "PRIMETIME." Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK. So, four Army soldiers in training were awarded scholarships at the \uc2 New York Jets\uc2  annual Salute to service event on Sunday. The four military recruits received the U.S. Army Minuteman Scholarship which covers tuition and fees at a school of their choosing in addition to a book allowance and a monthly stipend.\par \par \uc2 The recipients were all citizens servicemen members who work both in their community as well as either the Army National Guard or Army Reserve and they are all guaranteed placement in the Army Reserves after graduation. Since 2015, the Minuteman campaign has provided 5800 cadets with over $115 million in scholarships.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We knew you could get that "ONE MORE THING" in under time. Thanks, everybody. It's been great to be with you tonight. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hi, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 President Biden Holds Press Conference; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  (R-NH); Democrats Hold Off Red Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 Gerry Baker, Sandra Smith, Jacqui Heinrich, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 11,422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, we will have more on that big sell-off in just a second.\par \par \uc2 You're looking live at the White House, where the president is about to take questions on an election that's still shaking out this hour, though apparently better than he thought it would be, hence his appearance before the American people today.\par \par \uc2 So much we don't know. This much, we do. Democratic Senate candidates who distanced themselves from President Biden are doing well. Colorado Senator Michael Bennet is here. And Republican governors who ran away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did really well. New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  also here.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans in Florida, well, they did super-duper especially well. How will the president respond to the success of Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ? I'm talking about the last president. We're about to find out.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 We want to first go to Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, where the president is set to speak.\par \par \uc2 Originally, Jacqui, this wasn't supposed to happen. He wasn't going to address anyone on this. But something happened with those results, right?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It certainly did. And we're glad to have the opportunity to ask him questions.\par \par \uc2 The big takeaway here at the White House is that officials think that Democrats ran on the president's message, on his agenda, not away from it, and that the president spoke to issues that are most important to the American people, including abortion and including clean energy, and that it had some success.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's fair to question that, if only a little bit, given the president was a bit of an unwelcome visitor on the campaign trail in some of those swing states. His approval numbers have been difficult. And then also the strategy in the end was effectively putting Trump on the ballot once again.\par \par \uc2 This was a strategy that Democrats believed had worked before and could work again. And it did, by a lot of measures, granted that we don't know how many of these races are going to shake out. It was not the bloodbath that people had been predicting as far as Democrat losses go.\par \par \uc2 So now some folks are wondering, does the present use this moment to launch his own 2024 (AUDIO GAP) what the president wants to do in the second half of his term. Most of his closing midterm message focused on painting a stark picture of what a GOP-controlled Congress would look like, touting his own achievements.\par \par \uc2 We did not hear a lot about what he hopes to accomplish in the second half of his term, aside from codifying abortion rights, which, if Democrats don't maintain control of both chambers, looks to be very much a question mark. We also would need to figure out how he plans to engage with whoever the leader of the new Congress is, if Republicans take over the House.\par \par \uc2 So we have got a little insight into what the next few days and weeks look like, but we're hoping to get the president's view into what his plans are and how he plans to work with this Congress, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, looking forward to that, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 . He won by a country mile, as they say, almost 60 percent of the vote, for a fourth term.\par \par \uc2 Governor, very good to have you. And congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): Thanks. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Great win. Very proud.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you should be.\par \par \uc2 Let me get your sense of what you think you're going to hear out of President Biden. I guess the scenario was not as bad as some Democrats had feared, that there was no wave. That's how Democrats see it. How do you?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, there was clearly no red wave. I'm not going to predict what comes out of President Biden's mouth, because I don't think anyone -- even he, I don't think, can predict that at times.\par \par \uc2 But, clearly, America stood up and said, look, inflation is important, but we don't think that the Republicans that want to go to Washington are the short-term fix for that right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think, more than anything American said, we know we have to fix policy, but we're going to fix extremism first. And that's what I -- that's the message I took from it, not that Republicans weren't successful in certain parts of the country, but, clearly, by and large, the most extreme Republicans got voted out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You kept your distance from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Didn't hurt you, obviously, in your reelection efforts.\par \par \uc2 There did seem to be a bit of a pattern to that nationally. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, without a doubt.\par \par \uc2 Look, I don't think the former president did many candidates any favors, but it's not just him. I think there were individuals. I think \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  talking about abortion, nationalizing an abortion ban weeks before the election did no one any help.\par \par \uc2 I think some of the discussion on Social Security and Medicare out of why Washington that made people scared on that issue did nobody any help, and the fact that there was a message out of D.C. that kept talking about getting rid of Pelosi and payback and all that kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 That's not what people want to see right now. They want to see people that are going to deliver results. And so, by and large, I think the messaging wasn't really where it needed to be, and the quality of the candidate, the quality of the individual that has to connect with the voter, that really does matter to folks, at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, next week, I think, six days from tonight, we're expecting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to make an announcement, presumably that he's going to run for president.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne Conway was just on our network saying that that might want to wait for a while here until this whole midterm season is resolved. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, it's a terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's one of the -- I don't know who's advising the former president to make an announcement to run for president again next week. It's -- they should all be fired. Nobody wants to think about politics right now after - - right after the election.\par \par \uc2 The only thing people are going to be really focusing on, as you know, are there lawsuits in Arizona, the run-off election that's going to happen in Georgia. These are really impactful things that are going to affect stuff over the next couple of months that everyone's going to be paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 No one's going to be surprised with his announcement. Nobody's going to care. It's not going to clear the field. And after the night we had yesterday, again, I think everyone's going to go, yes, what else is new, and go to the next page and the next story.\par \par \uc2 So, I just think it's a -- it's a horrible idea, horrible timing. People want to focus on Thanksgiving and their families and inflation and getting through Christmas and spending some time without politics encompassing them. So, it's a very impactful statement, if that's where he were to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He has indicated in the past, Governor, as you know, that it would be an act of disloyalty for someone to run when he's already indicated that he would. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Do you see a lot of people around saying, gee, I can't wait to be arm and arm with former President Trump going forward after the results we had yesterday?\par \par \uc2 I just -- I can't imagine that. I think there's going to be a lot of distancing, not just with candidates, with money, with supporters, with folks saying, we have clearly got to move on. Obviously, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  had a great night in Florida. And I think Ron is obviously very much thinking of running for president, as is Nikki Haley, and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and all these other really good, quality candidates that are out there.\par \par \uc2 You're going to have a very robust field. And it's not going to be about former President Trump. He's going to be one of a dozen candidates. And I'm not saying he can't win the nomination. But this isn't a field-clearing announcement that I think he thinks it might be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you feel, given the results and the candidates he was pushing, sir, dinged him a little bit, maybe a lot, that he isn't quite the Superman or the big party figure that he was, let's say, 24 hours ago?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, don't ask me.\par \par \uc2 Why don't you go ask Oz and Walker and Lake and the list of them? I know Vance did pretty well in Ohio. That's great. But, I mean, by and large, the most major candidates that he backed here had trouble overcoming not just him. It wasn't just about former President Trump, but overcoming that image, that brand that tended to really scare people.\par \par \uc2 I was surprised by it. I thought inflation was not just the key issue, but folks were going to look to those individuals to solve that issue. But it's still the key issue, but, clearly, folks didn't think that those folks were going to go solve it. And kind of the fear of that extremism overwhelmed them.\par \par \uc2 That surprised me, to be sure. I didn't predict the results. I thought we were going to have a pretty good red wave, so more like a red ripple. But it should have been a lot stronger than it was.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you proof that you don't have to kowtow to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Because, long before this, you were -- you were doing your own thing, and proving you can live to fight another day, not that you ever disparaged the president, but that you sort of moved on with your life.\par \par \uc2 Was that a signal you were sending?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, that's it.\par \par \uc2 Look -- yes, look, I'm not pro-, I'm not anti-Trump. I'm just -- I'm moving on. My job as a governor, as an elected official is constantly moving forward. If anything I think I'm proof of is, you got to be genuine. You got to be yourself. You got to be real and you got to be not about yourself, but about those folks that you want to represent.\par \par \uc2 And you got to connect with them empathetically. You got to listen. You got to understand what they are going through and talk to their issues, not to yours. This ain't about me. This -- I'm the governor. I'm telling you, this ain't about me and it's not about government. It's really about those individuals.\par \par \uc2 And you have got to make that connection. And if you're being genuine and just have a normal conversation with folks are good -- good retail politics, if you will, you can get elected. And, last time I checked, you can't govern if you don't win. So that's one big lesson we learned from yesterday. You got to have winners. And winners are the folks that make that genuine connection more often than not.\par \par \uc2 And then you can go after -- get good results for your state or your constituency and run on those results as a -- in a reelection. But so many people try to -- they try to overthink it, and it gets very messy for them. Just be yourself, man.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, I have talked to you on and off the air, and you're the same guy off the air as you are on the air, kind of in your face, but that's OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Governor, I'd be curious.\par \par \uc2 We're about a minute or so away from the president. And I know you didn't want to weigh on advising what he should say. But, clearly, there are no indications that he's going to pivot. He's not going to pull a \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . And the read we're getting from folks in the White House, sir, is that he feels no reason to, that there was no red wave, and so there's no reason to dramatically change anything.\par \par \uc2 If that is the posture he takes -- there's no way of knowing -- your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes. No, I think you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 I don't -- if I were him, I wouldn't see any need to pivot. I don't think he's got a mandate, by any means, but there's -- clearly, America said, again, we might not like your policies, but we're not -- we're not sending folks there to drastically change it either.\par \par \uc2 So that's a concern, not just from a party standpoint, but from someone who is worried about energy prices, fuel prices, cold snaps this winter. So that's the biggest part that upsets me, is that he clearly does not and I don't think will see a need to pivot at this point. And it could be a tough -- tough more two years for America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Governor, good luck coming out of your shell. It appears to be working.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But always good seeing you. Congratulations again.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Thank you, brother.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , nominated by, again, a 60 to roughly 40 percent margin last night, about the margin that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  won in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Waiting on the president here, but I don't want to wait on my colleagues and friend Sandra Smith, the co-anchor of "America Reports." She's like Jerry Lewis near the end of the telethon. She's had no sleep.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you wouldn't know it.\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker, he might have had sleep, "The Wall Street Journal at Large" host on FOX Business, which you don't get -- obviously, you do get it. You're fine, so you don't have to demand anything.\par \par \uc2 Good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Guys, I want to draw your attention -- and you're probably both aware of this, the Dow down, Sandra, session lows today, a 646-point hit largely on that old line, hating uncertainty, not knowing how this is all going to work out, that it could drag on well into December before we know the makeup of the Senate. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Let's be honest. That market was in line with predictions and rising hopes that the Republicans were going to solidly not just flip the House, but the Senate as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And markets, as you know, like divided government.\par \par \uc2 They were looking for...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They still will get it, though, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They're going to still get it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But -- and I say this as I point to our own voter analysis after last night that did reveal, as far as inflation factoring into people's votes, the number one concern wasn't gasoline, which is a major problem for so many American families and small businesses in this country.\par \par \uc2 It was groceries and food. And we have to wake up today and tomorrow and the day after and realize that none of what happened last night fixes these problems, right? Americans are still dealing with record-high, 40-year-high inflation. Many Americans have had to take out extra jobs, two or three jobs just to pay for that inflation.\par \par \uc2 They're taking on credit card debt. They're living paycheck to paycheck in this country, more than 60 percent of Americans. So this is still a major problem for folks. So you look at the stock market, and I think that's a very telling sign that there's disappointment in what we have seen so far from these election results.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gerry, we were speaking to Governor Sununu of New Hampshire, who said he was not going to advise the president on what to say today, but he doesn't think he's going to pivot. And the way Democrats see it, they feel no reason to pivot.\par \par \uc2 That kind of feeds into this argument we're going to have divided government, and stubbornly so. Wall Street might welcome that, but the uncertainty that it isn't a full sweep -- it might be. The Senate might flip, but your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes, I mean, they're not going to get much done, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it looks pretty clear that the Republicans have taken a majority in the House, small one, smaller than was expected, certainly, but enough certainly to block any legislation that, if the Democrats do take the Senate, if they want to -- that would be likely to pass the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So I think the chances of there being anything really significant getting done in the next couple of years, probably pretty small. I think the other problem also is, I don't think this result will call upon President Biden - - and we will see in a few minutes -- I don't think it really calls upon him to sort of put out the hand of friendship to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I think he's going to take a victory lap, to be honest with you, although improbable that that may seem. He's going to claim that this is one of the best midterm results of any president in his first term in the last 50 years, which is true. He's going to say that Republicans claimed they were going to make all kinds of gains, they haven't, and that it's a vindication of his agenda and a vindication of what he said about the state of the country.\par \par \uc2 So that, I think, doesn't bode well. But for Biden, the idea that Biden was going to turn around the day after the election and suddenly say, as \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did after 1994, you know what, I want to reach across the aisle, and now I realize that there's a need for bipartisan rule. I don't think he's going to think that.\par \par \uc2 That's not Biden's character. Nothing that Biden has done in his two years as president or indeed in his 50 years in public life suggests that he's that kind of a man. So I think that is a concern, that we're going to have -- although Sandra rightly says divided government, gridlock is often not a bad thing for markets, I think there is concern but there are things that need to be done.\par \par \uc2 I think there must be concern about things like the debt limit. The fiscal position is a great concern for the markets over the next six months or so. There will be -- there will be debates about the debt limit, about the budget. And you have got -- the other thing is you have got, with Kevin McCarthy probably with a wafer-thin majority in the House, he's going to be pretty dependent on some, let's say, pretty ornery Republicans who might actually hold the government hostage and hold the debt limit hostage and things like that.\par \par \uc2 So I think it is a pretty nervy time, I think, given that we have got interest rates rising, given that we have got economic uncertainty. As Sandra said, we're going to have probably another horrible inflation number later this week, and given that we're looking down the barrel of a recession, I'm not surprised that this result doesn't make the markets very happy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're quite right about that.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have a habit -- and, Sandra, one of the things as we wait to hear from the president is, Republicans turn on their own. \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  comes to mind. You realize that they can be their own worst enemy.\par \par \uc2 The president now addressing reporters.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Well, we had an election yesterday. And it was a good day, I think, for democracy. And I think it was a good day for America. Excuse me. I'm a little hoarse.\par \par \uc2 Our democracy has been tested in recent years, but, with their votes, the American people have spoken and proven once again that democracy is who we are.\par \par \uc2 The states across the country saw record voter turnout, and the heart and soul of our democracy, the voters, the poll workers, election officials, they did their job, and they fulfilled their duty, and apparently without much interference at all, without any interference, it looks like.\par \par \uc2 And that's a testament, I think, to the American people. While we don't know all the results yet -- at least I don't know them all yet -- here's what we do know. While the press and the pundits were predicting a giant red wave, it didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 And I know you were somewhat miffed by my obsessive optimism, but I felt good during the whole process. I thought we were going to do fine. While any seat lost is painful -- some good Democrats didn't win last night -- Democrats had a strong night, and we lost fewer seats in the House of Representatives than any Democratic president's first midterm election in the last 40 years.\par \par \uc2 And we had the best midterm for governors since 1986. And another thing that we know is that voters spoke clearly about their concerns, about raising costs, the rising costs that they're -- and the need to get inflation down. There's still a lot of people hurting. They're very concerned.\par \par \uc2 And it's about crime and public safety, and they sent a clear and unmistakable message that they want to preserve our democracy and protect the right to choose in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I especially want to thank the young people of this nation, who I'm told -- I haven't seen the numbers -- voted in historic numbers again, and just as they did two years ago. And they voted to continue addressing the climate crisis, gun violence, their personal rights and freedoms, and the student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Last night, I was pleased to call Maxwell Frost, a 25-year-old who got elected, I guess the youngest man ever elected to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , and I told him that he -- I told him that I was first elected the second youngest person elected to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  at 29, that I have no doubt he's off to an incredible start in what I'm sure will be a long, distinguished career.\par \par \uc2 And when he's president and they say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's out in the outer office, I don't want him to say, Joe who?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But the voters were also clear that they're still frustrated.\par \par \uc2 I get it. I understand it's been really tough few years in this country for so many people. When I came to office, we inherited a nation with a pandemic raging and an economy that was reeling, and we acted quickly and boldly to vaccinate the country and to create stable and sustained growth in our economy, long-term investment to rebuild America, and our roads, our bridges, our ports, our airports, clean water systems, high-speed Internet.\par \par \uc2 And we're just getting started. The interesting thing is that this is all going to really come into clear view for people in month -- in the months of January, February, March of next year. It's just getting under way, so I'm optimistic about how the public is going to be even more embracive of what we have done.\par \par \uc2 Historic investments that are leading companies to invest literally hundreds of billions of dollars combined to build semiconductor factories and other advanced manufacturing here in America that's going to create tens of thousands of good-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, a significant number of those jobs are going to pay an average of $126,000, $127,000. You don't need a college degree to get those jobs. And we're dealing with global inflation as a result of the pandemic and Putin's war in Ukraine. We are also handling it better than most other advanced nations in the world.\par \par \uc2 We're lowering gas prices. We're looking -- we're taking on powerful interests, lower prescription drug costs, health insurance premiums, and energy bills.\par \par \uc2 After 20 months of hard work, the pandemic no longer controls our life. It is still a concern, but no longer controls our lives. Our economic policies created a record 10 million new jobs since I came into office. Unemployment rate is down from 6.4 when I was sworn in to 3.7 percent, near a 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 And we have done all this while lowering the federal deficit in the two years by $1.7 trillion. Let me say it again, $1.7 trillion. No administration has ever cut the deficit that much.\par \par \uc2 And reducing the federal deficit is one of the best things we can do to lower inflation. But while we have made real progress as a nation, I know it's hard for folks to see that project -- that progress in their everyday lives. And it's hard to see the results from actions that we took while -- but we have to implement what we have done.\par \par \uc2 But I believe we took the right steps for the country and for the American people. In fact, if you look at the polls, an overwhelming majority -- I don't look at them much anymore because I'm not quite sure how to read them anymore. I'm hope you're uncertain as well.\par \par \uc2 But the overwhelming majority of the American people support the elements of my economic agenda, from rebuilding America's roads and bridges, to lowering prescription drugs costs, a historic investment in tackling the climate crisis, to making sure that large corporations begin to pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 I'm confident these policies are working and that we're on the right path. And we need to stick with them. All these initiatives take hold, as they do, from lead pipes being removed from schools and homes, to new factories being built in communities with the resurgence of American manufacturing that's already created, by the way, 700,000 brand-new manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 You have heard me say it ad nauseam. I don't know where it is written that says we can't be the manufacturing capital of the world. We are now exporting product, not jobs, around the world.\par \par \uc2 People across the country are going to see even more clearly the positive effects on their day-to-day lives, but I still understand why they're hurting right now, so many people are concerned.\par \par \uc2 As I have throughout my career, I'm going to continue to work across the aisle to deliver for the American people. And it's not always easy. But we did it the first term. And I will be -- surprise a lot of people, but we signed over 210 bipartisan laws since I have become president. And we're revitalizing American manufacturing, gun safety. We did it together, and dozens of laws positively impacting on our veterans.\par \par \uc2 Let me say this. Regardless, regardless of what the final tally in these elections show -- and there's still some counting going on -- I'm prepared to work with my Republican colleagues. The American people have made clear, I think, that they expect Republicans to be prepared to work with me as well.\par \par \uc2 In the area of foreign policy, I hope we will continue this bipartisan approach of confronting Russia's aggression in Ukraine. When I return from the G20 meetings in Indonesia with other world leaders, I'm going to invite the leaders of both political parties, as I have done in the past on my foreign trips, to the White House to discuss how we can work together for the remainder of this year and into the next Congress to advance the economic and national security priorities of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I'm open to any good ideas. I want to be very clear. I'm not going to support any Republican proposal that is going to make inflation worse. For example, the voters don't want to pay higher prescription costs for drugs. We have cut that now. We're going to kick into gear next year, the next calender year. And I'm not going to walk away from the historic commitments we just made to take on the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 They're not compromisable issues to me. And I won't let it happen. The voters don't want more taxes for the super -- tax cuts for the super wealthy and biggest corporations. And I'm going to continue to focus on cost-cutting for working and middle-class families and building the economy from the bottom up and the middle out.\par \par \uc2 I know you're hired of hearing me say that, but I genuinely mean it. That's what makes America grow. The wealthy do very well when the middle class is doing well and the poor have a way up, while continuing to bring down the federal deficit.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we look at tax cuts, we should be looking at tax cuts for working people and middle-class people, not the very wealthy. They're fine. Look, I -- if you can go out and be a multimillionaire, that's great. Just pay your fair share. That's all. That's all. Just pay your fair share.\par \par \uc2 It's like those 55 corporations in 2000 that made $40 billion and didn't pay a penny in federal taxes. That's not right. Everybody has an obligation. So now they have to pay a staggering 15 percent. You all pay more than that for your taxes.\par \par \uc2 So I'm going to keep my commitment that no one, no one earning less than $400,000 a year -- and that's a lot of money where I come from -- are going to see their federal taxes going up.\par \par \uc2 And I want to be very clear. Under no circumstances will I support the proposal put forward by Senators Johnson and the senator from down in Florida to cut or make fundamental changes in Social Security and Medicare. That's not on the table. I will not do that. I will veto any attempt to pass a national ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 But I'm ready to compromise with Republicans where it makes sense on many other issues. And I will always put the needs and interests of the American people first.\par \par \uc2 So, let me close with this. On this election season, the American people made it clear they don't want every day going forward to be a constant political battle. There's too much of that going on, and there's too much that we have to do.\par \par \uc2 The future of America is too promising, too promising to be trapped in an endless political warfare. And I really mean it. You have heard me say it time and again for the last 20 months or so. I am so optimistic about the prospects for America. We need to be looking to the future, not fixated on the past, and that future is bright as can be.\par \par \uc2 We're the only nation in the world that's come out of every crisis stronger than we went into the crisis, and that's a fact. I literally mean that. We have come out stronger than we have gone in. And I have never been more optimistic about America's future than I am today.\par \par \uc2 I was -- it's particularly because of all those young people I talk about, 18 to 30. They're showing up. They're the best-educated generation in American history. They're the least prejudiced generation in American history, the most engaged generation in American history and the most involved.\par \par \uc2 Look, after a long campaign season, I still believe what I always have. This is a great nation, and we're a great people, and it's never been a good bet to bet against America, never been a good bet to bet against America. There's nothing, nothing beyond our capacity if we work together.\par \par \uc2 We just need to remember who the hell we are. We're the United States of America, the United States of America. There's nothing beyond our capacity. And I'm pretty well convinced that we're going to be able to get a lot done.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have been given a list of 10 people that I'm supposed to call on, and you're all supposed to ask me one question, but I'm sure you will ask me more.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And so let me start off with a list I have been given.\par \par \uc2 Zeke Miller, Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. President. I have two questions for you.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: As you mentioned...\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How come we never hold you guys to the same standard you hold us to?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But anyway, go ahead. I'm teasing. I'm teasing. I'm teasing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You mentioned that Americans are frustrated.\par \par \uc2 In fact, 75 percent of voters say the country is heading into the wrong direction. Despite the results of last night, what in the next two years do you intend to do differently to change people's opinion of the direction of the country, particularly as you contemplate a run for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nothing, because they're just finding out what we're doing. The more they know about what we're doing, the more support there is.\par \par \uc2 Do you know anybody who wants us to get rid of the change we made on prescription drug prices and raise prices again? Do you know anybody who wants us to walk away from building those roads and bridges and the Internet and so on? I don't know any -- I think that the problem is, the major piece of legislation we passed, some of it bipartisan, takes time to be recognized.\par \par \uc2 For example, you have got over a trillion dollars' worth of infrastructure money, but not that many spades have been put in the ground. It's taking time. For example, I was on the phone congratulating a Californian recently, and then someone in -- up in Scranton, Pennsylvania, the congressman who got elected.\par \par \uc2 He said, can you help us make sure we're able to have high-speed rail service from Scranton to New York, New York City? I said, yes, we can. We can.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it would make it a lot easier, take a lot of vehicles off the road. And we have more money in the pot now already out there, we voted for, than the entire money we spent on \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  to begin with. It's the same way.\par \par \uc2 For example, I talked about through the campaign that we're going to limit the cost of insulin for seniors to $35 a month, instead of $400 a month. Well, it doesn't take effect until next year. So there's a lot of things that are just starting to kick in, and the same way with what we have in terms of the environmental stuff. It takes time to get it moving.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not going to change. As a matter of fact, there's some things I want to change and add to. For example, we have passed most bipartisan, we passed the most extensive gun legislation, anti -- rational gun policy in 30 years, and -- but we didn't ban assault weapons. I'm going to ban assault weapons, or going to try like the devil.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not going to change the direction. I said I ran for three reasons. I'm going to continue to stay where I am. And I know -- I fully understand the legitimate concern that what I'm saying is wrong, OK? One is that I said we're going to restore the soul of the country, begin to treat each other with decency, honor and integrity.\par \par \uc2 And it's starting to happen. People are -- the conversations are becoming more normal, becoming more -- how can I say it? Decent. the second thing I said was, I want to build a country from the middle out and the bottom up. And that way, everybody does fine. I'm tired of trickle down. Not a whole lot trickles when you just trickle down to hardworking folks.\par \par \uc2 The third thing I know is still very hard. I'm going to do everything in my power to see to that we reunite the country. It's hard to sustain yourself as a leading democracy in the world if you can't generate some unity.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not going to change anything in any fundamental way.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just on a different topic, Mr. President, Russia today claimed that it had evacuated the Kherson region and the Kherson city. Do you believe that this is potentially an inflection point in that conflict, and do you believe that Ukraine now has the leverage it needs to begin peace negotiations with Moscow?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: First of all, I found it interesting they waited until after the election to make that judgment, which we knew for some time that they were going to be doing.\par \par \uc2 And it's evidence of the fact that they have some real problems, Russian -- the Russian military, number one. Number two, whether or not that leads to -- at a minimum, it will lead to time for everyone to recalibrate their positions over the winter period, and it remains to be seen whether or not there will be a judgment made as to whether or not Ukraine is prepared to compromise for Russia.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to be going to the G20. I'm told that President Putin is not likely to be there, but other world leaders are going be there in Indonesia. And we're going to have an opportunity to see what the next steps will be.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Nancy Cordes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. President, I have a few questions.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: I have been saving them up.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: OK.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: First of all, Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy said last night that it is clear we are going to take the House back.\par \par \uc2 Do you think he's probably right about that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, based on when we know as of today, we have lost very few seats for certain. We still have a possibility of keeping the House, but it's going to be close.\par \par \uc2 And, for example, in Nevada, we won all three of those seats, contested seats. I went out for each and I spoke with each, for each of those folks. But we won them all. I didn't know that last night.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a moving target right now, but it going to be very close.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Can you describe your relationship with Mr. McCarthy? How often to you speak to him? What do you think of him?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think he's a Republican leader, and I haven't had much of occasion to talk to him. But I will be talking to him, I think -- I think I'm talking to him later today.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: When it comes to your legislative agenda, when you were vice president, your legislative agenda basically ran into brick wall two years in when Republicans took control of the House. And that lasted for the rest of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  presidency.\par \par \uc2 Is there any way for you to prevent that same fate from happening this time around...\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... if Republicans take control of the House?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, because it's going to be much closer if they take control.\par \par \uc2 Look, the predictions were -- and, again, I'm not being critical of anybody who made the predictions. I got it, OK?\par \par \uc2 This was supposed to be a red wave. You guys, you were talking about us losing 30 to 50 seats and this was going to -- we're nowhere near -- that's not going to happen. And so there's always enough people in the -- on the other team, whether it's Democrat or Republican, that the opposite party can make an appeal to and maybe pick them off to get the help.\par \par \uc2 And so it remains to be seen. But, look, I doubt whether or not -- for example, all the talk -- I'd ask rhetoric -- I don't expect you to answer, but the rhetorical question, do you think that Senator Johnson is going to move to cut Medicare and Social Security, and, if he does, how many Republicans are going to vote for it? So, it depends.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And then my final question.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Republicans have made it clear that, if they do take control of the House, that they want to launch a raft of investigations on day one into your handling of Afghanistan, the border. They want to look into some of your Cabinet officials. They want to investigate you.\par \par \uc2 They may even want to investigate your son. What's your message to Republicans who are considering investigating your family, and particularly your son Hunter's business dealings?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Lots of luck in your senior year, as my coach used to say.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the American public want us to move on and get things done for them. And I heard that there -- it was reported -- whether it's accurate or not, I'm not sure, but it was reported many times, that Republicans were saying and the former president said, how many times you going to impeach Biden, impeachment proceedings against Biden?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think the -- I think American people will look at all of that for what it is. It's just almost comedy. I mean, it's -- but, look, I can't control what they're going to do. All I can do is continue to try to make life better for the American people.\par \par \uc2 OK Phil, Phil Mattingly, CNN.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 I have 37 questions. I'm kidding.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sir, at a fund-raiser last month, you said -- quote -- "The rest of the world is lacking at this looking at this election, both the good boys and the bad guys."\par \par \uc2 You noted you're going to the G20 in a couple days. You will come face to face with many of those leaders, the same moment that your predecessor is considering launching his reelection effort.\par \par \uc2 How should those world leaders, both good guys and bad guys, view this moment both for America and your presidency?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Will, first of all, these world leaders know we're doing better than anybody else in the world, as a practical matter.\par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding the difficulties we have, our economy is growing. You saw the last report. We're still growing at 2.6 percent. We're creating jobs. We're still in a solid position. And there's not many other countries in the world are in that position.\par \par \uc2 And I promise you, from the telephone calls I still have and from the meetings I have with other heads of state, they're looking to the United States and saying, how are you doing it? What are you doing? What can we do together? How do we -- so I think that the vast majority of my colleagues, at least those colleagues who are \uc2 NATO\uc2  members, \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Japan, South Korea, et cetera, I think they're looking to cooperate and wanting to know how we can help one another.\par \par \uc2 And what was the other question?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Oh, I haven't asked it yet.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the -- one way to follow up on that is you noted that you felt like there was a shift in terms of people being willing to show more decency in this moment. You have often talked about breaking the fever or kind of a transition from this moment that we have faced over the last several years.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel like the election is what represents that? Do you feel like the fever is broken, I guess?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I don't think we're going to break the fever for the super mega MAGA Republicans, I mean, but I think they're a minority of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 I think the vast majority of the members of the Republican Party, we disagree strongly on issues, but they're decent, honorable people. We have differences of agreement on -- on issues.\par \par \uc2 But they -- I worked with a lot of these focus in the Senate and the House for a long time, and they -- they're honest and they're straightforward. They're different than mine, but they're -- they're decent folks.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that the rest of the world -- and a lot of you have covered other parts of the world and you know -- the rest of the world is looking at the United States. I guess the best way to say this is to repeat what you have -- something you have heard me say before.\par \par \uc2 The first \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting for the public -- that's the seven largest democracies when I went to right after we got elected in February, after I got sworn in January. And I sat down at a table, a roundtable with the six other world leaders from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the United -- and Canada, et cetera, and said, America's back.\par \par \uc2 And one of them turned to me and said: "For how long? For how long?"\par \par \uc2 It was a deadly earnest question, "For how long?"\par \par \uc2 And I looked at them. And then another went on to say -- and I'm not going to name them -- went on to say: "What would you say, Joe, if in fact, we went to bed tonight here in England, woke up the next morning and found out that 1,000 people had stormed the \uc2 Parliament of Great Britain\uc2 , gone down the hall, broken down the doors, two cops ended up dying, a number of people injured, and they tried to stop the confirmation of an election?"\par \par \uc2 It's not the same situation, obviously, as we have.\par \par \uc2 And they said: "What would you think?"\par \par \uc2 And what would -- I ask a rhetorical question. What would you all think? You would think England was really in trouble. You would think democracy was on the edge if that happened in Great Britain.\par \par \uc2 And so that's the way people were looking at us, like, when's this going to stop? Nothing like this has happened since the Civil War. I don't want to exaggerate, but, literally, nothing like this has happened since the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 And so what I find is that they want to know, is the United States stable? Do we know what we're about? Are we the same democracy we have always been?\par \par \uc2 Because, look, the rest of the world looks to us. I don't mean that we're always -- like we're always right, but if the United States tomorrow were to -- quote -- "withdraw from the world," a lot of things would change around the world. A whole lot would change.\par \par \uc2 And so they're very concerned that we are still the open democracy we have been, and that we have rules and the institutions matter. And that's the context in which, I think, that they're looking at, are we back to a place where we are going to accept decisions made by the court, by the Congress, by the government, et cetera?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: So, the entire genesis of that \uc2 G7\uc2  conversation was tied to your predecessor, who is about to launch another campaign.\par \par \uc2 So how do you reassure them, if that is the reason for their questioning, that the former president will not return or that his political movement, which is still very strong...\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... will not once again take power in the United States?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, we just have to demonstrate that he will not take power by -- if we -- if he does run, I'm making sure he, under the legitimate efforts of our Constitution, does not become the next president again.\par \par \uc2 Steve, Reuters. I'm sorry. Steve Holland.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 How do you interpret last night's results in terms of deciding whether you want to seek another term? Is it now more likely that you will run, and what's going to be your timeline for consideration?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, first of all, Jill and I have -- and, by the way, this is my wife, Jill...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... who's a hell of a lot more popular than I am in the Democratic Party too.\par \par \uc2 But, at any rate, all kidding aside, our intention is to run again. That's been our intention, regardless of what the outcome of this election was. And the fact that we won -- we -- I didn't run. The fact that the Democratic Party outperformed anything anyone expected and did better than any off-year presidency since John Kennedy is one that gives everybody, like, whew, sigh of relief, that the mega Republicans are not taking over the government again, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And so my judgment of running, when I announce, if I -- and my intention is that I will run again, but I am a great respecter of fate. And this is ultimately a family decision. I think everybody wants me to run, but we're going to have discussions about it.\par \par \uc2 And I don't feel any hurry one way or another to make that judgment today, tomorrow, whenever, no matter what my predecessor does.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: By the end of the year, or early next year, or what's your thinking?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, my guess is -- I hope Jill and I get a little time to actually sneak away for a week afternoon between Christmas and Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And my guess is, it will be early next year we make that judgment.\par \par \uc2 But it is my plan to do it now, I mean, but -- OK, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 Karen, Karen Travers of ABC Radio.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner today was moved to a Russian penal colony to serve out her nine-year sentence. Do you have an update right now on her condition? What do you know about that? And does this mark a new phase in negotiations with the Russians to secure her release? Can the U.S. now fully engage in talks and a prisoner swap? And a follow-up, if I can.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, we have been engaging on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 I have been spending a fair amount of time with her wife about what's going on with her. And my guess is, my hope is that, now that the election is over, that Mr. Putin will be able to discuss with us and be willing to talk more seriously about prisoner exchange.\par \par \uc2 That is my intention. My intention is to get her home. And we have had a number of discussions so far, and I'm hopeful that, now that our election is over, there's a willingness to negotiate more specifically with us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And, if I can, your press secretary had said that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has continued to follow up on that significant offer, but also proposed alternative potential ways forward with the Russians.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us what those alternative ways forward are and how Russia has responded to those?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I can, but I won't.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can't. I mean, it would not be a wise thing to do, in order to see if they would move forward.\par \par \uc2 But it is my -- I'm telling you, I am determined to get her home and get her home safely,along with others, I might add.\par \par \uc2 April Ryan.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Of The Grio.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Of The Grio. Excuse me. I beg your pardon.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I got it right last time we did this.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Yes, you did. Yes, you did.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, I have a couple of questions on several issues, one, the Supreme Court. As you know, the Supreme Court has before it the issue of college admissions and affirmative action.\par \par \uc2 What can and are you planning in case of a rollback that is expected? There are legal analysts that say that there will be drastic implications, there are tentacles from this, and they even say this could impact Brown v. Board, the decision from Brown v. Board.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, first of all, I asked our Justice Department to defend the present policy before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And like a lot of pundits, I'm not prepared to believe that the Supreme Court is going to overrule the previous -- existing decision. That's far from certain, and I don't believe that, but number one.\par \par \uc2 So, number one, what I did to try to change it is object to it before the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , our administration.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I -- there are a number of things that we can and must do to make it -- and, by the way, this is a case involving an Asian American, in terms of getting into school, and whether there's affirmative action makes sense at all from the standpoint of those who are arguing against it.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that we're -- we're also in a circumstance where there's a lot that we can do in the meantime to make sure that there's an access to good education across the board. And that is by doing things that relate to starting education at age three -- formal schooling at age three -- which it increases -- not day care, but school. All the studies over 10 years show that that increases the prospect of someone making it through 12 years without any difficulty, no matter what the background they come from, by 56 percent.\par \par \uc2 And I also think that we should be making sure that we have the ability to provide for two years of education beyond that, whether it's apprenticeships or community colleges.\par \par \uc2 And we also are in a situation where I think that -- for example, I want to make sure we -- a lot of it has to do with finances as well -- that we make sure that we have help for people who come from modest means to be able to get to school.\par \par \uc2 You know, the cost of college education has increased fourfold. And it used to be that a Pell Grant would cover something like 70 percent of the college tuition. Now it covers significantly less than that. So I want to increase the Pell Grants as well.\par \par \uc2 But let's see what the Supreme Court decides. And I'm -- I am hopeful. And our team and our -- the lawyers who argued for us are not nearly as certain as the people you quoted as saying it's going to be overruled.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Next question, sir. The issue is inflation. The Grio and KFF conducted a study of Black voters that said inflation was the number one issue, and we saw it in this midterm election.\par \par \uc2 What can you promise concretely in these next two years that will help turn the pocketbook for the better in the midst of staving off a recession?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, a number of things. First of all, un -- Black unemployment is almost cut in half under my administration just since I began. More Black businesses have opened up -- small businesses -- than ever before.\par \par \uc2 We're now in a situation where we're providing, through the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 , down payments for people buying homes, because most people accumulate wealth in the value of their home, most middle-class families like mine. My dad bought a home, didn't have -- just scraped together to get a home. By the time he was able to retire, he was -- he had built up equity in a home. That's how most people do that.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but what I can't do is I can't guarantee that we're going to be able to get rid of inflation, but I do think we can.\par \par \uc2 We brought -- we've already brought down the price of gasoline about $1. 20 a gallon across the board. And I think that the oil companies are really doing the nation a real disservice.\par \par \uc2 They've made -- six of them made over $100 billion in the last quarter in profit. A hundred billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 In the past, if they had done the two things that they had done before -- one, invest in more refineries and producing more product and/or passing on the rebates to the gas stations that -- you know, they sell the oil at a cheaper rate than they have to -- than they are selling it now, not taking advantage. And that lowers the price of the total gallon of gas because that gets passed on.\par \par \uc2 So there's a whole lot of things that we can do that are -- that are difficult to do, but we're going to continue to push to do them.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is that one of the things that makes a gigantic difference is what are the costs that exist in the average family and the average Black community. One, prescription drug costs. Well, we're driving those down precipitously, beginning next year.\par \par \uc2 And I will bet you know a lot of people in the African American and Caucasian community that need to take insulin for diabetes. Well, we're going to reduce that cost. They're not going to pay more than $35 for the insulin instead of four -- average of $400.\par \par \uc2 And I can go down the list of the things that -- my dad used to say it a different way. At the end of the month, the things you have to pay for, from your mortgage to food on the table to gasoline in the automobile, do you have enough money to do it? And when it's done, do you have anything left over? And medical bills are a big piece of that, particularly in the African American community and the poor -- and poorer communities. They need help.\par \par \uc2 And so we're driving down all of those costs. And we've already passed the legislation to do that; it's just taking effect.\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot of things we can do to affect the things that people need on a monthly basis to reduce their inflation, their cost of living.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but I am optimistic, because we continue to grow and at a rational pace, we're not anywhere near a recession right now, in terms of the growth. But I think we can have what most economists call a "soft landing." I'm convinced that we're going to be able to gradually bring down prices so that they, in fact, end up with us not having to move into a recession to be able to get control of inflation.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And, Mr. President, last question on humanity. I know, everybody else got some.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Not everybody else.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Well, you're coming.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: OK, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Last question on humanity. Sir, you can't legislate and you can't executive order out the issue of empathy or the lack thereof in the midst of this rhetoric -- this heated political rhetoric. What's next?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Part of what I think leadership requires -- and I hope I meet the standard -- is letting people know you understand their problem.\par \par \uc2 Again, my dad used to have an expression. He said, "I don't expect the government to solve my problems, but I expect them to at least know what they are, understand them."\par \par \uc2 And like a lot of you, we've been very fortunate as a family, but we've also been through a lot of fairly tough times. And it's not -- and I've had the great advantage of having a family to get through them.\par \par \uc2 When my first wife and daughter were killed when a tractor trailer broadsided them and killed my wife and -- killed my -- my first wife and killed my daughter, and my two boys were expected to die; they were in the -- it took the Jaws of Life three hours to get them out. They were on top of their dead mother and dead sister.\par \par \uc2 I understand what that pain is like.\par \par \uc2 And when Jill and I lost Beau after a year in Iraq, winning the Bronze Star and Conspicuous Service Medal, a major in the United States military, came home with Stage 4 glioblastoma because he lived about 200, 5 -- between 2 - - and 500 yards from burn pit that's 10 feet deep and as big as a football field, burning every toxic waste you could find.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think that we understand what it's like to lose family members, mothers, fathers, to can -- all of you have been through that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 We've been fortunate, though. We've had each other. We've had strong families -- Jill's sisters, my brothers, my sister.\par \par \uc2 And so what we can do to deal with that empathy is make sure there's help available, make sure there's people who are there to help -- whether they are a psychologist or whether they're medical doctors or whether they're social workers -- to be there to help, to help just hold a hand.\par \par \uc2 And, for example, we can do an awful lot for a lot of families, the families you're talking about, if we re -- reinstate this Child Tax Credit. It cut child poverty by 40 percent when it was in place. I couldn't get it passed the second time around.\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot we can do. And the empathy is not just talking about it, it's communicating to people you genuinely understand. And I hope a lot of people don't understand, because they -- I don't want people having to know the pain.\par \par \uc2 But the second piece of that is: Let them know that you are there to help. You're there to help.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things I've talked with Vivek Murthy about -- and a lot of you have written about it, and you've written it well about it -- is the need for mental health care in America. You know, when we got elected, there were something like, I don't know, 2-, 3-, 5 million people who had gotten their -- their COVID shots.\par \par \uc2 Well, in the meantime -- I've got over 220 million people all three shots. But in the meantime, what happened? We lost over a million dead. A million dead.\par \par \uc2 I read one study that for those million people, they had nine people who were -- each one of them had, on average, nine people close to them. A relative, someone they're married to, a child -- someone close.\par \par \uc2 The impact has been profound. It's been profound. Think of all the people - - think of all your children or your grandchildren who didn't have that senior prom, who didn't have that graduation party, who didn't have all the things we had that we took for granted -- the impact on their psyche.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a lot we have to do. And empathy reflects itself not just on what a person demonstrates they understand -- of knowing what people need and helping to make it happen. And we're trying to do that. And a lot of Republicans are trying to do it, too. I don't mean this is a partisan thing. A lot of people are trying to do it because they know we got a problem.\par \par \uc2 OK, excuse me. These 10 questions are really going quickly.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Stick around for more.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I've got to meet with some of my -- talk to some of the Republican leadership soon. But -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 Jenny Leonard, Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. President. Two questions. One, shifting back to your trip to Asia. When you meet with President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  of China, will you tell him that you're committed to defending Taiwan militarily? And what are you hoping to get out of this meeting that will make it a success? Are you willing to make any concessions to him?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, look, I'm not -- I'm not willing to make any fundamental concessions because what I -- what I've told him in the beginning -- and this is -- we've -- I've spent over 78, I think they told me, hours with him so far -- 67 in person, when I was Vice President.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Obama\uc2  knew he couldn't spend time with the Vice President of another country, so I traveled 17,000 miles with them in China and around - - and the United States. I've met with him many times.\par \par \uc2 And I've told him: I'm looking for competition, not -- not conflict.\par \par \uc2 And so what I want to do with him when we talk is lay out what the -- what kind of -- what each of our red lines are, understand what he believes to be in the critical national interests of China, what I know to be the critical interests of the United States, and to determine whether or not they conflict with one another. And if they do, how to resolve it and how to work it out.\par \par \uc2 And so -- and the Taiwan doctrine has not changed at all from the very beginning -- the very beginning. So, I'm sure we'll discuss China -- excuse me, Taiwan. And I'm sure we'll discuss a number of other issues, including fair trade and relationships relating to his relationship with other countries in the region.\par \par \uc2 And -- and so, anyway. So there's a lot we're going to have to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Do you want another question?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Everybody else got one.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You didn't say if...\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Or two or three.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You didn't say if you will tell \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  personally that you are committed to defending Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm going to have that conversation with him.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: That wasn't my second one, sorry.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sorry, I actually have an unrelated question too.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, do you think Elon Musk is a threat to U.S. national security? And should the U.S. -- and with the tools you have -- investigate his joint acquisition of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with foreign governments, which include the Saudis?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think that Elon Musk's cooperation and/or technical relationships with other countries is worthy of being looked at.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Polls In Key States Close Minutes From Now; Soon: Pools Close In Battleground State Of Georgia; Polls In Key States Close Minutes From Now; Arizona And New York Among Tonight's Key Governor Races; Republicans And Democrats Focuses on Different Issues; Midterm Elections Polls Near Closing In Eastern States; President Biden States Democracy On Ballot for Midterm Elections; Economic Inflation Likely To Play Major Role In Midterm Elections Results; Voters Discuss Their Reasons For Viewing Current Midterm Elections As Exceptionally Important; Poor Weather Conditions May Affect Voter Turnout In Some Parts Of U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Bill Hemmer, Bryan Llenas, Aishah  Hennie, Shannon Bream, Dana Perino, Brit Hume, Alicia Acuna, Alexis  McAdams, Karl Rove, Jacqui Heinrich, Larry Kudlow, Harris Faulkner \par \uc2 8,061 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): After months of campaigning, media coverage and campaign ads, the battle for Congress all coming down to tonight.\par \par \uc2 Just under one hour now to the first poll closings in key battleground states, in the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, everyone. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Good evening, everyone. I'm Martha MacCallum, and it is election night, folks.\par \par \uc2 All that counts now is the actual vote. The final tally's across the nation will determine which party can call the shots on the Hill for the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): Right now we've got a split Senate, right down the middle folks. 50 Democrats, 50 Republicans, with the vice president able to cast that deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 Now, one seat here could really change everything. The Biden agenda would hang in the balance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Voters had been lining up at the polls throughout the day today. Georgia's polls close in the next hour. And Pennsylvania, the hour after that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But not all states count and report their votes the same way. And here is where it's going to get pretty tricky. And we're going to guide you through this tonight.\par \par \uc2 Georgia, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin, are known to be historically slow counting states. They start opening the mail-in ballots at 7:00 a.m. on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Other states like Florida and Texas, they start processing those ballots that come in before Election Day. Therefore, it will happen there probably much faster.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Virginia is quick too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Easy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's why we always warn against what's called the red or blue mirage. It may seem in the early hours of the night, that one party has a clear advantage, but it may be short lived. We'll guide you through this as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this election comes as our nation deals with crises on multiple fronts. We've got runaway inflation, we have out of control crime, and we have a disaster that has unfolded on the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Tonight will be the difference between what gets prioritized and what gets put to the side.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): Here is how the numbers break down.\par \par \uc2 First, on the Senate side of the Congress where both parties have no room for error, there are 50 Republicans and 48 Democrats in the Senate, but you've got two Independents who caucus with the Democrats. That makes a total of 50 for each party.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats have control because they have Vice President Harris's tie breaking vote. Republicans need to gain just one seat to take the majority. So, the Senate could come down to just a few key races.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Now, over to the House side of Capitol. Democrats hold a razor thin majority with 220 seats, while Republicans have 212. There are three vacancies with one likely to remain in Republican hands after a special election.\par \par \uc2 That would mean the GOP needs a net gain of five seats to take control of the House.\par \par \uc2 We have more than 25 Reporters spread across the battleground states throughout the country. Take a look at them there. We'll get to all of them throughout the evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): But first, let's head over to Bill Hemmer manning the billboard. Here we go, Bill.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Hi there, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Yes, this thing is loaded, and ready to go. Martha, good evening to you. Bret, good evening. And here we go.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the Senate side for Republicans to get control of that chamber. They must win and hold the five following seats, in effect, keeping them in the red column.\par \par \uc2 First of all, Pennsylvania, where Dr. Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  have been locked in a seesaw battle. In Ohio, also in the Buckeye State, that's where J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  right now are battling it out throughout the evening.\par \par \uc2 From Ohio, we'll move down here to the Tar Heel State. And the state of North Carolina where \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is hoping to hold off Cheri Beasley; could be a tight race and Carolina as well.\par \par \uc2 Back in the upper Midwest, however, in Wisconsin, we find Ron Johnson trying to find a third term defending his seat against a progressive newcomer by the name of Mandela Barnes. He is 35, a lieutenant governor there in the State of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That's four or five. The fifth one is down here in Florida, where \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is looking to hold off Val Demings. That closes 8:00, and they count pretty fast too.\par \par \uc2 So, if Republicans are successful in all five of those races, they still need to knock off a race somewhere else across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, the most likely possibilities could be, here in the state of Georgia, where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is trying to take down \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . We will check in there momentarily.\par \par \uc2 Also in the American West, stay up late for us tonight because it might be in the State of Nevada, where \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  has been leading against \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . Republicans think they've got a good shot at that race. We'll see whether or not they are right.\par \par \uc2 Also, in the American Southwest in the State of Arizona. Mark Kelly is trying to hold off Blake Masters, a battle we've been watching now for months.\par \par \uc2 Now, a victory in just one of those states, coupled with that five for five scenario would give Republicans the majority.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the GOP is having a good night, we might see other close races pop up in other states, maybe New Hampshire in the northeast, or Colorado in the Mountain West. Or even the State of Washington, some people believe that could be in play tonight, and therein lies the drama for this election.\par \par \uc2 On the House side, however, as we enter the evening, Democrats hold 220 seats, the Republican 212. 218 is a simple majority. Our Fox News Power Rankings for 223 seats in the red column, 186 in the blue, and we believe in the middle, there are 26 seats that are considered toss ups. And they will determine the margin and the House, and we may not know that for several days.\par \par \uc2 So, buckle up. It's, night one, perhaps as we begin now. Bret and Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Bill.\par \par \uc2 So, two of the biggest battlegrounds in this cycle that Bill was just talking about, Georgia, and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 We've got "TEAM FOX COVERAGE", Aishah Hennie at Herschel Walker's campaign headquarters in Atlanta. But let's start tonight with Bryan Llenas, who is live from a polling location in Bensalem, Pennsylvania. Hi, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, good evening. Polls in Pennsylvania close at 8:00 p.m., except in Luzerne County, where a judge has ordered polling places to stay open until 10:00 p.m. after some locations ran out of the paper needed to tabulate votes in voting machines.\par \par \uc2 Luzerne has a lot of Republican votes. President Trump won that county by 14 points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 This morning, polling workers began processing what could be as many as 1.4 million mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Statewide counting will continue overnight until they are finished. Initially, Philadelphia officials said they would be done counting by Wednesday morning. But now, it could take several days after the Board of Elections voted this morning to reinstate a security procedure to catch double votes.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is focus on undated and misdated mail -- excuse me -- mail- in and absentee ballots. A recent court decision said these ballots will not count.\par \par \uc2 But just yesterday, the Fetterman campaign filed a lawsuit arguing that they should count. Mail-in ballots could be a major path to victory for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  tonight, because about 70 percent were sent to register Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Senate race between Dr. Mehmet Oz and Fetterman is expected to be close, which could mean we won't know results for a few days.\par \par \uc2 The Oz campaign tells us they are feeling confident. The race is a tossup, but Oz has momentum, after erasing Fetterman's double digit lead over the summer by selling a bipartisan message on bringing balance to Washington.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): The Oz campaign, Martha, tells us, they are feeling good in places like deep red Butler Franklin in New York, where voter turnout has exceeded 2018 midterm levels by 4:00 p.m. today -- before people even get -- got out to work. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. And an extra hour for Luzerne County. Bryan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, let's head over to Atlanta, where Aishah Hasnie is reporting from Herschel Walker's campaign headquarters. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Martha and Bret. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Look, Republican insiders are telling us tonight that they do believe that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  could squeeze out a victory tonight and avoid a runoff, and there are a couple of reasons why.\par \par \uc2 Number one, they say that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has the momentum. Did have the momentum going into Election Day.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): He was able to close the gap with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and he was able to shore up his base in the end despite those early abortion claims, which of course, he denied.\par \par \uc2 Number two, he is getting a pretty big poll from incumbent Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and that successful campaign. And finally, this might be the biggest factor of all, Bret. Inflation is the top issue for voters in Georgia, by a mile. And this election really could be a referendum on Democrats and their policies.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has already been hinting towards a runoff that he's preparing for it. The Democrat even hinted this at a canvassing event earlier today.\par \par \uc2 He said whether it's tonight or next month that he believes Georgia will pick the right candidate. Now, the way to avoid a runoff in Georgia is to break the 50 percent threshold coming in today.\par \par \uc2 We were looking at a whopping 2.5 million early voters already. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Fox News voter analysis will explain the trends that we're seeing as the results start to roll in. Shannon is going through all that.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, good evening to you. What have you seen so far?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): All right, it is good to see you guys.\par \par \uc2 We've been asking voters around the country, more than 100 and 1000 of them, how they voted and why? So, this is our first look tonight at Fox News voter analysis.\par \par \uc2 We've got a new method to analyze their decisions. And to tell you why they voted the way they did.\par \par \uc2 The top concern for voters, not surprising, it is the economy. Nearly half of the electorate, 47 percent that we talked to say the economy is the most important issue facing the country. That's nearly five times as much as the next issue.\par \par \uc2 Abortion as a distant second at 10 percent, immigration at nine percent. The climate at nine person; Now, the highest numbers, if you dig in here, you can see there are sections of the country where these colors are most intense.\par \par \uc2 So, right here, I would say we've got a group Idaho, Wyoming, and Utah. And Oklahoma, in the middle of the country, they say there that the economy is their biggest worry; essentially, it's the economy stupid.\par \par \uc2 So, let's check in here to see what else we can tell you about the number so far. Inflation at a 40-year high. We asked who do voters' blame.\par \par \uc2 54 percent of them say the president and his policies. 46 percent say, though, they think it's out of his control.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's tease one of the hot races for you. In Ohio, we're watching closely tonight. Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is distancing himself from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 The Republican, in this race, J.D. Vance was endorsed by President Trump, yet, roughly equal numbers of Buckeye voters think that Ryan supports Biden too much and that Vance supports Trump too much. We'll see how that filters out in the votes tonight.\par \par \uc2 And a final tidbit for you, with midterms often is a referendum on the president, a third of voters say that they are actually falling behind financially. That is twice as many who felt that way two years ago. That could signal trouble for the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 We'll dig deeper into the numbers and get back to you on this soon. Bret and Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's bring in Fox News senior political analyst Brit Hume. And Dana Perino, co-anchor of "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM", co-host to "THE FIVE".\par \par \uc2 Dana, you just got off set. You've -- what do you thinking about tonight?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): From the outside.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, it was nice, nice night out there in New York City, as ball is happening.\par \par \uc2 Well, it was very interesting to be sitting with my colleagues of "THE FIVE", as I'm sure it is for you, in many ways, to think about how this midterm cycles really been going on for -- since January 20th to 2021.\par \par \uc2 And the history repeats itself, midterms are usually won by the opposing party from the president. I think one of the most interesting things tonight is to watch this Fox News voter analysis.\par \par \uc2 This -- Washington and many of the states across the country are going to have a lot of changes when they wake up tomorrow or by the end of the week when we have final results. And some -- a lot of what informs their decisions going forward on how to govern the country, what the people are asking for. If government is responsive to the people, it will have to really take a listen to what people are saying in that Fox News voter analysis. It's very interesting tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brit, great to have you with us, as always -- as always.\par \par \uc2 You know, it is. It's a measure of where the country stands. How it feels about the current president and what's been going on. It's an opportunity to weigh in on that tonight.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): If you look at that right track, wrong track number, and where the party stand on the -- on the issues, where the president stands, you see what would normally be the setting for a huge Republican sweep.\par \par \uc2 The polls don't indicate that at least not in the Senate, which is what we're watching so carefully, because it seems so close. The fact that they're in the House, the Republicans picked up 14 seats two years ago, so a lot of -- lot of their best possibilities have already been exhausted, they've -- there are people, Republicans already in those seats.\par \par \uc2 So, it looks close and closer than you might imagine with these conditions and with the public feeling the way it does about, about the economy and about the direction of the country.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this is -- this is interesting. And --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit --\par \par \uc2 HUME: And control is on the line. It isn't always in these midterms. Usually, it just tilts one way or the other. It's very much on the line.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is -- there's some shifts that we're seeing in these numbers in different demographics, Hispanics, Blacks?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Hispanics and blacks moving more toward -- I mean, they're not -- Republicans, not many Republican to win the Hispanic or Black vote, but they're going to win more of it than they have in the past from what we can tell from early analysis here. And that obviously bodes well for another -- that's another thing that didn't bode well for the Republicans tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. We have a lot of analysis coming.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we are just on the cusp of all of this, because we're going to have numbers rolling in about 45 minutes from now. We wait for those results.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): Also, we will take a look at some really interesting governor's races all across the country, because \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  turn New York City, where we are tonight red, and will Kari Lake prevail in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Karl Rove and Kellyanne Conway Join us as we count down to the 7:00 p.m. poll closings in a number of states -- six, actually, including all important Georgia. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back. While there's a lot of focus on the Senate and the House, 36 states tonight are holding races for governor this year. We're closely watching contests in Georgia, Michigan, Nevada, New Mexico, as well as New York and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's check in with Alexis McAdams and the surprisingly tight New York governor's race in just a moment. But first, let's go over to Alicia Acuna who is in Scottsdale, Arizona, following Kari Lake's campaign. Hi, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Hi, Bret. Yes, the race between Republican Kari Lake and Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  can arguably be one of the most watched in the country.\par \par \uc2 Lake and her opponent not only dramatically differ in policy but tenor and tone.\par \par \uc2 Hobbs, the sitting Secretary of State established herself in the spotlight during the 2020 election by not only certifying President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's win in 2020 but becoming a staunch defender of Arizona's election integrity.\par \par \uc2 She is promising to cut sales taxes and has centralized her message on defending democracy. At one rally warning "this could be the last election of our lifetime".\par \par \uc2 Lake a former local T.V. news anchor who is endorsed by former President Trump has found traction and not only attacking her opponent but the media. Lake has garnered national attention with her unabashed statements, questioning the legitimacy of Biden's win and promising to declare an invasion at the Arizona Mexico border on day one.\par \par \uc2 Both have heavily courted Maricopa County voters. No candidate wins a statewide race here without it as the second largest voting jurisdiction in the country. Maricopa makes up two thirds of the entire Arizona electorate.\par \par \uc2 Heading into today, 80 percent of voters had cast their ballots with 300,000 more expected today.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, Maricopa County elections officials say they resolved a technical problem that hit 27 percent of voting locations earlier today.\par \par \uc2 For a number of hours, this morning, affected tabulation machines would not count the ballots at the voting sites.\par \par \uc2 However, Bret and Martha, Maricopa County elections officials now assuring folks here every ballot will be counted. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks Alicia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In New York, Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has made a surprisingly strong showing against Democratic incumbent Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams is live at a polling location here in New York City, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Bret. Yes, all eyes around this governor's race here in deep blue New York.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, it's pretty surprising just how close it is. I think it was a shocker according to some experts that say that Governor Hochul's campaign did not think it would be this close this late in the game. Republican Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  says it's all a numbers game at this point. If you ask him, he says, if he gets more than 30 percent of the vote in New York City, he'll be the next governor of New York which be a stork for him.\par \par \uc2 So, as we know, that gap has really been shrinking in this race. And in recent weeks as the gap closes, voters say they're energized here by this race because they don't want to see where this is going. They don't like the movement this is heading in here in New York.\par \par \uc2 Zeldin would be the first Republican elected in 20 years to statewide office. Incumbent Democrat \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  took over for disgraced Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  last year and is looking to make history as the first woman elected to be governor of New York.\par \par \uc2 Crime the hot topic in this race, not surprising. Zeldin has focused his campaign around that issue, Bret slamming \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  for having what he says are soft on crime policies, trying to convince as many independent voters as possible that he is their guy.\par \par \uc2 Governor Hochul shifted her focus to crime in recent weeks here, launching a new subway safety initiative which has been important here.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live, we can tell you that we've been talking to voters all day who are looking forward to seeing who's elected tonight and it could be close, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, let's bring in a few more members of our panel. Karl Rove is with us, former deputy White House Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and Kellyanne Conway, pollster and former senior counselor to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also with us, Fox News contributor Juan Williams, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former congressman from South Carolina and host of "SUNDAY NIGHT IN AMERICA" and \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  senior political correspondent for Axios.\par \par \uc2 All right, Karl, what are you looking at right now? You see some of these results from our Fox News voter analysis and you have all the knowledge you have.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I'm not (INAUDIBLE). What's interesting to me is this is like a campaign with two different agendas being talked about.\par \par \uc2 Normally, we'd fight over big things, the war in Vietnam, the war in Iraq, but this is two different issues. Set of -- sets of issues, the Republicans are talking about the economy and inflation, are talking about crime, are talking about the border. And the Democrats are talking about abortion, threats to democracy and climate. And these things don't intersect.\par \par \uc2 And so, each party is out there talking to a different segment of the electorate. The problem for the Democrats twofold, one is the Republican issues are more important to the voters. We saw that with the issue of 51 percent think the economy and inflation is the number one issue. So, their issue set is not as important.\par \par \uc2 Second of all, problematic for them, independents in this election are looking more like Republicans, that is to say, their concerns are more like the Republican concerns than the Democratic concerns.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the interesting thing to me is one of the issues that Democrats have put a lot of emphasis on is threats to democracy. And yet, there's a new ABC Washington Post poll that showed that their lead on this issue is 47-43. That is to say they are thought by 47 percent of people to be better able to handle that issue than the Republicans who got 43.\par \par \uc2 And why? Because each side has its own description of what those threats to democracy are. So, you have conservatives and Republicans saying, you know, a government that doesn't -- you know, gives away a half a trillion dollars without passing a law through Congress or one party rule or the politicization of the DOJ or the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All of these kinds of things are seen by people on the right as a threats to democracy as well as the January 6th attack on the Capitol as seen by people on the left and right as a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kellyanne it's a great point. I mean, at the world that we live in where both each side has the issues that they're most fired up about. And it kind of seems like they're living in two separate countries almost at times.\par \par \uc2 You've looked at the Fox News, voter analysis as we get a gauge of what's on people's minds. What do you think so far?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, POLLSTER AND FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: So, Martha, this country no doubt is divided, our Senate is divided. Washington is divided, some of our households are divided, but the country is not divided on the biggest issues that are motivating them to the polls in these midterms.\par \par \uc2 Over 70 percent of Americans tell the Fox News pollsters and other pollsters, that they are very -- are extremely concerned about crime, about inflation, about immigration and border security.\par \par \uc2 And yet, the Democrats have taken a huge gamble on creating this parallel universe and talking about something totally different. We'll see if their gamble pays off.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that is not in doubt is that you're dealing with a very unpopular president whose disapproval rating has been over 50 percent for a year, independents are leaning far more toward Republicans. Will they show up?\par \par \uc2 I think for independents, the big question in midterms is not just for whom to vote, but whether to bother at all.\par \par \uc2 Some of those partisan, particularly Democrats who voted early. I think the Democrats were able to bank their votes early.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is the Republicans I think this time have gambled on expanding the electorate. And now the Republican Party's electorate, the voters look a lot more like America, and so do their candidates. And we'll see if that gamble actually pays off, I believe it will.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans have invested in what I call the non-sexy stuff in politics, not just the big T.V. ads or the rallies or the candidate.\par \par \uc2 They have talked to voters, 100 million contacts, one million volunteers, the data and digital from 10 years ago when Romney lost and they did their autopsy is much better.\par \par \uc2 One of the thing I'm looking at tonight, if it's real that the suburban women 20 percent of the electorate are trending Republican, I think many of them are not voting their gender, they're voting their kids. And they're still out there as parents, a year after Glenn Youngkin took Virginia and \uc2 Jack Ciattarelli\uc2  in New Jersey almost did.\par \par \uc2 They are still parents, they're still upset about loss learning and reduced texts -- test scores, a little bit of abortion, a lot inflation and crime, but so much education is a sleeper issue in this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we'll see how that breaks down.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're counting down to the first poll closings in six battleground states or six states total, I should say. Next, he's not on the ballot. But President Biden has loomed large over this election. So does he help or hurt vulnerable Democrats?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the economy is a top issue driving voters to the polls, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  on how record high inflation will impact who wins tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Less than 30 minutes until all polls close in six states, including Georgia. The peach state has a marquee race in the Senate as well as the state House, and then 30 minutes later polls close in Ohio and North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But first, President Biden ramping up campaign appearances after keeping a relatively low profile for most of the midterm cycle. His legacy, his agenda could be on the line tonight. Jacqui Heinrich is live on the North Lawn of the White House. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret. President Biden urged people to vote in a radio interview a short while ago, saying that Republicans on the ballot are, quote, "a different breed of cat," pointing to 300 who he called election deniers, and warning that they may not accept today's results.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Despite a hopeful closing midterm message --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I want you to know we'll meet this moment.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president admits there's a real risk Democrats will lose control of the House.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it's going to be tough. I think we can. I think we will win the Senate, and I think the House is tougher.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's strategy to pull Democrats over the finish line paint an alarming picture of a GOP controlled Congress.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Democracy on the ballot. Political violence and intimidation are on the rise all across America. And you remember January 6th, the angry mob that stormed the U.S. Capitol, attacked law enforcement, hunted down officials, erected gallows to hang Vice President Pence.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House shifting focus away from what Biden hopes to get done in second half of his term with his legislative reach very much in question.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Why not just tell the country what we have done? Why not just lay this out? Which we have. We have laid out what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president's former spokesperson admitted in September Biden's unpopularity gave Democrats a narrow path to win.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I think that Democrats, if the election is about who is the most extreme, then they are going to win. If it is a referendum on the president, they will lose. And they know that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden seizing on that and sticking with it until the very end.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We know in our bones that our democracy is at risk, and we know that this is your moment to defend it, preserve and protect it, choose it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The vice president Harris today would also not answer how she would advance the Biden agenda if Republicans control Congress, saying only that she is working to keep that from happening, and today is still Election Day. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So America's number one issue is the economy. Inflation has skyrocketed under this president's watch, forcing Americans to pay more for gas and groceries. There is a graph of what it looks like on the wall.\par \par \uc2 So here to talk about it now is \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 . Larry, good evening, happy election evening to you. Your thoughts as we get ready for some numbers here to roll in at 7:00 p.m. tonight?\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: First of all, thanks for having me. Look, inflation is number one, for good reason. Too much spending, too much money printing, too much regulating, the war against fossil fuels, all these things contributed to a big rise of inflation, really, by the middle of 2021. And I think the problem right now is that inflation has become embedded into the economy. It's become embedded into wages. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is having to crack down on raising interest rates and cutting back on the money supply. Recession looms large. Democrat economists tried to warn them against it. That failed. I don't know, right now, it's a very difficult story. No wonder it's number one. It's absolutely the key issue in this campaign, has been from day one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Larry, Democrats didn't really turn to the economic message until the end of this campaign. But what kind of message is that? I mean, they are saying that the economy is strong, inherently. Is that viable?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: No, I don't think it is. Look, statistically, Bret, the first two quarters were negative of this year. The third quarter was slightly positive. The fourth quarter may be slightly positive. But it's still a very weak economy. You are in a terrible housing slump, for example.\par \par \uc2 And probably, look, the soft underbelly of the Biden economy, frankly, is the decline in real wages. Year-to-year change, I think it's down 18 consecutive months. That's just a killer. And people don't want to work for less than what they had before. And so it shows up in grocery prices. It shows up even in lower gasoline prices still way ahead of a year ago.\par \par \uc2 But this is part of the issue of getting embedded into the economy. Once you start inflation, you know, eight, nine, ten percent is very difficult to bring it down. Expectations begin to form. Labor unions get restless. Railroad strike may be on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 And again, one of the key factors here is the war against fossil fuels, all right. This need not have happened. We could have had an all of the above strategy. But instead, we are still producing 2 million, 3 million barrels per day fewer oil than -- fewer barrels than we had pre-pandemic. And a lot of people, including myself, don't understand this. Why not side by side? Why not renewables? Why not fossil fuels?\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, another inflation factor. Refined petroleum products permeate this economy, literally 150 or more. I mean from eyeglasses to MRIs to fertilizers to clothing, there's no end to it. These prices have all gone up because of the problem with fossil fuels. We're not producing enough. And too much federal spending, and too much regulating, and too much money printing. It's very hard for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  now. And that's where the pain is going to come in next year.\par \par \uc2 By the way, if the Republicans take both houses, they are going to be failed with the very difficult situation. You are going to probably have a recessionary economy and continued high inflation. So, this is a difficult one.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we have heard a lot about layoffs, and that's part of the pain that you expect to come with all of these rate hikes that we have had over the last several months. Larry, thank you. We will talk to you Larry. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Larry.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we will hear from you, the voters. Harris Faulkner asks a live audience about the issues most important to them and what they think is at stake in this election. As we count down to the first poll closings of the night, it's election night, 2022. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Of course, today is the day when voters make their voices herds at polling sites all across the country. Before polls opened today, more than 40 million Americans, that's a record, had voted early. And Harris Faulkner is here with some of the voter voices live and in person. Hi, Harris.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We're so excited, Martha, and Bret, thank you very much. The midterm elections, obviously, are upon us. And we have heard so much from the candidates and the pundits in the weeks and months leading to this big night. Now it's time to hear the voters' voices. Throughout the night we'll hit the top issues that voters say drove them to the polls.\par \par \uc2 And a little bit about our audience now, our live audience. First time ever we have done this. People from all walks of life all along the political spectrum on the right, on the left, everywhere in between. And like all Americans, they have enormous stake in the outcome of tonight's races.\par \par \uc2 Also with me tonight, pollster Lee Carter is here. She is partner and president of Maslansky and Partners, and she and her fancy team have used their special dial technology to gauge voters' reactions to the top issues of this critical midterm election. I want us to talk directly to the voters, so I'm going to get up and start talking to people. Kristin (ph), you are here. First of all, let's do a quick show of hands. Does this election to any of you feel different, like more is at stake, a raise of hands if so? Is that everybody?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It looks like everybody.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, that's unanimous. Kristin (ph), I'm coming to you first. Why is that? Why do you feel like this means so much?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, from my perspective, I would say that there have been things happening lately that are enormous issues, like the Roe v Wade being overturned, and I feel like women's reproductive rights is so important to me. And I feel that this is the midterm that matters to make our voices known, to say that that's not OK. We need women's reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right, and I'm guessing you voted Democrat today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I did a little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: You did a little bit of both. We will get that that later. A lot of people did some ticket splitting because they were split on different issues. David (ph), I'm going to ask you the same question, why is this election different for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This election is very different because whoever wins there is no in between, whoever wins gets to make the decisions moving forward, and that's very important of who our leader are and what decisions they are making.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And I know that you voted Republican today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right. OK, we'll move. Watch the lines on your screen now. Red represents Republicans' reaction, blue is for Democrats, and gray is for independents. First up tonight, the most powerful Republican in the House, Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy, on this big election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Which direction does America want to go? That's why we have the commitment to America. We literally spell out what we'll do to make us energy independent, give us an economy that's strong, secure the border, fund the police, stop the prosecutors who are woke, give you a parent's bill of rights. I think the entire nation should look at what has -- the Democrats have done with one party rule, look at what the commitment says, and make a decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right, so the lines that you saw correlate with how people responded. \uc2 Lee\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: You can see Democrats, that blue line, they gave it a D, Republicans an A, and independents a B plus. Very different reactions across the board there.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: So I know you dug deep and asked people why they did what they did. By the way, along party lines 