they gave it a C minus.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right, we're going to go back to Bret and Martha. I know they've got some races called, more voters' voices coming up a bit later.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Interesting stuff. Harris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another factor in tonight's results, mother nature, the weather potentially affecting some turnout in key states. FOX Weather Meteorologist Amy Freeze has more. Good evening, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY FREEZE, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Good evening, Bret. Really across the country, east of the Mississippi, things have been pretty mild. Even at this hour, Dallas has 78 degrees, but out west where the polls are still open it has been a real storm that has come through. Did not reach into Arizona. In fact, Maricopa County, no problems there today, but just a little bit further to the west. Look at the mess that's over Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Reno, this is Owasso County, they have had very tricky travel, temperatures right near freezing all day, the Sierra getting up to four feet of snow. And that's not it. They also had stormy weather in northern California, maybe a tornado touching down outside of Sacramento on Highway 99. We have flooding rains at this hour right now over Los Angeles. In fact, flood watches up for much of southern California. Look at those strong storms coming over the Five and the Grapevine.\par \par \uc2 And then of course, on the east post after the polls have closed, but it will be interesting to see what happens, because we have tropical storm Nicole. It won't be making landfall until tomorrow night, but they already had evacuation orders today in Bradford County. And as we all know, weather does impact people's choices to get out to the polls. Hopefully people will make that effort tonight even with the storms out west. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Amy.\par \par \uc2 Let's get back to the rest of our panel and get them into the mix here. We'll start with Juan Williams. Juan, your thoughts as we are just a few minutes away from getting results tonight?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think post COVID, Martha, I think there has been a real change in American politics, which is that we can see today that Democrats vote early and oftentimes vote by mail. Republicans vote on Election Day. It's almost like you have two elections going on. And we are going to see that play out in terms of how we report the results. And we have to be very cautious about that because some, well, some speculation, mirages may appear here and there.\par \par \uc2 The other thing to say very quickly is, for all the talk about election fraud and the big lie and the big steal and all that, today has gone fairly smoothly. We've had some trouble in Maricopa County in Arizona, as you have heard, some machines not functioning properly. I think it's like six voting areas in Georgia, six polling places, they have been told to stay open later because of some problems with the balloting. And the judges have said we need to make sure those people are able to vote.\par \par \uc2 But overall, despite the many election deniers, despite the fact that you have 30, I think, secretaries of states running who deny the results of the 2020 election, this has been a fairly smooth election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maricopa says they have fixed those machine problems in those precincts and they're moving on. Trey, your thoughts early?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: I think Dana put her finger on it. Every tailwind you can have, the economy, inflation, gas prices, history is with the Republicans. So if they underperform tonight, somebody needs to ask how and why? And if there is a wave, what does that portend for 2024. That's what I will be watching.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": I'm looking at the disconnect between the House and the Senate. We're going to be getting some House numbers in Virginia and Indiana early on, and it will give us an early sense of the public's mood. But Senate races are about candidates. And Republicans have nominated some untested rookies in the political landscape, like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Dr. Oz and Blake Masters. So I'm really going to be closely watching. I know folks on the House races put on the red and blue jerseys, but on the Senate races they look at the candidates, and the candidates matter in those contests.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a great point. And Bill is going to take us through those Virginia races as we get a look at the three races, two, seven, and ten, that we think are going to give us early indications tonight of the mood out there. But it's also really important what Juan was saying, that in some places they're going to start counting today. They do the mail-in ballots first and then they do today's vote. In some places it's the exact opposite of that. So we're going to take you through that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, you want to weigh in?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Voter analysis says something like 40 percent of the electorate will vote on Election Day, which means that change that Juan was talking about has evolved now. And now so that tells you, for example, that in Pennsylvania the people, a majority of Pennsylvania voters, if that holds for Pennsylvania, and there is no reason why it wouldn't, voted in this election without seeing either of the candidates in debate, without seeing \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and the difficulty that he had that were so striking to all of us. That -- is that an argument for more early voting or not? Good question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That is a good question. It's definitely an argument for more debates.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are waiting now for the final moments here before the next poll closings, including the closely watched state of Georgia. Jesse Watters, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , Laura Ingraham will join our special coverage of the 2022 midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020221109eib800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Americans Vote In Critical Midterm Elections; Candidates Make Their Final Pitch To Voters; Arizona Votes In Hard-Fought Senate And Governors Races; Sen. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  (R-FL), Is Being Interviewed About GOP's Funding Fight In Key Races; New Hampshire Voters Cast Ballots In Critical Senate Race; Voting Underway In Closely-Watched Gov Races; Legal Battle In Pennsylvania Over Mail-In Ballots; Control Of Congress At Stake As Americans Vote; Some Dems Fret Over Party's Midterm Messaging\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Alicia Acuna, Bryan Llenas, Aishah Hasnie, Marc  Thiessen, Juan Williams, Hillary Vaughn, Matt Finn, Grady Trimble, Alexis  McAdams, Rich Edson, Byron York, Brit Hume \par \uc2 9,435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL METEOROLOGIST (on camera): This winter weather. John, back to you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Max. Well, they know about snow there in Reno. No question about that. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 For more Fox Weather, download the Fox Weather app or streaming Fox Weather on your favorite connected TV device. All right Sandra, that does it for us. Nothing to do but wait.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK. Thanks so much for joining us on Election Day. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We'll see you tomorrow with the results, well, some anyways. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you so much John and Sandra. Happy Election Day everyone.\par \par \uc2 Happy Election Day, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. We just heard that man talk about how much he loves the experience of getting out there and voting. And that is what Americans across the country are doing today. The polls closed across the nation in the midterm elections that could shift the country's political landscape, we will see what happens and no doubt then the conversation as you know, starts rolling right over to 2024. But let's watch it what's going on right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My opponent is the most extreme nominee for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in modern New Hampshire history.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I get asked, how are you going to fix the economy? Well, I'm going to do the exact opposite of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: By January, I'll be even better, but he'll -- Dr. Oz will still be a fraud.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: We already have 40-year high inflation. I don't know how much worse we could have done recklessly spending our children's money, but \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  thought that was important.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): My opponent calls abortion, demonic or religious sacrifice. He says he wants to punish the doctors.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: I think that Mark Kelly is the very worst United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): I've got an opponent who's trying to run the bus Justice Department all, but I think dreamers and TPS recipients should be here.\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT\uc2  (R), NEVADA SENATE CANDIDATE: She had the opportunity to stand with Nevada to tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  she wasn't going to spend more money, but she cast that vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): I have faith in the people of Georgia. And whether it's tonight or a few nights from now, you mark my word that people in Georgia are going to get this right.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: And you listen to our president said that, you know, the biggest threat to democracy is voting for a Republican. No, the biggest threat to democracy is keeping them in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And in there with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia, and this quote last night, as the President made his closing, closing argument before Election Day, he said, "If we lose the House and Senate, it's going to be a horrible two years." That was from President Biden last night, watch some of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we'll win the Senate. I think the House is tougher.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you think awaits you if it is a Republicans controlled Congress?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What do I think what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Awaits you. What's, what's your new reality going to be like in Washington?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: More difficult.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's one way to put it. Marc Thiessen and Juan Williams here live in studio to kick things off and also National Republican Senatorial Committee Chair, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , will join us. A lot of this effort has been under his organization to elect Republican senators. We'll see how he is feeling today as people head to the polls, also Brit Hume, our Senior Political Analyst gets a look at how things are looking turnout wise, and some of these election issues that are going on in Arizona and other places. We'll dig into all that with Brit.\par \par \uc2 But first, we have an extraordinary team that is spread out across the nation. We have 25 reporters in the key battleground states all across the nation. The folks you're looking at, you know them well. They're in Pennsylvania, Georgia, and Arizona. Let's start with Alicia Acuna who is on the ground in Paradise Valley, Arizona today where we just heard from the governor, gubernatorial candidate, Kari Lake there. Hi, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, and they say every vote counts. And that includes those cast by the candidates. And you mentioned, gubernatorial Republican candidate, Kari Lake. We just saw her on her air. She did go into Phoenix to cast her ballot within the last hour. Lake is in a super tight race against Democrat and sitting Secretary of State for Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . But right now, Maricopa County, which is the second largest voting jurisdiction in the country, is trying to get things back up and running after officials said 20 percent of their tabulation machines are not working, which means they are not counting the ballots on site where folks are voting. Here's what Kari Lake had to say when she was done voting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: They got to fix this problem. This is incompetency. I hope it's not malice, but we're going to fix it, we're going to win. And when we win, it's going to be come to Jesus for elections in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Elections officials say they immediately went into a backup system which allows the ballot to be dropped into a secure box, which will then be transferred to the central office to be counted tonight. Before this happened, the Maricopa County elections predicted 95 to 99 percent of the results here would be in by Friday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES (R), MARICOPA COUNTY, ARIZONA SUPERVISOR: I don't anticipate that that timeline should change. We may have fewer ballots that will be counted and reported here on election night or in the wee hours of the morning tomorrow. But I think that that's what's happened here so far today should not impact that estimate for the Friday totals.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (voiceover): Also, we're also watching this closely, the campaigns for Senate as incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly is in the fight of his political life, trying to fend off a challenge by political newcomer, Republican Blake Masters. And Martha, right now, Kari Lake is you know, encouraging people to get in line and stay in line and also vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): But this glitch with the Maricopa County Elections Division could not come at a worse time. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No doubt, need to figure that out. And they got to figure it out quickly. Alicia, thank you very much. Let's go over to Bryan Llenas, our correspondent in Bensalem, Pennsylvania right now, home to the high stakes Senate race between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz. That one also could determine the control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Tell us what's going on where you are today, Bryan?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. Well, no major issues to report from polling issues nation, polling sites statewide, which is good news. We have reports of strong turnout in western P.A. and here in Bensalem, just north of Philadelphia. They tell me here at this polling site, it's been very good and steady turnout. $312 million have been spent on this Senate race, the most expensive in the country, and it all comes down to today.\par \par \uc2 It's expected to be a very close race. Dr. Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Lieutenant Governor, cast their ballots. This morning Fetterman had a double digit lead all the way until about late August when Dr. Oz challenged him to multiple debates. Fetterman refused to debate and to release his medical records and things took a turn after both candidates agreed to do one debate two weeks ago, a debate that led to more questions about whether Fetterman was fit for Office following his stroke in May.\par \par \uc2 Last night, both candidates reminded voters what's at stake in this election. And now, you're looking live at poll workers in Delaware County who are processing mail in ballots under the law. Counties had to wait until 7:00 a.m. today to begin sorting through those mail ballots, and they must work overnight until the count is done. It was four days before we knew a winner of the 22, the 2020 election. That was the first time they use mail in ballot voting here in Pennsylvania. That was 2.6 million mail- in ballots, Martha.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing about half, expecting half of that this time around. They are optimistic, statewide, that they can get this done quickly. But if this race is razor thin, all bets are off, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Long night expected in a lot of ways. Bryan, thank you very much. Now, to Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie, who has been covering this Georgia race for us throughout the last weeks and months and she is there on election day. It's a state where voting records have been shattered in the crucial Senate and governor's races in the early voting part of all of this. Hi, Aisha.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, and early voting has been so popular in fact, both the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidates actually went ahead and also cast their ballots ahead of today, Election Day. And take a look at this more than 36 percent of the state's nearly seven million active voters have also already cast their ballots. That is huge.\par \par \uc2 Election officials hope to release those results by 7:15 p.m. tonight, and this could all get very interesting because if a candidate does not get more than 50 percent of the votes tonight, the race then heads to a runoff. Now, the issues not the candidates, seem to be driving voter turnout today, the pressure on the GOP to prevent a repeat of 2020 when Georgia handed Democrats a Senate and inflation, Martha, could really help \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not too much, too much breaking the bank, like living paycheck to paycheck. But I am noticing a difference and hopefully, you know this election also turned around.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Money don't go anywhere anymore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Especially when you're on a fixed income, it doesn't go anywhere at all. It's, it's very bad.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voiceover): Martha, the only major voting issue we've seen in the last 48 hours was right here in Cobb County, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  filed a lawsuit over about a thousand absentee ballots that were never sent to voters all because of a human error, a mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): A judge ordered those ballots to be overnighted to those folks. Election officials did that and as long as they are postmarked by 7:00 tonight, they will be counted, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Aishah, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Marc Thiessen, American Enterprise Institute Scholar and Fox News Contributor; and Juan Williams, Fox News Political Analyst. It's Election Day, we are all back together again. It's great to see both of you today. So, I guess your top line thoughts, as you listen to these reporters in these really important states of Arizona and Georgia and Pennsylvania. Marc, let me start with you, what goes through your mind today?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, I'm hoping the red wave is going to be strong enough to pull some of these weaker Senate candidates across the finish line but I'm worried about it. These races are closer than they ought to be. And there's a number of reasons for that, you can have \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  on in a few minutes. Republican Senatorial Committee blew through $180 million by August and had to pull out of New Hampshire and other states because they ran out of money because they mismanaged it.\par \par \uc2 And so, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had to come in and save the day. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has spent as of this morning $240.7 million on these, saving these Trump, Trump-nominated candidates. $32 million dollars in Ohio to rescue JD Vance, $57 million in Pennsylvania, $38 million in Georgia, $37 million in North Carolina -- and that's just to defend territory the Republicans already have. And then, that because he had to spend money and like, you know, JD Vance is running for a seat. Rob Portman won by 20 points. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  is winning by 19 points. Right now, the (INAUDIBLE), it's close.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to spend $32 million just to win that seat, and I think we're going to be able to do it. But that means he couldn't spend money in New Hampshire, he couldn't spend money for Tiffany Smiley in, in Washington State. He couldn't, he didn't have enough money for, for Arizona for Blake Masters, because that was a really close race. So, we may leave some, some money on the table tonight that we could have had, if, if we if things had been managed better and we have different candidates.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we're going to talk about that with \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  coming up, he had some criticism that you have just pointed out as the leader of the Republican Senatorial Committee, he has said in the past, he's going to win a lot of these races tonight. And we'll see where that leaves his reputation and his relationship with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . So, we're going to be talking about that in just a few minutes. So, you want to stick around for that. Juan, what's your take on this election day?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I mean, we just heard so much talk about, you know, machines not working in Maricopa County, questions about how people are lining up in Georgia, but turnout is strong. We've also had some instances where you see the Republican saying, we don't want votes that were cast by mail counted if they didn't have a specific date placed on them, or they didn't have a witness.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's all those questions that are at play. This is a moment in American political history, I think, where you see right now, people on the Democratic side saying, you know what, we are worried that there's going to be election denial from Republicans who might lose and say, you know, what, we don't believe the process. And that's why you say people saying we worry about the process itself about democracy being threatened.\par \par \uc2 But overall, I think right now, what we've seen is the success of Republican messaging, especially when it comes to crime --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is it messaging, or is it policy?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think it's messaging. I don't think it's a matter of policy, we wouldn't see the success. I mean, look, the historical trend for any midterm is the party in power loses seats and loses it the big way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: When you listen to these voters, Juan. You know, you hear that guy, he said, you know, inflation is eating up all my money, right? That's not a message.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a reality for that man.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, no, you said policy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he doesn't understand what's going on?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, no, wait, wait, hold on. You said policy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: There is no policy articulated by Republicans in Arizona and everywhere else that says, yes, this is going to solve it. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , again, you can ask him and by the way as senator --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: How about, how about not spending 1.9 trillion dollars in --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM: Hang on a second, Marc, but ask Senator McConnell how he felt about \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  saying, oh, yes, we -- our cure for the economy, we're going to cut into Medicare, Social Security. That's just not, that's just not going to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's not what he said and you know it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Wait a second, that's what he wrote in his policy. That was his policy. That's what he wrote down as part of his agenda that every two years we should look at this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He said it should be checked in every five years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Five years, right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But no Republican supports that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: First of all, let me just get one thing. It's all going to be gone if they don't start checking on it, because it's due to expire.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Let me tell you --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We can't even fill the Social Security --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You asked the American people if we should be viewing support for Social Security.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they can lie to you about whether or not they're going to ever see Social Security.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: This is a line that the Democrats have been using and failing for years. Remember, the ad where they were trying to push, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , was pushing a little old lady over the over the edge of a cliff? This is, this is like another failed Democratic talking point that has not worked in previous elections. It's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, if that's the argument, then you got to find a way to fund it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: To fund, to fund social --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To fund social security.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We can work on it, but I don't think anybody wants to end it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We can work on it?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM: And I said, fund it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's going to end itself, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, let me just say, Americans don't want it to end, Martha. That's the point and they want it protected, if that's your policy --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well then somebody needs to take responsibility to figure out how to fund it because it's not fully funded.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right, but if it's, if it's a policy speaking to economic issues, what do Republicans have? Zilch.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Oh my gosh, Juan.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't want to go down the rabbit hole, but it is really important. You can't just tell people it's going to be there forever if you don't figure out a way to make it be there forever. So, everybody would like to have the money come back that they've paid in. There's no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's what I'm saying to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You better make sure it's going to actually be there.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Fine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, you guys.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to be seeing you throughout the night and we're always glad to have you with us for lively discussion. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, GOP Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , the aforementioned senator from Florida is running the operation to regain Republican control in the Senate, where he predicts that seats will flip from blue to red tonight, and we're going to talk to him about that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Democrat incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  won her New Hampshire Senate seat by 0.1, four years ago. She is now in a very tough fight against retired Army Brigadier General Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 National Republican Senatorial Committee chair \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , says that if Bolduc beats Hassan, it is a precursor of what will happen across the country, and he joins me in moments on that.\par \par \uc2 But first, to Fox Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn live in Stratham, New Hampshire today. Hi, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Well, Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  said it herself. She is not surprised that this is a close race. She's also no stranger to a nail biter.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (voice over): You mentioned, she won by just over 1,000 votes the first time she ran. And this time, her opponent, Don Bolduc has eaten into her lead, leaving this race neck and neck.\par \par \uc2 Both candidates stopping by polling places to cast their votes today. Fox Digital caught up with Senator Hassan. Her closing message to undecided voters is that democracy and abortion rights are on the ballot. Today caps off a two-year campaign marathon.\par \par \uc2 For the general, we caught up with him today. He tells me, he's put in the time and he is confident it will pay off. He also says this election boils down to one big issue, inflation. Heating and eating.\par \par \uc2 So, we asked voters today after they voted what was the number one issue on their mind that impacted their vote?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women's reproductive rights would be pretty much first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Protecting the rights of women. And saying also just keeping my eye on Social Security at my age and Medicare --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not happy with prices of heating and gas, and food.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The big issue is the economy. It's not a coincidence that things have turned to what they have. The changes that have been occurred since the last two years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Got to be the inflation. Inflation stinks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): Martha, in New Hampshire, there is no early voting, just absentee and same day in-person voting. So, this is a state where a lot of minds did not need to be made up until today. And a lot of them were not.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state also says they're expecting the turnout today to break records. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. Hillary, thank you very much. Hillary Vaughn in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , in-charge of helping the GOP take back the Senate as chair of the Republican Senatorial Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much. I know this is a very big day for you. So, we do appreciate you coming by.\par \par \uc2 You know, I heard you say a few weeks ago, you know that you thought you would win all of these toss up Senate races. Do you still believe that today?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): I think we're going to get 52 plus seats. We got great candidates. They're running great races. We got great grassroots. And, by the way, the voters are showing up. You look at -- look all around the country, voters showed up because they know the Biden agenda is a horrible agenda.\par \par \uc2 But, when we win, you should thank all these candidates that went out and ran great races.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to ask you about your leadership at the RSC. And we spoke about this before because you have gotten a lot of pushback about the way you handled things from folks inside your party and people who run some of the GOP PACs that also support these candidates.\par \par \uc2 And Marc Thiessen was just here moments ago. I don't know if you heard him, but he basically said that the RSC ran out of its $180 million in August, because you spent too much money at the -- at the front half in the very beginning of all this and ran out of money.\par \par \uc2 And then, the SLC, one of the largest organizations that is part of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's venue, ended up having to spend $240 million and save people in states that they felt they shouldn't have to spend money in. What is your response to that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, first off, you know, the D.C. crowd wants to give people credit or pay him blame. It's really the candidates to win these races.\par \par \uc2 But here is what we did. We did the opposite of what they did in Georgia two years ago. In Georgia, they didn't define Warnock early. So, I told people early on.\par \par \uc2 I took over right after we lost the Senate back in 2021. And I said we're going to invest in to find our opponents early. And guess what, we're in the hunt.\par \par \uc2 Now, it would be hard to get there, but we're in the hunt to pick up half of the Democrat's seats right now. I mean, we're going to, I think, we're going to pick up Georgia, we're going to pick up Nevada.\par \par \uc2 But we have ever reasonably, we can pick up Arizona and New Hampshire, possibly Washington, possibly Colorado. We did that because we defined our opponents early.\par \par \uc2 We started doing that early, we continue to raise money. We continue to help our candidates. But in the end, we are winning because our candidates have run good races. The voters like what our candidates talked about, they hate the Biden agenda, and we have great turnout.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say about Ohio, where DeWine is ahead by double digits and Vance, it took him until mid-October to start to finally know that gap, and it took more than $30 million to be brought in late in the game by the SLC when they could have been spreading that money around to some of these other races and expanding the horizon.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I should you know. We have -- we had heated primaries. J.D. Vance went through a heated primary. And when you do that, you use up all your money and you got to start raising money again.\par \par \uc2 So, what the Senate committee did that I ran is on all those primaries, we invested right afterwards. Because if you look at historically what happens, is if we don't invest right away, and help our candidates in defining their opponents, we get so far behind like what happened to him in Georgia two years ago that you won't have a chance to win.\par \par \uc2 Warnock was never defined early. And so, after the November race for a win to the runoff, they spent millions of dollars and couldn't be Warnock because he had already defined himself.\par \par \uc2 So, what we did is we helped our candidates right when they got out of the primaries, when they were out of money, and we defined our opponents right away.\par \par \uc2 So, we're in the hunt now. We're in the hunt. Think about it. We've got seven states that we could possibly pick up. Now, that would be a great night if we did that. But we're going to pick up two --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And we're going to pick up more than that, I believe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you one more question. And that has to do with after tonight. And if you do get the 51-52 seats that you feel you're going to be able to win for Republicans, here is a quote just a short time ago from the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said that \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  -- he said he's looking for a leadership change on the Senate Republican side. And he said, "\uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida could be a likely candidate, calling him a very talented guy who is highly underrated. I think \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is the likely candidate."\par \par \uc2 And then, he said, "he hates the guy," referring to the current leader in the Senate, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . What do you say, sir?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, first of all, we always like when people say nice things about you. So, I'm appreciative that the president said that. My focus is make sure we get a majority back tonight. That's my focus. I'm not worried about what happens tomorrow or next week. My focus is doing exactly what I've done the last two years, make sure we get a majority.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And would you be interested in becoming the Senate Majority Leader? A. And b, is the former president correct that you hate \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I don't just like anybody. I mean, I have my beliefs about, you know, about what I would like to get accomplished up here. But I don't -- I don't just like people.\par \par \uc2 And are you interested in being leader of the Senate -- majority leader of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'm focused on tonight, Martha. This is -- I have a job right now to make sure we have a Republican majority leader. That's my focus.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We will all be watching. And we thank you very much for taking the questions and for spending some time with us today. Obviously, a very important day for you and the work that you have done leading up to this and we'll be watching it into the wee hours.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Bye.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Bye, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bye by now.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, our election coverage continues with Brit Hume coming up this hour.\par \par \uc2 And a live check on the governor's races to watch with our Fox correspondents on the ground across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: And we're feeling great. All the early reports is that, throughout the entire state, our supporters are coming out strong today. So, if we keep that up, it's going to be a hard-fought well-earned victory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's always exciting to watch Election Day in action. And that's the kind of the back part of the voting booths and people are lined up coming into them. Not crazy busy at that particular precinct in New York City at 3:30 this afternoon where the governor's race has become very tight in recent weeks, and six other states have governor's races that are toss up. So we -- all of us who are during the coverage tonight will be unfolding all of these races as they come in this evening, and giving some great analysis of what is happening in all of them.\par \par \uc2 One of them is Wisconsin where we're going to take you live in just a moment. But first to correspondent Matt Finn, let's swing over to the southwest to New Mexico, which is also a toss up state he's in a basketball Jam, which is often where we find voters on a day like this. Hi, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the latest numbers from New Mexico Secretary of State at this hour, so far about 72,000 more Democrats have voted in this date, in early person and in person voting. But we talked a short while ago to Republican candidate for governor Mark Ronchetti. He says he's not concerned because his team anticipates that Republicans will be showing up to vote in person today.\par \par \uc2 And right now we are in Bernalillo County. This is the largest county in New Mexico. It's also a Democratic stronghold. This voting location has been organized we've seen no issues. In fact Republican candidate for Governor Mark Ronchetti cast his vote here today.\par \par \uc2 Democratic governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  is considered the front runner but this race has tightened. Ronchetti's path to victory needs to include winning over Hispanics who make up about 40% of New Mexico's voters. Governor Grisham campaigned on keeping abortion legal, Ronchetti campaigned on reducing crime and restoring the National Guard to New Mexico's border. But the number one issue is the economy.\par \par \uc2 We talked to a handful of voters and they told us they are voting today on inflation and gas prices. Also worth noting in the past week, Ronchetti released text messages that he says a local TV employee sent to Grisham's campaign during her 2018 run. He claims the text tipped off Grisham about debate topics and questions. Ronchetti says those texts are irrelevant in this race because they reveal Governor Grisham is a cheater. We caught up with the governor and asked her if she wanted to deny receiving those texts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Governor Mark Ronchetti says you have not denied that your campaign received those 2018 debate text. Do you want to deny that you received those texts?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D), NEW MEXICO GOVERNOR: All of those character attacks are completely outrageous at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is your campaign received the text or are you willing to deny it?\par \par \uc2 LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : It's denied.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: Our understanding is that was the first time the governor denied receiving those texts on camera, former campaign spokesperson for the governor who settled a sexual assault claim with the governor tells us that she did in fact receive those text and her campaign use them to their advantage. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting reporting. Matt, thank you very much. Matt Finn in New Mexico. Let's go to another one of these toss up races which is in Wisconsin today, Democratic governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  facing Republican challenger \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , who's a businessman from Wisconsin. Fox Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports live on this one from \uc2 Oshkosh\uc2 . Hi, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. The Badger State's governor's race has been one of the most expensive in the country, but it could all come down to that one kitchen table issue that voters across the country are concerned about, inflation. Republican \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  has made the case that he's better equipped to tackle that issue partly because of his background in business. He's also been hitting Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  on crime and education. Every voter I've talked to today, though, says the economy and inflation is their number one issue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is impacted our savings and our retirement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we're headed in a wrong direction that is, and it needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Going back to the businesses, everything's getting more expensive for them. A lot of businesses are having to close their doors and we don't want to see that anymore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (Inaudible) my wallet is empty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: There's also a Senate seat up for grabs in Wisconsin today. Incumbent Republican Senator Ron Johnson voted here this morning. He told me he's feeling cautiously optimistic about his chances against progressive Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes. But he says Republicans should assume it's a dead even race and it very well could be in both the Senate race and the governor's race. In fact, if both of those races on the same ballot are decided by less than three points, Martha, that would be a first in Wisconsin state history. And this is a state that's used to close elections, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And not a particularly quick counting state, so we're going to be patient and hang in there late Night for Wisconsin. Brady, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's swing back to the East Coast now and take a look at this fascinating race that has played out in New York. Fox Power Rankings shifted this governor's race from likely Democrat where it was pretty much throughout this whole thing to just leaning Democrat just a few weeks ago. It was solid blue, as I said prior to that. Correspondent Alexis McAdams is live at a polling place here in New York City. What do you know, Alexis?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, it hasn't been too busy right here in the past half hour or so, but it's been picking up throughout the day and talking to those voters, a lot of them say crime is their top issue in this race, all eyes around the New York governor's race a lot closer than expected.\par \par \uc2 In Deep Blue New York, Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Martha, says it's all a numbers game at this point. And he says if he gets more than 30% of the vote in New York City, he'll be the next governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R) CANDIDATE FOR NEW YORK GOVERNOR: We have enough support out there to win the race, but people have to get to the polls, right? Inside New York City, we can't get less than 30%. We get less than 30% of the vote in New York City, we just don't see how we can win the race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Congressman Zeldin has been focusing on crime specifically in New York City, trying to convince as many Independent voters as possible that he's there guy. Voters I've talked to today say they're fed up with the direction the city is heading in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would like to go back to the way it was. They tinkered with these things and it caused a lot of trouble.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: New York City voters are a priority for Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  as well. She spent a lot of time in the city recently. In this video, she was campaigning at a senior center on the Upper West Side yesterday. Hochul says if Democrats come out, she's going to win.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D), NEW YORK GOVERNOR: New York City's incredibly important but I believe that we're going to see a major turnout all over. Just right here in Manhattan, Brooklyn, early voting numbers are astounding. That is a very good barometer for us. But again, those who know me, I got it out to the end. You got to go through the tape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So as of noon, the New York City Board of Elections says more than 884,000 people have cast their ballots in the five boroughs including early voters. Back out here live in the Upper East Side, we can tell you voters are still trickling in to make sure their voices are heard. Polls close tonight at 9:00, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: 9:00 tonight Eastern Time, the New York polls close, that's right. Alexis, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So Democrat Pennsylvania Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  takes legal action in an effort to count mail in ballots with incorrect or no dates on them. They're still figuring this out in Pennsylvania. Byron York, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair, also joining us Reince Priebus when our Election Day coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's take a live look at some of these polling places. We just cruise all around the country and take a look at them as people come in and out. This one is Bensalem, Pennsylvania, outside of Philadelphia, as they effort -- the purpose -- the voting world, right, we're going to figure out who's going to be the senator in Pennsylvania, who is going to be the governor in Pennsylvania. And counting is always a big issue when it comes to Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and other Democrats filed a lawsuit to allow mail-in ballots with the incorrect date or with no date on them to be admissible. Our Fox News Power Rankings call this a toss up race Fetterman and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz need every vote they can get. And they'll see that across the board in this. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson reports live on this from Pittsburgh today. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Martha. You know, you were talking about states that are slow and counting votes. Let me introduce you to Pennsylvania that's really expected here.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's campaign headquarters tonight is taking place pretty close to where we are right now. They'll be opening the doors soon and that election night event will take place in a couple of hours. It could go long into the night, could be an early night, no idea. It depends on how the vote counting goes, and there are plenty of factors outstanding that could influence that.\par \par \uc2 First off, polls opened here 7:00 this morning. When the doors open, there had already been lawsuits filed and Supreme Court decisions made. The latest lawsuit comes from Fetterman's campaign suing so counties can include their vote tallies mail in votes with incorrect or missing dates. Democrats tend to make up the majority of mail in votes.\par \par \uc2 State law says mail-in voters must write the date on the outside of the envelope. Republicans had previously sued for counties to exclude ballots without dates or invalid dates. State Supreme Court cited last week with the GOP, now some counties are letting voters correct their ballots.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH CHAPMAN, PA ACTING SECRETARY OF STATE: The Department of State's position that voters who make you know minor errors on their ballot, if they forget to sign it if they forget to date it, you know they should be able to go to their county and fix that issue, and have, you know, a chance to have their vote counted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That's one of the counties here, Allegheny, where you can correct your vote. Other counties like Lancaster, once your votes in it's over. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  not taking anything to chance, he voted in person today earlier. We asked whether he thought Democrats would hold the Senate, he did not respond though his campaign says he has trouble processing audio since his stroke.\par \par \uc2 Allegheny County here, they say they've already scanned 130,000 ballots. For comparison four years ago in the midterms, they had about 540,000 total votes. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rich, thank you very much, Rich Edson. So let's bring in Byron York, Washington Examiner, chief political correspondent and Fox News contributor and Reince Priebus former White House Chief of Staff for President Trump and President of the Michael Best Law Firm. Good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Reince, let me start with you. What's your reaction to this lawsuit that has been brought by \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and his people on it? It says the date on a mail-in ballot envelope has no bearing on the voter's qualifications and serves no purpose other than to erect barriers to qualified voters exercising their fundamental constitutional right. I think we have this we can put up on the screen for people to see, to vote. This unnecessary impediment violates the civil rights and the first and 14th amendments to the US Constitution. Reince?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I practice in this area of law back in the day. States in this country, most states, they originally had Election Day voting with excuse absentee ballot voting. But as the laws changed over the years, state legislatures like Pennsylvania put in absentee ballot voting, early voting, and in this case, these new mail-in voting procedure.\par \par \uc2 In that statute, though, Martha, it lists out the exact things that a voter needs to do in order to execute a valid ballot before election. And one of those things in that statute says they shall date, sign, witness and seal the envelope. And if one of those things is not done, that envelope and that vote is up for interpretation. This gal that was on the air with you in your package there, that you just aired said, well, if you make minor mistakes, they should be added.\par \par \uc2 Now, what those minor mistakes are, is that instead of you filling in the bubble, you might X or circle, the bubble or the name where you can draw the intent of the voter. But all of those things, the date, the witness, the signature, those are things that a voter must do in order to execute a valid ballot. And the statutes clear, they wouldn't say shall, they wouldn't say do your best efforts. It says they shall do it. And if they do it, that's a spoiled ballot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In advance, they open them up, and then if there's a problem, you can come in and cure your ballot, or fix it. Pennsylvania starts the whole process today on Election Day. Byron, what's your take on this whole thing? The politics of it and the legal side.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I'm afraid we're at the beginning of this litigation rather than the end of this litigation. The Fetterman campaign has retained a man named Mark Elias, kind of a legendary figure, Democratic lawyer in election terms. He helped change a lot of state election laws into 2020, using the pandemic as a pretext to change a lot of laws on terms that made him more favorable to Democrats. And he's most famous among Republicans as being the man who commissioned the Steele dossier, which they view as one of the worst dirty tricks around.\par \par \uc2 Now, the issue of whether you have to correctly date a ballot is -- whether that's being nitpicky or not, that is something you have to decide before Election Day. Once Election Day comes around, it seems it's time to just observe what's in the law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. On the ballot that I did, there were all kinds of instructions on it. And it said, you must do this, double check, have you signed this, have you dated this before you close it. You know, you have to do it the way it's supposed to be done.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you. Reince and Byron, I know we're going to see lots of you over the coming hours through Election Day. Thank you very much. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're now two hours out from the first polls closing as we gear up for our special election night coverage. We'll be joined by senior political analyst Brit Hume live in New York with his final thoughts before we start to get to the counting when our coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's look around some of the polling places here, Georgia, Las Vegas, Phoenix and Philadelphia. You know, pretty busy, I guess. They used to be busier before so many people voted from home. But, you know, I think we're seeing a pretty good turnout from the reports that we're getting. And we'll see how it develops throughout the rest of the day. Those are all toss up states, and they will have a say in who has control of the Senate as we look at that. Some have already slammed their own party for sort of missing the mark and the Democrat Party. In the closing arguments, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: There was this kind of fool's goal, this idea that, you know, the threat to democracy is so severe that that was something that you had to talk about. But you also have to talk about the economy.\par \par \uc2 HILARY ROSEN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think the President's closing message last night about democracy is on the ballot again, is frustrating to people like me who, you know, don't want to see half the voters in America alienated or saying we're being accused of aligning with fascists because they're voting for their pocketbook.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume is with me here in New York, getting ready for our big election night coverage, kicking off about two hours from now. Brit, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Thank you. Glad to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hilary Rosen, obviously a veteran Democrat strategist and the Clinton camp, she's just throwing the red flag up.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, she's not wrong in the sense that the Democrats seem obsessed with issues that are not that high on voter's priority list. But if you're going to talk about crime and inflation, and you're a Democrat, and you voted for what you voted for and supported what you've supported, what are you going to say? Everything I did was wrong, my votes were wrong, and obviously, it's a case by case deal. But you're going to say something.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you're going to talk about something, you got to have something to say. And it's not clear to me at this point that Democrats really had a lot to say that didn't contradict what they've done and said before at this stage of this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's the question of whether or not it was the messaging or actual policy that's underlying it.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Results, it's the result.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right? Here's \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  talking about it. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA GOVERNOR: We're getting crushed on narrative. We're going to have to do better in terms of getting on the offense and stopping on the damn defense. I'm not paid to say that I'm paid to say, you know, we have faith in God. I mean, I get like, I mean, I could be the cheerleader, I'm also a prime minister, you feel it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, this party has the presidency, the House and the Senate. So, you know, they're going to be defending everything that they've been doing for the last two years, just by the nature of that.\par \par \uc2 HUME: The truth is, with respect to what Newsom says, they've been on the offensive. They've been trying to go on the offensive. That's what this whole democracies on the ballot nonsense is about. Democracy is the ballot, by the way. And they've been trying their best to go on the offensive on abortion. The issues that they think cut in their favor.\par \par \uc2 The problem is if they want to talk about, as we just pointed out, if they want to talk about the issues that are top of mind with voters right now, they got it, you know, they got to figure out something to say, and they're on defense, and there's no way off of it, because it's -- these are the policies that have given us the inflation and the other problems that we're having, crime, border mess and all of that. There's -- I don't see how they get on the offensive on those issues.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, you wonder if this is something, it seems like incompetence always wait until after the midterms to sort of say, OK, so we got a message, right? Do you see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if this turns out to be a difficult night for Democrats, we don't know yet. Do you see him doing that?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I see a couple of scenarios. One is, if they lose the House, but it's not a complete blowout, right, not a massive wave, and managed to hold on to the senator loser only narrowly. The President may decide, hey, we came pretty close. We did better than expected, no need to make a big change here and reverse myself on all kinds of things. But if it's -- if they lose both, and the margin is significant, and everybody says big wave blowout, what are you going to do, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 You know, he has some history of being a dealmaker. He and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  used to do a lot of business. They got along very well. And they were friendly. Biden is a very friendly guy, by the way. He's easy to get along with personally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's how he said all that when he was running.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mr. Hume and I worked together for years.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Exactly. So he could revert to that. I've heard some people say, been dominance a little. I've talked to him a little while ago and he was saying how he thinks with the candidates that are left, if the lollies moderates such so called, a little more moderate members of the Democratic caucus lose, the caucus is going to be even more leftist than it is now. And it'll be harder for a president and a party leader to steer them into negotiations with Republicans. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 He'll have to make some kind of course correction. You just can't keep on doing what you're doing. That's the definition of insanity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What's your feel for what's going to happen tonight? I mean, obviously, we can't really predict anything until we see what starts happening. But having watched a lot of elections, what does your gut tell you right now?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I'm always one who believes that condition circumstances, right? Results are what matter at the ballot box. And looking at the -- what we've seen so far, just the conditions in the country looks to me like a big Republican Wipeout. But the map is bad for the Republicans, really bad.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're only -- they're looking -- Democrats only defending 14 seats. That's not that many opportunities to pick up. Republicans defending 21, so it's a bad map.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Because of gains that were made in the last.\par \par \uc2 HUME: In part, yes, exactly. Well, no, not just that but just because what seats are up. And then, of course, they gained. They didn't lose a single seat in 2020. Republicans in the House didn't lose a single seat, and so they're facing a circumstance where a lot of low hanging fruit has already been picked. So it's tough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I have the pleasure of sitting next to Brit tonight on the panel. So I'm looking forward to that --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Pleasure is mine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have fun and we're going to have some great coverage. Thank you, Brit. Thanks for coming by today --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- on Election Day. We will see you later.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for right now but THE STORY is just going to continue all throughout the night, and probably into the wee hours of the morning when Bret and I will take you through the Election Night coverage. We have the privilege of doing that and we look forward to it. Have a great afternoon. We'll see you in a couple hours.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221109eib800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Liberal Think Tank Calls Democrats "Out Of Touch"; Republican Optimism In Oregon Governor Race; Most Competitive Senate Races In 2022 Elections; Biden Energy Policy An Issue In Pennsylvania; Voter Discontent Could Reshape Biden Agenda; Clinton And Gingrich Cooperated After 1994 Rout; Nevada Senate Candidates Highlights Their Key Differences; Arizona Senate Race Focuses On Economy And Border; Former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley Campaign For Georgia Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Ahead Of Election; Republican And Democratic Campaigns Preparing For Possible Lawsuits After Midterm Elections; President Biden Promotes Wind And Solar Energy And Says Coal And Oil Plants Will Be Shutting Down Across U.S.; Polling Indicates Advantage From Republican Party In Upcoming Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Rich Edson, Brit Hume, Alicia Acuna,  Alexandria Hoff, Aishah Hasnie, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Right, Greg?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I can watch that every day.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): We need -- we need more glass doors to fight crime.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's true.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, the plain doors may, I think, we need to fight crime.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Defund glass doors. Defund glass doors.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. Don't forget to vote tonight. It's very -- tomorrow -- anytime --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): And tonight -- right?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Or early -- for whenever you can. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bret. Did you vote yet?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): I have not voted, Judge. How about you?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I didn't hear. Did you ask if I did tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Did you vote that? Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Judge. We'll see you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Wait, how was --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you live tonight from Fox News World Headquarters in New York. This is the final day of campaigning and what has been a long and intense midterm election season.\par \par \uc2 The economy has emerged as the top issue, with skyrocketing inflation, soaring interest rates. Democrats have largely sought to obscure that issue with abortion politics, talking about at the beginning, and concerns about a threat to democracy as they close.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Vice President Harris are with several candidates tonight in Maryland for a rally to begin in about an hour. White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Maryland tonight with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is making his final midterm push in safe democratic territory after rallying his base in a pair of virtual DNC receptions. Conveying optimism about Democrats chances, but warning as he has before that fundamental rights and freedoms are in jeopardy if MAGA Republicans win.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The final midterm message from the White House returns to top voter issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Independent economists are warning that Republicans in Congress want to worsen inflation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But only after the president, his democratic predecessor, and democratic surrogates, spent the weekend barnstorming the country with fire and brimstone warnings about what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , 44TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you've got election denier serving, then, democracy as we know it may not survive in Arizona --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: This is not an abstraction. Government start telling you what books you can read and which ones you can. Dissident start getting locked up, reporters start getting locked up.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: House Majority Whip \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): To loosen this democracy could very well be the end of the world.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Something of a walk back when pressed.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Nobody said the world is ending.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): You did say there, though, it could be the end of the world.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: The world did not -- the end.\par \par \uc2 No, democracy will be ending. The world will continue to exist. The world was here before Hitler, the world was here after Hitler. That's what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Democrats now admitting flaws in the strategy.\par \par \uc2 HILARY ROSEN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: When voters tell you over and over, and over again that they care mostly about the economy. Listen to them. Stop talking about democracy being at stake.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The day before the election, left leaning Think Tank third wave sounding the alarm on the party's flaws, calling Democrats, "out of touch on priorities", "out of touch ideologically", "out of touch on values". And, "Despite a roster of GOP candidates who are extreme by any standard, voters see Democrats as just as extreme, as well as far less concerned about the issues that most worry them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, traditionally, presidents hold a press conference the day after the midterms, win or lose to talk about the signal from voters, and plans to engage with the new Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now, after considerable pressure from the press. President Biden still has not committed to take questions, despite his own warning that democracy is on the ballot, and his past statements that a free press is indispensable to the functioning of democracy. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As mentioned earlier, President Biden is participating in a rally in the State of Maryland. And at that rally, will be the Democratic gubernatorial candidate. Wes Moore, he's expected to be there. The author and Army veteran will face Republican Dan Cox, who's a member of Maryland's House of Delegates. Incumbent Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is term limited, can't seek re- election there.\par \par \uc2 Our Fox power rankings have this race as a solid democratic pickup. But let's take a look around the governor's races around the country.\par \par \uc2 If we head out here to Oregon, Republicans are optimistic they can flip some of these out west. Former Representative Christine Drazan could become the first Republican governor of Oregon in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 She faces former Democratic Representative -- State Representative \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2 . And there is also the Democrat turned Independent Betsy Johnson, expected to take some votes away from Kotek in this race. That along with strong fundraising on the Republican side really gives Drazan a good shot here out in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 Let's take you now to Nevada, and this governor's race. Clark County Sheriff, Republican gubernatorial candidate Joe Lombardo, challenging incumbent governor Democrat \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lombardo has held a lead in recent polls, leading to Election Day. Sisolak, meantime, just lost a major endorsement from a top Teachers Union.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we stay in Nevada, and we turn it over to the Senate races, there is a close one out here, and that is Republican Laxalt taking on the incumbent \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Each one of these races is going to be a major battle, because they are all very, very close. And you can see how the breakdown happens. These are our four tossup states. Nevada being one of them. Arizona, Georgia, Pennsylvania. The others are, as we put them red or blue based on Republican or Democrat.\par \par \uc2 If let's say Laxalt picks up Nevada, and the recent polls are right, there you get to 50.\par \par \uc2 However, if Democrats have a great night across the board, they pick up Arizona, and Georgia, and Pennsylvania, you're right where we are right now. 50-50, the split.\par \par \uc2 Then, you have the possibility of a long shot in New Hampshire with Don Bolduc. Then its control of the Senate is changed.\par \par \uc2 You have the possibility of -- in Washington State, Smiley, a candidate out there changes the control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's just say, for the sake of argument that there is this giant red wave and they do pick up Arizona, they do pick up Georgia, Pennsylvania, New Hampshire, and where else, Colorado? Look at that. That's the most, 56-44. The giant red wave takes action. And this is where the tossups are.\par \par \uc2 One of the most contested races in the country is in Pennsylvania. Republican Mehmet Oz has used the economy as a hammer against Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, President Biden may have inadvertently hurt Fetterman on another crucial issue. Energy in Pennsylvania, specifically, jobs in that local industry. Important.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson is in Pittsburgh tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Are you all in?\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: We're going to be that 51st vote in D.C.?\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): But over the weekend, bigger crowds, and bigger names.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Vote for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, Philadelphia!\par \par \uc2 EDSON: But one day after sharing the stage with the Democratic Senate nominee, President Biden reinforced his push to move away from fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No more drilling. There is no more drilling. I haven't form any new drilling.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And before that, on Friday.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to be shutting these plants down all across America.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: On the Republican side, former President Trump traveled to Western Pennsylvania and attacked Democrats on energy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Biden and the far left lunatics are waging war on Pennsylvania energy, crushing Pennsylvania jobs, gutting Pennsylvania communities.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Fetterman's campaign did not respond to a request for comment on the president's statement on coal. Fetterman has said he supports fracking and a transition to green energy that preserves the union way of life, citing natural gas workers.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, an increased focus. Whipping the GOP on the economy.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Republicans now are looking to cut Medicare and social security.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Boo!\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Now, with my vote --\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Some Democrats have criticized their candidates for avoiding economic issues for much of the midterm season.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports, Democrats have spent nearly $320 million on ads focused on abortion rights, compared to only $31 million on inflation ads.\par \par \uc2 That's according to a media tracking firm, Ad Impact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Oz has largely built his campaign message on crime in the economy.\par \par \uc2 As for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , a final campaign event tonight before Election Day, he'll be rallying at a union hall, a Carpenter's union hall here in Pittsburgh. That's scheduled to begin in just a few hours. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson, live in Pennsylvania. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume, and \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , senior political correspondent for Axios.\par \par \uc2 Brit, you have a number of newcomers, political newcomers. And there was a lot of criticism for some Republicans saying maybe they weren't the best choices. But all of these races on the Senate side are really close.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): They are. And, you know, when you given the conditions in the country, you would think Republican wave.\par \par \uc2 But there's a factor here that maybe hasn't been fully discussed. And that is we -- this country is so divided in such a partisan way that the time used to be when people would always tell you they voted for the candidate or the person or the man, and not the party.\par \par \uc2 Today, I think people are voting for the party in larger numbers than they ever used to that. Party becomes first, which means that a candidate like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who has shown some real weaknesses as a -- as a -- in a way that you would need to not be weak as a senator, that is to say, speak and argue and understand your colleagues and all that.\par \par \uc2 Probably stands a little better with people are saying, I'm sorry, he's a Democrat. I'm voting for him because I'm a Democrat. And or I want the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We learned in 2020 how important control of the two chambers is. Where there a lot of things that would never have happened if Biden hadn't narrowly gotten his party in control of both Houses? So --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You said today that was one of the biggest things that happened for President Biden, on the 