 previous point, Brit, that was an adjustment made by President Clinton. We're going to digest a lot after tomorrow. But that's one of the things, the direction this administration, this president take.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Now, if you, you know, as the Republicans get in, even if they only get one House. Obviously, the president is going to have to make some adjustments. And this kind of ambitious agenda that he's had is not going to fly, because he can't -- he's not going to be able to pass it through the House.\par \par \uc2 So, he's going to have to make some adjustments. It will be interesting to see if he's agile enough to do it. And if he's -- and what message he takes away from these election?\par \par \uc2 So far, it's not clear to me that he -- that he sees this coming, and that he's prepared to make a big shift. It will be interesting to see if he can do it.\par \par \uc2 Clinton did it pretty well. We're going to see to see if this man can do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Josh, do you think we'd be able to see a wave early tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: There are a few early signs we'll get in election night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Virginia.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Virginia is the big one. The three House races, there are two that are in pretty solidly Biden turf in Northern Virginia. Those are going to be two races to watch. New Hampshire is also, these are -- these are races in New Hampshire where the more MAGA Trump oriented candidates won Republican nominations to the point where Republicans stopped spending money for some of the candidates up there, including Don Bolduc in the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 If you see Don Bolduc ahead early on in New Hampshire, that's an early sign of a possible wave.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Big day tomorrow. Gentlemen, thank you,\par \par \uc2 HUME: You bet.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks were up on the final day of campaigning, The Dow surged 424 today. The S&P 500 finished ahead