dded up to make Nevada an absolute disaster zone.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Now, so far, just under 640,000 early votes have been cast. That's up to 100,000 from 2018. Democrats have a 1.4 percent advantage there over Republicans, but Nevada has over half a million active nonpartisan voters and they will likely decide the outcome of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Now, as far as turnout tomorrow, weather could play an impact, it is very blustery right now, rain is in the forecast tomorrow for Las Vegas and snow in Reno, where weather will not be playing a factor is down south in Arizona. That is where my colleague Alicia Acuna is now following those races, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In their final pitch, candidates are talking to Arizonans about their top issues, that would be inflation and the border crisis. Incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly is in the fight of his political wife often distancing himself from the Biden administration and its policies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): I've had legislation to help border businesses. I've had legislation to prevent the administration from ending Title 42. And I tell the president, you know, when he's wrong, and he's about to make this crisis worse.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (voice over): His opponent Republican Blake Masters wants to "militarize the border" and accuses Democrats of weak approaches to the problem.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: If you just throw the border wide open, you think that's going to cause a crisis? Yes, they did it on purpose. Not only did they tell illegal aliens, hey, come on in. We're not going to deport you. They incentivized people to break the law.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: In the governor's race GOP hopeful Kari Lake is pushing back on accusations she and her supporters are too extreme. This as Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , who is also Arizona's current Secretary of State has made election integrity central to her messaging.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: We've been right here solid conservatives for a long time. We haven't moved. Guess who moved? The Democrats. They moved further and further and further, they've fallen off the stage, OK?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: I know we say every election this is the most important election in our lifetime. This is possibly the last election in our lifetime if we don't elect the right people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): And an indication of how heated this state is, the Maricopa County Elections Division just wrapped a news conference on "predicting false election narratives", Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia Acuna live in Phoenix. Alicia, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go down to Georgia for the final day of campaigning in that crucial Senate race.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. North Korea's military says its recent barrage of missile tests were practices to mercilessly strike key South Korean and U.S. targets such as airbases and operation command systems with a variety of missiles that likely included nuclear capable weapons.\par \par \uc2 North Korea fired dozens of missiles and flew war planes toward the sea last week triggering evacuation alerts in some South Korean and Japanese areas. North Korea also denied selling arms to Russia.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight, French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  meets with British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  on the sidelines of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s climate conference, the leaders were expected to discuss the issue of small boat crossings across the English Channel amid a surge in the number of asylum seekers attempting to reach Britain by boat.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is talking about the future and her potential role in the next Congress. The interview is the Speaker's first since her husband was attacked at the couple's San Francisco home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN ANCHOR: Have you made the decision in your mind, whatever that decision might be?\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I have to say my decision will be affected by what happened in the last week or two.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Will your decision be impacted by the attack in any way?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pelosi would lose her speakership, obviously, if Republicans take control of the House.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are urging their voters not to listen to media predictions of a red wave and to show up at the polls tomorrow. In Georgia, both Senate candidates are using surrogates to help rally supporters on the last day of campaigning, get out the vote. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Macon tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): Are you ready to win this election?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's the final sprint for the reverend and the football star.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R-GA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: I'm going to tell all of you to vote for me, and we all are going to get to the promise land.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: In a race that could once again decide the fate of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Republicans churning out big names and taking their final shots.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR: Legal immigrants are more patriotic than the leftists these days. So the only person that we need to make sure we deport is Warnock.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is demonstrating to you