are excited to use our new innovative survey approach again this election. Our FOX News voter analysis combines a massive national survey, more than 100,000 voters with real time vote data so we can understand who voted, and more importantly, why. By interviewing voters on their cellphones, land lines, online starting way back on October 31st through the end of voting on Election Day, this FNVA, as we call it, provides a full profile of American voters, including the tens of millions who are now voting early or by mail, and they wouldn't be represented in those traditional in person exit polls.\par \par \uc2 We will ask who they intend to support Tuesday or how they already voted. Of course, that is not all. Why are they supporting a particular candidate? Well, for instance, is inflation the most important factor in their voting decision? If so, that could help Republican candidates. Or, is the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade and the impact on abortion the biggest factor? That probably helped Democratic candidates. What message does the electorate want to send President Biden, is their vote expressing support for him or opposition, or maybe it wasn't a factor at all. Maybe they're more about the issues.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to have specific questions in key races. For example, asking Pennsylvania voters how concerned they are about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's health versus Mehmet Oz's so-called carpetbagger status. Our FOX News voter analysis survey will tell us all of that and much more. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, we both know Republican and Democratic campaigns are already lawyering up -- I can say that five times. But what about the potential for legal challenges?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Last I heard from one source tracking these, they said there are more than 100 cases that have already been filed. There are a couple in key states too. In Pennsylvania they are fighting about those ballots that come. They're dated incorrectly or have a problem with them. The Supreme Court there in Pennsylvania told them set those aside and don't count them. They are still fighting, though, over whether voters should now be able to make up the mistakes that are found.\par \par \uc2 And in Georgia, Cobb County, another critical Senate race in a critical place there, Cobb County. A judge has ruled that they are going to extend the extension for an absentee ballot to come in by November 14th because over 1,000 ballots did not get mailed out there to people who requested absentees. So the legal strategies back and forth, it has already started, and I would expect after we get the votes done tomorrow there will be a whole new slew of legal action to follow, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I am ready for it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the final day of the midterm election campaign.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in Atlanta as Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  directs all available state resources to help local responders deal with a chemical plant fire on Colonels Island. Authorities ordered the evacuation of about 100 homes there. The plant is located 70 miles south of Savannah. One firefighter was taken to the hospital, but there are no other reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Minute Maid Park in Houston from FOX 26. One of the big stories there tonight, the victory parade for the World Series champion \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2 . The team clinched its second title in six years by closing out the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  Saturday. Some schools canceled classes so students could attend the celebration. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's also now cheaper to generate electricity from wind and solar than it is from coal and oil. Literally cheaper, not a joke. We are going to be shutting these plants down all across America, and having wind and solar.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Some of you were there. It was -- it was loud and hard to hear, I think, or maybe not, exactly what -- what was being said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The White House defending those coal comments, saying the president's words were twisted.\par \par \uc2 So, let's bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and my colleague Martha MacCallum, anchor and executive editor of "THE STORY," co- anchor tomorrow tonight. Martha, let me start with you. That is interesting to see the White House backpedaling a couple days in a row.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: This is a comment you would really not expect from the president at this stage. There has been a lot of pushback on energy prices. To double down on the idea that we're not going to drill anymore and to talk about wind and solar at this moment in the campaign I think is a reflection of the president missing the mark on where people are right now. We have seen it time and time again.\par \par \uc2 And I think when you look at what is about to happen tomorrow night, and we'll see what it is. We're going to watch it unfold in real time. The question is, to what extent are voters dissatisfied? How does that register? Does it register in what I'm calling a pink ripple, or is it the red tsunami at the other side? And I think that's going to give us a sense of how people feel about energy independence in this country, about education.\par \par \uc2 When I look at the education numbers, you see almost an even split between support for Republicans and Democrats, and it's never been that way in the past. Democrats have always owned that issue. They don't own it anymore. And the Hispanic vote is the same thing. They don't own the Hispanic vote anymore.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to get a sense, I think, of where the country is headed and where their allegiances are right now. It's going to be quite interesting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, when you look at the environment, let's set aside the polls. Let's just say the environment, the issues, it does set up well for Republicans. And even some prominent Democrats are questioning the closing messages. Here is Van Jones.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: I think there was this kind of fool's gold, this idea that the threat to democracy is so severe in the wake of this insurrection and the wake of these election deniers possibly grabbing control of the government, that that was something that you had to talk about. But you also had to talk about the economy. I think the tragedy here is that the Democrats have something to say on the economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: I think he's largely right. Thanks for having me on, first off. I think Democrats after tomorrow, regardless of what happens, whether they hold the Senate, lose a few seats, win a few more seats in the House -- I think conventional wisdom is they lose both -- I think Democrats need a rendezvous with reality around I think the most important issue, which is energy security. We probably need one with crime, also, because if things also don't work out here in New York it is all because of crime if we lose the governor's race here in New York.\par \par \uc2 This happens in elections. This is what democracy is all about. This is when voters give politicians an upbring, a little bit of an upcoming, saying you're not talking about the things that we care deeply about. I hear Democrats talking about numbers and stats and facts. If people don't feel safe, guess what, they're not safe. If people feel like they are auto not doing well economically, they're not doing well economically. You can point to every stat you want.\par \par \uc2 So I won't be surprised if what happens, if what Martha's projecting may happen tomorrow happens. Also, I'm a little confused. You look at these issues which favor Republicans so heavily, and you would expect five, six, seven point differences between Democrats, Republicans in Senate races and even gubernatorial races. But these races are -- these margins are very, very fight, and no one seems to be at 50. So frankly, tomorrow, I'm going to be surprised whatever the outcome is, because the polling seems to be misaligned from the reality of how people say they are feeling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And we're going to have some indications early in the night. We'll also have a feel of turnout, and that will tell us some story early. Ben, it's interesting to see the president is not the pitch man in chief. He is in Maryland tonight. He was in Westchester here in New York, blue of blue. He has not been at the front lines.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I was there in South Carolina, as I'm sure many of us were, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  clinched the nomination effectively for the Democratic Party. And he did so by ignoring so many of these issues that were driven by leftist voices on social media, things that were not the concerns that people actually had about their pocketbook or their future.\par \par \uc2 And right now, I think that we see a White House that has, unfortunately, not continued that. They have really gotten out of touch. It's as if the kitchen table issues are completely out of sight. If you care about the cost of energy, if you care about crime, if you care about the border, if you care about all these things that we see voters saying they care about, then why are you closing with a message that effectively says to people I'm going to make your energy costs more, because that's what they hear when they listen to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  say he is shutting down the coal plants, no more drilling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the message of the threat to democracy, I assume it's a base generating, scare everybody to get out to the polls, I suppose.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One of the problems is when we look at the polls, and again, we're going to find out if these numbers are accurate, eight out of ten people think that there won't be questions about the outcome of the election. So even that, even though we hear a lot of very high emotional response to the potential threat to democracy, we're not sensing that it exists in a real significant way at the voter level. And we'll see what these challenges look like, and what crops up, and if there is a reason to fill undermined in this. But so far the polls don't indicate that this is a top of mind issue for people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If the New York governor's race goes to the Republicans, is that the earth-shattering race for tomorrow no matter what happens?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, it would have to be right there, top one or two. You look at there's a race out in Oregon, a governor's race out there which I think is close as well. But for Democrats to lose in New York, and if not, but if they do, to speculate to your question, this would be the reset that I think every Democrat across the country would have to say to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we are not looking at issues the right way. We're not helping people face their challenges and overcome their fears. We have to be smarter.\par \par \uc2 New York, again, if she loses, you had everybody come here, and not able to push her over. I still think she could win this thing, but the fact that we are even talking about it gives you a sense of where Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right, panel, stand by. Up next, we will get some specific predictions of individual races from the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR: America is the best country in the world, and we are not going to stop fighting for her until we get her back and we make her strong again.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: This is the year we're going to take back the House. We're going to take back the Senate, and we are going to take back America.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: If you help Democrats keep the House and get a few more seats in the Senate, you can guarantee he'll make more progress on the issues you care about.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Anybody who votes for a Republican for Congress or the Senate, is voting to cut Social Security, Medicare, and raise the price of insulin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The final pitch from all sides. We're back with the panel. I asked everybody to pick a race they are looking at, interesting. Martha, start with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we talked about New York and the governor's race. It's fascinating that we are in a situation the day before the election where you have toss-ups, about four or five House toss-up races and the Governor's race in New York all in contention. So \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , head of the \uc2 DCC\uc2 , that was my governor pick, but this one is New York 17 running against Mike Lawler. It's been an awkward situation for \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2 . He carved out this district and moved into it and pushed out \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , who is no longer in the House anymore, and it was supposed to be favorable area for him. It's basically a Biden plus 10 district. And he is on the ropes, which is a very difficult situation for him to be in. So, now he is sort of scrambling to get right