; News Polling Shows Close Race For Michigan Governor Between Republican Tudor Dixon And Incumbent Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 ; Oregon Republican Gubernatorial Candidate Christine Drazan Interviewed On Surprisingly Close Race Against Democratic Candidate \uc2 Tina Kotek\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Charlie Hurt, Ben Domenech, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 8,840 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Tomorrow's election is now in your hands and its all about the things that if you're happy with inflation, record high gas prices, open borders, if you're happy with no bail laws, defund the police and woke education, vote for the Democrats, otherwise save the country. Let not your heart be troubled. Laura--\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham, and this is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from New York tonight. In just eight hours, the first polls of the 2022 midterms open, and candidates are not leaving anything on the field.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Who's ready to take this state back? Who's ready to save this country?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , CANDIDATE FOR U.S. SENATOR: This is the greatest country in the world today, and it's time - it is time for us to stand up for America.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ, CANDIDATE FOR U.S. SENATOR: If we're all in, we win. All in? All in all in? All in?\par \par \uc2 J. D. VANCE, CANDIDATE FOR U.S. SENATOR: Are we ready tomorrow to take back this country?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you ready to take the state back?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS, CANDIDATE FOR U.S. SENATOR: Who's ready to send Mark Kelly back to space tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now we're going to hear from reporters and candidates across the nation tonight, including our own Mark Meredith, in Ohio, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still delivering what was billed as a highly anticipated speech.\par \par \uc2 But first, "The Great Realignment", that's the focus of "Tonight's Angle." With Republicans about to take control of both the House and the Senate, most Democrats aren't interested in serious soul searching about how it all unraveled. For a bunch so worried about election denial, they seem to be preemptively questioning the legitimacy of the impending Republican takeover.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How concerned are you that the upheaval at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will result in a flood of misinformation disinformation right before the election.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): And it actually goes beyond \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Today, there are reports that, in fact, Russian bots, news reports have come back outside interference coming back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Of course, the regime media, as always, picks up the Democrat baton and runs with it. And the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  it's Russian bots, and trolls and interference. Oh my!\par \par \uc2 Well think about this, the same group that has been droning on for years about how a vote for them is a vote to protect democracy. Well, we now see what they really meant by that. Democracy only works when they win.\par \par \uc2 Now, we should always assume that our adversaries are mucking around in our system. In fact, a Russian businessman, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , today admitted to using social media to influence our elections. And it only stands to reason that China is meddling as well. But it's too sophisticated to get caught, let's face it.\par \par \uc2 But what Biden and his band of reality deniers ignore is this. Neither Russia nor China nor any other foreign countries created the pain that American families are feeling right now from inflation. And they didn't cause the increase in violent crime in New York in subways. And I'll tell you it, Putin didn't design the sexually deviant curricula polluting schools across America.\par \par \uc2 When more suburban women, Hispanics, African Americans vote Republican, it's not because of some Putin inspired disinfo lurking on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and circulating on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That paradigm shift will occur because Democrats misread their mandate, and threw in with the Far Left to destroy our prosperity and our public safety. There are a few Democrats, though, who seem to be kind of sort of getting it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILARY ROSEN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I'm a loyal Democrat, but I am not happy. I just think that we are - you know, we did not listen to voters in this election. And I think we're going to have a bad night. And you know, this conversation is not going to have much impact on Tuesday, but I hope it has an impact going forward. Because when voters tell you over and over and over again, that they care mostly about the economy, listen to them, stop talking about democracy being at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's not the only deafness, the only quicksand that they're stepping into. As Americans are facing record high prices for heating oil this winter, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went and he told the truth over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No more drilling. There is no more drilling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, oh, man. Well, a few days earlier, he knocked off the entire coal industry as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No one's building new coal plants, because they can't rely on it. We're going to be shutting these plants down all across America, and having wind and solar.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And since we already know how they feel about fracking for natural gas, they're against it. What does this exactly leave us as a nation? Now even with the hot air perpetually blowing in DC, it's not nearly enough to power America. So either Biden is a complete ignoramus on our existing energy framework, that could very well be, or he's setting the stage for another self-imposed shutdown of the entire country.\par \par \uc2 Oil, natural gas and coal are responsible for nearly 80 percent of American energy consumption. So without that, life as we know it in the United States, will come to a screeching halt.\par \par \uc2 Folks, this is their closing message. And this so-called Master communicator, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , he didn't do much better. Before he headed out on the trail for the Democrats, he must have had, I don't know, a \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , create some kind of playlist based on John Lennon song "Imagine."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, 44TH U.S. PRESIDENT: Imagine if we had been able to fix our broken immigration system back in 2011. Imagine if we been able to pass meaningful gun safety legislation. Imagine, if we've been able to reduce our emissions if we had kept the Senate in 2014, we'd have a very different Supreme Court making decisions about our most basic rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Imagine if Martha's Vineyard wanted to keep all those migrants, all 50 of them. OK, imagine that.\par \par \uc2 All right. Imagine if we had a Democrat party that wasn't so out of step with our middle class, out of step with Hispanics and out of step with independent voters. Let's imagine that. Now it's really been something to behold. The party that tanked our GDP in less than two years, the party that got us into a pointless proxy war with Russia. The party that played by - got played by the Saudis on oil production, is now claiming to be the champions for the middle class?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Middle class gets stiffed, the poor get poor under their policy. This all happened last time under my predecessor. The economy was in ruins. He lost manufacturing jobs and hundreds of small business here in the state and 100,000 nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The elocution, notwithstanding, Liar, Liar Pants on Fire. Now under Trump, we had the strongest gains in real median income in over two decades. And before the pandemic destroyed everyone's economies America had the highest GDP growth and the G7. Now we're six. And he fought his own party's establishment to get tough on China's trade abuses. But nice try Joe.\par \par \uc2 Now let's face it. It's tough to be a Democrat, this midterm. The lies, eventually they catch up to you pretty quickly. And their party's elders who are about to lose their chairmanships are getting very cranky, so cranky that they're skipping the race card and going right to the Nazi card.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 I'm stuck with history, all of my life.\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): I taught history. And I'm telling you, what I see here are parallels to what the history was this world back in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So congressman than--\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: In Germany, in Italy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: it's hard to call someone a Nazi when you're about to go to a Luau. It's very hard. Now, this is when you know that the Democrats are just getting get shellacked. When conservatives actually focus on the issues that voters care about, it's now fascist? That's their closing message?\par \par \uc2 And I have this simple question. Did Democrats actually pay some consultant, somewhere for this campaign advice? Now we're going to see where things end up in this election. But one thing is for sure, the future of the Republican Party is the same as what I said it was on election night 2020 when I was at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The same coalition we saw in Florida back in 2020, a rising populist coalition that takes the solid Republican base and adds more working people, more Hispanic voters, more suburban women, and more people on the coasts who are tired of crime and economic chaos, that is getting a lot stronger.\par \par \uc2 Now for 12 years, ever since the rise, the Tea Party, this coalition has been gaining strength. First we stopped Obama, then we defeated Hillary, then we helped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reach the White House. We held together through COVID, through lies, through attacks on the big business community from the regime media, and now we're competitive all across the country.\par \par \uc2 I hope, and I believe that tomorrow will be a great night for the Republicans and the Republic. But we're not finished yet. Americans are ready for peace and prosperity. They're ready for a Congress and a president that works hard and tells them the truth. And that's "The Angle."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Charlie Hurt, Washington Times Opinion Editor, and Ben Domenech, Editor-at-Large for The Spectator, both are Fox News Contributors, both here in New York with me.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, from censorship now to political violence, the media, the administration, just they're not turning down the temperature in this race one bit. But they're looking for both?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, yes. No, and they really aren't. And we've gotten this sort of strange thing, where we have the pre-election election denying, which I think is a first. But the other thing that we have - and obviously you know, politicians always sort of shade the truth and don't always speak honestly.\par \par \uc2 But what is so alarming, what's so astounding about this is to listen to the Biden Administration, and to listen to these Democrats lie to people's faces and tell them what they know is going on in their own personal lives is not going on. It not only does it belittle those people, but it also completely destroys their credibility.\par \par \uc2 And I agree with you. I think this is a great realignment. But I think it's even more than this. I think that what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing to the Democrat Party right now, and what Democrats are doing to that party right now is a generational damage that could take 10 years, could take decades for them to recover, because nobody believes what they're saying anymore.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's excellent point. Now, Ben, the big news today out of Pennsylvania was that the Fetterman campaign is now suing to have undated and misstated ballots counted, and apparently, some aren't signed either. Now, they argue that the handwritten date requirement is just a technicality, and not used to determine whether the votes were legally cast. And that throwing them all out violates federal civil rights laws. Ben, this is another desperate move on the part of the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEW CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, but it's also totally predictable. I mean, they're completely hypocrites on all of this stuff. But one of the things that I think, and this is such a great point that Charlie just made. The ramifications of what we're seeing going on, the realignment that you talked about, we are talking about a sweeping away of a generation of Democratic leadership that has dominated in Washington, it has controlled and directed their party.\par \par \uc2 And what we're going to see is not just them swept away, but a whole group of Gen Xers as well, who were supposed to take the reins, swept away. People like \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 . I went back and calculated over $200 million has been spent over the last 10 years on \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  on trying to make Beto happen. He's not happening. OK. He's going to get clobbered.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is going to get completely swept away by \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . And I think that one of the things that we should understand here is, we are going to move to a Democratic Party very quickly, that is all being led by these millennial Girlboss energy types. And that's going to be crazy town in terms of what they're going to offer to the country and what they're going to say.\par \par \uc2 So as much as I think, you know, you had this whole false approach by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to return to normalcy. The actual result of his policies, and this terrible White House is going to be an even crazier, more Leftist Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and to your point about how the old dinosaurs are kind of going to be pushed off the stage or lumber off the stage. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was on CNN tonight being interviewed by Anderson Cooper. It seemed like she was - to me her body language - aside from the fact she said she never spoke about the attack with her husband, which - that was an interesting comment. But she seemed like she was on her way out, to me. I mean, not that she just kind of defeated, but she was going to leave. That's what - that was my sense.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Well, certainly the body language would suggest that, but the fact that they don't speak openly about it - and I get it, it's ahead of the election, so maybe it's sort of difficult. But it goes to the heart of - you know, destroying that whatever credibility they have left. And honestly, I really don't - what is - how are they going to respond to this election? Are they going to learn a lesson, are they going to do--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, no.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: That's the big thing. None lessons will be learned. No lessons will be learned.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: None.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: They have convinced themselves we are having this moral stand up thing for in defense of democracy and it's just a bunch of fascists and racists who are going to come beat them. The problem, of course, is that this is going to be one of the most diverse coalitions Republicans have ever formed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The old talking points don't work anymore.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: It doesn't work anymore. No one believes that. People understand that - I mean, when you see the governor of New York come out and say that, you know, it's just hysterical to complain about crime. You know, Black, Hispanic, and Asian Americans in these New York communities who have seen the increases in crime, they know that that's a lie. They see right through it, and they aren't going to be called racist in order for - because of their actually pointing it out.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, we talked about how politicians tend to lose their mojo over time, when they stay in the game too long. I never thought I'd say this about Obama. But we played a little bit of it on "The Angle," but he dropped a particularly unhinged line over the weekend watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: When true democracy goes away, people get hurt. It has real consequences. This is not an abstraction. Dissidents start getting locked up. Reporters start getting locked up if they're not toeing the party line. Corruption reigns because there's no accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, he was successful to the extent he was, because he was able to keep the Far Left at bay. Remember he was for traditional marriage and all this old days of Obama. He kept them kind of a little bit at arm's length, but now it's like, 'Katy, bar the door'.\par \par \uc2 HURT: He's such a liar. And of course, of course, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  never would have run on the campaign that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has run. And he never would have won reelection. Except that, you know, his 2008 campaign was a brilliant campaign. It was hopeful. But from that moment on, it has been darkness and dishonesty from him.\par \par \uc2 And listen, that clip you played earlier where he's talking about, you know, if we had the majority in 2014. They had a filibuster proof majority for two years at the beginning of his term. If he wanted to fix immigration then, he could have done it. They could have done anything they wanted to, and they failed to do.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: And they didn't want to do it. They didn't want to do it. They wanted to keep the issue.\par \par \uc2 HURT: And they wanted to take over a sixth of the economy, because there's more money in it for them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But his whole imagine if this had, it's like an old lounge act. It's like imagine if I was still--\par \par \uc2 HURT: He is an ageing--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was like Spinal Tap. Imagine the stadium was still full and I wasn't playing like circling around, theater in around. Charlie and Ben, great to see you both on set here.\par \par \uc2 All right, a new "Wall Street Journal" poll spells trouble for the Democrats, not just for tomorrow, but as we were just talking about, perhaps for a generation. GOP gaining support among black and Latino voters. Now Biden's 2020 pollster who conducted the poll warned that we are seeing a paradigm shift. So where could this make the most difference?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Illinois Congressman \uc2 Darin LaHood\uc2 , Finance Chair for the NRCC; and Tom Bevan, Real Clear Politics President and Co-Founder.\par \par \uc2 But Tom, which state in which race do you think, if you can narrow it down to one, would mean the most, make the most difference?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, PRESIDENT, REALCLEARPOLITICS: In terms of a coalition shift?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: For this year, it's probably Arizona, and Nevada, I would put in that category as well. To the extent that Hispanics if they vote, if they shift to the GOP in those kinds of numbers, that'll make the difference for two Senate seats, the pickups for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, I'm looking at Michigan, because we see in Michigan, a young, conservative Tudor Dixon, comes from a steel family, understands the working class sentiment and ideals. People didn't give her much of a chance. We featured her first I believe on "The Ingraham Angle." She's making a real run for this against \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  who is the lockdown queen and I think parents are still angry about that.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARIN LAHOOD (R-IL): Well, you're absolutely right, Laura, the race I look at is Northwest Indiana, Derry (ph), Indiana, where we have a great candidate and Jennifer-Ruth Green. Navy pilot in her mid-30s, African American, and she has a great chance of winning that district.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that's one to watch tomorrow. It's going to be a great night if we can get her across the finish line. But she's the future of the Republican Party. And it goes to show with Hispanic voters and African American voters, they're coming to us because Biden's policies have failed on the border. We have out of control crime, and we have a disastrous economy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Tom, the noted election denier, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , has a theory now about black men. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D) GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We know that black voters have often discounted, and unfortunately this year, black men have been a very targeted population for misinformation. Not misinformation about what they want, but about why they want what they deserve. And my campaign has been the only one that has very intentionally, thoughtfully and consistently reached out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tom, I have no idea what she's talking about. But it sounds like she's saying that, you know, you can't hold the bar too high or in a too sophisticated way for that demographic. I don't know what her point at all, but it doesn't - it seems to fall flat.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: No, she didn't make a lot of sense there. Look, you know, African American turnout in Georgia is - has been particularly in the early vote has been through the roof. And so it's just not going the way that she wants and I think she sees the writing on the wall.\par \par \uc2 But to the point that Darin made earlier, I mean, one - there are two issues at play here. One is that it's not just race and ethnicity, it's class. The inflation is hitting working people the hardest of all races, and in hitting working people the hardest. And so that's why you're seeing the shift. That's one of the things is driving it.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Republicans have fielded probably the most diverse set of candidates this cycle than they ever have - more Hispanic candidates, more African American candidates. And so I think that, you know, Democrats, I think have taken those constituencies for granted for too long, and Republicans are taking that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And the National Party deserves a lot of credit. They get beaten up by a lot of us, a lot of the time. They did deserve a lot of credit for the recruitment. They did.\par \par \uc2 Now, Congressman Henry Olsen had a fantastic, in my view, analysis in today's "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." He said, "Republicans will gain support in almost every voter demographic, but they'll make especially large inroads among Hispanics, and middle income suburbanites.\par \par \uc2 The white working class' abandonment of the Democrats in the 2010 midterms foreshadowed its movement into the GOP as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  broke Republican orthodoxy. A similar unorthodox move and 2024 could turn the tables on the Democrats creating the foundation for a long term Republican resurgence." Congressman, you've had your hands on the data, is this the trend line?\par \par \uc2 LAHOOD: Well, I think what we're going to see tomorrow night is really a sea change when it comes to working class Hispanic voters, working class African American voters, because they're turned off from the Biden policies.\par \par \uc2 And they're - think about this, Laura, just in the last year, Hispanic working class voters have lost one month of salary because of inflationary, you know, policies put in place by Biden. And so they can relate to that. Right? They can relate to defunding police by Democrats. They can relate to having $5, $6 A gallon gas, right?\par \par \uc2 So I mean, these are working class issues that whether you're Hispanic, whether you're African American or whether you're working class, like I represented Peoria, Illinois, or Rockford, Illinois, these policies don't work. And that's why people are gravitating to freedom and opportunity and what we bring.\par \par \uc2 The point that you made, Laura, is we have to make sure we understand that and we govern that way qhen we take back this majority. We don't waste that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, I think the populous conservative playbook is what's going to continue to grow this movement. I've been saying it for a long time, and I think we're going to see this tomorrow night. Congressman LaHood, Tom, great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Now a lot of eyes are on Ohio tonight, where former President Trump just wrapped a rally for Republican Senate candidate J.D. Vance. That was just minutes ago. But it was the big expectation of a big 2024 announcement that many were tuning in for. Fox News, Mark Meredith is in Dayton, for all the details. Mark, what happened? Apparently no announcement?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Oh, Laura, there's a slight announcement, and maybe just to stay tuned at this point. Because as you were talking about, the former President held a rally for about an hour and 45 minutes. He had a few thousand people braving the cold along with the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 But it was at the end of the rally after he got done speaking about the state of the country, as well as tomorrow's midterms, that the former president said that he will be making the major announcement, which of course, is 99.9 percent going to be about his future 2024 plans, a week from tomorrow. And that that announcement would be happening at the Mar-a- Lago Club - this club just outside of Palm Beach, Florida. And so the former president made it very clear that that's when he's expected to make the news that everybody was talking about today.\par \par \uc2 Laura, you're right. There was a lot of speculation that he was going to make some sort of announcement about his run on the eve of the election. That would have been a shocker because of course, it would have a big impact on what people were thinking when they go to the polls tomorrow. He held back kept it focused on the Republican candidates as well as the issues he thinks voters should be carrying about tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But I also thought it's fascinating because, as I mentioned, it's going to be a week from Tuesday. It's the same day that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 's book is due out and you'll have President Biden still overseas. I believe he'd be in Bali at that point at the G20. So obviously, you're going to have all sorts of important international headlines colliding at the same time. But all attention will be on of course, with the former President will announce then.\par \par \uc2 He had members of his family here with him tonight, Laura. There was a lot of speculation that maybe this will be there. But no Melania Trump, the former First Lady was not with him. So then, of course, gave us another hint that maybe it was going to be delayed a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Former President seemed like he was thrilled to be back out on the stump tonight. He talked a lot about what he would like to see happen between now and the 2024 election. He didn't hold back on his criticism, as you can imagine. He did bring up the polls, which shows him leading the Republican presidential possibility pack. However, he also refrained from attacking some of his other potential Republican candidates. Keeping it fairly polite, if you will, as polite as it could possibly be in presidential politics. Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now Mark, have you ever done a report while YMCA is playing in the background? And do you have do you have a request? Maybe you should just turn around and ask them play your favorite song at this point. My goodness. Are they still dancing or --?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: I think they're over heater? Yes, anything - anything to warm up. It's like 45 degrees. And I will tell you what people they were really eager to hear from him. But they braved a long day in the cold to come see this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Mark. It's amazing. We're already here, 2024 right around the corner. Great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tomorrow voters in New York have an opportunity to reject the lawlessness they're seeing on their streets. And today, a shocking story gives them another reason to vote for \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . Now, according to reporting from "The New York Post," a gang that preys on gay men, is suspected of killing at least two victims and of drugging and robbing more than a dozen others.\par \par \uc2 But the Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's office is impeding the investigation. Joining us now is Miranda Devine who broke this story in "The. New York Post." Miranda, have you heard anything from Bragg and the Manhattan DA's office at all about this case?\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA DEVINE, NEW YORK POST COLUMNIST: Look, we finally got some sort of a response this afternoon. But it was only to say that you know, they sent their sympathy and that the investigation was ongoing. I do know that there was a meeting this morning in the district attorney's office about these cases, about our story, obviously, that's what's prompted it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it I know that that the police have been trying desperately to get some action from the DA's office and they've just been met with complete stonewalling. When, I asked last night what was going on, I was told - the official line from the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  was that there were delays with the medical examiner.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the, the first of these young men that we know about who was preyed upon in Hell's Kitchen at a gay club, was drugged and robbed and left to die. That was back in April. And then there was the second young man a visitor from Washington, DC, John Umberger, whose mother I spoke to, that was back in May.\par \par \uc2 So they - the toxicology reports have come back. They know that both those young men basically were murdered by this gang that has also has the same ammo in the same gay clubs within half a mile of each other in Hell's Kitchen. And no warning has been given to New Yorkers about this menace that has been going on for at least--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean, do you think, Miranda - sorry to jump in. Do you think this is - I mean, how can there be no warning? I mean, that smacks of well the politicization of crime reporting. I mean, right? That's a big constituency for the Democrats. That's bizarre.\par \par \uc2 DEVINE: Absolutely. Just on Saturday, you had \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  was having a sort of a rally with Andy Cohen to get out the LGBTQ vote. No mention of the fact that this menace is stalking the gay community right in New York. The bars don't seem to want to publicize that, you know, just a warning to gay men to stay with a friend. Do not go alone because the chances are that these people - three men will prey on you and you could end up dead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Miranda, thank you for doing this reporting and shame on \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  for treating this crime as issue, as she as she has. Thank you so much, Miranda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON, 42ND U.S. PRESIDENT: \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , she makes - he makes it sound like \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  gets up every morning, goes to the nearest subway stop and hands out billy clubs and baseball bats to everybody who gets on the subway. Don't he? Well he--\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't even know what to say to that. In the roughly 48 hours since Slick Willie's comedy routine there. At least six people were brutally attacked on the subway. It's not a joke. Including one man stabbed while trying to defend a woman who herself was being harassed.\par \par \uc2 Now with that knowledge in hand Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  blamed her opponent, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  for noticing this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): He has been hyperventilating, trying to scare people for months. And New Yorkers are on to it. All the legitimate media organizations have called him out for what he is doing - fear mongering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New York Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . Congressman, CNBC reported today that Hochul's donors are now sounding the alarm over your surge and begging her - begging her in these waning hours to pivot to crime. But I guess that advice wasn't taken all that seriously.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): Yes, and the polls are getting close in just under 24 hours now. The time for a pivot to being a crime fighter has, obviously, come and gone. Not only has she missed out on an opportunity to actually connect with voters on an issue that is most important to so many of them, she's ridiculed them.\par \par \uc2 When she says that she doesn't understand why it's so important to me that we need to lock up criminals? That's a message that is sent to all New Yorkers. What do you do when you dig a hole, she kept digging. She went on MSNBC, and then she said that this is a conspiracy. And then she says that we're data deniers. So she kept digging.\par \par \uc2 She went on CNN. What she didn't realize was that there was a side by side, where next to her while she's continuing this narrative that you're talking about, they're showing all the different major crime categories that are going up. So what did she do?\par \par \uc2 She went on MSNBC again, but the host actually cut her off and said, listen, I'm not safe. When I go to the pharmacy, everything's locked up and I'm not riding the subway. New Yorkers of all walks of life, forget about just Republicans. We're talking about Independents and Democrats as well have decided that she's not up for the job.\par \par \uc2 She's not going to be offering up bold solutions. She's not going to hold \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  accountable for his refusal to enforce the law. She won't roll back pro-criminal laws like Castle spell (ph). She's not going to unapologetically back our men and women in blue to give them the tools and resources they need to do their job. So as a result, come tomorrow, I believe that she's going to get fired by the voters of New York who want to take their streets back.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: On "NBC Nightly News," you'll be shocked to know that Lester Holt delivered a completely impartial report, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, JOURNALIST: An issue finding traction for many campaigns is voter worries about crime. But as we found, the state of crime in America is not always what it appears to be.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, fear is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Vote like your life depends on it, because it does.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Fueled, some argue, not by reality but by videos of rampant lawlessness and some unsettling headlines.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, the media clearly see how potent an issue crime is, and they are essentially, as I said earlier, picking up the baton from Hochul and company to say, look, I'm not saying there's no crime, but it's not really as bad as people like you make it out to be.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: I mean, Lester Holt is playing this video of me on my front lawn with my wife and my kids where right there, just a few weekends ago, there was a gang drive-by shooting. My two 16-year-olds were at home at the kitchen table doing homework on a quiet Sunday afternoon. There was a bullet found about 30 feet away, two people shot laying on the ground about 10 feet away. This is real life. It was the 1980s when elected officials were trying to convince the public that it was just a perception that there was rising crime, and it wasn't until the early 90s that New Yorkers of all walks of life, Republican, Democrat, independent decided enough.\par \par \uc2 Don't try to tell me that this is just perception. Don't tell me there's nothing to see here, look away. We want people in government to actually do what needs to get done to take back our streets and subways. Well, fast forward to 2022, here we are again. And New Yorkers who are hitting their breaking point in this state, New York leading the entire nation out- migration, one of the key aspects is the affordable of life in New York, attack on freedom, attack son the quality of our education, but also safety. And they've decided, you know what, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's not up for the job. We're seeing strong support, great energy, good momentum. We just have to make sure everyone shows up and votes tomorrow. Vote like your life depends on it, because I believe that it does.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for New York to make a decision. Do you want to save yourselves or not? If you don't want to save yourselves, just keep doing what you're doing. But Congressman, thank you and good luck tomorrow. Can't wait to watch.\par \par \uc2 How desperate are Democrats getting? Raymond Arroyo is here in moments with their rather pathetic 11th hour hijinks. You don't want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we explore the cultural side of the midterm campaign. For that we turn to the author of "The Wise Men Who Found Christmas," FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Ray, now, the Democrats are going into the midterms leaning into one big issue. What is it?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's abortion for some reason.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Still?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Even though the Supreme Court returned the issue to the states, Democrats have been trying to turn this into a national concern. Though when \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  sounds off on abortion, Laura, I think he might be trying to have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I run on Roe v Wade. I celebrate the demise of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'd like to spin the wheel, Pat.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What is he doing?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What is he trying to say, Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why is he doing this?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Look, they're doing this because they think it is an important wedge issue and they can win votes, and to underscore just how important Democrats consider abortion, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  returned to \uc2 Howard University\uc2  for a BET special with black voters tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: One does not need to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree the government should not be telling her what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 When people start coming for -- at someone to take away their rights, we're all vulnerable. It will just be a minute before they come for some of your rights. So let's not let this train leave the station.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, it was all abortion from beginning to end.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is the best thing she has to offer the African American community.\par \par \uc2 However, when you look at the \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 , 81 percent of black Americans consider violent crime the number one issue they're focused on, 76 percent in The Grio poll found that their top concern was inflation. This is not even in the top five. But here she was, prime time, one hour, devoted to abortion. And "SNL" apparently also got the memo. They abandoned laughs this weekend to push this abortion PSA.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Gas prices are up and families are really hurting, but that's not going to magically disappear no matter who you vote for. But what will keep disappearing is safe access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 I hope to hell everyone votes --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- because remember, we all love someone who's had an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my god, it's so unfunny. It's aggressively unfunny. I'm sorry. They did do a funny COVID skit, though. That was funny.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She's talking about things disappearing. What's going to disappear is the audience because they're not telling jokes. This about as entertaining as that macabre NPR audio abortion they played the other day, Laura. I don't know why the Democratic Party has decided this is going to be their deliverance, this is their wedge issue. It doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 But my favorite tactic from some of these politicians is when confronted with real questions, they just revert to the old, what? Oh, huh? What? Do you hear any -- I don't hear anything. Can you hear something? I don't.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We see your handling of the pandemic. We all know the critics over the past few years. Do you have any regrets and --\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , (D) MICHIGAN: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Go ahead governor. Can you hear me OK? You got me governor?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Did I do that when I touched the ear thing? It was starting to fall out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, no, it's OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can't hear you, water running. Remember that old commercial.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's the same thing.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, conveniently Whitmer's audio got locked down, so it was perfect timing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When I touched it, it's falling out. Well, put it back in, sweetheart. Is it that hard to figure that out?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Exactly. She had enough to curse on air, but couldn't respond to the question about her lockdown. This is like the White House press secretary trying to spin Biden's vow to close all coal plants, Laura, a great closing argument in Pennsylvania, by the way. Here's how the press secretary explained things.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: It was loud and hard to hear, I think, or maybe not, exactly what -- what was being said. The president's words, we believe, were twisted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: His words were twisted. That argument is what's twisted. A third of our coal plants, Laura, are already closed. How are we going to power our electrical grid building all these stations?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We talked about it in the Angle, they want to get rid of drilling, coal, OK. They hate cars, they hate fracking. So that leaves us with the gasbags in Washington powering the whole country. That's it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, just so you know, and I'm going to leave you with this, the president is closing this entire midterm season by displaying the kind of leadership tonight that you'd expect -- confident, steady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Two, 10, 12, 15 -- whoops, stepping on a -- there's a -- that's black. Anyway. We're going to codify Roe v Wade in January and make it the rule of the land.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Jill is always there pointing the way, Laura. They never send him to an event wound a minder -- Obama, Jill. But I love the moment where he - - as that the closing argument. That worked well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Again, elocution is coming any day now. Raymond, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 My earpiece is -- in moments we take you to the two states where we might see some big surprises tomorrow, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: One state that THE ANGLE has been watching for many, many months now where we could see a surprise is Michigan where Republican Tudor Dixon has made a late and impressive charge against incumbent Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . FOX News national correspondent Garrett Tenney is live in Grand Rapids, Michigan, with the latest there. Garrett, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This race is going to be a really interesting one to watch. A lot has changed in the last month from Tudor Dixon trailing \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  by as much as 18 points in the polls to now being behind by just four points in the Real Clear Politics average. And Democrats are now sounding the alarm as well about large numbers of black voters in deep blue Detroit who are expected to stay home this election. The city clerk says based on early voting numbers so far, she expects around 39,000 fewer voters in motor city will cast ballots compared to 2018, which would obviously be a huge blow to Whitmer's reelection hopes. And at a rally in Detroit this weekend, she was asked how she's trying to overcome that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , (D) MICHIGAN: Having people like President Obama coming in and Kerry Washington, what we have seen is a lot of energy. We've made it easier for people to participate in this democracy. But I think it's really important that we continue to educate people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: That answer wasn't convincing to a columnist for "The Detroit News." Bankole Thompson, who is black and who wrote this of Democratic candidates, "They seem to believe the Republican candidates running for statewide offices have no chance of getting elected because they were endorsed by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The problem with that kind of foolish thinking is if enough black people don't come out to make a difference, Democrats could be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 The other thing we're going to be keeping a close eye on tomorrow night is what results look like in the suburbs. That's where Democrats won the state in 2020, and that is where Republicans need to perform well tomorrow if they are going to be able to pull off this upset. Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Garrett, thank you. Great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And 40 years, that's how long it's been since Oregon elected a Republican governor. But that could all change tomorrow night, and it's got even "Good Morning America" worried.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That race is so uncomfortably close that the Democrats had to bring in \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , Elizabeth Warren, President Biden to stump for the democratic candidate \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2 . This is a state that President Biden won by 16 points, but soaring inflation, crime, homelessness have become these red meat issues to which the Democrats simply don't have an easy answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And voters can see that, which is why in a state the Democrats thought they would easily sweep, Republican Christine Drazan is trailing Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  by just four points. Christine Drazan joins me now. Christine, I came across this statistic, and it shocked me, and I thought I had seen it all. But last year Mexico City had a lower homicide rate per capita than Portland, Oregon? So how do voters keep validating that type of leadership in this climate?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE DRAZAN, (R) OREGON GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, here's the thing. It's not uncomfortably close for Oregonians. Oregonians are reaching change, because it's not good enough. There is no universe where the murder rate in Portland should be close to the murder rate in Mexico City. And Oregonians are done. They are done with it. They're done with the excuses. They're done with the poor leadership. They're done with single party control. There is no amount of spin that can cover up what it's like right now in our urban centers and across our state. Oregonians deserve better and they're demanding better.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Every time I look at sort of the news cycle for Oregon now, it has something to do with some horrific crimes that are taking place.\par \par \uc2 DRAZAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Like this morning it was a guy wielding some type of weapon, I guess he had a rifle trying to set fire to Mercedes. The police had to shoot at him to try to stop this because they were worried about explosions. This is at 8:52 in the morning.\par \par \uc2 DRAZAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And yet you've got Elizabeth Warren out there making a pitch to voters for your opponent. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): Everything is on the line, our reproductive rights, our healthcare, the needs of working families, and our democracy itself. And that is why it is so important to elect \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2 . Please, get that ballot in, and vote for \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  for Oregon governor. Let's get this done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Abortion and democracy. No wonder eastern Oregon wants to secede to Idaho with that pitch.\par \par \uc2 DRAZAN: It's tone deaf. It is tone deaf. You have to be completely oblivious to what life is like in Oregon to come here and make a national, D.C. elite pitch to Oregon voters right now. It's completely tone deaf. Oregonians across our state are talking about kitchen table issues, the things that actually affect real people. Inflation, rising costs, rising crime, lower quality of life across our state, and certainly education standards that have plummeted under Democrat leadership.\par \par \uc2 My opponent, \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  has been in charge. She has linked arms with Governor \uc2 Kate Brown\uc2  in these last six years and has driven our beautiful state, our great state of Oregon into the ditch. And Oregonians themselves are demanding better. The fact that "Good Morning America" would view it as dangerously close is laughable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Uncomfortably close.\par \par \uc2 DRAZAN: Oregonians deserve better, and our chance to turn this around is tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Uncomfortably close. Yes or no, will that ballot measure pushing for strict gun control pass, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DRAZAN: I say no.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's the only one of its kind in the country, and a very odd response to the crime rate going up in your state. Christine, we are very proud of this campaign you ran, and we're going to be watching this race very closely tomorrow night. Best of luck to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, just hours away from the polls opening, final thoughts on this wild last year when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're just seven hours until the first polls open, so my final message to you is don't take anything for granted. We have a new movement. It's Hispanics, it's African Americans, it's entrepreneurs, working class people. We can't live this way any longer. There's no reason for us to live this way. Crime, high inflation, high prices, we know how to do it the right way, and we're going to show the rest of the country just how we do it. I can't wait to see what unfolds tomorrow. I'll be on tomorrow night with special coverage.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gutfeld and the gang take is all from here. Take it away, Greg.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221108eib700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Final Push For Votes Ahead Of Crucial Elections; Candidates Make Final Pitches One Day Out; Karl Rove Forecasts Biggest Races On Election Eve; Nevada Senate Race A Toss-up A Day Before Election; GOP Sen. Candidate On Race To Unseat Vulnerable Dem; \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair On Potential 2024 Trump Run; Undecided Voters Weight Options With Hours To Go; GOP Looks To Colorado For Potential Senate Upset; Campaigns In Final Sprint Before Election Day\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Karl Rove, Alexandria Hoff, Jeff Paul, Bret  Baier, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): You just went -- these went after it. It went after it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): It wouldn't have drawn in sideways.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, thank you very much, guys. Thanks, Juan.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL ANALYST: Nice to be with you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks, Sean.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Always good to see you. See more of you tomorrow, right, big day tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. Thanks so much for joining us, everybody. It was a jam-packed two hours. We'll be back here for election day tomorrow. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We will see you for election coverage. What a day it's going to be. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Thank you, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. With me Karl Rove, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , Bret Baier, Shannon Bream all here in the final hours as we head towards the very big day, which starts tomorrow. All candidates, of course, spread out in these consequential races across the country as they pull out their final closing arguments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: By January, I'm going to keep feeling better and better. But Dr. Oz will still be a fraud.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: He seems to care more about the criminals than the people they've hurt.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): Character matters. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not only not ready to be in the Senate, he's not fit.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I'm going to stand before you right now and say he's a Marxist, because he believes in abolishing the police. He believes in abolishing the court system.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): We've got an extremist on one side, and we got an Ohio and on the other side.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has run a campaign where he pretends to believe things that his own record clearly shows that he doesn't believe.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2  (D), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: This is possibly the last election in our lifetime if we don't elect the right people, if we don't stop the election deniers.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: They closed our businesses, they shut our churches down. They defunded the police.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): it is not just about protecting a woman's right to choose. It is making sure everybody has a right to vote.\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT (R), NEVADA SENATE CANDIDATE: Democrats have been able to try to blame other people for their policy failures over the last few years. Guess what, there's no one else to blame.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that was \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . We will be speaking to him in a little bit. He's looking to flip that Nevada seat -- red, that would be a pickup for Republicans. He is up against Senate, Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who has been closing strong according to a number of different evaluations out there today. It is one of the closest and most crucial races when you look at the balance of power in the Senate. So, as I said he will be joining us in just a short while. So, we kick things off with Karl Rove, Former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and a Fox News Contributor. Karl, great to have you with us today. So, if you listen to some of those closing arguments, you see the discussion about this could be the last election of our lives coming from \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  and Catherine, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , and I'm like, please, is that what people think the election is all about? No. They think that it's, you know, do ABC poll, economy 80 percent; inflation 77; education, school 75; threats to democracy down there at 74. But what's interesting to me is that it's pretty much even between who has the best message on threats to democracy, same poll 44 -- excuse me 43, Republican 47 Democrat. So, that's even that's relatively even. So, why are the Democrats doing this? I don't know. Because it's not the issue that's a top of mine. The economy, inflation and crime rank higher, and not only that, I guess the reason that they're not talking about those issues is the Republicans have advantage on all three of them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, you make an interesting point about people's understanding of the election and whether or not it will be fair, it's split between both sides in terms of caring about that as an issue. And it's not one of the highest-ranking issues. So, it's, you know, interesting to see this poll that we've talked about yesterday, which said eight out of 10 voters said that they think the election will be decided, you know, in a fair way that they can digest and accept. Here's Chuck Todd talking about the warning signs that he sees when you look at demographic groups in the election for black voters, young enthusiasm, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: There are two enthusiasm numbers in particular that I want to point, I want to highlight here: African-American voters and young voters. And as you can see, remember, Democrats had a good night in 2018. And right now, African-American voters are not quite at their 2018 levels; and young voters are not quite at their, at their 2018 levels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he's talking about voter intensity, something you talk about a lot, Karl, when you look at enthusiasm, party to party, how's that looking?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, we're starting to get to numbers because we can look at these early votes. For example, in Texas, the early vote among young voters, which has traditionally been more Democrat is collapsed. I mean, it is a, it's about half of what it was four years ago. And we're also seeing some movement in these polls. I've been talking to some of these campaigns. For example, the DeSantis campaign, very bullish on the Hispanic vote in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Same in Texas. Governor Abbott is going to have his election night party, not in Austin, Texas, the capital, but in McAllen, Texas in the heart of the Rio Grande Valley, why? Because he thinks he's got a chance to get 50 percent of the Hispanic vote. And then we're seeing some real angst in the among Democrat ranks about the black vote not only the level of turnout, is it going to be as high as they need it to be, but about defections, particularly among younger black men.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you see that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , is saying that black men in Georgia have been subjected to misinformation.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that that's why they're not turning out in the numbers that she would have expected.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, isn't it something to insult their intelligence --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: They're too dumb to figure out that they're being misled. I mean, they're changing because their issue cluster is more akin to Republican than Democrats. They're waking up and saying, wait a minute, I'm concerned about getting a good job. I'm worried about the making my paycheck stretch to fit my family's needs, and I'm worried about the safety of my neighborhoods. And the Republican candidates like Governor Kemp and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  seem to be thinking and talking more like I want, then then the Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's see what you got there.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I just, this was the Republican advantage on the big issues.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: 52-38 on the economy and the ABC poll; on inflation, 50-38; on crime 54-34, this, these are issues where they're pretty closely matched. But what's interesting to me education in the schools is 44 Democrat, excuse me, 44, Republican, 47 percent Democrat. That's a big shift. This used to be a huge advantage for the Democrats, and it's melted away. And then threats to democracy: 43 Republican; 47 Democrat. Remember, some people are sitting there saying I think one party rule like we've seen for the last couple of years, is a threat to the future of our country.\par \par \uc2 We got some people, particularly in the Hispanic community, and particularly in Florida, who are saying, I think the Democrats are going too far to the left. I don't want socialism in our country. And then the, the issue that works for the advantage of the Democrats 50 -- 37 percent Republican; 50 percent Democrat, say the Democrats can handle the abortion issue better. The problem is that of the issues tested in the ABC poll is number six, in importance -- and even less so when you try and isolate it. Like in the Gallup poll, they say what's the most important issue? Four percent say abortion is the most important issue\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, I got to let you go. But red wave or red tsunami, what are you feeling today?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I'm feeling red wave. But, but you know, waves can be big and can be small. I don't think it's a tsunami, in part because the Republicans did well in the congressional races in 2020, picking up 14 seats while they were losing the White House. That, the party that loses the White House only picks up seats and a half at about one out of every five times.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, Republicans are at 212 right now. And when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  lost 63 seats, I think they were in the 170s, right?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, thank you very much. We'll see you later. We'll be seeing you all day long and all tomorrow and probably into Wednesday. Thanks, Karl.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, our election coverage continues at this hour, Bret Baier and Shannon Bream will stop by to talk to us about their outlook for what we can expect in the next days to come. And two former presidents hit the trail in the tossup state of Nevada to campaign for Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who is considered one of the most vulnerable Democrats in the Senate, her Republican challenger who hopes to be the senator who flips a seat or to become that Senator, Attorney General \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , 42ND PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In the next few days, it a big turn out and she'll win. Don't believe that the right now crowd really cares more about the (INAUDIBLE) that went down. All they really care is more about the gas prices going up, it's just not so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, one of our toss-ups in the Fox Power Rankings that could decide control the Senate is the state of Nevada. You can see it out there and that is where \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is the incumbent senator. She's in a very tight race with Republican challenger, the former Attorney General there \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , and he will join us in just a moment. But first to our correspondent on the ground there, Alexandria Hoff, outside of Las Vegas in Henderson. Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, well, Democratic heavy hitters have been called into the state to try to spark some last-minute enthusiasm. Right now, we have Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , he's inside here helping with a canvassing launch that's taking place right now. And yesterday, former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  while, he was in Las Vegas alongside Senator \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , where he urged voters not to cast a protest vote on issues like the economy and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: If you cast a protest vote, here's the problem: you don't get a protest vote, you don't just vote against something, you vote for something.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voiceover): Now, at that event, the former president admitted that he had not liked all the deficit spending that's come out of Washington but said it was necessary. We asked \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  if Democratic spending would stop or slow down if she were to be re-elected?\par \par \uc2 MASTO: Oh, it already has. We already did that as part of the inflation Reduction Act that reduced the deficit by 300 billion. You just heard that that reduction in, right now. That's a lot of the work that we've done already and we will continue to do --\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But on the economy and crime, well, that's really where Republican challenger, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , has gained steam here. In the last couple of weeks, he has been continuing on a bus tour hitting every county and the rural ones, they stand a chance of flipping the script here. The latest tally shows around 640,000 early votes have been cast. That's up 100,000 compared to 2018. 1.4 percent more Democrats have returned a ballot, but as we know, Republicans in general are more likely to turn out on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): But Martha, weather it could play a role in a turnout here. It's kind of a blustery day, but tomorrow the forecast predicts that in Las Vegas, there will be rain and snow in Reno, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Snow in Reno. Thank you, Alex. Good to have you with us. So, let's bring in \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican candidate for Senate in Nevada. He, we also invited Senator Cortes Masto when you hear back from her in the final hours of this.\par \par \uc2 So, good to have you with us today, the former Attorney General, thanks for being here. So, I want to ask you first about this piece of news that people watch closely in Nevada and that is Jon Ralston sort of look at the early independent voting that he sees happening out there.\par \par \uc2 And he, this is obviously been a very close race all the way through. Everybody who watches it seems to feel that it's within a point. But he basically says that he thinks that Sisolak, the governor will lose, and he thinks that your opponent, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the Senator will win this race.\par \par \uc2 And he basically goes after you and says that you're the weaker opponent in this. What do you say about all that?\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT (R-NV), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I say this guy is discredited himself for eight straight years in a personal vendetta against me. He also predicted I was going to lose in '14, and we won that race.\par \par \uc2 You see, the real clear average has me almost at three points ahead. We've been up in every single poll in the last few weeks. We've run a really strong race. We've been credited as winning -- running one of the strongest Senate races in America by making sure that we stay on the issues.\par \par \uc2 The issues that are crushing our state, our 16 percent inflation. You just saw that news segment, and your -- the national audience gets an idea of how weak Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 She's asked about spending, and she says we just passed the bill to stop spending. Well, everybody knows they spent more money. Everyone knows they made inflation worse just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 She had the opportunity to stand with Nevada to tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  she wasn't going to spend more money, but she cast that vote, just like she's cast every other vote with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when he's needed her. All these things have added up to make Nevada an absolute disaster zone, on inflation on gas prices, on lawn order issues, and our open border, which is killing not just Vegas, but our rural communities all over our great state.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask --\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you a little bit about what to expect. You're in Washoe right now, in Washoe County. That's an area that Ralston says that he thinks that \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  could possibly pull out a win.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm asking what you're looking for.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): What should we all be watching for in the county that you're in, which is where Reno is. Obviously, a pivotal county in all of this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): What do you looking at right now in terms of indications of what has you in -- you know, how you're positioned?\par \par \uc2 LAXALT: Yes, look, here is the great news is that the Democrats, after two weeks of early vote, that's in person male, this is their time, this is when they're supposed to build their big firewall, there are only up just over one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 We are going to win Election Day. And as long as people turn out on Election Day, on top of our margins in the polling, we're going to coast in this race.\par \par \uc2 We feel we're exactly where we need to be. We are winning independents in every single poll that's come out in the last few weeks. The Latino voters, as you've covered have moved 20 to 30 points our direction. And so as long as our people vote tomorrow, we're going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, and you don't think the snow and rain will dissuade them?\par \par \uc2 LAXALT: It's not going to deter our voters, they got to get out. This is our chance to save our state and save our country.\par \par \uc2 Well, in Nevada is a place where we're going to watch the Hispanic vote come -- as it comes in really closely to evaluate how it has moved. And it's a real center for watching that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for being with us. Thanks for being here, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LAXALT: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): So, coming up, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  injecting some fear on the trail this weekend, warning voters that electing Republicans would be a threat to the country, a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , 44TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I understand that democracy might not seem like a top priority right now. Especially when you're worried about paying the bills.\par \par \uc2 But when true democracy goes away, we seen throughout history, we seen around the world, when true democracy goes away, people get hurt. It has real consequences.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , the \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair joins us with her reaction to that. And it's a line of campaigning we're seeing in a lot of places on the Democrat side, only on THE STORY after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , echoing what many in his party have claimed will happen if certain Republicans win their races tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: When true democracy goes away, we've seen throughout history, we've seen around the world. When true democracy goes away people get hurt. It has real consequences. This is not an abstraction. Governments start telling you what books you can read in which ones you can. Dissident start getting locked up. Reporters start getting locked up if they're not toeing the party line. Corruption reigns because there's no accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this is a theme that we have heard from a number of Democrats, and also from someone who is close to the White House, and as a historian. This is Michael Beschloss, and here's what he said. And then, we'll bring in \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN, NBC NEWS: A historian will say what was at stake tonight, and this week was the fact whether we will be a democracy in the future, whether our children will be arrested and conceivably killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a pretty dark scenario for this closing argument. Ronna. Welcome, good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Yes, great to be with you. I know Halloween was a couple days ago. But really, they're unleashing the scare tactics.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Democrats want to get rid of the filibuster. They want to stack the Supreme Court. They are the ones who wanted to take away all the voting measures at every state and federalize it. It's ridiculous, ridiculous what they're pushing.\par \par \uc2 But it's interesting to hear \uc2 Obama\uc2  say, oh, when they're talking about inflation, just ignore that. Well, that's easy for him because he lives in Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 But average Americans right now, affordability is the number one issue with gas, groceries, rent, all the things are dealing with. And the Democrats keep saying, oh, forget about that. It's not a big issue.\par \par \uc2 When you can't put food on the table. That's a big issue. And the Democrats are refusing to even acknowledge the problems that they have caused for the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, it's interesting that this is such a big part of the closing argument. And when you look at how these voting-related issues that I think would fall under that broader category of preserving democracy, they don't rank towards the top of people's concerns.\par \par \uc2 So, it's just races questions about, you know, about their political tactic here, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: It does. You know, they keep using this denier phrase. Listen, the Democrats have been inflation deniers, they have been crime deniers, border deniers, fentanyl deniers, education deniers, literally, as we kept -- they kept our kids out of the classroom, and were seeing these deficits.\par \par \uc2 They won't even acknowledge the pain and suffering the American people feel. Because of their policies. They could have unleashed American energy. They can shut the border. They can stop these drugs from coming across.\par \par \uc2 They could have said instead of funding 87,000, \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, let's get more Border Patrol, let's make sure we get more teachers in the classroom for these kids with deficits.\par \par \uc2 So, they are the ones denying that these problems even exist. And I think that's really insulting to the intelligence of the voters who are feeling this pain every single day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll see what message the voters send and what they care about when we get a look at our Fox News voter analysis and all of those benchmarks.\par \par \uc2 It will be really interesting to see what actually motivated voters on either side of the equation here. I want to ask you a quick question about the former President Trump because there was a lot of discussion earlier today that he was going to announce his candidacy that he's running in 2024 tonight at the rally with J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Now, I've been told that, that has been kind of put to the backburner for now, and that, that announcement won't come until mid-November.\par \par \uc2 Have you heard an answer to that question about tonight?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I just know the president is going to Ohio tonight. He was in Florida last night. He is been in Pennsylvania. I know that every one of our candidates is saying please, President Trump, come in and help us close this deal.\par \par \uc2 And every Democrat president is saying please Biden stay away. Trump also was the biggest fundraiser from -- for every committee. He did 50 fundraisers for candidates across the country.\par \par \uc2 He has been the MVP of making sure that Republicans get the Senate and the House this cycle.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Would it be good or bad for your candidates if he announced tonight at the Ohio rally for J.D. Vance that he is running again?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: You know, I think he's going in to help J.D. Vance. So, I think he wants J.D. to win, Madison Gesiotto, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 . He wants all the candidates in Ohio to win. He's been out there helping every single candidate across this country. We're working as a team because we want to win back the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did you talk to him about this announcement either way today?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I haven't talked to him about what -- his specific timing. But I know he is going in Ohio tonight. And he's really excited. He won by eight points in Ohio. He is like one of the most popular people in Ohio, and I think he's going to help J.D. and JD is very excited that he's coming in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Well, we'll all be watching, Ronna. Thank you very much. Ronna. McDaniel, always good to have you with us. We'll be in touch as we watch this unfold over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Ronna.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): So, three undecided voters with just one day to go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They are still stewing it over and deciding what they're going to do from battleground states. Tell us why they're leaning the way they're leaning with just hours to go in the races that will determine the control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So more than 30 million Americans have already cast their votes in the midterms. But with hours to go in control of Congress hanging the balance, some people of course have still not completely made up their minds and they will vote on Election Day, and we want to check in with three of them. Bernadette Wright joins us from Georgia, Kimberly Brown is from Ohio, and Dore Wallace is from Nevada. Good to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Dore, Let me start with you. We just spoke with one of the candidates there, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the former attorney general. What's the most important issue to you? And I think when you're undecided and you walk into that voting booth, what do you think is going to be tugging at you?\par \par \uc2 DORE WALLACE, UNDECIDED NEVADA VOTERS: I'm honestly surprised so many people are decided. What I'm looking to here is the post-COVID world conversation about systemic health, is what I'm really been waiting and hoping someone mentions before I decide.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know that you told us that you're a registered Democrat voter. Your husband is a Republican voter generally, I guess. So, what, you know, so you -- obviously, you homeschool your kids, I think you said as well, post-COVID. So is neither one of these candidates giving you a sense of what kind of world you want to see post-COVID?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Absolutely not, no. I don't think anyone is really speaking to bipartisan solutions for everyone, which is what I think we all kind of are waiting for to hear.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is it education that you're concerned about?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Less -- education is of course important, but I think general systemic issues, democratic health, you know, health of voting systems, health of economic systems, and bipartisan. Not issue-focused but solution- focused, and I just don't hear a lot of language that sounds solution- focused.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you leaning one way or the other at this moment as we talk?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I think I'm going to, I'm leaning towards keeping balance. So, you know, whether I vote for \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  it will be because I think the system requires balance itself. But I would prefer to hear both parties speaking about system health.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you see yourself splitting your vote at the governor level and the Senate level?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I do. I do see myself splitting the vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, very interesting. Bernadette, let's go to you in Georgia. That was really coming down to the wire. Which way do you think you're going to go here and why?\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE WRIGHT, UNDECIDED GEORGIA VOTER: I'll be -- first, thank you for having us. And I'll be honest with you, I'm leaning towards Kemp. I'm sitting here thinking -- I've actually been thinking about it and praying about it. And I feel like it's going to take a person to change things. There's all of this political rhetoric that's out there right now.\par \par \uc2 And I need someone who actually stands for something and you know what you're getting, like we talked about before. Economy is a big thing and inflation is a big thing. As small business owners, we understand that small business owners are the ones who employ people, especially here in Georgia. And the only person who seems to be talking about that right now is Kemp. There are lots of things I don't like about him, there are some things I don't like about her, I feel like we don't really have the best choice. But if I had to make a choice today, it would definitely be for Kemp.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what about at the Senate level between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Oh, well, that choice was made weeks ago. My vote still is for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, yes.\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: I feel like he's the one who can reach across the aisle and talk to people, and he kind of knows what he's doing already.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So each of you are talking about splitting your ticket, one Republican vote, one democrat vote in Georgia and in Nevada which is fascinating.\par \par \uc2 So, Kimberly, let's go to you in Ohio. I know you -- we have a billboard that is a \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  billboard that's in your neighborhood. And we'll put that up on the screen. It was the subject of one of their debates and folks can read it. There are some interesting photos, interesting images on there. You get to see that every day when you leave your neighborhood. So, how are you looking at this between JD Vance right now and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 ? Which way are you going to go?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY BROWN, UNDECIDED OHIO VOTER: Well, actually, unfortunately, I have not made a definite decision. JD Vance scares me, and he scares me because in the beginning, he was not a supporter of President Trump, former President Trump and now he is, and I think it's only because Trump came out to endorse him. So Trump picked him up from the bottom of the barrel, and got him to the top of the playing field.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has been in the House for 10 years, so we know that he's experienced. But the issue that I have with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is that he's a tough talker. I don't need a tough talker, I need a tough doer. So with all the tough talk, we still have not seen concrete evidence about what he can bring to the table. So what concerns me, of course, is inflation, the high gas prices, you know, the dollar is decreasing and prices are rising. And I haven't heard either candidate talk about what policies of inflation that they would support in order to make Americans comfortable the way we should be number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I'm still concerned about the reverse of Roe vs. Wade. Now, JD Vance was on a podcast called Catholic Current, and he made a comment that compared abortion to slavery. Now, being an African American Black woman, whatever you want to call me, I need to be able to get clarification about what did he mean? Because if he understood the history of slaves, he knew that a lot of slaves were raped. So is he saying that because people are raped that their options of an abortion is null and void. I need to get clarification.\par \par \uc2 I need to also understand what happened on January 6. We know what happened on January 6, and the insurrection. How do you support a candidate who cried about the fact that he didn't win at all heck broke loose at the White House? So here again, I'm on the fence because I want to make sure that whoever gets that seat is going to be down for the American people and not just for somebody's political party.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Kimberly, I hope you -- when you make up your mind, let us know. We'd love to know what you end up deciding. Kimberly, Bernadette, Dore, thank you very much from three of the most interesting states to watch. Great to have you all with us. Thank you, ladies.\par \par \uc2 So, "he's going to win the race," that is from New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  who is predicting a come from behind victory in Colorado, where Republican Joe O'Dea is battling it out to unseat two term incumbent Democrat Senator Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R), FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: I think on Tuesday night is Joe O'Dea, the Republican candidate for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Colorado, is going to beat Michael Bennet. And that's going to be a huge surprise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: O'Dea joins us live for a quick chat after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: I think on Tuesday night is Joe O'Dea, the Republican candidate for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Colorado, is going to beat Michael Bennet. And that's going to be a huge surprise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bennet doesn't have the support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: He does not have the support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He flat out said that he believes that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the legitimately elected president of United States and had Trump attack him. And in Colorado, he is driving Michael Bennet to distraction. He's going to win the race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, there you have it from the former governor of New Jersey, predicting that Republicans are going to have an upset in the state of Colorado. That would be a flip for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, our Fox Power Rankings have this as a leaning Democrat race in favor of Michael, of Senator Michael Bennet, who is a two term senator there. We're going to talk to that Republican candidate Joe O'Dea. We stepped off the campaign trail for a moment. We'll check in with him in a second but first to correspondent Jeff Paul with the very latest from Denver. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha. Had we been out here just even a week ago here in Colorado, talking about this race, we'd be saying that it leans in favor of Democrats. But now there are some indications that it is now tightening, with some even saying that it could be a toss up at the moment.\par \par \uc2 So on the left, you have Senator Michael Bennet. He has been serving since 2009. He's the Democrat. And recently he has spent some time distancing himself from President Biden, disagreeing with him on how he's handled the border and also student loan debt relief.\par \par \uc2 On the right, you have a political newcomer, Joe O'Dea. He's a construction company CEO. And what makes his candidacy interesting is that, he has not only not got the support of former President Trump but has said that he will campaign against Trump if he runs again in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So we spent some time talking with Colorado voters asking them about that, and wanted to know what they think of Joe O'Dea's chances.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHON COBBS, COLORADO VOTER: I appreciate that he tries to make that distinction. I'm skeptical of it. I don't necessarily believe it because I think Trump has such a stronghold on the party.\par \par \uc2 JIM CAROLLO, COLORADO VOTER: He probably doesn't have a great chance but we -- he probably better chance than normal. Meaning, a little bit more right than normal here in Colorado, but I think we're still pretty progressive state.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS NUNN, COLORADO VOTER: I don't think it helps him because it's not as believable as you would think it would be. He says a lot of things but I haven't seen any examples of his actions and what he's done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now as we watch folks pull up to this drive thru location here and drop off their mail-in ballots, elections officials tell us here in Denver, do not expect results for maybe a couple of days, especially here in this area. He told us that many people with those mail-in ballots have yet to turn them in. Tomorrow is Election Day and, Martha, I know you've got Joe O'Dea coming up, we talked to him earlier. He told us he still hasn't cast his ballot. So get on him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We'll find out who he's voting for. Thank you, Jeff. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 So with that we bring in Colorado Republican Senate candidate Joe O'Dea. We've also invited Senator Bennet to join us as well, waiting to hear back from him. Joe, welcome. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 You hear the sort of laid back. We're not sure what's going to happen in our home state. From some of those sound bites and voters that we talked to what's your sense on the ground of whether or not this is tightening and whether or not you can do what \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has said he thinks she will do.\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), COLORADO SENATE CANDIDATE: Martha, thanks for having me on. I got to tell you, this is tightening up. We've got great energy across the state. It's a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Michael Bennet. They've single- handedly ruined this economy. We've got 5 trillion in spending that sent the inflation through the roof. People are feeling the pinch, they're feeling it on their groceries, they're feeling it on the price of gas, the war on energy that's caused diesel to go to $5.50 here in the state, and driving a lot of this inflation. That's what people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 I built a huge coalition. It's MAGA supporters, it's GOP supporters, its Independents, it's Democrats that are disgruntled. They don't feel good about their party, but they feel good about a working American who's stepping up to serve this country, to make sure that we have a voice in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  here. And I'm excited about our chances. We're going to get across the finish line here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what do you say to those? I know the other side has been trying to tie you to President Trump even though you've said that you would literally campaign against him. But they say that you'll end up being a MAGA vote in Congress. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, I am who I am. I'm not a politician, I'm a contractor. And I'm going to represent the state of Colorado. That's why I got in this race. I don't care about people. I don't care about endorsements. What I care about is the voters of Colorado, we can do better. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has single-handedly hammered our economy. And this election is a referendum on Biden and Bennet who supports him 98% of the time.\par \par \uc2 A vote for Joe O'Dea is a vote to stop \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's agenda. And that's where I'm at and that's what people of Colorado want. And that's why we've got so much excitement behind this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's good to talk to you. You really got everyone's attention when you won the primary there and now you've got the former governor of New Jersey's attention. He says you're going to be an upset. So we will be watching all of these races really closely. Joe O'Dea, thank you. Good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Thanks, Martha. Joeodea.com, thanks again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So Bret Baier and Shannon Bream join me live from New York as we count down to Election Day and they will tell you their websites as well when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Take a look at this. An attack on a supporter of New York Republican candidate for governor \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . It happened to add a rally for Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  that was taking place in Manhattan. We've seen a little too much of this kind of stuff, oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 So that is that video in slow motion, not good. Supporter tells fox that she was peacefully protesting when a woman grabbed her sign and then a man tried to choke her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANGELICA TORRES, ZELDIN SUPPORTER: Oh, she like a sub human, like my feelings didn't matter that, you know, just because I have an opinion that's different than theirs that they have a right to know an act of violence against me. And I feel like it was just completely unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So she said she did get some medical treatment after that. It's horrible. And she was pretty shaken up who can blame her. So the New York governor's race is very tight, and that may be why you see some of those tensions spilling over our latest Fox Power Rankings shifted this from likely Democrat to just lean Democrat. A few weeks ago, it was solid blue. It's one of the big contests that we will be watching tomorrow night, along with "Special Report' anchor Bret Baier and "Fox News Sunday" anchor Shannon Bream who join me now in New York. And we'll all be out there together tomorrow night as we cover this story as it unfolds.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, there's a lot of tension out there, Bret, And you see that, you know, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  dealt with two different violent episodes over the course of this whole thing. And as much as we try to, you know, believe that it all goes smoothly there are these outbursts that make you a little nervous.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Right. And it's got to be a concern for a lot of authorities around the country. There's stepped up security and there's a lot of emotion around a big elections. We are going to be watching everything, hopefully it all goes smoothly. But we know that things pop up and things will happen tomorrow that we can't predict right now.\par \par \uc2 This New York race could be one of those. I talked to some prominent Democrats in New York who truly believe that Zeldin is going to win. And that's pretty --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's very tight. And you can easily talk to consultants on either side who say, I think she's going to pull it out by a point or two. Shannon, you know, in terms of the tenor of this race and the language that has come out in the last couple of weeks about -- concerns about democracy. You spoke to James Clyburn who really doubled down on his concerns about the parallels to Nazism, pre-World War II. Here he is with you on Sunday, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Rep. James Clyburn (D-SC): Election deniers setting up procedures by which little committees about governors can overturn the results of election, to call the press the enemy of the people. And I'm telling you, what I see here are parallels to what the history of this world back in the 1970s --\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So, congressman, then --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So he did not back off of that at all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He didn't. He stood by it when I asked him. So are voters out there to infer that if they decide to vote, not for you, but for the other side of the ticket that they now are somehow responsible for giving rise to a Hitler or something that happened in the 1930 Germany. He kept saying, well, this is about election deniers, and which I reminded him, Democrats spent more than $40 million, getting people that they consider extreme and election deniers through their Republican primaries thinking they would be better candidates in the general for Democrats to take out. So what responsibility to you bear to getting some of those people onto the general election ballot where a lot of them have a good chance of now winning?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Democrats play this game, Republicans play this game. But when you enabled with your dollars to get those people there, then you can't then decry. If you vote for them, you're destroying democracy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And in Nazi Germany, you could not wear that shirt. I mean, just could not do it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He has his Sunday clothes on. He was hanging out at home.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He was ready for a party.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's a little bit of an incongruent message to be, so fearful in your Hawaiian shirt.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Buy, hey, it was Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, he's been around a long time and very influential.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we appreciate him coming on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Always.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And he absolutely appreciate him coming on. And he always has his finger on the pulse of what's happening. He really was one of the main factors that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  do this to become the nominee from James Clyburn.\par \par \uc2 Bret, give me your big picture look, talking to folks and what's your feel for where this is headed tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it either could be a massive red wave or Democrats could tie the thing. How about that?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The end.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be back after this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Really? You (inaudible) way out (inaudible), Shannon. How about you?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think there really is, if you look at an election heading into it, the environment is obviously in the Republican's favor. But on the Senate races, they are so tight on a knife's edge that each individual race could tip either way. And that's why there's so much drama tomorrow. It's probably tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow, but we'll see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I think this is going to be something we're going to talk a lot about. Because in Pennsylvania, it's going to take them --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They've told.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They've already said. Well, there's no way we're going to have an answer on Tuesday night. I mean, Shannon, you know, it's rhetorical but at what point did they start getting --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's election season.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's Election Day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now officially election season since COVID. And since the advent of the mail-in ballot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And the constitution gives every state the power to handle this, how they want to. So we have 50 laboratories of democracy on how they count votes and hold their elections. But there are other states that start counting these absentees way in advance as they come in so that on Election Day, they're able to much more quickly add those to the totals where we always caution people day of voting tends to benefit Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you see that lead going into that night, but this is a state that has not started counting the mail-ins and absentees just brace yourself. We're going to have to be a little bit patient, which is not what people want to hear on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And we also have the information at our fingertips, Bret, to explain to people, this state does it that way, the state does it this way, because there was so much confusion last time around. And that led to a lot of people being concerned about what was going on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. And we're going to try to lay that down, be transparent in each individual instance. The other thing we'll be able to say is here's the vote that's remaining out and where that's coming from and where that traditionally leans. So we're going to kind of talk about the process by which we make these calls to be able to help people understand how they're coming down the pike.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And, Shannon, you're doing what we used to call the exit polls, but we now call Fox News Voter Analysis. Give us a quick thought.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The thing I love about this is it's better than an exit poll, which just catches people literally walking out from the ballot box but what we do is contact people before. So we get people who are who are early voters and absentee voters and mailing voters. So we're getting that slice which is only become a bigger and bigger part of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there's hundreds of thousands of voters, it is huge, huge --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. And we start talking to them end of October and through Election Day, so you're getting a picture people that have already voted in those who are going to the ballot box that day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Shannon, thank you. Bret, thank you. See you later on Special Report, great to be with both of you. So join us, all of us and more on our election night coverage kicking off tomorrow at 6:00 Eastern here on Fox News Channel and heading into the wee hours of the night, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for now on this Monday, the day before the midterm elections. THE STORY goes on and on throughout the coming hours and days. I'll see you back here tomorrow at 3:00. I'll see you on Special Report later tonight. Take care, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221108eib700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Americans Prepare To Reject Dems' Wreckage; Retaking America: Pennsylvania; Fetterman Stumbles Toward Finish Line; Fetterman Thinks He's Tough On Crime; What Is The GOP'S Plan When They Control Congress?; Retaking America: Ohio; Biden Pledges To Shut Down Coal Plants; How Can Economy Grow In The Face Of Big Govt?; Retaking America: Nevada; Dem Heavyweights Can't Save Sen \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 ; Dems Running On Hate, Fear And Division; Raymond Hits The Streets Of New York; New York Republican Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  Discusses Issue Of Crime In His Campaign Against Democratic Incumbent Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 ; Former Democratic Party Member \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  Campaigns For Georgia Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ; Polling Indicates GOP Advantage Over Democrats In Upcoming Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Sean Duffy, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 9,928 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alte