2 ARROYO: What is the number one issue driving your vote this midterm says?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would say the economy. We have terrible inflation, and Phoenix is one of the highest in the country. I'm an economist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Crime is out of control. Definitely the economy and crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody has to look over their shoulder. And you have to have your head on a swivel.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Prices of everything is going up. My credit card bill is going up. I have to charge everything\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My full time job is now grocery shopping.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have to go to all the sides pick out what's on sale because I dint buying it. If I don't sell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Abortion.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Abortion. Why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because it's a right. And it's being severely threatened.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Why abortion?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because I do believe that it's it's a it's a life. It's no matter what at conception is alive.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Your reaction when you heard the President say this ultra-MAGA movement could destroy democracy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Peace me off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can't say it on air, but it starts with bull.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Does it end with?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It absolutely does. It's not an end to democracy. Democracy is what we had. And it's by taking it away from us, that our votes don't count, and by calling us names, especially in the south, all you're doing is pushing my button. You push my button, make me mad, I'll get out and knock on doors and tell people we've got to vote this fool out.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Guys, you don't want to mess with these sisters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They are hilarious. Raymond, that's so funny. I love it.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of New York City, yesterday we told you about the brutal early- morning rape of a jogger. The perpetrator had 25 priors and was arraigned this afternoon. Last month, Keaira Bennefield, 30-years-old, was fatally shot in front of her three children less than 24 hours after her estranged husband was released from custody without bail for allegedly beating heart. Despite refusing to touch the bail reform laws, Democrat New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  refuses to confront reality.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN ANCHOR: Why are Republicans winning on this whole crime issue? That has been the thing that his fueled the \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 's campaign.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: Because they are being dishonest about it. They're not having a conversation about real solutions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Republican candidate for governor of New York, joins us now.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Congressman, we see this strategy here. Hillary used it yesterday and Hochul is using it now, that crime and the threat of crime is a tactic being used by you and others, it's an exaggeration, it's a perception problem more than a reality problem.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2 , (R-NY): So here's the best part of the clip you just showed is that she is saying that right next to them showing all the different major crime categories that are way up year-over-year. So she says it's data denial to be saying that we have a crime issue, she says it's a conspiracy. And even CNN this morning when she is doing that side- by-side, it's showing how major crime category one after another. So this morning I'm at Pier 45 where there was that terrible attack that you just referenced, and in New York City, rape is up 15 percent year-over-year.\par \par \uc2 So what people want is for their candidates, whether it's the governor or the person running for that position, to be talking about how we understand why you're upset about this, here are my solutions to fix it, and we'll have a zero tolerance for crime. We're going to support our men and women in law enforcement. We're going to fire rogue district attorneys who refuse to enforce the law. We're going to roll back these pro criminal laws that they have passed.\par \par \uc2 But you're not getting that leadership. Instead, they just want the deflection and distraction to talk about something else. They want us focused on these issues like crime and the economy. They don't want to see us trying to come up with some excuse for why there's no responsibility to deal with any of this. If you don't want to deal with it, then don't run for the position.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We have a question from Gabriel Arteaga, an \uc2 NYU\uc2  student. Gabriel?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL ARTEAGA, \uc2 NYU\uc2  STUDENT: Good evening, Congressman Zeldin. My name is Gabriel Arteaga, I'm from Brooklyn, New York, and I'm also a first-year college student here in New York City. In recent years I have seen the city that I love deteriorate and become a shell of itself because our elected leaders do not want to enforce the laws that keep us safe. What would you do as governor to address my concerns about public safety?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: The first thing that I would do the first day that I'm in office is tell the Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  that he's being removed for his refusal to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: While Plan A is for the state legislature to come to the table and work with me, they're saying that they're not going to work together with the need to roll back these pro criminal laws like cashless bail, so what we're going to do on day one to force them to the table is that we are going to declare a crime emergency here in the state of New York and we are going to suspend cashless bail, less is more, raise the age, the HALT Act, the discovery law changes. We have to force them to come to the table.\par \par \uc2 You deserve your governor to be doing absolutely everything in their power to make your life here in New York City safer on the streets, in the subways. You're not getting that now. And I also am relieved to think about that law enforcement officer who's going to be able to go to sleep on January 1st knowing that once again they have a governor who has their back.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Congressman, people have heard me say so many times because I worked for President Reagan that Reagan campaigned in the Bronx in 1980. He ended his campaign, right in the last week or two, ended in the Bronx and made several stops in New York. He took his campaign to all 50 states. He went to the belly of the beast of liberalism to sell conservatism. You did that today, which I love, in Greenwich Village. Talk about the belly of the beast. I love Greenwich Village, but it's really leftwing. You were met with a little bit of protest. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Go back to long island.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get out of here. You're a phony.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, so that was like four people. But go back to Long Island. This is this idea again that if you're not dyed in the wool Democrat, you are not a real New Yorker. Member \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  said that not so long ago. What about that?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: She declared that I'm no longer a New Yorker because I'm challenging her. She demanded that I get on a bus and move to Florida. If you want to be the governor, you have to be willing to and want to be the governor for all New Yorkers. But she's not willing to do that. I want to be the governor for all New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So why did you go? Why was it important? Why was it important for you to go to Greenwich Village?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Because the people there want to see their leaders showing up. Yesterday, a woman is jogging, and she gets choked and assaulted and raped. And the people who live in that community are shocked by what happened. We all should be there. We should all be there talking about our ideas to make sure that this doesn't happen again.\par \par \uc2 So what we were just talking about with that last question when I mentioned that on day one I will do a crime emergency, those people who are shouting, one of the women shouts out there is no crime emergency. No, actually there is, and people want to see that leadership from us, and they're not getting it from \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . They are not getting it when \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  show up. There is a reason why when that rally was going on with them yesterday with a couple hundred people there, at the same time we had about 3,000 people up in the capital region. We have the momentum, we have the energy.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: And losing is not an option.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you getting rid of all these vaccine mandates. Are those going?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: I do not support any COVID vaccine mandate in any way, shape, or form on anyone for any purpose.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Healthcare workers, police, all gone.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Every single person who has been fired should get their jobs back with backpay.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Love it. Congressman, best of luck, I am so excited. And we are so happy you came tonight. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 After leaving the Democrat Party, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  has been crisscrossing the nation speaking in support of GOP candidates. Her latest stop today was in support of Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . They are both here next.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, (D) FORMER HAWAII REPRESENTATIVE: Let's take a stab for freedom and for this country that we love so we can continue to celebrate all that we hold dear. Join me now in welcoming your next U.S. senator \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Former Democrat Congresswoman and 2020 presidential candidate \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , who currently serves as lieutenant colonel in the Army Reserves as well, joins me here in New York in studio. Tulsi, it's great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And we are happy to be joined by the man she just introduced earlier today. And I believe, I believe the first time he has ever been on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, tsk-tsk, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . It's so great to have you on. Herschel, thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R-GA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Herschel, I have a confession to make. I'm a big Alabama fan. OK, sorry, but I'm a big Alabama fan. That's my first --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, no. Don't worry about it. Everyone makes mistakes. You're going to overcome it. Don't worry about it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, I knew you were going to say that.\par \par \uc2 Herschel, let me start with you, though, because you've been campaigning at this unbelievable pace. What are you hearing from the folks that you have been encountering ahead of Tuesday? Is it the personal scandals, is it the economy, is it crime? What is it?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: People are talking about this inflation. They are talking about the crime, they are talking about this border wide open. And that is what Senator Warnock do not want to talk about. He wants to mislead people, he wants to lie to the people. And I want people to know that, put me in office. I'm going to help to reduce your taxes. I'm going to help to get this border under control, and I'm also going to try to get men out women's sports whereas Reverend Warnock voted to put men in women's sports. And I hope that they saw the debate and they saw then who can be a senator today, and that is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tulsi, not only have you been campaigning for Herschel in Georgia, you were in New Hampshire for Bolduc, you have been crisscrossing the nation. Again, the Democrats are running on January 6th, abortion, and, I don't really -- greeniac policies, I guess, saving the planet. I don't understand what their strategy is at this point. It really confounds me.\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, (D) FORMER HAWAII REPRESENTATIVE: Whatever it is, it is out of touch with what everyday Americans are looking for in their leaders right now all across the country, both in these statewide races and these federal races. And that what I've been hearing from a lot of people in they are just hearing a lot of smears and lies and attacks coming from the Democrats, but not really what do they stand for, what record can they stand on, because they can't. There is nothing but these radical, so-called woke policies that they are trying to push on the people, and the people are saying no. We are not going to let you take over raising our kids for us. Parents are really, really concerned about this across the country. We're not going to let you continue to make are communities less safe.\par \par \uc2 The border is a real issue. I was with Mayra Flores down in McAllen on the border, went with her and her husband, and we saw the gaps in the wall where they stopped building it, and heard from those agents what they are dealing with on a daily basis that actually poses a threat to our country's security. So these are the issues that are so important. These are the issues that candidates like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  are addressing and talking about what they will do on day one in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Herschel, we've had a huge shift of women voters to the GOP in the last several weeks. Crime is one of the major issues, but the cost- of-living is also the other one. How important is it for Republicans to really try to appeal to other demographic groups. Obviously suburban women so important, but African Americans who have traditionally been thought of as wedded to the Democrat Party pretty much no matter what, how important has been that to your appeal?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: It's very important. It's important because we are all people here. And I think one thing that we have done is we've let people on the left get in office and try to separate people. They try to separate you because of the color of your skin. The guy I'm running against, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , in his church he speaks about separation and he talks about America needs to apologize for their whiteness. He talks about the problem America has is racism.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think we are good people. We have our problems, but we can get those problem taken care of. In the Bible I read, God doesn't talk about the color of your skin. He talks about your heart. That's what want people to get back to, worrying about their heart, worrying about this country. And as they always said, ask not what you country can do for you, but ask what you can do for your country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We've forgotten first principles, Tulsi. There are so many of your former colleagues in the House of Representatives, like \uc2 Seth Moulton\uc2  and others, who are saying you are selling out your principles, you're going to campaign for these Republicans. I see a Democrat Party that I don't recognize. It was a party of JFK and FDR, but you're the sellout?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: It is sad to hear people like him say that because really what the Democrat Party leaders have sold out is the promise that they made to uphold and defend the Constitution, the promise that they made to protect and stand up for the freedom of every one of us as Americans. And that is the main reason I left the Democrat Party, because this party is not only not defending our freedom. They are taking every opportunity to attack it, to silent and censor those of us who challenge or differ in views that they are pushing. They are hostile towards people of faith and spirituality, attacking every bit of our God-given rights enshrined in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 And for them to choose partisan politics over standing up for the foundation of this country when it is under threat by those in power, every American, I hope, takes notice of that as they are looking at who they are going to go and vote for. It's not about party. We are talking about the future of this country and our ability to continue to live in a free country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Freedom. Our freedom.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Our freedom and pragmatism, our sovereignty. We're not a country if we don't have a border.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As Herschel was saying. And Herschel, an opinion contributor from "USA Today" called you that black puppet of the GOP in an article today, OK. I worked for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . One of my favorite people on the whole face of the planet is Justice Thomas. They said several similar things about him. They elites still don't believe that if you're African American you can be conservative. They will call you Uncle Tom, they'll do all the usual stuff. Is this racist, what they are saying about you? Do you consider it racist?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: It is racist, but it doesn't bother me because this seat is much more important than name-calling. And they're not going to hurt my feelings because I see so much crime on these streets. They're not going to hurt my feelings because they've ruined this economy. They're not going to hurt me because this border is wide open. They're putting men in women's sports. Or hurt my feelings when the biggest threat to this democracy is to have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House. The biggest threat to the democracy is to have --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: -- Senator Warnock out there, to have Senator Warnock voting to put men in women's sports. That's the biggest threat to democracy, because we get a chance to vote. And because we get that chance to vote, we can get them out of office. And that's what we've got to do right now, because if we don't get them out of office we will not recognize this country after November.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tulsi and Herschel, it's great to see both of you. And Herschel, maybe we will see you in the national championship. OK, just maybe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And ahead, Chris Bedford is back at the Ingraham big board, and we're doing something a little bit different tonight. Instead of individual races, we're going to go revealing the three most likely scenarios for the GOP on Thursday. You don't want to miss it. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time once again for THE INGRAHAM ANGLE'S top races to watch. With just four days away from the midterm elections, I cannot believe it, we are back at the big board with Chris Bedford, who is executive editor for the Common Sense Society. First up, what we'll see on Tuesday that will signal a good night for Republicans if the swing districts start to go back to read. A key race to watch is Yesli Vega verse \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 . Spanberger has been listed among "Politico's" future Democratic stars at the risk of getting wiped out, and has spent a $8.3 million this year according to Open Secrets. So Democrat star, or maybe more of a Democrat dud? Chris Bedford, what do we think about that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, SENIOR EDITOR, "THE FEDERALIST": This is a bad day, this is going to be a bad week to be one of the rising Democratic stars. A lot of their young candidates, a lot of their bench, are going to get wiped out in a year that may have even been a little beyond their control. She came in in 2018. She took the seat from Dave Brat. And people remember him as the guy who knocked off Cantor, the House Republican Leader, in a primary. So she came in, but it's still as swing district. It could still go back and forth either way.\par \par \uc2 And if she loses, which it looks like she probably will, then if you're a Republican pollster, a Republican consultant, you can breathe easy and say, all right, this is going at least the way I planned it to.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She is another fake moderate Democrat. These people like Ryan and Ohio, and she is, oh, I'm a moderate. She's not a moderate. There is nothing moderate about her. So I think the voters know that.\par \par \uc2 Another race to watch that would signal a good night for the GOP would be Michelle Vallejo and Monica De La Cruz. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE'S last town hall was down in Houston highlighting some of these races on the border. So what's your prediction there?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: This is a Biden plus two district, which is not in effect if you are a Democrat who is trouble. This is one the Democrats are basically expecting to lose. De La Cruz has run a very solid campaign, and also they have reporters now going down to the Texas and the border region saying what's going on with Hispanic. It turns out they don't like any of the woke politics. Illegal immigration affects them directly. There are bodies found in their ranches and their farms. They're turning.\par \par \uc2 So not only are they now worried that De La Cruz is going to win, but there are some districts heavily Hispanic around there that are Biden plus 16 that they are now putting among those they are worried about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, boy. All right, next up are the races that would mean the GOP is having a great night. The first race would be if Michael Lawler can beat the \uc2 DCC\uc2  Chair Pat Maloney. What can you tell us about that race?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: These are some of the first east coast races you're going to be watching where it's Biden plus nine, 10, 11. And if Republicans start to win in those races, it is going to be a really rough night for the Democratic Party. They haven't knocked off a DCCC chairman in about 40 years, since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 And right now, it is not looking great for them. It's looking good for the Republican Party. And if you see people like this start to fall, then the Democrats are not going to have a good night.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Next up race if you see the Republicans ahead on Tuesday night, it will signal a midterm bloodbath for the Republicans. The first one is that New Hampshire Senate race between Don Bolduc and \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: This would be a bloodbath. This is one where the Republicans are putting the pool cue across the bar door and saying no one can leave. He's raised about $2 million. She's raised about $40 million.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my God. Wow.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: She is the incumbent. She has got all the money. He's barely even able to get on television. Literally the Democrats spent more money than the Republicans did on his primary because they thought he was too extreme to beat. And he has come out with some populist ideas, retired military. He has not back down from anything.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: General, obviously a former general.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Exactly. And New Hampshire is the most independent state in the union as far as voters go who are non-affiliated. Democrats might be wishing that they hadn't spent so much money to put him in the Senate on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What has \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  done? Literally, I wouldn't even know if I bumped into her on the street. What has she done?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: She supported \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and that is a very unpopular thing to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, our panel is back with us. Sean, which of these scenarios do you see happening here?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY\uc2 , FORMER WISCONSIN CONGRESSMAN: I'm a House guess, so I think in the House Republicans have a 30 plus seat majority, which is a banner night. But in the Senate I'm not sure. I think it is a-51 seat majority for Republicans in the Senate. I'm not sure we get to 54.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF TREASURY: I am hoping for that extinction level event, because I think the American people will put up with a lot. They will be pushed and pushed and they'll take it until the moment they don't. And I think this election cycle is one of those moments where the American people have had enough. I agree with Sean in the House, I think maybe 35, 45 seats. And in the Senate, I'm going to put my money on about 53, 54 seats.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, people will remember this. Reagan won handily in 1980, but the Democrats kept offering up really liberal candidates. Republicans had to win like three times for there to be a real transformation of the country. So one election, people think it's one election. To me this is going to be beating them again and beating them again and beating them again, including in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And we have to look closely at what is driving the electorate, what is driving the Hispanic vote, what's driving suburban women. It's inflation and its crime. The Republicans may win this largely by default, with apologies to many of the great candidates out there. They have to now make coherent policy arguments and creates a movement, winning those people definitively over to their ideas and not just because they are the alternative to this disaster before them.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, and Sean, that is what a lot of people who are skeptical of Washington, and you were there, had been there way too long, way too long, but Republicans get in power, and it's like they are better in the minority than they are in the majority. That is the risk they run if it's rhetoric not backed up by what is your legislation, what are you going to do? Can't do it.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: There has to be a balance, because you have to do oversight on the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration, but you also have to make lives better. And I think Kevin McCarthy has laid out a smart plan, he's talked about a transition team. I think they're right on page where you're at. We've got to legislate and fix people's lives.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, the commitment to America, to me it's a lot of headlines, a lot of general headlines, we've got to do this. But how are you going to do this? Biden is still the president. And so you always hear we can't do anything until we have the presidency. I don't know if that's true. You have the power of the purse. Start defunding these things.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: That is exactly right. So they need to when they come in the majority in both Houses, they need to stop Biden's destructive legislative agenda, that is number one, including all of the insane spending feeding this inflationary spiral. That is number one. Do not care about being called obstructionists. Stop everything. And Biden can still do a lot of damage through executive order, but Congress can put a stop to a lot of this. And then they have to offer voters a positive compelling reason to continue to vote for them with a positive legislation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Happy warriors. We can't be talking about 2020 every day. We've got to be going forward and taking this country in a new direction. Raymond, three words, you have three words to describe what is going to happen on Tuesday. What are they?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Excommunication, shock, MAGA.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sean, do you have any words?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Congressional Review Act. The Republican majority can pull back these bad policies of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Congressional Review Act.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Three words for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Extinction level event.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, that's it for us tonight. Thank you to this amazing New York City crowd. You've all been tremendous. I wish we had another hour. We could easily do another hour. Don't forget, get out there and vote either now or on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. Remember, it's America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld and the gang take it all from here.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221104eib400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Walks Fine Line Over Latest Jobs Report; GOP Reports Alleges \uc2 FBI\uc2  Leadership Problems; Arizona And Pennsylvania Could Determine Senate Control; Pennsylvania Senate Nominees Make Last Plea To Voters; Top Issues Are Inflation And Border Security On Arizona Senate Battle; Widespread Layoffs Begin; Employees Tweet About Being Laid Off; O'Dea Trails Bennet In Narrowing Colorado Senate Race; Colorado Republican Senate Candidate Joe O'Dea Interviewed On His Campaign Against Democratic Incumbent Michael Bennet; Washington And New Hampshire Senate Races Closer Than Expected For Democratic Candidates; Republican House Members Indicate Likely Investigation Into Possible Bias And Abuse Of Power In \uc2 FBI\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Anita Vogel, Bryan Llenas, Kelly O'Grady,  Connell McShane, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Don't miss that.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do you believe -- I can't even believe I'll be awake.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think I have my money on -- well, Dana lost last sink or swim.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I've lost every time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: She's lost every time. So, you're due.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I hope.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right, that's it for us tonight. We'll see you back here on Sunday at 5:00.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, with just four days left until the midterm vote, President Biden and the Democrats are trying to turn to the economic narrative and turn it in their favor.\par \par \uc2 The October jobs report shows another solid month of hiring with a slight increase in the unemployment rate. But the headwinds of inflation and skyrocketing interest rates, maybe too much to overcome and that could give control of Congress to the GOP, maybe one chamber, maybe both chambers.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. In the closing days of this campaign cycle, part of the president's pitch is an actual fairy tale as he tries to make the case that today's economic report is not too cold and is not too hot, but rather just right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: New York Times media reports is called -- they call the report the Goldilocks report. I have my shotgun waiting for the wolf.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden talking about a strong October jobs report and saying about 261,000 jobs created while comments by Republican leadership sure seem to indicate they are rooting for a recession. The U.S. economy continues to grow and add jobs even as gas prices continue to come down.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is trusting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to address inflation by raising rates. But officials there admitted this week, they don't know what will happen next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm wondering, has the window for a soft landing narrowed? Do you still think it's possible?\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: Has it narrowed? Yes. Is it still possible? Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president is not ignoring higher prices.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We also know folks are still struggling with inflation it's our number one priority.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But voters appear unsatisfied, listing inflation as the most important issue to their Senate vote in three critical battlegrounds Pennsylvania, Arizona, and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN WALSH\uc2 , LABOR SECRETARY: Somewhere in the future, there might be a recession. There's still lots of positive signs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That is not on the radar in the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are no meetings or anything happening like that in preparing for a recession.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On one hand, President Biden is telling midterm voters the elections aren't about him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Everybody talks about a referendum. It's not a referendum. It's a choice.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On the other, he's telling midterm voters the elections are about him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've already been told, if they win back the House and Senate, they're going to impeach me. I don't know what they'll impeach me for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden stops on the campaign trail this weekend. We'll be in Chicago, New York and then Pennsylvania, where he may have just complicated \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's Senate campaign because the president told a crowd in California that he will be shutting down coal fired power plants all across America, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is broken. That is the conclusion of a scathing report by GOP lawmakers in the house. It makes some very serious allegations against Bureau leadership.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Anita Vogel has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANITA VOGEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Just days before the midterm elections, which will determine control of Congress, House Judiciary Committee Republicans have released a report claiming the \uc2 FBI\uc2  under the stewardship of \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is broken.\par \par \uc2 Unnamed whistleblowers describing the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's leadership showing a political bias against conservatives and describing the agency's Washington hierarchy as "rotted at its core" with a systemic culture of unaccountability and full of rapid corruption, manipulation and abuse.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): America is not America if you have a Justice Department that treats people differently under the law. It's supposed to be equal treatment under the law. That's not happening.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: The bulk of the report contains previously released letters from House Republicans to DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials full of complaints, from the federal investigation into the president's son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 's controversial school board memo released last year.\par \par \uc2 Garland's one page memo from October 2021 doesn't include the word parent, but parents across the country felt the documents spelled politics.\par \par \uc2 Days before the \uc2 National School Boards Association\uc2  sent a letter to President Biden, arguing some parental action should be considered a "form of domestic terrorism and hate crimes", the group later apologized.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade, the Republicans argue attacks on pro-life facilities are going unpunished.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : We have seen a number of attacks against faith based organizations, pregnancy resource centers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL (on camera): There have been zero prosecutions this year of any pro- choice activist linked to any attacks.\par \par \uc2 Now, should the Republicans take back the House next Tuesday night? Look for them to bring both \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ officials before the public and frequently.\par \par \uc2 And we just received this late breaking statement from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  minutes ago which says in part, we follow the facts without regard for politics. While outside opinions and criticism often come with the job, we will follow -- we will continue to follow the facts wherever they lead. Do things by the book and speak through our work, that from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tonight, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Anita, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT spotlight, we have reached the grand finale. If you've been watching SPECIAL REPORT over the last seven weeks, we've looked at key midterm races and ballot measures in 48 states across America.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we check off our final two states, Pennsylvania and Arizona and they have implications in the fight for control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, you have incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly hoping to win his first full term in the toss up state and help Democrats maybe hold on to the majority.\par \par \uc2 Republican challenger Blake Masters however has closed the gap. Our latest Fox News poll has it at two points. But as you see here, it is within the margin of error. Some other polls have Masters taking the lead in this race.\par \par \uc2 A large amount of Arizona voters cast ballots early or in person and they actually have those results right after about an hour after polls closed.\par \par \uc2 Mail-in ballots can be dropped off until 7:00 p.m. local time and on election night. Those could take several days to be counted because they require signature verification.\par \par \uc2 Since 2020, Arizona's Republican legislature also eased the threshold for an automatic recount to a half percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, in Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz fight to replace retiring GOP senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 . Fetterman has a three point lead over Oz in our latest poll, but again, within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Democrats tend to do well in this -- the counties right around Philadelphia and the big cities, Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. They look at places like here Montgomery County, if you look back to 20. There you go. 2020, 62, 36. If you go up here to Bucks, it's a little bit less and there you have 58, 40.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are going to be looking a little bit further out to the West around Pittsburgh, Butler County, for example.\par \par \uc2 If you look at this, this is big Trump country, and that's where Republicans are looking to get that vote up.\par \par \uc2 Like Arizona, it could be a while before we learn the outcome of the Senate contest in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas tells us why tonight from Upper Darby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): With just four days to go before Election Day, Senate candidates Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: And Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz are making their final pitches to voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I will be the 51st vote in D.C. and to want to get all the good things that we want to accomplish, we got to eliminate the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want to bring balance back into this equation. I want to make sure that the extreme ideas of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  aren't there.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: All indications are this race is a tossup.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of Dr. Oz?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, I'm not that crazy about him, but you know, I'm going to vote for him. You know, I like him better than the other guy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm probably going to vote for Fetterman even though I'm a lifelong Republican.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: As both campaigns try to seize momentum, tonight, Fetterman is touting an endorsement from Oprah Winfrey.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY, THE OPRAH WINFREY SHOW HOST: If I lived in Pennsylvania, I would have already cast my vote for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: She's an icon. I mean, it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The late endorsement is particularly salient, given Oprah launched Dr. Oz into T.V. stardom.\par \par \uc2 In a statement the Oz campaign writes, Dr. Oz loves Oprah and respects the fact that they have different politics.\par \par \uc2 In a new memo, the Department of State ordered all counties to segregate and not count all mail-in ballots that are either missing handwritten dates on envelopes or that have incorrect dates.\par \par \uc2 DAVID VOYE, ELECTIONS MANAGER, ALLEGHENY COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA: We don't know exactly what wrong date is. But it's not -- doesn't have the month, it doesn't contain the year. We don't know what that is. So, we're not going to make that determination without further guidance from the current state.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: It's a recipe for confusion, lawsuits and delayed results, especially if the margin of victory is small.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It's a close race. It's a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): And whoever wins this jump ball could control the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and with so much on the line, both parties are sending their all- stars to Pennsylvania, former President Trump, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and President Biden will be holding campaign rallies this weekend from Pittsburgh to Philadelphia, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas at the center of the political universe in Pennsylvania. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now to Arizona, where inflation and the border are the top issues. Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady is in Phoenix tonight with the latest. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Yes, the Arizona Senate race is heating up even more in these final days. According to the latest Fox News polling, just two points separates the Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly from Blake Masters and the GOP candidate has room to grow with libertarian candidate Mark Victor dropping out.\par \par \uc2 And we've been on the ground talking to voters and this really comes down to inflation, border security and abortion as you said. 26 percent of registered Arizona voters say inflation is most important as they head to the polls.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to a restaurant owner right here in Phoenix who shared he may have to close down with costs so high.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, stories like that are not unique as Arizona's Capitol is experiencing the worst inflation in the country.\par \par \uc2 CPI data shows the average for U.S. city was 8.3 percent in August. Phoenix topped that at 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Phoenix sits in Maricopa County which represents over 60 percent of registered Arizona voters. Border Security also top of mind with Border Patrol telling us the Tucson sector seeing the most getaways in the country.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters leaning into that concern today at a GOP roundtable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Mark Kelly surrendered our southern border, our sovereignty to get it right over to the Mexican narco terrorists drug organizations, they're like business partners.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: But like many other Democratic candidates, Kelly is focusing heavily on abortion instead. Arizona has one of the strictest laws in the country and Kelly is seeking to turn voters against Masters on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MARK KELLY (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: He wants a national abortion ban that is so strict that even in the case of when a woman is raped, she will not be able to make this decision herself. He thinks he gets to make this choice instead of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): Now, voters here are incredibly passionate about all three issues but we'll have to wait until next week to see which influences their vote most. Back to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly O'Grady live in Arizona. Kelly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today, the Dow gained 402, the S&P 500 finished ahead 51, the NASDAQ jumped 132.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost 1-4/10 percentage points, the S&P 500 was down 3-1/3. The NASDAQ fell 5-2/3.\par \par \uc2 Up next, workers at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are getting some very bad news tonight. We'll tell you what that is, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The chairman of former President Trump's inaugural committee has been acquitted at a federal corruption trial. \uc2 Tom Barrack\uc2  was accused of using his personal access to then-President Trump, to secretly promote the interests of the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 The jury deliberated for three days before finding Barrack not guilty of acting as an unregistered agent of a foreign government, obstruction of justice, and making false statements.\par \par \uc2 The rate of deaths that can be directly attributed to alcohol rose nearly 30 percent in the U.S. during the first year of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 That's according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , the CDC. That agency had previously said the overall number of such deaths rose in 2020 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 Companywide layoffs are underway at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tonight. The new boss, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is expected to cut around half of his payroll at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , about 17 -- I'm sorry, 3,700 jobs in total.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some details tonight from Connell McShane of Fox Business, who joins us from New York. Good evening, Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 You know \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is cutting back in such a big way, because its new owner says there's been a massive drop in its revenue.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (voice-over): \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  blaming that decline on "activist groups pressuring advertisers, even though nothing has changed with content moderation," and as he says, "we did everything we could to appease the activists."\par \par \uc2 At an investor conference that was held here in New York today, Musk said much of this actually predated his ownership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, OWNER AND CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, TWITTER: To be frank, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was having pretty serious revenue challenges and cost challenges before the acquisition talks started. And any company that is dependent on advertising has had a hard time.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well, the big name advertisers pausing their business now, include food company \uc2 General Mills\uc2 , and automakers like \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and \uc2 Audi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  today started what appears to be a massive round of layoffs. The Wall Street Journal says that roughly 50 percent of the workforce has been hit by this. And this is a company that had more than 7,500 employees when the year began.\par \par \uc2 As for the workers, they found out their fate in an e-mail. Some impacted employees now taking to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Michelle Austin headed up public policy there, tweeting: "Woke up to the news that my time working at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has come to an end. I am heartbroken. I am in denial."\par \par \uc2 Others already are trying to push back through the courts. A federal lawsuit was filed on Thursday with a handful of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees, claiming, the company violated the law by not providing enough warning about the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Musk, in his first week as the owner already have fired top executives, including the CEO and the CFO.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (on camera): And he's introduced a new plan to charge $8 a month for so-called blue checkmark verification.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , should be noted is not the only social media company that's cutting jobs \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 's parent company, letting 20 percent of its staff go. And \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , reportedly planning to reduce staff as well. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Connell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  are condemning threats to use atomic weapons in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Scholz warns Russia risks crossing a line in the international community by restoring to nuclear -- resorting, rather, to nuclear force. Scholz's visit to China is the first by a G7 leaders since the COVID-19 pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll talk live with Republican Colorado Senate nominee Joe O'Dea.\par \par \uc2 First, BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): South Korea scrambles about 80 military aircraft including advanced F-35 fighter jets after tracking about 180 flights by North Korean war plans inside South Korean territory.\par \par \uc2 The North's foreign ministry calls the action an appropriate response to joint military exercises by the U.S. and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Iran marks the 1979 takeover of the U.S. Embassy in Tehran, as its theocracy faces nationwide protests after the September death of a 22-year- old woman in police custody.\par \par \uc2 Iranian state-run television aired live feeds of various commemorations around the country. Iran's president criticized those people taking part in anti-government demonstrations, saying they are stepping in the direction of the country's enemies.\par \par \uc2 And 1000s of revelers returned to Hong Kong Stadium for a highly anticipated international rugby sevens tournament. It's the first such event since the COVID-19 pandemic began. It's part of the government's drive to restore the city's image as a vibrant financial hub after its scrapped mandatory hotel quarantines for travelers.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Back to the midterms now. One of the big races that could determine which party controls the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Let's bring in Colorado Republican Senate nominee Joe O'Dea. He was trying to unseat Democratic incumbent Senator Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 Joe, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, COLORADO: Bret, thanks --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The RealClearPolitics Average in the polls in this race. It split by about a little 4-1/2. But RCP today move Colorado Senate to -- from lean Democrat to tossup. We're still at lean Democrat, but it is closing. What's your closing argument?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, I got to be honest with you. When you're talking about the issues that affect working Americans here in Colorado, that's what's moving the needle. We're on spot.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about record inflation. We're talking about record crime here, gas prices that are over the top, diesel still over $5 here in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 That's what matters to working Americans. And that's why the needle is moving.\par \par \uc2 And we feel, we built a huge coalition. We're marching forward. We're going to get over the finish line here on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we talk -- take a look at candidates final adds just a little sliver of them. This is your closing add, a piece of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Michael Bennet is their yes man. Joe O'Dea is his own man. O'Dea is about action, secure the border, get tough on crime, attack inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Colorado has been elusive for Republicans. It's kind of blue-ish, purple to blue-ish. You think you can change that?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, we've got to build a huge coalition. Its Trump supporters, its GOP supporters, its Independents, and Democrats just came from a rally.\par \par \uc2 I'm up in Steamboat Colorado right now. I had five Democrats walk up to me said they'd never voted for a Republican before. They are voting with us because they know, I'm spot on, in what needs to get done in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to represent working Americans.\par \par \uc2 Michael Bennet has dropped the ball. He's been totally ineffective, hasn't gotten anything done for our state and people recognize that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Here is a Senator Bennet at your last debate with some charges against you. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): This people like Joe O'Dea are getting of -- what the richest people in America, are getting away with murder. They're not paying their taxes.\par \par \uc2 On guns, I just wanted to be very clear. Joe de said he supports no gun laws beyond what we already have as a society. He is against the Red Flag Law we have in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 I don't think Joe O'Dea should be in that hospital room with her when she's got to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking to there about abortion. Now, your response to those charges.\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, you know, he's just making it up as he goes. He's trying to link me to anything he can. He failed to talk about the record inflation that he has caused. He was the deciding vote on the $1.9 trillion rescue plan that sent this economy into a tailspin.\par \par \uc2 I asked him about it and it was crickets. He didn't have any answers. I said, did you regret the spending? Nada. Nothing at all. He doesn't regret it. And if we want six more years of that, that's what we're going to get with Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They have used the abortion issue numerous times. Explain your stance on abortion?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: I've been very clear. For the first five months, I believe that belongs between a woman and her doctor. After that, with the exceptions of rape, incest, life of the mother, medical necessity, that belongs with a woman and her doctor, and I'm going to vote that way. Michael Bennet is extreme. He has been voted for elective abortion up to and including the moment of birth, and he wants to use taxpayer dollars to pay for it. That's extreme.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You had Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  endorse your campaign there. Obviously, the former president has not done that and has said some things. "Slate" writes it this way, "A tight rope, O'Dea's effort to navigate that slim path to victory, though, has been a case study on what it is like to get hammered from both the right and the left and to have to choose every word with the utmost delicacy." Have you been choosing words with delicacy?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Look, I'm a contractor. I'm not a politician. I said what I said, and I'm going to speak my own mind. I am an independent thinker. I'm about Colorado and about working Americans here in Colorado that need a break. They need a break from this record inflation. They need a break from this record crime that Michael Bennet and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have put upon us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is inflation, do you think, the biggest thing that is driving your race, your support?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Working Americans are talking about the cost of groceries. They're talking about the cost of gas, they are talking about the cost of rent. We are just getting our first heating bills. They are up 30 percent here in Colorado. And people can't take much more of that. We have lost 10 percent of our buying power, and the cost of goods is extreme right now. They are having to make tough choices. That's why I'm going to be the next U.S. senator in November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing. Many Democrats, including the president, are closing with the threats to democracy and election denialism. What are your thoughts on that and how effective or ineffective that messaging is?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Look, the only threat to democracy is six more years of Michael bennet here in Colorado backing up \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's plan for our country. It is failing. If you look back 22 months ago, we had $2.38 gas, we had $3.50 diesel, and the economy was booming, wages were growing. There are a lot of people that remember that time, and that's where we are going back to when we replace Michael Bennet and I am your next U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow the race closely. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Bret, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet. Up next, our special midterm panel takes a look at the final weekend before election, and we'll bounce around the country.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 31 in Denver, not far from where Joe O'Dea just was, where police report at least 100 vehicles were involved in a crash this morning after the first snowfall of the season. Only minor injuries reported there.\par \par \uc2 FOX 35 in Orlando as \uc2 NASA\uc2  rolls out its Artemis 1 moon rocket. It will sit on the pad until its next launch attempt scheduled for November 14th. The mission will send an unmanned spacecraft on a month-long orbit around the moon and back.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Seattle from our affiliate FOX 13. One in the big stories there tonight and all over the country, the Powerball jackpot hits an estimated $1.6 billion, with a "b," making it the largest lottery prize in history. That translates to about $782 million if the cash option is taken. I highly recommend the cash option. The game has gone 39 consecutive drawings without a winner. Your next chance is tomorrow night. I'm not pushing it, but you never know.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Four, that's how many days we've got left, four more days. And this is big.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): You can feel it happening. There is so much momentum. Everywhere you go the red wave is washing across Virginia again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They get a little bit more excited as you get closer to Election Day, both sides do. We're going to check in with the different races around the country with reporters who are covering them. Let's bring in our special panel tonight, Jason Rantz, radio talk show host at KTTH in Seattle, Julia Manchester, political reporter for "The Hill," and Chris Ryan, "New Hampshire Today," iHeart Radio host. Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 Jason, I want to start out with you and this Washington Senate race where the RCP average now has the incumbent \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  at 49.3 and the challenger, Smiley, at 46.3, and you can see the bar graph where this race has changed and really, as it comes to the end here, getting tight. And it's hard to believe, Jason, is it real on the ground?\par \par \uc2 JASON RANTZ, TALK RADIO HOST: It's absolutely real, and what is driving this are independent voters. When you're looking at some of the most recent polls, that's where the shifts are. And when you want to win as a Republican statewide in Washington state, you are going have to over perform outside of King County. So King County is where Seattle. You get about 40 percent of the vote, you actually have a shot.\par \par \uc2 However, she's not going to win, Tiffany Smiley, though King County. If she gets about 30, 35 percent and she overperforms everywhere else, that's how she wins. And since she is talking about the issues that particularly on the east side of the state people really care about. We are talking about inflation and the economy along with crime, that's how she is getting this kind of momentum. And there is actual energy on the ground that for the first time in a long time it feels like a Republican can actually win this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's interest. And one thing, Jason. What is the closing argument for each? Is \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  going to threats to democracy and abortion? How is her close?\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yes, \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is talking to the Seattle voter. This is all about abortion, you're going to lose your right to access abortion, and democracy is on the line. And Tiffany Smiley is talking all about getting the crime situation under control and then bringing down the cost of just everyday life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's head to Virginia, and Julia, I tell you, I look at these three House races, and a little inside baseball. On election night, these three House races, Virginia two, Virginia 10 and Virginia seven are going to be indications. This is Spanberger-Vega, this is Wexton-Cao, and this is \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and Jennifer Kiggans. Fair to say that these are the canaries in the coalmine on election night to tell us how things are going?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, "THE HILL": Yes, absolutely, Bret. And starting in the second district, I would say that \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is the most vulnerable Democrat out of these three incumbent Democrats who were first elected in the 2018 blue wave, so they have been in Congress for a while now. \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 's district in Virginia Beach area was redistricted in a way that favors Republicans.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, her opponent Jen Kiggins has really been tapping into this national environment that's favorable to Republicans, so talking about those issues we mentioned before, crime and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Moving up north to the seventh district with Spanberger, I would say Spanberger is a bit safer than Luria. Her district moved closer to Washington, D.C., and further away from Richmond, so more Democratic leaning voters. At the same time, though, she still is considered somewhat vulnerable. We know that Prince William County, for example, is somewhat of a blue stronghold. However, there are rural areas in that neck of the woods that could lean red.\par \par \uc2 And in the tenth district I would say \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  is the safest out of these three. If Hung Cao ousts here, that would be an indication of a very strong red wave. The 10th district is located just outside of Washington D.C. It's blue leaning, was redistricted in a way that favors Democrats as well. So I would say, though, we are looking at these races because Glenn Youngkin performed relatively well for a Republican in some of these districts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have it that Virginia Two goes GOP, it's a decent night, Virginia seven goes, it's a good night, Virginia ten goes to the GOP, it's a really, really good night for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 All right, Chris, let's head out to New Hampshire and the Senate race there, which is another one which has really close in the final days. Don Bolduc catching up to the incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , and that again, on that bar chart, on that graph, rather, is converging right at Election Day. What is the latest on the ground?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: Yes, you are absolutely right. This was the preferred candidate for \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . The DSCC put in money in favor of Bolduc. It helped him over the finish line here in New Hampshire. But Bolduc has been able to close. The granite state is very finicky, particularly when it comes to economic issues, and there is a feeling that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  has tied herself up a little bit too tightly to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And Don Bolduc is more of an independent.\par \par \uc2 He has his negatives, he has his positives, but overall, it feels like this race has tightened. Bolduc does have some momentum. The question is, is he going to be able to get it over the finish line? Is it more that 1.7 percent that you have, or is it more like three percent and is Bolduc a little bit too far off the race? That's what we're going to find out over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 But Bolduc has certainly outperformed. He barely had any money. He has been buoyed by a lot of D.C. third-party groups. But does the incumbent have enough to hang on? It is going to be interesting to see how this breaks down on election night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and very quickly, the congressional race there, Republicans are poised to do well?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: So, in the two congressional races, CD-2 is a little safter, and that one is probably going to go with \uc2 Annie Kuster\uc2 . What we're going to watch really on election night is if this race is really close, the Senate race, that means that Karoline Leavitt has likely won in CD-1. If \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is able to win by three or four percent, that means that the Pappas- Leavitt in CD-1 is going to really close. Leavitt is an individual that has been very firm in her support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and Pappas is your typical kind of moderates Republican -- moderate Democrat who has broken with the president on a number of occasions and is trying to hang on for dear life here in what may be a red wave in the granite state, and, as others are noting, across the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Jason, Chris, Julia, we really appreciate the time. It's great to check in in all these places.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the SPECIAL REPORT all-star panel with the Friday Lightning Round. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Our job is to get the facts to the people, and then let's hope at some point we have a Justice Department that actually holds people accountable for their wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): The subpoenas are coming, that's another part of our commitment to America. And people know that the DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , they have a different set of standards based upon your political affiliation.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: If there are allegations of misconduct by \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees, we want to make sure that we get that information so that we can use the tools that we have to go after that conduct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In what could be a foreshadowing of a GOP House investigation of partisanship at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the House GOP put out a report, "The potential abuse of this power or even the appearance of abuse erodes the fundamental principle of equality under the law and confidence in the rule of law. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 's tremendous power is precisely why the people's elected representatives in Congress must conduct vigorous oversight, particularly in light of allegations of abuse and misconduct made to date."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is senior political correspondent for "Axios," chief legal correspondent, FOX NEWS SUNDAY anchor Shannon Bream, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Hugh, the timing of this is right before the election, but it's still powerful what is in this report, or the allegations against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: It fuels based turnout. I think \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  has done a fine job. I'm one of the people in the minority in the Republican Party who think he has done a fine job. I think President Trump made a good choice. But there are problems at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that people are reminded up by this report and these headline that go back to Peter Strzok and \uc2 Lisa Page\uc2 , that go back to all of the scandals of the Trump era and what is perceived as persecution of the former president that continues. So it's an important report, and it's certainly timed to get people who were very outraged by the Bureau's conduct motivated for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not only what they did, but also what they chose not to do, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, that's exactly right. And it comes up in the context now of this pro-life, pro-choice discussions and whether there have been arrests in the firebombing and arson and vandalism cases versus entire SWAT teams showing up to arrest peaceful pro-life protesters. That's just one example where even though the \uc2 FBI\uc2  statement says we don't follow the politics, and we trust them not to, that's a visualization that Director Wray has to be very aware of and how it plays publicly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, publicly, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is now a private company. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , though, dealing with a lot of turmoil, including firing a lot of people. Reuters says "Musk orders \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to cut infrastructure costs by $1 billion. Steep infrastructure cuts could put the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  website and app at risk to going down during critical events when users are rushing to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to consume and share information such as moments of crisis or major political events." We have a major political event coming up. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  just moments ago saying that 2,000 content moderators were not affected by any of these layoffs tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": There is a lot of overreaction on all sides, but look, I think there is a concern that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  needed be shaken up and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought it for $44 billion. He needed to cut costs. But at the same time there seems to be an impulsive nature to a lot of the moves he made firing almost half the staff within a week of owning the company. There's a lot at play. The advertisers make a bulk of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  revenue, and if they are not going to play ball with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , that's going to be a larger problem for the future of the social media platform.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of companies that are pulling out, Hugh. It's really interesting to watch.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It's extraordinary. We are paying more attentions to changes in the C Suite at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  than we are to the changes in the Israeli government which is a nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: And so it just to me is kind of crazy. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , we were talking in the green room, has done very great things with \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 . He's done great things with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , SpaceLink. Everything he touches turns out OK. I think we will be fine, but really, it's just a company.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So as we get ready for this election, there's a lot of midterm rhetoric, Shannon, a lot of charges that people are trying to heat things up. Here are some Democrats, including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , talking about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Maybe you don't watch television, but if you did, you would see what I see, which are ads about crime every 30 seconds, right? No solutions. They don't care about keeping you safe. They want to keep you scared.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): This country is on track to repeat what happened in Germany when it was the greatest democracy going, elected a chancellor who then co-opted to the media.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, PRESIDENT HISTORIAN: A historian will say what was at stake tonight and this week was the fact of whether we will be a democracy in the future, whether our children will be arrested and conceivably killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, that is scary, the Germany and the killing, that is scary. But you know, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was saying that Republicans are the ones that are making people scared and angry. By the way, you have James Clyburn on this weekend on FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do. We're going to ask him about the comment, because any time that you invoke anything regarding the holocaust, Naziism, you get a lot of pushbacks by people who say it devalues the suffering of millions of people who lost their lives. So this isn't the first time he has used that imagery. So we'll talk with that.\par \par \uc2 He also said this week that if you don't vote the correct way, which is presumably for Democrats, it could be not only the end of the democracy here in the U.S., but the end of the world. So there's definitely some hyperbolic language.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But this threat to democracy close, does it work, do you think?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: No, and it feels like a Hail Mary pass. You hear a lot of this in the final week of an election for the party that's losing, democratically, elections and campaigns, and the fact that --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: High voter turnout in Georgia, midterm early vote is higher than it has ever been.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: The Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in New York state trying to be bail out Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . There is a possibility this is going to be an outright rejection of Democratic leadership, and the talk of democracy is not responsive to the concerns about economic issues, crime, and immigration that are at the top of many voters' lists.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, as we get closer to an election, we go to a place called Candidate Casino. I don't think you have been to the Casino.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have $100 in chips, you have to put them down on control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Where do you go?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I put them all on the Republican side, and I would have been tentative about that two weeks ago. Now I'm putting them all on there. I think Majority Leader McConnell is coming back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Black chip. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Being neutral and partisan, I am going 50-50.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, so good. You're so good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm not much of a gambler. I did bet on Rich Strike at the Kentucky Derby this year, so I feel like all of my good betting karma is used up. So I'm just going to keep it neutral.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, you're excused.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: I still have it at $60 for the Republicans, $40 for the Democrats. Look, the wave is real, the red wave is probably going to sweep the Republicans to a House majority, but these Senate polls are really close.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, winners or losers. Winner first, Hugh.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Winners are McConnell and Kevin McCarthy are going to be in leadership again come the new Congress. The loser is President Biden who can only campaign in deep blue states which is a rejection of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Winner and loser, Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: My \uc2 Liberty University\uc2  football team, the flames have cracked the AP top 25, go Flames. Their one loss is to Wake Forest by one point, that's my winter. Loser, all of us who have to change our clocks again this weekend. Does anybody else hate this? There is a bill that passed in the Senate, it stalled in the House. I call on them in the lame duck session to get this beautiful, bipartisan thing to get done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I agree. Winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Winner, Dusty Baker, the manager of the Astros, one win away from his first World Series after winning more than 2,000 games. He deserves that, even though the Astros did a little cheating a few years ago. Loser Kyrie Irving, the \uc2 Brooklyn Nets\uc2  star who posted an anti-Semitic neo-Nazi like movie on his social media feed. It took him forever to apologize. It's not a good look for the \uc2 NBA\uc2  either, that campaign on social justice a few years ago, and it took them a long time to condemn anti- Semitism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We made it through, panel. The loser is the beautiful sunsets, they go away with the clock shift, unfortunately. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is Friday, and that means it's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is not a referendum. It's a choice. It's a fundamental choice.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Republicans have talked a lot about inflation. They've done nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My campaign has been focused on one issue, and it's inflation, inflation, inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A show of hands, how many here think inflation and the economy is the number one issue in this race? And you are one of them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am one of them, of course.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Is who will fight for you? That's the choice in this election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Somebody would target a political leader's home, target her family.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who would you caucus with?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would not caucus with the Democrats or the Republicans. I'm going to maintain my independence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): They Democratic Party controls the House, the Senate, the White House. Everything is worse than it was 22 months ago.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: How many of you believe that the border is secure in this group?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Show of hands.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: We've really got to get control of this. It's killing our people.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Millions more will cast their ballots in the final days leading up to November the 9th.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If somebody told you as a democrat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is here to help you, you'd jump out the damn window because he is so unpopular.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Isn't November the 8th the Election Day. All right, well, there you go, that's one week.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the final day of campaigning before the crucial midterm elections. And please join Shannon, FOX NEWS SUNDAY this weekend, there are her guests. We talked about them.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221105eib400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Midterm Elections Just Five Days Away\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Pete Hegseth, Tammy Bruce \par \uc2 8,379 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST, "TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT": It's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , ladies and gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I didn't know you were a TikTok fan. I didn't put Tucker --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I've never been on TikTok. Come on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Just checking, just checking.\par \par \uc2 All right. Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 And welcome to "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 All right. Tonight in just five days, you, the American people, make that critical decision -- you will rehire the politicians who kept you out of work, kept your kids out of school, opened borders, emptied prisons, dismantled the police, drove inflation through the roof, a 40-year high, or you can send them packing, your choice.\par \par \uc2 Polls are now showing the possibility of a red wave, but it is not all -- at all certain, so be careful. It's not even close. I'm telling everyone to assume that every single race is that close that you yourself will be the deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 Anything can happen. Turnout will be key. Take nothing for granted and as of now, nearly every major race is either dead even in the polls or within one to three points.\par \par \uc2 Now, that includes the live free or die great state of New Hampshire where Republican Don Bolduc is now up by one in two brand new polls. Straight ahead, General Bolduc will join us, along with probably one of the most popular governors in the entire country, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 . Now, we will also hear from South Carolina senator on this race and others, Tim Scott. And New Mexico, their gubernatorial candidate Mark Ronchetti, who is now polling neck and neck with the state's Democratic incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in just a few moments, we will check in with Kari Lake and Blake Masters together in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Senate race in the great state of Arizona is now tied, according to a new Insider Advantage poll. And I got to imagine it's going to mean something that the libertarian has now bailed out. They were polling around six percent.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, less than a point separates Dr. Oz and, yep, the trust fund baby brat in a hoodie, Fetterman. In Nevada, it's a two-point race. In Georgia, it's less than a point. In North Carolina, it's about five. In Wisconsin, it's anywhere from three to five. Ohio, same thing, between three and five.\par \par \uc2 Let me put it this way. It is likely that less than one hundred thousand votes from all of these states combined will decide the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , and right now, there is no reason to celebrate. These races could go either way.\par \par \uc2 You might remember three states in 2016, combined made the difference, 77,000 votes that put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the top. And in 2020, three states, forty three thousand votes uh made the difference for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . No one can predict what will happen come election day, but I do know the Democrats are running very scared.\par \par \uc2 Now we know tonight, we are witnessing a full-on state of panic on the left. And of course, there was no panic when your gas prices were spiking or when inflation went through the roof was supposedly transitory. They told us it wasn't a big deal and for months they didn't even know that there was a baby formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 They frequently accuse us of overreacting to violent crime, CRT, Afghanistan, the border crisis, the fentanyl crisis. And yet, they are totally unbothered by any of the very real pain and suffering that the American people are now facing. Seventy percent of Americans fully are living paycheck to paycheck, barely making ends meet. Many of those people are not make making ends meet and just for bare necessities, they're spending on their credit card.\par \par \uc2 And only now faced with a very real possibility of losing an election Democrats are now claiming that the sky is falling. Democracy is in peril. America itself is doomed, high in their Ivory Tower. Liberals seem to believe that a Republican majority in the House or the Senate is the greatest threat known to man. And by the way, the only way to save democracy is to vote for them again, and give them more power, give them power and perpetuity. One party rule forever and ever amen.\par \par \uc2 Does that sound like democracy to you? Doesn't to me.\par \par \uc2 Right on cue, yesterday, Biden declaring that the mega MAGA Republicans are a threat to the republic. And today, his brainless chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  referred to the speech as Joe's final warning to the country. I hope you're watching.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: The president decided a few days ago that it was important to issue one final warning on this issue, to make very clear to leave no doubt that we have people out there still peddling the big lie, people now raising the issue of election denial in this election and, of course, the horrible incident involving Speaker Pelosi and her family, you know, showing the rising threat of political violence.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think all those things came together and led the president make the strong remarks that he made last night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, that Stacy Abrams, needs to stop that. I agree.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, final warning, the rise of political violence because of Republicans? Now, we have been consistent we condemn all violence. We condemned it in real time on January 6th. We condemned it all through the summer of 2020, 574 riots, dozens of dead Americans, thousands of cops injured.\par \par \uc2 We have another example. A Republican House candidate in North Carolina, he just had his house shot up. Look at that, right there. Bullets right into his home after his Democratic opponent featured the house in a campaign ad.\par \par \uc2 And last night, someone tried to accost General Bolduc as he was walking into his own debate.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the campaign, a man tried, as we showed you, to stab \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  if he didn't act quickly. He was going right for his carotid, on his neck, on stage.\par \par \uc2 A man assaulted \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  and broke six of his ribs.\par \par \uc2 A deranged \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 /Rachel Maddow superfan shot up a baseball field, nearly killed Steve Scalise.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, unlike the Democrats, I don't blame \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . I don't blame the Democratic Party for any of it. But political violence is not this one-sided Republican issue. There are psychos in both parties and it's shameful the Democrats are smearing conservatives for, you know, a cheap political win because they can't run on any issues that they have been successful at.\par \par \uc2 Instead of condemning all violence, nope, they're just going to blame Republicans. Unfortunately, many on the left have totally lost touch with reality. If you want to a case in point, just listen to the official presidential historian for NBC News, Chicken Little himself. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, MSNBC GUEST: A historian 50 years from now, if historians are allowed to write in this country, and if there are still free publishing houses and a free press which I'm not certain of, but if that is true, a historian will say what was at stake tonight and this week was the fact, whether we will be a democracy in the future, whether our children will be arrested and conceivably killed, we're on the edge of a brutal authoritarian system, and it could be a week away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's just trembling with fear from his D.C. mansion. He sees January around every turn. He can't possibly fathom why voters are worried about inflation, gas prices, violent crime, open borders, and the like, woke education. Kids aren't learning to read, write, do math, science, history and, of course, computers.\par \par \uc2 And Chicken Little so painfully detached from reality that it seems to be rotting his brain. He's not alone. So-called journalists across the country now believe that the end is near, these predictions of a Republican win means it's life as we know it is over. Take a look..\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST: Democracy is in danger all over the world, here in the United States as well, but it's all over the world, Secretary Clinton.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: They're pushing out all of these polls, this sort of muddy up the averages and sort of create this narrative of a red wave. If it doesn't happen, aren't we going to be in more danger of violence because they've created a narrative and if it doesn't happen they're going to say the whole election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What the president said tonight is that there is a serious chance that if this doesn't go well, we don't have democracy anymore.\par \par \uc2 ZOLAN KANNO-YOUNG, NEW YORK TIMES: For the president and the White House and many Americans across the country, there is still this concern about the threat to democracy on multiple fronts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think what the president did last night is motivate a lot of Democratic base voters who I think quite rightly are worried about the existence of our democracy if these Trumpy election liars get in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Try and keep up, try and keep track with this. According to Democrats, if the GOP wins an election, democracy will be over. Cities will burn, people will die and then Republicans will abolish Medicare and Social Security, and they will defund the V.A. just for good measure. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They want a gut Social Security. And Medicare and then giver me some more tax breaks to the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Every year, every single year, it should be in the chopping block, along with veterans benefits and everything else in federal budget. Congress doesn't vote affirmatively, keep it, it goes away, gone. You've been paying in Social Security your whole life, you earned it. Now, these guys want to take it away. Who in the hell do they think they are? Excuse my language.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: So, if you're worried about the economy, cost of living, getting by right now, think of what it will be like if they go back to higher prescription drug prices, undo what the Democrats did, go after Social Security and Medicare to literally pull it up by the roots. I mean, those are serious threats to anybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'll go back to GDP of, what, one point -- gross domestic product of 1.4 percent. Not 8.5, a 40-year high.\par \par \uc2 I'll go back to two dollar gallon gasoline. I'll go back to secure borders, and I'll go back to energy independence any day, I'd love to do that.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, what they're doing here, Obama, Biden, Hillary, they're making it all up. It is all desperate fear-mongering. It's lying. It's manipulating the elderly and the sick for votes. That shows how desperate Democrats really are.\par \par \uc2 This big lie that Republicans are going to cut Social Security and Medicare, they roll it out every single election. Republicans are racist, sexist, homophobic, xenophobic, Islamophobic, transphobic, their plan, Obama said, is for dirty air and water. And now, they want to take away grandma and grandpa's Social Security. And throw them over a cliff in a wheelchair.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they've been using this lie now for decades, yet Republicans have never laid a finger on Medicare or Social Security, not one Republican has said they would and it's clear Democrats have nothing positive to run on. Their agenda failed miserably. They have not no positive story to tell, both at home and abroad, and Americans are now worse off by every single measure.\par \par \uc2 And yet, they're demanding that you reward them, and you reward their lies, and you reward their failures and you reward their hysteria, and vitriol, and it's all delusional. But thankfully, in five days, you will have the power to send them a message, right at the polls, they all need to be fired.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here now with reaction, New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  is with us, along with New Hampshire Senate candidate, Republican, General Don Bolduc is with us.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both. Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 General, first of all, a guy took a swipe at you last night. I know you served, what, 10 tours of duty, I believe, in Afghanistan. You have two Purple Hearts, five Medals of Valor, you're a true American hero. I'm sure getting, you know, somebody taking a swing at you is not on you usual to you, not comfortable though on the other hand.\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE FROM NEW HAMPSHIRE: Well, thank you, Sean. It's great to be on tonight, and my best to you viewers.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, I mean, it -- it's just one of those things that, you know, you have to guard against all the time. I'm thankful for the police, being there to respond quickly, certainly had some supporters that responded quickly. But, you know, this guy identified himself quickly. We all kept a good eye on him and I was able to react in plenty of time, so that, you know, nothing happened and the police reacted in plenty of time and the gentleman was arrested and removed from the scene.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, you did a great job during the debate.\par \par \uc2 Governor Sununu, you know, you're one of the most popular governors, Republican governors in the country. I mean, you're leading your race by 18 points. You have to go up for re-election every two years. I know your dad is probably very proud of you, as he should be.\par \par \uc2 And the reason I would argue you're so successful is you are a simple common sense conservative. You know, when I listen to you speak, it doesn't get complicated. You know, you're there to serve the people in New Hampshire and they appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE: Yeah, look, politics isn't hard. It's just that we have so many entrenched politicians that frankly just try to -- try to convince something of someone they're not. They don't contact -- connect with folks on a very genuine level. But here in New Hampshire, that's what we're all about. We're about that kind of grassroots, grass tops connection.\par \par \uc2 We're about local control, local communities, limited government. That's why I think General Bolduc is frankly doing so well. You have a someone right here that has done what like 70, 80 different town halls, always willing to talk about any issue to anyone.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn't mean we're trying to convince folks that it's our policy or the highway, it's just making sure that we connect with folks. That we listen with an open ear, understand their issues.\par \par \uc2 And General Bolduc's running against, again, an entrenched politician like \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  who doesn't do any town halls, who doesn't connect with anybody, who's been really absent for the last five and a half years through the Senate. Now, she's spending 50 million bucks trying to convince folks she's been here.\par \par \uc2 So, it's really -- it's just what New Hampshire is about and I think that's why the general is having so much success. Just be yourself, be normal, it ain't tricky.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Governor, I look at your policies and I watched the debate last night.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, General Bolduc, I thought you won that debate hands down. I thought you did a terrific job, and I was watching a very polished, very seasoned politician that wouldn't even answer a simple question. Do you have any restrictions on abortion? The answer was obviously no.\par \par \uc2 And I was thinking about your policies, Governor, in particular and what is so successful, what's made you so popular, there's not a single thing that I heard from Senator Hassan last night that matches up to what you're doing that's working so well in New Hampshire, in the Granite State. I can't imagine why the people would elect you by 18 points, 20 points, and then also elect her, because I didn't -- the senator agrees with nothing you stand for.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. Look, that's why it's not going to happen. I mean, we're going to have a lot of success here in New Hampshire on Tuesday, the Democrats -- again, their entire campaign, if you look at what they've tried to push now just with me but with the general, it's all negative. They're not about anything, but that's what you have to be.\par \par \uc2 Look, in New Hampshire, more than anywhere else, we got to look in the eye we got to buy off on you as a person first, then we'll get to the policy, and that's why those town halls the general does is so important.\par \par \uc2 That's why him always being out there 24/7 for the last year, doesn't mean we agree on every single point, but he's connecting with folks, and he's listening to them. People want to know that as individuals, they're going to be treated as individuals. Not about big government, not about a big system. It sure as heck ain't about us. It's really about those individuals and kind of creating that opportunity for them.\par \par \uc2 And that's exactly what the general is going to bring to Washington. Beholden to nobody, shake things up, we all want Washington to change, even Democrats want Washington to change, fundamentally. But at the end of the day, you can't keep sending the same people back and expect something to be different.\par \par \uc2 I think that's where you're going to see change all across this country. It's going to be a good week next week.\par \par \uc2 You know, we say that general that being a politician, you're supposed to be a public servant. Now, you've served this country for over 30 years. I mentioned two Purple Hearts, five Medals of Valor, you served your country.\par \par \uc2 And I listened to Senator Hassan last night, referred to all the positions that you took and the things that you were advocating, and you said, I never voted on any of these because I wasn't there. You were the one that was -- that was there voting with Biden and Schumer 98 percent of the time. She was pretty much a rubber stamp.\par \par \uc2 I thought that was a pretty effective tactic of yours. What do you say to the people of New Hampshire tonight now having the great support of a very popular governor. I think is very powerful for you. I thought your message was common sense conservatism.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: Well, I'm thankful to have the governor's support, and I'm thankful to have his leadership in the Republican Party here in the in New Hampshire. He has unified the party, and that's really what's scaring the Democrats here is a unified Republican Party. And, you know, he has managed to support the ticket from the bottom of the top and the top to the bottom.\par \par \uc2 And that is what's going to make the difference, a unified Republican Party. And I want to thank him for doing that, because it allows me on the ground to be more effective. You know, his ability to resonate with the political electorate, you know, from Republican, Democrat, independent, a libertarian free stater is very powerful, and that --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me --\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: -- that enables us as well.\par \par \uc2 But the point that he makes about being yourself and getting out there on the ground, two years, you know, 78 to be exact, we did two today, town halls, \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  and Mercedes Schlapp were here, you know, participating.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's amazing.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: This was awesome.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, let me ask this --\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: And this is the way it has to be done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I read, Governor, in "The Wall Street Journal" and the "Epoch Times" that there might be a literal blackout this winter in all of New England because they don't have enough natural gas available to power the grid, also I read that people can expect their heating bills to go up a minimum of 30 percent be it gas or oil. Is that what New Hampshire is facing?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Without a doubt. Unfortunately, all in New England is tied to this energy grid and so some of the bad -- some of the bad decisions by not just our neighboring states but specifically out of Washington, normally when demand goes up on a really cold couple weeks in January, your production goes up out of the Marcella shale. All our natural gas comes through New York, it all gets distributed across New England.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, now, there's no incentive to increase production. There's actually disincentive to do that. We're not tapping into our own resources. Heating oil is a huge deal for us, and people are now paying 1,300 bucks a month just to put heating oil. There's a lack of kerosene that's out there. There's a lack of CDL drivers because the new federal regulations on CDL drivers.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it's a lack of materials. It's the inflationary cost and it again it just baffles me that -- that they still keep going back to this stuff from a couple years ago and what happened in 2020. Voters look forward, voters vote in their family's interest, as they should, and that's exactly why inflation and the cost of fuel and the cost of heating and frankly the realistic fear that people have about what may come in a cold winter in New England, that's going to drive the vote this Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I couldn't agree more.\par \par \uc2 General Bolduc, I think it's great that you have Governor Sununa's support. Governor, we appreciate you and you being on the program.\par \par \uc2 And, General, we're watching your race. I think a very winnable race, and it would be a benefit to the governor and the whole state of New Hampshire to -- well, send Senator Hassan back home to New Hampshire or maybe she can learn a lesson or two and come back with more independence from the Democratic Party. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Senator Tim Scott will join us after the break.\par \par \uc2 Also today, well, "The View" compared female Republican voters to cockroaches. We've got the tape.\par \par \uc2 We also have the great one, Mark Levin. He's fired up about these midterms and five short days, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I've been trying to tell you I can't convey through words the real urgency that I feel the midterms and what it means for the country in five days.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here now with reaction, South Carolina senator, our friend Tim Scott. He's done a phenomenal job. He's traveled all over the country on behalf of Republican candidates everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you back, Senator. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here we are five days to go, closing arguments, Democrats are desperate. They say all Republicans want to cut Social Security, Medicare, V.A. benefits, it's all lie. There's not one person that's advocating that and they seem desperate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I smell desperation in the air.\par \par \uc2 Sean, you're 100 percent right. What you said during the monologue is so important. It's crystal clear. If we are to win the majority in the Senate, it takes every single voter in a battleground state, who is sick and tired of being sick and tired. Republicans, Democrats and independents to show up and vote for the Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 Remember this, Sean, in the state of Georgia, January 5th, 2021, the loss of a single Senate seat cost the American taxpayers more than trillion dollars. One seat, that seat could be held tomorrow as we get through this election by General Don Bolduc, a true American patriot, winning in New Hampshire, might be the state that takes us across and gives us a majority. In Georgia, North Carolina, Ohio, Wisconsin, we have to win, Nevada, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Every single voter, hear me, clearly your vote matters today more than it has ever mattered. We must finish this race. Don't run to the finish line, run through the finish line. There's too much at stake.\par \par \uc2 I understand the power of American dream.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Don't forget Pennsylvania. Don't forget the state of Washington, either, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Washington, Tiffany Smiley, I spoke with her day before yesterday. She is closing the gap. We're sending more resources her way because we know that the red wave is coming, but it doesn't have an all by itself. We have to make sure from sea to shining sea, we show up get our voters out and make sure that you call your friends. If you don't have one, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says make one today. We need every single voter showing up, showing out and we will have a majority in the Senate. We'll have a 25-seat majority in the House and we will restore sanity to the greatest nation on God's green earth.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If Republicans win, Senator, you have been one of the most powerful advocates for so many people, a big part of their success will be because of your hard work and efforts traveling around the country.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott, we always appreciate you being on. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, earlier today, on that hard-hitting news show "The View", co-host Sunny Hostin literally compared suburban women voting Republican to roaches. Okay, take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, CO-HOST, THE VIEW: But what's also surprising to me is the abortion issue. I read a poll just yesterday that white Republican suburban women are now going to vote Republican.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why?\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: It's almost like roaches voting for Raid, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do we love democracy or not? Because just saying that, it's insulting to the voter. People make up decisions on what's right for their family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If you're a white Republican suburban woman and you're voting Republican, you're a roach.\par \par \uc2 "FOX and Friends Weekend" co-host Pete Hegseth joins us, along with FOX News contributor Tammy Bruce.\par \par \uc2 I don't know, Tammy, consider me old-fashioned, I don't think comparing women to roaches is a particularly kind, nice, or politically correct thing to do.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, it wasn't, and it was more than insulting. Look, we've seen this is the Democrat closing argument. It is shocking. It's not surprising. It starts with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  declaring half of America fascist or bigots, or racist or threats to democracy, whether it was Philadelphia or at Union Station.\par \par \uc2 There's no conversation about the issues most important to the voters, like the economy, like crime, those things don't matter. And now, beyond being called fascists and threats to democracy, you know, groups that effectively over the last century have killed a hundred million human beings, but now, we're insects. Now that also has a horrible history politically when you begin to not just demonize people, but cast them as sub-human. It makes it easier and even encouraging to hurt them.\par \par \uc2 And this, Sean, from the party and the people complaining about rhetoric from Republicans that's encouraging violence. There's only one group in this country that is putting out rhetoric that that demonizes and suggests people are sub-human who disagree with them politically, and that's the Democrats. I believe multiple examples of Republicans and young conservatives being attacked.\par \par \uc2 When you begin -- when you move into a direction of saying, not even suggesting that women who simply think for themselves are insects, that goes too far. But this is what the Democrats have revealed themselves.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Not only that. I mean, if it's like calling somebody a rat. If you're going to be a bug, you could be a beautiful butterfly or you know, if you call me Hegseth roach, I mean, it's probably pretty well-deserved.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, CO-HOST, FOX AND FRIENDS WEEKEND: Not wrong.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Probably not wrong, yeah, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Pete, in all seriousness though, you know if you said that, I said that, we'd be excoriated for weeks. It would be never ending. It'd be calls for boycotts, calls for firing, it would never end. And yet, Democrats seem to get away with everything.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Well, they're on the same team with the people that they're attempting to elect and the media are an extension of that. We have no -- we have no illusions about that at this point.\par \par \uc2 You could play the game of imagine, if it was -- if it was any other race or any other segment. You can also play the game of any other network, and you could also play the game of in reality, the characterization that suburban women, white women might care about something other than abortion? They -- these women of "The View" live in a bubble. They live in a bubble, on their set in Manhattan, where this is the only issue and they agree with everything \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in Union Station about threats to democracy and January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, suburban women like women of all shades in all places care about the safety of their kids, the education of their kids, whether or not people are here illegally. This is a loser, silly closing argument because they have nothing else to run on. So they're demagoguing the other side in ways we could never get away with and it won't work.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I agree. Well said.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: It's also who they are. This is what they are. This is what they think of people exactly, and they will pay a price, Sean, on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. Five days, we'll know, Tammy. Thank you. Thank you, Pete Hegseth.\par \par \uc2 Here now with more on the midterms, the host of the number one show on weekends right here on the FOX News Channel, "Life, \uc2 Liberty\uc2 , and Levin". I call him the great one. He's also nationally syndicated radio host and number one best-selling "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " author. Thank me, God bless us, how am I?\par \par \uc2 How are we, Mark? We got five days to go. I pointed out at the beginning of this segment or in my monologue tonight that probably when you look at Georgia and North Carolina and New Hampshire and Pennsylvania and Ohio and Wisconsin and Nevada and Arizona and all of these Senate races, they're either even or there's a one or two point margin. That probably all of those states combined will be decided by less than a hundred thousand people.\par \par \uc2 And then there are people all over the place saying red wave tsunami, red wave tsunami, it's possible for sure. There's no doubt about it. The Democrats have destroyed the country. However, I'm not counting on until the votes come in.\par \par \uc2 MARK LEVIN, "LIFE, LIBERTY & LEVIN" HOST: Let's all pretend we're 10 points behind everywhere across the country. That's what we ought to do, whether we are or not, it doesn't much matter to me. But we got to get our ass in gear and defeat these people or they're going to destroy the country.\par \par \uc2 This is the biggest lineup of Democrat party reprobates who want to serve in the Senate and the House that I've ever seen in my life. The worst, most extreme hate America crowd you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 You look at these states, you look at Wisconsin, you look at -- you look at North Carolina, you look at Georgia. These are radical, almost Marxist type candidates.\par \par \uc2 You look at all these other races as a matter of fact and I would say this to the American people, do you like being called names by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his party? You ever have a president of the United States who's trashing the American people? Lincoln, I don't think so. John Kennedy, Reagan, Trump, you actually have this guy \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who's been a damnable Washington bureaucrat his entire life on our payroll and the communist Chinese payroll, who hasn't worked a real job in the private sector in his entire life. And that goes for Pelosi and Schumer, too.\par \par \uc2 And they are inflation deniers. And they are gas price increase deniers. And they are open border deniers. And they are fentanyl deniers, and they are massive debt deniers, and they are -- well, they're destroying our economy.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, they're desperate. They want you to actually believe that if you vote for these wild crazy rightist Republicans who are well-known for overthrowing the country, who are well known for burning down cities who are well known for attacking cops and the military, who are well-known for burning the American flag and the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution, that we will not have our country the day after the election. Really? How about we bet $10 million on that right now, Joe? You got enough money from the communist Chinese. I'll bet you $10 million right now, that will have a democracy, actually a republic, the day after the election.\par \par \uc2 And let me say something else to the Washington establishment, including McConnell, you trashed our candidates before. I have never been so proud and excited about the Republican candidates ever. We have citizen candidates from every corner of this country, people who are cared about -- who care about working people in this country, with all kinds of backgrounds, all kinds of races, all kinds of faiths, coming together uniting to save this country and defeat the one force that is destroying our classrooms, destroying our currency, destroying our homes and our families, destroying our economy, destroying people's ability to get fuel and that is the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat Party did in 20 months what the communist Chinese in China haven't been able to do in 70 years, what the Russians haven't even been able to do since 1917. They did it in 20 months.\par \par \uc2 And to speak as Reagan would speak, are you better off today than you were two years ago? The answer is yes, you're not. Or has Trump would say, what do you have to lose? You're going to vote for these people some more?\par \par \uc2 And so, I would say Republicans unite. I would say to independents, help us. I would say to Democrats, wash this dirt off your hands and join us. This is election between those who love America and those who hate America.\par \par \uc2 We have a man in the Oval Office, every speech he gives, he's cringing, he's grinding his teeth, he's angry and he's angry at you. Long past the days of when Carter talked about malaise in effect. This guy's literally attacking the American people because he's afraid that they won't vote Democrat.\par \par \uc2 This Democrat party is ideologically crazy. It is utterly corrupt and so are the media that represent it. They're the ones that have put their boot on the throat of the energy industry. We were energy independent, something we've sought to be for years. They're the ones harming our farmers. They're the ones making food prices go up and gasoline prices go up.\par \par \uc2 You're going to have a hell of a winter in parts of this country that are cold. Thanks to the Democrat Party. They have nothing to say except abort our babies, abort our babies, abort our babies.\par \par \uc2 They are obsessed. They are radical and they are extreme. That's it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Mark Levin, the great one. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. We appreciate you being with us.\par \par \uc2 We're going out to the state of New Mexico where Republican gubernatorial candidate Mark Ronchetti is now in a razor thin race with pro-criminal Democrat, Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , who has refused to take the issue of rising crime seriously and New Mexico voters are taking notice.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, Republican gubernatorial candidate in New Mexico, Mark Ronchetti, is with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm looking at your candidate's record and all I can see is just like Fetterman, just like Mandela Barnes, just like most Democrats running, you got a radical, leftist, climate alarmist, Biden-Schumer rubber stamp radical Democrat running. And that's probably why it's so close.\par \par \uc2 Do you think in your blue state in New Mexico, you can win this race?\par \par \uc2 MARK RONCHETTI (R), NEW MEXICO GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: There's no doubt we can, Sean. And what we're seeing in this is exactly what you talked about. When we went into this campaign, we had a governor who was still releasing people early under COVID orders from prison. Now, our prison population, 26 percent lower than it was when she took office here. Meanwhile, violent crimes gone up 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 And the people of this state have had enough, and they -- what we see here is we're the second most violent state in the country right now.\par \par \uc2 We have soft on crime judges which she's appointed and we have a soft on crime parole board and so as we continue to see crime spike, that's a huge issue. You've been talking about the inflation issue here. It's a massive problem here in New Mexico. The average New Mexican makes $30,000 a year, and we're seeing prices spike here. They're not talking about it at all, bad energy policy, hurts a state like us, and we're the number two energy producer in the country.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're seeing people more and more realizing that this isn't about Republican and Democrat. This is about looking out for New Mexicans and realizing at this point we're on a path that is hurting working families and hurting the safety of the families across New Mexico, and that's why we've got so much momentum. It's why we've closed the gap and it's why we're going to win on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 So, anti-domestic energy production, part of the climate alarmist cult, anti-second amendment, pro-drug legalization. She herself had her own little ethics problem, a groping incident witnessed by many people. She ended up paying some money to settle in that case.\par \par \uc2 On abortion, no restrictions. On the 2020 protest, she said they're not going to use COVID as a reason to prevent protests, but they can't use it as an excuse for political organizing, instead of criticizing the violence. Is she out of touch with the people in New Mexico?\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: Sean, when we stood on the debate stage and we went back and forth, it was very apparent how out of touch she is, and not only from the crime aspect, from the personal finance aspect.\par \par \uc2 And then education, Sean, don't forget that. We're 51st in education. Our kids stayed out of school, some of the longest duration times of any children in the country, and there's been --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How can there being 51st?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: -- to catch them up.\par \par \uc2 So we're seeing people across the country talking about these shutdowns. We're here in New Mexico, they were even worse and we're 51st in education. No plan to catch kids up.\par \par \uc2 We have a plan to catch kids up. We're going to do that. We're going to put more money in people's pockets, and we're also going to end catch and release, and the sanctuary state policies, along with making sure that we secure our border, Sean.\par \par \uc2 We have a major fentanyl problem here. We lose one New Mexican a day to drug overdose.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask --\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: It hasn't been addressed. And we will absolutely address it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. What, the 51st, what are we, including D.C. in that?\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: Yeah, we're adding D.C. into that, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK.\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: And just one other number that's staggering, 18 percent of our kids can read to grade level, 13 percent can do math. We need extra learning time. We've got to catch them up.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Sean, we're seeing the \uc2 DGA\uc2  pour more money into this race than just about any other race, but we have a lot of help from people on the ground here. And we love the help as well, markronchetti.com/donate. We have the small dollar donors here that helped us across the country. We appreciate it and we're going to win it, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hope so. We're following it closely. That would be a big win, out in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead, Obama -- he was in Arizona today campaigning against Kari Lake and Blake Masters. Both of them join us next, as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We head now to the state of Arizona. Republican gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake and Senate candidate Blake Masters, they are continuing to put Democrats into panic mode. And there he was, the anointed one, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , out in Arizona, pretty angry on the campaign trail this time, campaigning against them earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake. She has a pretty big lead over her opponent.\par \par \uc2 And also, Blake Masters, who's got some great news this week. Not only did the libertarian that was running for the Senate in Arizona drop out of the race, but that libertarian candidate endorsed Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Kari, let me start with you. I'm sure you're all upset that Barack Obama went out to campaign against you.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Oh, first, it was \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  last week, and I just laughed that off. And now, I'm living rent- free in \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's mind. And then \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 But I was a little concerned today, I'm going to be honest, when I saw Hillary Clinton bad mouthing me. And she looked -- she looked angry and actually scared.\par \par \uc2 And just completely unrelated, I want you to know, just in case you're wondering, I'm in perfect health. My brakes on my car are in good shape. And I'm not suicidal.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to win this thing on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think one of the things that has endeared you to people of Arizona and around the country, you have a great sense of humor, and you have a good come back for everything.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters, this is big news for you because, look, in the polls that I was seeing, it was 4, 5, 6 percent, the libertarian was drawing. Those are votes against Mark Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Mark Kelly is a rubber stamp for all things \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's been on the forefront of supporting every bit of the agenda that they are advocating, and he hasn't lifted a finger to secure the border in your state.\par \par \uc2 I would say now that you are the odds-on favorite because of this endorsement. This is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: It is a big deal, Sean. And I'm honored that the libertarian candidate, I think he graciously bowed out, honored to have his endorsement. It's a good boost of momentum here. And we've got all of the momentum in this race.\par \par \uc2 Mark Kelly, he is floundering. He is desperate. I think \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is desperate, too.\par \par \uc2 They're so desperate, they don't want to talk about their own ideas. They don't want to talk about their own track records.\par \par \uc2 So, what do they do? They dust off \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . They drag this guy out of retirement to come and distract everybody, right? He talks about how we're dangerous --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think the reason he's angry is because he likes his schedule. He doesn't feel like working every day. You know, he is mad he has to get up and do the work because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can't do it. And nobody wants --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: He barely showed up. They almost filled a high school gymnasium. They almost filled a high school gym.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was low energy. It was pathetic. You know, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Kari, you said something that you take up a lot of space in people's heads. It's that a lot of politicians are afraid to speak their mind. You're not. And that I think has defined your race because I think that people in Arizona find it refreshing frankly.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Yeah. I think people, Sean, are tired of being silenced by the news, tired of being silenced by big tech, tired of worrying about if they speak common sense, they're going to be cancelled. And I'm seeing a lot of people who are just saying, this is the time for us to stand up and speak out. If we don't, it's going to be too late.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we're seeing in our polling, the Latinos are coming our way. The soccer moms are coming our way. The suburban moms are coming our way because they're worried about real issues like crime, the wide open border, the fentanyl crisis, the brainwashing of their kids in school.\par \par \uc2 And they realize this is the election when we can finally solve these problems.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, one of the things I noticed when I was out in Arizona with both of you is that, you know, you're very much like a team. Kari, what you're going to do on the state level, and, Blake, what you'll do in Washington really will complement, you know, service for people of Arizona. You don't see that in every state.\par \par \uc2 We just had -- we just saw with Governor Sununu and General Bolduc, who's now leading in that race, which is a surprise for a lot of people. But if you win your race, if Bolduc wins, if Herschel wins, if Oz wins -- I mean, this could be a blowout. But yet, they're all very close races.\par \par \uc2 Blake, what's your take?\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: You know, I think we could easily end up with 54 Senate seats here, if not more.\par \par \uc2 And it is important, right? When I'm in the Senate, I'm going to be working with Governor Kari Lake. You know, Kari Lake is going to pull power back from Washington, D.C. to the state. Well, I'm going to help push that power back from D.C. to the states.\par \par \uc2 We have to work together. It helps that we're friends, it helps that we're running on this America First agenda, right?\par \par \uc2 And our opponents are so bad. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is probably the worst candidate in the country.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: She's really --\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Second maybe only to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: And Mark Kelly is right there with him. I mean, this guy votes in lock step for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's agenda. What kind of guy promises the good people of Arizona to be an independent only to get to D.C. and surrender to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and vote for open borders and fentanyl and 300,000 illegals coming through every month?\par \par \uc2 Sorry, this guy needs to be fired. And that's what we're doing next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You get the last 20 second -- Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, I -- I think we have a real opportunity, Arizona, to get things on track here. Everyone wants a secure border, everybody wants safe streets. And they know I'm working for the people of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Sean, for having us on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's great to have you. This election is simple, it's about inflation, it's about record high gas prices, open borders. It's about defund, dismantle, no bail laws, safety and security for the American people. It's about woke education and reading, writing, math, science, history and computers. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 Blake, thank you. Kari, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A lot more "Hannity" straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening. As always, thank you for being with us. We hope you'll set your DVR so you never, ever, ever miss an episode of "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 Don't forget, for news anytime, every time, all the time, foxnews.com, hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 Uh-huh, I am getting a heads up in my ear. Something is going on. Laura is up to something.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled. "The Ingraham Angle" is now.\par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020221104eib300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Joe Biden Stumps For Candidates In New Mexico And California; GOP Nominee Blames \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  For Political Division; Close Races Predicted In Nevada; Sen. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  (R-FL) Closing Argument Ahead Of Election Day; Centrist Group Explores Bipartisan Presidential Ticket; Florida Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  Interviewed On His Campaign Against Incumbent Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  And Political Effectiveness Of Abortion Issue; U.S. And South Korea Defense Delegations Meet As North Korea Sends Dozens Of Missiles Toward South Korea In Recent Days; President Biden Gives Speech Claiming Republican Party Poses Threat To American Democracy\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Alexandria Hoff, Hillary Vaughn, Jacqui  Heinrich, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 8,347 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's the mimosa maker, you top off your bubbly just enough O.J. to taste good but still get drunk.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's the best idea ever.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you can start this on November 6th, and tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME", I go to war with my alma mater. Just a regular Thursday night show.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Why didn't you get that Tropicana thing for free?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You know, I really could. I blew it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Bret, how you doing?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You should have mimosas with the barbecue, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Just like that weekend I spent with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, moving on. See you, good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We're coming to you live tonight from Miami, the largest metropolitan area and one of the most important battleground states in the country, of course a crucial \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race and an important governor's contest just five days from conclusion.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with gubernatorial Democratic nominee \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and Republican incumbent Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  in just a few minutes. We asked, we should say Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings to come on as well. They declined.\par \par \uc2 First up tonight though, President Biden is taking part in a rally for a New Mexico governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  and other Democrats in Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 Later tonight, he'll attend an event for a congressional candidate in San Diego. The president is trying to frame the election as crucial to the future of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. President Biden is suggesting the American experiment could fail if certain Republican candidates are elected. And despite that heavy warning, he is also campaigning in New Mexico trying to remind young people that he's the one trying to cancel some student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Remember, this is not a referendum, this is a choice.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Biden is backing the incumbent New Mexico governor once believed to be a V.P. shortlister \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , fending off a challenge from Republican Mark Ronchetti.\par \par \uc2 MARK RONCHETTI (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF NEW MEXICO: He's not here to discuss the biggest issues that New Mexicans care about. And that is skyrocketing prices, a border where we have more and more fentanyl pumping into the state right now and it is driving our violent crime.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Crime, something this White House doesn't consider their problem.\par \par \uc2 KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS, WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER: This uptick in crime was experienced in 2020. This is when this began and the American people know who the president was at that time.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Trump may not be on the ballot this cycle. But Biden officials say they're worried Trump like challenges to election results could up end the entire system.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, CHIEF OF STAFF, WHITE HOUSE: I think the president decided a few days ago that it was important to issue one final warning on this issue.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Democrats haven't always been this concerned about election denying Republicans like this summer when the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee tried to help one win, Michigan's third district, John Gibbs, believing he'd be easier for Democrats to beat than the more moderate Congressman Peter Meijer.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Other people say we're kind of funding their campaigns, it's ridiculous. We're attacking them. But you are absolutely right. We thought he was an easier candidate.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As President Biden focuses on the elections as a whole.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We must vote. Knowing what's at stake and not just the policy of the moment.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is making battleground specific pitches.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Democracy as we know it may not survive in Arizona. That's not an exaggeration. That is a fact.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: \uc2 Obama\uc2  is drawing bigger crowds this cycle. But Biden remains the leader of the party.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our best days are really ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Our best days were in danger of disappearing just last night, along with certain freedoms and the fate and soul of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, we're seeing President Biden trying to strike a balance, talking about both threats to democracy and the pluses of Democratic Party policy, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks. We'll talk about both of those things with those candidates later in this show.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today ahead of tomorrow's October jobs report. The Dow lost 146-1/2, the S&P 500 fell 40, the NASDAQ dropped 182.\par \par \uc2 Get ready for that music a lot. The New Hampshire Senate race could determine the balance of power in that chamber following the election. There was a different kind of power on display last night as the Republican nominee saw firsthand just how seriously some people are taking the contest.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Hillary Vaughn is in Kingston, New Hampshire tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD BOLDUC (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE NEW HAMPSHIRE: Yesterday we had a little incident outside of the debate hall.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): With just five days left until this year's midterm elections, candidates are targeting swing voters. But last night, Republican New Hampshire Senate candidate Don Bolduc says someone tried to take a swing at him.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: He charge from his side of the sidewalk and he made a slight contact with me, I'd call it a graze as a result.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: After Bolduc says the activist charged a quick and chaotic confrontation followed as Bolduc supporters and police rushed in. Bolduc says this type of political division is being driven by the commander-in- chief.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: He's got responsibility to have a national narrative that we should be respecting each other. We certainly didn't see it yesterday in his comments.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Police arrested 37-year-old Joseph Hart for trespassing. Incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 's campaign says the same activist got aggressive at one of their events with campaign volunteers.\par \par \uc2 Hassan calls the behavior "despicable".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): It is absolutely essential that we tone down the rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: On the debate stage, Hassan and Bolduc got testy over the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Former President Trump just confirmed that he's an election denier this week.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: The bottom line is we need to focus on the future.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And inflation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What one item have you bought that gave you sticker shock?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Well, it was actually my husband who was planning on buying eggs and the sticker shock there was considerable, he said he gasped.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: I've been standing in line when people had to take stuff off the -- off the belt. That's her fault. She should gasp at it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: (on camera): Some Republicans did gasp when Buldoc clinched the nomination in the primary because they thought he was too controversial to beat Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  but the race is neck and neck. Republicans have rallied behind him. The latest to endorse Bolduc today, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hillary Vaughn in New Hampshire, one to watch. Hillary, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another very tight Senate contest is in Nevada, and what used to be a reliably blue seat occupied by one of the top Democrats in Washington, but that could change next week.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff shows us tonight from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Republican Nevada Senate candidate \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is on day three of his bus tour across the state as polls show him in a dead heat with Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF NEVADA: We're going to every single community to make sure that everybody understands your vote matters.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  has kept a lower profile this election cycle, seeking to retain the seat she won in 2016 when Senator Harry Reid retired, but the Democratic machine rebuilt is at risk of coming undone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our vote count, it matters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need to stay blue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HOFF: This week, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  held a rally in Las Vegas alongside \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF NEVADA: We know we can't trust Laxalt when it comes to a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Like Cortes Masto, Laxalt served as Nevada's attorney general and has a political legacy behind him. Laxalt's grandfather served as senator and governor of Nevada, his father represented New Mexico in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Laxalt lost his gubernatorial bid to current Democratic Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 STEVE SISOLAK\uc2  (D-NV): My opponent says that affordable health care is B.S. I think he's B.S.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The incumbent is now locked in an ultra-tight matchup with his own, with Republican Joe Lombardo.\par \par \uc2 JOE LOMBARDO (R), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE OF NEVADA: When you look at how the dynamics and fighting crime and crime across the United States too, include state in Nevada, it's changed and that is a result of flood legislation.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Lombardo serves as sheriff of Clark County. Today, he cast his ballot early and said voters concerned with public safety among other issues are siding with him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): The governor's team responded to that saying that crime has taken place under Sheriff Lombardo's watch.\par \par \uc2 Now, another major point of concern among voters is the economy, gas prices in particular, they're averaging about $5.00 a gallon across the state, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexandria Hoff in Las Vegas. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now turning to the Senate race here in Florida. I spoke with Republican incumbent Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  earlier today here in Miami about his campaign for re-election, democratic messaging and his closing argument.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, how you feeling?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): I'm feeling good. I'm feeling good, few days to go, obviously an important election. There's a lot of enthusiasm certainly on our side of it. So, I feel very optimistic. We just got to run through the tape.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the closing argument?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I think it's the same as the opening argument. And really it is that you know, this one party, the Democratic Party controls the House, the Senate, the White House, everything's worse than it was 22 months ago, from immigration, to crime, to inflation, to the economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have this ad out your closing argument called America.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: This is our country. And if you don't love it, you're free to leave it. But we will never allow anyone to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the message with that?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I think the message is just reminding people what the stakes are here. This is not college football. We're not just running elections, because we want to be the winning team in a sport or in a game. It's not a game.\par \par \uc2 What's at stake here is the greatness of this country and all the things that made it such a special country. That's what's at stake, because we are headed in a direction that will -- that will destroy all that, that will take away all the things that have made America special and unique.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last night, President Biden, his closing argument dealt with extreme MAGA Republicans. Here's a piece.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's estimated that there are more than 300 election deniers on the ballot all across America this year.\par \par \uc2 We can't ignore the impact this is having on our country, it's damaging, it's corrosive, and it's destructive.\par \par \uc2 Extreme MAGA Republicans aim to question not only the legitimacy of past elections, but elections being held now and into the future.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: It's not a new message. It's the one they've been using for months now. I think he's trying to sort of lay the groundwork for the fights he plans to have with Congress.\par \par \uc2 And so, when we say, when we pass bills, or try to pass bills to allow more oil exploration, he's going to say we're extremists. When we try to enforce our immigration laws, he's going to say we're extremists.\par \par \uc2 I think he's trying to create the framework for how he's going to fight against the Republican Congress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But aren't you worried at all about that whole threat to democracy message, if you have people who don't accept elections?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, we have historic turnout, the Republican surge in voting is enormous. And it's the Democrats who aren't voting and aren't excited about their candidates.\par \par \uc2 So, if people didn't believe in democracy, why would they be turning up to vote? Yes, there are states that don't have good election laws.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, we have good election laws, you have to have a system of counting that's accurate, obviously. But you also have to have public confidence. And so, it's important to have laws that protect that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your opponent over the past couple of weeks has really hit on the issue of abortion, she has before but really in the past couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF FLORIDA: \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  wants to take us back to a time when women were treated like second class citizens. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 's new federal abortion ban is just the beginning. He wants to ban abortions with no exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, that's been an issue that's been debated in this country for 50 years. I'm not interested in taking anyone's rights on the country. The reason why I'm pro-life, is because I want to protect the right of an unborn human being to live. It's the most fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 Without life, there's -- you don't have liberty, you can't pursue happiness. None of these other rights exist without life.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, that's a conflict of issue in this country. And people have different opinions about it. And I think the political branches at the state level are going to be having that debate and states are going to have different abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 Sure, people have opinions on it. But there's -- you put gas in your car every day. People have to buy groceries for their family every day. And food prices are up 22 percent and just in the last year, that's the food inflation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, those are the issues that people want us to fix right now immediately, that impact everyone every day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have a rally Sunday with the former president.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here in Miami, they're running the show.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yes, well, they've -- I think they've gotten pretty good at putting on these rallies. He's also a voter in Florida. So, I got to make sure I secure those two votes over there in Mar-a-Lago for Florida.\par \par \uc2 Look, he's the most popular well-known Republican in America. He's -- I know no one who fires up our voters to come out and vote more than he does.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's a lot of coverage about Governor DeSantis not being at that rally. He's going to have his own rally someplace in Hillsborough County. (INAUDIBLE) Switzerland in all that --\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: No, I'll be with Governor DeSantis actually today and probably again on I think on Saturday. We do -- I don't get -- it's not about Switzerland. I just think that's sort of political drama that I understand, people have covered politics that we have to talk about. Those things have a have a way of taking care of themselves, haven't run for president, haven't gone through all that before. Who knows what the world is going to look like in two years? Who knows what it's going to look like in two months? I know there's an election on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's say Republicans take control of the Senate and the House. You've got President Biden, for somebody who says, well, then what's going to get done, what is going to happen?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: One of our most important jobs is we have to stop bad things from happening. That includes nominees, and that includes laws, we don't have to wake up every day, fearing \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  may have changed his mind overnight on something and the filibuster has now gone, and they're going to take over federal elections and the like. So, I think that's an important thing.\par \par \uc2 I think also on spending, if you look at how we fund our government, a Republican majority in the Senate and House will have to force the White House to compromise. And hopefully, there'll be senators that because of the instinct of self-preservation, will see some of this and say, yes, maybe we should be drilling for oil, maybe we do need to secure that border.\par \par \uc2 And then obviously, it'll be up to the president, whether he wants to veto it or sign. But when we pass things that make sense, that we pass things that people support, then I think you'll have a decision to make and you'll be held accountable by voters if he runs again in '24.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you sense that wave, you're talking about that?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, I can't speak for the whole country, and I don't pay a lot of it -- I actually don't pay any attention to polls. I just don't think they're reliable anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think the Republicans are under counted?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I don't know. I just think that it's harder to pull Americans than it's ever been. And polarization is also real.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think there's much less undecided voters than there's ever been, people may not have decided to vote yet. But you can pretty much predict how most people in America are going to vote if they do show up.\par \par \uc2 Look, it's not a popular thing to say as a Republican. But I think it's good for America to have two political parties that maybe one to the left, the other ones to the right, but they generally both operate in the world of reality.\par \par \uc2 But that's not what the Democratic Party is right now. It's been captured by at the top levels by laptop liberals and Marxist misfits and that controls the agenda and that needs to change. And hopefully sending a strong message in this election will begin that process of that happening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, I'm going to write that one down, laptop, liberals, Marxist misfits,\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Thanks for doing this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's Senator Rubio. Throughout the show tonight, we'll take you to some spots around Miami. We'll talk to some voters about what's driving them to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Later, I'll be joined by Democratic gubernatorial nominee \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . And when we come back, some breaking news, if some people have their way, the 2024 presidential ballot could look very different.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're here in Domino Park in Little Havana, Miami Dade County. Traditionally, a Democratic stronghold. There are some warning signs though for Democrats in the early vote.\par \par \uc2 As of Wednesday, more registered Republicans had voted than registered Democrats. If that holds, that is a bad sign for Democrats and the political dominoes could be falling the GOP way in Florida.\par \par \uc2 "SPECIAL REPORT" continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, to tonight's spotlight races.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): In Alaska, Republican incumbent Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  takes on GOP challenger \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\uc2 , and Democrat Patricia Chesbro in the state's ranked choice voting system.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and several Democrats have endorsed Murkowski. She voted to impeach former President Trump over the January 6th riot.\par \par \uc2 Tshibaka has Trump's endorsement as publicly called to oust McConnell from leadership.\par \par \uc2 Our latest Fox Power Ranking show Republicans remain favorites to take the House. 26 toss-up races will likely determine the majority. Several vulnerable House Democrats are now fighting to try to hold on to their seats.\par \par \uc2 One of those tossups is in Minnesota. Republican Tyler Kistner is hoping to unseat Democrat \uc2 Angie Craig\uc2 . In a 2020 rematch, the polling in this race shows a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 We are days away from the 2022 election, but there is a group already looking ahead to 2024 that has an unconventional idea about a presidential ticket.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has breaking details tonight from Albuquerque, New Mexico, where President Biden is speaking. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. A centrist group called no labels is quietly mobilizing a major bid to launch a bipartisan unity ticket in 2024, as an alternative for voters, as former President Trump and President Biden each move closer to launching their own reelection bids.\par \par \uc2 Now, no labels insist that it is not actively considering any specific candidates for the unity ticket, which would feature a Republican and a Democrat, but whispers of political moderates being eyed for the job, include Democratic Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Republican governors \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  and \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): The group also says it will stand down if the unity ticket looks like it could become a \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2 -type spoiler, but they're doing the groundwork now to get signatures needed for ballot access.\par \par \uc2 So, a third choice will be ready if the two major parties nominate two divisive candidates.\par \par \uc2 This election cycle, both Biden and Trump had been trying to boost midterm voter turnout for their respective parties by painting the other side as extreme and as a threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And we need a landslide so big that the radical left, they can't rig, they just can't. The radical left cannot rig or steal.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Extreme MAGA Republicans just don't threaten our personal economic rights. They embrace political violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We'll soon see a measure of their sway among voters. But no labels, Chief Strategist Ryan Clancy tells Fox, "Three-quarters of the country thinks we're on the wrong track, and that includes majorities of Democrats, Independents, and Republicans. Most Americans don't have favorable views of either party or the top leaders in them, and they think both sides are too captive to the extremes."\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): No label says their internal polling shows that a unity ticket could win the Electoral College, and at least 23 States representing more than 270 electoral votes. And they are tracking a major convention for the first quarter of 2024 to potentially announce this unity ticket. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Have an election ahead, but already 2024 talk. Jacqui Heinrich with the president in New Mexico. Jacqui thanks.\par \par \uc2 Manhattan judge says he will appoint an independent monitor for former President Trump's real estate empire that would restrict his company's ability to freely make deals, sell assets and change its corporate structure.\par \par \uc2 The judge is presiding over a lawsuit in which New York Attorney General Letitia James alleges the former president and the company routinely misled banks and others about the value of prized assets, including golf courses, and hotels bearing his name.\par \par \uc2 The husband of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is out of the hospital tonight. Paul Pelosi was attacked by an intruder at the couple's San Francisco home last week. He underwent surgery for a fractured skull.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is a Canadian man who was in the U.S. illegally. David DePape is charged with attempted murder, burglary, and several other crimes. His lawyer has entered a plea of not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Up next, he was governor more than a decade ago here in Florida when he was a Republican. Now, he wants the job again as a Democrat. We will talk with Democratic Congressman \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Florida has a large Hispanic population. Large Cuban, Venezuelan, Puerto Rican, Colombian. Miami Dade County is actually 72 percent Hispanic.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, there has been a shift towards the GOP, not just here in Florida, but across the country. In that demographic, two, three, four points in any of these races could actually make a big difference. Something to watch on election night.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Most importantly security, freedom. I love the way that our governor is basically allowing us to make our own decisions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The rent and food and gasoline, these are not luxury items. These are things that people need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can I ask you who you ended up with?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For the governor, I chose \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . And for the Senate I chose Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The economy, crime, what? What drives you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Crime is definitely way up there, because it's just really, really sad what we're going through right now, and we need to get back and get some type of tight system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to Miami. One of the most familiar political figures in the last two decades here in Florida is back on the ballot. A short time ago I talked with the Democratic gubernatorial nominee \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Crist, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (D-FL), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Bret. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How are you feeling? The countdown is on here in the final days.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: It sure is. We've got five days to go. It's the final countdown. I feel good about things. I think we are in a competitive situation. It's Florida. It's always close. I think that's going to be the case in this election as well. Look forward to being on the ticket with Val Demings. She's a great candidate for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  here in the sunshine state. So things are good. We've got a lot of energy, a lot of juice, and can't wait until November the 8th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I wanted to ask you about your closing argument. You have an ad out. I'm going to play just a piece of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm an independent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I'm a lifelong Republican.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need to elect \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  if we want to protect our freedom to choose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the message in your closing?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: The message is to reach across the aisle, to unify Florida, and to respect a woman's right to choose in the sunshine states. As you know, with the Dobbs decision from the Supreme Court, this has been really left to states. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  does not want to give a woman a right to choose. He's already signed a bill that doesn't allow for it and a 15-week ban. But the worst part about it is it also have no exceptions for rape or incest, Bret. And I just find that unconscionable, and I believe women do deserve a choice, and I think that Republicans and Democrats and independents can agree on this issue, and I hope that they do for their own freedom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that a lot of voters are making their choice when they go to the ballot box on that issue?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: I do. But I also think it's affordability, Bret. I mean, here in the sunshine state we have been ranked by "Forbes" magazine as the most expensive state to live in America. More than California, more than New York. That's appalling. And what has happened is our governor has gotten his eye off the ball. He's very interested, as most people know, in running for president in 2024. I know that the former president, Trump, is going to have a rally with \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , but no \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is invited. There is a tension there. I don't know exactly what it's all about.\par \par \uc2 But I do know that in the next five days a lot of Floridians are making up their minds, and if they want a governor who will protect a woman's right to choose, if they want a governor who makes sure that utility rates are kept down, that our property insurance rates that are skyrocketing we can bring down, then they should vote for \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . I did it as their attorney general and governor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, the president went out last night and delivered another speech about ultra MAGA Republicans. He did it at union station, talking about the threats to democracy as he sees it. You recently tweeted, "I'm going head-to-head with one of the biggest threats to democracy this country has seen, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . We have six days to build the people power we need to fight back this MAGA extremism. Who's with us?" Do you think that that is the right closing message when you are trying to reach, as you said at the beginning, across the aisle to Republicans who may like policies in the previous administration?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, sure, I do, because I think people need to know what the differences are between us. And look, I'm pro-democracy. I'm trying to make it easier to vote. I have already stated in this campaign if elected I will make Election Day in Florida a state holiday. On the contrary, the governor has made it harder to exercise mail-in voting. My 90-year-old dad, my 88- year-old mother love to vote by mail. It's more difficult now.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to black and brown voters in our state, he has made it more difficult for them to have drop boxes to get their ballots in community sections all over the sunshine state. That's not pro-democracy. Everybody should have the right to vote and be able to exercise that right to vote. And if they would like to help us do it, I hope they go to CharlieCrist.com. We have a good race here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you look at polls? If you look at the average of polls, do you look at them, as you are heading down the road here? You are trailing significantly if you look at the polls.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, that's true and it's not true, Bret. We just got a poll two days ago from the Listener Group has us up over DeSantis at this stage of the race by eight points, 54 to 46. And most of that is attributable to the women's vote. He edges us among men, to be candid with you, in this poll at least, by 53 to 47. But among women, we get almost 58 percent of the vote, and that's where the difference is in this election. That's why this freedom to choose is such an important issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've had a debate most recently. I want to play something from that, from Governor DeSantis talking about your voting record.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  has voted with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  100 percent of the time. Over the last two years we now have record inflation all across this country at 40-year highs. He supports Biden's energy policies which has made gas and utilities more expensive.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So at a time when you have right track, wrong track numbers, especially here in Florida, significantly on the wrong track, above 70 percent, and some are blaming the administration directly, how do you respond to that if you voted with this administration 100 percent of the time?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, I don't know if it was 100 percent, but here's the real deal. He's not running against President Biden, not yet. And so that was another question posed during the course of the debate. And I asked him, point blank, Ron, if you do get reelected on November the 8th, will you look the Floridians in the eye tonight directly and tell them that you will fulfill a full four-year term? Bret, he wouldn't answer the question. And I just don't think that's fair. If you are running for governor and you are asking for people to reelect you as governor, at least you ought to have the decency to commit to them that if they do give you that honor, that you'll serve the full four-year term. And it really is shocking to me that he wouldn't do it, but that's where we are. And I think Floridians deserve better, and that's why I think we are going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Congressman, I read someplace that you are going to get married before Election Day?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Not before Election Day. It will be after Election Day. But I just got engaged back in June to Chelsea. She is a wonderful lady. She is amazing. A good Minnesotan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Congressman Crist, we appreciate your time, fair and balanced, thanks.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Thank you, Bret. My pleasure, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): I did offer equal time to Governor DeSantis as well, as Congresswoman Val Demings, just to point that out.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, please join me for my new podcast "Common Ground," expanding on the weekly segment on this show featuring individuals from different perspectives, different sides of the aisle, different points of view, lawmakers, CEOs, celebrities, average folks coming together to talk about the big issues of the day. In Columbus, Ohio, this week I spoke to voters from different points of view who found some common ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hear a lot about people wanting civility and wanting parties to reach across the aisle and talk to each other. Is that possible, do you think, today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm hopeful. Really, most elections are decided kind of in the middle anyway, so I think that's where most of the electoral -- the population sits. And probably 80 percent of the issues should be able to be solved because most people agree on most of the issues.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I agree. I think that we definitely need to think about our immigration policy and our immigration system. It's not working for a multitude of reasons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "Common Ground" can be found under the Bret Baier podcast at FOXNewspodcast.com, or wherever you download podcasts.\par \par \uc2 Up next, more missiles fly in one of the world's most dangerous neighborhoods. We'll get reaction from the Pentagon. And later our panel on the closing days of the election and the issues that are driving voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can I ask you about what is driving your vote?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have been a Republican my entire adult life, and I'm just afraid of where things are going. I don't like the way it has become extreme on all sides, but especially on the Republican side. So, I have been avoiding anything I see as extreme.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: After seeing Biden's speech yesterday, that wonderful, kind, unifying speech.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You are being sarcastic?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Absolutely sarcastic.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So can I ask you how voted for?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Of course. DeSantis. I went all the way red, as you see in my shirt. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has done what no other governor has done. And I am politically obsessed.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The South Korean defense minister met with Pentagon officials today following another major missile test by his northern neighbors. This time the fear spread to people in Japan. North Korea has sent dozens of missiles toward South Korea in recent days. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin tells us what the U.S. is doing about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Alerts warning people to shelter in place halted train services across Japan as North Korea launched an intercontinental ballistic missile 1,200 miles into the air.\par \par \uc2 FUMIO KISHIDA, JAPANESE PRIME MINISTER: The continuous launch of ballistic missiles is outrageous and absolutely intolerable.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: South Korean officials say the missile failed during the second stage separation phase during normal flight. The test came as Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  hosted his South Korean counterpart at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: Our alliance is ironclad.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In one of the strongest statements yet from the Pentagon directed at Kim Jong-un, Austin issued a joint statement with South Korea, a stark warning to the Kim regime.\par \par \uc2 LEE JONG-SUP, SOUTH KOREAN DEFENSE MINISTER: Secretary Austin and I affirmed that any nuclear attack by the DPRK, including the use of tactical nuclear weapons, is unacceptable and will result in the end of Kim Jong- un's regime.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: We are focused on making sure that they don't choose to deploy a nuclear weapon or choose to attack South Korea.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: North Korea launched 23 missiles yesterday, the most a single day, and three more today as the U.S. and South Korean militaries for the first time since 2017 held an around the clock military exercise including stealth F-35 fifth generation fighter jets. Kim Jong-un says he believes the exercise is to practice an invasion of North Korea. Austin announced exercise will be extended in the wake of North Korea's latest missile test.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): U.S. officials say intelligence shows North Korea is sending artillery shells to Russia to use in Ukraine, a sign of how badly Russia's military is faring. U.S. officials believe North Korea is preparing to test a nuclear device for the seventh time. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the final five days before the midterm elections. First, Beyond our Borders tonight. The operator of European's largest nuclear power plant located in Ukraine says Russian shelling has damaged lines connecting it to the country's electrical grid. Repeated attacks near the plant have raised concerns about possible radiation leaks. The facility will rely on emergency diesel generators for now.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2 's office says he has conceded defeat to former prime minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  in this week's election in Israel. A statement says Lapid has instructed his office to prepare an organized transition of power. It was Israel's fifth election in four years.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  has arrived in Bahrain for official visits and a public mass Saturday. It's not clear whether he will publicly discuss alleged discrimination against the Muslim nation Shiite majority. Activists want the poppy to urge Bahraini authorities to end the death penalty and political repression in the Persian Gulf kingdom.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a path to chaos in America. It's unprecedented, it's unlawful, and it's un-American. As I've said before, you can't love your country only when you win.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It is an uphill battle in a midterm election to convince people to get out and vote, whereas the other side is motivated because they want change at any cost.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: This is failing effort at the end of a campaign that should have been about inflation and should have been about the economy.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: They're desperate. The president was desperate yesterday going to Union Station. I'm going to five states. He goes down the block.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the countdown is on. Associated Press about President Biden, "In campaign swing focuses on incumbent Democrats. President Biden's travel plan for last big campaign swing before Tuesday's midterm elections reveal his defensive stance in the campaign's closing days. He's spending the bulk of his time trying to hang on to seats that his party already holds."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, if you want to see a graphic that shows the wind at the GOP's back, this is it. This is Gallup. Take a look for the presidential approval, there is President Biden 2022 at 40. U.S. satisfaction, this is essentially heading into the right track in the country, at 17, the lowest in all of these years. Obviously, we don't know the seat loss or gain yet, but you could look at the seat loss or gain the midterms in the past and how that all tracks together. That is pretty graphic in that Gallup poll.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is senior political correspondent for "Axios." Josh, you have been listening to the interviews with Senator Rubio and \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . This last pitch by the president about threats to democracy in this environment, where do you think it stacks?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes, well, Bret, there is a disconnect between what the public says that they are concerned about, the swing voters, worried about the economy and worried about crime and quality of life issues, and the president's late pitch talking again, giving a sort of a repeat of his Philadelphia speech about protecting democracy.\par \par \uc2 And look, there is something of a paradox, a little bit of a mixed message from the president and leading Democrats, because they are claiming democracy is at risk in this election, but they are sort of rejecting what they sees as a potential democratic outcome where voters are clearly expressing a preference for change and to put a check on the Democratic control of power in Washington. So it's not a very convincing message when you are almost rejecting what is looking like a democratic outcome a week or so before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And Marie, you add to that that some of these races, the Democrats actually supported some of the candidates that they're facing that actually have closed the gap in some of these races. In other words, in the primaries they put money into the candidates that they thought that they could face easier.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Bret. Well, look, that was certainly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's message last night about democracy, which we actually do know that somewhere between five percent and 10 percent of poll respondents say they care about. Some of these races are going to be so close that that five percent or 10 percent actually matters.\par \par \uc2 But if I look at the folks out in the states doing the closing messages, whether it's \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , still a great campaigner, or other, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , other Democratic leaders, they are talking about the economy, they are talking about the fact that if Republicans take Congress, they will cut your Social Security, they will cut your Medicare, they will raise your drug prices. So they're actually making an economic argument.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it is worth pointing out there is some 300 Republican candidates around the country who want to win this democratic election and then have said they don't believe the last one was actually democratic. So they are using democratic means to advance nondemocratic principles. That's what people are concerned about, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Well, here is President Biden, here's former president \uc2 Obama\uc2 , they do talk about the other stuff we just mentioned. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's estimated that there are more than 300 election deniers on the ballot all across America this year. We can't ignore the impact this is having on our country.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Democracy as we know it may not survive in Arizona. That's not an exaggeration. That is a fact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So my point, Byron, is that democracy may not survive if these folks are elected, but Democrats pumped money into the primary to get them to be the candidate that they faced, but I'm going to tell you in the closing argument democracy may not survive if they win. I mean, that's connecting the dots there.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": And we are seeing huge, huge turnouts, all around the country. Listen, in the final days, we have now entered the hysteria and desperation stage of the campaign. President Biden hastily arranged a speech at Union Station last night to try squeeze a little political benefit out of a deranged man's attack on Paul Pelosi. The president is trying to ensure that people do not, do not see this election as a referendum on his time in office. Instead, he wants it to be seen as a choice. And the way he is presenting the choice is you can either elect Democrats or you can face the end of American democracy, the end. The 233-year experiment is over.\par \par \uc2 And as Josh was saying, this simply does not address the concerns that we know that voters have everywhere, not just with inflation but with the fear of a recession coming, with rising mortgage interest rates that are making it impossible to buy a house, with 401(k)s that have taken enormous hits. This is not an argument that is going to satisfy voters who are worried about those things.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, I want to tick down some Real Clear Politics average. We had a ton of polls over the last couple of days. Let's go first to Georgia. This is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . This race now in the average of recent polls has Walker up just slightly. Pennsylvania, Mehmet Oz versus Lieutenant Governor Fetterman, again, 0.3 percent. Some polls have Oz up a couple points, but it's definitely within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Now, New Hampshire, this is a race that has closed dramatically to the point where now General Don Bolduc has, at least in the average of polls, a lead. Is this thing changing, Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: This is what you see in the final week or so of a wave election where the undecided voters tend to break towards the challenging party, the Republican Party in this case. So Dr. Oz took the lead for the first time ever in the Real Clear Politics average today. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  pulled ahead recently in that polling average. Don Bolduc is getting awfully close to Senator Hassan in a race that looked like a reach for Republicans. So this is very consistent with a wave election that would give Republicans, in all likelihood, control of the House, but also perhaps a healthy Senate majority as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Panel, thank you. Countdown is on.\par \par \uc2 Finally, tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. A man riding his paramotor rescued a woman after her car ended up in a Florida canal. Thanks to quick action \uc2 Cristiano Piquet\uc2  and his friend were able to throw a rope and rescue that woman and bring her to safety.\par \par \uc2 And in Canada a family was alerted to a fire in their home in the nick of time. After noticing the blaze, neighbor Travis Levitsky repeatedly banged on the door to wake the family. He entered the house, rescued a girl sleeping in the basement. Congratulations to both.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight from Miami. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Back in D.C. tomorrow. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221104eib300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pennsylvania Race Could Decide Senate Control; Race Between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Remains Close; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  To Focus On Threats To Democracy; House And Senate Control At Risk For Democrats; Conservative Coalition Poised To Take Over Parliament; Palestinians React To Rightward Power Shift; Famous Democratic Names Including Former Presidents \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  And \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  Campaign For Democratic Candidates In Close Midterm Elections Across U.S.; Recent Polling Shows Voter Shift Towards Republican Party For Upcoming Midterms; Some Immigrants Crossing Across Southern Border Into U.S. Using Uber Rideshare; President Biden To Give Speech At Union Station.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Aishah Hasnie, Rich Edson, Peter Doocy, Brit Hume,  Trey Yingst, Mike Emanuel, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 7,694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Included a 2.4 mile swim and a 112 mile bike ride and 26.2 mile run. The expedition took eight hours, 33 minutes and 46 seconds to complete, with Sodaro's 18 month old daughter Skylar waiting for her at the finish line.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Good for her.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Why do they call it the Iron Man? It makes no sense to me.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What are they putting in the mugs up there these days? All right, thanks, Jesse. All right, good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the countdown is on. We are now in the final week of the midterm campaign season at state control of the U.S. House and Senate and the fate of President Biden's legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 We have new Fox polls on two Senate races that could determine which party controls that chamber. Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's lead over Mehmet Oz is down to three points in our last poll in Pennsylvania, matching the margin of error, following a debate performance that left many Pennsylvanians wondering if the Democrat was up to the task.\par \par \uc2 The gubernatorial race there is not that close according to our poll, with Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  holding a 16 point advantage over \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  continues to chip away at the lead of incumbent senator Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  while the Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is maintaining a six point advantage.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight. Aishah Hasnie is in Atlanta, but we'll begin with Senior National Correspondent rich Edson in Lancaster, Pennsylvania tonight. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, good evening, Bret. A burst of new polling numbers has hit the Pennsylvania Senate race. The specifics vary, but the general idea, this race is close, within the margin of error as Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz campaign across the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: My promise, the bargain that I'm giving to each and every one of you, if you send me to Washington, D.C. to be that 51st vote.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I want to go to Washington and bring balance. So, you actually have the partisan bickering fall away. And we address the problems that are plaguing people in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Fetterman and Oz are intensifying their travel schedules in this final week before Election Day, eight days after their only debate.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I don't -- I don't -- I support fracking.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Fetterman is still confronted with questions about his performance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you understand why people are now questioning your ability to be our senator from the state of Pennsylvania because of moments like that?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I believe that that my support of fracking has always been one that in the past, it was some of the environmental concerns.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you understand why people are concerned about your ability to be our senator?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I was very much aware that I had a stroke. And I was in the position to show up.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Republicans also secured a significant win at the Pennsylvania Supreme Court. It ruled officials cannot count ballots with missing or incorrect dates, they have to set those ballots aside.\par \par \uc2 State law says voters have to handwrite the date on the outer envelope when they return their mail-in ballots. We'd like to think that Supreme Court cleared it up, the P.A. Supreme Court but who's to say that that won't be appealed to another court?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): With a massive expansion of mail-in voting here since 2020, and the state law that requires those ballots to remain sealed until Election Day, officials are expecting some delays. The Pennsylvania Secretary of State's office says counties may take a few days to have all their votes in, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, and that's why that ruling is really important, right?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: It is, definitely, Bret. And that's something that's really going to play forward here. And again, that could be litigated. So, while they're saying it's settled, it just might not be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Rich Edson in Pennsylvania. Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's go down to Georgia. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  continues to gain ground as he tries to unseat Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . The economy seems to be much more important as an issue to voters there than anything else, and that is putting Warnock's state in jeopardy. Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Atlanta tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At Ralph's diner in Cartersville. Georgia.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HILL, GEORGIA VOTER: In my opinion, we're in a recession right now.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The slowing economy is on the menu and Charles Hill is looking for a change.\par \par \uc2 HILL: If this keeps on, they're going to drive us to a depression.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He's voting for Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: It's time to take our country back.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Despite two women accusing Walker of forcing them to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: They're going to try to throw everything at me.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  doesn't think voters have to choose though between a candidate's credibility and their ability to fix the economy.\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: I think that the voters of Georgia can walk and chew gum at the same time. Competence matters. Character matters, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  fails on both counts.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But even some of his own supporters aren't convinced enough voters think that way.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIANO DEPANI, GEORGIA VOTER: I think a lot of -- a lot of very persuadable voters in terms of seeing the economy as something that politicians can materially affect.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: New Fox News polling out today reveals inflation still ranks the most important issue in this race with abortion 20 points behind. And even though voters are seven points more likely to feel extremely concerned that Walker is unqualified than that Warnock is out of touch, the two are pulling neck and neck in this final stretch before Election Day and could be headed for a runoff.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And where Senator Warnock could get a boost here Bret is from those split voters. That is something to watch here in Georgia. We've talked with conservatives, independents, even Democratic voters who say they voted for Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , a Republican, but did not vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live in Atlanta. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is largely being avoided by many Democratic candidates this midterm season. He is delivering some speeches and will do so again tonight in Washington without candidates.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is at Union Station in Washington where this speech will be. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Polls show that voters are most concerned with inflation. But President Biden plans once again to warn about the MAGA Republican. I asked him for details this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Mr. President, ahead of your remarks tonight, after the midterms --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Listen to the manual and you'll be impressed.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Tonight's speech will be near Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN, WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER: Why will he be making the speech from Capitol Hill? Because on January 6th, we saw violence geared towards subverting democratic processes there.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As Biden struggles to sell his economic plan.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our watch for the first time in 10 years, seniors are getting the biggest increase in Social Security checks, period.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: A tweet to that effect today was fact checked by \uc2 Twitter\uc2  where officials added context leading to the White House to later delete it.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Usually when we put out a tweet, we posted with context and it did not have that context.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Though inflation remains a top concern for 