uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the president is going with whatever message he can conjure up because he and his party really have no solution to inflation. They've -- you know, they've tried some things. They've released oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and taking some other steps, but voters are clearly not satisfied with those steps. And they have no more really to offer.\par \par \uc2 So, they're dealing with a situation where voters are unhappy with what they've got. They associate him with it. His approval rating is way underwater.\par \par \uc2 And so, he's really not that big a help to his party as a more popular president might be. So, you see this event with no candidates present as an example.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's some surrogates that are helpful. Democratic candidates have asked for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , as we've mentioned. Also former President Clinton, he was campaigning for a Democratic candidate for Congress, a Democratic Congressman up in upstate New York. Here's what he said about Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Republicans are pretty simple, actually. They're pretty straightforward. They say, I want you to be very miserable. And I want you to be very angry. And I vote -- I want you to vote for us. And we'll make it worse, but we'll blame them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think of that, Brit?\par \par \uc2 HUME: That sounds like the \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  I knew and covered all those years ago. He was -- he always had some sort of clever formulations but they weren't always correct, aren't always true. But that's the kind of stuff he says, and he makes it sound good. And he gets laughs. So, he's out on the trail again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Speaking of which, you're covering of that president, President Clinton, let's take you back to 1994. This is after the big midterm loss. And the press conference in the East Room of the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUME: Mr. President, did you mean to say here, sir, that the message the voter sent yesterday was basically an extension of the demand for change they made when you were elected in 92?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: I think they were saying two things to me. Or maybe three, they were saying -- maybe 300. Democrats were in charge. We're holding you accountable. Can we hope you hear this, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who was that guy asking those questions?\par \par \uc2 HUME: The guy with more hair than I now have, fewer wrinkles.\par \par \uc2 I remember that election very well. That was the one where \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  went around the country trying to rally his party in the midterms, by talking about a set of promises the Republicans have made that they call the Contract for America -- Contract with America, he went around the country calling to contract on America, to try to persuade voters not to do what voters ultimately did, which was to throw out Democrats by the metric ton and they threw a whole lot of mountain and Democrats lost control of the House of Representatives for the first time in decades.\par \par \uc2 So, he's -- you know, he's clever with his messages, but they always work. And one wonders if that works this time, because the circumstances are quite similar.\par \par \uc2 He says vote Republicans want voters angry. Well, voters are angry, and you can you can see it all over the place. So, we'll see what he has to say if there's another sweep this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it's interesting you ask that question after the day after the election of President Biden's team was asked whether he'll have a press conference, they didn't commit to one. But it's an interesting time to get a president to reflect the day after. Usually a loss may be a big loss like 94.\par \par \uc2 Brit, as always. Thank 