e who back the far-right president gathered today outside one of the Army's eight regional headquarters. Yesterday, Bolsonaro stopped short of conceding the election, but authorized his chief of staff to begin the transition process.\par \par \uc2 Diplomatic efforts have locked in a wartime agreement allowing Ukrainian grain and other commodities to reach world markets. Russia says it will stick to the deal after Ukraine pledged not to use a designated Black Sea corridor to attack Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 And Taiwan welcomes a pair of experts from China to help an ailing panda in a rare opportunity for contact between the sides. The expert arrived as the animal which is suspected to have a malignant brain tumor took a turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 The giant panda and his mate were gifted to the zoo in 2008 during a time of warming relations between China and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to talk about the political polling tonight. We'll have analysis from pair of pollsters in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel reports on what we're learning about a key voter demographic. Now, it seems to be breaking in one direction.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Suburban women have shifted significantly toward Republicans in the closing days of this midterm campaign, according to a new Wall Street Journal poll.\par \par \uc2 White suburban women make up 20 percent of the electorate. They are now favoring Republicans by 15 points. That's a 27 point shift since the Wall Street Journal's August poll.\par \par \uc2 That also suggests the issue of abortion rights has faded in importance after Democrats initially saw energy on the issue this past summer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YESLI VEGA (R-VA), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Are you all fired up?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: In the affluent Northern Virginia suburbs, Republican Yesli Vega is campaigning with Governor Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 VEGA: And I don't know about you, but I find myself having to go to three different grocery stores trying to make that dollar stretch.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Vega is running against Democratic incumbent \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , the Fox News Power Rankings considers that race a tossup in Virginia's seventh congressional district.\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): We have many priorities. But certainly, the issue of codifying Roe is an important one.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Similar to Illinois' sixth district, Connecticut Five, New York's fourth district, and Oregon Five.\par \par \uc2 Suburban districts and mostly affluent and typically blue or democratic seats. In another suburban New York district today, former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  suggested, Republicans don't have the answers.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , 42ND PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We do have too much inflation. And we do have too much crime. But all this tough talk doesn't amount to a hill of beans that the Republicans are making.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): White suburban women were a significant force in helping Democrats to major victories in House races in 2018 when they regained control of the House. The latest polling suggests now they trust Republicans to handle the economy and inflation and could deliver big for the GOP. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS PODCAST" CO-HOST: The party in power if they have low approval rating, as this one does with President Biden around 40 percent, or a right track, wrong track in the direction of the country somewhere in that 35 to 40 percent range, or a big disapproval on the economy, you have late deciding voters pushed away from the party in power. Now, you have all three of those factors this year. And so you see in all of these states beginning a pretty significant shift towards the right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh Holmes of the "Ruthless Podcast" been around the block with politics for some time. Let's get more information about what the polls are telling us right now within one week. With us this evening, Chris Anderson, president of Beacon Research, also a member of the FOX News Decision Desk, and Stephen Kent, operations director for Echelon Insights. Gentlemen, thanks for being here. I want to put up this Real Clear Politics average of the generic vote, the congressional vote, Democrat, Republican over the past six months, and it has shifted significantly just in really the past month or so. In fact, every poll, Stephen, that we have seen has made in its own poll a major shift to Republicans, whether it's two, four, or six points, it's R plus something. That signifies that there is a shift.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN KENT, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Absolutely. And every different polling shop approaches these polls with different methodologies. Online surveys tend to skew a little bit more in Democrats' favors than traditional phone surveys. Here at Echelon Insights, we have still come up, as of October, the very end of last month, with D plus two for the generic congressional ballot.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have to weight that against where we have been trending for several months. Just last month in September we were looking at D plus five. That is tightening and tightening as more people make up their mind as we enter the final days of the midterm. In addition to that you also have to look at GOP versus Democrat enthusiasm. And the GOP Republican voters have maintained their edge for 12 months with people being more enthusiastic and likely to vote when push comes to shove, and that matters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Chris, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANDERSON, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: Yes. There is no doubt that there has been a shift in the generic ballot towards Republicans. I wouldn't characterize it as huge. And I would also say that part of it is because most pollsters started modeling their likely voter model. So there was a shift in the fall from showing the registered voter, which is a much broader universe, to the likely voter model as we get closer to the election. Frankly, I think that two years ago it seemed like Democrats probably had a two-year lease on the House just with the slim margin that they had and knowing the advantage of having the out-of-party having more energy behind it.\par \par \uc2 But I think the one thing that I'm not ready concede is that overturning Roe is not going to have an impact on this election. That was a seismic event for a lot of voters, and I don't think we'll really know if it is influencing this election until we see who comes out to vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's a good point.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: An increase in the percentage of women could really make a difference for Democrats in some races.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Stephen. What about the early vote? As you look at these numbers, I always say it's not good to read too much into early vote and how it breaks down. But you could look into how many people are doing it.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Yes, absolutely. And at Echelon Insights we've predicted that we are going to see a total turnout this midterm year right around $125 million, and that is a new all-time high for midterm voting. But you shouldn't get two granular on whether that means that we're seeing things blow towards the Democrats or for the Republicans. What we do know is that typically more ideological voters tend to get out and vote early. I think you are going to see a lot of Democrats run up their numbers early as they tend to, but then we're going to see people who maybe have not been activated in prior elections show up here in this final week. So probably not too good to overanalyze that number while we get to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chris, last thing. Mike brought us the suburban women vote and how it's shifted in recent polls. Other polls have shown that Hispanics have made a bit of a shift from where they have -- and it's not a monolithic group, but where they have traditionally voted, or at least suggests that they were going to vote. And those issues about the economy seem to be striking home, especially in some of those border states.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Yes, I am seeing that also. Certainly not a groundswell change, but just Democratic margins decreasing a little bit among Hispanic voters. But it's different state to state and different issues state to state. We have a couple key swing states that are borders, border states, and then a couple in the upper Midwest. So, very different dynamics in the different states, and Hispanic voters could certainly be determinative in a couple of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and two, three points makes a difference on that margin for Democrats as they look at those numbers. Could be a long night, maybe a long couple of nights, hopefully not beyond that. But, Stephen and Chris, we appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's spotlight races we look at two states Democrats can feel good about before Tuesday. In Hawaii Democratic Governor \uc2 David Ige\uc2  is term limited, leaving current Lieutenant Governor Joshua Green and former Lieutenant Governor Duke Aiona to battle before the open seat. This is the last year before Hawaii adopts ranked choice voting for special federal and county council elections.\par \par \uc2 And in Delaware, President Biden voted early over the weekend in person. He did show his I.D., by the way. In person early voting starts -- began Friday. The state's lone congressional district features a 2020 rematch. Democratic incumbent \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  faces Republican Lee Murphy. Rochester previously defeated Murphy by 17 points.\par \par \uc2 Up next, you can take \uc2 Uber\uc2  when you want a ride. Now human smugglers may be following your lead. A report from the southern border you want to see coming up.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel with analysis of latest developments in the midterm campaign as the countdown is on.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The owner of the \uc2 Washington Commanders\uc2 , the \uc2 NFL\uc2  team, hiring \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  to consider the possible sale of the \uc2 NFL\uc2  franchise. Dan Snyder come under fire in recent years and is facing pressure to sell the team. The congressional investigation found he played a significant role in fostering a toxic work environment, pointing to the evidence suggesting Snyder impeded the \uc2 NFL\uc2 's independent probe into those allegations. No comment about the sale potential.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning about a new way smugglers are getting people across the southern border and into the U.S. It's something many of you have experience with. Correspondent Bill Melugin shows us how \uc2 Uber\uc2  is now being used as avenue for human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In the Rio Grande Valley, Texas DPS pulled over \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver they say was smuggling five illegal immigrants in his vehicle. The driver cooperated, showing troopers his \uc2 Uber\uc2  route that showed a client hired him to pick up the Mexican nationals by the river in Mission Texas, then dropped them off at local mall. Texas DPS suspects a human smuggling organization may have ordered that \uc2 Uber\uc2 , and because they couldn't prove criminal intent, the driver was not arrested.\par \par \uc2 In Normandy, Texas, early Wednesday morning a massive group of migrants crosses illegally into this tiny town on the outskirts of Eagle Pass. Hundreds of them escorted by the Texas National Guard and Border Patrol in a single file line stretching almost as far as the eye can see.\par \par \uc2 Hi, where are you from? Cuba. Where are you from? Nicaragua. Where are you from? Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 The migrants are predominately from countries that Title 42, a policy that allows authorities to expel migrants on the basis of public health, is rarely enforced on, meaning they won't be expelled to Mexico. At the same time, another group of 400 crossed illegally into Eagle Pass, most of them single adult men. Shortly before FOX News got on scene, sources tell us a 48-year-old Cuban woman went into cardiac arrest here and died.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Tony Gonzales represents the area. He says the midterms will show voters are ready to reject Democrats' handling of this border crisis.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES, (R-TX): There's a simple fix to it -- enforce the laws that are already on the books. I look forward to House Republicans winning the majority and doing exactly that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And immigration is top of mind in another border state as new FOX News polling shows it is the second most important issue to voters in the Arizona Senate race. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, there's less than a week left in the midterm campaign. Our panel looks at where things stand right now.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami as victims' families make statements during the sentencing hearing for the teenager who killed 17 people at a south Florida high school four-and-a-half years ago. Nikolas Cruz was formally sentenced to life without parole for the massacre on Valentine's Day of 2018.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Denver. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 31, our affiliate and across the country, customers line up to buy tickets for this evening's Powerball drawing. The jackpot now estimated at $1.2 billion with a "b," the second largest Powerball jackpot in history. Your chance of winning on a single entry, one in 292.2 million. But you you're saying there's a chance.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Who will fight for you? That's the choice in this election. Who will fight for you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2 , (R-FL): If you want somebody's vote, you should tell them exactly what you are going to do. That's what our candidates are doing.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The Republicans talk a good game, but they rarely do anything other than try to take away your freedoms. Why reward that behavior?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON, (R) ARKANSAS: They are not reaching out to independents. They are not reaching out to those crossover voters. And that's where it's really a signal that the momentum is on the GOP side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I actually think as we get closer to an election people talk with a lot more fierce power. The soundbites are a little choppier. Anyway, we are getting closer to an election, and two big races in the Senate will determine the control of the Senate. Pennsylvania, Lieutenant Governor Fetterman is up three on Mehmet Oz, within the margin of error. But here you see the breakdown, Democrats, Republicans, and look at independents, 31-31. As you look at Georgia, that is one point lead for the Democratic incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  over \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Again, margin of error there, but there you see the breakdown, and independents break the other way. But there are undecideds in each one of these that are pretty high, which is interesting. We'll talk about that in a second.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, "The Wall Street Journal" has the issues, Democrats and Republicans on the issues, who handles it better. And there you see Republicans up on the economy, inflation big, crime, abortion, the Democrats, education, Democrats, border, a big lopsided number for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and Vince Coglianese, editorial director at "The Daily Caller." Matthew, as you look at those numbers, what do you see? And we are always reading into the tea leaves here, but there's not much change usually that happens in the final, final days.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Right. What I see, Bret, is that on the issue of concern to voters, the economy, inflation, and crime, Republicans have the distinct advantage. If I were Oz and Walker, I would be a little bit concerned about those independent numbers. But as you mentioned, the surveys also showed large numbers of undecided voters heading into the final days. There's a chance that those undecideds and the rest of the independents will break heavily for Republicans. And I think that would give us the Republican Senate as well as an almost assured Republican House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we have talked a lot about President Biden, he is giving a speech at Union Station tonight about, again, we believe the MAGA threat, in his words, much like we saw his previous speech on this. He is not doing a ton of campaigning. He will go to Pennsylvania again with an event with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . "Politico" writes, "Why top progressives are keeping their campaign trail distance from some vulnerable Dems. That strategy is a tacit acknowledgment that despite top progressive skills at fundraising and turning out the Democratic base, the party's battleground district candidates are still worried about Republican attacks that try to associate them with liberal policies toxified as socialist." So there, a couple of stops for \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren, but not many.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: No. And this is because the majority of our party, the majority of those elected and the majority of voters, including myself, in the Democratic Party, are moderates. They are centrist. And this is one of the reasons that the far left, the \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warrens, AOCs within our party, can sometimes do more harm than good, especially in those areas that might be more conservative fiscally, but more liberal socially.\par \par \uc2 And having said that, I don't think these campaign stops, and stats prove me right, make a huge difference. I really don't. I think, look, the president has a lower approval rating than all of the Democrats that are running for Senate. And so that's not helpful, necessarily. And having \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , even though people love him within the party, this is a very different time.\par \par \uc2 So, having these people come out, even if you love them, we have seen it happen with Clinton, sometimes we have seen it happen with Trump, and certainly with President Biden and former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . I don't think it's going to move the needle. I would agree with almost, almost everything Matt said. We are closer, and like you said, it's not going to change much, but we do always see the gap close just a couple of weeks before. So this is to be expected. I would be concerned, when you look at a red state like Georgia, when you have such, almost a tie there for your candidate in such a red state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vince, what about the president's remarks tonight? We don't have a script yet. But we can assume that it's going to follow pretty closely to the speech he gave a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 VINCE COGLIANESE, "THE DAILY CALLER": And if it does, it will be very divisive. Remember, Americans responded to that speech, the red speech in front of Independence Hall, by saying that it was way too divisive for them. One poll finding nearly 60 percent of Americans thought that. This is at least the third iteration of my mind much a speech like this that he has probably given this year. Remember, he gives the one in front of Independence Hall in January of this year. He went to Georgia, and he said that Republicans were bringing about Jim Crow 2.0, and that they were very much like Bull Connor, Jefferson Davis. And of course, as we have seen these votes come in in Georgia, there is no truth to that. It was a vicious slander that he had thrown at his political opponents.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we will hear tonight is an effort to link the attack on Paul Pelosi to the Republican Party broadly. That attack, by my assessment, will be completely unfair. We are looking at very mentally ill person, we believe, who attacked Paul Pelosi, a guy who tried to buy fairy houses on \uc2 Etsy\uc2  "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reported, but he said that fairies couldn't live in them because the doors were painted the wrong colors. This is nuts. But I think the president is going to try to connect him to Republicans broadly tonight, and that will be a very divisive issue going into these final days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Matthew, we're going to be in Miami tomorrow. We'll talk to the candidates. We will be talking to voters. And some interesting races down there. Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has a lead heading into the election, final days, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the governor, has a big lead. But what's interesting here is the local media, "Florida GOP takes the lead in early voting in traditionally blue Miami-Dade. In an extraordinary sign of trouble for Democrats in Florida ahead of Election Day, the number of registered Republicans voting in Miami-Dade County surpassed Democrats on Wednesday, one day after President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  visited the area on a campaign swing." I don't remember a time when Republicans won the early vote in Miami-Dade.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: You don't remember a time, Bret, because I don't think it's ever happened before. And the days of Florida as being a swing state are coming to an end. I think that DeSantis and rubio will have good election nights. DeSantis could win the Hispanic vote in Florida. He could win by several points reelection. One of the big stories coming out of next week will be the rise of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and what that portends for the future of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, and obviously the other big story, Leslie, will be what happens with former President Trump, and does he decide to declare. That's a whole other story line as we get down to the next election as we count down to this one.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Yes, most definitely. And Republican red Florida, which I think it is, look, it concerns me as the Democrat when I see numbers among Latinos, even though Cubans who are the majority of the Latin population in Miami-Dade historically voted Republican, I'm surprised by these numbers for a couple of reasons. One, you have a lot of senior, a very large senior population in Miami-Dade. And, of course, there are those seniors that are fearful that Republicans will take a hatchet to Medicare and to Social Security. And there are just younger voters.\par \par \uc2 But as a Democrat, sadly, I look at the numbers in my own party, Bret, and we just don't come out in the midterms. So, I'm not surprised to see Republicans coming out more than Democrats even in Miami-Dade. I am surprised when we're seeing some of the numbers we're seeing out of Miami- Dade, especially among Latinos, non-Cuban Latinos in Miami-Dade.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Vince, last word. There is a lot of talk about now in the final day from Democrats, Social Security and Medicare. It comes up, really, every election. There is never some hatchet to Social Security and Medicare in any Republican plan, nor do most Republicans talk about it, despite \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  putting out a plan that mentioned that as one of the items of dealing with the big picture.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: It's a form -- it's a desperate talking point truly the Democrats are attempting to wield right now because they have nothing else to cling to. In fact, think about the last 24 hours. The White House put out a tweet suggesting that it was great, it was leadership on the part of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that Social Security payments have risen more than they have in an entire decade. The reason for that, the law. Inflation is out of control. So, of course the Social Security payments have to go to keep up. The White House has taken that tweet down after it caught a bunch of flack for that kind of statement.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you so much. As we said, the countdown is on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, from classmate to hero. When a second grader, Kyan, saw another student gasping for air, he leapt into action. Kyan patted on the back very hard as the girl choked on an orange until a teacher ran over to help. Together the two were able to dislodge the orange, potentially saving the life of eight-year-old Malaya. Kyan received the school's superhero award for quick thinking and bravery. Second grade, nice job.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, as we mentioned, we will be coming to you live from Miami where we will talk to voters as well as Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Democratic gubernatorial nominee, former governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221103eib200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Democrats Are Trying To Tarnish The GOP Six Days Before The Election; President Biden Saying Republicans Are A Threat To Democracy And Hillary Clinton Insulting American Voters Saying They Do Not Know What Is At Stake In The Midterms; White Suburban Women Are Swinging And Shifting To Vote For The GOP Leaving The Democrats; A Big Group Of Illegal Immigrants Has Attached Border Patrol Agents In Texas; Border Crisis Battle: Massive Group Of Illegal Immigrants Attack Agents In TX; The End Of Affirmative Action? Supreme Court Could Stop Race-Based College Admissions; Hello? You're Pronouncing Adele's Name Wrong!\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Jessica Tarlov, Katie  Pavlich, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: Boy, how time flies. By the way, speaking time flying, of course, this weekend is going to be crazy with some of the biggest names on each side supporting their candidates. We are live on Saturday, special edition of "Cavuto Live" 10:00 a.m. Eastern Time following everyone who's anyone all over the country. Here is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Katie Pavlich, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Democracy apparently going to end in six days if you don't vote for the Democrats. President Biden will be giving a last-minute speech later tonight on preserving and protecting our democracy because he's got nothing else to run on.\par \par \uc2 Biden flipping back a few chapters in his playbook to the one about MAGA Republicans being evil and only his party can save the soul of the nation. Our very own SOB, Peter Doocy, challenging the White House about what the president will say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president has long talked about our nation being at an inflection point. He has been clear democracy is under assault. Unfortunately, we have seen mega MAGA Republican officials who don't believe in the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Does that mean President Biden thinks it is a threat to democracy if somebody votes Republican?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: No. That's a ridiculous question. No.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Why is that a ridiculous question?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Because American people should have the right to vote for whomever they want. The voting is a sacred right. It is something that the president wants to protect.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, President Biden thinks democracy can survive even if Democrats are not in charge of Congress?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president is optimistic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: While \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gears up to attack literally every Republican on the ballot, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  says "hold my beer," by insulting voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Are you concerned that maybe voters are not putting those pieces together, that having a Republican House would mean that the people you're talking about would be even more empowered?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLIONTON, FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think with all of the noise that we've got in this election season, I don't think people are able to really grasp that. But more importantly, I'm not sure they really understand the threats to their way of life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dana, the strategy behind going out, this seems a little haphazard to me.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Oh, yes. I think that --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What's going on here?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This is what I think happened. I think they got some numbers overnight. They saw that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is out there. He's the only one getting crowds, only one getting Democrats somewhat excited at least to come and see him, but then they realize he's not on the ballot. And they get their numbers overnight in the White House in the morning they're like we have to do something. And they have this brainstorming for like what should we do.\par \par \uc2 Well, last week in the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " they said that relative to turnout in 2018 there has been a surge of participation from women black voters and voters over the age of 50. This is who they're trying to go for especially -- that was in Georgia. So, they're trying to figure out a way how can we make sure that everyone's paying attention to this.\par \par \uc2 However, first of all let me just talk about the setting. Union Station? Has anybody -- that was -- that was my favorite place to go when I first moved to Washington in 1995.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's beautiful.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's absolutely beautiful, marble columns, it was the -- it had the coolest food court. There was the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  you could go to, the fancy restaurants everything. And do you know what it is today? A complete and total hellscape.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It has been taken over by homeless, drug addicts. It's almost like walking over here to Penn Station. It is an absolute travesty. You walk outside of Penn Station, there are tents everywhere. And so, I'm kind of curious like what is the backdrop they're going to use? Remember the backdrop they used for his first mega MAGA speech in Philadelphia --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- where they used all the red lights and it was all so angry. I'm like, why do they think all of a sudden this is going to work. The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  left the Union Station. The other thing is it's right there at the Acela Corridor. Who is he talking to? He is not talking to working class voters.\par \par \uc2 They're not talking to the people that are saying we're worried about crime, the border, education, inflation. Is there something that you could do to help us? No, he is going to talk about this. I'm going to tell you what I think he should have done tonight instead.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Don't do it yet.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Why?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because they're watching.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's too late. Even if he was going to get booed, he and the First Lady should have gone to the game four --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- of the Phillies-Astros series and been there like a regular Joe. Instead, he's going to give a lecture about elections.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Or he could have tried to throw out the first pitch but that -- I don't know about all that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, he'll get really booed then. Greg, is this going to be another mega MAGA, ultra MAGA trickle-down MAGA deal?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: First of all, Hillary did not look well and it appeared she might have thrown up on \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 's shirt. He is going to probably mention January 6th because that's all he ever can mention because he has nothing else and January 6th has now become the Kevin Bacon of terrible events for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Everything could be linked to January 6th. I just hope when he brings up election deniers, which is a threat to democracy, he does include dear Hillary who already called into question 2024. So, he better include that. It is amazing though how extreme the demonization has become. It's a trip. You're watching the same people who claimed rhetoric caused the Pelosi violence.\par \par \uc2 Then, mere minutes later in another segment, we'll say Republicans are a threat to democracy and a threat -- and then possibly if the Republicans take over and we've heard this, worse than 9/11, worse than Watergate, worse than CNN plus all combined. Who's painting targets on who? And when they say it's a threat to democracy, I think what they're really saying is a threat to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because it's the only time they feel a sense of urgency is when they're about to be tossed out of power. And when you look at what they call a threat to Democrats or a threat to democracy, it's voter I.D.'s, it's free speech which is now being redefined as disinformation. It's Elon Musk buying \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Somehow that's going to end the world. It's Fox News which just presents a fair and balanced view of the news, which has so long been biased.\par \par \uc2 So, and what's not a threat to them, crime. If you bring up crime, they make fun of you. The riots, the looting, with social justice. So, I think they kind of put themselves in a corner because they've been demonizing people for so long, they don't know where to go. And the president's credibility is -- he's like -- he comes off as angry, habitually lying and confused.\par \par \uc2 He's like -- he's like an old confidence man and the medicine show ditched him in the middle of the night and he's just stuck in your town.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Katie, when Re