ans stay on message on those issues, they can win anywhere in any part of this country from Orange County to Washington to New Hampshire and every place in between.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Scott, we have launched our no state left behind mantra over the last really, 10 or 11 months, OK. I think California, not just Orange County, but California is ripe for a comeback into reality. Do you see this brewing, maybe not everything obviously, this election, but moving forward after Tuesday to 2024?\par \par \uc2 BAUGH: Yes, of course, I do. \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  has spent $24 million this cycle. She spends money worse than in her campaign than she does for government. But the bottom line for \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is that she wants open border, she wants reparations. We call her open-border Porter out here. She wants the defund the police. She wants to criticize the police. In fact, she called the police force in Irvine a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 We want a crime illegal again in California, and the voters of my district, they're tired of this overspending. They want government to live within their means. They want a commonsense approach, not an extreme approach. There is no spending itch \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  won't scratch. And so they are tired of the inflation, they're tired of the high gas prices, they're tired of what they're seeing with diesel, the shortages coming on. They want to change and they're going the see that change November 8th.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Scott, we are very excited for your race. And Stephen, before we go, FOX has obtained video showing a group of Venezuelans crossing illegally into El Paso in Texas, and then being pushed back by Border Patrol agents with pepper balls. This has, not surprisingly, gotten backlash from the left that the pepper balls were actually used. Is this the new whipping scandal, Stephen?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, it's the same thing, the same smear campaign. And of course, Biden is nowhere to be seen defending the agents that he put in this position by having his open-border policy. And ultimately this issue, in my opinion, is going to be the biggest issue on the mines of tens of millions of voters come Tuesday, because they understand that if we don't have a border, if we have the kinds of chaos and violence and bloodshed we are seeing in this country because of her open border, there won't be a country anymore. That's existential, that's a matter of survival. And millions of voters are going to show up in every part of this country to say we want a border.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2  and Stephen Miller, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Do I have a fun one next. Biden the imploding gasbag. My take on the president's purposeful destruction of American energy and his ridiculous so-called solution. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's time for these companies to stop more profiteering, meet their responsibilities to this country, and give the American people a break and still do very well. My team will work with Congress to look at these options that are available to us and others.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's the perfect soundbite to kick off my examination of Biden, the imploding gasbag.\par \par \uc2 Let's lay out some of the actual truth here. First, Biden wants gas prices to go higher. He and the left agree that Americans use too much gasoline. So they've implemented a series of policies designed to drive gas prices higher.\par \par \uc2 Second, Biden knew, or he should have known, that picking a fight with Russia would lead to even higher gas prices. He picked the fight anyway. And now he refuses to seek peace.\par \par \uc2 Third, Biden knew or should have known that if gas prices went up, oil and gas producers would make more money. That's just how the law of supply and demand works.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, Biden has spent this summer shrinking the Strategic Petroleum Reserve in a desperate attempt to lower prices before the midterms, but those effects were always going to be temporary and would lead to even higher prices once markets realized that the federal government needed to replenish the reserve.\par \par \uc2 So there is a direct and clear link between Biden's actions and higher gas prices. But now Biden wants to threaten the oil and gas companies and say that they'll be punished unless they produce more oil and gas. Why is there any reason to believe these threats are going to work? The energy companies, they know that Biden wants higher gas prices and that he'll block drilling, and soon -- and he'll do as soon as he thinks that he could do so without paying any political prices.\par \par \uc2 If Biden didn't want higher gas prices, he should have adopted very different policies from the very beginning. To think that he could keep the same policies and somehow generate vast amounts of new oil and gas production, simply through threats, it's ludicrous.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Robert Bryce, host of the "Power Hungry" podcast and author of "A Question of Power, Electricity and the Wealth of Nations." Robert, what I left unmentioned is the thing that could further cripple the U.S. economy, of course the looming diesel shortage. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BRYCE, AUTHOR, "A QUESTION OF POWER": Well, right now, Laura -- thanks for having me on -- diesel inventories or the amount of diesel we have in storage is at the lowest level since 2008. And I guess what my message here is, we need energy realism. This country runs on gasoline, diesel fuel, and cheeseburgers, and that's not going to change for decades to some. And unfortunately, this administration wants to beat up on the oil industry. The reality, nearly everything we buy at the grocery store, the department store, is delivered by diesel fuel trucks, and now, we are seeing diesel fuel prices accelerating. They're going up very rapidly, particularly in the northeast.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be bad, it's going to increase inflation, and expensive energy is the enemy of the poor. And so I think this is going to just further add fuel to the inflation in the economy. And I say that as not a person. I am not a Republican, I'm not a Democrat. I'm disgusted, but I just don't think this administration is really taking a realistic look at what needs to happen to assure energy security, which means abundant energy that's affordable. And we don't have that right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Back to my point, the higher energy prices, that is the policy. The only reason they're talking about it is because it's going to hurt them politically right now. But here is how John Kirby responded when he was pressed about this diesel fuel shortage issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are we doing to prepare for the winter and to ramp up the supply of diesel?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We have doubled our commitment, the commitment we made in March for natural gas exports to Europe. We doubled that commitment in terms of actual, getting things over there, getting natural gas over there. And we are working with foreign suppliers of natural gas and oil to see if we can't help our European partners diversify their own storage and supplies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Robert, how insane is this? He wants to double down on our exports to Europe during a gas crisis?\par \par \uc2 BRYCE: Listen, Europe needs help, and Europe needs to help themselves. And I was really disappointed. We didn't come here to talk about Europe, but \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , the new prime minister, banning fracking is just ridiculous. This is I have a piece coming out in "The Hill" tomorrow saying this is a gift to Putin. Why would the Brits ban fracking? They need to start drilling if they are serious about energy security.\par \par \uc2 But my bottom line here, Laura, is the world runs on oil. It is easy to hate the oil companies, but if oil didn't exist, we'd have to invent it. It's a miracle substance. And our economy runs on oil, our transportation system depends in particular on diesel fuels. And so what I fear is this could get worse, particularly you had an earlier guest talking about energy prices in the northeast, distillate fuel oil, these are going to go up in price. And so this is a problem, and it's going to continue, I fear, for much to come.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Robert, I think Americans are going to die in their homes because of oil price hikes that are just untenable for most families. Robert, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A big announcement, and it's big. So stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Our Houston townhall last week on the Hispanic move to the GOP was so successful, we decided to do another one. So, the big apple better be ready because THE INGRAHAM ANGLE Retaking America townhall is coming this Friday, November 4th, live at 10:00 p.m. eastern. I'm going to be joined by Dr. Oz, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , J.D. Vance, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Adam Laxalt, and other surprises. So make sure, if you're not around, come one. Set your DVR. You're not going to want to miss it. Our final townhall before all the good news starts rolling in on election night. I cannot wait. I'm so excited. I'm such a political geek.\par \par \uc2 "GUTFELD!" next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221102eib100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Decisive GOP House Win Expected, Toss-Up In Senate; GOP Gains House Momentum, Senate Up For Grabs; Candidates Enter Final Week Of Intense Campaigning; Fetterman Blames Inflation On Trump Tax Cuts; Report: At Least One Cop Shot In Newark, New Jersey; 3-Year-Old Among 14 Hurt In Chicago Shooting; Cities See Deadly Shootings As Election Day Nears; Ohio Senate Race Locked In Tight Battle One Week Out; Ohio Voters Weigh In One Week From Election Day; Pence Campaigns For Kemp In Tight Georgia Gov Race\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bret Baier, Bill Hemmer, Rich Edson, Green, Mike  Tobin, Gianno Caldwell, Mark Meredith, Geraldo Rivera, Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 8,837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): According to the bail application request, an officer grabbed his hand, police recovered a loaded pistol from his pocket. An analysis showed that the pistol was the one that was used, October 9th outside of our home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, so Green is being held on 750,000 cash, 1.5 million bond, 7.5 million partially secured bond. He's due back in court on Friday. That does it for us. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm John Roberts, we'll see you again tomorrow. THE STORY with Martha in Ohio starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): One week from election night and tonight, THE STORY is live in Columbus, Ohio. Tonight, a townhall event with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance. They're locked in a very tight Senate race, which could go either way. So, this afternoon, Bret and I will sit down with some of the people who will be part of tonight's town hall and we'll find out what's on their minds.\par \par \uc2 That's right, everybody. THE STORY coming to you live from Ohio's capital of Columbus, where a short time from now we will bring Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance up close and personal with voters, voters who have the power to flip control of the Senate back to Republicans possibly, seven days from now. I'm joined now for this hour by Bret Baier as we preview our live townhall airing tonight at 6:00 Eastern. But first, we've got these brand-new power rankings that are out today. And the final forecast of the midterm cycle is out as well. So, Bret, great to have, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here in Columbus. And we're looking forward to this afternoon. And tonight, we've been sort of poring over all the issues that we'll be discussing in this town hall this --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: May cover everything, they cover everything that we've been talking about in this election. And these voters really want answers to make their decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's great to give these two contenders an opportunity to be face to face with voters on a national platform and talk about these issues. So, that's what we're going to see tonight. But our good friend Bill Hemmer --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right now, live at the billboard in New York. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Hey guys, tell my fellow Buckeyes I said hello there, back in Ohio. You mentioned the power ranking just so our viewers know right here, we think Republicans are solid in 223 seats, which is above the majority to a team. We think Democrats are solid 186 that the toss up is really where you find the drama for next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at this little setup we have here, Republicans to the right, Democrats to the left, all these yellow toss-up seats, we believe as of today, is on the Democratic side of the ledger, they have a much higher hurdle based on our estimations for next Tuesday night. Let me just come forward a couple times here to give you a little better lay of the land right now.\par \par \uc2 You see this number here on the far-right? Among those toss-ups, if Republicans have a very good night, you could see a gain of what? 35 seats and take up to 249. I mean, that's a possibility based on some of the polling we're seeing, and based on some of the money that's been moved around right now, in a lot of these districts. On the Senate side, we think Democrats are solid in 47, we think Republicans are solid in 49.\par \par \uc2 The toss-up in yellow in the middle, the little area that we'll show you on the map in a moment, that's going to hold a ton of drama for us next Tuesday night. However, Democrats right now in the Senate, they've got this possibility here now of keeping the majority with 51 Senate seats, that, that still is out there as a possibility and a lot of Democrats are hanging their hat on that.\par \par \uc2 Why do we say that? How can we say that? Obviously, the ones in yellow are clear toss-ups, but there are different shades of red and blue on this map. And they could, they could, depending on what happens here. Go, really, tilt the balance either way. I'm talking about Wisconsin. We think Ron Johnson's got the lead.\par \par \uc2 Talking about North Carolina and we think \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  has the lead, where you guys are there in Ohio. We think J.D. Vance has the lead, but these other states, Pennsylvania, Georgia, Arizona and Nevada, it's going to be something to watch come election night. I want to wrap this up here and just take you back to, to Ohio here because this has been a fought over seat now for back-to-back campaigns, right?\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and back-to-back elections won the state by eight points - - 2016 and then again in 2020 by eight points. 50, I'm -- you're right to the number Martha: 53.3, 45.3. You guys right here in Columbus, Franklin County, you're on this little blue island, right, smack dab in the middle of state? That was a county that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by almost 65 percent of the vote just two years ago. So, looking forward to tonight, enjoy and I'll be watching. Bret and Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're really sorry about your bangles, by the way.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I appreciate that, but you know --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Four subject.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: -- the Bucks fans are elated.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They are, indeed. We've met some of them already. Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it is the race that could flip the Senate power back to Republicans. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Lieutenant Governor of Pennsylvania; Dr. Mehmet Oz making their final pitches to Pennsylvania voters one week out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: If you're thinking more about protecting the criminals than the innocent, than the victims, which again, what Fetterman did was to push against the other members of the parole board to get murderers out.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: He still believes that you know, with the same kind of abortion thoughts with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who believed that local political leaders are the need to be part of the decision between women and their doctors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I believe that was Mehmet Oz that said that in their debate the other night. Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson is on the ground with reaction from voters in Bucks County, Pennsylvania today. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, good afternoon, Martha and Bret, and what you're looking at here in Bucks County, it's a swing county here in Eastern Pennsylvania. You look at the last couple of Senate races here. One year goes for the Democratic candidate a few years before then goes for the Republican candidates. We've been talking to voters over the last couple of days, who've told us what's important to them. This midterm election season.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The border, and immigration, and inflation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Cost of living, you know with the medical and food everything going up, cost of living. Because you know, it's getting harder and harder, you know, to you know, support yourself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I always tell my friends; we have a Democrat already in the Senate. We don't need another one. Just soon have a Republican.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voiceover): Both campaigns say they're focusing on this county. Dr. Mehmet Oz has an event here in a few hours in Bucks County. This is what Oz's campaign saying about this county: "We're gaining support from Republicans, Democrats and independents across the Commonwealth who are tired of the damaging Biden-Fetterman agenda, which has resulted in skyrocketing crime and people struggling to make ends meet."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Fetterman campaign aide pointed out that Democrats have rallied here for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Bucks County, he has done so throughout the campaign they say, and they say they have this initiative, Republicans for Fetterman trying to get Republicans to cross over and vote their way. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson live in Langhorne, Pennsylvania. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM So, Steven Law is the President of the Senate Leadership Fund, the leading Super PAC for Senate Republicans it has raised hundreds of millions of dollars this midterm cycle and is pouring millions into the Pennsylvania race and other places as well. Steven, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 I think you know what a lot of people would be curious to ask you is where you feel good right now in the Senate races and where you're putting sort of a little bit of maybe risky money, but you think you have a shot?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN LAW, PRESIDENT, SENATE LEADERSHIP FUND: Well, Pennsylvania is at the top of that list. It's the largest single investment we're making. We put an additional 12 million into the race in just the last few weeks because if we win Pennsylvania, we will win the Senate majority. And Pennsylvania has turned out to be the turnaround story of the 2022 cycle.\par \par \uc2 Everybody counted Dr. Oz out. He's become the comeback kid. He's surging right now. And several months ago, no one had laid a glove on \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . And now, he's on defense, on crime and on inflation, and on his health, and we think we're going to be well-poised to pick up the seat. And if we do, as I said, we will take back the majority next week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Steven, Bret Baier here, you know, Lieutenant Governor Fetterman was on CNN this morning, he was asked about inflation. Take a listen to his answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST: What do you think the biggest cause of inflation is, and should the Biden administration be doing more?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I just do, I think that simply is, also we had this talk about the trillions in massive tax, tax cuts to the corporate tax structure as well -- true. You know, trillions of dollars that have added to the deficit. And, and now, they still want to support those as well, true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lot of people have talked about his health, about his debate performance, but when it comes down to it, isn't it the control of the Senate that's moving this race, whether it's Democrats or Republicans, that that's really it, not the individual battle on the ground in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 LAW: But a lot of different things. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's answer just goes to show that he doesn't really have a clear response to what most voters are thinking in Pennsylvania and all across the country, which is that the reckless spending by the Biden administration over the last few years is what triggered this vast surge in inflation and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  among all Democrats, completely supported it. In fact, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wanted to spend trillions more than Biden did, which would have made inflation even worse.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Steven, thank you very much. A lot on the line and we'll be watching the outcome, of course, on Tuesday night. Thanks very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 LAW: Thanks, and good luck tonight in Ohio at your town hall. We'll be watching too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great. Thank you very much, Steven, good to see you. So, breaking just moments ago, this from the Supreme Court they have refused to block a subpoena for Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  to testify in an election interference investigation that's ongoing in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham argued that the Speech and Debate Clause in the Constitution protected him from being forced to testify. The justices say that he can still go back to a lower court to object to specific questions. His grand jury testimony is set for November 17th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's interesting. I mean, it's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's something that he can argue about the specific questions, but he has to show up November 17th. So, that advances the ball in that case down there in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, indeed, it does. So, coming up, a brand-new poll from Gallup finds more Americans than ever believe that crime is up where they live. And so, it is a key issue, of course, for voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As violent crime rock several cities on Halloween night, including Chicago, where a 3-year-old is among 14 shop.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gianno Caldwell, his brother, was shot and killed earlier this summer in Chicago. There he is with his little brother, Christian, he joins us when our special coverage from Ohio continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quarter after the hour, and this story is breaking right now. A police officer, reportedly shot in New Jersey by a suspect with a long gun. Another officer is said to be injured. They were reportedly serving a warrant at home in Newark, which is just outside of New York City when someone opened fire, possibly from a roof or a window. No word yet on the condition of these officers, and so far, no suspect is in custody.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Correspondent Lauren Green is reporting live from New York on this. Lauren, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Bret, Martha, you know, this situation in New York -- Newark is developing.\par \par \uc2 GREEN (voice-over): And several law enforcement departments, including SWAT, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and ATF are now on the scene.\par \par \uc2 Let's take some aerials here. We can see people, a police on rooftops and on the ground with streets blocked off in all directions. The neighborhood on lockdown, of course.\par \par \uc2 Local reports say one, possibly, two police officers have been shot by a man with a long gun.\par \par \uc2 Now, the conditions of the officers is not yet known. One officer was shot in the leg, another in the neck, according to reports. And they were reportedly serving a warrant out of home in the state's largest city when the gunfire erupted.\par \par \uc2 And According to reports, the suspect is not yet in custody. But police say the situation is under control and there is no longer any threat to the local community.\par \par \uc2 The governor, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , tweeted that he was monitoring the situation. He tweeted this. He says, "I am aware of and closely monitoring an unfolding situation in Newark. I am in touch with local authorities and the State is providing resources as requested. We will continue to support local law enforcement and ensure that all residents are safe."\par \par \uc2 We have calls out and a crew in route to the scene and we hope to have more details soon and we'll have an update as soon as possible. Bret, Martha?\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): All right. Lauren Green, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): And three children, one just 3 years old, doing among more than a dozen injured in a drive by shooting on Chicago's west side. It happened on Halloween. Eye witnesses describe complete chaos. They say it was like a war scene in this part of Chicago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In moments, Fox News political analyst Gianno Caldwell, who lost his teenage brother to the crime crisis this summer.\par \par \uc2 First, we head to senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 . Mike, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good afternoon, Bret and Martha. It's not going to surprise to anyone that there was another drive by shooting in Chicago, and certainly, not going to surprise anyone that had happened, the troubled west side. This mass shooting happened about 9:30 p.m. last night.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): People had gathered for a vigil.\par \par \uc2 Police say it was an SUV that pulled up. Two gunman opened fire and sprayed bullets indiscriminately into the crowd.\par \par \uc2 The whole thing was over in about three seconds. 14 people were injured. Witnesses say one man was shot in the head, one person was hit by a car in the chaos.\par \par \uc2 Among the victims, a 3-year-old boy and 11-year-old girl, and a 13-year-old boy. The rest of the victims are said to be adults.\par \par \uc2 The vigil was for a cancer victim. So, police are not immediately linking the shooting to gang retaliation. But because of the shooting last night, police now need to be ready for retaliation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROWN, CHICAGO POLICE SUPERINTENDENT: We're deploying extra police resources in the area to make sure that we can prevent any retaliation if that's something that's real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: And just hours after that hail of gunfire and four miles to the east of the mass shooting, the security detail assigned to Chicago's Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  got into a gunfight as they happen to onto an armed robbery.\par \par \uc2 No one was shot, no one was arrested. One officer received minor injuries. All of the officers involved in the shooting are taken off the street while a gunfight is investigated.\par \par \uc2 And Houston, another hip hop star was killed by gunfire. A rapper who went by the name, Takeoff was shot and killed. He is from the trio Migos. It happened about 2:30 in the morning at a party in a bowling alley.\par \par \uc2 The other members of the trio were not injured, including Offset who is famously married to Cardi B. But two others on the scene were injured.\par \par \uc2 Philadelphia saw four separate shootings and three people killed.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): A man was killed in Brooklyn, and another man was killed outside of a nightclub in California. Bret, Martha.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  live in Chicago. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now, Fox News political analyst Gianno Caldwell. His 18-year-old brother Christian was gunned down in Chicago on June 24th. His killer is still on the loose.\par \par \uc2 Gianno, it's good to have you with us. It is a -- it's a tragic story for your family, and a tragic story for many more families after this weekend in Chicago, sadly.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GIANNO CALDWELL, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But we are in politics season.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And there is an effort to sort of suggest that this is all just kind of as conspiracy to make it look bad. This is \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , the governor of New York talking about this. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: These are master manipulators. They have this conspiracy going all across America to try and convince people that in democratic states are not as safe. Well, guess what? They're also not only election deniers, they're data deniers.\par \par \uc2 It's the Republican states where they have almost no restrictions on guns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gianno, what do you say to that, that this is all sort of a cooked up data conspiracy to make Democrat leadership look bad?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, first and foremost, Martha, Bret, I got to tell you, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  should be ashamed of himself, to be used like that, knowing that black people are dying on a daily basis and cities like Chicago, New York, Philadelphia, California, across the country.\par \par \uc2 He allowed this woman, the governor of Michigan to come onto his show and lie.\par \par \uc2 She is lying and many other members on the left are lying, and gas lighting individuals to believe that they can't believe their own eyes. They can't believe the stories of their families who've been murdered, who've been stabbed, who've been raped.\par \par \uc2 Crime is up all across the country. It's really up in Chicago, you're talking almost 40 percent, 35 percent to be exact, crime overall up. Since January 1st, 2016, up into December 31st, 2020. You got over 3,000 people who have been murdered, Martha, Bret. You got over 13,000 shot.\par \par \uc2 We see that what happened last night with a three year old, a 14 year old. But we also seen these on very recent weekends, whether it's children, infants, who are murdered.\par \par \uc2 And then, to have individuals who went on television in 2020, and said, we have to defund the police. Who said that we don't need the police, we should send counsellors? They should be ashamed of themselves.\par \par \uc2 And they have blood on their hands, they have a price to pay. Because my family is now paying the price, Martha. I am pissed the hell off from what I'm seeing from many of these folks, who have caused this derelict of duty when it comes to the prosecutors, whether it be \uc2 Kim Foxx\uc2 , or George Gascon in California, or anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 We need to bring back law and order. And I am happy to see that this is an issue that voters care about, because it impacts us all.\par \par \uc2 And anybody could be next. And that's why it's so important. This is not a black issue. It's not a Hispanic issue. It's an issue that America needs to attack head on. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs to speak out and say that enough is enough. And he isn't doing that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gianno, thank you very much. And Governor Hochul from New York is obviously in a much tighter race now than she was in recent weeks and months, largely because of the crime issue.\par \par \uc2 Gianno, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Gianno.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hours from now, Ohio voters will have a chance to press candidates on crime.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The issue we just talked about in the toss up race for Senate at our live Fox town hall with Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've got the feeling in my gut from folks I associated with that it's going to be J.D. Vance. The biggest problem was, he kind of flip flop in the beginning, you know, that he didn't like Trump. Now, I liked Trump, and that doesn't matter to me.\par \par \uc2 What matters to me is what he stands for now. And what he's going to represent for us and -- when he goes to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that this election and the number one issue spot, it is the economy. And here in Ohio, some voters will soon get the chance to ask candidates how they plan to cool the sky-high prices.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance join our town hall, a little bit later, Republican Rob Portman, the Senator is retiring so this would be a flip for the Democrats if Ryan was able to win.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith, live here in Columbus with the preview. Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret and Martha, good afternoon to you guys. Also, from here in Columbus, as you mentioned right now, there is a bitter battle underway between Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  as well as Republican J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Both men ready to be Ohio's next senator, but also trying to run as the anti-establishment candidate that may be a little bit difficult for the congressman because he didn't serving in Congress since 2003.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): The latest polls show that this race remains neck- and-neck.\par \par \uc2 Ryan had a lead in the polls ever so slightly just a few points up until a few weeks ago. But the latest spectrum, Siena College poll shows that both men are tied.\par \par \uc2 During tonight's town hall, we expect Congressman Ryan to talk about all of his efforts to bring jobs back to his community of Youngstown, Ohio. And while he says he is a proud Democrat, he's also made it clear, he's not thrilled with everything coming out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't want to see President Biden run again in 2024. Today, he was out campaigning with Ohio Democratic Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For his part, J.D. Vance beat out a crowded field of different Republican candidates to win the nomination. He also has the endorsement of former President Trump, which certainly generated some excitement in his campaign early on and we've seen that continue to impact his race.\par \par \uc2 Trump is even to come back here to Ohio right before the election. He's going to be holding a rally in Dayton for Vance.\par \par \uc2 Today, former Congresswoman \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , who recently left the Democratic Party, she endorsed Vance via tweet. She put online, "\uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  represents everything that is wrong with the warmongering Washington establishment. I'm endorsing Vance because he knows the cost of war, and that our government exists to serve the people, not the other way around."\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): And then, just about an hour and a half ago, we also heard about another endorsement. This one coming from Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who's going to be leaving Congress after her term because she lost her primary.\par \par \uc2 She is made it clear she is not a fan of J.D. Vance, and she's throwing her support behind Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . She says, she would vote for Ryan if she lives here in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But as you both know, that this race is likely to come down more to issues than endorsements. And I'm sure we'll hear a lot about the issues a little bit later on tonight. Bret, Martha?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Interesting to hear that from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , what lane she is carving out. Let's play that sound bite because we have it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who do you prefer in this race?\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I would not vote for JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So if you were a Buckeye, Buckeye State voter, you'd be voting for \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I would.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, this is interesting. I mean, obviously, first of all, in midterm elections overall, we don't see surrogates moving the needle with endorsements that much. But with somebody like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , if you look to Arizona and Kari Lake, she's used it to raise money on that Cheney endorsement, saying thank you. Because she's had people come out in support of her candidacy.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is taking on the Trump endorsed candidates. And in her role, I don't know if that moves the needle inside Ohio that much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's interesting because we talk about the big numbers and majority in the House, majority in the Senate. But all of these races are local and we've seen \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  really trying to hone to the middle. And he's complained that he hasn't gotten real support from the Democrat Party, that the money's going other places. And he claims that they've abandoned him. JD Vance says he doesn't think that's actually true. He thinks Ryan, you know, has done OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, yes. Obviously, if Ryan was even to come really close, that would be -- if they managed to win in Ohio would be a huge upset because this is a Republican leaning state. You look at those polls, and they're tight. But you look at Governor DeWine, who's really running ahead of his Democratic opponent, for governor.\par \par \uc2 Tonight's going to be interesting, though. You know, as Ryan tries to distance himself from the Biden administration, his voting record is not. And that will be something that obviously some of these voters are going to want to hear about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we spoke with people on the street today, since both of us got here. It's interesting to get the feel for what's going on. And people -- I had touch a couple of people today who wanted to see a different tone in politics, that they're tired of all of the chaos and the angst surrounding politics. And also people who just said, I can't take it. Everything is so expensive right now. So inflation and sort of the tenor of politics are kind of the two big themes that were talked about this morning.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, and as we talk about the race from afar, and we try to analyze what's coming out of it, these questions are hitting all of those issues, like right honing in. And we're not saying what you should ask, they're coming to us saying this is what's important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're going to speak to some of the folks that are going to be here later tonight, and get a taste of this townhall crowd and these Ohio voters. And just sort of ask them you know, we got a small group of the folks who are coming tonight and said would you like to come early and sort of just talk to us about what's generally on your mind. And there they are waving away. So Bret and I will go down and we'll talk to them and get a little bit of a preview of what you're going to see more of tonight when those candidates are standing in front of them. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Anytime we go to the grocery store, everything costs more. When I drive to work, it's an hour from here. I work over to Department of Education. I mean, the cost of gas goes up and, you know, sometimes you're barely getting by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All righty. Well, this is exciting. We are in Columbus, Ohio. It's great to be on the road and great to be alongside Bret Baier. And we have a preview of tonight's townhall here in Ohio. We're speaking with some of the voters who will be in the audience with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. What do they want to hear from the Senate candidates, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , Republican JD Vance. Thank you all for coming early. You're the warm up act here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But we're going to also have Geraldo who's going to weigh in after the panel with his thoughts because I grew up, you know, with Geraldo although as a New Yorker. But for the past 10 years, Geraldo, he's been living in Cleveland with his beautiful wife, Erica. And so he's a Midwesterner now so he's going to join us afterwards with some of those thoughts.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I'd like to start, first of all, thank you all for coming in a couple of hours early here today. We really appreciate that. Let me just ask a show of hands. Do you think the country is headed in the right direction? Or let's do it this way, do you think the country is headed in the wrong direction?\par \par \uc2 Almost everybody. Half a hand over there but maybe a little bit. So who would like to tell me why?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, ma'am. Kimberly?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY BROWN, OHIO VOTER: Yes. Well, I believe that, you know, no one is really paying attention, number one, to post-COVID. We know exactly what happened when COVID erected. And I believe that, you know, people talk a good game but when it comes to actually fixing the economy, nobody is doing that. You gave away a whole lot of money that people probably didn't need. So basically, now in our economy, you got people who don't want to work.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of businesses have shut down, they're not going to reopen and nobody's paying attention to that. And that goes hand in 