g something that will prepare you to join the workforce, you know, fixing our high school education, making sure people are ready to enter their workforce, and that there are jobs ready for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Joe, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to ask you what you think about these two candidates, so yes. You know, what do you what do you think about \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and JD Vance having watched them campaigning over the last many months?\par \par \uc2 BJORKMAN: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has spent his entire career really fighting for the people of Ohio, working to bring jobs to our state, working to make sure that, you know, we bring the union, good paying union jobs to our state, something that we desperately need. And there's no better way to I think, look at that than the clean energy revolution. We're talking about the opportunity to take action on climate change and bring jobs and investment into our state.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, JD Vance is coming back from California where he spent most of his adult life working with millionaires, not working for the people of Ohio. And he's saying that there is no climate crisis. We don't need this investment. We don't need the Inflation Reduction Act in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 I find that hard to believe when we have thousands of jobs coming to our state. Good paying union jobs in Toledo, Youngstown, and electric vehicle manufacturing, and solar manufacturing and battery manufacturing for electric vehicles. JD Vance will not deliver those jobs but \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has shown time and time again that he's going to go to the Senate and he's going to fight for Ohioans, not for the Democratic Party and not for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's a mix of people here. John, you're a Vance supporter. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: I am.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And why is that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN, OHIO VOTER: I think that one of the things that I'm concerned about is what we've seen under what I call