ut the bad people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to hear from a lot of you tonight. We really appreciate you coming early.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you so much. It's going to be really interesting to hear the answers to your questions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Thanks for your thoughtful discussion. And we really appreciate all of you coming in early.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Geraldo who has been listening to all of this. And he's been watching politics and culture for many, many years as a correspondent at large. He lives in Cleveland, Ohio now, about two hours from here. Geraldo, what do you think as you listen to these voters who have joined us today?\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT AT LARGE: Well, first of all, Martha. Bret, welcome to the Buckeye State. Go Cavs. Go Browns. Too bad Bengals, you know, it's two different states. It's a tale of two states up here. We have our own version of the coastal elite. You know, the suburbs of the big city of Cleveland, which is a majority black city. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  should do well up here, down where you are with the exception of Columbus and OSU, and the other college towns. That's where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  racked up huge, huge votes. He won by eight percentage points in the last two elections here actually.\par \par \uc2 It is I think -- crime is very big, the economy huge, abortion, as the young lady mentioned, is on the minds particularly of the progressive suburbs. It will be interesting. I didn't like JD Vance at the beginning because he switched positions on Trump. Trump called him and ass kisser. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  had his best moment in the debates when he said you don't need an ass kisser in Ohio, needed an ass kicker. So I am shocked that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has made it as competitive as it is with the Trump voters here in Ohio. We'll see how it goes but it has been very, very competitive.\par \par \uc2 I think one thing that has not been mentioned. I would just add that we have an extremely popular governor here. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , he's brought Intel into Ohio. He's brought Honda into Ohio. He's a multi-term. I don't really know, I forget how many terms. Wonderful man, got to know him well. Republican, he's up by double digits over \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2  who was supported by my wife, Erica. I think that Governor DeWine will carry a lot of the Republican ticket and maybe even JD Vance, Martha and Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Geraldo, this state, as you mentioned, has leaned Republican in recent elections. But this race is close. What is your sense of what turns this race? What is the issue? You know, we talk about inflation and right track wrong track, can somebody like JD Vance say \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has voted 100% of the time with the Biden administration, thereby there's a, you know, an effect of that.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: It's possible. You know, I saw -- it could be something trivial. I saw a spot, read a couple of days ago where \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  was throwing a football with Bernie Kosar, the Cleveland Brown great quarterback back when Erica was in high school. It could be something as simple as that, that feeling that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is a hometown guys worked his whole life in that Youngstown Area, trying to get jobs into Youngstown.\par \par \uc2 But JD Vance, very charming in his own way. You know, he went to Yale Law School had Amy Chua of the Tiger Mom as his professor, as did my daughter, Simone, you know. So I have that kind of psychic connection to her maybe. But I think it's going to be something simple. I think it's, you know, couple of weeks ago would have given Ryan, you know, a very, very strong chance of victory.\par \par \uc2 Now so much. I think that the momentum has shifted more to the natural Republican to the red side. So I think that timing is everything in life. I don't know what else is going to happen in next seven days. I'm delighted that you and Martha are in town listening to the voters. You get to see their vibe. But this is a state that could very well decide who controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo, thank you. Great to have you with us, Geraldo Rivera joining us today from Cleveland. And again, thanks to all of you, and we'll see you all later tonight as our Democracy 2022 Coverage continues with a deep dive into the battleground state of Georgia. We're going to head there for a few minutes where former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I'm going to tell you something. This may not be the most important election of our lifetime, this may actually be the most important election in the life of our nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , is in Georgia right now making two stops in support of Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . Latest polls show Kemp has a comfortable lead over his challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  there, but the possibility of a runoff always a reality in Georgia. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie reporting live from Cumming, Georgia. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Martha. They just wrapped up their first campaign stump here in Cumming, Georgia and now about to begin one with the former vice president in the nearby town of Gainesville. The former vice president praising Kemp for promoting freedom and free enterprise. He used the example of Kemp's handling of the pandemic. You may recall that two years ago what he did was considered a very controversial decision that even then President Trump was uncomfortable with.\par \par \uc2 Two years later Kemp is embracing it as part of his campaign platform and it's winning the praise of the former vice president. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Georgia was not one of the first states to reopen its economy and the shutdowns, Georgia was the first state in America to open up again. And Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  led the way.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Looking at the other camp, former President Obama join \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  for a rally last week. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with lower popularity ratings has so far avoided the Georgia campaigns, but in an interview on \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 's The Daily Show, Abrams defended her ties to national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF GEORGIA: The resources that have come to Georgia, the billions of dollars that have kept the state afloat came because of federal Democrats not because of the current governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Recent polls show Abrams trailing Kemp but her campaign says heavy early voter turnout numbers could tip the scales in their favor. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jonathan, thank you very much, Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 So before we say goodbye, I'm going to ask our great group here. One more question. So raise your hands if you believe that inflation is the most important issue to you. About five or six. Is crime the most important issue to you? No. What's the most important issue to you? Shout it out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The direction of the country, the control by the left of disinformation, big tech and government working together to censor people who have a different opinion. This is unAmerican.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Real quick.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The climate change is a crisis that is an existential crisis, but it's also a huge economic opportunity for Ohio. Let's bring those jobs to our state. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , we'll do that. JD Vance will never fight for us\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks guys. More from all of them later, we'll look forward to it. Thanks for being with us tonight. That is THE STORY for this Tuesday, November the first. But THE STORY goes on and it continues this evening when Bret and I see you at 6:00 o'clock. Do not miss it. The townhall live here in Columbus with JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221102eib100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Ohio Town Hall with Rep. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  (D-OH) and J.D. Vance (R), Ohio Senatorial Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bret Baier, Brit Hume \par \uc2 11,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (voice-over): One week from election night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (voice-over): And Ohio's open Senate seat is up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: We have to be committed to bringing it home.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: We're going to send this guy home. We're going to take this country back.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a battle between seasoned politician Democratic Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  had his chance. It hasn't worked out. It's time for new leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And lawyer, author and political newcomer Republican J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Let's get to work.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: He's not going to win. We're going to win this race and they're going to get more and more desperate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Both candidates are here to answer the tough questions tonight from voters in their state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the Ohio town hall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome to Columbus, Ohio. I'm Bret Baier with a great crowd.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have a great crowd here tonight. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome to a special edition of "Special Report" with limited commercial interruptions, thanks to our sponsor this hour, Tunnel to Towers.\par \par \uc2 We are coming to you tonight live from The Fives event venue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance will both join us live here in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Both nominees will have equal time. And our audience of Republicans and Democrats and independents will be asking them questions throughout.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First, we want to give you a look at some of the other headlines today.\par \par \uc2 The latest FOX News power rankings shows Republicans with 49 possible seats in the Senate after next week's midterms. Those are the projections, Democrats 47, four races we call toss-ups. We have shifted two contests toward Republican contests -- candidates, rather, Washington state still considered likely Democrat. Florida's now in the likely Republican column.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The House forecast is 223 Republican, with 186 Democrat; 26 are still in that toss-up column. We have six races in this recent power rankings that just came out today that have moved towards the Democrats, and 12 that you see on the right-hand side of your screen have been deemed to have moved towards Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In governor's races, six are considered toss-ups. Two of them, Pennsylvania and Oklahoma, are moving toward Democrats. New York is moving toward the Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We are releasing brand-new FOX News polls tonight.\par \par \uc2 Republican Kari Lake is now in the lead in the Arizona governor's contest, and Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is one point behind, so it's a very close race there.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also, in the Arizona Senate race, it is tightening as well. Republican challenger Blake Masters has moved within two points in our latest poll tonight of Democratic incumbent Senator Mark Kelly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And, in Wisconsin, incumbent Republican Ron Johnson is up by three to Democrat Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, a White House spokesperson says the U.S. is concerned about threats to Saudi Arabia tonight from Iran and will not hesitate to respond if necessary.\par \par \uc2 This comes after The Wall Street Journal reported that Saudi Arabia has shared intelligence with the U.S. warning of an imminent attack from Iran on targets in the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has lifted a temporary hold on Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's testimony in a Georgia investigation of the possible illegal interference in the 2020 election by then-President Trump and his allies.\par \par \uc2 The High Court left no legal impediments in the way of Graham's appearance before a special grand jury, which is now scheduled for November 17.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The markets today, the Dow lost 80, the S&P 500 fell 16, the Nasdaq was off 97.\par \par \uc2 OK, J.D. Vance is standing by live. We will speak to him near the bottom of the hour. And there you see him live.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We wanted to get you updated on all the headlines. And now let's go.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you. Welcome to Ohio.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Our first question is from Valerie from Deerfield.\par \par \uc2 Valerie, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hello, Congressman. Thanks for coming.\par \par \uc2 Can you look me in the face and tell me that government expenditures on green energy subsidies through the Inflation Reduction Act that increase our national debt are in any way lessening my burden at the gas station and the grocery store?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I could not say that right now in the present moment.\par \par \uc2 I think the investments that were coming in to the Inflation Reduction Act, especially what we did in natural gas -- there was a huge natural gas component in the Inflation Reduction Act which I supported and worked to get in there. I think natural gas is the bridge fuel that we need to move forward.\par \par \uc2 I was responsible for helping land two natural gas power plants in my congressional district. We're working on a cracker plant in Eastern Ohio that will create thousands of union construction jobs. So, in the short term, no.\par \par \uc2 I think, when it comes to inflation, we need a tax cut. We need to put money in people's pockets if we're going to weather the storm here, so a tax cut in the short term, but then move to natural gas, all in, streamline. I supported Senator Manchin's permitting bill to streamline permitting.\par \par \uc2 You want to permit some of these projects, it takes five or six years. You have to deal with 11 federal agencies, which is insane. We have got to streamline that process as well. But we do need to move into electric vehicles, solar panels.\par \par \uc2 Just in my congressional district alone, \uc2 Foxconn\uc2  bought the old \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant. The plant was empty. There are now five products in Youngstown, Ohio, outside of Youngstown, Ohio, being made in that plant, a truck, three cars and an electric tractor, all-electric. Across the street is a battery plant.\par \par \uc2 In Toledo, we have solar panels that are being made. They have made hundreds of millions of dollars in investments into Ohio. Honda just announced a $4 billion battery plant here, based on what we're doing moving into the clean energy space.\par \par \uc2 I want Ohio to be the manufacturing powerhouse of the world. If it's not us, it's China. So we have to go all in on these products of the future. But where I think I'm different as a Democrat, I think we go all in on natural gas. How do we create union construction jobs here in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 How do we streamline the permitting process? And then how do we export it to our friends in Europe, so we can stick our finger in the eye of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , this bully and what he's doing in Ukraine, and then eventually get it to China as well? And we can address a lot of our climate goals doing that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, Congressman, your party--\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your party is not there. Your party overall is not there.\par \par \uc2 You have voted with your party, your critics say, 100 percent of the time. So you may push for those things, as Senator Manchin has, but your party overall is not there and is in control.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, we have got too many politics happening.\par \par \uc2 Look, I helped get the natural gas provisions in the Inflation Reduction Act. I publicly supported Senator Manchin's permitting bill to streamline permitting. It was the Republicans, Bret, that deep-sixed the permitting bill. He was able to get almost every Democrat aligned.\par \par \uc2 And this was what Republicans wanted too. Who's not for streamlining government. Who's not for reducing it from five or six years to permit a natural gas or even a clean technology project, streamline it down? Eleven agencies, it's nuts.\par \par \uc2 It was Republicans who pulled the plug on that. I want to stop all the stupid politics here. There's no reason they shouldn't have supported that. And we could be exporting this stuff to our allies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask a question to everybody in the audience here, a show of hands.\par \par \uc2 How many here think inflation and the economy is the number one issue in this race?\par \par \uc2 And you're one of them as well?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm one of them, yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But her -- this woman's question was about the Inflation Reduction Act. And you said, yes, actually, it's not changing your life right now.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: In the short term. I said in the short term.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you voted for trillions of dollars in spending that you're now, I think, saying isn't working right now.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, here's it -- that's not what I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 I'm saying, in the short term, we need a tax cut. We have to put money in people's pockets. Child tax credit, advance it. Earned Income Tax Credit, advance it. Put it in people's bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How are you going to get it through your party?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, you're asking me what I'm -- what I would like to do. I don't have control over everybody else.\par \par \uc2 I think this is the solution. I have been screaming at Democrats too for a year-and-a-half to pass a tax cut. That's the short term. My point was, that's not going to help you today. That's why we need a tax cut. It will help us bring supply chains back. It's going to create tens of thousands of union construction jobs here.\par \par \uc2 Like I said, Ohio is right in the middle all this, because, once we passed that, we saw huge investments into the solar industry. Honda's putting a $4 billion battery plant there. We have a battery plant up in Youngstown. We have five vehicles, soon to be more, at \uc2 Foxconn\uc2 , natural gas in the southeast. Like, Ohio is so primed for this. We just got to stop the stupid politics on this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me--\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you have voted against fracking in the past on federal lands, and you said you wanted it banned.\par \par \uc2 So, now the president is saying that he wants to tax energy companies, a windfall tax, because of the profits that they have made. So, the same kind of thing that you're arguing with green energy, that you have to make that investment now, why not make that investment in making us energy- independent?\par \par \uc2 Because, right now, we are far from it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm 1000--\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm 1000 percent for that, 1000 percent.\par \par \uc2 I'm just saying, you can't -- we -- I don't want -- we don't want to do fracking on federal lands. I'd like to do it wherever else we can, do it in an environmentally conscious way. If we can export this natural gas from Eastern Ohio to Eastern Europe and eventually to China -- China's building one coal-fired power plant a week.\par \par \uc2 If we can export this to them, we will dramatically meet our climate goals and create thousands of union construction in Eastern Ohio. It's a great--\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about the windfall tax that President Biden mentioned, a tax on oil companies' profits? Are you for that?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I haven't looked at exactly what he wants to do. I think the fact that oil companies are making huge profits and having stock buybacks right now, while an average person here is paying nearly $4 a gallon, I think that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 And I think we need to crack down.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We need to crack down on it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to move on. We're going to move on.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, critics say, have you ever seen something that you tax that you get more of it?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: No, probably not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's go to Johnny from Sunbury.\par \par \uc2 Johnny, are you here? Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Congressman Ryan, thank you so much for being here tonight.\par \par \uc2 I'm a senior at Cedarville University studying political science. And one of the things we have been talking about is rising crime. All over the country, we're seeing judges let criminals, not innocent people, but criminals, out on bail with little or -- little or no bail.\par \par \uc2 And community safety is put being put at risk. There's a constitutional amendment on the ballot in Ohio this year about a case that happened in Ohio. So, if you're elected to the Senate, what exactly will you do to address this issue and prevent criminals from being released in our cities with no incentive to prevent them from committing another crime or return to court?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I'm -- that's a great question. Thank you. It takes a lot of guts to stand up here. So I appreciate you doing that.\par \par \uc2 I support making sure that judges have the right in the constitutional amendment -- I'm voting yes on that -- to be able for the -- for the judges to be able to balance their decision with regard to public safety. And crime is an issue. I don't care what anybody says about that. And we need more cops. We need better paid cops.\par \par \uc2 And we need to make sure that we invest into police training. And that's why we put hundreds of millions, billions of dollars into the rescue package for state and local government, so that we can fund the police.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of my career, I have brought back $500 million to the state of Ohio for police, for staffing, for technology. And we also have to figure out, how in the hell are all these criminals getting their hands on all these guns? How does that happen?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: So, again, it's not an either/or thing.\par \par \uc2 That's why I keep saying we got to stop these stupid fights. This is a real problem. Some of the answer is going to be conservative. Some of the answer may be a little more investment and progressive. But we're never going to know if we don't talk to each other.\par \par \uc2 And that's what I want to bring to the Senate for Ohio, the conversation. I'm not going to agree with everybody on everything. I mean, I'm married. We don't -- my wife and I don't agree with every -- each other on everything. Like, you are too.\par \par \uc2 But we're Americans. We have to solve these problems. And that means adult discussions that are thoughtful, and not politicized. And crime is a perfect example of that.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But let me ask you a quick follow-up on that.\par \par \uc2 You have said in the past that you would be for eliminating cash bail, and a lot of people see people getting arrested. The next day, they're back on the street again. You also said that you thought that nonviolent criminals should be released from prison. And a lot of people fall into that nonviolent category, including drug traffickers, including drug possession, including people who distribute child pornography.\par \par \uc2 So all those people should be let out?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, what I'm talking about here is marijuana crimes.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to legalize marijuana. I think--\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you said all nonviolent criminals out, for sure.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You said all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You said all nonviolent criminals out, for sure.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think the focus is marijuana crimes, in my mind, right?\par \par \uc2 We spend -- this may be this stupidest expenditure of federal money and public taxpayer money in history of our country. You get caught with marijuana, then you end up in prison. And that is insane for us. We should take -- we should tax the marijuana, and we should put it into addiction treatment. We should use it to keep fentanyl and these hardcore drugs out of our country, out of our society.\par \par \uc2 We should invest in the shop class. We should invest into early childhood education. There's so many better ways to spend in this money than locking somebody up for marijuana crimes, and especially when -- when white and black people use marijuana equally, black people are six times more likely to go to prison -- like, that's an unfair system.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm sorry, it just doesn't make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, I want to turn to another issue that comes out a lot in this race, your ads, your supporters' ads about the issue of abortion. In Ohio, abortion is banned after 22 weeks. I've asked you on "Special Report" before, what is your limit?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I believe, if it's later in the term, that there should not be an abortion unless there is a medical emergency, like, at that point, you know, people are -- they have a nursery; they have binkies; they have blankets. They have names picked out. They're ready to have a party.\par \par \uc2 If it happens late in term, that's tragedy. It's absolute tragedy. And so I don't think we should have abortions later in the term unless there's a medical emergency. And what's happening now in Ohio, Bret, is sad. Because we see a story or two every single week of people, women, who are in this tragic situation; their life becomes at risk; they have to go to Illinois. Now, J.D. Vance wants a national abortion ban, and he wouldn't be happy with these people going to Illinois. He wants them to have to get a passport and go to Canada. He called rape "inconvenient."\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is after 15 weeks, is what he's talked about? We're going to ask all these questions.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: After 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So what's your number? What's the -- you say you don't want it afterward. Is there a number?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think we go back to Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which--\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Roe v. Wade was in the third term--\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 -- in the third term -- in the third term of Roe v. Wade, you could only do it if there was some kind of medical emergency. We don't want J.D. Vance and Ted Cruz and all these guys in the doctor's office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And do you think that the Democratic bills to codify, so-called codify Roe v. Wade, have some sort of limit in them?\par \par \uc2 Because we haven't seen it. The one that was in the House did not have a limit.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I -- my--\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So do you have a limit?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: My personal view is we go back to Roe v. Wade. We move away from this chaos that we're in now. We had 50 years of case law. We had stability. And now we have women -- and we have doctors, Bret, who won't -- won't perform what they need to perform on a woman whose life is in jeopardy, and then the doctor says, "I'm sorry, I have my own kids at home and I'm not going to prison over this stuff."\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I guess, at what point -- 22 weeks is the law in Ohio right now. That's -- that's more than four and a half months. It's five months pregnant. I mean, five months is a long time of being pregnant. So that's not an OK time to -- 22 weeks? You don't agree with your state law?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I just -- I think we -- no, I don't agree with our state law at all, because, if you get raped in Ohio, it is a state mandated pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you'd have five months to have that abortion in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, you would. But sometimes -- sometimes, if there's a medical necessity; if there's a medical problem, you don't know that until the end. And here, the point is, this is America. This is a country built on freedom, right? And this is the largest governmental overreach into the private lives of individual citizens in the history of our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And -- and, look, you have -- you know, I thought my friends on the other side were, like, against big government, against invasion into the private lives of people.\par \par \uc2 This is the largest thing ever. It completely steals personal freedom and liberty away from women. I think it's anti-American. And I think we need to go back and just codify Roe v. Wade as it was, and let's all move on with our lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we're going to move on.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's -- this is actually a tweet that came in from Paul. And Paul asks, why did Ryan -- "What did Ryan mean about 'kill and confront,'" when you said this in a statement with regard to MAGA, the movement, is what you said specifically. "In today's political climate, shouldn't you apologize and retract that statement?" says Paul.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I will say that this extremist movement absolutely needs to be confronted.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But "kill?"\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think -- well, to kill the movement. And maybe that wasn't a great choice of words. Absolutely confront and absolutely stop the extremist movement happening. And we just saw -- and as I was watching the previous show on Fox, what happened to Paul Pelosi, I think, is an absolute tragedy, OK? I don't care what your politics are. I really don't. I don't care how you feel about \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and her politics or anything. She is the third- ranking constitutional officer in the United States of America -- president, vice president, speaker of the House. Her husband is 82 years old and got beat over the head with a hammer. And that is wrong across the board.\par \par \uc2 But now we have people saying, "Well, it's wrong, but maybe if you're a Democrat." We had right-wing talk radio show hosts saying that they should go bail this guy out. What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jr., posted, he posted a pair of underwear that, you know, a grandpa would wear, with a hammer on it, and said "This is my Halloween uniform. I'm going as Paul Pelosi."\par \par \uc2 That is -- that is an atrocious thing to do.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I don't care -- I'm sorry--\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm sorry -- I'm sorry, I don't care what your politics are. It's not about that. Is this the kind of country we're going to leave to our kids and our grandkids, where it's OK, if you disagree politically with the speaker of the House, that you can go in there and beat up her -- her husband with a hammer? If that was a republican, I would -- I would absolutely condemn it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that's -- that's obviously an extreme situation.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: J.D. Vance said nothing about this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll ask him about when he comes out.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You should ask him, because Rob Portman did, and \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  did, and J.D. Vance was silent. And I just think this is a tragedy, and regardless of politics, we've got to get back to being Americans first.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I -- I think that everybody would agree with you that that was a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I could point to a number of horrific attacks in my home city, in New York, that are also absolute tragedies, where people were beaten to a pulp by crazy people who had just been let out of prison. So, you know, there's a lot of violence going on.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: There is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A lot of violence going on.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But I just want to ask you, what is--\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 What is your definition -- when you say that the MAGA movement should be killed and confronted, who is in that category?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: The guys who stormed the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: They are the lead -- they are the leaders.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 They are -- they're the leaders. This is what I'm saying. They stormed the Capitol on January 6th. They beat up 140 police officers, killed one, OK? They killed one.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD BOOING)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We all--\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We all watched--\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: (inaudible) liar (ph).\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We all watched the video.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Liar (ph).\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We all watched the video.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, guys. Let him speak. Let him speak.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let him finish, please.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And I work -- I'm happy to have this conversation. I'm not afraid to have this conversation. These are the conversations we need to have this in this country. I welcome you guys. Let's just be respectful. But I'm not afraid to stand here and defend my position.\par \par \uc2 On January 6th, 140 -- I sit on the subcommittee that funds the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 . These are my friends. One hundred forty of them got hurt. And some of them still can't go back to work because they were beat up with lead pipes. They were sprayed with pepper spray. They were beaten with flag poles. OK? They were trying to overthrow the government. They were trying to stop the peaceful transition of the government. What else were they doing there? They're going to kill \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , right? They wanted to kill \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Now somebody's beaten up \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband with a hammer. J.D. Vance raised money for the insurrectionists. He made several social media posts to raise money for them. Now, again, I don't care what your politics are. But Americans should say no. We have to say no to that.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to try to get as many questions as we can from the audience.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've got Colin from Portsmouth. Colin from Portsmouth, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you for being here. Fentanyl is coming across the border from Mexico, flooding our communities. And you've accepted that this is a problem. It's a big problem in Portsmouth, where I'm from. But yet you voted against funding for border security. You voted against funding for the border wall. You voted against funding for drug enforcement. And you called for defunding ICE. Why?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I absolutely never called for defunding ICE -- not one time -- not one time.\par \par \uc2 No, you can laugh all you want, not one time, OK? Never. And I have voted to increase funding for border security. I think we need more border patrol, not less. I have a resolution in the House of Representatives that would designate Fentanyl as a weapon of mass destruction.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 So that would allow us -- that would allow us -- that would allow us to use the whole-of-government approach to provide the resources necessary to stop this junk from coming in. We need to be tougher on China, where this comes from. We need to be tougher on Mexico, who processes it and sneaks it over the border and finds a million different ways to get it here.\par \par \uc2 So we need more Border Patrol. I have voted to support a barrier in certain places. The problem with the wall is that El Chapo can dig under it. And people could put ladders up and climb over it. Is it a piece of it? Yes. I started what's called the Border Security Technology Caucus. We need to marshal all of the technology that we have in this country and apply it to the border so that we can keep this junk out.\par \par \uc2 There's 8 billion people in the world. Most of them want to live here. We need an orderly process for how to get them into the United States. And we should be big-hearted enough to take refugees whose daughters may get raped and thrown into the sex trade or sons may get beat up and thrown into gangs -- like, we should be big-hearted enough to do that.\par \par \uc2 But we need an orderly process so people come here the right way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, one thing you did vote for--\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And this is what your Republicans critics said, is that you did vote for tens of thousands of \uc2 IRS\uc2  officials and agents to be added to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 --\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Um-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- instead of sending more Border Patrol to the border. That's what your Republican critics say. And you did vote for that. So how do you explain that?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, this is why I'm glad you're here. Because you have a very smart audience. And -- and I appreciate you. I really do. It's not an either/or. It's not an either/or. We are back -- so 40,000 \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents are going to retire in the next 10 years. So this is over 10 years. We're going to replace those people. But we also have a lot of Fortune 500 companies and a lot of really rich people who have teams of accountants, teams of lawyers; they don't pay any taxes. I think, if you should be able to build yourself and fly yourself into space, you should pay your fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: So we need enough agents -- we need enough agents to be able to go after these billionaires who are avoiding taxes at all cost. There is a protection in there that nobody under 400,000 is even going to get touched. I would never vote to increase taxes, which is another line in this campaign, on working class people. I come from working class people. I represent working class people. I don't want them paying any more in taxes. I've been calling for a tax cut. So a lot -- a lot of things flying around in the campaign, as you know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I understand.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I understand, I'm just -- that's why we appreciate--\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You could do the border and you can do -- you can do going after the billionaires.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are going to keep it exactly to time for each one of you. So we appreciate your time, Congressman Ryan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for all the questions for Congressman Ryan.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we'll be following the race closely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you. We're going to shock the world. We're going to win this one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we come back--\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- we talk live with Republican Senate nominee J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to our Ohio special town hall. In our last segment we talked with Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , running for Senate. Now the Republican Senate nominee, J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us. We have a great crowd here tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, everybody -- and a lot of applause.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's get to our first question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's get to our first question so we can get a lot of questions in with Mr. Vance here. Lisa (ph) from Hilliard, Ohio. Lisa (ph), take it away.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hi. It's Hilliard.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hilliard.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: As a single mom one of my greatest concerns is the high cost of groceries and the high cost of prescription medications. So what will you do to help lower those costs so that people are literally not having to choose between buying food and buying their medications?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure. Sure. Well first of all, thank you, Lisa, for the question and thanks for being here and thank you guys for having me.\par \par \uc2 You know I was raised by a single mom, too. I was raised by my mammaw (ph) in big part, my grandmother, because of some of the issues that we had our family. But I certainly know the struggles that working families are going through. And I'd say a couple of big things, right?\par \par \uc2 So one of the reasons why we have this terrible inflation is because the Biden administration, backed by Tim, supported a lot of borrowing and spending, throwing fuel on the fire which has caused the price of everything to go up.\par \par \uc2 Now we have to remember, Biden said that he wouldn't raise taxes on middle class. But if you are a family right now dealing with the grocery prices and the energy prices, certainly you feel like your taxes are gone up a lot because the cost of living and good life in this country has gone up a lot.\par \par \uc2 The other really crazy thing is that we really went to war against America's energy sector. So, energy goes into everything, right? It goes into the diesel fuel that the trucks use to take our food to the grocery store. It goes into the manufacturing process. And so when you have energy getting so, so expensive, that raises the price of everything else as well. And so I think we get those two things under control. We stop the borrowing and spending, and we actually get -- open up Ohio's energy markets and then we will start to bring those prices under control.\par \par \uc2 I also think that, look, I mean, I'm not going to disagree with the Democrats on everything. I think one of the good things that came out of the Inflation Reduction Act, maybe the only good thing to come out of the Inflation Reduction Act, is that it does empower Medicare to actually negotiate with some of the prescription drug providers on prescription drugs. That's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It brings down costs -- brings down costs for families. And look, you know, we're the greatest country in the world. You should not be able to -- you should not be unable to afford insulin in the greatest country in the world because you can't afford it. That's got to change, and I think we are making good progress there.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: J.D., President Biden has been out on the trail lately saying that if Republicans win that their policies will cause more inflation. Understand that Republicans think totally opposite than that. But to Lisa's point, from Hilliard, is it like the dog catches the car and do you worry that you can actually pass, even if you have the Senate and the House, something that can affect Lisa's life directly?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think we absolutely have to work together, OK? And that's one of the things Tim talks about working together but when Republicans win the majority as I think that we do, we have to act like we have the majority. We have to do things not just talk about doing things. And we have to accomplish some important things for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Now, what can we do immediately to make Lisa's life better? Well, one thing we can do is open up the pipelines and open up our energy industry. That will cause a reduction\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That -- that will cause a reduction in prices pretty immediately. And again, if you stop borrowing, if you stop the spending, you will actually reduce price pressures on the overall economy. That's a big thing that will lead to immediate relief as well.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So immigration of course is a big issue in the Border States and a lot of states across the country but also a factor in this election in Ohio. This evening we hear from a man who wants changes. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Jackson based Larry Kidd owns a staffing firm called :hire in Southeastern Ohio. :Hire helps its clients find employees across the variety of sectors, but recent worker shortages have created major problems for Kidd's business.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KIDD, FOUNDER, PRINCIPAL, AND CEO, :HIRE: There was a talent shortage prior to the pandemic and the pandemic only exasperated that. One of the ways to fix this problem is fix the immigration problems.\par \par \uc2 BAIR (voice-over): Kidd hopes for policies that bring workers from other countries to the U.S. on a legal basis.\par \par \uc2 KIDD: There just simply aren't enough workers in the United States to fill the jobs that are open.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): But he wants to put the GOP Senate candidate on the spot to see what policies Vance has in mind to address worker shortages.\par \par \uc2 KIDD: What's your stance on immigration and how can we help solve the employment issue in the United States?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Great. Well, great question. And first of all, I've come out in favor of -- we're talking about legal immigration here. I have come out in favor of Tom Cotton's the RAISE Act. He's a senator from Arkansas. And here's what the RAISE Act would do. So step back a little bit. What our immigration policy does right now is it ask the question, who do you know, and how -- if you know somebody, then that's how you get into the United States legally.\par \par \uc2 I think the immigration policy in the United States should be about what skills and what attributes do you bring to the table that's going to enrich the entire American nation. That's what the RAISE Act would do. That's what most other First World economies actually do. You let people into your country based on merit. Not on who they know, based on merit and I think that's where we need to take the immigration policy in this country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Now -- now, this is really important, Bret. So, the illegal immigration question -- I know the question was about our legal immigration policy. But the illegal immigration side of this is really important, too because we know that the fentanyl -- and I've been affected in my family very personally by the opioid problem. But when the fentanyl is coming into our country at such an extraordinary degree, brought in by the Mexican drug cartels, that's actually one of the reasons why we have the labor shortage.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we think about it most of all because it kills our citizens, we think about it most of all because it orphans our -- our children, that's obviously very important. It also creates labor shortages because this fentanyl takes people out of the workforce, makes them unable to work in good jobs. So I actually think that the question really if we get control of the illegal immigration, that will actually help this problem as well.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we talked a lot about inflation, about the economy as being the number one and two issues really in this election. How many of you believe that the border is secure in this group?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Show of hands.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is the border secure? Our southern border?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't think --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's one person back there who thinks it is, one person over there thinks it's secure.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: A couple.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, that -- that's pretty phenomenal because you've heard from the applause tonight, we have people who are Democrats here, Republicans here --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and 99 percent of them don't think we have a secure border.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So Loretta (ph) has a question about the border. Loretta (ph), here you go.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes, I think you partially answered this but what would you do to stop the crisis at the southern border, to stop the criminals, the human trafficking, and the drugs from entering? What would you do to stop the drug crisis that's killing our young Ohioans?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Specifically, if you are a senator with a president from another party?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes. So, one, I've had such a good time campaigning across the state of Ohio, but one of the really sad parts is that every single stop that we make I meet somebody who's lost a son, a grandson, a daughter, a granddaughter to the opioid problem. We really got to get control of this. It's killing our people. It's totally unnecessary and it's a totally self- inflicted wound.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things that I think we can do -- and I want to talk about this question, Bret, what can you do with a Democratic president. So two things we really need to do is -- we actually learned over the last six years border walls work. We need to finish the border wall and actually complete the southern border.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Now the other crazy thing is that we know that the drug cartels are actually destabilizing Mexico. Like, our Mexican allies don't like what's going on with the cartels right now. I think they would love it if we declared the cartels a terrorist organization and actually brought the full force of American law against them.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Now, fair, OK, maybe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't want to do some of this stuff. But I actually think there is a good chance because, look, the country is not going in the right direction. I think probably 65 percent of people in the state of Ohio would say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies are not working for the state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think that if we -- if we get Republican majorities in Congress, the Democrats are going to be forced to work with us on a couple of things. OK? And we need to be willing to reach across the aisle and say, look, if you are willing to get things done for the sake of this country, of course we're willing to work with you. The immigration issue and the border crisis and especially the energy crisis I think those are two issues where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and some of the democrats might be willing to do some work with us. I hope they will those are the two biggest crises and they're the two most self-inflicted problems we have in our country right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you this because your critics say that you started, after you wrote your book, which was so famous and well received that you started our Ohio renewal.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And to help people who had drug problems in Ohio. And they say you shut it down shortly after you got the nomination. What do you say to those critics?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, it's one of the things that really frustrates me. I'm not here to beat up on \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . I'm here to talk to all of you and everybody who's watching on TV. He's run millions of dollars in negative ads saying that I took money out of the nonprofit. I put $80,000 of my own money into that nonprofit. OK. And we did help people and we did do some good. And I'm proud of the good that we did.\par \par \uc2 Why shut it down? Because it's very expensive. And I'm about to enter a world of public service where I'm going to have to take a big pay cut. It's a worthwhile pay cut because I want to do good for the people in this room and we got to accomplish a lot of good things. But that also means I can't continue to fund the nonprofit by itself, I can't continue to focus on it. And so that's why we decided to wind it down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Melissa (ph) from Gahanna. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Gahanna.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gahanna, I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Gahanna.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to get there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gahanna.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: These guys aren't from Ohio.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So we'll have to give them a little credit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm very sorry.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It's OK.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's fair. It's fair.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Hilliard and Gahanna.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know, we'll get there.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Do you believe in the integrity of Ohio's elections and what will be your message to Ohioans after the election if you don't win?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, ma'am. Very important question, I appreciate you asking it. I do believe in the integrity of Ohio's elections. I think we have great elections in the state of Ohio. I think, frankly, our secretary of state, if I can put a little plug in for \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , does a great job of administering our elections in the state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So I expect to win. I expect to get the great honor of going to Washington, D.C. and representing the people of Ohio in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . But, of course, if things don't go the way that I expect, I'll support the guy who wins and I'll try to be as supportive as I possibly can, even accepting that we are going to disagree on some big issues.\par \par \uc2 I will say just on this question of election integrity. I think there is a lot that gets caught up in the back and forth and the political, you know, we have a country with a really high temperature and so let's just be clear about the things that I think we can do to make our elections a little bit more secure.\par \par \uc2 I think -- again, I think Ohio is in a great place. But I think some other states could do a lot of work here. One is why don't we have universal voter I.D.? If you are going to vote, you ought to be able to present a voter I.D. you know, 80 percent of Americans across different demographic groups, across political parties agree with that and I think that there is some commonsense things that we can do to make our elections more secure to make sure the world's greatest constitutional republic stays that way.\par \par \uc2 And I think that advocating those commonsense measures does not mean, as some folks in the media say, that you are somehow an election denier. I just want our elections to be as good as they possibly can be. Ohio is a great model but I think other states could do a lot better.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you have said that you think the last presidential election was stolen. So define what you mean by that.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, I -- look, I have said that, and I won't run away from it. And here is particularly what I mean by that and here is what I worry about. So two things in particular.\par \par \uc2 Number one, a number of state courts, including lower courts in the state of Pennsylvania have found they didn't conduct their election in accordance with their own state constitution, in accordance with their own state laws. That's not me making it up. That's actually what these lower courts have held.\par \par \uc2 Well, you want important battleground states to do what Ohio has done, which is run free and fair elections, consistent secure elections that's really, really important. The second thing that I really worry about, and you hear a lot in America people worried about our democracy worried -- about threats to our democracy. I happen to think the biggest threat to American democracy today is big technology companies in bed with the communist Chinese who are censoring information about American politics.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: And this is -- this is really, really important because, look, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , you know, all these companies are now the digital public square. If those companies, which some of them have financial stakes in Communist China, if those companies are censoring information in a way that helps one political party or hurts another political party, I don't care if it's my political party being helped or hurt, I don't want these companies interfering in our elections and I think that happened in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIR: I assume -- I assume you heard Congressman Ryan before, and he had a lot to say about violence and about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: About Paul Pelosi, about other things. Not getting specific here but do you want to reply to any of those things that he brought up?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, I mean Tim should hire better researchers here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: What happens -- look, what happened to Paul Pelosi was disgusting. And I think everybody, whether we're Democrats or Republicans or somewhere in the middle are very glad that it looks like he's about to make a full recovery. Hopefully he is doing well. Certainly our thoughts and prayers are with Paul Pelosi, but I have condemned the violence against Paul Pelosi from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 I think it's preposterous, I think it's disgusting, and I think it's all -- something that all of us should condemn. What I've also said is that I think the effort to turn this into a political issue actually is a real problem here. Because Paul Pelosi was attacked --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, Paul Pelosi was attacked by a person who is an illegal alien in our country, should have never been here in the first place. My view very simply is that we need to deport violent illegal aliens, OK?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: And when an illegal alien attacks Paul Pelosi it's tragic and it's terrible but it's not reflective of Republicans. It's reflective of the fact that we let way too many violent people live freely in our country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask one more follow-up on just because this is what \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  brought up as well with regard to abortion. He said you want to ban abortion in Ohio and across this country. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, I'm pro-life. I am pro-life.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: The bill that I've -- now, look, there is a very, very important question about whether this should happen at the federal level or at the state level. And I happen to think it should be a little bit of a mix. In other words, 90 percent of abortion policy is going to be made by the state of Ohio. I think that's absolutely the right way it should be. Ohio is going to want to have a different abortion policy from California and I think every person in Ohio, whether you are pro-choice or pro-life, like I am, absolutely should be able to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a federal piece of legislation moving through the Senate right now and here's what it does. Very simply, it provides reasonable exceptions, but it also sets a minimum national standard.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that's a good idea. We should not in this country be aborting babies who can feel pain, who are fully formed.\par \par \uc2 That's my view and I'm certainly willing to support legislation that would make that a reality.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm going to go to Allan.\par \par \uc2 Allan from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  writes this: Maybe the chaos in society these days is a product of how our leaders behave towards one another. Maybe more civility towards each other and genuine attempts to understand opposing views might be helpful in turning this nation around.\par \par \uc2 What could you contribute towards that end?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, that's a great question, Allan, and I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I guess the most important thing that maybe I could do as a United States senator representing the great people in this room is -- is just to listen and communicate it a little bit better. You know what, I think one of the things that I worry about -- and, by the way, I mentioned the big tech issue earlier, I think the big tech industry has made this worse is, we don't talk to one another, OK?\par \par \uc2 We assume the very worst about everybody who disagrees with us. We assume that the people we see on television are reflective of everybody else who holds that particular viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I hope to bring to the Senate -- look, I'm a conservative, I'm pro-life, I think the inflation crisis is terrible and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 's policies have caused it. I also think that I can listen to people and have a conversation with people and that's unfortunately something we do way too little of in this country. Hopefully, I can make that better.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's get back to the audience. Joe has a question for you.\par \par \uc2 Joe?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're not going to say your town, Joe. Just not going to say it. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Looks (ph) like a Cleveland guy who -- they beat up on the Bengals last night. So, you're just feeling pretty good, Joe.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So, every year teachers like my mom from all over Ohio face challenges -- low pay, increasing pressure to accommodate parents, being overworked.\par \par \uc2 What are you going to do in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to make sure that teachers are helped out and that we have better schools for -- in all over Ohio?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, sir, yeah. Great question.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So, Joe, please send your mom my appreciation, I was actually brought up in a public school. And I think that one of the reasons why I came from a poor family and one of the things worked out pretty well for me is not just because of my mammaw (ph) and other people in my family, but because I had a lot of school teachers who took special interest in me, and I appreciate what people like your mom are doing all across the state of Ohio for kids who grew up like I did.\par \par \uc2 I think, obviously, a lot of our public school teachers could use better pay. They could use more resources. I have talked to a lot of public school teachers in the last couple of years who've talked about just how miserable the job is when you have federal mandates that have nothing to do with reading, writing and arithmetic but have to do with really ridiculous --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Have to do with cultural issues that I don't think the schools should be involved in one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, I talk to a lot of teachers who feel like the COVID pandemic actually -- not just the COVID pandemic but our leadership's response to it actually made it really hard for them to build good relationships with their kids and with the parents in the schools.\par \par \uc2 And I -- and I hope that, look, I'm not here to cast blame. I'm not here to sort of say, look, we made a lot of mistakes the last couple of years, all of us agree with that. I'm not here to blame the people who made the mistakes.\par \par \uc2 But we need to accept that we made a lot of mistakes that made kids' lives more miserable. It made teachers' lives more miserable. And I hope that we can learn and go forward and not repeat those mistakes in the future.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think we combine that with some resource support for our teachers. We'll hopefully have the next 20 years much better for our school teachers than the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think we have a teacher.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, we do have a teacher here with us who has a question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Linda (ph)?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Linda (ph)?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here you go.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hi. Mr. Vance, tell me about the most challenging manual labor job you ever held and what it taught you about life.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That's a good -- well --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a teacher question.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That's a teacher question, Linda (ph). That's right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're a teacher, of course.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. Thank you, Linda (ph).\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That's -- that's -- that's a very good question.\par \par \uc2 So, right after I -- I graduated from college, actually, I worked at a tile distributor. And if you know, floor tile is really, really heavy and probably that was the most challenging manual labor job that I've had. Though I've had a couple of manual labor jobs in my life.\par \par \uc2 And what did it teach me? I mean, I guess what it taught me is that -- look, you know, I went to college, I went to law school. I'm running for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  -- a lot of people that I talk to assume that people who work in manual labor jobs aren't that smart and they're just wrong. OK? That's one of the things that I learned, is that figuring out how to load 17 different types of floor tile on the same exact pallet requires a lot of smarts. OK?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You got to be brawny, but you got to be smart, too.\par \par \uc2 And it also taught me that, you know, the business that I worked at was a great local business in southwestern Ohio where I grew up. And the people who worked there earned really good wages and had a path to promotion and a path to betterment.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the worries that I have with a lot of the jobs that exist in our society today, because we did ship a lot of our manufacturing base off to China, is that people don't see a pathway in their jobs, right? You shouldn't just have a career pathway if you went to college. You should have a career pathway if you didn't go to college, OK?\par \par \uc2 And that, to me, is something that I learned at that job and, you know --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Unfortunately, I hate to say it, but I think a lot of our service sector jobs maybe don't provide that pathway or at least they don't make that pathway as clear to a lot of people that are out there.\par \par \uc2 But when you do that, I mean, people work harder, they feel like they've got a stake in what they're doing. They're much happier at work.\par \par \uc2 It was a cool place to work, I really enjoyed it. I had a great time. But I also spent time with people who were working their rear ends off but felt like they were doing something valuable.\par \par \uc2 And if we -- we make workers feel like they're doing something valuable because they all are. The question is whether we compensate them the right way. The question is whether people like me and other leadership send the right message.\par \par \uc2 If we do that, then I think we can have a very, very healthy working class in this country and that's what we want, because if you don't have healthy workers, you're never going to have great American success stories.\par \par \uc2 Isn't that what we all want in this country? No matter where you came from, no matter who your parents were, you get to live a good life in this country. It's really simple.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Being in the Marines was a little --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Manual labor there as well.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yeah, that's true. I -- but you actually gave me a softball.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: What I should have talked about was the Marine Corps.\par \par \uc2 Look, obviously, a lot -- a lot of manual labor in the Marine Corps. And I guess the thing that I learned from the Marine Corps is that you really can, if you have good leadership, you can take a Puerto Rican kid from the Bronx and a white hillbilly from southwestern Ohio and make them feel like they're part of the same team. That's one of the great things about (inaudible) --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. That's leaves us for our last question and that is -- it's more of a process question and it's a little in D.C. question. But it comes from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . A \uc2 Twitter\uc2  question here that says: will J.D. Vance vote for Leader McConnell for GOP leader in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 It would be one of your first votes as U.S. senator --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Maybe my first vote, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- if you win.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I'm certainly not voting for \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , OK? That's -- that's my view. So --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, I expect to win this Senate seat. I'm certainly working my rear end off for the next seven days to win this seat.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is the only person that runs, that makes it a very easy decision, OK? We'll see what happens when I get to Washington, D.C. but, at the end of the day, I'm going to represent the people of Ohio and I plan to be an independent voice for the people of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Whether you're Republican, whether you're a Democrat, whether you're an independent, nobody is going to own my vote card. Nobody is going to own how I actually represent the people of Ohio. That's the thing that I intend to keep in mind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: J.D. Vance, we wanted to keep it exactly the same time and we have effectively done that. We appreciate your time. Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, J.D. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we come back, a special Ohio town hall discussion for big issues and what we've heard so far tonight. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A show of hands, how many here think inflation and the economy is the number one issue in this race?\par \par \uc2 And you're one of them as well (ph).\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm one of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yeah, of course. When it comes to inflation, we need a tax cut. We need to put money in people's pockets.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: One of the reasons why we have this terrible inflation is because the Biden administration, backed by Tim, supported a lot of borrowing and spending, throwing fuel on the fire which has caused the price of everything to go up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to our town hall in Ohio. Just heard from Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance, the Republican Senate nominee.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in senior political analyst Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, let's get your thoughts on tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it was an interesting exchange --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, also we've got Haley BeMiller --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Haley BeMiller's with us --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hold on -- hold on, Brit.\par \par \uc2 Haley BeMiller of "\uc2 USA Today Network\uc2 ", the Ohio bureau of political reporter, and Nic Evans, reporter with "The Ohio Capitol Journal".\par \par \uc2 OK, Brit, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Good exchange. Both men conducted themselves effectively. Very competent candidates, both of them.\par \par \uc2 It was interesting, though, Congressman Ryan, it seemed to me, had a bit of an additional burden because his party is unpopular and his president is unpopular. So what you saw him do is to try to run away from his party on some issues, for example calling for a tax cut. When is the last time you heard a Democrat do that? And running toward on others such as on abortion, for example.\par \par \uc2 The issue I thought might burden his challenger, J.D. Vance, was Trump. But Trump's name was never mentioned. So J.D. Vance, who got pretty well snuggled up to Trump, has been able to move away from him, I guess, at least in terms of the emphasis in his campaign and I think he had a pretty good night.\par \par \uc2 All in all, you can see why this race is close. Two competent candidates.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, with regard to that, Brit, you know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  talked about candidate quality. And in that group were a number of people who are running for Senate who have now pretty much tied things up in places like Pennsylvania and Georgia and here in Ohio. What do you think about that in this stage of the game?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, it's interesting to see that happen. But it's -- look, if you look at the playing field, Martha, in terms of the issues and circumstances of this election, it's not surprising to see Republicans gaining ground at this stage of the race and earlier as well. That's where the -- that's where races are usually decided.\par \par \uc2 We are so polarized, however, Martha, that may help overcome the advantage Republicans enjoy in terms of the circumstances --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HUME: -- and the landscape that we all face at this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Brit.\par \par \uc2 Haley, your thoughts tonight as this town hall and what came out of it?\par \par \uc2 HALEY BEMILLER, POLITICAL REPORTER, USA TODAY NETWORK OHIO BUREAU: You know, really not surprising that economy and inflation were top of mind. It was also interesting to hear Ryan clarify his views on abortion a little bit more. He had been hesitant to discuss, you know, his thoughts on what should happen later into the pregnancy. He made it clear tonight that he does not support abortion late in a pregnancy unless there are medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Inflation, abortion, those have been two big issues for Ohio.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it's interesting. When you follow a campaign, you listen to those nuances, the things that change. Nick, did you hear other things tonight?\par \par \uc2 NICK EVANS, REPORTER, OHIO CAPITAL JOURNAL: It was interesting to hear Vance bring up the RAISE Act. I hadn't heard him mention that before. The - - his three-part plan for the border he's been talking about since January. So it was interesting to hear that come back up.\par \par \uc2 It was interesting to hear \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  talk about Issue 1. That has to do with whether or not judges have to consider the amount of cash when they set bail. They can already think about public safety when they -- when they set terms. But they don't -- they don't get to use public safety to determine the cash amount.\par \par \uc2 So, that was interesting to hear him go with the opposite of what the Democratic Party in the state is backing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's fascinating.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We heard that a few times, yeah.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, you guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Haley, Nick, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, that concludes our special Ohio town hall tonight. We want to thank our sponsor this hour, Tunnel to Towers. Donate $11 a month to Tunnel to Towers at t2t.org. It's fantastic organization.\par \par \uc2 And we want to thank Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance for being with us tonight, for taking all the questions from our audience.\par \par \uc2 And we want to thank our fantastic live audience for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very nicely done. Very nicely done.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's if for "The Special Report" -- fair, balanced and unafraid.\par \par \uc2 "Jesse Watters Primetime" starts right now. And we are one week away from election.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221102eib100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 With A Week To Go Until Midterms, Republicans Are Ahead In Key States\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kellyanne Conway, Mike Huckabee, Ari Fleischer,  Kayleigh McEnany \par \uc2 8,539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. And, Tucker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here we are only eight days to go, tonight, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Dr. Oz, Senator Ron Johnson, they will all join us straight ahead. These races will likely determine the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and it absolutely matters to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will bring you the latest polls analysis as now Republicans continue to surge all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, even Washington state is very much in play. That Senate race, look at this right here, is a statistical tie. Real Clear Politics just moved it into the toss-up column, but let's be clear.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that does matter is what happens on election day, so take nothing for granted. That means everybody has to vote. Everybody has to participate.\par \par \uc2 Now, still at this point, Democrats, they are looking increasingly desperate. And as per usual and as we predicted, they are attempting to scare you, the American people, with the same old lies and smears that we see every single election year.\par \par \uc2 I said at the very beginning of this election year, the Democrats, they have nothing positive to run on. That has not changed. They will run on three things I said. One, January 6th, I hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That has fizzled.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision and that in many ways has backfired since so many Democrats want zero restrictions on abortion, meaning that they support abortion up until the moment of birth. That is quite out of touch with most of the American people.\par \par \uc2 Number three, you know the drill according to them, Republicans you are racist sexist homophobic xenophobic, Islamophobic, transphobic, you want dirty air and water, you want to throw granny and grandpa off a cliff, and you want deep cuts you, want to eliminate Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 Of course, none of that is true, but after two horrific years under the most incompetent president and Democratic Party in history, this is all they have left. There's nothing in their playbook.\par \par \uc2 So now as "Politico" rightly points out, Democrats are turning to the anointed one. He's back and he's trying to hope and pray that he can help drive home their lying, dishonest narrative and save them from a midterm shellacking. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They want to gut Social Security and Medicare, and then give some more tax breaks to the wealthy. And the reason -- the reason I know that's their agendas -- listen, that's their answer to everything. If there was an asteroid headed towards earth, it's going to land in like two weeks.\par \par \uc2 If you went in the Republican caucus and you said what do you want to do -- well, we need a tax break for the wealthy. That's going to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And if that asteroid was headed towards earth, you'd blame \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and say he sent it.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, \uc2 Obama\uc2 's lying. There's not a single Republican candidate planning to cut Medicare or Social Security, not one. Sadly, Democrats no longer seem to value truth and logic unless it helps them politically. Remember, according to the White House, inflation is only transitory. We have zero inflation at a point when it was a 41-year high. Gas prices are down -- no, they're double what they were. The border is secure.\par \par \uc2 And as New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  claims America's violent crime surge has nothing more than a vast right-wing conspiracy, it's not true, it's not happening, don't believe your very eyes when you watch the news every night. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D), NEW YORK: Reverend Al, these are master manipulators. They have this conspiracy going all across America to try and convince people that in Democratic states, they're not as safe. Well, guess what, they're also not only election deniers, they're data deniers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay, here's some data for \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and for the Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 Now, for last year, New York City recorded the single highest murder rate in a decade, the most shootings in 15 years, carjacking's governor they're up over 200 percent, major crime is up 36 percent, retail theft because you don't arrest people that steal less than a thousand dollars, that's surging like never before.\par \par \uc2 It's not just New York either, crime is spiking almost everywhere. Democrats are in charge by the -- as a matter of fact 11 of the 15 deadliest places to live in this country are totally run by Democrats, not by Republicans. So make no mistake. The left and their defund dismantle no bail movement has wreaked havoc on major cities and towns all across the country.\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to the horrific attack on Speaker Pelosi's husband Paul. According to reports, he was assaulted with a hammer inside his own home by an intruder, quote, looking for Nancy.\par \par \uc2 The assailant we now know to be an illegal immigrant is a hyper paranoid, pro-nudity activists who allegedly lives in a storage shed and believes in QAnon conspiracies and attacks like this by the way. They should never happen. We believe in law and order as conservatives.\par \par \uc2 And as I've said over and over again, every elected official and their families must be protected.\par \par \uc2 You know what? We provide a private jet. It's my understanding for Speaker Pelosi to travel to San Francisco and back, I would assume for security reasons. Why wouldn't we provide her Secret Service protection or any speaker of the House? After the vice president, it is the speaker of the House that would be in line to be president.\par \par \uc2 We wish Paul Pelosi a full and speedy recovery. This is not political. Everyone deserves to be safe, everyone.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, many on the left, they want this to be political. They want voters to believe that the illegal immigrant nudists to attack Paul Pelosi is representative of conservatives in America. He's not there. By the way, even accusing Republicans of fomenting violence. That is a lie too.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, they were singing a very different tune. Remember when that \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  super fan shot up a baseball field full of Republican lawmakers, and you might remember just this year, they all but ignored the assassination attempt against Supreme Court Justice Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 And when Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , remember he was violently assaulted, many on the left, they just mocked it. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's own daughter even praised the attack and the media mob, they thought it was hysterical. Just a quick reminder.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: New details today on the incident that left Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  with six broken ribs. This might be one of my favorite stories, although, of course, we don't -- one is clearly Senator Paul is still struggling.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter Jonathan Martin says some sort of, quote, flora issue around the properties caused the neighborhood fracas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're flora, very serious.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They really do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The singer said on social media, quote, I'll say it again. If I ever meet \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 's neighbor, I'm going to hug him and buy him as many drinks as he can consume.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now many in the media mob have the gall to bash President Trump or what waiting two whole days before condemning the attack. I thought they didn't want to hear from Mr. Trump. Now, they want to hear from Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump called the assault a horrible thing, a terrible thing. Also condemned the violent crime wave in San Francisco, Chicago, and other major cities.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, Democrats they didn't quite like that part. Remember, the only value the truth how if it helps them politically. In other words, if the violence, they can use it for political purposes, oh, let's talk about it. You might recall in the summer of 2020, we had 574 riots, we had dozens of dead, Americans we had bricks, rocks, bottles, Molotov, cocktails thrown to police officers, thousands of them were injured. We had billions in property damage and most Democrats were either silent or lied to us and told us they were mostly peaceful protests. Where were they then?\par \par \uc2 And by the way Paul Pelosi was the victim of a deranged individual. It's sad. But it's happening in city after city and town after town, and it's becoming increasingly lawless in this country for everyone in large part because of Democratic policies.\par \par \uc2 When you stop prosecuting crimes, when you let violent felons out of jail, when you prevent the federal government from deporting illegal immigrants, when you excuse away violent riots like we saw in the summer of 2020, when you believe in defund and dismantle police departments, when you demoralize police, when you come up with these idiotic no bail laws, yet guess what? Crime goes up in communities and towns and cities and people suffer and law-abiding Americans, they are victimized except we don't know their names. They don't get the coverage that Paul Pelosi got.\par \par \uc2 And anybody that gets hurt because of these bad policies, at what point do we blame the politicians that put these idiotic, imbecilic policies in place? Now, this could have happen anywhere including the great state of Georgia, where \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is now threatening to upend the safety and security in her state. During her debate, she even suggested that the police in Georgia are racist, good old boys. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  (R), GEORGIA: Men and women in law enforcement know who is going to be with them, who has had their back and will continue to have their back and that is me. And that's why we have the endorsement of sheriffs around this state.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDDATE: As I pointed out before, I'm not a member of the good old boys club. So, no, I don't have 107 sheriffs who want to be able to take black people off the streets who want to be able to go without accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , she's not telling the truth. But again, Democrats, they don't value the truth.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here now with more senior counselor to President Trump, Kellyanne Conway, former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  and FOX News contributor \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, you look at \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , for example, and then you look at the statistics, the murder rate, the crime rate, the arson rate, the burglary rate, and what part of what's happening is in her state is she not understanding, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Sure, I think her blunder on the debate stage was akin to \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  a year ago when he said I'm going to make sure parents don't have a say in their child's education and he lost. And this is -- for \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  to not realize that, not only is reality, reality, we see it's an occupational hazard to use the subway.\par \par \uc2 We see that people are walking down the street, being randomly assaulted and worse. But also when it comes to crime perception is reality. If the people of New York don't feel safe and so many of them are telling us they don't feel safe, that's enough for a governor to take action. But \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has run a masterful campaign by putting crime front and center and public safety, and, of course, the Democrats cannot walk back defund the police. Those are the three most poisonous words said by a political party in quite some time, Sean. They can't walk it back.\par \par \uc2 And let me say this, it's not just in New York and it's not just in our major cities which are run by Democrats where the rates of crime are going up. It really is everywhere. If people feel insecure and unsafe, if this election is about everyday affordability but it's also about physical security, border security, national security, health security, economic security, if it's about all of that, then, of course, that's going to help the party that is willing to go out there and say no cashless bail. We are here to punish criminals not to punish the victims.\par \par \uc2 And people right now in New York, we just released a poll where locals only up one point, both she and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  are 46-45, under 50 percent, and he's really closing the gap because he has put crime front and center. But let's also not forget, Sean, that unfortunately the Zeldin family knows crime very personally. He was attacked on the stage by a crazy person with a weapon and then his two teenage daughters were home doing their homework, and somebody was on his porch.\par \par \uc2 So this is very personal to many folks. 1994 top two issues, crime in the economy. We had the Contract with America. But now, those two issues are so much more personal and front and center.\par \par \uc2 And you have an entire Democratic party telling the country, don't believe what you see, believe what I say. That's why voters are rejecting that.\par \par \uc2 You know, Governor Huckabee, you look at things. Their economic policies caused the 40-year high of inflation. Their decision not to produce energy domestically, that has caused the price of gasoline to soar as high as it is, and by the way, wait until people get their heating bills this winter.\par \par \uc2 They went with open borders and abandoned the policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that were working. They're the ones that want a woke education versus, you know, the old-fashioned reading, writing, math, and history education. They're the ones that want defund, dismantle and no bail loss.\par \par \uc2 They have made decisions and the consequences are now evident to everybody. In eight days, the American people get to vote on whether they like these policies or don't like these policies.\par \par \uc2 Are you better off than you were, what, 20 months ago, 21 months ago. I think the answer is overwhelmingly no. I don't think they have anything successful to run on and I don't see a lot of success for them in this midterm election.\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think what you just said is exactly right. I think it's interesting that their strategy right now is to bring \uc2 Obama\uc2  out of mothballs. But, you know, I'm going to say something that you'll probably disagree with, Sean, but I kind of find it entertaining that \uc2 Obama\uc2  is back on this stage.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's blowing smoke out of both nostrils both ears and the rest of the parts of his body and he's telling a bunch of stuff that isn't true, like we're going to kill Social Security and Medicare. Total nonsense.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this, it was kind of entertaining to watch him because at least he's somewhat funny and he can put a whole sentence together and if you look across the Democrat spectrum, they've got a bunch of candidates --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Governor, that's a pretty -- that's a pretty high bar. He could actually put a sentence together. That's an amazing accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: And, Sean, when he's finished with his speech, he doesn't shake hands with the ghost and he doesn't wander around the stage trying to figure out how the heck do I leave here.\par \par \uc2 So, yeah, if I were a Democrat running, I'd bring \uc2 Obama\uc2  in too because it's all he got.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, I don't think that's pet advice, Ari Pleasure. I'm not sure that that's going to actually resonate.\par \par \uc2 Look, Americans, if we're going to break it down simply, they vote for peace, they vote for prosperity. I can't think of a single policy Democrats and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have implemented that have benefited the American people, \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , and to me, it's that simple, it's that fundamental, it's that basic.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: On the other big fundamental simple thing is the first midterm election of a president's time in office is always a course correction. But this year, Sean, it looks like it really is a genuine wave.\par \par \uc2 And the way I look at it, if the Democrats had a perfect night, they could win 40 seats in the Senate. They'd have to knock off the Republicans in Pennsylvania, Ohio, North Carolina and Wisconsin. I think Republicans can hold all four of those seats now, that's pretty clear.\par \par \uc2 Republicans on a perfect wave night could win 56 seats. A week ago, I would have told you I don't see it happening. I don't see 56. Now I can tell you, Sean, I think there's about a 25 percent chance the wave is big enough Republicans are going to end up with 56 seats. That's New Hampshire. That's Georgia. That's Nevada. That's Arizona, Colorado and Washington state.\par \par \uc2 That's what happens in a wave. People wake up shocked. They just didn't see it coming but in 2014, the second midterm of \uc2 Obama\uc2 , Republicans gained nine seats in the Senate and no one saw that coming either. That's the thing about a wave election, you don't know it until you're soaked, you're under it, and then the other party is jubilant.\par \par \uc2 That is the possibility a week from tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That may be a pretty happy guy a week from tomorrow if that in fact happens.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne, what do you see happening?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Oh, it's going to be a big Republican night. I mean, the Democrats aren't even trying to solve the problem, Sean, they're still arguing with the public about what the problems are.\par \par \uc2 But I think the \uc2 Obama\uc2  question is a really important one. Vintage Obama was about hope and change. But now, he's out on a recovery and rescue mission, and he seems like he has a boulder on his shoulder, you know, peddling anger and lying about the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And may I just remind everybody of \uc2 Obama\uc2 's record when he's not on the ballot. He's hardly the Mariano Rivera, the closer for these midterm elections. On his watch, over eight years, the Democrats lost 12 governor seats went from 28 to 16. They lost nine Senate seats. They lost 950 state legislative seats, and they lost -- they went from 254 to 196 in the House, as 62 seats lost in the House.\par \par \uc2 So his batting average, if you will, is not so great, when he's not on the ballot. And look, I also think \uc2 Obama\uc2 's message is tone deaf. He did a really good thing a couple weeks ago on a podcast, he said, hey, stop talking about all these other issues, start talking about the economy and then he goes out and he talks about everything but the economy. But I'm with Ari on this, I actually make another prediction, I don't think a single Republican House or Senate incumbent will lose.\par \par \uc2 There are a couple who are embattled right now. I think they'll be dragged across the finish line in 2020, not a single House Republican incumbent lost. We turned 15 seats from blue to red. Every one of those seats was taken by a woman, a veteran, or a candidate of color or some combination thereof. We've recruited such unbelievably diverse candidates this time in the Republican Party, and they're there and now they now the party's investing in them.\par \par \uc2 Six digit investment in some of these congressional districts, Sean, and gentlemen where Biden carried those districts by points or so double digits two short years. We'll say that big night.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I will say, I am impressed that you remembered every single election post-\uc2 Obama\uc2 . And you're right, he did have a terrible track record in terms of people that he endorsed over time. Governor Huckabee, besides a huge victory that we're both expecting in the great state of Arkansas, and a new governor there, what do you see happening a week from tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: I think Ari has nailed it pretty well, and I want to make sure you understand, I didn't say \uc2 Obama\uc2  was effective. I just said he was entertaining. There's a big difference.\par \par \uc2 And I hope he continues to go out there because he reminds everybody that his policies and the things that he did set up for the Republicans to take power in 2016 and he may remind him, that maybe it's time to go back to the Republicans yet again. I think that's what we're going to see, and I would second the motion to what Ari had to say and I think he may be spot on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hope Ari is -- Ari, if you're right, you're going to get the gold star, you get the last word.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Well, the other thing about President \uc2 Obama\uc2  because I just can't resist, remember, he endorsed Hillary when he was a sitting president. And how did that work out?\par \par \uc2 So he was at the height of his power, an incumbent in office. He played an active role campaigning for and hoping for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. So, you know, endorsements really don't mean much in the modern political era. What matters are the fundamentals. That's the economy, that's crime, that's inflation. And it's the wave.\par \par \uc2 Wave elections are rare, but when they hit. They're big, and that's what we're heading into, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think we are too. I hope we're all right.\par \par \uc2 All right. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we'll look at some of these important races. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Dr. Oz, Senator Ron Johnson, they will all join us. We'll get the latest on their campaigns. We'll look at the latest polls.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz just picked up a massive endorsement. We'll tell you about that straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Here we are, only eight short days from the most crucial midterm elections, I can't really convey through words the urgency that I feel for the country. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the Democrat Party, our country is clearly now imploding. Nothing they have done is working and nothing they are advocating for will work.\par \par \uc2 And now, we turn to the state of Georgia tonight. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has now pulled ahead of the far left incumbent. That is Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in a neck and neck race, holding a three-point lead, according to Insider Advantage. But now, even the extremely left-wing liberal "AJC", no friend of mine, well, they have Herschel up.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it's why Democrats are now desperate as ever and they're now turning as we said earlier to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , in this case, to smear \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , claiming he's nothing more than a celebrity politician. And he's not a pilot. You wouldn't want to fly a plane. Well, neither is \uc2 Obama\uc2 . You wouldn't want him to fly a plane.\par \par \uc2 Obama\uc2  can't defend the record of Warnock, can't defend the radical record of \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . So, he retreats to the politics of personal destruction, typical, predictable. I told you at the beginning of the year, it would happen.\par \par \uc2 Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: In the case of Reverend Warnock's opponent, there is very little evidence that he has taken any interest bother to learn anything about or displayed any kind of inclination towards public service or volunteer work or helping people in any way, at least we don't really know about it, and that does make you suspect. Seems to me he's a celebrity who wants to be a politician.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, he was a politician who never showed up and he voted present more often than not when he was in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 Here now is Republican Senate candidate from Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, I just might remember you might have been on the president's physical fitness program. You were on that board if I'm not mistaken.\par \par \uc2 And if I'm not mistaken, for how many years have you been running camps, fitness camps for children, and you might want to talk a little bit about your other volunteering work and other things you've done that apparently \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  knows nothing about.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: Well, he don't care to know about it because right now, they would say and do anything to win this seat but they're not going to win it from me. This seat is too important, and I want the Georgia people to know that right now, they're doing whatever they can and they got to go through me to win that seat, so they're trying to put me down, but they're not going to win. And they didn't talk about the crime, did they?\par \par \uc2 Right now, crime is up. They didn't talk about this inflation. Inflation is up. This border is wide open. They didn't talk about that and the reason why is because of Senator Warnock. Senator Warnock was a deciding factor in many of these things. He even voted to put men in women sports. And they don't want to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Well, they're going to have to come through me because they're coming from me right now, they're coming for you tomorrow. But they're not going to get to you because they got to go through me first.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And I will fight for this because Senator Warnock is not right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, one thing they have in common is they both praise the Reverend Jeremiah Wright. I got a picture of \uc2 Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Louis Farrakhan\uc2 . Warnock has praised Farrakhan and in Warnock's case, I don't even think \uc2 Obama\uc2  did this, but Warnock praised \uc2 Fidel Castro\uc2 . So they seem to have an inclination to like pretty extreme people.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, they have been extreme (ph) for a lot of things. You see what he said about abortion. He believes that a baby that survives an abortion, he voted to deny that baby medical care. How extreme is that?\par \par \uc2 But yet, they want to talk about me. And I said, come for me if you like, I'm going to fight for this seat because this seat is too important. To have someone like a Marxist like Senator Warnock representing the great people of Georgia, because I am Georgia born, Georgia bred, and when I die, I'll be Georgia dead.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to let them continue to put the Georgia people down through their racist, what -- the things they say about the Georgia people is not true and I want people to get out and vote. Let's not send someone like Senator Warnock back to Washington to represent us because he only represent one side, that's going to be the black side.\par \par \uc2 I represent everybody, black, white, yellow, green, everyone that's a Georgia citizen, you're my family, and I'm not going to let Reverend Warnock and I'm not going to let ex-President \uc2 Obama\uc2  affect my family or hurt my family.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: America must repent for its whiteness. She cannot serve God and the military. Praise the nation of Islam, compared police officers to thugs and gangsters. Is that the guy you're running against?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, that's the guy I'm running against. A guy that says all those things, he hide behind these sealed court records. He hides behind so many things. He's a hypocrite.\par \par \uc2 I called him out and everyone should have known the adult in the room during the debate with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because I took him to school. When these boys have taken me to school, he was supposed to be the one that was going to teach me, but I taught him. He wouldn't even answer questions anymore. I hope people saw that he was not the person he claimed to be. He was that wolf in sheep clothing that I've been talking about, and he don't represent Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And I showed it he represented \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he's been doing it the whole time and to have President \uc2 Obama\uc2  to come here when he told us to vote for the President Biden. Are you serious? And you see where we're at today. In less than two years, this is what we got. Do you think we can take six more years of this? We can't. We can't take six more years of this because they would destroy this country.\par \par \uc2 So that's why I encourage everyone to go to teamhearsal.com and I have to say that, Sean, because we got to stop these people. I cannot believe that they won't talk about the crime, they won't talk about this open border. They won't talk about this inflation. They won't talk about men and women's sports. They won't talk about things people out in the streets are talking about.\par \par \uc2 People are worried about. They want to try to smear me, but do what you want with me because sticks and stone may break my bone, but words are never going to hurt me. I'm going to fight for this seat because it's too important for them to be sitting in it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And clearly, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has you on his mind in that hot mic moment. I think they were talking about you, Herschel. Anyway, the important race. Republicans, that would be a major pickup for them.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Also, major breaking news out of this commonwealth of Pennsylvania tonight where the hometown paper of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , that's right, the trust fund brat, "The \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2 " has endorsed officially Dr. Oz in what was a scathing editorial, laying out all of Fetterman's struggles from the editorial.\par \par \uc2 Quote: Fetterman's lack of transparency however in refusing to release his medical records is troubling. It suggests an impulse to conceal and a mistrust of the people. It continues: Mr. Fetterman, despite his hoodies and shorts and has little experience in holding real jobs or facing the problems of working people.\par \par \uc2 The editorial closes with this: In a race on which much depends and little is certain, Mehmet Oz has shown that he is better equipped to lead and deliver for Pennsylvania. Fetterman is not fit to serve and his pro- criminal record would be a disaster for the great people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Fetterman voted over and over and over again to release violent criminals while on the parole board, even convicted murderers. He also said that he wants to release a third or more prisoners from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And get this, newly resurfaced audio now shows Fetterman suggesting Republican voters all of you people in Pennsylvania, you're a bunch of racist. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Now there are some in the Republican Party that are unreachable. Nationalism, xenophobia, homophobia, just overall -- I mean, that's the southern strategy, right?\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Isn't this the same guy that chased an innocent African-American that was out jogging, doing nothing wrong down the street? And according to that individual, he took a shotgun pointed it right at his chest and held him hostage until the police came? That's \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  that did that. Not a Republican.\par \par \uc2 Also, not to worry because according to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Fetterman, he's my kind of guy. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yeah, I thought he was really good. I thought he knew what he was doing. I thought he was strong. And look, Fetterman is Pennsylvania. I mean, Fetterman is everything that he appears to be. You know where he stands, he has a great courage, he has no reluctance to say what he thinks. He's my kind of guy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: My kind of guy. Okay, Dr. Oz is ahead in this race now by three points, according to the latest Insider Advantage poll, surging at just the right time. Fetterman is now struggling as much as ever, especially after the debate disaster.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Pennsylvania Senate candidate, Dr. Oz, is with us.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz, welcome back to the program.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, I guess \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's making the rounds. He's going to be headed to the commonwealth of Pennsylvania. You think he'll have any impact on the race?\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I doubt it and I think what you witnessed during the debate was that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  can't defend his radical views. I don't think of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  can't either, nor can \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . These extreme positions are out of touch with Pennsylvania values.\par \par \uc2 And you just did a couple examples out there, but fundamentally, if you're thinking more about protecting the criminals than the innocent than the victims, which again what Fetterman did was to push against the other members of the parole board to get murderers out, because he's acknowledged that if he had a magic wand, the one thing you'd want to do is get rid of life in prison for felony murder. This is just bizarre.\par \par \uc2 And especially at a time when we have a murder rate here in Philadelphia, it's the highest we have ever had. Just had our thousandth carjacking. The city is an unmanageable mess, big businesses are leaving.\par \par \uc2 But here's the part that catches me off guard. I wonder if the viewers agree with me. After that debate, when there was issue after issue after issue where Fetterman could not defend taking radical positions, he was able to raise millions of dollars because all the Democrats want to do is control the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So help me fight back. Go to doctoroz.com, com. We will be able to win this race if I can tell the truth about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  more than he lies about me. But what you're witnessing is a general belief by the Democratic Party that you don't have to address the kitchen table issues of crime, of the border, which is allowing fentanyl to make every state a border state, of the economic crises that we're facing now, getting worse by the day, gas prices went up today, again, and it's going to be ten dollars a gallon in Europe. But it's going to get back to five dollars a gallon here in Pennsylvania and probably much of the country if we don't do something about it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He lied during the debate when he said he was never for a moratorium on fracking. We have multiple tapes. We played him many times. In fact, he did say it, numerous times. He wants no restrictions on abortion. There's never been a candidate in the history of the Senate that I can think of that's advocated to release more convicted murderers and violent criminals than him, a guy that brags about his association with the group Black Lives Matters and the street gang, the Crips.\par \par \uc2 He wants taxpayers to pay for heroin injection sites. He wants sanctuary city and state status all over Pennsylvania, and then you have that little shotgun incident as he dares to call Republicans racist.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, you just listed a litany of problems that he has with law enforcement. That's why the Paternal Order of Police endorsed me unanimously, because they dislike him so much, and they -- his hometown paper, he just mentioned "The \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2 " which is a fabulous paper, generally wouldn't endorse the Republican. God bless them they came up to our side. They've had enough as well. Their big paper in Pittsburgh, the biggest.\par \par \uc2 And so, everyone's sort of saying the same thing. Let cops do their job. Make prosecutors do their job. Let politicians like Fetterman and me, we should both answer questions from voters. He has not done that yet on the campaign trail. He's not answered questions from the press on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 And that debate was the first and only time he'll take questions for me. They were trying to run the clock out, but they didn't get away with it. And now, Pennsylvanians pretty smart folk are looking at the results of the debate, thinking about why he can't answer simple questions about his radical positions, and realize he doesn't have good defenses for them. They sound great in 140 character tweet. You know, it's great if you're going to be the AOC of the Senate, which is his goal.\par \par \uc2 But I'm not going to let him get there. Pennsylvania is too important and listen this is important. We do not have a Republican senator north of North Carolina on the Atlantic Coast, until you get to Maine if I don't hold this seat. And there's been a Republican senator in Pennsylvania most of my life, I'm going to keep one here as well. And this is a state that because it's so important to the country, a top five state, we want a Republican presence here to make sure it's well-represented, along with other states that have our issues.\par \par \uc2 Big cities like Philadelphia that have some issues around poverty, a lot of energy in the state, we've got to make sure these issues are addressed Republicans can do it well.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, I'd like to remind people when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is there campaigning for Mr. Fetterman, that it was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  who referred to the people of Pennsylvania as bitter Americans that cling to their god, guns, their bibles and religion. I hope people didn't forget that part and maybe somebody in the media might have enough guts to ask \uc2 Obama\uc2  if he still believes that about the people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz, thanks for being with us. We'll follow this race closely the next eight days.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are now turning as we say to \uc2 Obama\uc2  to try and avoid that midterm wipeout, rather than focus on actual issues facing voters, like the border, inflation, surging crime. \uc2 Obama\uc2  is resorting to the same old tired talking points and the same old lies about Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Here he's suggesting that Senator Ron Johnson wants to gut Social Security. It's an absolute lie but he says it anyway. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Some of your parents are on Social Security. Some of your grandparents are on Social Security. You know why they ask Social Security? Because they worked for it.\par \par \uc2 They worked hard jobs for it. They have chapped hands for it. They had long hours and sore backs and bad knees to get that Social Security.\par \par \uc2 And if Ron Johnson does not understand that, if he understands giving tax breaks for private planes more than he understands making sure that seniors who've watched all their lives are able to retire with dignity and respect, he's not the person who's thinking about you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Sore knees, bad backs, working your whole life -- I wonder how those same people are feeling now that the average American household is paying seven thousand two hundred dollars on average because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  inflation. Is anybody in the media ever going to ask the anointed one that question?\par \par \uc2 Johnson is now facing another extremist. His name, Mandela Barnes, he supports the pro-left pro-criminal agenda like ending cash bail. He's also glorified violent rioting in the summer of 2020. He is a rubber stamp of the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 I hope people might remind people \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  pointed out that the average household now has to pay because of Biden inflation seven thousand two hundred dollars on average for the very same items that they were buying under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I would argue that the people that need their Social Security and Medicare that you're not going to take away from them are getting hurt more that way than by the lies \uc2 Obama\uc2  is now telling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Yeah,